                <h4>The Kubernetes Release Cycle — What an Admin Must Track</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes ships <strong>three minor versions a year</strong> (~every 4 months: v1.31, v1.32, v1.33…). As the admin, keeping the cluster inside the supported window — and upgrading it safely — is <em>your</em> recurring job, and a guaranteed exam task. Each release has a life:</p>
                <div class="evolution-strip">
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🆕</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">Month 0</div>
                        <h5>New Release</h5>
                        <p>e.g. v1.33 ships — new features, deprecations, and bug fixes land.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Latest</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item active">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🛡️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">~0–14 months</div>
                        <h5>Supported</h5>
                        <p>Gets patch releases (security &amp; bug fixes). The CKA tests within this window.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Patched</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">⚠️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">14+ months</div>
                        <h5>End of Life</h5>
                        <p>No more patches. Running EOL = a growing security risk → you must upgrade.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status bad">❌ Unsupported</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Golden Upgrade Rule: one minor version at a time</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes only supports upgrading <strong>one minor version at a step</strong> — <code>1.31 → 1.32 → 1.33</code>. You <strong>cannot skip</strong> (no 1.31 → 1.33 jump). There's also a <strong>version-skew</strong> rule: the kubelet may lag the API server by up to 3 minors but never run ahead of it. The CKA puts you in front of a real cluster and asks you to upgrade it with <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (control plane first, then workers) — you'll do exactly this in Chapter 28.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Exam Day Actually Looks Like</h4>
                <p>Knowing the format removes half the stress. The CKA is taken <strong>remotely, in your browser</strong>, proctored live through the PSI platform — here's the flow end-to-end:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Check-in</h5><p>Show photo ID, do a 360° room scan via webcam. Desk must be clear; you're watched the whole time.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>The terminal</h5><p>A real Linux terminal in the browser, with multiple clusters. You SSH/context-switch between them per task.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>15–20 tasks</h5><p>Each is weighted and independent. A score bar shows weights — do the cheap, high-value ones first.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Docs allowed</h5><p>One extra tab to <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code> (+ blog, GitHub). Nothing else. Practice copy-paste from the docs.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Flag &amp; move on</h5><p>Stuck &gt; a few minutes? Flag it, move on, return later. Never leave a task blank — partial credit is real.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Results &amp; retake</h5><p>Score by email in ~24 h. 66% to pass. One <strong>free retake</strong> included; the cert is valid <strong>3 years</strong>.</p></div>
