                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🧠 Control Plane Components — the admin must know these (Architecture = 25%)</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📡</span> kube-apiserver</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>front door</strong> — ALL communication goes through it (kubectl, node heartbeats, controllers). It's the <em>only</em> component that talks to etcd. If it's down, the cluster is blind.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗄️</span> etcd</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The cluster's <strong>memory</strong> — a distributed key-value store holding ALL cluster state. <strong>CKA tests backup &amp; restore.</strong> Lose etcd with no backup = lose the whole cluster.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> kube-scheduler</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>matchmaker</strong> — watches for unscheduled Pods and picks the best node by resources, affinity, and taints/tolerations.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> kube-controller-manager</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>watchdog</strong> — runs control loops that compare desired vs actual and act ("only 2 of 3 replicas → create 1 more").</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">☁️</span> cloud-controller-manager</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Cloud glue</strong> (optional) — runs the cloud provider's controllers: <strong>node</strong> lifecycle, <strong>route</strong> setup, and <strong>service</strong> load-balancers. (Volume/disk attachment is handled by out-of-tree <strong>CSI</strong>, not here.) Absent on bare-metal.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What Happens When Each Component Goes Down?</h4>
                <p>Memorizing what each component <em>does</em> is half the battle; the deeper understanding — and what the 30% troubleshooting domain really tests — is knowing what <strong>breaks</strong> when each one fails. This table is worth more than any definition, because it turns "the scheduler schedules pods" into "if pods are stuck <code>Pending</code>, look at the scheduler":</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>If this is down…</th><th>What you observe</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td><code>kubectl</code> fails; nothing can be changed. <em>Running pods keep running.</em></td><td>It's the only door to the cluster — no reads/writes, no scheduling, no controller actions. The cluster "freezes" but doesn't crash.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>API server can't serve → whole control plane unusable. Lost with no backup = cluster gone.</td><td>It holds <em>all</em> state. No etcd, no source of truth to read or write.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>New pods stay <code>Pending</code> forever. Existing pods are unaffected.</td><td>Nothing is left to assign pods to nodes — they sit unscheduled.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td>No self-healing: a crashed pod isn't replaced, scaling/rollouts stall, dead nodes aren't acted on.</td><td>The reconciliation loops live here — without them, desired ≠ actual is never corrected.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong> (on a node)</td><td>That node goes <code>NotReady</code>; after a grace period its pods are rescheduled elsewhere.</td><td>No agent to run/report pods on that node, so the control plane writes it off.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The one insight that makes troubleshooting click</h5>
                    <p>The control plane is the <strong>brain</strong>; running pods are the <strong>body</strong>. Knock out the brain (API server / etcd) and the body keeps moving on reflex — your apps stay up, you just can't <em>change</em> anything. This is why an etcd or apiserver outage is silent to users but paralyzing to admins, and why "my app still works but <code>kubectl</code> is dead" points straight at the control plane, not the workload. Match the <em>symptom</em> to the <em>component</em> and you've solved most of the exam's troubleshooting tasks before typing a command.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>💪 Worker Node Components</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🤖</span> kubelet</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>node agent</strong> — takes PodSpecs from <em>several</em> sources (the API server <strong>and</strong> static-pod manifests on local disk), makes the containers run &amp; stay healthy, and reports node status back. It only manages containers Kubernetes created.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔀</span> kube-proxy</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>network plumber</strong> (<em>optional</em>) — programs the node's dataplane rules (iptables/IPVS) so Service traffic reaches the right pods. Some CNIs (e.g. Cilium) forward Service traffic themselves and replace it.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🐋</span> container runtime</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>engine</strong> that actually runs containers — containerd (default) or CRI-O via the CRI. Docker-shim was removed in v1.24.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Desired vs Actual State — the Declarative Magic</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Reconciliation Loop</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
                 ┌────────────────── 🔄 ONE CONTROLLER, RUNNING FOREVER ──────────────────┐
                 │                                                                        │
     ┌───────────▼───────────┐      ┌───────────────────┐      ┌─────────────────────────┐
     │ 1. 👁️ OBSERVE           │ ───▶ │ 2. 🧮 DIFF          │ ───▶ │ 3. 🛠️ ACT                 │
     │ read ACTUAL (cluster)   │      │ does ACTUAL match  │      │ create / delete / update │
     │ + DESIRED (from etcd)   │      │ DESIRED? a gap?    │      │ objects to close the gap │
     └─────────────────────────┘      └───────────────────┘      └────────────┬────────────┘
                 ▲                                                             │
                 └──────────────────────────── repeat ◀─────────────────────────┘

  easypj tick-by-tick — the Deployment controller, desired replicas = 3:
    t0   observe 0 pods    · diff: need 3    · act: create 3            → 3 running
    t1   observe 3 pods    · diff: none       · act: nothing  (idempotent — no churn)
    t2   a pod crashes 💥   · observe 2 pods  · diff: need 1   · act: create 1  → 3 running
    t3   you set image v2  · diff: v1 ≠ v2    · act: replace pods one-by-one  → all v2

  💡 Recovery is NOT a special code path — it's the SAME loop noticing a NEW gap.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>How a Pod Gets Created — the Full Journey</h4>
                <p>Here's the single most clarifying flow in all of Kubernetes: what <em>actually</em> happens between you typing <code>kubectl apply</code> and easypj serving traffic. Watch how every component plays its part. (Chapter 3 dissects each step; this is the map.)</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubectl apply → a running easypj pod</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  👩‍💻 kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml
        │  (1) HTTPS request
        ▼
  📡 kube-apiserver ──(2) authn → authz → admission → validate──┐
        │                                                       │
        │  (3) write DESIRED object                             │
        ▼                                                       │
  🗄️ etcd  "Deployment easypj wants 3 pods"  ◀─────────────────┘
        │
        │  (4) controller-manager sees Deployment → makes ReplicaSet → 3 Pod objects
        │      (Pods exist but have NO node yet → "Pending")
        ▼
  📋 kube-scheduler  (5) "which node fits?"  → binds Pod → Node 2 (writes to API→etcd)
        │
        ▼
  🤖 kubelet on Node 2  (6) "a pod was assigned to me!"
        │   ├─ 🐋 tells containerd to PULL easypj:v2 and START the container
        │   └─ 🌐 CNI plugin gives the pod its IP
        ▼
  📦 easypj pod RUNNING :8000   (7) kubelet reports status ──▶ API server ──▶ etcd
        │
        ▼
  🔄 controllers keep watching forever: actual=3 = desired=3  ✅  (reconciled)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Submit</h5><p>kubectl sends your YAML to the API server over TLS.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Admit</h5><p>API server authenticates, authorizes (RBAC), runs admission, validates.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Persist</h5><p>Desired state is written to etcd — the single source of truth.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Expand</h5><p>Controllers turn the Deployment into a ReplicaSet into Pod objects (Pending).</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Schedule</h5><p>The scheduler picks the best node and binds each pod to it.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Run</h5><p>That node's kubelet pulls the image, starts the container, CNI assigns an IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">7</div><h5>Report</h5><p>kubelet reports status up; controllers reconcile forever.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Why this matters for troubleshooting</h5>
                    <p>Every "my pod won't run" problem lives at <em>one</em> of these steps. <strong>Pending</strong> = stuck at step 5 (scheduler can't place it). <strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> = step 6 (kubelet can't pull). <strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> = step 6 (container starts then dies). Knowing the journey tells you exactly <em>where</em> to look — the core skill behind the 30% troubleshooting domain.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Anatomy of a Pod — What's Actually Inside the Smallest Unit</h4>
                <p>"A pod is one or more containers" undersells it. A pod is a small shared <em>environment</em> the containers live in together. Understanding what's inside explains sidecars, why containers reach each other on <code>localhost</code>, and why a <em>pod</em> — not a container — is the thing Kubernetes schedules.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Inside one easypj pod — a shared sandbox holding several containers</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   📦 POD  "easypj-web-6d8f9-x7k2"   ── ONE IP (10.244.1.5), ONE lifecycle ──
   ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  🫙 pause (sandbox) container  ← created FIRST, holds what's shared:      │
   │       • the network namespace  (the pod's single IP + port space)       │
   │       • the IPC namespace; it also anchors the shared volumes           │
   │                                                                         │
   │  ⏱️ initContainers — run to completion, IN ORDER, before the app starts   │
   │       e.g. "wait-for-postgres"  →  "django migrate"                     │
   │                                                                         │
   │  🐍 app container   "web"   :8000   (gunicorn easypj.wsgi)               │
   │  📊 sidecar         "logger"        tails app logs from a shared volume  │
   │        └── app &amp; sidecar talk over  localhost:8000  (same net namespace)│
   │                                                                         │
   │  💾 volumes (emptyDir / ConfigMap / Secret / PVC) mounted into any       │
   │             container above — the way containers share files            │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   Rule: every container in a pod is co-scheduled on the SAME node, shares the SAME
         IP, and they live &amp; die TOGETHER. You scale by adding whole PODS, never
         by adding containers to a pod.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Container role</th><th>When it runs</th><th>Typical easypj use</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>pause / sandbox</strong></td><td>first, invisibly — holds the shared namespaces</td><td>you never write it; the kubelet creates it so app containers can restart without the pod losing its IP</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>init container</strong></td><td>to completion, in order, before app containers</td><td>wait for <code>postgres:5432</code>; run <code>manage.py migrate</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>app container</strong></td><td>the main process, for the pod's whole life</td><td>the Django / gunicorn server on <code>:8000</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>sidecar</strong></td><td>alongside the app (a long-running init container in modern K8s)</td><td>a log shipper or metrics proxy sharing a volume + <code>localhost</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The two facts that follow from this model</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">(1) Because containers in a pod <strong>share one network namespace</strong>, a sidecar reaches the app at <code>localhost:8000</code> — no Service required. (2) Because a pod is <strong>ephemeral</strong> and its containers share one fate, <em>durable state must live outside the pod</em> (a PVC or an external database) — which is exactly why easypj's PostgreSQL data goes on a PersistentVolume, never inside the web pod. This single model underpins Parts 4–6.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why declarative matters for the CKA</h5>
                    <p>The exam rewards working declaratively: declare desired state, <code>kubectl apply -f</code>, and trust the controllers. It's also the key to troubleshooting — when something breaks, you compare <strong>desired state</strong> (in etcd) against <strong>actual state</strong> (on the nodes) and find the gap.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.5 The Kubernetes Ecosystem</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes is a <strong>core that everything plugs into</strong>. It sits at the center of a large ecosystem of runtimes, plugins, and platforms — all governed by the <strong>Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)</strong>. As an admin you'll touch many of these daily.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The ecosystem orbit — a core everything plugs into</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
                  📦 PACKAGING                          🔄 GitOps / DELIVERY
               Helm · Kustomize                          Argo CD · Flux
                        ╲                                    ╱
                         ╲                                  ╱
   🐋 RUNTIME (CRI)    ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐    🌐 NETWORK (CNI)
   containerd · CRI-O ─┤                                        ├─ Calico · Cilium · Flannel
                       │        ☸️   KUBERNETES  CORE             │
   💾 STORAGE (CSI)    │   the orchestration engine:            │    📊 OBSERVABILITY
   EBS · PD · Ceph ───┤   schedule · heal · scale · reconcile   ├─ Prometheus · Grafana
   NFS                 │        (drives desired → actual)        │    Loki · OpenTelemetry
                       └────────────────────────────────────────┘
                         ╱                                  ╲
                        ╱                                    ╲
                  🕸️ SERVICE MESH                       🛡️ SECURITY / POLICY
               Istio · Linkerd                          Kyverno · OPA Gatekeeper

   ☁️  Everything around the core is governed by the CNCF (Cloud Native Computing Foundation).
   💡 CRI · CNI · CSI are the three "sockets" Kubernetes DEFINES — vendors just supply the plugs.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>Key Ecosystem Categories for the CKA</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div>
                        <h5>Container Runtimes (CRI)</h5>
                        <p><strong>containerd</strong> (default since v1.24), <strong>CRI-O</strong>. K8s talks to them via the Container Runtime Interface; the Docker shim was removed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>CNI Plugins (networking)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Calico</strong> &amp; <strong>Cilium</strong> (with NetworkPolicy), <strong>Flannel</strong> (simple overlay), <strong>Weave</strong>. You install a CNI during cluster setup — no CNI = NotReady nodes.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Packaging</h5>
                        <p><strong>Helm</strong> (templated charts — the "apt for K8s") and <strong>Kustomize</strong> (template-free overlays). Both are 2025 CKA topics.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>GitOps</h5>
                        <p><strong>ArgoCD</strong>, <strong>FluxCD</strong> — Git is the source of truth; a controller syncs the cluster to match and self-heals drift.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🕸️</div>
                        <h5>Service Mesh</h5>
                        <p><strong>Istio</strong>, <strong>Linkerd</strong> — observability, traffic management, and mTLS between services without touching app code.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📊</div>
                        <h5>Observability</h5>
                        <p><strong>Prometheus</strong> (metrics), <strong>Grafana</strong> (dashboards), <strong>Loki</strong> (logs), <strong>Jaeger</strong> / <strong>OpenTelemetry</strong> (traces). The CKA expects <code>kubectl top</code> + metrics-server.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CNCF — who governs Kubernetes?</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>Cloud Native Computing Foundation</strong> (part of the Linux Foundation) stewards Kubernetes and 170+ related projects through three maturity levels — <strong>Sandbox → Incubating → Graduated</strong>. Kubernetes was the first to graduate (2018). The CNCF also runs the CKA &amp; CKAD certifications.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="arch-layers">
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-app">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🎓</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Graduated</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Production-proven, widely adopted</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>Kubernetes</span><span>Prometheus</span><span>containerd</span><span>CoreDNS</span><span>etcd</span><span>Helm</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-k8s">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🧪</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Incubating</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Growing adoption, maturing features</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>Argo</span><span>Cilium</span><span>Knative</span><span>Kyverno</span><span>Backstage</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-infra">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🌱</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Sandbox</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Early-stage, experimental</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>Headlamp</span><span>OpenCost</span><span>Inspektor Gadget</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What "Cloud Native" Actually Means</h4>
                <p>"Cloud native" is the phrase behind the CNCF's name and gets thrown around constantly — but it's rarely defined. It's not "runs in the cloud." It's a <strong>way of building software</strong> so it can be operated by software (like Kubernetes). Five properties define it, and easypj will gain each one as we go:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Containerized</h5><p>Each piece is packaged with its dependencies into an image — portable and isolated. (easypj → a container in Ch 2.)</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧩</div><h5>Loosely coupled</h5><p>Independent services talk over the network (web ↔ DB), so each can scale, fail, and deploy on its own.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🤖</div><h5>Dynamically orchestrated</h5><p>A platform schedules, scales, and heals it automatically — no human placing processes on machines.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>Declarative</h5><p>Desired state lives in version-controlled manifests, not in someone's head or a runbook.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>Resilient &amp; observable</h5><p>It expects failure and recovers; it exposes metrics, logs, and traces so you can see what it's doing.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why the term matters to you</h5>
                    <p>Every tool in the orbit above exists to deliver one of these five properties. So "cloud native" isn't marketing — it's the <em>checklist</em> the whole ecosystem is built around. When you finish this guide, easypj will satisfy all five, and you'll be the administrator who keeps the platform that makes that possible running.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Three "Sockets" — How Kubernetes Stays Pluggable (CRI · CNI · CSI)</h4>
                <p>Why does such a huge ecosystem exist? Because Kubernetes itself doesn't run containers, wire up networks, or attach disks. Instead it defines three <strong>standard interfaces</strong> — think of them as universal sockets — and lets vendors plug in. This is <em>the</em> reason Kubernetes runs identically on your laptop, on bare metal, and on every cloud.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes defines the socket; you choose the plug</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
                       ☸️  KUBERNETES  (defines 3 standard interfaces)
                                       │
        ┌──────────────────────┬───────┴───────────────┬──────────────────────┐
        ▼                      ▼                        ▼
   🔌 CRI                  🔌 CNI                    🔌 CSI
   Container Runtime IF    Container Network IF      Container Storage IF
   kubelet asks:           K8s asks:                 K8s asks:
   "run / stop this        "give this pod            "attach this disk
    container"              an IP &amp; connect it"        to the pod"
        │                      │                         │
   any plug speaking ▼     any plug speaking ▼       any plug speaking ▼
   🐋 containerd · CRI-O   🌐 Calico · Cilium ·       💾 AWS EBS · GCP PD ·
                             Flannel                    Ceph · NFS
        │                      │                         │
        ▼                      ▼                         ▼
   starts the easypj       easypj pod 10.244.1.5     postgres data lives on a
   container on the node   reaches any pod, no NAT   real disk, survives restarts

   💡 Swap any plug (e.g. containerd → CRI-O) and Kubernetes ITSELF doesn't change.
      THAT is why one set of easypj manifests runs on a laptop, bare metal &amp; every cloud.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div>
                        <h5>CRI — Runtime</h5>
                        <p>How the kubelet asks something to <strong>run a container</strong>. Default: <strong>containerd</strong>. (You'll configure this in Chapter 9 when installing the cluster.)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>CNI — Network</h5>
                        <p>How pods get an <strong>IP and reach each other</strong>. You install a CNI (Calico/Cilium) right after <code>kubeadm init</code> — without one, nodes stay NotReady (Chapter 17).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>CSI — Storage</h5>
                        <p>How a <strong>real disk</strong> gets attached to a pod for durable data — exactly how easypj's PostgreSQL keeps its files (Chapter 22).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <h4>How the Three Sockets Are Actually Wired — the Files an Admin Touches</h4>
                <p>The socket model is elegant in theory; as an <em>admin</em> you also need the concrete plumbing, because when one socket is misconfigured the cluster breaks in a very specific, recognizable way. Here is where each plug physically lives and how the kubelet reaches it:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Socket</th><th>Who calls it &amp; how</th><th>Where it lives on the node</th><th>Break it and…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>🐋 <strong>CRI</strong></td><td>the <strong>kubelet</strong> calls the runtime over a local gRPC <strong>Unix socket</strong></td><td><code>/run/containerd/containerd.sock</code> (or <code>crio.sock</code>); debug with <code>crictl</code>, not <code>docker</code></td><td>runtime down → node <code>NotReady</code>, pods can't start</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>🌐 <strong>CNI</strong></td><td>the kubelet execs a CNI <strong>binary</strong> when a pod sandbox is created</td><td>plugin binaries in <code>/opt/cni/bin/</code>; config in <code>/etc/cni/net.d/*.conf</code></td><td>no/broken CNI → nodes <code>NotReady</code>, pods stuck <code>ContainerCreating</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>💾 <strong>CSI</strong></td><td>a <strong>controller</strong> plugin provisions/attaches; a <strong>node</strong> plugin mounts</td><td>runs as pods (a Deployment + a DaemonSet); registers under <code>/var/lib/kubelet/plugins/</code></td><td>driver missing → PVCs stay <code>Pending</code>, pods can't mount storage</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One pod starting — all three sockets fire, in sequence</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   🤖 kubelet is told "run easypj-web on this node"
        │
        │ ①  CRI  → gRPC to containerd.sock:  RunPodSandbox   (creates the 🫙 pause container)
        ▼
        │ ②  CNI  → exec /opt/cni/bin/&lt;plugin&gt; using /etc/cni/net.d config:
        │            "give this sandbox an IP" → 10.244.1.5, wire it into the pod network
        ▼
        │ ③  CRI  → PullImage easypj:v2 → CreateContainer → StartContainer
        ▼
        │ ④  CSI  (only if the pod has a PVC) → node plugin mounts the volume into the container
        ▼
   📦 easypj-web RUNNING :8000 — on the pod network, with its storage attached
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why this ordering is a troubleshooting shortcut</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">The failure <em>symptom</em> tells you which socket broke: a <code>ContainerCreating</code> that never finishes is almost always <strong>CNI</strong> (no IP); <code>Pending</code> tied to a PVC is <strong>CSI</strong> (no volume); a node stuck <code>NotReady</code> is <strong>CRI</strong> (runtime) or CNI. You'll configure CRI in Ch 9, CNI in Ch 17, and CSI in Ch 22 — but this map is worth carrying now.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The big idea</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes is a <strong>platform for building platforms</strong>: it owns the orchestration logic and delegates the "hardware-touching" parts to pluggable drivers. Whenever you see "install a CNI" or "pick a StorageClass," you're choosing a plug for one of these sockets. Keep this model in your head — it makes Parts 5 (networking) and 6 (storage) click instantly.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

