            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 1.2 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 1.2 — Watch Self-Healing Happen (Reconciliation, Live)</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 5 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Prove to yourself that Kubernetes is <em>level-triggered</em>, not event-driven. On any cluster (Minikube is fine): create an <code>easypj</code> Deployment with <strong>3 replicas</strong>, delete one pod by hand, and confirm the count returns to 3 <em>without you doing anything</em>. Then show that the <strong>only</strong> way to reduce the count for real is to change the <em>desired</em> state.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Deleting a pod changes <em>actual</em> state, not <em>desired</em> state — the ReplicaSet controller sees the gap (2 ≠ 3) and closes it. To remove pods permanently you must lower <code>replicas</code> (desired) with <code>kubectl scale</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">reconciliation, observed live</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Declare desired state: 3 replicas</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=nginx --replicas=3
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj              <span class="comment"># 3 pods Running</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Break ACTUAL state — delete ONE pod by name, then look immediately</span>
POD=$(kubectl get pods -l app=easypj -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl delete pod "$POD"
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj              <span class="comment"># still 3 — a fresh pod already replaced it</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. The count is enforced. To actually reduce it, change DESIRED state:</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=1
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj              <span class="comment"># now 1 (desired changed → actual follows)</span>

kubectl delete deployment easypj            <span class="comment"># cleanup</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the deleted pod came back because you only touched <em>actual</em> state — the controller's next OBSERVE → DIFF → ACT pass saw 2 ≠ 3 and created one. Scaling worked because it changed <em>desired</em> state, which is what the loop reconciles toward. If you internalized <em>"to change what runs, change what's declared,"</em> you have Chapter 1's core model cold.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 1.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 1.3 — Match the Symptom to the Broken Component</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 4 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>This is the heart of the 30% Troubleshooting domain. For each symptom, name the <strong>single most likely component</strong> at fault and one command to confirm it — <em>before</em> you touch anything:</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><code>kubectl get pods</code> hangs / times out — but easypj keeps serving <code>/jobs/</code>.</li>
                        <li>A new easypj pod is stuck <code>Pending</code> forever; existing pods are fine.</li>
                        <li>You delete an easypj pod and it is <em>never</em> replaced; scaling does nothing either.</li>
                        <li>One worker shows <code>NotReady</code> and its pods get rescheduled elsewhere.</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> "app works but I can't manage it" points at the control plane; <code>Pending</code> = placement; "no self-healing" = the reconciliation loops; a single NotReady node = that node's agent.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Broken component</th><th>Why</th><th>Confirm with</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1</td><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong> (or etcd behind it)</td><td>It's the only door to the cluster — a dead API means no reads/writes, yet running pods keep running on reflex.</td><td><code>crictl ps | grep apiserver</code> · <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> · inspect <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2</td><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>Nothing is left to bind the pod to a node, so it sits unscheduled.</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep scheduler</code> · <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pending&gt;</code> (Events)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3</td><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td>The reconciliation loops (ReplicaSet, etc.) live here — no loop, no gap-closing.</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep controller-manager</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>4</td><td><strong>kubelet</strong> on that node</td><td>No node agent to run/report pods, so the control plane marks the node NotReady.</td><td><code>ssh &lt;node&gt;; systemctl status kubelet; journalctl -u kubelet</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> you diagnosed all four <em>without running a single fix</em> — you matched the symptom to the component using the mental model from §1.4. That "name the component first" reflex turns a 10-minute troubleshooting task into a 2-minute one on the exam.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 1.4 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 1.4 — Turn an Imperative Command Into Declarative State</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>You deployed easypj imperatively in a hurry. Now make it reproducible: <strong>generate</strong> a declarative manifest from an imperative command (don't hand-write YAML), <code>apply</code> it, and <em>prove it is idempotent</em> by applying the same file again with no change. This hybrid is the exam-winning workflow.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> prints the manifest instead of creating anything. Re-running <code>kubectl apply</code> on an unchanged file reports <code>unchanged</code>, not an error — that is idempotency.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate imperatively → apply declaratively</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Scaffold YAML from an imperative command — creates nothing yet</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 --replicas=3 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; easypj.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. Apply it — the desired state is now a versionable file</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj.yaml        <span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj created</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Prove idempotency — same file, run again</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj.yaml        <span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj unchanged  ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Contrast: the imperative equivalent is NOT idempotent</span>
kubectl create -f easypj.yaml       <span class="comment"># Error: deployments.apps "easypj" already exists</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> step 3 said <code>unchanged</code> because <code>apply</code> sends <em>desired state</em> and reconciliation found no gap; step 4 errored because <code>create</code> is imperative ("make this now"). The takeaway the exam rewards: <strong>generate with <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code>, edit, then <code>apply</code></strong> — imperative speed with declarative safety.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

