                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Your single-stage easypj image is bloated with build tooling. Convert it to a <strong>multi-stage</strong> build (builder + slim non-root runtime), rebuild, and <strong>prove the size dropped</strong> with <code>docker images</code>. Add a <code>.dockerignore</code> so <code>.env</code> and <code>db.sqlite3</code> can never leak into the image.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Stage 1 installs into <code>--prefix=/install</code>; stage 2 starts <em>fresh</em> from <code>python:3.13-slim</code> and copies only <code>/install</code> + your source. Add <code>USER app</code> so it runs non-root.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Dockerfile.production + proof</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-docker"><span class="comment"># ── stage 1: builder (discarded) ──</span>
FROM python:3.13-slim AS builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt

<span class="comment"># ── stage 2: slim non-root runtime ──</span>
FROM python:3.13-slim
RUN useradd -m app
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /install /usr/local
COPY . .
RUN python manage.py collectstatic --noinput
USER app
EXPOSE 8000
CMD ["gunicorn","easypj.wsgi:application","-b","0.0.0.0:8000"]</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">.dockerignore + build &amp; compare</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cat &gt; .dockerignore &lt;&lt;'EOF'
.git
__pycache__
*.pyc
db.sqlite3
.env
venv/
EOF

docker build -t easypj:v1-fat -f Dockerfile .              <span class="comment"># single-stage</span>
docker build -t easypj:v1 -f Dockerfile.production .       <span class="comment"># multi-stage</span>
docker images | grep easypj
<span class="comment"># easypj  v1-fat  ~900MB   ← compilers, caches, build deps</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj  v1      ~150MB   ← runtime only ✅</span>
docker run --rm easypj:v1 whoami                            <span class="comment"># app  (non-root ✅)</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the builder stage — with pip's caches, compilers, and headers — was thrown away; only <code>/install</code> and your source reached the final image. Smaller image = faster pulls + fewer CVEs, and <code>USER app</code> means a breakout isn't root. This exact pattern is the production baseline for every image in this guide.</p>
                    </details>
