            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 2.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 2.3 — Fix an ImagePullBackOff (Private Registry)</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A pod for <code>registry.example.com/easypj:v1</code> is stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. Diagnose the <em>exact</em> cause from the Events, fix it with a registry Secret, and verify the pod starts. (Assume the registry needs a login.)</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>describe</code> first — "unauthorized" means credentials, "not found" means a bad name/tag. The fix for credentials is a <code>docker-registry</code> Secret referenced by <code>imagePullSecrets</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose → fix → verify</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. DIAGNOSE — the Events line names the real cause</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web | grep -A6 Events
<span class="comment">#   Failed to pull image "registry.example.com/easypj:v1":</span>
<span class="comment">#   ... 401 Unauthorized  ← credentials, not a typo</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. FIX — create the registry credential Secret</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.example.com \
  --docker-username=deploy --docker-password='S3cret!' \
  --docker-email=ops@easypj.io

<span class="comment"># 3. Reference it from the pod spec…</span>
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -p \
  '{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"imagePullSecrets":[{"name":"regcred"}]}}}}'

<span class="comment">#    …or attach it to the ServiceAccount so EVERY pod inherits it</span>
kubectl patch serviceaccount default -p '{"imagePullSecrets":[{"name":"regcred"}]}'

<span class="comment"># 4. VERIFY — new pods pull successfully</span>
kubectl get pods -w        <span class="comment"># ContainerCreating → Running ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Events say…</th><th>Real cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>unauthorized</code> / <code>401</code></td><td>missing/bad <code>imagePullSecret</code></td><td><code>docker-registry</code> Secret + reference it</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>not found</code> / <code>manifest unknown</code></td><td>wrong image name or tag</td><td>fix the ref (remember the <code>docker.io/library/…:latest</code> defaults)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>timeouts / DNS errors</td><td>registry unreachable from the node</td><td>check node network/proxy/rate-limits</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> only means "a failed pull is being retried with backoff" — the <em>Events</em> name the actual failure. You fixed the credential case; the table gives you the other two family members so the whole error class is now routine.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 2.4 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 2.4 — Debug a Node with crictl (kubectl Is Dead)</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>The apiserver is down — every <code>kubectl</code> command times out — but easypj containers are still running on <code>worker-1</code> and users report errors. SSH to the node and, using <strong>only runtime-level tools</strong>: list the running containers, read the easypj container's logs, find its exit code from its last crash, and confirm the runtime itself is healthy.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>crictl</code> talks straight to <code>/run/containerd/containerd.sock</code> — no kubelet or apiserver required. <code>crictl ps -a</code> shows exited containers too; <code>crictl inspect</code> carries the exit code.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on worker-1 — the CRI is your only window</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. What's running (and what crashed)?  -a includes exited containers</span>
sudo crictl ps
sudo crictl ps -a | grep easypj

<span class="comment"># 2. Read the app's logs straight from the runtime</span>
CID=$(sudo crictl ps -a --name easypj -q | head -1)
sudo crictl logs "$CID" | tail -30

<span class="comment"># 3. Exit code of the last crash (137 = OOMKilled, 143 = SIGTERM, 139 = segfault)</span>
sudo crictl inspect "$CID" | grep -i exitCode

<span class="comment"># 4. Is the runtime itself healthy?</span>
sudo systemctl status containerd
sudo crictl info | grep -iA2 runtime      <span class="comment"># runtime name + socket endpoint</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> you diagnosed a live workload with <em>zero</em> Kubernetes control plane — because containers are just processes managed by containerd, and <code>crictl</code> speaks CRI directly to it. This is the exam's "node is broken, kubectl returns nothing" reflex: SSH in, switch tools, keep debugging. (Chapter 32 builds a full method on this.)</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 2.5 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 2.5 — Kubelet Won't Start: the cgroup-Driver Mismatch</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A freshly provisioned node refuses to join: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> shows a crash-loop and the journal mentions cgroups. Diagnose the classic cause — the kubelet and containerd disagree on the cgroup <em>driver</em> — and fix both sides to <code>systemd</code>.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> containerd's default is <code>cgroupfs</code>; kubeadm clusters expect <code>systemd</code> on both. The containerd side lives in <code>/etc/containerd/config.toml</code> under the runc options; the kubelet side in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose → align both drivers → restart</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. DIAGNOSE — read the kubelet's own words</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -20
<span class="comment">#   "failed to run Kubelet: misconfiguration: kubelet cgroup driver: \"systemd\"</span>
<span class="comment">#    is different from docker/containerd cgroup driver: \"cgroupfs\""</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. FIX containerd → systemd driver</span>
sudo sed -i 's/SystemdCgroup = false/SystemdCgroup = true/' /etc/containerd/config.toml
<span class="comment">#   ([plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runc.options])</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. CONFIRM the kubelet side matches (kubeadm default is already systemd)</span>
grep cgroupDriver /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml     <span class="comment"># cgroupDriver: systemd</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. RESTART the pair, runtime first</span>
sudo systemctl restart containerd
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># 5. VERIFY</span>
sudo systemctl is-active containerd kubelet        <span class="comment"># active active ✅</span>
kubectl get nodes                                  <span class="comment"># node Ready ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the kubelet and the runtime <em>both</em> write cgroup limits for containers, so they must speak the same dialect — <code>systemd</code> on modern distros. The journal names the mismatch outright, the fix is one line of TOML, and the restart order is runtime-then-kubelet. Bonus fact for v1.35+: the node must also be on cgroups <strong>v2</strong> (v1 is deprecated) — check with <code>stat -fc %T /sys/fs/cgroup/</code> (<code>cgroup2fs</code> = good).</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

