                </table>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key file paths to memorize</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — static-pod manifests (control plane)</li>
                        <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> — all cluster certs (CA, server, client)</li>
                        <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/admin.conf</code> — the admin kubeconfig</li>
                        <li><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> — kubelet config (incl. <code>staticPodPath</code>)</li>
                        <li><code>/var/lib/etcd/</code> — etcd data directory</li>
                        <li><code>/etc/cni/net.d/</code> &amp; <code>/opt/cni/bin/</code> — CNI config &amp; binaries</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 3.7 NODE HEALTH & CP↔NODE COMMS — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.7 Node Health &amp; Control-Plane ↔ Node Communication</h3>
                <p>§3.3 named the node's components; this is how the control plane <em>knows a node is alive</em>, <em>how much it can schedule onto it</em>, and <em>how the two halves talk securely</em>. It's the backbone of "node NotReady" troubleshooting (30% domain).</p>

                <h4>① Node status conditions — the health report</h4>
                <p>Each Node object carries a set of <strong>conditions</strong> (see them with <code>kubectl describe node</code>). <code>Ready=True</code> means healthy &amp; schedulable; the four "pressure" conditions are early-warning flags.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Problem state</th><th>Typical cause</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Ready</code></td><td><code>True</code> = healthy · <code>False</code>/<code>Unknown</code> = problem</td><td>kubelet down, lost heartbeat, runtime/CNI broken</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>MemoryPressure</code></td><td><code>True</code> = low memory</td><td>node RAM nearly exhausted → starts evicting pods</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>DiskPressure</code></td><td><code>True</code> = low disk</td><td>full disk / image cache → eviction + image GC</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>PIDPressure</code></td><td><code>True</code> = too many processes</td><td>fork bomb / runaway workload</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>NetworkUnavailable</code></td><td><code>True</code> = node network not configured</td><td>CNI not installed / route missing</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>② Heartbeats, the node Lease &amp; the eviction timeline</h4>
                <p>A node proves it's alive two ways: the kubelet renews a lightweight <strong>Lease</strong> in the <code>kube-node-lease</code> namespace (one per node, ~every 10s — the cheap, frequent heartbeat) <strong>and</strong> posts a fuller <code>.status</code> update whenever something changes (or periodically). When heartbeats stop, the node controller runs a fixed clock:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what happens when a node goes silent</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   t = 0s     kubelet stops renewing its Lease / status   (node crashed, network cut)
   t ≈ 50s    node controller marks the node  Ready = Unknown   (node-monitor-grace-period)
              └─ adds taint  node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute   (or …/not-ready)
   t ≈ 5min   pods without a matching toleration are EVICTED &amp; rescheduled elsewhere
              └─ eviction is rate-limited so a big outage doesn't stampede the cluster
   kubectl get lease -n kube-node-lease     ← see each node's heartbeat
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0">🔑 This is why "I rebooted a node and my pods vanished a few minutes later" is <em>expected</em> — the ~5-minute eviction timer fired. A pod's <code>tolerations</code> for <code>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable</code> / <code>not-ready</code> (with <code>tolerationSeconds</code>) control how quickly it's moved.</p></div>

                <h4>③ Capacity vs Allocatable — what the scheduler actually sees</h4>
                <p>A node's <strong>Capacity</strong> is its total hardware; <strong>Allocatable</strong> is what's left for your pods after reservations for the kubelet, the OS, and an eviction buffer. The scheduler only ever fills <em>Allocatable</em> — which is why a pod can sit <code>Pending</code> even though the node "looks" half-empty.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Capacity vs Allocatable — the slice the scheduler actually fills</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   NODE CAPACITY   (e.g. 4 CPU / 16Gi)
   ├─ kube-reserved       kubelet, container runtime
   ├─ system-reserved     OS, sshd, systemd
   ├─ eviction-threshold  safety buffer (e.g. memory.available &lt; 100Mi)
   └─ ALLOCATABLE   ◀── the only slice the scheduler can place pods into
   kubectl describe node node-1     → shows Capacity and Allocatable side by side
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>④ Control-plane ↔ node communication — the trust asymmetry</h4>
                <p>Traffic from a node <em>into</em> the control plane is secured by default; traffic from the control plane <em>out</em> to nodes is the leg you must harden.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Path</th><th>Security</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Node → control plane</strong></td><td>kubelet / kube-proxy → apiserver <code>:6443</code></td><td>✅ TLS + auth + RBAC by default — the one hub everything dials</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CP → kubelet</strong></td><td>apiserver → kubelet <code>:10250</code> (logs, <code>exec</code>, <code>port-forward</code>, metrics)</td><td>⚠️ HTTPS, but the apiserver won't verify the kubelet's cert unless you set <code>--kubelet-certificate-authority</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CP → nodes / pods / services</strong></td><td>apiserver → cluster IPs</td><td>⚠️ plain HTTP by default — unsafe over untrusted networks; use the <strong>Konnectivity</strong> service for a secured tunnel</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><p class="mb-0">💡 Mental model: <strong>everything dials the apiserver</strong> (secure, single front door); the apiserver dialing back out to nodes is the weaker leg — <strong>Konnectivity</strong> (the konnectivity-agent/server pair) wraps it in a managed tunnel for production.</p></div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Konnectivity replaced SSH tunnels — and it dials the OTHER way</h5>
                    <p>Older Kubernetes docs mention securing apiserver→node traffic with <strong>SSH tunnels</strong> — that approach is now <strong>deprecated</strong> ("don't use it unless you know exactly why"). Its replacement, <strong>Konnectivity</strong>, flips the connection direction on purpose: the <code>konnectivity-agent</code> (running near the nodes) <em>initiates</em> the connection <em>to</em> the <code>konnectivity-server</code> (beside the apiserver) and keeps it open. The apiserver's outbound calls then ride that pre-established tunnel — which is <em>why</em> Konnectivity works even when nothing on the control-plane side could otherwise dial into a node's network (a common shape in locked-down or NAT'd cloud VPCs).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0">📖 <strong>How pods reach the apiserver, symmetrically:</strong> every namespace gets a <code>kubernetes</code> Service (check <code>kubectl get svc kubernetes</code> in <code>default</code>) whose ClusterIP kube-proxy redirects to the real apiserver endpoint — so a pod never needs to know the apiserver's real address, only the name <code>kubernetes</code>. And on the node-registration side, production clusters normally automate kubelet certificate issuance via <strong>kubelet TLS bootstrapping</strong> rather than hand-copying client certs to every node (Ch 26 covers the CSR mechanics behind it).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>⑤ Node registration — how a node joins, and the cordon-vs-drain distinction</h4>
                <p>A node doesn't need to be manually "added" to the cluster object list — by default the kubelet <strong>self-registers</strong> (<code>--register-node</code>, true by default) the moment it starts and can reach the apiserver, which is exactly what happens during <code>kubeadm join</code>. Two commands then let <em>you</em> control whether a registered node accepts new work — and they are not the same operation:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Marks unschedulable?</th><th>Evicts existing pods?</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td><td>✅ yes (taints <code>SchedulingDisabled</code>)</td><td>❌ no — running pods stay put</td><td>you want to stop <em>new</em> pods landing there, but not disturb what's already running</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt;</code></td><td>✅ yes (cordons first)</td><td>✅ yes — evicts everything (respecting PDBs)</td><td>you're about to take the node down (OS patch, decommission) — full depth in Ch 30</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td><td>reverses cordon</td><td>n/a</td><td>bring a node back into scheduling after maintenance</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0">📖 <code>cordon</code> alone is the safer first move when you just want to <em>freeze</em> a node's pod count while you investigate — nothing gets rescheduled, so you can debug a suspect node without an eviction storm. <code>drain</code> is the deliberate "empty this node" operation you reach for only once you actually intend to take it offline.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how the control plane stays consistent &amp; highly available</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The components in §3.2 are simple to name; the <em>machinery</em> that makes several copies of them cooperate without conflict is where real understanding lives. Four mechanisms do the heavy lifting.</p>

                    <h5>① Leader election via Lease — how scheduler &amp; controller-manager run HA</h5>
                    <p>You can run 3 schedulers and 3 controller-managers for high availability, but if all of them acted at once they'd fight (three controllers each creating the "missing" pod). So they're <strong>active-passive</strong>: only one is the leader at a time, chosen by a <strong>Lease</strong> object the others watch.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   3 kube-scheduler replicas         🗄️ a Lease object in kube-system
   ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ sched-1  │ │ sched-2  │ │ sched-3  │ ────▶ │ Lease "kube-scheduler"    │
   │ LEADER ✅ │ │ standby  │ │ standby  │      │ holder: sched-1           │
   └────┬─────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘      │ renewTime: …(every ~2s)   │
        │ only the leader schedules            └──────────────────────────┘
        └─ stops renewing (crash) → a standby grabs the Lease → becomes leader
   kubectl get lease -n kube-system   ← see who holds it
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why it matters:</strong> "I have 3 control-plane nodes but only one scheduler seems to work" is <em>correct</em> — the other two are hot standbys. Check <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code> to see the current holder.</div>

                    <h5>② The Binding subresource — how the scheduler actually places a pod</h5>
                    <p>The scheduler doesn't "move" a pod. It computes the best node, then makes a tiny API call — a <strong>Binding</strong> — that sets the pod's <code>nodeName</code>. The kubelet on that node then notices "a pod is assigned to me" and runs it. Scheduling is just one field being written.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   scheduler picks node2  ──POST /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods/easypj-web-x/binding──▶ 📡 apiserver
   apiserver sets pod.spec.nodeName = "node2"  ──▶ 🗄️ etcd
   🤖 kubelet(node2) is watching pods where nodeName == its own → sees it → starts it
   💡 you can hand-schedule by setting nodeName yourself (bypasses the scheduler entirely)
</pre>

                    <h5>③ API Priority &amp; Fairness (APF) — keeping the apiserver alive under load</h5>
                    <p>Because <em>everything</em> goes through the apiserver, a flood of requests (a buggy controller, a stampede of kubelets) could starve it. <strong>APF</strong> classifies incoming requests into priority levels and fair-queues them, so critical traffic (leader election, node heartbeats) isn't drowned out by bulk list calls.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>FlowSchema</td><td>Matches requests (by user/verb/resource) → assigns them to a priority level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>PriorityLevelConfiguration</td><td>Reserves concurrency shares + a fair queue per level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Effect</td><td>A misbehaving client is throttled (HTTP 429) instead of taking down the API for everyone</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h5>④ The aggregation layer — extending the API itself</h5>
                    <p>Beyond CRDs, you can register a <strong>second API server</strong> behind the main one via an <code>APIService</code>. The apiserver proxies a slice of the API path to it. That's how <code>metrics-server</code> serves <code>metrics.k8s.io</code> (powering <code>kubectl top</code>) — it's not a CRD, it's an aggregated apiserver.</p>
                    <h5>⑤ REST + watch — the API machinery everything is built on</h5>
                    <p>The apiserver is a <strong>RESTful</strong> server: every object is a URL, and the usual HTTP verbs act on it. The magic extra is <strong>WATCH</strong> — a long-lived stream of changes that lets components react instantly instead of polling. That single mechanism is how the scheduler, controllers, and kubelets all stay in sync.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   REST verbs on a resource:
     GET    /apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments/easypj-web   read one
     LIST   /apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments              read all
     POST / PUT / PATCH / DELETE                                     create / replace / modify / remove

   WATCH (the key to reactivity):
     GET …/deployments?watch=true&amp;resourceVersion=4821
        ──▶ keeps the connection OPEN and streams events as they happen:
            ADDED {…}   MODIFIED {…rv:4822}   MODIFIED {…rv:4830}   DELETED {…}
     each event carries the new resourceVersion → a client can resume the stream
     after a disconnect from exactly where it left off (no missed &amp; no duplicate work)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>LIST then WATCH</td><td>Get the full state once, then only the deltas — the pattern every informer uses</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resourceVersion</code></td><td>A cursor: "stream changes since here" + the basis of optimistic concurrency</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>watch cache</td><td>The apiserver serves watches from memory, so thousands of watchers don't hammer etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl get -w</code></td><td>Is literally a watch stream you can see in your terminal</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this is the whole design:</strong> components never call <em>each other</em> — they all watch the apiserver and react to changes. That's the loose coupling that makes Kubernetes resilient: a component that was briefly down just re-LISTs and resumes watching, catching up to current state automatically.</div>

                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Tie it together:</strong> the apiserver isn't just a CRUD endpoint — it's a REST + watch hub (the API machinery), a consistency &amp; fairness hub (APF), a placement coordinator (Binding), an HA arbiter (Leases), and an extensible surface (aggregation). Master these and "the control plane" stops being a black box.</div>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> reads can be served from the apiserver's <em>watch cache</em> (fast, slightly behind) or forced consistent; every object carries a <code>resourceVersion</code> for optimistic concurrency (Ch 1 UTH); controllers are built on <strong>informers</strong> (shared list-watch + cache) feeding <strong>workqueues</strong> with rate-limiting and retries — packaged for reuse as the <strong>controller-runtime</strong> library, which is what most operators (Ch 38) are built on; and the node controller's NotReady (~40s) → eviction (~5min) timers are why a briefly-down node doesn't instantly lose its pods.</p>

                    <h5>⑥ The full pod-creation trace — what happens <em>inside</em> §3.4's 8 steps</h5>
                    <p>§3.4 showed the <em>journey</em>; here is the <em>internal trace</em> — the named admission plugins, the scheduler's filter/score plugins, and the exact <strong>CRI</strong> calls the kubelet makes. This is the depth CKA troubleshooting questions live at.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
kubectl run easypj-web --image=easypj:v1
   │
   ▼  STAGE 1 — apiserver: authN → authZ → admission → etcd
 📡 kube-apiserver
   ├─ a. AuthN      validate the bearer token (who are you?) → user: easypj-admin
   ├─ b. AuthZ      RBAC: may easypj-admin CREATE pods in ns easypj? → yes
   ├─ c. Admission chain (mutate, then validate):
   │       MutatingAdmissionWebhook   → inject sidecars / defaults
   │       LimitRanger                → apply default requests &amp; limits
   │       PodSecurity                → enforce baseline / restricted
   │       ValidatingAdmissionWebhook → reject if a policy is violated
   ├─ d. WRITE      /registry/pods/easypj/easypj-web  → 🗄️ etcd (quorum write)
   └─ e. RETURN     201 Created → "pod/easypj-web created"
   │
   ▼  STAGE 2 — scheduler: watch → filter → score → bind
 📋 kube-scheduler  (watches pods where .spec.nodeName == "")
   ├─ FILTER (predicates) — drop nodes that CAN'T run it:
   │     NodeReady? · CPU/mem fits requests? · nodeSelector/affinity? · taints tolerated? · hostPort free?
   ├─ SCORE  (priorities) — rank the survivors 0–100:
   │     NodeResourcesFit(LeastAllocated) · NodeResourcesBalancedAllocation · ImageLocality · NodeAffinity
   └─ BIND: POST …/pods/easypj-web/binding → sets .spec.nodeName = "node-1" → 🗄️ etcd
   │
   ▼  STAGE 3 — kubelet → CRI → containerd → runc
 🤖 kubelet(node-1)  (watches pods assigned to it)
   └─ calls the CRI (gRPC), in order:
        RunPodSandbox   → create the pause container + network namespace (CNI assigns the IP)
        PullImage       → fetch easypj:v1 layers from the registry
        CreateContainer → set up namespaces + cgroups + overlay rootfs
        StartContainer  → exec the easypj process
      containerd builds the OCI bundle → runc create → runc start
        runc: clone() with namespace flags · write cgroups · pivot_root · exec
   └─ kubelet reports status → Running → Ready (once probes pass) → 🗄️ etcd
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Map a failure to its stage:</strong> hangs at <code>kubectl</code> → Stage 1 (apiserver unreachable); stuck <code>Pending</code> → Stage 2 (nothing survived filtering — taints / resources / selector); stuck <code>ContainerCreating</code> → Stage 3 <code>RunPodSandbox</code>/<code>PullImage</code> (CNI, or image/registry); <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> → Stage 3 <code>StartContainer</code> (the app itself — read <code>kubectl logs</code>).</div>

                    <h5>⑦ Static pods — the bootstrap paradox solved</h5>
                    <p>The control plane (apiserver, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager) runs <em>as pods</em> — which is a chicken-and-egg problem: how do you run pods before the apiserver that schedules them even exists? The answer is the <strong>static pod</strong> — the kubelet reads manifests straight from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and runs them itself, with no apiserver and no scheduler involved.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   boot order on a control-plane node
   ① kubelet starts             (a systemd service — it does NOT need the API)
   ② reads /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
   ③ runs the apiserver static pod    (CRI → containerd → runc)
   ④ the apiserver is now up
   ⑤ kubelet registers a read-only MIRROR pod for it in the API
        → visible as kube-apiserver-&lt;nodename&gt;   (that's where the suffix comes from)
   ⑥ kubelet runs the remaining manifests: etcd, scheduler, controller-manager
   ⑦ control plane operational → normal, API-driven scheduling begins
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔁 Restarting a static pod (you can't <code>kubectl delete</code> it):</strong> move the manifest out and back —
                    <code>sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/ &amp;&amp; sleep 20 &amp;&amp; sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — the kubelet sees the file vanish (stops the pod), then reappear (recreates it). Editing the manifest in place also triggers a restart.</div>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA insight:</strong> if you SSH to a control-plane node and <code>kubectl</code> won't respond, the apiserver static pod may be down — there's no API to ask. Check it directly: <code>sudo crictl ps | grep kube-apiserver</code> (is the container running?) and <code>sudo ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (is the manifest present?). A missing or corrupt manifest takes the entire control plane down.</div>

                    <h5>⑧ A few more control-plane internals worth knowing</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Lease fields</strong></td><td>a leader-election Lease carries <code>holderIdentity</code> (who holds it), <code>leaseDurationSeconds</code> (TTL — ~15s for scheduler/controller-manager) and <code>renewTime</code> (the leader rewrites it every couple of seconds). If a renewal is missed past the TTL, a standby claims the Lease — so failover takes up to about one lease duration.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>APF: 429 vs 503</strong></td><td><strong>429 Too Many Requests</strong> = API Priority &amp; Fairness fair-queued you and the queue/wait was exceeded → <em>back off &amp; retry</em>. <strong>503</strong> = a priority level with no queueing, or the server is genuinely overloaded → <em>investigate</em>. <code>exempt</code> flows (e.g. <code>system:masters</code>, health checks) bypass APF so a misconfig can't lock you out of the API.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Admission <code>failurePolicy</code></strong></td><td>if a webhook errors or times out: <code>Fail</code> (default) rejects the request — safe for policy/security; <code>Ignore</code> lets it through — for optional webhooks. <strong>Mutating runs before validating</strong> by design, so validators judge the <em>final</em> object (after sidecars/defaults are injected).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Control plane decides, worker nodes run</strong> — sort every problem into "decision" vs "run" and you've found the half to look in.</li>
                    <li><strong>Only the apiserver talks to etcd</strong> — and everything else talks only to the apiserver (the single choke point).</li>
                    <li><strong>The kubelet is a systemd service, not a pod</strong> — debug it with <code>systemctl</code> / <code>journalctl</code>, never <code>kubectl</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>The control plane runs AS static pods</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — edit the YAML, the kubelet reloads.</li>
                    <li><strong>CRI · CNI · CSI</strong> are the three plug points (runtime · network · storage).</li>
                    <li><strong>etcd uses Raft</strong> — ports <code>2379</code> (client) / <code>2380</code> (peer); a write commits only when a quorum acks. Back it up or lose the cluster.</li>
                    <li><strong>Scheduler = FILTER → SCORE → BIND</strong> — and it only fills <em>Allocatable</em> (Capacity − reservations), never raw Capacity.</li>
                    <li><strong>Everything DIALS the apiserver</strong> — via authN → authZ (RBAC) → admission → etcd; node health is tracked by Leases in <code>kube-node-lease</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Match symptom → component:</strong> NotReady=kubelet · Pending=scheduler · lost state=etcd · kubectl dead=apiserver.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubelet ≠ pod.</strong> It's systemd — <code>kubectl get pods</code> won't show it; <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> will.</li>
                    <li><strong>You can't <code>kubectl delete</code> a static pod</strong> — the kubelet recreates it. Remove the manifest file from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>apiserver is the single choke point.</strong> If it's down, <code>kubectl</code> is useless — switch to <code>crictl</code> / <code>journalctl</code> on the node.</li>
                    <li><strong>Static pod vs DaemonSet.</strong> Both = one pod per node, but static = kubelet-managed (mirror pod, <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> suffix), DaemonSet = API object.</li>
                    <li><strong>Scheduler vs controller-manager.</strong> Scheduler only <em>places</em> pods; the controller-manager handles replication, nodes, endpoints. Don't conflate.</li>
                    <li><strong>A half-empty node can still reject a pod.</strong> The scheduler counts <em>Allocatable</em> (Capacity − kube/system-reserved − eviction threshold), so a pod sits Pending though the node "looks" free.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code> is deprecated.</strong> Use the health endpoints instead: <code>kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>apiserver → kubelet (<code>:10250</code>) doesn't verify the kubelet's cert</strong> unless <code>--kubelet-certificate-authority</code> is set — a trust asymmetry worth knowing (everything else <em>dials</em> the apiserver over verified TLS).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>apiserver down?</strong> Debug from the node: <code>crictl ps -a</code>, <code>crictl logs &lt;apiserver-id&gt;</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pods Pending?</strong> Suspect the scheduler — <code>kubectl get pods -A | grep Pending</code> then check the scheduler pod/logs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Editing a control-plane component?</strong> Just edit its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — the kubelet restarts it for you.</li>
                    <li><strong>Force-restart a control-plane static pod:</strong> move its manifest <em>out</em> of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>, wait for the pod to vanish, move it back.</li>
                    <li><strong>Node NotReady? Check in this order:</strong> kubelet (systemd) → CNI (<code>/etc/cni/net.d/</code>) → runtime (<code>crictl</code>) — it's the dependency chain.</li>
                    <li><strong>Per-check apiserver health:</strong> <code>kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'</code> breaks the health down check-by-check (etcd, informers, post-start hooks).</li>
                    <li><strong>Pod stuck Pending?</strong> <code>kubectl describe pod</code> → the scheduler's Events name the exact filter that rejected every node (taints, insufficient CPU, affinity…).</li>
                    <li><strong>Find the HA leader:</strong> <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Find etcd's endpoints fast:</strong> <code>cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep -i etcd</code> — reads the <code>--etcd-servers</code>/cert flags the apiserver itself uses, no guessing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Check which kube-proxy mode is active:</strong> <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-proxy-*</code> — the startup log states iptables/nftables/IPVS explicitly.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

