                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is the apiserver the cluster's single choke point?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Every read/write of state, every component's watch, and every kubectl call flows through it — and it's the only thing that touches etcd. Down apiserver → no scheduling, no reconciliation, no kubectl (though running pods continue).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>apiserver down: ❌ stops</th><th>✅ keeps working</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>kubectl (every command)</td><td>already-running pods (kubelets act on cached state)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>scheduling &amp; reconciliation (all watches are blind)</td><td>Service routing on each node (kube-proxy rules already programmed)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>new deploys, scaling, self-healing</td><td>node-level debugging: <code>crictl</code>, <code>journalctl</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>That's also why it gets HA replicas behind a load balancer and APF to stay alive under load (UTH).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain a controller's reconcile loop using the ReplicaSet example.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>OBSERVE → COMPARE → ACT, forever — a deleted pod is just the next gap to close:</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">the reconcile loop, one ReplicaSet tick</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
        ┌──────────── reconcile loop (runs forever) ───────────┐
        ▼                                                      │
   OBSERVE actual ──▶ COMPARE to desired ──▶ ACT to close gap ─┘
   (2 pods now)        (spec replicas: 3)     (create 1 pod)
   delete a pod → next pass sees 2 ≠ 3 again → recreates it
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Level-triggered: it reacts to current state, not events, so it self-heals even if an event is missed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a "mirror pod" and why does its name end in <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>When the kubelet runs a static pod, it creates a read-only <strong>mirror pod</strong> in the API so you can <code>kubectl get</code> it. The kubelet appends its node name (e.g. <code>etcd-controlplane</code>) to show which node owns it — and to keep names unique across nodes.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">the file is the truth; the mirror is a reflection</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  📁 /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml     ← SOURCE OF TRUTH (a file on the node)
        │ kubelet watches the folder &amp; runs it
        ▼
  📦 real pod on the node  ──registers──▶  📡 API: mirror pod "etcd-controlplane"
                                              │ read-only: kubectl can SEE it,
                                              ▼ but delete = it comes right back
  to manage it: edit/move the FILE, never the mirror
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>You can view but not control a mirror pod via the API; the real lifecycle lives with the kubelet and the manifest file.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

