                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 3 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken state or a "trace it" question, with the real answer.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You SSH to the control plane node and find that <code>kubectl get nodes</code> returns <em>"The connection to the server was refused"</em>. The kubelet is running. What is the most likely problem and how do you diagnose it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The <strong>kube-apiserver</strong> is down. Since it's a static pod, diagnose as follows:</p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if the api server container is running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check if the API server container is running</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep kube-apiserver

<span class="comment"># 2. If not running, check the static pod manifest</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Check kubelet logs for manifest errors</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet | grep -i "error\|apiserver" | tail -20

<span class="comment"># 4. Common fixes:</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix YAML syntax errors in the manifest</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix wrong certificate paths (--tls-cert-file, --tls-private-key-file)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix wrong etcd endpoints (--etcd-servers)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix port conflicts (--secure-port)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is a <strong>classic CKA troubleshooting scenario</strong>. The kube-apiserver runs as a static pod managed by the kubelet. If the manifest file in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> has errors, the kubelet cannot start the pod. Key diagnostic path: <code>crictl ps</code> → <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> → check manifest → fix → kubelet automatically restarts the static pod. No <code>kubectl apply</code> needed — static pods are managed by the kubelet directly from filesystem watching.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are <strong>static pods</strong>? Where are they defined, which component manages them, and how are they different from regular pods created via the API server?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>Static pods</strong> are pods managed directly by the <strong>kubelet</strong> on a specific node, without going through the API server.</p>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Feature</th>
                                            <th>Static Pods</th>
                                            <th>Regular Pods</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Managed by</td>
                                            <td>kubelet (filesystem watcher)</td>
                                            <td>API server → scheduler → kubelet</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Defined in</td>
                                            <td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></td>
                                            <td>etcd (via kubectl/API)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Visible in API</td>
                                            <td>Yes, as a <em>mirror pod</em> (read-only)</td>
                                            <td>Yes, fully managed</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Can be deleted via kubectl</td>
                                            <td>No — delete the file to remove</td>
                                            <td>Yes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Static pods are how the control plane bootstraps itself — <code>kube-apiserver</code>, <code>etcd</code>, <code>kube-scheduler</code>, and <code>kube-controller-manager</code> all run as static pods. The kubelet watches the <code>staticPodPath</code> (configured in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>) and automatically creates/restarts pods from YAML files found there. On the CKA, you may be asked to create a static pod: simply place a valid pod manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and the kubelet will start it.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the <strong>kube-scheduler's</strong> decision process. When a new pod is created without a <code>nodeName</code>, what steps does the scheduler take to place it on a node?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The scheduler follows a <strong>two-phase process</strong>:</p>
                                <p><strong>Phase 1: Filtering</strong> — Eliminates nodes that can't run the pod:</p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Insufficient CPU/memory resources</li>
                                    <li>Taints that the pod doesn't tolerate</li>
                                    <li>Node affinity rules not satisfied</li>
                                    <li>PodAntiAffinity constraints</li>
                                    <li>Unschedulable nodes (<code>cordon</code>ed)</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p><strong>Phase 2: Scoring</strong> — Ranks remaining nodes by preference:</p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Resource balance (spread workloads evenly)</li>
                                    <li>Node affinity preferences (soft rules)</li>
                                    <li>Pod topology spread constraints</li>
                                    <li>Image locality (node already has the image cached)</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p>The highest-scoring node wins. The scheduler then sets <code>spec.nodeName</code> via the API server.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">filter → score → bind, on a 4-node cluster</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  new pod (nodeName: "")   requests: cpu 500m · toleration: none
        │
        ▼ FILTER (who CAN run it?)
  node-1 ✅ fits     node-2 ❌ insufficient cpu    node-3 ❌ NoSchedule taint    node-4 ✅ fits
        │
        ▼ SCORE (who's BEST?)          node-1: 82 (image cached, balanced)
                                       node-4: 61 (image must pull)
        ▼ BIND
  POST …/pods/&lt;pod&gt;/binding  →  spec.nodeName = node-1  →  kubelet(node-1) starts it
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The CKA tests scheduling concepts heavily (15% of the exam). Key scenarios: (1) Pod stuck in <code>Pending</code> — all nodes filtered out (check taints, resources, node selectors). (2) Pod scheduled to wrong node — scoring preferences not configured correctly. (3) Manually bypass the scheduler by setting <code>spec.nodeName</code> directly in the pod spec. If a pod is Pending, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> events will show <em>"0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had taint..."</em> — this tells you exactly which filter failed.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">The <code>kube-controller-manager</code> runs many controllers. Name at least 5 built-in controllers and describe what each one is responsible for.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Controller</th>
                                            <th>Responsibility</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Node Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Monitors node heartbeats (every 10s), marks NotReady after 40s, evicts pods after 5min</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>ReplicaSet Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Ensures the correct number of pod replicas are running at all times</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Deployment Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Manages ReplicaSets for rolling updates, rollbacks, and scaling</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Endpoint Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Populates Endpoints objects (maps Services to pod IPs)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>ServiceAccount Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Creates default ServiceAccount + token for every new namespace</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Job Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Tracks Job completions and creates new pods until the desired completion count is reached</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Namespace Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Cleans up resources when a namespace is deleted (cascading deletion)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>All these controllers run as goroutines inside a single <code>kube-controller-manager</code> binary/pod. Each controller follows the same <strong>Observe → Diff → Act</strong> pattern. On the CKA exam, understanding controllers helps diagnose issues: if replicas aren't being created, the controller-manager might be down. If nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> for too long without pod eviction, check the node controller's <code>--pod-eviction-timeout</code> flag.</p>
