                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the ownership chain for a pod named <code>easypj-web-7d4-x9k2</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The name literally encodes the chain; each layer links up via <code>ownerReferences</code>:</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">the ownership chain, read from the pod's own name</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   🚀 Deployment   easypj-web
        │ owns (ownerReference)
        ▼
   🎛️ ReplicaSet   easypj-web-7d4            ← Deployment name + pod-template-hash
        │ owns
        ▼
   📦 Pod          easypj-web-7d4-x9k2       ← ReplicaSet name + random suffix
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The name literally encodes the chain — and that chain drives cascading deletion.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why are Pods "ephemeral," and what's the implication for IPs?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A pod is never repaired — when it dies it's replaced by a <em>new</em> pod with a new name and a new IP. So you must never hard-code a pod IP; reach pods through a <strong>Service</strong> (stable VIP + DNS) instead.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">pods churn; the Service address never does</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  🔌 Service easypj-web   VIP 10.96.142.10  ·  DNS easypj-web.easypj.svc   ← STABLE, forever
        │ endpoints track whichever pods currently match + are Ready
        ▼
  t0:  📦 …-x9k2 (10.244.1.5)   💥 dies
  t1:  📦 …-m4p8 (10.244.2.9)   ← NEW name, NEW IP — clients never noticed
  ❌ hard-coded 10.244.1.5 → broken at t1      ✅ easypj-web (Service DNS) → always works
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the entire reason Services exist — stable networking over a moving set of pods.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List the 4 Service types and who can reach each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Reachable by</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><strong>ClusterIP</strong> <em>(default)</em></td><td>in-cluster only</td><td>internal service-to-service</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>NodePort</strong></td><td><code>&lt;nodeIP&gt;:30000–32767</code></td><td>dev / bare-metal external access</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td><td>a cloud load balancer</td><td>cloud production</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>ExternalName</strong></td><td>DNS CNAME (no proxying)</td><td>alias an external host</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>ClusterIP for internal, NodePort for dev/bare-metal, LoadBalancer for cloud prod, ExternalName to alias something outside.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You delete a Pod managed by a Deployment. What happens and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The ReplicaSet immediately creates a <strong>replacement</strong> — its reconcile loop sees "want 3, have 2" and acts. To actually remove it, lower the desired state (<code>kubectl scale</code>) or delete the Deployment.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>You do</th><th>What changed</th><th>Controller's reaction</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>kubectl delete pod …</code></td><td><em>actual</em> state only (3→2)</td><td>gap detected → new pod created (back to 3)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kubectl scale --replicas=2</code></td><td><em>desired</em> state (3→2)</td><td>no gap → the removal sticks ✅</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kubectl delete deploy …</code></td><td>desired state removed entirely</td><td>cascade deletes RS + pods ✅</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>You changed actual, not desired; the controller just closes the gap again.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">From the relationship map: how does a ConfigMap reach a container?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The pod template references it three ways; the kubelet injects it when the container starts:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">3 ways a ConfigMap reaches a container</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ① all keys → env vars</span>
envFrom:
- configMapRef:
    name: easypj-config
<span class="comment"># ② one key → one env var (optionally renamed)</span>
env:
- name: DB_HOST
  valueFrom:
    configMapKeyRef:
      name: easypj-config
      key: db_host
<span class="comment"># ③ keys → files in a mounted volume (can hot-reload)</span>
volumes:
- name: cfg
  configMap:
    name: easypj-config</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Env values are fixed at start; file mounts can hot-reload. Full detail in Chapter 23.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the default RollingUpdate values, and what does each control?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong><code>maxSurge: 25%</code></strong> and <strong><code>maxUnavailable: 25%</code></strong> — the two knobs that pace a zero-downtime roll.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Knob</th><th>Controls</th><th>With <code>replicas: 4</code></th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>maxSurge</code></td><td>how many <em>extra</em> pods may exist above desired during the roll</td><td>up to 5 pods alive at once</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>maxUnavailable</code></td><td>how many of the desired pods may be <em>down</em> during the roll</td><td>at least 3 always serving</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The analogy: changing tires while driving — <code>maxSurge</code> bolts a spare on first; <code>maxUnavailable</code> is how many wheels you'll briefly run without. The <code>Recreate</code> strategy skips the choreography entirely — and causes downtime.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A namespace is stuck in <code>Terminating</code> — likely cause, and how do you check?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A <strong>finalizer</strong> is blocking deletion — on the namespace itself or on a resource inside it whose controller can't finish cleanup.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find the blocker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get ns easypj-test -o yaml | grep -B2 -A6 finalizers   <span class="comment"># what's holding it</span>
kubectl get ns easypj-test -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}'  <span class="comment"># names the stuck resource type</span>
kubectl api-resources --verbs=list --namespaced -o name \
  | xargs -n1 kubectl get -n easypj-test 2&gt;/dev/null            <span class="comment"># anything left inside?</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Deletion sets <code>deletionTimestamp</code>, then <em>waits</em> until every finalizer clears. Fix the blocked resource/controller rather than force-stripping the finalizer (that can orphan real resources) — the full walkthrough is in Chapter 1's Scenario Q14 and Chapter 32.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

