            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 4.2 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 4.2 — Roll Forward, Roll Back (Zero Downtime)</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>easypj-web runs 3 replicas of <code>easypj:v1</code>. Ship <code>v2</code> with a rolling update, watch it complete, inspect the revision history — then discover v2 is buggy and roll back <em>instantly</em>. Finally, prove the old ReplicaSet machinery made the rollback cheap.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>set image</code> triggers the roll; <code>rollout status/history/undo</code> is the lifecycle trio. After the rollback, <code>kubectl get rs</code> shows the trick — the "old" ReplicaSet never died, it was just scaled to 0.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">roll → verify → undo → understand</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 0. Baseline (annotate the cause so history is readable)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3

<span class="comment"># 1. ROLL FORWARD to v2 — pods replaced in waves (maxSurge/maxUnavailable 25%)</span>
kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web easypj-web=easypj:v2
kubectl rollout status deploy/easypj-web        <span class="comment"># waits until the roll completes ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. INSPECT the history</span>
kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web       <span class="comment"># revision 1 (v1), revision 2 (v2)</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. v2 is bad → ROLL BACK instantly</span>
kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web          <span class="comment"># or --to-revision=1</span>
kubectl rollout status deploy/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># 4. THE TRICK — the old ReplicaSet was kept at 0 replicas the whole time</span>
kubectl get rs -l app=easypj-web
<span class="comment">#  easypj-web-6d8f9  3  3  3   ← v1, scaled back UP by the undo</span>
<span class="comment">#  easypj-web-7c4b2  0  0  0   ← v2, now parked at 0 for a future redo</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> a rollback isn't a rebuild — the Deployment just <em>rebalances replicas between ReplicaSets</em> it kept around (<code>revisionHistoryLimit</code>, default 10). That's why <code>undo</code> is instant and safe, and why the RS naming (<code>-&lt;pod-template-hash&gt;</code>) matters: one RS per template version. This exact sequence is a guaranteed exam task class.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 4.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 4.3 — "Running but No Traffic": the Readiness Cut-off</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Users say easypj is down, yet <code>kubectl get pods</code> shows all pods <code>Running</code>. Reproduce the classic cause: give a pod a readiness probe that fails, and watch Kubernetes silently pull it out of the Service — <em>without restarting it</em>. Then explain the liveness-vs-readiness distinction from what you observed.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>Running</code> is a <em>phase</em>; joining a Service requires the <code>Ready</code> <em>condition</em>. Watch <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> and the pod's <code>READY 0/1</code> column — that's where the story is.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">break readiness → watch the endpoints drain</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. A deployment + service with a readiness probe that will FAIL</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=nginx --replicas=2 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; d.yaml
<span class="comment"># edit d.yaml — add to the container:</span>
<span class="comment">#   readinessProbe:</span>
<span class="comment">#     httpGet: { path: /nope, port: 80 }   # 404 → probe fails</span>
<span class="comment">#     periodSeconds: 5</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml
kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80

<span class="comment"># 2. OBSERVE — Running, but 0/1 Ready, and the Service has NO backends</span>
kubectl get pods                       <span class="comment"># STATUS Running · READY 0/1  ← the tell</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-web       <span class="comment"># ENDPOINTS: &lt;none&gt;</span>
kubectl describe pod -l app=easypj-web | grep -A3 "Readiness"
<span class="comment">#   Readiness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 404</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. FIX the probe path (path: /) and re-apply</span>
sed -i 's|/nope|/|' d.yaml &amp;&amp; kubectl apply -f d.yaml
kubectl get endpoints easypj-web       <span class="comment"># pod IPs appear ✅ traffic flows</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Probe</th><th>Question it asks</th><th>On failure</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><strong>readiness</strong></td><td>"open for business?"</td><td>remove from Service endpoints — <em>no restart</em></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>liveness</strong></td><td>"alive at all?"</td><td><em>restart</em> the container</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>startup</strong></td><td>"still booting?"</td><td>holds the other probes off until it passes</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the pod never restarted (RESTARTS stayed 0) — readiness only gates <em>traffic</em>. The observable chain is: probe fails → <code>Ready</code> condition false → <code>READY 0/1</code> → EndpointSlice drops the IP → users get nothing while the pod "looks" fine. That chain <em>is</em> the answer to one of the most common troubleshooting questions on the exam.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 4.4 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 4.4 — Namespaces: Isolation, Scope &amp; Cross-NS DNS</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Build the two-environment layout: namespaces <code>easypj-dev</code> and <code>easypj-prod</code>, one deployment in each. Then answer with commands, not opinions: (1) can two deployments share one name? (2) which resources ignore namespaces entirely? (3) how does a dev pod reach the prod Service by DNS?</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Names must be unique <em>per namespace</em>, not per cluster. <code>kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false</code> lists the cluster-wide escapees. Cross-namespace DNS is the FQDN: <code>svc.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">two environments, one cluster</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Two namespaces, same-named deployment in each — no conflict</span>
kubectl create ns easypj-dev
kubectl create ns easypj-prod
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 -n easypj-dev
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v2 --replicas=3 -n easypj-prod
kubectl get deploy -A | grep easypj-web    <span class="comment"># both exist — names are per-namespace ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. What ignores namespaces? the cluster-scoped resources</span>
kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false | head
<span class="comment">#   nodes · persistentvolumes · storageclasses · namespaces · clusterroles …</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Cross-namespace DNS — short name inside, FQDN across</span>
kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 -n easypj-prod
kubectl run tmp -n easypj-dev --image=busybox:1.28 --rm -it --restart=Never -- \
  nslookup easypj-web.easypj-prod.svc.cluster.local   <span class="comment"># resolves ✅</span>
<span class="comment">#   (plain "easypj-web" from easypj-dev would NOT resolve — wrong namespace)</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Set your working namespace once instead of typing -n forever</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-prod</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> namespaces gave you name isolation, quota/RBAC boundaries, and DNS scoping — but <em>not</em> node, PV, or network isolation (nodes and PVs are cluster-scoped; traffic fencing needs NetworkPolicy, Ch 20). And deleting a namespace deletes everything <em>namespaced</em> inside it while cluster-scoped objects survive — the exact distinction <code>api-resources --namespaced</code> makes visible.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 4.5 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 4.5 — Label Surgery: Orphan a Pod, Watch the Controller React</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Labels aren't decoration — they're <em>membership</em>. Prove it: on a 3-replica easypj Deployment, change one pod's <code>app</code> label. Predict first, then observe: what does the ReplicaSet do? What happens to the relabeled pod? Then use the trick for what it's actually good for — <strong>quarantining a sick pod for debugging</strong> without losing it.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> The ReplicaSet counts only pods matching its selector. Relabel one and the RS suddenly sees 2/3 — it creates a replacement. The relabeled pod keeps running, but now belongs to nobody (and left the Service too).
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">membership is a label match</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Baseline: 3 pods, all labeled app=easypj-web</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=nginx --replicas=3
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj-web            <span class="comment"># 3 pods</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. SURGERY — relabel ONE pod out of the selector</span>
POD=$(kubectl get pods -l app=easypj-web -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl label pod "$POD" app=quarantine --overwrite

<span class="comment"># 3. OBSERVE — the RS instantly backfills; the orphan keeps running</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels
<span class="comment">#   easypj-web-xxx-NEW    app=easypj-web   ← replacement (RS saw 2/3)</span>
<span class="comment">#   easypj-web-xxx-OLD    app=quarantine   ← orphaned: still Running, out of the</span>
<span class="comment">#                                            Service, owned by nobody</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. WHY it's useful — debug the sick pod at leisure, no user impact</span>
kubectl exec -it "$POD" -- sh                 <span class="comment"># poke around the quarantined pod</span>
kubectl delete pod "$POD"                     <span class="comment"># clean up when done (nothing respawns it)</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> three deep facts surfaced at once — (1) a ReplicaSet's <em>membership is the label match</em>, nothing more; (2) the Service dropped the pod for the same reason (selectors again); (3) the orphan didn't respawn when deleted, because no controller claimed it anymore. The quarantine move — relabel to detach, debug, delete — is a genuinely useful production technique built entirely on Chapter 4's mental model.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 4 Visual Summary — the objects, wired together</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   🌍 Ingress/Gateway ─routes─▶ 🔌 Service ─selector(app=easypj)─▶ 📦 Pods
                                    ▲                                ▲ owned by
                               🚀 Deployment ─manages─▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet ┘ creates (self-heal)
                                    │ pod template pulls in:
                                    ├─ ⚙️ ConfigMap / 🔒 Secret (envFrom)
                                    ├─ 💾 PVC ─▶ PV ─▶ 🧱 StorageClass
                                    └─ 🛡️ ServiceAccount ◀─ RBAC governs access
   🗂️ Namespace contains it all   ·   🧯 NetworkPolicy firewalls it   ·   🔖 LABELS are the glue
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   You create a Deployment → it owns a ReplicaSet → which owns Pods; a Service finds those Pods
   by LABEL and gives them a stable address. That sentence is 80% of every CKA workload task.
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> The objects nest. You create a <strong>Deployment</strong>; it <em>manages</em> a <strong>ReplicaSet</strong>; the ReplicaSet <em>creates and self-heals</em> the <strong>Pods</strong>. You never make the lower layers by hand — you describe the top one and ownership cascades down.</p>
                    <p>A pod's template pulls in everything it needs: <strong>ConfigMap/Secret</strong> for configuration (<code>envFrom</code>), a <strong>PVC → PV → StorageClass</strong> chain for storage, and a <strong>ServiceAccount</strong> (governed by RBAC) for API access. Out front, a <strong>Service</strong> uses a <em>label selector</em> (<code>app=easypj</code>) to find the ever-changing pods and give them one stable address; <strong>Ingress/Gateway</strong> routes external traffic to that Service. Around it all, a <strong>Namespace</strong> contains the objects and a <strong>NetworkPolicy</strong> firewalls them.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> <em>labels are the glue</em> — nothing is wired by name, everything is matched by label. "Create a Deployment → it owns a ReplicaSet → which owns Pods → a Service selects them by label" is the single sentence behind roughly 80% of every CKA workload task.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
