            <!-- ─────────── 5.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.3 Installing kubectl</h3>
                <p><code>kubectl</code> is the CLI for <em>any</em> cluster — local or production. It's your cluster's remote control, and it reads <code>~/.kube/config</code> to know where to point and who you are.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">install kubectl (per OS)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">choco install kubernetes-cli            <span class="comment"># Windows</span>
brew install kubectl                     <span class="comment"># macOS</span>
<span class="comment"># Linux — fetch the stable build, then install it onto PATH</span>
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
sudo install kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
kubectl version --client                <span class="comment"># confirm it's installed</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">How kubectl reaches your cluster</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   👤 YOU                          ~/.kube/config tells kubectl        ☸️ CLUSTER
   ┌──────────────┐               WHERE + WHO                         ┌──────────────┐
   │ $ kubectl    │  1 read kubeconfig → 2 find server URL            │ 📡 apiserver  │
   │   get pods   │  3 authenticate (cert/token) → 4 REST over TLS ──▶│   :6443       │
   │ ┌──────────┐ │                                                   │      │        │
   │ │ kubectl  │ │ ◀── 6 format &amp; display ── 5 JSON response ───────│      ▼        │
   │ └──────────┘ │                                                   │ 🗄️ etcd       │
   └──────────────┘                                                   └──────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>⚡ Set up aliases &amp; completion FIRST (exam speed)</h4>
                <p>Do this as the very first thing in any practice or exam shell — it pays for itself within minutes:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">~/.bashrc</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ── the aliases that save real minutes (set these FIRST on the exam) ──</span>
alias k=kubectl
alias kn='kubectl config set-context --current --namespace'   <span class="comment"># kn easypj-dev → switch ns</span>
alias kgp='kubectl get pods'
alias kgn='kubectl get nodes'
alias kga='kubectl get all'
alias kd='kubectl describe'
alias kaf='kubectl apply -f'
alias kdel='kubectl delete'
alias kl='kubectl logs'
alias ke='kubectl exec -it'
<span class="comment"># ── env-var shortcuts you'll type constantly ──</span>
export do='--dry-run=client -o yaml'    <span class="comment"># $do = generate a manifest, don't create</span>
export now='--force --grace-period=0'   <span class="comment"># $now = delete immediately</span>
<span class="comment"># ── completion (and make it work for the `k` alias) ──</span>
source &lt;(kubectl completion bash)
complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k
<span class="comment"># verify:  k run nginx --image=nginx $do &gt; pod.yaml   ← should print YAML, not create</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🔌 kubectl plugins worth knowing (via <code>krew</code> — not on the exam)</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>What it does</th><th>Install</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>ctx</strong> (kubectx)</td><td>fast interactive context switching</td><td><code>kubectl krew install ctx</code></td><td><code>kubectl ctx dev</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ns</strong> (kubens)</td><td>fast namespace switching</td><td><code>kubectl krew install ns</code></td><td><code>kubectl ns easypj-dev</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>neat</strong></td><td>strips clutter from <code>-o yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl krew install neat</code></td><td><code>kubectl get pod web -o yaml | kubectl neat</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>tree</strong></td><td>shows ownership hierarchy</td><td><code>kubectl krew install tree</code></td><td><code>kubectl tree deploy easypj-web</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.4 Understanding kubeconfig</h3>
                <p>The <code>~/.kube/config</code> file is the single most important file for <code>kubectl</code>: it says <strong>where</strong> to connect (cluster), <strong>who</strong> you are (user), and <strong>which namespace</strong> is the default — bundled into a <strong>context</strong>.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubeconfig — three building blocks → one context</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   ~/.kube/config
   ├─ clusters:  WHERE   minikube · easypj-staging · easypj-prod   (server URL + CA)
   ├─ users:     WHO     minikube-user (cert) · dev-user (token) · admin-user (cert)
   └─ contexts:  COMBO   minikube = minikube + minikube-user
                         dev      = easypj-staging + dev-user  + ns:easypj-dev
                         prod     = easypj-prod    + admin-user + ns:easypj-prod
   current-context: minikube     ◀── kubectl acts HERE

         🖥️ Cluster (WHERE)  +  👤 User (WHO)  +  📂 Namespace (SCOPE)  =  📋 Context
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">~/.kube/config (trimmed)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Config
current-context: minikube          <span class="comment"># ← the active context</span>
clusters:
- cluster:
    certificate-authority: ~/.minikube/ca.crt
    server: https://192.168.49.2:8443   <span class="comment"># WHERE</span>
  name: minikube
users:
- name: minikube-user                    <span class="comment"># WHO</span>
  user:
    client-certificate: ~/.minikube/profiles/minikube/client.crt
    client-key: ~/.minikube/profiles/minikube/client.key
contexts:
- context:
    cluster: minikube
    user: minikube-user
    namespace: default                   <span class="comment"># default ns for this context</span>
  name: minikube</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Always check your context first</h5><p>Every exam question names a context. Acting in the wrong one = right work on the wrong cluster = <strong>zero points</strong>. Run <code>kubectl config current-context</code> before you answer, every time.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.5 Managing Clusters &amp; Contexts</h3>
                <p>In real life — and on the exam — you juggle several clusters. These four commands are your lifeline; the first one is the opening move of nearly every task.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">context management</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List every context (★ = current)</span>
kubectl config get-contexts
<span class="comment"># CURRENT   NAME       CLUSTER    AUTHINFO        NAMESPACE</span>
<span class="comment"># *         minikube   minikube   minikube-user   default</span>
<span class="comment">#           dev        staging    dev-user        easypj-dev</span>
<span class="comment">#           prod       prod       admin-user      easypj-prod</span>

<span class="comment"># Which context am I on right now?</span>
kubectl config current-context
<span class="comment"># minikube</span>

<span class="comment"># SWITCH context — the opening move of nearly every exam task</span>
kubectl config use-context dev
<span class="comment"># Switched to context "dev".</span>

<span class="comment"># Set the default namespace for the CURRENT context (stop typing -n)</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># Context "dev" modified.</span>

<span class="comment"># Create a brand-new context from existing cluster + user</span>
kubectl config set-context easypj-staging \
  --cluster=staging --user=dev-user --namespace=easypj-staging

<span class="comment"># Merge several kubeconfig files into one merged view</span>
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/.kube/cfg-staging:~/.kube/cfg-prod
kubectl config get-contexts        <span class="comment"># now lists all merged contexts</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The 4-beat rhythm of every exam task</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   📋 read the question  ─▶  🔄 use-context  ─▶  ✅ current-context (verify)  ─▶  ⌨️ answer
        the 4-beat rhythm of EVERY exam task — never skip the verify step
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Practise the exact exam workflow</h5><p>Make 3 throwaway contexts on Minikube, then drill "switch → verify → act" until it's reflex:</p></div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">context drill (all point at minikube)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Setup — 3 throwaway contexts, all pointing at the one minikube cluster</span>
kubectl config set-context cka1 --cluster=minikube --user=minikube --namespace=default
kubectl config set-context cka2 --cluster=minikube --user=minikube --namespace=kube-system
kubectl config set-context cka3 --cluster=minikube --user=minikube --namespace=easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># ── Simulated exam Q1: "Use context cka1. List pods in default." ──</span>
kubectl config use-context cka1
kubectl config current-context        <span class="comment"># verify → cka1</span>
kubectl get pods

<span class="comment"># ── Simulated exam Q2: "Use context cka2. Find the kube-proxy pods." ──</span>
kubectl config use-context cka2
kubectl config current-context        <span class="comment"># verify → cka2</span>
kubectl get pods | grep kube-proxy

<span class="comment"># ── Simulated exam Q3: "Use context cka3. Scale deploy easypj to 3." ──</span>
kubectl config use-context cka3
kubectl config current-context        <span class="comment"># verify → cka3</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=3

<span class="comment"># Cleanup the practice contexts</span>
kubectl config delete-context cka1
kubectl config delete-context cka2
kubectl config delete-context cka3
kubectl config use-context minikube</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.6 Verifying Your Cluster</h3>
                <p>After setup — and on every fresh exam cluster — orient yourself with these five checks before doing anything else.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">orient yourself on any cluster</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Cluster info — where is the API server &amp; CoreDNS?</span>
kubectl cluster-info
<span class="comment"># Kubernetes control plane is running at https://192.168.49.2:8443</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS is running at https://192.168.49.2:8443/api/v1/.../kube-dns:dns</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Nodes — all Ready? (status, version, IP, OS)</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION   INTERNAL-IP    OS-IMAGE</span>
