            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how kubectl finds, merges &amp; authenticates with a cluster</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>"kubectl just connects" hides a precise resolution order. Knowing it turns "why am I on the wrong cluster?" into a two-second fix.</p>
                    <h5>① Which kubeconfig wins — strict precedence</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   1. --kubeconfig=FILE       ← explicit flag (highest priority)
   2. $KUBECONFIG=a:b:c       ← colon-separated list → MERGED (first file wins a clash)
   3. ~/.kube/config         ← the default
   chosen file → read clusters/users/contexts → act on current-context (or --context=X)
</pre>
                    <p>This precedence isn't kubectl-specific plumbing — it's implemented by <strong>client-go</strong>, the shared Go library every Kubernetes tool is built on. <code>kubectl</code>, custom controllers, operators, and CI tooling written in Go all resolve their cluster connection through the exact same <code>ClientConfig</code> rules above, which is why a <code>--kubeconfig</code> flag or <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> env var works identically whether you're running <code>kubectl</code> by hand or a controller in a CI pipeline. In CI specifically, the standard pattern is to point <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> at a freshly-generated, pipeline-scoped file so each job targets the right ephemeral cluster without ever touching (or clobbering) your personal <code>~/.kube/config</code>.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Merging:</strong> with <code>$KUBECONFIG=a:b:c</code> every context is visible at once and you switch with <code>use-context</code> — no single giant file needed. On a name clash the <em>first</em> file wins — and per the official docs, that's a <strong>whole-entry</strong> win, not a field-by-field one: if two files both define a <code>cluster</code> named <code>prod</code>, the <em>entire</em> first-file entry is kept and the second file's version is discarded outright, even for fields the first entry didn't set. Never assume a "gap" in one file gets filled in from another.</div>
                    <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 Only trust kubeconfig files from sources you trust</h5><p>A kubeconfig isn't inert data — a <code>user.exec</code> entry runs an <strong>arbitrary local binary</strong> to fetch credentials. A maliciously-crafted kubeconfig (e.g. from a "helpful" script or a phishing attachment) can execute code on your machine the instant a <code>kubectl</code> command reads it, or exfiltrate files via a poisoned <code>certificate-authority</code>/<code>client-certificate</code> path. Treat an unfamiliar kubeconfig exactly like an unfamiliar shell script — read it before you point kubectl at it.</p></div>
                    <h5>② Identity &amp; auth tricks worth knowing</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>exec credential plugin</strong></td><td>kubeconfig calls an external binary for a short-lived token — how cloud auth works (<code>aws eks get-token</code>, <code>gke-gcloud-auth-plugin</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>impersonation</strong></td><td><code>--as=jane --as-group=dev</code> acts as another identity (needs RBAC) — the fastest way to test "can this user do X?"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>impersonation, deeper</strong></td><td><code>--as-uid=abc-123</code> impersonates a specific UID; <code>--as-user-extra=key=value</code> impersonates extra identity fields some authenticators attach — both need the <code>impersonate</code> RBAC verb, same as <code>--as</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>insecure-skip-tls-verify</code></td><td>skips the API server cert check — convenient in labs, a real risk in prod</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubectx / kubens</strong></td><td>community tools for instant context/namespace switching (krew) — handy off-exam</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h5>③ How kubectl actually authenticates — the mutual-TLS handshake</h5>
                    <p>Connecting isn't just "open a socket." kubectl and the apiserver run a <strong>mutual TLS</strong> handshake: the client verifies the server <em>and</em> the server verifies the client — and the client's certificate <em>is</em> its identity.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl (client)                                   📡 apiserver :6443
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ① TCP + ClientHello ────────────────────────────▶
   ② ◀─ server Certificate (CN=kube-apiserver,          client verifies it against
        SANs: kubernetes · kubernetes.default ·         certificate-authority(-data) from
        10.96.0.1 · the node IP)                        kubeconfig ✅ (mismatch → conn refused)
   ③ client Certificate ───────────────────────────▶   apiserver verifies it was
        (CN=kubernetes-admin, O=system:masters)          signed by the cluster CA ✅
   ④ encrypted session ──── kubectl get pods ───────▶   authN done → authZ (RBAC) → respond
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   🔑 the cert's  CN → your USERNAME  ·  O → your GROUP(s)
      RBAC RoleBindings then grant that username/group its permissions (Ch 24)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Auth method</th><th>Identity comes from</th><th>Used by</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>TLS client certificate</strong></td><td>cert <code>CN</code> → username · <code>O</code> → group</td><td>kubeadm admin (<code>kubernetes-admin</code> / <code>system:masters</code>), control-plane components</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Bearer token (JWT)</strong></td><td>token subject &amp; claims</td><td>ServiceAccounts (pods), OIDC users, CI, managed clusters</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The insight cka_1 only hints at:</strong> Kubernetes has <em>no</em> "User" objects — your identity is whatever the apiserver <em>extracts</em> from your cert or token at handshake time. That's why "creating a user" (Ch 26) really means <em>issuing a client cert with the right <code>CN</code>/<code>O</code></em>, and why <code>insecure-skip-tls-verify: true</code> is dangerous — it disables step ②, so a spoofed apiserver would be trusted blindly.</div>

                    <h5>④ Two facts that make it all click: switching context is local, auth is per-request</h5>
                    <p>Two mechanics trip up almost everyone — and the moment they click, kubeconfig stops feeling like magic:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   SWITCHING CONTEXT = editing ONE local field — zero network, nothing in the cluster changes
     kubectl config use-context prod
        └─ just sets  current-context: prod  in ~/.kube/config
           no connection is opened; the NEXT command simply reads the new cluster + user + namespace

   EVERY API CALL re-authenticates from scratch — HTTP is stateless, there is NO login/session
     kubectl get pods   → TLS handshake → present cert/token → authN → authZ → response
     kubectl get svc    → TLS handshake → present cert/token → authN → authZ → response   (all over again)
        no cookie, no "logged-in" state — each request independently carries and proves its identity
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this matters:</strong> because there is no session, a rotated or expired credential breaks the <em>very next</em> command (not "after you log out") — a classic "it worked five minutes ago" exam panic. And because switching context is purely local, you flip between the exam's ~6 clusters instantly with zero latency. An <em>exec credential plugin</em> (cloud auth) is also invoked per request, but it caches the token until expiry — which is why the first call after a token expires pauses briefly while it refreshes, then is fast again.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ How real teams manage kubeconfig at scale — cloud CLIs, security, and GitOps</h5>
                    <p>Minikube hands you one context in one file. A real platform team juggles dozens of clusters across clouds and environments — here's how that's actually done outside the exam, and the security habits that go with it.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   CLOUD CLIs GENERATE (or merge) kubeconfig entries — you rarely hand-write clusters/users
     AWS    aws eks update-kubeconfig --name my-cluster --region us-east-1
              → appends a cluster+user+context to ~/.kube/config; the "user" is an EXEC PLUGIN
                (aws eks get-token) that mints a short-lived token from your AWS credentials
     GCP    gcloud container clusters get-credentials my-cluster --zone us-central1-a
              → same idea, exec plugin = gke-gcloud-auth-plugin, backed by your gcloud login
     Azure  az aks get-credentials --name my-cluster --resource-group my-rg
              → same idea again — three clouds, one pattern: kubeconfig holds a POINTER to your
                cloud identity, not a long-lived cluster credential

   WHY THIS MATTERS: your kubeconfig's "user" is only as strong as the exec plugin's source —
   revoke the AWS/GCP/Azure identity centrally, and every kubeconfig pointing at it goes dead
   instantly. No per-cluster credential to rotate or track down.
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Practice</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>chmod 600 ~/.kube/config</code></td><td>the file holds credentials (certs, tokens, or exec-plugin config) — world/group-readable is a local privilege-escalation path on shared machines.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Never commit a kubeconfig to git</td><td>static tokens/certs inside it grant direct cluster access; treat it like a password file, not documentation. Use <code>.gitignore</code> and secret scanners as a backstop.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Prefer exec-plugin/OIDC auth over long-lived static tokens</td><td>a static <code>ServiceAccount</code> token embedded in a file never expires until manually revoked; an exec plugin mints short-lived tokens tied to your identity provider — compromise has a much smaller blast radius.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>One kubeconfig <em>context</em> per environment, least-privilege RBAC per context</td><td>a <code>dev</code> context bound to a ServiceAccount that can only touch <code>easypj-dev</code> makes "I ran the wrong command on prod" structurally impossible, not just a discipline problem.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>GitOps controllers (Argo CD, Flux) don't use your kubeconfig at all</td><td>they run <em>inside</em> the cluster with a ServiceAccount and reconcile from a git repo — no human kubeconfig ever touches production, which is exactly why platform teams increasingly push routine changes through GitOps instead of <code>kubectl apply</code> from a laptop.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The exam vs the real world:</strong> the CKA hands you a pre-built kubeconfig with static contexts because it's testing your <code>kubectl</code>/cluster skills, not your cloud IAM setup. In production, the kubeconfig you actually run day-to-day is almost always <strong>generated by a cloud CLI backed by your own identity</strong> — which is also why "who can access this cluster" is answered by your <em>identity provider's</em> access policy, not by hunting down copies of a static file.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Minikube to learn, kubeadm to pass</strong> — the exam builds clusters with kubeadm (Part 3).</li>
                    <li><strong>kubeconfig = clusters (where) + users (who) + contexts (the combo)</strong>; <code>current-context</code> is active.</li>
                    <li><strong>Switch context FIRST, verify, then act</strong> — the 4-beat rhythm of every exam task.</li>
                    <li><strong>Set a default namespace</strong> with <code>set-context --current --namespace=…</code> so you stop typing <code>-n</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify any cluster</strong> with <code>cluster-info</code> · <code>get nodes</code> · <code>get pods -n kube-system</code>; add-ons unlock <code>top</code>/ingress.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubectl is ONE binary that talks to ANY cluster</strong> — laptop, cloud, or exam; the kubeconfig alone decides which. Re-pointing it is a context switch, not a reinstall.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Wrong context = 0 marks.</strong> Every question names one — <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code> before you touch anything.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgotten namespace.</strong> Miss <code>-n</code> (or a default ns) and your object lands in <code>default</code> — graded as missing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Completion not loaded.</strong> If <code>kubectl &lt;TAB&gt;</code> is dead: <code>source &lt;(kubectl completion bash)</code> (+ the <code>complete … k</code> line for your alias).</li>
                    <li><strong>Minikube ≠ kubeadm.</strong> The exam's kubeadm clusters have real systemd kubelets, real certs, and real multi-node topology — practise on kubeadm too.</li>
                    <li><strong>No metrics-server → <code>kubectl top</code> and HPA silently fail</strong> ("error: metrics not available yet"). It's the add-on most labs forget to enable.</li>
                    <li><strong>A stale default namespace makes <code>get pods</code> look "empty".</strong> The pods exist — you're just looking in the wrong namespace. Check with <code>kubectl config get-contexts</code> (NAMESPACE column) or <code>get pods -A</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>insecure-skip-tls-verify: true</code> hides cert problems</strong> in dev that bite in prod (man-in-the-middle risk) — fine in a lab, never anywhere real.</li>
                    <li><strong>Single-node Minikube can't rehearse scheduling.</strong> Taints, affinity, cordon and drain all need multiple nodes — start with <code>--nodes=3</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

