                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Your cluster shows 3 nodes when you run <code>kubectl get nodes</code>, but one node shows <code>NotReady</code>. You need to SSH to that node and diagnose the issue. What are the first three things you check?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">from control plane: identify the notready node</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># From control plane: identify the NotReady node</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
kubectl describe node &lt;node-name&gt; | grep -A 10 Conditions

<span class="comment"># SSH to the node</span>
ssh &lt;node-name&gt;

<span class="comment"># Check 1: Is kubelet running?</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># If not: sudo systemctl start kubelet &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl enable kubelet</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 2: Are there kubelet errors?</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -30

<span class="comment"># Check 3: Is the container runtime running?</span>
sudo systemctl status containerd
<span class="comment"># If not: sudo systemctl start containerd</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>A node shows <code>NotReady</code> when its kubelet hasn't sent a heartbeat to the API server within the timeout (default: 40 seconds). The three most common causes: (1) <strong>kubelet stopped</strong> — start it with systemctl, (2) <strong>kubelet config error</strong> — wrong certificate paths, wrong API server address in kubelet config, (3) <strong>container runtime down</strong> — containerd crashed or is not installed. On the CKA, always check <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> and <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> — the logs tell you exactly what's wrong.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

