            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 5.2 — Prove the KUBECONFIG Precedence Chain</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>You have your default <code>~/.kube/config</code> plus a second file <code>work.yaml</code> from a teammate. Prove all three precedence levels with experiments: (1) merge both via <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> and list the union of contexts, (2) show that <code>--kubeconfig</code> beats the env var, (3) show where a <em>write</em> (like <code>use-context</code>) lands when files are merged.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Precedence is <code>--kubeconfig</code> flag &gt; <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> list &gt; <code>~/.kube/config</code>. In a merged list, the <em>first file that defines a key wins</em>, and writes go to the first file that can hold them.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">three experiments, one precedence chain</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. MERGE — the union of contexts from both files, in memory only</span>
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/work.yaml
kubectl config get-contexts          <span class="comment"># contexts from BOTH files listed</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. FLAG BEATS ENV — point at one file explicitly; the env var is ignored</span>
kubectl config get-contexts --kubeconfig=~/work.yaml
<span class="comment">#   only work.yaml's contexts appear, despite $KUBECONFIG being set</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. WHERE DO WRITES GO? switch context while merged, then inspect the files</span>
kubectl config use-context work-cluster
grep current-context ~/.kube/config   <span class="comment"># ← the write landed HERE (first file)</span>
grep current-context ~/work.yaml      <span class="comment"># unchanged</span>

unset KUBECONFIG                      <span class="comment"># back to the default file only</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the chain is <strong>flag &gt; env &gt; default</strong>, merging is <em>in-memory</em> (no file is rewritten by reading), first-definition-wins on conflicts, and writes land in the first writable file. Knowing this cold explains every "kubectl is talking to the wrong cluster!?" mystery you'll ever hit — including in CI, where <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> points at generated files.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

