            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 5.5 — The Vanishing Pods: a Wrong-Namespace Rescue</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A teammate swears they deployed easypj "but <code>kubectl get pods</code> shows nothing." Reproduce their mistake: deploy into <code>easypj-dev</code>, then "lose" the pods by looking from the wrong namespace. Rescue the situation: find where the pods actually are, then fix the context so it can't happen again.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Nothing is lost — <code>get pods</code> without <code>-n</code> only searches the context's <em>default namespace</em>. <code>-A</code> searches everywhere; <code>set-context --current --namespace</code> makes the fix permanent.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">reproduce → find → fix permanently</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. REPRODUCE — deploy into a namespace, then look from the wrong one</span>
kubectl create ns easypj-dev
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=nginx -n easypj-dev
kubectl get pods                       <span class="comment"># "No resources found in default namespace." 😱</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. FIND — the pods were never lost</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep easypj      <span class="comment"># there they are, in easypj-dev ✅</span>
kubectl config get-contexts            <span class="comment"># NAMESPACE column: empty → means "default"</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. FIX — set the context's default namespace once</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev
kubectl get pods                       <span class="comment"># now they show without -n ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> "my pods vanished" is almost never a cluster problem — it's a <em>viewpoint</em> problem. The triage habit: <code>get pods -A</code> to locate, <code>config get-contexts</code> to see which namespace you're defaulting to, <code>set-context --current --namespace</code> to fix it. On the exam this same mistake in reverse (creating into the wrong namespace) is graded as a missing object — which is why the namespace check opens every task.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

