                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write a single <code>kubectl</code> command to list all pods across all namespaces, showing their node assignment, IP address, and status. Then filter to show only pods that are NOT in <code>Running</code> state.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all pods with node and ip info</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all pods with node and IP info</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o wide

<span class="comment"># Filter for non-Running pods</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running

<span class="comment"># Alternative: use field selector for specific statuses</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running

<span class="comment"># JSONPath for custom columns</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o custom-columns=\
NAME:.metadata.name,\
NAMESPACE:.metadata.namespace,\
NODE:.spec.nodeName,\
STATUS:.status.phase,\
IP:.status.podIP</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>-A</code> (or <code>--all-namespaces</code>) is essential for getting a cluster-wide view. <code>-o wide</code> adds NODE and IP columns. The <code>grep -v Running</code> approach is the fastest on the exam. Field selectors work at the API server level (more efficient but limited to certain fields). Custom columns with <code>-o custom-columns</code> let you show exactly the fields you need — useful for complex output formatting questions on the CKA.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Use <code>kubectl</code> to find the exact YAML field path for a pod's restart policy, resource limits, and liveness probe. Which command acts as an in-cluster documentation system?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the kubectl explain command — your in-cluster docs</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The kubectl explain command — your in-cluster docs</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.restartPolicy
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources.limits
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.livenessProbe

<span class="comment"># Recursive view to see ALL fields</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive | head -80

<span class="comment"># Search for specific fields</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive | grep -i "liveness\|readiness"

<span class="comment"># Drill into nested fields</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.livenessProbe.httpGet</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl explain</code> is your <strong>most valuable tool on the CKA exam</strong>. It's faster than the official docs browser and works offline. The <code>--recursive</code> flag shows ALL field paths, and piping to <code>grep</code> helps you find exact field names. On the exam, if you can't remember whether it's <code>livenessProbe</code> or <code>liveness-probe</code>, just run <code>kubectl explain pod.spec.containers --recursive | grep -i probe</code>. Combine with <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to generate the scaffold, then use <code>explain</code> to add advanced fields.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
