                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write a <code>kubectl</code> command to get the logs of a pod named <code>api-pod</code> that has crashed. The current container has just started (so its logs are empty). How do you see the logs from the previous crash?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">view logs of the previous (crashed) container…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View logs of the previous (crashed) container instance</span>
kubectl logs api-pod --previous

<span class="comment"># View only the last 100 lines</span>
kubectl logs api-pod --previous --tail=100

<span class="comment"># Stream logs in real-time (for current container)</span>
kubectl logs api-pod -f

<span class="comment"># Multi-container: specify which container's logs</span>
kubectl logs api-pod -c main-app --previous

<span class="comment"># All containers in the pod</span>
kubectl logs api-pod --all-containers=true

<span class="comment"># Logs from all pods matching a label</span>
kubectl logs -l app=api --previous</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The <code>--previous</code> flag is <strong>critical for CKA troubleshooting</strong>. When a container is in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>, the current instance may have empty logs or only startup messages. The <code>--previous</code> flag shows logs from the <em>last terminated</em> container instance — this is where the actual error that caused the crash is recorded. On the exam, always try <code>--previous</code> if the current logs don't show the error. Note: Kubernetes only stores the last terminated container's logs — if it has crashed multiple times, you only see the most recent crash.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
