                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Use <code>kubectl</code> to generate a Deployment YAML file for an application named <code>backend</code> with image <code>node:18-alpine</code>, 3 replicas, in namespace <code>staging</code> — without creating the resource. Then explain the dry-run flag.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate yaml without creating anything</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Generate YAML without creating anything</span>
kubectl create deployment backend \
  --image=node:18-alpine \
  --replicas=3 \
  -n staging \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml > backend-deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Two types of dry-run:</span>
<span class="comment"># --dry-run=client  →  generates YAML locally (no API server contact)</span>
<span class="comment"># --dry-run=server  →  sends to API server for validation but doesn't persist</span>

<span class="comment"># Edit the generated YAML to add more fields</span>
vim backend-deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Apply when ready</span>
kubectl apply -f backend-deploy.yaml</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> is the <strong>CKA exam's most used flag combination</strong>. It generates valid YAML for any resource without creating it. <code>client</code> mode doesn't contact the API server (works even if API server is down). <code>server</code> mode validates against the actual API server (catches invalid field names, missing namespaces, quota violations). Strategy: always start with <code>--dry-run=client</code> to generate the scaffold, add advanced fields (resources, probes, volumes), then <code>kubectl apply</code>. This is 10x faster than writing YAML from memory.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Permanently delete a stuck pod named <code>zombie-pod</code> that has been in <code>Terminating</code> state for 10 minutes. The normal <code>kubectl delete</code> doesn't work. What do you do?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">force delete with zero grace period</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Force delete with zero grace period</span>
kubectl delete pod zombie-pod --grace-period=0 --force

<span class="comment"># If the pod had a finalizer preventing deletion</span>
kubectl patch pod zombie-pod -p '{"metadata":{"finalizers":null}}'
kubectl delete pod zombie-pod

<span class="comment"># If it's a pod managed by a deployment, delete won't permanently remove it</span>
<span class="comment"># The replicaset will recreate it. Instead:</span>
kubectl scale deployment &lt;deploy-name&gt; --replicas=0
<span class="comment"># OR delete the deployment</span>
kubectl delete deployment &lt;deploy-name&gt;</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Stuck <code>Terminating</code> pods occur when: (1) The kubelet on the node is down and can't confirm termination, (2) The container's process ignores SIGTERM, (3) finalizers prevent deletion. The <code>--grace-period=0 --force</code> combination tells the API server to remove the pod object immediately from etcd, regardless of whether the kubelet has confirmed container shutdown. On the CKA, this is essential for cleanup when a node is unreachable. Caution: force-deleting doesn't stop the actual container — if the node comes back, the container may still be running (orphaned).</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            
