            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 6.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 6.3 — CrashLoopBackOff: Get the Logs That Matter</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Create a pod that crashes on purpose (<code>kubectl run crasher --image=busybox -- sh -c "echo BOOM: bad config; exit 1"</code>). It enters CrashLoopBackOff. Now recover the actual error message — and demonstrate <em>why</em> plain <code>kubectl logs</code> often shows nothing useful on a crash-looping pod.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Each restart is a <em>new</em> container with fresh (possibly empty) logs. The crash evidence lives in the <em>previous</em> (terminated) container — that's what <code>--previous</code>/<code>-p</code> reads.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the --previous reflex</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Create the crasher and watch it loop</span>
kubectl run crasher --image=busybox -- sh -c "echo BOOM: bad config; exit 1"
kubectl get pod crasher -w            <span class="comment"># Error → CrashLoopBackOff (backoff 10s→20s→40s…)</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. What the restart counter + state tell you</span>
kubectl describe pod crasher | grep -A5 "Last State"
<span class="comment">#   Last State: Terminated · Reason: Error · Exit Code: 1   ← app error class</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. THE KEY MOVE — read the PREVIOUS container's logs</span>
kubectl logs crasher --previous
<span class="comment">#   BOOM: bad config                  ← the actual error ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># (plain `kubectl logs crasher` may race a brand-new container → empty/partial)</span>
kubectl delete pod crasher            <span class="comment"># cleanup</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the triage triple for any crash-looping pod: <code>describe</code> (exit code + reason: 1 = app error, 137 = OOMKilled) → <code>logs --previous</code> (the dying container's words) → fix the cause. The exponential backoff you watched (10s → 5min cap) is also why a "fixed" pod can take minutes to retry — <code>kubectl delete pod</code> it to force an immediate fresh start under its controller.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 6.4 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 6.4 — Shell Into the Un-shellable (kubectl debug)</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Ops hands you two "impossible" targets: (1) a running pod whose image has <strong>no shell</strong> (distroless) that you must inspect from the inside; (2) a <strong>node</strong> you must examine without SSH credentials. Solve both with <code>kubectl debug</code>, and explain what an ephemeral container actually is.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox --target=&lt;ctr&gt;</code> injects a tool container <em>into the pod's namespaces</em>; <code>debug node/&lt;node&gt;</code> runs a privileged pod with the host filesystem at <code>/host</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">two impossible shells</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># TARGET 1 — a distroless pod (kubectl exec would fail: no shell in the image)</span>
kubectl exec -it easypj-web -- sh
<span class="comment">#   error: … "sh": executable file not found  ← the image ships no shell</span>

kubectl debug -it easypj-web --image=busybox --target=easypj
<span class="comment">#   / #    ← a busybox EPHEMERAL container inside the SAME pod:</span>
/ # wget -qO- localhost:8000/jobs/   <span class="comment"># same net namespace → app on localhost ✅</span>
/ # ps aux                            <span class="comment"># --target shares the app's PID namespace ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># TARGET 2 — a node, without SSH</span>
kubectl debug node/worker-1 -it --image=busybox
/ # chroot /host                      <span class="comment"># the node's real filesystem</span>
/ # cat /etc/kubernetes/kubelet.conf | head    <span class="comment"># inspect node config ✅</span>
/ # exit; exit

<span class="comment"># The injected ephemeral container is visible — and permanent — in the pod:</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -o jsonpath='{.spec.ephemeralContainers[*].name}{"\n"}'</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> an <em>ephemeral container</em> is a debug-only container injected into a live pod — it shares the pod's network (and optionally PID) namespaces, takes no ports/probes/resources, is never restarted, and can't be removed (it dies with the pod). It's the <em>only</em> way into distroless or crashed pods; <code>debug node/</code> is the same idea for the node itself. Both are pure exam gold for the Troubleshooting domain.</p>
