            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 6.5 — Validate Before You Create: server dry-run, diff &amp; -v=8</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Prove three "trust but verify" tools on one manifest: (1) show that <code>--dry-run=client</code> happily renders an <em>invalid</em> manifest while <code>--dry-run=server</code> rejects it; (2) preview a change with <code>kubectl diff</code> before applying; (3) reveal the actual HTTP call behind a simple <code>get</code> with <code>-v=8</code>.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Client dry-run never contacts the apiserver — so it can't catch schema errors. Server dry-run runs the full admission/validation pipeline and then discards the object.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">client vs server · diff · raw API</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Make a deployment file with a TYPO (replica instead of replicas)</span>
kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=nginx $do &gt; d.yaml
sed -i 's/replicas: 1/replica: 1/' d.yaml

kubectl apply -f d.yaml --dry-run=client   <span class="comment"># "created (dry run)" — no validation! ⚠️</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml --dry-run=server   <span class="comment"># error: unknown field "spec.replica" ✅ caught</span>
sed -i 's/replica: 1/replicas: 1/' d.yaml  <span class="comment"># fix it</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml                    <span class="comment"># create for real</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Preview a change BEFORE applying (exit code 1 = there are diffs)</span>
sed -i 's/replicas: 1/replicas: 3/' d.yaml
kubectl diff -f d.yaml | head              <span class="comment"># shows the +/- lines the apply would make</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Peek behind the curtain — kubectl is just HTTP</span>
kubectl get deploy easypj-web -v=8 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -E "GET|Response Status" | head -4
<span class="comment">#   GET https://…:6443/apis/apps/v1/namespaces/default/deployments/easypj-web</span>
<span class="comment">#   Response Status: 200 OK</span>
kubectl delete -f d.yaml                   <span class="comment"># cleanup</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the workflow that never wastes exam minutes: <strong>generate</strong> (client dry-run) → <strong>validate</strong> (server dry-run) → <strong>preview</strong> (<code>diff</code>) → <strong>apply</strong>. And <code>-v=8</code> demystified everything — the URL you saw <em>is</em> the API structure from §1.7 (<code>/apis/apps/v1/namespaces/…/deployments</code>), which is why understanding groups/versions pays off in the terminal.</p>
