                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 6 Visual Summary — one command, four parts, total fluency</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   kubectl   VERB   RESOURCE   NAME   FLAGS
       │
       ├─ 🔎 explain &lt;res&gt;.field --recursive            ← what's the YAML? (the in-exam manual)
       ├─ ⚡ create … $do &gt; f.yaml → edit → apply -f     ← generate, don't hand-write
       ├─ 📤 -o jsonpath / custom-columns / -l / --field-selector  → &gt; file   ← extract
       └─ 🐛 logs --previous → exec → kubectl debug      ← climb the debug ladder
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   it's all REST: kubectl ─▶ apiserver (authN → authZ → admission) ─▶ etcd.  `-v=8` reveals it. ☸️
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> every kubectl command is the same four parts — <em>verb · resource · name · flags</em>. From there, four moves cover almost everything: <code>explain</code> tells you a field's YAML, <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> generates a manifest to edit and <code>apply</code>, the output flags (jsonpath / custom-columns / selectors) extract any value (and <code>&gt;</code> saves it), and the debug ladder (logs → exec → <code>kubectl debug</code>) gets you inside a broken pod.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> kubectl is a REST client — it parses your command, discovers the API path, authenticates, and renders the JSON back. Speed comes from aliases + completion + <code>$do</code>, and when anything is "forbidden" or "refused," <code>kubectl … -v=8</code> shows you the exact call to fix.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

