            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 7.2 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 7.2 — Break the Golden Rule: selector ≠ template labels</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>The single most common self-inflicted manifest bug: a Deployment whose <code>selector.matchLabels</code> doesn't match its <code>template.metadata.labels</code>. Build one on purpose, read the apiserver's exact rejection, fix it — and then explain <em>why</em> the API refuses rather than "just fixing it for you".</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Generate a valid deployment with <code>$do</code>, then edit only the template label. <code>--dry-run=server</code> shows the rejection without touching the cluster.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">break → read → fix</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Generate a correct manifest, then sabotage the template label</span>
kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; d.yaml
sed -i 's/app: easypj-web/app: easypj-web-TYPO/' d.yaml   <span class="comment"># changes BOTH? check!</span>
grep -n "app:" d.yaml
<span class="comment">#   make sure only the TEMPLATE label is changed — selector stays app: easypj-web</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Ask the apiserver — it rejects with a precise message</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml --dry-run=server
<span class="comment">#   The Deployment "easypj-web" is invalid:</span>
<span class="comment">#   spec.template.metadata.labels: Invalid value: … `selector` does not match template `labels`</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Fix — make them identical again, validate, apply</span>
sed -i 's/app: easypj-web-TYPO/app: easypj-web/' d.yaml
kubectl apply -f d.yaml --dry-run=server   <span class="comment"># created (server dry run) ✅</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the API refuses because a controller <em>defines its membership by that selector</em> — if it didn't select its own template's pods, it would create pods it instantly disowns (or adopt strangers). And since <code>.spec.selector</code> is <strong>immutable</strong>, getting it right at creation matters: a wrong selector can't be edited later, only recreated. One rule, two exam bugs prevented.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 7.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 7.3 — One File, Whole App: Multi-Document Streams</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Ship easypj as <em>one file</em>: Namespace + ConfigMap + Deployment + Service, separated by <code>---</code>, in an order that works on first apply. Then split them into a <code>k8s/</code> directory and apply the whole folder. Explain why document <em>order</em> mattered in the single file.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> kubectl submits documents <em>in file order</em> — the Namespace must exist before anything is created inside it. Directories apply in alphabetical filename order (hence the <code>00-</code>, <code>01-</code>… convention).
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-all.yaml — order matters</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace              <span class="comment"># ① FIRST — everything below lives inside it</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap              <span class="comment"># ② config before the consumer</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj
data:
  DEBUG: "False"
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment             <span class="comment"># ③ consumes the ConfigMap</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector: { matchLabels: { app: easypj } }
  template:
    metadata: { labels: { app: easypj } }
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: easypj:v1
        envFrom: [ { configMapRef: { name: easypj-config } } ]
        ports: [ { containerPort: 8000 } ]
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service                <span class="comment"># ④ wires to the pods by label</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  selector: { app: easypj }
  ports: [ { port: 80, targetPort: 8000 } ]</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">apply as one file, then as a directory</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f easypj-all.yaml        <span class="comment"># 4 objects created, in order ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># split into a directory — filenames control order</span>
mkdir k8s
<span class="comment">#   k8s/00-namespace.yaml · 01-configmap.yaml · 02-deploy.yaml · 03-svc.yaml</span>
kubectl apply -f k8s/                   <span class="comment"># applies alphabetically ✅</span>
kubectl get all -n easypj               <span class="comment"># verify the whole stack</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> a <code>---</code> file is a <em>document stream</em> — each document is an independent API request, sent in file order, so producers (Namespace, ConfigMap) precede consumers (Deployment). If the Namespace had come last, every earlier object would have failed with "namespace not found". Directory applies follow filename order — which is exactly why real repos number their manifests.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 7.4 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 7.4 — Watch <code>apply</code> Revert Your Manual Edit (the 3-Way Merge)</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Reproduce the most confusing production incident in the object-management world: apply a deployment from a file, scale it imperatively with <code>kubectl scale</code>, then re-apply the <em>unchanged</em> file — and explain precisely why your scale survived or didn't. Then repeat with a field the file <em>does</em> set, and watch the revert.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Client-side apply diffs your file against the <code>last-applied-configuration</code> annotation. Fields your file <em>never mentioned</em> are left alone; fields it <em>sets</em> get restored to the file's value.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the drift experiment</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. A file WITHOUT replicas (imperative generators omit it if 1)</span>
kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=nginx $do | grep -v "replicas" &gt; d.yaml
kubectl apply -f d.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. Scale imperatively — a SECOND writer touches the object</span>
kubectl scale deploy easypj-web --replicas=4
kubectl get deploy easypj-web            <span class="comment"># 4 replicas</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Re-apply the SAME file — replicas survive! (file never claimed that field)</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml
kubectl get deploy easypj-web            <span class="comment"># still 4 ✅ — apply leaves others' fields</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Now put replicas: 2 IN the file and re-apply — the revert happens</span>
sed -i '/^spec:/a\  replicas: 2' d.yaml
kubectl apply -f d.yaml
kubectl get deploy easypj-web            <span class="comment"># back to 2 ❗ your manual 4 was reverted</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. See the mechanism — the annotation apply diffs against</span>
kubectl get deploy easypj-web -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.kubectl\.kubernetes\.io/last-applied-configuration}' | head -c 200</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the 3-way merge (last-applied ⋄ live ⋄ new file) <em>sets what your file sets, clears what your file dropped, and leaves what others wrote</em>. So drift persists only in fields your file doesn't claim — the moment the file claims a field, apply enforces it. This is why "someone scaled it by hand and the deploy pipeline undid it" happens, and why the rule is: <strong>one management style per object</strong> (or server-side apply, where <code>managedFields</code> makes ownership explicit and conflicts loud).</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 7.5 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 7.5 — YAML Type Traps: Strings, Booleans &amp; Block Scalars</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A ConfigMap for easypj keeps failing validation. Fix three classic type traps in one manifest: (1) <code>DEBUG: False</code> and <code>PORT: 8000</code> rejected in a ConfigMap; (2) a multi-line startup script that must keep its newlines; (3) prove what unquoted <code>yes</code> becomes. State the rule that prevents all three.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> ConfigMap <code>data:</code> values must ALL be strings. <code>|</code> keeps newlines, <code>&gt;</code> folds them to spaces. Unquoted <code>true/false/yes/no/on/off</code> parse as booleans; bare numbers as ints/floats.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the fixed ConfigMap — every trap disarmed</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
data:
  DEBUG: "False"        <span class="comment"># ① quoted → string ✅ (bare False = boolean → rejected)</span>
  PORT: "8000"          <span class="comment"># ① quoted → string ✅ (bare 8000 = integer → rejected)</span>
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: easypj.example.com    <span class="comment"># plain words are already strings</span>
  startup.sh: |         <span class="comment"># ② block scalar — newlines PRESERVED</span>
    python manage.py migrate
    python manage.py collectstatic --noinput
    gunicorn easypj.wsgi:application -b 0.0.0.0:8000
  motd: &gt;               <span class="comment"># folded scalar — newlines become spaces (one long line)</span>
    Welcome to easypj —
    the friendly job board.</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prove the boolean trap ③</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">python3 -c "import yaml; print(yaml.safe_load('v: yes'))"    <span class="comment"># {'v': True}  ❗</span>
python3 -c "import yaml; print(yaml.safe_load('v: \"yes\"'))" <span class="comment"># {'v': 'yes'} ✅</span>

kubectl apply -f cm.yaml --dry-run=server     <span class="comment"># validates clean ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the one rule that prevents all three traps: <strong>when a string is required, quote anything that could parse as something else</strong> — booleans (<code>true/false/yes/no/on/off</code>), numbers (<code>8000</code>, <code>1.20</code>, <code>08000</code>), and null-ish values (<code>~</code>, <code>null</code>). ConfigMap <code>data:</code> is the place this bites hardest because <em>every</em> value there must be a string. And you now own both block scalars: <code>|</code> for scripts (keep newlines), <code>&gt;</code> for prose (fold them).</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 7 Visual Summary — the manifest skeleton, colour-coded by the 4 fields</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   apiVersion: apps/v1        ◀ ① which API group/version   (kubectl api-resources)
   kind: Deployment           ◀ ② what object
   metadata:                  ◀ ③ identity
     name: easypj-web
     namespace: easypj-dev
     labels: { app: easypj }
   spec:                      ◀ ④ desired state
     replicas: 3
     selector: { matchLabels: { app: easypj } } ─┐  MUST
     template:                                    │  match
       metadata: { labels: { app: easypj } } ◀────┘
       spec: { containers: [{ name: web, image: easypj:v1, ports: [{containerPort: 8000}] }] }
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   write it → $do generates it → --dry-run=server validates it → diff previews → apply. ☸️
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> every object is the same four-field skeleton — <code>apiVersion</code> (which API), <code>kind</code> (what), <code>metadata</code> (identity) and <code>spec</code> (desired state). YAML is whitespace-significant: spaces only, 2-per-level, colon+space. The one rule that breaks rollouts is <em>selector must equal the template labels</em>.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> don't hand-write — <strong>generate</strong> with <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code>, <strong>validate</strong> with <code>--dry-run=server</code> (it runs admission + schema, unlike client), <strong>preview</strong> with <code>diff</code>, then <strong>apply</strong>. <code>apply</code> tracks your last-applied config to add/clear/leave fields — that's declarative management.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

