            <section class="section-block" id="s8-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.2 Step 1 — Create the Namespace</h3>
                <p>Isolate the app in its own namespace — tidy, and trivial to tear down later.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">namespace + make it your default</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># stop typing -n</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Or declaratively</h5><p>A 3-line manifest works too: <code>apiVersion: v1 / kind: Namespace / metadata: {name: easypj-dev}</code>. Every object below sets <code>namespace: easypj-dev</code>. Worth adding <code>labels: {project: easypj, environment: dev}</code> on the Namespace itself too — it costs nothing and lets you <code>kubectl get ns -l environment=dev</code> across a cluster with many namespaces.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.3 Step 2 — ConfigMap (non-secret config)</h3>
                <p>Plain, non-sensitive settings go in a ConfigMap so config lives outside the image.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-config.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj-dev
data:                          <span class="comment"># every value MUST be a string (quote it)</span>
  DEBUG: "False"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*"
  DB_HOST: "easypj-db"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE: "easypj.settings"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">…or generate it imperatively</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create configmap easypj-config -n easypj-dev \
  --from-literal=DEBUG=False --from-literal=ALLOWED_HOSTS='*' \
  --from-literal=DB_HOST=easypj-db --from-literal=DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=easypj.settings</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Values are strings</h5><p>ConfigMap <code>data:</code> values must be strings — quote <code>"False"</code> so YAML doesn't coerce it to a boolean (Ch 7 §7.1).</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.4 Step 3 — Secret (sensitive config)</h3>
                <p>Credentials go in a Secret. Use <code>stringData</code> so you write plaintext and Kubernetes base64-encodes it for you.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-secret.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secret
  namespace: easypj-dev
type: Opaque                  <span class="comment"># arbitrary key/value (the default Secret type)</span>
stringData:                   <span class="comment"># plaintext IN → apiserver base64-encodes it at rest</span>
  SECRET_KEY: "change-me-in-prod"
  DB_PASSWORD: "supersecret"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">…or imperatively</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create secret generic easypj-secret -n easypj-dev \
  --from-literal=SECRET_KEY='change-me-in-prod' --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD='supersecret'</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 base64 ≠ encryption</h5><p>Secrets are only <strong>base64-encoded</strong> and sit <strong>unencrypted in etcd</strong> by default. Never commit real Secrets to Git — or better, keep manifests with real values out of version control entirely and use a <strong>sealed-secrets</strong> or <strong>external-secrets</strong> workflow (encrypt at rest in git, or pull from a real vault at deploy time) instead of raw Secret YAML in a repo. Protect what does land in etcd with <strong>encryption-at-rest</strong> + <strong>RBAC</strong> (Ch 11 / Ch 24). <code>data:</code> holds base64; <code>stringData:</code> takes plaintext and encodes it.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Hand-encoding a value with <code>data:</code> (when you're not using <code>stringData</code>)</h5><p>If a manifest uses <code>data:</code> instead of <code>stringData:</code>, YOU must base64-encode each value yourself before writing it — the apiserver does not encode <code>data:</code> values, only <code>stringData:</code> ones:</p>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">manual base64 for data:</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">echo -n 'supersecret' | base64          <span class="comment"># → c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQ=  (-n avoids a trailing newline!)</span>
echo 'c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQ=' | base64 -d      <span class="comment"># → supersecret        (decode to verify)</span></code></pre>
</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the data: form</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata: { name: easypj-secret, namespace: easypj-dev }
type: Opaque
data:                          <span class="comment"># values MUST already be base64 — no auto-encoding here</span>
  DB_PASSWORD: c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQ=</code></pre>
</div>
                <p class="mb-0">This is exactly why <code>stringData</code> is the recommended path for hand-written manifests: skip the manual encode/decode round-trip entirely, and let the apiserver do it.</p></div>
                <h4>🔑 Creating the typed Secrets (not just generic)</h4>
                <p>Beyond <code>generic</code> Secrets, two typed ones come up constantly — registry pull creds and TLS certs. Memorise these imperative forms; they're far faster than hand-encoding the JSON/PEM:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">typed secret creation</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># docker-registry: private-image pull creds → reference via imagePullSecrets</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.example.com \
  --docker-username=admin --docker-password='s3cret' \
  --docker-email=admin@example.com -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># tls: a cert + key (for Ingress HTTPS / webhooks)</span>
kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls \
  --cert=path/to/tls.crt --key=path/to/tls.key -n easypj-dev</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.5 Step 4 — The Deployment</h3>
                <p>The heart of it: pods from <code>easypj:v1</code>, configuration pulled from the ConfigMap + Secret via <code>envFrom</code>, resource bounds, and probes so traffic only flows when the app is ready.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-deploy.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-dev
  labels:
    app: easypj
    tier: web
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                       <span class="comment"># ← MUST match the template labels below</span>
      tier: web
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1                       <span class="comment"># at most 1 EXTRA pod during a rollout</span>
      maxUnavailable: 0                 <span class="comment"># never drop below desired while rolling</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj                     <span class="comment"># ← MUST match the selector above</span>
        tier: web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
          name: http
        envFrom:                        <span class="comment"># inject ALL keys as env vars…</span>
        - configMapRef:
            name: easypj-config         <span class="comment"># ← every ConfigMap key</span>
        - secretRef:
            name: easypj-secret         <span class="comment"># ← every Secret key</span>
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"                 <span class="comment"># ← scheduling floor (0.1 core)</span>
            memory: "128Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "500m"                 <span class="comment"># ← hard ceiling (0.5 core)</span>
            memory: "256Mi"
        readinessProbe:                 <span class="comment"># gates traffic — only Ready pods get requests</span>
          httpGet:
            path: /jobs/
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 10
          periodSeconds: 5
        livenessProbe:                  <span class="comment"># restarts a wedged container</span>
          httpGet:
            path: /jobs/
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 30
          periodSeconds: 10</code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>🔬 The deployment manifest — field by field</h4>
                <p>Every line above earns its place. Here's what each field does and why it's there:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it does</th><th>Get it wrong and…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>replicas: 2</code></td><td>run 2 identical pods; the ReplicaSet maintains this count, recreating any that die</td><td>too few = no resilience; omitted = defaults to 1</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>selector.matchLabels</code></td><td>the query that says "these pods are mine" — links the Deployment to its pods</td><td>≠ template labels → apiserver rejects the manifest</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>strategy: RollingUpdate</code></td><td>replace pods gradually; <code>maxSurge:1</code> adds one new pod before removing an old, <code>maxUnavailable:0</code> keeps full capacity</td><td><code>Recreate</code> instead = brief full downtime during updates</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>template.metadata.labels</code></td><td>stamped on every pod; what the selector (and the Service) matches on</td><td>≠ selector → rollout error; ≠ Service selector → 0 endpoints</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>image: easypj:v1</code></td><td>the container image; a tag change here is what triggers a rollout</td><td>wrong tag/registry → <code>ImagePullBackOff</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>envFrom</code></td><td>injects <em>every</em> key of the ConfigMap + Secret as env vars at container start</td><td>missing CM/Secret → <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>resources.requests</code></td><td>the guaranteed floor the scheduler reserves a node for</td><td>too high → pod stuck <code>Pending</code> (no node fits)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>resources.limits</code></td><td>the hard ceiling; CPU is throttled, memory over-limit is <code>OOMKilled</code></td><td>too low memory → container killed &amp; restarted in a loop</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>readinessProbe</code></td><td>"can it serve traffic <em>now</em>?" — controls EndpointSlice membership</td><td>fails → pod silently dropped from the Service (no traffic)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>livenessProbe</code></td><td>"is it still alive?" — kubelet restarts the container on failure</td><td>too aggressive → restart loop on a slow-starting app</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Two selector rules the official docs state explicitly</h5><p><strong>(1) Never let two controllers' selectors overlap</strong> — e.g. give a second Deployment or a StatefulSet the same <code>app: easypj</code> selector as this one. Kubernetes does <em>not</em> stop you from creating this; the two controllers will instead fight over the same pods, each convinced it owns them, with results that are confusing and hard to reverse. Keep every controller's selector unique (an extra label like <code>tier: web</code> is enough). <strong>(2) Never manage a Deployment's ReplicaSets directly</strong> — don't hand-edit or scale the ReplicaSet it created; go through the Deployment. It owns the ReplicaSet lifecycle, and manual changes there get overwritten or fought the next time the Deployment reconciles.</p></div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔢</div><h5>replicas + selector</h5><p>how many pods, and which pods are "mine" (by label)</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📥</div><h5>envFrom</h5><p>inject every key of the ConfigMap &amp; Secret as env vars</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚖️</div><h5>resources</h5><p>requests = scheduling floor · limits = hard ceiling (Ch 15)</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">❤️</div><h5>probes</h5><p>readiness gates traffic; liveness restarts a dead container</p></div>
                </div>
                <h4>❤️ Readiness vs liveness — same endpoint, opposite jobs</h4>
                <p>Both probes can hit <code>/jobs/</code>, yet they react in completely different ways — and confusing them is one of the most common troubleshooting traps on the exam:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>readinessProbe</th><th>livenessProbe</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Question it asks</strong></td><td>"can it serve traffic <em>right now</em>?"</td><td>"is it alive, or wedged?"</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>On failure</strong></td><td>pod is <strong>removed from the Service endpoints</strong> — traffic stops, the container keeps running</td><td>the kubelet <strong>restarts the container</strong></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Use it for</strong></td><td>warm-up, temporary overload, waiting on a dependency (DB not up yet)</td><td>deadlocks / hangs that only a restart can clear</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>easypj example</strong></td><td>app slow under load → drops out of rotation, recovers, rejoins — no restart storm</td><td>gunicorn worker deadlocks but the process stays up → restart to recover</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what each probe failure does</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   readiness FAILS ─▶ ❌ removed from EndpointSlice ─▶ Service stops routing ─▶ (container still running, may recover)
   liveness  FAILS ─▶ 🔁 kubelet kills + restarts the container ─▶ restartCount++ ─▶ back to readiness gate
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ The classic restart-loop trap</h5><p>A liveness probe that's too aggressive (tiny <code>initialDelaySeconds</code>/<code>timeoutSeconds</code>) on a slow-booting app becomes a <strong>restart loop</strong> — the app never finishes starting before liveness kills it. The fix: a <strong>startupProbe</strong> for slow boots (it suspends liveness until the app is up), generous <strong>readiness</strong> for warm-up, and a forgiving <strong>liveness</strong> reserved only for true hangs.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>⚡ Scaffold then edit</h5><p><code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 -n easypj-dev $do &gt; d.yaml</code>, then add <code>envFrom</code>, <code>resources</code>, and probes — far faster than hand-writing.</p></div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Rolling Update — Zero-Downtime Deployment</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Initial State (replicas: 2)
  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐
  │ Pod v1 ✅ │  │ Pod v1 ✅ │   Both running, serving traffic
  └──────────┘  └──────────┘

  Step 1: maxSurge=1 → Create new pod with v2
  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐
  │ Pod v1 ✅ │  │ Pod v1 ✅ │  │ Pod v2 🔄 │  New pod starting
  └──────────┘  └──────────┘  └──────────┘

  Step 2: v2 passes readinessProbe → remove one v1
  ┌──────────┐  ╔══════════╗  ┌──────────┐
  │ Pod v1 ✅ │  ║ Pod v1 ❌ ║  │ Pod v2 ✅ │  v2 ready, old v1 terminating
  └──────────┘  ╚══════════╝  └──────────┘

  Step 3: Start another v2 → remove last v1
  ╔══════════╗  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐
  ║ Pod v1 ❌ ║  │ Pod v2 ✅ │  │ Pod v2 🔄 │  Last v1 terminating
  ╚══════════╝  └──────────┘  └──────────┘

  Final State: All v2, zero downtime
  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐
  │ Pod v2 ✅ │  │ Pod v2 ✅ │   Update complete!
  └──────────┘  └──────────┘

  Key: maxSurge=1 means at most 3 pods during update (2+1)
       maxUnavailable=0 means traffic always has 2 healthy pods
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">rolling-update-commands.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Update the image (triggers rolling update)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web web=easypj:v2 -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># Watch the rollout in real time</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "easypj-web" rollout to finish: 1 of 2 updated...</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment "easypj-web" successfully rolled out</span>

<span class="comment"># View rollout history</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj-web -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         &lt;none&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Rollback to previous version (CKA important!)</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># Rollback to specific revision</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web --to-revision=1 -n easypj-dev</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.6 Step 5 — The Service</h3>
                <p>Pods come and go with new IPs; a Service gives easypj a <strong>stable VIP + DNS name</strong> and load-balances across the pods its selector matches.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-svc.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  type: ClusterIP             <span class="comment"># default — in-cluster stable VIP (Part 5 covers NodePort/LB/Ingress)</span>
  selector:                   <span class="comment"># MUST match the pod labels, or 0 endpoints</span>
    app: easypj
    tier: web
  ports:
  - name: http
    port: 80                  <span class="comment"># the Service port (what clients call)</span>
    targetPort: 8000          <span class="comment"># the container port (where gunicorn listens)</span>
    protocol: TCP</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">…or imperatively, then check endpoints</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 -n easypj-dev
kubectl get svc,endpoints easypj-web -n easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># endpoints should list pod IPs</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Service → Pod traffic flow</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   client request
        │
        ▼
   ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🔌 Service: easypj-web        │
   │    ClusterIP: 10.96.45.123    │
   │    port: 80                   │
   └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                  │ selector: app=easypj, tier=web   (matches every Ready pod)
          ┌───────┴────────┐
          ▼                ▼
   ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐
   │ 📦 Pod 1     │ │ 📦 Pod 2     │   each listening on :8000
   │   :8000      │ │   :8000      │   labels: {app: easypj, tier: web}
   └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘
   kube-proxy load-balances (round-robin / random) across the EndpointSlice
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Service Types — Visibility Layers</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  External Traffic (Internet)
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────
       │
       ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  LoadBalancer     ← Cloud provider LB     │
  │  (AWS ELB, GCP LB, Azure LB)             │
  └─────────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                        ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  NodePort           ← All nodes listen    │
  │  (Port 30000-32767 on every node)         │
  └─────────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                        ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ClusterIP          ← Internal only       │
  │  (Virtual IP, accessible within cluster)  │
  │  Service: 10.96.45.123:80                 │
  └─────────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                        ▼
              ┌─────────┴──────────┐
              ▼                    ▼
         ┌─────────┐         ┌─────────┐
         │  Pod 1   │         │  Pod 2   │
         │  :8000   │         │  :8000   │
         └─────────┘         └─────────┘

  Each type INCLUDES the ones below it:
  LoadBalancer = NodePort + ClusterIP + external LB
  NodePort = ClusterIP + exposed port on all nodes
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ 0 endpoints?</h5><p>Selector doesn't match pod labels, or no pod is Ready. Compare <code>spec.selector</code> with the pod labels, and check the readiness probe. (Types/ports in depth: Ch 4 §4.9 &amp; Part 5.)</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.7 Step 6 — Access It</h3>
                <p>For a quick local test, forward a port straight to the Service — no external exposure needed.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">port-forward → browser</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl -n easypj-dev port-forward svc/easypj-web 8000:80
<span class="comment"># now open  http://localhost:8000/jobs/   ✅</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 On Minikube specifically — skip the manual tunnel</h5><p><code>minikube service easypj-web -n easypj-dev</code> allocates a reachable URL for the Service and opens it in your default browser automatically — handy for quick local checks without babysitting a <code>port-forward</code> in a second terminal. It only works against a Minikube (or similarly Minikube-aware) cluster; on a real/remote cluster, <code>port-forward</code>, <strong>NodePort</strong>, or <strong>Ingress</strong> is what you have.</p></div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">port-forward — how it works</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   your laptop                         Kubernetes cluster
   ┌─────────────────┐                ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🌐 browser       │                │                              │
   │ localhost:8000  │──kubectl tunnel─│─▶ 🔌 Service:80 ─▶ 📦 Pod:8000│
   │                 │                │      easypj-web      gunicorn │
   └─────────────────┘                └──────────────────────────────┘
   traffic path:  browser → kubectl → apiserver → pod   (a private, session-scoped tunnel)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Reachable from</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>port-forward</code></td><td>your laptop only (kubectl tunnel)</td><td>quick local test / debug — what we did here</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ClusterIP</strong> (default)</td><td>inside the cluster only</td><td>pod-to-pod / service-to-service traffic</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>NodePort</strong></td><td><code>&lt;anyNodeIP&gt;:30000–32767</code></td><td>simple external access without a cloud LB</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td><td>a cloud-provisioned external IP</td><td>production on a cloud provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ExternalName</strong></td><td>no proxying — returns a DNS <code>CNAME</code> to an external host</td><td>alias an in-cluster name to an outside service (e.g. a managed DB)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Ingress</strong></td><td>one external IP, HTTP routing by host/path</td><td>many services behind one entrypoint (easypj graduates here, Part 5)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Port-forward is for testing only</h5><p>It tunnels through your kubectl session. Real external access uses <strong>NodePort</strong>, <strong>LoadBalancer</strong>, or <strong>Ingress</strong> (Part 5) — easypj graduates to Ingress there.</p></div>
            </section>

