  envFrom:                        <span class="comment"># ① EVERYTHING from the ConfigMap</span>
  - configMapRef:
      name: easypj-config
  env:
  - name: DJANGO_SECRET           <span class="comment"># ② ONE Secret key, RENAMED</span>
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: easypj-secret
        key: SECRET_KEY
  - name: DEBUG                   <span class="comment"># ③ explicit env OVERRIDES envFrom's DEBUG</span>
    value: "True"</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prove it inside the pod</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml -n easypj-dev
POD=$(kubectl get po -n easypj-dev -l app=easypj -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')

kubectl exec -n easypj-dev "$POD" -- printenv DEBUG          <span class="comment"># True   ← env beat envFrom ✅</span>
kubectl exec -n easypj-dev "$POD" -- printenv DJANGO_SECRET  <span class="comment"># change-me… ← renamed ✅</span>
kubectl exec -n easypj-dev "$POD" -- printenv DB_HOST        <span class="comment"># easypj-db ← bulk import ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the decision rule is now concrete — <code>envFrom</code> for "give me everything", <code>env</code>+<code>*KeyRef</code> for "just this key (maybe renamed)", and explicit <code>env</code> always wins on collision. That precedence is a real exam trap: a stray explicit entry can silently mask the ConfigMap value the task asked you to set.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 8.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 8.3 — "I Changed the ConfigMap but Nothing Happened"</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Ops edits <code>easypj-config</code> to set <code>DEBUG: "True"</code>, refreshes the app… and nothing changes. Reproduce the confusion: edit the live ConfigMap, prove the running pod still has the <em>old</em> value, explain why, and apply the correct fix. Then name the consumption mode that <em>would</em> have updated live.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>envFrom</code>/<code>env</code> are resolved by the kubelet <em>once, at container start</em>. Only fresh containers re-read the source — that's what <code>rollout restart</code> is for. (Volume-mounted ConfigMaps auto-sync; <code>subPath</code> mounts don't.)
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the frozen-env experiment</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Edit the live ConfigMap</span>
kubectl patch cm easypj-config -n easypj-dev -p '{"data":{"DEBUG":"True"}}'
kubectl get cm easypj-config -n easypj-dev -o jsonpath='{.data.DEBUG}{"\n"}'   <span class="comment"># True ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. But the RUNNING pod still has the old value — env is frozen at start</span>
POD=$(kubectl get po -n easypj-dev -l app=easypj -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl exec -n easypj-dev "$POD" -- printenv DEBUG          <span class="comment"># False ❗ (old)</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. THE FIX — restart the pods so fresh containers re-read the source</span>
kubectl rollout restart deploy/easypj-web -n easypj-dev
kubectl rollout status  deploy/easypj-web -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify on the NEW pod</span>
POD=$(kubectl get po -n easypj-dev -l app=easypj -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl exec -n easypj-dev "$POD" -- printenv DEBUG          <span class="comment"># True ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Consumption mode</th><th>Sees ConfigMap edits live?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>env</code> / <code>envFrom</code></td><td>❌ frozen at container start → needs <code>rollout restart</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>volume mount</td><td>✅ auto-syncs (within ~the kubelet sync period)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>volume mount with <code>subPath</code></td><td>❌ one-time copy, never updates</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the kubelet resolves env injection <em>once</em>, at container start — the pod's environment is a snapshot, not a subscription. So "edit the ConfigMap and restart the workload" is the standard operating pair, and volume-mounting is the choice when the app can hot-reload config files. This distinction is asked on the exam and bites in production weekly.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 8.4 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 8.4 — Double-Bug Hunt: ImagePullBackOff + Pending</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A teammate's easypj deploy is a mess: one pod is <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> (image tag <code>easypj:v1-typo</code>) and, after you fix that, the replacement pod goes <code>Pending</code> (they set <code>requests.memory: 8Gi</code> on a 4Gi node). Diagnose <em>both</em> from the Events alone, fix each, and state the general rule for which component each failure implicates.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>describe pod</code> → Events names both causes verbatim. Pull failures live on the <em>kubelet/runtime</em> arrow; <code>Pending</code> with "Insufficient memory" is the <em>scheduler</em> filtering out every node.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">two bugs, one method: read the Events</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># BUG 1 — ImagePullBackOff</span>
kubectl describe pod -n easypj-dev -l app=easypj | grep -A4 Events
<span class="comment">#   Failed to pull image "easypj:v1-typo": … not found   ← names the bad tag</span>
kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web web=easypj:v1 -n easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># fix the ref</span>

<span class="comment"># BUG 2 — the new pod sits Pending</span>
kubectl get po -n easypj-dev              <span class="comment"># STATUS Pending</span>
kubectl describe pod -n easypj-dev -l app=easypj | grep -A4 Events
<span class="comment">#   0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient memory.   ← scheduler filtered ALL nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># fix the impossible request (8Gi → 256Mi) — patch or edit the deployment</span>
kubectl patch deploy easypj-web -n easypj-dev --type=json -p \
  '[{"op":"replace","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/resources/requests/memory","value":"256Mi"}]'
kubectl get po -n easypj-dev -w           <span class="comment"># Pending → ContainerCreating → Running ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Failing component</th><th>Where the truth is</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>ImagePullBackOff</code> / <code>ErrImagePull</code></td><td>kubelet ↔ registry (pull arrow)</td><td>Events: exact registry error (not found / unauthorized)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Pending</code></td><td>scheduler (no node passed filtering)</td><td>Events: "0/N nodes available: &lt;reason per node&gt;"</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> two different failure classes, one identical method — <em>never guess, read the Events</em>. They told you the bad tag <em>and</em> the exact scheduler filter ("Insufficient memory"). Mapping symptom → component (pull = kubelet/registry, Pending = scheduler) is the Chapter 3 mental model earning its keep on a real deploy.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 8.5 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 8.5 — Trace One Request: Laptop → Service → Pod → Django</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Prove you understand the full traffic path by <em>verifying every hop</em> of one request to <code>/jobs/</code>: the Service's ClusterIP and port mapping → the EndpointSlice holding the pod IP → the pod actually listening on 8000 → the tunnel from your laptop. Produce one piece of evidence per hop, then draw the chain from memory.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> One command per hop: <code>get svc</code> (VIP + 80→8000), <code>get endpoints</code> (pod IPs), <code>exec … curl localhost:8000</code> (app up), <code>port-forward</code> + <code>curl</code> (the tunnel). If any hop fails, the previous hop tells you why.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">evidence per hop</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># HOP 1 — the Service: stable VIP, port 80 → targetPort 8000</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-web -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment">#   CLUSTER-IP 10.96.142.10   PORT(S) 80/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># HOP 2 — the endpoints: which pod IPs answer for the VIP</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-web -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment">#   ENDPOINTS 10.244.1.5:8000   ← a Ready pod is wired in ✅ (empty = selector/readiness bug)</span>

<span class="comment"># HOP 3 — the app: is Django really listening on 8000 in the pod?</span>
POD=$(kubectl get po -n easypj-dev -l app=easypj -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl exec -n easypj-dev "$POD" -- curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" localhost:8000/jobs/
<span class="comment">#   200 ✅ (the app answers inside the pod)</span>

<span class="comment"># HOP 4 — the tunnel: laptop → Service → pod</span>
kubectl -n easypj-dev port-forward svc/easypj-web 8000:80 &amp;
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" http://localhost:8000/jobs/
<span class="comment">#   200 ✅ — easypj is LIVE, end to end 🎉</span>
kill %1   <span class="comment"># stop the port-forward</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">The chain you just verified — and where each failure would point</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  💻 laptop ──port-forward──▶ 🔌 Service :80 ──selector──▶ 📇 EndpointSlice ──▶ 📦 pod :8000 ──▶ 🐍 /jobs/
      │                          │                            │                     │
      tunnel dead?               VIP/port wrong?              EMPTY? selector or    app down? logs /
      (apiserver path)           (svc spec)                   readiness bug         probe / exec curl
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> each hop has exactly one owner — the Service <em>spec</em> (ports), the <em>selector + readiness</em> (endpoints), the <em>app</em> (listening on targetPort), and the <em>tunnel</em>. When "the site is down", walking this chain hop-by-hop finds the broken link in under two minutes — which is precisely how Part 5 and Part 9 will teach you to debug Services at exam speed.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
