                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> you isolated easypj in a <strong>Namespace</strong>, put plain settings in a <strong>ConfigMap</strong> and credentials in a <strong>Secret</strong>, ran the app as a <strong>Deployment</strong> (pulling both via <code>envFrom</code>, gated by a readiness probe), fronted it with a <strong>Service</strong> (80→8000), and reached <code>/jobs/</code> through a port-forward.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> this exact order — <strong>ns → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service → verify</strong> — is the template behind most Workloads &amp; Services exam tasks. Generate with <code>$do</code>, wire labels/selectors carefully, and always confirm the Service has endpoints.</p>
