            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 10.2 — Merge Three kubeconfig Files and Prove the Precedence</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>You inherit three kubeconfig files: your default <code>~/.kube/config</code>, a teammate's <code>~/work/dev.conf</code>, and an ops-team <code>~/work/prod.conf</code> — and <code>dev.conf</code> and <code>prod.conf</code> both happen to define a cluster named <code>shared</code> with different server URLs. Merge all three, prove which <code>shared</code> definition wins, then bake the merge into one portable file.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the merge is in-memory and ordered — list the files in the order you want conflicts resolved.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">merge, prove precedence, flatten</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. merge all three — order matters, first file wins on a name clash</span>
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/work/dev.conf:~/work/prod.conf
kubectl config get-contexts                     <span class="comment"># union of every context from all 3 files</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. prove which "shared" cluster definition won</span>
kubectl config view -o jsonpath='{.clusters[?(@.name=="shared")].cluster.server}'
<span class="comment"># prints dev.conf's server URL — because dev.conf comes before prod.conf in the list</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. re-order to make prod.conf win instead</span>
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/work/prod.conf:~/work/dev.conf
kubectl config view -o jsonpath='{.clusters[?(@.name=="shared")].cluster.server}'
<span class="comment"># now prints prod.conf's server URL</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. bake the merge into one self-contained file</span>
kubectl config view --flatten > ~/.kube/merged-config
mv ~/.kube/merged-config ~/.kube/config
unset KUBECONFIG                                <span class="comment"># back to using just the default file</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the merge is <em>in-memory only</em> — reading via <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> never rewrites the source files. On a name clash the <strong>first file in the list</strong> wins, full stop, no partial merging of fields. <code>--flatten</code> is the only step that actually writes a new file, and it embeds every cert/key so the result is portable — safe to copy to another machine without broken file-path references.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 10.3 — Break the apiserver, Recover Without kubectl</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Deliberately break your own cluster: back up <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, then introduce a real typo (misspell a flag, e.g. <code>--etcd-servers</code> → <code>--etc-servers</code>) and save. Watch <code>kubectl</code> die, diagnose <em>without</em> kubectl, then recover.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> when the apiserver is down, <code>kubectl</code> can't help you diagnose it — you need the container runtime's own CLI (<code>crictl</code>) directly on the node.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">break it → diagnose without kubectl → recover</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. back up FIRST (the whole point of this drill)</span>
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/kas-good.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. break it</span>
sudo sed -i 's/--etcd-servers/--etc-servers/' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. kubectl now hangs or "connection refused" — don't fight it, go to the node</span>
kubectl get nodes                          <span class="comment"># ❌ The connection to the server ... was refused</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. diagnose WITHOUT kubectl</span>
sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver         <span class="comment"># container is repeatedly restarting / Exited</span>
sudo crictl logs $(sudo crictl ps -a --name kube-apiserver -q | head -1)
<span class="comment"># error: unknown flag: --etc-servers   ← the exact cause, in plain text</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. recover — restore the backup</span>
sudo cp /tmp/kas-good.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
sleep 20
kubectl get nodes                          <span class="comment"># ✅ working again</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> this is the single most valuable exam reflex in this chapter — <code>kubectl</code> being down does NOT mean the cluster is unrecoverable, it means you switch tools. <code>crictl</code> talks to the container runtime directly, bypassing the apiserver entirely, so it works exactly when you need it most. The backup you took in step 1 turns a potential disaster into a 20-second fix.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 10.4 — Change the kubelet's Eviction Thresholds</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A node keeps running out of disk because the kubelet's default eviction threshold is too permissive for your hardware. Tighten <code>nodefs.available</code> to <code>15%</code> in the kubelet config, apply it correctly, and prove the change took — without breaking the node.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> this is a <code>config.yaml</code> change, not a systemd unit change — which restart step does it actually need?
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">edit config.yaml → restart → verify</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. back up (habit, always)</span>
sudo cp /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml /tmp/kubelet-config.yaml.bak

<span class="comment"># 2. edit the eviction threshold</span>
sudo vim /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
<span class="comment"># evictionHard:
#   nodefs.available: "15%"      ← was 10%, now stricter</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. apply — config.yaml only needs a restart, NOT daemon-reload
#    (daemon-reload is only for systemd UNIT / drop-in changes)</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 20 --no-pager   <span class="comment"># confirm it restarted cleanly, no errors</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. verify the running kubelet actually has the new value</span>
sudo cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep -A2 evictionHard
kubectl get nodes                              <span class="comment"># still Ready — didn't break anything</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the trap this drill defuses is reaching for <code>daemon-reload</code> out of habit — it's harmless but unnecessary here, since you only touched <code>config.yaml</code>, not the systemd unit/drop-in. Save that step for when you edit <code>/etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/*.conf</code> instead. Mixing the two up is why "I changed the kubelet config and nothing happened" is such a common complaint.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 10.5 — Enforce Default Resource Limits with LimitRanger</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Developers keep forgetting to set resource requests/limits on easypj Pods. Rather than chase every manifest, make the <strong>admission chain</strong> do it for you: create a <code>LimitRange</code> in <code>easypj-dev</code> so any Pod created without limits automatically gets sane defaults, then prove it by creating a bare Pod.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> you don't need to touch the apiserver manifest for this one — <code>LimitRanger</code> is enabled by default. You just need to create the <code>LimitRange</code> object.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">LimitRange → bare pod → prove the mutation</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># limitrange.yaml</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-defaults
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container
    default:                    <span class="comment"># applied if a container has NO limits</span>
      cpu: "500m"
      memory: "256Mi"
    defaultRequest:             <span class="comment"># applied if a container has NO requests</span>
      cpu: "100m"
      memory: "128Mi"</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">apply and prove it</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f limitrange.yaml

<span class="comment"># create a Pod that names NO resources at all</span>
kubectl run bare-pod --image=nginx -n easypj-dev
kubectl get pod bare-pod -n easypj-dev -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}'
<span class="comment"># {"limits":{"cpu":"500m","memory":"256Mi"},"requests":{"cpu":"100m","memory":"128Mi"}}
# ← LimitRanger (a MUTATING admission controller for this default-injection job) stamped these on at admission time —
#   they were never in the YAML you submitted</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> this is §10.5's admission pipeline made concrete — <code>LimitRanger</code> is a <strong>mutating AND validating</strong> controller (it defaults resources here <em>and</em> can reject a request that violates the LimitRange's min/max), and its default-injection runs in the mutating wave, before your object is ever validated or persisted — exactly why <code>kubectl get pod -o yaml</code> shows resources you never wrote. It's a real-world pattern too: platform teams use LimitRanges to enforce sane defaults cluster-wide without policing every developer's YAML by hand.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

