                    <h5>④ Why etcd needs fast disks — the real numbers behind "fsync-bound"</h5>
                    <p>Every earlier section said etcd is "fsync-bound" without saying what that costs in practice. This is the single most common way a healthy-looking production etcd cluster degrades — not from a bug, but from someone putting it on the wrong storage.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   THE FSYNC CHAIN — why etcd's write latency = disk latency, almost exactly
     client write → leader appends to WAL → fsync() forces it to PHYSICAL disk
       → only THEN does the leader consider it "logged" and start replicating
     the Raft commit (the WRITE steps above) needs a MAJORITY to have fsync'd — so etcd's write
     latency is bounded below by the SLOWEST disk among the quorum members

   TYPICAL fsync LATENCY BY STORAGE TYPE (order of magnitude, not exact numbers)
     local NVMe SSD  ......... ~1-3 ms      ✅ what production etcd wants
     local SATA SSD  ......... ~3-10 ms     ⚠️ acceptable, watch it under load
     network-attached SSD .... ~10-50+ ms   ❌ classic silent-degradation cause
     spinning HDD / slow NAS .. 50-200+ ms   ❌ do not run etcd on this, ever
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   etcd's own health check: `etcdctl check perf` benchmarks exactly this — a
   "slow fsync" warning in etcd's logs means the DISK, not etcd, is the problem
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Root cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>intermittent <code>kubectl apply</code> slowness, no other explanation</td><td>disk fsync latency creeping up under load — often a "noisy neighbor" on shared network storage</td><td>move etcd to dedicated local NVMe/SSD; never share its disk with other I/O-heavy workloads</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>frequent leader elections with no network changes</td><td>slow disk makes the leader miss its heartbeat deadline to followers — they assume it died</td><td>faster disk, or tune <code>--heartbeat-interval</code>/<code>--election-timeout</code> up (only as a stopgap)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>etcdserver: request timed out</code> under moderate load</td><td>the fsync path can't keep up with the write rate — not a network or apiserver problem</td><td>faster disk is the real fix; reducing write volume (fewer/slower controllers) only delays it</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The production sizing rule of thumb:</strong> etcd wants dedicated, local, low-latency SSD/NVMe storage — never a network filesystem, never a disk shared with logging/monitoring workloads, and never spinning disk. This is also why cloud "general purpose" network-attached volumes are a common source of mysterious etcd slowness: they're fine for most workloads but etcd's fsync-per-write pattern exposes their latency tail immediately. On the exam this rarely comes up directly, but "why is my cluster slow" troubleshooting in the real world traces back here more often than any Kubernetes-layer misconfiguration.</div>
