            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 11.2 — Enable Encryption at Rest for Secrets</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 10 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Security flagged that <code>easypj-secret</code> is sitting in etcd as plaintext base64. Enable <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code>, re-encrypt every existing Secret, and <em>prove</em> — by reading the raw etcd key — that it's now ciphertext, not just trust the apiserver's word for it.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> enabling the config only encrypts <em>future</em> writes. You must force a rewrite of existing Secrets, and provider order in the config file matters.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate key → wire it in → re-encrypt → prove it</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. generate the 32-byte key</span>
head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64
<span class="comment"># M2VmMjQ4YTQ... (example — yours will differ)</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. write the config (aescbc FIRST, identity LAST — order = write priority)</span>
sudo mkdir -p /etc/kubernetes/enc
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
- resources: ["secrets"]
  providers:
  - aescbc:
      keys:
      - name: key1
        secret: M2VmMjQ4YTQ...
  - identity: {}
EOF

<span class="comment"># 3. wire it into the apiserver manifest (back up first!)</span>
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># add: - --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml
# add a hostPath volume + volumeMount for /etc/kubernetes/enc</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. re-encrypt every EXISTING Secret (enabling the flag only affects new writes)</span>
kubectl get secrets --all-namespaces -o json | kubectl replace -f -

<span class="comment"># 5. prove it — read the RAW value straight from etcd, bypassing the apiserver</span>
sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/easypj-dev/easypj-secret \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key | hexdump -C | head -3
<span class="comment"># bytes start with  6b 38 73 3a 65 6e 63 3a 61 65 73 63 62 63 3a 76 31 3a  =  "k8s:enc:aescbc:v1:" ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> if you'd skipped step 4, <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code> would still work fine (the apiserver decrypts transparently) — the bug would be invisible until you specifically inspected etcd's raw bytes, exactly as step 5 does. That's the whole point of "prove it," not "assume it": the apiserver hides both correct <em>and</em> incorrect encryption equally well.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 11.3 — Diagnose a Quorum-Loss Scenario</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>On a simulated 3-member etcd cluster, 2 members go down (a real outage, not a drill this time). <code>kubectl apply</code> hangs. Diagnose that this is a quorum problem — not a network or RBAC problem — using only <code>etcdctl</code>, and state the recovery path.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>member list</code> shows who's <em>configured</em>; <code>endpoint health</code> against each member individually shows who's actually <em>reachable</em> — the gap between the two numbers tells the story.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose quorum loss</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. symptom: writes hang, reads may still trickle through briefly</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml    <span class="comment"># hangs / times out</span>
kubectl get pods                        <span class="comment"># may still work (cached read) — misleading!</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. check each member INDIVIDUALLY, not just the local one</span>
sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list --write-out=table $CERTS
<span class="comment"># shows 3 configured members — but that's just etcd's OWN config, not who's alive</span>

sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health \
  --endpoints=https://10.0.0.10:2379,https://10.0.0.11:2379,https://10.0.0.12:2379 $CERTS
<span class="comment"># https://10.0.0.10:2379 is healthy
# https://10.0.0.11:2379 is unhealthy: context deadline exceeded   ← down
# https://10.0.0.12:2379 is unhealthy: context deadline exceeded   ← down
# → only 1 of 3 reachable. quorum needs 2. THIS IS QUORUM LOSS, not a network blip on one node.</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself — the recovery path (state it, don't necessarily run it):</strong> with only 1 of 3 members alive, you cannot simply "restart the other two" if their data is gone — you restore from the most recent verified snapshot using <code>--force-new-cluster</code> on the one surviving member to bootstrap a fresh single-member cluster, verify it's healthy, then <code>member add</code> the others back as fresh members. This is exactly why "always take a fresh snapshot before touching a struggling cluster" (this chapter's Speed Tips) exists — you may need it seconds later.</p>
