            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 11.4 — Deploy the Nightly etcd Backup CronJob</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Manual snapshots are fine for the exam, but a real cluster needs backups automated. Deploy a CronJob that snapshots etcd nightly at 02:00 and writes timestamped files to a host directory — then manually trigger it once to prove it works today, without waiting until 2 AM.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the backup Job must land on the control-plane node (etcd's certs and <code>127.0.0.1:2379</code> only exist there) — that means a nodeSelector, a matching toleration, and <code>hostNetwork: true</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">etcd-backup-cronjob.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata: { name: etcd-backup, namespace: kube-system }
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"                 <span class="comment"># 02:00 daily</span>
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          hostNetwork: true             <span class="comment"># reach 127.0.0.1:2379 — etcd only listens on the host net</span>
          nodeSelector: { node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: "" }
          tolerations:
          - operator: Exists            <span class="comment"># control-plane nodes are usually tainted NoSchedule</span>
          containers:
          - name: backup
            image: registry.k8s.io/etcd:3.5.12-0
            command: ["/bin/sh", "-c",
              "ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-$(date +%F-%H%M).db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key"]
            volumeMounts:
            - { name: certs, mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd, readOnly: true }
            - { name: backup, mountPath: /backup }
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          volumes:
          - { name: certs, hostPath: { path: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd } }
          - { name: backup, hostPath: { path: /var/backups/etcd, type: DirectoryOrCreate } }</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">deploy + trigger a run right now</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f etcd-backup-cronjob.yaml

<span class="comment"># don't wait until 2 AM — manually spin up one Job from the CronJob's template</span>
kubectl create job --from=cronjob/etcd-backup etcd-backup-test -n kube-system
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l job-name=etcd-backup-test -w   <span class="comment"># watch it Complete</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system job/etcd-backup-test
ls -la /var/backups/etcd/                          <span class="comment"># on the control-plane node — a fresh .db file ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> every piece here answers a "why is my backup Job not running" real-world failure — no <code>nodeSelector</code> means it might land on a worker with no etcd certs; no toleration means it never schedules at all (control-plane taints); no <code>hostNetwork</code> means <code>127.0.0.1:2379</code> resolves to the Pod's own network namespace, not the host's. <code>kubectl create job --from=cronjob/…</code> is the fast way to test a CronJob without editing the schedule or waiting.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 11.5 — Full etcd Health Sweep Before a Maintenance Window</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 5 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>You're about to do risky maintenance (an apiserver flag change, a node reboot). Before touching anything, run a complete etcd health sweep and explicitly state what "healthy" looks like for each check — the pre-flight discipline that turns "maintenance" into "safe maintenance."</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> four checks, each catching a different failure mode: is it reachable, who's the leader, how full is it, and — the one people forget — is there a recent verified backup.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the 4-check pre-flight sweep</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">alias e='sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key'

<span class="comment"># 1. Is it reachable at all?</span>
e endpoint health                     <span class="comment"># "is healthy: successfully committed proposal" ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Who's the leader, and is DB size sane?</span>
e endpoint status --write-out=table   <span class="comment"># exactly ONE member IS LEADER=true; DB SIZE well under ~2GB quota</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Are all members present and started?</span>
e member list --write-out=table       <span class="comment"># every row should show "started", none "unstarted"</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Do I have a FRESH, VERIFIED backup, right now, before I touch anything?</span>
e snapshot save /opt/pre-maintenance-$(date +%F-%H%M).db
e snapshot status /opt/pre-maintenance-*.db --write-table   <span class="comment"># non-zero TOTAL KEYS confirms it's real</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Check fails?</th><th>It means</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>① health times out</td><td>network/cert problem to that endpoint — stop, don't proceed with maintenance yet</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>② no member is leader</td><td>an election is in progress or quorum is shaky — wait and re-check</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>③ a member shows unstarted</td><td>that member never joined correctly — investigate before adding load</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>④ snapshot has 0 keys</td><td>you do NOT have a real backup — do not proceed until this is fixed</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> this sweep is the etcd equivalent of Ch 5's cluster pre-flight check — cheap insurance that turns "the apiserver edit broke something and I have no fallback" into "I have a snapshot from 90 seconds ago, restore and retry." Skipping step 4 specifically is the single most expensive shortcut an admin can take.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

