            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 12.2 — Diagnose a Dead apiserver Without kubectl</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A teammate breaks the apiserver manifest (wrong image tag, e.g. <code>kube-apiserver:v1.34.9999</code> — an image that doesn't exist) and doesn't tell you which file. <code>kubectl</code> is completely dead. Find the cause and fix it using only tools that work when the apiserver is down.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> follow the decision tree from §12.2 top to bottom — kubelet status, then <code>crictl ps -a</code>, then <code>crictl logs</code> on whatever's not Running.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bottom-up diagnosis, no kubectl</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. confirm kubectl really is dead, then stop trying to use it</span>
kubectl get nodes                          <span class="comment"># ❌ The connection to the server ... was refused</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. is the kubelet itself even alive? (it's the one thing NOT a static pod)</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet              <span class="comment"># active (running) → kubelet is fine, problem is a static pod</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. which container is unhealthy?</span>
sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver   Exited (1)   12s ago   ← repeatedly restarting</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. WHY did it exit?</span>
sudo crictl logs $(sudo crictl ps -a --name kube-apiserver -q | head -1)
<span class="comment"># Failed to pull image "registry.k8s.io/kube-apiserver:v1.34.9999": not found</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. now you know the exact cause — fix the manifest</span>
sudo grep image /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
sudo sed -i 's/v1.34.9999/v1.34.0/' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># 6. wait, then confirm kubectl is back</span>
sleep 30
kubectl get nodes                          <span class="comment"># ✅ working again</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the sequence matters — jumping straight to "guess and fix" without step 4's <code>crictl logs</code> means you're debugging blind. This drill is deliberately structured around the §12.2 decision tree because that's the exact reflex the exam rewards: don't panic that kubectl is dead, just switch to the tool that still works.</p>
