                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 12.3 — Hand-Author a Static Pod on a Worker, Then Remove It</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Prove to yourself that static pods are file-driven, not API-driven: create one on a worker node, watch its mirror pod appear, try to delete it the "normal" way (and watch that fail), then remove it correctly.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>kubectl delete pod</code> on a static pod's mirror is a trap — it looks like it worked, then the pod reappears seconds later.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create → prove kubectl delete fails → remove correctly</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. on the WORKER node, drop a manifest into its staticPodPath</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/kubernetes/manifests/easypj-static.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata: { name: easypj-static }
spec:
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: nginx:1.25
EOF

<span class="comment"># 2. from the control plane — the mirror pod appears within seconds</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep easypj-static
<span class="comment"># default   easypj-static-worker-1   1/1   Running   ← name suffixed with the node</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. try the "obvious" way to remove it — watch it come right back</span>
kubectl delete pod easypj-static-worker-1
kubectl get pods -A | grep easypj-static     <span class="comment"># ← IT'S BACK. kubectl delete was a no-op long-term.</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. remove it CORRECTLY — delete the file, on the node that owns it</span>
sudo rm /etc/kubernetes/manifests/easypj-static.yaml
kubectl get pods -A | grep easypj-static     <span class="comment"># now gone for good ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> step 3 is the whole point of this drill — the pod reappearing after a "successful" <code>kubectl delete</code> is exactly the confusing behavior static pods produce in the real world, and now you've seen why: the mirror pod is read-only, the <em>file</em> is the source of truth, and the kubelet recreates whatever the file describes. This same logic is why you fix a broken control-plane component by editing its manifest, never by deleting its pod.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 12.4 — PriorityClass Preemption in Action</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Fill a small node with low-priority "batch" pods until it's full, then schedule one high-priority <code>easypj-critical</code> pod that needs the same resources. Watch the scheduler evict a batch pod to make room, and explain why in terms of §12.4's PriorityClass mechanics.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> preemption only fires when the pod genuinely can't be scheduled otherwise — pack the node full enough that there's truly no room left.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">priority-classes.yaml + fill + preempt</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata: { name: batch-low }
value: 1000
description: "preemptible background jobs"
---
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata: { name: easypj-critical }
value: 1000000
preemptionPolicy: PreemptLowerPriority
description: "must-run web tier"</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">fill the node, then force a preemption</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f priority-classes.yaml

<span class="comment"># fill available capacity with low-priority pods requesting most of a node's resources
# (adjust replica count / requests to your test node's actual capacity)</span>
kubectl create deployment batch-filler --image=nginx --replicas=4
kubectl patch deployment batch-filler --type=json -p='[
  {"op":"add","path":"/spec/template/spec/priorityClassName","value":"batch-low"},
  {"op":"add","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/resources","value":{"requests":{"cpu":"800m"}}}]'
kubectl get pods -o wide                      <span class="comment"># confirm the node is now full</span>

<span class="comment"># now request a high-priority pod that needs room that doesn't exist</span>
kubectl run easypj-critical --image=nginx --overrides='{"spec":{"priorityClassName":"easypj-critical"}}' \
  --requests='cpu=800m'

<span class="comment"># watch the events — a batch-filler pod gets EVICTED to make room</span>
kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | grep -i preempt
<span class="comment"># Preempted   Preempting Pod batch-filler-xxxx on node worker-1 to accommodate easypj-critical</span>
kubectl get pods                              <span class="comment"># easypj-critical is now Running; one batch-filler pod is gone</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> preemption is a <em>last resort</em>, not the scheduler's first move — it only evicts when a higher-priority pod genuinely cannot fit any other way. This is why the drill needs the node actually full first; on a mostly-empty test cluster, <code>easypj-critical</code> just schedules normally and you'll never see preemption fire. The evicted pod isn't deleted forever — its Deployment's controller immediately recreates it, which then waits Pending for room, exactly like any other capacity-constrained workload.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 12.5 — Read a Leader-Election Lease to Find the Active Scheduler</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 4 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>On an HA control plane (or a simulated one), a colleague asks "which control-plane node is actually running the scheduler right now?" Answer using only the Lease object — no SSH-and-guess.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> leader-elected components write their identity into a <code>Lease</code> object in <code>kube-system</code>, named after the component.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">read the Lease, decode the leader</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># list every leader-elected component's Lease</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get leases
<span class="comment"># NAME                      HOLDER                       AGE
# kube-controller-manager   controlplane-2_a1b2c3         14d
# kube-scheduler            controlplane-1_ghi789         14d   ← this line answers the question</span>

<span class="comment"># the full picture — holder identity + how fresh the heartbeat is</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get lease kube-scheduler -o yaml
<span class="comment"># spec:
#   holderIdentity: controlplane-1_ghi789    ← "controlplane-1" IS the active node
#   leaseDurationSeconds: 15
#   renewTime: "2026-07-02T21:45:03Z"         ← must be within the last ~15s, or the leader is stuck</span>

<span class="comment"># sanity check: is that renewTime actually recent?</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get lease kube-scheduler -o jsonpath='{.spec.renewTime}'</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the node name is embedded in <code>holderIdentity</code> (before the underscore) — no SSH round-trip needed. The extra value here is the <code>renewTime</code> check: if it's stale (much older than 15s) despite the Lease still naming a holder, that node's scheduler process is wedged but hasn't released the lease yet — a real "why isn't anything scheduling" root cause that a simple "is the pod Running" check would miss entirely.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

