            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"If I <code>kubectl delete</code> a pod that belongs to a Deployment, it's gone."</td>
                            <td>It's gone for about a second. The ReplicaSet controller's reconcile loop notices desired (N) no longer equals actual (N-1) and immediately creates a replacement — you can't really fire a managed employee this way, the manager just hires again instantly. This is exactly why <code>kubectl delete pod</code> is a safe, common way to force a restart.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"A DaemonSet's one-per-node pods bypass the scheduler — there's nothing to decide, it just runs everywhere."</td>
                            <td>Since Kubernetes v1.17, DaemonSet pods go through the <em>normal</em> scheduler like any other pod — the DaemonSet controller just injects a per-node <code>nodeAffinity</code> term naming that exact node, so the scheduler's usual taint/toleration and resource checks still apply. A DaemonSet pod can still fail to schedule if the node is tainted against it or too full.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"A Pod stuck in <code>Pending</code> will schedule eventually — just give it time."</td>
                            <td>If its requested CPU/memory doesn't fit on any current node, it will sit Pending <strong>forever</strong> — the scheduler doesn't retry-and-wait-for-capacity on its own. Something has to change: a node is added, another pod is evicted or scaled down, or you lower the request. Chapter 14 and 15 both drill this exact failure mode because it's one of the most common real Pending-pod causes on the exam.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
