                                                                          ↑ NEW       ↑ NEW
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1c">c) Rolling Updates</h4>
                <p>Rolling updates replace pods <strong>one at a time</strong>, ensuring your application stays available throughout the upgrade. This is the default strategy and is <strong>heavily tested on the CKA</strong>.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Triggering a Rolling Update</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Update image — triggers rolling update</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj django=easypj:v2

<span class="comment"># Watch rollout progress (CKA exam: verify your work!)</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "easypj" rollout to finish:</span>
<span class="comment">#   1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment">#   2 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment">#   deployment "easypj" successfully rolled out</span>

<span class="comment"># Check rollout history</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         &lt;none&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Add change cause for audit trail</span>
kubectl annotate deployment/easypj \
  kubernetes.io/change-cause="Update to v2 — new job search feature"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Rolling Update: easypj v1 → v2 (Zero Downtime)</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  maxSurge: 1       (1 extra pod allowed during update)
  maxUnavailable: 0 (never fewer than 3 running)

  Time 0:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [📦v1]              ← All running v1
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 1:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [📦v1] [🆕v2]       ← v2 pod starting (surge=1)
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 2:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [🗑️  ] [✅v2]       ← v2 ready → old v1 terminating
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 3:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [✅v2] [🆕v2]       ← Next v2 pod starting
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 4:  [📦v1] [🗑️  ] [✅v2] [✅v2]       ← Second v1 terminating
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 5:  [📦v1] [✅v2] [✅v2] [🆕v2]       ← Last v2 starting
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 6:  [✅v2] [✅v2] [✅v2]              ← All v2 ✅ Zero downtime! 🎉

  Key insight: At NO point were there fewer than 3 healthy pods serving traffic!
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1d">d) Rollbacks</h4>
                <p>If v2 is broken, you can instantly rollback. Kubernetes keeps a revision history of ReplicaSets.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam Alert: Rollback Commands</h5>
                    <p>Rollback questions are extremely common. Know these cold:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME</code> — go back one revision</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME --to-revision=N</code> — go to specific revision</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl rollout history deployment/NAME</code> — see all revisions</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Rollback Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Undo last update (go back to previous revision)</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Go to a specific revision</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj --to-revision=2

<span class="comment"># Check what happened</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj --revision=2

<span class="comment"># Verify rollback succeeded</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].image}'
<span class="comment"># easypj:v1 easypj:v1 easypj:v1  ← back to v1!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Rollback Flow: v2 Broken → Instant Recovery to v1</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Deployment: easypj
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                          │
  │  Revision 1 (v1) ─── Revision 2 (v2) ─── Revision 3    │
  │  ┌──────────┐        ┌──────────┐        ┌──────────┐   │
  │  │ReplicaSet│        │ReplicaSet│        │ReplicaSet│   │
  │  │ (scaled  │  ──→   │ (scaled  │  ──→   │ = rev 1  │   │
  │  │  to 0)   │        │  to 0)   │  undo  │ (active) │   │
  │  └──────────┘        └──────────┘        └──────────┘   │
  │                        ↑ 💀 broken          ↑ ✅ fixed  │
  │                                                          │
  │  kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj                  │
  │  → K8s scales old RS back up, scales broken RS down     │
  │  → Pods running v1 again within seconds! 🎉             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Create</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create deployment</code> or <code>kubectl apply -f</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Verify</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get deploy,rs,pods</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Scale</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl scale deploy --replicas=N</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Update</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl set image deploy/NAME</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Monitor</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl rollout status</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">6</div>
                        <h5>Rollback</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl rollout undo</code> if needed</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1e">e) Pause &amp; Resume — Batch Multiple Changes</h4>
                <p>Sometimes you need to make multiple changes (image + resources + env vars) in one rollout instead of triggering separate updates for each. Pause the deployment, make all changes, then resume to trigger a single rollout.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Pause &amp; Resume Workflow</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pause — no changes will trigger a rollout</span>
kubectl rollout pause deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Make multiple changes (none trigger rollout yet)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj django=easypj:v3
kubectl set resources deployment/easypj -c django \
  --requests=cpu=500m,memory=512Mi
kubectl set env deployment/easypj DEBUG=false

<span class="comment"># Resume — all changes deploy in ONE rollout</span>
kubectl rollout resume deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Verify single rollout applied all changes</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pause/Resume vs Normal — Rollout Count</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  WITHOUT Pause/Resume:              WITH Pause/Resume:

  Change image → Rollout #1  🔄      kubectl rollout pause
  Change CPU   → Rollout #2  🔄      Change image  ┐
  Change env   → Rollout #3  🔄      Change CPU    ├─ no rollouts
                                      Change env    ┘
  3 separate rollouts!               kubectl rollout resume
  3 ReplicaSets created!              → Rollout #1 only  🔄
  ⚠️ Wasteful &amp; slower               ✅ Single clean rollout!
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1f">f) Rollout Status &amp; Conditions — What <code>kubectl rollout status</code> Is Actually Reading</h4>
                <p><code>kubectl rollout status</code> doesn't poll pods directly — it watches three fields on the Deployment's <code>.status</code>: <code>updatedReplicas</code>, <code>availableReplicas</code>, and a set of <strong>conditions</strong>. Understanding these fields turns "the rollout looks stuck" from a guess into a precise diagnosis.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Reading the raw status</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The full status block kubectl rollout status is built from</span>
kubectl get deployment easypj -o yaml | grep -A20 "^status:"
<span class="comment"># status:
#   observedGeneration: 4          # which spec version the controller has actually seen
#   replicas: 4                    # total pods that exist right now (old + new)
#   updatedReplicas: 3             # pods running the NEW pod template
#   readyReplicas: 4               # pods passing their readiness probe
#   availableReplicas: 3           # ready AND past minReadySeconds
#   conditions:
#   - type: Available
#     status: "True"
#     reason: MinimumReplicasAvailable
#   - type: Progressing
#     status: "True"
#     reason: ReplicaSetUpdated</span>

<span class="comment"># The one-liner exam-fast version of the same read:</span>
kubectl get deployment easypj -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions[*].type}={.status.conditions[*].reason}'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Condition <code>type</code></th><th>What "True" means</th><th>What "False" means (and the reason)</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Progressing</strong></td><td>the controller is actively creating/scaling/updating pods, OR the rollout finished (reason <code>NewReplicaSetAvailable</code>)</td><td><code>ProgressDeadlineExceeded</code> — no progress within <code>progressDeadlineSeconds</code> (default 600s). This is the official definition of a "stuck" rollout.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Available</strong></td><td>at least <code>minAvailable</code> pods (derived from <code>maxUnavailable</code>) have been ready for <code>minReadySeconds</code></td><td><code>MinimumReplicasUnavailable</code> — not enough healthy pods right now, independent of whether an update is in progress</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ReplicaFailure</strong></td><td>a ReplicaSet failed to create a pod (e.g. quota exceeded, admission webhook denied) — normally absent</td><td>(absent = no failure to report)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 <code>observedGeneration</code> vs <code>metadata.generation</code> — the "did my edit even register?" check</h5>
                    <p>Every <code>spec</code> change bumps <code>metadata.generation</code> (an integer). The controller copies that number into <code>status.observedGeneration</code> once it has processed the change. If <code>generation</code> is 5 but <code>observedGeneration</code> is still 4, the controller <strong>hasn't reacted yet</strong> — worth knowing before you conclude a rollout is stuck when really the controller just hasn't picked it up. <code>kubectl rollout status</code> checks this exact gap internally before it starts reading the replica counts.</p>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1g">g) <code>kubectl rollout restart</code> — Restart Every Pod Without Changing the Spec</h4>
                <p>Sometimes you need every pod recreated even though nothing in the Deployment's spec actually changed — a new base-image was pushed under the same tag, a mounted Secret/ConfigMap rotated (pods don't auto-pick-up a changed Secret value), or you just suspect memory-leaked pods and want a clean slate. Editing the image tag would work but is a hack; <code>kubectl rollout restart</code> is the real command for this.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Force a rolling restart</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Restart every pod, rolling — same maxSurge/maxUnavailable rules apply</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Under the hood: this patches a timestamp annotation on the pod TEMPLATE</span>
kubectl get deployment easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.metadata.annotations}'
<span class="comment"># {"kubectl.kubernetes.io/restartedAt":"2026-07-03T09:15:00Z"}</span>

<span class="comment"># Because the POD TEMPLATE changed (an annotation is part of it), this creates a</span>
<span class="comment"># genuinely NEW ReplicaSet — it is a real rollout, not a "delete all pods" trick,</span>
<span class="comment"># so rollout status / rollout undo work on it exactly like any other update.</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Why not <code>kubectl delete pods -l app=easypj</code> instead?</h5>
                    <p>Deleting pods directly is NOT a rolling restart — the ReplicaSet controller notices missing pods and replaces them, but with no ordering, no <code>maxUnavailable</code> pacing, and no new revision in <code>rollout history</code>. If several pods die close together you can dip well below your desired capacity. <code>rollout restart</code> is the only command that gives you a paced, tracked, undo-able restart.</p>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1h">h) Deleting a Deployment — Cascade Behavior You Must Choose Correctly</h4>
                <p>Deleting a Deployment doesn't just remove one object — by default it deletes the ReplicaSet(s) and every Pod they own too. Kubernetes calls this <strong>cascading deletion</strong>, and the propagation policy you choose changes what survives.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Propagation policy</th><th>Command</th><th>What happens to the ReplicaSet + Pods</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Foreground</strong> (safe default for scripts)</td><td><code>kubectl delete deploy easypj --cascade=foreground</code></td><td>the Deployment stays visible (in a "deleting" state) until ALL dependents are gone first — <code>kubectl delete</code> blocks until the whole tree is actually deleted</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Background</strong> (kubectl's actual default)</td><td><code>kubectl delete deploy easypj</code></td><td>the Deployment is deleted immediately; the garbage collector deletes the ReplicaSet + Pods asynchronously moments later — the command returns before cleanup finishes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Orphan</strong></td><td><code>kubectl delete deploy easypj --cascade=orphan</code></td><td>only the Deployment is deleted — the ReplicaSet and its Pods are <strong>left running</strong>, now unowned</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 When would you ever want <code>--cascade=orphan</code>?</h5>
                    <p>The classic case: you're migrating a workload to a differently-named Deployment (or a Helm release rename) and want zero pod churn during the switch. Orphan-delete the old Deployment, then create the new one with a selector that matches the SAME pod labels — the new Deployment adopts the still-running pods instead of the traffic dropping to zero while replacements start. See §13.2's adoption mechanics below for exactly how that "adoption" match works.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.2 ReplicaSets — Under the Hood</h3>
                <p>A ReplicaSet ensures a specified number of pod replicas are running at any time. You <strong>rarely create ReplicaSets directly</strong> — Deployments create and manage them for you. But understanding them is essential for troubleshooting.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why You Need to Know This</h5>
                    <p>When <code>kubectl get pods</code> shows a pod stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>, you need to trace: <strong>Pod → ReplicaSet → Deployment</strong> to find the root cause. The CKA tests this troubleshooting chain.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod Chain</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  DEPLOYMENT: easypj                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ You manage this layer                                       │ │
  │  │ "I want 3 pods running easypj:v1"                          │ │
  │  └──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                             │ creates &amp; manages                 │
  │                             ▼                                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  REPLICASET: easypj-7d9f8b6c5d                           │   │
  │  │  Kubernetes manages this layer automatically              │   │
  │  │  selector: matchLabels: { app: easypj }                  │   │
  │  │  replicas: 3                                              │   │
  │  └───────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────┬────────────┘   │
  │          │ owns             │ owns             │ owns           │
  │          ▼                  ▼                  ▼                │
  │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐          │
  │  │ POD          │  │ POD          │  │ POD          │          │
  │  │ easypj-..abc │  │ easypj-..def │  │ easypj-..ghi │          │
  │  │ 📦 easypj:v1│  │ 📦 easypj:v1│  │ 📦 easypj:v1│          │
  │  │ labels:      │  │ labels:      │  │ labels:      │          │
  │  │  app: easypj │  │  app: easypj │  │  app: easypj │          │
  │  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘          │
  │                                                                  │
  │  Delete a pod? → ReplicaSet auto-creates a replacement! 🔄     │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">replicaset-example.yaml (for understanding — rarely created directly)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: ReplicaSet
metadata:
  name: easypj-replicaset
  labels:
    app: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:                 <span class="comment"># Which pods this RS owns</span>
      app: easypj
    <span class="comment"># Advanced: matchExpressions</span>
    <span class="comment"># matchExpressions:</span>
    <span class="comment">#   - key: app</span>
    <span class="comment">#     operator: In           # In, NotIn, Exists, DoesNotExist</span>
    <span class="comment">#     values: [easypj, easypj-staging]</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div>
                        <h5>matchLabels</h5>
                        <p>Simple equality: <code>app: easypj</code>. All labels must match. Most common approach.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div>
                        <h5>matchExpressions</h5>
                        <p>Operators: <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>DoesNotExist</code>. For complex multi-value selectors.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Inspecting the Chain</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># See all three layers at once</span>
kubectl get deploy,rs,pods -l app=easypj

<span class="comment"># NAME                     READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj   3/3     3            3           5m</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                                DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># replicaset.apps/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d  3         3         3       5m</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-abc12  1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-def34  1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-ghi56  1/1     Running   0          5m</span>

<span class="comment"># See owner references (pod → RS → Deployment)</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-abc12 -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].name}'
<span class="comment"># easypj-7d9f8b6c5d  ← owned by the ReplicaSet</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Never Delete ReplicaSets Directly</h5>
                    <p>If you delete a ReplicaSet that belongs to a Deployment, the Deployment controller will immediately recreate it. To scale down, always modify the <strong>Deployment</strong>. Delete the Deployment to clean up all RS and Pods together.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 revisionHistoryLimit — Control Revision History</h5>
                    <p>By default, Kubernetes keeps <strong>10 old ReplicaSets</strong> (revision history) for rollback. Each old RS consumes etcd storage. You can control this with <code>spec.revisionHistoryLimit</code>:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>revisionHistoryLimit: 5</code> — keep last 5 revisions (good for production)</li>
                        <li><code>revisionHistoryLimit: 0</code> — keep none (⚠️ no rollback possible!)</li>
                        <li><code>revisionHistoryLimit: 10</code> — default</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Ownership, Adoption &amp; Orphaning — How a ReplicaSet Decides Which Pods Are "Its"</h4>
                <p>The diagram above shows a ReplicaSet "owning" pods, but the real mechanism is a <strong>label selector match plus an <code>ownerReferences</code> entry</strong> — not a fixed list the RS remembers. This distinction has real consequences the exam tests directly.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">The full ownerReference, not just the name</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pod easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-abc12 -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0]}' | python3 -m json.tool
<span class="comment"># {
#   "apiVersion": "apps/v1",
#   "kind": "ReplicaSet",
#   "name": "easypj-7d9f8b6c5d",
#   "uid": "3f2a1b9c-...",
#   "controller": true,          ← this RS is the CONTROLLING owner (only one allowed)
#   "blockOwnerDeletion": true   ← foreground-delete of the RS waits for this pod first
# }</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div>
                        <h5>Adoption</h5>
                        <p>A bare pod with NO owner, whose labels match a ReplicaSet's selector, is <strong>adopted</strong> — the RS stamps its own <code>ownerReferences</code> onto it and counts it toward <code>replicas</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔓</div>
                        <h5>Release</h5>
                        <p>Change a running pod's labels so they no longer match the RS selector, and the RS <strong>releases</strong> it (removes the ownerReference) — then immediately creates a fresh replacement pod, since it's now short one.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👻</div>
                        <h5>Orphaning</h5>
                        <p>Delete the owning ReplicaSet with <code>--cascade=orphan</code> and its pods lose their <code>ownerReferences</code> entirely — they keep running, unmanaged, until something else adopts or deletes them.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The gotcha this creates: two Deployments can accidentally fight over the same pods</h5>
                    <p>If Deployment B's ReplicaSet selector happens to match pods that Deployment A's ReplicaSet already owns, the two controllers can race — since a pod can only have one <em>controlling</em> owner (<code>controller: true</code>), one RS's create gets rejected while the other's scale-down fights it. This is exactly why a Deployment's <code>spec.selector</code> is immutable after creation — mutable, overlapping selectors would make this adoption/release machinery dangerously unpredictable in production.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 An adopted bare pod can get killed the instant it's adopted</h5>
                    <p>Adoption isn't always a safe no-op: if a label-matching bare pod is adopted and that pushes the ReplicaSet's count OVER its desired <code>replicas</code>, the RS controller may terminate that pod right away to get back to the correct count. Never assume hand-creating a pod with matching labels "joins the fleet safely" — it might be adopted and immediately scheduled for deletion instead.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Garbage collection — what actually deletes an orphaned pod's owner-less state</h5>
                    <p>The mechanism above (adoption/release/orphaning) is Kubernetes' general-purpose <strong>garbage collector</strong>, and it isn't specific to ReplicaSets — the same <code>ownerReferences</code>/<code>controller</code>/<code>blockOwnerDeletion</code> fields cascade Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod, Job → Pod, and (Ch16) StatefulSet → Pod, CronJob → Job. Any object can declare an owner reference to any other object, which is how <code>kubectl delete deploy</code> knows to clean up two layers down without the Deployment controller ever touching a Pod directly.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.3 Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)</h3>
                <p>The HPA <strong>automatically scales</strong> your Deployment based on observed CPU, memory, or custom metrics. When traffic spikes, HPA adds pods. When traffic drops, it removes them. This is <strong>critical for production</strong> and tested on the CKA.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Prerequisite: Metrics Server</h5>
                    <p>HPA requires the <strong>Metrics Server</strong> to be installed in the cluster. Without it, HPA has no data to make scaling decisions. Verify: <code>kubectl top nodes</code> — if this works, Metrics Server is running.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">HPA — Imperative (CKA Exam Speed)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create HPA bound to deployment</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment easypj --min=3 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70

<span class="comment"># Check HPA status</span>
kubectl get hpa
<span class="comment"># NAME     REFERENCE           TARGETS   MINPODS   MAXPODS   REPLICAS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj  Deployment/easypj   45%/70%   3         10        3          2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Detailed view</span>
kubectl describe hpa easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-hpa.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: easypj-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj
  minReplicas: 3
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 70    <span class="comment"># Scale when avg CPU > 70%</span>
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 80    <span class="comment"># Also watch memory</span>
  behavior:
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 30   <span class="comment"># React quickly to spikes</span>
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300  <span class="comment"># Wait 5 min before scaling down</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">HPA Scaling Flow — Automatic Response to Load</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  HPA: Horizontal Pod Autoscaler for easypj                      │
  │                                                                  │
  │  📊 Metrics Server reports: CPU = 85%  (above target of 70%)   │
  │       │                                                          │
  │       ▼                                                          │
  │  🤖 HPA evaluates every 15 seconds:                             │
  │     desiredReplicas = ceil(currentReplicas × (85% / 70%))       │
  │     desiredReplicas = ceil(3 × 1.21) = 4                        │
  │       │                                                          │
  │       ▼                                                          │
  │  📈 HPA updates: deployment.spec.replicas = 4                   │
  │       │                                                          │
  │       ▼                                                          │
  │  📦📦📦📦  4 pods running → CPU drops to 60%                    │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ... traffic keeps climbing ...                                  │
  │                                                                  │
  │  📊 CPU = 90%  →  HPA: replicas = 6                             │
  │  📦📦📦📦📦📦  6 pods running → CPU stabilizes at 55%           │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ... traffic decreases (night time) ...                          │
  │                                                                  │
  │  📊 CPU = 25%  →  HPA waits 5 min (stabilization window)       │
  │               →  then scales down to minReplicas = 3            │
  │  📦📦📦  3 pods — cost efficient! 💰                             │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⬆️</div>
                        <h5>Scale Up (Fast)</h5>
                        <p>Default stabilization: <strong>0 seconds</strong>. HPA reacts immediately to load spikes to protect users.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⬇️</div>
                        <h5>Scale Down (Slow)</h5>
                        <p>Default stabilization: <strong>300 seconds</strong>. Avoids flapping — waits 5 min to confirm load is truly gone.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Tip: HPA Requires Resource Requests</h5>
                    <p>HPA can only measure utilization as a percentage if <code>resources.requests</code> is set on the container. Without requests, HPA cannot calculate CPU percentage and will report <code>&lt;unknown&gt;/70%</code>. Always set resource requests on Deployments that use HPA.</p>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Multiple metrics configured (e.g. CPU AND memory)</td><td>HPA computes a desired-replica count PER metric independently, then acts on whichever metric yields the MOST replicas — the safest choice, never the average or the first one checked</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>10% default tolerance</td><td>HPA skips scaling entirely if the desired/current replica ratio is within 10% of 1.0 — this is exactly why CPU sitting at 71% against a 70% target often does NOT trigger a scale-up; it's inside the tolerance band, not a bug</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>autoscaling/v1 vs v2 — Why Every YAML Example Here Uses v2</h4>
                <p>You'll see <code>autoscaling/v1</code> in a lot of older tutorials and blog posts — it's worth knowing exactly what it can't do, so you recognize a v1 manifest as legacy the moment you see one.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Capability</th><th><code>autoscaling/v1</code></th><th><code>autoscaling/v2</code></th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Metric type</td><td>CPU utilization ONLY, and only as <code>targetCPUUtilizationPercentage</code> (a top-level int, not a <code>metrics</code> list)</td><td>CPU, memory, custom, and external metrics — any number of them combined</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Scaling behavior tuning</td><td>none — hard-coded internal stabilization defaults you cannot change</td><td>full <code>behavior.scaleUp</code>/<code>scaleDown</code> block (§ below)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl autoscale</code> output</td><td>generates v1 by default on old clusters; v2 on modern ones</td><td>always what <code>kubectl get hpa -o yaml</code> shows on a current cluster — v1 objects are transparently served as v2 under the hood since v2 became stable</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>Because v2 is a strict superset — every v1 HPA is representable in v2 — there is no reason to hand-write v1 YAML today. If you inherit one, the fields translate directly: <code>targetCPUUtilizationPercentage: 70</code> becomes a single <code>metrics</code> entry of <code>type: Resource, resource: {name: cpu, target: {type: Utilization, averageUtilization: 70}}</code>.</p>

                <h4>Custom &amp; External Metrics — Scaling on Something Other Than CPU/Memory</h4>
                <p>Resource metrics (CPU/memory) answer "is the container itself under load?" — but often the real signal is business-level: requests-per-second, queue depth, or active WebSocket connections. HPA v2 supports this via two additional metric types, sourced from a <strong>custom metrics adapter</strong> (e.g. Prometheus Adapter) rather than the built-in metrics-server.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-hpa-custom-metrics.yaml — scale on requests-per-second AND a queue</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: easypj-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj
  minReplicas: 3
  maxReplicas: 20
  metrics:
  - type: Pods                        <span class="comment"># "Pods" metric = averaged across all easypj pods</span>
    pods:
      metric:
        name: http_requests_per_second
      target:
        type: AverageValue
        averageValue: "500"           <span class="comment"># scale so each pod handles ~500 req/s</span>
  - type: External                    <span class="comment"># "External" metric = NOT tied to any pod (e.g. a queue depth)</span>
    external:
      metric:
        name: sqs_queue_depth
        selector:
          matchLabels:
            queue: easypj-jobs
      target:
        type: AverageValue
        averageValue: "30"            <span class="comment"># scale so each pod handles ~30 queued jobs</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Metric <code>type</code></th><th>Source</th><th>Meaning of the number</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Resource</strong></td><td>metrics-server</td><td>CPU/memory utilization or raw value, per-container</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Pods</strong></td><td>custom metrics API</td><td>a metric exposed BY the pods themselves, averaged across all of them (e.g. requests-per-second per pod)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Object</strong></td><td>custom metrics API</td><td>a metric describing ANOTHER Kubernetes object, e.g. an Ingress's requests-per-second</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>External</strong></td><td>external metrics API</td><td>a metric from OUTSIDE the cluster entirely — a cloud queue depth, a managed load balancer's connection count</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Custom/External metrics need an adapter — they don't work out of the box</h5>
                    <p>Unlike CPU/memory (metrics-server), <code>Pods</code>/<code>Object</code>/<code>External</code> metrics require a separate <strong>metrics adapter</strong> deployed in the cluster (commonly the Prometheus Adapter) that implements the <code>custom.metrics.k8s.io</code> or <code>external.metrics.k8s.io</code> API. Without it, the HPA reports the metric as unavailable, identical in symptom to the missing-resource-requests gotcha — always check <code>kubectl get apiservices | grep metrics</code> to confirm the right adapter is actually registered.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The <code>behavior</code> Block in Full — Tuning Scale Speed Beyond Just a Stabilization Window</h4>
                <p>The YAML earlier in this section used <code>stabilizationWindowSeconds</code> alone, but the full <code>behavior</code> block supports rate-limiting <em>policies</em> too — capping how many pods can be added/removed per unit time, not just how long to wait before deciding.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Full behavior block — policies, not just stabilization</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">  behavior:
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 0     <span class="comment"># react immediately (0 = no smoothing delay)</span>
      policies:
      - type: Percent
        value: 100                      <span class="comment"># can DOUBLE the pod count per period</span>
        periodSeconds: 60
      - type: Pods
        value: 4                        <span class="comment"># OR add at most 4 pods per period</span>
        periodSeconds: 60
      selectPolicy: Max                 <span class="comment"># use whichever policy allows MORE change (faster scale-up)</span>
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300   <span class="comment"># wait 5min of sustained low load before acting</span>
      policies:
      - type: Percent
        value: 10                       <span class="comment"># remove at most 10% of pods per period</span>
        periodSeconds: 60
      selectPolicy: Min                 <span class="comment"># use whichever policy allows LESS change (gentler scale-down)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Field</th><th>Controls</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>stabilizationWindowSeconds</code></td><td>how long to look BACK before acting — HPA picks the highest recommended replica count seen in that window, smoothing out a brief spike/dip before actually resizing</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>policies[].type: Pods</code> / <code>Percent</code></td><td>an absolute count vs a percentage cap on how much the replica count may change within <code>periodSeconds</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>selectPolicy: Max</code> / <code>Min</code> / <code>Disabled</code></td><td>when multiple policies are listed, which one wins — <code>Max</code> for aggressive scale-up, <code>Min</code> for cautious scale-down, <code>Disabled</code> to turn that direction off entirely (e.g. <code>scaleDown: {selectPolicy: Disabled}</code> makes an HPA scale-up-only)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>HPA (this section)</h5>
                        <p>Adds/removes <strong>POD replicas</strong> based on load. Edits the <code>/scale</code> subresource (UTH §②).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📏</div>
                        <h5>VPA (Vertical Pod Autoscaler)</h5>
                        <p>Right-sizes a pod's CPU/memory <strong>requests</strong> — not the replica count. Not built-in; a separate project.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🖥️</div>
                        <h5>Cluster Autoscaler</h5>
                        <p>Adds/removes <strong>NODES</strong> when pods can't be scheduled or nodes sit idle (Ch 36.6) — the layer above HPA.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Never run HPA and VPA on the SAME metric for the SAME workload</h5>
                    <p>VPA changing a container's <code>resources.requests</code> changes the denominator HPA uses to compute utilization percentage out from under it — the two fight, causing oscillation. If you need both horizontal and vertical scaling, target VPA at a different metric than HPA's, or use VPA in "recommendation only" mode (<code>updateMode: "Off"</code>) purely to size initial requests, letting HPA own live scaling.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.4 Deployment Strategies</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes supports two built-in deployment strategies. Choosing the right one depends on your application's ability to handle multiple versions running simultaneously.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Strategy</th>
                                <th>How It Works</th>
                                <th>Downtime</th>
                                <th>When to Use</th>
                                <th>easypj Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>RollingUpdate</strong></td>
                                <td>Replace pods gradually, one (or few) at a time</td>
                                <td>❌ None</td>
                                <td>Default — use for most updates</td>
                                <td>New Django feature release</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Recreate</strong></td>
                                <td>Kill ALL old pods first, then start ALL new pods</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes — brief outage</td>
                                <td>Breaking changes, DB schema migrations, incompatible API versions</td>
                                <td>Major Django version upgrade with DB migration</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">RollingUpdate vs Recreate — Visual Comparison</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ROLLINGUPDATE STRATEGY (default)          RECREATE STRATEGY
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  T0: [v1] [v1] [v1]                       [v1] [v1] [v1]
       │                                         │
  T1: [v1] [v1] [v1] [v2] ← surge              [💀] [💀] [💀] ← all killed
       │                                         │
  T2: [v1] [v1] [v2]      ← replace 1           [  ] [  ] [  ] ← downtime! ⚠️
       │                                         │
  T3: [v1] [v2] [v2]      ← replace 2           [v2] [v2] [v2] ← all new
       │
  T4: [v2] [v2] [v2]      ← complete ✅

  Availability:                             Availability:
  ██████████████████ 100%                   ████░░░░████ ~70%
  Never less than minAvailable              Brief outage window

  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>RollingUpdate — Tuning Parameters</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">strategy-examples.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Conservative — zero downtime, slow rollout</span>
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: 1          <span class="comment"># Only 1 extra pod at a time</span>
    maxUnavailable: 0    <span class="comment"># Never fewer than desired replicas</span>

<span class="comment"># Aggressive — faster rollout, allows brief capacity dip</span>
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: "50%"      <span class="comment"># Up to 50% extra pods</span>
    maxUnavailable: "25%" <span class="comment"># Allow 25% to be unavailable</span>

<span class="comment"># Recreate — kill all, then create all</span>
strategy:
  type: Recreate         <span class="comment"># No rollingUpdate params needed</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ <code>maxSurge</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code> can never BOTH be 0</h5>
                    <p>The API rejects that combination outright — with no surge allowed AND no unavailability allowed, the rollout would have no way to ever make progress (it couldn't add a new pod OR remove an old one). At least one of the two must permit some movement; every valid example above (including <code>maxSurge:1/maxUnavailable:0</code> and <code>maxSurge:0/maxUnavailable:1</code> from this chapter's practice drills) always leaves exactly one of them non-zero.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="stat-bar-group">
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">RollingUpdate — Safety</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★★★★</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width: 100%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">RollingUpdate — Speed</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★★☆☆</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width: 60%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Recreate — Safety</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★☆☆☆</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width: 40%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Recreate — Speed</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★★★★</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width: 100%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Exam Strategy</h5>
                    <p>On the exam, the default RollingUpdate is almost always what you want. Only use <code>Recreate</code> if the question explicitly says "all pods must be running the same version" or mentions database migrations that can't handle mixed versions.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Beyond Built-in: Blue-Green &amp; Canary Deployments</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes only has <strong>RollingUpdate</strong> and <strong>Recreate</strong> built in. Two other common strategies require manual setup or tools like Argo Rollouts / Istio:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Blue-Green</strong> — Run two full Deployments (blue=current, green=new). Switch the Service selector from blue to green once verified. Instant rollback by switching back.</li>
                        <li><strong>Canary</strong> — Deploy new version alongside old with fewer replicas (e.g., 1 of 10). Route a small percentage of traffic to canary. If healthy, gradually increase. Popular with service meshes.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Blue-Green vs Canary — Not Built-in but Important to Know</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  BLUE-GREEN:                           CANARY:

  ┌─────── Service ──────┐              ┌─────── Service ──────┐
  │  selector: ver=blue  │              │  selector: app=web   │
  └──────────┬───────────┘              └──────────┬───────────┘
             │                                     │
    ┌────────┴────────┐                   ┌────────┴────────┐
    ▼                 ▼                   ▼                 ▼
  Blue Deploy      Green Deploy        v1 Deploy (9)    v2 Deploy (1)
  (current ✅)     (new, testing)      (90% traffic)    (10% canary)
  [v1][v1][v1]     [v2][v2][v2]        [v1]×9           [v2]×1

  Switch: change   Promote: scale      Promote: scale v2 up,
  selector to      green, delete       scale v1 down gradually
  ver=green        blue
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>A Real "Poor Man's Canary" — Two Deployments, One Service, No Extra Tooling</h4>
                <p>You don't need a service mesh to run a basic canary — a second Deployment whose pods share the SAME labels the primary Service selects on is enough. The traffic split is purely a function of replica-count ratio, because a Service load-balances evenly across every matching Endpoint regardless of which Deployment created it.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-canary.yaml — a 2nd Deployment sharing the primary's Service selector</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-canary
spec:
  replicas: 1                    <span class="comment"># 1 canary pod</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                <span class="comment"># SAME "app" label as the primary Deployment</span>
      track: canary               <span class="comment"># but a distinct "track" label to keep the two RS's apart</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj                <span class="comment"># ← this is what the Service actually selects on</span>
        track: canary
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v2            <span class="comment"># the NEW version under test</span>
---
<span class="comment"># The existing easypj Service is untouched — its selector is just app: easypj,</span>
<span class="comment"># so it happily load-balances across BOTH Deployments' pods:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  selector:
    app: easypj                     <span class="comment"># matches primary (9 pods) AND canary (1 pod) → ~10% traffic to v2</span>
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Promoting or rolling back a poor-man's canary</h5>
                    <p><strong>Promote</strong> (canary is healthy): <code>kubectl set image deploy/easypj django=easypj:v2</code> on the PRIMARY Deployment (a normal rolling update), then <code>kubectl delete deploy easypj-canary</code>. <strong>Roll back</strong> (canary is broken): just <code>kubectl delete deploy easypj-canary</code> — the primary was never touched, so there's nothing to undo. This asymmetry (rollback = delete one small Deployment, no rolling-update math involved) is exactly why canarying catches bad releases cheaper than rolling the change out to 100% first.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Argo Rollouts — What a Real Canary/Blue-Green Controller Adds</h4>
                <p>The poor-man's canary above has a real limitation: YOU decide when to promote by watching metrics manually, and the traffic split is coarse (whole-pod-count ratios only — 1-of-10 is 10%, you can't do 3%). Progressive-delivery controllers like <strong>Argo Rollouts</strong> replace the Deployment object with a <code>Rollout</code> CRD that automates exactly this, with fine-grained traffic percentages and automatic analysis.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Argo Rollouts — automated canary steps (conceptual, beyond the CKA syllabus)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Rollout                        <span class="comment"># NOT "Deployment" — a drop-in CRD replacement</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 10
  strategy:
    canary:
      steps:
      - setWeight: 10                <span class="comment"># send exactly 10% of traffic to the new version</span>
      - pause: {duration: 5m}        <span class="comment"># hold here, run automated analysis (error rate, latency)</span>
      - setWeight: 50
      - pause: {duration: 5m}
      - setWeight: 100               <span class="comment"># fully promoted — old version scaled to 0</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Not on the CKA syllabus — why it's worth knowing anyway</h5>
                    <p>Argo Rollouts, Flagger, and similar tools are NOT part of the CKA exam — they're third-party CRDs, not core Kubernetes. But real production teams running canary deployments at any real scale almost always use one of these instead of hand-managing a second Deployment, because they add automated metric-based promotion/rollback (via a Prometheus query, for example) instead of a human watching a dashboard. Recognizing a <code>Rollout</code> object in someone else's cluster and knowing it "is a Deployment, but smarter about traffic shifting" will save you real confusion on the job.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Which Strategy for Which Failure Mode — a Decision Guide</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Situation</th><th>Right strategy</th><th>Why</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Routine feature release, stateless app</td><td>RollingUpdate (default)</td><td>zero downtime, cheap to configure, safe to leave as the default for 95% of workloads</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Breaking DB schema migration (old code can't read new schema)</td><td>Recreate</td><td>old and new pods literally cannot coexist safely — RollingUpdate would run both against the same DB simultaneously and corrupt data</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>High-risk release you want to verify under REAL traffic before full rollout</td><td>Canary (poor-man's or Argo Rollouts)</td><td>limits the blast radius to a small percentage of users if something's wrong, with cheap/instant rollback</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Need an instant, whole-fleet cutover AND instant whole-fleet rollback (e.g. a major version where you want zero mixed-version risk but also zero downtime)</td><td>Blue-Green</td><td>both versions are fully up simultaneously; the "switch" is just a Service selector change, sub-second either direction</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ <code>progressDeadlineSeconds</code> barely means anything under <code>Recreate</code></h5>
                    <p>§13.1f covered <code>progressDeadlineSeconds</code> and the <code>Progressing</code> condition in terms of a RollingUpdate's incremental pod-by-pod progress. Under <code>Recreate</code>, there is no incremental progress to measure — it's just "old pods gone" then "new pods starting" — so the deadline mostly just bounds how long the brief downtime window is allowed to last before the whole rollout is marked <code>ProgressDeadlineExceeded</code>. Don't rely on it to catch a bad Recreate rollout early the way it catches a stuck RollingUpdate.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.5 Health Probes &amp; Self-Healing Primitives</h3>
                <p>A Deployment keeps N pods <em>running</em> — but "running" isn't the same as "working." A Django process can be alive yet deadlocked, or alive yet still running migrations and unable to serve traffic. <strong>Probes</strong> are how the kubelet decides whether a container is actually healthy, and they are one of the most heavily tested "robust deployment" topics on the CKA.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Terms — the three probe types</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>livenessProbe</strong> — "is it ALIVE?" Fails → kubelet <strong>RESTARTS</strong> the container. Use to recover from deadlocks or hung processes.</li>
                        <li><strong>readinessProbe</strong> — "is it READY for traffic?" Fails → pod is removed from the Service's <strong>Endpoints</strong> (no traffic) but is <em>NOT</em> restarted.</li>
                        <li><strong>startupProbe</strong> — "has it FINISHED booting?" Protects slow-starting apps; while it runs, liveness &amp; readiness are suspended. Once it passes once, it never runs again.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <p>All three probe types share the same four handlers: <code>httpGet</code>, <code>tcpSocket</code>, <code>exec</code> (a command), and <code>grpc</code>. And the same tuning knobs: <code>initialDelaySeconds</code>, <code>periodSeconds</code>, <code>timeoutSeconds</code>, <code>failureThreshold</code>, <code>successThreshold</code>.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj container — all three probes</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
      - containerPort: 8000
    startupProbe:                 <span class="comment"># protects a slow boot; gates the other two</span>
      httpGet:
        path: /healthz
        port: 8000
      failureThreshold: 30
      periodSeconds: 5            <span class="comment"># up to 150s to start before liveness applies</span>
    readinessProbe:                <span class="comment"># gate traffic (endpoints) — no restart</span>
      httpGet:
        path: /ready
        port: 8000
      initialDelaySeconds: 5
      periodSeconds: 10
    livenessProbe:                 <span class="comment"># restart if truly dead</span>
      httpGet:
        path: /healthz
        port: 8000
      initialDelaySeconds: 15
      periodSeconds: 20
      failureThreshold: 3</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Which Probe, When — Decision Card</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Use which probe</th><th>Effect on failure</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>App hangs / deadlocks</td><td><code>livenessProbe</code></td><td>Container <strong>RESTARTED</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Warming up / DB not ready yet</td><td><code>readinessProbe</code></td><td>Removed from Endpoints; <strong>NO</strong> traffic, <strong>NO</strong> restart</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Slow boot (migrations running)</td><td><code>startupProbe</code></td><td>Suspends liveness/readiness until boot completes</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Self-Healing Loop — probe result → kubelet action</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
                    probe runs (HTTP / TCP / Exec / gRPC)
                              │
                  ┌───────────┴────────────┐
                  │                        │
           liveness FAILS           readiness FAILS
                  │                        │
                  ▼                        ▼
        kubelet RESTARTS the      pod dropped from Service
        container (new PID,      Endpoints — 503s to clients,
        same pod)                 pod is NOT restarted
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔁</div>
                        <h5>ReplicaSet</h5>
                        <p>Pod or node lost → recreate to keep the replica count.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">❤️</div>
                        <h5>Probes</h5>
                        <p>Liveness fails → restart. Readiness fails → drop from endpoints (no traffic, no restart).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>PodDisruptionBudget</h5>
                        <p>Drain/upgrade → keeps <code>minAvailable</code> up during voluntary disruption.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Robust Self-Healing Trio (an official CKA competency)</h5>
                    <p><strong>ReplicaSet</strong> (replace lost pods) + <strong>Probes</strong> (detect sick pods) + <strong>PodDisruptionBudget</strong> (survive voluntary disruption) — the exam expects you to know all three work together, not that any one of them alone makes a Deployment "robust."</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 A too-aggressive liveness probe causes restart storms</h5>
                    <p>If <code>initialDelaySeconds</code> is too short or <code>failureThreshold</code> too low, the kubelet kills pods that are merely <em>slow</em>, not broken — triggering a restart loop that never lets the app finish starting. <strong>readiness ≠ liveness</strong>: a failing readiness probe empties the Service's endpoints (clients get no new connections) <em>without</em> restarting the pod. When in doubt on the exam, prefer readiness over an aggressive liveness setting, and always pair a slow-booting container with a <code>startupProbe</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Four Handlers in Practice — <code>exec</code> and <code>grpc</code>, Not Just <code>httpGet</code></h4>
                <p>Every example so far used <code>httpGet</code>, but two of the other three handlers show up constantly outside simple web apps: <code>exec</code> for anything you can check with a shell command (databases, message queues, legacy apps with no health endpoint), and <code>grpc</code> for gRPC services, which speak a binary protocol an HTTP probe can't parse.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">exec probe — for a component with no HTTP endpoint (e.g. a Redis sidecar)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">livenessProbe:
  exec:
    command:
    - redis-cli
    - ping
  initialDelaySeconds: 5
  periodSeconds: 10
<span class="comment"># The kubelet runs this command INSIDE the container's namespace.</span>
<span class="comment"># Exit code 0  → probe SUCCEEDS.  Any other exit code  → probe FAILS.</span>
<span class="comment"># No network call, no port — works for anything with a CLI health check.</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">grpc probe — for a gRPC-speaking easypj microservice</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">readinessProbe:
  grpc:
    port: 9090
    service: easypj.HealthCheck    <span class="comment"># optional: a specific gRPC health-check service name</span>
  periodSeconds: 10
<span class="comment"># Requires the app to implement the standard gRPC Health Checking Protocol</span>
<span class="comment"># (grpc.health.v1.Health) — the kubelet speaks that protocol directly, no sidecar needed.</span>
<span class="comment"># Before this handler existed (added in v1.24, stable v1.27), teams had to run a</span>
<span class="comment"># separate exec-based grpc_health_probe binary as a workaround — you may still see that</span>
<span class="comment"># pattern in older manifests; it's no longer necessary on a modern cluster.</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Handler</th><th>Best for</th><th>Success condition</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>httpGet</code></td><td>web apps, REST APIs</td><td>HTTP status 200–399</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>tcpSocket</code></td><td>anything where "the port accepts a connection" is a good-enough signal (a raw TCP service, or as a cheap smoke test before a real check exists)</td><td>TCP handshake succeeds</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>exec</code></td><td>databases, CLIs, legacy apps, anything scriptable</td><td>command exits 0</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>grpc</code></td><td>gRPC microservices</td><td>the gRPC Health Checking Protocol returns <code>SERVING</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Probe Timing Math — Working Out Real Detection Time From the Tuning Knobs</h4>
                <p>"Set a liveness probe" isn't enough — the five tuning fields interact to produce a concrete number of seconds before Kubernetes actually acts on a failure. Getting this arithmetic right (or wrong) is the difference between a probe that catches real problems and one that's either a hair-trigger or useless.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Worked example — time to first restart after a container truly hangs</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  livenessProbe:
    httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8000}
    initialDelaySeconds: 15    ← probe doesn't even START until 15s after container start
    periodSeconds: 20          ← then checks every 20s
    timeoutSeconds: 3          ← each check waits at most 3s for a response (default 1s)
    failureThreshold: 3        ← must fail 3 CONSECUTIVE times before kubelet acts

  worst-case detection time = initialDelaySeconds + (periodSeconds × failureThreshold)
                             = 15 + (20 × 3) = 75 seconds
                             (the app could hang up to 75s before a restart is triggered)

  successThreshold (default 1 for liveness/startup; can raise for readiness) =
    consecutive SUCCESSES needed to flip back to healthy — for a flapping app,
    a successThreshold > 1 stops a single lucky probe from re-adding it to
    Service Endpoints prematurely
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>Tuning direction</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>initialDelaySeconds</code></td><td>0</td><td>set to roughly your app's typical (not worst-case) startup time — a <code>startupProbe</code> is the correct tool for a widely-varying boot time, not a huge <code>initialDelaySeconds</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>periodSeconds</code></td><td>10</td><td>lower = faster detection but more load on the endpoint being probed; raise for expensive health checks (e.g. one that queries a DB)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>timeoutSeconds</code></td><td>1</td><td>too low on a loaded app causes false failures (the app was fine, just slow to respond under load) — a frequent cause of mysterious restart storms</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>failureThreshold</code></td><td>3</td><td>higher = more tolerant of transient blips, slower to actually react to a real failure — the core safety/speed trade-off of the whole probe system</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>successThreshold</code></td><td>1 (must be 1 for liveness/startup; readiness can go higher)</td><td>raise for a readiness probe on a flappy dependency to require sustained health before re-admitting traffic</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> still applies to a liveness-triggered restart</h5>
                    <p>When a liveness probe fails enough times, the kubelet doesn't <code>SIGKILL</code> the container instantly — it sends <code>SIGTERM</code> and waits up to <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> (default 30s, container-spec level, distinct from any probe field) before force-killing. For a container that's genuinely deadlocked and won't respond to SIGTERM either, this means the ACTUAL restart can take up to 30s longer than the probe-math above suggests — worth knowing when a stuck pod seems to take unexpectedly long to come back after "the liveness probe should have caught it already."</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 A probe can override the pod-level grace period with its OWN, shorter one</h5>
                    <p>Both <code>livenessProbe</code> and <code>startupProbe</code> accept their own <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> field, which — when set — overrides the pod-level default JUST for a failure of that specific probe. This is the actual knob for "make a hung container die faster than my normal 30s grace period" without shortening the grace period for every other kind of termination (a normal rolling update, a manual delete) too.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">livenessProbe:
  httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8000}
  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 5   <span class="comment"># overrides the pod-level 30s, ONLY for a liveness failure</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Rolling Updates Actually Work: ReplicaSet Lifecycle, maxSurge/maxUnavailable Math, and Revision History</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Rolling Update — Step-by-Step with ReplicaSet Math</h5>
            <p>When you update a Deployment's image, Kubernetes doesn't modify the existing ReplicaSet. It creates a NEW ReplicaSet and orchestrates a gradual handover. Understanding the math behind maxSurge and maxUnavailable lets you tune updates for speed vs safety.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ROLLING UPDATE — Complete ReplicaSet Lifecycle                               │
│                                                                              │
│  INITIAL STATE: Deployment easypj-web, replicas=3, image=v2.0                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old):                                                         │    │
│  │   desired=3, ready=3                                                │    │
│  │   Pods: [web-abc, web-def, web-ghi] (all v2.0)                     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web easypj=easypj:v2.1                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  STEP 1: Deployment creates NEW ReplicaSet with updated pod template         │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (new):                                                         │    │
│  │   desired=0, ready=0  <span class="comment">← Starts at 0                                 </span>│    │
│  │   Pods: []                                                           │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): unchanged (still 3/3)                                  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: Scale up RS-v2 by maxSurge (default 25% = 1 pod)                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (new): desired=1, ready=0→1 (pod starting...)                  │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): desired=3, ready=3                                     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  TOTAL PODS: 4 (3 old + 1 new)  <span class="comment">← maxSurge allows extra pod         </span>│    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: New pod is Ready → Scale down RS-v1 by 1                            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (new): desired=1, ready=1                                     │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): desired=2, ready=2  <span class="comment">← One pod terminated               </span>│    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  TOTAL PODS: 3 (2 old + 1 new)  <span class="comment">← Back to desired count             </span>│    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  Repeat STEP 2→3 until RS-v1=0, RS-v2=3 (full cutover):                     │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  RS-v2: 1→2→3 (scaling up)                                           │    │
│  │  RS-v1: 2→1→0 (scaling down)                                         │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  FINAL STATE:                                                                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (current): desired=3, ready=3                                  │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): desired=0, ready=0  <span class="comment">← Kept for rollback!               </span>│    │
│  │   Pods: all terminated                                               │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  maxSurge & maxUnavailable MATH:                                             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ maxSurge:  EXTRA pods allowed ABOVE desired count                   │    │
│  │ maxUnavailable: pods allowed to be UNAVAILABLE during update        │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ Example: replicas=10, maxSurge=25%, maxUnavailable=25%               │    │
│  │   maxSurge pods:     ceil(10 × 0.25) = 3 extra pods allowed         │    │
│  │   maxUnavailable pods: floor(10 × 0.25) = 2 pods can be down        │    │
│  │   → During update: 10-13 pods running, at least 8 pods available    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ ZERO-DOWNTIME CONFIG (safest, slowest):                             │    │
│  │   maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0                                    │    │
│  │   → 1 new pod created, wait for Ready, then 1 old pod removed       │    │
│  │   → NEVER below desired replicas, 1 extra at a time                 │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ FAST UPDATE CONFIG (fastest, riskiest):                              │    │
│  │   maxSurge: 50%, maxUnavailable: 50%                                 │    │
│  │   → Half the pods replaced at once                                   │    │
│  │   → Application must handle 50% traffic loss gracefully             │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  REVISION HISTORY (stored in ReplicaSets):                                   │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Each image update creates a NEW ReplicaSet. Old ones are kept for    │    │
│  │ rollback (default: last 10 revisions).                                │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ $ kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web                          │    │
│  │ REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE                                                │    │
│  │ 1         Initial deploy v2.0                                         │    │
│  │ 2         Update to v2.1                                              │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ $ kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web                              │    │
│  │ → Scales RS-v1 back to 3, scales RS-v2 to 0 (instant rollback!)     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, a stuck rollout nearly always means the new pods can't become Ready. Check: <code>kubectl rollout status deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code> (shows progress), <code>kubectl get rs</code> (see which RS is scaling), <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;new-pod&gt;</code> (find the failure). Common causes: wrong image tag, CrashLoopBackOff, insufficient cluster resources, or readiness probe failing. You can PAUSE a rollout: <code>kubectl rollout pause deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code> and RESUME: <code>kubectl rollout resume deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code>.
            </div>

            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Layer that "does" the rollout</th><th>What it actually does</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Deployment controller</td><td>computes, every reconcile, how many NEW-RS pods and OLD-RS pods <em>should</em> exist right now given maxSurge/maxUnavailable — it never touches a Pod directly</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>ReplicaSet controller(s)</td><td>each RS independently drives its own pod count toward whatever the Deployment controller last set on its <code>spec.replicas</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Scheduler + kubelet</td><td>place and run the individual pods each RS creates — completely unaware a "rollout" is even happening</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> a "rolling update" is not one component executing a plan — it's the Deployment controller repeatedly nudging two ReplicaSets' <code>replicas</code> fields a little closer to the target split, on every reconcile, until one reaches the full count and the other reaches zero. The five subsections below go under the five pieces of machinery that make that nudging safe, resumable, and collision-free.</p>

            <h5>① Pod-Template-Hash — How a ReplicaSet Gets Its Name and Why Two Rollouts Never Collide</h5>
            <p>Every ReplicaSet name you've seen (<code>easypj-7d9f8b6c5d</code>) has a suffix that looks random but isn't — it's a deterministic hash of the pod template, computed by the Deployment controller and stamped onto the ReplicaSet as both part of its name AND a label (<code>pod-template-hash</code>). This one mechanism is what lets a Deployment "find" the correct ReplicaSet for the current spec without keeping a separate lookup table.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web django=easypj:v2.1
        │
        ▼
   Deployment controller computes: hash(spec.template) = 7d9f8b6c5d
        │
        ├─ does a ReplicaSet with label pod-template-hash=7d9f8b6c5d already exist?
        │     NO  → create ReplicaSet "easypj-web-7d9f8b6c5d"
        │            with selector matchLabels: {app: easypj-web, pod-template-hash: 7d9f8b6c5d}
        │            AND inject that same label into ITS pod template
        │            (so every pod it creates carries the hash label too)
        │
        │     YES (you re-applied the SAME template you had before, e.g. an undo) →
        │            re-use that existing ReplicaSet instead of creating a new one
        ▼
   Pods inherit the label chain: app=easypj-web + pod-template-hash=7d9f8b6c5d
   → this is EXACTLY what makes "kubectl get pods -l pod-template-hash=7d9f8b6c5d"
     show you only the pods from ONE specific revision, old or new
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>pod-template-hash</code> label</td><td>added to the ReplicaSet AND every pod it creates — the exact mechanism the Deployment uses to tell "which revision is this pod from" apart</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>spec.template</code> change</td><td>ANY change (image, env var, label, resources) recomputes the hash → a genuinely different string → a NEW ReplicaSet, never an edit to the old one</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>status.collisionCount</code></td><td>on the rare occasion the computed hash already exists for a DIFFERENT template (a hash collision), the controller increments this counter and rehashes — invisible in normal operation, but it's why the field exists in the API at all</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is why <code>rollout undo</code> is instant.</strong> "Undo" doesn't rebuild anything — it recomputes the hash of the OLD template, finds the ReplicaSet still sitting there (kept per <code>revisionHistoryLimit</code>) with that exact <code>pod-template-hash</code>, and scales it back up. There is no template reconstruction step, which is exactly why rollback is so much faster than a forward rollout of a brand-new image.</div>

            <h5>② The <code>/scale</code> Subresource — Why HPA Never Needs to Know What a Deployment Is</h5>
            <p>§13.3 shows the HPA "editing the Deployment's replicas." That's a simplification — HPA never reads or writes a Deployment's full spec at all. It talks to a tiny, generic API called the <strong><code>/scale</code> subresource</strong>, which every scalable workload type (Deployment, ReplicaSet, StatefulSet, and even the legacy ReplicationController) exposes identically.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   GET /apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments/easypj-web/scale
   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ {                                                            │
   │   "kind": "Scale",                                          │
   │   "spec":   { "replicas": 3 },        ← the ONLY writable field│
   │   "status": { "replicas": 3,                                │
   │               "selector": "app=easypj-web" }                │
   │ }                                                            │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   HPA's control loop, every ~15s:
     1. GET  .../scale               → read status.replicas (current count)
     2. compute desiredReplicas from metrics (§13.3's formula)
     3. PATCH .../scale  {"spec":{"replicas": desiredReplicas}}
     4. done — HPA has NEVER seen a container name, an image, a probe, anything

   kubectl scale deploy/easypj-web --replicas=5   ──▶  is the SAME PATCH, by hand
</pre></div>
            <p><strong>Why a separate subresource, instead of PATCHing the Deployment directly?</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>A stable, minimal schema means ONE HPA implementation autoscales Deployments, ReplicaSets, AND StatefulSets — the controller doesn't need type-specific code for each</li>
                <li>Narrower RBAC surface: a ServiceAccount can be granted <code>update</code> on <code>deployments/scale</code> without also getting <code>update</code> on the full <code>deployments</code> resource (which would allow changing the image, too)</li>
                <li>Fewer moving parts to conflict on — scaling doesn't risk a 409 against someone editing an unrelated field like <code>resources.limits</code> at the same moment</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if HPA "isn't working," check the subresource path itself: <code>kubectl get --raw "/apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments/easypj-web/scale"</code> tells you exactly what the HPA sees and can write — a faster, more precise diagnostic than re-reading the whole Deployment YAML.</div>

            <h5>③ Proportional Scaling — What Happens If You Scale WHILE a Rollout Is Mid-Flight</h5>
            <p>Every diagram so far assumes a calm sequence: update finishes, THEN you scale. But what if traffic spikes (or HPA fires) while a rolling update is only 40% done — two ReplicaSets both partially populated? Kubernetes doesn't pause the rollout or dump the extra replicas onto just one RS; it <strong>proportionally</strong> splits the new total across old and new, preserving roughly the same ratio the rollout was already at.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Mid-rollout: replicas=10 total, RS-old=6, RS-new=4  (60/40 split so far)
        │
        │  HPA fires: desiredReplicas jumps 10 → 15  (scale UP by 5, mid-rollout)
        ▼
   Deployment controller proportionally scales BOTH ReplicaSets, not just the new one:
        RS-old: 6  → 6 + round(5 × 6/10) = 6 + 3 = 9
        RS-new: 4  → 4 + round(5 × 4/10) = 4 + 2 = 6
        (9 + 6 = 15 ✅ total matches, and the 60/40 ratio the rollout was already at is preserved)

   Without proportional scaling, a naive implementation might dump all 5 new
   pods onto RS-new — which would silently accelerate the rollout's pace far
   beyond the maxSurge/maxUnavailable you configured, defeating the whole
   point of a controlled, paced update.
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>What proportional scaling guarantees</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Scale UP mid-rollout</td><td>both old and new RS grow, keeping their current ratio — the rollout keeps advancing at its configured pace, not faster</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Scale DOWN mid-rollout</td><td>both RS shrink proportionally too — you don't accidentally kill 100% of the NEW (safer) pods just because they happen to be fewest in count</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>maxSurge/maxUnavailable</td><td>still respected throughout — proportional scaling changes the TARGET counts, not the pacing rules that get you there</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is genuinely advanced, rarely-taught behavior</strong> — most material stops at "HPA changes replicas." Knowing that scaling and rolling updates interact proportionally, rather than one blocking the other, explains why you'll sometimes see <code>kubectl get rs</code> show BOTH ReplicaSets growing at once during a traffic spike instead of one finishing first.</div>

            <h5>④ minReadySeconds &amp; the Available Condition — the State Machine Behind "Ready" vs "Available"</h5>
            <p>§13.1f introduced the <code>Available</code> condition. Here is the actual pod-level state machine that feeds it — and why a pod can be "Ready" in <code>kubectl get pods</code> for several seconds before the Deployment considers it truly available.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   ONE new pod's journey during a rolling update:

   Pending ──▶ Running ──▶ readinessProbe passes ──▶ READY
                                                          │
                                                          │  must stay Ready for
                                                          │  minReadySeconds (default 0)
                                                          ▼
                                                     AVAILABLE
                                                          │
                                              ONLY NOW does the Deployment controller
                                              count this pod toward availableReplicas
                                              and consider scaling down an old pod

   Why minReadySeconds exists: a pod that passes readinessProbe once but then
   crash-loops 3 seconds later (bad config, slow DB connection pool warm-up)
   would otherwise trick the rollout into declaring victory too early and
   tearing down the last-known-good old pod before the new one has PROVEN itself.
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>readyReplicas</code></td><td>pods currently passing their readiness probe RIGHT NOW — can flap up and down</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>availableReplicas</code></td><td>pods that have been Ready continuously for at least <code>minReadySeconds</code> — the number the <code>Available</code> condition and <code>maxUnavailable</code> math actually use</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>minReadySeconds: 0</code> (default)</td><td>Ready = Available instantly — fine for a stable app, risky for one with a slow warm-up crash pattern</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if a rollout that "should" be zero-downtime is briefly dipping below your expected pod count, check <code>minReadySeconds</code> — a value that's too low relative to how long your app takes to reveal a bad deploy is a common, quiet root cause that doesn't show up in <code>kubectl describe</code> as an obvious error.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Three-Way Strategic Merge — What <code>kubectl apply</code> Is Actually Diffing</h5>
            <p>Chapter 1's Speed Tips say "prefer <code>apply</code>, it's idempotent" — but idempotent HOW? <code>apply</code> doesn't just overwrite the live object with your file. It computes a genuine three-way diff so that fields changed by <em>other</em> actors (like HPA writing <code>replicas</code>, or a mutating webhook injecting a sidecar) survive your next <code>apply</code> instead of being silently reverted.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   THREE inputs to every `kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml`:

   1. LAST-APPLIED   →  stored verbatim in an annotation on the live object:
                         kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration
                         (this is YOUR previous apply, not the current live state)
   2. LIVE STATE     →  what's actually in etcd right now (may have HPA-set
                         replicas, or fields another controller/webhook added)
   3. NEW CONFIG     →  the file you just ran `apply -f` with

   MERGE LOGIC (strategic merge patch, per-field):
     field in NEW but not LAST-APPLIED     → ADD it
     field in LAST-APPLIED but not NEW     → REMOVE it (you deleted it from your YAML)
     field in LIVE but not LAST-APPLIED    → LEAVE IT ALONE (someone else owns it —
                                              e.g. HPA's replicas, a webhook's sidecar)
     field changed in NEW vs LAST-APPLIED  → UPDATE it in LIVE
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Patch type</th><th>Merge granularity</th><th>Used by</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Strategic merge patch</td><td>field-aware; understands lists like <code>containers</code> should merge BY NAME, not by array index</td><td><code>kubectl apply</code> (default), <code>kubectl patch</code> without a <code>--type</code> flag</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>JSON merge patch (RFC 7386)</td><td>generic; replaces whole arrays wholesale — no name-aware merging</td><td><code>kubectl patch --type=merge</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>JSON patch (RFC 6902)</td><td>explicit operations (<code>add</code>/<code>remove</code>/<code>replace</code>) at exact paths</td><td><code>kubectl patch --type=json</code> — used throughout this guide's practice drills for surgical one-field edits</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this matters for HPA + apply together.</strong> If you <code>kubectl apply</code> a Deployment YAML with <code>replicas: 3</code> hard-coded, and HPA has since scaled it to 7, a plain merge patch would see "replicas changed 3→3" (no-op against YOUR last-applied) and leave the live 7 alone — apply and HPA coexist peacefully specifically because of this three-way diff. This is also exactly why editing a Deployment with <code>kubectl edit</code> or hand-written <code>kubectl patch --type=json</code> is safer than re-running an old, stale YAML file that still has a hard-coded replica count from before autoscaling was added.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Hierarchy:</strong> Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods. Manage the Deployment; let it manage the rest — never hand-edit a Deployment-owned ReplicaSet.</li>
                    <li><strong>Rolling update + <code>rollout undo</code></strong> = safe ship + instant rollback. <code>maxSurge:1</code> + <code>maxUnavailable:0</code> with "add new, wait Ready, then remove old" gives true zero downtime.</li>
                    <li><strong>HPA needs BOTH</strong> resource requests on the containers AND the metrics-server installed; it edits the Deployment's <code>/scale</code> subresource, not the YAML you wrote.</li>
                    <li><strong>The robust self-healing trio</strong> = ReplicaSet (replace lost pods) + Probes (detect sick pods) + PodDisruptionBudget (survive voluntary disruption).</li>
                    <li><strong>liveness = restart; readiness = traffic gate; startup = boot protection.</strong> An aggressive liveness probe without a startup probe causes restart storms on slow-booting apps.</li>
                    <li><strong>Old ReplicaSets are kept</strong> (scaled to 0) up to <code>revisionHistoryLimit</code> — that is what makes <code>--to-revision</code> possible.</li>
                    <li><strong>RollingUpdate is the default</strong> and correct 95% of the time; <code>Recreate</code> is only for versions that truly cannot coexist (e.g. a breaking DB schema migration).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Rolling update is the default</strong> — If the question doesn't specify a strategy, it's <code>RollingUpdate</code>. Only use <code>Recreate</code> when explicitly asked (kills all pods first)</li>
                    <li><strong><code>maxUnavailable</code> + <code>maxSurge</code></strong> — Defaults are 25% each. The exam may ask you to set specific values. Remember: <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> + <code>maxSurge: 1</code> = zero-downtime</li>
                    <li><strong>Rollback requires history</strong> — <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> only works if there's a previous revision. Add <code>--record</code> (deprecated) or use <code>--to-revision=N</code> for precision</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl set image</code> container name</strong> — You must specify the container name: <code>kubectl set image deployment/web nginx=nginx:1.25</code>. Get it from <code>kubectl get deploy web -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[*].name}'</code></li>
                    <li><strong>The selector is IMMUTABLE</strong> — you cannot change <code>matchLabels</code> on a live Deployment; delete and recreate</li>
                    <li><strong>HPA does NOTHING without resource requests</strong> (no denominator for %) and does nothing without metrics-server installed</li>
                    <li><strong>An aggressive liveness probe causes RESTART STORMS</strong> — it kills slow-but-fine pods. Give it a <code>startupProbe</code> or a higher <code>failureThreshold</code>/<code>initialDelaySeconds</code>. Remember: readiness ≠ liveness — a failing readiness drops traffic, does NOT restart</li>
                    <li><strong>Recreate strategy = downtime</strong> — never use it unless versions are truly incompatible</li>
                    <li><strong>Never hand-edit a Deployment-owned ReplicaSet</strong> — the controller reverts it; edit the Deployment instead</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Deployment lifecycle in 60 seconds:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create deploy web --image=nginx:1.24 --replicas=3</code><br>
                <code>kubectl set image deploy/web nginx=nginx:1.25</code><br>
                <code>kubectl rollout status deploy/web</code><br>
                <code>kubectl rollout history deploy/web</code><br>
                <code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/web</code> — instant rollback<br>
                <code>kubectl scale deploy/web --replicas=N</code> — scale fast<br>
                <code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/web --to-revision=N</code> — roll back to a specific rev<br>
                <code>kubectl create deploy web --image=nginx --replicas=3 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; deploy.yaml</code> — generate YAML, don't type it<br>
                <code>kubectl get pods -w</code> — watch pods churn live during any rollout</p>
            </div>

