            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 13 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the mental model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why doesn't a Deployment manage Pods directly — what sits in between, and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A ReplicaSet sits in between. The Deployment owns one ReplicaSet per pod-template version and only manages rollout <em>history</em> and <em>strategy</em>; the ReplicaSet's sole job is keeping exactly <code>spec.replicas</code> pods matching its selector alive.</p>
<pre>
  Deployment easypj  ──owns──▶  ReplicaSet easypj-7d9f8b6c5d  ──owns──▶  Pods
  (rollout history,             (keeps N copies of ONE                 (your easypj
   strategy, revisions)          pod template alive)                    containers)
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Splitting the two lets Kubernetes keep old ReplicaSets around (scaled to 0) as a rollback target — a rolling update is really "scale the new RS up, scale the old RS down," which is only possible because they're separate objects.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The Deployment's <code>selector.matchLabels</code> is immutable. What does that actually mean in practice?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Once a Deployment is created, you cannot <code>kubectl apply</code> a change to <code>spec.selector</code> — the API server rejects it. To re-point a Deployment at a different label set you must delete and recreate it.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl patch deployment easypj --type=json \
  -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/spec/selector/matchLabels/app","value":"easypj-v2"}]'
<span class="comment"># Error from server: Deployment.apps "easypj" is invalid: spec.selector: Invalid value:
# ...field is immutable</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The selector is how the Deployment finds "its" ReplicaSets and how a ReplicaSet finds "its" Pods. Letting it change live would let one Deployment silently start (or stop) owning arbitrary pods — an immutability rule that protects against exactly that.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>matchLabels</code> vs <code>matchExpressions</code> on a ReplicaSet selector — what's the difference?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>matchLabels</code> is simple key/value equality (all must match). <code>matchExpressions</code> adds set-based operators: <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>DoesNotExist</code> — e.g. <code>tier In (web, api)</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th><th>Expresses</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>matchLabels</code></td><td><code>app: easypj</code></td><td>exact equality only</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>matchExpressions</code></td><td><code>{key: tier, operator: In, values: [web, api]}</code></td><td>OR / set membership / existence</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Both can appear in the same selector (they're ANDed together). <code>matchExpressions</code> is rarely needed for a single Deployment's own ReplicaSet, but it's the same selector syntax used by NetworkPolicy and node affinity, so recognizing it here transfers directly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">HPA reports <code>&lt;unknown&gt;/70%</code> and never scales. What's the single most common cause?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The container has no <code>resources.requests.cpu</code> set. HPA computes utilization as a percentage of the CPU <em>request</em> — with no request, there is no denominator, so it has nothing to compute.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe hpa easypj
<span class="comment"># Name:      easypj
# Reference: Deployment/easypj
# Metrics:   ( current / target )
#              &lt;unknown&gt; / 70%   ← no denominator: fix by adding resources.requests.cpu</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The second most common cause is metrics-server not installed or not reporting yet — check with <code>kubectl top pods</code>. On the CKA, always confirm both prerequisites (requests set + metrics-server running) before troubleshooting further.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">With 3 replicas at 85% CPU and a 70% target, what does the HPA's control-loop formula give for desired replicas?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>desiredReplicas = ceil(currentReplicas × (currentMetric / targetMetric))</code> → <code>ceil(3 × 85/70) = ceil(3.64) = 4</code>.</p>
<pre>
  desiredReplicas = ceil( 3 × (85 / 70) )
                   = ceil( 3 × 1.214... )
                   = ceil( 3.64... )
                   = 4 replicas
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>HPA re-evaluates roughly every 15s. It always rounds UP so it never under-provisions, and by design it scales up fast (default 0s stabilization) but scales down slow (default 300s) to avoid flapping on bursty traffic.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the three Kubernetes autoscalers and what each one actually resizes.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>HPA</strong> — adds/removes POD replicas based on load. <strong>VPA</strong> — right-sizes a pod's CPU/memory REQUESTS (not the replica count). <strong>Cluster Autoscaler</strong> — adds/removes NODES when pods can't be scheduled.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Autoscaler</th><th>Resizes</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>HPA</td><td>pod REPLICA COUNT</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>VPA</td><td>a pod's own CPU/memory REQUESTS</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Cluster Autoscaler</td><td>NODE COUNT</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Don't run HPA and VPA on the same metric (e.g. CPU) for the same workload — they'll fight each other, since VPA changing the request changes HPA's percentage denominator out from under it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the four probe handler types, and which single field distinguishes a <code>startupProbe</code> from the other two behaviorally?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Handlers: <code>httpGet</code>, <code>tcpSocket</code>, <code>exec</code> (command), <code>grpc</code>. What makes <code>startupProbe</code> different isn't a field — it's behavior: while it's running, liveness and readiness are <em>suspended</em>, and once it succeeds once, it never runs again for the life of the container.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">startupProbe:
  httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8000}
  failureThreshold: 30
  periodSeconds: 5        <span class="comment"># up to 150s to boot before liveness/readiness even start checking</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is why a slow-booting app (e.g. running Django migrations on startup) should get a <code>startupProbe</code> with a generous <code>failureThreshold × periodSeconds</code> window — it protects the pod from a liveness restart before it's even finished starting.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod fails its readinessProbe. Is it restarted? Does it still receive traffic?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No restart. The pod is removed from the owning Service's Endpoints, so it receives <strong>no new traffic</strong> — clients simply stop being routed to it until it passes the probe again.</p>
<pre>
  readinessProbe FAILS
       │
       ▼
  pod removed from Service Endpoints  →  0 new traffic routed here
  pod itself keeps RUNNING            →  RESTARTS counter stays unchanged
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Only a failing <strong>liveness</strong> probe restarts a container. Confusing the two is the single most common probe mistake — readiness is a traffic gate, liveness is a restart trigger.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does the "robust self-healing" trio consist of, and what does each piece cover that the others don't?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>ReplicaSet</strong> (recreates a pod after node failure or deletion) + <strong>Probes</strong> (detect a pod that's running but unhealthy) + <strong>PodDisruptionBudget</strong> (limits how many pods a voluntary action like a drain can take down at once).</p>
                        <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                            <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔁</div><h5>ReplicaSet</h5><p>Involuntary pod loss</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">❤️</div><h5>Probes</h5><p>Silent unhealthiness</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>PDB</h5><p>Voluntary disruption</p></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Each covers a different failure class: involuntary pod loss, silent unhealthiness, and voluntary disruption. On the exam, "make this Deployment robust" usually means checking all three are present, not just one.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Under the hood, what does <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> actually do — is anything deleted?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Nothing is deleted. It scales the chosen old (kept-at-0) ReplicaSet back up and scales the current, broken one down to 0 — which itself creates a brand-new revision number in the history.</p>
<pre>
  BEFORE undo:  RS-v2 (broken) desired=3   RS-v1 (old, good) desired=0
  AFTER  undo:  RS-v2 (broken) desired=0   RS-v1 (old, good) desired=3
                └── nothing deleted — just two replica counts swapped ──┘
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is only possible because old ReplicaSets are retained (up to <code>revisionHistoryLimit</code>, default 10) instead of being deleted after a rollout completes — the "rollback" is just another rolling update, run in reverse.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

