            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 13 Visual Summary — ship, scale &amp; self-heal easypj</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   Deployment easypj ──owns──▶ ReplicaSet (per revision) ──owns──▶ Pods
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   SCALE:   kubectl scale --replicas=N  ·  edit YAML  ·  HPA (needs requests + metrics-server)
   UPDATE:  kubectl set image  ──▶  maxSurge (+extra) / maxUnavailable (-tolerated)  ──▶  zero downtime
   ROLLBACK: kubectl rollout undo  →  scales an OLD (kept-at-0) RS back up, new RS to 0
   SELF-HEAL: ReplicaSet (recreate) + Probes (liveness=restart, readiness=traffic gate) + PDB    ☸️
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> a Deployment never touches Pods directly — it owns a ReplicaSet per revision, and a rolling update is just scaling the new RS up while scaling the old RS down, one step at a time, bounded by <code>maxSurge</code>/<code>maxUnavailable</code>. <code>rollout undo</code> is the same mechanism in reverse, which is why it's instant. HPA layers automatic scaling on top — but only once resource requests and metrics-server are both in place.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> "robust" means all three self-healing primitives together — ReplicaSet replaces lost pods, probes catch pods that are running but broken (liveness restarts, readiness gates traffic), and a PodDisruptionBudget protects you from your own maintenance. Master this chapter and Deployments, the single most-tested CKA workload object, become fast and safe.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

