            <!-- ─────────── §14.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.1 How the Scheduler Works</h3>
                <p>The <code>kube-scheduler</code> runs as a control plane component. When a new pod is created without a <code>nodeName</code>, it enters the scheduling queue. The scheduler then executes a multi-phase pipeline to find the best node.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy: The Scheduler Is a Recruiter</h5>
                    <p><strong>Filtering</strong> is the résumé screen — do you even meet the hard requirements? <strong>Scoring</strong> is the interview ranking — of the qualified candidates, who's the best fit? <strong>Binding</strong> is the signed offer letter.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Filtering</h5>
                        <p>Which nodes <strong>CAN</strong> run this pod? Checks: sufficient resources, node taints, node selectors, affinity rules, port availability.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Scoring</h5>
                        <p>Which passing node is the <strong>BEST</strong> fit? Scores based on: resource balance, affinity preference, spread, image locality.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Binding</h5>
                        <p>The highest-scoring node wins. Scheduler sets <code>pod.spec.nodeName</code> and the kubelet on that node starts the container.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Scheduler Decision Flowchart — easypj Pod Placement</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  NEW POD: easypj (requests: 256Mi memory, 250m CPU)
       │
       ▼
  ════════════════ PHASE 1: FILTERING ════════════════
  "Which nodes CAN run this pod?"

  Node 1 (512Mi free, 500m CPU free)   → ✅ PASSES — has enough resources
  Node 2 (128Mi free, 100m CPU free)   → ❌ FAILS  — insufficient memory
  Node 3 (1Gi free, 1000m CPU free)    → ✅ PASSES — has enough resources
  Node 4 (taint: gpu=true:NoSchedule)  → ❌ FAILS  — no matching toleration
       │
       ▼ (only passing nodes continue)
  ════════════════ PHASE 2: SCORING ═════════════════
  "Which passing node is the BEST fit?"

  Node 1: Score 65  — good fit, resources balanced
  Node 3: Score 82  — better fit, more headroom,
                      topology spread bonus, affinity match
       │
       ▼
  ════════════════ PHASE 3: BINDING ═════════════════

  🏆 Winner: Node 3 (highest score: 82)
  → pod.spec.nodeName = "node-3"
  → kubelet on Node 3 pulls image → starts container
  → Pod status: Running ✅
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 What happens on a tie? It's random, not deterministic</h5>
                    <p>If two or more nodes end up with the EXACT same final score, the scheduler picks one <strong>at random</strong> among them — not the first alphabetically, not the first added to the cluster. If a pod keeps landing on a node you didn't expect despite two candidates looking equally suited, a genuine tie (resolved randomly) is a real, official explanation, not a bug.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>Resource Fit</h5>
                        <p>Does the node have enough CPU &amp; memory to satisfy the pod's requests?</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div>
                        <h5>Label Match</h5>
                        <p>Does the node have the labels required by nodeSelector or nodeAffinity?</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>Taint Check</h5>
                        <p>Does the pod tolerate the node's taints? No toleration = rejected.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📊</div>
                        <h5>Spread Score</h5>
                        <p>How well does this placement spread pods across nodes/zones?</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Bin-Packing vs Spreading — What the Score Weights Are Actually Trading Off</h5>
                    <p>The scoring plugins in the table above don't all pull the same direction. <strong>Bin-packing</strong> weights (favoring the MOST-loaded node that still fits) pack pods tightly onto fewer nodes — cost-efficient, since idle nodes can be scaled down. <strong>Spreading</strong> weights (favoring the LEAST-loaded node, or an even zone distribution) maximize resilience — a single node failure takes out fewer pods. There's no universally "correct" setting; it's a real cluster-operator trade-off between cost and blast-radius, tuned via each scoring plugin's relative weight.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Static Pods — Bypass the Scheduler</h4>
                <p>Static pods are managed directly by the <strong>kubelet</strong> on a specific node, not by the API server. They're used for control plane components (etcd, apiserver, scheduler itself). The CKA may ask you to create one.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Creating a Static Pod</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Static pods live in the kubelet's manifest directory</span>
<span class="comment"># Default: /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</span>

<span class="comment"># Find the staticPodPath on any node</span>
ssh worker-1 "cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath"
<span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># Create a static pod (just place a YAML file there)</span>
ssh worker-1 "cat > /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-nginx.yaml" &lt;&lt;'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: static-nginx
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:1.25
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
EOF

<span class="comment"># kubelet auto-starts it! Check from control plane:</span>
kubectl get pods
<span class="comment"># NAME                       READY   STATUS    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># static-nginx-worker-1      1/1     Running   10s</span>
<span class="comment">#                  ^^^^^^^^^ node name appended!</span>

<span class="comment"># Delete: remove the file from /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl delete won't work — kubelet will recreate it!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam: Static Pod Key Facts</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Static pod names always end with <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code></li>
                        <li>They can only be created/deleted by adding/removing YAML from the manifest directory</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl delete</code> on a static pod will NOT permanently remove it — kubelet recreates it</li>
                        <li>Control plane components (apiserver, etcd, scheduler) are static pods on the control plane node</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Diagnosing <code>Pending</code> — Reading the Scheduler's Own Explanation</h4>
                <p>When filtering eliminates every node, the scheduler doesn't just fail silently — it writes a <code>FailedScheduling</code> Event naming the EXACT reason each node was rejected. Learning to read this one message replaces guesswork with a direct answer.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Reading the FailedScheduling event</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe pod easypj-pending -n easypj | grep -A5 Events
<span class="comment"># Events:
#   Type     Reason            Message
#   ----     ------            -------
#   Warning  FailedScheduling  0/4 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated taint
#            {node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: }, 2 node(s) didn't match Pod's
#            node affinity/selector, 1 Insufficient memory.</span>

<span class="comment"># The message is a per-node TALLY of filter-plugin rejections — read it left to right:</span>
<span class="comment">#   "1 node(s) had untolerated taint ..."       → a TaintToleration filter rejection</span>
<span class="comment">#   "2 node(s) didn't match ... affinity"       → a NodeAffinity filter rejection</span>
<span class="comment">#   "1 Insufficient memory"                     → a NodeResourcesFit filter rejection</span>
<span class="comment"># Add these up and they equal the total node count — every node is accounted for.</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Two more scheduler-bypass mechanisms worth knowing</h5>
                    <p><strong><code>spec.nodeName</code></strong> — setting this directly on a pod skips filtering/scoring/binding entirely; the kubelet on that exact node just runs it (this is literally how the kubelet places static pods internally). If the named node doesn't exist, the pod stays <code>Pending</code> forever with no scheduler event at all, since the scheduler never even saw it. <strong><code>spec.schedulerName</code></strong> — a cluster can run more than one scheduler (a custom one for GPU-aware bin-packing, say); a pod naming a <code>schedulerName</code> that isn't running also stays silently <code>Pending</code> — always confirm the named scheduler is actually deployed before assuming a placement rule is the problem.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.2 Node Selectors — Simple Placement</h3>
                <p>The simplest way to constrain a pod to specific nodes. Label a node, then reference that label in the pod spec. It's a <strong>hard requirement</strong> — the pod won't schedule if no node matches.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Label a Node, Then Select It</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Label the node</span>
kubectl label node worker-1 disk=ssd

<span class="comment"># Verify label was applied</span>
kubectl get node worker-1 --show-labels | grep disk

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create pod with nodeSelector (see YAML below)</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-ssd-pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify pod landed on the right node</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-ssd -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME         READY   STATUS    NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-ssd   1/1     Running   worker-1   ← correct!</span>

<span class="comment"># Clean up: remove label</span>
kubectl label node worker-1 disk-</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-ssd-pod.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-ssd
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    disk: ssd                    <span class="comment"># Only schedule on nodes with disk=ssd</span>
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 nodeSelector vs nodeAffinity</h5>
                    <p><code>nodeSelector</code> is simple but limited — it only supports exact equality matching. For "prefer but don't require" or "match any of these values" logic, use <strong>Node Affinity</strong> (see 14.3).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Built-in Labels — Every Node Already Has These, No <code>kubectl label</code> Needed</h4>
                <p>You don't have to hand-label nodes for common placement needs — the kubelet self-reports a standard set of labels at registration time. Knowing these by heart saves real exam minutes over inventing your own custom label scheme.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Label</th><th>Example value</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code></td><td><code>worker-1</code></td><td>"this exact node" — also the standard <code>topologyKey</code> for per-node spread/anti-affinity</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code></td><td><code>us-east-1a</code></td><td>zone-aware placement/spread on any cloud that populates it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>topology.kubernetes.io/region</code></td><td><code>us-east-1</code></td><td>region-level placement — coarser than zone</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/arch</code></td><td><code>amd64</code> / <code>arm64</code></td><td>mixed-architecture clusters — pin an image built for one CPU arch</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/os</code></td><td><code>linux</code> / <code>windows</code></td><td>mixed-OS clusters</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/instance-type</code></td><td><code>m5.xlarge</code></td><td>pin to a specific cloud VM size/family</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">See exactly what's already there before adding a custom label</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get nodes --show-labels
kubectl get node worker-1 -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels}' | tr ',' '\n'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 The <code>node-restriction.kubernetes.io/</code> prefix — why a compromised node can't fake its way past isolation labels</h5>
                    <p>If you're labeling nodes for security/isolation purposes (e.g. "only GPU-team pods on GPU nodes"), be aware that a kubelet can normally self-label its own node — which would let a COMPROMISED node simply relabel itself to defeat your nodeSelector/nodeAffinity rules. Labels under the reserved <code>node-restriction.kubernetes.io/</code> prefix are the exception: the <code>NodeRestriction</code> admission plugin only allows the kubelet to set labels under this prefix on its OWN node, enforced via the Node authorizer. Use this prefix specifically for any label an isolation or security policy depends on.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.3 Node Affinity — Advanced Placement</h3>
                <p>Node affinity is the <strong>more powerful version of nodeSelector</strong>. It supports operators (<code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>Gt</code>, <code>Lt</code>) and offers two types: hard rules (required) and soft rules (preferred).</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Type</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>When Pod Can't Match</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>requiredDuringScheduling<wbr>IgnoredDuringExecution</strong></td>
                                <td>Hard rule — MUST match</td>
                                <td>Pod stays Pending forever</td>
                                <td>GPU workloads, compliance zones</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>preferredDuringScheduling<wbr>IgnoredDuringExecution</strong></td>
                                <td>Soft rule — TRY to match</td>
                                <td>Scheduled anywhere available</td>
                                <td>Prefer SSD, prefer same zone</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-node-affinity.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      affinity:
        nodeAffinity:
          <span class="comment"># HARD: Must be on an SSD node</span>
          requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
            nodeSelectorTerms:
            - matchExpressions:
              - key: disk
                operator: In
                values:
                - ssd
                - nvme
          <span class="comment"># SOFT: Prefer us-east zone (but ok if not available)</span>
          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
          - weight: 80           <span class="comment"># 1-100, higher = stronger preference</span>
            preference:
              matchExpressions:
              - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
                operator: In
                values:
                - us-east-1a
                - us-east-1b
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Node Affinity Decision Matrix</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Pod: easypj (requires: disk ∈ {ssd, nvme}, prefers: zone ∈ {us-east-1a})

  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Node          disk    zone          Required  Preferred   │
  │  ──────────    ────    ──────────    ────────  ─────────   │
  │  worker-1      ssd     us-east-1a   ✅ PASS   ✅ Match    │ ← Best (score 82)
  │  worker-2      ssd     us-west-1a   ✅ PASS   ❌ No match │ ← OK (score 65)
  │  worker-3      hdd     us-east-1a   ❌ FAIL   —           │ ← Rejected
  │  worker-4      nvme    us-east-1b   ✅ PASS   ✅ Match    │ ← Good (score 78)
  │  worker-5      —       us-east-1a   ❌ FAIL   —           │ ← Rejected
  │                                                             │
  │  Result: Pod scheduled on worker-1 (highest combined score)│
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ "IgnoredDuringExecution" — What It Means</h5>
                    <p>If you <strong>remove a node's label after a pod is already scheduled there</strong>, the pod keeps running. The affinity rule is only checked at scheduling time, not continuously. A future Kubernetes version may add <code>RequiredDuringExecution</code> to also evict running pods.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>How Multiple Terms Combine — the OR/AND Rules That Make Affinity YAML Actually Work</h4>
                <p>Writing a correct node affinity rule requires knowing exactly how its nested lists combine — get this backwards and you'll write a rule that's silently way too strict or way too loose.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Combines with</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Multiple <code>nodeSelectorTerms</code> (top-level list)</td><td><strong>OR</strong> — a node matches if it satisfies ANY one whole term</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Multiple <code>matchExpressions</code> within ONE term</td><td><strong>AND</strong> — a node must satisfy EVERY expression in that term</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>nodeSelector</code> + <code>nodeAffinity</code> used together</td><td><strong>AND</strong> — both must be satisfied; a node failing either is filtered out</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 This is exactly the mechanism §14.3's zone-OR example relies on</h5>
                    <p>Putting <code>values: ["us-east-1a", "us-east-1b"]</code> on ONE <code>In</code> expression is OR-across-values within that expression. To OR across genuinely different KEYS (e.g. "zone=A OR instance-type=large"), you need two separate <code>nodeSelectorTerms</code> entries instead — a single term's expressions are always ANDed together, never ORed.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The <code>Gt</code>/<code>Lt</code> Operators — Numeric Comparison, Not Just Set Membership</h4>
                <p><code>In</code>/<code>NotIn</code>/<code>Exists</code>/<code>DoesNotExist</code> cover set membership, but two more operators — <code>Gt</code> (greater-than) and <code>Lt</code> (less-than) — do numeric comparison against a label's value, treated as an integer.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Only schedule on nodes with 32+ CPU cores (a numeric label you've set)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">affinity:
  nodeAffinity:
    requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
      nodeSelectorTerms:
      - matchExpressions:
        - key: cpu-cores               <span class="comment"># a custom label: kubectl label node worker-1 cpu-cores=64</span>
          operator: Gt
          values: ["31"]                <span class="comment"># values is ALWAYS a list of strings, even for Gt/Lt — "31" not 31</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why <code>Gt</code>/<code>Lt</code> are rare in practice</h5>
                    <p>They only work against custom labels YOU set with a numeric value (Kubernetes doesn't auto-populate anything like "CPU core count" as a label) — so they show up mostly in custom scheduling setups (e.g. tiered hardware generations, licensing-seat counts) rather than everyday cluster use. Know they exist for the exam, but don't expect to reach for them often in a typical easypj-style deployment.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Required node affinity can quietly fight the Cluster Autoscaler</h5>
                    <p>If a pod's <code>requiredDuringScheduling</code> rule targets a node characteristic that no node group in your cloud provider actually provisions (e.g. a very specific instance type combined with a zone that group doesn't span), the Cluster Autoscaler has no node pool it can scale up to satisfy the pod — it stays <code>Pending</code> even though the autoscaler is working correctly and adding capacity elsewhere. Keep required rules matched against your ACTUAL node group topology, and reach for <code>preferred</code> whenever "nice to have, not mandatory" is really what you mean.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.4 Pod Affinity and Anti-Affinity</h3>
                <p>While <strong>Node Affinity</strong> places pods relative to <em>nodes</em>, <strong>Pod Affinity/Anti-Affinity</strong> places pods relative to <em>other pods</em>. This is essential for performance (co-locate) and high availability (spread out).</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🤝</div>
                        <h5>Pod Affinity</h5>
                        <p><strong>"Place me near this pod."</strong> Put easypj Django on the same node/zone as its Redis cache for low latency.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">↔️</div>
                        <h5>Pod Anti-Affinity</h5>
                        <p><strong>"Keep me away from that pod."</strong> Spread easypj replicas across different nodes so one node failure doesn't take all of them down.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-pod-antiaffinity.yaml — HA Deployment</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      affinity:
        <span class="comment"># Anti-Affinity: Spread replicas across nodes</span>
        podAntiAffinity:
          requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
          - labelSelector:
              matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values:
                - easypj
            topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname  # "different nodes"

        <span class="comment"># Affinity: Place near Redis cache</span>
        podAffinity:
          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
          - weight: 100
            podAffinityTerm:
              labelSelector:
                matchExpressions:
                - key: app
                  operator: In
                  values:
                  - easypj-redis
              topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone  # "same zone"
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod Anti-Affinity — easypj Replicas Spread Across Nodes</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Deployment: easypj (replicas: 3)
  podAntiAffinity: kubernetes.io/hostname (no 2 on same node)

  ┌────────────────┐  ┌────────────────┐  ┌────────────────┐
  │   worker-1     │  │   worker-2     │  │   worker-3     │
  │ ┌────────────┐ │  │ ┌────────────┐ │  │ ┌────────────┐ │
  │ │ easypj-abc │ │  │ │ easypj-def │ │  │ │ easypj-ghi │ │
  │ │   📦 v1    │ │  │ │   📦 v1    │ │  │ │   📦 v1    │ │
  │ └────────────┘ │  │ └────────────┘ │  │ └────────────┘ │
  │                │  │                │  │                │
  │ ┌────────────┐ │  │                │  │                │
  │ │ redis-xyz  │ │  │                │  │                │
  │ │   🔴 cache │ │  │                │  │                │
  │ └────────────┘ │  │                │  │                │
  └────────────────┘  └────────────────┘  └────────────────┘

  ✅ Each node has exactly 1 easypj pod — survives any single node failure!
  ✅ easypj-abc co-located with redis (podAffinity) for low latency
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>Combining Multiple Preferred Terms — How the Weights Actually Add Up</h4>
                <p>A pod can list several <code>preferredDuringScheduling</code> terms at once, each with its own <code>weight</code> (1-100). The scheduler doesn't pick "the best matching term" — it sums the weights of EVERY term a candidate node satisfies, and that sum becomes part of the node's overall score.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Worked example — 3 preferred pod-affinity terms, summed per node</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  podAffinity.preferredDuringScheduling terms:
    weight:  50  → prefer near "redis-cache"
    weight:  30  → prefer near "same-team pods" (label team=platform)
    weight:  20  → prefer near a node already running "easypj" (packing)

  Node A: near redis-cache ✅ (+50)  near team pods ✅ (+30)  near easypj ✅ (+20) = 100
  Node B: near redis-cache ✅ (+50)  near team pods ❌ ( +0)  near easypj ❌ ( +0) =  50
  Node C: near redis-cache ❌ ( +0)  near team pods ❌ ( +0)  near easypj ✅ (+20) =  20

  Node A wins — not because any ONE term was decisive, but because it satisfied
  the most WEIGHT overall. A node matching one high-weight term can still lose
  to a node matching several smaller-weight terms, and vice versa.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>topologyKey</code> must be a real node label — you can't invent one on the fly</h5>
                    <p>Both pod affinity and anti-affinity require <code>topologyKey</code> to name a label that actually exists on your nodes (built-in like <code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code>, or one you've applied yourself). If you typo it or reference a label no node carries, the rule doesn't error — it simply never matches any node, silently turning a <code>required</code> rule into permanent <code>Pending</code>. Always <code>kubectl get nodes --show-labels</code> to confirm the key exists before trusting a pod-affinity rule that references it.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.5 Taints and Tolerations</h3>
                <p>Taints are the <strong>opposite of affinity</strong> — taints <em>repel</em> pods from a node, while tolerations let specific pods <em>override</em> that repulsion. This is a <strong>CKA must-know concept</strong> and appears on almost every exam.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The Analogy</h5>
                    <p>Think of a node's taint as a "No Entry" sign. A toleration is the "VIP pass" that lets a pod in despite the sign. Without the pass, the pod is rejected.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Effect</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>Running Pods</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>NoSchedule</strong></td>
                                <td>New pods WITHOUT tolerance are rejected</td>
                                <td>Existing pods stay</td>
                                <td>Dedicated GPU nodes, team isolation</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>PreferNoSchedule</strong></td>
                                <td>Try NOT to schedule here, but allow if no other option</td>
                                <td>Existing pods stay</td>
                                <td>Soft preference — avoid but don't block</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>NoExecute</strong></td>
                                <td>Reject new pods AND <strong>evict running pods</strong> without tolerance</td>
                                <td><strong>Evicted!</strong></td>
                                <td>Node going into maintenance, toxic node</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Taint &amp; Toleration Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add a taint to a node</span>
kubectl taint node worker-1 gpu=true:NoSchedule

<span class="comment"># Verify taint</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 Taints
<span class="comment"># Taints:  gpu=true:NoSchedule</span>

<span class="comment"># Remove a taint (add dash at the end)</span>
kubectl taint node worker-1 gpu=true:NoSchedule-

<span class="comment"># See control plane taints (this is why your pods don't run on master!)</span>
kubectl describe node control-plane | grep Taints
<span class="comment"># Taints:  node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pod-with-toleration.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: ml-training-job
spec:
  tolerations:
  - key: "gpu"
    operator: "Equal"        <span class="comment"># Match exact value</span>
    value: "true"
    effect: "NoSchedule"
  <span class="comment"># Alternative: tolerate ANY value for the key</span>
  <span class="comment"># - key: "gpu"</span>
  <span class="comment">#   operator: "Exists"    # Don't care about value</span>
  <span class="comment">#   effect: "NoSchedule"</span>
  containers:
  - name: trainer
    image: ml-trainer:v1
    resources:
      limits:
        nvidia.com/gpu: 1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Taints &amp; Tolerations — Access Control</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌────────────────── Node: gpu-node ──────────────────┐
  │ Taint: gpu=true:NoSchedule   🚫                    │
  │                                                     │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  easypj pod (no toleration)                  │   │
  │  │  → ❌ REJECTED — "You don't have a VIP pass" │   │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                     │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  ml-job pod (toleration: gpu=true)           │   │
  │  │  → ✅ SCHEDULED — "VIP pass accepted!"      │   │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ┌────────────── Control Plane Node ──────────────────┐
  │ Taint: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:      │
  │        NoSchedule                                   │
  │                                                     │
  │  That's why your pods DON'T run on control plane!  │
  │  Only system pods (with tolerations) run here:     │
  │  ├── kube-apiserver                                 │
  │  ├── kube-scheduler                                 │
  │  ├── kube-controller-manager                        │
  │  └── etcd                                           │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Trap: Taint Removal Syntax</h5>
                    <p>To <strong>remove</strong> a taint, append a <code>-</code> (minus sign) at the end. Many candidates forget this on the exam:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Add: <code>kubectl taint node worker-1 key=value:NoSchedule</code></li>
                        <li>Remove: <code>kubectl taint node worker-1 key=value:NoSchedule<strong>-</strong></code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Well-Known Taints the Node Controller Applies Automatically — Nobody Ran <code>kubectl taint</code></h4>
                <p>Not every taint you'll see in <code>kubectl describe node</code> was hand-applied. The node controller and kubelet automatically taint an unhealthy node so the scheduler stops sending it new pods AND (for the <code>NoExecute</code> ones) existing pods evacuate — this is the exact mechanism behind "a node dies and its pods eventually move," covered from the timing side in Chapter 1's UTH ⑥.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Auto-applied taint</th><th>Effect</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/not-ready</code></td><td>NoExecute</td><td>the node's kubelet stopped reporting <code>Ready</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable</code></td><td>NoExecute</td><td>the node controller lost contact entirely (can't tell if it's down or just partitioned)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/memory-pressure</code></td><td>NoSchedule</td><td>the node is low on memory and beginning to evict pods (kubelet eviction manager)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure</code></td><td>NoSchedule</td><td>the node is low on disk space</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/pid-pressure</code></td><td>NoSchedule</td><td>the node is running low on process IDs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/network-unavailable</code></td><td>NoSchedule</td><td>the node's network isn't correctly configured yet (common briefly during boot, before CNI finishes)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable</code></td><td>NoSchedule</td><td>applied by <code>kubectl cordon</code> — this IS the mechanism cordon uses under the hood, not a separate flag</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 <code>tolerationSeconds</code> — surviving a brief blip instead of an instant eviction</h5>
                    <p>Every pod gets a default toleration for <code>not-ready</code> and <code>unreachable</code> with <code>tolerationSeconds: 300</code> — this is precisely why a pod doesn't vanish the instant a node has a 2-second network hiccup; it rides out up to 5 minutes before the <code>NoExecute</code> eviction actually fires. You can shorten this for latency-sensitive workloads that want to fail over faster, or lengthen it for a workload where a spurious reschedule is worse than a brief outage:</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Shortening the default 300s grace window to 30s</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">tolerations:
- key: "node.kubernetes.io/not-ready"
  operator: "Exists"
  effect: "NoExecute"
  tolerationSeconds: 30     <span class="comment"># evict after only 30s instead of the 300s default</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.6 Node Management for Admins</h3>
                <p>Cluster administrators regularly need to perform node maintenance — OS upgrades, kernel patches, hardware replacement. Kubernetes provides three commands for safe node management. This is a <strong>very common CKA exam scenario</strong>.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Cordon</h5>
                        <p>Mark node as <strong>unschedulable</strong>. Existing pods stay, but no new pods can be scheduled here.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Drain</h5>
                        <p><strong>Safely evict</strong> all pods to other nodes. Respects PDB, grace periods, and pod lifecycle.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Maintenance</h5>
                        <p>Perform the actual work — OS upgrade, kernel patch, reboot, hardware swap.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Uncordon</h5>
                        <p>Mark node as <strong>schedulable</strong> again. New pods can now be placed here.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Node Maintenance Workflow</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Cordon — stop new pods from scheduling</span>
kubectl cordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 cordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify: node shows SchedulingDisabled</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME        STATUS                     ROLES    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1    Ready,SchedulingDisabled   worker   30d</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Drain — safely evict all pods</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># evicting pod default/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-abc12</span>
<span class="comment"># evicting pod default/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-def34</span>
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 drained</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Perform maintenance (OS upgrade, reboot, etc.)</span>
<span class="comment"># ... work happens here ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Uncordon — allow pods again</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 uncordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify: node is Ready again (no SchedulingDisabled)</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME        STATUS   ROLES    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1    Ready    worker   30d</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Node Maintenance Flow — Safe Pod Migration</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌─────── worker-1 (BEFORE) ───────┐     ┌─────── worker-2 ────────┐
  │                                  │     │                          │
  │  📦 easypj-abc    📦 redis-xyz  │     │  📦 easypj-ghi          │
  │  📦 easypj-def                  │     │                          │
  │                                  │     │                          │
  └──────────────────────────────────┘     └──────────────────────────┘

  Step 1: kubectl cordon worker-1
  ┌─── worker-1 (🚫 SchedulingDisabled) ──┐
  │  📦 easypj-abc  📦 redis-xyz  (stay)  │  ← existing pods remain
  │  📦 easypj-def                (stay)  │  ← new pods blocked
  └────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Step 2: kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets
  ┌─── worker-1 (EMPTY) ────┐     ┌─────── worker-2 ────────────┐
  │                          │     │  📦 easypj-ghi (existing)   │
  │  (all pods evicted)      │     │  📦 easypj-abc (migrated)   │
  │                          │     │  📦 easypj-def (migrated)   │
  │  🔧 Maintenance time!   │     │  📦 redis-xyz  (migrated)   │
  └──────────────────────────┘     └──────────────────────────────┘

  Step 3-4: Maintenance → uncordon
  ┌─── worker-1 (✅ Ready) ──┐
  │  Now accepts new pods!    │
  └───────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Drain Gotchas</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> is required if DaemonSet pods exist (they can't be evicted)</li>
                        <li><code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> is needed if pods use <code>emptyDir</code> volumes</li>
                        <li><code>--force</code> only if there are standalone pods (not managed by RS/Deployment) — data will be lost!</li>
                        <li>Without these flags, drain will refuse and show an error — know which flag to add!</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>What <code>drain</code> Actually Calls — the Eviction API, Not a Plain Delete</h4>
                <p><code>kubectl drain</code> doesn't <code>kubectl delete</code> each pod directly. It calls the dedicated <strong>Eviction API</strong> (a <code>POST</code> to <code>/api/v1/namespaces/&lt;ns&gt;/pods/&lt;pod&gt;/eviction</code>) for every pod, one at a time — and that API is exactly where PodDisruptionBudget enforcement lives.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">delete vs evict — the difference that makes drain "safe"</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   kubectl delete pod easypj-abc          kubectl drain worker-1 (per pod, internally)
        │                                       │
        ▼                                       ▼
   apiserver deletes the pod            POST .../pods/easypj-abc/eviction
   IMMEDIATELY — no PDB check                  │
   (a ReplicaSet just creates a         apiserver checks: would this eviction
   replacement; nothing protected)      violate ANY matching PodDisruptionBudget?
                                                │
                                     ┌──────────┴──────────┐
                                     ▼                      ▼
                              NO → 200 OK,           YES → 429 Too Many Requests
                              pod is evicted          eviction REFUSED, pod stays
                              normally (SIGTERM,       — drain retries later,
                              grace period, etc.)       waits for room to open up
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Command</th><th>Honors PodDisruptionBudget?</th><th>When to use</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl delete pod</code></td><td>❌ No — always succeeds</td><td>you genuinely want this exact pod gone right now (debugging, forcing a restart)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl drain</code> (Eviction API)</td><td>✅ Yes — blocks/retries if it would violate a PDB</td><td>planned node maintenance where you must not drop below the app's minimum availability</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Preview what a drain WOULD do before committing</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl drain worker-1 --dry-run=client --ignore-daemonsets</code> lists exactly which pods would be evicted without actually evicting anything — a low-risk way to double-check the blast radius (especially which bare/unmanaged pods would need <code>--force</code>) before running the real command against a production node.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ A crash-looping pod can make a PDB block <code>drain</code> forever</h5>
                    <p>If an unhealthy (e.g. CrashLoopBackOff) pod still counts toward a PDB's <code>minAvailable</code>, the Eviction API can refuse to evict it — and everything else on that node — indefinitely, since evicting it would (on paper) violate the budget even though the pod isn't actually serving traffic. Since Kubernetes 1.26, setting <code>unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy: AlwaysAllow</code> on the PDB fixes this: it lets drain evict unhealthy pods regardless of the budget, while still protecting genuinely healthy ones. This is the official recommended setting for exactly this reason.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Letting drain evict unhealthy pods regardless of budget</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata: {name: easypj-pdb}
spec:
  minAvailable: 2
  selector: {matchLabels: {app: easypj}}
  unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy: AlwaysAllow   <span class="comment"># recommended — don't let a crash-looping pod block drain forever</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 A pod pinned with <code>nodeName</code> ignores cordon AND drain protection entirely</h5>
                    <p>§14.1 covered <code>nodeName</code> bypassing the scheduler. That same bypass means a pod created with <code>spec.nodeName</code> set directly will still land on a node you've JUST cordoned — cordon only stops the scheduler from placing new pods there, but a <code>nodeName</code>-pinned pod never goes through the scheduler at all. If you're debugging "why did a pod land on the node I just drained," a hardcoded <code>nodeName</code> is a real, if uncommon, root cause.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.7 Topology Spread Constraints</h3>
                <p>Topology spread constraints give you fine-grained control over how pods are distributed across topology domains (zones, nodes, regions). Unlike pod anti-affinity which is "avoid same node", topology spread says "distribute <strong>evenly</strong>".</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-topology-spread.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 6
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      topologySpreadConstraints:
      - maxSkew: 1                           <span class="comment"># Max diff in pod count between zones</span>
        topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone  <span class="comment"># Spread across zones</span>
        whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule     <span class="comment"># Hard constraint</span>
        labelSelector:
          matchLabels:
            app: easypj
      - maxSkew: 1
        topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname   <span class="comment"># Also spread across nodes</span>
        whenUnsatisfiable: ScheduleAnyway    <span class="comment"># Soft constraint</span>
        labelSelector:
          matchLabels:
            app: easypj
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Topology Spread — Even Distribution Across Zones</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  6 replicas, maxSkew: 1, topologyKey: zone

  ┌───── us-east-1a ──────┐  ┌───── us-east-1b ──────┐  ┌───── us-east-1c ──────┐
  │  📦 easypj-1           │  │  📦 easypj-3           │  │  📦 easypj-5           │
  │  📦 easypj-2           │  │  📦 easypj-4           │  │  📦 easypj-6           │
  │  Count: 2              │  │  Count: 2              │  │  Count: 2              │
  └────────────────────────┘  └────────────────────────┘  └────────────────────────┘

  maxSkew = max(count) - min(count) = 2 - 2 = 0  ✅ (within maxSkew of 1)

  ❌ Without topology spread (all could land on one zone):
  ┌───── us-east-1a ──────┐  ┌───── us-east-1b ──────┐  ┌───── us-east-1c ──────┐
  │  📦📦📦📦📦📦           │  │  (empty)               │  │  (empty)               │
  │  Count: 6   ← BAD!    │  │  Count: 0              │  │  Count: 0              │
  └────────────────────────┘  └────────────────────────┘  └────────────────────────┘
  Zone failure = total outage! 💀
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Topology Spread Parameters</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📏</span> maxSkew</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Maximum allowed difference in pod counts between topology domains. Lower = more even.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗺️</span> topologyKey</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Node label to group by: <code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code> (node), <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code> (zone)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🚫</span> whenUnsatisfiable</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>DoNotSchedule</code> (hard — pod stays Pending) or <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> (soft — best effort)</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Multiple Constraints Combine with AND, Not OR</h4>
                <p>The YAML earlier in this section lists TWO <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> entries — one per zone, one per node. A pod must satisfy <strong>every listed constraint simultaneously</strong>; the scheduler does not treat them as alternatives.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Zone-spread AND node-spread, both enforced at once</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   Constraint 1: maxSkew=1, topologyKey=zone       (spread evenly across 3 zones)
   Constraint 2: maxSkew=1, topologyKey=hostname   (spread evenly across nodes WITHIN each zone)

   A candidate node is only viable if placing a pod there keeps BOTH skews ≤1:
     ✅ zone still balanced AFTER this placement, AND node still balanced too
     ❌ if EITHER constraint would be violated, that node is filtered out — not
        "pick whichever constraint is easier to satisfy"

   This is how you get TRUE even distribution: not just "3 zones" but
   "3 zones, and within each zone, spread across that zone's own nodes too."
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>minDomains</code> — requiring a MINIMUM number of zones before spreading even starts</h5>
                    <p>A newer field, <code>minDomains</code>, tells the scheduler "don't consider the spread satisfied unless at least N distinct topology domains are actually in use" — useful when you want to force replicas across, say, at least 3 zones even if maxSkew math alone would technically allow all of them to land in just 1 or 2. Combined with <code>whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule</code>, this guarantees a genuine minimum spread rather than just a balanced-but-possibly-single-zone outcome.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Newer field</th><th>Solves</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>matchLabelKeys</code></td><td>during a rolling update, the OLD and NEW ReplicaSet's pods share the same <code>app</code> label but different <code>pod-template-hash</code> — without this field they get lumped into the same skew calculation, causing spurious skew violations mid-rollout. Setting <code>matchLabelKeys: [pod-template-hash]</code> makes the spread calculation consider only pods from the CURRENT revision</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>nodeAffinityPolicy</code> / <code>nodeTaintsPolicy</code></td><td>(GA in v1.33) controls whether nodes excluded by a pod's own affinity rules or taints still count as an available "domain" in the skew math — <code>Honor</code> (default, excludes them) vs <code>Ignore</code> (counts them anyway)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.8 Priority and Preemption</h3>
                <p>PriorityClasses let you assign importance levels to pods. When the cluster is full, a high-priority pod can <strong>preempt</strong> (evict) a lower-priority pod to make room.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">priority-classes.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># High priority — critical production workloads</span>
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: high-priority
value: 1000000
globalDefault: false
preemptionPolicy: PreemptLowerPriority
description: "Critical production workloads like easypj"
---
<span class="comment"># Low priority — batch jobs, can be evicted</span>
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: low-priority
value: 100
globalDefault: false
preemptionPolicy: Never          <span class="comment"># Don't preempt others</span>
description: "Batch jobs and background tasks"
---
<span class="comment"># Usage in Deployment</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      priorityClassName: high-priority   # ← assign priority
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Preemption — High Priority Pod Evicts Low Priority</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Cluster is FULL — no free capacity

  ┌──── worker-1 ─────┐  ┌──── worker-2 ─────┐
  │ 📦 batch-job-1     │  │ 📦 batch-job-3     │
  │ (low-priority:100) │  │ (low-priority:100) │
  │ 📦 batch-job-2     │  │ 📦 batch-job-4     │
  │ (low-priority:100) │  │ (low-priority:100) │
  └────────────────────┘  └────────────────────┘

  NEW POD: easypj (high-priority: 1000000) needs scheduling!

  Scheduler: "No room, but easypj has higher priority..."
  → PREEMPTS batch-job-1 on worker-1

  ┌──── worker-1 ─────┐  ┌──── worker-2 ─────┐
  │ 🌟 easypj          │  │ 📦 batch-job-3     │
  │ (high-priority!)   │  │ (low-priority:100) │
  │ 📦 batch-job-2     │  │ 📦 batch-job-4     │
  │ (low-priority:100) │  │ (low-priority:100) │
  └────────────────────┘  └────────────────────┘

  batch-job-1 was evicted to make room for easypj  ⚡
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 When to Use PriorityClasses</h5>
                    <p>Use <strong>high priority</strong> for production-critical workloads (web servers, APIs). Use <strong>low priority</strong> for batch jobs, CI/CD runners, dev environments. This ensures production always gets resources first.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Scheduling Mechanisms Comparison — When to Use What</h5>
                    <div class="comparison-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Mechanism</th>
                                    <th>Controls</th>
                                    <th>Direction</th>
                                    <th>Best For</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>nodeSelector</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod → Node</td>
                                    <td>🟢 Attract (hard)</td>
                                    <td>Simple label matching</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Node Affinity</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod → Node</td>
                                    <td>🟢 Attract (hard/soft)</td>
                                    <td>Complex rules, preferred placement</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Pod Affinity</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod → Pod</td>
                                    <td>🟢 Attract (co-locate)</td>
                                    <td>Low latency (app near cache)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Pod Anti-Affinity</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod → Pod</td>
                                    <td>🔴 Repel (spread)</td>
                                    <td>HA (replicas on different nodes)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Taints/Tolerations</strong></td>
                                    <td>Node → Pod</td>
                                    <td>🔴 Repel + allow</td>
                                    <td>Dedicated nodes, maintenance</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Topology Spread</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod distribution</td>
                                    <td>⚖️ Even spread</td>
                                    <td>Balance across zones/nodes</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Static Pods</strong></td>
                                    <td>Fixed to node</td>
                                    <td>📌 Pinned</td>
                                    <td>Control plane components</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>PriorityClass</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod importance</td>
                                    <td>⬆️ Preempt lower</td>
                                    <td>Critical vs batch workloads</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Built-in System PriorityClasses — Reserved Names You Shouldn't Squat On</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes ships two PriorityClasses with values far above anything a normal workload should use, protecting the control plane and per-node infrastructure from ever being preempted by application pods.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>PriorityClass</th><th>Value</th><th>Used by</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>system-cluster-critical</code></td><td>2,000,000,000</td><td>cluster-wide critical add-ons (e.g. CoreDNS)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>system-node-critical</code></td><td>2,000,001,000</td><td>per-node critical daemons (e.g. kube-proxy, the CNI plugin) — even higher, because losing these breaks the node itself</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Don't hand-create a PriorityClass with a value anywhere near these</h5>
                    <p>Application PriorityClasses (like this section's <code>high-priority</code> at 1,000,000) should stay well below the billion range reserved for system components. If your "critical" workload's value creeps up toward <code>system-cluster-critical</code>'s territory, you risk it preempting cluster infrastructure pods during a genuine capacity crunch — exactly the outage scenario priority was supposed to prevent.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 PodDisruptionBudgets during preemption are honored on a best-effort basis, not guaranteed</h5>
                    <p>The scheduler tries to avoid preempting pods that would violate a PDB, but if EVERY eviction candidate on a node is PDB-protected, it will still preempt one anyway rather than leave a high-priority pod permanently unscheduled. This is a deliberate design trade-off: priority ultimately outranks availability guarantees when they conflict.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Legal Value Range, and Why PriorityClass Values Need Guarding</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Legal <code>value</code> range for user-defined classes</td><td>any 32-bit integer from <strong>-2,147,483,648 to 1,000,000,000</strong> — values above 1 billion are reserved for system classes like the two above</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Negative values are legal</td><td>useful for marking truly disposable/best-effort work explicitly BELOW the implicit default of 0 (unset <code>priorityClassName</code> = priority 0)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 A real cluster-security concern: priority abuse</h5>
                    <p>Any user who can create pods and reference a high-value PriorityClass can cause OTHER pods to be preempted — a malicious or careless user creating pods at the highest allowed priority could evict legitimate workloads cluster-wide. The official mitigation: use a <strong>ResourceQuota scoped to a specific PriorityClass</strong> (via <code>scopeSelector</code>, Chapter 15 §15.4) to cap how many pods — or how much aggregate resource — a namespace may create at any given priority tier, preventing exactly this abuse.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.9 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-9">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.9 Practice Exercises</h3>
                <p>These exercises mirror actual CKA exam questions. Practice each one until you can complete it in under 5 minutes.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏋️</div>
