            <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> "the scheduler" is really a plugin host — nearly every behavior in this chapter (nodeSelector, affinity, taints, topology spread, even resource fit) is implemented as a small plugin registered at one of eleven well-defined extension points, not hard-coded logic. That's what makes custom schedulers and scheduler plugins possible without forking Kubernetes itself.</p>

            <h5>① The Scheduling Framework — the Real Pipeline Behind "Filter → Score → Bind"</h5>
            <p>The 3-phase model earlier in this UTH (filtering → scoring → binding) is the textbook simplification. The actual <strong>Scheduling Framework</strong> defines eleven ordered extension points, and every mechanism in this chapter — nodeSelector, affinity, taints, topology spread — is a plugin registered at one or more of them.</p>
