            <h5>② Preemption Internals — Victim Selection and the "Nominated Node"</h5>
            <p>§14.8 says preemption "evicts a lower-priority pod," but WHICH one, out of possibly many candidates? The algorithm specifically tries to preempt the FEWEST, LOWEST-priority pods necessary — not just the first one it finds.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   PostFilter runs (Filter eliminated every node) for a high-priority pending pod:

   1. For each node, simulate: "if I removed every lower-priority pod here,
      would the pending pod now fit?"
   2. Among nodes where the answer is YES, pick the node requiring the
      FEWEST pod evictions (minimize disruption)
   3. Among the pods on that node, evict only the MINIMUM set needed to
      free enough room — not the whole node
   4. The pending pod's status gets .status.nominatedNodeName set to that
      node BEFORE the victims are even deleted
   5. Victims receive a normal graceful termination (SIGTERM + grace period)
      — preemption does NOT force-kill instantly
   6. Once victims are gone, the NEXT scheduling cycle actually binds the
      pending pod — nomination is a hint, not a guarantee (another pod
      could theoretically win the freed space first)
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field / concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>status.nominatedNodeName</code></td><td>set on the PREEMPTING pod once a victim node is chosen — visible via <code>kubectl get pod -o yaml</code>, useful for confirming preemption actually decided something before the delete finishes</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Graceful eviction of victims</td><td>preempted pods still get SIGTERM + their normal <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> — preemption is not a force-kill, which is part of why it can take a few seconds to actually free the node</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Minimal disruption</td><td>the algorithm actively avoids evicting MORE pods or HIGHER-priority pods than strictly necessary — it never preempts a pod at or above the pending pod's own priority</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if a preemption "isn't working," check whether <code>preemptionPolicy: Never</code> is set on the pending pod's PriorityClass (§14.8) — that pod is allowed to wait patiently but will never trigger PostFilter's eviction logic at all, by design.</div>

            <h5>③ The Descheduler — Why the Scheduler Never Moves an Already-Running Pod</h5>
            <p>Here's a gap every one of this chapter's mechanisms shares: <code>nodeAffinity</code>, taints, and topology spread are all "IgnoredDuringExecution" (§14.3's warning) — they only apply at scheduling time. If cluster conditions change afterward (new nodes join, labels change, a topology spread skew drifts), the scheduler itself does <strong>nothing</strong> to already-running pods. A separate, optional component called the <strong>Descheduler</strong> exists specifically to close this gap.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Day 0: easypj pods perfectly spread across 3 nodes (topology spread satisfied)
   Day 5: 2 new nodes join the cluster — the ORIGINAL spread is now stale/uneven
          (the scheduler placed those pods correctly ON Day 0 — it has no reason
           to revisit that decision on its own)

   The Descheduler (run as a CronJob or Deployment, NOT part of core Kubernetes):
     1. periodically evaluates CURRENT policy against CURRENTLY RUNNING pods
     2. finds violations: "these 3 pods now violate the topology spread we WANT"
     3. EVICTS those pods (via the same Eviction API §14.6 covers)
     4. the normal scheduler then re-places them — now correctly, given the
        new node count
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The scheduler PLACES; the descheduler RE-BALANCES.</strong> They're deliberately separate components with separate responsibilities — this keeps the scheduler's own decision fast and side-effect-free (it never has to second-guess or undo a running pod's placement), while rebalancing becomes an explicit, auditable, separately-scheduled operation you opt into.</div>

            <h5>④ RuntimeClass &amp; Pod Overhead — Accounting for the Runtime's Own Cost</h5>
            <p>Every filter in this UTH's PHASE 1 diagram assumes a pod's resource footprint is exactly what its containers request. That's not quite true for a <strong>sandboxed</strong> runtime (like gVisor or Kata Containers, run via <code>RuntimeClass</code>) — the sandbox itself consumes real CPU/memory the scheduler must also account for, or it will overcommit the node.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Pod requests: 500m CPU, 512Mi memory (the containers' own ask)
   RuntimeClass "gvisor-sandboxed" declares overhead:
     podFixed: { cpu: "100m", memory: "128Mi" }   ← the SANDBOX's own cost

   What NodeResourcesFit actually checks:
     total reservation = pod requests + RuntimeClass overhead
                        = (500m + 100m) CPU, (512Mi + 128Mi) memory
                        = 600m CPU, 640Mi memory  ← THIS is what must fit the node

   Skip this and a sandboxed-runtime-heavy node can silently overcommit —
   the containers' own requests fit fine, but the sandboxes' combined
   overhead doesn't, leading to real memory pressure the scheduler never saw coming.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Not on the core CKA syllabus, but explains a real production surprise:</strong> if you ever see a node under memory pressure despite <code>kubectl describe node</code> showing requests comfortably under capacity, and that node runs a sandboxed/gVisor workload, Pod Overhead accounting (or the lack of a correctly-configured RuntimeClass) is a real, if uncommon, root cause worth ruling out.</div>

            <h5>⑤ The Scheduler's Assumed-Pod Cache — Why Binding Doesn't Race Itself</h5>
            <p>The scheduler processes ONE pod at a time through Filter/Score, but binding (writing <code>nodeName</code> + creating the actual API object) is asynchronous — it doesn't block the NEXT pod's scheduling cycle. Without a safeguard, two pods scheduled back-to-back could both "see" the same free resources and both get placed on a node that can only actually fit one.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Pod A finishes Filter/Score → wins node-3 → scheduler ASSUMES the bind will
   succeed and immediately updates its LOCAL cache: "node-3 now has Pod A's
   resources reserved" — even though the real Bind API call to etcd hasn't
   necessarily completed yet.

   Pod B (scheduled microseconds later) reads node-3's AVAILABLE capacity from
   that SAME local cache — which already reflects Pod A's assumed reservation
   — so Pod B correctly sees less room and is filtered/scored accordingly.

   If the real Bind call later actually FAILS (rare — a race with someone
   else, or an API error), the assumption is rolled back and node-3's
   capacity in the cache is corrected.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is the same "read from a fast local cache, not etcd" pattern as Chapter 1's informer/list-watch UTH — reused here so the scheduler can process pods rapidly (thousands per second at scale) without a slow round-trip to etcd blocking every single decision.</strong></div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Scheduler = FILTER (can it run here?) → SCORE (best fit?) → BIND (assign).</strong> A pod with no matching node stays <code>Pending</code> forever with a <code>FailedScheduling</code> event.</li>
                    <li><strong>Placement levers escalate in strength:</strong> <code>nodeSelector</code> &lt; <code>nodeAffinity</code> &lt; taints/tolerations &lt; topology spread.</li>
                    <li><strong>nodeSelector/affinity ATTRACT</strong> pods to nodes; <strong>taints REPEL</strong>; <strong>tolerations merely PERMIT</strong> — they never attract on their own.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>required*</code> affinity = hard</strong> (Pending if unmet); <strong><code>preferred*</code> = soft</strong> (weighted, falls back). <code>IgnoredDuringExecution</code> means the rule is only enforced at scheduling time — a later label change never evicts a running pod.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pod anti-affinity / topology spread = HA</strong> by spreading replicas; <strong>pod affinity = co-locate</strong> for latency (e.g. app next to its cache).</li>
                    <li><strong>Node maintenance:</strong> cordon (stop new) → drain (evict existing, PDB-honored) → maintain → uncordon. Forgetting to uncordon leaves a healthy node idle.</li>
                    <li><strong>Control-plane nodes carry <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code></strong> by default — that's why ordinary pods never land there.</li>
                    <li><strong>PriorityClass ranks pods</strong>; a higher-value pod can preempt (evict) strictly lower-priority pods when the cluster is full.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>nodeSelector vs nodeAffinity</strong> — <code>nodeSelector</code> is simple key=value matching. <code>nodeAffinity</code> supports <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>DoesNotExist</code>. The exam tests both</li>
                    <li><strong>Taint effect matters</strong> — <code>NoSchedule</code> = new pods blocked. <code>NoExecute</code> = existing pods EVICTED. <code>PreferNoSchedule</code> = soft preference. Mixing them up = wrong answer</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl drain</code> flags</strong> — Without <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> it fails on DaemonSet pods. Without <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> it fails on emptyDir volumes. Always use both</li>
                    <li><strong>Cordon ≠ Drain</strong> — <code>cordon</code> only prevents NEW pods. <code>drain</code> evicts existing pods AND cordons. After maintenance: <code>uncordon</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Toleration must EXACTLY match taint</strong> — key, value, AND effect must match. A toleration with the wrong effect won't work</li>
                    <li><strong>"Why is my pod Pending" has more causes than just taints/affinity</strong> — also check: insufficient cpu/memory on every node, a required nodeAffinity/nodeSelector matching no node, required podAntiAffinity with more replicas than nodes, topology spread with all domains full, an unbound PVC or volume-node affinity mismatch, and <code>spec.schedulerName</code> naming a scheduler that isn't running</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

