                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the scheduler's three phases in order, and what question each one answers.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Filtering</strong> — "which nodes CAN run this pod?" (a yes/no predicate). <strong>Scoring</strong> — "of those, which is the BEST fit?" (a 0-100 rank). <strong>Binding</strong> — write the winning node into the pod so the kubelet starts it.</p>
<pre>
  Filtering  ──▶  Scoring  ──▶  Binding
  "CAN it run       "which is        pod.spec.nodeName
   here?" (yes/no)   BEST?" (0-100)  is written; kubelet starts it
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Older docs call filtering "predicates" and scoring "priorities" — same pipeline, different era's terminology. If NO node survives filtering, the pod stays <code>Pending</code> and a <code>FailedScheduling</code> event names the exact reason.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Rank <code>nodeSelector</code>, <code>nodeAffinity</code>, taints/tolerations, and topology spread from weakest to strongest "pull" on placement.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>nodeSelector</code> &lt; <code>nodeAffinity</code> &lt; taints/tolerations &lt; topology spread.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Direction</th><th>Strength</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>nodeSelector</code></td><td>Attract</td><td>weakest — exact match only</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>nodeAffinity</code></td><td>Attract</td><td>hard or soft, richer operators</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Taints/Tolerations</td><td>Repel + permit</td><td>opt-in only, actively excludes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Topology Spread</td><td>Even distribution</td><td>strongest — enforces balance, not just placement</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>nodeSelector/affinity <em>attract</em> pods toward nodes (a suggestion the scheduler can satisfy from many candidates); taints actively <em>repel</em> everything except pods with a matching toleration — a fundamentally stronger, opt-in-only mechanism.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does "IgnoredDuringExecution" mean in <code>requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The rule is enforced only at scheduling time. If a node's label changes AFTER a pod is already running there, the pod is not evicted — the affinity rule is simply never re-checked.</p>
<pre>
  T0: pod scheduled — node HAS matching label   → rule satisfied ✅
  T1: label REMOVED from the node               → rule now violated
  T1: pod status:                                  still Running, NOT evicted
      (only re-checked at scheduling time — "IgnoredDuringExecution")
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every affinity rule in current Kubernetes uses this suffix — there is no built-in "evict if the label changes" behavior. Only a <code>NoExecute</code> taint actively evicts running pods.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment uses REQUIRED pod anti-affinity with <code>topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname</code> and has 5 replicas but only 3 nodes. What happens?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>3 pods schedule (one per node); the other 2 stay <code>Pending</code> forever — required anti-affinity with a per-host topology key caps replicas at the node count.</p>
<pre>
  3 nodes, 5 replicas, REQUIRED anti-affinity (kubernetes.io/hostname):

  worker-1: [pod-1] ✅   worker-2: [pod-2] ✅   worker-3: [pod-3] ✅
  pod-4: ❌ Pending (no 4th node — required anti-affinity forbids co-location)
  pod-5: ❌ Pending (same reason)
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a classic exam trap. Use <code>preferred</code> anti-affinity for elastic HA that never blocks scheduling, or switch to topology spread constraints (§14.7) which are designed for "spread evenly" rather than "never together."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the three taint effects, and which one is the only one that evicts already-running pods?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>NoSchedule</code> (block new pods only), <code>PreferNoSchedule</code> (soft, avoid if possible), <code>NoExecute</code> (blocks new AND evicts existing non-tolerating pods).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Effect</th><th>New pods</th><th>Running pods</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>NoSchedule</code></td><td>blocked</td><td>untouched</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>PreferNoSchedule</code></td><td>soft-avoided</td><td>untouched</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>NoExecute</code></td><td>blocked</td><td><strong>evicted</strong></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A toleration for a <code>NoExecute</code> taint can add <code>tolerationSeconds</code> to ride out a brief disruption before eviction — this is exactly how every pod tolerates <code>node.kubernetes.io/not-ready</code> for 300s by default, which is why pods don't vanish the instant a node blips.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Does a toleration ever cause a pod to prefer a tainted node over an untainted one?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No. A toleration only PERMITS scheduling onto a tainted node — it never attracts. To both keep a node exclusive AND pull specific pods onto it, you must combine a taint (repel everyone else) with node affinity (pull the right pods in).</p>
<pre>
  toleration ALONE:      "I CAN land there, but might land anywhere else too"
  toleration + affinity: "I CAN land there, AND I'm actively pulled there"
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a very common misconception — candidates assume a toleration works like affinity. It doesn't; without an affinity rule too, a tolerating pod could just as easily land on an untainted node instead.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between <code>cordon</code> and <code>drain</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>cordon</code> only marks the node unschedulable — existing pods keep running. <code>drain</code> cordons AND evicts every existing pod so they reschedule elsewhere.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Blocks new pods?</th><th>Evicts existing pods?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>kubectl cordon</code></td><td>✅ yes</td><td>❌ no</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kubectl drain</code></td><td>✅ yes (cordons first)</td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>For a reboot or OS patch you want <code>drain</code>, not just <code>cordon</code> — cordon alone leaves the very pods you're trying to protect sitting on the node you're about to take down.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does a PodDisruptionBudget make <code>kubectl drain</code> safe?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>drain</code> honors a PDB's <code>minAvailable</code>/<code>maxUnavailable</code>: it evicts pods gradually and BLOCKS rather than ever dropping the app below its budget.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata: {name: easypj-pdb}
spec:
  minAvailable: 2        <span class="comment"># drain BLOCKS once evicting the next pod would drop below 2</span>
  selector: {matchLabels: {app: easypj}}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Without a PDB, drain can evict all replicas of an app on that node at once — if that node happens to host most of your replicas, that's an outage. A PDB is what turns "drain" from a blunt instrument into a safe, budget-aware operation.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>maxSkew: 1</code> mean for a topology spread constraint with 6 replicas across 3 zones?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The pod count in any two zones can differ by at most 1 — with 6 replicas over 3 zones that works out to exactly 2 per zone.</p>
<pre>
  6 replicas, maxSkew: 1, 3 zones:
  zone-a: 2 pods   zone-b: 2 pods   zone-c: 2 pods
  max(2,2,2) - min(2,2,2) = 0  ≤ maxSkew 1  ✅
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If a zone is already full and <code>whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule</code> is set, surplus pods stay Pending rather than violate the skew — switch to <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> if you'd rather have a best-effort spread than a blocked rollout.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Two pods have <code>priorityClassName: easypj-high</code> (value 1000000) and <code>priorityClassName: batch-low</code> (value 100). The cluster is full. What can the scheduler do, and what can't it do?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It CAN preempt (evict) the <code>batch-low</code> pod to make room for the <code>easypj-high</code> pod, because 1000000 &gt; 100 — strictly lower priority. It CANNOT preempt another pod that is equal or higher priority.</p>
<pre>
  easypj-high (1000000)  vs  batch-low (100)     → 1000000 &gt; 100 → CAN preempt ✅
  easypj-high (1000000)  vs  another-high (1000000) → equal        → CANNOT preempt ❌
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Preemption also respects PodDisruptionBudgets on a best-effort basis, and <code>globalDefault: true</code> on a PriorityClass makes it apply automatically to any pod that names no <code>priorityClassName</code> at all.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
