            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 14 Visual Summary — a pod's path from unscheduled to a chosen node</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   easypj pod created (nodeName="") ──▶ FILTER (drop nodes too small/wrong labels/untolerated taints)
        ──▶ SCORE (rank survivors: headroom + affinity + spread) ──▶ BIND (write winning node) ──▶ 🤖 kubelet runs it
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ADMIN LEVERS (weakest → strongest): nodeSelector < nodeAffinity < taints/tolerations < topology spread
   MAINTENANCE: cordon (stop new) → drain (evict, PDB-honored) → work → uncordon
   FULL CLUSTER: PriorityClass — higher value can PREEMPT strictly-lower-priority pods              ☸️
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> every pod starts life unscheduled, and the kube-scheduler's job is to filter out every node that physically can't host it, score the survivors on fit and preference, then bind the winner. As an admin you bias every step: <code>nodeSelector</code>/<code>nodeAffinity</code> attract pods toward the right nodes, pod (anti-)affinity and topology spread co-locate or spread replicas, and taints/tolerations fence pods off nodes entirely (repel, never attract).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> to service hardware safely you cordon a node (stop new pods), drain it (evict existing pods, protected by a PodDisruptionBudget), do the work, then uncordon to resume scheduling. And when the cluster is genuinely full, PriorityClass decides who gets to stay — preemption only ever evicts strictly lower-priority pods.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch15">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 15 · Resource Management</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Every container in Kubernetes can request a minimum amount of CPU and memory, and be limited to a maximum. Understanding requests, limits, QoS classes, LimitRanges, and ResourceQuotas is critical for <strong>cluster stability</strong> — and prominently tested on the CKA exam. A misconfigured resource setting can crash an entire node.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📊 Resources</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Set CPU and memory requests (scheduling guarantee) and limits (hard ceiling)</li>
                    <li>✅ Understand QoS classes — Guaranteed, Burstable, BestEffort</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure LimitRange for namespace-wide default limits</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure ResourceQuota to cap total namespace consumption</li>
                    <li>✅ Know what happens when CPU is exceeded (throttle) vs memory (OOMKilled)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

