            <!-- ─────────── §15.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.1 Resource Requests and Limits</h3>
                <p>Every container can specify <strong>requests</strong> (minimum guaranteed) and <strong>limits</strong> (maximum allowed). The scheduler uses requests to decide where to place pods. The kubelet enforces limits at runtime.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy: Hotel Reservation vs Fire-Code Occupancy</h5>
                    <p>A <strong>request</strong> is your hotel reservation — the room is held for you even if you arrive late. A <strong>limit</strong> is the room's fire-code occupancy: cross it for memory and security throws you out (OOMKilled); cross it for CPU and they just slow the elevator (throttled).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Requests</h5>
                        <p><strong>"I need at least this much."</strong> Used by the scheduler for placement. The node must have this amount free or the pod won't be scheduled.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚧</div>
                        <h5>Limits</h5>
                        <p><strong>"Don't use more than this."</strong> Enforced by the kubelet. CPU exceeds limit → throttled. Memory exceeds limit → OOMKilled.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-resources.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:v1
    resources:
      requests:                  <span class="comment"># Scheduling guarantee</span>
        cpu: "250m"              <span class="comment"># 0.25 CPU cores (250 millicores)</span>
        memory: "256Mi"          <span class="comment"># 256 MiB RAM</span>
      limits:                    <span class="comment"># Hard ceiling</span>
        cpu: "500m"              <span class="comment"># 0.5 CPU cores max</span>
        memory: "512Mi"          <span class="comment"># 512 MiB RAM max</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>CPU &amp; Memory Units</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚙️</span> CPU: 1000m = 1 core</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>100m</code> = 0.1 core, <code>250m</code> = 0.25 core, <code>1</code> = 1 full core, <code>2</code> = 2 cores</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💾</span> Memory: Mi, Gi</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>128Mi</code> = 128 MiB, <code>1Gi</code> = 1 GiB. Use <strong>Mi/Gi</strong> (binary) not MB/GB (decimal).</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⏳</span> CPU exceeded</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Throttled</strong> — process slows down but keeps running. Not killed.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> Memory exceeded</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>OOMKilled</strong> — the kernel terminates the container once it detects the memory-pressure overage (reactive, not instantaneous — a brief transient spike may not be caught immediately). Restarts per restartPolicy.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>⏳ CPU Exceeded → Throttled</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Container is <strong>NOT killed</strong></li>
                            <li>Process slows down, capped at the limit</li>
                            <li>No restarts, no OOM events</li>
                            <li>Hard to detect — app just becomes slow</li>
                            <li>Diagnose: <code>kubectl top pod</code> shows CPU pinned at limit</li>
                            <li><strong>Fix:</strong> Increase <code>limits.cpu</code> or optimize app</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>💀 Memory Exceeded → OOMKilled</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Container is killed by the kernel <strong>once memory pressure is detected</strong> (SIGKILL) — reactive, not a guaranteed-instant hard cap</li>
                            <li>Exit code <strong>137</strong> in pod description</li>
                            <li>Pod restarts → often leads to CrashLoopBackOff</li>
                            <li>Easy to detect: status shows OOMKilled</li>
                            <li>Diagnose: <code>kubectl describe pod</code> → Last State → OOMKilled</li>
                            <li><strong>Fix:</strong> Increase <code>limits.memory</code> or fix memory leak</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Node Capacity: 4 CPU, 8Gi Memory
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────── easypj container ─────────────┐                  │
  │  │                                            │                  │
  │  │  CPU:    [====request 250m====|...limit 500m....]             │
  │  │          ├─── guaranteed ────┤├── can burst ──┤               │
  │  │                                            │                  │
  │  │  Memory: [====request 256Mi===|...limit 512Mi...]             │
  │  │          ├─── guaranteed ────┤├── can use ───┤                │
  │  │                                   ↑ exceed = OOMKill 💀      │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────┘                  │
  │                                                                  │
  │  What happens:                                                   │
  │  ├── Actual CPU  = 300m  → ✅ Within limit, no throttle         │
  │  ├── Actual CPU  = 600m  → ⏳ Throttled to 500m                 │
  │  ├── Actual Mem  = 400Mi → ✅ Within limit, fine                │
  │  └── Actual Mem  = 600Mi → 💀 OOMKilled! Container restarts    │
  │                                                                  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Never Skip Resource Settings in Production</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>No requests</strong>: Scheduler doesn't know what the pod needs → random placement → resource contention</li>
                        <li><strong>No limits</strong>: Pod can consume ALL available node memory → OOMs other pods → cascading failure</li>
                        <li><strong>Always set both</strong> on every production container!</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Monitoring Resource Usage</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check actual usage vs requests/limits</span>
kubectl top pods
<span class="comment"># NAME      CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj   120m         180Mi</span>

kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       CPU(cores)   CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)   MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   850m         42%    3200Mi           40%</span>

<span class="comment"># Find pods with no resource limits (dangerous!)</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq '.items[] | 
  select(.spec.containers[].resources.limits == null) | 
  .metadata.name'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Troubleshooting OOMKilled Pods</h4>
                <p>One of the most common issues on the CKA and in production. Here's a systematic approach:</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Find OOMKilled Pods</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pods -A | grep OOMKilled</code> or <code>grep CrashLoopBackOff</code> (often caused by repeated OOM)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Confirm OOM Reason</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe pod NAME</code> → look for <code>Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Check Current Limits</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pod NAME -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}'</code> → see current memory limit</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Check Actual Usage</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl top pod NAME</code> → if usage is close to limit, the limit is too low</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Fix &amp; Verify</h5>
                        <p>Increase memory limit: <code>kubectl set resources deployment/NAME --limits=memory=512Mi</code>. Watch pod restart successfully.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">OOMKilled Diagnosis Steps</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find OOMKilled pods</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep OOMKilled
kubectl get pods -A | grep CrashLoopBackOff   <span class="comment"># often caused by OOM</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check the pod's last state</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-abc12 | grep -A5 "Last State"
<span class="comment">#   Last State:  Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#     Reason:    OOMKilled</span>
<span class="comment">#     Exit Code: 137       ← 137 = SIGKILL (memory limit hit)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check current resource config</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-abc12 -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}'
<span class="comment"># {"limits":{"memory":"256Mi"},"requests":{"memory":"128Mi"}}</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check actual usage (if pod is running)</span>
kubectl top pod easypj-abc12
<span class="comment"># CPU: 100m    MEMORY: 245Mi   ← close to 256Mi limit!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix — increase memory limit</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/easypj -c django \
  --limits=memory=512Mi --requests=memory=256Mi</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">OOMKill Decision Tree — Is It the Container or the Node?</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Pod terminated with Exit Code 137?
       │
       ├── YES → OOMKilled!
       │        │
       │   Was the pod using more memory
       │   than its spec.resources.limits.memory?
       │        │
       │   ┌────┴────┐
       │   YES        NO
       │   │          │
       │   Container  Node-level OOM
       │   limit hit  (node ran out of memory)
       │   │          │
       │   Fix:       Fix: add more nodes,
       │   Increase   reduce other workloads,
       │   memory     or fix memory leaks
       │   limits
       │
       └── Exit Code 1 = app error (not OOM)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>Beyond CPU/Memory — Ephemeral Storage and Extended Resources</h4>
                <p>Requests and limits aren't limited to CPU and memory. Two more resource types show up constantly in production, and both have a rule that trips people up.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Resource</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Key rule</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>ephemeral-storage</code></td><td>the writable container layer + logs + <code>emptyDir</code> volumes — scratch disk that isn't a PersistentVolume</td><td>works exactly like CPU/memory: request = scheduling floor, limit = eviction trigger if exceeded (kubelet evicts the whole pod, not just throttles)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Extended resources (e.g. <code>nvidia.com/gpu</code>, <code>hugepages-2Mi</code>)</td><td>anything a device plugin advertises — GPUs, FPGAs, huge pages — as an integer-quantity resource</td><td><strong>request MUST equal limit</strong> — you cannot "request" 1 GPU but "limit" 2; extended resources are always all-or-nothing and non-overcommittable, unlike CPU/memory</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">A container requesting ephemeral storage and a GPU</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "1Gi"
    ephemeral-storage: "2Gi"
  limits:
    cpu: "1"
    memory: "2Gi"
    ephemeral-storage: "4Gi"
    nvidia.com/gpu: 1              <span class="comment"># NOTE: no "requests.nvidia.com/gpu" needed —</span>
                                    <span class="comment"># Kubernetes copies the limit into requests automatically,</span>
                                    <span class="comment"># the same general "a limit with no matching request defaults</span>
                                    <span class="comment"># the request to that limit" rule applied to CPU/memory too</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Node-Allocatable — What "Free Capacity" Actually Means to the Scheduler</h4>
                <p>§14.1's filtering phase checks "does the node have enough free resources" — but a node's <em>capacity</em> (total physical RAM/CPU) and its <em>allocatable</em> (what pods can actually use) are different numbers, and the scheduler only ever looks at the second one.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">capacity → allocatable, the subtraction the scheduler actually uses</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   node capacity            :  8 CPU, 32Gi memory   (the physical machine)
   − kube-reserved          :  0.5 CPU, 1Gi          (reserved for kubelet + container runtime)
   − system-reserved        :  0.5 CPU, 1Gi          (reserved for OS-level daemons: sshd, journald...)
   − eviction-threshold      :  —      , 500Mi        (a memory buffer kept free to avoid a hard OOM)
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   = allocatable            :  7 CPU, 29.5Gi memory   ← THIS is what NodeResourcesFit checks against

   kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 Allocatable
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Sum of REQUESTS must fit allocatable; sum of LIMITS may exceed it — that's overcommit, by design</h5>
                    <p>The scheduler only enforces the <em>requests</em> side of this math. It's entirely normal — and often desirable for cost efficiency — for the SUM of every pod's <code>limits</code> on a node to exceed allocatable capacity, banking on the fact that not every pod bursts to its limit simultaneously. This is exactly why a node can OOM even though every individual pod's <em>request</em> fit fine at scheduling time: overcommitted limits collided under real, simultaneous load.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §15.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.2 QoS Classes — Quality of Service</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes automatically assigns a <strong>QoS class</strong> to every pod based on its resource configuration. This determines the <strong>eviction priority</strong> when a node runs out of resources.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>QoS Class</th>
                                <th>Criteria</th>
                                <th>Eviction Priority</th>
                                <th>easypj Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Guaranteed</strong></td>
                                <td>Every container has requests == limits (both CPU &amp; memory set and equal)</td>
                                <td>Last to be evicted (safest) 🛡️</td>
                                <td>✅ Production easypj</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Burstable</strong></td>
                                <td>At least one container has requests &lt; limits (or only requests set)</td>
                                <td>Evicted after BestEffort ⚠️</td>
                                <td>⚠️ Dev/staging easypj</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>BestEffort</strong></td>
                                <td>No requests or limits set on any container</td>
                                <td>First to be evicted (riskiest) 💀</td>
                                <td>❌ Never in production</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">QoS Eviction Order — Node Under Memory Pressure</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Node memory: 95% used — kubelet starts evicting pods!

  Eviction Order:
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                   │
  │  💀 FIRST: BestEffort pods                       │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ Pod: test-pod (no requests, no limits)      │ │
  │  │ → EVICTED immediately                       │ │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │          │                                        │
  │          ▼ (still need more memory?)              │
  │  ⚠️ SECOND: Burstable pods                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ Pod: dev-easypj (requests &lt; limits)         │ │
  │  │ → EVICTED if using more than requests       │ │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │          │                                        │
  │          ▼ (extreme pressure only)                │
  │  🛡️ LAST: Guaranteed pods                       │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ Pod: prod-easypj (requests == limits)       │ │
  │  │ → Only evicted as absolute last resort      │ │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">QoS Examples</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Guaranteed QoS — requests EQUAL limits</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"          <span class="comment"># same as request</span>
    memory: "512Mi"      <span class="comment"># same as request</span>
<span class="comment"># → QoS: Guaranteed ✅</span>

---
<span class="comment"># Burstable QoS — requests LESS THAN limits</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "250m"
    memory: "256Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"          <span class="comment"># more than request</span>
    memory: "512Mi"      <span class="comment"># more than request</span>
<span class="comment"># → QoS: Burstable ⚠️</span>

---
<span class="comment"># BestEffort QoS — NO resources at all</span>
<span class="comment"># (just don't include the resources section)</span>
<span class="comment"># → QoS: BestEffort 💀</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Check Pod QoS Class</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check QoS class of a pod</span>
kubectl get pod easypj -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}'
<span class="comment"># Guaranteed</span>

<span class="comment"># Check QoS of all pods</span>
kubectl get pods -o custom-columns='NAME:.metadata.name,QOS:.status.qosClass'
<span class="comment"># NAME              QOS</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-abc12      Guaranteed</span>
<span class="comment"># dev-app-xyz       Burstable</span>
<span class="comment"># test-pod          BestEffort</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production QoS Strategy for easypj</h5>
                    <p>For your production stack, aim for these QoS classes:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Guaranteed</strong>: easypj Django (production), PostgreSQL — set requests == limits</li>
                        <li><strong>Burstable</strong>: easypj staging, Redis cache — allow bursting for efficiency</li>
                        <li><strong>BestEffort</strong>: Only for throwaway test pods in dev. Never in production!</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Pro tip: Use <code>Guaranteed</code> for anything facing users. Use <code>Burstable</code> for internal services that benefit from occasional burst capacity.</p>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Nuance</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>BestEffort is CPU/memory-specific</td><td>a container can still declare requests/limits for OTHER resources (e.g. <code>nvidia.com/gpu</code>) and remain BestEffort — the classification only looks at CPU and memory</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>QoS class is immutable for a pod's whole life</td><td>computed once at admission; an in-place resource resize (patching <code>resources</code> on a running pod without recreating it) is REJECTED by admission if it would change the pod's QoS class</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Scheduler preemption (§14.8) is QoS-independent</td><td>the kube-scheduler does NOT consider QoS class when choosing which pod to preempt — that decision is driven purely by PriorityClass value. QoS only governs the KUBELET's node-pressure eviction order, a completely separate mechanism</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>Multi-Container Pods — QoS Is Computed Across EVERY Container, Worst Case Wins</h4>
                <p>A pod's QoS class isn't set per-container — it's ONE value for the whole pod, computed from ALL its containers together (including init containers). A single misconfigured sidecar can silently downgrade an otherwise-perfect Guaranteed pod to Burstable.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One sidecar without matched requests/limits demotes the whole pod</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Pod: easypj-web  (2 containers)

  container "django":     requests: {cpu: 500m, mem: 512Mi}
                           limits:   {cpu: 500m, mem: 512Mi}   ← perfectly matched

  container "log-shipper": requests: {cpu: 50m,  mem: 64Mi}
                           limits:   {cpu: 100m, mem: 128Mi}   ← NOT matched (limit > request)

  Pod QoS = Burstable  ← the ENTIRE pod, even though django alone would be Guaranteed

  RULE: Guaranteed requires EVERY container (app AND every sidecar/init
  container) to have requests == limits for BOTH cpu and memory. One
  mismatched container anywhere in the pod caps the whole pod at Burstable.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ This is why "just add a logging sidecar" can quietly change your eviction risk</h5>
                    <p>Teams often bolt a monitoring or log-shipping sidecar onto an existing Guaranteed production Deployment without matching its requests to its limits — and don't notice the pod silently dropped to Burstable until it's evicted earlier than expected during a real memory-pressure incident. Always check <code>kubectl get pod -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}'</code> AFTER adding any sidecar to a QoS-sensitive workload.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>QoS Also Affects CPU Contention, Not Just Memory Eviction</h4>
                <p>Every example so far frames QoS around the OOM-eviction order, but the class also sets each pod's <strong><code>cpu.shares</code></strong> — its relative weight when MULTIPLE pods compete for CPU time on a busy node, even with nobody hitting a hard CPU limit or getting OOMKilled.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>QoS class</th><th>Relative CPU weight under contention</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Guaranteed</td><td>highest — <code>cpu.shares</code> is set proportional to its (matched) request, which tends to be a meaningfully-sized reservation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Burstable</td><td>proportional to its own request — a pod with a tiny request gets a tiny share even if its limit is generous</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>BestEffort</td><td>the lowest fixed <code>cpu.shares</code> value the kernel allows — always loses a CPU-contention fight against any pod with an actual request</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 In short</h5>
                    <p>A BestEffort pod isn't just first to be OOM-evicted — it's also first to be CPU-starved on a busy node, well before memory pressure ever becomes a factor. This is one more reason "just leave requests/limits off for a quick test pod" degrades more than only your eviction odds.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §15.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.3 LimitRange — Namespace Default Limits</h3>
                <p>A LimitRange sets <strong>default</strong> resource requests and limits for containers in a namespace. It also enforces min/max boundaries. This prevents pods from running without any resource configuration.</p>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 A LimitRange does NOT validate its own defaults against explicit requests you write</h5>
                    <p>If a container sets an explicit <code>requests.cpu</code> HIGHER than the namespace's LimitRange <code>default.cpu</code> (the injected LIMIT) and doesn't set its own limit, the pod is rejected: <em>"must be less than or equal to cpu limit"</em> — the request the developer wrote is bigger than the limit the LimitRange is about to inject. Kubernetes does not sanity-check that its own defaults are internally consistent with what containers explicitly declare; it's the admin's job to keep <code>default</code> comfortably above whatever requests real workloads are likely to set.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">limitrange.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-limits
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container
    default:                     <span class="comment"># Default LIMITS (if not specified)</span>
      cpu: "500m"
      memory: "512Mi"
    defaultRequest:              <span class="comment"># Default REQUESTS (if not specified)</span>
      cpu: "250m"
      memory: "256Mi"
    max:                         <span class="comment"># Maximum allowed</span>
      cpu: "2"
      memory: "2Gi"
    min:                         <span class="comment"># Minimum allowed</span>
      cpu: "50m"
      memory: "32Mi"
  - type: Pod
    max:
      cpu: "4"
      memory: "4Gi"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">LimitRange in Action</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create namespace and LimitRange</span>
kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl apply -f limitrange.yaml

<span class="comment"># Create pod WITHOUT specifying resources</span>
kubectl run test-pod --image=nginx -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># Check — LimitRange injected default values!</span>
kubectl get pod test-pod -n easypj-dev -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}'
<span class="comment"># {"limits":{"cpu":"500m","memory":"512Mi"},"requests":{"cpu":"250m","memory":"256Mi"}}</span>

<span class="comment"># Try to create pod exceeding max</span>
<span class="comment"># → Error: maximum cpu usage per Container is 2, but limit is 4</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">LimitRange — Automatic Defaults</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Namespace: easypj-dev (with LimitRange)

  Pod created WITHOUT resources:        Pod received DEFAULTS:
  ┌──────────────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────────────────┐
  │ spec:                    │   →     │ spec:                        │
  │   containers:            │   →     │   containers:                │
  │   - name: nginx          │   →     │   - name: nginx              │
  │     image: nginx         │   →     │     image: nginx             │
  │     <span class="comment"># no resources!      │   →     │     resources:</span>               │
  │                          │   →     │       requests:              │
  │                          │   →     │         cpu: "250m"    ← injected! │
  │                          │   →     │         memory: "256Mi" ← injected!│
  │                          │   →     │       limits:               │
  │                          │   →     │         cpu: "500m"    ← injected! │
  │                          │   →     │         memory: "512Mi" ← injected!│
  └──────────────────────────┘         └──────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>Beyond Container/Pod — <code>maxLimitRequestRatio</code> and PVC Size Limits</h4>
                <p>A LimitRange can do more than inject defaults and cap absolute values. Two lesser-known fields are genuinely useful and occasionally exam-relevant: capping HOW MUCH a container may burst relative to its own request, and bounding PersistentVolumeClaim sizes.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">maxLimitRequestRatio + a PVC-storage LimitRange, in the same namespace</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-full-governance
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container
    default: {cpu: "500m", memory: "512Mi"}
    defaultRequest: {cpu: "250m", memory: "256Mi"}
    maxLimitRequestRatio:            <span class="comment"># limit can be AT MOST this many times the request</span>
      cpu: "4"                       <span class="comment"># e.g. request 250m → limit can be at most 1000m</span>
      memory: "2"                    <span class="comment"># e.g. request 256Mi → limit can be at most 512Mi</span>
  - type: PersistentVolumeClaim      <span class="comment"># a DIFFERENT type — governs storage requests, not compute</span>
    min:
      storage: "1Gi"                 <span class="comment"># reject a PVC asking for less than this (prevents tiny-PVC sprawl)</span>
    max:
      storage: "50Gi"                <span class="comment"># reject a PVC asking for more (prevents one team eating all storage)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Field</th><th>What it prevents</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>maxLimitRequestRatio</code></td><td>a container requesting almost nothing but setting an enormous limit — capping how aggressively a pod can "burst," independent of the absolute min/max bounds</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>type: PersistentVolumeClaim</code> min/max</td><td>developers requesting a 1Ti PVC by typo, or so many tiny PVCs that per-PVC overhead adds up — the exact same admission-time REJECT behavior as Container min/max, just scoped to storage</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 A LimitRange can carry multiple <code>limits</code> entries, one per <code>type</code></h5>
                    <p>Each entry in the <code>spec.limits</code> list is scoped by its own <code>type</code> (<code>Container</code>, <code>Pod</code>, or <code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code>) — a single LimitRange object can govern all three simultaneously in one namespace, as shown above, rather than needing three separate objects.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §15.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.4 ResourceQuota — Namespace Capacity Control</h3>
                <p>A ResourceQuota limits the <strong>total resources</strong> consumed across all pods in a namespace. This prevents one team from using all cluster resources and is a key tool for multi-tenant clusters.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">resourcequota.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  hard:
    <span class="comment"># Compute resources</span>
    requests.cpu: "4"            <span class="comment"># Total CPU requests across all pods</span>
    requests.memory: "8Gi"       <span class="comment"># Total memory requests</span>
    limits.cpu: "8"              <span class="comment"># Total CPU limits</span>
    limits.memory: "16Gi"        <span class="comment"># Total memory limits</span>

    <span class="comment"># Object count limits</span>
    pods: "10"                   <span class="comment"># Max 10 pods in this namespace</span>
    services: "5"                <span class="comment"># Max 5 services</span>
    persistentvolumeclaims: "5"  <span class="comment"># Max 5 PVCs</span>
    configmaps: "20"
    secrets: "20"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">ResourceQuota Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply quota</span>
kubectl apply -f resourcequota.yaml

<span class="comment"># Check quota usage</span>
kubectl get resourcequota -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># NAME           AGE   REQUEST                                              LIMIT</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-quota   5m    requests.cpu: 750m/4, requests.memory: 768Mi/8Gi    limits.cpu: 1500m/8, limits.memory: 1536Mi/16Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Detailed view</span>
kubectl describe resourcequota easypj-quota -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># Name:                   easypj-quota</span>
<span class="comment"># Namespace:              easypj-dev</span>
<span class="comment"># Resource                Used    Hard</span>
<span class="comment"># --------                ----    ----</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.cpu              1500m   8</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.memory           1536Mi  16Gi</span>
<span class="comment"># pods                    3       10</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.cpu            750m    4</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.memory         768Mi   8Gi</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Resource Control Hierarchy — 3 Levels</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  LEVEL 1: ResourceQuota (namespace total budget)               │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ "easypj-dev can use max 4 CPUs, 8Gi memory total"       │  │
  │  │  Used: 750m / 4 CPU    Used: 768Mi / 8Gi memory         │  │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │       │                                                        │
  │       ▼                                                        │
  │  LEVEL 2: LimitRange (per-container defaults &amp; bounds)       │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ "Each container: default 250m req, 500m limit"           │  │
  │  │ "Min: 50m CPU, Max: 2 CPU per container"                 │  │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │       │                                                        │
  │       ▼                                                        │
  │  LEVEL 3: Pod spec (individual container settings)            │
  │  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐              │
  │  │ easypj-1   │  │ easypj-2   │  │ easypj-3   │              │
  │  │ req: 250m  │  │ req: 250m  │  │ req: 250m  │              │
  │  │ lim: 500m  │  │ lim: 500m  │  │ lim: 500m  │              │
  │  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘              │
  │  Total: 750m request, 1500m limit (within quota ✅)           │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ ResourceQuota Requires Resource Specs</h5>
                    <p>When a ResourceQuota is active in a namespace, <strong>every pod must specify resource requests and limits</strong>. Without them, the pod creation will be rejected. Use a LimitRange to auto-inject defaults so developers don't have to specify them manually.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-icon">🔥</span>
                        <h4>Scenario: "Pod Creation Rejected — Forbidden"</h4>
                        <span class="badge badge-exam">CKA EXAM</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-content">
                        <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> <code>kubectl run test --image=nginx -n dev</code> → <em>Error: forbidden: failed quota: dev-quota: must specify limits.cpu,limits.memory</em></p>
                        <div class="scenario-steps">
                            <div class="scenario-step">
                                <span class="step-label">Step 1 — Check quota</span>
                                <p><code>kubectl describe resourcequota -n dev</code> → quota exists and enforces limits. No LimitRange present to auto-inject defaults.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="scenario-step">
                                <span class="step-label">Step 2 — Two fixes</span>
                                <p><strong>Option A:</strong> Add resources to pod spec. <strong>Option B:</strong> Create a LimitRange with <code>default</code> and <code>defaultRequest</code> so future pods get automatic defaults.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="scenario-step">
                                <span class="step-label">Step 3 — Verify</span>
                                <p>Re-run <code>kubectl run test --image=nginx -n dev</code> → pod created. <code>kubectl describe resourcequota -n dev</code> → Used values increased.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-outcome">
                            <strong>Lesson:</strong> When ResourceQuota exists, always pair it with a LimitRange. This is a classic CKA trap — the exam expects you to know both work together.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Per-StorageClass Quota and <code>scopeSelector</code> — Targeting a Quota at a Subset of Pods</h4>
                <p>Two ResourceQuota features let you govern more precisely than "everything in this namespace, uniformly": capping storage per StorageClass separately, and applying a quota ONLY to pods matching a specific scope (like a PriorityClass or QoS tier).</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Per-StorageClass caps + a scoped quota for BestEffort pods only</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-storage-quota
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  hard:
    persistentvolumeclaims: "10"                    <span class="comment"># total PVCs, any class</span>
    fast-ssd.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage: "100Gi"   <span class="comment"># cap JUST the "fast-ssd" class</span>
    standard.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage: "500Gi"   <span class="comment"># a separate, larger cap for "standard"</span>
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-besteffort-cap
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  hard:
    pods: "5"                          <span class="comment"># at most 5 BestEffort pods allowed...</span>
  scopeSelector:                       <span class="comment"># ...because this quota ONLY counts pods matching this scope</span>
    matchExpressions:
    - operator: In
      scopeName: PriorityClass
      values: ["low-priority"]         <span class="comment"># only pods using the low-priority PriorityClass count here</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Lets you</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Per-StorageClass keys (<code>&lt;class&gt;.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage</code>)</td><td>give expensive fast storage a tighter cap than cheap standard storage, in the SAME quota object or split across several</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>scopeSelector</code> with <code>PriorityClass</code></td><td>enforce "at most N low-priority/batch pods" separately from your main compute quota — stops batch workloads from silently eating the whole namespace's pod-count budget</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Built-in scopes: <code>BestEffort</code>, <code>NotBestEffort</code>, <code>Terminating</code>, <code>NotTerminating</code></td><td>target a quota at, e.g., ONLY BestEffort pods (govern the "no resources set" crowd separately from properly-configured production pods)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Multiple ResourceQuota objects in one namespace are additive constraints, not alternatives</h5>
                    <p>A namespace can have several ResourceQuota objects active simultaneously (as in the example above) — a pod creation must satisfy EVERY quota that applies to it, not just one. This is how you layer a broad "whole namespace" cap together with a narrower "just the low-priority pods" cap without them conflicting.</p>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Nuance</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>A Deployment ITSELF never fails on quota</td><td>only the underlying Pod creation does — <code>kubectl create deploy</code> against an exhausted quota succeeds and the Deployment object is created fine, but it silently sits at partial replica count forever. Always check the Pods, not just "did the Deployment apply," when troubleshooting a quota-related stall</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>"Must specify requests/limits" is CPU/memory-specific</td><td>a quota on <code>ephemeral-storage</code> or an object count (<code>pods</code>, <code>services</code>) does NOT force every pod to declare that resource the way a <code>cpu</code>/<code>memory</code> quota does — the mandatory-declaration rule only applies to compute resources</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Extended resources (e.g. GPU) can only be quota'd via <code>requests.&lt;resource&gt;</code></td><td>there is no <code>limits.nvidia.com/gpu</code> quota key — extended resources aren't overcommittable at all (§15.1), so only the request side needs a cap</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How CPU and Memory Limits Are Actually Enforced by the Linux Kernel: CFS Quotas, Cgroups v2, OOM Scoring, and QoS Classes</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Resource Enforcement — From Kubernetes YAML to Kernel Mechanisms</h5>
            <p>Kubernetes resource limits aren't just suggestions — they're enforced by the Linux kernel through cgroups. Understanding the exact mechanism helps you set correct limits and debug OOMKilled pods.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUBERNETES RESOURCES → LINUX CGROUPS MAPPING                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Pod YAML:                           Kernel Cgroup (cgroups v2):             │
│  ┌────────────────────────────┐      ┌──────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ resources:                 │      │ /sys/fs/cgroup/kubepods/         │   │
│  │   requests:                │      │   burstable/                     │   │
│  │     cpu: 250m              │─────▶│   pod&lt;uid&gt;/                      │   │
│  │     memory: 256Mi          │      │     cpu.weight: ~25              │   │
│  │                            │      │     memory.min: 256Mi             │   │
│  │   limits:                  │      │                                   │   │
│  │     cpu: 500m              │─────▶│     cpu.max: 50000 100000        │   │
│  │     memory: 512Mi          │      │     memory.max: 512Mi             │   │
│  └────────────────────────────┘      └──────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CPU LIMIT ENFORCEMENT (CFS — Completely Fair Scheduler):                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  Linux CFS uses QUOTA and PERIOD:                                    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │   limits.cpu: 500m → 0.5 CPU cores                                  │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │   CFS settings (kernel):                                             │    │
│  │     cpu.cfs_period_us  = 100000  (100ms — the accounting period)    │    │
│  │     cpu.cfs_quota_us   = 50000   (50ms — max CPU time per period)   │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │   RESULT: Container gets at MOST 50ms of CPU every 100ms.            │    │
│  │           This equals 0.5 cores = 500 millicores.                     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  WHAT HAPPENS WHEN CPU LIMIT IS HIT:                                 │    │
│  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │    │
│  │  │ CPU is THROTTLED (not killed):                               │    │    │
│  │  │ • Process is paused by CFS scheduler                         │    │    │
│  │  │ • Resumes in next accounting period                          │    │    │
│  │  │ • Observable as: container_cpu_cfs_throttled_seconds_total   │    │    │
│  │  │ • Effect: increased latency, NOT pod termination             │    │    │
│  │  │                                                               │    │    │
