            <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> everything above shows a SINGLE container's resources being enforced. The five subsections below zoom out one level: how the kubelet decides among MANY pods when the whole node is under pressure, and how quota accounting stays correct when many pods are created at once.</p>

            <h5>① The Kubelet Eviction Manager — Deciding WHICH Pod to Kill When the NODE Is Low, Not Just One Container</h5>
            <p>OOMKilled (covered above) is the KERNEL reacting to ONE container crossing ITS OWN limit. Node-level eviction is different: the kubelet's own <strong>eviction manager</strong> proactively kills whole pods BEFORE the kernel OOM-killer would even trigger, based on the node's overall resource pressure — not any single container's limit.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Eviction SIGNALS the kubelet watches (examples):
     memory.available    < eviction-hard threshold (e.g. "100Mi")
     nodefs.available    < eviction-hard threshold (e.g. "10%")
     imagefs.available   < eviction-hard threshold

   TWO threshold types:
     eviction-hard   → evicts IMMEDIATELY once crossed, no grace period
     eviction-soft   → must stay crossed for eviction-soft-grace-period
                        (e.g. 90s) before the kubelet acts — absorbs a brief spike

   WHEN eviction triggers, ranking of WHICH pod(s) to kill:
     1. QoS class first:  BestEffort → Burstable → Guaranteed  (this section's table)
     2. WITHIN a class:    whichever pod is using the MOST of the pressured
                           resource beyond its OWN request  (biggest offender first)

   This is a DIFFERENT decision than the kernel OOM-killer: the kubelet acts
   PROACTIVELY on NODE-wide pressure; the kernel OOM-killer only fires
   REACTIVELY once a specific cgroup's memory.max is actually exceeded.
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Trigger</th><th>Decides by</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Kernel OOM-killer</td><td>a container's OWN cgroup <code>memory.max</code> exceeded</td><td>highest <code>oom_score</code> process WITHIN that one cgroup — a single-container event</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Kubelet eviction manager</td><td>NODE-wide signal (memory.available, disk, etc.) crosses a threshold</td><td>QoS class, then usage-over-request — a whole-node, cross-pod decision</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> a pod reported as <code>Evicted</code> (not <code>OOMKilled</code>) in <code>kubectl get pods</code> means the KUBELET made this decision, not the kernel — check <code>kubectl describe node</code>'s Conditions for <code>MemoryPressure: True</code> and the node's Events for the exact eviction signal that fired, rather than assuming it's the same OOMKilled path this section covered.</div>

            <h5>② Admission-Time Quota Enforcement — How "Used" Stays Accurate Under Concurrent Creates</h5>
            <p>§15.4 shows <code>kubectl describe resourcequota</code> reporting a live Used/Hard count. Keeping that number correct when MULTIPLE pods are being created simultaneously — a real race condition risk — is handled by making ResourceQuota checking part of the admission pipeline itself (Chapter 1's UTH ②), not a background reconciler.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Two pod creates arrive milliseconds apart, both requesting cpu=500m,
   against a quota with requests.cpu hard=1, used=600m (400m of headroom left):

   Request A reaches the ResourceQuota admission plugin FIRST:
     checks: 600m used + 500m requested = 1100m > 1000m hard → ❌ REJECTED

   Request B reaches it fractions of a second later:
     checks: 600m used + 500m requested = 1100m > 1000m hard → ❌ REJECTED too
     (NEITHER succeeds, because the check-and-reserve happens SYNCHRONOUSLY
      inside the SAME admission step that would persist the object — there's
      no window where both could read "600m used" and both proceed)

   This is why ResourceQuota enforcement is an ADMISSION CONTROLLER, not a
   controller that reconciles usage after the fact — the check and the
   reservation happen atomically, in the same request, before etcd ever
   sees the new pod.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Contrast with the OTHER "used" numbers in this chapter.</strong> <code>kubectl top pod</code> (§15.1) reads LIVE usage from the metrics pipeline — it can lag reality by seconds. ResourceQuota's "Used" column is the opposite: it's always exactly correct at any instant, because it's enforced synchronously at admission, not sampled periodically.</div>

            <h5>③ The Real Cgroup Hierarchy — Where QoS Classes Actually Live on Disk</h5>
            <p>This UTH's kernel diagram above showed ONE pod's cgroup path. The full hierarchy nests by QoS class first, which is the literal on-disk structure that makes "BestEffort pods evicted first" mechanically enforceable — they're not just ranked in software, they live in a differently-configured branch of the cgroup tree.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   /sys/fs/cgroup/kubepods/                     ← the kubelet's cgroup root
     ├── guaranteed/                            ← Guaranteed pods live directly here
     │     └── pod&lt;uid&gt;/
     │           └── &lt;container-id&gt;/            cpu.max = EXACT (request==limit)
     │
     ├── burstable/                             ← a SEPARATE branch, its own memory ceiling
     │     └── pod&lt;uid&gt;/
     │           └── &lt;container-id&gt;/            cpu.max = the container's OWN limit
     │
     └── besteffort/                            ← lowest priority branch
           └── pod&lt;uid&gt;/
                 └── &lt;container-id&gt;/            cpu.shares = minimum (§15.2's CPU-contention point)

   Each QoS branch can ALSO carry its OWN aggregate memory limit (derived from
   node allocatable) — so "burstable" pods collectively can be capped even
   before any INDIVIDUAL burstable pod hits ITS OWN limit.
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>cgroup version</th><th>Key files</th><th>Practical difference</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>cgroup v1 (legacy)</td><td><code>cpu.cfs_quota_us</code>, <code>memory.limit_in_bytes</code></td><td>separate hierarchies (controllers) per resource type — more moving parts, being phased out</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>cgroup v2 (modern, default on current distros)</td><td><code>cpu.max</code>, <code>memory.max</code>, <code>memory.high</code></td><td>ONE unified hierarchy for all controllers — simpler, and adds soft-limit features like <code>memory.high</code> (see ⑤)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>kubectl describe node | grep -i cgroup</code> and checking the node's kubelet config (<code>cgroupDriver</code>) tells you which version is active — mismatched cgroup drivers between the kubelet and container runtime is a real (if uncommon) cause of a node failing to join or pods failing to start, worth knowing exists even outside this chapter's resource-governance focus.</div>

            <h5>④ CPU Throttling Metrics &amp; PSI — Proving a Pod Is Throttled When <code>kubectl top</code> Looks Fine</h5>
            <p>This UTH's own diagram above covered the CFS quota/period math. Here's the practical problem it causes: a pod can be SEVERELY throttled while <code>kubectl top pod</code> still reports CPU usage well under its limit — because <code>top</code> shows AVERAGE usage over its sampling window, hiding many short bursts that each individually hit the 100ms CFS quota wall.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   kubectl top pod shows: easypj-web  CPU: 180m  (limit: 500m)  ← "looks fine!"

   But the REAL per-period picture (100ms accounting windows):
     window 1: burst to 500m for 20ms, then IDLE for 80ms  → throttled, avg looks low
     window 2: burst to 500m for 20ms, then IDLE for 80ms  → throttled again
     ...repeated hundreds of times per second under bursty request patterns...

   THE METRIC THAT REVEALS IT (not kubectl top):
     container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total   ← how many 100ms windows hit the wall
     container_cpu_cfs_periods_total             ← total windows measured
     throttle ratio = throttled_periods / total_periods
     → a HIGH ratio (even with "fine" average CPU%) means: raise the limit,
       the app is being paused constantly even though its AVERAGE looks safe

   PSI (Pressure Stall Information) — a newer, more direct kernel signal:
     /sys/fs/cgroup/.../cpu.pressure  → reports the % of time tasks were
     literally STALLED waiting for CPU — a more honest number than CFS
     throttle counters alone, available on cgroup v2 kernels.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> "the pod seems slow but resource usage looks normal" is a classic real-world ticket that resource metrics alone (CPU%, memory%) genuinely cannot diagnose — <code>container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total</code> (via Prometheus/metrics, not <code>kubectl top</code>) is the number that actually answers it. Know this metric name exists even though the CKA itself won't ask you to query Prometheus directly.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Memory QoS — A Softer Warning Before the Hard OOM Wall (cgroup v2)</h5>
            <p>Everything in this chapter treats memory as strictly binary: under the limit (fine) or over it (instant OOMKilled). cgroup v2's <code>memory.high</code> adds a THIRD state — a soft ceiling that throttles memory allocation (slows the app down) before the hard <code>memory.max</code> kill wall, giving a struggling container a chance to recover instead of being killed outright.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   memory.high  (soft, throttling)         memory.max  (hard, OOM-kill)
        │                                        │
        ▼                                        ▼
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶  actual usage
   0                        ~request              limit

   Cross memory.high: kernel starts RECLAIMING memory aggressively from this
   cgroup and slowing its allocations — painful, but NOT a kill.

   Cross memory.max: instant OOM-kill, exactly as described earlier in this UTH.

   Kubernetes derives memory.high automatically from a pod's requests/limits
   when the "MemoryQoS" feature is enabled — giving Burstable/Guaranteed pods
   a graceful degradation zone instead of a hard cliff, entirely at the
   kernel level, with no change needed to the pod's own YAML.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Not universally enabled yet, but shows where this is heading.</strong> MemoryQoS depends on cgroup v2 and is not on by default on every distribution — but it's the direction Kubernetes resource management is evolving: softer, more gradual pressure responses instead of the current all-or-nothing throttle/OOM split this entire chapter has otherwise described.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Requests = scheduling FLOOR</strong> (guaranteed, trusted by the scheduler); <strong>limits = hard CEILING</strong> (enforced by the kubelet via cgroups).</li>
                    <li><strong>CPU over limit → THROTTLED</strong> (slowed, never killed — compressible); <strong>memory over limit → OOMKilled</strong> (exit 137 — incompressible).</li>
                    <li><strong>QoS (Guaranteed / Burstable / BestEffort) is COMPUTED</strong> from requests vs limits, never set directly, and fixes the eviction order: BestEffort dies first, Guaranteed last.</li>
                    <li><strong>Guaranteed requires requests == limits for BOTH cpu AND memory</strong> — matching only one field still yields Burstable.</li>
                    <li><strong>LimitRange</strong> = per-container defaults/min/max within a namespace; <strong>ResourceQuota</strong> = namespace-wide totals (cpu/mem requests &amp; limits, pods, PVCs, object counts).</li>
                    <li><strong>With a cpu/mem ResourceQuota active, every pod MUST set the matching request/limit</strong> — or a LimitRange must supply it, or creation is rejected.</li>
                    <li><strong>No requests → the scheduler is blind; no limits → a pod can eat the node.</strong> Always set both in production.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Requests vs Limits</strong> — <code>requests</code> = guaranteed minimum (used for scheduling). <code>limits</code> = maximum allowed (enforced at runtime). Scheduler uses requests, NOT limits</li>
                    <li><strong>CPU units</strong> — <code>1</code> = 1 vCPU. <code>500m</code> = 0.5 CPU. <code>100m</code> = 0.1 CPU. The exam loves asking you to convert between these</li>
                    <li><strong>Memory units</strong> — <code>Mi</code> (mebibytes) ≠ <code>M</code> (megabytes). Use <code>Mi</code> for consistency. <code>128Mi</code> = 134MB</li>
                    <li><strong>QoS classes</strong> — <code>Guaranteed</code> (requests=limits for all containers), <code>Burstable</code> (some requests set), <code>BestEffort</code> (no requests/limits). OOM killer targets BestEffort first</li>
                    <li><strong>LimitRange applies to NEW pods only</strong> — Existing pods aren't affected. ResourceQuota blocks creation when the namespace total is exceeded</li>
                    <li><strong>A request bigger than any node's allocatable</strong> leaves the pod <code>Pending</code> forever with "0/N nodes are available: Insufficient cpu/memory" — a SCHEDULING failure, not a runtime crash. Compare the pod's requests against <code>kubectl describe node</code>'s Allocatable, not against total capacity</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

