            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 15 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the resource-governance mental model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">In one sentence, what's the functional difference between a request and a limit?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A request is a promise the <strong>scheduler</strong> trusts when deciding where a pod fits; a limit is a fence the <strong>kubelet</strong> enforces once the pod is already running.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>request</th><th>limit</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Enforced by</td><td>scheduler (placement only)</td><td>kubelet (runtime, via cgroups)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>When it's checked</td><td>once, at scheduling time</td><td>continuously, for the pod's whole life</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Violated → </td><td>pod never gets placed here (a different node is picked)</td><td>throttled (CPU) or OOMKilled (memory)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Two different components, two different jobs — the scheduler never looks at limits, and the kubelet never re-checks whether a node "had room" once the pod is already placed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is exceeding a CPU limit survivable but exceeding a memory limit fatal to the container?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>CPU is <strong>compressible</strong> — the kernel can just give the process less CPU time (throttling) without losing any state. Memory is <strong>incompressible</strong> — there's no "slow down" option, so the kernel OOM killer must terminate the process to reclaim it.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Over limit →</th><th>Reversible?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>CPU (compressible)</td><td>⏳ Throttled — paused until the next CFS period</td><td>✅ yes, automatically, every 100ms</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Memory (incompressible)</td><td>💀 OOMKilled — process terminated</td><td>❌ no — the process is gone, only a restart recovers it</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This asymmetry is why memory limits deserve far more caution than CPU limits — an undersized CPU limit just makes the app slow (visible in <code>kubectl top</code>), but an undersized memory limit causes hard crashes and CrashLoopBackOff.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A container declares only <code>limits</code> and no <code>requests</code>. What does Kubernetes do, and what QoS class results?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Kubernetes automatically sets the request equal to the limit for each resource that has a limit but no request. If that ends up true for both cpu and memory, the pod is <strong>Guaranteed</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">what you write vs. what actually gets admitted</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># You write:</span>
resources:
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"

<span class="comment"># Kubernetes admits it as:</span>
resources:
  requests:              <span class="comment"># auto-filled, equal to the limit</span>
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"
<span class="comment"># status.qosClass: Guaranteed</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a common exam surprise — omitting requests doesn't make a pod BestEffort if limits are set; it can actually produce the SAFEST QoS class, Guaranteed, by implication.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod has <code>requests.memory: 256Mi</code> and <code>limits.memory: 256Mi</code>, but no CPU requests or limits at all. What QoS class is this?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Burstable</strong> — not Guaranteed.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">resources:
  requests: {memory: "256Mi"}      <span class="comment"># memory matches...</span>
  limits:   {memory: "256Mi"}      <span class="comment"># ...but cpu is never set at all</span>
<span class="comment"># status.qosClass: Burstable  ← memory alone matching isn't enough</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Guaranteed requires requests == limits for BOTH cpu AND memory on every container. Matching just one resource, or matching on some containers but not others in a multi-container pod, still yields Burstable.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A LimitRange sets both <code>default</code> and <code>defaultRequest</code> for CPU. A pod's container sets <code>limits.cpu</code> explicitly but no request. What request does it get?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The request defaults to the container's own explicit <strong>limit</strong> value — NOT to the LimitRange's <code>defaultRequest</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Container declares</th><th>Request it actually gets</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>neither request nor limit</td><td><code>defaultRequest</code> from the LimitRange</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>a limit, but no request</td><td>request = that container's OWN limit (not <code>defaultRequest</code>)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>both request and limit</td><td>exactly what the container wrote — LimitRange defaults never apply</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>defaultRequest</code> only fires when the container specifies NEITHER a request nor a limit for that resource. Once any limit is present, "request = limit" takes precedence over the namespace default — a genuinely easy-to-miss ordering rule.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A container in a LimitRange-governed namespace requests <code>cpu: 3</code> but the LimitRange's <code>max.cpu</code> is <code>2</code>. What happens to the pod?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The pod is <strong>rejected at admission</strong> — "forbidden: maximum cpu usage per Container is 2, but limit is 3." It is never created, not silently clamped down to 2.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f oversized-pod.yaml
<span class="comment"># Error from server (Forbidden): error when creating "oversized-pod.yaml":
# pods "oversized" is forbidden: maximum cpu usage per Container is 2, but limit is 3
# ← the pod object is NEVER created; nothing to clamp, nothing to clean up</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Candidates often assume out-of-range values get capped automatically — they don't. LimitRange min/max is a hard admission gate, same category of failure as a ResourceQuota rejection.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A namespace has a ResourceQuota on <code>requests.cpu</code> but no LimitRange. A developer tries <code>kubectl run test --image=nginx</code> with no resources block. What happens, and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Rejected: <em>"must specify limits.cpu, requests.cpu"</em>. Once a quota tracks a compute resource, the apiserver must know how much to charge every new pod — with no explicit request and no LimitRange to inject one, it has no number to check against the quota.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl run test --image=nginx
<span class="comment"># Error from server (Forbidden): pods "test" is forbidden: failed quota: dev-quota:
# must specify limits.cpu,requests.cpu
# ← fix: add resources to the pod spec, OR apply a LimitRange to inject defaults</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the single most common ResourceQuota exam trap — the standard fix is pairing every compute ResourceQuota with a LimitRange in the same namespace so ordinary pods keep working.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Does a ResourceQuota's <code>requests.cpu: 4</code> track ACTUAL live CPU usage, or something else?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It tracks the SUM of every pod's declared <code>requests.cpu</code> in the namespace — not live usage. A pod sitting completely idle still counts its full request against the quota.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe resourcequota dev-quota -n dev
<span class="comment"># Resource       Used    Hard
# requests.cpu   3800m   4      ← the SUM of every pod's declared request,
#                                  regardless of how much CPU they're actually using
kubectl top pods -n dev
# NAME       CPU(cores)
# idle-app   2m           ← "requested" 500m but is USING almost nothing —
#                             still counts as 500m against the quota above</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is why a quota can be "full" (blocking new pods) even while <code>kubectl top pods</code> shows the whole namespace barely using any CPU — quota accounting and live metrics answer different questions.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Node memory is under pressure and the kubelet must evict pods. In what order does it choose, and what's the tie-breaker within a QoS class?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>BestEffort first, then Burstable, then Guaranteed last. Within a class, it prefers evicting the pod that is exceeding its own <em>requests</em> by the largest margin.</p>
<pre>
  Node memory pressure — eviction order:
  💀 1st: BestEffort   (no requests/limits at all)
  ⚠️ 2nd: Burstable    (tie-break: furthest OVER its own request wins... loses)
  🛡️ 3rd: Guaranteed   (requests == limits — evicted only as an absolute last resort)
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This means two Burstable pods aren't equally at risk — the one consuming furthest beyond what it originally asked for is evicted first, which rewards accurately-sized requests even within the same QoS tier.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where does the kernel enforcement of a CPU limit actually live — what two numbers does Kubernetes translate <code>limits.cpu: 500m</code> into?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The Linux CFS (Completely Fair Scheduler) quota/period pair: <code>cpu.cfs_period_us = 100000</code> (100ms accounting window) and <code>cpu.cfs_quota_us = 50000</code> (50ms of CPU time allowed per window) — 50ms of every 100ms window equals 0.5 cores, i.e. 500m.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># On the node, inside the container's cgroup:</span>
cat /sys/fs/cgroup/.../cpu.cfs_period_us   <span class="comment"># 100000  (100ms — the accounting window)</span>
cat /sys/fs/cgroup/.../cpu.cfs_quota_us    <span class="comment"># 50000   (50ms of CPU time allowed per window)</span>
<span class="comment"># 50000 / 100000 = 0.5 cores = 500m, exactly matching limits.cpu: "500m"</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Once the container spends its quota within a 100ms period, the CFS scheduler simply pauses it until the next period — this is throttling, visible as <code>container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total</code>, and is why a pod can look "fine" on raw CPU% while still being throttled.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 15 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of requests, limits &amp; QoS — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Set a container's CPU request to 250m and memory limit to 512Mi. What do <code>m</code> and <code>Mi</code> mean, and what happens if the container exceeds its memory limit?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pod with resource requests and limits</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod with resource requests and limits:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: resource-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: "250m"        <span class="comment"># 250 millicores = 0.25 CPU core</span>
        memory: "256Mi"    <span class="comment"># 256 Mebibytes</span>
      limits:
        cpu: "500m"        <span class="comment"># 500 millicores = 0.5 CPU core</span>
        memory: "512Mi"    <span class="comment"># 512 Mebibytes</span>

<span class="comment"># CPU units:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1 CPU = 1000m (millicores)</span>
<span class="comment"># 250m = 0.25 = 25% of one core</span>
<span class="comment"># 100m = 0.1 = 10% of one core</span>

<span class="comment"># Memory units:</span>
<span class="comment"># Mi = Mebibytes (1024 * 1024 bytes) — binary</span>
<span class="comment"># M  = Megabytes (1000 * 1000 bytes) — decimal</span>
<span class="comment"># Gi = Gibibytes, G = Gigabytes</span>

<span class="comment"># What happens when limits are exceeded:</span>
<span class="comment"># CPU:    Container is THROTTLED (slowed down, NOT killed)</span>
<span class="comment"># Memory: Container is OOM-KILLED (terminated, CrashLoopBackOff)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CPU is compressible — when a container hits its CPU limit, it's throttled (slowed) but continues running. Memory is incompressible — when a container exceeds its memory limit, the kernel OOM-killer terminates it. The pod restarts and eventually enters <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> if it keeps exceeding the limit. On the CKA, always use <code>Mi</code> (mebibytes) not <code>M</code> (megabytes) — they're subtly different. <code>250m</code> CPU = quarter core. The <code>requests</code> field determines scheduling (scheduler finds a node with enough free resources), while <code>limits</code> enforce runtime caps.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain the three QoS (Quality of Service) classes in Kubernetes. How is the QoS class determined, and which class gets OOM-killed first?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>QoS Class</th>
                                        <th>Condition</th>
                                        <th>OOM Kill Priority</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="color:#4ade80;"><strong>Guaranteed</strong></td>
                                        <td>All containers have requests = limits (both CPU &amp; memory)</td>
                                        <td>Last to be killed (highest priority)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="color:#facc15;"><strong>Burstable</strong></td>
                                        <td>At least one container has a request or limit set</td>
                                        <td>Killed after BestEffort</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="color:#f87171;"><strong>BestEffort</strong></td>
                                        <td>No requests or limits set on any container</td>
                                        <td>First to be killed (lowest priority)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check a pod's qos class</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check a pod's QoS class</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}'
<span class="comment"># Guaranteed</span>

<span class="comment"># Guaranteed example — requests MUST equal limits:</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "256Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"       <span class="comment"># Same as request</span>
    memory: "256Mi"   <span class="comment"># Same as request</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>QoS classes determine which pods are killed first when a node runs out of memory. <code>BestEffort</code> pods are evicted first (no resource guarantees), <code>Burstable</code> pods are next, and <code>Guaranteed</code> pods are last. On the CKA, if a question asks "how to ensure a critical pod is the last to be evicted," the answer is QoS class <code>Guaranteed</code> (set requests = limits for ALL containers in the pod). The QoS class is automatically determined by Kubernetes — you can't set it manually.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a <code>LimitRange</code> in namespace <code>dev</code> that sets default CPU request to 100m, default CPU limit to 500m, and prevents any container from requesting more than 2 CPU.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: cpu-limits
  namespace: dev
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container
    default:          <span class="comment"># Default LIMITS (applied if not specified)</span>
      cpu: "500m"
    defaultRequest:   <span class="comment"># Default REQUESTS (applied if not specified)</span>
      cpu: "100m"
    max:              <span class="comment"># Maximum allowed</span>
      cpu: "2"
    min:              <span class="comment"># Minimum allowed</span>
      cpu: "50m"

kubectl apply -f limitrange.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl describe limitrange cpu-limits -n dev
<span class="comment"># Type      Resource  Min   Max   Default  Default Request</span>
<span class="comment"># Container cpu       50m   2     500m     100m</span>

<span class="comment"># Now any pod in namespace 'dev' without CPU specs</span>
<span class="comment"># automatically gets: requests.cpu=100m, limits.cpu=500m</span>
<span class="comment"># Any pod requesting more than 2 CPU is REJECTED</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>LimitRange</code> operates at the namespace level and applies to INDIVIDUAL pods/containers. <code>default</code> sets the default limit, <code>defaultRequest</code> sets the default request. <code>max</code> and <code>min</code> enforce hard boundaries — pods that violate them are rejected at admission. On the CKA, LimitRange prevents developers from deploying pods without resource constraints. Confusable with <code>ResourceQuota</code> — LimitRange = per-pod/container defaults and constraints, ResourceQuota = total namespace-wide limits.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a <code>ResourceQuota</code> in namespace <code>production</code> that limits the namespace to maximum 10 pods, 4 CPU cores total, and 8Gi total memory for requests.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative (fastest on cka)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative (fastest on CKA):</span>
kubectl create quota prod-quota \
--hard=pods=10,requests.cpu=4,requests.memory=8Gi \
-n production

<span class="comment"># Equivalent YAML:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: prod-quota
  namespace: production
spec:
  hard:
    pods: "10"
    requests.cpu: "4"
    requests.memory: "8Gi"
    limits.cpu: "8"
    limits.memory: "16Gi"

<span class="comment"># Check current usage vs limits</span>
kubectl describe resourcequota prod-quota -n production
<span class="comment"># Resource          Used   Hard</span>
<span class="comment"># --------          ----   ----</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.cpu        2500m  8</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.memory     4Gi    16Gi</span>
<span class="comment"># pods              6      10</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.cpu      1200m  4</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.memory   3Gi    8Gi</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>ResourceQuota</code> limits the TOTAL resources consumed by ALL pods in a namespace. Once a quota is set, every new pod MUST specify <code>resources.requests</code> — otherwise creation fails. This is why <code>LimitRange</code> and <code>ResourceQuota</code> are often used together: LimitRange provides defaults so pods without explicit resources still work under the quota. On the CKA, <code>kubectl create quota</code> is the fastest imperative command. <code>kubectl describe resourcequota</code> shows the Used vs Hard columns — essential for troubleshooting "forbidden: exceeded quota" errors.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is being OOM-killed repeatedly. <code>kubectl describe pod</code> shows <code>Last State: Terminated — Reason: OOMKilled</code>. How do you diagnose and fix this?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check current memory limits</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check current memory limits</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -o jsonpath=\
'{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}'
<span class="comment"># 128Mi  ← Too small!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check actual memory usage (if pod is running)</span>
kubectl top pod oom-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME      CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># oom-pod   50m         245Mi   ← Using 245Mi but limit is 128Mi!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Increase the memory limit</span>
<span class="comment"># For a Deployment:</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp -c=app \
--limits=memory=512Mi --requests=memory=256Mi

<span class="comment"># For a standalone pod (must delete and recreate):</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -o yaml > pod.yaml
<span class="comment"># Edit pod.yaml: change limits.memory to 512Mi</span>
kubectl delete pod oom-pod
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify pod is stable</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -w
<span class="comment"># Watch for a few minutes — no more restarts</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>OOMKilled means the container exceeded its memory limit and was killed by the Linux kernel's Out-Of-Memory killer. The fix is almost always: increase <code>limits.memory</code>. But first check <code>kubectl top pod</code> to see actual usage — if a pod is using gigabytes of memory, increasing the limit is just a band-aid; the app may have a memory leak. On the CKA, OOMKilled troubleshooting is: (1) describe pod → see OOMKilled in Last State, (2) top pod → see actual usage, (3) increase limit or fix the application. For Deployments, <code>kubectl set resources</code> triggers a new rollout.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>requests</code> and <code>limits</code>? What happens if you set limits but not requests?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">requests = guaranteed minimum resources</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># requests = GUARANTEED minimum resources</span>
<span class="comment"># limits   = MAXIMUM allowed resources</span>

<span class="comment"># Scheduling: scheduler uses REQUESTS to find a node</span>
<span class="comment"># A node with 2 CPU and 4Gi can fit:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Pod A: requests.cpu=500m, requests.memory=1Gi → 1.5 CPU, 3Gi left</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Pod B: requests.cpu=1,    requests.memory=2Gi → 0.5 CPU, 1Gi left</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Pod C: requests.cpu=1 → DOESN'T FIT (only 0.5 CPU left)</span>

<span class="comment"># What if you set limits but NOT requests?</span>
resources:
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "256Mi"
<span class="comment"># requests will be automatically set EQUAL to limits!</span>
<span class="comment"># This creates a Guaranteed QoS pod</span>

<span class="comment"># What if you set requests but NOT limits?</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"
<span class="comment"># No upper bound — container can use all available resources</span>
<span class="comment"># This creates a Burstable QoS pod</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>requests</code> determine scheduling — the scheduler ensures the node has enough free (unrequested) resources. <code>limits</code> enforce runtime caps. When you set limits without requests, Kubernetes automatically sets requests = limits (creating a Guaranteed pod). This is important because: (1) the pod gets exactly what it needs, no more, (2) it's the last to be OOM-killed. On the CKA, understanding this automatic behavior prevents confusion about QoS classes. The total requests on a node can never exceed node capacity, but total limits CAN (overcommit).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod creation fails with: <em>"Error from server (Forbidden): pods "test" is forbidden: exceeded quota: dev-quota, requested: requests.cpu=500m, used: requests.cpu=3800m, limited: requests.cpu=4"</em>. What's wrong and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the quota math</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The quota math:</span>
<span class="comment"># Quota limit:    requests.cpu = 4 (4000m)</span>
<span class="comment"># Currently used: requests.cpu = 3800m</span>
<span class="comment"># Requesting:     requests.cpu = 500m</span>
<span class="comment"># Total would be: 3800m + 500m = 4300m > 4000m limit → DENIED</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 1: Reduce the new pod's CPU request</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "200m"   <span class="comment"># 3800m + 200m = 4000m ← fits exactly</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 2: Delete unused pods to free quota</span>
kubectl get pods -n dev
<span class="comment"># Find idle/unused pods and delete them</span>
kubectl delete pod idle-pod -n dev

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 3: Increase the quota (if authorized)</span>
kubectl edit resourcequota dev-quota -n dev
<span class="comment"># Change hard.requests.cpu to "8"</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current quota usage</span>
kubectl describe resourcequota dev-quota -n dev
<span class="comment"># Resource       Used    Hard</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.cpu   3800m   4</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The error message is very clear: used (3800m) + requested (500m) = 4300m exceeds the hard limit (4000m). On the CKA, quota errors are a common troubleshooting scenario. Read the error carefully — it tells you the exact numbers. Fix by: (1) reducing the pod's request, (2) freeing up quota by deleting other pods, or (3) increasing the quota. <code>kubectl describe resourcequota</code> is your diagnostic tool. Important: quotas track REQUESTS, not actual usage — even if pods are idle, their reserved quota counts against the limit.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you use <code>kubectl top</code> to check resource consumption? What prerequisite must be installed?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prerequisite: metrics-server must be installed</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Prerequisite: metrics-server must be installed</span>
<span class="comment"># Check if metrics-server is running:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep metrics-server

<span class="comment"># Check node resource usage</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           CPU(cores)  CPU%  MEMORY(bytes)  MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># controlplane   450m        22%   1200Mi         31%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1       280m        14%   890Mi          23%</span>

<span class="comment"># Check pod resource usage (current namespace)</span>
kubectl top pods
<span class="comment"># NAME        CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-1    120m        256Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># redis       80m         128Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># All namespaces</span>
kubectl top pods -A

<span class="comment"># Sort by CPU (find resource hogs)</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=cpu

<span class="comment"># Sort by memory</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=memory

<span class="comment"># Specific pod containers</span>
kubectl top pod webapp-1 --containers
<span class="comment"># POD       NAME    CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-1  nginx   100m        200Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-1  sidecar 20m         56Mi</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl top</code> requires <code>metrics-server</code> to be installed and running in the cluster. Without it, you get "error: Metrics API not available." On the CKA exam environment, metrics-server is usually pre-installed. <code>kubectl top</code> shows ACTUAL current usage (not requests/limits). Use <code>--sort-by=cpu</code> or <code>--sort-by=memory</code> to quickly find the heaviest consumers. The <code>--containers</code> flag breaks down usage per container in a pod — useful when a sidecar might be the memory hog, not the main container.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>kubectl set resources</code> command? Show how to update a Deployment's container resources without editing YAML.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">set both requests and limits for a specific…</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Set both requests and limits for a specific container</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp -c=nginx \
--requests=cpu=200m,memory=256Mi \
--limits=cpu=500m,memory=512Mi

<span class="comment"># Set only limits (requests auto-set to same value)</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp -c=nginx \
--limits=cpu=1,memory=1Gi

<span class="comment"># Remove all resource constraints</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp -c=nginx \
--limits=cpu=0,memory=0 --requests=cpu=0,memory=0

<span class="comment"># This triggers a new rollout (new ReplicaSet)</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/webapp

<span class="comment"># Verify the change</span>
kubectl get deployment webapp -o jsonpath=\
'{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}'
<span class="comment"># {"limits":{"cpu":"500m","memory":"512Mi"},</span>
<span class="comment">#  "requests":{"cpu":"200m","memory":"256Mi"}}</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl set resources</code> is the imperative way to modify resource requests/limits without editing YAML. It's much faster than <code>kubectl edit</code> on the CKA exam. The <code>-c</code> flag specifies which container (required for multi-container pods). This command modifies the Deployment's pod template, triggering a rolling update. It's equivalent to editing the YAML and applying it. Combine with <code>rollout pause/resume</code> if you need to make multiple changes in one rollout.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A namespace has a <code>ResourceQuota</code> but no <code>LimitRange</code>. You try to create a pod without specifying resources and get: <em>"Error: failed quota: must specify requests.cpu, requests.memory"</em>. Why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the problem</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># The problem:</span>
<span class="comment"># When a ResourceQuota exists that tracks cpu/memory,</span>
<span class="comment"># EVERY pod must specify requests for those resources.</span>
<span class="comment"># Without a LimitRange providing defaults, quota can't</span>
<span class="comment"># calculate how much to charge for the pod → rejected.</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 1: Add resources to the pod spec</span>
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: "100m"
        memory: "128Mi"

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 2: Create a LimitRange for defaults</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: default-limits
  namespace: dev
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container
    defaultRequest:
      cpu: "100m"
      memory: "128Mi"
    default:
      cpu: "500m"
      memory: "256Mi"

<span class="comment"># Now pods without explicit resources get the defaults</span>
<span class="comment"># and quota can track them properly</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>When a <code>ResourceQuota</code> tracks CPU/memory, Kubernetes MUST know how much each pod "costs" to charge against the quota. Without explicit requests on the pod or a <code>LimitRange</code> providing defaults, the admission controller can't determine the cost → rejects the pod. This is why best practice is: always pair <code>ResourceQuota</code> with a <code>LimitRange</code> in the same namespace. On the CKA, this is a common "gotcha" — the error message tells you to specify resources, but creating a LimitRange with defaults is the scalable fix.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            
