            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 15 Visual Summary — from one container's request to a namespace's total budget</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   ResourceQuota (namespace TOTAL: 4 cpu req / 8 cpu lim / 10 pods / 5 PVCs)
      └─ LimitRange (per-container default/defaultRequest/min/max)
            └─ Pod spec (easypj: 250m req / 500m lim → Burstable QoS)
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   REQUEST = scheduling floor (scheduler trusts it)    LIMIT = kubelet-enforced ceiling
   CPU over limit → THROTTLED (slowed)                 MEMORY over limit → OOMKilled (137)
   QoS eviction order: BestEffort (first) → Burstable → Guaranteed (last)                 ☸️
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> resource governance nests in three layers. A ResourceQuota caps the namespace as a whole; inside it, a LimitRange supplies per-container defaults plus min/max guardrails so pods that omit resources still get sane values (or are rejected outright, not clamped); at the bottom, each pod's own requests/limits determine its QoS class and therefore its eviction priority.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> a request is a promise the scheduler trusts; a limit is a fence the kubelet enforces — two different components, two different failure modes (Pending vs throttled/OOMKilled). Set both, on every production container, and pair any compute ResourceQuota with a LimitRange so ordinary pod creation keeps working.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
