            <section class="section-block" id="s16-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.1 Jobs — Run to Completion</h3>
                <p>A Job creates one or more pods and ensures they <strong>run to successful completion</strong>. Unlike Deployments (which run forever), Jobs stop once the task is done. Perfect for batch processing, database migrations, and one-off scripts.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy: Picking the Right Worker for the Job</h5>
                    <p>A <strong>Deployment</strong> is a pool of interchangeable temp workers — any one will do, swap them freely. A <strong>StatefulSet</strong> is a numbered team with assigned desks (db-0, db-1) and personal lockers (PVCs) that survive even after the person goes home. A <strong>Job</strong> is a contractor hired for one task who leaves the moment it's done. A <strong>CronJob</strong> is the cleaning crew that shows up on a schedule. A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> is the smoke detector you install in every single room of the building.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Imperative Job Creation</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create a simple job</span>
kubectl create job db-migrate --image=easypj:v1 -- python manage.py migrate

<span class="comment"># Generate YAML first (exam tip: always verify before applying)</span>
kubectl create job db-migrate --image=easypj:v1 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml -- python manage.py migrate > job.yaml

<span class="comment"># Check job status</span>
kubectl get jobs
<span class="comment"># NAME         COMPLETIONS   DURATION   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># db-migrate   1/1           12s        30s</span>

<span class="comment"># View job pods</span>
kubectl get pods --selector=job-name=db-migrate
<span class="comment"># NAME               READY   STATUS      RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># db-migrate-abc12   0/1     Completed   0          45s</span>

<span class="comment"># Check logs</span>
kubectl logs job/db-migrate</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-migrate-job.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-db-migrate
spec:
  completions: 1              <span class="comment"># How many pods must complete successfully</span>
  parallelism: 1              <span class="comment"># How many pods run simultaneously</span>
  backoffLimit: 3             <span class="comment"># Retry up to 3 times before marking failed</span>
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 300  <span class="comment"># Timeout: kill after 5 minutes</span>
  ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 60 <span class="comment"># Auto-delete finished job after 60s</span>
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never    <span class="comment"># Jobs: Never or OnFailure (NOT Always!)</span>
      containers:
      - name: migrate
        image: easypj:v1
        command: ["python", "manage.py", "migrate"]
        env:
        - name: DATABASE_URL
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: easypj-db-secret
              key: url</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Job Configuration Fields</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">✅</span> completions</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How many pods must succeed. Default: 1. Set to N for batch: process N items.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚡</span> parallelism</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How many pods run at once. Default: 1. Increase for faster batch processing.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> backoffLimit</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Retry count before job is marked Failed. Default: 6. Set lower for fast failure.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⏱️</span> activeDeadlineSeconds</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Hard timeout. Job is killed if it runs longer, regardless of retries.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗑️</span> ttlSecondsAfterFinished</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Auto-cleanup. Completed/failed job is deleted after this many seconds.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔁</span> restartPolicy</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Never</strong> (creates new pod on failure) or <strong>OnFailure</strong> (restarts container in same pod). Never <code>Always</code>!</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ <code>activeDeadlineSeconds</code> takes precedence over <code>backoffLimit</code></h5>
                    <p>If both limits are set and the wall-clock deadline is hit BEFORE <code>backoffLimit</code> retries are exhausted, the Job is still terminated immediately (reason <code>DeadlineExceeded</code>) — the deadline wins regardless of how many retries were left. Also note: with <code>restartPolicy: OnFailure</code>, in-place container restarts (the SAME pod restarting) still count toward <code>backoffLimit</code>, not just brand-new pod creations under <code>restartPolicy: Never</code> — a container that keeps crash-looping in place will still exhaust the limit and fail the Job.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Job Lifecycle — Run, Complete, Stop</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Job: easypj-db-migrate (completions: 1, backoffLimit: 3)

  Attempt 1:
  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Pod: migrate │ → Runs python manage.py migrate
  │   🔄 Running │ → ...
  │   ✅ Success │ → Exit code 0
  └──────────────┘
  Job Status: Complete ✅  (1/1 completions)

  ─── What if it fails? ───

  Attempt 1:
  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Pod: migrate │ → DB connection refused
  │   ❌ Failed  │ → Exit code 1
  └──────────────┘
  Attempt 2:  (backoff: 10s wait)
  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Pod: migrate │ → DB now available
  │   ✅ Success │ → Exit code 0
  └──────────────┘
  Job Status: Complete ✅  (1/1 completions, 1 retry)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Trap: restartPolicy</h5>
                    <p>Jobs CANNOT use <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> (that's for Deployments). If you forget this, the Job will be rejected. Use <code>Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Batch Processing with Parallelism</h4>
                <p>Jobs can process multiple items in parallel. Set <code>completions</code> for how many must finish and <code>parallelism</code> for how many run at once.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">batch-processing-job.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-image-processor
spec:
  completions: 10       <span class="comment"># 10 total items to process</span>
  parallelism: 3        <span class="comment"># Run 3 pods at a time</span>
  backoffLimit: 5
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
      - name: processor
        image: easypj:v1
        command: ["python", "manage.py", "process_images"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Batch Job: 10 Completions, 3 Parallel</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Job: easypj-image-processor (completions: 10, parallelism: 3)

  Wave 1: [📦 Pod-1] [📦 Pod-2] [📦 Pod-3]   ← 3 running
               ✅         ✅         ✅
  Wave 2: [📦 Pod-4] [📦 Pod-5] [📦 Pod-6]   ← 3 running
               ✅         ✅         ✅
  Wave 3: [📦 Pod-7] [📦 Pod-8] [📦 Pod-9]   ← 3 running
               ✅         ✅         ✅
  Wave 4: [📦 Pod-10]                        ← 1 running (last item)
               ✅
  Job Status: Complete ✅  (10/10 completions)

  Total time: ~4 waves instead of 10 sequential runs!
  Parallelism gives ~3x speedup ⚡
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>Indexed Jobs — Giving Each Pod a Fixed, Known Index</h4>
                <p>The parallel Job above has NO way for a pod to know "which of the 10 items am I processing?" — pods start in any order with no identity. <code>completionMode: Indexed</code> fixes this by giving each pod a stable index (0 through <code>completions</code>-1), enabling sharded work without any external coordination.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Indexed Job — each pod knows its own shard number</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-sharded-import
spec:
  completions: 10
  parallelism: 10
  completionMode: Indexed          <span class="comment"># ← the key field</span>
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
      - name: importer
        image: easypj:v1
        command: ["sh", "-c", "python manage.py import_shard --index=$JOB_COMPLETION_INDEX"]
        <span class="comment"># JOB_COMPLETION_INDEX is auto-injected as an env var (0..9 here) — no</span>
        <span class="comment"># external coordination service needed to assign each pod its shard</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Field</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>completionMode: NonIndexed</code> (default)</td><td>pods are interchangeable — any pod completing counts toward <code>completions</code>, no per-pod identity</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>completionMode: Indexed</code></td><td>each pod gets a fixed <code>JOB_COMPLETION_INDEX</code> env var (and a matching pod hostname suffix) — the Job isn't "complete" until EVERY index 0..N-1 has succeeded, not just any N pods</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>podFailurePolicy</code></td><td>lets you treat SPECIFIC container exit codes as fatal (fail the whole Job immediately, ignoring <code>backoffLimit</code>) vs retryable — e.g. exit code 42 = "config error, don't bother retrying" vs exit code 1 = "transient, retry normally"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>spec.suspend: true</code></td><td>pauses a Job (or CronJob) WITHOUT deleting it — existing pods are deleted, no new ones created, and setting it back to <code>false</code> resumes exactly where completions left off</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>ttlSecondsAfterFinished</code> — automatic cleanup instead of manual deletion</h5>
                    <p>By default, a completed Job's pods linger forever (§16's Gotchas note this) until you manually <code>kubectl delete job</code>. Setting <code>spec.ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 3600</code> makes the Job (and its pods) automatically garbage-collected 1 hour after completion — genuinely useful for high-frequency Jobs (like the ones a busy CronJob spawns) where manual cleanup doesn't scale.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §16.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.2 CronJobs — Scheduled Tasks</h3>
                <p>CronJobs are like Unix <code>cron</code> but in Kubernetes. They create Jobs on a schedule — perfect for backups, reports, cleanup tasks, and periodic data processing.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Imperative CronJob Creation</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create a CronJob — daily backup at 2 AM</span>
kubectl create cronjob easypj-backup \
  --image=easypj:v1 \
  --schedule="0 2 * * *" \
  -- python manage.py dumpdata --output=/backup/db.json

<span class="comment"># Check CronJob</span>
kubectl get cronjobs
<span class="comment"># NAME             SCHEDULE      SUSPEND   ACTIVE   LAST SCHEDULE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-backup    0 2 * * *     False     0        30s             5m</span>

<span class="comment"># View recently created Jobs</span>
kubectl get jobs --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-backup-cronjob.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: easypj-backup
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"          <span class="comment"># Daily at 2:00 AM</span>
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid       <span class="comment"># Don't start new if previous still running</span>
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3   <span class="comment"># Keep last 3 successful job records</span>
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1       <span class="comment"># Keep last 1 failed job record</span>
  startingDeadlineSeconds: 200    <span class="comment"># If job can't start in 200s, skip this run</span>
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      backoffLimit: 2
      template:
        spec:
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          containers:
          - name: backup
            image: easypj:v1
            command:
            - /bin/sh
            - -c
            - "python manage.py dumpdata | gzip > /backup/$(date +%Y%m%d).json.gz"
            volumeMounts:
            - name: backup-volume
              mountPath: /backup
          volumes:
          - name: backup-volume
            persistentVolumeClaim:
              claimName: backup-pvc</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Schedule</th>
                                <th>Meaning</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>*/5 * * * *</code></td><td>Every 5 minutes</td><td>Health checks, cache refresh</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>0 2 * * *</code></td><td>Daily at 2:00 AM</td><td>Database backup</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>0 0 * * 0</code></td><td>Every Sunday at midnight</td><td>Weekly report generation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>0 */6 * * *</code></td><td>Every 6 hours</td><td>Data sync, cleanup</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>30 8 1 * *</code></td><td>1st of each month at 8:30 AM</td><td>Monthly invoice generation</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Cron Expression Format</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌───────────── minute (0-59)
  │ ┌───────────── hour (0-23)
  │ │ ┌───────────── day of month (1-31)
  │ │ │ ┌───────────── month (1-12)
  │ │ │ │ ┌───────────── day of week (0-6, Sun=0)
  │ │ │ │ │
  * * * * *

  Special characters:
  *      every value              a-b    a range (e.g. 1-5 = Mon-Fri)
  */n    every n units            a,b    a list (e.g. 1,3,5 = Mon,Wed,Fri)

  Examples:
  0 2 * * *     = "At 02:00 every day"
  */5 * * * *   = "Every 5 minutes"
  0 0 * * 0     = "At midnight every Sunday"
  30 8 1 * *    = "At 08:30 on the 1st of every month"
  30 8 * * 1-5  = "At 08:30 on weekdays (Mon-Fri)" ← range syntax in action
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>concurrencyPolicy: Forbid</h5>
                        <p>Don't start new job if previous is still running. Prevents overlap. Safest for DB backups.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>concurrencyPolicy: Replace</h5>
                        <p>Kill running job and start a new one. Good for "latest data only" scenarios.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Third Option: concurrencyPolicy: Allow (default)</h5>
                    <p><code>Allow</code> lets multiple jobs run simultaneously. If CronJob fires every 5 minutes but a job takes 7 minutes, you'll have overlapping runs. This is fine for independent tasks (e.g., sending reports) but <strong>dangerous for database operations</strong> where concurrent writes could corrupt data. Always set <code>Forbid</code> for DB backups.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Timezones and Missed Schedules — Two Fields That Prevent Real Production Surprises</h4>
                <p>A CronJob's clock isn't your laptop's clock, and a CronJob that's briefly unable to fire doesn't silently "catch up" by default — both behaviors are configurable, and both have caused real incidents when assumed rather than checked.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Pinning a timezone and bounding how "late" a missed run may still fire</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: easypj-nightly-report
spec:
  schedule: "0 9 * * 1-5"            <span class="comment"># "9am weekdays" — but 9am WHERE?</span>
  timeZone: "America/New_York"       <span class="comment"># pins it to a real timezone, DST-aware</span>
  startingDeadlineSeconds: 200       <span class="comment"># if the controller was down/late, only fire</span>
                                      <span class="comment"># if we're within 200s of the scheduled time —</span>
                                      <span class="comment"># otherwise skip this tick entirely, don't "catch up"</span>
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          containers:
          - name: report
            image: easypj:v1
            command: ["python", "manage.py", "send_daily_report"]</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Field</th><th>Without it</th><th>With it</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>spec.timeZone</code></td><td>the schedule runs in the kube-controller-manager's OS timezone — almost always UTC, NOT your local time</td><td>runs in the named IANA timezone, correctly handling daylight-saving shifts (a fixed UTC offset does NOT handle DST)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>startingDeadlineSeconds</code></td><td>a missed tick (controller was down, or the previous run was still going under <code>Forbid</code>) is simply skipped forever — no catch-up run ever fires</td><td>bounds HOW missed the tick can be before giving up — set generously if a late-but-still-useful run is better than none, or tightly if a stale run would be actively harmful</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ "0 2 * * *" without <code>timeZone</code> is UTC 2am, not YOUR 2am — a real production incident pattern</h5>
                    <p>Teams that assume the cluster's local time equals their own timezone have shipped backup jobs that fire during peak business hours instead of the intended off-peak window. Always set <code>spec.timeZone</code> explicitly for anything time-sensitive rather than trusting an implicit UTC assumption to match your intent.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §16.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.3 DaemonSets — One Pod Per Node</h3>
                <p>A DaemonSet ensures that <strong>exactly one copy of a pod runs on every node</strong> (or every selected node). When a new node joins the cluster, the DaemonSet automatically schedules a pod there. When a node is removed, the pod is garbage-collected.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Common DaemonSet Use Cases</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>📋 <strong>Log collectors</strong> — Fluentd, Filebeat, Promtail</li>
                        <li>📊 <strong>Monitoring agents</strong> — Node Exporter, Datadog Agent</li>
                        <li>🌐 <strong>Network plugins</strong> — Calico, Cilium, Flannel (CNI)</li>
                        <li>💾 <strong>Storage daemons</strong> — Ceph, GlusterFS</li>
                        <li>🔐 <strong>Security agents</strong> — Falco, Twistlock</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">fluentd-daemonset.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: fluentd
  namespace: kube-system
  labels:
    app: fluentd
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: fluentd
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: fluentd
    spec:
      tolerations:
      <span class="comment"># Tolerate control plane taint to run on ALL nodes</span>
      - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
        operator: Exists
        effect: NoSchedule
      containers:
      - name: fluentd
        image: fluentd:v1.16
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "200Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "200m"
            memory: "400Mi"
        volumeMounts:
        - name: varlog
          mountPath: /var/log
        - name: varlibdockercontainers
          mountPath: /var/lib/docker/containers
          readOnly: true
      volumes:
      - name: varlog
        hostPath:
          path: /var/log
      - name: varlibdockercontainers
        hostPath:
          path: /var/lib/docker/containers</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">DaemonSet — Automatic Pod Per Node</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  DaemonSet: "fluentd"   (replicas: N/A — auto-managed)

  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐
  │    worker-1      │  │    worker-2      │  │    worker-3      │
  │  ┌────────────┐  │  │  ┌────────────┐  │  │  ┌────────────┐  │
  │  │  fluentd   │  │  │  │  fluentd   │  │  │  │  fluentd   │  │
  │  │  📋 logs   │  │  │  │  📋 logs   │  │  │  │  📋 logs   │  │
  │  └────────────┘  │  │  └────────────┘  │  │  └────────────┘  │
  │  + easypj pods   │  │  + easypj pods   │  │  + easypj pods   │
  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘

  ┌──────────────────┐
  │    worker-4      │  ← NEW node joins cluster!
  │  ┌────────────┐  │
  │  │  fluentd   │  │  DaemonSet controller AUTOMATICALLY
  │  │  📋 logs   │  │  schedules a pod here! 🔄
  │  └────────────┘  │
  └──────────────────┘

  ✅ Every node always has exactly 1 fluentd pod
  ✅ New nodes get a pod automatically
  ✅ Removed nodes — pod is cleaned up automatically
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">DaemonSet Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View DaemonSets</span>
kubectl get daemonsets -A
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE     NAME         DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   UP-TO-DATE   NODE SELECTOR   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-system   fluentd      4         4         4       4            &lt;none&gt;          10d</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-system   kube-proxy   4         4         4       4            &lt;none&gt;          30d</span>

<span class="comment"># DaemonSet on specific nodes only (using nodeSelector)</span>
<span class="comment"># Add nodeSelector to template.spec to limit which nodes get the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Check which nodes have DaemonSet pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l app=fluentd -o wide</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Tip: No Imperative Command for DaemonSets</h5>
                    <p>Unlike Deployments and Jobs, there's no <code>kubectl create daemonset</code> command. You must write YAML. Quick trick: generate a Deployment YAML, then change <code>kind: Deployment</code> to <code>kind: DaemonSet</code> and remove the <code>replicas</code> field.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>DaemonSet Update Strategy</h4>
                <p>DaemonSets support two update strategies, similar to Deployments:</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Strategy</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>RollingUpdate</strong> (default)</td>
                                <td>Pods updated node by node — old pod killed, new pod started on each node sequentially</td>
                                <td>Most updates — zero gap per node</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>OnDelete</strong></td>
                                <td>Pods only updated when you manually delete them — full control over rollout pace</td>
                                <td>Critical system daemons where you want to control timing (e.g., CNI plugin)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">DaemonSet Update Strategy YAML</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># In the DaemonSet spec:</span>
spec:
  updateStrategy:
    type: RollingUpdate        <span class="comment"># or OnDelete</span>
    rollingUpdate:
      maxUnavailable: 1        <span class="comment"># Update 1 node at a time (default)</span>
      maxSurge: 1               <span class="comment"># stable since v1.25 — creates the NEW pod on a node</span>
                                 <span class="comment"># BEFORE killing the old one there (like a Deployment's</span>
                                 <span class="comment"># surge), avoiding a gap in per-node coverage during updates.</span>
                                 <span class="comment"># The only real constraint: they can't BOTH be 0 (something</span>
                                 <span class="comment"># has to allow the update to actually make progress).</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>DaemonSet Scheduling — Node Affinity Under the Hood, Not a Special Code Path</h4>
                <p>DaemonSet pods LOOK like they bypass the scheduler entirely, but since v1.17 they don't run through a separate mechanism — the DaemonSet controller simply injects a <code>requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution</code> node affinity term naming that EXACT node, and the normal scheduler binds it like any other pod. This unification is why DaemonSet pods correctly respect taints, resource pressure, and other filters just like everything else in this chapter.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">What the DaemonSet controller actually generates per node</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   You write:
     apiVersion: apps/v1
     kind: DaemonSet
     spec:
       template: {spec: {containers: [...]}}    ← no nodeName, no nodeSelector needed

   The DaemonSet controller, PER ELIGIBLE NODE, generates a pod with an
   INJECTED affinity term the controller adds automatically:
     spec:
       affinity:
         nodeAffinity:
           requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
             nodeSelectorTerms:
             - matchFields:
               - {key: metadata.name, operator: In, values: ["worker-1"]}

   The NORMAL scheduler then binds this pod — it still runs Filter (so an
   untolerated taint on worker-1 correctly blocks it) and Score is a no-op
   (only one node can ever match "metadata.name = worker-1").
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 This is exactly why an untolerated taint blocks a DaemonSet pod too — it's not a special exception</h5>
                    <p>Because DaemonSet pods go through the SAME Filter phase as any other pod (§14.1), a control-plane taint without a matching toleration correctly excludes them — which is precisely why real DaemonSets like <code>kube-proxy</code> ship with an explicit <code>tolerations: [{operator: Exists}]</code> (tolerate everything) baked into their manifest, rather than DaemonSets having some inherent taint-bypass privilege.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The DaemonSet admission controller auto-injects a WHOLE SET of tolerations, not just one</h5>
                    <p>Beyond whatever you write by hand, every DaemonSet pod automatically gets tolerations for: <code>node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute</code>, <code>.../unreachable:NoExecute</code>, <code>.../disk-pressure:NoSchedule</code>, <code>.../memory-pressure:NoSchedule</code>, <code>.../pid-pressure:NoSchedule</code>, <code>.../unschedulable:NoSchedule</code>, and <code>.../network-unavailable:NoSchedule</code> (host-network pods only). This is exactly why a logging or monitoring DaemonSet pod keeps running on a node that just went <code>NotReady</code> or under memory pressure, while an ordinary Deployment pod would be evicted or blocked — and it's automatic, not something you configure yourself.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §16.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.4 StatefulSets — Stateful Applications</h3>
                <p>StatefulSets are for applications that need <strong>stable, unique identity</strong> and <strong>persistent storage</strong>. Unlike Deployments (random pod names, shared storage), StatefulSets provide predictable pod names, ordered deployment, and per-pod persistent volumes.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Deployment</th>
                                <th>StatefulSet</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Pod Names</strong></td>
                                <td>Random: <code>app-x7k2z</code>, <code>app-m9p4q</code></td>
                                <td>Ordered: <code>app-0</code>, <code>app-1</code>, <code>app-2</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>DNS Identity</strong></td>
                                <td>Only via Service (shared)</td>
                                <td>Each pod gets own DNS: <code>app-0.svc.ns</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Storage</strong></td>
                                <td>Shared or none</td>
                                <td>Per-pod PVC (survives pod restarts)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Creation Order</strong></td>
                                <td>All at once (parallel)</td>
                                <td>Sequential: 0 → 1 → 2 (ordered)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Deletion Order</strong></td>
                                <td>Random</td>
                                <td>Reverse: 2 → 1 → 0 (ordered)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Use Case</strong></td>
                                <td>Stateless apps (easypj Django)</td>
                                <td>Databases, Kafka, Redis Cluster, ZooKeeper</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">postgresql-statefulset.yaml — easypj Database</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Headless Service (required for StatefulSet DNS)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: postgres-headless
spec:
  clusterIP: None               <span class="comment"># Headless — no cluster IP</span>
  selector:
    app: postgres
  ports:
  - port: 5432
    name: postgres
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: postgres
spec:
  serviceName: "postgres-headless"  <span class="comment"># Required: links to headless service</span>
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: postgres
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: postgres
        image: postgres:16
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5432
        env:
        - name: POSTGRES_DB
          value: "easypj"
        - name: PGDATA
          value: "/var/lib/postgresql/data/pgdata"
        volumeMounts:
        - name: data
          mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "500m"
            memory: "1Gi"
          limits:
            cpu: "1"
            memory: "2Gi"
  volumeClaimTemplates:            <span class="comment"># Per-pod PVC template</span>
  - metadata:
      name: data
    spec:
      accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
      storageClassName: standard
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 10Gi</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">StatefulSet — Ordered Pods with Stable Identity &amp; Per-Pod Storage</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  StatefulSet: "postgres"    replicas: 3
  serviceName: "postgres-headless"

  Deployment Pods:             StatefulSet Pods:
  (random names)               (ordered, predictable)
  ┌──────────────┐             ┌──────────────┐
  │ app-x7k2z    │             │ postgres-0   │ ← Created FIRST (primary)
  ├──────────────┤             │  DNS: postgres-0.postgres-headless
  │ app-m9p4q    │             ├──────────────┤
  ├──────────────┤             │ postgres-1   │ ← Created SECOND (must wait for 0)
  │ app-j3r8w    │             │  DNS: postgres-1.postgres-headless
  └──────────────┘             ├──────────────┤
                               │ postgres-2   │ ← Created THIRD
                               │  DNS: postgres-2.postgres-headless
                               └──────────────┘

  Per-Pod Persistent Volumes (survive pod restarts!):
  ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐
  │postgres-0│     │postgres-1│     │postgres-2│
  │┌────────┐│     │┌────────┐│     │┌────────┐│
  ││data-   ││     ││data-   ││     ││data-   ││
  ││postgres││     ││postgres││     ││postgres││
  ││-0      ││     ││-1      ││     ││-2      ││
  ││ 10Gi   ││     ││ 10Gi   ││     ││ 10Gi   ││
  │└───┬────┘│     │└───┬────┘│     │└───┬────┘│
  └────┼─────┘     └────┼─────┘     └────┼─────┘
       ▼                ▼                ▼
  ┌─────────┐     ┌─────────┐     ┌─────────┐
  │  PV-0   │     │  PV-1   │     │  PV-2   │
  │ (disk)  │     │ (disk)  │     │ (disk)  │
  └─────────┘     └─────────┘     └─────────┘

  Key guarantees:
  ✅ Stable network identity  — postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc
  ✅ Ordered deployment       — 0 → 1 → 2 (each must be Ready before next)
  ✅ Ordered deletion         — 2 → 1 → 0 (reverse order)
  ✅ Stable storage           — PVC survives pod restart (data preserved!)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ StatefulSet Requires a Headless Service</h5>
                    <p>You <strong>must</strong> create a headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>) for the StatefulSet. The <code>serviceName</code> field in the StatefulSet spec must match the Service name. This enables DNS records for each pod like <code>pod-name.service-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 StatefulSet Deletion — PVCs Are NOT Deleted!</h5>
                    <p>When you delete a StatefulSet (even with <code>--cascade=foreground</code>), the <strong>PersistentVolumeClaims are preserved</strong>. This is by design to prevent data loss. You must manually delete them:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>kubectl delete statefulset postgres</code> → Pods deleted, PVCs remain ✅</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl get pvc</code> → <code>data-postgres-0</code>, <code>data-postgres-1</code>, <code>data-postgres-2</code> still exist</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl delete pvc data-postgres-0 data-postgres-1 data-postgres-2</code> → Manually clean up</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>This is intentional — recreating the StatefulSet will automatically reattach to existing PVCs, preserving all data!</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy</code> — this "NEVER deletes PVCs" behavior is now just the DEFAULT, not the only option</h5>
                    <p>Everything above describes <code>Retain</code>, the default policy. Since Kubernetes v1.32 (GA), <code>spec.persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy</code> lets an admin explicitly opt IN to auto-deletion via two independent sub-policies:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy:
    whenDeleted: Retain    <span class="comment"># Retain (default) or Delete — what happens to PVCs when the WHOLE StatefulSet is deleted</span>
    whenScaled:  Retain    <span class="comment"># Retain (default) or Delete — what happens to PVCs when replicas SCALE DOWN</span></code></pre>
                    <p class="mb-0">Setting either to <code>Delete</code> makes Kubernetes automatically clean up the corresponding PVCs instead of leaving them orphaned — genuinely useful for ephemeral/test StatefulSets where manual PVC cleanup is pure toil, but a real footgun if set carelessly on a production database. Know this field exists so "PVCs are never auto-deleted" doesn't become a stale assumption on a newer cluster.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">StatefulSet Scaling — Ordered Operations</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Scale up — pods created in order (3, 4, 5...)</span>
kubectl scale statefulset postgres --replicas=5
<span class="comment"># postgres-3 created (waits until Ready)</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-4 created (waits until Ready)</span>

<span class="comment"># Scale down — pods deleted in REVERSE order (4, 3)</span>
kubectl scale statefulset postgres --replicas=3
<span class="comment"># postgres-4 terminated first</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-3 terminated second</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-0, 1, 2 untouched ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># PVCs from scaled-down pods are PRESERVED</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># data-postgres-3 and data-postgres-4 still exist</span>
<span class="comment"># Re-scaling up will reattach to them!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Choosing the Right Workload Type</h4>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚀</div>
                        <h5>Deployment</h5>
                        <p>Stateless apps that run forever.<br><strong>easypj Django web server</strong>, nginx, API services.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗄️</div>
                        <h5>StatefulSet</h5>
                        <p>Apps needing stable identity &amp; storage.<br><strong>PostgreSQL</strong>, MySQL, Kafka, Redis Cluster.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>DaemonSet</h5>
                        <p>One pod on every node.<br><strong>Log collectors</strong>, monitoring agents, CNI plugins.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Job / CronJob</h5>
                        <p>Run-to-completion / scheduled tasks.<br><strong>DB migrations</strong>, backups, batch processing.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Decision Tree: Which Workload Type Do I Need?</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
                        ┌──────────────────┐
                        │  New Workload?   │
                        └────────┬─────────┘
                                 │
                    ┌────────────┴────────────┐
                    │                         │
              Runs forever?              Runs once/scheduled?
                    │                         │
              ┌─────┴─────┐             ┌─────┴─────┐
              │           │             │           │
        All nodes?   Some pods?    On schedule?   One-time?
              │           │             │           │
         DaemonSet  ┌─────┴─────┐   CronJob      Job
                    │           │
              Needs stable   Stateless?
              identity/PVC?     │
                    │       Deployment
              StatefulSet

  ╔════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  easypj Stack Mapping:                                ║
  ║                                                        ║
  ║  📦 easypj Django      → Deployment (stateless)       ║
  ║  🗄️ PostgreSQL         → StatefulSet (needs storage)  ║
  ║  📋 Fluentd logging    → DaemonSet (every node)       ║
  ║  🔄 DB migration       → Job (run once)               ║
  ║  💾 Nightly backup     → CronJob (scheduled)          ║
  ╚════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>StatefulSet Update Strategy — Partitioned Rollouts as a Canary for Stateful Apps</h4>
                <p>A StatefulSet's default <code>RollingUpdate</code> strategy updates pods in REVERSE ordinal order (highest first — the opposite of creation order), one at a time, each waiting for the previous to be Ready. The <code>partition</code> field turns this into a genuine canary mechanism for databases and other stateful apps that can't use the Deployment-style two-Deployment canary from Chapter 13.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Partitioned update — canary ONE replica of a StatefulSet</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  updateStrategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      partition: 2              <span class="comment"># ONLY pods with ordinal >= 2 get updated</span>
  replicas: 3                   <span class="comment"># pods: easypj-db-0, easypj-db-1, easypj-db-2</span>
  <span class="comment"># With partition:2 and replicas:3, ONLY easypj-db-2 updates when you change</span>
  <span class="comment"># the image — db-0 and db-1 stay on the OLD version untouched. Watch db-2</span>
  <span class="comment"># behave correctly, THEN lower partition to 0 to roll out the rest.</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Field</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>rollingUpdate.partition: N</code></td><td>only ordinals ≥ N are updated when the pod template changes — everything below N is left alone, giving you a manual canary gate for a stateful workload</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>podManagementPolicy: OrderedReady</code> (default)</td><td>strict one-at-a-time creation/deletion, each waiting for the previous pod to be Ready — required for apps where startup order genuinely matters (e.g. a primary must exist before a replica)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>podManagementPolicy: Parallel</code></td><td>all pods created/deleted AT ONCE, no ordering wait — appropriate for a clustered app that handles concurrent bring-up itself (e.g. Cassandra), trading the ordering guarantee for much faster scale-out</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why partition beats "just don't touch it yet" for a real canary</h5>
                    <p>Without <code>partition</code>, updating a StatefulSet's image starts rolling immediately from the highest ordinal down — you'd have to manually pause it (there's no <code>rollout pause</code> for StatefulSets the way Deployments have one). <code>partition</code> is the StatefulSet-native way to hold a rollout at an exact, controlled boundary, verify the canary replica, then lower the number to let the rest follow.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>updateStrategy.rollingUpdate.maxUnavailable</code> (Beta, opt-in) — updating more than one pod at a time</h5>
                    <p>Everything above assumes strict one-pod-at-a-time updates — the long-standing default. A newer, still-Beta field lets you set <code>maxUnavailable</code> on a StatefulSet's rolling update the same way a Deployment does, allowing several pods to update simultaneously ("bursting") in exchange for speed. It's off by default specifically because most stateful apps (databases, queues) depend on the strict one-at-a-time guarantee for correctness — only enable it for a stateful app you've confirmed tolerates concurrent member updates.</p>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Each Workload Controller Manages Pod Lifecycles: Job Completion, CronJob Scheduling, DaemonSet Placement, and StatefulSet Identity</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Controller Comparison — Four Different Pod Management Strategies</h5>
            <p>Jobs, CronJobs, DaemonSets, and StatefulSets each solve a different problem. Their controllers use fundamentally different reconciliation strategies. Understanding these differences helps you choose the right workload type.</p>
            <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Controller</th><th>Pod Lifecycle</th><th>Identity</th><th>When Pod Dies</th><th>Use Case</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Deployment</strong></td><td>Pod runs indefinitely</td><td>Random suffix (-abcde)</td><td>Replaced by RS controller</td><td>Stateless web apps</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Job</strong></td><td>Pod runs to COMPLETION</td><td>Random suffix</td><td>NOT replaced (if succeeded)</td><td>Batch processing, DB migrations</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>CronJob</strong></td><td>Creates Job on schedule</td><td>Job name + timestamp</td><td>Jobs auto-cleaned (history limit)</td><td>Scheduled tasks, backups</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>DaemonSet</strong></td><td>ONE pod per node</td><td>Pod name = &lt;ds-name&gt;-&lt;node&gt;</td><td>Recreated on same node</td><td>Logging, monitoring agents</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>StatefulSet</strong></td><td>Ordered, predictable</td><td>Ordinal index (-0, -1, -2)</td><td>Replaced with SAME name</td><td>Databases, Kafka, ZK</td></tr>
            </tbody>
            </table>
            <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> each controller below reconciles toward a fundamentally different definition of "correct" — a Job's is a COUNT of successes, a CronJob's is a TIME, a DaemonSet's is a SET of nodes, and a StatefulSet's is an ORDERED SEQUENCE. Same watch-diff-act loop from Chapter 1, four different notions of "done."</p>

            <h5>① Job Controller — Completion and Parallelism Internals</h5>
            <p>§16.1 shows <code>completions</code> and <code>parallelism</code> as configuration fields — here's the controller loop that actually turns them into pod-creation decisions, and exactly how a failure counts toward <code>backoffLimit</code>.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  JOB CONTROLLER — Completion Tracking                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Job: easypj-db-migrate (completions=1, backoffLimit=6)                      │
│                                                                              │
│  1. Job Controller creates Pod: easypj-db-migrate-abcde                       │
│  2. Pod runs "python manage.py migrate"                                      │
│  3. Pod exits with code 0 (SUCCESS)                                           │
│  4. Job Controller updates Job status:                                        │
│     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐                  │
│     │ status.succeeded: 1                                   │                  │
│     │ status.completionTime: 2026-06-02T10:00:05Z          │                  │
│     │                                                       │                  │
│     │ Job is COMPLETE (status.conditions[type=Complete])    │                  │
│     │ Pod is NOT deleted (kept for logs)                    │                  │
│     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Failure Handling:                                                            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Pod fails → backoffLimit counter increments                         │    │
│  │ Pod retries with exponential backoff (10s, 20s, 40s, 80s, ...)      │    │
│  │ After 6 failures → Job marked as FAILED                              │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ PARALLELISM:                                                         │    │
│  │ completions=10, parallelism=3                                        │    │
│  │ → Job controller maintains 3 running pods at all times               │    │
│  │ → As each pod completes, a new one starts (up to 10 total)           │    │
│  │ → Total time: ~ceil(10/3) × single-pod-time ≈ 3-4x speedup          │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field the controller is tracking</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>status.succeeded</code></td><td>count of pods that exited 0 — the ONLY thing that advances a Job toward <code>completions</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>status.failed</code></td><td>count toward <code>backoffLimit</code> — once this reaches the limit, the Job stops creating new pods and is marked Failed permanently</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Exponential backoff (10s, 20s, 40s...)</td><td>prevents a rapidly-crash-looping Job from hammering the API server with pod creates — capped at a maximum delay (6 minutes)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>kubectl describe job</code>'s Events section shows each attempt's outcome — for a Job that "isn't completing," check <code>status.failed</code> against <code>backoffLimit</code> first; a Job sitting at <code>failed: 6/6</code> has permanently given up, and re-running it means deleting and recreating the Job object, not waiting longer.</div>

            <h5>② CronJob Controller — Scheduling Internals</h5>
            <p>A CronJob isn't a timer that directly runs pods — it's a thin polling loop that watches the clock and delegates everything else to the Job controller from ①. Seeing that hand-off explicitly clarifies why CronJob issues (missed runs, overlap) and Job issues (stuck pods, backoff) are diagnosed with two different toolsets.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CRONJOB CONTROLLER — Schedule Evaluation                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  CronJob: easypj-backup (schedule: "0 2 * * *")                              │
│                                                                              │
│  1. CronJob controller checks clock every 10 seconds                         │
│  2. Evaluates schedule: "Should a job have been created since last check?"   │
│  3. If YES → creates Job: easypj-backup-1717296000 (epoch timestamp)         │
│  4. Job controller takes over (creates pods, tracks completion)              │
│                                                                              │
│  overlap POLICY (concurrencyPolicy):                                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Allow (default):    New job runs even if previous job still running │    │
│  │ Forbid:             Skip this run if previous job hasn't finished   │    │
│  │ Replace:            Kill previous job, start new one                │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  missed SCHEDULES (startingDeadlineSeconds):                                  │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ If control plane was DOWN when a scheduled run was due:              │    │
│  │   startingDeadlineSeconds: 200 (EXAMPLE — the field is optional      │    │
│  │                             with NO default; unset = no deadline)    │    │
│  │   → set to 200: if CronJob is 100s late, it still runs the missed job│    │
│  │   → set to 200: if CronJob is 300s late, it SKIPS that missed job    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  THE HARD CUTOFF — no startingDeadlineSeconds does NOT mean "always catch   │
│  up eventually": if a CronJob accumulates MORE THAN 100 missed schedules   │
│  (e.g. after a multi-day controller outage), the controller gives up       │
│  entirely and logs "too many missed start times (&gt; 100)" — NO catch-up    │
│  Job is ever created, even once the cluster recovers. A silently-stopped   │
│  nightly backup after a long outage is exactly this failure mode.          │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>10-second polling loop</td><td>the CronJob controller doesn't fire pods itself — it just creates Job objects on schedule and hands off; the Job controller (① above) takes it from there</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Job name suffix (epoch timestamp)</td><td>guarantees a unique, sortable name per tick — <code>kubectl get jobs</code> sorted by name shows execution history in order</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>successfulJobsHistoryLimit</code> / <code>failedJobsHistoryLimit</code></td><td>old Job objects (and their pods) beyond these counts are garbage-collected automatically — this is WHY completed Jobs don't accumulate forever the way a plain Job's pods can (§16.1's <code>ttlSecondsAfterFinished</code> note)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> a CronJob "not firing" usually traces to one of three things — <code>suspend: true</code> left on from testing, <code>concurrencyPolicy: Forbid</code> combined with a previous run that's stuck and never finishing, or the schedule itself being written in a different timezone than intended (§16.2). Check all three, in that order, before assuming the controller itself is broken.</div>

            <h5>③ DaemonSet Controller — One-Per-Node Guarantee</h5>
            <p>§16.3's body already covered the modern (v1.17+) scheduling mechanism DaemonSets use — this traces the controller's full reconcile loop around it: how it watches the node list, decides where a pod is missing, and cleans up after a node leaves.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  DAEMONSET CONTROLLER — Node-by-Node Placement                               │
│                                                                              │
│  DaemonSet: fluentd-logger                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  1. DaemonSet Controller WATCHES node list                                   │
│  2. For each node that matches nodeSelector/tolerations:                     │
│     → Check: Does a fluentd-logger pod already run on this node?            │
│     → NO  → Create a pod TARGETING that node (see §16.3's mechanism)         │
│     → YES → Do nothing                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  3. NEW NODE joins cluster:                                                  │
│     → DaemonSet Controller detects new node                                  │
│     → Creates fluentd-logger-&lt;newnode&gt;                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  4. NODE is DELETED:                                                         │
│     → DaemonSet pod on that node is garbage collected (orphaned)             │
│                                                                              │
│  Since v1.17, DaemonSet pods go through the NORMAL scheduler — the           │
│  controller injects a node-specific affinity term (§16.3), it does NOT      │
│  set pod.spec.nodeName directly the way static pods do:                      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ pod.spec.affinity.nodeAffinity:                                      │    │
│  │   requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:                    │    │
│  │     nodeSelectorTerms:                                               │    │
│  │     - matchFields:                                                   │    │
│  │       - key: metadata.name                                           │    │
│  │         operator: In                                                 │    │
│  │         values: ["worker-1"]                                         │    │
│  │  ← this pod STILL runs through Filter (taints etc. correctly apply)  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Old mental model (pre-v1.17)</th><th>Current reality</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>"DaemonSet pods bypass the scheduler via direct <code>nodeName</code>"</td><td>they go through the SAME Filter/Score/Bind pipeline as any pod — just with an injected affinity that can only ever match one node</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>"Taints don't apply to DaemonSets"</td><td>taints DO apply — this is exactly why <code>kube-proxy</code>'s and CNI DaemonSets ship an explicit blanket toleration (§16.3), not because DaemonSets get a free pass</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This correction matters for troubleshooting:</strong> a DaemonSet pod stuck Pending on a specific node behaves EXACTLY like any other stuck-Pending pod — read its <code>FailedScheduling</code> event (§14.1) rather than assuming DaemonSets have some special bypass that makes normal scheduling diagnostics not apply.</div>

            <h5>④ StatefulSet Controller — Stable Identity and Ordered Operations</h5>
            <p>§16.4 establishes WHAT a StatefulSet guarantees (stable names, stable storage, ordered operations) — this is the controller mechanism that actually delivers each of those three guarantees, tying pod identity directly to storage identity.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STATEFULSET CONTROLLER — Identity + Ordering                                │
│                                                                              │
│  StatefulSet: easypj-db (replicas=3, serviceName=easypj-db)                  │
│                                                                              │
│  POD NAMING (predictable, stable):                                            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ easypj-db-0  <span class="comment">← Created FIRST (ordinal 0)                            </span>│    │
│  │ easypj-db-1  <span class="comment">← Created AFTER db-0 is Ready                          </span>│    │
│  │ easypj-db-2  <span class="comment">← Created AFTER db-1 is Ready                          </span>│    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ POD IDENTITY COMPONENTS:                                             │    │
│  │ 1. Stable hostname: easypj-db-0.easypj-db.default.svc.cluster.local │    │
│  │ 2. Stable storage: PVC easypj-data-easypj-db-0 (unique per pod)     │    │
│  │ 3. Stable network ID: ordinal index preserved across rescheduling   │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ IF easypj-db-0 DIES:                                                 │    │
│  │ → Controller creates easypj-db-0 again (SAME name)                   │    │
│  │ → Reattaches SAME PVC (easypj-data-easypj-db-0)                      │    │
│  │ → Gets SAME network identity                                         │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  SCALE DOWN (reverse order):                                                 │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ easypj-db-2 terminated FIRST (highest ordinal)                       │    │
│  │ easypj-db-1 terminated SECOND                                        │    │
│  │ This ensures the "leader" (often db-0) is not accidentally removed  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  HEADLESS SERVICE (required for StatefulSet):                                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ apiVersion: v1                                                       │    │
│  │ kind: Service                                                        │    │
│  │ spec:                                                                │    │
│  │   clusterIP: None  <span class="comment">← HEADLESS (no virtual IP)                       </span>│    │
│  │   selector: {app: easypj-db}                                        │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ DNS returns individual pod IPs (not a single ClusterIP):            │    │
│  │ $ nslookup easypj-db                                                │    │
│  │ easypj-db-0.easypj-db → 10.244.1.5                                  │    │
│  │ easypj-db-1.easypj-db → 10.244.2.3                                  │    │
│  │ easypj-db-2.easypj-db → 10.244.3.7                                  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>PVC naming: <code>&lt;template&gt;-&lt;statefulset&gt;-&lt;ordinal&gt;</code></td><td>deterministic, not random — this is precisely what lets a recreated <code>easypj-db-0</code> find and reattach ITS OWN old PVC rather than a fresh empty one</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Ordered creation (0→1→2, each waits for Ready)</td><td>the controller's reconcile loop literally will not create ordinal N+1 until ordinal N's pod passes readiness — see §16.4's <code>podManagementPolicy: Parallel</code> for opting out of this</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Ordered deletion (2→1→0, reverse)</td><td>protects whichever ordinal your application treats as "primary" (usually 0) from being torn down before its replicas during a scale-down</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> The CKA tests workload controller selection. Remember: Deployment for stateless web apps (random names, rolling updates), Job for one-time tasks (exits on completion), CronJob for scheduled tasks (cron syntax), DaemonSet for node-level agents (one per node), StatefulSet for stateful apps (stable identity, ordered scaling, headless service required). Matching the wrong controller to a use case is a common exam mistake.
            </div>

            <h5>⑤ Garbage Collection Across All Four — the Same <code>ownerReferences</code> Chain From Chapter 13</h5>
            <p>Every controller in this chapter cleans up its children through the exact same mechanism Chapter 13's §13.2 introduced for Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod — <code>ownerReferences</code> cascading deletion. Nothing here is a special case; it's the same general-purpose garbage collector, four more times.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   kubectl delete job easypj-db-migrate
        │  the Job's pods carry ownerReferences pointing back to the Job
        ▼
   garbage collector deletes the owned pods too (Chapter 13 UTH ①'s mechanism)

   kubectl delete cronjob easypj-backup
        │  the CronJob's Jobs carry ownerReferences pointing back to the CronJob
        │  (and each Job's OWN pods point back to that Job — a THREE-level chain)
        ▼
   deleting the CronJob cascades through Jobs AND their pods, transitively

   kubectl delete statefulset easypj-db
        │  pods carry ownerReferences to the StatefulSet...
        │  ...but PVCs from volumeClaimTemplates do NOT (§16.4's Gotcha)
        ▼
   pods cascade-delete normally; PVCs are deliberately ORPHANED, not owned —
   this is a conscious exception to protect data, not an oversight
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The StatefulSet PVC exception is the one deliberate break in an otherwise-universal pattern.</strong> Recognizing that "everything cascades via ownerReferences EXCEPT StatefulSet PVCs, on purpose" turns a chapter's worth of separate facts (Job cleanup, CronJob history limits, DaemonSet node-removal cleanup, StatefulSet PVC retention) into one mental model with a single, deliberate carve-out.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Pick the type:</strong> Deployment (stateless, scalable), StatefulSet (stable id + per-pod storage), DaemonSet (one pod per node), Job (run-to-completion), CronJob (scheduled). Know WHEN to use each.</li>
                    <li><strong>A Job runs to completion and STOPS</strong>; <code>completions</code> = how many successes, <code>parallelism</code> = how many at once, <code>backoffLimit</code> = retries before Failed. <code>restartPolicy</code> must be <code>OnFailure</code>/<code>Never</code> — never <code>Always</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>A CronJob creates a Job per scheduled tick</strong>; <code>concurrencyPolicy</code> (Allow/Forbid/Replace) controls overlap; the schedule runs in the cluster's timezone (UTC unless <code>spec.timeZone</code> is set).</li>
                    <li><strong>A DaemonSet runs exactly one pod per eligible node</strong> and auto-adds/removes pods as nodes join/leave — there is no <code>replicas</code> field.</li>
                    <li><strong>A StatefulSet needs a HEADLESS Service</strong>; it gives ordered stable names (db-0, db-1), stable DNS, and a PVC per pod via <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code>. Scaling DOWN does NOT delete the PVCs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deployments manage ReplicaSets manage Pods</strong>; rolling update + <code>rollout undo</code> give safe ships and instant rollbacks (Ch 13 recap).</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> part of the 15% Workloads domain. Expect <code>create job</code>/<code>cronjob</code>, <code>--from=cronjob</code>, and "pick the right type" questions.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Job <code>backoffLimit</code></strong> — Default is 6 retries. If your job fails 6 times, it stops. Set <code>backoffLimit: 0</code> for no retries, or increase for flaky tasks</li>
                    <li><strong>CronJob schedule syntax</strong> — <code>*/5 * * * *</code> = every 5 minutes. The exam expects you to write cron expressions. Fields: minute, hour, day-of-month, month, day-of-week</li>
                    <li><strong>DaemonSet skips control plane</strong> — By default, DaemonSets don't run on control plane nodes (they have a <code>NoSchedule</code> taint). Add a toleration if needed</li>
                    <li><strong>StatefulSet pods are ordered</strong> — <code>web-0</code> must be Running before <code>web-1</code> starts. Deletion is reverse order. This matters for databases</li>
                    <li><strong>StatefulSet needs a headless Service</strong> — <code>clusterIP: None</code>. Each pod gets a stable DNS: <code>web-0.headless-svc.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Completed Job pods linger</strong> — a finished Job's pods stay around as <code>Completed</code> (not auto-deleted) until you delete the Job or set <code>ttlSecondsAfterFinished</code> (§16.1)</li>
                    <li><strong><code>concurrencyPolicy</code> defaults to <code>Allow</code></strong> — a long-running CronJob can pile up overlapping runs unless you explicitly set <code>Forbid</code> (§16.2)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
