                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What single field is illegal in a Job's pod template, and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>restartPolicy: Always</code> — it's rejected by the API server. A Job's pod template must use <code>Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f bad-job.yaml
<span class="comment"># Error from server: Job "x" is invalid: spec.template.spec.restartPolicy: Unsupported
# value: "Always": supported values: "OnFailure", "Never"</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>Always</code> is the Deployment default and would defeat the entire point of a Job — the container would complete, restart, complete, restart, forever, and the Job would never reach a Complete state.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Job has <code>completions: 5</code> and <code>parallelism: 2</code>. In your own words, what do the two numbers each control?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>completions</code> is the FINISH LINE — how many total successes are required before the Job is done. <code>parallelism</code> is the LANE COUNT — how many pods run concurrently at any one time.</p>
<pre>
  completions: 5, parallelism: 2
  Wave 1: [pod-1][pod-2]        ← 2 running (the "lanes")
  Wave 2: [pod-3][pod-4]        ← replace each as it succeeds
  Wave 3: [pod-5]               ← last one, Job now shows 5/5 (the "finish line")
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>With 5/2, the Job keeps 2 pods running, replacing each as it succeeds, until 5 total have completed — not "2 pods run 5 times each" and not "5 pods run 2 at a time forever."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What timezone does a CronJob's <code>schedule</code> fire in by default, and how would you change it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>UTC, by default — the kube-controller-manager's clock, not your laptop's local time. Set <code>spec.timeZone</code> on the CronJob to use a different one.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"
  timeZone: "America/New_York"   <span class="comment"># now really fires at 2am Eastern, DST-aware</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>"0 2 * * *" scheduling a backup for "2am" is a common exam/production trap when the reader assumes it means their own local 2am — always confirm the cluster's timezone assumption before trusting a schedule.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the three <code>concurrencyPolicy</code> values for a CronJob and what each does when a run is still active at the next scheduled tick.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>Allow</code> (default) — let them overlap. <code>Forbid</code> — skip the new tick entirely. <code>Replace</code> — kill the running Job and start the new one.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>If the previous run is still going</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>Allow</code> (default)</td><td>new run starts anyway — they overlap</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Forbid</code></td><td>this tick is skipped entirely</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Replace</code></td><td>old run is killed, new run starts</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Use <code>Forbid</code> for anything where overlap causes corruption (e.g. two simultaneous <code>pg_dump</code> writes to the same file) — it's the safe default for backups specifically, not the CronJob default overall.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A DaemonSet has no <code>replicas</code> field. How does Kubernetes decide how many pods to run?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The count auto-tracks the number of eligible nodes — one pod per node that passes the DaemonSet's nodeSelector/affinity and whose taints are tolerated. Add a node, get a pod; remove a node, its pod is garbage-collected.</p>
<pre>
  3 nodes  →  3 DaemonSet pods
  + worker-4 joins  →  4th pod auto-created (no action from you)
  − worker-2 leaves →  its pod is garbage-collected automatically
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is fundamentally different from a Deployment, where the scheduler is free to place N replicas anywhere — a DaemonSet's controller instead targets EVERY matching node individually (via an injected per-node affinity, still through the normal scheduler — §16.3's UTH ③), no more and no less.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why won't a plain DaemonSet's pods land on control-plane nodes by default, and how do you fix that?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Control-plane nodes carry the <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code> taint. Add a matching toleration (<code>operator: Exists</code>, that key, effect <code>NoSchedule</code>) to the DaemonSet's pod template to also run there.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">tolerations:
- key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
  operator: Exists
  effect: NoSchedule</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is exactly how real cluster DaemonSets like kube-proxy and CNI agents achieve "every node including masters" coverage — a plain DaemonSet without this toleration would silently skip control-plane nodes.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does a StatefulSet require a headless Service specifically, rather than a normal ClusterIP Service?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A normal Service load-balances across all matching pods behind ONE virtual IP, hiding individual pod identity. A headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>) instead creates a distinct DNS A-record per pod (<code>pod-0.svc-name...</code>), which is what gives each StatefulSet replica its stable, individually-addressable identity.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Normal Service</th><th>Headless Service</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>DNS resolves to</td><td>ONE virtual IP, load-balanced</td><td>one A-record PER pod</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>"Which pod am I talking to?"</td><td>unknowable — any of them</td><td>addressable: <code>db-0.svc</code>, <code>db-1.svc</code>...</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Clustered systems (Postgres replication, Kafka brokers, etcd) need to address specific peers by name — "the primary is db-0" only works if db-0 resolves to a stable, individual address, not a load-balanced pool.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the PVC names for StatefulSet <code>db</code> with 3 replicas, whose <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> entry is named <code>data</code>? Do they survive a scale-down to 1 replica?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>data-db-0</code>, <code>data-db-1</code>, <code>data-db-2</code> — following the pattern <code>&lt;volumeClaimTemplate&gt;-&lt;statefulset&gt;-&lt;ordinal&gt;</code>. Yes: scaling down to 1 replica deletes pods db-1 and db-2 but leaves <code>data-db-1</code> and <code>data-db-2</code> intact.</p>
<pre>
  kubectl scale sts db --replicas=1
  db-1, db-2 pods:      DELETED
  data-db-1, data-db-2 PVCs:   ← still exist! kubectl get pvc still shows them
  kubectl scale sts db --replicas=3   ← db-1/db-2 REATTACH to those same PVCs
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is intentional data protection — Kubernetes assumes the volumes may hold irreplaceable state. If you scale back up, the returning pods reattach to their original PVCs automatically; to actually reclaim the storage you must delete those PVCs yourself.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Walk through the decision tree, in order, for choosing a workload type for a brand-new component.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) Does it need stable identity + per-pod storage? → StatefulSet. (2) If not, does it need exactly one pod per node? → DaemonSet. (3) If not, does it run once to completion? → Job (or CronJob if scheduled). (4) Otherwise → Deployment.</p>
<pre>
  stable identity + storage?  ──YES──▶ StatefulSet
       │NO
  one pod per node?           ──YES──▶ DaemonSet
       │NO
  runs once to completion?    ──YES──▶ Job  (scheduled? → CronJob)
       │NO
  otherwise ──────────────────────────▶ Deployment
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Working the tree in this order (most-constrained need first) avoids the common mistake of defaulting to Deployment for everything and only noticing the stable-identity or one-per-node requirement after something breaks in production.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

