                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 16 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of workload-controller behavior — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Job named <code>batch-job</code> that runs <code>busybox</code>, executes <code>echo "Processing complete"</code>, and requires 5 successful completions with a parallelism of 2. Show the imperative and YAML approach.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative: generate base yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative: generate base YAML</span>
kubectl create job batch-job --image=busybox \
--dry-run=client -o yaml -- /bin/sh -c "echo Processing complete" \
> batch-job.yaml

<span class="comment"># Edit batch-job.yaml to add completions and parallelism:</span>
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: batch-job
spec:
  completions: 5       <span class="comment"># Total successful runs needed</span>
  parallelism: 2       <span class="comment"># Run 2 pods at a time</span>
  backoffLimit: 4      <span class="comment"># Max retries before marking failed</span>
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: batch-job
        image: busybox
        command: ["/bin/sh", "-c", "echo Processing complete"]
      restartPolicy: Never  <span class="comment"># Jobs require Never or OnFailure</span>

kubectl apply -f batch-job.yaml

<span class="comment"># Monitor progress</span>
kubectl get jobs batch-job -w
<span class="comment"># NAME       COMPLETIONS   DURATION</span>
<span class="comment"># batch-job  0/5           5s</span>
<span class="comment"># batch-job  2/5           12s</span>
<span class="comment"># batch-job  4/5           20s</span>
<span class="comment"># batch-job  5/5           28s</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Jobs run pods to completion. <code>completions</code> = how many TOTAL successful runs, <code>parallelism</code> = how many pods run simultaneously. With completions=5 and parallelism=2, Kubernetes runs 2 pods at a time, waits for them to succeed, then starts the next batch, until 5 total succeed. <code>restartPolicy</code> MUST be <code>Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code> (never <code>Always</code> — that's for Deployments). <code>backoffLimit</code> controls how many failures before the Job gives up. On the CKA, you'll likely need to add completions/parallelism to a generated YAML since the imperative command doesn't support them directly.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a CronJob that runs <code>curl http://health-check:8080/ping</code> every 5 minutes. Set it to keep 3 successful and 1 failed history.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative:</span>
kubectl create cronjob health-check \
--image=curlimages/curl \
--schedule="*/5 * * * *" \
-- curl -s http://health-check:8080/ping

<span class="comment"># Full YAML with history settings:</span>
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: health-check
spec:
  schedule: "*/5 * * * *"     <span class="comment"># Every 5 minutes</span>
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3  <span class="comment"># Keep 3 successful</span>
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1      <span class="comment"># Keep 1 failed</span>
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid      <span class="comment"># Don't overlap runs</span>
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          containers:
          - name: health-check
            image: curlimages/curl
            command: ["curl", "-s", "http://health-check:8080/ping"]
          restartPolicy: OnFailure

<span class="comment"># Cron schedule reference:</span>
<span class="comment"># ┌───────── minute (0-59)</span>
<span class="comment"># │ ┌─────── hour (0-23)</span>
<span class="comment"># │ │ ┌───── day of month (1-31)</span>
<span class="comment"># │ │ │ ┌─── month (1-12)</span>
<span class="comment"># │ │ │ │ ┌─ day of week (0-6, Sun=0)</span>
<span class="comment"># * * * * *</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CronJobs create Jobs on a schedule (like Unix cron). <code>successfulJobsHistoryLimit</code> and <code>failedJobsHistoryLimit</code> control how many old Job objects are kept (default: 3 successful, 1 failed). <code>concurrencyPolicy</code> can be: <code>Allow</code> (default, overlapping ok), <code>Forbid</code> (skip if previous still running), or <code>Replace</code> (kill previous, start new). On the CKA, <code>kubectl create cronjob</code> handles the basics imperatively, but you'll need to edit the YAML for history limits and concurrency policy. The cron schedule format is the same as standard Unix cron — memorize the 5-field order.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you manually trigger a one-time run from an existing CronJob? Show the command and explain what happens.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create a job from an existing cronjob</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create a Job from an existing CronJob</span>
kubectl create job manual-health-check \
--from=cronjob/health-check

<span class="comment"># This creates a Job using the CronJob's jobTemplate</span>
<span class="comment"># The Job runs immediately, regardless of schedule</span>

<span class="comment"># Monitor the triggered job</span>
kubectl get jobs
<span class="comment"># NAME                    COMPLETIONS  DURATION</span>
<span class="comment"># manual-health-check     1/1          5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the logs</span>
kubectl logs job/manual-health-check
<span class="comment"># OK</span>

<span class="comment"># The manually created job is independent:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not counted against history limits</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not affected by concurrencyPolicy</span>
<span class="comment"># - Must be deleted manually if needed</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl create job --from=cronjob/NAME</code> creates a one-off Job using the CronJob's template. This is essential for testing CronJobs without waiting for the next scheduled run. On the CKA, this command appears in scenarios like "verify the CronJob works before waiting." The <code>--from</code> flag copies the jobTemplate spec from the CronJob. The resulting Job is a standalone object — it won't appear in the CronJob's history, and the CronJob's concurrencyPolicy won't block it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between a DaemonSet and a Deployment? When must you use a DaemonSet?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Aspect</th>
                                        <th>Deployment</th>
                                        <th>DaemonSet</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Pod placement</td>
                                        <td>Scheduler decides which nodes</td>
                                        <td>Exactly ONE pod per node</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Scaling</td>
                                        <td>Manual or HPA (replicas count)</td>
                                        <td>Auto (scales with cluster size)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Use case</td>
                                        <td>Stateless apps, web servers</td>
                                        <td>Node-level agents, log collectors</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Examples</td>
                                        <td>nginx, app servers</td>
                                        <td>fluentd, kube-proxy, node-exporter</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">daemonset yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># DaemonSet YAML:</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: log-collector
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: log-collector
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: log-collector
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: fluentd
        image: fluentd:latest
        volumeMounts:
        - name: varlog
          mountPath: /var/log
      volumes:
      - name: varlog
        hostPath:
          path: /var/log</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>DaemonSets guarantee exactly ONE pod per node (or per matching node with nodeSelector). When a new node is added to the cluster, the DaemonSet automatically adds a pod to it. When a node is removed, the pod is garbage-collected. Use DaemonSets for: log collection (fluentd, filebeat), monitoring agents (node-exporter, datadog), network plugins (calico, weave). On the CKA, DaemonSets don't have a <code>replicas</code> field — the replica count equals the number of eligible nodes. There's no <code>kubectl create daemonset</code> — you must write YAML (tip: generate a Deployment YAML and change <code>kind: DaemonSet</code>, remove <code>replicas</code>).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a StatefulSet and how does it differ from a Deployment? What are its three guarantees?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">statefulset's 3 guarantees</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># StatefulSet's 3 guarantees:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Stable network identity → web-0, web-1, web-2 (not random)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Ordered deployment      → web-0 before web-1 before web-2</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Persistent storage      → Each pod gets its own PVC</span>

<span class="comment"># StatefulSet requires a headless Service:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: web-headless
spec:
  clusterIP: None       # ← Headless (no cluster IP)
  selector:
    app: web
  ports:
  - port: 80
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: web
spec:
  serviceName: "web-headless"    # ← Required
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx
  volumeClaimTemplates:     # ← Each pod gets its own PVC
  - metadata:
      name: data
    spec:
      accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 1Gi

<span class="comment"># DNS entries created:</span>
<span class="comment"># web-0.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># web-1.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># web-2.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>StatefulSets are for stateful applications like databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL, Redis cluster, Kafka). Pods get stable hostnames (web-0, web-1, web-2 — not random hashes), ordered startup/shutdown, and each pod gets its own PersistentVolumeClaim. A headless Service (clusterIP: None) is required to provide individual DNS names. On the CKA, know that: (1) deleting a StatefulSet does NOT delete the PVCs (data is preserved), (2) scaling down goes in REVERSE order (web-2 first, then web-1), (3) <code>serviceName</code> must match the headless Service name. StatefulSets are less commonly tested than Deployments but appear in advanced scenarios.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Job has <code>backoffLimit: 4</code> and <code>activeDeadlineSeconds: 120</code>. Explain what each does and what happens when either limit is reached.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: deadline-job
spec:
  backoffLimit: 4              <span class="comment"># Max 4 retries</span>
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 120   <span class="comment"># Max 2 minutes total</span>
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: worker
        image: busybox
        command: ["sh", "-c", "exit 1"]   <span class="comment"># Always fails</span>
      restartPolicy: Never

<span class="comment"># Scenario 1: backoffLimit reached first</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod fails → retry after ~10s → fails → retry after ~20s →</span>
<span class="comment"># fails → retry after ~40s → fails (4th failure)</span>
<span class="comment"># Job status: Failed, reason: BackoffLimitExceeded</span>

<span class="comment"># Scenario 2: activeDeadlineSeconds reached first</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod is still running at 120s → KILLED</span>
<span class="comment"># Job status: Failed, reason: DeadlineExceeded</span>
<span class="comment"># ALL running pods for this Job are terminated</span>

<span class="comment"># Check why a Job failed:</span>
kubectl describe job deadline-job | grep -A3 "Conditions"
<span class="comment"># Type    Status  Reason</span>
<span class="comment"># Failed  True    BackoffLimitExceeded</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>backoffLimit</code> limits the number of retry attempts (default: 6). Failed pods are retried with exponential backoff (10s, 20s, 40s, ..., capped at 6 minutes). <code>activeDeadlineSeconds</code> is a hard wall clock deadline — if the Job isn't complete by then, ALL pods are killed regardless of progress. On the CKA, <code>activeDeadlineSeconds</code> is critical for preventing runaway Jobs. <code>backoffLimit: 0</code> means no retries — fail immediately. These two limits work independently: whichever is reached first causes the Job to fail.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you suspend and resume a CronJob? What happens to already-running Jobs when you suspend?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">suspend a cronjob (prevent future scheduled runs)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Suspend a CronJob (prevent future scheduled runs)</span>
kubectl patch cronjob health-check \
-p '{"spec":{"suspend":true}}'

<span class="comment"># Or use kubectl edit</span>
kubectl edit cronjob health-check
<span class="comment"># Set spec.suspend: true</span>

<span class="comment"># Check suspension status</span>
kubectl get cronjob health-check
<span class="comment"># NAME           SCHEDULE      SUSPEND  ACTIVE  LAST SCHEDULE</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check   */5 * * * *   True     0       2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Resume</span>
kubectl patch cronjob health-check \
-p '{"spec":{"suspend":false}}'

<span class="comment"># Important: suspending does NOT kill running Jobs!</span>
<span class="comment"># Already-running Jobs continue to completion</span>
<span class="comment"># Only NEW scheduled runs are blocked</span>

<span class="comment"># To also stop running Jobs:</span>
kubectl delete job &lt;running-job-name&gt;</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>spec.suspend: true</code> prevents the CronJob from creating new Jobs at the scheduled time. Already-running Jobs continue — suspension only affects FUTURE runs. To completely stop everything, you need to also delete the active Jobs. On the CKA, suspending CronJobs is tested in maintenance scenarios: "pause this scheduled task while we perform an upgrade." The SUSPEND column in <code>kubectl get cronjob</code> quickly shows whether it's suspended. Missed schedules are not retroactively created when you resume — they're simply skipped.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a DaemonSet that runs a <code>node-exporter</code> container on ALL nodes including the control plane. What toleration is needed?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: node-exporter
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: node-exporter
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: node-exporter
    spec:
      tolerations:
      <span class="comment"># Tolerate control-plane taint to run on master</span>
      - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
        operator: Exists
        effect: NoSchedule
      <span class="comment"># Or tolerate ALL taints:</span>
      <span class="comment"># - operator: Exists</span>
      containers:
      - name: node-exporter
        image: prom/node-exporter:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 9100
          hostPort: 9100    # ← Expose on node's port

<span class="comment"># Verify it runs on ALL nodes:</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=node-exporter -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME                 READY  NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># node-exporter-abc    1/1    controlplane</span>
<span class="comment"># node-exporter-def    1/1    worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># node-exporter-ghi    1/1    worker-2</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Control-plane nodes have the taint <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code>. Without a matching toleration, the DaemonSet pod won't run on control-plane nodes. <code>operator: Exists</code> (without specifying a value) matches the taint regardless of its value. Use <code>hostPort</code> with DaemonSets to expose the pod on the node's IP — useful for monitoring agents. On the CKA, the "run on ALL nodes including master" phrasing signals that you need the control-plane toleration. Tip: generate a Deployment YAML, change <code>kind</code> to DaemonSet, remove <code>replicas</code>, and add the toleration.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What <code>restartPolicy</code> settings are valid for Jobs? What happens if you use <code>restartPolicy: Always</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">valid restartpolicy for jobs</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Valid restartPolicy for Jobs:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Never:     Don't restart failed containers, create new pods</span>
<span class="comment"># - OnFailure: Restart failed containers in the SAME pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Invalid for Jobs:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Always:    REJECTED by the API server</span>
<span class="comment">#   Error: spec.template.spec.restartPolicy: Unsupported value:</span>
<span class="comment">#   "Always": supported values: "OnFailure", "Never"</span>

<span class="comment"># Behavior comparison:</span>
<span class="comment"># restartPolicy: Never</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Failed container? Create a NEW pod</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Each attempt = new pod (can see all pods with kubectl get pods)</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Useful for debugging (logs preserved in each pod)</span>

<span class="comment"># restartPolicy: OnFailure</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Failed container? Restart in SAME pod</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Pod's restart count increments</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Uses less resources (no new pod overhead)</span>
<span class="comment">#   → kubectl get pods shows restart count increasing</span>

<span class="comment"># For CronJobs, the same rules apply (CronJob creates Jobs)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Jobs MUST use <code>Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code>. <code>Always</code> would create an infinite loop — the container completes, restarts, completes, restarts... which defeats the purpose of a Job (run to completion). With <code>Never</code>, each failure creates a new pod (up to <code>backoffLimit</code>), which is useful for debugging because each pod's logs are preserved. With <code>OnFailure</code>, the container restarts in-place, using fewer resources. On the CKA, if you generate a pod YAML and convert it to a Job, remember to change <code>restartPolicy</code> from <code>Always</code> to <code>Never</code> — it's a common mistake that causes a validation error.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List all Jobs created by a CronJob, find failed ones, and check their logs. Show the complete troubleshooting flow.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all jobs owned by the cronjob</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: List all Jobs owned by the CronJob</span>
kubectl get jobs --selector=job-name
<span class="comment"># Or by owner reference:</span>
kubectl get jobs | grep health-check
<span class="comment"># NAME                        COMPLETIONS  DURATION  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412345       1/1          5s        10m</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412350       1/1          4s        5m</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412355       0/1          2m        2m  ← FAILED?</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check the failed Job</span>
kubectl describe job health-check-28412355
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning BackoffLimitExceeded ... Job has reached the specified backoff limit</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Find the pods created by the Job</span>
kubectl get pods --selector=job-name=health-check-28412355
<span class="comment"># NAME                              READY  STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412355-abc         0/1    Error</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412355-def         0/1    Error</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check logs of the failed pod</span>
kubectl logs health-check-28412355-abc
<span class="comment"># curl: (7) Failed to connect to health-check port 8080: Connection refused</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check events</span>
kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=health-check-28412355 \
--sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CronJobs create Jobs with predictable names: <code>cronjob-name-timestamp</code>. Each Job creates pods. The debugging chain is: CronJob → Job → Pod → Logs. On the CKA, troubleshooting failed CronJobs follows this exact flow. Use <code>kubectl get jobs</code> to find failed Jobs (COMPLETIONS shows 0/1), <code>kubectl describe job</code> to see why, and <code>kubectl logs</code> on the pod for application errors. The <code>successfulJobsHistoryLimit</code> and <code>failedJobsHistoryLimit</code> control how many old Jobs are kept — if you can't find old Jobs, the limit may have cleaned them up.</p>
