            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 16 Visual Summary — picking the right controller for the job</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   Need stable identity + per-pod storage?  ──YES──▶ 🗄️ StatefulSet   (postgres: db-0, db-1 + PVCs)
        │NO
   Need exactly one pod per node?           ──YES──▶ 🔁 DaemonSet     (log/monitoring agent)
        │NO
   Runs once to completion?                 ──YES──▶ ⚡ Job          (easypj manage.py migrate)
        │           on a schedule? ──────────────────▶ ⏰ CronJob     (nightly db-backup)
        │NO
   otherwise ────────────────────────────────────────▶ 🔄 Deployment  (easypj-web: stateless)          ☸️
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> not every workload is a stateless web server. A Job runs pods until a target number SUCCEED, then stops — perfect for one-off migrations. A CronJob is just a schedule that creates a fresh Job per tick. A DaemonSet guarantees exactly one pod per eligible node with no <code>replicas</code> field at all — the controller tracks the node count for you. A StatefulSet is the one to reach for when an app needs a stable name, stable DNS, and its own durable storage that survives a pod restart.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> walk the decision tree in order — stable identity/storage first, then one-per-node, then run-once/scheduled, and only default to Deployment once none of the more specialized needs apply. Getting this choice right the first time avoids painful migrations later (you cannot simply convert a Deployment into a StatefulSet in place).</p>
                </div>
            </div>
