            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"I created a Service, so my app is now reachable."</td>
                            <td>A Service is just a stable address — a front-desk extension with nobody assigned to answer it yet. If the Service's <code>selector</code> doesn't match any pod's real labels, <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> shows <code>&lt;none&gt;</code> and every request silently drops. Checking endpoints, not just the Service object, is the actual proof of reachability.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"NetworkPolicies block traffic by default — that's the whole point of a firewall."</td>
                            <td>Kubernetes ships wide open: every pod can reach every other pod until a NetworkPolicy specifically SELECTS it. Applying a policy to the database pod doesn't touch any other pod's traffic at all — isolation is opt-in, one pod (or label group) at a time, not a cluster-wide default.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"<code>nslookup</code> resolved the name, so the connection should work."</td>
                            <td>DNS and connectivity are two completely independent layers. A successful <code>nslookup</code> only proves CoreDNS turned a name into a ClusterIP — it says nothing about whether that Service has healthy endpoints or whether a NetworkPolicy is silently dropping the actual traffic. "Can't connect" bugs almost always require checking BOTH layers separately.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
