            <section class="section-block" id="s17-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.1 The Kubernetes Networking Model</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes enforces a <strong>flat, NAT-free networking model</strong>. This is fundamentally different from Docker's default bridge networking, and understanding these rules is critical for every CKA topic that follows.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">1️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Every Pod Gets a Unique IP</h5>
                        <p>No two pods share an IP address — every pod gets a real, cluster-wide routable IP the instant it's scheduled.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">2️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Pod-to-Pod Without NAT</h5>
                        <p>Any pod can reach any other pod directly using its IP — even across nodes. No network address translation needed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">3️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Node Agents ↔ Same-Node Pods, Without NAT</h5>
                        <p>Agents running ON a node — the kubelet, system daemons — can reach every pod running ON THAT SAME node directly, without NAT. (Official wording is deliberately node-LOCAL, not "any node reaches any pod" — see the callout below.)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">4️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Containers in a Pod Share One Network Namespace</h5>
                        <p>All containers in the same pod share the pod's single network namespace — same IP, same port space, same <code>localhost</code>. This is why a sidecar container can reach the main container via <code>localhost</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Rule 3 Is Node-Local, Not Cluster-Wide — a Precise Wording Matters Here</h5>
                    <p>It's tempting to remember Rule 3 as "any node can reach any pod in the cluster" — but the official Kubernetes networking model states it more narrowly: <em>agents on a node can communicate with all pods on THAT node</em>. The practical consequence is real: it's exactly why a node's own kubelet can always run liveness/readiness probes against pods scheduled to it, even under a default-deny NetworkPolicy (Ch 20) — a NetworkPolicy governs pod-to-pod traffic, not this node-local agent path. Cross-node pod reachability is what Rule 2 already covers.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The 4 Fundamental Kubernetes Networking Rules</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  KUBERNETES FLAT NETWORKING MODEL                                        │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Rule 1: Every Pod gets a unique IP                                     │
  │  ┌────────────────┐  ┌────────────────┐  ┌────────────────┐            │
  │  │  easypj-abc    │  │  postgres-0    │  │  redis-xyz     │            │
  │  │  10.244.1.5    │  │  10.244.2.8    │  │  10.244.3.2    │            │
  │  │  (Node 1)      │  │  (Node 2)      │  │  (Node 1)      │            │
  │  └────────────────┘  └────────────────┘  └────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Rule 2: Pod-to-Pod — direct, no NAT (cluster-wide, ANY node)           │
  │  easypj (10.244.1.5) ────────────────────→ postgres (10.244.2.8) ✅    │
  │                       no translation needed, even cross-node!            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Rule 3: Node agents ↔ SAME-node pods only — direct, no NAT             │
  │  kubelet on Node 1 ───────────────────────→ easypj-abc (10.244.1.5) ✅ │
  │                       (Node 1's OWN pod — this is the official scope)   │
  │  kubelet on Node 1 ─ ─ ─X─ ─ ─▶ postgres-0 on Node 2   (NOT Rule 3 —    │
  │                       cross-node pod reachability is Rule 2's job)      │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Rule 4: Containers in ONE pod share a network namespace                │
  │  easypj pod: django container + nginx sidecar, both see 10.244.1.5     │
  │  nginx → curl http://localhost:8000  ✅  (reaches django, same pod)    │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
  │  CONTRAST: Docker default networking (WITHOUT Kubernetes)                │
  │  Container A → NAT → Host IP → NAT → Container B   ❌ Complex!        │
  │  Container sees 172.17.0.2, outside sees host IP    ❌ Different!      │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why This Model Matters</h5>
                    <p>The flat networking model means you can take any application that communicates over TCP/IP and run it in Kubernetes <strong>without modifying the code</strong>. Your easypj Django app connects to <code>postgres:5432</code> exactly the same way whether running locally or in a Kubernetes cluster. No port mapping gymnastics, no NAT tables to debug.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Containers Sharing a Pod Network Namespace</div>
