                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Beyond curl/nslookup: Packet Capture and eBPF-Native Observability</h5>
                    <p>Every technique so far treats the network as a black box you probe from the outside (curl, nslookup, nc). When those tools all say "should work" but something's still wrong, the next level down is actually watching packets cross the wire.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">tcpdump inside a netshoot pod — see the actual packets</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># netshoot ships tcpdump — capture traffic on the pod's own interface</span>
kubectl run net --rm -it --image=nicolaka/netshoot -- bash
tcpdump -i eth0 -n port 5432
<span class="comment"># watch for: SYN sent but no SYN-ACK back (firewall/NetworkPolicy dropping silently)</span>
<span class="comment">#            vs SYN-ACK then immediate RST (nothing listening on that port)</span>
<span class="comment">#            — these look identical from curl's "connection refused/timeout", but</span>
<span class="comment">#              are completely different root causes, and tcpdump is the only way</span>
<span class="comment">#              to tell them apart with certainty</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>On a Cilium-based cluster specifically, <strong>Hubble</strong> (Cilium's eBPF-native observability layer) goes further — it shows a live, flow-by-flow view of every connection in the cluster, INCLUDING whether a flow was dropped and by which exact NetworkPolicy rule, without needing to manually correlate tcpdump output across multiple nodes.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">hubble observe — Cilium's eBPF flow visibility</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># On a Cilium cluster with Hubble enabled:</span>
hubble observe --pod easypj-web --verdict DROPPED
<span class="comment"># shows exactly which flows were dropped and the policy responsible — the eBPF</span>
<span class="comment"># equivalent of "kubectl describe networkpolicy" but for REAL observed traffic,</span>
<span class="comment"># not just the policy's declared intent</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mb-0">Neither tool is CKA-exam material, but both are what a real production incident actually looks like once <code>curl</code>/<code>nslookup</code>/<code>nc</code> have told you "connectivity is broken" without telling you WHY — this is the honest next step beyond this chapter's basic toolkit.</p>
                </div>
