            <h5>① iptables vs IPVS — Why Big Clusters Switch Dataplanes</h5>
            <p>The comparison table back in §17.4 says IPVS is "better at scale," but the actual reason is algorithmic, not just marketing. iptables and IPVS solve the identical problem — "route a Service VIP to a pod IP" — with fundamentally different data structures, and that difference only starts to bite once you cross a few hundred Services.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   iptables dataplane                          IPVS dataplane
   ────────────────────                        ──────────────────
   Every Service = a NEW chain appended         Every Service = ONE entry in a
   to a linear list the kernel walks             kernel HASH TABLE the kernel
   top-to-bottom for EVERY packet.                looks up directly.

   Service #1  ┐
   Service #2  ├─ kernel checks each rule        Service #1 ─┐
   Service #3  │  in order until one matches      Service #2 ─┼─▶ O(1) hash lookup
      ⋮        │  → O(n) lookup, n = #Services    Service #3 ─┘   regardless of n
   Service #5000┘  gets slower as n grows

   Also gives REAL load-balancing algorithms: round-robin (rr), least-connection
   (lc), weighted round-robin (wrr), source-hashing (sh) — iptables only does
   pseudo-random selection via probability chaining.
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Trait</th><th>iptables (default)</th><th>IPVS</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Lookup cost</td><td>O(n) — sequential rule chain</td><td>O(1) — kernel hash table</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Load-balancing</td><td>Pseudo-random only</td><td>rr / lc / wrr / sh and more</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Rule-update cost on Service churn</td><td>Rewrites large rule blocks</td><td>Incremental hash updates</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Sweet spot</td><td>Small/medium clusters (&lt;1000 Services)</td><td>Large clusters (1000+ Services)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> you don't need to memorize the algorithm names for the exam, but "IPVS scales better because it's a hash table, not a rule chain" is exactly the kind of one-sentence justification that separates a memorized fact from an understood one — and it's the difference between guessing and reasoning when a question describes a 3000-Service cluster with slow Service routing.</div>

            <h5>② eBPF — When Cilium Removes kube-proxy Entirely</h5>
            <p>Everything in §17.4 assumes kube-proxy is programming iptables or IPVS rules in kernel space via traditional netfilter hooks. Cilium's eBPF dataplane takes a third path: it can replace kube-proxy ENTIRELY, doing Service load-balancing (and NetworkPolicy enforcement) with small verified programs attached directly to kernel hook points — no iptables, no IPVS, no separate kube-proxy process at all.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Traditional path                       eBPF path (Cilium, kube-proxy-free mode)
   ─────────────────                      ──────────────────────────────────────
   packet → netfilter hooks               packet → eBPF program attached at the
     → iptables/IPVS rule chain             socket / XDP / tc hook, running
     → kube-proxy keeps these rules         DIRECTLY in the kernel — no separate
       in sync as Services change            userspace process reacting to changes
                                             at all; the eBPF map IS the routing
                                             table, updated in place.

   Extra capability unlocked: the SAME eBPF layer can also enforce NetworkPolicy
   and do L7-aware routing/observability — one dataplane, three jobs.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if a cluster question mentions Cilium AND you don't see a <code>kube-proxy</code> DaemonSet in <code>kube-system</code>, that's not a bug — Cilium in "kube-proxy replacement" mode is expected to run without one. Don't "fix" a missing kube-proxy on a Cilium cluster without checking this first.</div>

            <h5>③ BGP vs VXLAN — Routing vs Encapsulating, and the MTU Cost of Overlays</h5>
            <p>§17.3's cross-node diagram shows the CNI either "encapsulating" or "routing" a packet — those are two genuinely different mechanisms with a real performance trade-off, and the choice explains a classic hard-to-diagnose symptom: pings work, but large HTTP responses hang.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   VXLAN overlay (Flannel default, Calico VXLAN mode)
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   [ original pod packet ] gets WRAPPED in a new outer UDP packet:
   [ outer IP+UDP header (~50 bytes) [ original pod packet ] ]
   → works on ANY underlying network (cloud, bare-metal, doesn't matter)
   → costs ~50 bytes of every packet's MTU budget

   BGP routing (Calico BGP mode)
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Node routing tables get a REAL route: "10.244.2.0/24 via 192.168.1.11"
   → packet leaves the node NATIVELY, no wrapping, no overhead
   → requires the physical/cloud network to actually route those routes
     (works great on bare-metal/on-prem; some cloud VPCs need extra config)

   THE MTU TRAP:
   Node NIC MTU = 1500   |   VXLAN overhead = ~50 bytes   |   Usable pod MTU ≈ 1450
   A ping (small packet)     fits fine either way              ✅ works
   A large HTTP response     needs fragmentation THAT the        ❌ hangs/drops if
   (unfragmented)             overlay's actual usable MTU           CNI's interface
                              wasn't lowered to match                MTU is still 1500
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Overhead</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>VXLAN overlay</td><td>Encapsulate in outer UDP</td><td>~50 bytes/packet, shrinks usable MTU</td><td>None — works over any L3 network</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>BGP routing</td><td>Advertise real pod-CIDR routes</td><td>None</td><td>Underlying network must route them</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> "ping works, but curl to a real endpoint hangs at the TLS handshake or a large response" is the textbook MTU-mismatch symptom (from §17.5's own troubleshooting table) — <code>ip -d link show flannel.1</code> shows the overlay interface's actual MTU so you can confirm it was correctly lowered below the physical NIC's MTU.</div>

            <h5>④ conntrack Limits, Dual-Stack, and Per-Node Pod-CIDR Allocation</h5>
            <p>Three smaller but genuinely exam-relevant mechanics live beneath everything covered so far: how the kernel remembers which reply belongs to which request, how a cluster can run both IPv4 and IPv6 at once, and how pod IPs never collide across nodes without any central coordination per-packet.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   conntrack — the kernel's "who asked for this reply" table
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Every DNAT'd connection (a Service VIP hit) gets ONE entry in
   /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_count, so the return packet's
   source can be un-DNAT'd back to the VIP the client actually dialed.

   Busy node, thousands of short-lived connections
        → nf_conntrack_count approaches nf_conntrack_max
        → new connections get SILENTLY DROPPED ("conntrack table full")
        → fix: raise nf_conntrack_max (sysctl), or reduce churn

   Per-node pod-CIDR allocation — why IPs never collide
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   cluster pod CIDR: 10.244.0.0/16
     node-1 owns:  10.244.1.0/24   (kubelet requests a /24 slice)
     node-2 owns:  10.244.2.0/24
     node-3 owns:  10.244.3.0/24
   Each node's local IPAM only ever hands out IPs from ITS OWN slice —
   no cross-node coordination needed per-pod-creation, which is exactly
   why pod scheduling doesn't bottleneck on a central IP allocator.

   Dual-stack — a pod/Service can carry BOTH families at once
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   spec.podCIDRs: [10.244.1.0/24, fd00:10:244:1::/64]  ← IPv4 AND IPv6
   A dual-stack Service gets both a ClusterIP and a ClusterIPv6.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> §17.5's troubleshooting table lists "random dropped connections on a busy node" → conntrack exhaustion as a real, tested symptom — it looks like flaky networking but the fix is a sysctl, not a CNI reinstall.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Service Mesh at a Glance — the Layer Above Everything in This Chapter</h5>
            <p>Everything covered so far (CNI, kube-proxy, DNS in Ch19, NetworkPolicy in Ch20) is the base network. A service mesh is an OPTIONAL layer on top that adds L7-aware routing, automatic mutual TLS between pods, and per-request observability — without changing application code. It's not required for the CKA, but recognizing the two architectural styles avoids confusing a mesh problem for a base-network problem.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Sidecar model (classic Istio)              Ambient / sidecarless model
   ──────────────────────────────              ────────────────────────────
   ┌─── Pod ──────────────────┐                Node-level shared proxy handles
   │ ┌────────┐  ┌──────────┐ │                ALL pods on that node — no
   │ │ easypj │──│  Envoy   │ │◀── mTLS         extra container per pod, lower
   │ │  app   │  │ sidecar  │ │    L7 routing   resource overhead, but less
   │ └────────┘  └──────────┘ │    metrics      per-pod isolation than a
   └───────────────────────────┘                dedicated sidecar.
   +1 container PER POD
   (resource cost scales with pod count)
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if a symptom looks like "mTLS handshake failure" or "traffic split isn't working as configured" and the cluster has an <code>istio-proxy</code> or similar sidecar container in every pod, you're debugging the MESH, not the CNI/kube-proxy stack this chapter covers — a different, higher layer with its own control plane.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>The K8s network model has 4 rules: every pod gets a unique IP; pods talk to pods WITHOUT NAT; nodes talk to pods without NAT; a pod sees its own real IP (the same IP others use to reach it). One flat network.</li>
                    <li>CNI (a plugin you install — Calico/Cilium/Flannel/Weave) implements that model. No CNI = nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> and pods never get an IP.</li>
                    <li>Choosing and troubleshooting the CNI is the <strong>ADMIN's</strong> job; Kubernetes ships no networking by itself.</li>
                    <li><code>kube-proxy</code> is <strong>NOT</strong> in the pod-to-pod path. It only programs the dataplane (iptables/IPVS/nftables) that turns a Service VIP into a real pod IP.</li>
                    <li>Same-node traffic crosses a local bridge (veth → cbr0/cni0); cross-node traffic is encapsulated (VXLAN) or routed (BGP) by the CNI, then hits the peer node's bridge.</li>
                    <li>Flannel cannot enforce NetworkPolicy — pick Calico or Cilium when you need a pod firewall.</li>
                    <li>A successful pod-to-pod ping proves the CNI works; a successful Service-VIP curl additionally proves kube-proxy works — they are two separate, independently-failing layers.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

