                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the network-model internals — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a veth pair, and where does it sit in the pod-to-pod packet path?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A virtual ethernet CABLE with two ends: one lands inside the pod's network namespace as <code>eth0</code>, the other lands on the node's root namespace attached to the bridge (<code>cbr0</code>/<code>cni0</code>). It's the CNI-created link that connects an isolated pod netns to the node's network.</p>
                        <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Pod netns              Node root netns
   ┌────────┐             ┌──────────────┐
   │  eth0  │◀── veth ───▶│ veth-side-B  │── attached to cbr0 bridge
   └────────┘   pair       └──────────────┘
</pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every pod gets exactly one veth pair when the CNI's <code>ADD</code> operation runs. It's the physical (virtual) wire that makes "the pod has its own IP" real — without it there's no path from the pod's isolated namespace to anywhere else.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does IPVS scale better than iptables once a cluster has thousands of Services?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>iptables evaluates Service rules as a linear chain — an O(n) lookup that gets slower as Service count grows. IPVS uses a kernel hash table — an O(1) lookup regardless of how many Services exist.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Data structure</th><th>Lookup cost</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>iptables</td><td>Sequential rule chain</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>IPVS</td><td>Kernel hash table</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is why the CKA/production guidance is "IPVS at scale": it's not a vague performance claim, it's a specific algorithmic one. IPVS also unlocks real load-balancing algorithms (round-robin, least-connection) that iptables' probability-chain trick can't match.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>ping</code> between two cross-node pods succeeds, but a large HTTP response hangs. What's the likely cause and the check command?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>An MTU mismatch caused by overlay encapsulation overhead. VXLAN wraps every packet in ~50 extra bytes; if the pod-facing interface's MTU wasn't lowered to account for that, small packets (like ping) fit fine but large, unfragmented packets get dropped.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ip -d link show flannel.1   <span class="comment"># check the overlay interface's actual MTU</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This exact symptom — "curl hangs at the TLS handshake, ping works fine" — is one of the most-repeated real-world CNI gotchas. The fix is aligning the CNI/interface MTU, not restarting anything.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">At the packet level, what does BGP-mode Calico do differently from VXLAN-mode Flannel?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>BGP advertises the pod-CIDR as a REAL route in the node's routing table, so cross-node packets leave the node natively with no wrapping. VXLAN instead wraps the whole original packet inside a new outer UDP packet before sending it — encapsulation, not routing.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Packet-level effect</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>BGP (Calico)</td><td>Native route, zero extra bytes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>VXLAN (Flannel default)</td><td>Encapsulated, +~50 bytes/packet</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>BGP needs the underlying network to actually be willing to route those pod-CIDR routes (easy on bare-metal, sometimes restricted on managed cloud VPCs), which is the trade-off for its lower overhead.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A requirement mandates NetworkPolicy enforcement. Which CNI from §17.2's table fails that requirement outright, and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Flannel. It has no NetworkPolicy controller — policies can be created and will show as applied via <code>kubectl get networkpolicy</code>, but Flannel silently does nothing to enforce them. Calico, Cilium, or Canal (Flannel networking + Calico policy engine) are required instead.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f netpol.yaml   <span class="comment"># accepted, no error — but on Flannel, does nothing</span>
kubectl get networkpolicy      <span class="comment"># shows it "exists" — this alone does NOT prove enforcement</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a classic exam trap: the NetworkPolicy YAML is 100% correct, applies cleanly, and traffic still flows freely — because the object was accepted by the API server but the CNI never reads it. Always confirm the CNI is policy-aware before debugging the YAML itself.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is conntrack, and what's the symptom when its table fills up on a busy node?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>conntrack is the kernel's table of active connections, used to remember which reply belongs to which DNAT'd request (e.g. a Service VIP hit). When <code>nf_conntrack_count</code> approaches <code>nf_conntrack_max</code>, NEW connections are silently dropped.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max
<span class="comment"># raise the ceiling if the node is legitimately busy:</span>
sysctl -w net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max=262144</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The symptom looks like flaky, random networking — some connections work, some don't, no clear pattern — which makes it easy to misdiagnose as a CNI or app bug instead of a kernel table limit.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Cilium cluster has zero kube-proxy pods anywhere. Is the cluster broken?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Not necessarily. Cilium's eBPF dataplane can run in "kube-proxy replacement" mode, doing Service load-balancing (and NetworkPolicy enforcement) directly in-kernel via eBPF programs — no separate kube-proxy process is needed at all.</p>
<pre>kubectl get ds -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># cilium         3   3   3   ...   Running   ← the eBPF dataplane, present
# (kube-proxy)   ← ABSENT — expected on a kube-proxy-replacement cluster</span></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Before "fixing" a missing kube-proxy DaemonSet, confirm which CNI is running and its configured mode — a missing kube-proxy is EXPECTED and correct on a Cilium-kube-proxy-replacement cluster, and reinstalling it would be the wrong action.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why must the pod network CIDR and the Service network CIDR never overlap?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>They serve different purposes at different layers — pod IPs are real, routable addresses assigned to actual network interfaces; Service (Cluster)IPs are virtual addresses that exist only as iptables/IPVS rules, never bound to a real interface. If the ranges overlapped, the kernel couldn't tell whether a given IP means "route to this real pod" or "DNAT to some other pod via this virtual VIP."</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Range</th><th>kubeadm flag</th><th>Nature</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Pod CIDR</td><td><code>--pod-network-cidr</code></td><td>Real, routable — bound to actual interfaces</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Service CIDR</td><td><code>--service-cluster-ip-range</code></td><td>Virtual — exists only as dataplane rules</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr</code> sets one range and <code>--service-cluster-ip-range</code> sets the other — a kubeadm cluster build with overlapping ranges is a genuine, if rare, exam-style misconfiguration to watch for.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A service mesh sidecar is injected into every easypj pod. What new layer does this add, and how is it different from what this chapter covers?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The mesh (e.g. Istio's Envoy sidecar) adds an L7-aware layer ON TOP of the CNI/kube-proxy base network — automatic mutual TLS between pods, header/weight-based routing, and per-request observability — none of which the base network (this chapter) or Services (Ch18) know anything about.</p>
<pre>base network (this chapter):  IP-level reachability, kube-proxy VIP routing
                    ▲
service mesh (on top):        mTLS · header/weight routing · observability
                               (a SEPARATE control plane — Ch17/18 don't see it)</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If a symptom is "mTLS handshake failure" or "traffic split isn't behaving," you're debugging the MESH's own control plane, not the CNI/kube-proxy stack — a different, higher layer entirely, with its own separate troubleshooting path.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Trace a packet end-to-end: Pod A on node1 curls a Service VIP that load-balances to Pod B on node2. Name every hop.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Pod A's app → Pod A's eth0/veth → node1's kube-proxy dataplane (iptables/IPVS) DNATs the VIP to Pod B's real IP → node1 cbr0 bridge → CNI encapsulates (VXLAN) or routes (BGP) → node1 eth0 → physical network → node2 eth0 → decapsulate/route → node2 cbr0 → Pod B's veth → Pod B's eth0/app.</p>
                        <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Pod A ──▶ kube-proxy DNAT (VIP→PodB IP) ──▶ CNI overlay/route ──▶ Pod B
             (§17.4, the ONLY VIP-aware hop)   (§17.3, pure pod-to-pod after this)
</pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The key insight worth memorizing: kube-proxy resolves WHICH pod exactly once (at the DNAT step), and every hop after that is ordinary pod-to-pod traffic on the flat CNI network — kube-proxy is never touched again for the rest of the journey.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

