                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the 4 fundamental Kubernetes networking rules? Why are they important for the CKA exam?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the 4 kubernetes networking rules</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The 4 Kubernetes Networking Rules:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Every pod gets its own unique IP address</span>
<span class="comment">#    → No port conflicts between pods</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Pods are treated like VMs from a networking perspective</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Pods on ANY node can communicate with ALL other pods</span>
<span class="comment">#    WITHOUT NAT (Network Address Translation)</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Flat network — no routing hacks needed</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Agents on a node (kubelet, kube-proxy) can communicate</span>
<span class="comment">#    with all pods on that node</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Node components can health-check and manage pods</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Pods in the host network of a node can communicate</span>
<span class="comment">#    with all pods on ALL nodes without NAT</span>
<span class="comment">#    → hostNetwork:true pods participate in the flat network</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify rule #1: Each pod has unique IP</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  STATUS   IP            NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-1   1/1    Running  10.244.1.5    worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-2   1/1    Running  10.244.2.8    worker-2 (different subnet!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify rule #2: Cross-node pod communication</span>
kubectl exec webapp-1 -- curl -s 10.244.2.8:8080
<span class="comment"># Response from webapp-2 (no NAT needed)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>These 4 rules define the Kubernetes network model. Every CNI plugin (Calico, Flannel, Weave, Cilium) MUST implement these rules. The flat network model means any pod can reach any other pod by IP — no NAT, no port mapping. This is fundamentally different from Docker's default bridge networking. On the CKA, understanding this model helps you: (1) debug connectivity issues (if pod-to-pod fails, the CNI is broken), (2) understand why Services exist (pods have dynamic IPs), (3) know that NetworkPolicies are the ONLY way to restrict traffic (everything is allowed by default).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A newly joined node shows <code>NotReady</code> status. <code>kubectl describe node</code> shows <em>"runtime network not ready: NetworkReady=false reason:NetworkPluginNotReady"</em>. What's wrong?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnosis: cni plugin is not installed on this node</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Diagnosis: CNI plugin is NOT installed on this node</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check if CNI is installed cluster-wide</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E "calico|flannel|weave|cilium"
<span class="comment"># (no output — CNI not installed!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Install the CNI plugin</span>
<span class="comment"># Calico:</span>
kubectl apply -f https://docs.projectcalico.org/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Flannel:</span>
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Wait and verify</span>
kubectl get nodes -w
<span class="comment"># NAME      STATUS   ROLES       AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1  Ready    worker      5m   ← Now Ready!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify CNI pods are running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=calico-node
<span class="comment"># calico-node-abc   1/1   Running   worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># calico-node-def   1/1   Running   worker-2</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The CNI (Container Network Interface) plugin is responsible for setting up pod networking. Without it, the kubelet can't configure network namespaces for pods, so the node reports <code>NotReady</code>. On the CKA, this is a CLASSIC troubleshooting question. After <code>kubeadm init</code> or <code>kubeadm join</code>, the CNI is NOT automatically installed — you must apply it manually. The exam question will tell you which CNI to use and may provide the URL. Check the <code>kubeadm init</code> output notes or the question instructions.</p>
