                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is kube-proxy and what modes can it operate in? How do you check which mode is being used?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kube-proxy modes</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># kube-proxy modes:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. iptables (default) — uses iptables rules for Service routing</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. IPVS — uses Linux IPVS for load balancing (better performance)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. userspace — legacy, rarely used (proxies in userspace)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current mode:</span>
kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | grep mode
<span class="comment"># mode: ""          ← empty string = iptables (default)</span>
<span class="comment"># mode: "ipvs"      ← IPVS mode</span>
<span class="comment"># mode: "iptables"  ← explicit iptables</span>

<span class="comment"># Or check kube-proxy logs:</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy | grep "Using"
<span class="comment"># "Using iptables proxier"</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify kube-proxy is running on all nodes:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy -o wide
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-abc   1/1   Running   controlplane</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-def   1/1   Running   worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-ghi   1/1   Running   worker-2</span>

<span class="comment"># kube-proxy runs as a DaemonSet:</span>
kubectl get ds -n kube-system kube-proxy</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>kube-proxy runs on every node as a DaemonSet and maintains network rules that allow Service traffic to reach pods. In <code>iptables</code> mode, it creates nat table rules. In <code>IPVS</code> mode, it uses Linux Virtual Server for more efficient load balancing. On the CKA, know that: (1) kube-proxy handles Service → Pod routing (not pod-to-pod, which is CNI), (2) if kube-proxy is down, Services break but direct pod-to-pod communication still works, (3) the mode is configured in the <code>kube-proxy</code> ConfigMap in <code>kube-system</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Pod CIDR and Service CIDR? How do you find these ranges in an existing cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pod cidr: ip range for pod ips</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pod CIDR: IP range for pod IPs</span>
<span class="comment"># Service CIDR: IP range for Service ClusterIPs</span>

<span class="comment"># Find Pod CIDR:</span>
kubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m1 cluster-cidr
<span class="comment"># --cluster-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</span>

<span class="comment"># Or from the controller manager:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-controller-manager \
-o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep cluster-cidr
<span class="comment"># --cluster-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</span>

<span class="comment"># Find Service CIDR:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-apiserver \
-o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep service-cluster
<span class="comment"># --service-cluster-ip-range=10.96.0.0/12</span>

<span class="comment"># Per-node Pod CIDR:</span>
kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.podCIDR}{"\n"}{end}'
<span class="comment"># controlplane    10.244.0.0/24</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1        10.244.1.0/24</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2        10.244.2.0/24</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pod CIDR is set during <code>kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code> and split into per-node subnets by the controller-manager. Service CIDR is set via <code>--service-cluster-ip-range</code> on the API server. These ranges MUST NOT overlap with each other or with the host network. On the CKA, knowing where to find these values is essential for: (1) troubleshooting IP conflicts, (2) configuring CNI plugins, (3) understanding why a pod IP is 10.244.x.x but a Service IP is 10.96.x.x.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you launch a network troubleshooting pod with all common tools? Show how to test connectivity between pods.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">launch a debug pod with network tools</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Launch a debug pod with network tools:</span>
kubectl run nettest --image=nicolaka/netshoot \
--rm -it --restart=Never -- bash

<span class="comment"># Inside the debug pod:</span>
<span class="comment"># Test DNS resolution</span>
nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># Server: 10.96.0.10 (kube-dns)</span>
<span class="comment"># Name: kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.0.1</span>

<span class="comment"># Test connectivity to a service</span>
curl -s http://web-svc:80
<span class="comment"># (HTML response)</span>

<span class="comment"># Test pod-to-pod directly by IP</span>
curl -s http://10.244.1.5:8080

<span class="comment"># Check network path</span>
traceroute 10.244.2.8

<span class="comment"># Test TCP port connectivity</span>
nc -zv web-svc 80
<span class="comment"># Connection to web-svc 80 port [tcp/http] succeeded!</span>

<span class="comment"># Minimal alternative (no netshoot image):</span>
kubectl run test --image=busybox --rm -it -- wget -qO- http://web-svc:80
<span class="comment"># Or:</span>
kubectl exec existing-pod -- curl -s http://web-svc:80</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>nicolaka/netshoot</code> image includes curl, nslookup, dig, traceroute, nc (netcat), tcpdump, and more. On the CKA, you can also use <code>busybox</code> (has wget, nslookup) or the <code>curlimages/curl</code> image. The <code>--rm -it --restart=Never</code> flags ensure the pod is cleaned up after exit. Key debugging pattern: (1) Test DNS with <code>nslookup</code>, (2) Test HTTP with <code>curl</code>/<code>wget</code>, (3) Test TCP with <code>nc -zv</code>. If DNS fails, the issue is CoreDNS. If DNS works but curl fails, the issue is kube-proxy or NetworkPolicy.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to pod networking when kube-proxy crashes on a node? Can pods still communicate?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">when kube-proxy is down on a node</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># When kube-proxy is down on a node:</span>

<span class="comment"># STILL WORKS:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Pod-to-pod direct communication (by pod IP)</span>
kubectl exec webapp-1 -- curl http://10.244.2.8:8080  <span class="comment"># Works!</span>

<span class="comment"># BROKEN:</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ Service → Pod routing (ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer)</span>
kubectl exec webapp-1 -- curl http://web-svc:80  <span class="comment"># Times out!</span>
<span class="comment"># (iptables/IPVS rules aren't being updated)</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS MAY WORK:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ DNS resolution still works (CoreDNS is independent)</span>
kubectl exec webapp-1 -- nslookup web-svc
<span class="comment"># Returns the ClusterIP, but traffic to that IP won't route</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify kube-proxy status:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy -o wide
<span class="comment"># Check if the pod on the affected node is CrashLoopBackOff</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix:</span>
kubectl delete pod -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy \
--field-selector spec.nodeName=worker-1
<span class="comment"># DaemonSet recreates it</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>kube-proxy only handles Service-to-Pod routing by maintaining iptables/IPVS rules. Pod-to-pod networking is handled by the CNI plugin. So when kube-proxy is down: Services break (ClusterIP doesn't route) but direct pod IP communication works fine. On the CKA, this distinction is critical for diagnosing network issues: "Service doesn't work but pods can ping each other" = kube-proxy problem. "No pod communication at all" = CNI problem. Deleting the kube-proxy pod lets the DaemonSet recreate it, which usually fixes the issue.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>hostNetwork: true</code> and a regular pod? When would you use it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">regular pod: gets its own network namespace with a…</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Regular pod: gets its own network namespace with a pod IP</span>
<span class="comment"># hostNetwork pod: uses the node's network namespace directly</span>

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: host-network-pod
spec:
hostNetwork: true    # ← Uses node's IP and ports
containers:
- name: nginx
image: nginx
ports:
- containerPort: 80  <span class="comment"># Actually binds to the NODE's port 80!</span>

<span class="comment"># Regular pod:</span>
<span class="comment">#   IP: 10.244.1.5 (pod network)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Can bind any port without conflict with other pods</span>

<span class="comment"># hostNetwork pod:</span>
<span class="comment">#   IP: 192.168.1.100 (same as node!)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Port conflicts with other host-network pods</span>
<span class="comment">#   Can access node-level network interfaces</span>

<span class="comment"># Use cases:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Control plane components (kube-apiserver, etcd)</span>
<span class="comment"># - CNI plugins (need host network access)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Network monitoring tools</span>
<span class="comment"># - kube-proxy itself uses hostNetwork</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>hostNetwork: true</code> makes the pod share the node's network stack — it sees the node's interfaces, uses the node's IP, and can bind to the node's ports. This is how control-plane components work: the API server binds to port 6443 on the node's IP. On the CKA, <code>hostNetwork</code> appears in questions about: (1) static pods (control-plane components), (2) why certain system pods have node IPs instead of pod IPs, (3) networking troubleshooting where <code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> shows a node IP instead of the expected pod CIDR IP.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check if a CNI plugin is installed and which one is being used?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check running cni pods</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: Check running CNI pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -iE "calico|flannel|weave|cilium"
<span class="comment"># calico-node-abc           1/1   Running   (Calico is installed)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Check CNI configuration on the node</span>
ls /etc/cni/net.d/
<span class="comment"># 10-calico.conflist    ← Calico config file</span>

cat /etc/cni/net.d/10-calico.conflist | head -5
<span class="comment"># {"name": "k8s-pod-network", "cniVersion": "0.3.1", ...}</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Check CNI binaries</span>
ls /opt/cni/bin/
<span class="comment"># calico  calico-ipam  bandwidth  bridge  flannel ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Check node annotations</span>
kubectl get node worker-1 -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations}' | python3 -m json.tool | grep cni

<span class="comment"># Method 5: Check kubelet config</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep cni
<span class="comment"># --cni-bin-dir=/opt/cni/bin --cni-conf-dir=/etc/cni/net.d</span></code></pre>
