                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CNI plugins install themselves as DaemonSets (one pod per node) and place their config in <code>/etc/cni/net.d/</code> and binaries in <code>/opt/cni/bin/</code>. The kubelet reads the CNI config to set up pod networking. On the CKA, the fastest way to identify the CNI is <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> — look for calico-node, flannel, weave-net, or cilium pods. If NO CNI pods exist and nodes are <code>NotReady</code>, you need to install one. The exam question will specify which CNI to use.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod can reach other pods on the SAME node but not pods on DIFFERENT nodes. What's likely wrong?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">same-node works, cross-node fails → cni overlay…</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Same-node works, cross-node fails → CNI overlay issue</span>

<span class="comment"># Diagnostic steps:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Verify the problem</span>
kubectl exec pod-on-node1 -- ping 10.244.1.5  <span class="comment"># Same node → works</span>
kubectl exec pod-on-node1 -- ping 10.244.2.8  <span class="comment"># Other node → fails</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check CNI pods on both nodes</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep calico
<span class="comment"># calico-node on node1: Running</span>
<span class="comment"># calico-node on node2: CrashLoopBackOff!  ← Problem!</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Check CNI pod logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system calico-node-xyz

<span class="comment"># 4. Check node routes</span>
<span class="comment"># On node1:</span>
ip route | grep 10.244
<span class="comment"># 10.244.1.0/24 → local (works)</span>
<span class="comment"># 10.244.2.0/24 → via 192.168.1.102 (node2's IP)</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Check if nodes can reach each other</span>
<span class="comment"># SSH to node1:</span>
ping 192.168.1.102  <span class="comment"># Can node1 reach node2?</span>

<span class="comment"># Common causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - CNI pod crashed on one node</span>
<span class="comment"># - Firewall blocking VXLAN (port 4789) or IP-in-IP traffic</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing routes between node subnets</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Same-node pod communication uses the local bridge (handled by the CNI on that node). Cross-node communication requires the CNI's overlay network (VXLAN, IP-in-IP, or BGP routing). If cross-node fails: (1) Check if the CNI DaemonSet pod is running on both nodes, (2) Check for firewall rules blocking overlay protocols (VXLAN uses UDP 4789, Calico IP-in-IP uses protocol 4), (3) Verify node-to-node connectivity. On the CKA, this is an advanced troubleshooting scenario — check CNI pods first, then logs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that the Kubernetes networking model is working correctly? Show a systematic verification approach.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">systematic network verification (5 checks)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Systematic network verification (5 checks):</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Node status (CNI installed?)</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># All nodes should be Ready</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Pod-to-Pod same node</span>
kubectl run test1 --image=busybox --restart=Never -- sleep 3600
kubectl run test2 --image=busybox --restart=Never -- sleep 3600
<span class="comment"># Force same node with nodeName or check with -o wide</span>
kubectl exec test1 -- wget -qO- http://$(kubectl get pod test2 -o jsonpath='{.status.podIP}')

<span class="comment"># 3. Pod-to-Pod cross node</span>
<span class="comment"># Schedule pods on different nodes and test connectivity</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Pod-to-Service (kube-proxy working?)</span>
kubectl exec test1 -- wget -qO- http://kubernetes.default.svc:443 --no-check-certificate

<span class="comment"># 5. DNS resolution (CoreDNS working?)</span>
kubectl exec test1 -- nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># Should return 10.96.0.1</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick all-in-one test:</span>
kubectl run netcheck --image=nicolaka/netshoot --rm -it -- sh -c \
"echo '=== DNS ===' && nslookup kubernetes.default && \
echo '=== SVC ===' && curl -sk https://kubernetes.default:443/healthz && \
echo '=== PASS ==='"</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A systematic network check covers all layers: (1) Node-level (CNI installed, nodes Ready), (2) Pod-level (same-node communication), (3) Overlay (cross-node communication), (4) Service (kube-proxy routing), (5) DNS (CoreDNS resolution). On the CKA, if asked to "verify networking," start from the bottom up: nodes → pods → services → DNS. If step 1 fails, nothing else will work. If steps 1-3 work but step 4 fails, it's a kube-proxy issue. If steps 1-4 work but step 5 fails, it's CoreDNS.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>


