            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 17.2 — Take Down a Node's CNI and Watch It Go NotReady</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Identify the CNI DaemonSet running in <code>kube-system</code></li>
                        <li>Scale it down to exclude one specific node (or delete just that node's CNI pod)</li>
                        <li>Watch <code>kubectl get nodes -w</code> — that node transitions to <code>NotReady</code></li>
                        <li>Confirm existing pods on that node keep running, but NEW pods stay <code>Pending</code></li>
                        <li>Let the DaemonSet recreate the CNI pod and watch the node recover</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> deleting a DaemonSet pod doesn't reduce the DaemonSet's desired count — it gets recreated almost immediately, so you have a narrow window to observe <code>NotReady</code>. Cordon the node first if you want more time to look around.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get ds -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># calico-node   3   3   3   3   3   &lt;none&gt;   5d</span>

kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep calico-node
<span class="comment"># calico-node-xyz   1/1   Running   worker-2</span>

kubectl delete pod calico-node-xyz -n kube-system
kubectl get nodes -w
<span class="comment"># worker-2   NotReady   (briefly, until the DaemonSet recreates the pod)</span>

kubectl get pods -o wide
<span class="comment"># already-running pods on worker-2 stay Running (kubelet keeps them alive);</span>
<span class="comment"># any NEW pod scheduled there sits Pending until the CNI pod comes back</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 17.3 — Diagnose a "Same-Node Works, Cross-Node Fails" Bug</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 10 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <ol>
                        <li>You're told: two easypj pods on the same node can ping each other; a pod on a different node cannot reach either</li>
                        <li>Confirm the symptom with direct pod-IP pings from a netshoot pod on each node</li>
                        <li>Check the CNI DaemonSet's pod status on EVERY node, not just the ones involved</li>
                        <li>Identify which node has a crashing/missing CNI pod</li>
                        <li>State the fix in one sentence</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> same-node success + cross-node failure almost always means the overlay/routing hop is broken on ONE specific node — check <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide</code>, not just "is calico running," to see WHICH node's pod is unhealthy.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep calico-node
<span class="comment"># calico-node-a   1/1   Running          worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># calico-node-b   0/1   CrashLoopBackOff worker-2   ← found it</span>

kubectl logs calico-node-b -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># (shows the actual crash reason — e.g. BGP peering failure, bad config)</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: describe + logs to find root cause, then restart/reconfigure that</span>
<span class="comment"># specific node's CNI pod. Since same-node traffic never leaves worker-2's</span>
<span class="comment"># local bridge, it worked even with a broken CNI pod — only the CROSS-node</span>
<span class="comment"># overlay/routing hop, which the CNI pod itself programs, was affected.</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 17.4 — Identify kube-proxy's Mode and Prove It From the Rules</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Determine which dataplane mode kube-proxy is running in (iptables, IPVS, or nftables) two different ways</li>
                        <li>If iptables mode: find the actual NAT rule for a specific Service</li>
                        <li>If IPVS mode: find the actual virtual-server entry for a specific Service</li>
                        <li>Explain in one sentence why the rule/entry you found proves kube-proxy — not the CNI — owns Service routing</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the kube-proxy ConfigMap's <code>mode:</code> field and the kube-proxy pod logs' "Using ... proxier" line should always agree — if they don't, something restarted kube-proxy without picking up a ConfigMap change yet.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | grep mode
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy | grep -i "using"
<span class="comment"># both should agree, e.g. both say "iptables"</span>

<span class="comment"># iptables mode — find the rule (run on a node):</span>
iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES | grep easypj-web

<span class="comment"># IPVS mode — find the virtual server (run on a node):</span>
ipvsadm -Ln | grep -A2 "10.96.45.2:80"
<span class="comment"># TCP  10.96.45.2:80 rr
#   -&gt; 10.244.1.5:8000   Masq   1   0   0</span>
<span class="comment"># Proves kube-proxy: the VIP 10.96.45.2 has NO real network interface —</span>
<span class="comment"># it exists ONLY as this rule, which only kube-proxy writes/maintains.</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 17.5 — Full Bottom-Up Network Health Sweep</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 10 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Run the 5-layer bottom-up check from §17.5's process steps against a live cluster: CNI → kube-proxy → pod IP → Service IP → DNS</li>
                        <li>At each layer, record PASS or FAIL with the exact command used</li>
                        <li>If everything passes, you've proven the whole networking stack end-to-end in under 2 minutes</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> always go bottom-up, never top-down — a DNS failure could be caused by a broken CNI three layers below it, so checking CNI first saves you from chasing a red herring at the DNS layer.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. CNI</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -Ei 'calico|cilium|flannel'   <span class="comment"># PASS: all Running</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. kube-proxy</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy                <span class="comment"># PASS: all Running</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. pod IP</span>
kubectl run net --rm -it --image=nicolaka/netshoot -- curl -s &lt;pod-ip&gt;:&lt;port&gt;   <span class="comment"># PASS</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Service IP</span>
kubectl exec net -- curl -s http://easypj-web.default.svc.cluster.local          <span class="comment"># PASS</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. DNS</span>
kubectl exec net -- nslookup kubernetes.default                                  <span class="comment"># PASS</span>
<span class="comment"># All 5 pass ⇒ the entire stack (Ch17-19's worth of components) is healthy.</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 17 Visual Summary — a packet's journey pod → Service → pod</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   easypj-web pod ──curl easypj-db (ClusterIP)──┐
                                                 ▼
                             ┌─────────────────────────────────┐
                             │ kube-proxy DNAT: VIP → pod IP    │
                             │ (iptables/IPVS, conntrack-tracked)│
                             └────────────────┬─────────────────┘
                                              ▼
                     CNI flat network (cbr0 bridge; VXLAN/BGP cross-node)
                                              ▼
                                     easypj-db pod (real pod IP)

   4 rules: unique IP · no-NAT pod↔pod · no-NAT node↔pod · pod sees its own real IP
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> a request that names a Service first hits kube-proxy's dataplane, where the Service VIP is DNAT'd to one READY pod endpoint and recorded in conntrack. From that point the packet is just pod-to-pod traffic on the CNI's flat network: it crosses the local bridge if the target pod is on the same node, or is encapsulated (VXLAN) / routed (BGP) across the physical network to the peer node's bridge if not.</p>
                    <p>No NAT is applied between pods, so the destination pod sees the caller's real pod IP — that's Rule 4 in action. Same-node traffic stays entirely in-kernel and fast; cross-node traffic pays a small encapsulation cost (VXLAN) or a route-table lookup (BGP), and either way the CNI — not kube-proxy — owns that hop.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> if you remember just one picture, remember this — kube-proxy resolves WHICH pod, the CNI delivers the packet TO that pod, and pod-to-pod traffic by IP skips kube-proxy entirely. Every troubleshooting flow in this chapter is just asking "which of these two independent layers is broken?"</p>
                </div>
            </div>
