            <!-- ─────────── §18.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.1 How Services Work — Under the Hood</h3>
                <p>A Service is an API object that defines a <strong>logical set of pods</strong> (via a label selector) and a policy for accessing them. Here's what happens when you create a Service:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Service Creation — The Full Chain</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  kubectl apply -f easypj-svc.yaml
           │
           ▼
  ┌─────────────────────┐
  │    API Server        │  Stores Service object in etcd
  └─────────┬───────────┘
            │
            ▼
  ┌─────────────────────┐
  │  Endpoint Controller │  Watches pods matching selector
  │                      │  Creates Endpoints object:
  │                      │  easypj-svc → [10.244.1.5:8000,
  │                      │                10.244.2.3:8000]
  └─────────┬───────────┘
            │
            ▼
  ┌─────────────────────┐
  │    kube-proxy        │  Watches Service + Endpoints
  │    (on every node)   │  Creates iptables/IPVS rules:
  │                      │  10.96.45.2:80 → DNAT → pod IPs
  └─────────┬───────────┘
            │
            ▼
  ┌─────────────────────┐
  │      CoreDNS         │  Creates DNS record:
  │                      │  easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local
  │                      │  → 10.96.45.2
  └─────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Anatomy of a Service Object</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏷️</span> spec.selector</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Label selector — determines which pods receive traffic. <code>app: easypj</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📡</span> spec.ports</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>port</code> = Service port (what clients use), <code>targetPort</code> = pod port (where app listens)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔢</span> spec.clusterIP</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Virtual IP assigned from Service CIDR (10.96.0.0/12) by the API server. Immutable once created — but it doesn't have to be auto-assigned: setting <code>spec.clusterIP: 10.96.45.100</code> explicitly RESERVES that specific address instead, useful when other config (like a hard-coded DNS entry) needs a known, stable IP.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> Endpoints</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Auto-populated list of pod IPs matching the selector. Updated on pod add/remove.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> spec.type</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">ClusterIP (default), NodePort, LoadBalancer, or ExternalName</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Concept: Port vs TargetPort</h5>
                    <p><code>port: 80</code> is what <strong>clients connect to</strong> on the Service IP. <code>targetPort: 8000</code> is the <strong>container port</strong> where your app actually listens. These can be different! Your easypj Django app listens on 8000, but clients access <code>http://easypj-svc:80</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Beyond Ready/Not-Ready: EndpointSlice's <code>serving</code> and <code>terminating</code> Conditions</h5>
                    <p>The "only READY pods appear in endpoints" rule is true but incomplete for a modern cluster. Since Kubernetes 1.26 (stable), every EndpointSlice entry actually tracks THREE independent conditions, not one — and the extra two exist specifically to make rolling updates and pod termination less lossy than a strict binary ready/not-ready model would allow.</p>
                    <div class="comparison-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>What it means</th><th>Why it exists</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>ready</code></td><td>Passing its readiness probe right now</td><td>The classic signal — gates NEW connections in normal operation</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>serving</code></td><td>Was ready, and its containers are still capable of serving — even mid-termination</td><td>A LAST-RESORT fallback signal: service proxies normally IGNORE terminating endpoints entirely, but MAY route to a serving+terminating one specifically when ALL of a Service's endpoints happen to be terminating at once</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>terminating</code></td><td>The pod has a deletion timestamp set — it's shutting down</td><td>Lets a Service-aware component (like an Ingress controller) proactively drain traffic BEFORE the pod actually disappears, not after</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mb-0">This is narrower than it first sounds: <code>serving</code>+<code>terminating</code> is NOT a general "let every in-flight request finish gracefully" mechanism — under NORMAL rolling-update conditions (at least one other endpoint is still healthy), kube-proxy simply stops sending new traffic to a terminating pod, same as before this feature existed. The 3-condition model specifically exists to prevent a WORST-CASE total outage — the rare moment where a full rollout wave makes every replica terminate simultaneously — from dropping 100% of traffic instead of gracefully finishing what it can.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.2 ClusterIP Services</h3>
                <p><strong>ClusterIP</strong> is the default and most common Service type. It assigns a virtual IP <strong>only reachable from within the cluster</strong> — pods and nodes can access it, but external clients cannot.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>When to Use</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Internal microservice communication</li>
                            <li>Database access (postgres-svc)</li>
                            <li>Cache layer (redis-svc)</li>
                            <li>Backend APIs not exposed externally</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div>
                        <h5>Limitations</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Not accessible from outside the cluster</li>
                            <li>Cannot be reached from your laptop</li>
                            <li>Need NodePort/LoadBalancer/Ingress for external access</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-clusterip-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-svc
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: easypj
    tier: web
spec:
  type: ClusterIP          <span class="comment"># Default — can be omitted</span>
  selector:
    app: easypj             <span class="comment"># Must match pod labels!</span>
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80              <span class="comment"># Service port (clients connect here)</span>
      targetPort: 8000      <span class="comment"># Container port (Django listens here)</span>
    - name: admin
      protocol: TCP
      port: 8080            <span class="comment"># Second port — Django admin</span>
      targetPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Imperative ClusterIP Creation &amp; Verification</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ClusterIP service (imperative — fast for CKA)</span>
kubectl expose deployment easypj --name=easypj-svc \
  --port=80 --target-port=8000 --type=ClusterIP

<span class="comment"># Verify Service created</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME         TYPE        CLUSTER-IP    EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-svc  ClusterIP   10.96.45.2    &lt;none&gt;        80/TCP    5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Check endpoints — are pods registered?</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME         ENDPOINTS                        AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-svc  10.244.1.5:8000,10.244.2.3:8000  5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Test from another pod</span>
kubectl exec -it debug-pod -- curl http://easypj-svc:80
<span class="comment"># HTTP 200 — Django responds!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Default Behavior</th>
                                <th>Configuration</th>
                                <th>When to Use</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Session Affinity</strong></td>
                                <td>None — random pod each request</td>
                                <td><code>spec.sessionAffinity: ClientIP</code></td>
                                <td>Sticky sessions, WebSocket apps</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Session Timeout</strong></td>
                                <td>10800s (3 hours)</td>
                                <td><code>spec.sessionAffinityConfig.clientIP.timeoutSeconds: 3600</code></td>
                                <td>Adjust affinity window</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Multi-Port</strong></td>
                                <td>Single port</td>
                                <td>Multiple entries in <code>spec.ports[]</code> (each needs a <code>name</code>)</td>
                                <td>App + metrics, HTTP + HTTPS</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Session Affinity — Sticky Sessions</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># All requests from the same client IP go to the same pod</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-sticky
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  sessionAffinity: ClientIP      # ← Enable sticky sessions
  sessionAffinityConfig:
    clientIP:
      timeoutSeconds: 3600       <span class="comment"># 1 hour affinity window</span>
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.3 NodePort Services</h3>
                <p><strong>NodePort</strong> exposes the Service on a static port on <strong>every node's IP</strong> in the cluster. External clients can access the Service via <code>&lt;NodeIP&gt;:&lt;NodePort&gt;</code>.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">NodePort — How External Traffic Reaches Pods</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  External client: http://192.168.1.10:30080

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                 │
  │  External Request → ANY node at port 30080                     │
  │                                                                 │
  │  ┌── Node 1 ──────────────┐    ┌── Node 2 ──────────────┐     │
  │  │  192.168.1.10:30080    │    │  192.168.1.11:30080    │     │
  │  │         │              │    │         │              │     │
  │  │         ▼ kube-proxy   │    │         ▼ kube-proxy   │     │
  │  │  ┌─────────────────┐   │    │  ┌─────────────────┐   │     │
  │  │  │ iptables/IPVS   │   │    │  │ iptables/IPVS   │   │     │
  │  │  │ DNAT to pod     │   │    │  │ DNAT to pod     │   │     │
  │  │  └────────┬────────┘   │    │  └────────┬────────┘   │     │
  │  │           │            │    │           │            │     │
  │  │  ┌────────▼─────────┐  │    │  ┌────────▼─────────┐  │     │
  │  │  │ easypj-pod-abc   │  │    │  │ easypj-pod-def   │  │     │
  │  │  │ 10.244.1.5:8000  │  │    │  │ 10.244.2.3:8000  │  │     │
  │  │  └──────────────────┘  │    │  └──────────────────┘  │     │
  │  └────────────────────────┘    └────────────────────────┘     │
  │                                                                 │
  │  Note: Request to Node 1:30080 can be routed to pod on Node 2  │
  │  NodePort range: 30000 - 32767 (configurable in API server)    │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-nodeport-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-nodeport
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80              <span class="comment"># ClusterIP port (internal)</span>
      targetPort: 8000      <span class="comment"># Container port</span>
      nodePort: 30080       <span class="comment"># External port on every node</span>
                            <span class="comment"># Range: 30000-32767</span>
                            <span class="comment"># Omit to auto-assign</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Working with NodePort Services</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create NodePort (imperative)</span>
kubectl expose deployment easypj --name=easypj-nodeport \
  --port=80 --target-port=8000 --type=NodePort

<span class="comment"># Service gets BOTH a ClusterIP AND a NodePort</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-nodeport
<span class="comment"># NAME              TYPE       CLUSTER-IP    PORT(S)        AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-nodeport  NodePort   10.96.80.12   80:31245/TCP   5s</span>
<span class="comment">#                                            ↑    ↑</span>
<span class="comment">#                                            |    └ NodePort (auto-assigned)</span>
<span class="comment">#                                            └ ClusterIP port</span>

<span class="comment"># Access from outside the cluster</span>
curl http://192.168.1.10:31245     <span class="comment"># via Node 1</span>
curl http://192.168.1.11:31245     <span class="comment"># via Node 2 — same result!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ NodePort Limitations</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Only 1 Service per port (limited range 30000-32767)</li>
                        <li>A <code>nodePort</code> value OUTSIDE 30000-32767 isn't just discouraged — the API server flatly REJECTS the manifest with a validation error, the same way an invalid <code>pathType</code> is rejected</li>
                        <li>No TLS termination — clients use HTTP</li>
                        <li>Clients must know node IPs — no load balancing across nodes</li>
                        <li>Not suitable for production — use <strong>LoadBalancer</strong> or <strong>Ingress</strong> instead</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>externalTrafficPolicy</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>Client Source IP</th>
                                <th>Trade-off</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Cluster</strong> (default)</td>
                                <td>Traffic can route to pods on <em>any</em> node — extra hop if pod is on a different node</td>
                                <td>❌ SNAT'd — original IP lost</td>
                                <td>Better load distribution, but loses client IP</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Local</strong></td>
                                <td>Traffic only routes to pods on the <em>same node</em> it arrived on</td>
                                <td>✅ Preserved — see real client IP</td>
                                <td>May have uneven load if pods aren't on every node</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Preserving Client Source IP</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Important for logging, rate limiting, geo-routing</span>
spec:
  type: NodePort
  externalTrafficPolicy: Local    # ← Preserves client IP
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8000
      nodePort: 30080</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.4 LoadBalancer Services</h3>
                <p><strong>LoadBalancer</strong> is the standard way to expose Services to the internet in <strong>cloud environments</strong> (AWS, GCP, Azure). It provisions an actual external load balancer that distributes traffic across your nodes.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">LoadBalancer Service — Cloud Integration</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Internet user: http://203.0.113.50:80

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                  │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐                    │
  │  │  Cloud Load Balancer (AWS ELB / GCP LB) │                    │
  │  │  External IP: 203.0.113.50              │                    │
  │  │  Provisioned automatically by K8s       │                    │
  │  └────────────┬────────────────┬───────────┘                    │
  │               │                │                                 │
  │     ┌─────────▼────┐    ┌─────▼────────┐                       │
  │     │ Node 1:30080 │    │ Node 2:30080 │   (NodePort used      │
  │     │ (kube-proxy) │    │ (kube-proxy) │    under the hood)    │
  │     └──────┬───────┘    └────┬─────────┘                       │
  │            │                 │                                   │
  │     ┌──────▼──────────┐  ┌──▼──────────────┐                   │
  │     │ easypj pod-abc  │  │ easypj pod-def  │                   │
  │     │ 10.244.1.5:8000 │  │ 10.244.2.3:8000 │                   │
  │     └─────────────────┘  └─────────────────┘                   │
  │                                                                  │
  │  LoadBalancer = ClusterIP + NodePort + External LB              │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-loadbalancer-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-lb
  annotations:
    <span class="comment"># Cloud-specific annotations</span>
    service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: nlb   <span class="comment"># AWS NLB</span>
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 8000
  <span class="comment"># Optional: restrict external access by IP</span>
  loadBalancerSourceRanges:
    - 10.0.0.0/8
    - 203.0.113.0/24</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Working with LoadBalancer Services</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create LoadBalancer service</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-loadbalancer-svc.yaml

<span class="comment"># Check — EXTERNAL-IP may show &lt;pending&gt; initially</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-lb -w
<span class="comment"># NAME        TYPE           CLUSTER-IP    EXTERNAL-IP     PORT(S)        AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-lb  LoadBalancer   10.96.90.1    &lt;pending&gt;       80:31890/TCP   5s</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-lb  LoadBalancer   10.96.90.1    203.0.113.50    80:31890/TCP   30s</span>

<span class="comment"># Access from the internet</span>
curl http://203.0.113.50:80
<span class="comment"># Django easypj responds!</span>

<span class="comment"># On bare metal without cloud LB — use MetalLB</span>
<span class="comment"># EXTERNAL-IP stays &lt;pending&gt; forever without a LB controller</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Bare Metal: Use MetalLB</h5>
                    <p>On-premises clusters have no cloud LB provider. <strong>MetalLB</strong> fills this gap by assigning real external IPs from a configured pool. Often used in CKA lab environments and homelab setups.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>EndpointSlices — The Modern Endpoints</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> Endpoints (legacy)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Single object listing ALL pod IPs for a Service. Becomes huge in large clusters — every pod change updates the entire object.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> EndpointSlices (v1)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Splits endpoints into chunks of ~100 pods each. Only the affected slice is updated — <strong>much more efficient</strong> at scale.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> View them</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=easypj-svc</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">✅</span> Default since</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Kubernetes 1.21+. Automatically created for every Service. <code>Endpoints</code> still exist for backward compatibility.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Real Cloud LBs: How Client-IP Preservation Actually Works Under the Hood</h5>
                    <p>§18.3's table already covers <code>externalTrafficPolicy: Local</code> as the Service-level lever for preserving the client's real source IP. On a REAL cloud LoadBalancer, that field alone isn't the whole story — the cloud provider's own LB has to cooperate, and different clouds do it differently.</p>
                    <div class="comparison-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Cloud LB</th><th>How it preserves client IP</th><th>What you must configure</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>AWS Network Load Balancer (NLB)</td><td>Passes the raw TCP connection through untouched (L4 passthrough) — the client IP is naturally preserved</td><td><code>externalTrafficPolicy: Local</code> on the Service, plus the NLB annotation on the Service</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>AWS Classic/Application LB</td><td>Terminates the connection and re-establishes a new one — client IP is LOST unless it's injected as an HTTP header</td><td>Enable the <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> header and have the app read it, since the raw TCP source IP is gone</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>GCP LoadBalancer (regional)</td><td>Uses PROXY protocol — wraps the original client IP in a small header prepended to the TCP stream</td><td>The receiving proxy (often the Ingress controller) must be explicitly configured to parse the PROXY protocol header, or it reads garbage</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mb-0">The practical lesson: <code>externalTrafficPolicy: Local</code> is necessary but sometimes NOT sufficient — always check whether your specific cloud's LB additionally needs PROXY-protocol support enabled on the receiving side, or client-IP preservation silently fails even with the Service field set correctly.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.5 ExternalName Services</h3>
                <p><strong>ExternalName</strong> is a special Service type that maps a Service name to an <strong>external DNS name</strong> via a CNAME record. No proxying, no ClusterIP — just DNS redirection.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔀</div>
                        <h5>How It Works</h5>
                        <p>When a pod resolves <code>external-db</code>, CoreDNS returns a CNAME pointing to the external hostname. Traffic goes directly to the external endpoint — kube-proxy is not involved.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Use Cases</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Point to an external database (RDS, Cloud SQL)</li>
                            <li>Migrate from external to internal service gradually</li>
                            <li>Abstract external dependencies behind K8s DNS</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">external-db-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: external-db
  namespace: default
spec:
  type: ExternalName
  externalName: mydb.example.com    <span class="comment"># External DNS name</span>
  <span class="comment"># No selector!</span>
  <span class="comment"># No ClusterIP!</span>
  <span class="comment"># No ports!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Using ExternalName Services</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># From inside a pod:</span>
nslookup external-db
<span class="comment"># external-db.default.svc.cluster.local → CNAME → mydb.example.com</span>

<span class="comment"># Django settings.py can just use:</span>
<span class="comment"># DATABASES = { 'HOST': 'external-db' }</span>
<span class="comment"># If you later migrate to an internal postgres pod, just</span>
<span class="comment"># delete the ExternalName service and create a ClusterIP service</span>
<span class="comment"># with the same name — zero code changes!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 externalName Can Also Be a Raw IPv6 Literal</h5>
                    <p><code>externalName</code> isn't limited to hostnames — it also accepts a bracketed IPv6 address literal (e.g. <code>externalName: "[2001:db8::1]"</code>). In that specific case, CoreDNS returns an AAAA record directly instead of a CNAME, and — just like the normal hostname form — still involves NO proxying at all.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.6 Headless Services</h3>
                <p>A <strong>Headless Service</strong> is a ClusterIP Service with <code>clusterIP: None</code>. Instead of a single virtual IP, DNS resolution returns the <strong>individual pod IPs directly</strong>. This is essential for <strong>StatefulSets</strong> where clients need to reach specific pods.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Headless vs Normal Service — DNS Comparison</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                    │
  │  NORMAL Service (ClusterIP: 10.96.45.2)                          │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  nslookup easypj-svc                                     │    │
  │  │  → 10.96.45.2  (single ClusterIP — kube-proxy routes)   │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                    │
  │  HEADLESS Service (clusterIP: None)                               │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  nslookup postgres-headless                               │    │
  │  │  → 10.244.1.15  (postgres-0)                             │    │
  │  │  → 10.244.2.18  (postgres-1)                             │    │
  │  │  → 10.244.3.22  (postgres-2)                             │    │
  │  │                                                           │    │
  │  │  PLUS individual pod DNS:                                 │    │
  │  │  postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local  │    │
  │  │  → 10.244.1.15                                           │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                    │
  │  Use headless when you need to reach SPECIFIC pods               │
  │  (StatefulSets, database replicas, leader election)              │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">postgres-headless-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: postgres-headless
spec:
  clusterIP: None            # ← This makes it headless!
  selector:
    app: postgres
  ports:
    - name: postgres
      port: 5432
      targetPort: 5432
---
<span class="comment"># Used with StatefulSet</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: postgres
spec:
  serviceName: postgres-headless    # ← Must reference headless service
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: postgres
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: postgres
          image: postgres:15
          ports:
            - containerPort: 5432</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Using Headless Service DNS</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># DNS resolves to ALL pod IPs</span>
kubectl exec debug-pod -- nslookup postgres-headless
<span class="comment"># Name: postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.15</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.2.18</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.3.22</span>

<span class="comment"># Access specific pod by ordinal name</span>
kubectl exec debug-pod -- nslookup postgres-0.postgres-headless
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.15    ← always the primary!</span>

<span class="comment"># Django connects to primary:</span>
<span class="comment"># DATABASES = { 'HOST': 'postgres-0.postgres-headless' }</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.7 Service Discovery — How Pods Find Services</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes provides <strong>two built-in methods</strong> for pods to discover Services. DNS is the primary method used in practice.</p>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🧪 CKA Practice Scenario: Service Debugging</h5>
                    <p><strong>Situation:</strong> You've deployed the easypj app and created a Service, but <code>kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc</code> shows <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>. The app pods are Running.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">Debug: Empty Endpoints</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check what the Service selector is looking for</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-svc -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'
<span class="comment"># {"app":"easypj-web"}     ← The selector value</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check what labels the pods actually have</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels | grep easypj
<span class="comment"># easypj-abc  Running  app=easypj,tier=web</span>
<span class="comment">#                      ↑ Label is "easypj" not "easypj-web"!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Fix — either update the Service selector or the pod labels</span>
<span class="comment"># Option A: Patch the Service selector</span>
kubectl patch svc easypj-svc -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"easypj"}}}'

