            <h5>① internalTrafficPolicy — the In-Cluster Mirror of externalTrafficPolicy</h5>
            <p>§18.3's table covers <code>externalTrafficPolicy</code> for traffic arriving from OUTSIDE the cluster. There's a parallel field for traffic from pods INSIDE the cluster: <code>internalTrafficPolicy</code>. It answers the same "hop vs locality" question, just for east-west traffic instead of north-south.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   internalTrafficPolicy: Cluster (default)      internalTrafficPolicy: Local
   ────────────────────────────────────────      ─────────────────────────────
   caller pod (node-1) ──▶ kube-proxy picks       caller pod (node-1) ──▶ kube-proxy
   ANY ready endpoint cluster-wide:                ONLY considers endpoints ON node-1:
     ┌── endpoint on node-1 (33%)                    ┌── endpoint on node-1 ✅ routed here
     ├── endpoint on node-2 (33%)  ◀── may hop        ├── endpoint on node-2 ✗ never considered
     └── endpoint on node-3 (33%)     cross-node       └── endpoint on node-3 ✗ never considered

   If node-1 has ZERO ready endpoints for this Service:
     Cluster policy → routes to node-2/3 anyway (extra hop, but WORKS)
     Local policy   → connection REFUSED (no fallback — by design)
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>internalTrafficPolicy</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>Cluster</code> (default)</td><td>Routes to ANY ready endpoint cluster-wide</td><td>Even load distribution, may add a cross-node hop</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Local</code></td><td>Routes ONLY to endpoints on the SAME node as the caller</td><td>Zero extra network hop, but fails if no local endpoint exists</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>internalTrafficPolicy: Local</code> is the tool for node-local sidecars (e.g. a logging agent every pod on a node talks to) — it guarantees zero cross-node hop, but ONLY works safely if that Service has at least one ready pod on every node, or callers on an uncovered node get connection refused.</div>

            <h5>② Dual-Stack Services — Carrying Both IPv4 and IPv6 at Once</h5>
            <p>A Service isn't limited to one IP family. <code>ipFamilyPolicy</code> and <code>ipFamilies</code> let a single Service carry both an IPv4 ClusterIP and an IPv6 ClusterIP simultaneously — the same dual-stack concept covered for pod CIDRs in Ch17's UTH now applied one layer up, at the Service layer.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   spec:
     ipFamilyPolicy: PreferDualStack   <span class="comment"># or RequireDualStack / SingleStack</span>
     ipFamilies: [IPv4, IPv6]          <span class="comment"># order = preference</span>

   kubectl get svc easypj-web -o jsonpath='{.spec.clusterIPs}'
   ["10.96.45.2", "fd00:10:96::a1b2"]    ← both families, one Service object
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> dual-stack requires the CLUSTER itself to have been provisioned dual-stack (pod and Service CIDRs for both families) — flipping <code>ipFamilyPolicy</code> on a single-stack cluster does nothing; the cluster-level prerequisite comes first.</div>

            <h5>③ Topology-Aware Routing — Keeping Traffic in the Same Zone</h5>
            <p>At real scale, EndpointSlices carry more than just IPs — they carry topology metadata (which zone each endpoint is in), which kube-proxy can use to prefer same-zone endpoints over a cross-zone hop.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Without topology hints              With topology-aware routing
   ─────────────────────                ──────────────────────────
   zone-a caller ──▶ any pod,           zone-a caller ──▶ PREFERS a
   possibly in zone-b                    zone-a pod if one is READY
   (cross-zone $$ + latency)             (falls back cross-zone only
                                          if no local-zone pod exists)
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this matters most on cloud providers that bill for cross-availability-zone data transfer — topology-aware routing is a real cost lever, not just a latency one, and it degrades gracefully (never drops traffic just to stay in-zone).</div>

            <h5>④ allocateLoadBalancerNodePorts — Skipping the NodePort Hop on Cloud LBs</h5>
            <p>§18.4 states "LoadBalancer = ClusterIP + NodePort + external LB" as the default layering. One field lets you opt OUT of the middle layer: when a cloud LB can route directly to pod IPs (some CNI/cloud combinations support this), allocating a NodePort at all becomes unnecessary overhead.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   allocateLoadBalancerNodePorts: true (default)   allocateLoadBalancerNodePorts: false
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────  ───────────────────────────────────
   Internet ──▶ Cloud LB ──▶ NodePort ──▶ ClusterIP ──▶ Pod   Internet ──▶ Cloud LB ──▶ Pod
                    ↑ allocated but NEVER hit                      (direct pod routing —
                    by any real client — pure waste                requires CNI/cloud LB
                    of one 30000-32767 slot                        support for this path)
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>allocateLoadBalancerNodePorts: true</code> (default)</td><td>NodePort is allocated even though clients only ever hit the cloud LB</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>allocateLoadBalancerNodePorts: false</code></td><td>No NodePort consumed at all — only works when the LB targets pods directly</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this is a real, if niche, resource-conservation lever on clusters running many LoadBalancer Services — each unused NodePort still consumes one slot out of the finite 30000-32767 range across the WHOLE cluster.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Headless-Service SRV Records — The Full DNS Picture for StatefulSets</h5>
            <p>§18.6 shows headless DNS returning individual pod IPs (A records) and per-pod ordinal names. There's one more record type CoreDNS synthesizes for headless Services that named ports unlock: SRV records, which carry BOTH the port and the target host in one lookup.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Headless Service "postgres-headless" with a named port "pg" (5432):

   A records (one per ready pod):
     postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local → 10.244.1.15, 10.244.2.18, ...
   Per-pod ordinal A records:
     postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local → 10.244.1.15
   SRV record (port + host, in ONE answer):
     _pg._tcp.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
       → priority=0 weight=100 port=5432 target=postgres-0.postgres-headless...
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> SRV records matter for clients that discover BOTH the port and the host dynamically (some database drivers and service-mesh control planes do this) — most application code just hard-codes the port and only needs the A record, which is why SRV records are the least-visible DNS form despite being fully supported.</div>
            </div>
