            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>A Service is a stable VIP + DNS name in front of a moving set of pods. It selects pods by label; K8s auto-maintains an EndpointSlice of their IPs.</li>
                    <li>Only READY pods appear in the endpoints. A failing readiness probe silently removes the pod from rotation (graceful, no 500s to clients) — a liveness-probe failure instead restarts the pod.</li>
                    <li>Endpoints are updated automatically as pods die and start — you never hand-edit the IP list.</li>
                    <li>Five types: ClusterIP (internal default), NodePort (node IP:30000-32767), LoadBalancer (cloud LB), ExternalName (DNS CNAME to an external host), Headless (<code>clusterIP: None</code> → returns pod IPs, used by StatefulSets).</li>
                    <li><code>port</code> is the Service's port; <code>targetPort</code> is the container's port. They are independent — port 80 → targetPort 8000 is normal for easypj-web.</li>
                    <li>Type layering: NodePort ⊃ ClusterIP, and LoadBalancer ⊃ NodePort — the richer types still carry a ClusterIP underneath.</li>
                    <li>Service discovery is DNS-first: <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>, with short forms for same-namespace lookups. Django just sets <code>HOST: 'easypj-db'</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>ClusterIP vs NodePort vs LoadBalancer</strong> — ClusterIP = internal only. NodePort = external via <code>&lt;NodeIP&gt;:30000-32767</code>. LoadBalancer = cloud only (won't work on kubeadm clusters without MetalLB)</li>
                    <li><strong>Empty endpoints = selector mismatch</strong> — If <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> shows no IPs, the Service selector doesn't match any pod labels. This is the #1 service debugging scenario on the exam</li>
                    <li><strong>targetPort vs port</strong> — <code>port</code> is what clients connect to (the Service port). <code>targetPort</code> is where traffic goes (the container port). If your app listens on 8000, <code>targetPort: 8000</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Headless Service</strong> — <code>clusterIP: None</code> — no virtual IP, DNS returns individual pod IPs. Required for StatefulSets. The exam tests this</li>
                    <li><strong>ExternalName Service</strong> — Returns a CNAME record. No proxying. Used for external database references. Rarely tested but know it exists</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

