            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 18 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the Service internals — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What replaced the legacy single <code>Endpoints</code> object, and why was the change necessary?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>EndpointSlice</code>. The legacy <code>Endpoints</code> object listed every backend IP in ONE resource — any single pod change rewrote the whole object, which didn't scale. EndpointSlice shards backends across multiple objects (~100 endpoints each) and adds topology metadata.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Backend storage</th><th>Scaling behavior</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Endpoints (legacy)</td><td>One object, ALL IPs</td><td>Whole object rewritten on any pod change</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>EndpointSlice</td><td>Sharded, ~100 IPs/slice</td><td>Only the affected slice updates</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>kubectl get endpoints</code> still works as a friendly aggregate view — kube-proxy itself programs the same iptables/IPVS rules from either object type, so this is purely a control-plane scaling improvement, invisible to the dataplane.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does a successful <code>nslookup</code> for a Service NOT guarantee the app can actually connect?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>DNS and connectivity are two separate, independently-failing layers. <code>nslookup</code> only proves CoreDNS resolved the name to a ClusterIP — it says nothing about whether that Service has any READY endpoints for kube-proxy to route to.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">nslookup easypj-db          <span class="comment"># PASS — proves DNS only</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-db   <span class="comment"># still need to check this separately</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This split explains why "DNS works but connection times out" is such a common symptom — the name resolved fine, but the Service's selector doesn't match any Ready pod, so there's simply nothing for the VIP to DNAT to.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>internalTrafficPolicy: Local</code>, and what's its failure mode?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It restricts in-cluster (pod-to-Service) traffic to ONLY endpoints on the same node as the caller, avoiding a cross-node hop. Its failure mode: if the calling pod's node has zero ready endpoints for that Service, the request fails outright — it does NOT fall back to a remote node.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th><code>Cluster</code> (default)</th><th><code>Local</code></th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Caller's node HAS a ready endpoint</td><td>May route there, or to another node</td><td>Routes there — zero extra hop</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Caller's node has NO ready endpoint</td><td>Routes to another node (works)</td><td>Connection refused (no fallback)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the in-cluster mirror of <code>externalTrafficPolicy: Local</code> from §18.3 — same trade-off (locality/speed vs guaranteed availability), just applied to east-west traffic instead of north-south.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Can a single Service carry both an IPv4 and an IPv6 ClusterIP at the same time?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Yes — with <code>ipFamilyPolicy: PreferDualStack</code> (or <code>RequireDualStack</code>) and <code>ipFamilies: [IPv4, IPv6]</code>, one Service gets a ClusterIP in each family, PROVIDED the underlying cluster was itself provisioned dual-stack.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get svc easypj-web -o jsonpath='{.spec.clusterIPs}'
<span class="comment"># ["10.96.45.2", "fd00:10:96::a1b2"]</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Flipping this field on a single-stack cluster does nothing — the cluster-level pod/Service CIDR dual-stack prerequisite must already be in place; the Service-level field only opts a specific Service into using both families once they're available.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Two identical-looking Services both select <code>app: easypj</code>, but Service A gets traffic and Service B stays empty. What's the most likely YAML difference?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Service B is very likely in a DIFFERENT namespace than the pods, or its selector is a subtly different key/value (e.g. <code>App: easypj</code> vs <code>app: easypj</code> — labels are case-sensitive and exact-match only).</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get svc svcB -n &lt;ns&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj -n &lt;ns&gt;   <span class="comment"># confirm same namespace, exact match</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Selectors never cross namespaces implicitly — a Service can only select pods in its OWN namespace, which is a frequent source of "identical selector, empty endpoints" confusion.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>allocateLoadBalancerNodePorts: false</code> do, and why would a large cluster care?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It skips allocating a NodePort at all for a LoadBalancer Service, used when the cloud LB routes directly to pod IPs. Every allocated NodePort — used or not — consumes one slot out of the cluster-wide finite 30000-32767 range, so at scale with many LoadBalancer Services this is a real resource-conservation lever.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  allocateLoadBalancerNodePorts: false   <span class="comment"># no NodePort reserved at all</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>By default, <code>LoadBalancer</code> Services silently reserve a NodePort even though clients only ever hit the cloud LB's IP — this field is how you opt out of that unnecessary reservation when the cloud/CNI combination supports direct pod routing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A headless Service has a named port <code>pg</code> on 5432. What DNS record type lets a client discover BOTH the port and target host in one lookup?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>An SRV record: <code>_pg._tcp.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</code> returns priority, weight, port, AND target host in one answer — unlike a plain A record, which only returns an IP.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">nslookup -type=SRV _pg._tcp.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># service = 0 100 5432 postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Most application code hard-codes the port and only needs the plain A record, which is why SRV records are the least-visible DNS form in practice despite being fully supported — some database drivers and service-mesh control planes are the main real-world consumers.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is <code>sessionAffinity: ClientIP</code> described as "the only affinity option Kubernetes supports at the Service level" — what's missing compared to a typical load balancer?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>There's no cookie-based affinity at the Service (L4) level — a Service operates below HTTP, so it has no concept of cookies. Cookie-based (L7) session affinity requires an Ingress or a service mesh, which operate at the HTTP layer.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Affinity mechanism</th><th>Where configured</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>L4 (Service)</td><td><code>ClientIP</code> only — pins by source IP</td><td><code>spec.sessionAffinity</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>L7 (Ingress/mesh)</td><td>Cookie-based — pins by session cookie</td><td>Ingress controller annotation (Ch 20)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a useful layering check: if a requirement says "sticky by cookie," that's an Ingress/L7 concern (Ch20), not something <code>sessionAffinity: ClientIP</code> on the Service itself can satisfy.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Topology-aware routing prefers same-zone endpoints. What happens if the calling pod's zone has zero ready endpoints for that Service?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It falls back gracefully to a cross-zone endpoint — topology-aware routing is a PREFERENCE that degrades gracefully, not a hard restriction like <code>internalTrafficPolicy: Local</code>, which fails outright with no local endpoint.</p>
<pre>zone-a caller ──▶ zone-a endpoint READY?  ──yes──▶ routed same-zone (preferred)
                          │no
                          ▼
                  falls back to ANY other zone's ready endpoint  (never dropped)</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This distinction matters: topology hints optimize for cost/latency without ever dropping traffic, while <code>internalTrafficPolicy: Local</code> trades availability for a hard guarantee of zero cross-node hops — pick the right tool depending on whether some cross-zone traffic is acceptable.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment's readiness probe starts failing for one replica. What EXACTLY changes in the Service's EndpointSlice, and what does NOT change?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>That pod's entry is removed from the EndpointSlice's ready-endpoints list — no traffic routes to it. What does NOT change: the pod itself keeps running (it is not restarted or deleted), and it remains visible in <code>kubectl get pods</code> as Running (just not Ready).</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get endpoints easypj-web -w
<span class="comment"># the failing pod's IP disappears from the list, others remain</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the precise mechanical difference between readiness (controls endpoint membership, graceful) and liveness (controls pod lifecycle, restarts) — worth stating exactly this precisely on the exam since the two are easy to conflate.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

