                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ClusterIP Service that exposes a Deployment <code>webapp</code> on port 80, targeting container port 8080. Show the imperative and YAML approach.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative (fastest on cka)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative (fastest on CKA):</span>
kubectl expose deployment webapp \
--port=80 --target-port=8080 \
--type=ClusterIP --name=webapp-svc

<span class="comment"># YAML equivalent:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: webapp-svc
spec:
type: ClusterIP        <span class="comment"># Default — internal only</span>
selector:
app: webapp           <span class="comment"># Must match Deployment pod labels!</span>
ports:
- port: 80             <span class="comment"># Service port (clients connect here)</span>
targetPort: 8080     <span class="comment"># Container port (traffic forwarded here)</span>
protocol: TCP

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get svc webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME        TYPE       CLUSTER-IP    PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-svc  ClusterIP  10.96.45.23   80/TCP</span>

kubectl get endpoints webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME        ENDPOINTS</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-svc  10.244.1.5:8080,10.244.2.8:8080,10.244.1.9:8080</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl expose</code> creates a Service using the Deployment's pod labels as the selector. <code>port</code> is what clients use (Service's listening port), <code>targetPort</code> is where traffic is forwarded (container's port). If <code>targetPort</code> is omitted, it defaults to the same value as <code>port</code>. On the CKA, always check <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> after creating a Service — if it shows no endpoints, the selector doesn't match any pod labels. This is the #1 Service debugging scenario.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the 4 Service types in Kubernetes? Explain each with a use case.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Type</th>
                                        <th>Access</th>
                                        <th>Use Case</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; color: #22c55e;">ClusterIP</td>
                                        <td>Internal only (default)</td>
                                        <td>Backend services, databases</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; color: #3b82f6;">NodePort</td>
                                        <td>External via &lt;NodeIP&gt;:30000-32767</td>
                                        <td>Dev/test, bare-metal access</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; color: #a855f7;">LoadBalancer</td>
                                        <td>Cloud load balancer + external IP</td>
                                        <td>Production cloud deployments</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; color: #eab308;">ExternalName</td>
                                        <td>CNAME DNS redirect</td>
                                        <td>External service aliases</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">nodeport example</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># NodePort example:</span>
kubectl expose deployment webapp --type=NodePort \
--port=80 --target-port=8080
<span class="comment"># Access: curl http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:&lt;assigned-nodeport&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># LoadBalancer (cloud only):</span>
kubectl expose deployment webapp --type=LoadBalancer \
--port=80 --target-port=8080
<span class="comment"># Access: curl http://&lt;external-ip&gt;:80</span>

<span class="comment"># ExternalName:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: external-db
spec:
type: ExternalName
externalName: db.example.com  <span class="comment"># CNAME record</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Each type builds on the previous: <code>NodePort</code> includes a <code>ClusterIP</code>, <code>LoadBalancer</code> includes a <code>NodePort</code> + <code>ClusterIP</code>. <code>ExternalName</code> is different — it just returns a CNAME record (no proxying, no ClusterIP). On the CKA, <code>LoadBalancer</code> won't get an external IP on kubeadm clusters (stays <code>&lt;pending&gt;</code>) — only works on cloud providers or with MetalLB. NodePort range is always 30000-32767. Know these boundaries and which type to use in each scenario.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Service has no endpoints. <code>kubectl get endpoints webapp-svc</code> shows <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>. How do you diagnose and fix this?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check the service selector</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check the Service selector</span>
kubectl get svc webapp-svc -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'
<span class="comment"># {"app":"webapp"}</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check pod labels</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  LABELS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc    1/1    app=web         ← Mismatch! "web" ≠ "webapp"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Fix — either change the Service selector or pod labels</span>

<span class="comment"># Option A: Fix the Service selector</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"web"}}}'

<span class="comment"># Option B: Fix the pod labels</span>
kubectl label pod web-abc app=webapp --overwrite

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify endpoints appear</span>
kubectl get endpoints webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME        ENDPOINTS</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-svc  10.244.1.5:8080</span>

<span class="comment"># Common causes of empty endpoints:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Selector doesn't match pod labels (most common!)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. No pods exist with those labels</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Pods exist but aren't Ready (failed readinessProbe)</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Pods are in a different namespace than the Service</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Empty endpoints = the Service selector doesn't match any pod labels. This is the #1 Service debugging scenario on the CKA exam. The fix is always: compare <code>service.spec.selector</code> with <code>pod.metadata.labels</code>. They must match exactly (key AND value). Also check: pods must be <code>Ready</code> (passing readinessProbe) AND in the same namespace. A pod that exists but fails its readiness probe won't appear in endpoints either. On the CKA, this 3-step pattern solves most Service issues: (1) get svc selector, (2) get pod labels, (3) compare.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a headless Service? How is it different from a regular ClusterIP Service and when do you need one?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">headless service: clusterip: none</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Headless Service: clusterIP: None</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: db-headless
spec:
clusterIP: None        # ← No virtual IP assigned
selector:
app: postgres
ports:
- port: 5432

<span class="comment"># Regular ClusterIP:</span>
<span class="comment"># DNS lookup → returns the Service's virtual IP (10.96.x.x)</span>
<span class="comment"># nslookup db-svc → 10.96.0.50</span>

<span class="comment"># Headless Service:</span>
<span class="comment"># DNS lookup → returns individual pod IPs directly</span>
<span class="comment"># nslookup db-headless → 10.244.1.5, 10.244.2.8</span>
<span class="comment"># (one IP per pod matching the selector)</span>

<span class="comment"># Use cases for headless Services:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. StatefulSets (each pod needs its own DNS name)</span>
<span class="comment">#    web-0.db-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment">#    web-1.db-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Client-side load balancing (app picks which pod)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Service discovery without proxying</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A headless Service has no ClusterIP — DNS returns pod IPs directly instead of a single virtual IP. This is required for StatefulSets because each pod needs its own stable DNS name (e.g., <code>postgres-0.db-headless.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>). Without a headless Service, StatefulSets can't provide stable network identities. On the CKA, the key signals for headless: (1) question mentions StatefulSet, (2) "individual pod DNS names needed," (3) "client-side load balancing." Set <code>clusterIP: None</code> — that's the only difference from a normal ClusterIP Service.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Expose a pod named <code>nginx-pod</code> as a NodePort Service on port 30080. Show the command and verify external access.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create nodeport with specific port</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Create NodePort with specific port</span>
kubectl expose pod nginx-pod --port=80 --type=NodePort \
--name=nginx-nodeport

<span class="comment"># The nodePort is randomly assigned (30000-32767)</span>
<span class="comment"># To set a specific nodePort, use YAML:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: nginx-nodeport
spec:
type: NodePort
selector:
run: nginx-pod    <span class="comment"># Match the pod's labels</span>
ports:
- port: 80
targetPort: 80
nodePort: 30080   # ← Specific port

kubectl apply -f nodeport-svc.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get svc nginx-nodeport
<span class="comment"># NAME             TYPE      CLUSTER-IP   PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># nginx-nodeport   NodePort  10.96.15.8   80:30080/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Test access from ANY node's IP:</span>
curl http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:30080
<span class="comment"># Welcome to nginx!</span>

<span class="comment"># Works on ALL nodes, even nodes where nginx-pod isn't running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy routes the traffic to the correct node</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>NodePort exposes the Service on a static port on every node. You can access it via <code>&lt;any-node-IP&gt;:&lt;nodePort&gt;</code>. kube-proxy ensures traffic reaches the correct pod regardless of which node you hit. On the CKA, use <code>kubectl expose --type=NodePort</code> for quick creation, but you'll need YAML to specify a custom <code>nodePort</code>. The <code>nodePort</code> field must be in the 30000-32767 range. Note: NodePort also creates a ClusterIP — so the Service is accessible both internally (via ClusterIP) and externally (via NodePort).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to Service endpoints when a pod fails its readiness probe? How is this different from a liveness probe failure?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">readiness probe failure</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Readiness probe failure:</span>
<span class="comment"># → Pod is REMOVED from Service endpoints</span>
<span class="comment"># → No traffic is routed to it</span>
<span class="comment"># → Pod keeps running (not restarted)</span>
<span class="comment"># → When probe passes again, pod is re-added to endpoints</span>

<span class="comment"># Liveness probe failure:</span>
<span class="comment"># → Pod is RESTARTED (killed and recreated)</span>
<span class="comment"># → During restart, it's also removed from endpoints</span>
<span class="comment"># → After restart, if readiness passes, re-added to endpoints</span>

<span class="comment"># Combined behavior:</span>
spec:
containers:
- name: app
readinessProbe:     <span class="comment"># Controls endpoint membership</span>
  httpGet:
    path: /ready
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 5
livenessProbe:      <span class="comment"># Controls pod lifecycle</span>
  httpGet:
    path: /healthz
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 10

<span class="comment"># Watch endpoints change in real-time:</span>
kubectl get endpoints webapp-svc -w
<span class="comment"># ENDPOINTS</span>
<span class="comment"># 10.244.1.5:8080,10.244.2.8:8080  (2 healthy pods)</span>
<span class="comment"># 10.244.1.5:8080                   (pod 2 failed readiness)</span>
<span class="comment"># 10.244.1.5:8080,10.244.2.8:8080  (pod 2 recovered)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Readiness probes control traffic routing: failing = removed from endpoints (no traffic). Liveness probes control pod lifecycle: failing = pod restarted. A pod can fail readiness but pass liveness — it stays running but receives no traffic (useful for warm-up periods). On the CKA, if a Service intermittently doesn't reach a pod, check its readiness probe. If a pod keeps restarting, check its liveness probe. Best practice: use BOTH — readiness for traffic gating, liveness for crash recovery.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you change an existing Service from ClusterIP to NodePort without deleting it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl edit</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl edit</span>
kubectl edit svc webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># Change spec.type from ClusterIP to NodePort</span>
<span class="comment"># Save — a nodePort is auto-assigned</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl patch</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc \
-p '{"spec":{"type":"NodePort"}}'

<span class="comment"># Method 3: With specific nodePort</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc \
-p '{"spec":{"type":"NodePort","ports":[{"port":80,"nodePort":30080}]}}'

<span class="comment"># Verify the change:</span>
kubectl get svc webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># TYPE      CLUSTER-IP   PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># NodePort  10.96.45.23  80:30080/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Note: Going FROM NodePort TO ClusterIP requires removing nodePort:</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc \
--type='json' \
-p='[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/ports/0/nodePort"},
   {"op":"replace","path":"/spec/type","value":"ClusterIP"}]'</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>You CAN change Service types in-place without deleting. Going ClusterIP → NodePort is straightforward. Going NodePort → ClusterIP requires removing the <code>nodePort</code> field first. Going to/from LoadBalancer depends on the cloud provider. On the CKA, <code>kubectl patch</code> is faster than <code>kubectl edit</code> for type changes. The ClusterIP is preserved during the change, so internal consumers aren't affected. Only the external access method changes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does Kubernetes discover Services? What are the two discovery mechanisms?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">mechanism 1: environment variables (legacy)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Mechanism 1: Environment Variables (legacy)</span>
<span class="comment"># Every pod gets env vars for each Service in the SAME namespace</span>
kubectl exec webapp-pod -- env | grep -i redis
<span class="comment"># REDIS_SVC_SERVICE_HOST=10.96.78.12</span>
<span class="comment"># REDIS_SVC_SERVICE_PORT=6379</span>
<span class="comment"># REDIS_SVC_PORT=tcp://10.96.78.12:6379</span>

<span class="comment"># Limitation: Service must exist BEFORE the pod starts!</span>
<span class="comment"># Pods don't get env vars for Services created after them</span>

<span class="comment"># Mechanism 2: DNS (preferred, always works)</span>
kubectl exec webapp-pod -- nslookup redis-svc
<span class="comment"># Server: 10.96.0.10 (CoreDNS)</span>
<span class="comment"># Name: redis-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.78.12</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS formats:</span>
<span class="comment"># Same namespace:     redis-svc</span>
<span class="comment"># Cross namespace:    redis-svc.other-ns</span>
<span class="comment"># Full FQDN:          redis-svc.other-ns.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS always works regardless of creation order</span>
<span class="comment"># This is why DNS is the recommended discovery method</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubernetes provides two discovery mechanisms: environment variables (injected at pod start) and DNS (via CoreDNS, always available). DNS is preferred because: (1) it works regardless of creation order, (2) it works cross-namespace, (3) it's dynamic (reflects Service changes). Environment variables only contain Services that existed when the pod started. On the CKA, always use DNS names (<code>redis-svc</code>) instead of ClusterIPs or environment variables. For cross-namespace access, use <code>svc-name.namespace</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Service that exposes multiple ports (HTTP 80 and HTTPS 443) for a single Deployment.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: webapp-multi-port
spec:
selector:
app: webapp
ports:
- name: http          <span class="comment"># Name is REQUIRED for multi-port</span>
port: 80
targetPort: 8080
protocol: TCP
- name: https         <span class="comment"># Name is REQUIRED for multi-port</span>
port: 443
targetPort: 8443
protocol: TCP

<span class="comment"># CRITICAL: When a Service has multiple ports,</span>
<span class="comment"># each port MUST have a unique "name" field!</span>
<span class="comment"># Without names, the API rejects the Service</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get svc webapp-multi-port
<span class="comment"># PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># 80/TCP,443/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Test each port:</span>
kubectl exec test -- curl http://webapp-multi-port:80
kubectl exec test -- curl -k https://webapp-multi-port:443</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Multi-port Services are common in production — web servers often serve both HTTP and HTTPS. The key CKA gotcha: when defining multiple ports, each MUST have a unique <code>name</code> field — otherwise the API server rejects the definition. Single-port Services don't require a name (it's optional). On the CKA, if you're asked to expose both ports, remember: (1) add <code>name</code> to each port, (2) ensure <code>targetPort</code> matches the container's actual listening ports, (3) names must be lowercase alphanumeric with hyphens.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>sessionAffinity</code> in a Service? How do you configure it so that all requests from the same client go to the same pod?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">default: no session affinity (round-robin)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Default: No session affinity (round-robin)</span>
<span class="comment"># Requests from same client may hit different pods</span>

<span class="comment"># Enable session affinity (sticky sessions):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: webapp-svc
spec:
selector:
app: webapp
sessionAffinity: ClientIP      # ← Sticky by client IP
sessionAffinityConfig:
clientIP:
  timeoutSeconds: 10800      # ← Sticky for 3 hours (default)
ports:
- port: 80
targetPort: 8080

<span class="comment"># Or via kubectl patch:</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc \
-p '{"spec":{"sessionAffinity":"ClientIP"}}'

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl describe svc webapp-svc | grep -i session
<span class="comment"># Session Affinity: ClientIP</span>

<span class="comment"># Now all requests from the same source IP</span>
<span class="comment"># go to the same backend pod for 3 hours</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>sessionAffinity: ClientIP</code> makes the Service route all requests from the same client IP to the same backend pod. This is useful for applications with server-side sessions (though stateless apps are preferred). The only option Kubernetes supports is <code>ClientIP</code> (no cookie-based affinity at the Service level — use Ingress for that). On the CKA, session affinity is rarely tested directly, but understanding it helps debug "inconsistent responses" where different pods have different state.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create an <code>ExternalName</code> Service that maps <code>external-db</code> to <code>db.prod.example.com</code>. How does it differ from other Service types? When would you use it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create externalname service</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Create ExternalName Service</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: external-db
namespace: default
spec:
type: ExternalName
externalName: db.prod.example.com   # ← FQDN, not an IP!

<span class="comment"># Or imperatively:</span>
kubectl create service externalname external-db \
--external-name=db.prod.example.com

<span class="comment"># How it works:</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod does DNS lookup for "external-db" or</span>
<span class="comment">#   "external-db.default.svc.cluster.local"</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS returns a CNAME record → db.prod.example.com</span>
<span class="comment"># No proxy, no ClusterIP, no Endpoints — just DNS aliasing</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get svc external-db
<span class="comment"># NAME          TYPE           CLUSTER-IP   EXTERNAL-IP             PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># external-db   ExternalName   &lt;none&gt;       db.prod.example.com     &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Key differences from other Service types:</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ No ClusterIP assigned</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ No Endpoints object created</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ No label selector — doesn't match any pods</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ Cannot use with IP addresses (must be DNS name)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Works purely at DNS level (CNAME redirect)</span>

<span class="comment"># Use case: point to external databases, APIs, or</span>
<span class="comment"># services in other namespaces without hardcoding URLs</span>

<span class="comment"># Cross-namespace alias example:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: postgres
namespace: dev
spec:
type: ExternalName
externalName: postgres-svc.production.svc.cluster.local</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>ExternalName</code> is the simplest Service type — it creates a DNS CNAME alias with no proxying. When a pod resolves <code>external-db</code>, CoreDNS returns a CNAME pointing to the external FQDN. No ClusterIP, no Endpoints, no kube-proxy rules. It's used to reference external services (databases, APIs) or services in other namespaces by a local name. Two key limitations: (1) it only works with DNS names, not IP addresses, (2) it doesn't support ports — the client must know the target port. On the CKA, know that ExternalName is the only Service type that creates no Endpoints and has no ClusterIP.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            
