            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 18 Visual Summary — the 5 Service types (who can reach them)</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   ┌──────────────┬────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┐
   │ ClusterIP    │ inside cluster only (def.)  │ easypj-web internal   │
   │ NodePort     │ &lt;anyNodeIP&gt;:30000-32767     │ dev/test external     │
   │ LoadBalancer │ cloud external LB → nodes    │ prod on cloud         │
   │ ExternalName │ DNS CNAME to external host   │ managed psql alias    │
   │ Headless     │ clusterIP:None → pod DNS     │ StatefulSet (db-0..)  │
   └──────────────┴────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘
   Type layering: NodePort ⊃ ClusterIP · LoadBalancer ⊃ NodePort ⊃ ClusterIP
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> a ClusterIP Service is the internal default, reachable only by other pods (this is how easypj-web is consumed in-cluster). A NodePort opens a static port (30000-32767) on every node's IP so you can hit the app from outside in dev/test or on bare-metal. A LoadBalancer asks the cloud for a real external IP and front-ends the nodes for production.</p>
                    <p>An ExternalName is not a real proxy at all — it is a DNS CNAME that aliases an outside host (like a managed PostgreSQL endpoint) so the app keeps using a friendly in-cluster name. A Headless Service sets <code>clusterIP: None</code> so DNS returns the individual pod IPs and gives each StatefulSet replica a stable per-pod name (<code>postgres-0.easypj-db...</code>).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> each richer type builds on the simpler one — NodePort includes a ClusterIP, and LoadBalancer includes a NodePort. Whatever type you pick, the underlying mechanism never changes: selector → EndpointSlice → kube-proxy dataplane rule.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch19">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 19 · DNS &amp; CoreDNS</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">DNS is the <strong>service discovery backbone</strong> of Kubernetes. Every time your easypj pod connects to <code>postgres-svc</code>, a DNS query resolves that name to a ClusterIP. <strong>CoreDNS</strong> is the cluster DNS server that handles all of this — and knowing how to configure and troubleshoot it is a core CKA skill.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔍 DNS</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
