            <section class="section-block" id="s19-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.1 How Kubernetes DNS Works</h3>
                <p>Every pod in Kubernetes is automatically configured to use the cluster DNS server (CoreDNS). Here's the complete resolution flow:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">DNS Resolution Flow — End to End</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  easypj pod runs: curl http://postgres-svc:5432

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                      │
  │  Step 1: Pod checks /etc/resolv.conf (auto-configured by kubelet)   │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │  nameserver 10.96.0.10                                        │ │
  │  │  search default.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local           │ │
  │  │         cluster.local                                          │ │
  │  │  ndots:5                                                       │ │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                                                                      │
  │  Step 2: "postgres-svc" has fewer than 5 dots → search path used   │
  │          → Queries: postgres-svc.default.svc.cluster.local          │
  │                                                                      │
  │  Step 3: Query sent to 10.96.0.10 (CoreDNS ClusterIP)             │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐                    │
  │  │  CoreDNS (kube-system namespace)           │                    │
  │  │  Pod: coredns-abc-123                      │                    │
  │  │  ClusterIP: 10.96.0.10 (kube-dns Service) │                    │
  │  │                                            │                    │
  │  │  Looks up Service "postgres-svc" in        │                    │
  │  │  namespace "default"                        │                    │
  │  │  → Found!  ClusterIP: 10.96.88.5          │                    │
  │  └──────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘                    │
  │                     │                                                │
  │  Step 4: Returns A record: 10.96.88.5                              │
  │                                                                      │
  │  Step 5: Pod connects to 10.96.88.5:5432                           │
  │          → kube-proxy DNAT → actual postgres pod IP                │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Pod's /etc/resolv.conf — Explained</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔢</span> nameserver</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>10.96.0.10</code> — The ClusterIP of the <code>kube-dns</code> Service in kube-system (points to CoreDNS pods)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> search domains</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>default.svc.cluster.local</code> — Allows short names like <code>postgres-svc</code> to resolve within the namespace</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔢</span> ndots:5</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">If the name has fewer than 5 dots, append search domains first. <code>postgres-svc</code> (0 dots) → search path used. <code>api.example.com</code> (2 dots) → also search path first!</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 ndots:5 Performance Consideration</h5>
                    <p>The default <code>ndots:5</code> means external domains like <code>api.example.com</code> generate 4-5 DNS queries before the real one succeeds. For performance-sensitive apps, you can override this in the pod spec with <code>dnsConfig: { options: [{ name: ndots, value: "2" }] }</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>DNS Resolution Decision Path</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Pod Makes DNS Query</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>App calls <code>postgres-svc</code> → reads <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> → name has 0 dots (&lt; ndots:5)</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Search Path Applied</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Tries: <code>postgres-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → <code>postgres-svc.svc.cluster.local</code> → <code>postgres-svc.cluster.local</code></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>CoreDNS Lookup</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>CoreDNS checks its in-memory cache → watches K8s API for Services/Endpoints → finds match → returns ClusterIP</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Connection Made</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Pod connects to ClusterIP → kube-proxy DNAT rules → routed to actual pod IP → connection established</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>dnsPolicy</th>
                                <th>resolv.conf Source</th>
                                <th>Can Resolve Services?</th>
                                <th>When Used</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ClusterFirst</strong> (default)</td>
                                <td>CoreDNS (10.96.0.10)<br>+ search domains</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>Standard pods — always use this</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Default</strong></td>
                                <td>Node's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code></td>
                                <td>❌ No — can't see Services</td>
                                <td>Rarely used; hostNetwork pods</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ClusterFirstWithHostNet</strong></td>
                                <td>CoreDNS (10.96.0.10)</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>Pods with <code>hostNetwork: true</code> that need Service DNS — ⚠️ not supported on Windows nodes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>None</strong></td>
                                <td>Must supply <code>dnsConfig</code> manually</td>
                                <td>Depends on config</td>
                                <td>Custom DNS (e.g., external resolver only)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Custom DNS Configuration in Pod Spec</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Override DNS settings for a specific pod</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-custom-dns
spec:
  dnsPolicy: None           # ← Take full control of DNS
  dnsConfig:
    nameservers:
      - 10.96.0.10           <span class="comment"># CoreDNS</span>
      - 8.8.8.8              <span class="comment"># Google DNS as fallback</span>
    searches:
      - default.svc.cluster.local
      - svc.cluster.local
    options:
      - name: ndots
        value: "2"           # ← Fewer search attempts for external domains
  containers:
    - name: easypj
      image: easypj:latest</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §19.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.2 CoreDNS Configuration</h3>
                <p>CoreDNS is deployed as a <strong>Deployment</strong> in the <code>kube-system</code> namespace, typically with 2 replicas for high availability. Its configuration lives in a <strong>ConfigMap</strong> called <code>coredns</code>.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CoreDNS Architecture in Kubernetes</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────── kube-system namespace ────────────────────┐
  │                                                                │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │  Deployment: coredns (replicas: 2)                       │ │
  │  │  ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐              │ │
  │  │  │ coredns-abc-111 │   │ coredns-abc-222 │              │ │
  │  │  │ (Pod 1)         │   │ (Pod 2)         │              │ │
  │  │  └────────┬────────┘   └────────┬────────┘              │ │
  │  └───────────┼─────────────────────┼────────────────────────┘ │
  │              │                     │                           │
  │  ┌───────────▼─────────────────────▼────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │  Service: kube-dns (ClusterIP: 10.96.0.10)              │ │
  │  │  Ports: 53/UDP, 53/TCP, 9153/TCP (metrics)              │ │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                                                                │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │  ConfigMap: coredns                                      │  │
  │  │  Contains: Corefile (plugin configuration)               │  │
  │  │  Mounted at: /etc/coredns/Corefile                       │  │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">The Default Corefile — Annotated</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: coredns
  namespace: kube-system
data:
  Corefile: |
    .:53 {                          <span class="comment"># Listen on port 53 for all zones</span>
        errors                       <span class="comment"># Log errors to stdout</span>
        health {                     <span class="comment"># Health check endpoint on :8080</span>
            lameduck 5s              <span class="comment"># Wait 5s before shutting down</span>
        }
        ready                        <span class="comment"># Readiness probe on :8181</span>
        kubernetes cluster.local in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa {
            pods insecure            <span class="comment"># Enable pod DNS records</span>
            fallthrough in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa
            ttl 30                   <span class="comment"># Cache TTL: 30 seconds</span>
        }
        prometheus :9153             <span class="comment"># Metrics endpoint for monitoring</span>
        forward . /etc/resolv.conf {  <span class="comment"># Forward external queries to</span>
            max_concurrent 1000       <span class="comment"># upstream DNS (node's resolv.conf)</span>
        }
        cache 30                     <span class="comment"># Cache all responses for 30s</span>
        loop                         <span class="comment"># Detect and stop DNS loops</span>
        reload                       <span class="comment"># Auto-reload Corefile on change</span>
        loadbalance                  <span class="comment"># Round-robin A/AAAA records</span>
    }</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Common CoreDNS Customizations</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add a custom DNS entry (stub domain)</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit the ConfigMap to forward *.example.com to a specific DNS server</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Add this block inside the Corefile:</span>
<span class="comment">#   example.com:53 {</span>
<span class="comment">#       forward . 10.0.0.1</span>
<span class="comment">#   }</span>

<span class="comment"># Add a static host entry (hosts plugin)</span>
<span class="comment"># Add inside the main server block, before kubernetes:</span>
<span class="comment">#   hosts {</span>
<span class="comment">#       10.10.10.50 mydb.internal</span>
<span class="comment">#       fallthrough</span>
<span class="comment">#   }</span>

<span class="comment"># After editing — CoreDNS auto-reloads (reload plugin)</span>
<span class="comment"># Verify: check CoreDNS logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns --tail=20</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam: Never Delete the CoreDNS ConfigMap</h5>
                    <p>If you're asked to modify DNS configuration, use <code>kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system</code>. The <code>reload</code> plugin means changes take effect automatically within 30 seconds. Do NOT restart CoreDNS pods unless explicitly required — they auto-reload.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>CoreDNS Plugin</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                                <th>Key Config</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>kubernetes</strong></td>
                                <td>Resolves Service and Pod DNS names from the K8s API</td>
                                <td><code>kubernetes cluster.local</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>forward</strong></td>
                                <td>Forwards non-cluster queries to upstream DNS servers</td>
                                <td><code>forward . /etc/resolv.conf</code> or <code>forward . 8.8.8.8</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>cache</strong></td>
                                <td>Caches DNS responses to reduce API server load</td>
                                <td><code>cache 30</code> (seconds)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>loop</strong></td>
                                <td>Detects DNS forwarding loops and stops CoreDNS</td>
                                <td><code>loop</code> (no arguments)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>reload</strong></td>
                                <td>Auto-reloads Corefile when ConfigMap changes</td>
                                <td><code>reload</code> (default: every 30s)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>hosts</strong></td>
                                <td>Serves static DNS entries (like <code>/etc/hosts</code>)</td>
                                <td><code>hosts { 10.0.0.1 mydb.local }</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>errors</strong></td>
                                <td>Logs DNS errors to stdout (visible via <code>kubectl logs</code>)</td>
                                <td><code>errors</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>health</strong></td>
                                <td>HTTP health check endpoint on port 8080</td>
                                <td><code>health { lameduck 5s }</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ready</strong></td>
                                <td>Readiness probe endpoint on port 8181</td>
                                <td><code>ready</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>prometheus</strong></td>
                                <td>Exposes metrics for monitoring on port 9153</td>
                                <td><code>prometheus :9153</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📈</div>
                        <h5>Scaling CoreDNS Itself</h5>
                        <p>CoreDNS is just a Deployment — it can be scaled like any other. Real clusters often run a <strong>cluster-proportional-autoscaler</strong> (a small controller in <code>kube-system</code>) that adjusts CoreDNS's replica count proportionally to cluster size (e.g. 1 replica per N nodes/cores), instead of a fixed 2 replicas regardless of whether the cluster has 5 nodes or 500.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get deploy coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># manual scale (fine for small/static clusters):</span>
kubectl scale deploy coredns -n kube-system --replicas=4</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌍</div>
                        <h5>external-dns: Syncing K8s Names to REAL Public DNS</h5>
                        <p>Everything in this chapter is about names INSIDE the cluster. <strong>external-dns</strong> is the widely-used real-world tool that watches Ingress/Service/HTTPRoute objects and automatically creates matching records in an EXTERNAL DNS provider (Route53, Cloud DNS, Cloudflare) — so <code>easypj.example.com</code> pointing at the Ingress's real IP happens automatically on every deploy, instead of a human hand-editing a DNS zone file.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CoreDNS Down ≠ external-dns Down — Two Completely Separate Failure Domains</h5>
                    <p>It's tempting to lump "DNS problems" into one bucket, but CoreDNS (this chapter, resolves names INSIDE the cluster) and external-dns (a separate optional Deployment, publishes names to the OUTSIDE world) are entirely independent components with no runtime dependency on each other. CoreDNS being perfectly healthy says nothing about whether external-dns successfully published <code>easypj.example.com</code> to Route53 — and vice versa. Diagnose them as two separate systems, never assume one implies the other is fine.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §19.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.3 DNS Records in Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes automatically creates DNS records for Services and (optionally) Pods. Understanding these record formats is essential for cross-namespace communication.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Record Type</th>
                                <th>DNS Name Format</th>
                                <th>Resolves To</th>
                                <th>Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>A Record</strong> (Service)</td>
                                <td><code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>ClusterIP</td>
                                <td><code>easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → 10.96.45.2</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>A Record</strong> (Headless)</td>
                                <td><code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>All Pod IPs</td>
                                <td><code>postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → 10.244.1.15, 10.244.2.18</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>A Record</strong> (Pod via Headless)</td>
                                <td><code>pod-name.svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>Specific Pod IP</td>
                                <td><code>postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → 10.244.1.15</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>SRV Record</strong></td>
                                <td><code>_port-name._protocol.svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>Port + hostname</td>
                                <td><code>_http._tcp.easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → 0 10 80 ...</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>CNAME</strong> (ExternalName)</td>
                                <td><code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>External hostname</td>
                                <td><code>external-db.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → mydb.example.com</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>A Record</strong> (Pod direct)</td>
                                <td><code>pod-ip-dashed.namespace.pod.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>Pod IP</td>
                                <td><code>10-244-1-5.default.pod.cluster.local</code> → 10.244.1.5</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 What Actually Produces the "postgres-0.postgres-headless..." Name — the Pod-Spec Mechanism</h5>
                    <p>The per-pod ordinal DNS name in the table above isn't magic tied only to StatefulSets — it comes from two ordinary pod-spec fields, <code>hostname</code> and <code>subdomain</code>, which any pod can set directly. A StatefulSet just automates setting them for you on every replica it creates.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  hostname: postgres-0        <span class="comment"># becomes the "postgres-0" part</span>
  subdomain: postgres-headless <span class="comment"># must match a headless Service's name</span>
  <span class="comment"># Result: postgres-0.postgres-headless.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</span></code></pre>
                    <p class="mb-0">Two real limits worth knowing: the resulting FQDN is capped at 64 characters total (a long StatefulSet name + long headless-Service name can genuinely hit this), and <code>setHostnameAsFQDN: true</code> (stable since v1.22) additionally makes the pod's own <code>hostname</code> command return the full FQDN instead of just the short hostname — useful for apps that self-register using their own hostname.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Exploring All DNS Record Types</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Launch a DNS debug pod</span>
kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.28 --rm -it -- sh

<span class="comment"># A record — Service ClusterIP</span>
nslookup easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.45.2</span>

<span class="comment"># A records — Headless Service (returns all pod IPs)</span>
nslookup postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.15</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.2.18</span>

<span class="comment"># A record — specific pod via headless service</span>
nslookup postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.15</span>

<span class="comment"># SRV record — discover port and protocol</span>
nslookup -type=SRV _http._tcp.easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># service = 0 10 80 easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod DNS (IP dashes)</span>
nslookup 10-244-1-5.default.pod.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.5</span>

<span class="comment"># Cross-namespace resolution</span>
nslookup easypj-svc.prod.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.88.5  (Service in "prod" namespace)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Short Names vs FQDN</h5>
                    <p>Within the <strong>same namespace</strong>, use short names: <code>easypj-svc</code> or <code>easypj-svc:80</code>. Across namespaces, include the namespace: <code>easypj-svc.prod</code>. Full FQDNs are rarely needed in practice but may appear on the CKA exam.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>📛 Short Names (Same Namespace)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>easypj-svc</code> → resolves using search path</li>
                            <li><code>easypj-svc:80</code> → with port</li>
                            <li>Queries: <code>easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                            <li><strong>Pro:</strong> Simple, clean code</li>
                            <li><strong>Con:</strong> Won't work cross-namespace</li>
                            <li><strong>Use for:</strong> Most app-to-service calls</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>🌐 FQDN (Cross-Namespace)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>easypj-svc.prod.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                            <li><code>db-svc.database.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                            <li>Bypasses search path → single DNS query</li>
                            <li><strong>Pro:</strong> Works from any namespace, slightly faster</li>
                            <li><strong>Con:</strong> Verbose, hardcoded namespace</li>
                            <li><strong>Use for:</strong> Cross-namespace calls, external DNS</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔤</div>
                        <h5>A Records (Address)</h5>
                        <p>Map service/pod names to IP addresses. ClusterIP services get one A record. Headless services return all pod IPs. Most common record type in K8s.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📡</div>
                        <h5>SRV Records</h5>
                        <p>Include port number + hostname. Format: <code>_port._protocol.svc.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>. Used to discover both port and address of a service.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>CNAME Records</h5>
                        <p>Created by <code>ExternalName</code> services. Maps a K8s service name to an external hostname. No proxying — just DNS aliasing.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div>
                        <h5>Pod DNS Records</h5>
                        <p>Pod IPs get dashed-IP format: <code>10-244-1-5.ns.pod.cluster.local</code>. Via headless services: <code>pod-name.svc-name.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §19.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.4 Troubleshooting DNS Issues</h3>
                <p>DNS failures are one of the most common Kubernetes problems — and one of the most tested on the CKA. Here's a systematic approach to diagnose and fix DNS issues.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Is CoreDNS Running?</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — both pods should be Running</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Is the kube-dns Service Up?</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system</code> — should have ClusterIP 10.96.0.10</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Can Pods Reach CoreDNS?</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl exec POD -- nslookup kubernetes.default</code> — tests basic DNS connectivity</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Check CoreDNS Logs</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — look for errors</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Check Corefile Config</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get cm coredns -n kube-system -o yaml</code> — verify configuration is correct</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>DNS Symptom</th>
                                <th>Likely Cause</th>
                                <th>Diagnostic</th>
                                <th>Fix</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>nslookup</code> times out</td>
                                <td>CoreDNS pods not running</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></td>
                                <td>Check CoreDNS deployment, restart pods</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>SERVFAIL</code> response</td>
                                <td>Corefile misconfiguration</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></td>
                                <td>Fix Corefile syntax in ConfigMap</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>NXDOMAIN</code> — name not found</td>
                                <td>Service doesn't exist or wrong namespace</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get svc -A | grep svc-name</code></td>
                                <td>Create the Service or use correct namespace prefix</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>External domains fail</td>
                                <td><code>forward</code> plugin pointing to wrong upstream</td>
                                <td>Check <code>forward . /etc/resolv.conf</code> in Corefile</td>
                                <td>Fix upstream DNS (e.g., <code>forward . 8.8.8.8</code>)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>DNS works in some pods, not others</td>
                                <td>Pod's <code>dnsPolicy</code> is set to <code>Default</code></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pod POD -o yaml | grep dnsPolicy</code></td>
                                <td>Change to <code>dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst</code> (the default)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>DNS loop detected</td>
                                <td>Node's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points to a local DNS that forwards back to CoreDNS</td>
                                <td>CoreDNS logs: <code>"Loop ... detected"</code></td>
                                <td>Change <code>forward . /etc/resolv.conf</code> to <code>forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
