            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, DNS is a common troubleshooting domain. If a pod can't resolve a Service name: (1) <code>kubectl exec pod -- nslookup &lt;svc&gt;</code> — test resolution, (2) <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — are CoreDNS pods running? (3) <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — any errors? (4) Check <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> inside the pod — is nameserver set to kube-dns Service IP? If DNS is completely broken, the cluster is effectively dead.
            </div>

            <h5>① Record Synthesis — Why CoreDNS Never Hits the API Per-Query</h5>
            <p>The plugin-chain diagram above shows the <code>kubernetes</code> plugin "querying the K8s API" for illustration, but that's a simplification worth correcting: it does NOT make a live API call for every DNS question. It opens a WATCH once, builds an in-memory map, and answers every subsequent query straight from memory — which is exactly what makes CoreDNS fast enough to sit in the hot path of every Service call in the cluster.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   WRONG mental model:                    ACTUAL mechanism:
   ─────────────────────                  ──────────────────────
   query → CoreDNS → API server           startup: CoreDNS opens ONE watch on
           (live GET, every time)           Services/Endpoints/EndpointSlices
                                          query → CoreDNS → in-memory map (no
                                                   network call to the API at all)
                                          API server pushes changes → map updates
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this is why a CoreDNS pod restart briefly disrupts resolution (the in-memory map has to rebuild from a fresh watch) even though the API server itself never went down — a useful distinction when a "DNS blip" coincides with a CoreDNS pod restart in the events log.</div>

            <h5>② Negative Caching — Why a Just-Created Service Can Still 404 for a Few Seconds</h5>
            <p>The <code>cache</code> plugin in the Corefile caches BOTH positive answers (a resolved IP) and negative ones (NXDOMAIN, "no such name") for its TTL window. That second half is easy to forget, and it explains a specific, confusing symptom: you create a Service, curl it immediately, and it still fails — even though the Service definitely exists now.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   t=0s   pod queries "easypj-new-svc" BEFORE the Service exists → NXDOMAIN
          CoreDNS caches the NEGATIVE answer for `cache 30`'s TTL (30s)
   t=2s   Service "easypj-new-svc" is created — but CoreDNS doesn't know to
          invalidate the cached NXDOMAIN early
   t=5s   pod queries again → CoreDNS serves the STALE cached NXDOMAIN
          (even though the Service is real now) — until the TTL expires
   t=31s  cache entry expires → next query hits the live kubernetes plugin
          map → resolves correctly
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if a brand-new Service "doesn't resolve" but <code>kubectl get svc</code> clearly shows it exists, don't assume CoreDNS is broken — a stale negative cache from a query that raced the Service's creation is a real, if narrow, explanation, and it self-resolves once the cache TTL passes.</div>

            <h5>③ rewrite and autopath — Plugins That Transform or Shortcut a Query</h5>
            <p>Two Corefile plugins beyond the default chain are worth recognizing even though they're rarely enabled by default: <code>rewrite</code> transforms a query before the <code>kubernetes</code> plugin sees it, and <code>autopath</code> can shortcut the multi-step search-domain walk from §19.1 into fewer round-trips.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   rewrite — transforms the QUERY before resolution
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   query "old-db-svc" ──▶ [rewrite plugin] ──▶ query becomes "new-db-svc"
                                                        │
                                                        ▼
                                            [kubernetes plugin resolves THIS name]

   autopath — shortcuts the search-domain WALK using the caller's own namespace
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   WITHOUT autopath: "easypj-db" from pod in ns "easypj" tries, in order —
     1. easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local     ✅ FOUND (but only after CoreDNS
                                                   evaluates the query — the client's
                                                   own resolver already appended this
                                                   first via /etc/resolv.conf's search)
   WITH autopath: CoreDNS itself infers the caller's namespace server-side and
     answers directly — fewer round-trips on very large, chatty clusters.
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example use</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>rewrite</code></td><td>Transforms a query name before further plugins process it</td><td>Silently redirect a legacy service name to its new name during a migration</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>autopath</code></td><td>Short-circuits the search-domain walk (§19.1's 3-suffix retry) using the CLIENT pod's own namespace, cutting failed lookups</td><td>Reduces DNS query volume on very large, chatty clusters</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Practice Drill 19.1 (below) has you add a <code>rewrite</code> rule by hand — recognize it as "transform the query," distinct from <code>forward</code> ("send elsewhere") which is the plugin most people reach for by default.</div>

            <h5>④ NodeLocal DNSCache — a Per-Node Cache in Front of CoreDNS</h5>
            <p>On large, DNS-chatty clusters, every pod's query still crosses the network to a CoreDNS pod (which may be on a different node) even for a cache hit. NodeLocal DNSCache adds a small caching DNS server as a DaemonSet — one per node — so most lookups never leave the node at all.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Without NodeLocal DNSCache             With NodeLocal DNSCache
   ─────────────────────────              ─────────────────────────
   pod → network hop → CoreDNS pod        pod → local node-cache (same node,
   (possibly on a different node,          no network hop) → cache hit answers
   every single query, cache miss or hit)  instantly; cache MISS still forwards
                                            to real CoreDNS, same as before

   Benefits: lower CoreDNS load, lower tail latency, avoids a UDP conntrack
   race that can occur under very high per-node query volume.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> NodeLocal DNSCache is an optional add-on (a DaemonSet you deploy, not a default), so its ABSENCE on a given cluster is completely normal — don't "fix" a cluster by installing it unless a symptom (CoreDNS overload, high DNS tail latency at scale) actually calls for it.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── BRIDGE: DNS → INGRESS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-ingress-preview"></span>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>🌉 Related Entry Point — Ingress (External Traffic Into the Cluster)</h5>
                <p>Everything in this chapter resolves names INSIDE the cluster. The mirror-image concern is how traffic gets IN from outside — that's Ingress (Chapter 20), which often relies on external DNS (<code>app.example.com</code>) pointing at a load balancer. This diagram is the external counterpart to the internal resolution flow you just learned; keep both mental models side by side.</p>
