            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Cluster DNS is CoreDNS — a Deployment in <code>kube-system</code> fronted by the <code>kube-dns</code> Service (commonly 10.96.0.10). Every pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points its nameserver at that ClusterIP.</li>
                    <li>DNS records are created AUTOMATICALLY for Services; you never write them — the <code>kubernetes</code> plugin synthesizes them by watching the API.</li>
                    <li>The Corefile (a ConfigMap) configures CoreDNS via plugins: <code>kubernetes</code> (resolve), <code>forward</code> (send everything else upstream), <code>cache</code>, <code>errors</code>, <code>health</code>, <code>ready</code>, <code>reload</code>.</li>
                    <li>Service A records resolve to the ClusterIP; headless Services and pods resolve to pod IPs. SRV records exist for named ports.</li>
                    <li>Name forms: bare name = same namespace; <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;</code> = cross namespace; <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code> = full FQDN.</li>
                    <li>Short names work because of search domains + <code>ndots:5</code> in resolv.conf — the resolver tries <code>&lt;name&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code> etc. automatically.</li>
                    <li>A successful <code>nslookup</code> proves DNS, not connectivity — check endpoints too; the two are independent failure layers.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>DNS format matters</strong> — <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code> for services. <code>&lt;pod-ip-dashed&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.pod.cluster.local</code> for pods. The exam tests exact FQDN knowledge</li>
                    <li><strong>CoreDNS is a Deployment, not DaemonSet</strong> — Typically 2 replicas. Its ConfigMap is named <code>coredns</code>. The Service is <code>kube-dns</code> (not coredns!) in <code>kube-system</code></li>
                    <li><strong>DNS loop</strong> — If the node's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points to <code>127.0.0.53</code> (systemd-resolved), CoreDNS's <code>forward . /etc/resolv.conf</code> creates a loop. Fix: use explicit upstream <code>8.8.8.8</code></li>
                    <li><strong><code>ndots: 5</code> default</strong> — Pods first try appending search domains. <code>nslookup postgres</code> actually tries 4-5 FQDNs before the bare name. This slows DNS. For external names, use trailing dot: <code>google.com.</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Scaling CoreDNS to 0 (or eviction during node pressure) silently breaks EVERYTHING that resolves names</strong> — not just DNS lookups: Services, ExternalName aliases, and any app that dials another workload by hostname all fail cluster-wide, all at once, with no single obvious error pointing at "CoreDNS." This is one of the most severe single-points-of-failure in the whole cluster</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

