            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 19 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of CoreDNS internals — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Does the CoreDNS <code>kubernetes</code> plugin make a live API call for every single DNS query it answers?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No. It opens ONE watch on Services/Endpoints/EndpointSlices at startup, builds an in-memory map, and answers every query from that map — no per-query network call to the API server at all.</p>
<pre>startup: CoreDNS ──ONE watch──▶ API server (Services/Endpoints/EndpointSlices)
every query: pod ──▶ CoreDNS ──▶ in-memory map  (zero API calls per query)</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is why CoreDNS is fast enough to sit in the hot path of every Service resolution in the cluster — a live API round-trip per query at cluster scale would be far too slow.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You create a Service and curl it immediately — it fails with "no such host," even though <code>kubectl get svc</code> confirms it exists. What's a real, non-obvious explanation?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A stale negative cache. If a query for that name happened to run BEFORE the Service existed, CoreDNS's <code>cache</code> plugin cached the NXDOMAIN answer for its TTL (default 30s) — and will keep serving that cached failure until the TTL expires, even though the Service is real now.</p>
<pre>t=0s   query "new-svc" BEFORE it exists → NXDOMAIN cached for `cache 30`'s TTL
t=2s   Service "new-svc" created — cache does NOT invalidate early
t=5s   query again → STALE cached NXDOMAIN served (even though it's real now)
t=31s  TTL expires → next query resolves correctly</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The <code>cache</code> plugin caches BOTH positive and negative answers — the negative half is easy to forget, and this exact race (query-before-creation) is the classic symptom it produces.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between the Corefile's <code>rewrite</code> and <code>forward</code> plugins?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>rewrite</code> TRANSFORMS a query name before other plugins process it (e.g. redirect a legacy service name during a migration). <code>forward</code> SENDS a query elsewhere entirely — to an upstream resolver for names outside the cluster domain.</p>
<pre>rewrite:  query "old-svc" ──▶ becomes "new-svc" ──▶ kubernetes plugin resolves it
forward:  query "google.com" ──▶ sent AS-IS to an external DNS server, e.g. 8.8.8.8</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>They solve different problems: <code>rewrite</code> changes what's being asked; <code>forward</code> changes who answers. Confusing the two is a common mistake when first reading an unfamiliar Corefile.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What problem does NodeLocal DNSCache solve, and is it installed by default?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It's an OPTIONAL DaemonSet (NOT installed by default) that adds a small caching DNS server on every node, so most lookups never leave the node — reducing CoreDNS load and tail latency, and avoiding a UDP conntrack race that can occur under high per-node query volume.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Without NodeLocal DNSCache</th><th>With NodeLocal DNSCache</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Query path</td><td>pod → network hop → CoreDNS pod</td><td>pod → local node-cache (no hop) → CoreDNS only on a cache miss</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Installed by default?</td><td>N/A</td><td>No — an optional DaemonSet you deploy</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Its absence on a cluster is completely normal; don't treat a missing NodeLocal DNSCache as a misconfiguration unless an actual symptom (CoreDNS overload, high DNS tail latency) points to needing it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod with <code>hostNetwork: true</code> can't resolve <code>easypj-db</code>. What single field fixes it, and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet</code>. A hostNetwork pod uses the NODE's own <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> by default (bypassing CoreDNS entirely) unless this policy explicitly tells it to still use cluster DNS.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  hostNetwork: true
  dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet   <span class="comment"># without this, dnsPolicy: Default silently applies</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The default <code>dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst</code> is what normal pods get automatically — hostNetwork pods are the specific exception that needs this explicit override, a frequent exam trap.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the precise difference between a Service's plain A record and its SRV record?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>An A record returns ONLY an IP address. An SRV record additionally returns the PORT and target hostname in the same answer — useful for clients that discover both port and host dynamically instead of hard-coding the port.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">nslookup easypj-db
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.45.9                              ← A record: IP only</span>
nslookup -type=SRV _pg._tcp.easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># service = 0 100 5432 easypj-db...                 ← SRV: port AND host</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>SRV records are the least-visible DNS form in practice because most application code just hard-codes the port and only ever needs the A record — but some database drivers and service-mesh control planes do rely on SRV.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does querying an external FQDN like <code>api.example.com</code> sometimes feel slower than querying an in-cluster Service name?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>ndots:5</code> means any name with FEWER than 5 dots gets the search domains appended and tried FIRST. <code>api.example.com</code> has only 2 dots, so the resolver tries 3 failing in-cluster suffixes before finally trying the bare name — several wasted round-trips.</p>
<pre>query "api.example.com" (2 dots, &lt; ndots:5) → tries, IN ORDER:
  1. api.example.com.default.svc.cluster.local   ❌ NXDOMAIN
  2. api.example.com.svc.cluster.local            ❌ NXDOMAIN
  3. api.example.com.cluster.local                ❌ NXDOMAIN
  4. api.example.com                              ✅ FOUND (4th try, not 1st)</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A trailing dot (<code>api.example.com.</code>) or a pod-level <code>dnsConfig</code> with a lower <code>ndots</code> value skips the search-domain walk entirely for names you know are external.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">CoreDNS pods are CrashLoopBackOff with "Loop ... detected for zone '.'" in the logs. What's the root cause and the fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The node's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points at a local stub resolver (e.g. systemd-resolved at 127.0.0.53) that forwards BACK to CoreDNS — creating an infinite loop. CoreDNS's <code>loop</code> plugin deliberately crashes rather than loop forever.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Fix: point forward at explicit upstream servers instead of the node's resolv.conf</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># forward . /etc/resolv.conf  →  forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is one of the most common real-world CoreDNS failures on kubeadm clusters built on systemd-resolved distros — recognizing the exact log message is often faster than re-deriving the cause from scratch.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What Service does CoreDNS's Deployment sit behind, and why is that name slightly confusing?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <code>kube-dns</code> Service — a legacy name kept for backward compatibility, even though the actual Deployment and pods are named <code>coredns</code> (CoreDNS replaced the older kube-dns implementation years ago but the Service name stuck).</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get deploy,pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/coredns   ← the Deployment/pods are named "coredns"</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># service/kube-dns          ← but the Service is still named "kube-dns"</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> is the correct label selector for CoreDNS pods specifically because of this historical naming — a small but genuine exam gotcha.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">nslookup succeeds and returns the correct ClusterIP, but the app still can't connect. Is this a DNS bug?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No. DNS resolution and Service connectivity are two independent layers — a successful nslookup only proves the NAME resolved to a ClusterIP; it says nothing about whether that Service has any Ready endpoints for kube-proxy to route to.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;   <span class="comment"># check separately — DNS being right doesn't mean this is populated</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This split is why the fixed troubleshooting order matters: nslookup first (is DNS itself up, and did it resolve?), THEN check endpoints (does the Service actually have healthy backends?) — treating them as one problem wastes time.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 19 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of DNS resolution &amp; CoreDNS — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the full DNS format for a Service in Kubernetes? Show the FQDN for a Service named <code>web-svc</code> in namespace <code>production</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">service dns format</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Service DNS format:</span>
<span class="comment"># &lt;service-name&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># Full FQDN for web-svc in production:</span>
<span class="comment"># web-svc.production.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS resolution from different contexts:</span>
<span class="comment"># Same namespace (production):</span>
nslookup web-svc                    <span class="comment"># Works!</span>
nslookup web-svc.production         <span class="comment"># Works!</span>
nslookup web-svc.production.svc     <span class="comment"># Works!</span>
nslookup web-svc.production.svc.cluster.local  <span class="comment"># Full FQDN</span>

<span class="comment"># Different namespace (from default):</span>
nslookup web-svc                    <span class="comment"># FAILS (wrong ns)</span>
nslookup web-svc.production         <span class="comment"># Works!</span>
nslookup web-svc.production.svc.cluster.local  <span class="comment"># Works!</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod DNS format (different!):</span>
<span class="comment"># &lt;pod-ip-dashed&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.pod.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod IP 10.244.1.5 → 10-244-1-5.production.pod.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># StatefulSet pod DNS:</span>
<span class="comment"># &lt;pod-name&gt;.&lt;headless-svc&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># web-0.web-headless.production.svc.cluster.local</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubernetes DNS has strict formats: Services use <code>svc-name.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>, pods use <code>ip-dashed.ns.pod.cluster.local</code>, StatefulSet pods use <code>pod-name.headless-svc.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>. Short names like <code>web-svc</code> only work within the SAME namespace because of the search domain in <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>. For cross-namespace access, you MUST use at least <code>svc-name.namespace</code>. On the CKA, DNS FQDN questions are common — memorize the format.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">CoreDNS is not working — all pods fail DNS resolution. Show the complete troubleshooting flow.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">test dns from a pod</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Test DNS from a pod</span>
kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it -- nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># ;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check CoreDNS pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># NAME                      READY  STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-5d78c9869d-abc    0/1    CrashLoopBackOff  ← Problem!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check CoreDNS logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># "Loop detected" ← DNS forwarding loop!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kube-dns Service</span>
kubectl get svc -n kube-system kube-dns
<span class="comment"># NAME       TYPE       CLUSTER-IP   PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-dns   ClusterIP  10.96.0.10   53/UDP,53/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check CoreDNS ConfigMap</span>
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for: forward . /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># If node's resolv.conf points to 127.0.0.53 → loop!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Change forward to explicit upstream DNS</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Change: forward . /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># To:     forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4</span>

<span class="comment"># Restart CoreDNS to apply</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The DNS loop is a classic CKA troubleshooting scenario. On many Linux systems, <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points to <code>127.0.0.53</code> (systemd-resolved), which forwards to... CoreDNS, creating a loop. Fix by pointing to external DNS (8.8.8.8). The troubleshooting flow: (1) test DNS from a pod, (2) check CoreDNS pods in <code>kube-system</code>, (3) check logs for errors, (4) check the <code>coredns</code> ConfigMap. Remember: the Service is named <code>kube-dns</code> (not coredns) but the pods are <code>coredns-*</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the CoreDNS Corefile and how do you modify it? Show how to add a custom DNS record.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the corefile is stored in the coredns configmap</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The Corefile is stored in the coredns ConfigMap:</span>
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml

<span class="comment"># Default Corefile:</span>
<span class="comment"># .:53 {</span>
<span class="comment">#     errors</span>
<span class="comment">#     health { lazystart }</span>
<span class="comment">#     ready</span>
<span class="comment">#     kubernetes cluster.local in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa {</span>
<span class="comment">#         pods insecure</span>
<span class="comment">#         fallthrough in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa</span>
<span class="comment">#     }</span>
<span class="comment">#     prometheus :9153</span>
<span class="comment">#     forward . /etc/resolv.conf {</span>
<span class="comment">#         max_concurrent 1000</span>
<span class="comment">#     }</span>
<span class="comment">#     cache 30</span>
<span class="comment">#     loop</span>
<span class="comment">#     reload</span>
<span class="comment">#     loadbalance</span>
<span class="comment"># }</span>

<span class="comment"># Add a custom DNS rewrite rule:</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Add inside the .:53 block:</span>
<span class="comment">#     rewrite name db.myapp.local postgres-svc.production.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># Add a custom hosts entry:</span>
<span class="comment"># Add inside the .:53 block:</span>
<span class="comment">#     hosts {</span>
<span class="comment">#         192.168.1.100 legacy-server.company.com</span>
<span class="comment">#         fallthrough</span>
<span class="comment">#     }</span>

<span class="comment"># CoreDNS auto-reloads (reload plugin)</span>
<span class="comment"># Check reload:</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns | grep reload</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The Corefile configures CoreDNS behavior. Key plugins: <code>kubernetes</code> (resolves cluster DNS), <code>forward</code> (upstream DNS for external names), <code>cache</code> (caching), <code>loop</code> (loop detection), <code>reload</code> (hot-reload on ConfigMap changes). On the CKA, you may be asked to: (1) modify the forward directive, (2) add custom rewrites, (3) fix a broken Corefile. The <code>reload</code> plugin means CoreDNS picks up ConfigMap changes automatically (within ~30 seconds) without pod restart. However, <code>kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system</code> forces immediate reload.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check what DNS configuration a pod is using? What does <code>ndots: 5</code> mean and why does it matter?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check a pod's dns config</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check a pod's DNS config:</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># nameserver 10.96.0.10</span>
<span class="comment"># search default.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># options ndots:5</span>

<span class="comment"># ndots:5 means:</span>
<span class="comment"># If a name has FEWER than 5 dots, append search domains first</span>
<span class="comment"># "google.com" has 1 dot (< 5), so DNS tries:</span>
<span class="comment">#   1. google.com.default.svc.cluster.local → NXDOMAIN</span>
<span class="comment">#   2. google.com.svc.cluster.local → NXDOMAIN</span>
<span class="comment">#   3. google.com.cluster.local → NXDOMAIN</span>
<span class="comment">#   4. google.com → RESOLVED! (4 queries for 1 lookup!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Optimization: add trailing dot for external names</span>
<span class="comment"># "google.com." → treated as FQDN, only 1 query</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- nslookup google.com.

<span class="comment"># Override DNS config in pod spec:</span>
spec:
dnsPolicy: "None"
dnsConfig:
nameservers:
- 8.8.8.8
searches:
- default.svc.cluster.local
options:
- name: ndots
  value: "2"            # ← Reduce DNS queries</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>ndots:5</code> is the Kubernetes default — any name with fewer than 5 dots tries all search domains first. This is why looking up <code>google.com</code> generates 4+ DNS queries before the final resolution. For performance-critical apps, lower <code>ndots</code> or use FQDNs (trailing dot). On the CKA, <code>dnsPolicy</code> options are: <code>ClusterFirst</code> (default, use CoreDNS), <code>Default</code> (use node's DNS), <code>None</code> (custom DNS config). Know how to read <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> inside a pod and understand why DNS might be slow (ndots causing extra queries).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">From namespace <code>frontend</code>, how do you connect to a Service named <code>api-svc</code> in namespace <code>backend</code>? What DNS name do you use?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cross-namespace dns access</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Cross-namespace DNS access:</span>
<span class="comment"># Short form (usually enough):</span>
curl http://api-svc.backend:8080

<span class="comment"># Full FQDN:</span>
curl http://api-svc.backend.svc.cluster.local:8080

<span class="comment"># Why "api-svc" alone doesn't work:</span>
<span class="comment"># The pod's /etc/resolv.conf has:</span>
<span class="comment"># search frontend.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># So "api-svc" resolves to:</span>
<span class="comment">#   api-svc.frontend.svc.cluster.local → NOT what we want!</span>

<span class="comment"># You MUST include the namespace:</span>
kubectl exec frontend-pod -n frontend -- \
curl -s http://api-svc.backend:8080
<span class="comment"># Response from backend API</span>

<span class="comment"># Test DNS resolution:</span>
kubectl exec frontend-pod -n frontend -- \
nslookup api-svc.backend
<span class="comment"># Name: api-svc.backend.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.78.90</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Cross-namespace Service access requires including the target namespace in the DNS name: <code>svc-name.namespace</code>. This is because the search domain in a pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> only includes its own namespace. Using just <code>api-svc</code> would resolve to <code>api-svc.frontend.svc.cluster.local</code> (wrong namespace). On the CKA, cross-namespace communication is commonly tested — remember to include the namespace. Note: there are no network restrictions by default between namespaces — you need NetworkPolicies to restrict cross-namespace traffic.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>kube-dns</code> Service and how does it relate to CoreDNS? Why the naming confusion?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">coredns components</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># CoreDNS components:</span>
kubectl get all -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># Deployment: coredns (runs CoreDNS pods)</span>
<span class="comment"># Pods: coredns-5d78c9869d-abc, coredns-5d78c9869d-def (2 replicas)</span>
<span class="comment"># Service: kube-dns (ClusterIP: 10.96.0.10) ← Named "kube-dns"!</span>

<span class="comment"># Why "kube-dns" not "coredns"?</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-dns was the original DNS server (before v1.12)</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS replaced it, but the Service kept the name</span>
<span class="comment"># for backward compatibility</span>

<span class="comment"># All pods' /etc/resolv.conf points to:</span>
<span class="comment"># nameserver 10.96.0.10  ← kube-dns Service IP</span>
<span class="comment"># This IP is configured in kubelet:</span>
<span class="comment">#   --cluster-dns=10.96.0.10</span>

<span class="comment"># Key facts:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Deployment name: coredns</span>
<span class="comment"># - Service name: kube-dns (confusing but intentional)</span>
<span class="comment"># - ConfigMap name: coredns</span>
<span class="comment"># - Label: k8s-app=kube-dns (on everything)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This naming mismatch is a classic CKA trap. The Deployment is <code>coredns</code>, the ConfigMap is <code>coredns</code>, but the Service is <code>kube-dns</code>. All share the label <code>k8s-app=kube-dns</code>. The Service IP (usually 10.96.0.10) is hardcoded in kubelet's <code>--cluster-dns</code> flag and injected into every pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>. On the CKA, if you're asked to check DNS, remember: <code>kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system</code> (not coredns). CoreDNS runs as a Deployment (typically 2 replicas for HA), NOT as a DaemonSet.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you set a pod's <code>dnsPolicy</code>? What are the available policies and when do you use each?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Policy</th>
                                        <th>Behavior</th>
                                        <th>Use Case</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>ClusterFirst</td>
                                        <td>Use CoreDNS, fall back to node DNS (default)</td>
                                        <td>Normal pods</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>ClusterFirstWithHostNet</td>
                                        <td>ClusterFirst for hostNetwork pods</td>
                                        <td>hostNetwork: true pods needing cluster DNS</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Default</td>
                                        <td>Use node's /etc/resolv.conf</td>
                                        <td>Pods needing host DNS only</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>None</td>
                                        <td>Custom — must set dnsConfig</td>
                                        <td>Full DNS control</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">hostnetwork pod that needs cluster dns</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># hostNetwork pod that needs cluster DNS:</span>
spec:
hostNetwork: true
dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet  # ← Required!
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The default <code>ClusterFirst</code> policy sends all DNS queries to CoreDNS first. For hostNetwork pods, the default would use the node's DNS — so you need <code>ClusterFirstWithHostNet</code> explicitly to use CoreDNS. <code>None</code> requires <code>dnsConfig</code> to be set manually. On the CKA, <code>ClusterFirstWithHostNet</code> appears when a <code>hostNetwork: true</code> pod can't resolve Service names — that's the fix. Confusing name: <code>Default</code> is NOT the default policy; <code>ClusterFirst</code> is.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you quickly test if DNS is working in a cluster? What is the fastest one-liner?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">fastest one-liner</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Fastest one-liner:</span>
kubectl run dnscheck --image=busybox:1.28 --rm -it --restart=Never \
-- nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># Server:    10.96.0.10</span>
