                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does DNS work for a headless Service backing a StatefulSet? Show the DNS records created.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">setup</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Setup:</span>
<span class="comment"># StatefulSet: web (3 replicas)</span>
<span class="comment"># Headless Service: web-headless (clusterIP: None)</span>
<span class="comment"># Namespace: default</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS records created:</span>

<span class="comment"># Service DNS (returns ALL pod IPs):</span>
nslookup web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.5   (web-0)</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.2.8   (web-1)</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.12  (web-2)</span>

<span class="comment"># Individual pod DNS:</span>
nslookup web-0.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.5   (only web-0)</span>

nslookup web-1.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.2.8   (only web-1)</span>

nslookup web-2.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.12  (only web-2)</span>

<span class="comment"># Format: &lt;pod-name&gt;.&lt;headless-svc&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># This is why StatefulSets REQUIRE a headless Service:</span>
<span class="comment"># It creates per-pod DNS entries!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Headless Services for StatefulSets create two types of DNS entries: (1) the Service DNS returns all pod IPs (for discovery), (2) individual pod DNS records (<code>pod-name.svc-name.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>) for direct pod access. This is how database replicas find each other — <code>web-0</code> can always reach <code>web-1</code> by DNS name, even if its IP changes. On the CKA, knowing this format is essential for StatefulSet questions. The Service name in the DNS must match the StatefulSet's <code>spec.serviceName</code> field.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">CoreDNS pods keep crashing with <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>. The logs show <em>"plugin/loop: Loop ... detected"</em>. How do you fix this?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">problem: dns forwarding loop</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Problem: DNS forwarding loop</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS forwards to /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># Node's resolv.conf points to 127.0.0.53 (systemd-resolved)</span>
<span class="comment"># systemd-resolved forwards to... CoreDNS → infinite loop!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 1: Edit CoreDNS ConfigMap (recommended for CKA)</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Change:</span>
<span class="comment">#   forward . /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># To:</span>
<span class="comment">#   forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4</span>

<span class="comment"># Restart CoreDNS pods to pick up change immediately:</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Fix 2: Disable loop detection (NOT recommended)</span>
<span class="comment"># Remove the "loop" line from Corefile</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 3: Fix node's resolv.conf (node-level fix)</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit /etc/resolv.conf on each node:</span>
<span class="comment"># Change nameserver 127.0.0.53 to nameserver 8.8.8.8</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify fix:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># Both coredns pods: Running</span>

kubectl run test --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it -- nslookup google.com
<span class="comment"># Name: google.com</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 142.250.x.x ← Working!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The DNS loop is the most common CoreDNS failure on kubeadm clusters. On systems with systemd-resolved, <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points to <code>127.0.0.53</code> — a local stub resolver that may forward back to CoreDNS, creating an infinite loop. The <code>loop</code> plugin detects this and crashes CoreDNS intentionally (better to crash than infinite loop). On the CKA, the fix is always: change the <code>forward</code> directive in the <code>coredns</code> ConfigMap to explicit upstream servers (8.8.8.8). Edit the ConfigMap, restart the Deployment, verify with nslookup.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
