                    <ol>
                        <li>From a debug pod, <code>nslookup</code> a Service name that does NOT exist yet — confirm NXDOMAIN</li>
                        <li>Immediately create that exact Service</li>
                        <li><code>nslookup</code> again right away — observe it may still fail</li>
                        <li>Wait past the Corefile's <code>cache</code> TTL (default 30s) and query once more — confirm it now resolves</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> this timing window is narrow and TTL-dependent — if the retry already succeeds, the cache had already expired or the query landed on a fresh CoreDNS replica that hadn't cached the miss.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl run dns-test --rm -it --image=busybox:1.28 -- nslookup not-yet-created-svc
<span class="comment"># server can't find not-yet-created-svc: NXDOMAIN</span>

kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --name=not-yet-created-svc --port=80 --target-port=8000

kubectl run dns-test2 --rm -it --image=busybox:1.28 -- nslookup not-yet-created-svc
<span class="comment"># may STILL fail if hitting a CoreDNS replica with the cached negative answer</span>

<span class="comment"># after ~30s (the default `cache 30` TTL):</span>
kubectl run dns-test3 --rm -it --image=busybox:1.28 -- nslookup not-yet-created-svc
<span class="comment"># resolves correctly now</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 19.4 — Diagnose a hostNetwork Pod That Can't Resolve Names</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Deploy a pod with <code>hostNetwork: true</code> and NO explicit <code>dnsPolicy</code></li>
                        <li>Exec in and try to resolve an in-cluster Service name — observe the failure</li>
                        <li>Check its actual <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> — confirm it's the NODE's, not CoreDNS's</li>
                        <li>Add <code>dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet</code> and confirm resolution now works</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> a pod's own <code>cat /etc/resolv.conf</code> is the fastest single command to distinguish "using CoreDNS" (nameserver 10.96.0.10-ish) from "using the node's resolver" (whatever the node itself uses).
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata: {name: hostnet-test}
spec:
  hostNetwork: true
  containers: [{name: test, image: busybox:1.28, command: ["sleep","3600"]}]
EOF

kubectl exec hostnet-test -- nslookup easypj-web
<span class="comment"># fails — using the node's own resolver, not CoreDNS</span>
kubectl exec hostnet-test -- cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># nameserver is the NODE's, not 10.96.0.10</span>

kubectl patch pod hostnet-test -p '{"spec":{"dnsPolicy":"ClusterFirstWithHostNet"}}'
<span class="comment"># (pods are immutable here in practice — recreate with the field set instead)</span>
<span class="comment"># with dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet in the spec, nslookup succeeds</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

