                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> a workload asks for a name; CoreDNS (the kube-system Deployment at the <code>kube-dns</code> ClusterIP) answers from records it synthesized by watching the API, returning a Service ClusterIP (or pod IPs for headless). kube-proxy then routes that ClusterIP to a live pod — a completely separate step DNS knows nothing about.</p>
                    <p>Break any single link — CoreDNS down, a bad Corefile, the wrong namespace, an ndots surprise, a stale negative cache, or empty endpoints — and the symptom looks identical from the app's point of view ("can't connect"). That's exactly why the fixed troubleshooting order matters.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> always run <code>nslookup kubernetes.default</code> first — it proves the DNS PLANE itself is healthy before you spend any time on the specific name you actually care about.</p>
