                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Default Backend &amp; 404 Handling</h5>
                    <p>If no Ingress rule matches the request path, the controller returns a <strong>404</strong>. You can configure a <strong>default backend</strong> in the Ingress spec to serve a custom error page: <code>spec.defaultBackend.service.name: default-http-backend</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Real-World Pairing: cert-manager Issues the TLS Secret Automatically</h5>
                    <p>§20.1's Ingress YAML references <code>secretName: easypj-tls</code> as if that TLS Secret just exists. In a real production cluster, a human almost never runs <code>kubectl create secret tls</code> by hand — <strong>cert-manager</strong> (a separate, near-universal add-on) watches for a <code>Certificate</code> object (or an annotation directly on the Ingress), automatically requests a certificate from an issuer like Let's Encrypt, proves domain ownership (HTTP-01 or DNS-01 challenge), and writes the resulting cert/key into exactly the Secret the Ingress expects — then repeats the whole process automatically before the certificate expires.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">Annotation-driven automatic TLS — cert-manager fills the Secret for you</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj
  annotations:
    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod   <span class="comment"># ← cert-manager watches for this</span>
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  tls:
  - hosts: [easypj.example.com]
    secretName: easypj-tls        <span class="comment"># cert-manager creates/renews this Secret automatically —</span>
                                   <span class="comment"># you never run `kubectl create secret tls` by hand</span>
  rules: [ ... ]</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mb-0">This isn't CKA-exam material (the exam typically hands you a pre-made cert), but recognizing <code>cert-manager.io/*</code> annotations and a <code>Certificate</code> CRD in a real cluster — and knowing they mean "TLS is automated, don't hand-manage this Secret" — is exactly the kind of practical knowledge that separates exam-only familiarity from running a real cluster.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.2 Ingress Controller Setup</h3>
                <p>An <strong>Ingress resource is just configuration</strong> — it does nothing without an <strong>Ingress Controller</strong>. The controller is the actual reverse proxy (NGINX, Traefik, HAProxy) that reads Ingress rules and routes traffic.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Ingress Resource</h5>
                        <p>The <strong>rules</strong> — path routing, TLS config, host rules. Just a YAML API object. Does nothing by itself.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚙️</div>
                        <h5>Ingress Controller</h5>
                        <p>The <strong>implementation</strong> — NGINX/Traefik pod that reads rules and configures the reverse proxy. Must be installed separately.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Ingress Controller</th>
                                <th>Provider</th>
                                <th>Features</th>
                                <th>Best For</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>NGINX Ingress</strong></td>
                                <td>Kubernetes community</td>
                                <td>Path/host routing, TLS, rate limiting, auth</td>
                                <td>CKA exam, most clusters</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Traefik</strong></td>
                                <td>Traefik Labs</td>
                                <td>Auto-discovery, Let's Encrypt, middleware</td>
                                <td>Dynamic environments</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>HAProxy</strong></td>
                                <td>HAProxy Technologies</td>
                                <td>High performance, TCP/HTTP load balancing</td>
                                <td>High-traffic production</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>AWS ALB</strong></td>
                                <td>AWS</td>
                                <td>AWS Application Load Balancer integration</td>
                                <td>EKS clusters</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Installing NGINX Ingress Controller</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Install NGINX Ingress Controller (bare metal / kubeadm)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/ingress-nginx/controller-v1.10.0/deploy/static/provider/baremetal/deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify the controller is running</span>
kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx
<span class="comment"># NAME                                       READY   STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># ingress-nginx-controller-abc12-xyz         1/1     Running</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the IngressClass</span>
kubectl get ingressclass
<span class="comment"># NAME    CONTROLLER                           PARAMETERS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># nginx   k8s.io/ingress-nginx                 &lt;none&gt;       30s</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify the controller Service</span>
kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx
<span class="comment"># NAME                        TYPE       EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># ingress-nginx-controller    NodePort   &lt;none&gt;        80:31080/TCP,443:31443/TCP</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam: Ingress Controller Must Be Running</h5>
                    <p>If the exam asks you to create an Ingress resource and it's not working, <strong>first check if an Ingress Controller is installed</strong>: <code>kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx</code>. An Ingress resource without a controller does nothing — the ADDRESS field will be empty.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.3 Network Policies — Firewall for Pods</h3>
                <p>By default, Kubernetes allows <strong>all pods to communicate with all other pods</strong> — no restrictions. <strong>Network Policies</strong> let you define fine-grained rules controlling which pods can send traffic to which other pods, implementing a <strong>zero-trust network</strong>.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Default Behavior vs Network Policy Applied</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────── DEFAULT (No NetworkPolicy) ──────────────┐
  │                                                                   │
  │  easypj ─────────→ postgres  ✅                                  │
  │  easypj ─────────→ redis     ✅                                  │
  │  random-pod ─────→ postgres  ✅  ← anyone can access DB!        │
  │  random-pod ─────→ easypj   ✅  ← no isolation at all          │
  │                                                                   │
  │  EVERYTHING is allowed. Any pod talks to any pod.                │
  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ┌──────────────── WITH NetworkPolicy on postgres ──────────────────┐
  │                                                                   │
  │  NetworkPolicy: "only pods with app=easypj can reach postgres"  │
  │                                                                   │
  │  easypj ─────────→ postgres  ✅  (label matches)                │
  │  random-pod ─────→ postgres  ❌  BLOCKED                        │
  │  redis ──────────→ postgres  ❌  BLOCKED                        │
  │                                                                   │
  │  easypj ─────────→ redis     ✅  (no policy on redis)           │
  │  random-pod ─────→ easypj   ✅  (no policy on easypj)          │
  │                                                                   │
  │  ⚠️ Policy only affects the TARGET pod (podSelector)            │
  │  ⚠️ Pods with NO policy → still fully open                     │
  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>Key Concepts</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Policies are <strong>additive</strong> — multiple policies are OR'd together</li>
                            <li>Applying ANY policy to a pod makes it <strong>deny by default</strong></li>
                            <li>No policy = all traffic allowed (default open)</li>
                            <li>Requires CNI with NetworkPolicy support (Calico, Cilium)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Critical Requirement</h5>
                        <p><strong>Flannel does NOT support NetworkPolicies</strong>. If you apply a NetworkPolicy with Flannel, it's silently ignored. The CKA exam uses Calico or Cilium — both support NetworkPolicy.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.4 Network Policy Types &amp; Patterns</h3>
                <p>Network Policies have two directions: <strong>Ingress</strong> (incoming traffic TO the pods) and <strong>Egress</strong> (outgoing traffic FROM the pods). You can control both independently.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Policy Type</th>
                                <th>Direction</th>
                                <th>Controls</th>
                                <th>Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Ingress</strong></td>
                                <td>Incoming → to selected pods</td>
                                <td>Who can SEND traffic to these pods</td>
                                <td>Only easypj pods can reach postgres on port 5432</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Egress</strong></td>
                                <td>Outgoing ← from selected pods</td>
                                <td>Where these pods can SEND traffic</td>
                                <td>easypj pods can only reach postgres and DNS</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Both</strong></td>
                                <td>Bidirectional</td>
                                <td>Controls both directions</td>
                                <td>Fully locked-down pods — explicit allow required</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Network Policy Selectors — Who Can Talk?</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> podSelector</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Match pods by labels in the <strong>same namespace</strong>. <code>matchLabels: { app: easypj }</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📁</span> namespaceSelector</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Match all pods in namespaces with specific labels. <code>matchLabels: { env: prod }</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> ipBlock</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Match traffic from/to specific IP ranges. <code>cidr: 10.0.0.0/8, except: [10.0.1.0/24]</code></div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>Here are the most common Network Policy patterns you'll encounter on the CKA exam, using our easypj stack as context.</p>

                <h4>Pattern 1: Deny All Ingress (Default Deny)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deny-all-ingress.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Deny ALL incoming traffic to ALL pods in "default" namespace</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: deny-all-ingress
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector: {}          <span class="comment"># Empty = selects ALL pods in namespace</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress              <span class="comment"># Only control incoming traffic</span>
  <span class="comment"># ingress: []            # Omitted = no traffic allowed in</span>
                           <span class="comment"># (there are no "allow" rules)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 2: Allow Specific Pod Access</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">allow-easypj-to-postgres.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Only easypj pods can reach postgres on port 5432</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-easypj-to-postgres
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:              <span class="comment"># Target: postgres pods</span>
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:                  <span class="comment"># Allow FROM:</span>
        - podSelector:       <span class="comment"># Pods with these labels</span>
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj
      ports:                 <span class="comment"># On these ports only</span>
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 3: Allow from Specific Namespace</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">allow-from-monitoring-ns.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Allow monitoring namespace to scrape all pods in default namespace</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-monitoring
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector: {}           <span class="comment"># All pods in default</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              name: monitoring    <span class="comment"># Namespace must have this label!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 4: Egress — Restrict Outgoing Traffic</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-egress-policy.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj pods can only reach postgres and DNS</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: easypj-egress
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
  egress:
    - to:                    <span class="comment"># Allow TO postgres</span>
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: postgres
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432
    - to: []                 <span class="comment"># Allow DNS (required!)</span>
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 5: Deny All Ingress + Egress (Total Lockdown)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deny-all-traffic.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Complete isolation — no traffic in or out</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: deny-all
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector: {}           <span class="comment"># All pods</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    - Egress
  <span class="comment"># No ingress or egress rules = deny everything</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: AND vs OR Logic in Network Policies</h5>
                    <p>Within a single <code>from</code> block, multiple selectors are <strong>AND</strong>ed. Separate <code>- from:</code> entries are <strong>OR</strong>ed.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">AND vs OR — Critical Difference</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># AND — pod must match BOTH selectors</span>
ingress:
  - from:
      - namespaceSelector:          <span class="comment"># AND</span>
          matchLabels: { env: prod }
        podSelector:                <span class="comment"># AND (same list item!)</span>
          matchLabels: { app: easypj }

<span class="comment"># OR — pod matches EITHER selector</span>
ingress:
  - from:
      - namespaceSelector:          <span class="comment"># OR</span>
          matchLabels: { env: prod }
      - podSelector:                <span class="comment"># OR (separate list item!)</span>
          matchLabels: { app: easypj }</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 6: Allow from Specific IP Range (ipBlock)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">allow-from-office-ips.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Allow only office network to reach the easypj admin panel</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-office-only
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 10.0.0.0/8        <span class="comment"># Allow entire 10.x.x.x range</span>
            except:
              - 10.0.99.0/24         <span class="comment"># Except the guest WiFi subnet</span>
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: nginx-ingress     <span class="comment"># Also allow Ingress controller</span>
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 7: Full Production Lockdown (Ingress + Egress)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-full-lockdown.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj pods: accept from Ingress only, talk only to postgres + DNS</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: easypj-lockdown
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    - Egress
  ingress:
    - from:                         <span class="comment"># Only accept from Ingress controller</span>
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              name: ingress-nginx
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app.kubernetes.io/name: ingress-nginx
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8000
  egress:
    - to:                           <span class="comment"># Can reach postgres</span>
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: postgres
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432
    - to: []                        <span class="comment"># Can reach DNS (required!)</span>
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Beyond Hand-Written YAML: Enforcing NetworkPolicy Org-Wide With Policy-as-Code</h5>
                    <p>Every pattern above assumes a human writes a correct NetworkPolicy for each new namespace. At real organizational scale, that assumption breaks — a new team spins up a namespace, forgets the default-deny policy entirely, and their pods sit wide open with nobody noticing. <strong>Policy-as-code admission controllers</strong> — <strong>Kyverno</strong> or <strong>OPA/Gatekeeper</strong> — solve this by AUTOMATICALLY generating (or blocking the creation of a namespace without) baseline NetworkPolicies, instead of relying on every team remembering to write one.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">Kyverno ClusterPolicy — auto-generate a default-deny on every new namespace</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: auto-default-deny-netpol
spec:
  rules:
  - name: generate-default-deny
    match:
      resources: {kinds: [Namespace]}
    generate:
      apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
      kind: NetworkPolicy
      name: default-deny-ingress
      namespace: "{{request.object.metadata.name}}"     <span class="comment"># the NEW namespace, whatever it's named</span>
      data:
        spec: {podSelector: {}, policyTypes: [Ingress]}
<span class="comment"># Result: EVERY new namespace gets a default-deny NetworkPolicy automatically,</span>
<span class="comment"># the instant it's created — no team has to remember to write §20.3's Pattern 1</span>
<span class="comment"># by hand, and it can never be forgotten.</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mb-0">This is a genuinely important production pattern beyond the exam syllabus: the hand-written NetworkPolicy patterns in this section are what you author for ONE workload; a policy-as-code admission controller is how a platform team guarantees that EVERY workload across an entire organization gets a security baseline, without trusting every individual developer to remember.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.5 Testing Network Policies</h3>
                <p>Always verify your Network Policies work as expected. Here's a systematic testing approach using our easypj stack.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Testing Network Policy Enforcement</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Apply the "allow-easypj-to-postgres" policy</span>
kubectl apply -f allow-easypj-to-postgres.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify policy is created</span>
kubectl get networkpolicies
<span class="comment"># NAME                        POD-SELECTOR     AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># allow-easypj-to-postgres   app=postgres     5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Describe the policy for details</span>
kubectl describe networkpolicy allow-easypj-to-postgres
<span class="comment"># Allowing ingress traffic:</span>
<span class="comment">#   To Port: 5432/TCP</span>
<span class="comment">#   From:</span>
<span class="comment">#     PodSelector: app=easypj</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Test ALLOWED traffic (easypj → postgres)</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- nc -zv postgres-svc 5432
<span class="comment"># postgres-svc (10.96.88.5:5432) open   ✅ ALLOWED</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Test BLOCKED traffic (random pod → postgres)</span>
kubectl run test-blocked --image=busybox --rm -it -- nc -zv postgres-svc 5432
<span class="comment"># → hangs/timeout  ❌ BLOCKED!   (This pod has no "app: easypj" label)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Test that non-policy pods are still open</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- curl -s http://redis-svc:6379
<span class="comment"># ✅ Works — redis has no NetworkPolicy, so it's still fully open</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
