            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How NetworkPolicy Is Enforced at the Kernel Level: From YAML Rules to iptables/eBPF Filters, and How Ingress Controllers Route External Traffic</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ NetworkPolicy — From YAML to Kernel Rules</h5>
            <p>NetworkPolicy YAML looks simple, but it translates into complex iptables rules (or eBPF programs with Cilium). Understanding the translation helps you debug "why isn't my policy working" scenarios.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NETWORKPOLICY → IPTABLES TRANSLATION (Calico example)                       │
│                                                                              │
│  YAML:                                                                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1                                      │   │
│  │ kind: NetworkPolicy                                                    │   │
│  │   podSelector:                                                        │   │
│  │     matchLabels: {app: easypj, tier: backend}                        │   │
│  │   ingress:                                                             │   │
│  │   - from:                                                              │   │
│  │     - podSelector: {role: frontend}                                   │   │
│  │     ports:                                                             │   │
│  │     - port: 8000                                                       │   │
│  │       protocol: TCP                                                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  GENERATED IPTABLES RULES (on every node where matching pods run):          │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># 1. Mark packets from frontend pods as ALLOWED                       │</span>   │
│  │ -A cali-fw-cali12345 -m comment --comment "allow frontend→backend" \ │   │
│  │   -m set --match-set cali40-frontend-src src \                       │   │
│  │   -p tcp --dport 8000 -j MARK --set-mark 0x10000                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># 2. Allow MARKED packets (matching policy)                           │</span>   │
│  │ -A cali-fw-cali12345 -m mark --mark 0x10000 -j ACCEPT                │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># 3. DEFAULT DENY (no matching mark → DROP)                           │</span>   │
│  │ -A cali-fw-cali12345 -j DROP                                          │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  NETWORKPOLICY RULES (memorize for CKA):                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. Policies are ADDITIVE — multiple policies UNION their rules       │   │
│  │ 2. Empty podSelector = ALL pods in namespace                         │   │
│  │ 3. Empty ingress = ALLOW ALL traffic (not useful alone)              │   │
│  │ 4. Empty from = ALL sources (any pod, any namespace, any IP)         │   │
│  │ 5. namespaceSelector + podSelector in same rule = AND (both must     │   │
│  │    match: pods from namespace X AND with label Y)                    │   │
│  │ 6. namespaceSelector OR podSelector in DIFFERENT rules = OR          │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ DEFAULT DENY ALL ingress:                                             │   │
│  │   podSelector: {}          <span class="comment"># selects all pods                        │   │</span>
│  │   ingress: []              <span class="comment"># empty list = deny all                   │   │</span>
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>

            <h5>🔬 Ingress Controller — External Traffic Routing</h5>
            <p>Ingress is NOT a built-in controller — you must install one (nginx, traefik, etc.). It runs as a pod and watches Ingress resources to configure itself:</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  INGRESS CONTROLLER — How External Traffic Reaches Your Pod                  │
