            <h5>① IngressClass and the "Default Class" Mechanism</h5>
            <p>Every Ingress in this chapter so far explicitly sets <code>ingressClassName: nginx</code>. What happens if that field is OMITTED entirely? Kubernetes doesn't leave it unrouted — it falls back to whichever <code>IngressClass</code> object is marked default, a mechanism worth understanding before it surprises you.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
   kind: IngressClass
   metadata:
     name: nginx
     annotations:
       ingressclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"   <span class="comment"># ← THIS makes it the fallback</span>
   spec:
     controller: k8s.io/ingress-nginx

   Ingress WITHOUT ingressClassName set → automatically uses the class marked
   is-default-class: "true" → if TWO classes both claim default, the API
   server's behavior is undefined/ambiguous — always check for exactly one.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>kubectl get ingressclass -o yaml | grep is-default-class</code> is the fastest way to confirm which controller an omitted-<code>ingressClassName</code> Ingress will actually route through — don't assume, verify, since a cluster with two default-annotated classes is a real (if rare) misconfiguration to catch.</div>

            <h5>② endPort — Allowing a Contiguous Port Range in One Rule</h5>
            <p>Every NetworkPolicy example so far lists a single <code>port:</code>. For a workload that needs a contiguous RANGE (e.g. a monitoring agent's metrics ports 9100-9105), writing one rule per port is unnecessary — <code>endPort</code> lets a single rule span a range.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   WITHOUT endPort — one entry per port (verbose, error-prone at scale)
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ports:
   - {protocol: TCP, port: 9100}
   - {protocol: TCP, port: 9101}
   - {protocol: TCP, port: 9102}
   - {protocol: TCP, port: 9103}     ← 6 entries to maintain, easy to
   - {protocol: TCP, port: 9104}        typo or forget one when the
   - {protocol: TCP, port: 9105}        range later changes

   WITH endPort — one entry for the whole contiguous range
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ports:
   - {protocol: TCP, port: 9100, endPort: 9105}   ← same effect, 1 line
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>port: 9100</code> (no endPort)</td><td>Allows exactly ONE port, 9100</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>port: 9100</code> + <code>endPort: 9105</code></td><td>Allows the CONTIGUOUS range 9100-9105 inclusive, in one rule</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>endPort</code> requires a CNI that actually implements it (all major policy-aware CNIs do) — it's a genuine YAML-level convenience, not just documentation sugar, and it keeps a policy readable instead of ballooning into one list entry per port.</div>

            <h5>③ Ingress Annotations — the Vendor-Specific Escape Hatch (and Why It's a Problem)</h5>
            <p>§20.1's Ingress YAML included one small but consequential line: <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /</code>. This is an ANNOTATION, not a spec field — and annotations are how every real-world Ingress controller adds capability the core <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code> spec never defines. Understanding this mechanism explains both Ingress's practical power AND its biggest limitation.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   The Ingress spec itself is DELIBERATELY minimal:
     host + path rules, a TLS secret reference, a backend Service — that's it.

   Everything else real controllers support — rewrite rules, rate limiting,
   custom auth, canary weights, CORS headers, websocket timeouts — is bolted
   on via metadata.annotations, using a CONTROLLER-SPECIFIC key prefix:

     nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /        ← nginx-specific
     traefik.ingress.kubernetes.io/router.middlewares: x   ← Traefik-specific
     alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip             ← AWS ALB-specific

   Switch controllers (nginx → Traefik) and EVERY annotation using the old
   prefix silently stops working — the Ingress still applies, but the
   extra behavior it configured is just gone, with no error anywhere.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this exact fragility — vendor-specific annotations that silently stop working on controller migration — is the concrete problem Gateway API's Policy Attachment pattern (Ch 20b UTH ⑤) was purpose-built to solve. Reading this subsection right before Ch20b makes that motivation click into place instead of feeling abstract.</div>

            <h5>④ What a NetworkPolicy CANNOT Express — the L3/L4 Ceiling</h5>
            <p>Every NetworkPolicy pattern in §20.3-20.5 operates at L3 (IP/namespace) and L4 (TCP/UDP/SCTP port). It's worth being explicit about where that ceiling is, because "NetworkPolicy can't do X" is a real, frequently-hit limitation, not a YAML skill gap.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   What NetworkPolicy actually SEES — the packet header only
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  IP header: src=10.244.1.5  dst=10.244.2.8                  │  ◀── L3: podSelector /
   │  TCP header: sport=45678  dport=5432                        │  ◀── L4: port          namespaceSelector /
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        ipBlock all live HERE
   ════════════════════ POLICY DECISION MADE HERE ════════════════════
                              (everything below is INVISIBLE to it)
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  HTTP: GET /admin  Host: api.example.com                     │  ◀── L7: method/path/host —
   │  Body: {"jwt": "eyJhbGci..."}                                 │  ◀── needs a service mesh
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        or CiliumNetworkPolicy
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Can a plain NetworkPolicy express this?</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Allow traffic by IP/namespace/label, on a TCP/UDP/SCTP port</td><td>✅ Yes — this is the entire core spec</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Allow only <code>GET</code> requests, block <code>POST</code></td><td>❌ No — L7 (HTTP method/path) is invisible to plain NetworkPolicy</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Allow by DNS name (e.g. "only allow egress to api.example.com")</td><td>❌ No — the core spec only understands IPs/CIDRs, never hostnames</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Allow based on a JWT claim or mTLS identity</td><td>❌ No — that requires a service mesh's L7-aware policy engine</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> when a real requirement needs L7 rules (HTTP method, path, FQDN-based egress, or identity-based policy), the answer is a CNI-specific extension (Cilium's <code>CiliumNetworkPolicy</code> adds L7/FQDN rules) or a service mesh's policy engine (Ch17 UTH ⑤) layered on top — plain <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code> NetworkPolicy was never designed to express these, and no amount of YAML cleverness makes it possible.</div>
            </div>
