            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Ingress = Layer 7 HTTP/HTTPS routing: host + path rules map to backend Services. The Ingress OBJECT is inert — an Ingress CONTROLLER (nginx, Traefik) must be installed or Ingress does NOTHING.</li>
                    <li><code>ingressClassName</code> ties an Ingress to a controller. TLS is a secret reference (type <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code>) named in <code>spec.tls</code>.</li>
                    <li>NetworkPolicy = firewall rules for pods. Pods are allow-all UNTIL a policy selects them; once selected, only what the policy permits is allowed (implicit deny). Multiple policies on a pod are a UNION.</li>
                    <li>NetworkPolicy needs a policy-aware CNI (Calico ✅, Cilium ✅, Flannel ❌). It has SEPARATE ingress and egress rules; the safe pattern is default-deny + explicit allows — and you MUST re-allow DNS egress (UDP/TCP 53 to kube-system) or names break.</li>
                    <li>Within one <code>from</code>/<code>to</code> list item, multiple selectors are ANDed; separate list items are ORed — a frequent source of policy bugs.</li>
                    <li>DNS pattern still applies: <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>; CoreDNS resolves it — most "can't connect" bugs are DNS, not NetworkPolicy.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Ingress requires a controller</strong> — Creating an Ingress resource does nothing without an Ingress Controller (nginx, traefik). The exam cluster has one pre-installed — check with <code>kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Ingress <code>pathType</code> is REQUIRED</strong> — Must be <code>Prefix</code>, <code>Exact</code>, or <code>ImplementationSpecific</code>. Missing it = validation error. <code>Prefix</code> matches <code>/app</code> AND <code>/app/sub</code></li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy default behavior</strong> — By default, ALL traffic is allowed. Once you apply ANY NetworkPolicy that selects a pod, all non-matching traffic to that pod is DENIED. This catches people off guard</li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy is namespace-scoped</strong> — A policy in <code>prod</code> doesn't affect <code>dev</code>. To allow cross-namespace traffic, use <code>namespaceSelector</code> in the ingress/egress rules</li>
                    <li><strong>Egress policies block DNS too</strong> — If you create an egress policy, you must explicitly allow DNS (port 53 UDP to <code>kube-system</code>) or all name resolution breaks</li>
                    <li><strong><code>extensions/v1beta1</code> Ingress is REMOVED</strong> — an old manifest using <code>extensions/v1beta1</code> (with the old <code>serviceName</code>/<code>servicePort</code> field names instead of <code>service.name</code>/<code>service.port.number</code>) will be flatly rejected on any modern cluster. Always use <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code> — a plausible exam trap if handed a stale/legacy manifest to fix</li>
                    <li><strong>A default-deny NetworkPolicy does NOT block kubelet's own health/readiness probes</strong> — probes reach a pod via the node-local agent path (Ch 17's Rule 3), which NetworkPolicy governs pod-to-pod traffic, not this node-local mechanism. This is exactly why probes keep working even under a strict default-deny policy that blocks everything else</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

