            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 20 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of Ingress and NetworkPolicy internals — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An Ingress object has no <code>ingressClassName</code> field at all. Which controller routes it, if any?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Whichever <code>IngressClass</code> is annotated <code>ingressclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"</code>. If no class is marked default, the Ingress stays unrouted.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get ingressclass -o yaml | grep is-default-class</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a genuine fallback mechanism, not an error — omitting <code>ingressClassName</code> is valid YAML, it just defers the choice to whichever class claims the default annotation.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod needs a NetworkPolicy to allow monitoring traffic on ports 9100 through 9105. What's the concise way to write this, versus the verbose way?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Concise: one rule with <code>port: 9100</code> and <code>endPort: 9105</code> covers the whole contiguous range. Verbose (avoidable): six separate port entries, one per port.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">ports:
- protocol: TCP
  port: 9100
  endPort: 9105   <span class="comment"># covers 9100-9105 inclusive, in ONE entry</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>endPort</code> only works for a genuinely contiguous range — non-contiguous ports (e.g. 80 and 9100) still need separate entries.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A NetworkPolicy's <code>from</code> block has a <code>namespaceSelector</code> AND a <code>podSelector</code> listed as two SEPARATE items in the list (not nested in one item). What does this combination mean?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>OR — the source matches if it satisfies EITHER selector independently (any pod in the matching namespace, OR any pod anywhere with the matching label). This is the opposite of nesting both selectors inside ONE list item, which would mean AND.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># OR — two separate list items</span>
from:
- namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {env: prod}}
- podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: easypj}}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This exact AND-vs-OR distinction (same list item = AND, separate list items = OR) is one of the most commonly mis-written parts of a NetworkPolicy — always double-check the YAML indentation, since it silently changes the logic.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You apply a default-deny-egress policy on a namespace. Every pod immediately loses the ability to resolve DNS names. Why, and what's the one-rule fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Default-deny-egress blocks ALL outbound traffic, including the UDP/TCP 53 queries pods send to CoreDNS in <code>kube-system</code> — DNS is just another kind of traffic the policy doesn't know to exempt. Fix: add an explicit egress rule allowing UDP+TCP 53 to the <code>kube-system</code> namespace.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">egress:
- to:
  - namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system}}
  ports: [{protocol: UDP, port: 53}, {protocol: TCP, port: 53}]</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is one of the single most common NetworkPolicy exam traps — a "correct" default-deny-egress policy that breaks the ENTIRE namespace's name resolution because DNS egress wasn't explicitly re-allowed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Two separate NetworkPolicy objects both select the same pod — one allows traffic from A, the other allows traffic from B. Does the pod end up accepting from A only, B only, or both?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Both. Multiple policies selecting the same pod combine as a UNION of their allow rules — there's no ordering, precedence, or deny-override between separate policy objects.</p>
<pre>Policy 1 (selects pod X): allow from A       ┐
Policy 2 (selects pod X): allow from B       ┘── UNION → pod X accepts from BOTH A and B</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This means you can never use a SECOND policy to narrow what a FIRST policy already allowed — policies only ever add more allowed traffic, never restrict what another policy already permits.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubectl get ingress easypj</code> shows an empty ADDRESS column. Is the Ingress YAML necessarily wrong?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Not necessarily. An empty ADDRESS most often means no Ingress Controller is running (or it hasn't finished reconciling yet) — the YAML itself can be perfectly valid and still show empty ADDRESS if nothing is watching/programming it.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx   <span class="comment"># check this BEFORE assuming the YAML is broken</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The Ingress object and the Ingress Controller are two independently-failing things — always verify the controller is running before spending time re-reading the Ingress YAML for a typo that isn't there.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the fastest way to prove a NetworkPolicy actually works, without any external tooling?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Exec into an ALLOWED pod and curl the target (should succeed), then exec into a NON-selected pod and curl the same target (should hang or refuse). One success + one failure, from the SAME target, is definitive proof.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl exec easypj-web -- curl -m3 easypj-db:5432    <span class="comment"># should SUCCEED</span>
kubectl exec other-pod -- curl -m3 easypj-db:5432     <span class="comment"># should HANG/REFUSE</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Testing only the allowed path proves nothing on its own — the pod could have been reachable anyway if the policy silently isn't enforcing (e.g. wrong CNI). You need BOTH the positive and negative test to actually confirm enforcement.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A NetworkPolicy is applied but traffic still flows freely to a pod it should be blocking. List two distinct root causes.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) The CNI doesn't enforce NetworkPolicy at all (e.g. Flannel) — the object is accepted by the API server but silently has no effect. (2) The policy's <code>podSelector</code> doesn't actually match the target pod's labels, so the pod was never "selected" in the first place and remains in its default allow-all state.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Root cause</th><th>Fast check</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>CNI doesn't enforce policy</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> — is it Flannel?</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Selector doesn't match</td><td><code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code> vs the policy's <code>podSelector</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Both causes produce the identical symptom ("policy applied, traffic still flows") — always check the CNI first (fast, cluster-wide check), then the selector match (per-policy check).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does an Ingress path list <code>/api</code> before <code>/</code> matter, given both use <code>pathType: Prefix</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A bare <code>/</code> with <code>pathType: Prefix</code> matches EVERY path, including <code>/api</code>. If it were listed and evaluated first, it could swallow requests meant for the more specific <code>/api</code> rule. Listing more-specific paths before general ones avoids this ambiguity.</p>
<pre>request "/api/jobs" ──▶ rule "/api" (specific)  ✅ intended match
                    ──▶ rule "/"    (catch-all)  ⚠️ would ALSO match if evaluated first</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Different Ingress controllers have different exact tie-breaking rules for overlapping prefixes, so relying on implementation-specific ordering behavior is risky — writing paths most-specific-first is the portable habit.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Can a NetworkPolicy defined in namespace <code>prod</code> restrict traffic to a pod in namespace <code>dev</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — NetworkPolicy is namespace-scoped. A policy's <code>podSelector</code> only ever targets pods within its OWN namespace. To involve pods from another namespace at all, you use a <code>namespaceSelector</code> inside the <code>from</code>/<code>to</code> rules — as a SOURCE or DESTINATION, never as the policy's own target.</p>
<pre>NetworkPolicy in ns "prod"
  podSelector: {app: X}   ──▶ can ONLY target pods living in "prod" itself
  from: [namespaceSelector: {env: dev}]  ──▶ can reference "dev" as a SOURCE only</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a frequent point of confusion: <code>namespaceSelector</code> controls WHO can reach the policy's target pods (or where they can send), it does NOT let one namespace's policy reach into and restrict another namespace's pods directly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

