            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 20 Visual Summary — north-south + east-west, into easypj and locked down</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
              🌍 internet
                  │  HTTPS easypj.example.com/jobs
                  ▼
        🌍 Ingress Controller (nginx)  ── reads Ingress rules
                  │  host/path → backend
                  ▼
        🔌 easypj-web Service ─► 📦 web pods
                  │  app traffic
                  ▼  🧯 NetworkPolicy: ONLY easypj-web may reach db:5432
        🔌 easypj-db Service ─► 🐘 postgres
   ⚠️ Ingress needs a CONTROLLER or it does nothing; NetworkPolicy needs a
      policy-aware CNI (Calico/Cilium — NOT Flannel).
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> easypj's traffic story runs in two directions. NORTH-SOUTH, requests arrive from the internet over HTTPS, hit the Ingress controller, get matched by host/path rules, and land on the easypj-web Service and its pods. EAST-WEST, the web pods reach the database — but ONLY because a NetworkPolicy explicitly allows easypj-web → easypj-db on 5432, while a default-deny blocks everything else, and an allow-DNS egress keeps name resolution alive.</p>
                    <p>Ingress objects are inert without a controller; NetworkPolicies are inert without a policy-aware CNI. Both failure modes look identical to an untrained eye ("nothing happens") but are diagnosed completely differently — one is "is a controller pod running," the other is "does my CNI even enforce this object type."</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> install the machinery, write the rules, then PROVE them by curling from an allowed and a denied pod — never trust that a policy "looks correct" without a live test.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
