            <!-- ─────────── §20b.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20b-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">20b.1 Why Gateway API</h3>
                <p>Gateway API is the role-oriented <strong>successor</strong> to Ingress, now on the exam. Ingress packed everything into one object plus a sprawl of controller-specific annotations and a single role. Gateway API splits the concern across three resources owned by different teams — and because it's a real Kubernetes API (CRDs) rather than nginx annotations, it natively supports header matching, weighted traffic splitting, and gRPC/TLS routes that Ingress could only approximate through vendor-specific extensions.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Ingress</th><th>Gateway API</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Shape</td><td>One object + controller-specific annotations</td><td><code>GatewayClass</code> / <code>Gateway</code> / <code>HTTPRoute</code> — 3 resources</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Roles</td><td>Single role — whoever edits the Ingress owns everything</td><td>Role-separated — cluster ops, platform team, app team each own a piece</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Portability</td><td>Behavior varies by controller via annotations</td><td>Portable — the spec itself (not annotations) carries the behavior</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Advanced routing</td><td>Limited, controller-specific hacks</td><td>Native header matching, weighted traffic-split, gRPC/TLS routes</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Gateway API Is CRDs, Not a Built-In API</h5>
                    <p>Unlike <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code> Ingress (built into every cluster), Gateway API resources are Custom Resource Definitions — the cluster has ZERO idea what a <code>Gateway</code> or <code>HTTPRoute</code> is until you install the CRD bundle. This is the single most common first-time failure: applying a Gateway before its CRDs exist.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Controller</th><th>Backing project</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>nginx-gateway-fabric</td><td>The nginx project itself</td><td>The natural Gateway API successor for teams already running ingress-nginx</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Envoy Gateway</td><td>Envoy proxy project</td><td>A dedicated, purpose-built Gateway API implementation on top of Envoy</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Istio Gateway</td><td>Istio service mesh</td><td>Lets a mesh already running Istio use the same data plane for north-south Gateway API routing</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Cilium Gateway API</td><td>Cilium CNI</td><td>Runs Gateway API routing on the SAME eBPF dataplane already handling this cluster's CNI (Ch 17 UTH ②) and NetworkPolicy</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Cloud-managed (GKE Gateway, etc.)</td><td>Cloud provider</td><td>A fully-managed controller — no data-plane pods to run yourself, but tied to that one cloud</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p class="mb-0">The practical decision rule: if a CNI or mesh is ALREADY running in the cluster (Cilium, Istio), its own Gateway API implementation is usually the path of least resistance — one fewer moving data plane to operate. Starting from nothing, nginx-gateway-fabric or Envoy Gateway are the most widely adopted general-purpose choices.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20b.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20b-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">20b.2 The Gateway API Resource Model</h3>
                <p>Three resources, three owners, one pipeline: <code>GatewayClass</code> (infra type) → <code>Gateway</code> (listener/IP/TLS, platform team) → <code>HTTPRoute</code> (rules, app team) → Service.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Gateway API — Roles Split Across Resources (vs. One Ingress Blob)</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   🛠️ Cluster ops   ──►  GatewayClass   (which controller/infra type)
                              │ implemented by
   🏗️ Platform team ──►  Gateway        (listeners: :80/:443, IP, TLS)
                              │ routes attach to a listener
   👩‍💻 App team      ──►  HTTPRoute      (host/path/header/weight rules)
                              │ backendRefs
                              ▼
                         🔌 easypj-web Service ─► 📦 pods
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>The 3 Resources, What Each One Owns</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🛠️</span> GatewayClass</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Cluster-scoped, owned by cluster operators. Names WHICH controller implements Gateways of this class (e.g. <code>nginx</code>, <code>istio</code>, <code>envoy-gateway</code>) — analogous to <code>IngressClass</code>.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏗️</span> Gateway</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Namespaced, owned by the platform team. Declares LISTENERS (port, protocol, TLS mode) and gets a real IP provisioned by the controller.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👩‍💻</span> HTTPRoute</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Namespaced, owned by the app team. Declares host/path/header/weight rules and <code>backendRefs</code> — attaches to a Gateway's listener via <code>parentRefs</code>.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Status Conditions Tell You Whether Attachment/Provisioning Actually Succeeded</h5>
                    <p>Routes ATTACH to a Gateway listener via <code>parentRefs</code>; backends are referenced by <code>backendRefs</code> (Services). Status conditions — <code>Accepted</code> and <code>Programmed</code> — tell you whether attachment and provisioning succeeded. A clean <code>kubectl apply</code> does NOT guarantee a working route; always check status conditions next.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20b.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20b-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">20b.3 Routing easypj with Gateway API</h3>
                <p>Gateway API supports header, path, and weight matching plus traffic splitting — capabilities Ingress could only reach through controller-specific annotations. Here's a real Gateway + HTTPRoute pair routing easypj.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-gateway.yaml — Gateway + HTTPRoute</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Gateway
metadata:
  name: easypj-gw
spec:
  gatewayClassName: nginx          <span class="comment"># which controller implements it</span>
  listeners:
  - name: http
    protocol: HTTP
    port: 80
---
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: easypj-route
spec:
  parentRefs:
  - name: easypj-gw                <span class="comment"># attach to the Gateway listener</span>
  hostnames: ["easypj.example.com"]
  rules:
  - matches:
    - path:
        type: PathPrefix
        value: /jobs
    backendRefs:
    - name: easypj-web
      port: 80</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Header Matching &amp; Weighted Traffic Split — Ingress Can't Do This Cleanly</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Route only requests carrying a specific header to a canary backend</span>
rules:
- matches:
  - headers:
    - name: X-Env
      value: canary
  backendRefs:
  - name: easypj-web-canary
    port: 80

<span class="comment"># Split traffic 90/10 between v1 and v2 — two backendRefs, weighted</span>
rules:
- backendRefs:
  - name: easypj-web-v1
    port: 80
    weight: 90
  - name: easypj-web-v2
    port: 80
    weight: 10</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔧 Under the Hood: How a Controller Turns This Into Real Routing</h5>
                    <p>A controller (nginx-gateway, Envoy Gateway, etc.) reconciles <code>GatewayClass</code>/<code>Gateway</code>/<code>HTTPRoute</code>, provisions the data plane (an Envoy/nginx fleet + an IP), and reports back via status conditions. Header matching and weighted <code>backendRefs</code> map to data-plane routing rules — a 90/10 split is literally just two <code>backendRefs</code> with weights 90 and 10, no annotation gymnastics required.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20b.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20b-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">20b.4 Ingress vs Gateway API (Decision Guide)</h3>
                <p>Both are valid, supported ways to route north-south traffic. The choice comes down to what the cluster's controller supports and how much routing sophistication the workload actually needs.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Pick Ingress When</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>A needed controller only speaks Ingress</li>
                            <li>The cluster lacks the Gateway API CRDs (and installing them isn't an option)</li>
                            <li>Routing is trivial/single-role — one team owns everything anyway</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚪</div>
                        <h5>Pick Gateway API When</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>You want role separation (ops/platform/app teams)</li>
                            <li>Portability across controllers matters</li>
                            <li>You need header-based routing, weighted traffic splitting, or gRPC/TLS routes</li>
                            <li>Starting a new design — new designs lean Gateway API</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Migration step</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1. Install</td><td>Apply the Gateway API CRD bundle + deploy a Gateway controller</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2. Recreate rules</td><td>Turn the Ingress's host/path rules into an <code>HTTPRoute</code> attached to a <code>Gateway</code> listener</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3. Validate</td><td>Check <code>status.conditions</code> for <code>Accepted</code>/<code>Programmed</code> — NOT just "did apply succeed"</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>4. Cut over</td><td>Point DNS/LB at the new Gateway once validated</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20b-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How a Gateway Controller Reconciles GatewayClass/Gateway/HTTPRoute Into a Live Data Plane, and the Deeper Mechanics Behind Status Conditions, TLS Modes, and Cross-Namespace Routing</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ The Reconciliation Loop — From YAML to a Provisioned Data Plane</h5>
            <p>Every Gateway API resource is inert data until a controller reconciles it — the same "object vs controller" split that governs Ingress in Chapter 20, just spread across three resources instead of one.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  GATEWAY API RECONCILIATION — YAML → Live Routing                            │
│                                                                              │
│  1. GatewayClass created — names the controller (e.g. "nginx")               │
│  2. Controller watches for Gateways referencing its GatewayClass             │
│  3. Gateway created — controller provisions a real data plane (Envoy/nginx   │
│     fleet) + an IP, sets status.conditions: Accepted, Programmed             │
│  4. HTTPRoute created — controller checks parentRefs, attaches it to the     │
│     named Gateway's listener, sets status.conditions: Accepted               │
│  5. Controller compiles the attached routes into real data-plane config      │
│     (header matches, weighted backendRefs → actual load-balancing rules)     │
│                                                                              │
│  If step 2 never happens (no controller for that GatewayClass) → Gateway     │
│  stays unprogrammed FOREVER, silently — same failure shape as a missing      │
│  Ingress Controller in Ch20, just one layer removed.                         │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>kubectl get gateway easypj-gw -o yaml</code> and check <code>status.conditions</code> BEFORE debugging anything else — a Gateway stuck un-Programmed means no controller ever picked it up, which is a completely different problem than an HTTPRoute failing to attach to an otherwise-healthy Gateway.</div>

            <h5>① TLS Modes — Terminate vs Passthrough</h5>
            <p>A Gateway listener with <code>protocol: HTTPS</code> needs to declare HOW it handles TLS — the same two fundamental choices that exist for any TLS-terminating proxy.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   tls.mode: Terminate                            tls.mode: Passthrough
   ────────────────────                            ──────────────────────
   client ──HTTPS(encrypted)──▶ Gateway            client ──HTTPS(encrypted)──▶ Gateway
                                    │ DECRYPTS here                              │ forwards UNTOUCHED
                                    │ can read headers/path                      │ (still encrypted)
                                    ▼                                            ▼
                            HTTP (plain) ──▶ backend                    TLS stream ──▶ backend
                                                                          (backend itself decrypts;
                                                                           Gateway only reads SNI —
                                                                           the hostname in the
                                                                           handshake — nothing else)
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>TLS mode</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Requires</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>Terminate</code></td><td>The Gateway decrypts HTTPS itself, then forwards plain HTTP to the backend</td><td><code>certificateRefs</code> pointing at a TLS Secret</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Passthrough</code></td><td>The Gateway forwards the still-encrypted TLS stream untouched — the BACKEND terminates TLS</td><td>The backend itself must be able to handle TLS (SNI-based routing only, no L7 HTTP rules possible at the Gateway)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>Passthrough</code> mode means the Gateway can only route by SNI (the hostname in the TLS handshake) — it CANNOT read HTTP headers or paths, because the traffic is still encrypted at that point. If a design needs header-based routing, it needs <code>Terminate</code> mode, full stop.</div>

            <h5>② ReferenceGrant — Permission for Cross-Namespace Routes</h5>
            <p>An HTTPRoute in namespace <code>app</code> that wants to send traffic to a Service in namespace <code>data</code> can't just declare it — the DATA namespace must explicitly consent via a <code>ReferenceGrant</code>, or the cross-namespace reference is refused.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Namespace "app"                    Namespace "data"
   ┌──────────────────┐               ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ HTTPRoute         │  backendRef  │ ReferenceGrant (lives HERE,   │
   │  wants: Service    │─────────────▶│  in the TARGET namespace)     │
   │  "db-svc" in "data")│              │  from: {kind: HTTPRoute,      │
   └──────────────────┘               │         namespace: app}       │
                                       │  to:   {kind: Service}        │
                                       └──────────────────────────────┘
   Without the ReferenceGrant: the cross-namespace backendRef is REFUSED,
   not silently ignored — the HTTPRoute's status shows the rejection reason.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this is a deliberate security boundary, not a bug — it prevents an HTTPRoute in a less-trusted namespace from silently routing traffic into a more-sensitive namespace's Services without that namespace's own explicit opt-in.</div>

            <h5>③ Route Precedence — When Multiple HTTPRoutes Could Match</h5>
            <p>Multiple HTTPRoutes can attach to the same Gateway listener. When more than one COULD match a given request, Gateway API defines a deterministic precedence order rather than leaving it to controller-specific behavior (unlike Ingress's more ambiguous overlapping-path behavior noted in Ch20).</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Three HTTPRoutes all attached to the same Gateway listener,
   all COULD match a request to /api/v2/jobs:

     Route A: matches path PathPrefix "/"           ← least specific
     Route B: matches path PathPrefix "/api"        ← more specific
     Route C: matches path PathPrefix "/api/v2"     ← MOST specific ✅ WINS

   Request "/api/v2/jobs" ──▶ evaluated against all 3 ──▶ longest matching
                                                             prefix wins: Route C
   (ties broken by: 2nd creationTimestamp, 3rd alphabetical namespace/name)
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Precedence rule (in order)</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>1. More specific hostname wins (across routes)</td><td><code>easypj.example.com</code> beats a wildcard <code>*.example.com</code> route on the same listener</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2. Most specific path match wins</td><td>An <code>Exact</code> match beats a <code>PathPrefix</code> match for the same request</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>3. Longer path prefix wins</td><td><code>/api/v2</code> beats <code>/api</code> for a request to <code>/api/v2/jobs</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>4. More header matches wins</td><td>A rule matching 2 headers beats one matching only 1, if both otherwise tie</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>5. More query-param matches wins</td><td>Same idea, one level further down the tie-break chain</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>6. Older route wins (creationTimestamp)</td><td>Tie-break when specificity is STILL identical after tiers 1-5</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>7. Alphabetical by namespace/name</td><td>Final deterministic tie-break</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this precedence is PART OF THE SPEC, not left to each controller to interpret differently — a genuine portability win over Ingress, where overlapping-path behavior can vary between nginx, Traefik, and other controllers. In practice, tiers 4-7 rarely matter — well-designed routes almost never tie on hostname AND path specificity — but knowing the full chain exists (not just "longer path wins") matters if the exam or a real debugging session hands you a genuinely ambiguous multi-route setup.</div>

            <h5>④ GRPCRoute and TLSRoute — Beyond HTTP</h5>
            <p>HTTPRoute is the workhorse, but the Gateway API family includes sibling route types for protocols HTTPRoute doesn't fit — worth recognizing by name even if easypj itself only needs HTTPRoute today.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   Same Gateway, same listener model — different route KIND per protocol:

   Gateway (one or more listeners)
        │
        ├── HTTPRoute  ──▶ parses HTTP/HTTPS, matches path/header/method
        ├── GRPCRoute   ──▶ parses gRPC (HTTP/2 framing), matches by gRPC service/method
        └── TLSRoute    ──▶ NO parsing at all — routes by SNI only (still-encrypted TLS)

   All three share: parentRefs (attach to a Gateway) · backendRefs (point at
   a Service) · the same status.conditions (Accepted/Programmed) model.
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Route type</th><th>Protocol</th><th>When you'd need it</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>HTTPRoute</code></td><td>HTTP/HTTPS (L7)</td><td>Everything in this chapter — easypj's web traffic</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>GRPCRoute</code></td><td>gRPC (HTTP/2-based RPC)</td><td>A microservice mesh using gRPC instead of REST</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>TLSRoute</code></td><td>Raw TLS (L4, SNI-based only)</td><td>Passthrough TLS to a non-HTTP TCP service</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> all three route types share the SAME <code>parentRefs</code>/attachment/status-condition model — learning HTTPRoute's mechanics transfers directly to GRPCRoute/TLSRoute if a future project needs them.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Policy Attachment — Extending Behavior Without Forking the Spec</h5>
            <p>Gateway API deliberately keeps the core spec (GatewayClass/Gateway/HTTPRoute) narrow. Vendor-specific behavior — rate limiting, custom auth, retries — attaches via a separate "Policy Attachment" pattern instead of bloating the core resources with annotations, which is exactly the annotation-sprawl problem Gateway API was designed to avoid in the first place.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   The Ingress way (what Gateway API is escaping)      The Policy Attachment way
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────    ─────────────────────────
   kind: Ingress                                        kind: Gateway  (unchanged,
   metadata:                                             stays vendor-neutral)
     annotations:                                       ---
       nginx.io/rate-limit: "100"      ← vendor-        kind: RateLimitPolicy   ← a SEPARATE
       nginx.io/rate-limit-burst: "20"    specific,        CRD, defined by whichever
       nginx.io/retry-attempts: "3"       breaks if         controller implements it
       ...                                 you switch     spec:
                                            controllers      targetRef:            ← points AT
                                                                kind: Gateway          the Gateway/
                                                                name: easypj-gw        HTTPRoute
                                                              requests: 100/s        BY REFERENCE

   Result: the core Gateway/HTTPRoute YAML never changes when you add,
   remove, or swap out a vendor-specific policy — only the referenced
   Policy object does.
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Trait</th><th>Ingress annotations</th><th>Policy Attachment</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Where the config lives</td><td>Inline, inside the Ingress object itself</td><td>A separate CRD, referencing the Gateway/HTTPRoute</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Portability</td><td>Breaks when switching controllers (annotation keys differ)</td><td>The core object stays vendor-neutral regardless of controller</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Ownership</td><td>Whoever owns the Ingress owns the annotation too</td><td>Can be owned/managed by a different team (e.g. security owns rate-limit policy)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if a Gateway API question on the exam mentions a vendor-specific policy CRD (e.g. a rate-limit policy object) attached BY REFERENCE to a Gateway or HTTPRoute rather than baked into it, that's the Policy Attachment pattern working as designed — not a sign you're missing a "real" Gateway API field.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20b-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Gateway API is the role-oriented SUCCESSOR to Ingress: it splits one blob into <code>GatewayClass</code> (cluster ops — controller), <code>Gateway</code> (platform team — listeners/IP/TLS), <code>HTTPRoute</code> (app team — host/path/header/weight rules).</li>
                    <li>Routes ATTACH via <code>parentRefs</code>; backends via <code>backendRefs</code>; status conditions (<code>Accepted</code>, <code>Programmed</code>) confirm it actually worked, not just that the YAML applied cleanly.</li>
                    <li>It ships as CRDs you must install and is now an exam competency as of the 2025 syllabus.</li>
                    <li>Header matching, weighted traffic splitting, and gRPC/TLS routes are native — capabilities Ingress could only reach via controller-specific annotations.</li>
                    <li>TLS listeners choose <code>Terminate</code> (Gateway decrypts, reads HTTP) or <code>Passthrough</code> (Gateway can only route by SNI, backend decrypts).</li>
                    <li>Cross-namespace routing requires a <code>ReferenceGrant</code> in the TARGET namespace — a deliberate security boundary, not a bug.</li>
                    <li>Route precedence (most-specific-path, then longer-prefix, then age, then name) is part of the spec itself — a portability win Ingress never had.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20b-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The Gateway API CRDs are NOT built in</strong> — install them first, or every Gateway/HTTPRoute apply fails with "no matches for kind."</li>
                    <li><strong><code>gatewayClassName</code> must match a RUNNING controller</strong> (nginx-gateway, Envoy Gateway); otherwise the Gateway never becomes <code>Programmed</code>, silently.</li>
                    <li><strong>An HTTPRoute with no valid <code>parentRef</code></strong> (wrong Gateway name/namespace) stays unattached and silently routes nothing — check its status conditions, don't assume a clean apply means it's live.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cross-namespace routing requires a <code>ReferenceGrant</code></strong> — without it the attachment is REFUSED (with a visible reason in status), not silently ignored.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>Passthrough</code> TLS mode can't do header/path routing</strong> — the traffic is still encrypted at the Gateway, so only SNI-based (hostname) routing is possible.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

