            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 20b — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the Gateway API resource model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the 3 core Gateway API resources and who owns each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>GatewayClass</code> (cluster ops — which controller), <code>Gateway</code> (platform team — listeners/IP/TLS), <code>HTTPRoute</code> (app team — host/path/header/weight rules).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Owner</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>GatewayClass</code></td><td>Cluster ops — which controller/infra</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Gateway</code></td><td>Platform team — listeners/IP/TLS</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>HTTPRoute</code></td><td>App team — host/path/header/weight rules</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This role split is the core value proposition over Ingress's single-object, single-role design — each team edits only the resource it owns.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You apply a Gateway and get "no matches for kind Gateway." What's missing?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The Gateway API CRDs aren't installed. Unlike Ingress (built into every cluster), Gateway/HTTPRoute/GatewayClass are CRDs that must be applied first.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/gateway-api/releases/download/v1.1.0/standard-install.yaml
<span class="comment"># THEN re-apply the Gateway — "no matches for kind" is now resolved</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the single most common first-time Gateway API failure — always confirm the CRD bundle is installed before troubleshooting anything else.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Gateway's <code>status.conditions</code> never shows <code>Programmed: true</code>. What's the most likely cause?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>gatewayClassName</code> doesn't match any RUNNING controller — no controller is watching that GatewayClass, so nothing ever reconciles the Gateway into a live data plane.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get gateway easypj-gw -o yaml | grep -A3 conditions
<span class="comment"># type: Programmed, status: "False" ← no controller ever picked this up</span>
kubectl get gatewayclass   <span class="comment"># confirm the class named in gatewayClassName actually exists</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This mirrors "Ingress with no controller installed" from Chapter 20 — the object is valid data, but nothing is implementing it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between TLS <code>Terminate</code> and <code>Passthrough</code> mode on a Gateway listener?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>Terminate</code>: the Gateway decrypts HTTPS and can read headers/paths for L7 routing. <code>Passthrough</code>: the Gateway forwards the still-encrypted stream untouched, so it can ONLY route by SNI (hostname) — no header or path matching is possible.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Can read headers/paths?</th><th>Who decrypts</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>Terminate</code></td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>The Gateway itself</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Passthrough</code></td><td>❌ No — SNI only</td><td>The backend</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If a design needs header-based routing, it needs <code>Terminate</code> mode — <code>Passthrough</code> physically cannot see inside the encrypted traffic.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An HTTPRoute in namespace <code>app</code> needs to route to a Service in namespace <code>data</code>. What extra object is required, and where does it live?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A <code>ReferenceGrant</code>, created in the TARGET namespace (<code>data</code>), explicitly permitting HTTPRoutes from namespace <code>app</code> to reference its Services.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: ReferenceGrant
metadata: {name: allow-app, namespace: data}   <span class="comment"># lives in the TARGET namespace</span>
spec:
  from: [{group: gateway.networking.k8s.io, kind: HTTPRoute, namespace: app}]
  to: [{group: "", kind: Service}]</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Without it, the cross-namespace <code>backendRef</code> is REFUSED (visible in status), not silently ignored — a deliberate security boundary that prevents unauthorized cross-namespace routing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Two HTTPRoutes attached to the same Gateway listener could both match a request to <code>/api/v2/jobs</code> — one matches <code>/api</code>, the other matches <code>/api/v2</code>. Which wins, and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>/api/v2</code> wins — Gateway API's spec-defined precedence rule is "longer path prefix wins" when both are valid <code>PathPrefix</code> matches.</p>
<pre>Route A: PathPrefix "/api"      (8 chars)
Route B: PathPrefix "/api/v2"   (11 chars) ✅ WINS — longer prefix</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This precedence is PART OF THE SPEC (not left to each controller to decide differently), a genuine portability improvement over Ingress's more implementation-dependent overlapping-path behavior.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you split traffic 90/10 between two backend versions with Gateway API?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>One rule with TWO <code>backendRefs</code>, each carrying a <code>weight</code> field (90 and 10) — the controller load-balances proportionally.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">backendRefs:
- {name: easypj-web-v1, port: 80, weight: 90}
- {name: easypj-web-v2, port: 80, weight: 10}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>No annotations, no controller-specific canary object — weighted splitting is a first-class field in the core HTTPRoute spec.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's a GRPCRoute, and does it share the same attachment model as HTTPRoute?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A sibling route type for gRPC (HTTP/2-based RPC) traffic instead of plain HTTP. Yes — it shares the same <code>parentRefs</code>/attachment/status-condition model as HTTPRoute, just for a different protocol.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Route kind</th><th>Protocol</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>HTTPRoute</code></td><td>HTTP/HTTPS</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>GRPCRoute</code></td><td>gRPC (HTTP/2 RPC)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>TLSRoute</code></td><td>Raw TLS (SNI-only)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Learning HTTPRoute's mechanics transfers directly to GRPCRoute or TLSRoute if a future project needs gRPC or raw-TLS routing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A vendor-specific rate-limiting object is attached BY REFERENCE to an HTTPRoute rather than being a field inside it. Is this a sign the HTTPRoute is misconfigured?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — this is the Policy Attachment pattern working as designed. Gateway API deliberately keeps its core spec narrow; vendor-specific behavior (rate limiting, custom auth, retries) attaches separately instead of bloating the core resources.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">kind: HTTPRoute        <span class="comment"># stays vendor-neutral, unchanged</span>
---
kind: RateLimitPolicy  <span class="comment"># SEPARATE CRD, references the HTTPRoute by name</span>
spec:
  targetRef: {kind: HTTPRoute, name: easypj-route}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is exactly the annotation-sprawl problem Gateway API was designed to solve — pushing vendor extensions into their own referenced objects instead of overloading the core spec with implementation-specific fields.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">When would you still choose Ingress over Gateway API for a brand-new design?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>When the needed controller only speaks Ingress, the cluster lacks (and can't install) the Gateway API CRDs, or the routing is trivial/single-role with no benefit from role separation.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Pick</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Ingress</td><td>Controller-locked, no CRDs, trivial single-role routing</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Gateway API</td><td>Role separation, portability, header/weight routing needed</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Gateway API is the forward-leaning choice for new designs, but Ingress remains a fully valid, supported option — the decision is about constraints and complexity, not about one being deprecated.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

