                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 20b — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of Gateway API setup &amp; troubleshooting — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Map the three core Gateway API resources to their owning roles.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>GatewayClass</code> = cluster ops (selects the controller/infra). <code>Gateway</code> = platform team (listeners, IP, TLS). <code>HTTPRoute</code> = app team (host/path/header/weight routing rules).</p>
<pre>🛠️ Cluster ops   ──►  GatewayClass
🏗️ Platform team ──►  Gateway        (attaches to a GatewayClass)
👩‍💻 App team      ──►  HTTPRoute      (attaches to a Gateway)</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This role separation is the headline difference from Ingress, which bundles all three concerns into one object any single team must fully own.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Gateway with an HTTP listener on port 80.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Gateway
metadata: {name: easypj-gw}
spec:
  gatewayClassName: nginx
  listeners:
  - {name: http, protocol: HTTP, port: 80}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>gatewayClassName</code> must reference a GatewayClass backed by a running controller, or this Gateway will never reach <code>Programmed: true</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Route <code>easypj.example.com/jobs</code> to <code>easypj-web:80</code> with an HTTPRoute.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata: {name: easypj-route}
spec:
  parentRefs: [{name: easypj-gw}]
  hostnames: ["easypj.example.com"]
  rules:
  - matches: [{path: {type: PathPrefix, value: /jobs}}]
    backendRefs: [{name: easypj-web, port: 80}]</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>parentRefs</code> is what attaches this route to the Gateway's listener — get the name/namespace wrong and the route stays unattached with zero traffic routed, no error thrown.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you match on an HTTP header instead of a path?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">rules:
- matches:
  - headers: [{name: X-Env, value: canary}]
  backendRefs: [{name: easypj-web-canary, port: 80}]</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Only requests carrying that exact header value take this rule's backend — a native capability Ingress can only approximate via controller-specific annotations.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Split traffic 90/10 between easypj-web v1 and v2.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>One rule with two <code>backendRefs</code>, each given a weight (90 and 10); the controller load-balances proportionally.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">backendRefs:
- {name: easypj-web-v1, port: 80, weight: 90}
- {name: easypj-web-v2, port: 80, weight: 10}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>See §20b.3's full example — no separate canary object needed, weight is a first-class field on each backendRef.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">When would you still pick Ingress over Gateway API?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>When a needed controller only speaks Ingress, the cluster lacks the CRDs, or the routing is trivial single-role. New designs lean Gateway API.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Pick</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Ingress</td><td>Controller-locked, no CRDs, trivial routing</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Gateway API</td><td>Role separation, portability, advanced routing</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Both remain fully valid — this is a constraint-driven decision (what does the controller/cluster support), not "Ingress is deprecated."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubectl apply</code> of a Gateway returns "no matches for kind Gateway." Fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The Gateway API CRDs aren't installed. Apply the CRD bundle first, then re-apply the Gateway.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/gateway-api/releases/download/v1.1.0/standard-install.yaml
kubectl apply -f easypj-gateway.yaml   <span class="comment"># now succeeds</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Unlike Ingress, nothing about Gateway/HTTPRoute/GatewayClass is built into a stock cluster — this is always the first thing to check on a fresh cluster.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your HTTPRoute routes nothing. How do you diagnose it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe httproute</code> and check status: parentRef <code>Accepted</code>? If unattached, the <code>parentRefs</code> name/namespace or listener is wrong, or a <code>ReferenceGrant</code> is missing for cross-namespace.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe httproute easypj-route
<span class="comment"># Status → Parents → Conditions: Accepted=False, Reason: NoMatchingParent</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>An unattached route is silent — no error on apply, just zero traffic ever reaching it — so checking status conditions is not optional, it's the only way to know.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Terminate TLS on a Gateway listener — what's required?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A listener with <code>protocol: HTTPS</code>, <code>port: 443</code>, <code>tls.mode: Terminate</code>, and <code>certificateRefs</code> pointing at a TLS secret.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">listeners:
- name: https
  protocol: HTTPS
  port: 443
  tls: {mode: Terminate, certificateRefs: [{name: easypj-tls}]}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the Gateway API equivalent of Ingress's <code>spec.tls</code> + <code>secretName</code> — same underlying Secret type (<code>kubernetes.io/tls</code>), different resource shape.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An HTTPRoute in namespace <code>app</code> targets a Service in namespace <code>data</code>. What extra object is needed?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A <code>ReferenceGrant</code> in the <code>data</code> namespace permitting HTTPRoutes from namespace <code>app</code> to reference its Services.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: ReferenceGrant
metadata: {name: allow-app, namespace: data}
spec:
  from: [{group: gateway.networking.k8s.io, kind: HTTPRoute, namespace: app}]
  to: [{group: "", kind: Service}]</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Same answer as Lab Q&amp;A Q5 — worth memorizing cold, since cross-namespace routing without a ReferenceGrant is a guaranteed exam trap.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

