            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 20b Visual Summary — GatewayClass → Gateway → HTTPRoute → easypj Service</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   🛠️ GatewayClass (which controller)
        │ implemented by
        ▼
   🏗️ Gateway (listeners :80/:443, IP, TLS)  ── status: Accepted, Programmed
        │ routes attach via parentRefs
        ▼
   👩‍💻 HTTPRoute (host/path/header/weight rules)  ── status: Accepted (per parentRef)
        │ backendRefs
        ▼
   🔌 easypj-web Service ─► 📦 pods
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Gateway API replaces one Ingress blob with three role-owned resources reconciled by a controller into a live data plane. <code>GatewayClass</code> names the controller; <code>Gateway</code> (owned by the platform team) declares listeners and gets a real IP; <code>HTTPRoute</code> (owned by the app team) attaches to that Gateway and declares host/path/header/weight rules pointing at Services.</p>
                    <p>Every layer reports its own status conditions — <code>Accepted</code> and <code>Programmed</code> on the Gateway, <code>Accepted</code> per-parentRef on the HTTPRoute — and a clean <code>kubectl apply</code> proves NOTHING about whether traffic is actually routing; only the status conditions do. Cross-namespace routing additionally requires a <code>ReferenceGrant</code> in the target namespace, a deliberate security boundary.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> Gateway API and Ingress solve the same north-south routing problem with different trade-offs — Gateway API for role separation, portability, and native advanced routing; Ingress where a controller constraint or simplicity favors the older, simpler model. Both remain fully valid choices.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
