        <section class="part-cover" id="part6">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 6 · Storage</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Don’t Lose the Data</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 21–23 · CKA 10% · <em>"State that survives a pod"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Pods are ephemeral; data must not be. Part 6 covers the volume model and its lifecycle, the PV/PVC binding that decouples storage requests from storage supply, StorageClasses for dynamic provisioning, and how ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets inject configuration and credentials into easypj.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> attach emptyDir/hostPath volumes; statically and dynamically provision PVs via PVCs and StorageClasses; resize and reclaim volumes; and mount ConfigMaps/Secrets as env vars and files for easypj.</div>
            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🏠 Real-World Analogy: From Hotel Room to Rented Apartment</h5>
                <p>Part 6 moves easypj from a hotel room to a proper rented apartment. A container's writable layer is the hotel room — anything you scribble on its walls vanishes at checkout (pod deletion). An <code>emptyDir</code> is the room's in-room whiteboard: it survives housekeeping tidying up (a container restart) but is gone the moment you check out. A PersistentVolumeClaim is a rental application — you describe what you need (10Gi, read-write) and the landlord (a StorageClass) supplies a matching unit (the PV); you never pick the physical unit yourself, the leasing office matches you to one. <code>reclaimPolicy</code> is what happens after you move out: <code>Delete</code> tears the unit down, <code>Retain</code> keeps your belongings boxed up until someone manually clears them. And ConfigMaps/Secrets are the filing cabinet in the apartment — your ID (Secret) and mail-forwarding instructions (ConfigMap) live outside the apartment's own structure, so the same floor plan (image) works for any tenant.</p>
<pre>  REAL WORLD                                       KUBERNETES
  ──────────                                       ──────────
  🏨 Hotel room, wiped at checkout ───────────────▶ Ch 21 — container's writable layer (gone on restart)
  📝 In-room whiteboard, survives housekeeping ───▶ Ch 21 — emptyDir (survives container restart, not pod deletion)
  📋 Rental application (what you need) ──────────▶ Ch 22 — PersistentVolumeClaim (request)
  🏢 Landlord supplies a matching unit ───────────▶ Ch 22 — PersistentVolume / StorageClass (supply)
  🔑 What happens after you move out ─────────────▶ Ch 22 — reclaimPolicy (Delete vs Retain)
  🗄️ Filing cabinet: ID + mail instructions ─────▶ Ch 23 — Secret (credentials) + ConfigMap (settings)</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"I created a PVC, so my storage now exists."</td>
                            <td>A PVC is just a request — a rental application. It stays <code>Pending</code> until something actually supplies storage: a matching pre-created PV, or a StorageClass's dynamic provisioner. <code>kubectl describe pvc</code> showing "no persistent volumes available" means the request was filed but nobody built the unit yet.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Deleting my PVC deletes my data."</td>
                            <td>Depends entirely on <code>reclaimPolicy</code>. <code>Delete</code> does destroy the backing disk; <code>Retain</code> leaves it sitting there as <code>Released</code> — invisible unless you specifically go looking — until an admin manually clears the stale <code>claimRef</code> and reclaims it. Assuming one behavior when the class is set to the other is a common real-world capacity leak.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Secrets are encrypted, so it's safe to store credentials there."</td>
                            <td>Secrets are only base64-<strong>encoded</strong> by default — reversible by anyone with read access to the object or to etcd. Real protection requires <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> at rest PLUS RBAC restricting who can <code>get</code>/<code>list</code> secrets.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
