            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 21 · Volumes &amp; Persistent Storage</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Containers are <strong>ephemeral by design</strong> — when a container dies, everything inside it vanishes. For our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django app, that means uploaded resumes and profile photos would be wiped on every restart — and in local/dev mode, where easypj still uses SQLite (a single on-disk file), even the entire database would be lost. Volumes solve this problem by providing storage that outlives containers. Once easypj runs in Kubernetes it graduates to PostgreSQL for exactly this reason — a real database server backed by a proper PVC (Chapter 22) instead of a single file sitting in the container's disposable filesystem. In this chapter you'll understand why volumes exist, explore the different volume types Kubernetes offers, and master <code>emptyDir</code> and <code>hostPath</code> — the two simplest volume types that form the foundation for everything in Chapters 22 and 23.</p>
