                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-emptydir-sidecar.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj Django + Nginx sidecar sharing static files via emptyDir</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  labels:
    app: easypj
    component: web
spec:
  initContainers:
  - name: collect-static
    image: easypj:latest
    command: ["python", "manage.py", "collectstatic", "--noinput"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: static-files              # ◀── writes static files here
      mountPath: /app/staticfiles
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:latest
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000
    command: ["gunicorn", "easypj.wsgi:application", "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8000"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: static-files              # ◀── Django can still access them
      mountPath: /app/staticfiles
    - name: tmp-scratch               # ◀── scratch space for processing
      mountPath: /tmp/processing
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:alpine
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
    volumeMounts:
    - name: static-files              # ◀── Nginx serves the same files
      mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html/static
      readOnly: true                  <span class="comment">#     Nginx only READS, never writes</span>
  volumes:
  - name: static-files               # ◀── Shared between all containers
    emptyDir: {}
  - name: tmp-scratch                 # ◀── Only used by Django
    emptyDir:
      sizeLimit: 100Mi               <span class="comment">#     Optional: limit disk usage</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 emptyDir Medium Options</h5>
                    <p>By default, <code>emptyDir</code> uses the node's disk. You can also use RAM-backed storage with <code>emptyDir: { medium: Memory }</code>. This is faster but counts against the container's memory limit. Useful for high-speed temp files, but remember: RAM is more expensive than disk and the data is still lost when the pod dies.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">emptydir-memory-backed.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># RAM-backed emptyDir — fast but size-limited</span>
volumes:
- name: cache-volume
  emptyDir:
    medium: Memory          # ◀── Uses RAM instead of disk (tmpfs)
    sizeLimit: 256Mi        <span class="comment">#     Pod evicted if exceeds this limit</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔬 emptyDir Lifecycle — What Survives What?</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> Container restart</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Data SURVIVES ✅</strong> — emptyDir lives at the pod level, not the container level. If Django crashes and kubelet restarts it, the static files are still there.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> Pod deletion</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Data LOST ❌</strong> — When the pod is deleted (scaling down, rescheduling, node failure), the emptyDir is permanently deleted. Cannot be recovered.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> Rolling update</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Data LOST ❌</strong> — A rolling update creates a NEW pod. The old pod (and its emptyDir) is deleted. The new pod gets a fresh empty emptyDir.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📍</span> Node failure</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Data LOST ❌</strong> — Pod is rescheduled to a different node. The emptyDir was on the old node's disk. New pod starts fresh.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §21.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s21-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.4 hostPath — Node-Level Storage</h3>
                <p>A <code>hostPath</code> volume mounts a <strong>file or directory from the node's filesystem</strong> directly into the pod. Unlike <code>emptyDir</code>, the data exists on the node's disk and persists even after the pod is deleted — but only on <strong>that specific node</strong>.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ hostPath Is Dangerous in Production</h5>
                    <p><code>hostPath</code> gives the container <strong>direct access to the node's filesystem</strong>. This is a security risk (containers can read/write sensitive host files) and a portability problem (pod must always run on the same node). Use it only for development, testing, or accessing node-level system files like logs.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">hostPath — Pod Reads Node's Log Directory</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────── Node: worker-1 ──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                    │
  │  Node Filesystem:                                                  │
  │  /var/log/                                                         │
  │  ├── syslog                                                        │
  │  ├── kern.log                                                      │
  │  ├── containers/                                                   │
  │  │   ├── easypj-abc.log                                            │
  │  │   └── postgres-0.log                                            │
  │  └── pods/                                                         │
  │                                                                    │
  │  ┌────────────────────── Pod: log-collector ──────────────────┐   │
  │  │                                                              │   │
  │  │  Container: fluentd                                         │   │
  │  │  mountPath: /var/log/host  ◀──── hostPath: /var/log/        │   │
  │  │                                                              │   │
  │  │  $ ls /var/log/host/                                        │   │
  │  │  syslog  kern.log  containers/  pods/                       │   │
  │  │                                                              │   │
  │  │  ✅ Can read all node logs!                                 │   │
  │  │  ⚠️ Only works on THIS node                                │   │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                                    │
  │  If pod moves to worker-2 → different /var/log/ directory!        │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">hostpath-log-collector.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Log collector pod using hostPath to access node logs</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: log-collector
spec:
  containers:
  - name: fluentd
    image: fluent/fluentd:latest
    volumeMounts:
    - name: node-logs
      mountPath: /var/log/host        # ◀── appears here inside container
      readOnly: true                  <span class="comment">#     ALWAYS use readOnly for safety</span>
    - name: container-logs
      mountPath: /var/lib/docker/containers
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: node-logs
    hostPath:
      path: /var/log                  # ◀── actual path on the NODE
      type: Directory                 <span class="comment">#     must exist, must be a directory</span>
  - name: container-logs
    hostPath:
      path: /var/lib/docker/containers
      type: DirectoryOrCreate        <span class="comment">#     creates if doesn't exist</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>hostPath Type</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>Fails If...</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>""</code> (empty)</td>
                                <td>No checks before mounting</td>
                                <td>Never (unsafe)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>DirectoryOrCreate</code></td>
                                <td>Creates directory if missing</td>
                                <td>Never (auto-creates)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Directory</code></td>
                                <td>Must exist as a directory</td>
                                <td>Path doesn't exist or isn't a dir</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>FileOrCreate</code></td>
                                <td>Creates file if missing</td>
                                <td>Never (auto-creates)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>File</code></td>
                                <td>Must exist as a file</td>
                                <td>Path doesn't exist or isn't a file</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Socket</code></td>
                                <td>Must exist as a UNIX socket</td>
                                <td>Not a socket file</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>CharDevice</code></td>
                                <td>Must exist as a character device</td>
                                <td>Not a char device (e.g. <code>/dev/null</code>-style node)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>BlockDevice</code></td>
                                <td>Must exist as a block device</td>
                                <td>Not a block device (e.g. <code>/dev/sdb</code>-style node)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Legitimate hostPath Uses</h5>
                        <p>Accessing node logs (<code>/var/log</code>), Docker socket (<code>/var/run/docker.sock</code>), etcd data dir for backups, <code>/etc/kubernetes</code> for kubelet config. Always with <code>readOnly: true</code> when possible.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⛔</div>
                        <h5>Never Use hostPath For</h5>
                        <p>Application databases, user uploads, any data that needs to move between nodes. If the pod gets rescheduled to a different node, it will see a different (or empty) directory. Use PVC instead.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Need Real Node-Pinned Storage? Use <code>local</code>, Not hostPath</h5>
                        <p>If you genuinely need a workload pinned to one node's local disk (not just node-agent access), the official recommended path is a dedicated <code>local</code> PersistentVolume — it enforces the node pinning via a mandatory <code>nodeAffinity</code> field, something hostPath has no equivalent for (Chapter 22 UTH ⑥ covers this in full).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">CKA Exam: etcd backup using hostPath</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># CKA Exam Classic: etcd backup pod needs hostPath for certificates</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: etcd-backup
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  containers:
  - name: etcd-backup
    image: registry.k8s.io/etcd:3.5.12-0
    command:
    - /bin/sh
    - -c
    - |
      etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
        --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
        --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
        --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
        --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
    volumeMounts:
    - name: etcd-certs                # ◀── CKA: etcd certs on control plane
      mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd
      readOnly: true
    - name: backup-dir                # ◀── where snapshot is saved
      mountPath: /backup
  volumes:
  - name: etcd-certs
    hostPath:
      path: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd  # ◀── actual certs on the node
      type: Directory
  - name: backup-dir
    hostPath:
      path: /opt/etcd-backup          # ◀── backup saved on node filesystem
      type: DirectoryOrCreate
  hostNetwork: true
  nodeName: controlplane              # ◀── must run on control plane node</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🏋️ CKA Practice Scenario: Volume Troubleshooting</h5>
                    <div class="scenario-content">
                        <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A developer reports that their easypj pod keeps losing uploaded resumes every time it restarts. They've configured the pod as shown:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header">
                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                                <span class="code-lang">broken-pod.yaml (spot the problem)</span>
                            </div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># What's wrong with this pod?</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:latest
    volumeMounts:
    - name: uploads
      mountPath: /app/media/uploads
  volumes:
  - name: uploads
    emptyDir: {}        # ◀── 🤔 Is this the right volume type?</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> <code>emptyDir</code> data is <strong>deleted when the pod is deleted</strong>. Every restart creates a new, empty directory. For data that must persist, they need a <code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code>.</p>
                        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Replace <code>emptyDir: {}</code> with a PVC reference:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header">
                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                                <span class="code-lang">fixed-pod.yaml</span>
                            </div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Fixed: Use PVC instead of emptyDir for persistent data</span>
  volumes:
  - name: uploads
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: easypj-uploads-pvc   # ◀── data survives pod restarts!</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Chapter 21 Volume Troubleshooting Quick Reference -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>🔧 Volume Troubleshooting Quick Reference</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Symptom</th>
                                <th>Likely Cause</th>
                                <th>Fix</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Pod won't start — <code>MountVolume.SetUp failed</code></td>
                                <td>hostPath directory doesn't exist on node</td>
                                <td>Use <code>type: DirectoryOrCreate</code> or pre-create the directory</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Data disappears after pod restart</td>
                                <td>Using <code>emptyDir</code> for persistent data</td>
                                <td>Switch to <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Container can't write to volume</td>
                                <td><code>readOnly: true</code> set on volumeMount</td>
                                <td>Remove <code>readOnly</code> or set to <code>false</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Permission denied inside container</td>
                                <td>Container runs as non-root but volume owned by root — for a <code>hostPath</code> specifically, files created inside it are ALWAYS owned by root unless the container itself runs as root (kubelet applies no ownership adjustment at all, unlike PVCs where <code>fsGroup</code> helps)</td>
                                <td>For PVCs: add <code>fsGroup</code> in <code>securityContext</code>. For hostPath: either run the container as root, or pre-adjust host file permissions — <code>fsGroup</code> does not help here</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>emptyDir exceeds size — pod evicted</td>
                                <td><code>sizeLimit</code> exceeded (or node disk pressure)</td>
                                <td>Increase <code>sizeLimit</code> or clean up data inside the volume</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Node silently runs low on disk, but the hostPath-using pod never hit a "sizeLimit exceeded" event</td>
                                <td>Unlike emptyDir, <code>hostPath</code> has NO size cap at all — the kubelet applies zero resource-limit enforcement to it; files written there consume node disk/inodes completely untracked</td>
                                <td>Never rely on hostPath as a bounded scratch space; use emptyDir with <code>sizeLimit</code> (bounded) or a PVC (quota-able) instead</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Volume shows different data after reschedule</td>
                                <td><code>hostPath</code> is node-specific — different node = different data</td>
                                <td>Use PVC with network storage (NFS, cloud) for multi-node access</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Pod with a huge PVC takes minutes just to reach <code>Running</code></td>
                                <td>Default <code>fsGroupChangePolicy: Always</code> recursively <code>chown</code>s every file on the volume before the container starts</td>
                                <td>Set <code>securityContext.fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 fsGroupChangePolicy — Why a Huge PVC Can Make a Pod Slow to Start</h5>
                    <p>When <code>fsGroup</code> is set, the kubelet recursively changes ownership of every file on the volume to that group ID before the container ever starts — on a multi-hundred-thousand-file volume, that <code>chown</code> walk alone can take minutes. <code>fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch</code> tells the kubelet to skip the recursive walk entirely if the volume's ROOT directory already has the correct group ownership (the common case for a volume it's mounted before) — a genuine, measurable perf win with zero downside for the normal "same pod, restarted" scenario.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">fsGroupChangePolicy</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  fsGroup: 2000
  fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch   <span class="comment"># ◀── default is "Always" (always walks the whole tree)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §21.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s21-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.5 CSI &amp; the Storage Plug Point</h3>
                <p>Before CSI existed, every storage backend had to be compiled directly into Kubernetes itself — the so-called "in-tree" volume plugins. That bloated the core codebase and chained a storage vendor's release cadence to Kubernetes' own release cadence. The <strong>Container Storage Interface (CSI)</strong> solved this by moving all of that logic out into independent, vendor-shipped drivers that speak one common gRPC contract — the same "extension interface" pattern CNI uses for networking and CRI uses for the container runtime.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term: CSI (Container Storage Interface)</h5>
                    <p>A standard plug point that lets storage vendors ship drivers <strong>out-of-tree</strong> that provision and attach real storage — AWS EBS, GCE Persistent Disk, Azure Disk, Ceph, Portworx, and dozens more. Kubernetes core stays completely storage-vendor-agnostic; a StorageClass simply names which CSI driver (the <code>provisioner</code>) to call (Ch 22 covers this in full).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CSI — Who Does What</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────┐     provision/attach      ┌─────────────────────────┐
  │  Kubernetes  │ ───────────────────────►  │  CSI Driver (vendor)    │
  │  (PVC/PV)    │  ◄─── disk ready ───────  │  EBS · PD · Ceph · ...  │
  └──────────────┘                            └──────────┬──────────────┘
                                                          │ real disk
                                                          ▼
                                                    ┌───────────┐
                                                    │ 💾 Volume  │
                                                    └───────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Day-2: How This Plays Out in the Cloud</h5>
                    <p>In a real cloud cluster, easypj's PostgreSQL PVC is satisfied by a CSI driver that carves out a REAL cloud disk and attaches it to whichever node the pod happens to land on. That attach step — driven entirely by the CSI driver, not Kubernetes core — is exactly what makes the data survive a pod reschedule onto a different node.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Reach for</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Just sharing scratch space between containers in one pod</td><td><code>emptyDir</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Hot cache that must be fast and can be lost</td><td><code>emptyDir</code> with <code>medium: Memory</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Read node logs / mount a host socket (agent only)</td><td><code>hostPath</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Inject config files</td><td><code>configMap</code> volume (Ch 23)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Inject credentials</td><td><code>secret</code> volume (Ch 23)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Anything that MUST survive pod delete/reschedule</td><td>PVC — real durable storage (Ch 22)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>This decision table is the single most useful thing to memorize from this chapter — every volume question on the exam (and in real cluster design) reduces to "how long does this data need to live, and does it need to survive a reschedule?"</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">bash — inspect what CSI is actually installed on THIS cluster</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Which CSI drivers are registered cluster-wide?</span>
kubectl get csidrivers
<span class="comment"># NAME                     ATTACHREQUIRED   MODES</span>
<span class="comment"># ebs.csi.aws.com          true             Persistent</span>

<span class="comment"># Which drivers are actually running on EACH node right now?</span>
kubectl get csinodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       DRIVERS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># node-1     1         30d</span>

<span class="comment"># What StorageClasses can I actually use?</span>
kubectl get storageclass
<span class="comment"># NAME                 PROVISIONER         RECLAIMPOLICY</span>
<span class="comment"># fast-ssd (default)   ebs.csi.aws.com     Delete</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ PodSecurityAdmission Can Silently Reject hostPath — Before the Scheduler Ever Sees It</h5>
                    <p>hostPath is forbidden starting at the <code>baseline</code> Pod Security Standard already — NOT only at the stricter <code>restricted</code> tier (a common misconception; <code>restricted</code> simply inherits this rule from <code>baseline</code>, it doesn't introduce it). A pod using <code>hostPath</code> in a namespace enforcing either standard is rejected at admission time, before the scheduler even runs — it never even gets a <code>FailedScheduling</code> event, because it never got that far. If a hostPath pod fails immediately with an admission-webhook-style error instead of a normal scheduling failure, check the target namespace's <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce</code> label first.</p>
                    <p><code>restricted</code> goes further still: it allows only 8 volume types at all — <code>configMap</code>, <code>csi</code>, <code>downwardAPI</code>, <code>emptyDir</code>, <code>ephemeral</code>, <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code>, <code>projected</code>, and <code>secret</code>. Notably, <strong>this means the <code>nfs</code> volume example earlier in this chapter (§21.2) would ALSO be rejected under <code>restricted</code></strong>, not just hostPath — any volume type outside that allow-list is out for a namespace enforcing the strict tier.</p>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Volume Mounts Are Actually Implemented: Bind Mounts, tmpfs, and the Container Filesystem Stack</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ The Container Filesystem Stack — Where Volumes Fit</h5>
            <p>When a container mounts a volume, the kubelet instructs the container runtime to create an additional mount point that overlays the container's root filesystem. Understanding where volumes sit in the filesystem stack helps you debug mount failures and data persistence issues.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CONTAINER FILESYSTEM STACK — Layers from Top to Bottom                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Container's view of /:                                                      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  /app/                                                               │    │
│  │  ├── media/          <span class="comment">← VOLUME MOUNT (emptyDir/hostPath/PVC)          </span>│    │
│  │  │   └── uploads/    <span class="comment">← Survives container restart                   </span>│    │
│  │  ├── manage.py       <span class="comment">← From image layer 3 (COPY . .)               </span>│    │
