            <h5>① CSI's Two-Phase Lifecycle — Why "Attach" and "Mount" Are Different RPCs</h5>
            <p>The diagram above shows the kubelet "attaching" a PV, but for CSI-backed disks that's actually two separate gRPC calls made to two different parts of the CSI driver — understanding the split explains a lot of real-world "volume stuck" symptoms.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   PHASE 1 — CONTROLLER PLUGIN (runs once, cluster-wide, talks to the cloud API)
   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  ControllerPublishVolume  ("Attach")                                 │
   │  • Called by the attach-detach controller (inside kube-controller-  │
   │    manager) — NOT by the kubelet                                     │
   │  • Tells the cloud API: "attach disk vol-0a1b2c to instance i-0xyz" │
   │  • Result: the raw block device now exists on the NODE (e.g.        │
   │    /dev/xvdf on AWS) but has no filesystem mounted yet               │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                    │
                                    ▼
   PHASE 2 — NODE PLUGIN (runs on every node as a DaemonSet, talks to the OS)
   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  NodeStageVolume  ("Global Mount")                                    │
   │  • Formats the raw device (if not already formatted) and mounts it  │
   │    ONCE into a node-global staging directory                        │
   │  • Runs only the FIRST time any pod on this node needs the volume   │
   │                                                                        │
   │  NodePublishVolume  ("Bind Mount Into the Pod")                       │
   │  • Bind-mounts from the staging directory into THIS pod's actual    │
   │    mount namespace at the container's mountPath                     │
   │  • Runs once per pod that mounts the volume (supports RWX fan-out   │
   │    from one staged volume into many pod mounts on the same node)    │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Which phase is stuck</th><th>Where to look</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Pod stuck <code>ContainerCreating</code>, event says "volume is already exclusively attached"</td><td>Phase 1 (Attach) — often a leftover attachment from a crashed node</td><td><code>kubectl describe volumeattachment</code>, cloud console for stray attached disks</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Pod stuck <code>ContainerCreating</code>, event says "MountVolume.SetUp failed"</td><td>Phase 2 (NodeStage/NodePublish)</td><td>kubelet logs on the node, and the CSI node-plugin container's logs (<code>kubectl logs -n kube-system &lt;csi-node-pod&gt; -c &lt;driver-name&gt;</code>)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this split explains why RWX (ReadWriteMany) is fundamentally a NodePublish-level concept, not an Attach-level one — a network filesystem is staged once per node and then bind-mounted into as many pods as need it, which is exactly why block storage (which can only ever be Attached to one node) structurally cannot support RWX.</div>

            <h5>② Projected Volumes — Merging Secret + ConfigMap + downwardAPI + serviceAccountToken Into One Directory</h5>
            <p>A <code>projected</code> volume isn't a new storage backend — it's a way to combine several different SOURCES into a single mounted directory, so an application that expects "one config folder" can get its TLS cert, its settings, its pod metadata, and its API token all in one place without four separate <code>volumeMounts</code>. Four sources (<code>secret</code>, <code>configMap</code>, <code>downwardAPI</code>, <code>serviceAccountToken</code>) cover the vast majority of real use cases; two newer beta sources — <code>clusterTrustBundle</code> and <code>podCertificate</code> — round out the full set for CA-bundle and workload-identity-certificate use cases. All sources in one projected volume must be in the SAME namespace as the pod.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   volumes:
   - name: all-in-one
     projected:
       sources:
       - secret:
           name: easypj-tls              # → /etc/app/tls.crt, tls.key
       - configMap:
           name: easypj-config            # → /etc/app/settings.ini
       - downwardAPI:                     # → /etc/app/pod-name, pod-namespace
           items:
           - path: "pod-name"
             fieldRef: { fieldPath: metadata.name }
       - serviceAccountToken:              # → /etc/app/token (short-lived, audience-scoped)
           path: token
           expirationSeconds: 3600
           audience: vault

   RESULT: /etc/app/
   ├── tls.crt          ◀── from the Secret
   ├── tls.key          ◀── from the Secret
   ├── settings.ini     ◀── from the ConfigMap
   ├── pod-name         ◀── from downwardAPI
   └── token            ◀── a fresh, auto-rotating, audience-scoped SA token
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>What it contributes</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>secret</code></td><td>One or more Secret's keys, same as a plain <code>secret</code> volume</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>configMap</code></td><td>One or more ConfigMap's keys, same as a plain <code>configMap</code> volume</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>downwardAPI</code></td><td>Pod/container metadata (name, namespace, labels, annotations, resource requests/limits) as files</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>serviceAccountToken</code></td><td>A short-lived, audience-scoped, auto-rotating token from the <code>TokenRequest</code> API — the modern replacement for the old long-lived ServiceAccount Secret</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if two sources project a key with the same filename, the pod fails to be created (a validation error) — projected volumes require every combined key across all sources to be unique, since they all land in one flat directory. <code>expirationSeconds</code> on a <code>serviceAccountToken</code> source defaults to 3600 (1 hour) and has a hard MINIMUM of 600 seconds (10 minutes) if you set it explicitly — a pod requesting a shorter token lifetime than that is rejected at admission time.</div>

            <h5>③ Generic Ephemeral Volumes, CSI Ephemeral Volumes, and Image Volumes — Three Different "Temporary but CSI-Backed" Mechanisms</h5>
            <p>emptyDir is ephemeral AND simple (no CSI driver involved). But there are two more ephemeral mechanisms that DO go through a CSI driver — easy to confuse with emptyDir since they're all "gone when the pod goes," but structurally very different.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   GENERIC EPHEMERAL VOLUME                    CSI EPHEMERAL (INLINE) VOLUME
   ──────────────────────────                  ──────────────────────────
   volumes:                                     volumes:
   - name: scratch                              - name: secrets-store
     ephemeral:                                   csi:
       volumeClaimTemplate:                         driver: secrets-store.csi.k8s.io
         spec:                                       readOnly: true
           accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]               volumeAttributes:
           resources:                                   secretProviderClass: "vault-db-creds"
             requests: { storage: 5Gi }

   • K8s auto-creates a REAL PVC named          • NO PVC object is ever created at all
     "{pod-name}-{volume-name}"                 • The CSI driver mounts data directly,
   • That PVC is owned by the pod — deleted       driven entirely by volumeAttributes
     automatically when the pod is deleted      • Only usable with CSI drivers that
   • Uses your NORMAL StorageClass/dynamic        explicitly declare ephemeral support
     provisioning — a real disk gets created     • Common use: pulling secrets live from
   • Stable since v1.23                           Vault/AWS Secrets Manager per-pod

   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   IMAGE VOLUMES (newer feature, still progressing through alpha→beta→stable —
   check `kubectl explain pod.spec.volumes.image` on YOUR cluster to confirm
   its current status): volumes[].image lets a pod mount another OCI image/
   artifact's filesystem directly as a read-only volume — e.g. shipping a large
   ML model or a shared binary as its own small image, then "mounting" it into
   an application pod instead of baking it into the app's own image layers.
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Creates a real PVC?</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Generic ephemeral volume</td><td>Yes (auto-created &amp; auto-owned)</td><td>Pod-scoped scratch disk that needs REAL dynamic-provisioned storage (bigger/faster than emptyDir's node-local space)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>CSI ephemeral (inline) volume</td><td>No</td><td>Pulling live data from an external system per-pod (secrets stores, config services) via a purpose-built CSI driver</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Image volume</td><td>No</td><td>Mounting a separate OCI artifact's content into a pod without rebuilding the app image</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> a generic ephemeral volume's PVC is real — it shows up in <code>kubectl get pvc</code> and consumes a StorageClass's quota just like any other PVC, it's just owned by the pod instead of being manually managed. Deleting the pod deletes the PVC (and, per reclaimPolicy, potentially the underlying disk) automatically. Two real limitations to know: storage-capacity-aware scheduling does NOT apply to generic ephemeral volumes — the disk is created only AFTER the pod is already scheduled to a node, so provisioning "must be unlikely to fail" or the pod gets stuck; and they're not covered by kubelet's storage resource-usage limits the way emptyDir is. You CAN make one outlive its pod deliberately — use a StorageClass with <code>reclaimPolicy: Retain</code> and the resulting PV survives pod deletion just like any Retain-policy PV would, turning "ephemeral" into "quasi-permanent" on purpose.</div>

            <h5>④ subPathExpr and mountPropagation — the Two Volume-Mount Escape Hatches</h5>
            <p>Two lesser-known <code>volumeMounts</code> fields solve two very specific problems: giving each replica of a SHARED volume its own private subdirectory, and letting a container see mount changes that happen on the HOST after the container already started.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   subPathExpr — PER-REPLICA SUBDIRECTORIES OF ONE SHARED VOLUME

   volumeMounts:
   - name: shared-logs
     mountPath: /var/log/app
     subPathExpr: "$(POD_NAME)"     # ◀── expands at runtime, NOT hardcoded
   env:
   - name: POD_NAME
     valueFrom: { fieldRef: { fieldPath: metadata.name } }

   3 replicas of ONE Deployment, ONE shared PVC:
   /var/log/app/easypj-web-7d9f-abc12/   ◀── replica 1 only sees its own subdir
   /var/log/app/easypj-web-7d9f-def34/   ◀── replica 2 only sees its own subdir
   /var/log/app/easypj-web-7d9f-ghi56/   ◀── replica 3 only sees its own subdir
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   mountPropagation — SEEING HOST-SIDE MOUNT CHANGES LIVE

   None (default)          → container's view of the mount is frozen at start;
                              new mounts made on the HOST after that are invisible
   HostToContainer          → new mounts made on the HOST propagate INTO the
                              container automatically (one-way: host → container)
   Bidirectional            → mounts propagate BOTH ways; the container can create
                              mounts the HOST (and other containers) will also see
                              — powerful and dangerous, requires privileged: true
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Real-world use case</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>subPathExpr</code></td><td>A shared log-aggregation volume where each StatefulSet/Deployment replica needs its own isolated subdirectory without hand-authoring N different subPaths</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>mountPropagation: Bidirectional</code></td><td>CSI node-driver DaemonSets and storage-injecting sidecars (e.g. Istio's old init pattern) that must mount NEW volumes into other containers' namespaces after those containers are already running</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> unlike plain <code>subPath</code> (which is set once at pod creation and never re-evaluated), <code>subPathExpr</code> is still only evaluated ONCE at container start — it expands the environment variable at startup, it does NOT re-expand on every access. It solves "which subdirectory," not the hot-reload problem UTH ⑤ of Chapter 22 covers for ConfigMap subPath mounts.</div>

            <h5>⑤ downwardAPI as Environment Variables, and How emptyDir Eats Into Node Ephemeral-Storage</h5>
            <p>The downwardAPI section earlier in this chapter showed pod metadata as mounted FILES. The far more common real-world pattern injects the same metadata as plain environment variables — no volume at all needed. And every default-medium emptyDir you create draws from a node-wide capacity pool the kubelet actively polices.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   downwardAPI AS ENV VARS (no volume needed at all)

   env:
   - name: POD_NAME
     valueFrom: { fieldRef: { fieldPath: metadata.name } }
   - name: POD_IP
     valueFrom: { fieldRef: { fieldPath: status.podIP } }
   - name: NODE_NAME
     valueFrom: { fieldRef: { fieldPath: spec.nodeName } }
   - name: CPU_LIMIT
     valueFrom: { resourceFieldRef: { resource: limits.cpu } }   ◀── resourceFieldRef,
                                                                     not fieldRef!
   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   NODE EPHEMERAL-STORAGE — THE POOL EVERY DEFAULT emptyDir DRAWS FROM

   Node's disk  ──splits into──▶  ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
                                   │ container images + writable layers  │
                                   │ + ALL default-medium emptyDir usage │
                                   │ + logs                              │
                                   └─────────────────┬─────────────────┘
                                                       ▼
                          kubelet tracks total ephemeral-storage USED
                          against the node's ephemeral-storage CAPACITY
                                                       │
                              too much usage ──────────┤
                                                       ▼
                          kubelet evicts pods (highest usage / lowest
                          QoS first) to relieve NODE-WIDE disk pressure
                          — this can evict a pod whose OWN emptyDir never
                          hit its sizeLimit, if OTHER pods on the same
                          node crowded out the shared disk
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field type</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>fieldRef</code></td><td>Pod-level metadata: name, namespace, UID, labels, annotations, nodeName, hostIP, podIP</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>resourceFieldRef</code></td><td>Container resource requests/limits: <code>limits.cpu</code>, <code>requests.memory</code>, etc. — reads the SPEC value, not live usage</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> a pod evicted for "node ran out of ephemeral-storage" is a genuinely confusing real-world incident because <code>kubectl describe pod</code> on the EVICTED pod may show its OWN emptyDir usage was perfectly reasonable — the node-wide pool being exhausted by a NEIGHBOR is the actual cause. Always check <code>kubectl describe node</code>'s Allocated resources / Conditions section, not just the evicted pod, when diagnosing this.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>A container's writable layer is ephemeral — it dies with the CONTAINER. Volumes attach storage with longer lifecycles outside that layer.</li>
                    <li>The lifecycle spectrum: emptyDir (lives with the POD, survives container restarts) → hostPath (lives on ONE node) → PVC/PV (cluster-durable, survives pod delete/reschedule/scale — Ch 22).</li>
                    <li>A <code>volume</code> is declared at pod level (the source); a <code>volumeMount</code> places it at a path inside a container. Many containers can mount the same volume — that is how sidecars share files.</li>
                    <li>CSI is the storage extension interface: out-of-tree drivers provision and attach real disks (EBS/PD/Ceph), replacing the old in-tree plugins.</li>
                    <li><code>configMap</code> and <code>secret</code> volumes mount as read-only config files (Ch 23); they are not durable storage.</li>
                    <li>easypj-web is stateless (no PVC needed); PostgreSQL needs a PVC because its data MUST persist.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">emptyDir persists across pod restarts</span> — it persists across <strong>container</strong> restarts, NOT pod deletions</li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">emptyDir survives container crashes but dies with the pod</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">hostPath works the same on any node</span> — it reads <strong>that specific node's</strong> filesystem. Different node = different data</li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">hostPath is node-specific — pod rescheduling changes the data</span></li>
                    <li><code>volumes[]</code> and <code>volumeMounts[]</code> names <strong>must match exactly</strong> — typo = mount failure</li>
                    <li><code>emptyDir: {medium: Memory}</code> counts against the container's <strong>memory limit</strong>, not disk</li>
                    <li>Forgetting <code>readOnly: true</code> on hostPath mounts is a <strong>security risk</strong></li>
                </ul>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong><code>volumeMounts</code> vs <code>volumes</code></strong> — <code>volumes:</code> declares the volume at pod level. <code>volumeMounts:</code> mounts it into a specific container. Forgetting either causes the pod to fail or the mount to not appear</li>
                        <li><strong><code>subPath</code> prevents directory overwrite</strong> — Without <code>subPath</code>, mounting a volume at <code>/etc/nginx</code> replaces ALL existing files in that directory. With <code>subPath: nginx.conf</code>, only that one file is mounted</li>
                        <li><strong><code>readOnly: true</code></strong> — Add this to volumeMounts for security when the container should only read data. The exam may ask you to mount a config volume as read-only</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

