                        <tr><td>View pod events (mount errors)</td><td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; | grep -A5 "Events:"</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Watch pod status during restart</td><td><code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -w</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>list volumes in JSON</td><td><code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.volumes}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>list mounts</td><td><code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].volumeMounts}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>verify mount from inside</td><td><code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- cat /proc/mounts | grep &lt;mount-path&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>quick volume summary</td><td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; | grep -A15 Volumes:</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 21 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the volume-lifecycle model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Share a single file between two containers in one pod. Which volume type and how?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Declare an <code>emptyDir</code> volume at pod level and mount it (same volume name) into both containers' <code>volumeMounts</code> at whichever paths each container needs — both containers see the same underlying files.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">volumes: [{name: shared, emptyDir: {}}]
containers:
- {name: a, volumeMounts: [{name: shared, mountPath: /data}]}
- {name: b, volumeMounts: [{name: shared, mountPath: /data}]}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The volume NAME is what links a pod-level source to a container-level mount — the mountPath can differ per container, but the name must match exactly.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Does an <code>emptyDir</code> survive a container crash? A pod deletion?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Survives a container restart (it's pod-scoped, not container-scoped) but is DESTROYED when the pod is deleted or rescheduled to another node.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>emptyDir data</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Container crashes/restarts</td><td>✅ Survives</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Pod deleted or rescheduled</td><td>❌ Lost</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the single most-tested distinction in the chapter — "restarted" and "rescheduled" sound similar but have opposite outcomes for emptyDir data.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Mount the node's <code>/var/log</code> into a pod. Which volume type, and what's the catch?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>hostPath</code> with <code>path: /var/log</code>. The catch: this only works for THAT specific node — reserve it for node-level agents (log shippers, monitoring DaemonSets), never for application data.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Workload type</th><th>hostPath /var/log?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>DaemonSet (log shipper, monitoring agent)</td><td>✅ Correct pattern</td><td>Runs exactly one pod per node — each pod naturally sees only ITS OWN node's logs</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Deployment (application data)</td><td>❌ Anti-pattern</td><td>Pod can be rescheduled to any node — it would see a different (or empty) directory each time</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A DaemonSet using hostPath is fine because it runs one pod per node anyway — each pod naturally reads its OWN node's logs. A Deployment using hostPath for app data is the anti-pattern.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does the hostPath <code>type</code> field actually guard against?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It validates or creates the target path before mounting: <code>Directory</code> requires it to already exist as a directory, <code>DirectoryOrCreate</code> creates an empty one if missing, and similarly for <code>File</code>/<code>FileOrCreate</code>/<code>Socket</code>/etc. The wrong type for a not-yet-existing path leaves the pod stuck in <code>ContainerCreating</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>type value</th><th>Guards against</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>Directory</code></td><td>Path missing or isn't a directory — fails, never creates</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>DirectoryOrCreate</code></td><td>Never fails — auto-creates an empty directory if missing</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>File</code> / <code>FileOrCreate</code></td><td>Same pair, but for a single file instead of a directory</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Socket</code> / <code>CharDevice</code> / <code>BlockDevice</code></td><td>Path must already exist as that exact special-file type</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>When unsure whether the path exists yet, <code>DirectoryOrCreate</code> is the safe default — it never fails on a missing path, unlike the strict <code>Directory</code> type.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create an in-memory scratch volume. How, and what's the real cost?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>emptyDir</code> with <code>medium: Memory</code> — a tmpfs (RAM-backed) volume. It's fast, but it COUNTS AGAINST the pod's memory limit and is completely lost on a node reboot.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">emptyDir: {medium: Memory, sizeLimit: 256Mi}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a genuine trade-off, not a free performance win — every byte written to a Memory-medium emptyDir is a byte no longer available to the container's actual application memory.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj-web restarts and an uploaded resume file is gone. What's the likely root cause and the fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The file was written either to the container's own writable layer, or to an <code>emptyDir</code> on a pod that then got rescheduled. Either way: for data that MUST persist, the fix is a PVC (Ch 22), not emptyDir and definitely not the container filesystem.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Diagnose: what volume (if any) actually backs the upload path?</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web-xyz -o jsonpath='{.spec.volumes}' | jq
kubectl get pod easypj-web-xyz -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].volumeMounts}' | jq
<span class="comment"># No volume backing /app/uploads at all → writes go to the container's</span>
<span class="comment"># own writable layer → gone on ANY restart, not just reschedule</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>"Data disappeared after a restart" is the single most common storage symptom on the exam — the diagnosis is always "what volume type (if any) was actually backing that path?"</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What specific problem does CSI solve that existed before it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Before CSI, every storage backend had to be compiled directly into Kubernetes core ("in-tree" plugins) — bloating the codebase and tying a vendor's release cadence to Kubernetes' own. CSI moved that logic to independent, out-of-tree drivers speaking one common gRPC contract.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Before CSI (in-tree)</th><th>After CSI (out-of-tree)</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Where driver code lives</td><td>Compiled into Kubernetes core itself</td><td>Independent binary, deployed separately</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>New storage vendor?</td><td>Requires a Kubernetes RELEASE to add support</td><td>Vendor ships their own CSI driver anytime</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Bug in one driver?</td><td>Risks the whole kubelet/apiserver binary</td><td>Isolated to that one driver's process</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>CSI is the same "extension interface" pattern as CNI (networking, Ch 17) and CRI (container runtime) — recognizing this pattern once means understanding all three plug points at once.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the precise difference between a <code>volume</code> and a <code>volumeMount</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <code>volume</code> (declared once under <code>pod.spec.volumes</code>) defines the storage SOURCE. The <code>volumeMount</code> (declared per-container) maps that source to a specific PATH inside that particular container.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  volumes:                          <span class="comment"># ◀── ONE source, declared ONCE at pod level</span>
  - name: shared-data
    emptyDir: {}
  containers:
  - name: writer
    volumeMounts:
    - {name: shared-data, mountPath: /write-here}   <span class="comment"># ◀── mount 1</span>
  - name: reader
    volumeMounts:
    - {name: shared-data, mountPath: /read-here}    <span class="comment"># ◀── mount 2, SAME source, different path</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>One source, potentially many mounts — this is exactly the mechanism that lets multiple containers in one pod see the same underlying data at (potentially different) paths.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Can one volume be mounted into several containers in the same pod at once?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Yes — reference the same volume name in each container's <code>volumeMounts</code>. That's the entire mechanism behind the sidecar log/file-sharing pattern (e.g. Django writing static files that Nginx then serves).</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">containers:
- name: django
  volumeMounts:
  - {name: static-files, mountPath: /app/static}       <span class="comment"># writes static files here</span>
- name: nginx
  volumeMounts:
  - {name: static-files, mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html}  <span class="comment"># serves the SAME files</span>
volumes:
- {name: static-files, emptyDir: {}}                    <span class="comment"># ◀── ONE volume, THREE containers could mount it</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>There's no limit on how many containers can mount the same volume — the constraint is only that the volume NAME must match exactly across every mount that wants it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you mount a volume read-only into just ONE of several containers sharing it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Set <code>readOnly: true</code> on that specific container's <code>volumeMount</code> entry — <code>readOnly</code> is a per-mount setting, not a per-volume one, so one container can have write access while another mounting the SAME volume is read-only.</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">volumeMounts:
- {name: static-files, mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html, readOnly: true}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is exactly the pattern used in the Django+Nginx sidecar example above: Django writes (read-write mount), Nginx only serves (read-only mount) — same volume, different access per container.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

