            <div class="comparison-table">
                <h4>📋 Capstone: emptyDir vs hostPath vs PVC, Side by Side</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Axis</th><th>emptyDir</th><th>hostPath</th><th>PVC (Ch 22)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Survives container restart?</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Survives pod deletion/reschedule?</td><td>❌ No</td><td>⚠️ Only if rescheduled to the SAME node</td><td>✅ Yes, always</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Node-portable?</td><td>N/A (dies with pod anyway)</td><td>❌ Tied to one specific node</td><td>✅ Follows the pod (CSI attach/detach)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Security risk</td><td>Low</td><td>High — direct host filesystem access, blocked by <code>restricted</code> PSA</td><td>Low — mediated entirely through the CSI driver</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Typical owner</td><td>Developer (pod spec only)</td><td>Cluster admin / node-level agents only</td><td>Developer requests (PVC), admin supplies (PV/StorageClass)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Setup complexity</td><td>Trivial — zero external dependencies</td><td>Trivial YAML, but operationally fragile</td><td>Requires a StorageClass/CSI driver to be installed first</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 21 Visual Summary — the volume lifecycle spectrum</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   container-only      pod-scoped         node-scoped      cluster-durable
   (writable layer) ◄─ emptyDir ─►    ◄─ hostPath ─►   ◄─ PVC ─► PV ─►
   dies with         dies with the      tied to ONE      survives pod delete,
   CONTAINER         POD (survives a     node             reschedule & scaling
                      container restart)
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> storage in Kubernetes is a lifecycle ladder. At the bottom, the container's writable layer dies with the container. One rung up, <code>emptyDir</code> lives as long as the POD and is shared between its containers (the sidecar pattern), with an optional RAM-backed Memory medium. <code>hostPath</code> reaches the node's own filesystem but pins the pod to that node and is reserved for agents and dev.</p>
                    <p>At the top, PVC/PV (backed by CSI drivers — the out-of-tree extension interface that replaced Kubernetes' old in-tree plugins) give cluster-durable storage that survives pod deletion, reschedule and scaling — which is why easypj's web tier stays stateless while PostgreSQL and user uploads ride on PVCs, covered in full in Chapter 22.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> always remember — a <code>volume</code> is the source declared once at pod level; a <code>volumeMount</code> places it inside a container, and many containers can mount the same source. Every volume decision in this chapter reduces to one question: how long does this data actually need to live?</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

