            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> PVC stuck in Pending? Check: (1) <code>kubectl describe pvc</code> — what's the error? "no persistent volumes available" = create a PV or StorageClass. (2) <code>kubectl get pv</code> — any Available PVs with sufficient capacity and matching access mode? (3) StorageClass with <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code> delays binding until a pod uses the PVC — this is NORMAL (pod must be scheduled first for topology-aware provisioning). Once the pod IS scheduled, the provisioner creates the disk in that node's exact zone and stamps the resulting PV's <code>spec.nodeAffinity</code> with that zone/node — permanently restricting where the PV can ever attach, which is exactly why binding had to wait for scheduling in the first place.
            </div>

            <h5>① volumeMode: Filesystem vs Block — Handing a Database Raw Disk Access</h5>
            <p>Every PVC in this chapter so far implicitly uses the default <code>volumeMode: Filesystem</code> — the PV is formatted with a filesystem and mounted as a normal directory. There's a second, less common mode: <code>Block</code>, which hands the container a raw block device instead, with no filesystem at all.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   volumeMode: Filesystem (default)          volumeMode: Block
   ─────────────────────────────────          ──────────────────────
   PV is formatted (ext4/xfs) and             PV is exposed as a raw
   mounted at volumeMounts.mountPath           block device at
   as a normal directory                       volumeDevices.devicePath
   ┌─────────────────┐                        ┌─────────────────┐
   │ /var/lib/pg/data │  ← files, dirs         │ /dev/xvdf        │  ← no filesystem,
   │ base/ global/... │                        │ (raw bytes)      │     app manages it
   └─────────────────┘                        └─────────────────┘
   Almost every workload                       Databases that manage
   uses this (PostgreSQL                       their OWN filesystem
   included, normally)                         layout for performance
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>Block</code> mode is genuinely rare in practice — it exists for specialized databases that implement their own storage engine and want to bypass the kernel filesystem layer entirely for performance. Recognizing <code>volumeDevices</code>/<code>devicePath</code> in a PV/pod spec (instead of the usual <code>volumeMounts</code>/<code>mountPath</code>) is the tell that Block mode is in play.</div>

            <h5>② CSI VolumeSnapshots &amp; Clones — Backing Up a PVC Without Leaving Kubernetes</h5>
            <p>A Retain reclaim policy protects against accidental PVC deletion, but it doesn't give you POINT-IN-TIME backups. CSI VolumeSnapshots do — they let you snapshot a live PVC and later restore that snapshot into a brand new PVC, all through native Kubernetes objects.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   1. Snapshot a live PVC                    2. Restore into a NEW PVC
   ─────────────────────────                 ──────────────────────────
   VolumeSnapshot ──references──▶ PVC        PVC (new) ──spec.dataSource──▶
        │                                         VolumeSnapshot
        ▼ CSI driver creates
   VolumeSnapshotContent                     Result: a fresh PVC pre-populated
   (the actual snapshot data,                 with the snapshot's data — a
    vendor-side)                              full clone, ready to mount
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>VolumeSnapshot</code></td><td>The user-facing request — "snapshot THIS PVC now"</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>VolumeSnapshotClass</code></td><td>The SC-equivalent for snapshots — names the CSI driver and its own <code>deletionPolicy</code> (Delete/Retain), just like a StorageClass does for PVs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>VolumeSnapshotContent</code></td><td>The actual snapshot data, managed by the CSI driver (like a PV is to a PVC)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>New PVC with <code>spec.dataSource</code></td><td>Restores (or clones) from a snapshot into a fresh, independent PVC</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this is the real-world answer to "how do I back up easypj's PostgreSQL PVC before a risky migration" — snapshot it, run the migration, and if it goes wrong, restore a new PVC from the pre-migration snapshot in minutes instead of reaching for an external backup tool. Just like a PV's <code>reclaimPolicy</code>, a <code>VolumeSnapshotClass</code>'s <code>deletionPolicy: Retain</code> keeps the underlying snapshot data even if the <code>VolumeSnapshot</code> object is deleted — the same Retain-vs-Delete safety tradeoff, one layer up. <strong>Important limitation:</strong> a VolumeSnapshot is CSI-driver-local — it protects against a bad migration or a fat-fingered <code>kubectl delete</code> on the SAME cluster, but it is NOT a disaster-recovery plan for losing the whole cluster or region. For that, real production setups pair VolumeSnapshots with a cross-cluster tool like <strong>Velero</strong>, which can export snapshot data to off-cluster object storage (S3, GCS) and restore into a completely different cluster. One more finalizer-style protection to know: deleting a PVC while it's actively being used as a snapshot SOURCE is postponed (not blocked forever, unlike the pod-mount case) until that snapshot either finishes (<code>readyToUse: true</code>) or aborts — the same "don't yank storage out from under an in-progress operation" pattern as the pod-mount finalizers, just for a different in-flight operation.</div>

            <h5>③ Online Volume Expansion — What Actually Happens When You Grow a PVC</h5>
            <p>§22.6's "PVC Expansion" tip shows the one-line patch to grow a PVC, but the mechanism behind it involves two distinct CSI calls, not one — and understanding both explains why expansion can sometimes require a pod restart and sometimes doesn't.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   kubectl patch pvc ... requests.storage: 20Gi   (was 10Gi)
        │
        ▼
   1. ControllerExpandVolume  ── the CSI controller resizes the underlying
                                   disk itself (e.g. the cloud block volume)
        │
        ▼
   2. NodeExpandVolume        ── the CSI node plugin grows the FILESYSTEM
                                   on top of the now-larger disk
                                   (often happens automatically, online,
                                   without restarting the pod)

   Requires: allowVolumeExpansion: true on the StorageClass.
   Direction: GROW only — shrinking a PVC is never allowed, at all.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> the two-step nature explains a real symptom: <code>kubectl get pvc</code> can show the new, larger <code>requests.storage</code> immediately after the patch, while <code>df -h</code> INSIDE the pod still shows the old, smaller size for a few moments — that's the gap between step 1 (disk resized) and step 2 (filesystem grown) completing.</div>

            <h5>④ fsGroup and Why RWX Needs a Network Filesystem</h5>
            <p>Two smaller but genuinely exam-relevant mechanics round out the PV/PVC model: how a non-root container gets write permission on a freshly-mounted volume, and why block storage (EBS, GCE PD) can never satisfy a ReadWriteMany request no matter how the PVC is written.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   fsGroup — group-ownership for non-root containers
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   securityContext:
     fsGroup: 1000        ← kubelet chowns the volume's group to 1000 on mount

   fsGroupChangePolicy: Always (default)     vs   OnRootMismatch
   recursively chowns EVERY file on           only chowns if the root directory's
   EVERY mount — slow on huge volumes         group ownership doesn't already match
                                               (skips the expensive walk when it's
                                                already correct — big perf win)

   Why RWX needs a network filesystem
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   EBS / GCE PD / Azure Disk = BLOCK storage → physically attached to ONE node
   at a time → can only ever be RWO (or ROX at best), never RWX.
   RWX (many nodes, read-write) needs a filesystem the NETWORK can serve to
   multiple nodes at once: NFS, CephFS, EFS/Filestore CSI drivers.
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> a PVC requesting <code>ReadWriteMany</code> against an EBS/PD-backed StorageClass will simply never bind, no matter how long you wait — this isn't a bug to debug, it's a fundamental mismatch between block storage's physical nature and what RWX demands; the fix is switching to an NFS/CephFS-backed class, not troubleshooting the existing one further.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Reclaiming a Released PV, and the subPath Update Trap</h5>
            <p>Two practical gotchas close out this chapter's deep-dive: a Retain-policy PV that seems "stuck" after its PVC is deleted, and a mount-path trick that silently stops receiving live updates.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   RETAIN PV, STUCK "RELEASED"                    subPath, NOT LIVE-SYNCED

   PVC deleted                                    ConfigMap UPDATED
        │                                               │
        ▼                                               ▼
   PV → Released                                  Pod's mounted file:
   spec.claimRef still points                     UNCHANGED (subPath copied
   at the dead PVC's UID ──▶ binder                once at container start,
   refuses to reuse it                             never re-synced)
        │                                               │
        ▼                                               ▼
   kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt;                        must restart the pod, OR
   -p '{"spec":{"claimRef":null}}'                 mount the whole directory
        │                                          (no subPath) to get
        ▼                                          live-reload behaviour
   PV → Available → rebindable
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>What's really happening</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Retain PV stays <code>Released</code> forever, never rebinds</td><td>Its <code>spec.claimRef</code> still points at the OLD, now-deleted PVC — the binder won't reuse it while that stale reference exists</td><td><code>kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt; -p '{"spec":{"claimRef":null}}'</code> (or edit it out), then it returns to <code>Available</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>A ConfigMap/Secret mounted with <code>subPath</code> never picks up updates</td><td><code>subPath</code> copies the file ONCE at container start — it's a one-time snapshot, not a live-synced mount</td><td>Use a full-directory mount (no subPath) when hot-reload matters; accept the staleness (and a pod restart) when subPath is required</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>A <code>subPath</code> mounted from a <code>hostPath</code> volume exposes more than expected</td><td><code>subPath</code> resolves symlinks on the HOST — a malicious or misconfigured symlink inside a hostPath tree can point <code>subPath</code> at files well outside the intended directory</td><td>Never combine <code>subPath</code> with an untrusted/writable <code>hostPath</code>; prefer CSI-backed volumes for anything subPath-mounted in production</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> a "leaked" Retain PV that never rebinds is a genuinely common real-world capacity problem on long-running clusters — nobody deleted it, it just silently sits there consuming disk while <code>kubectl get pv</code> shows it as <code>Released</code> instead of <code>Available</code>, invisible unless you specifically go looking. For the update-staleness problem specifically, <code>subPathExpr</code> (Chapter 21 UTH ④) doesn't fix hot-reload either — it only lets the PATH itself be computed from an env var at container start; it's still a one-time snapshot, not a live sync.</div>

            <h5>⑥ Local PersistentVolumes, Storage-Object-In-Use Protection, and Why a PVC Delete Can Hang</h5>
            <p>Every static PV example in this chapter used <code>hostPath</code> for simplicity — but hostPath was never designed to be a real persistent-storage backend (Chapter 21 covers why: no capacity tracking, no node-affinity enforcement, easy to schedule a pod onto the WRONG node). The proper node-pinned PV type is <code>local</code>, and it comes with a mandatory field hostPath doesn't have.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   apiVersion: v1
   kind: PersistentVolume
   metadata:
     name: easypj-local-ssd
   spec:
     capacity: { storage: 100Gi }
     accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]
     persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain     # local volumes: ALWAYS Retain in practice
     storageClassName: local-storage
     local:
       path: /mnt/disks/ssd1                    # ◀── real path on ONE specific node
     nodeAffinity:                                # ◀── MANDATORY — not optional like hostPath
       required:
         nodeSelectorTerms:
         - matchExpressions:
           - key: kubernetes.io/hostname
             operator: In
             values: [node-3]                    # ◀── scheduler is FORCED to place the pod here

   WHY nodeAffinity IS MANDATORY: unlike a network-attached CSI disk, a `local` volume's
   data physically only exists on ONE node's disk. Without nodeAffinity telling the
   scheduler that constraint, a pod could be scheduled to node-7, try to mount a PV
   that only exists on node-3's disk, and fail. hostPath has no such field — it will
   happily let the scheduler place your pod on the wrong node, silently giving you an
   EMPTY directory instead of your actual data. This is the single biggest reason
   `local` PVs, not hostPath, are the correct pattern for node-pinned production storage.
   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   WHY A PVC DELETE SOMETIMES HANGS — Storage Object in Use Protection

   kubectl delete pvc easypj-postgres-pvc
   persistentvolumeclaim "easypj-postgres-pvc" deleted     ◀── command returns, but...
   kubectl get pvc easypj-postgres-pvc
   NAME                   STATUS        VOLUME    ...
   easypj-postgres-pvc    Terminating             ...      ◀── ...it's STUCK here

   CAUSE: a `kubernetes.io/pvc-protection` finalizer is automatically added to every
   PVC that's actively mounted by a running Pod. The object can't actually be removed
   from etcd until that finalizer clears — which only happens once the consuming Pod
   is deleted. (The matching `kubernetes.io/pv-protection` finalizer does the same
   for a Bound PV.) This is a SAFETY feature, not a bug: it stops you from accidentally
   yanking storage out from under a live workload.
   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   A THIRD FINALIZER — preventing a leak if the ADMIN deletes the PV first
   (GA in Kubernetes 1.33; beta since 1.31): every CSI-backed PV also gets an
   `external-provisioner.volume.kubernetes.io/finalizer`. Without it, an admin
   deleting a PV BEFORE its PVC (the wrong order) would remove the Kubernetes
   object while the REAL disk on the storage backend silently survives, orphaned
   forever — a genuine leaked-money incident on cloud disks. This finalizer blocks
   PV removal from the cluster until the backend disk is actually deleted too.
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Real cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Pod on the wrong node sees an empty/wrong directory</td><td>Used <code>hostPath</code> for node-pinned data — it has no node-affinity enforcement</td><td>Use a <code>local</code> PV with <code>spec.nodeAffinity</code> instead</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>kubectl delete pvc</code> hangs on <code>Terminating</code></td><td><code>pvc-protection</code> finalizer — a Pod is still mounting it</td><td>Delete/scale-down the consuming Pod first, then the PVC delete completes automatically</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>kubectl delete pv</code> hangs on <code>Terminating</code> (CSI-backed PV)</td><td><code>external-provisioner.volume.kubernetes.io/finalizer</code> — the backend disk hasn't actually been deleted yet</td><td>Always delete the PVC first, let reclaimPolicy handle the PV; this finalizer clears automatically once the CSI driver confirms the real disk is gone</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> never <code>kubectl patch</code> the finalizer off a stuck-Terminating PVC/PV just to "unblock" it — that bypasses the exact safety check the finalizer exists to enforce, and can leave a Pod holding a mount to storage Kubernetes now considers deleted. Delete the consuming workload first; let the finalizer clear naturally.</div>

            <h5>⑦ Cloning, Capacity-Aware Scheduling, Topology Restriction, and In-Place Attribute Changes</h5>
            <p>Four more CSI-era capabilities round out the provisioning picture beyond plain dynamic provisioning: copying an existing PVC's data into a new one, letting the scheduler know when a storage pool is actually full, restricting WHERE a class can provision at all, and tuning a live volume's performance without a full resize.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   PVC CLONING — copy an existing PVC's data into a brand-new PVC

   apiVersion: v1
   kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
   metadata:
     name: easypj-postgres-dev-copy
   spec:
     storageClassName: fast-ssd
     accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]
     resources: { requests: { storage: 20Gi } }   # must be >= source's capacity
     dataSource:
       kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
       name: easypj-postgres-pvc                   # ◀── clone THIS PVC's data
   # CSI-only. Source and dest must be in the SAME namespace and SAME VolumeMode.
   # Source PVC must be Bound and NOT actively in use by a pod at clone time.
   # Real use: spin up a full copy of prod's database volume for a dev/staging pod.
   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   dataSourceRef — the GENERALIZED version of dataSource

   dataSource only accepts kind: PersistentVolumeClaim or kind: VolumeSnapshot.
   dataSourceRef accepts those PLUS arbitrary custom resources, letting
   third-party "volume populator" controllers fill a brand-new PVC from
   sources dataSource could never express — e.g. a Backup CRD from a backup
   vendor, or a DatabaseSnapshot CRD from an operator. Same PVC-request
   mechanism, pluggable beyond the two built-in kinds.
   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   CAPACITY-AWARE SCHEDULING — CSIStorageCapacity objects (GA since v1.24)

   Without it: scheduler picks a node → WaitForFirstConsumer provisions →
               provisioning FAILS because that zone's storage pool is full →
               pod stuck retrying on a node that can never work
   With it:    CSI driver publishes CSIStorageCapacity objects per storage pool →
               scheduler filters out nodes whose pool lacks capacity BEFORE
               ever trying to provision → no wasted failed-provision cycles
   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   allowedTopologies — restrict a StorageClass to specific zones

   provisioner: pd.csi.storage.gke.io
   volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
   allowedTopologies:
   - matchLabelExpressions:
     - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
       values: [us-east1-b, us-east1-c]    # ◀── NEVER provision outside these zones,
                                               even if a pod gets scheduled elsewhere
   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   VolumeAttributesClass (GA since v1.34) — change IOPS/throughput WITHOUT resize/recreate

   Old way: need more IOPS → must grow the whole disk (allowVolumeExpansion) even
            if you don't need more SPACE, just more speed
   New way: apply a new VolumeAttributesClass to the PVC → CSI driver adjusts the
            disk's performance tier in place (e.g. gp3 IOPS/throughput on AWS)
            — no capacity change, no pod restart, for CSI drivers that support it
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Solves</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>PVC cloning (<code>dataSource</code>)</td><td>"I need a real copy of this data in a new, independent PVC" — dev/staging snapshots, safe pre-migration copies</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>dataSourceRef</code> (volume populators)</td><td>Populating a new PVC from an arbitrary custom resource (a vendor's Backup CRD, an operator's DatabaseSnapshot CRD) — the generalized extension point beyond the two built-in <code>dataSource</code> kinds</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>CSIStorageCapacity</code></td><td>Scheduler avoiding nodes whose backing storage pool is already exhausted, instead of failing provisioning after the fact</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>allowedTopologies</code></td><td>Compliance/cost rules — "this StorageClass may only ever provision in these specific zones," regardless of where pods get scheduled</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>VolumeAttributesClass</code></td><td>Tuning a live disk's performance characteristics (IOPS/throughput) without the downtime/risk of a capacity resize</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> PVC cloning looks identical in YAML to restoring from a VolumeSnapshot (UTH ②) — both use <code>spec.dataSource</code> — the only difference is whether <code>kind</code> is <code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code> (clone a live PVC right now) or <code>VolumeSnapshot</code> (restore a point-in-time snapshot). Same mechanism, two different data sources.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>PV/PVC is separation of concerns: an admin (or a StorageClass) supplies the PV (the actual storage); a developer files a PVC (a request). Kubernetes binds them: PVC Pending → Bound; after the PVC is deleted the PV goes Released (and is cleaned up or kept per reclaimPolicy).</li>
                    <li>A pod NEVER names a PV — it names a PVC. That decoupling is the whole design.</li>
                    <li>A StorageClass enables DYNAMIC provisioning — its provisioner creates a PV on demand the moment a matching PVC appears, so you never pre-create PVs.</li>
                    <li>Access modes describe NODES, not pods: RWO = one node read-write, ROX = many nodes read-only, RWX = many nodes read-write, RWOP = one pod read-write. Most block storage is RWO; RWX needs a network filesystem.</li>
                    <li><code>reclaimPolicy</code> controls the disk's fate after the PVC is gone: <code>Delete</code> (destroy it) or <code>Retain</code> (keep it, manual cleanup). <code>Recycle</code> is deprecated.</li>
                    <li>StatefulSets use <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> so each replica gets its own durable PVC/PV that survives restart, reschedule and scale.</li>
                    <li>A PVC binds on three criteria: storageClassName match, compatible access modes, and request ≤ capacity.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">PVC size must exactly match PV size</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">PVC request must be ≤ PV capacity — K8s picks the smallest matching PV</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">ReadWriteOnce means only one pod can mount it</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">RWO means one NODE. Multiple pods on same node can all mount it</span></li>
                    <li><code>storageClassName</code> must match EXACTLY between PV and PVC — empty string ≠ omitted</li>
                    <li>PV is <strong>cluster-scoped</strong> but PVC is <strong>namespaced</strong> — pod and PVC must be in same namespace</li>
                    <li><code>Recycle</code> reclaim policy is <strong>deprecated</strong> — use <code>Retain</code> or <code>Delete</code></li>
                    <li>After PVC deletion with <code>Retain</code> policy, PV stays <code>Released</code> — clear its stale <code>spec.claimRef</code> (<code>kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt; -p '{"spec":{"claimRef":null}}'</code>) to return it to <code>Available</code> and rebindable; no need to delete and recreate the PV itself</li>
                </ul>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>PVC stuck in <code>Pending</code></strong> — Top 3 causes: (1) No PV matches the requested size/accessMode, (2) StorageClass name mismatch, (3) No default StorageClass and PVC doesn't specify one. Debug with <code>kubectl describe pvc</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Access modes must match</strong> — PV offers <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> but PVC requests <code>ReadWriteMany</code>? Won't bind. Know the modes: <code>RWO</code> (single node), <code>ROX</code> (many nodes read-only), <code>RWX</code> (many nodes read-write)</li>
                        <li><strong>Reclaim policies</strong> — <code>Retain</code>: PV data preserved after PVC deletion (manual cleanup). <code>Delete</code>: PV and backing storage removed. <code>Recycle</code>: deprecated. Default for dynamic PVs is <code>Delete</code> — dangerous for production!</li>
                        <li><strong><code>storageClassName: ""</code> vs omitting it</strong> — Empty string means "no StorageClass" (only matches PVs with no class). Omitting it uses the cluster's default StorageClass. This subtle difference is a classic CKA trap</li>
                        <li><strong>PV is cluster-scoped, PVC is namespace-scoped</strong> — <code>kubectl get pv</code> (no -n flag needed). <code>kubectl get pvc -n &lt;ns&gt;</code>. A PVC in namespace <code>prod</code> can bind to any available PV</li>
                        <li><strong>A bound PVC's spec is mostly immutable — once a field has an explicit VALUE</strong>, once created (and especially once Bound), <code>accessModes</code>, an already-SET <code>storageClassName</code>, <code>volumeName</code>, and <code>selector</code> can NEVER be changed. Only <code>resources.requests.storage</code> is mutable, and only to INCREASE it (never shrink). Trying to change any other already-set field returns "spec is immutable after creation except resources.requests for bound claims" — you must delete and recreate the PVC instead</li>
                        <li><strong>Setting a default StorageClass later applies retroactively — but only to fill in an ABSENT field, not change an existing one</strong> — a PVC created with <code>storageClassName</code> entirely OMITTED (not immutable yet, since it was never set) stays correctly unclassed until an admin later marks a StorageClass default (<code>storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"</code>); Kubernetes then retroactively fills in that old PVC's still-empty <code>storageClassName</code> to reference the new default. This does NOT contradict the immutability rule above — it's filling a blank, not overwriting a value. Don't be confused if an old "no class" PVC suddenly shows a <code>storageClassName</code> you never explicitly set</li>
                        <li><strong>More than one default StorageClass?</strong> Kubernetes doesn't error — it silently picks the MOST RECENTLY CREATED one marked default for any PVC that omits <code>storageClassName</code>. If a PVC unexpectedly bound to the "wrong" class, check <code>kubectl get storageclass</code> for more than one <code>(default)</code> annotation and compare creation timestamps</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — PV/PVC binding checklist (memorize this):</strong><br>
                For a PVC to bind to a PV, ALL must match:<br>
                ✅ Sufficient capacity (PV size ≥ PVC request)<br>
                ✅ Compatible access mode (at least one in common)<br>
                ✅ Matching StorageClass (or both empty)<br>
                ✅ PV status must be <code>Available</code> (not <code>Released</code> or <code>Bound</code>)<br>
                ✅ Label selectors match (if PVC uses <code>selector</code>)</p>
