            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 22 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the PV/PVC binding model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>volumeMode: Block</code>, and when would a real workload actually need it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It exposes a PV as a raw block device (e.g. <code>/dev/xvdf</code>) with NO filesystem at all, instead of the default formatted-and-mounted <code>Filesystem</code> mode. It's for specialized databases that implement their own storage engine and want to bypass the kernel filesystem layer entirely for performance.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>What the container sees</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>Filesystem</code> (default)</td><td>A mounted directory — files and subdirectories</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Block</code></td><td>A raw device path — the app manages its own layout</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is genuinely rare in practice — recognizing <code>volumeDevices</code>/<code>devicePath</code> instead of the usual <code>volumeMounts</code>/<code>mountPath</code> in a spec is the tell that Block mode is in use.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to back up easypj's PostgreSQL PVC before a risky migration, entirely within Kubernetes. What's the mechanism?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A CSI <code>VolumeSnapshot</code> — it snapshots the live PVC. If the migration goes wrong, you restore a NEW PVC from that snapshot via <code>spec.dataSource</code>, giving you a fresh, independent clone of the pre-migration data.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: snapshot.storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: VolumeSnapshot
metadata: { name: postgres-pre-migration }
spec:
  source: { persistentVolumeClaimName: easypj-postgres-pvc }
<span class="comment"># restore into a brand-new PVC:</span>
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata: { name: easypj-postgres-restored }
spec:
  dataSource: { kind: VolumeSnapshot, name: postgres-pre-migration }</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The <code>VolumeSnapshotContent</code> object holds the actual vendor-side snapshot data — it's the PV-equivalent for snapshots, while <code>VolumeSnapshot</code> is the PVC-equivalent user-facing request.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You grow a PVC from 10Gi to 20Gi. <code>kubectl get pvc</code> immediately shows 20Gi, but <code>df -h</code> inside the pod still briefly shows the old size. Why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Expansion is TWO separate CSI calls, not one: <code>ControllerExpandVolume</code> resizes the underlying disk first, THEN <code>NodeExpandVolume</code> grows the filesystem on top of it. The brief gap you're seeing is the window between step 1 completing and step 2 finishing.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>CSI call</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1</td><td><code>ControllerExpandVolume</code></td><td>Cloud API grows the underlying disk itself (e.g. EBS volume 10Gi→20Gi)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2</td><td><code>NodeExpandVolume</code></td><td>Kubelet grows the filesystem (ext4/xfs) to fill the now-larger disk</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This two-step mechanism (not a single atomic operation) is exactly why expansion is usually — but not always instantly — online without a pod restart.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A PVC requests <code>ReadWriteMany</code> against a StorageClass backed by AWS EBS. It never binds. Is this a misconfiguration to debug?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — it's a fundamental mismatch, not a bug. EBS (like GCE PD, Azure Disk) is BLOCK storage, physically attached to exactly one node at a time — it can only ever satisfy RWO (or ROX at best), never RWX. The fix is switching to an NFS/CephFS-backed StorageClass, not troubleshooting the existing one.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Backend</th><th>Physical nature</th><th>Max access mode</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>EBS / GCE PD / Azure Disk</td><td>Block storage — attaches to ONE node</td><td>RWO (ROX at best)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>NFS / CephFS / EFS</td><td>Network filesystem — servable to MANY nodes</td><td>RWX ✅</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>RWX genuinely requires a filesystem the NETWORK can serve to multiple nodes simultaneously — block storage's physical nature makes this categorically impossible, no matter how the PVC/PV YAML is written.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch</code> optimize, compared to the default <code>Always</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>Always</code> recursively chowns EVERY file on EVERY mount — slow on huge volumes. <code>OnRootMismatch</code> only performs that expensive recursive walk if the root directory's group ownership doesn't already match <code>fsGroup</code> — skipping it entirely when it's already correct.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  fsGroup: 2000
  fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch   <span class="comment"># skip the walk if root already matches</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On a large PVC (tens of GB with millions of files), this is a real, measurable pod-startup-time difference — not a cosmetic setting.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A <code>Retain</code>-policy PV stays stuck in <code>Released</code> forever after its PVC is deleted — it never rebinds to a new PVC. Why, and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Its <code>spec.claimRef</code> still points at the OLD, now-deleted PVC — the binder refuses to reuse a PV with a stale claimRef. Clear it (<code>kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt; -p '{"spec":{"claimRef":null}}'</code>) and the PV returns to <code>Available</code>.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pv easypj-data-pv
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Released     CLAIM: default/old-deleted-pvc  ◀── stale claimRef</span>

kubectl patch pv easypj-data-pv -p '{"spec":{"claimRef":null}}'

kubectl get pv easypj-data-pv
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Available    CLAIM:                          ◀── rebindable again</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a genuinely common real-world capacity leak on long-running clusters — the PV silently sits there consuming disk, invisible unless you specifically check for <code>Released</code> (not <code>Available</code>) status.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does a ConfigMap mounted with <code>subPath</code> stop hot-reloading, when the same ConfigMap mounted as a full directory DOES hot-reload?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>subPath</code> copies the file ONCE at container start — a one-time snapshot, not a live-synced mount. A full-directory mount stays synced (~60s) because the kubelet swaps the whole backing directory atomically; subPath bypasses that mechanism entirely.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Mount style</th><th>ConfigMap updated?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Full-directory mount (no subPath)</td><td>✅ Hot-reloads within ~60s (atomic symlink swap)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>subPath</code> mount</td><td>❌ Frozen at container start — needs a pod restart</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>When hot-reload matters, avoid subPath; when subPath is required (e.g. mounting one file without clobbering a directory), accept that a pod restart is needed to pick up changes.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between a generic ephemeral CSI volume and a normal PVC-backed volume?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A generic ephemeral volume (<code>volumes[].ephemeral.volumeClaimTemplate</code>) is declared INLINE in the pod spec and is created and deleted WITH the pod — no standalone, independently-managed PVC object exists. A normal PVC-backed volume references a PVC that outlives the pod and can be reused by a replacement pod.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Generic ephemeral volume</th><th>Normal PVC-backed volume</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>PVC lifetime</td><td>Tied to the pod — auto-created/deleted</td><td>Independent — outlives the pod</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Reused by a replacement pod?</td><td>❌ No, gone with the old pod</td><td>✅ Yes, if it references the same PVC name</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Use generic ephemeral volumes for genuinely pod-lifetime CSI-backed scratch space (e.g. a fast local NVMe scratch disk) where you explicitly do NOT want the storage to survive the pod.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> matter specifically for ZONAL cloud disks?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>With <code>Immediate</code> binding, the disk could be provisioned in a zone BEFORE the scheduler picks a node — risking a disk in zone A while the pod lands in zone B, which can never attach. <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> delays provisioning until AFTER the scheduler picks a node, so the disk is created in the SAME zone/topology the pod actually landed in.</p>
                        <pre>
   Immediate binding                          WaitForFirstConsumer
   ──────────────────                         ─────────────────────
   1. Disk provisioned in zone-A                1. Scheduler picks node in zone-B
   2. Scheduler picks node in zone-B             2. Disk provisioned in zone-B ✅
   3. Attach FAILS — wrong zone ❌                  (matches where the pod landed)
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is why <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> is the correct default for zonal cloud storage — <code>Immediate</code> binding is a real cross-zone-attach-failure risk on multi-zone clusters.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Two PVCs each request 10Gi, RWO, class "manual". Three Available PVs exist: 10Gi, 15Gi, and 50Gi (all matching class/mode). Which PV does the FIRST PVC bind to?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The 10Gi PV — the binder selects the SMALLEST sufficient PV (smallest PV where capacity ≥ request), not just any match. The 50Gi PV is deliberately NOT chosen even though it technically satisfies the request, to avoid wasting capacity.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Available PV</th><th>Satisfies 10Gi request?</th><th>Selected?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>10Gi</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>🏆 Selected — smallest sufficient</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>15Gi</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Skipped — larger than needed</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>50Gi</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Skipped — would waste 40Gi</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Understanding this "smallest-sufficient-wins" rule explains why a cluster with mixed-size static PVs doesn't waste its largest volumes on small requests — the binder is actively capacity-conscious, not first-match.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

