            <div class="comparison-table">
                <h4>📋 Capstone: Static PV vs Dynamic (StorageClass) vs Local PV</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Axis</th><th>Static PV</th><th>Dynamic (StorageClass)</th><th>Local PV</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Who creates the PV?</td><td>Admin, by hand, ahead of time</td><td>Provisioner, on-demand, automatically</td><td>Admin, by hand — but tied to ONE node's disk</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Backing disk</td><td>Any pre-existing storage the admin points at</td><td>Freshly created by the CSI driver each time</td><td>A real physical/local disk already attached to a specific node</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Requires <code>nodeAffinity</code>?</td><td>No (unless also using <code>local</code>)</td><td>No — StorageClass topology handles it</td><td>✅ Yes, mandatory (Ch 22 UTH ⑥)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Production fit</td><td>Rare — legacy/special-case only</td><td>Standard practice everywhere</td><td>High-performance workloads needing local NVMe speed (e.g. some databases)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 22 Visual Summary — StorageClass → PV → PVC → pod</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   StorageClass ──provisions──► dynamic PV ──binds──► PVC ──mounted──► postgres pod
   (Available → Bound → Released)        request ≤ capacity, class &amp; accessModes match
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> a StorageClass (provisioner + parameters + binding mode + reclaim policy) dynamically provisions a PV the moment a matching PVC is created. The PVC binds to that PV when storageClassName, access modes and size all agree, moving Pending → Bound. A pod consumes the PVC by claimName and mounts it at a path.</p>
                    <p>For easypj's PostgreSQL, a StatefulSet's <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> give each replica its own durable PVC — deleting the StatefulSet does NOT delete those PVCs, by design. With reclaimPolicy Retain, the disk and its data survive pod deletion, restart, reschedule and scale — and after the PVC is deleted the PV goes Released until an admin explicitly clears its claimRef and cleans it up.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> every binding decision reduces to three criteria — storageClassName match, compatible access modes, and request ≤ capacity — and a pod NEVER names a PV directly, only the PVC. That decoupling, plus CSI as the vendor-neutral plug point underneath it all, is the entire design of this chapter.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch23">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 23 · ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets</h2>
