            <section class="section-block" id="s23-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.1 ConfigMaps — Non-Sensitive Configuration</h3>
                <p>A ConfigMap stores <strong>key-value pairs</strong> of non-sensitive configuration data. It decouples application configuration from the container image, so the same image can run with different settings in different environments.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ConfigMap — Same Image, Different Config Per Environment</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ONE IMAGE — THREE ENVIRONMENTS — THREE CONFIGMAPS                       │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                                    │
  │  │  easypj:latest   │  ◀── same image everywhere                        │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                                    │
  │           │                                                              │
  │     ┌─────┼──────────────────┐                                          │
  │     │     │                  │                                          │
  │     ▼     ▼                  ▼                                          │
  │  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐                      │
  │  │ DEV      │  │ STAGING      │  │ PRODUCTION   │                      │
  │  │          │  │              │  │              │                      │
  │  │ ConfigMap│  │ ConfigMap    │  │ ConfigMap    │                      │
  │  │ DEBUG=   │  │ DEBUG=       │  │ DEBUG=       │                      │
  │  │  True    │  │  True        │  │  False       │                      │
  │  │ DB_HOST= │  │ DB_HOST=     │  │ DB_HOST=     │                      │
  │  │  localhost│  │  staging-db  │  │  prod-db     │                      │
  │  │ REPLICAS=│  │ REPLICAS=    │  │ REPLICAS=    │                      │
  │  │  1       │  │  2           │  │  5           │                      │
  │  │ LOG=     │  │ LOG=         │  │ LOG=         │                      │
  │  │  verbose │  │  info        │  │  warning     │                      │
  │  └──────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘                      │
  │                                                                          │
  │  No image rebuild needed! Just change the ConfigMap and restart pods.   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div>
                        <h5>Create from Literals</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create configmap easypj-config --from-literal=DEBUG=False --from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres</code> — quick key-value pairs, great for simple settings.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📄</div>
                        <h5>Create from File</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create configmap nginx-conf --from-file=nginx.conf</code> — entire file becomes a key (filename) with file contents as value. Perfect for config files.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Create from Env File</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create configmap app-env --from-env-file=.env</code> — reads a <code>.env</code> file and creates one key per line. Great for migrating from Docker Compose.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>Create from YAML</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml</code> — full control over keys and values using declarative YAML. Best for version-controlled configs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧩</div>
                        <h5>Generate via Kustomize</h5>
                        <p><code>configMapGenerator:</code> in <code>kustomization.yaml</code> — GitOps-native, auto-appends a content hash to the name (<code>easypj-config-2f9k4t</code>) so every edit forces a rolling update. See Chapter 39's Kustomize coverage for the full pattern.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-configmap.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ConfigMap for easypj Django settings</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj-production
data:
  <span class="comment"># Simple key-value pairs → injected as env vars</span>
  DEBUG: "False"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*.easypj.com,localhost"
  DB_HOST: "postgres.easypj-production.svc.cluster.local"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  DB_NAME: "easypj_db"
  STATIC_URL: "/static/"
  MEDIA_URL: "/media/"

  <span class="comment"># Multi-line value → best mounted as a file</span>
  settings.ini: |
    [database]
    engine = django.db.backends.postgresql
    host = postgres
    port = 5432
    name = easypj_db

    [cache]
    backend = django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache
    location = redis://redis:6379/0

    [email]
    backend = django.core.mail.backends.smtp.EmailBackend
    host = smtp.easypj.com
    port = 587</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Two Ways to Inject ConfigMap Data into Pods</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  METHOD 1: Environment Variables        METHOD 2: Mounted Files          │
  │  (envFrom / env)                        (volumeMounts)                   │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ConfigMap: easypj-config               ConfigMap: easypj-config         │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐                ┌─────────────────────┐          │
  │  │ DEBUG=False          │                │ settings.ini: |     │          │
  │  │ DB_HOST=postgres     │                │   [database]        │          │
  │  │ DB_PORT=5432         │                │   engine=postgresql │          │
  │  └─────────┬───────────┘                └─────────┬───────────┘          │
  │            │                                      │                      │
  │            ▼                                      ▼                      │
  │  Pod sees env vars:                     Pod sees files:                  │
  │  $ echo $DEBUG                          $ ls /etc/config/                │
  │  False                                  settings.ini                     │
  │  $ echo $DB_HOST                        $ cat /etc/config/settings.ini   │
  │  postgres                               [database]                       │
  │                                         engine=postgresql                │
  │  ✅ Simple, fast access                 ✅ Complex configs, auto-update  │
  │  ❌ Requires pod restart to update      ✅ Updates without pod restart   │
  │  ❌ All keys become env vars            ✅ Selective file mounting       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 A Third, Less-Common Injection Path: <code>command</code>/<code>args</code></h5>
                    <p>Beyond env vars and volume mounts, a container's <code>command</code>/<code>args</code> can reference an already-injected env var using shell-style <code>$(VAR_NAME)</code> interpolation — Kubernetes substitutes it before the container starts. This only works for values ALREADY pulled in via <code>env</code>/<code>envFrom</code> first; you can't reference a ConfigMap key directly in <code>command</code>, only a resulting env var.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">env:
- name: DB_HOST
  valueFrom: { configMapKeyRef: { name: easypj-config, key: DB_HOST } }
command: ["python", "manage.py", "migrate", "--database-host=$(DB_HOST)"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deployment-with-configmap.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj Deployment using ConfigMap — both env vars AND mounted files</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000

        <span class="comment"># ── METHOD 1: All keys as env vars ──</span>
        envFrom:
        - configMapRef:
            name: easypj-config       # ◀── all keys become env vars
                                       <span class="comment">#     DEBUG, DB_HOST, DB_PORT, etc.</span>

        <span class="comment"># ── METHOD 1b: Selective env vars ──</span>
        env:
        - name: DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE
          value: easypj.settings       # ◀── hardcoded value
        - name: DATABASE_HOST           # ◀── rename the key
          valueFrom:
            configMapKeyRef:
              name: easypj-config
              key: DB_HOST              # ◀── specific key from ConfigMap

        <span class="comment"># ── METHOD 2: Mount as files ──</span>
        volumeMounts:
        - name: config-volume
          mountPath: /etc/easypj/config  # ◀── files appear here
          readOnly: true

      volumes:
      - name: config-volume
        configMap:
          name: easypj-config
          items:                         # ◀── selective: only mount these keys
          - key: settings.ini
            path: settings.ini           # ◀── /etc/easypj/config/settings.ini</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 ConfigMap Updates and Pod Behavior</h5>
                    <p>When you update a ConfigMap, <strong>volume-mounted files update automatically</strong> (within ~60 seconds, or on kubelet sync period). However, <strong>environment variables do NOT update</strong> — the pod must be restarted. This is a key distinction for the CKA exam. If you need live config reloads, use volume mounts.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 <code>data</code> vs <code>binaryData</code>, and Key-Naming Rules</h5>
                    <p>A ConfigMap actually has TWO data fields, not one: <code>data</code> holds UTF-8 string values (everything shown so far), while <code>binaryData</code> holds base64-encoded binary content — small certs, images, or any non-UTF-8 payload. A key may exist in EITHER field, never both (a key present in both is a validation error). Key names across both fields follow the same rule: alphanumeric characters plus <code>-</code>, <code>_</code>, and <code>.</code> only — the same DNS-subdomain-like constraint applied to the ConfigMap's own <code>metadata.name</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Why <code>DEBUG: "False"</code> Is Always Quoted — and the Django Bug It's Preventing</h5>
                    <p>Every numeric-looking or boolean-looking ConfigMap value in this chapter is deliberately quoted (<code>"False"</code>, <code>"5432"</code>). Unquoted YAML coerces <code>False</code>/<code>5432</code> into real YAML boolean/integer types — but <code>ConfigMap.data</code> only accepts STRINGS, so an unquoted value fails validation outright (<code>data[DEBUG]: Invalid value: ...: must be of type string</code>).</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># BROKEN — unquoted, apiserver rejects this ConfigMap entirely:</span>
data:
  DEBUG: False        <span class="comment"># ← YAML boolean, not a string — validation error</span>

<span class="comment"># FIXED — quoted, accepted:</span>
data:
  DEBUG: "False"       <span class="comment"># ← the STRING "False", exactly four characters</span></code></pre>
                    <p>Even once quoted correctly, a SECOND, subtler bug is common with the easypj Django app specifically: environment variables are always strings, and Python's <code>bool("False")</code> evaluates to <code>True</code> — any non-empty string is truthy! A naive <code>DEBUG = bool(os.environ.get('DEBUG'))</code> in Django's settings.py silently leaves debug mode ON in production. The fix is an explicit string comparison: <code>DEBUG = os.environ.get('DEBUG') == 'True'</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 23.2: Secrets — Sensitive Configuration -->
            <!-- ─────────── §23.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.2 Secrets — Sensitive Configuration</h3>
                <div class="chapter-meta" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                    <span class="badge badge-important">CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-warning">SECURITY</span>
                </div>
                <p>Secrets are the companion to ConfigMaps — they store <strong>sensitive</strong> data like passwords, API keys, and TLS certificates. While structurally similar to ConfigMaps, Secrets have additional protections and CKA exam-specific nuances you must understand.</p>

                <!-- ConfigMap vs Secret Comparison -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>📄 ConfigMap</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Non-sensitive config</li>
                                <li><strong>Storage:</strong> Plain text in etcd</li>
                                <li><strong>Data field:</strong> <code>data:</code> (plain text)</li>
                                <li><strong>Example:</strong> DB_HOST, LOG_LEVEL</li>
                                <li><strong>Safe to log:</strong> ✅ Yes</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>🔒 Secret</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Sensitive data</li>
                                <li><strong>Storage:</strong> Base64 in etcd (not encrypted by default!)</li>
                                <li><strong>Data field:</strong> <code>data:</code> (base64) or <code>stringData:</code> (plain)</li>
                                <li><strong>Example:</strong> DB_PASSWORD, SECRET_KEY</li>
                                <li><strong>Safe to log:</strong> ❌ Never</li>
                            </ul>
