                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 stringData vs data</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>stringData:</code> when writing YAML by hand — it accepts plain text and Kubernetes base64-encodes it automatically. Use <code>data:</code> when you already have base64-encoded values (e.g., from <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code>). On the CKA exam, <code>stringData:</code> saves time and prevents encoding mistakes.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Putting Plaintext Under <code>data:</code> Doesn't Just "Work Anyway" — It FAILS</h5>
                    <p>A common first-time mistake: writing <code>data: {DB_PASSWORD: postgres123}</code> expecting Kubernetes to "figure it out." It won't — <code>data:</code> values MUST already be base64. The API server rejects the apply outright with a decoding error. If you want to hand-write plain text, you must use <code>stringData:</code> instead; there is no auto-detection between the two.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f bad-secret.yaml
<span class="comment"># error: unable to decode "bad-secret.yaml":</span>
<span class="comment"># json: cannot unmarshal string into Go struct field .data of type []uint8</span></code></pre>
                </div>

