            <!-- Section 23.3: ConfigMap and Secret Best Practices -->
            <!-- ─────────── §23.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.3 ConfigMap and Secret Best Practices</h3>
                <div class="chapter-meta" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                    <span class="badge badge-concept">BEST PRACTICES</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
                <p>Real-world Kubernetes security depends on following strict practices around configuration management. These rules apply equally to the CKA exam and production clusters.</p>

                <!-- DO / DON'T Cards -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>✅ DO — Best Practices</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>ConfigMaps</strong> for non-sensitive config (DB_HOST, LOG_LEVEL, feature flags)</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>Secrets</strong> for sensitive data (passwords, API keys, TLS certs)</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <code>envFrom</code> for <strong>bulk injection</strong> — fewer lines, fewer mistakes</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>volume mounts</strong> when config is a complete file (nginx.conf, settings.ini)</li>
                                <li>✅ Enable <strong>encryption at rest</strong> for Secrets (<code>EncryptionConfiguration</code>)</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>RBAC</strong> to restrict who can <code>get</code>/<code>list</code> Secrets (full RBAC mechanics in Chapter 24)</li>
                                <li>✅ Set <code>readOnly: true</code> on Secret volume mounts</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <code>stringData:</code> in YAML for readability</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <code>immutable: true</code> for configs that should never change</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>❌ DON'T — Common Mistakes</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never commit Secrets to Git</strong> — use sealed-secrets or external vaults</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never hardcode secrets in Dockerfiles</strong> — they persist in image layers</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never log secret values</strong> — use placeholder text in logs</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never use ConfigMaps for passwords</strong> — they have no access restrictions</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never share Secrets across namespaces</strong> without good reason</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never give broad Secret access</strong> — <code>get secrets *</code> is dangerous</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never skip RBAC</strong> for Secret access — default allows too much</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never assume base64 = encrypted</strong> — it's just encoding</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never store large files in Secrets</strong> — 1MB limit per Secret</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Encryption at Rest Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔐 Secret Security Layers — Defense in Depth</h4>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Layer 1: RBAC (WHO can access)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Role: secret-reader                                         │
  │    rules:                                                    │
  │    - apiGroups: [""]                                         │
  │      resources: ["secrets"]                                  │
  │      verbs: ["get"]          ◀── only get, not list/watch   │
  │      resourceNames: ["easypj-secrets"]  ◀── specific secret │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Layer 2: Encryption at Rest (HOW it's stored in etcd)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Without encryption:  Secret → base64 → etcd (readable!)    │
  │  With encryption:     Secret → base64 → AES-* → etcd ✅    │
  │  (prefer aesgcm/KMS over aescbc below — see the warning box) │
  │                                                              │
  │  Configure via: /etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml                 │
  │  API server flag: --encryption-provider-config               │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Layer 3: Network (HOW it's transmitted)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  API server ←──TLS──→ kubelet ←──tmpfs──→ Pod              │
  │  Secrets mounted as tmpfs (memory) — never written to disk  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 RBAC on Secrets Isn't the Whole Story — the Pod-Create Escalation Path</h5>
                    <p>Layer 1's <code>verbs: ["get"]</code> restriction only stops someone from directly running <code>kubectl get secret</code>. It does NOT stop a much more common escalation: <strong>anyone who can CREATE a Pod in the namespace can reference that Secret in the Pod spec and read its value from inside the running container</strong> — even if their own RBAC role has zero <code>get</code>/<code>list</code>/<code>watch</code> permission on Secrets directly. Locking down <code>secrets</code> RBAC while leaving broad <code>pods</code>/<code>create</code> permission in the same namespace is a false sense of security.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Permission granted</th><th>Can they read the Secret's value?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>get</code> on the Secret directly</td><td>✅ Obviously yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>list</code>/<code>watch</code> on Secrets</td><td>✅ Yes — a list/watch response includes full object data, not just names</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>ONLY <code>create</code> on Pods, zero Secret RBAC at all</td><td>✅ Still yes — mount the Secret in a Pod spec, then <code>kubectl exec</code> in and read it</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>Real mitigations: use separate namespaces to isolate who can even create pods near sensitive Secrets; in a multi-container pod, wire the Secret's <code>volumeMount</code>/<code>env</code> reference only to the specific container that needs it (sibling containers in the same pod get zero access unless explicitly wired); and treat "who can create Pods here" as equally security-critical as "who can read Secrets here" — they are, in practice, the same permission.</p>
                </div>

