                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔐 Enabling Encryption at Rest for Secrets (CKA Advanced)</h5>
                    <p>By default, Secrets are stored <strong>unencrypted</strong> in etcd. To encrypt them, you configure the API server with an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> file. This is a key topic for cluster hardening — see Chapter 11's full etcd/backup coverage for how encryption-at-rest interacts with cluster backup and restore.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml — Encryption at Rest</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Encryption configuration for Secrets at rest in etcd</span>
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
  - resources:
    - secrets                             # ◀── only encrypt Secrets
    providers:
    - aescbc:                             # ◀── AES-CBC — official docs now mark this "Not recommended"
        keys:                              #     (vulnerable to padding-oracle attacks); shown here for
        - name: key1                       #     exam familiarity — prefer aesgcm or a KMS provider below
          secret: dGhpcyBpcyBhIDMyIGJ5dGUga2V5  # ◀── base64 of a 32-byte key
    - identity: {}                        # ◀── fallback: read unencrypted Secrets

<span class="comment"># API server flag:</span>
<span class="comment"># --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># After enabling, re-encrypt existing Secrets:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ aescbc Is No Longer the Recommended Provider</h5>
                    <p><code>aescbc</code> is real and functional — and still the most commonly taught example, which is why it's shown above — but current official guidance marks it "Not recommended" because it's vulnerable to padding-oracle attacks. Production clusters should prefer <code>aesgcm</code> (a stronger authenticated-encryption provider), or better yet, a <strong>KMS provider</strong> (<code>kind: KMSConfiguration</code>) that delegates key management to an external system like AWS KMS, GCP Cloud KMS, or HashiCorp Vault — so the encryption key itself never lives in a file on the control-plane node at all.</p>
                </div>

