            <h5>① Beyond the Basics — envFrom Prefixes, Kubelet Caching Strategies, and Safe Rotation</h5>
            <p>A handful of lesser-known knobs round out real-world ConfigMap/Secret operation: namespacing imported keys, how the kubelet actually decides which nodes see a Secret, and how teams rotate credentials without hand-editing YAML.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   envFrom PREFIX — namespacing imported keys           KUBELET SECRET DELIVERY STRATEGY
   ────────────────────────────────────────────         ──────────────────────────────────
   envFrom:                                              Watch  → kubelet opens a watch on
   - configMapRef:                                                every referenced secret;
       name: easypj-config                                       near-instant updates, higher
     prefix: DJANGO_    ◀── every key gets this                  API-server load
             prefix
                                                          Cache  → kubelet uses a TTL-based
   easypj-config key: DB_HOST                                     local cache; bounded staleness,
        │                                                         much lower API load
        ▼
   container env var: DJANGO_DB_HOST                     Get    → kubelet fetches on-demand at
                                                                   pod start only; simplest,
   (avoids collisions when two ConfigMaps                        least reactive to live changes
    both define DB_HOST)
   ────────────────────────────────────────────         only nodes running a pod that actually
                                                          references the secret ever receive it
   ROTATING A SECRET SAFELY (external-secrets / sealed-secrets pattern)

   External vault (Vault / AWS SM)  ──sync──▶  Kubernetes Secret  ──consumed by──▶  Pod
        │                                            │
        │ operator reconciles on a schedule           │ kubectl rollout restart
        ▼                                            ▼
   value rotated at the SOURCE               new pods pick up the new value;
   of truth, never hand-edited in            old pods keep serving on the old
   cluster YAML                              value until restarted
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Size limit</td><td>~1 MiB per object (an etcd value-size limit); use <code>binaryData</code> for non-UTF-8 content such as small binary certs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Extra Secret types</td><td><code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code> (imagePullSecrets), <code>service-account-token</code>, and bootstrap tokens — beyond the common <code>Opaque</code> and <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>envFrom</code> prefix</td><td>Adds a string in front of every imported key name, avoiding collisions when multiple ConfigMaps are merged into one container's env</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Kubelet delivery strategy</td><td>Configurable per-cluster via the KubeletConfiguration field <code>configMapAndSecretChangeDetectionStrategy</code>: Watch (real-time, costlier), Cache (TTL-bounded, cheaper), or Get (on-demand at pod start only)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Rotation at scale</td><td>Sealed-secrets or an External Secrets Operator reconciles cluster Secrets from an external vault; pair with <code>kubectl rollout restart</code> so env-var consumers pick up the new value</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> the exam itself rarely tests <code>prefix:</code> or caching strategy directly, but real clusters lean on both — <code>prefix:</code> to merge multiple ConfigMaps without key collisions, and a Cache/Get strategy on large clusters to keep the API server from being overwhelmed by thousands of nodes watching thousands of secrets.</div>

            <h5>② Disabling ServiceAccount Token Auto-Mount — Least Privilege for Pods That Never Call the API</h5>
            <p>A fact most guides skip entirely: EVERY pod, by default, automatically gets a ServiceAccount token mounted into it — even a pod that never makes a single Kubernetes API call. That's a real, avoidable attack surface for anything that doesn't need it (a static nginx server, a batch job that only talks to a database).</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   DEFAULT BEHAVIOR — every pod gets a token, whether it needs one or not

   Pod created (no explicit setting)
        │
        ▼
   kubelet auto-mounts a ServiceAccount token at:
   /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
        │
        ▼
   Any process (or attacker who compromises the container) inside the pod
   can now authenticate to the API server AS that pod's ServiceAccount
   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   DISABLING IT — two levels, either works, ServiceAccount-level is the better default

   # Level 1: at the ServiceAccount (affects EVERY pod using it — best default)
   apiVersion: v1
   kind: ServiceAccount
   metadata:
     name: easypj-web-sa
   automountServiceAccountToken: false      # ◀── no pod using this SA gets a token

   # Level 2: at the individual Pod (overrides the ServiceAccount's own setting)
   apiVersion: v1
   kind: Pod
   spec:
     serviceAccountName: easypj-web-sa
     automountServiceAccountToken: true     # ◀── Pod-level setting WINS over SA-level
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Setting location</th><th>Scope</th><th>Precedence</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>ServiceAccount</td><td>Every pod using that ServiceAccount</td><td>Default, overridden by a Pod-level setting if one exists</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Pod</td><td>Just that one pod</td><td>WINS if explicitly set — can turn auto-mount back on even if the SA disables it, or vice versa</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> a genuinely good production default is <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on every ServiceAccount that doesn't specifically need to call the Kubernetes API, then explicitly opting individual pods back IN at the Pod level only where required — least privilege by default, not by exception.</div>

            <h5>③ The Modern Bound Service Account Token — Why the Old Secret-Based Token Is "Legacy"</h5>
            <p>Earlier in this chapter the Secret-types table labeled <code>kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code> as "legacy." Here's what replaced it, and why the replacement is meaningfully safer.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   OLD MECHANISM (pre-1.24, "legacy")            NEW MECHANISM (current default)
   ──────────────────────────────────            ───────────────────────────────
   Every ServiceAccount auto-generates            Kubelet requests a token on-demand
   a long-lived Secret containing a               from the API server's TokenRequest
   NEVER-EXPIRING token                           API, then injects it via a
                                                   `serviceAccountToken` PROJECTED
   ┌─────────────────────────────┐                VOLUME (see Chapter 21 UTH ②'s
   │ Secret: easypj-sa-token-xyz │                projected-volumes example)
   │ token: eyJhbGci... (forever)│
   └─────────────────────────────┘                ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                                                    │ token: eyJhbGci...           │
   Problems:                                       │ expirationSeconds: 3600      │
   • Leaks are permanent — a stolen                │ audience: [api]              │
     token works FOREVER                           │ auto-rotated by kubelet      │
   • Not scoped to any specific                    └─────────────────────────────┘
     audience — usable anywhere
   • Clutters `kubectl get secrets`                Benefits:
     with one Secret per ServiceAccount            • Short-lived (default 1hr) — a
                                                      leaked token expires fast
                                                    • AUDIENCE-scoped — a token minted
                                                      for Vault can't be replayed
                                                      against the K8s API server
                                                    • Auto-rotates, kubelet refreshes
                                                      it before expiry transparently
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Legacy Secret token</th><th>Bound (projected) token</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Lifetime</td><td>Forever (until manually deleted)</td><td>Short-lived, default 1 hour, auto-rotated</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Audience scoping</td><td>None — usable anywhere</td><td>Scoped to a specific audience (e.g. only the API server, or only Vault)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Visible as</td><td>A separate <code>Secret</code> object</td><td>A file inside a <code>projected</code> volume — no separate object</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Static, generated once at SA creation</td><td>Dynamic, via the <code>TokenRequest</code> API</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> on modern clusters you'll rarely see a token-Secret at all in <code>kubectl get secrets</code> — that's expected, not broken. The token still gets mounted at the same familiar path (<code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code>), it just isn't backed by a long-lived Secret object anymore.</div>

            <h5>④ binaryData and the Real Size Budget — Why "1 MiB" Isn't the Whole Story for Binary Content</h5>
            <p>The 1 MiB limit quoted throughout this chapter is real, but it applies to the WHOLE serialized object as stored in etcd — and base64 encoding inflates binary data, so the effective usable space for <code>binaryData</code> content is meaningfully smaller than 1 MiB.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   THE 1 MiB LIMIT — an etcd value-size constraint, not a K8s-invented number

   Total serialized ConfigMap/Secret object (metadata + data + binaryData)
   ≤ 1 MiB (1,048,576 bytes) — this is etcd's own per-value size limit,
   Kubernetes just inherits it and enforces it at admission time

   ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   WHY binaryData EFFECTIVELY HOLDS LESS THAN 1 MiB OF REAL BINARY CONTENT

   Raw binary cert (750 KB)
        │
        ▼  base64 encode (inflates by ~33% — 4 output chars per 3 input bytes)
   Base64 string (~1000 KB / ~1 MiB)
        │
        ▼  stored in binaryData, counts toward the SAME 1 MiB object cap
   750 KB raw binary  ≈  1 MiB base64-encoded  ≈  the WHOLE budget consumed

   Rule of thumb: an object's binaryData can hold roughly
   750 KB of ACTUAL binary content before hitting the 1 MiB serialized cap
   (less once you account for any data: keys, labels, and metadata sharing
   the same object)
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Content</th><th>Effective real-world capacity</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>data</code></td><td>UTF-8 text — no encoding overhead</td><td>~1 MiB of actual text, minus metadata overhead</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>binaryData</code></td><td>base64-encoded binary — ~33% inflation</td><td>~750 KB of actual binary content, minus metadata overhead</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if you're embedding TLS certs, small binary assets, or bundled CA chains and hitting "the ConfigMap/Secret is too large" errors well before you expected, this base64-inflation math is almost always why — the fix is usually splitting into multiple objects, or switching to a CSI-driver-backed secrets store (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager via the Secrets Store CSI Driver from Chapter 21 UTH ③) that isn't bound by etcd's value-size limit at all.</div>

            <table>
                <thead><tr><th></th><th>Native K8s Secret</th><th>Secrets Store CSI Driver</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Ever stored as a K8s <code>Secret</code> object?</td><td>✅ Always</td><td>❌ Not by default — mounted directly as files, no Secret object at all</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Where the data actually lives</td><td>etcd (base64, optionally encrypted at rest)</td><td>External vault (Vault, AWS/Azure/GCP Secrets Manager) — never persisted in etcd</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Sync direction</td><td>N/A — it IS the source</td><td>One-way pull: vault → pod's mounted volume, on a poll interval</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>1 MiB etcd limit applies?</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>❌ No — bypasses etcd's value-size limit entirely</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h5>⑤ Static Pods Cannot Reference ConfigMaps or Secrets At All</h5>
            <p>One structural limitation surprises even experienced administrators: a static pod (the kubelet-managed manifests covered in Chapter 12) cannot use <code>envFrom</code>, <code>configMapKeyRef</code>, <code>secretKeyRef</code>, or ANY ConfigMap/Secret volume mount — not because of a permissions issue, but because of how static pods fundamentally work.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   NORMAL POD                                   STATIC POD
   ───────────                                  ──────────
   kubectl apply -f pod.yaml                    kubelet watches /etc/kubernetes/
        │                                       manifests/*.yaml directly on disk
        ▼
   API SERVER creates the Pod object                   │
        │                                              ▼
        ▼                                       kubelet creates a MIRROR Pod object
   kubelet resolves ConfigMapRef/SecretRef      in the API server (read-only reflection)
   by calling BACK to the API server            — but the kubelet itself NEVER calls
        │                                       back to the API server to resolve a
        ▼                                       configMapKeyRef/secretKeyRef, because
   container starts with config/secrets         static pods are designed to work even
   correctly injected                           when the API server is COMPLETELY DOWN
                                                 (that's the entire point of static pods
                                                 — they're how the control plane
                                                 bootstraps itself before an API server
                                                 even exists yet)

   RESULT: a static pod manifest referencing a ConfigMap/Secret either fails to
   start, or (depending on kubelet version) silently ignores the reference —
   there is no path by which a static pod can resolve one.
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Pod type</th><th>Can use ConfigMap/Secret?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Normal pod (via Deployment/ReplicaSet/bare Pod)</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>kubelet resolves references by calling the API server, which is always available for a normal pod's lifecycle</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Static pod (kubelet manifest)</td><td>❌ No</td><td>Static pods must be able to start even if the API server is unreachable — by design, they never depend on it for ANY field resolution</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this is exactly why control-plane components themselves (kube-apiserver, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager, etcd — all typically deployed as static pods) get their configuration via command-line flags and mounted host files/directories (<code>hostPath</code> volumes, covered in Chapter 21), never via ConfigMap/Secret. It's a hard architectural boundary, not an oversight.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>ConfigMaps hold non-sensitive config, Secrets hold sensitive data. Both inject via env vars or volume mounts, keeping configuration OUT of the image so one easypj image runs in every environment.</li>
                    <li>Three env/volume injection MECHANISMS: <code>env</code> (one key, renamed via <code>valueFrom</code>), <code>envFrom</code> (all keys become env vars), volume mount (each key becomes a file) — plus the rarer <code>command</code>/<code>args</code> <code>$(VAR)</code> interpolation path, which only works on top of an already-injected env var.</li>
                    <li>Volume-mounted keys hot-reload (~60s via an atomic symlink swap); env vars are fixed at pod start and only change on a pod restart.</li>
                    <li>Secrets are base64 <strong>encoding</strong>, NOT encryption. Anyone with read access (or etcd access) can decode them — enable encryption-at-rest and lock down RBAC on secrets. Never commit them to Git.</li>
                    <li><code>immutable: true</code> buys safety (no accidental edits) and performance (no kubelet watch) — version configs (<code>config-v2</code>) and roll forward instead of editing in place.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Secrets are NOT encrypted by default</strong> — They are only base64-encoded (not encryption!). Anyone with API access can decode them. Enable encryption at rest via <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> on the API server. The CKA tests this knowledge</li>
                    <li><strong><code>envFrom</code> vs <code>env</code></strong> — <code>envFrom</code> imports ALL keys from a ConfigMap/Secret as env vars. <code>env</code> with <code>valueFrom</code> imports one specific key. If the exam says "inject the key <code>DB_HOST</code> from configmap <code>app-config</code>", use <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Env vars do NOT auto-update</strong> — If you change a ConfigMap, pods using <code>envFrom</code> won't see the change until restarted. Volume-mounted ConfigMaps DO auto-update (within ~60s). This is a classic trick question</li>
                    <li><strong><code>immutable: true</code></strong> — Once set, the ConfigMap/Secret cannot be changed. This improves kubelet performance (stops watching for changes). You must delete and recreate to modify</li>
                    <li><strong>Secret types matter</strong> — <code>generic</code> (Opaque) for arbitrary data, <code>docker-registry</code> for image pull credentials, <code>tls</code> for TLS cert/key pairs. The exam may specify which type to create</li>
                    <li>The ConfigMap/Secret must exist BEFORE the Pod — otherwise the Pod gets stuck (<code>ContainerCreating</code> for a missing volume mount, <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code> for a missing <code>env</code>/<code>envFrom</code> reference — never <code>Pending</code>, since it already passed scheduling), unless marked <code>optional: true</code></li>
                    <li><code>data:</code> values are base64 in Secrets, but <code>stringData:</code> accepts plain text (auto-encoded)</li>
                    <li>Max ConfigMap/Secret size: <strong>1 MiB</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — ConfigMap/Secret creation cheat sheet:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-literal=key1=val1 --from-literal=key2=val2</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-file=config.properties</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-literal=password=S3cret!</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create secret tls mytls --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key</code><br>
                <strong>Decode a secret:</strong> <code>kubectl get secret mysecret -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d</code><br>
                <strong>Freeze a hot-path config:</strong> add <code>immutable: true</code> — fewer kubelet watches, no accidental edits<br>
                <strong>Force consumers to pick up new env values:</strong> <code>kubectl rollout restart deploy easypj-web</code></p>
