            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 23 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Ten conceptual questions probing WHY, not just HOW — full answer &amp; explanation each.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List the three ways to create a ConfigMap and how each source maps to keys/values.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>--from-literal=K=V</code> creates one key inline; <code>--from-file=app.conf</code> uses the filename as the key and the file's contents as the value; <code>--from-env-file=app.env</code> reads every <code>NAME=VALUE</code> line as its own key. All three produce the exact same <code>kind: ConfigMap</code> object — they're just different ways of populating <code>data:</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Resulting key(s)</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>--from-literal=DEBUG=False</code></td><td>One key: <code>DEBUG</code> = <code>"False"</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>--from-file=app.conf</code></td><td>One key: <code>app.conf</code> = entire file contents</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>--from-env-file=app.env</code></td><td>One key PER LINE in the file</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Knowing which source to reach for is a speed skill: a single setting → <code>--from-literal</code>; an existing config file (nginx.conf, settings.ini) → <code>--from-file</code>; a whole <code>.env</code> you already have from Docker Compose → <code>--from-env-file</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the practical difference between <code>envFrom</code> and <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>envFrom</code> imports EVERY key of a ConfigMap/Secret as an env var, using the key names verbatim. <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom.configMapKeyRef</code>/<code>secretKeyRef</code> pulls exactly ONE key and lets you rename it — e.g. ConfigMap key <code>DB_HOST</code> injected as env var <code>DJANGO_DB_HOST</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>envFrom</th><th>env + valueFrom</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Keys imported</td><td>ALL keys at once</td><td>ONE specific key</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Can rename?</td><td>❌ No, keeps original key name</td><td>✅ Yes, env var name is your choice</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Use <code>envFrom</code> for bulk config (fewer lines, less drift). Use <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code> when the app expects a different variable name than the key you stored it under, or when you only want one specific key exposed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You mount <code>easypj-config</code> as a volume at <code>/etc/easypj</code>. Where exactly do the keys land?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Each key becomes its own file directly under the mount path — e.g. key <code>DB_HOST</code> becomes the file <code>/etc/easypj/DB_HOST</code>, whose entire contents are the value. A multi-line key like <code>settings.ini</code> becomes <code>/etc/easypj/settings.ini</code> containing that whole block.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">$ ls /etc/easypj/
DB_HOST  DB_PORT  settings.ini

$ cat /etc/easypj/DB_HOST
postgres.easypj-production.svc.cluster.local</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is why volume mounts are the right choice for whole config files, while env vars suit single scalar settings — you can't cleanly stuff a multi-line file into one environment variable.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Is a Kubernetes Secret encrypted? Justify your answer precisely.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No, not by default. Secret values are base64-<strong>encoded</strong> and stored in etcd in the clear — encoding is reversible by anyone with the string, it provides zero confidentiality. Real protection requires two separate layers: <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> (encryption at rest in etcd) and RBAC rules restricting who can <code>get</code>/<code>list</code> the Secret object.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">$ echo -n "postgres123" | base64
cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=
$ echo "cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=" | base64 -d
postgres123        <span class="comment"># ◀── reversible in ONE command, zero secret key needed</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is one of the CKA's favorite trick questions. "Base64" and "encrypted" sound similar but are unrelated concepts — one is an encoding scheme, the other is confidentiality protection.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>immutable: true</code> actually buy you on a ConfigMap, beyond "you can't edit it"?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Two things: safety (no accidental live edits that could break running pods without warning) and a real performance win — the kubelet stops opening a watch on that object entirely, which matters at scale (thousands of ConfigMaps × thousands of nodes = a lot of watch connections saved).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Safety</td><td><code>kubectl edit</code>/<code>apply</code> updates are rejected outright</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Performance</td><td>Kubelet stops watching this object for changes — less API-server load</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Because it can't be unset, the standard pattern is versioning the NAME (<code>easypj-config-v2</code>, <code>-v3</code>) and updating the Deployment's reference, rather than trying to "unfreeze" an existing immutable object.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You update a ConfigMap that easypj-web consumes via <code>envFrom</code>. The running pods still show the old value five minutes later. Why, and what fixes it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Environment variables are baked into the container's environment block at process start and are immutable for that process's lifetime — the ConfigMap update simply never reaches an already-running container's env. Fix: <code>kubectl rollout restart deploy easypj-web</code> so new pods start with the new values. (If it had been volume-mounted instead, the files would have hot-reloaded within ~60 seconds with no restart needed.)</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create configmap easypj-config --from-literal=DEBUG=True \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
kubectl rollout restart deploy easypj-web    <span class="comment"># ◀── forces new pods, new env values</span>
kubectl rollout status deploy easypj-web</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This gap between "env vars are frozen at start" and "file mounts hot-reload" is the single most exam-relevant behavioral difference between the two injection methods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj needs to pull its image from a private registry. Which Secret type do you need, and how is it wired to the pod?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A <code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code> Secret, created with <code>kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred --docker-server=... --docker-username=... --docker-password=...</code>. It's referenced either per-pod via <code>spec.imagePullSecrets</code>, or attached to a ServiceAccount so every pod using that ServiceAccount inherits it automatically.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  imagePullSecrets:
  - name: regcred                       <span class="comment"># ◀── pod-level reference</span>
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: registry.example.com/easypj/web:v1</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Attaching to the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount in a namespace is the common production pattern — it means every new Deployment in that namespace can pull private images without re-declaring <code>imagePullSecrets</code> each time.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens if easypj-web's Deployment references a ConfigMap that doesn't exist yet?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The pod gets stuck — typically <code>ContainerCreating</code> if the missing object is mounted as a volume, or <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code> if it's referenced via <code>env</code>/<code>envFrom</code>. It will NOT crash-loop; it simply waits, and <code>kubectl describe pod</code> shows the missing-reference event. Setting <code>optional: true</code> on the reference lets the pod start anyway, treating the missing config as empty.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Reference style</th><th>Pod status when missing</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Volume mount</td><td><code>ContainerCreating</code> (waits indefinitely)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>env</code> / <code>envFrom</code></td><td><code>CreateContainerConfigError</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Either, with <code>optional: true</code></td><td>Starts normally, missing config treated as empty</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is a common CKA troubleshooting scenario: given a pod stuck not-Ready, check <code>kubectl describe pod</code> events first — a missing ConfigMap/Secret reference is one of the fastest root causes to spot there.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does <code>envFrom</code> silently drop the key <code>db.host</code> from a ConfigMap even though the key clearly exists?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>envFrom</code>-imported keys must be valid shell/environment-variable identifiers — letters, digits, and underscores only, not starting with a digit. A key containing a dot (<code>db.host</code>) or a dash isn't a legal env var name, so Kubernetes skips it silently and logs an event rather than failing the pod.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl exec easypj-web-xyz -- env | grep -i host
<span class="comment"># (nothing — "db.host" was silently skipped)</span>
kubectl describe configmap easypj-config
<span class="comment"># Data: db.host: postgres   ◀── key exists, just invalid as an env-var name</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is a real gotcha: nothing crashes, the pod just quietly doesn't have the variable you expected. Always name ConfigMap/Secret keys with env-var-safe names if you plan to consume them via <code>envFrom</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Design a minimal-disruption plan to rotate easypj's <code>DB_PASSWORD</code> Secret in production.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Update the Secret's value (directly, or via an external-secrets/sealed-secrets operator syncing from a vault), then trigger <code>kubectl rollout restart deploy easypj-web</code> so new pods pick up the value at start. If <code>DB_PASSWORD</code> were instead consumed as a mounted file (not env var), a re-reading application would hot-reload it within ~60 seconds with no restart at all.</p>
                        <pre>
   Vault/external store  ──sync──▶  K8s Secret  ──rollout restart──▶  new pods, new value
   (source of truth,                (updated in                      (old pods drain,
    rotated centrally)                place)                          zero downtime)
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The general principle: rotation always requires either a restart (env-var consumers) or an app that actively watches its mounted config files (volume consumers) — the Secret object updating in the API is necessary but not sufficient on its own.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

