                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ConfigMap named <code>app-config</code> with keys <code>DB_HOST=postgres</code> and <code>DB_PORT=5432</code> using an imperative command.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative creation (fastest for cka)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Imperative creation (fastest for CKA):</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config \
--from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres \
--from-literal=DB_PORT=5432

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get configmap app-config -o yaml
<span class="comment"># apiVersion: v1</span>
<span class="comment"># kind: ConfigMap</span>
<span class="comment"># metadata:</span>
<span class="comment">#   name: app-config</span>
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_HOST: postgres</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_PORT: "5432"     ← Note: stored as STRING</span>

<span class="comment"># Other creation methods:</span>
<span class="comment"># From file:</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-file=config.properties

<span class="comment"># From env file:</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-env-file=.env

<span class="comment"># From directory (each file becomes a key):</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-file=./configs/

<span class="comment"># Dry-run to generate YAML:</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config \
--from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres \
--dry-run=client -o yaml > configmap.yaml</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ConfigMaps store non-sensitive configuration data as key-value pairs. On the CKA, <code>--from-literal</code> is the fastest for simple key-value pairs. <code>--from-file</code> uses the filename as the key and file content as the value. <code>--from-env-file</code> reads KEY=VALUE lines from a .env file. Always use <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to generate YAML that you can modify. All values are stored as strings — even numbers like "5432".</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Secret named <code>db-creds</code> with <code>username=admin</code> and <code>password=S3cret!</code>. How do you decode the values?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create:</span>
kubectl create secret generic db-creds \
--from-literal=username=admin \
--from-literal=password='S3cret!'

<span class="comment"># View (values are base64-encoded):</span>
kubectl get secret db-creds -o yaml
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   password: UzNjcmV0IQ==</span>
<span class="comment">#   username: YWRtaW4=</span>

<span class="comment"># Decode a specific value:</span>
kubectl get secret db-creds \
-o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d
<span class="comment"># S3cret!</span>

<span class="comment"># Decode all values:</span>
kubectl get secret db-creds -o go-template='
{{range $k, $v := .data}}
{{$k}}: {{$v | base64decode}}
{{end}}'
<span class="comment"># username: admin</span>
<span class="comment"># password: S3cret!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ base64 is NOT encryption!</span>
<span class="comment"># Anyone with API access can decode secrets</span>
<span class="comment"># Enable EncryptionConfiguration for at-rest encryption</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Secrets are base64-encoded, NOT encrypted. On the CKA, decoding secrets with <code>jsonpath + base64 -d</code> is a commonly tested skill. The imperative command auto-encodes values to base64. If creating via YAML, you must base64-encode values yourself (<code>echo -n "admin" | base64</code>) or use <code>stringData</code> (plain text, auto-encoded on apply). Secret types: <code>generic</code> (Opaque), <code>docker-registry</code> (image pull), <code>tls</code> (cert/key).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod that injects ALL keys from a ConfigMap as environment variables using <code>envFrom</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: env-pod
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
envFrom:                       # ← Import ALL keys
- configMapRef:
    name: app-config           # ← All keys become env vars
- secretRef:
    name: db-creds             # ← All secret keys too

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl exec env-pod -- env | grep -E 'DB_|username|password'
<span class="comment"># DB_HOST=postgres</span>
<span class="comment"># DB_PORT=5432</span>
<span class="comment"># username=admin</span>
<span class="comment"># password=S3cret!</span>

<span class="comment"># vs. importing a SINGLE key:</span>
env:
- name: DATABASE_HOST              # ← Custom env var name
valueFrom:
configMapKeyRef:
  name: app-config
  key: DB_HOST                 # ← Specific key
- name: DB_PASS
valueFrom:
secretKeyRef:
  name: db-creds
  key: password</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>envFrom</code> imports ALL keys from a ConfigMap/Secret as environment variables. <code>env</code> with <code>valueFrom</code> imports ONE specific key with a custom name. On the CKA, know both patterns: (1) "inject all config" → use <code>envFrom</code>, (2) "inject specific key as <code>DATABASE_URL</code>" → use <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code>. Gotcha: if two sources have the same key name, the last one in the list wins. Invalid env var names (keys with dashes) are silently skipped with <code>envFrom</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Mount a ConfigMap as a volume. What happens when you update the ConfigMap?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">mount configmap as volume</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Mount ConfigMap as volume:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: config-vol-pod
spec:
volumes:
- name: config-vol
configMap:
  name: app-config
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: config-vol
  mountPath: /etc/config

<span class="comment"># Each ConfigMap key becomes a file:</span>
kubectl exec config-vol-pod -- ls /etc/config
<span class="comment"># DB_HOST  DB_PORT</span>

kubectl exec config-vol-pod -- cat /etc/config/DB_HOST
<span class="comment"># postgres</span>

<span class="comment"># Update the ConfigMap:</span>
kubectl edit configmap app-config
<span class="comment"># Change DB_HOST to "mysql"</span>

<span class="comment"># After ~60 seconds (kubelet sync period):</span>
kubectl exec config-vol-pod -- cat /etc/config/DB_HOST
<span class="comment"># mysql  ← Auto-updated!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ BUT env vars do NOT auto-update!</span>
<span class="comment"># envFrom values only change on pod restart</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ subPath mounts do NOT auto-update either!</span>
<span class="comment"># volumeMounts with subPath are frozen at pod creation</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Volume-mounted ConfigMaps auto-update within ~60 seconds (kubelet sync period). Environment variables do NOT auto-update. <code>subPath</code> mounts do NOT auto-update. On the CKA, this is a classic trick question: "How do you make your app pick up ConfigMap changes without restart?" Answer: mount as a volume (not env vars). Some apps also need to watch for file changes or receive SIGHUP to reload configuration. The auto-update uses symlinks internally — <code>/etc/config/DB_HOST</code> is actually a symlink to a timestamped directory.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>stringData</code> in a Secret YAML and how is it different from <code>data</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">using 'data' — values must be base64-encoded</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Using 'data' — values must be base64-encoded:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: my-secret
type: Opaque
data:
username: YWRtaW4=          <span class="comment"># echo -n "admin" | base64</span>
password: UzNjcmV0IQ==     <span class="comment"># echo -n "S3cret!" | base64</span>

<span class="comment"># Using 'stringData' — plain text (auto-encoded):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: my-secret
type: Opaque
stringData:                   # ← Plain text!
username: admin
password: S3cret!

<span class="comment"># Both produce the SAME Secret!</span>
<span class="comment"># stringData is write-only — after apply, API returns 'data' (base64)</span>

<span class="comment"># Mix both:</span>
data:
existing-key: YWJj
stringData:
new-key: plain text value   # ← Easier for new keys

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ stringData doesn't appear in kubectl get -o yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># After creation, everything is stored under 'data' as base64</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>stringData</code> is a convenience field that accepts plain text — Kubernetes auto-encodes to base64 on creation. <code>data</code> requires pre-encoded base64 values. On the CKA, <code>stringData</code> saves time when writing YAML manually (no need to run <code>echo -n | base64</code>). After creation, <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code> only shows <code>data</code> (base64). If both <code>data</code> and <code>stringData</code> have the same key, <code>stringData</code> wins. Use <code>stringData</code> to avoid base64 encoding mistakes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod that mounts a Secret as a volume with specific file permissions (<code>0400</code>).</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: secret-vol-pod
spec:
volumes:
- name: secret-vol
secret:
  secretName: db-creds
  defaultMode: 0400           # ← Octal: owner read-only
  <span class="comment"># Per-key permissions:</span>
  items:
  - key: password
    path: db-password         # ← Custom filename
    mode: 0400                # ← Per-file mode
  - key: username
    path: db-username
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: secret-vol
  mountPath: /etc/secrets
  readOnly: true              # ← Extra safety

<span class="comment"># Verify permissions:</span>
kubectl exec secret-vol-pod -- ls -la /etc/secrets/
<span class="comment"># -r--------  1 root root  7 Jan 1 00:00 db-password</span>
<span class="comment"># -r--------  1 root root  5 Jan 1 00:00 db-username</span>

kubectl exec secret-vol-pod -- cat /etc/secrets/db-password
<span class="comment"># S3cret!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>defaultMode</code> sets file permissions for all keys (octal format: 0644 = rw-r--r--, 0400 = r--------). Per-key <code>mode</code> overrides defaultMode for individual files. On the CKA, Secret volumes default to 0644 — which might be too permissive for passwords. Set <code>0400</code> for sensitive data. Adding <code>readOnly: true</code> on the volumeMount adds an extra layer. The <code>items</code> field controls which keys to mount and their filenames, similar to ConfigMap volumes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you make a ConfigMap immutable? What are the benefits?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
name: prod-config
data:
DB_HOST: prod-db.example.com
DB_PORT: "5432"
immutable: true                   # ← Cannot be modified after apply!

<span class="comment"># Trying to edit:</span>
kubectl edit configmap prod-config
<span class="comment"># error: configmaps "prod-config" is immutable</span>

<span class="comment"># To change: must delete and recreate</span>
kubectl delete configmap prod-config
kubectl create configmap prod-config \
--from-literal=DB_HOST=new-db.example.com

<span class="comment"># Benefits of immutable:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Protection against accidental changes</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Performance: kubelet stops watching for updates</span>
<span class="comment">#    (reduces API server load significantly)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Clear intent: "this config MUST NOT change"</span>

<span class="comment"># Works for Secrets too:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: prod-secret
immutable: true
stringData:
API_KEY: my-key-that-never-changes</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>immutable: true</code> prevents any modification. The main benefit is performance: kubelet stops watching for changes, reducing API server load (significant with many ConfigMaps). Once set, you CANNOT unset it — you must delete and recreate. On the CKA, this might appear as: "Ensure this ConfigMap cannot be accidentally modified." Apply <code>immutable: true</code>. Works for both ConfigMaps and Secrets. Note: pods mounting an immutable ConfigMap must be restarted after recreating the ConfigMap.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>docker-registry</code> secret type and how do you use it to pull images from a private registry?</div>
