                            <p>The <code>docker-registry</code> secret type stores Docker credentials in <code>.dockerconfigjson</code> format. Use it with <code>imagePullSecrets</code> in the pod spec or attach to a ServiceAccount for automatic injection. On the CKA, if a pod fails with <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> from a private registry, create a <code>docker-registry</code> secret and reference it. Attaching to the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount affects all pods in the namespace that don't specify a different SA.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod can't access its ConfigMap values. The ConfigMap exists and the pod is running. How do you troubleshoot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check 1: is the configmap in the same namespace?</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check 1: Is the ConfigMap in the same namespace?</span>
kubectl get configmap app-config -n production
<span class="comment"># Error: not found ← ConfigMap is in wrong namespace!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 2: Pod spec references correct name?</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o yaml | grep -A5 configMapRef
<span class="comment">#   configMapRef:</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: app-configg  ← Typo! Extra 'g'</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 3: env vs envFrom?</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- env | grep DB_HOST
<span class="comment"># (empty) ← Env var not set!</span>

<span class="comment"># Possible causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - envFrom only imports keys that are valid env var names</span>
<span class="comment">#   Key "my-config.key" with dot → SKIPPED silently!</span>
<span class="comment"># - env with key that doesn't exist → pod won't start</span>
<span class="comment">#   (pod stays ContainerCreating with error event)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 4: Volume mount present?</span>
kubectl describe pod my-pod | grep -A5 Mounts
kubectl exec my-pod -- ls /etc/config/
<span class="comment"># ls: /etc/config/: No such file or directory ← Mount missing!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 5: ConfigMap has the right keys?</span>
kubectl describe configmap app-config
<span class="comment"># Data</span>
<span class="comment"># ====</span>
<span class="comment"># db_host:   ← lowercase!</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod expects DB_HOST ← case mismatch!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ConfigMap troubleshooting checklist: (1) same namespace? (2) name spelled correctly? (3) key names valid for env vars? (4) volume mount correct? (5) key case match? On the CKA, the most common issues are namespace mismatch (ConfigMap in different ns than pod) and typos in names/keys. If using <code>envFrom</code>, keys with special characters are silently skipped. If using <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code> with a non-existent key, the pod fails to start — check Events with <code>describe</code>.</p>
