        <section class="part-cover" id="part7">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 7 · Security</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Lock It Down</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 24–27 · RBAC · PKI · <em>"Least privilege, end to end"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">A cluster anyone can do anything on is a breach waiting to happen. Part 7 is the access-control core: RBAC (Roles/Bindings) that grant least privilege, ServiceAccounts and projected tokens that identify workloads, the cluster PKI and certificate workflow that authenticates everyone, and Pod Security + admission control that constrain what pods may do.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> write Roles/ClusterRoles and bind them to users and ServiceAccounts; test access with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>; read the cluster PKI and sign a CertificateSigningRequest; and enforce Pod Security Standards via admission.</div>
            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🏢 Real-World Analogy: The Office Building</h5>
                <p>Every request that reaches the API server runs the same three-stage gauntlet an employee runs walking into a corporate office building. First, <strong>authentication</strong> is the photo badge scanned at the front turnstile — it proves you are who you claim to be (a signed TLS client cert for a human, or a projected ServiceAccount token for a workload like the easypj-web pod). Second, <strong>authorization</strong> is the access-control list wired into each individual door — your badge is genuine, but that alone doesn't open the server-room door; RBAC's Roles and RoleBindings are the list of exactly which doors your badge is keyed to. Third, <strong>admission control</strong> is the security guard standing at the door who can still stop you — badge valid, door on your list, but the guard checks you're not carrying something against house rules (no running with scissors — or in easypj's case, no container running as root); that's Pod Security Admission enforcing the namespace's baseline. Fail any one stage and you're turned away: a bad badge is a <code>401 Unauthorized</code>, a badge that doesn't open that door is a <code>403 Forbidden</code>, and a guard's refusal is an admission-webhook rejection.</p>
<pre>  OFFICE BUILDING                                  KUBERNETES SECURITY
  ────────────────                                  ────────────────────
  🪪 Photo badge scanned at the turnstile ────────▶ Ch 26 — TLS/PKI: certs prove human identity (AuthN)
  🤖 Contractor's temporary access card ──────────▶ Ch 25 — ServiceAccounts: pod/workload identity (AuthN)
  📋 Access-control list wired to each door ──────▶ Ch 24 — RBAC: Roles/RoleBindings decide what opens (AuthZ)
  💂 Guard at the door enforcing house rules ─────▶ Ch 27 — Pod Security Admission: what's let through the door</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Once I'm authenticated to the cluster, I can do anything unless something explicitly blocks me."</td>
                            <td>RBAC is <strong>deny-by-default</strong> — the opposite assumption. A valid badge (cert or token) gets you past the turnstile, but every door is locked until a Role/ClusterRole plus a matching RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding explicitly opens it. There are no "deny" rules in RBAC either — permissions are a pure union of every rule that applies to you; you can only add access, never subtract it.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"The <code>default</code> ServiceAccount in a namespace is a harmless placeholder — nothing uses it unless I say so."</td>
                            <td>Every pod that doesn't explicitly request a ServiceAccount is automatically issued the namespace's <code>default</code> SA, and its token is auto-mounted into the container's filesystem unless <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> is set. Pair that default SA with an over-permissive RoleBinding and every unlabeled pod in the namespace — including a compromised one — inherits a working contractor badge it never asked for.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Adding <code>securityContext: {runAsNonRoot: true}</code> to my Pod spec is what makes my pod \"secure.\""</td>
                            <td>That field is a request the guard can still overrule. <strong>Pod Security Admission</strong> enforces at the <em>namespace</em> level via <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce</code> labels (privileged/baseline/restricted) — if the namespace is labeled <code>restricted</code> and the Pod spec is missing required fields (or a sidecar tries to run privileged), the guard rejects the Pod at admission time regardless of what any individual container's <code>securityContext</code> claims. The namespace label is the house rule; the Pod spec is just one employee's paperwork.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
