            <!-- ─────────── §24.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.1 What is RBAC and Why It Matters</h3>
                <p>RBAC answers one question: <strong>"Is this user allowed to do this action on this resource?"</strong> Without RBAC, anyone who can reach the Kubernetes API server can create, delete, or modify anything — pods, secrets, nodes, the entire cluster.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Without RBAC vs With RBAC — The Security Gate</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ WITHOUT RBAC (or permissive RBAC)         ✅ WITH PROPER RBAC            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────┐                          ┌──────────────┐                 │
  │  │ 👨‍💻 Developer  │──┐                      │ 👨‍💻 Developer  │──┐              │
  │  └──────────────┘  │                      └──────────────┘  │              │
  │  ┌──────────────┐  │   ┌──────────┐       ┌──────────────┐  │  ┌────────┐ │
  │  │ 🤖 CI/CD Bot  │──┼──▶│ API      │       │ 🤖 CI/CD Bot  │──┼─▶│ RBAC   │ │
  │  └──────────────┘  │   │ Server   │       └──────────────┘  │  │ Gate   │ │
  │  ┌──────────────┐  │   │          │       ┌──────────────┐  │  │        │ │
  │  │ 🧪 Intern    │──┘   │ FULL     │       │ 🧪 Intern    │──┘  │ ✅ pods │ │
  │  └──────────────┘      │ ACCESS   │       └──────────────┘     │ ❌ nodes│ │
  │                         │ TO       │                            │ ❌ sec. │ │
  │  Everyone can:         │ EVERY-   │       Only allowed          │        │ │
  │  • Delete nodes 💀    │ THING    │       actions pass →       └───┬────┘ │
  │  • Read secrets 🔓    │          │                               │      │
  │  • Modify RBAC 😱     │          │                          ┌────▼─────┐ │
  │  • Break cluster 💣   └──────────┘                          │ API      │ │
  │                                                              │ Server   │ │
  │                                                              └──────────┘ │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Real-World Disaster Without RBAC</h5>
                    <p>In 2018, Tesla's Kubernetes dashboard was left open without RBAC. Attackers accessed the cluster, found AWS credentials in environment variables, and used Tesla's infrastructure for cryptocurrency mining. <strong>RBAC would have prevented this entirely.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The Three Questions RBAC Answers</h5>
                    <p>Every API request is checked against these three dimensions:</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👤</div>
                        <h5>WHO?</h5>
                        <p><strong>Subject</strong> — User, Group, or ServiceAccount making the request</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>WHAT?</h5>
                        <p><strong>Verb</strong> — The action: get, list, create, update, delete, watch, patch</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>WHICH?</h5>
                        <p><strong>Resource</strong> — pods, services, secrets, nodes, deployments, etc.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Request Pipeline — Where RBAC Sits, and What It Produces</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   kubectl / pod ──▶ [ AuthN ] ──▶ [ AuthZ (RBAC) ] ──▶ [ Admission ] ──▶  │
  │                        WHO?            WHAT?              mutate/         │
  │                         │                │                validate       │
  │                    cert / token      RBAC rules       webhooks /          │
  │                     / SA JWT        (this chapter)     quotas    ──▶ etcd │
  │                                                                          │
  │   Fail AuthN  ──▶  401 Unauthorized  ("I don't know who you are")       │
  │   Fail AuthZ  ──▶  403 Forbidden     ("I know who you are, but you      │
  │                                        may not do that.") ◀── RBAC says  │
  │                                        this — every 403 you see on the   │
  │                                        exam or in production traces      │
  │                                        back to a missing/wrong binding.  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 401 vs 403 — Read the Status Code Before You Touch RBAC</h5>
                    <p>These two errors point to completely different fixes, and confusing them wastes exam time. <strong>401 Unauthorized</strong> means authentication itself failed — an expired/invalid client cert, a bad or expired bearer token, a typo'd kubeconfig context. RBAC was never even consulted. Editing Roles or RoleBindings will not fix a 401. <strong>403 Forbidden</strong> means the API server knows exactly who you are and RBAC explicitly declined to authorize the request — this is where you check Roles, RoleBindings, and run <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>. If a pod's logs show <code>Unauthorized</code>, look at the ServiceAccount token/mount, not the Role.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 KEY TERM — "Deny by Default"</h5>
                    <p>If no binding grants a subject a given verb on a given resource, the answer is simply <strong>"no"</strong>. RBAC has <strong>zero deny rules</strong> — you can never write a rule that subtracts a permission. Your <em>effective</em> access is always the plain <strong>union</strong> of every RoleBinding and ClusterRoleBinding that applies to you (directly, through a Group, or through <code>system:authenticated</code>). This is why RBAC troubleshooting is additive-only: if a subject can do something it shouldn't, the fix is always to find and remove/narrow the binding that grants it — you cannot "cancel it out" with a second, more restrictive binding.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
