            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How RBAC Authorization Is Evaluated: Role/ClusterRole Resolution, Binding Matching, and the can-i Algorithm</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ RBAC Evaluation Algorithm — How the API Server Decides ALLOW/DENY</h5>
            <p>Every API request passes through the RBAC authorizer. Understanding the exact evaluation order helps you debug "why can't this user do X" and craft correct policies.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  RBAC AUTHORIZATION PIPELINE — Request Evaluation                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Request: User "jane" wants to GET /api/v1/namespaces/dev/pods               │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: Extract Subject Identity                                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ From TLS cert: CN=jane, O=developers, O=viewers                      │   │
│  │ OR from JWT token: sub=jane, groups=[developers, viewers]            │   │
│  │ → Subject: User=jane, Groups=[developers, viewers]                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: Build ALL Potential Roles (union of ALL bindings)                   │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Find RoleBindings matching jane OR [developers] OR [viewers]:         │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RoleBinding: pod-reader (namespace: dev)                               │   │
│  │   subjects: [{kind:User, name:jane}]  <span class="comment">← Direct match!                 </span>│   │
│  │   roleRef: {kind:Role, name:pod-reader}                               │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RoleBinding: view-ns (namespace: dev)                                  │   │
│  │   subjects: [{kind:Group, name:viewers}] <span class="comment">← Group match!               </span>│   │
│  │   roleRef: {kind:ClusterRole, name:view}                              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ → Collected roles: [pod-reader (Role), view (ClusterRole)]            │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: Check EACH Role for Matching Rules (UNION of all rules)             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Role: pod-reader (namespace: dev):                                     │   │
│  │   rules:                                                               │   │
│  │   - apiGroups: [""]     <span class="comment">← core API group (v1)                        </span>│   │
│  │     resources: ["pods"]  <span class="comment">← Match! (request is for pods)              </span>│   │
│  │     verbs: ["get","list","watch"] <span class="comment">← Match! (request verb is "get")   </span>│   │
│  │   → RULE MATCHED! Pod reader allows get pods in dev.                  │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ ClusterRole: view:                                                     │   │
│  │   rules:                                                               │   │
│  │   - apiGroups: [""]                                                    │   │
│  │     resources: ["pods","services","deployments",...]                 │   │
│  │     verbs: ["get","list","watch"]                                    │   │
│  │   → RULE ALSO MATCHED (but redundant — pod-reader already covers it) │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 4: FINAL DECISION                                                      │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ At least one matching rule found → ALLOW ✅                           │   │
│  │ No matching rules found → DENY ❌                                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RBAC IS ADDITIVE ONLY:                                                 │   │
│  │   ✅ Roles ADD permissions (union of all matching rules)              │   │
│  │   ❌ There is NO "deny" rule in RBAC                                 │   │
│  │   ❌ You cannot "subtract" a permission once granted                  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CLUSTERROLE vs ROLE — SCOPE MATTERS:                                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Rule:     apiGroups: [""]                                             │   │
│  │           resources: ["nodes"]                                        │   │
│  │           verbs: ["get"]                                              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ In a ROLE (namespaced):        In a CLUSTERROLE (cluster-wide):       │   │
│  │ → Can get pods in dev          → Can get pods in ALL namespaces      │   │
│  │ → Can get services in dev      → Can get nodes (cluster-scoped)       │   │
│  │ → CANNOT get nodes!             → Can get namespaces (cluster-scoped) │   │
│  │   (nodes are cluster-scoped)                                           │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ A ClusterRole can be bound via:                                        │   │
│  │   RoleBinding → grants ClusterRole permissions in ONE namespace       │   │
│  │   ClusterRoleBinding → grants ClusterRole permissions CLUSTER-WIDE    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  IMPERSONATION — Testing RBAC (CKA Superpower):                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=jane -n dev             │   │
│  │ yes  /  no                                                            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ $ kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n dev                          │   │
│  │ Resources  Non-Resource URLs  Resource Names  Verbs                   │   │
│  │ pods       []                 []              [get list watch]        │   │
│  │ configmaps []                 []              [get]                   │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> RBAC is a guaranteed CKA topic. The exam tests creating Roles + RoleBindings for specific scenarios. Key tips: (1) RBAC is additive only — no deny rules, (2) ClusterRole + RoleBinding = namespaced permissions, (3) ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding = cluster-wide, (4) Always test with <code>kubectl auth can-i --as=&lt;user&gt;</code> before submitting your answer, (5) RoleBinding subjects support User, Group, and ServiceAccount.
            </div>

            <h5>① How the Authorizer Evaluates Rules — the exact match/no-match algorithm</h5>
            <p>The walkthrough above traced one request end-to-end. Now formalize the rule itself: what, precisely, counts as a "match"? A single RBAC rule has up to four dimensions, and <strong>every one that's present must match</strong> for the rule to fire. Miss any dimension and the rule silently contributes nothing — no error, just no match.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   RULE:                                    REQUEST:
   ┌────────────────────────────┐          ┌────────────────────────────┐
   │ apiGroups:  [""]             │          │ apiGroup:  ""                │
   │ resources:  ["pods"]         │   ══▶    │ resource:  pods              │
   │ verbs:      ["get","list"]   │  match?  │ verb:      get                │
   │ resourceNames: (none)        │          │ name:      easypj-web-6d8f9   │
   └────────────────────────────┘          └────────────────────────────┘
        ALL FOUR must match (resourceNames only checked if the rule sets it)
                    │
        ┌───────────┴────────────┐
        ▼                        ▼
   ✅ every dimension        ❌ any one dimension
      matches → rule FIRES      differs → rule is a NO-OP
      (this rule contributes)   (try the next rule/role — still may ALLOW)
</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>What it filters</th><th>Common miss</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>apiGroups</code></td><td>Which API group the resource belongs to</td><td>Using <code>[""]</code> for Deployments (they're in <code>"apps"</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>resources</code></td><td>The resource type, and optionally its subresource</td><td>Granting <code>pods</code> but forgetting <code>pods/log</code> for logs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>verbs</code></td><td>The action: get/list/watch/create/update/patch/delete</td><td>Granting <code>get</code> but not <code>list</code> — <code>kubectl get pods</code> (list) then fails</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>resourceNames</code> (optional)</td><td>Restricts to specific named objects</td><td>Trying to use it with <code>list</code>/<code>create</code> — not supported, silently ignored by those verbs</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A rule is a filter, not a grant of "everything unless stated."</strong> If a dimension is present in the rule, the request must match it exactly (or via wildcard <code>"*"</code>). This is why "I gave them pods but exec still fails" is the #1 RBAC support ticket — <code>pods/exec</code> is a DIFFERENT resource string, not covered by <code>pods</code>.</div>

            <h5>② The Authorization Chain &amp; Order — Node, RBAC, and optional Webhook</h5>
            <p><code>--authorization-mode</code> on the API server is a comma-separated CHAIN of authorizer modules, almost always <code>Node,RBAC</code>. Modules are tried strictly in order, and the <strong>first module to return an explicit decision wins</strong> — the chain stops there. RBAC is just one link.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC        (the default on kubeadm clusters)

   Request arrives (already authenticated)
        │
        ▼
   ┌─────────────────┐   is subject a node (system:node:&lt;name&gt;)?
   │ 1️⃣ Node authorizer   │   restricts kubelets to READ only their own pods,
   │                      │   services, endpoints, and secrets/configmaps they
   │                      │   mount — even a compromised kubelet can't read
   └──────────┬───────────┘   another node's Secrets.
              │ not a node request → fall through
              ▼
   ┌─────────────────┐   evaluate ALL bound Roles/ClusterRoles
   │ 2️⃣ RBAC authorizer   │   (this whole chapter) → ALLOW if any rule matches,
   │                      │   otherwise NO OPINION (falls through, doesn't deny!)
   └──────────┬───────────┘
              │ optional — appended by cloud/enterprise setups
              ▼
   ┌─────────────────┐   delegates the decision to an external HTTP(S)
   │ 3️⃣ Webhook authorizer│   service (e.g. OPA/Gatekeeper, custom policy engine)
   └──────────┬───────────┘
              ▼
   No module explicitly ALLOWed  ──▶  final result: 403 Forbidden</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Scope</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Node</td><td>Kubelet identities only (<code>system:node:*</code> in group <code>system:nodes</code>)</td><td>Limits kubelet blast radius — always keep it first</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>RBAC</td><td>Everyone else — users, groups, ServiceAccounts</td><td>This chapter — Roles/ClusterRoles/Bindings</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Webhook</td><td>Any subject, delegates the call externally</td><td>OPA Gatekeeper, custom compliance engines, enterprise IAM bridges</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A module that finds no matching rule does NOT deny — it abstains.</strong> Only after EVERY module in the chain abstains does the API server return 403. This is why a Webhook authorizer appended after RBAC can still grant access RBAC alone wouldn't — the chain is permissive-first, deny-last.</div>

            <p><code>Node,RBAC</code> is only the chain a kubeadm cluster ships with — <code>--authorization-mode</code> is a broader menu, and knowing the FULL list keeps you from misreading an exam or interview question that names a mode you've never touched.</p>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>What it does</th><th>Where you'll meet it</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>Node</code></td><td>Restricts kubelets to their own pods/objects</td><td>Always first in the default chain</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>RBAC</code></td><td>Role/ClusterRole + Binding evaluation</td><td>This chapter — the mode nearly every cluster runs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Webhook</code></td><td>Delegates the allow/deny decision to an external HTTP(S) service</td><td>OPA/Gatekeeper, managed-cloud IAM bridges, enterprise policy engines</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>ABAC</code> (Attribute-Based Access Control)</td><td>Static, file-based policy JSON matching request attributes</td><td>Legacy — largely superseded by RBAC; you may see it named on the exam but should never need to author ABAC policy</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>AlwaysAllow</code></td><td>Approves every request unconditionally</td><td>Local dev/test clusters ONLY — never production, it disables authorization entirely</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>AlwaysDeny</code></td><td>Rejects every request unconditionally</td><td>Testing/break-glass lockdown only</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 "Access is denied by default" is the umbrella rule above RBAC itself.</strong> Every part of a request must be explicitly allowed by SOME configured authorizer or it's rejected — RBAC's own "deny by default" behavior is really just this same principle applied inside one specific module.</div>

            <h5>③ The <code>system:*</code> Built-in ClusterRoles and <code>system:masters</code></h5>
            <p>Kubernetes ships with a set of pre-created ClusterRoles so you rarely start from a blank rule set. Four are meant for humans; dozens more prefixed <code>system:</code> exist for control-plane components (kube-scheduler, kube-proxy, etc.) — never edit those.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   BUILT-IN "USER-FACING" CLUSTERROLES   (kubectl get clusterroles | grep -v system)

   cluster-admin ───────▶ verbs: ["*"]  resources: ["*"]  apiGroups: ["*"]
                           TRUE superuser — bypasses nothing, grants EVERYTHING

   admin ────────────▶ full read-write INSIDE a namespace via RoleBinding,
                           INCLUDING managing Roles/RoleBindings in that ns
                           (but NOT ResourceQuotas or the Namespace object itself)

   edit ───────────▶ read-write on most objects (pods, deployments, secrets)
                           but CANNOT view/edit Roles or RoleBindings

   view ───────────▶ read-only on most objects; explicitly EXCLUDES Secrets
                           (a viewer can't read Secret VALUES, only that they exist)

   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   system:masters — THE RBAC BYPASS
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ Any certificate whose Subject includes  O=system:masters            │
   │ is authorized for EVERYTHING — RBAC AND Webhook authorization are   │
   │ bypassed entirely, not just RBAC. This is how kubeadm's admin.conf  │
   │ works.                                                               │
   │ ⚠️ Never add a human's long-lived cert to this group in production. │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>ClusterRole</th><th>Can edit RBAC objects?</th><th>Can read Secrets?</th><th>Typical subject</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>cluster-admin</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Break-glass ops account only</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>admin</code></td><td>Yes (in-namespace)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Namespace owner / team lead</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>edit</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Developers who deploy but shouldn't grant access</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>view</code></td><td>No</td><td>No (existence only)</td><td>QA, auditors, monitoring dashboards</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Reuse before you reinvent.</strong> On the exam, "give jane admin rights in namespace dev" is <code>kubectl create rolebinding jane-admin --clusterrole=admin --user=jane -n dev</code> — zero custom YAML. Know the four tiers cold: view &lt; edit &lt; admin &lt; cluster-admin.</div>

            <p>Two more built-in objects matter even though nobody binds them manually: they define what a totally fresh identity — authenticated OR not — can already do before you write a single Role.</p>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Default ClusterRole</th><th>Grants</th><th>Bound (by default) to</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>system:basic-user</code></td><td><code>get</code> on <code>selfsubjectaccessreviews</code> and <code>selfsubjectrulesreviews</code> — i.e. the right to ask "what can I do?"</td><td>Group <code>system:authenticated</code> — every logged-in identity, human or ServiceAccount</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>system:discovery</code></td><td><code>get</code> on the API discovery endpoints (<code>/api</code>, <code>/apis</code>, <code>/version</code>, etc.) — enough to know what the cluster CAN do, not to touch anything in it</td><td>Group <code>system:unauthenticated</code> — even anonymous, unauthenticated callers get this much</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Kubernetes self-heals its own default RBAC objects.</strong> The built-in ClusterRoles/ClusterRoleBindings (<code>cluster-admin</code>, <code>admin</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>view</code>, all the <code>system:*</code> ones) are reconciled by the API server on every startup: if one is deleted or its rules are edited down, the control plane restores the shipped defaults automatically. You cannot permanently delete or weaken them by editing in place — to truly remove a default's effect you must stop binding subjects to it, not try to edit the object itself. Look for the <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/autoupdate: "true"</code> annotation on these objects — it's what triggers the auto-reconciliation.</div>

            <h5>④ Aggregated ClusterRoles — how <code>view</code>/<code>edit</code> automatically grow</h5>
            <p>How does installing a CRD operator make its custom resources show up under the built-in <code>view</code>/<code>edit</code> roles without anyone editing them? <strong>ClusterRole aggregation</strong>: a ClusterRole can declare label SELECTORS instead of (or in addition to) explicit rules, and the API server continuously merges in the rules of any other ClusterRole carrying a matching label.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
   kind: ClusterRole
   metadata:
     name: easypj-jobpost-viewer
     labels:
       rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-view: "true"   ◀── the magic label
   rules:
   - apiGroups: ["jobpost.easypj.io"]
     resources: ["jobposts"]
     verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]

           │  the API server's ClusterRoleAggregation controller watches for
           │  ANY ClusterRole with this label, and merges its rules INTO
           ▼  the built-in "view" ClusterRole automatically:

   ClusterRole "view"  (built-in)
     aggregationRule:
       clusterRoleSelectors:
       - matchLabels: {rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-view: "true"}
     rules:  [ ...original view rules... , ...easypj-jobpost-viewer's rules... ]
                                             ▲
                                   MERGED IN — no edit to "view" needed!</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Built-in aggregation label</th><th>Merges into</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-view</code></td><td><code>view</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-edit</code></td><td><code>edit</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-admin</code></td><td><code>admin</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is exactly how CRD operators extend the default roles.</strong> A Helm chart for a CRD-based operator (cert-manager, Prometheus Operator) typically ships a small ClusterRole with an aggregation label — install the chart, and suddenly <code>view</code>/<code>edit</code> users can see the new custom resources with zero manual RBAC work.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Subresources, Non-Resource URLs &amp; the Dangerous Verbs</h5>
            <p>Not everything RBAC governs is a plain resource. Subresources let you split "read the object" from "read/act on its child endpoint"; non-resource URLs cover bare HTTP paths like <code>/healthz</code>; and three verbs exist purely to control privilege escalation itself.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   SUBRESOURCES — separate permission from the parent resource
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ resources: ["pods"]           → get/list/watch/edit the Pod OBJECT  │
   │ resources: ["pods/log"]       → kubectl logs   (needs verb: get)    │
   │ resources: ["pods/exec"]      → kubectl exec   (needs verb: CREATE!)│
   │ resources: ["pods/portforward"] → kubectl port-forward (create)     │
   │ resources: ["deployments/scale"] → kubectl scale (get + update)     │
   │ resources: ["*/status"]       → status subresource of ANY resource  │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   NON-RESOURCE URLS — only valid inside a ClusterRole (never namespaced)
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ - nonResourceURLs: ["/healthz", "/metrics", "/livez", "/readyz"]     │
   │   verbs: ["get"]              ◀── verbs are limited to get/head     │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   THE THREE PRIVILEGE-ESCALATION VERBS — guardrails on RBAC itself
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ escalate  → create a Role/ClusterRole with MORE rules than you hold │
   │ bind      → create a *Binding referencing a role you don't fully   │
   │             possess yourself                                       │
   │ impersonate → act AS another user/group/ServiceAccount (--as)      │
   │                                                                     │
   │ Default: API server BLOCKS you from granting perms you lack,       │
   │ UNLESS your own bindings include escalate/bind on rbac resources.  │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Resource string</th><th>Verb</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>kubectl logs</code></td><td><code>pods/log</code></td><td><code>get</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>kubectl exec</code></td><td><code>pods/exec</code></td><td><code>create</code> (NOT get!)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>kubectl scale deploy</code></td><td><code>deployments/scale</code></td><td><code>get</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Prometheus scraping <code>/metrics</code></td><td>N/A — <code>nonResourceURLs</code></td><td><code>get</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 "I gave them pods, why can't they exec?"</strong> because <code>pods/exec</code> is a distinct resource string using the <code>create</code> verb, not <code>get</code> — the single most common RBAC subresource trap on the exam and in production.</div>

            <p>The three escalation-control verbs each have a specific, narrow trigger condition — knowing exactly when they're checked (not just that they exist) is what separates "I've heard of escalate" from being able to explain a real permission-denied error involving RBAC objects themselves.</p>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>Exactly when the API server checks it</th><th>How to legitimately bypass the check</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>escalate</code></td><td>You try to <code>create</code>/<code>update</code> a Role or ClusterRole containing rules you do not currently hold yourself</td><td>Hold explicit <code>escalate</code> verb on <code>roles</code>/<code>clusterroles</code> in <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code> — <strong>or</strong> be able to impersonate a ServiceAccount in the target namespace that already has those rules</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>bind</code></td><td>You try to <code>create</code>/<code>update</code> a RoleBinding or ClusterRoleBinding that references a Role/ClusterRole granting more than you hold</td><td>Hold explicit <code>bind</code> verb on <code>rolebindings</code>/<code>clusterrolebindings</code> — <strong>or</strong> already personally hold every rule the referenced role grants</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>impersonate</code></td><td>You use <code>--as</code>/<code>--as-group</code>/<code>--as-uid</code> on any request</td><td>Hold <code>impersonate</code> on the <code>users</code>/<code>groups</code>/<code>serviceaccounts</code> resources (<code>cluster-admin</code> has this by default)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The default is "you cannot grant what you don't have."</strong> Even a subject with broad <code>create</code>/<code>update</code> permission on Roles and RoleBindings still cannot mint itself <code>cluster-admin</code> by writing a new Role — the API server independently diffs the new rules against the caller's own effective permissions and rejects anything that exceeds them, unless that caller separately holds <code>escalate</code>/<code>bind</code>. This is the guardrail that stops "give me edit on Roles" from silently becoming full cluster compromise.</div>

            <h5>⑥ Auditing &amp; Impersonation — validating a grant before you hand it out</h5>
            <p><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> isn't just a yes/no checker — with <code>--as</code>/<code>--as-group</code> and <code>--list</code> it becomes a full impersonation and audit tool, letting you validate a binding's effect WITHOUT ever having that subject's real credentials.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   $ kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa -n easypj

   Resources          Non-Resource URLs   Resource Names   Verbs
   configmaps          []                  [easypj-config]  [get list watch]
   pods                []                  []               [get list watch]
   pods/log            []                  []               [get]
                              ▲
                  the FULL effective permission set — the union of
                  every Role/ClusterRole bound (directly or via group)
                  to this ServiceAccount, resolved without logging in as it

   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  SelfSubjectAccessReview  (what "can-i" calls under the hood)     │
   │  SelfSubjectRulesReview   (what "can-i --list" calls under the hood)│
   │  Both are just API objects you POST — can-i is a thin CLI wrapper │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Command form</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>auth can-i VERB RESOURCE</code></td><td>What can I (my current kubeconfig) do?</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=user</code></td><td>What can this USER do? (impersonation)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as-group=grp</code></td><td>What can any member of this GROUP do?</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>auth can-i --list --as=...</code></td><td>Dump the ENTIRE effective permission set</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Impersonation requires its own RBAC permission.</strong> To use <code>--as</code> at all, YOUR identity needs <code>verb: impersonate</code> on the <code>users</code>/<code>groups</code>/<code>serviceaccounts</code> resources — cluster-admin has this by default, which is why <code>--as</code> "just works" for admins testing other people's access.</div>

            <h5>⑦ Production RBAC Hardening — the Default ServiceAccount Trap, Reconciling, and Reverse Audits</h5>
            <p>The exam tests whether you can WRITE RBAC. Running a real cluster tests whether you can keep it from silently rotting into "everyone can do everything." Three habits catch the vast majority of real-world RBAC incidents.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   ⚠️ THE #1 REAL-WORLD RBAC INCIDENT: the "default" ServiceAccount

   Every namespace gets an auto-created ServiceAccount named "default".
   Every Pod that doesn't set spec.serviceAccountName runs AS that SA.
   Most teams never touch it — which is fine, UNTIL someone does this:

   $ kubectl create clusterrolebinding oops \
       --clusterrole=cluster-admin \
       --serviceaccount=easypj:default        <span class="comment">◀── "just to test something quickly"</span>

        │
        ▼
   Now EVERY pod in easypj namespace without an explicit serviceAccountName
   — including ones deployed months later by people who never knew this
   binding exists — is cluster-admin. A single compromised pod (RCE in a
   dependency, an exposed debug endpoint) is now a full cluster takeover.

   AUDIT FOR THIS SPECIFICALLY:
   $ kubectl get clusterrolebinding,rolebinding -A -o json | \
       jq -r '.items[] | select(.subjects[]? | .name=="default") |
              "\(.metadata.name) → \(.roleRef.name)"'
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Habit</th><th>Command</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Audit the <code>default</code> SA</td><td><code>kubectl get rolebinding,clusterrolebinding -A -o wide | grep ':default'</code></td><td>Catches the single most common real-world privilege-escalation bug</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Reverse-lookup "who can X"</td><td><code>kubectl who-can delete secrets -n easypj</code> (krew plugin <code>who-can</code>)</td><td><code>auth can-i</code> answers "can THIS subject"; <code>who-can</code> answers "which subjects can" — the audit direction you actually need</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Update without clobbering</td><td><code>kubectl auth reconcile -f role.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl apply</code> on a Role REPLACES rules wholesale; <code>reconcile</code> merges/adds and warns before removing, safer for shared RBAC files</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Give every workload its own named ServiceAccount.</strong> The fix for the default-SA trap isn't "never bind anything to default" (you can't prevent that mistake retroactively) — it's giving each Deployment its own <code>serviceAccountName</code> (e.g. <code>easypj-web-sa</code>) with only the Role it needs, and setting <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on the <code>default</code> SA itself so an accidental binding to it has no token to exploit. See Chapter 25 for the full ServiceAccount lifecycle.</div>

            <h5>⑧ RBAC's Place Among Kubernetes' Access-Control Layers</h5>
            <p>RBAC is frequently blamed for problems it cannot cause. "The pod can't reach the database" and "the pod got evicted for running as root" are NOT RBAC problems — they're enforced by two entirely different subsystems that this guide covers in nearby chapters. Knowing the boundary saves enormous debugging time.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   A SINGLE REQUEST PASSES THROUGH INDEPENDENT, UNRELATED GATES

   kubectl exec -it easypj-web-xyz -- curl postgres:5432
        │
        ▼
   ① RBAC (this chapter)          — is THIS user/SA allowed to call
        │  kube-apiserver             pods/exec on THIS pod at all?
        │  authorizer chain           "Forbidden" = RBAC said no
        ▼
   ② Pod Security Admission       — was the POD ITSELF allowed to be
        │  (Ch 27, admission-time)    created with these security settings?
        │  already decided at            (checked ONCE, at pod creation —
        │  pod-create time, not          not re-checked on every exec/API call)
        │  re-checked here
        ▼
   ③ NetworkPolicy                — can the pod's OUTBOUND packet
        │  (Ch 20, CNI-enforced,       actually reach postgres:5432
        │  every packet, always)       over the network?
        ▼
   Connection succeeds ONLY if ALL THREE independently say yes —
   passing ① does not exempt you from ② or ③, and a symptom that
   "smells like RBAC" (a hang, a refused connection) is very often ③.
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Governs</th><th>Enforced by</th><th>Covered in</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>RBAC</strong> (this chapter)</td><td>Calls TO the Kubernetes API — <code>kubectl</code>, controllers, ServiceAccount tokens</td><td>kube-apiserver authorizer chain</td><td>Chapter 24 (here)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>NetworkPolicy</strong></td><td>Network traffic BETWEEN pods/namespaces — pod-to-pod, pod-to-external</td><td>CNI plugin (Calico, Cilium…), NOT the API server</td><td>Chapter 20</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Pod Security Admission</strong></td><td>WHAT a pod spec is allowed to request — privileged mode, hostPath, root user</td><td>Admission controller at pod-create time</td><td>Chapter 27</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ServiceAccount identity</strong></td><td>WHICH identity a pod presents when it calls the API — the subject RBAC evaluates</td><td>kubelet (token projection) + kube-apiserver (authN)</td><td>Chapter 25</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 "Forbidden" is RBAC. Connection timeouts and refused connections are NetworkPolicy. "container has runAsNonRoot and image will run as root" is Pod Security Admission.</strong> A ServiceAccount with a perfect RBAC grant can still be network-isolated by a NetworkPolicy, and a workload with full RBAC access can still be rejected at admission time for its security context — these layers are independent and ALL must be satisfied, not just RBAC.</div>

            <h5>⑨ What the Authorizer Actually Evaluates — Request Attributes &amp; the HTTP-to-Verb Mapping</h5>
            <p>Every authorization decision — RBAC or otherwise — is a check against a fixed set of REQUEST ATTRIBUTES the API server extracts from the incoming call. Knowing this list explains oddities like why an HTTP <code>GET</code> can require either <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, or <code>watch</code> depending on what's being fetched.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   ATTRIBUTES EVERY AUTHORIZER (Node, RBAC, Webhook…) RECEIVES
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ user        → the authenticated username                          │
   │ group       → every group the user belongs to                     │
   │ extra       → arbitrary key/value data from the authenticator     │
   │ API / path  → whether it's an API call, or a raw path (/healthz)  │
   │ verb        → get/list/watch/create/update/patch/delete/...       │
   │ namespace   → which namespace the object lives in (if any)        │
   │ apiGroup    → "" (core), apps, batch, rbac.authorization.k8s.io…  │
   │ resource    → pods, deployments, secrets...                       │
   │ subresource → log, exec, status, scale...                         │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   Every RBAC rule you write (apiGroups/resources/verbs/resourceNames)
   is simply a filter over a SUBSET of these same attributes.</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Raw HTTP verb</th><th>Resulting request verb</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>POST</code></td><td><code>create</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>GET</code> / <code>HEAD</code> (single object)</td><td><code>get</code></td><td>Fetching one named object</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>GET</code> / <code>HEAD</code> (collection)</td><td><code>list</code></td><td>No object name in the URL — fetching all of them</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>GET</code> with <code>?watch=true</code></td><td><code>watch</code></td><td>Long-lived streaming connection</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>PUT</code></td><td><code>update</code></td><td>Full-object replace</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>PATCH</code></td><td><code>patch</code></td><td>Partial update — a DIFFERENT verb than update, in case you scope rules that tight</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>DELETE</code> (single object)</td><td><code>delete</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>DELETE</code> (collection)</td><td><code>deletecollection</code></td><td>Bulk delete of every matching object — a SEPARATE verb from <code>delete</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A single "read" gesture in kubectl can compile to three different verbs.</strong> This is exactly why granting <code>get</code> alone still leaves <code>kubectl get pods</code> (no name = a LIST call) Forbidden, and why the dimension table earlier in this Under-the-Hood flagged "granting get but not list" as the #1 miss — the request verb your rule needs depends on whether the caller names one object or asks for the whole collection.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>RBAC is authorization, not authentication</strong> — it runs AFTER the API server already knows WHO you are, and it answers WHAT you may do. It denies by default: there are NO deny rules, and your effective access is simply the UNION of every binding that applies to you.</li>
                    <li><strong>Four objects, two jobs</strong> — Role and ClusterRole hold PERMISSIONS (<code>apiGroups</code> + <code>resources</code> + <code>verbs</code>); RoleBinding and ClusterRoleBinding GRANT those permissions to subjects (User, Group, or ServiceAccount).</li>
                    <li><strong>The scope matrix</strong> — Role+RoleBinding stays in one namespace; ClusterRole+ClusterRoleBinding spans the whole cluster; ClusterRole+RoleBinding reuses a cluster-defined permission set but scopes it to one namespace. Role+ClusterRoleBinding is invalid — a RoleBinding's roleRef can never point at a Role from a ClusterRoleBinding.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cluster-scoped resources need a ClusterRole</strong> — nodes, PersistentVolumes, and namespaces are not namespaced, so a Role literally cannot express them. Any exam task mentioning "nodes" or "PVs" is a ClusterRole tell.</li>
                    <li><strong>The core API group is <code>""</code></strong> (empty string) — pods, services, configmaps, secrets. Named groups (<code>apps</code>, <code>batch</code>, <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code>) must be spelled out explicitly, or the rule silently matches nothing.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>roleRef</code> is immutable</strong> — once a binding is created you cannot repoint it at a different role. To change which role a binding grants, delete and recreate the binding (subjects, however, CAN be added/removed freely).</li>
                    <li><strong>Always verify with <code>kubectl auth can-i ... --as=&lt;subject&gt;</code></strong> — this is the single most exam-relevant command in the whole chapter, and <code>--list</code> dumps the complete effective permission set for fast debugging.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <p class="skeleton-note">The "RBAC Exam Traps" card at the top of this chapter already covered the fundamentals (ClusterRoleBinding-can't-reference-a-Role, apiGroups, verbs, SA subject format, <code>can-i</code>). The list below adds the deeper misconceptions that trip people up once they're past the basics.</p>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>Role vs ClusterRole</strong> — <code>Role</code> is namespace-scoped (pods, services). <code>ClusterRole</code> is cluster-wide (nodes, PVs, namespaces). If the exam says "allow listing nodes", you MUST use ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding</li>
                        <li><strong>RoleBinding can reference a ClusterRole</strong> — This is powerful and confusing. A RoleBinding in namespace <code>prod</code> pointing to a ClusterRole limits those permissions to <code>prod</code> only. The exam tests this</li>
                        <li><strong>Verbs are comma-separated strings, not arrays in CLI</strong> — <code>--verb=get,list,watch</code> NOT <code>--verb=[get,list,watch]</code>. In YAML they ARE arrays: <code>["get", "list"]</code></li>
                        <li><strong><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> is your verification tool</strong> — Always verify after creating RBAC: <code>kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=jane -n dev</code>. The <code>--as</code> flag impersonates a user</li>
                        <li><strong>API groups matter</strong> — Core resources (pods, services) use <code>apiGroups: [""]</code>. Deployments use <code>apiGroups: ["apps"]</code>. Forgetting the API group = silent permission denial</li>
                        <li><strong>Wildcards quietly grant far more than intended</strong> — <code>resources: ["*"]</code> or <code>verbs: ["*"]</code> looks like a shortcut but silently includes every future resource type/verb added to that API group too. Prefer explicit least privilege; reserve <code>"*"</code> for genuine break-glass roles like <code>cluster-admin</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Wrong subject kind/name = silent no-op, not an error</strong> — a RoleBinding with <code>kind: User</code> when the real caller is a ServiceAccount (or a typo'd/missing <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:</code> prefix) creates successfully and grants nothing. <code>kubectl describe rolebinding</code> to eyeball the subject block is the fastest sanity check.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

