            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 24 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (13)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the RBAC mental model — 13 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Role allowing read of pods in the <code>easypj</code> namespace and bind it to a ServiceAccount named <code>sa</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create role pod-reader -n easypj \
  --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods

kubectl create rolebinding rb -n easypj \
  --role=pod-reader --serviceaccount=easypj:sa</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The Role's rule is <code>apiGroups: [""]</code>, <code>resources: ["pods"]</code>, <code>verbs: ["get","list","watch"]</code>. The RoleBinding's <code>roleRef</code> points at that Role, and its subject is <code>kind: ServiceAccount</code> with the SA's name and namespace. Both objects live in <code>easypj</code> — Roles and RoleBindings are always namespaced.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to grant read access to <strong>nodes</strong>. Role or ClusterRole? Why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>ClusterRole</strong> — nodes are cluster-scoped, so a namespaced Role literally cannot express them. Grant it cluster-wide with a ClusterRoleBinding.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Scoped?</th><th>Required object</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>pods, services, configmaps</td><td>Namespaced</td><td>Role (or ClusterRole via RoleBinding)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>nodes, PersistentVolumes, namespaces</td><td>Cluster-scoped</td><td>ClusterRole (must use ClusterRoleBinding for cluster-wide grant)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A Role's rules only ever apply within the namespace the Role itself lives in — which is meaningless for an object type that has no namespace. <code>kubectl create clusterrole node-viewer --verb=get,list,watch --resource=nodes</code> is the fast path.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Bind the built-in <code>view</code> ClusterRole to ServiceAccount <code>easypj:web</code> in ONE namespace only.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">clusterrole + rolebinding</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create rolebinding web-view -n easypj \
  --clusterrole=view --serviceaccount=easypj:web</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A <strong>RoleBinding</strong> (namespaced) whose <code>roleRef</code> is the ClusterRole <code>view</code> scopes that ClusterRole's cluster-wide-defined permissions down to just the RoleBinding's own namespace. This reuses one built-in ClusterRole across as many namespaces as needed, instead of duplicating a Role in each one.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you test what a ServiceAccount can do?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">auth can-i</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:ns:name -n ns

kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:ns:name -n ns</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>--as</code> flag impersonates any subject, including a ServiceAccount — always spelled <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>. The single-verb form gives yes/no; <code>--list</code> gives the entire effective permission set in one shot.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Restrict a Role to a single ConfigMap named <code>easypj-config</code> instead of all ConfigMaps.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">role-scoped-configmap.yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-role
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["configmaps"]
  resourceNames: ["easypj-config"]   <span class="comment"># scope to ONE object</span>
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Add <code>resourceNames</code> to the rule. Note the limitation: <code>list</code> and <code>watch</code> cannot truly be name-restricted (they always return everything the OTHER dimensions allow) — only <code>get</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code>, and <code>delete</code> genuinely enforce the name filter.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the empty-string <code>apiGroup</code> for, and which resources live there?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>""</code> is the <strong>core</strong> API group — the original, ungrouped Kubernetes resources.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>apiGroups value</th><th>Resources</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>""</code> (core)</td><td>pods, services, configmaps, secrets, nodes, namespaces, PVs, PVCs, endpoints</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>"apps"</code></td><td>deployments, replicasets, statefulsets, daemonsets</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>"rbac.authorization.k8s.io"</code></td><td>roles, rolebindings, clusterroles, clusterrolebindings</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Named groups (<code>apps</code>, <code>batch</code>, <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code>, <code>networking.k8s.io</code>) must be spelled out explicitly — there is no wildcard-by-default. Use <code>kubectl api-resources</code> to look up any resource's group.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod gets <code>Forbidden</code> calling the API. Walk through the methodical fix.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">RBAC "Forbidden" Troubleshooting Flow</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  1. Identify the pod's ServiceAccount:
     kubectl get pod POD -n NS -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
        │
        ▼
  2. See the gap:
     kubectl auth can-i --as=system:serviceaccount:NS:SA -n NS --list
        │
        ▼
  3. Add the missing Role/ClusterRole rule + Binding for the exact
     verb/resource that's missing
        │
        ▼
  4. Re-test:
     kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=system:serviceaccount:NS:SA -n NS
        │
        ▼
  5. "yes" ✅ — done. "no" ✅ — re-check apiGroup/resource/verb spelling.
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Never guess-edit RBAC. Identify the exact subject, diff its actual permissions against what's needed with <code>--list</code>, patch only the gap, and re-verify with <code>can-i</code> before considering it fixed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is an aggregated ClusterRole?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A ClusterRole carrying an <code>aggregationRule</code> with label selectors; Kubernetes automatically MERGES the rules of any other ClusterRole bearing a matching label into it.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">aggregationrule</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">aggregationRule:
  clusterRoleSelectors:
  - matchLabels:
      rbac.example.com/aggregate-to-monitoring: "true"</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is exactly how the built-in <code>edit</code> and <code>view</code> ClusterRoles get extended by CRD operators — the operator's chart ships a small labeled ClusterRole and its resource permissions appear in <code>view</code>/<code>edit</code> automatically, with zero edits to the built-ins.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Grant a permission to everyone in group <code>easypj-devs</code> without creating per-person RBAC objects.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">rolebinding-group.yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">subjects:
- kind: Group
  name: easypj-devs
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-developer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A binding with subject <code>kind: Group</code> matches the group claim from the user's authentication (from the identity provider or cert). Add/remove people just by changing their IdP group membership — no new RBAC objects per person, which is least-privilege at scale.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you safely test a binding's effect before handing credentials to a real person?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">impersonation</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE \
  --as=USER --as-group=GROUP -n NS</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Impersonation via <code>--as</code>/<code>--as-group</code> confirms the effective permissions of any subject without ever logging in as them. This requires YOUR own identity to hold the <code>impersonate</code> verb on <code>users</code>/<code>groups</code>/<code>serviceaccounts</code> — cluster-admin has it by default.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A client gets a <code>401</code> on one call and a <code>403</code> on another. Distinguish them, and which one RBAC produces.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Fixed by</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>401 Unauthorized</code></td><td>"I don't know who you are" — authentication itself failed</td><td>Fix the cert/token/kubeconfig — NOT RBAC</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>403 Forbidden</code></td><td>"I know who you are, but you may not do that" — RBAC declined</td><td>Add/fix the Role or Binding, then re-verify with <code>can-i</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>RBAC only ever runs after authentication succeeds, so it can only ever produce a <strong>403</strong>. A 401 means the request never even reached the RBAC authorizer — editing Roles or RoleBindings will have zero effect on it. Diagnosing the wrong layer is the single most time-wasting RBAC mistake: always read the status code first.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A subject can <code>get</code> pods but <code>kubectl exec</code> into them still fails with <code>Forbidden</code>. What rule is missing?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">missing-exec-rule.yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods/exec"]   <span class="comment"># a DIFFERENT resource string than "pods"</span>
  verbs: ["create"]          <span class="comment"># exec opens a session — verb is create, not get!</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>pods/exec</code> is a distinct subresource from <code>pods</code>, and it uses the <code>create</code> verb because exec-ing opens a new interactive session rather than reading an existing object. Granting <code>get</code>/<code>list</code>/<code>watch</code> on <code>pods</code> never implicitly grants <code>pods/exec</code>, <code>pods/log</code>, or <code>pods/portforward</code> — each subresource must be listed and verbed explicitly.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Can you write a rule that DENIES a permission another binding already grants that subject?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>No.</strong> RBAC has no deny rules of any kind — every rule in every Role/ClusterRole only ever ADDS to a subject's effective permissions.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">why-you-cant-subtract.sh</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># jane has delete via role-A. This does NOT stop it:</span>
kubectl create role role-b -n easypj   --verb=get,list --resource=pods   <span class="comment"># adds MORE, doesn't remove delete</span>

<span class="comment"># The only real fix: find and remove/narrow the binding granting delete</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -n easypj -o wide | grep jane
kubectl delete rolebinding &lt;the-one-granting-delete&gt; -n easypj</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Effective access is the pure union of every applicable RoleBinding and ClusterRoleBinding — directly, through a Group, or through <code>system:authenticated</code>. To remove access you must find and delete or narrow the specific binding granting it; you cannot "cancel it out" with a second, more restrictive Role. This additive-only model is why over-broad bindings (wildcards, <code>cluster-admin</code> to a default ServiceAccount) are so dangerous — there is no safety net once granted.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

