            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-visual"></span>
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 24 Visual Summary — the RBAC chain, end to end</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   👤 WHO (subject)              🔗 BINDING                    📜 WHAT (rules)
   ┌────────────────────┐    ┌───────────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────┐
   │ User: jane          │    │ RoleBinding (ns)       │    │ Role (ns)            │
   │ Group: easypj-devs  │───▶│   -or-                 │───▶│   -or-               │
   │ SA: system:service  │    │ ClusterRoleBinding      │    │ ClusterRole          │
   │  account:easypj:web │    │  (cluster)              │    │  apiGroups+resources │
   └────────────────────┘    └───────────────────────┘    │  +verbs              │
                                                            └─────────────────────┘
   SCOPE MATRIX:
   Role        + RoleBinding        = one namespace only
   ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding = whole cluster (incl. cluster-scoped resources)
   ClusterRole + RoleBinding        = cluster-defined perms, reused in ONE namespace
   Role        + ClusterRoleBinding = ❌ INVALID — never works

   VERIFY IT:  kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=&lt;subject&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;
               kubectl auth can-i --list --as=&lt;subject&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;   (dump everything)

   DENY BY DEFAULT · NO deny rules exist · effective access = UNION of every binding
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> The whole of RBAC reduces to one left-to-right chain. A <strong>SUBJECT</strong> (a User, a Group, or a ServiceAccount named <code>system:serviceaccount:ns:name</code>) is connected by a <strong>BINDING</strong> (a namespaced RoleBinding or a cluster-wide ClusterRoleBinding) to a <strong>ROLE</strong> (a namespaced Role or a cluster-wide ClusterRole) that lists rules of <code>apiGroups</code> + <code>resources</code> + <code>verbs</code>.</p>
                    <p>Whether the grant is namespaced or cluster-wide depends entirely on the PAIR you choose: Role+RoleBinding stays in one namespace, ClusterRole+ClusterRoleBinding spans the whole cluster, and ClusterRole+RoleBinding reuses a cluster-defined permission set inside a single namespace. Cluster-scoped resources like nodes and PersistentVolumes always require a ClusterRole — a Role cannot express them at all.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> everything denies by default, there are NO deny rules in RBAC, and your effective access is simply the union of every binding that applies to you. You confirm any of it with <code>kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=&lt;subject&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code>, and audit the complete set with <code>--list</code>. For easypj this became a real three-team system in §24.6: developers with full namespace access, QA with read-only, and monitoring with cluster-wide read — each verified, none over-privileged.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
