            <section class="section-block" id="s25-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.1 User Accounts vs Service Accounts</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes has two types of identities, but only one is managed by the API server. Understanding this distinction is critical for RBAC and the CKA exam.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Aspect</th>
                                <th>User Account (Human)</th>
                                <th>Service Account (Pod)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Used by</strong></td><td>Humans (kubectl, dashboard)</td><td>Pods / Applications</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Created by</strong></td><td>External system (LDAP, OIDC, certs)</td><td>Kubernetes API (<code>kubectl create sa</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>Cluster-wide (no namespace)</td><td>Namespaced</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Credentials</strong></td><td>X.509 certificates / OIDC tokens</td><td>Auto-mounted projected tokens</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Managed by K8s</strong></td><td>❌ No — external to cluster</td><td>✅ Yes — API objects</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubectl create</strong></td><td>❌ Not possible</td><td>✅ <code>kubectl create sa</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>RBAC subject kind</strong></td><td><code>User</code> or <code>Group</code></td><td><code>ServiceAccount</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Group membership</strong></td><td><code>system:masters</code>, etc. (external, IdP-defined)</td><td><code>system:serviceaccounts</code> and <code>system:serviceaccounts:&lt;namespace&gt;</code> (built-in, automatic)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy — Contractor Badge vs Employee Badge</h5>
                    <p>A <strong>User</strong> is like a contractor carrying an ID badge issued by an <em>external</em> security firm — the building (the cluster) trusts the badge on sight but never printed it, never stores a copy, and can't reissue it if lost. A <strong>ServiceAccount</strong> is like an internal employee badge that the building's own HR department (the API server) prints, files in its own records, and can revoke or reissue at will. That's the whole distinction in one image: one identity is trusted-but-external, the other is owned-and-internal.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">User Account vs Service Account — Who Talks to the API Server?</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                              │
  │    HUMAN (User Account)                    POD (Service Account)             │
  │                                                                              │
  │    ┌──────────────┐                       ┌──────────────────────┐           │
  │    │  👨‍💻 kubectl   │                       │  📦 easypj-web pod   │           │
  │    │              │                       │                      │           │
  │    │  ~/.kube/    │                       │  /var/run/secrets/   │           │
  │    │  config      │                       │  kubernetes.io/      │           │
  │    │              │                       │  serviceaccount/     │           │
  │    │  🔑 x509 cert│                       │  ├── token  🎫      │           │
  │    │  or OIDC     │                       │  ├── ca.crt 📜      │           │
  │    │  token       │                       │  └── namespace 📝   │           │
  │    └──────┬───────┘                       └──────────┬───────────┘           │
  │           │                                          │                       │
  │           │     HTTPS + Certificate/Token             │     HTTPS + SA Token │
  │           │                                          │                       │
  │           └──────────────┬───────────────────────────┘                       │
  │                          ▼                                                   │
  │                 ┌────────────────┐                                           │
  │                 │  🏛️ API Server  │                                           │
  │                 │                │                                           │
  │                 │  1. Auth-N ────│── Verify identity                         │
  │                 │  2. Auth-Z ────│── Check RBAC                              │
  │                 │  3. Admit  ────│── Apply policies                          │
  │                 │  4. Persist ───│── Write to etcd                           │
  │                 └────────────────┘                                           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 Key Insight</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes does NOT manage User Accounts — there's no <code>kubectl create user</code> command. Users are created externally (certificates, LDAP, OIDC). But Service Accounts ARE Kubernetes objects that you create, list, and delete with kubectl.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.2 Default Service Account</h3>
                <p>Every namespace automatically gets a <code>default</code> Service Account. If you don't specify a <code>serviceAccountName</code> in your pod spec, it uses this one automatically.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">inspect-default-sa.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Every namespace has a "default" SA</span>
kubectl get sa -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME      SECRETS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># default   0         30d</span>

<span class="comment"># See what the default SA can do (spoiler: almost nothing)</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:default \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Check which SA a running pod uses</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># default</span>

<span class="comment"># See the mounted token inside a pod</span>
kubectl exec easypj-web -n easypj -- \
  cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Default SA is Not Safe for Production</h5>
                    <p>The default SA has a token auto-mounted into every pod. Even though it has minimal permissions, <strong>if your pod is compromised, an attacker gets that token</strong>. Best practice: create specific SAs per application and disable auto-mount where not needed.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>3 Auto-Mounted Files in Every Pod</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎫</span> token</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> — JWT token for authenticating to the API server. Auto-refreshed by kubelet before expiry (~1 hour).</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📜</span> ca.crt</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt</code> — Cluster CA certificate. Used to verify the API server's TLS certificate when making HTTPS calls.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> namespace</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace</code> — Plain text file containing the pod's namespace name. Used by SDK clients to discover their namespace.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>When to Use Default SA</h5>
                        <p>Simple pods that never call the Kubernetes API. Disable the token with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>When to Create Custom SA</h5>
                        <p>Any pod that needs API access: reading ConfigMaps, listing pods, managing deployments, or running as a controller.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The <code>default</code> SA Is Self-Healing</h5>
                    <p>You cannot permanently get rid of the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount by deleting it — the control plane's <code>serviceaccount</code> controller watches every namespace and automatically recreates a fresh <code>default</code> SA if it's ever missing. This is by design (workloads that don't specify a SA must always have one to fall back to), but it also means "just delete the default SA" is never a valid hardening step — the correct move is always to keep it unprivileged and disable its token mount instead, not to delete it.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.3 Creating and Using Service Accounts</h3>
                <p>Creating a dedicated SA for your easypj application is straightforward — create it, then reference it in the pod spec.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Decision Guide — Does This Pod Actually Need a Token?</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   Does the workload call the Kubernetes API?
   (a controller, an operator, a job that lists/patches resources,
    a sidecar that reads Secrets via the API — NOT via env vars/volumes)
                              │
                 ┌────────────┴────────────┐
                 ▼                          ▼
               YES                          NO
                 │                          │
                 ▼                          ▼
   Create a dedicated SA, mount the      automountServiceAccountToken: false
   token, bind least-privilege RBAC      The easypj-web/worker pods talk to
   (see §25.5)                           Postgres and Redis directly — NOT
                                          the Kubernetes API — so they get
                                          NO token by default.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Create the SA</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create sa easypj-sa -n easypj</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Create RBAC Role</h5>
                        <p>Define what permissions the SA needs</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Bind SA to Role</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create rolebinding sa-bind --role=... --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Assign to Pod</h5>
                        <p>Set <code>spec.serviceAccountName: easypj-sa</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Verify</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa</code></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-serviceaccount-setup.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># 1. ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
---
<span class="comment"># 2. Role — what easypj needs</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-app-role
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["configmaps", "secrets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]          <span class="comment"># read config at startup</span>
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]                   <span class="comment"># health-check readiness</span>
---
<span class="comment"># 3. RoleBinding</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-app-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
<span class="comment"># 4. Pod using the ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa            # ◀── use our custom SA
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:latest
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">disable-auto-mount.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod that does NOT need API access — disable token mount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-worker
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
  automountServiceAccountToken: false      # ◀── no token mounted!
  containers:
  - name: worker
    image: easypj:latest
    command: ["python", "manage.py", "process_jobs"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production: How easypj's OWN Code Actually Uses the SA Token</h5>
                    <p>Everything so far has shown <code>kubectl</code>/<code>curl</code> consuming the mounted token from the outside. In real production code, your application never hand-parses those files — you hand the client library a config object and it does the token discovery, TLS verification, and Bearer-header wiring for you. This is exactly what a Django management command inside easypj-worker would do if it needed to, say, list Jobs it manages or watch ConfigMaps for a live-reload feature.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj/management/commands/watch_config.py</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-python"><span class="comment"># Real Python client-library usage inside the easypj Django app.</span>
<span class="comment"># pip install kubernetes  (the official Python K8s client)</span>
from kubernetes import client, config

<span class="comment"># load_incluster_config() replicates exactly what kubectl/curl did manually:</span>
<span class="comment">#   1. reads the token from</span>
<span class="comment">#      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</span>
<span class="comment">#   2. reads the CA bundle from .../ca.crt for TLS verification</span>
<span class="comment">#   3. reads the namespace from .../namespace</span>
<span class="comment">#   4. points the client at the in-cluster API server address</span>
<span class="comment">#      (the KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST / KUBERNETES_SERVICE_PORT env vars</span>
<span class="comment">#      every pod gets automatically, no ConfigMap needed)</span>
config.load_incluster_config()

v1 = client.CoreV1Api()

<span class="comment"># The client automatically sends:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Authorization: Bearer &lt;contents of the mounted token file&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># exactly like the raw curl example above -- just without you</span>
<span class="comment"># touching a single file path yourself.</span>
cm = v1.read_namespaced_config_map(
    name="easypj-feature-flags",
    namespace="easypj",
)
print(cm.data)   <span class="comment"># {'ENABLE_NEW_SEARCH': 'true', ...}</span>

<span class="comment"># If easypj-web-sa's Role doesn't grant "get" on configmaps, this</span>
<span class="comment"># raises kubernetes.client.exceptions.ApiException with status=403 --</span>
<span class="comment"># the SAME 403 you'd see from kubectl auth can-i, just as a Python</span>
<span class="comment"># exception instead of a CLI exit code.</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Environment</th><th>Config loader</th><th>Token source</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Running inside a pod (production)</td><td><code>config.load_incluster_config()</code></td><td>Auto-mounted projected token + in-cluster env vars</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Running on a dev laptop</td><td><code>config.load_kube_config()</code></td><td><code>~/.kube/config</code> — your own User Account cert, NOT a SA</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production Insight</h5>
                    <p>This is why the CKA's SA/RBAC skills transfer directly to real operator and controller code (client-go in Go, the official Python/Java/JS clients, etc.) — every mainstream Kubernetes client library implements the exact same "in-cluster config" pattern: read the three mounted files, talk to <code>https://kubernetes.default.svc</code>, and let RBAC do the rest. If you can debug a 403 with <code>kubectl auth can-i --as</code>, you can debug the identical 403 raised by a Python <code>ApiException</code> or a Go <code>errors.IsForbidden()</code> check.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.4 Token Projection and Token Request API</h3>
                <p>Before Kubernetes 1.22, SA tokens were stored as Secrets and never expired. Modern K8s uses <strong>projected tokens</strong> — they're time-limited, audience-bound, and automatically refreshed by the kubelet.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Legacy Tokens vs Projected Tokens — Security Evolution</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ LEGACY (pre-1.22)                  ✅ PROJECTED (1.22+)                  │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌────────────────────┐               ┌────────────────────────┐            │
  │  │  Secret object     │               │  TokenRequest API      │            │
  │  │  ├── token: xyz... │               │  ├── audience: api     │            │
  │  │  │                 │               │  ├── expiry: 1 hour    │            │
  │  │  │  ⚠️ NEVER       │               │  ├── auto-refresh: ✅  │            │
  │  │  │    EXPIRES!     │               │  │                      │            │
  │  │  │                 │               │  │  🔒 Time-limited    │            │
  │  │  │  ⚠️ NO audience│               │  │  🔒 Audience-bound  │            │
  │  │  │    binding      │               │  │  🔒 Auto-rotated    │            │
  │  │  │                 │               │  │                      │            │
  │  │  │  Stored in      │               │  │  Generated on       │            │
  │  │  │  etcd forever   │               │  │  demand by kubelet  │            │
  │  └────────────────────┘               └────────────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  If token is stolen →                 If token is stolen →                   │
  │  UNLIMITED access 💀                  Expires in ≤1 hour 🛡️                 │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">projected-token-volume.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod with explicit projected token volume (advanced scenario)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-api-client
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
  containers:
  - name: api-client
    image: easypj:latest
    volumeMounts:
    - name: token-vol
      mountPath: /var/run/secrets/tokens
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: token-vol
    projected:
      sources:
      - serviceAccountToken:
          path: api-token               <span class="comment"># file name</span>
          expirationSeconds: 3600       <span class="comment"># 1 hour</span>
          audience: api                 <span class="comment"># intended audience</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">create-token-cli.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create a short-lived token from CLI (TokenRequest API)</span>
kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj --duration=3600s

<span class="comment"># Create a token for a specific audience</span>
kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj --audience=https://my-api.example.com

<span class="comment"># Decode and inspect a token (JWT)</span>
TOKEN=$(kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj)
echo $TOKEN | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq .
<span class="comment"># Shows: iss, sub, aud, exp, iat, kubernetes.io fields</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 CKA Exam Note</h5>
                    <p>The CKA rarely tests projected token details, but you must know that <code>kubectl create token</code> is the modern way to generate tokens, and that auto-mounted tokens now expire. Legacy Secret-based tokens are deprecated.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>Projected Token Lifecycle</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Pod Scheduled</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Kubelet calls TokenRequest API → gets a time-limited JWT token for the pod's ServiceAccount</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Token Mounted</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Token written to <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> as a projected volume</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>App Uses Token</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Application reads token → sends in <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header to API server</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Auto-Refresh</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Before token expires (~80% of TTL), kubelet automatically requests a new token and updates the mounted file</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">5</span>
                                <h5>Pod Deleted → Token Invalidated</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>The token isn't just time-bound — it's bound to the POD's lifetime too. It's invalidated roughly 60 seconds after the pod's <code>metadata.deletionTimestamp</code> (i.e. shortly after termination completes), even if its <code>exp</code> claim hasn't been reached yet</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Bound (default 1.22+)</th><th>Value</th><th>Configurable?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Default <code>expirationSeconds</code></td><td>3600s (1 hour)</td><td>Per-volume via <code>projected.sources[].serviceAccountToken.expirationSeconds</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Minimum <code>expirationSeconds</code></td><td>600s (10 minutes)</td><td>Hard floor — requests below this are rejected</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Cluster-wide maximum</td><td>No hard cap by default</td><td>Cluster admin can cap it with the API server flag <code>--service-account-max-token-expiration</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod-lifetime binding</td><td>~60s after pod <code>deletionTimestamp</code></td><td>Not configurable — always tied to the pod that requested the token</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production: Node-Bound Tokens &amp; Immutable serviceAccountName</h5>
                    <p>Two operational details that trip up even experienced admins: (1) <strong>Kubernetes 1.31+</strong> supports minting a token bound to a specific <em>Node</em> as well as a pod, via <code>kubectl create token &lt;sa&gt; --bound-object-kind Node --bound-object-name &lt;node&gt; --bound-object-uid &lt;uid&gt;</code> — useful for node-level agents that need a credential tied to node identity, not just SA identity. (2) <code>spec.serviceAccountName</code> can only be set when a Pod is <strong>created</strong> — you cannot <code>kubectl patch</code> a running Pod to change its SA. This is exactly why <code>kubectl set serviceaccount deployment/easypj-web easypj-web-sa</code> works (it updates the Deployment's pod <em>template</em>, which triggers a rollout that creates brand-new pods) while trying the same patch directly against a running Pod object fails.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.5 Binding RBAC to Service Accounts</h3>
                <p>The real power comes when you combine Service Accounts with RBAC — giving each application precisely the permissions it needs, nothing more.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 SA Subject Format in RBAC</h5>
                    <p>When using <code>--as</code> flag or in RoleBinding subjects, the format is:<br><code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;sa-name&gt;</code></p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj SA + RBAC — Least Privilege in Action</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │             easypj SERVICE ACCOUNT RBAC ARCHITECTURE                         │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 📦 easypj-web    │    │ 🔗 RoleBinding  │    │ 📋 Role              │     │
  │  │                  │    │                │    │ easypj-app-role      │     │
  │  │ SA: easypj-sa    │───▶│ easypj-sa ───▶│───▶│ configmaps: get,list │     │
  │  │                  │    │ Role           │    │ secrets: get         │     │
  │  │ Can read config  │    │                │    │ pods: get,list       │     │
  │  │ ✅ configmaps    │    └────────────────┘    └──────────────────────┘     │
  │  │ ✅ secrets       │                                                       │
  │  │ ❌ delete pods   │                                                       │
  │  │ ❌ create deploy │                                                       │
  │  └──────────────────┘                                                       │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 📦 easypj-worker │    │ No binding     │    │ No permissions       │     │
  │  │                  │    │                │    │                      │     │
  │  │ SA: easypj-sa    │    │ automount:     │    │ Token not mounted    │     │
  │  │ automount: false │    │ false          │    │ Can't call API       │     │
  │  │                  │    │                │    │ 🔒 Maximum security  │     │
  │  │ ❌ everything    │    └────────────────┘    └──────────────────────┘     │
  │  └──────────────────┘                                                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">bind-sa-complete.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Complete SA + RBAC setup (imperative — exam speed!)</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Create ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create sa easypj-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 2. Create Role</span>
kubectl create role easypj-app-role \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=configmaps,secrets,pods \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 3. Bind SA to Role</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-sa-binding \
  --role=easypj-app-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i get configmaps \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (least privilege working!)</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. List all SA permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Prove It From Inside the Pod — Raw curl Against the API Server</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl auth can-i --as</code> is the fast exam check, but it's a <strong>simulation</strong> run by your own kubectl credentials asking "what if this SA made the call" — it never actually authenticates as the SA. To prove the token itself really works end-to-end, exec into the pod and make the exact HTTP call the application would make, using only the three mounted files.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">raw-curl-from-inside-pod.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Exec into the pod running as easypj-sa (Role grants get/list/watch on</span>
<span class="comment"># configmaps/secrets/pods only -- see the Role YAML above)</span>
kubectl exec -it easypj-web -n easypj -- sh

<span class="comment"># Inside the pod, build the request from the mounted files -- this is</span>
<span class="comment"># EXACTLY what config.load_incluster_config() does for you automatically:</span>
TOKEN=$(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)
CACERT=/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt
NS=$(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace)

<span class="comment"># Allowed by the Role → 200 OK</span>
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" --cacert $CACERT \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/$NS/pods
<span class="comment"># 200</span>

<span class="comment"># NOT in the Role's rules → 403 Forbidden (authenticated, but denied)</span>
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" --cacert $CACERT \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/$NS/deployments
<span class="comment"># 403</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ SA Best Practices</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>One SA per application/workload</li>
                            <li><code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> if no API access needed</li>
                            <li>Bind with specific Role, not <code>cluster-admin</code></li>
                            <li>Use projected tokens (K8s 1.22+)</li>
                            <li>Always verify with <code>can-i</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>❌ SA Anti-Patterns</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Using <code>default</code> SA for everything</li>
                            <li>Binding <code>cluster-admin</code> to a SA</li>
                            <li>Leaving auto-mount on when not needed</li>
                            <li>Sharing SAs across unrelated apps</li>
                            <li>Using legacy Secret-based tokens</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Cross-Namespace Access: RoleBinding Isn't Enough</h5>
                    <p>Every example so far uses a <strong>Role</strong> + <strong>RoleBinding</strong>, scoped to one namespace — right for easypj-web reading its own ConfigMaps. But some SAs legitimately need visibility across the WHOLE cluster: a logging DaemonSet's SA that reads pod metadata in every namespace, or a monitoring SA scraping metrics cluster-wide. For that, bind a <strong>ClusterRole</strong> with a <strong>ClusterRoleBinding</strong> (cluster-wide) or, more safely, that same ClusterRole with a per-namespace <strong>RoleBinding</strong> repeated in only the namespaces that actually need it.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">log-shipper-clusterrolebinding.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># A log-shipper DaemonSet's SA needs to read pod metadata in EVERY namespace --</span>
<span class="comment"># a namespaced Role/RoleBinding physically cannot grant that; only a</span>
<span class="comment"># ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding reaches across namespace boundaries.</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-log-shipper-sa
  namespace: logging
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: pod-metadata-reader          <span class="comment"># cluster-scoped object -- no namespace field</span>
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "pods/log"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding             <span class="comment"># cluster-scoped binding -- grants in ALL namespaces</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj-log-shipper-binding
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: easypj-log-shipper-sa
  namespace: logging                 <span class="comment"># SA's home namespace, NOT a scope restriction</span>
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: pod-metadata-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

<span class="comment"># Imperative equivalent (fast in the exam):</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl create clusterrolebinding easypj-log-shipper-binding \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --clusterrole=pod-metadata-reader \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --serviceaccount=logging:easypj-log-shipper-sa</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Binding shape</th><th>Grants access in</th><th>Typical easypj use case</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Role + RoleBinding</td><td>One namespace only</td><td>easypj-web-sa reading its own ConfigMaps/Secrets</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>ClusterRole + RoleBinding</td><td>One namespace, but the rule set is reusable across many namespaces</td><td>Reuse a shared "configmap-reader" ClusterRole in both <code>easypj-staging</code> and <code>easypj-production</code> without duplicating the Role YAML</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding</td><td>Every namespace, cluster-wide</td><td>Logging/monitoring DaemonSet SA that must see pods everywhere</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Production: When a CI/CD Pipeline Needs a SA Token FROM OUTSIDE the Cluster</h5>
                    <p>Everything above assumes the token consumer is a pod running <em>inside</em> the cluster, where the kubelet handles minting and rotation transparently. But a GitHub Actions runner, a Jenkins agent, or an ArgoCD instance running outside the cluster has no kubelet to do that for it — it needs a token it can put in a <code>kubeconfig</code> file or CI secret store. There are two legitimate approaches, and picking the wrong one is a common production mistake.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Short-lived, manually refreshed</strong></td><td><code>kubectl create token deployer-sa -n easypj --duration=3600s</code> run by the pipeline itself at deploy time, injected as a CI secret for that single run</td><td>✅ Most secure — expires within the hour even if the CI log leaks it. ❌ Requires the pipeline to already have SOME cluster credential to run this command (usually a human's kubeconfig used once to bootstrap it, or a cloud-native OIDC federation — see below)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Long-lived Secret-based token</strong></td><td>Manually create a <code>type: kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code> Secret annotated with <code>kubernetes.io/service-account.name</code>; K8s populates <code>.data.token</code> and it never expires</td><td>✅ Simple — one static credential the CI system stores once. ❌ Never expires: if it leaks (log output, compromised CI secret store), it's valid until manually revoked. Must be stored in the CI system's own secret manager, never in a repo</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">ci-deployer-sa.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># A dedicated, least-privilege SA for a CI/CD pipeline that deploys easypj --</span>
<span class="comment"># scoped ONLY to what the pipeline needs (rollout Deployments), nothing else.</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-ci-deployer
  namespace: easypj
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: ci-deployer-role
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "patch", "update"]     <span class="comment"># enough to update the image and roll out</span>
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-ci-deployer-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: easypj-ci-deployer
  namespace: easypj
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: ci-deployer-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
<span class="comment"># Long-lived token Secret (opt-in since 1.24+ — the pipeline stores this</span>
<span class="comment"># in its OWN secret manager, e.g. GitHub Actions secrets, never in git)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-ci-deployer-token
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    kubernetes.io/service-account.name: easypj-ci-deployer
type: kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production Insight</h5>
                    <p>The best practice most mature platform teams land on is neither of the table's two rows in isolation — it's <strong>OIDC federation</strong>: GitHub Actions (or GitLab CI) has its own OIDC issuer, and cloud-managed clusters (EKS/GKE/AKS) can be configured to trust that issuer directly, so the pipeline exchanges a short-lived GitHub-issued OIDC token for temporary cluster credentials with zero static Kubernetes Secret ever stored in CI at all. This mirrors the IRSA-style federation pattern from the UTH section — same trust-the-issuer mechanic, just with GitHub as the identity provider instead of Kubernetes.</p>
                </div>

            <!-- ─── Service Account Verification Checklist ─── -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>Service Account Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get sa -n NS</code> → confirm SA exists</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe sa SA_NAME -n NS</code> → check token and secrets</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=system:serviceaccount:NS:SA -n NS</code> → verify RBAC</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code> → check token mount</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> → inspect mounted token</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl create token SA_NAME -n NS</code> → verify TokenRequest API works</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

                
