            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, you'll create SAs and bind them to Roles. Remember: <code>kubectl create sa &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> creates the SA. <code>kubectl create rolebinding &lt;name&gt; --role=&lt;role&gt; --serviceaccount=&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;sa&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> binds it. In pod YAML: <code>spec.serviceAccountName: &lt;sa&gt;</code>. SA subjects in RBAC use format: <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>.
            </div>

            <h5>① Token Minting &amp; Rotation — Inside the Kubelet's TokenRequest Loop</h5>
            <p>The mounted token isn't a static file the API server hands out once — it's continuously regenerated by a tight loop between the kubelet and the API server's TokenRequest endpoint. Understanding this loop explains why the token file's contents change over a long-running pod's lifetime, and why a stale copy you saved yesterday won't authenticate today.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  THE KUBELET ↔ TOKENREQUEST LOOP (per pod, per projected SA token volume)     │
│                                                                              │
│   1. Pod scheduled to node                                                   │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│   2. kubelet reads pod's projected-volume spec: expirationSeconds=3600       │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│   3. kubelet calls API Server's TokenRequest sub-resource:                   │
│      POST /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/serviceaccounts/easypj-web-sa/token      │
│      { "spec": { "audiences": ["api"], "expirationSeconds": 3600 } }         │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│   4. API Server signs a fresh JWT with its SA signing private key            │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│   5. kubelet writes JWT into the pod's tmpfs projected volume (in-memory)    │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│   6. Container reads /var/run/secrets/.../token like any other file          │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│   7. At ~80% of TTL (or 24h, whichever is sooner) → kubelet repeats step 3   │
│      — the SAME file path, a NEW token contents, transparently              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Actor</th><th>Responsibility in the loop</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>kubelet</td><td>Requests tokens on the pod's behalf, writes them to the projected tmpfs volume, watches for the ~80%-of-TTL refresh point</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>API Server (TokenRequest)</td><td>Issues short-lived, signed JWTs scoped to the requesting SA, namespace, audience, and expiry</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Container process</td><td>Just re-reads the same file path — it never needs to know a rotation happened</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> This is why <code>kubectl exec pod -- cat .../token</code> can return a DIFFERENT string if you run it again hours later on a long-lived pod — that's expected rotation, not a bug. Applications should always read the token from the file path fresh (never cache it beyond its own process lifetime).
            </div>

            <h5>② JWT Signature Validation — How the API Server Verifies Every Bearer Token</h5>
            <p>When a request arrives with <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>, the API server's ServiceAccount authenticator does three checks before authorization (RBAC) even runs. Any one failing produces a <code>401 Unauthorized</code> — the request never reaches RBAC, which is why an audience mismatch can look confusingly like "the SA has no permissions" when it's actually an authentication failure.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  TOKEN VALIDATION — 3 checks, in order, ANY failure = 401                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌───────────────────┐   ┌───────────────────┐   ┌───────────────────┐      │
│  │ 1. SIGNATURE       │   │ 2. EXPIRY          │   │ 3. AUDIENCE        │      │
│  │                    │──▶│                    │──▶│                    │      │
│  │ Verify JWT sig     │   │ Check exp claim    │   │ Check aud claim    │      │
│  │ against the SA     │   │ against current    │   │ includes THIS      │      │
│  │ signing PUBLIC key │   │ server time         │   │ API server's       │      │
│  │                    │   │                    │   │ identifier          │      │
│  │ ❌ tampered token  │   │ ❌ expired token   │   │ ❌ wrong audience  │      │
│  │    → 401           │   │    → 401            │   │    → 401            │      │
│  └───────────────────┘   └───────────────────┘   └───────────────────┘      │
│                                                            │                  │
│                                                            ▼                  │
│                                          ✅ ALL PASS → identity resolved to   │
│                                             system:serviceaccount:ns:name     │
│                                                            │                  │
│                                                            ▼                  │
│                                          RBAC now decides ALLOW/DENY (403)    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Failure mode</th><th>HTTP result</th><th>Looks like</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Bad signature (tampered/wrong key)</td><td>401 Unauthorized</td><td>"invalid bearer token"</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Expired token</td><td>401 Unauthorized</td><td>"token has expired" — common with saved/cached tokens</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Wrong audience</td><td>401 Unauthorized</td><td>Silent-looking failure — easy to mistake for missing RBAC</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Valid token, no RBAC grant</td><td>403 Forbidden</td><td>"cannot get resource ... is forbidden"</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> 401 vs 403 is a diagnostic gold mine — 401 means authentication failed (bad/expired/wrong-audience token), 403 means authentication succeeded but RBAC said no. If troubleshooting output shows 401 for a SA you're sure has the right RBAC, stop looking at Roles and check the token itself.
            </div>

            <h5>③ BoundServiceAccountTokenVolume — The GA Feature Behind Modern Auto-Mount</h5>
            <p>The projected, expiring token you get "for free" on every pod isn't magic — it's a specific, named Kubernetes feature (<code>BoundServiceAccountTokenVolume</code>) that graduated to GA in 1.22 and became the sole mechanism once legacy auto-created Secret tokens stopped being provisioned by default in 1.24, a change that was itself locked in permanently (GA) in 1.27. Knowing the feature's name and timeline matters because older documentation and blog posts online still describe the pre-1.22 Secret-based behavior as current.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  FEATURE TIMELINE — how "auto-mount" changed under the hood                   │
│                                                                              │
│  K8s ≤ 1.21    kubectl create sa → auto-creates a long-lived Secret          │
│                Pod mounts that Secret as a volume → token NEVER expires      │
│                                                                              │
│  K8s 1.22-1.23  BoundServiceAccountTokenVolume GOES GA                        │
│                Auto-mount now uses a PROJECTED volume (bound token)          │
│                But the legacy Secret is STILL auto-created (for compat)      │
│                                                                              │
│  K8s 1.24      LegacyServiceAccountTokenNoAutoGeneration enabled by default   │
│                Auto-created Secrets STOP being provisioned by default        │
│                (feature gate could still be flipped back on manually)        │
│                                                                              │
│  K8s 1.27+     LegacyServiceAccountTokenNoAutoGeneration reaches GA           │
│                Feature gate REMOVED — the no-auto-Secret behavior is now      │
│                permanent and can no longer be re-enabled. Only the           │
│                projected, bound token remains — this is what the CKA         │
│                exam (1.29+) exercises exclusively                            │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>K8s version</th><th>Auto-created Secret token?</th><th>Projected bound token?</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>≤ 1.21</td><td>✅ Yes (never expires)</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>1.22 – 1.23</td><td>✅ Yes (compat)</td><td>✅ Yes (default auto-mount)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>1.24 – 1.26</td><td>❌ No (default off, gate togglable)</td><td>✅ Yes (only mechanism)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>1.27+ (CKA today)</td><td>❌ No (permanent — must create manually)</td><td>✅ Yes (only mechanism)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> If you need a genuinely long-lived token today (rare — e.g. an external system polling the API without kubelet support), you must manually create a Secret with <code>type: kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code> and the <code>kubernetes.io/service-account.name</code> annotation. It's opt-in now, not automatic.
            </div>

            <h5>④ Audience Scoping — Why One Token Can't Impersonate Another Service</h5>
            <p>The <code>audience</code> claim is what stops a token minted for one purpose from being replayed somewhere else. Without it, any service that received a pod's token (a proxy, a logging sidecar, a misconfigured webhook) could turn around and use that same token to hit the Kubernetes API directly — audience scoping is the seatbelt that prevents this "confused deputy" scenario.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  AUDIENCE SCOPING — a token minted for "api" can't authenticate to "vault"    │
│                                                                              │
│   easypj-worker pod requests TWO separate projected token volumes:           │
│                                                                              │
│   volume: k8s-token        volume: vault-token                               │
│   audience: api            audience: vault.easypj.svc                       │
│        │                          │                                          │
│        ▼                          ▼                                          │
│   Kubernetes API Server      HashiCorp Vault                                 │
│   accepts (aud matches)      accepts (aud matches)                          │
│                                                                              │
│   ❌ If the vault-token is presented to the K8s API instead:                 │
│      aud=["vault.easypj.svc"] does NOT include the K8s API's own audience   │
│      → 401 Unauthorized, even though the signature is perfectly valid       │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> The exam default (auto-mounted token, no explicit <code>audience</code> field) is scoped to the API server itself. You only see explicit <code>audience:</code> fields in advanced scenarios — a pod calling an external service (Vault, a cloud STS endpoint) that also trusts the cluster's signing keys.
            </div>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📖 Emerging: Node-Level Audience Restriction</h5>
                <p>A newer feature gate, <code>ServiceAccountNodeAudienceRestriction</code>, further tightens this model at the node boundary: it limits which token audiences a given node's kubelet is even allowed to request, so a compromised node can't mint tokens scoped to audiences it has no legitimate workload for. This isn't part of the CKA syllabus and its behavior may still evolve, but it's worth knowing as a direction the audience-scoping model is heading in production clusters.</p>
            </div>

            <h5>⑤ imagePullSecrets on a ServiceAccount — Registry Credentials Without Per-Pod Config</h5>
            <p>Beyond identity and RBAC, a ServiceAccount can also carry <code>imagePullSecrets</code> — registry credentials that the kubelet automatically merges into every pod using that SA at creation time. This is the single most common production use of "extra" SA fields beyond auth.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  imagePullSecrets INHERITANCE — patch once, every pod inherits               │
│                                                                              │
│  🔒 Secret: regcred (kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson)                         │
│        │                                                                     │
│        │ attached via: kubectl patch sa easypj-web-sa                        │
│        │   -p '{"imagePullSecrets":[{"name":"regcred"}]}'                    │
│        ▼                                                                     │
│  🤖 ServiceAccount: easypj-web-sa                                            │
│        │  imagePullSecrets: [regcred]                                        │
│        │                                                                     │
│        ├──▶ 📦 easypj-web pod (created AFTER patch) → inherits regcred ✅    │
│        ├──▶ 📦 easypj-worker pod (created AFTER patch) → inherits regcred ✅ │
│        └──▶ 📦 easypj-old pod (created BEFORE patch, already running)        │
│              → does NOT retroactively gain it — needs a restart ⚠️           │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Configuration point</th><th>Scope</th><th>When to use</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>pod.spec.imagePullSecrets</code></td><td>One pod</td><td>One-off pod pulling from a unique registry</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>serviceaccount.imagePullSecrets</code></td><td>Every pod using that SA</td><td>Standard app-wide registry credential (most common)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Patching the <code>default</code> SA in a namespace with <code>imagePullSecrets</code> is the fastest exam fix for <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> against a private registry when the pod spec doesn't already declare its own — but remember it only affects pods created AFTER the patch; existing pods need a restart/recreate.
            </div>

            <h5>⑥ system:serviceaccounts Groups &amp; Cloud Identity Federation (OIDC/IRSA)</h5>
            <p>Two advanced mechanisms build on top of everything above. First, every SA is implicitly a member of built-in groups you can bind RBAC to directly. Second, because a bound SA token is a standards-compliant JWT, external systems — including cloud IAM — can trust it without any static credential ever touching the pod.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  A) BUILT-IN SA GROUPS                                                       │
│     system:serviceaccounts                  ← every SA, every namespace      │
│     system:serviceaccounts:easypj            ← every SA in ONE namespace     │
│     Bind a Role to the group → grants ALL current + future SAs in scope      │
│                                                                              │
│  B) CLOUD IDENTITY FEDERATION (e.g. AWS IRSA / GCP Workload Identity)        │
│                                                                              │
│     easypj-batch pod (SA: easypj-batch-sa)                                   │
│          │ presents projected JWT, aud=sts.amazonaws.com                     │
│          ▼                                                                   │
│     Cloud STS ── verifies sig via cluster's published OIDC public keys ────▶ │
│          │ checks IAM role trust policy: sub == system:serviceaccount:...    │
│          ▼                                                                   │
│     Temporary cloud credentials issued — NO static cloud keys in the pod ✅  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What it enables</th><th>Caution</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>system:serviceaccounts:&lt;ns&gt;</code> group bind</td><td>One Role grants all SAs in a namespace, including future ones</td><td>Easy to over-grant — silently includes SAs created later</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>OIDC discovery / IRSA-style federation</td><td>Pods get temporary cloud credentials with zero static secrets</td><td>Cloud-specific setup (issuer URL, trust policy) — not part of core K8s or the CKA syllabus itself</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> The CKA doesn't test cloud IAM federation directly, but understanding that a projected SA token is "just a JWT" — not a Kubernetes-proprietary format — explains why so much of the cloud-native ecosystem (service meshes, secret managers, cloud IAM) can plug directly into ServiceAccount identity with no extra credential store.
            </div>

            <h5>⑦ Who Can Mint Tokens for OTHER ServiceAccounts — RBAC on the <code>serviceaccounts/token</code> Subresource</h5>
            <p><code>kubectl create token</code> feels like a free, universal command, but it's actually calling the TokenRequest API's <code>serviceaccounts/token</code> subresource — and like every other verb/resource pair in Kubernetes, RBAC gates it. A user or automation identity needs an explicit <code>create</code> grant on that subresource before it can mint a token for a given SA. This is the exact mechanism a CI/CD pipeline's bootstrap identity needs (see the §25.5 production box on CI/CD tokens above), and it's a common source of confusing 403s when a platform team locks down "who can impersonate SAs."</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  MINTING A TOKEN IS ITSELF AN RBAC-GATED ACTION                              │
│                                                                              │
│   $ kubectl create token easypj-web-sa -n easypj                             │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼  under the hood, this is:                                          │
│   POST /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/serviceaccounts/easypj-web-sa/token         │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼  RBAC checks the CALLER's rights (your kubectl identity, or the    │
│            CI pipeline's bootstrap SA) — NOT the target SA's rights          │
│   apiGroups: [""]                                                            │
│   resources: ["serviceaccounts/token"]                                       │
│   verbs: ["create"]                                                          │
│   resourceNames: ["easypj-web-sa"]     ← optional: scope to ONE named SA     │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│   ✅ Grant present  → JWT minted, scoped to easypj-web-sa's OWN permissions  │
│   ❌ Grant missing  → 403 Forbidden — "cannot create resource                │
│                       serviceaccounts/token"                                 │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Role field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>resources: ["serviceaccounts/token"]</code></td><td>The TokenRequest subresource — separate permission from managing the SA object itself</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>resourceNames: ["easypj-web-sa"]</code></td><td>Optional — restricts token minting to ONE named SA instead of every SA in the namespace</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>verbs: ["create"]</code></td><td>The only verb that applies — there's no "list" or "get" for a token subresource</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> A confusing failure mode: the SA you're minting a token FOR might have plenty of RBAC rights, yet <code>kubectl create token</code> still fails with 403 — because that 403 is about YOUR identity's right to mint on its behalf, not the target SA's own permissions. If a CI bootstrap identity needs to mint tokens for exactly one deployer SA (least privilege), scope it with <code>resourceNames</code> rather than granting blanket <code>serviceaccounts/token</code> create rights across the whole namespace.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Users are HUMANS (external; certificate or OIDC, NOT K8s objects). ServiceAccounts are POD identities — real, namespaced API objects. Pods authenticate to the API server as their ServiceAccount.</li>
                    <li>Every namespace has a <code>default</code> SA, auto-mounted into every pod at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code>. Disable the mount (<code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>) unless the pod actually calls the API.</li>
                    <li>A ServiceAccount has NO rights until you bind a Role or ClusterRole to it — the starting point is always zero permissions.</li>
                    <li>Modern tokens are short-lived, PROJECTED tokens issued via the TokenRequest API — bound to the pod, with an expiry and an audience — not the old long-lived Secret-based token.</li>
                    <li>Bind RBAC to a SA using the canonical subject name <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>.</li>
                    <li>Best practice: one least-privileged SA per application; keep the <code>default</code> SA unprivileged and never bind broad rights (like <code>cluster-admin</code>) to it.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">The default SA has full access to the namespace</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">The default SA has almost NO permissions — create specific SAs with explicit RBAC</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">SA tokens never expire</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">Since K8s 1.22, projected tokens are time-limited and audience-bound. Legacy tokens are deprecated</span></li>
                    <li><code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on the pod overrides the SA-level setting</li>
                    <li>SA is <strong>namespaced</strong> — you cannot use a SA from namespace A in namespace B</li>
                    <li>Deleting a SA doesn't delete its RoleBindings — orphaned bindings remain</li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">A RoleBinding subject with the wrong namespace (or a missing <code>system:serviceaccount:</code> prefix on <code>--as</code>) just "doesn't work" like a permissions bug</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">It's not a permissions bug — the subject string simply doesn't match ANY real identity, so RBAC silently evaluates to deny. <code>kubectl create rolebinding ... --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa</code> (correct) vs a typo'd <code>--serviceaccount=easyjp:easypj-sa</code> (wrong namespace) produces a binding that looks valid in <code>kubectl get rolebinding -o yaml</code> but grants nothing to the real SA — always cross-check the subject's <code>namespace</code> field against the SA's actual namespace</span></li>
                    <li><strong>Use <code>serviceAccountName</code>, not <code>serviceAccount</code></strong> — the latter is a deprecated pod-spec field kept only for backward compatibility; if both are set and disagree, the API server rejects the pod. Also remember the SA must already exist before the pod is created, or the pod stays <code>Pending</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

