            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 25 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Ten conceptual questions probing WHY, not just HOW — full answer &amp; explanation each.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why can't you run <code>kubectl create user jane</code>? What does that tell you about how Kubernetes models identity?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>There is no such command because a User Account is <strong>not a Kubernetes API object</strong> — Kubernetes has no storage for it. Users are authenticated entirely by an external mechanism (an x509 client certificate signed by the cluster CA, an OIDC identity token, or an authenticating proxy) and the API server simply trusts whatever identity that mechanism hands it. A ServiceAccount, by contrast, IS a first-class object (<code>kind: ServiceAccount</code>) that the API server creates, stores in etcd, lists, and deletes.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Is it a K8s object?</th><th>Who authenticates it</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>User (human)</td><td>❌ No</td><td>External: x509 cert / OIDC / proxy</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>ServiceAccount (pod)</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Kubernetes itself (JWT it signs)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This distinction matters for RBAC subjects too: a <code>User</code> subject in a RoleBinding is just a string the cluster trusts on faith (backed by whatever external auth verified it), while a <code>ServiceAccount</code> subject references an object the cluster can independently verify exists.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is leaving every pod on the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount considered a security risk, even though the default SA starts with almost no permissions?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The risk isn't the default SA's <em>starting</em> permissions — it's the <strong>blast radius</strong>. The default SA's token is auto-mounted into <em>every</em> pod in the namespace that doesn't specify its own SA. If an operator (even accidentally) binds a Role to <code>default</code> to unblock one app, every other pod sharing that namespace instantly inherits the same rights. It also means a compromised pod anywhere in the namespace can reuse the same identity to probe what the default SA can do.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>If ONE pod is compromised</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Dedicated SA per app (e.g. <code>easypj-web-sa</code>)</td><td>Attacker inherits only that app's specific, scoped rights</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Every pod shares the namespace's <code>default</code> SA</td><td>Attacker inherits <em>whatever any</em> app ever accidentally got granted to <code>default</code> — across every co-located pod, forever</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="info-box tip">
                            <h5>💡 Insight</h5>
                            <p>Treat the <code>default</code> SA as a shared, unprivileged commons — never bind meaningful RBAC to it. Give each application its own dedicated SA instead.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On the CKA, this shows up as a "why did access leak to an unrelated pod" style question — the answer is almost always "someone bound a Role to the <code>default</code> SA."</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The easypj-web Deployment needs to list ConfigMaps but the pods are already running with a custom SA and still get 403. Walk through what could be wrong.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Work the chain from identity to permission, checking each link:</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1. Is the SA actually assigned?</td><td><code>kubectl get pod easypj-web-xxx -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2. Is the token mounted?</td><td><code>kubectl exec easypj-web-xxx -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3. Does a RoleBinding exist for this SA?</td><td><code>kubectl get rolebindings -n easypj -o wide | grep easypj-web-sa</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>4. Does the Role actually grant the verb/resource?</td><td><code>kubectl describe role configmap-reader -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>5. Confirm end-to-end</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i list configmaps --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The most common root cause is step 3 or 4 — the SA is correctly assigned and mounted, but nothing binds it to a Role, or the Role exists in the wrong namespace. Always verify with <code>auth can-i --as</code> rather than assuming — it evaluates the exact same policy the API server would.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What problem does the TokenRequest API solve that the old Secret-based SA token could not?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The old mechanism auto-created a Secret per SA holding a token that <strong>never expired</strong> and worked against <strong>any</strong> API server that trusted the signing key — a leaked token was valid forever, everywhere. The TokenRequest API mints a token on-demand, bound to a specific pod, a specific expiry (default 1h), and a specific audience. A leaked bound token is only useful for a short window and only against the intended recipient.</p>
                        <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   OLD: Secret token          NEW: TokenRequest API token
   ─────────────────          ─────────────────────────
   ❌ never expires           ✅ expires (default 1h)
   ❌ any audience             ✅ scoped audience
   ❌ stored in etcd forever   ✅ projected volume (tmpfs)
   ❌ manual rotation          ✅ kubelet auto-rotates
</pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the security rationale behind the change that shipped as GA (BoundServiceAccountTokenVolume) in K8s 1.22 and became the sole default once auto-created Secret tokens stopped being provisioned in 1.24+. The CKA (1.29+) exercises the new behavior only.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does a token's "audience" actually restrict, and why does that matter for security?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The audience is a claim embedded in the JWT (<code>aud</code>) that names the intended recipient service — typically the Kubernetes API server itself, but it can be any external service that understands K8s-signed tokens (e.g. a Vault instance, a cloud metadata service). The recipient MUST check that its own identifier appears in the token's audience list before accepting it; if the audiences don't match, the token is rejected even if the signature is valid and it hasn't expired.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Token minted with audience</th><th>Accepted by</th><th>Rejected by</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>https://kubernetes.default.svc</code> (default)</td><td>Kubernetes API server</td><td>Vault, cloud STS endpoints</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>vault.easypj.svc</code></td><td>HashiCorp Vault</td><td>Kubernetes API server</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>sts.amazonaws.com</code></td><td>AWS STS (IRSA federation)</td><td>Kubernetes API server, Vault</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This prevents "confused deputy" attacks: a token minted for talking to Service A can't be replayed against Service B, even if both trust the same signing key. If a token issue "looks like" a 403 but the SA definitely has RBAC, suspect an audience mismatch — the token never even reaches authorization; it fails at authentication.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on a ServiceAccount, but a pod using that SA still has a token mounted. Why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The pod spec's own <code>automountServiceAccountToken</code> field takes precedence over the SA-level setting. If the pod explicitly sets <code>automountServiceAccountToken: true</code>, it overrides the SA's <code>false</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>SA-level</th><th>Pod-level</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>false</td><td>(unset)</td><td>❌ Not mounted (SA default applies)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>false</td><td>true</td><td>✅ Mounted (pod wins)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>true</td><td>false</td><td>❌ Not mounted (pod wins)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(unset)</td><td>(unset)</td><td>✅ Mounted (K8s default is true)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Always check BOTH levels when a token unexpectedly appears or is missing — <code>kubectl get sa &lt;name&gt; -o yaml</code> and <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o yaml | grep automount</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>system:serviceaccounts:&lt;namespace&gt;</code> group, and why should you use it sparingly?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Every ServiceAccount is automatically a member of two built-in groups: the cluster-wide <code>system:serviceaccounts</code> group (every SA in every namespace) and the per-namespace <code>system:serviceaccounts:&lt;namespace&gt;</code> group (every SA in that one namespace). Binding a Role to that group grants the permission to <em>every</em> SA in the namespace at once — including ones created later.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bind-role-to-sa-group.sh</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Grant view access to EVERY ServiceAccount in the easypj namespace at once</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-all-sas-view \
  --clusterrole=view \
  --group=system:serviceaccounts:easypj \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># A new SA created in easypj tomorrow inherits "view" immediately —</span>
<span class="comment"># no RoleBinding update required. That's the power AND the risk.</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="info-box tip">
                            <h5>💡 Insight</h5>
                            <p>This is powerful for "any workload in this namespace may read this one ConfigMap" style policies, but it's easy to over-grant since it silently includes future SAs too. Prefer binding to individual SAs unless you deliberately want namespace-wide reach.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On the exam, if a question wants "every pod in this namespace" to have a permission, binding the group is faster than binding N individual SAs — but always double check that's actually the intent before doing it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does attaching <code>imagePullSecrets</code> to a ServiceAccount save you from repeating that config on every pod spec?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>When a pod runs under a ServiceAccount, the kubelet merges the SA's <code>imagePullSecrets</code> list into the pod's own list automatically at pod creation time — you don't have to declare <code>imagePullSecrets</code> in every Deployment/Pod manifest. Patch it once on the SA and every current and future pod using that SA inherits registry access.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">attach-imagepullsecret.sh</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Create the registry credential once</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.easypj.io \
  --docker-username=ci-bot \
  --docker-password=$REGISTRY_TOKEN \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 2. Attach to the SA — every pod using easypj-web-sa now inherits it</span>
kubectl patch sa easypj-web-sa -n easypj \
  -p '{"imagePullSecrets":[{"name":"regcred"}]}'</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the standard fix for <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> against a private registry when many workloads in a namespace pull from the same registry — patch the SA once instead of touching N manifests.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Conceptually, how could a cloud provider's IAM system (e.g. AWS IRSA) trust a token that a Kubernetes cluster issued, without the cluster ever sharing a static cloud credential?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The cluster publishes an OIDC discovery document and its ServiceAccount token signing public keys at a well-known issuer URL. The cloud IAM system is configured to trust that issuer. When a pod presents its projected SA token (a standard JWT) to the cloud API, the cloud provider verifies the JWT's signature against the cluster's published public key, checks the audience matches the cloud service, and — if valid — exchanges it for temporary cloud credentials scoped to whatever IAM role is mapped to that SA's subject.</p>
                        <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   easypj-worker pod (SA: easypj-batch-sa)
        │ presents projected JWT (aud: sts.amazonaws.com)
        ▼
   AWS STS ── verifies signature via cluster's OIDC public keys ──▶ IAM role trust policy
        │ matches sub: system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-batch-sa
        ▼
   Temporary AWS credentials (no static keys ever touched the pod)
</pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the mechanism behind AWS IRSA, and similar patterns exist for GCP Workload Identity and Azure AD Workload Identity. The CKA doesn't test cloud-specific IAM, but understanding that a bound SA token is just a standard, verifiable JWT explains why this federation is possible at all — no cloud secrets are ever baked into a pod.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate proposes binding <code>cluster-admin</code> to a ServiceAccount so a monitoring pod can "just read everything without hassle." What's wrong with this, and what should you do instead?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>cluster-admin</code> grants unrestricted access to every verb on every resource, cluster-wide — including <code>delete</code>, <code>create</code>, and Secrets in every namespace. A monitoring workload that only needs to <code>get</code>/<code>list</code>/<code>watch</code> metrics-relevant resources (pods, nodes, or custom metrics) doesn't need write access anywhere, let alone delete rights. If that monitoring pod is ever compromised (a dependency CVE, a malicious scrape target), the attacker inherits full cluster control.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Built-in ClusterRole</th><th>get/list/watch</th><th>create/update/patch</th><th>delete</th><th>Secrets</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>view</code></td><td>✅</td><td>❌</td><td>❌</td><td>❌ (excluded by design)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>edit</code></td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅ (most resources)</td><td>✅</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>cluster-admin</code></td><td>✅</td><td>✅ everywhere</td><td>✅ everywhere</td><td>✅ everywhere</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="scenario-box positive">
                            <h5>✅ Correct approach</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Create a purpose-built ClusterRole with only the verbs/resources actually needed (e.g. <code>get, list, watch</code> on <code>pods, nodes, services</code>)</li>
                                <li>Bind it with a ClusterRoleBinding (or RoleBinding per-namespace if scope allows) to the monitoring SA specifically</li>
                                <li>Verify with <code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:monitoring:prometheus-sa</code></li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the least-privilege principle in action — "just read everything without hassle" is exactly the kind of convenience shortcut the CKA (and real security reviews) expects you to push back on. Kubernetes ships a built-in <code>view</code> ClusterRole that's usually a much closer fit than <code>cluster-admin</code> for read-only monitoring use cases.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

