                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Every easypj pod authenticates to the API server as its ServiceAccount, presenting an auto-mounted, projected token that is time-limited, audience-bound, and auto-rotated by the kubelet — a sharp upgrade over the old, never-expiring Secret-based tokens. That identity starts with <strong>zero permissions</strong>: a ServiceAccount is just a name until you bind a Role or ClusterRole to it via a RoleBinding using the subject <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> keep the namespace's <code>default</code> SA unprivileged (it's auto-mounted into every pod that doesn't specify its own), create one dedicated, least-privilege SA per application, disable the token mount entirely for pods that never call the API, and always verify the exact permission set with <code>kubectl auth can-i --as=system:serviceaccount:...</code> rather than assuming. This is the identity layer Part 7's RBAC (Ch 24) and PKI (Ch 26) build directly on top of.</p>
