            <!-- ─────────── §26.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.1 Kubernetes PKI Overview</h3>
                <p><strong>PKI (Public Key Infrastructure)</strong> is the system of certificates that secure all communication in a Kubernetes cluster. At the top is a <strong>Root Certificate Authority (CA)</strong> that signs certificates for every cluster component.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Analogy: The Cluster CA Is a Passport Office</h5>
                    <p>Think of the cluster CA as a passport office. Every component carries a "passport" (its leaf certificate) stamped by that one office. When two components meet — say kubelet talking to the apiserver — each checks the other's passport against the office's seal (the CA certificate); there's no central login server they both have to call out to. An admin user is nothing special in this model: just another passport holder whose CN field happens to spell out their username instead of a component name.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes PKI Architecture — The Certificate Tree</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                      KUBERNETES PKI HIERARCHY                                │
  │                                                                              │
  │                    ┌──────────────────────┐                                  │
  │                    │   🏛️ ROOT CA          │                                  │
  │                    │   /etc/kubernetes/    │                                  │
  │                    │   pki/ca.crt          │                                  │
  │                    │       ca.key          │                                  │
  │                    └──────────┬───────────┘                                  │
  │                               │ signs                                        │
  │          ┌────────────────────┼────────────────────┐                         │
  │          │                    │                    │                         │
  │          ▼                    ▼                    ▼                         │
  │  ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐                 │
  │  │ 🌐 API Server │   │ 💾 etcd       │   │ 📡 kubelet    │                 │
  │  │               │   │               │   │               │                 │
  │  │ apiserver.crt │   │ server.crt   │   │ kubelet.crt   │                 │
  │  │ apiserver.key │   │ server.key   │   │ kubelet.key   │                 │
  │  │               │   │               │   │               │                 │
  │  │ Also needs:   │   │ peer.crt     │   │ Authenticates │                 │
  │  │ kubelet-client│   │ peer.key     │   │ to API server │                 │
  │  │ .crt/.key     │   │               │   └───────────────┘                 │
  │  └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘                                      │
  │          │                                                                   │
  │          │ also serves as client to:                                         │
  │          ▼                                                                   │
  │  ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐                 │
  │  │ 📋 scheduler  │   │ 🔄 controller │   │ 🌐 front-proxy│                 │
  │  │               │   │   manager     │   │               │                 │
  │  │ Uses kubeconf │   │ Uses kubeconf │   │ front-proxy-  │                 │
  │  │ with embedded │   │ with embedded │   │ ca.crt/key    │                 │
  │  │ client cert   │   │ client cert   │   │ (aggregation) │                 │
  │  └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘                 │
  │                                                                              │
  │  📁 All certificates live under: /etc/kubernetes/pki/                        │
  │  📁 etcd certs specifically: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>Encryption</h5>
                        <p>All component communication uses TLS — no plaintext data ever crosses the network.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🪪</div>
                        <h5>Identity</h5>
                        <p>Certificates prove component identity. The API server verifies kubelet is who it claims to be.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🤝</div>
                        <h5>Mutual TLS</h5>
                        <p>Both sides present certificates. API server authenticates kubelet AND kubelet authenticates API server.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Two DIFFERENT Auth Mechanisms — Don't Confuse Them</h5>
                    <p>This chapter's x509 client certs are ONE of two totally different ways a caller proves identity to the apiserver. Chapter 25 covered the other: ServiceAccount bearer tokens. They're not interchangeable, and mixing up which one applies to which caller is a common source of confusion.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>x509 client cert (this chapter)</th><th>ServiceAccount token (Ch 25)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Typical holder</td><td>Humans/admins, control-plane components</td><td>In-cluster workloads (Pods calling the API)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Format on the wire</td><td>X.509 certificate, presented during the TLS handshake itself</td><td>JWT bearer token in the <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> HTTP header</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Identity encoded as</td><td>Subject CN (user) / O (group)</td><td><code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Lifetime &amp; revocation</td><td>~1y expiry; revoke by not renewing / deleting the CSR trust</td><td>Short-lived (~1h) &amp; auto-rotated by the kubelet by default; revoke by deleting the SA or unbinding RBAC</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Issued by</td><td>Cluster CA, via the CertificateSigningRequest API (this chapter)</td><td>The TokenRequest API, auto-mounted into the Pod (Ch 25)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> both mechanisms feed the SAME downstream RBAC engine — a (user, groups) tuple either way — so a Role/RoleBinding never cares whether the caller authenticated with a cert or a token. What differs is entirely upstream, at the authentication step.</div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.2 Certificate Locations and Purpose</h3>
                <p>Every certificate in the Kubernetes cluster has a specific purpose. For the CKA, you must know where they live and what they do.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Where x509 auth fits in the FULL API request pipeline</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  kubectl create secret generic db-pass --from-literal=pw=hunter2
       │
       ▼
  ┌──────────────┐   ┌──────────────┐   ┌──────────────────────┐
  │ 🔐 AUTHN     │──▶│ ✅ AUTHZ     │──▶│ 🔍 ADMISSION         │
  │ THIS CHAPTER │   │ Ch 24 — RBAC │   │ Ch 27 — Pod Security  │
  │              │   │              │   │                       │
  │ x509 client  │   │ Role/Cluster │   │ Mutating: add         │
  │ cert (or SA  │   │ Role bindings│   │  defaults/sidecars    │
  │ token, Ch 25)│   │ decide CAN?  │   │ Validating: PSA,      │
  │ decides WHO? │   │              │   │  quotas, webhooks     │
  └──────────────┘   └──────────────┘   └──────────┬────────────┘
                                                     │
                                          ┌──────────▼───────────┐
                                          │ 💾 PERSIST TO ETCD   │
                                          │ (Ch 29 — own PKI too)│
                                          └───────────────────────┘

  ❌ reject at ANY stage → request denied      ✅ all stages pass → written to etcd
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> this chapter only ever answers the FIRST question — <em>who are you?</em> A cert that's perfectly valid and unexpired can still get a <code>403 Forbidden</code> at the AuthZ stage (wrong/missing RoleBinding, Ch 24) or get rejected at Admission (namespace doesn't meet the Pod Security Standard, Ch 27). Don't waste exam time re-checking a cert that's already authenticating fine — read the error message to know WHICH stage actually rejected the request.</div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>File</th>
                                <th>Used By</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>ca.crt</code> / <code>ca.key</code></td><td>Cluster CA</td><td>Root CA — signs all other certificates</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiserver.crt</code> / <code>.key</code></td><td>API Server</td><td>Serve HTTPS on port 6443</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiserver-kubelet-client.crt</code></td><td>API Server</td><td>API server authenticating to kubelets</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiserver-etcd-client.crt</code></td><td>API Server</td><td>API server authenticating to etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>etcd/server.crt</code> / <code>.key</code></td><td>etcd</td><td>etcd serving TLS to API server</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>etcd/peer.crt</code> / <code>.key</code></td><td>etcd</td><td>etcd-to-etcd peer communication (HA)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>front-proxy-ca.crt</code></td><td>Aggregation</td><td>API aggregation layer (metrics server)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sa.pub</code> / <code>sa.key</code></td><td>SA tokens</td><td>Signing/verifying ServiceAccount tokens</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">inspect-certificates.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all certificate files</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  ca.key  apiserver.crt  apiserver.key  ...</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  ca.key  server.crt  server.key  peer.crt  peer.key</span>

<span class="comment"># Inspect API server certificate details</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout

<span class="comment"># Key fields to check:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Issuer: kubernetes-ca (the CA that signed it)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Subject: CN=kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not After: expiry date</span>
<span class="comment"># - Subject Alternative Names (SANs): all valid hostnames/IPs</span>

<span class="comment"># Check who signed a certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -issuer -noout
<span class="comment"># issuer=CN = kubernetes-ca     (older kubeadm builds: plain "kubernetes")</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify cert is signed by the CA</span>
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
  /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
<span class="comment"># /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt: OK</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Certificate File Layout on Disk</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  /etc/kubernetes/
  ├── pki/                                 ◀── Main PKI directory
  │   ├── ca.crt                           ◀── Root CA certificate
  │   ├── ca.key                           ◀── Root CA private key 🔒
  │   ├── apiserver.crt                    ◀── API server TLS cert
  │   ├── apiserver.key                    ◀── API server private key 🔒
  │   ├── apiserver-kubelet-client.crt     ◀── API→kubelet client cert
  │   ├── apiserver-kubelet-client.key     ◀── API→kubelet private key 🔒
  │   ├── apiserver-etcd-client.crt        ◀── API→etcd client cert
  │   ├── apiserver-etcd-client.key        ◀── API→etcd private key 🔒
  │   ├── front-proxy-ca.crt              ◀── Aggregation CA
  │   ├── front-proxy-ca.key              ◀── Aggregation CA key 🔒
  │   ├── front-proxy-client.crt          ◀── Aggregation client cert
  │   ├── front-proxy-client.key          ◀── Aggregation client key 🔒
  │   ├── sa.pub                           ◀── SA token verification key
  │   ├── sa.key                           ◀── SA token signing key 🔒
  │   └── etcd/                            ◀── etcd-specific certs
  │       ├── ca.crt                       ◀── etcd CA (may be separate)
  │       ├── ca.key
  │       ├── server.crt                   ◀── etcd server TLS
  │       ├── server.key
  │       ├── peer.crt                     ◀── etcd peer communication
  │       ├── peer.key
  │       ├── healthcheck-client.crt       ◀── etcd health checks
  │       └── healthcheck-client.key
  ├── admin.conf                           ◀── cluster-admin kubeconfig
  ├── controller-manager.conf              ◀── controller-manager kubeconfig
  ├── scheduler.conf                       ◀── scheduler kubeconfig
  └── kubelet.conf                         ◀── kubelet kubeconfig
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Private Keys Are Secrets</h5>
                    <p>The <code>.key</code> files are private keys — anyone with access can impersonate that component. In production, restrict access to <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> to root only. On the CKA exam, you'll SSH into the control plane node to read these files.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>File type</th><th>Correct permission</th><th>What breaks if it drifts</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Certificates (<code>.crt</code>)</td><td><code>644</code> (world-readable)</td><td>Too restrictive → components can't even read their own public cert and fail to start</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Private keys (<code>.key</code>)</td><td><code>600</code>, owned by <code>root</code></td><td>Too permissive (e.g. <code>644</code>) → any local user can read <code>apiserver.key</code> and forge apiserver identity; <code>kube-bench</code>/CIS scans flag this immediately</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production Nuance: Perms Drift on Manual Copies, Not on kubeadm Itself</h5>
                    <p>kubeadm always writes keys with <code>600</code> automatically — the drift almost always happens AFTER the fact, when an admin manually <code>scp</code>s or <code>tar</code>s the <code>pki/</code> directory to a backup location or a second control-plane node and the copy tool doesn't preserve permissions (or worse, extracts as world-readable). A CIS benchmark scan (kube-bench) or a simple <code>find /etc/kubernetes/pki -name '*.key' ! -perm 600</code> audit catches this before an attacker does.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>X.509 Certificate Fields — What to Read</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👤</span> Subject (CN)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>CN=kube-apiserver</code> — The Common Name identifies the component. For user certs, CN becomes the username in Kubernetes RBAC.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👥</span> Subject (O)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>O=system:masters</code> — The Organization becomes the group. <code>system:masters</code> is a hard-coded superuser group that bypasses RBAC entirely (see the <code>admin.conf</code> vs <code>super-admin.conf</code> callout below — modern kubeadm no longer puts every admin cert in this group by default).</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏛️</span> Issuer</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>CN=kubernetes-ca</code> — the default Common Name kubeadm gives the cluster CA (older kubeadm builds and some exam sandboxes may still show the legacy plain <code>CN=kubernetes</code>). All certs signed by the SAME CA show this identical issuer string — apiserver and apiserver-kubelet-client share an issuer; etcd's certs show <code>CN=etcd-ca</code> instead, and the aggregation certs show <code>CN=kubernetes-front-proxy-ca</code> — three independent issuer strings for three independent trust chains.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📅</span> Not After</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Expiry date. Default 1 year for component certs. <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows all expiry dates at once.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> SANs</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Subject Alternative Names — list of valid hostnames &amp; IPs (<code>kubernetes</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc</code>, <code>10.96.0.1</code>, control plane IP).</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>admin.conf</code> vs <code>super-admin.conf</code> — a Recent kubeadm Security Hardening</h5>
                    <p>Modern kubeadm generates <strong>two</strong> distinct admin kubeconfigs during <code>kubeadm init</code>, not one — and they carry different identities:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Default CN</th><th>O (group)</th><th>Effective access</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>admin.conf</code></td><td><code>kubernetes-admin</code></td><td>a scoped admin group (bound to <code>cluster-admin</code> via a normal ClusterRoleBinding, e.g. <code>kubeadm:cluster-admins</code>)</td><td>Full access via RBAC — same practical permissions as before, but revocable by deleting/editing that ClusterRoleBinding</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>super-admin.conf</code></td><td><code>kubernetes-super-admin</code></td><td><code>system:masters</code></td><td>Bypasses RBAC entirely — cannot be locked out even by a broken/deleted ClusterRoleBinding</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>The point of the split: a certificate carrying the built-in <code>system:masters</code> group can never be denied by RBAC, which makes it the correct "break-glass" credential for disaster recovery but a dangerous one to leave lying around for everyday use. Day-to-day cluster administration should use <code>admin.conf</code> (revocable through normal RBAC); <code>super-admin.conf</code> should be locked away for the rare case where RBAC itself is misconfigured and you need a credential that ignores it.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.3 Certificate Signing Requests (CSR)</h3>
                <p>To add a new user to the cluster, you create a CSR and have the K8s CA sign it. This process is <strong>heavily tested on the CKA exam</strong>.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Generate Key</h5>
                        <p><code>openssl genrsa -out user.key 2048</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Create CSR</h5>
                        <p><code>openssl req -new -key user.key -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team"</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Submit to K8s</h5>
                        <p>Create CertificateSigningRequest YAML with base64-encoded CSR</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Approve</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl certificate approve dev-user</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Extract Cert</h5>
                        <p>Get the signed certificate from the CSR's <code>.status.certificate</code></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">csr-complete-workflow.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── COMPLETE CSR WORKFLOW — CKA EXAM PATTERN ──

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate a private key</span>
openssl genrsa -out dev-user.key 2048

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create a Certificate Signing Request</span>
<span class="comment"># CN = username, O = group (for RBAC)</span>
openssl req -new -key dev-user.key \
  -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team" \
  -out dev-user.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Encode the CSR in base64 (single line, no wrapping)</span>
CSR_BASE64=$(cat dev-user.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n')

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create the K8s CSR YAML</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
  name: dev-user
spec:
  request: ${CSR_BASE64}
  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
  usages:
  - client auth
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check CSR status</span>
kubectl get csr
<span class="comment"># NAME       AGE   SIGNERNAME                          CONDITION</span>
<span class="comment"># dev-user   5s    kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client  Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Approve the CSR</span>
kubectl certificate approve dev-user

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Extract the signed certificate</span>
kubectl get csr dev-user -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' \
  | base64 -d > dev-user.crt

<span class="comment"># Step 8: Verify the certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in dev-user.crt -text -noout
<span class="comment"># Subject: CN=dev-user, O=dev-team</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 9: Add user to kubeconfig</span>
kubectl config set-credentials dev-user \
  --client-certificate=dev-user.crt \
  --client-key=dev-user.key

kubectl config set-context dev-user@kubernetes \
  --cluster=kubernetes \
  --user=dev-user \
  --namespace=easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Shortcut: CSR Encoding</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>cat dev-user.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n'</code> to get the single-line base64 string for the CSR YAML. The <code>tr -d '\n'</code> removes line breaks that would break the YAML.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>CSR Approval Flow — From Key to Kubeconfig</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Generate Private Key</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>openssl genrsa -out user.key 2048</code> — creates RSA private key (stays with the user, never shared)</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Create CSR File</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>openssl req -new -key user.key -subj "/CN=user/O=group"</code> — CN becomes username, O becomes group in RBAC</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>Submit to Kubernetes</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Base64-encode the CSR → create CertificateSigningRequest YAML → <code>kubectl apply</code>. Status: <strong>Pending</strong></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Admin Approves</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>kubectl certificate approve user</code> — K8s CA signs the cert. Status: <strong>Approved, Issued</strong></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">5</span>
                                <h5>Extract &amp; Use</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Extract signed cert from <code>.status.certificate</code> → add to kubeconfig with <code>kubectl config set-credentials</code></p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗑️</div>
                        <h5>CSRs Clean Themselves Up</h5>
                        <p>You never have to manually <code>kubectl delete csr</code> in a healthy cluster. Approved, Denied, and Failed CSR objects are garbage-collected automatically <strong>1 hour</strong> after reaching that state; a CSR stuck <code>Pending</code> is garbage-collected after <strong>24 hours</strong>; and any CSR is removed once its issued certificate itself expires — whichever comes first.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔏</div>
                        <h5>Approving a CSR Is Itself an RBAC-Gated Action</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl certificate approve</code> isn't a free action available to anyone who can read CSRs — it requires <code>update</code> on the <code>certificatesigningrequests/approval</code> subresource PLUS the <code>approve</code> verb on the <code>signers</code> resource, scoped to the exact <code>signerName</code> (e.g. <code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code>). <code>cluster-admin</code> already has both baked in, which is why this is invisible in normal exam/lab use — but a scoped-down "CSR approver" role for a junior admin needs both grants explicitly.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.4 Checking Certificate Expiry</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes certificates expire after <strong>1 year</strong> by default. If they expire, cluster components stop communicating and the cluster goes down. Monitoring expiry is critical.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">check-expiry.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── METHOD 1: kubeadm (EASIEST — use on CKA exam) ──
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME   CERTIFICATE AUTHORITY   EXTERNALLY MANAGED</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d                                     no</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d            ca                       no</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client      Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d            etcd-ca                  no</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d            ca                       no</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager.conf    Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d                                     no</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-healthcheck-client    Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d            etcd-ca                  no</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-peer                  Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d            etcd-ca                  no</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-server                Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d            etcd-ca                  no</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client         Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d            front-proxy-ca           no</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf             Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d                                     no</span>

<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE AUTHORITY   EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME   EXTERNALLY MANAGED</span>
<span class="comment"># ca                      Apr 01, 2036 00:00 UTC   9y              no</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-ca                 Apr 01, 2036 00:00 UTC   9y              no</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-ca           Apr 01, 2036 00:00 UTC   9y              no</span>
<span class="comment"># NOTE: kubelet.conf never appears here — the kubelet self-manages its client cert</span>
<span class="comment"># lifecycle under /var/lib/kubelet/pki and auto-rotates it (see UTH ⑦ below).</span>

<span class="comment"># ── METHOD 2: openssl (manual — for specific cert) ──</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
  -noout -enddate
<span class="comment"># notAfter=Apr  1 00:00:00 2027 GMT</span>

<span class="comment"># ── METHOD 3: Check specific fields ──</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
  -noout -subject -issuer -dates
<span class="comment"># subject=CN = kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># issuer=CN = kubernetes-ca     (older kubeadm builds: plain "kubernetes")</span>
<span class="comment"># notBefore=Apr  1 00:00:00 2026 GMT</span>
<span class="comment"># notAfter=Apr  1 00:00:00 2027 GMT</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Reading the Real <code>check-expiration</code> Output</h5>
                    <p>The command actually prints <strong>two</strong> tables. The top one lists every leaf certificate/kubeconfig with a <code>CERTIFICATE AUTHORITY</code> column showing which CA signed it (<code>ca</code>, <code>etcd-ca</code>, or <code>front-proxy-ca</code>) — the fastest way to see at a glance that there are three independent trust chains under one directory. The bottom table lists the CAs themselves — this is where you'd notice a CA is approaching its own ~10-year expiry, a far rarer and much bigger event than a routine leaf renewal. The <code>EXTERNALLY MANAGED</code> column reads <code>yes</code> only when that certificate's CA has no private key on disk locally (external-CA mode, UTH ③) — kubeadm cannot renew those for you.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Expired Certificates = Cluster Down</h5>
                    <p>If the API server certificate expires, <code>kubectl</code> stops working entirely. If etcd certificates expire, the API server can't read/write state. <strong>Always check expiry at least monthly in production.</strong> On the CKA exam, <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> is the answer.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production Nuance: Don't Rely on a Human Remembering</h5>
                    <p>"Check monthly" only works if someone actually does it every single month, forever — which is exactly the kind of manual process real incidents happen around. In production, wire the check into automation instead: a nightly <code>CronJob</code> running <code>openssl x509 -checkend 2592000</code> (alerts if any cert expires within 30 days) posting to Slack, or a <strong>Prometheus <code>blackbox_exporter</code></strong> probe scraping <code>probe_ssl_earliest_cert_expiry</code> with an Alertmanager rule. Either way, the goal is the same: get a calm 30-day-advance warning instead of a 2am page when the apiserver's TLS handshake starts failing cluster-wide.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">"x509: certificate..." — a diagnostic decision tree for WHICH cert</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Symptom: an x509 error somewhere. WHERE did it come from?
  │
  ├─ kubectl itself fails immediately ──────▶ check ~/.kube/config's embedded
  │                                            client cert (admin.conf stale)
  │
  ├─ kubectl reaches the API but a SPECIFIC ─▶ check that component's OWN cert:
  │  component's actions fail (e.g.           apiserver-etcd-client.crt,
  │  "unable to connect to etcd")             apiserver-kubelet-client.crt, etc.
  │
  ├─ kubelet logs show TLS errors, pod ─────▶ check kubelet.conf / kubelet's
  │  operations (exec/logs) fail              client+serving certs (§UTH ⑦)
  │
  ├─ etcd logs show "tls: bad certificate" ─▶ check etcd/server.crt + etcd/peer.crt
  │  or peers can't reach quorum              — REMEMBER: etcd's CA is separate!
  │
  └─ ALL of the above at once, whole ───────▶ start with kubeadm certs
     cluster is down                          check-expiration — probably renew all
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> resist the urge to blanket <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> the instant you see ANY x509 error — under exam time pressure, ask "which component logged this?" first. A single mis-scoped cert (e.g. only <code>apiserver-etcd-client</code>) needs only <code>kubeadm certs renew apiserver-etcd-client</code>, which is faster and lower-risk than touching every cert in the cluster.</div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.5 Renewing Certificates</h3>
                <p>Certificate renewal is a critical cluster maintenance task and a common CKA exam topic.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">renew-certificates.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Renew ALL certificates at once ──
kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># [renew] Reading configuration from the cluster...</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate embedded in admin.conf renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver-kubelet-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Renew a SPECIFIC certificate ──</span>
kubeadm certs renew apiserver
kubeadm certs renew apiserver-kubelet-client
kubeadm certs renew etcd-server

<span class="comment"># ── CRITICAL: Restart control plane after renewal ──</span>
<span class="comment"># Control plane pods load certs at startup — they need restart!</span>

<span class="comment"># Option A: If using static pods (kubeadm default)</span>
<span class="comment"># Move and restore manifests to trigger restart:</span>
cd /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
mv kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># Wait for pod to stop...</span>
mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml .
<span class="comment"># Repeat for controller-manager, scheduler, etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># Option B: Force restart via crictl</span>
crictl pods --name kube-apiserver -q | xargs crictl stopp
crictl pods --name kube-controller-manager -q | xargs crictl stopp
crictl pods --name kube-scheduler -q | xargs crictl stopp

<span class="comment"># ── Verify renewal worked ──</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># All dates should now show ~1 year from today</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
