                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Don't Forget kubeconfig!</h5>
                    <p>After renewing certs, the kubeconfig files (<code>admin.conf</code>, etc.) also get updated. Copy the new admin.conf to <code>~/.kube/config</code> or <code>kubectl</code> will stop working with "certificate expired" errors!</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Automatic Renewal</h5>
                    <p><code>kubeadm upgrade</code> automatically renews all certificates during a cluster upgrade. If you upgrade regularly (recommended), you may never need manual renewal.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The 1y/10y Defaults Are Configurable, Not Hard-Coded</h5>
                    <p>Every "1 year" / "~10 year" figure in this chapter is kubeadm's out-of-the-box default, not a hard limit. A kubeadm <code>ClusterConfiguration</code> can override both explicitly:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubeadm-config.yaml excerpt</span></div>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kubeadm.k8s.io/v1beta4
kind: ClusterConfiguration
certificateValidityPeriod: 8760h    <span class="comment"># leaf certs — default 365d, Go duration format (h = hours, the largest unit)</span>
caCertificateValidityPeriod: 87600h <span class="comment"># the CA itself — default 3650d (10y)</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>A tighter <code>certificateValidityPeriod</code> (e.g. 90 days) shrinks the blast radius of a leaked leaf cert at the cost of needing renewal automation to run far more often — exactly the trade-off a security-conscious production team weighs, but almost never something the CKA exam itself asks you to configure.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Cross-Reference: Chapter 28 (Cluster Upgrades)</h5>
                    <p><code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> and <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> renew ALL certificates as a side effect by default — even ones nowhere close to expiry. This is usually a welcome bonus, but if your org has a strict change-control policy that treats cert rotation as its own controlled event, you can opt out with <code>--certificate-renewal=false</code> so the upgrade touches only binaries/manifests and leaves the existing certs alone. See Chapter 28 for the full upgrade procedure and version-skew rules.</p>
                </div>

