            <h5>① The Certificates API Signers — who is allowed to sign what</h5>
            <p>Every CertificateSigningRequest names a <code>signerName</code>, and the built-in controller-manager only honors a fixed set of them. Pick the wrong signer and the CSR is either rejected outright or gets approved-but-unusable — the certificate comes back with the wrong key usages baked in.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  BUILT-IN CERTIFICATE SIGNERS — certificates.k8s.io/v1                       │
│                                                                              │
│  kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client                                        │
│    └─▶ human/service client certs that authenticate TO the apiserver         │
│         usages: ["client auth"]           our CSR from §26.3 uses this       │
│                                                                              │
│  kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client-kubelet                                │
│    └─▶ kubelet's OWN client cert (kubelet → apiserver, TLS bootstrap)        │
│         usages: ["client auth"]           auto-approved by node-approver     │
│                                                                              │
│  kubernetes.io/kubelet-serving                                              │
│    └─▶ kubelet's SERVING cert (apiserver → kubelet, e.g. kubectl exec/logs)  │
│         usages: ["server auth"]           NOT auto-approved by default       │
│                                                                              │
│  kubernetes.io/legacy-unknown                                               │
│    └─▶ pre-1.19 clusters / manual signing outside the built-in flow          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>signerName</th><th>Used for</th><th>Auto-approved?</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>kube-apiserver-client</code></td><td>Human/app users authenticating to the API</td><td>No — an admin must run <code>kubectl certificate approve</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>kube-apiserver-client-kubelet</code></td><td>A new node's kubelet requesting its own client identity</td><td>Yes, via the node-authorizer (kubeadm TLS bootstrap)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>kubelet-serving</code></td><td>The kubelet's HTTPS serving cert for exec/logs/port-forward</td><td>No by default — a common CKA gotcha with new nodes</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if the CSR YAML in §26.3 used <code>kubernetes.io/kubelet-serving</code> by mistake instead of <code>kube-apiserver-client</code>, <code>kubectl certificate approve</code> would still succeed — but the resulting cert would carry <code>server auth</code> usage, not <code>client auth</code>, and your kubeconfig would fail to authenticate. Always match the signer to the actual use case.</div>

            <h5>② Key Usages — client auth vs server auth vs digital signature</h5>
            <p>A certificate's <strong>Extended Key Usage (EKU)</strong> extension is what actually restricts what it can be used for — TLS libraries enforce this at the protocol level, independent of any Kubernetes logic. This is why "approve the CSR" alone isn't the whole story: the <code>usages</code> field you request must match the role the cert will play.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   CSR spec.usages           what it enables                    typical holder
   ────────────────────────  ─────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────
   ["client auth"]           presents this cert as a CLIENT      users, kubelet→API
                              during a TLS handshake — the
                              PEER verifies it (that's YOU
                              proving your identity)

   ["server auth"]           presents this cert as a SERVER      apiserver, kubelet
                              — must carry SANs (DNS/IP) that     (serving), etcd
                              match the hostname being dialed

   ["digital signature",     low-level building blocks — most    rarely requested
    "key encipherment"]      "client auth"/"server auth" combos  directly
                              imply these under the hood
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Cert</th><th>Needs</th><th>Because</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>apiserver.crt</td><td><code>server auth</code> + SANs</td><td>kubelets/kubectl connect TO it and check the hostname</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>apiserver-kubelet-client.crt</td><td><code>client auth</code></td><td>apiserver connects TO a kubelet, proving ITS identity</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>a new user's cert (§26.3)</td><td><code>client auth</code> only</td><td>they only ever dial IN to the apiserver, never serve TLS</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> "server auth" ↔ SANs go together — a serving cert MUST list every hostname/IP clients will use to reach it, or they'll reject it with <code>x509: certificate is valid for X, not Y</code>. That's exactly the error you fix with <code>--apiserver-cert-extra-sans</code> when adding a load balancer.</div>

            <h5>③ External-CA Mode — running kubeadm without the CA private key</h5>
            <p>By default kubeadm keeps <code>ca.key</code> right next to <code>ca.crt</code> on disk, so it can sign new leaf certs itself. Some security-hardened environments instead keep the CA's private key in an offline HSM or a separate PKI team's system — kubeadm calls this <strong>external CA mode</strong>: only <code>ca.crt</code> (the public cert) exists locally, <code>ca.key</code> is absent.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NORMAL MODE                          EXTERNAL-CA MODE                       │
│  /etc/kubernetes/pki/                 /etc/kubernetes/pki/                   │
│  ├── ca.crt                           ├── ca.crt          (present)          │
│  └── ca.key   ◀── present             └── ca.key          ✗ ABSENT           │
│                                                                              │
│  kubeadm certs renew apiserver        kubeadm certs renew apiserver          │
│  ✅ signs a NEW leaf itself using      ❌ FAILS — no private key to sign      │
│     the local ca.key                     with; you must submit a CSR to      │
│                                           the external PKI team/HSM out of    │
│                                           band and install the result        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if <code>kubeadm certs renew</code> fails with something like "certificate authority does not have a private key," check <code>ls /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.key</code> — a missing key means external-CA mode, and renewal must go through the org's out-of-band signing process instead of the one-command kubeadm path.</div>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 The Actual Out-of-Band Flow: <code>--csr-only</code></h5>
                <p>External-CA mode isn't purely manual — kubeadm has a dedicated flag for exactly this handoff: <code>kubeadm certs renew apiserver --csr-only --csr-dir=/tmp/kubeadm-csr</code> generates the CSR file(s) into that directory <em>without</em> trying to self-sign, ready to send to the external PKI team or HSM. Once they return signed certs, you install them into <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> in place of the old ones and restart the affected component as usual. On the signing side, the org's separate controller-manager instance would typically run standalone with <code>--controllers=csrsigner</code> pointed at their own CA cert/key — a detail worth recognizing conceptually even though configuring it yourself is well beyond CKA scope.</p>
            </div>

            <h5>④ The front-proxy CA — a SEPARATE trust chain for API aggregation</h5>
            <p>Extension API servers (like the metrics-server, which registers <code>v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io</code> as an APIService) don't go through the main cluster CA at all. The apiserver proxies requests to them through the <strong>aggregation layer</strong>, authenticated by a completely separate CA pair: <code>front-proxy-ca.crt</code>/<code>.key</code> and <code>front-proxy-client.crt</code>/<code>.key</code>.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   kubectl top pods -n easypj
        │
        ▼
   📡 kube-apiserver (main entry point — authenticated by the CLUSTER CA as usual)
        │  "this path belongs to an APIService, not a native resource"
        ▼
   🔀 AGGREGATION LAYER — proxies to the extension apiserver, presenting
      front-proxy-client.crt (signed by front-proxy-ca.crt, NOT the cluster CA)
        │
        ▼
   📊 metrics-server (extension apiserver) — trusts requests bearing a cert
      signed by front-proxy-ca.crt, reads the impersonated user from a header
        │
        ▼
   returns pod CPU/memory → apiserver → kubectl
            </pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> don't confuse <code>front-proxy-ca.crt</code> with <code>ca.crt</code> when troubleshooting metrics-server or any custom APIService — "unable to handle the request" / "no route to host" style aggregation errors point at the front-proxy chain and the extension apiserver's own TLS config, not the main cluster CA.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Rotating the Cluster CA — the hard problem nobody wants</h5>
            <p>Renewing a <em>leaf</em> cert is a routine one-command operation. Rotating the <strong>CA itself</strong> is a different order of magnitude: every component and every admin kubeconfig trusts the OLD CA cert, so you can't just swap it — anything still holding the old CA cert would reject certs signed by the new one.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  WHY YOU CAN'T JUST SWAP THE CA                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  1. Generate new-ca.crt/new-ca.key                                          │
│  2. Every component's TRUST STORE still only has OLD ca.crt                  │
│     → certs signed by new-ca.key are REJECTED everywhere, instantly          │
│                                                                              │
│  SAFE PATTERN: overlap / bundle, never a hard cutover                        │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Phase 1: distribute a BUNDLE (old ca.crt + new ca.crt concatenated) │    │
│  │          to every node/component's trust store — both are trusted   │    │
│  │ Phase 2: re-issue every leaf cert, signed by the NEW ca.key          │    │
│  │ Phase 3: once nothing presents an old-CA-signed cert anymore,        │    │
│  │          remove the OLD ca.crt from the bundle                       │    │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> the exam won't ask you to perform a full CA rotation (it's a multi-hour, multi-node production procedure), but it WILL test that you understand <em>why</em> "just regenerate ca.crt" is wrong — the correct mental model is bundle-then-retire, never an instant swap.</div>

            <h5>⑥ cert-manager — automating APPLICATION TLS (not control-plane certs)</h5>
            <p>Everything in this chapter so far is <strong>control-plane</strong> PKI, managed by <code>kubeadm</code>. A totally different, much more common production need is TLS for YOUR applications — e.g. serving <code>https://jobpost.easypj.com</code> through an Ingress. That's the job of <strong>cert-manager</strong>, a separate CNCF project you install as an add-on.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   cert-manager CRDs — automating APPLICATION certificates
   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Issuer / ClusterIssuer   ── WHO signs: Let's Encrypt, an internal CA,   │
   │                              Vault, or a self-signed root                │
   │            │                                                            │
   │            ▼                                                            │
   │  Certificate (CRD)        ── WHAT to request: dnsNames, secretName,      │
   │                              renewBefore                                 │
   │            │                                                            │
   │            ▼                                                            │
   │  cert-manager controller  ── solves an ACME HTTP-01/DNS-01 challenge     │
   │                              (for Let's Encrypt), gets the cert signed,  │
   │                              writes it to a Secret, and auto-RENEWS it   │
   │                              before expiry — no kubeadm involved         │
   │            │                                                            │
   │            ▼                                                            │
   │  Ingress references the Secret via tls.secretName → HTTPS for jobpost   │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th></th><th>kubeadm PKI (this chapter)</th><th>cert-manager</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Secures</td><td>Control plane ↔ control plane, node ↔ control plane</td><td>Your app's external HTTPS (Ingress), internal webhooks</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Managed by</td><td><code>kubeadm certs</code> commands, manual restart</td><td>A controller running IN the cluster, fully automated</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>CKA relevance</td><td>Heavily tested — CSR flow, expiry, renewal</td><td>Conceptual awareness only; deep dive is CKAD/CKS territory</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> if a question mentions Let's Encrypt, ACME, an Issuer/ClusterIssuer, or automatic HTTPS renewal for an Ingress, that's cert-manager — a completely different system from the <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> control-plane certs this chapter covers. Don't reach for <code>kubeadm certs renew</code> to fix an expired Ingress cert.</div>

            <h5>⑦ Kubelet TLS Bootstrap &amp; Automatic Certificate Rotation — the ONE cert kubeadm doesn't hand-manage</h5>
            <p>Everything renewed so far in this chapter is a manual, admin-triggered operation (<code>kubeadm certs renew</code>). The kubelet's OWN client certificate is different: kubeadm sets it up to <strong>self-service</strong> both its initial issuance (bootstrap) and its ongoing renewal (rotation), entirely through the CSR API — no admin ever runs <code>kubeadm certs renew kubelet</code>, because that command doesn't exist. This is why <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code>'s output never lists a plain "kubelet" row: the kubelet's client cert isn't a kubeadm-managed PKI file at all, it's a live CSR the kubelet renews for itself.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUBELET TLS BOOTSTRAP — how a brand-new node gets its FIRST client cert     │
│                                                                              │
│  1. kubeadm join &lt;ip&gt;:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef                  │
│         │  the --token is a BOOTSTRAP TOKEN — a Secret of type               │
│         │  bootstrap.kubernetes.io/token in kube-system, NOT a real cert     │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  2. kubelet authenticates to the apiserver AS the bootstrap token            │
│     (group: system:bootstrappers) — enough identity to do exactly ONE thing  │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  3. kubelet auto-generates a key + submits its own CSR                       │
│     signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client-kubelet                  │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  4. the built-in NODE-AUTHORIZER controller auto-approves ONLY this          │
│     specific signer for this specific group — no human runs                 │
│     `kubectl certificate approve`                                           │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  5. kubelet receives its signed client cert (CN=system:node:&lt;name&gt;,         │
│     O=system:nodes) → deletes the bootstrap token's usefulness,             │
│     switches to using this cert for every future apiserver call             │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════   │
│  ONGOING ROTATION — the client cert renews itself before it expires          │
│                                                                              │
│  kubelet watches its own cert's Not-After date → at ~70-90% of its           │
│  lifetime, silently submits a NEW CSR (same signer) → node-authorizer        │
│  auto-approves again → kubelet hot-swaps to the new cert, ZERO downtime,     │
│  ZERO admin action, ZERO entry in `kubeadm certs check-expiration`           │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Certificate</th><th>Bootstrap (first issuance)</th><th>Rotation (renewal)</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>kubelet CLIENT cert<br><code>kube-apiserver-client-kubelet</code></td><td>Auto-approved by the node-authorizer using the bootstrap token's identity</td><td>Automatic and default-ON — kubelet self-renews, no admin step, ever</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>kubelet SERVING cert<br><code>kubelet-serving</code></td><td>Also requestable via bootstrap, but <strong>NOT auto-approved</strong> by the stock controller-manager</td><td>Requires <code>serverTLSBootstrap: true</code> in kubelet config PLUS a separate approver (e.g. the <code>kubelet-csr-approver</code> add-on) — otherwise CSRs pile up <code>Pending</code> forever</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> two traps this hides. First, don't be alarmed that <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> never shows a "kubelet client cert" line for worker nodes — it genuinely renews itself, that's expected, not a gap in your check. Second, if <code>kubectl logs</code>/<code>exec</code>/<code>port-forward</code> against a node starts failing with a serving-cert TLS error, remember the kubelet SERVING cert (unlike the client cert) is <strong>not auto-approved out of the box</strong> — <code>kubectl get csr</code> will show a growing pile of <code>Pending</code> <code>kubelet-serving</code> requests that need either manual <code>kubectl certificate approve</code> or a proper auto-approver add-on installed.</div>

            <h5>⑧ Monitoring Certificate Expiry in Production — beyond running the command by hand</h5>
            <p>Running <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> manually every so often works for a lab, but it depends on a human remembering — exactly the failure mode that causes real outages. Production clusters wire expiry checks into automated monitoring so an alert fires WEEKS before anything actually breaks, not the moment <code>kubectl</code> starts throwing x509 errors.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  PRODUCTION EXPIRY MONITORING — three common patterns, cheapest to richest   │
│                                                                              │
│  1️⃣ CronJob wrapping openssl -checkend                                      │
│     openssl x509 -in apiserver.crt -noout -checkend 2592000 || alert!       │
│     (2592000s = 30 days — exits NON-ZERO if inside that window)             │
│     Cheapest: a shell script + Slack/email webhook, zero extra components    │
│                                                                              │
│  2️⃣ Prometheus blackbox_exporter (module: tcp_tls / http probes)            │
│     scrapes probe_ssl_earliest_cert_expiry, alert on:                       │
│       (probe_ssl_earliest_cert_expiry - time()) / 86400 < 14                │
│     Richest: dashboards + Alertmanager routing, works for APP certs too      │
│                                                                              │
│  3️⃣ kubeadm upgrade cadence as passive renewal                             │
│     regular upgrades silently renew ALL certs as a side effect — teams      │
│     upgrading every few months rarely see manual renewal needed at all      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Production Insight:</strong> the exam only tests that you can run <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> reactively. Real clusters need it proactively — a CronJob or blackbox_exporter alert 30 days out gives an admin a calm scheduled-maintenance window instead of a 2am full-outage page when the apiserver cert silently crosses its Not-After date.</div>

            <h5>⑨ The <code>kube-root-ca.crt</code> ConfigMap — and why the Certificates API is NOT automatically the cluster CA</h5>
            <p>Every namespace automatically contains a ConfigMap named <code>kube-root-ca.crt</code>, holding a CA bundle that every Pod's mounted ServiceAccount token expects when it verifies the apiserver's TLS certificate over the in-cluster <code>kubernetes.default.svc</code> endpoint. It's easy to assume this is simply "the cluster CA, handed to every namespace" — and in a stock kubeadm cluster it usually is — but the official Kubernetes documentation is explicit that you should <strong>never rely on that as a general rule</strong>.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  kube-root-ca.crt (every namespace) — VERIFY-ONLY, NOT A GENERAL-PURPOSE CA  │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ CORRECT use: a Pod's code verifies the in-cluster apiserver              │
│     (the Service named "kubernetes" in the default namespace) using this    │
│     ConfigMap's bundle — exactly what client-go / most SDKs do by default    │
│                                                                              │
│  ❌ WRONG assumption: "certificates.k8s.io CSRs are always signed by         │
│     THIS SAME root CA, so my app can trust kube-root-ca.crt for its own      │
│     custom TLS needs too"                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Official warning: certs issued via the CertificateSigningRequest API are    │
│  signed by a DEDICATED signing CA that the cluster operator configures      │
│  (in kubeadm's default setup, that dedicated CA happens to BE ca.crt/ca.key  │
│  — but nothing guarantees this on every cluster). Never assume a             │
│  CSR-issued cert validates against kube-root-ca.crt on someone else's        │
│  cluster without checking.                                                   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Right tool</th><th>Why NOT kube-root-ca.crt</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Verify the in-cluster apiserver from a Pod</td><td><code>kube-root-ca.crt</code> ConfigMap (already there, no setup)</td><td>— this IS its documented job</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Issue your OWN application's TLS certs (e.g. an internal service mesh)</td><td>Generate a separate CA, distribute it via your own ConfigMap</td><td>Docs explicitly warn against overloading the built-in bundle for anything beyond internal Kubernetes endpoints</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Trust CSR API-issued certs from custom code</td><td>Explicitly verify against whatever CA the cluster operator actually configured as the signing CA</td><td>Not guaranteed to be the same file as <code>kube-root-ca.crt</code> on every cluster, even though it usually is under kubeadm</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> for the exam, on a standard kubeadm cluster, "the CSR signer uses <code>ca.crt</code>" is a safe working assumption — that's exactly what <code>kube-controller-manager --cluster-signing-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt</code> wires up by default. The nuance to remember for production is that this is a kubeadm DEFAULT, not a Kubernetes API guarantee — a custom cluster setup could point the signer somewhere else entirely, so application code should never hard-code that assumption.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Kubernetes runs on MUTUAL TLS.</strong> A cluster CA (<code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt</code>, ~10y) signs client/server certs for the apiserver, kubelet, controller-manager, scheduler, and admins; etcd usually has its OWN CA.</li>
                    <li><strong>Every component has its own certificate</strong> — the default home for all of them is <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> (etcd certs live in the <code>etcd/</code> subdirectory).</li>
                    <li><strong>A client certificate's CN is the username and O is the group</strong> — that is how a cert maps to an RBAC identity. No separate "user object" exists anywhere in Kubernetes.</li>
                    <li><strong>New human access</strong> = generate a key+CSR (CN=user, O=group), submit a CertificateSigningRequest, <code>kubectl certificate approve</code> it, then embed the signed cert in a kubeconfig.</li>
                    <li><strong>Leaf certs expire (~1y).</strong> <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> reports them and <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> renews them — and you MUST restart the control plane / kubelet afterward so the new certs are actually loaded.</li>
                    <li><strong>The cluster CA (~10y) outlives leaf certs (~1y)</strong> — an expired leaf breaks its component even while the CA is perfectly valid.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">CSR <code>request</code> field takes the raw CSR file content</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">The <code>request</code> field requires <strong>base64-encoded</strong> CSR content</span></li>
                    <li><code>signerName</code> is <strong>required</strong> since K8s 1.22 — use <code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code> for user certs</li>
                    <li>Certificates expire after <strong>1 year</strong> by default; the <strong>CA cert is 10 years</strong> — <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows every date at once. If a cert expires, that component stops working</li>
                    <li>After renewing certs, you MUST <strong>restart control plane pods</strong> (they load certs at startup) — for static pods (apiserver, controller-manager, scheduler), move the manifest out of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>, wait, then move it back. This is the tested pattern</li>
                    <li>The CN (Common Name) in the CSR becomes the <strong>username</strong> in K8s. The O (Organization) becomes the <strong>group</strong></li>
                    <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> is the default cert location — memorize this path. API server: <code>apiserver.crt</code> / <code>apiserver.key</code>. CA: <code>ca.crt</code> / <code>ca.key</code>. etcd has its own directory: <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code></li>
                    <li>The exam may ask you to create a CSR for a new user, submit it, and approve it with <code>kubectl certificate approve &lt;csr-name&gt;</code> — don't forget the CSR object needs the right <code>signerName</code> (<code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code>)</li>
                    <li><strong><code>openssl x509 -in cert.crt -text -noout</code></strong> — memorize this for inspecting any cert. Look at Subject, Issuer, Not After, and SANs (Subject Alternative Names)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Certificate inspection one-liners:</strong><br>
                <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A2 "Validity"</code> — check expiry<br>
                <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A1 "Alternative"</code> — check SANs<br>
                <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> — all certs at once<br>
                <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> — renew everything (then restart control plane)</p>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — CSR round-trip, no scratch files:</strong><br>
                <code>base64 -w0 dev.csr</code> — no-wrap base64 in ONE flag (skip the <code>| tr -d '\n'</code> pipe entirely)<br>
                <code>kubectl get csr dev -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d &gt; dev.crt</code> — pull the signed cert straight off the object, no intermediate step<br>
                <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> — the fastest way to bounce EVERY static pod at once after a renewal, instead of moving manifests one at a time</p>
            </div>

