kubectl get csr dev -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d &gt; dev.crt
kubectl config set-credentials dev --client-certificate=dev.crt --client-key=dev.key
kubectl config set-context dev --cluster=kubernetes --user=dev</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CN sets the username, O sets the group for RBAC — there is no separate step to "create a user." Once the kubeconfig context exists, <code>dev</code> can authenticate, but they still get <code>Forbidden</code> on every request until a RoleBinding grants them something.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check when the cluster's certificates expire — both the fast way and the single-cert way?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Every kubeadm-managed cert + CA at once</td><td><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>One specific cert's dates</td><td><code>openssl x509 -noout -dates -in &lt;cert&gt;</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Script-friendly "will it expire soon?"</td><td><code>openssl x509 -noout -enddate -checkend 2592000 -in &lt;cert&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> is the CKA go-to — one table, every kubeadm cert, no guessing which files to inspect. <code>-checkend SECONDS</code> is the automation-friendly variant: it exits non-zero if the cert expires within that window, perfect for a monitoring cron job.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You ran <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code>. List every remaining step before the cluster is actually using the new certs.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Renew is only step 1 of 3</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  1️⃣ kubeadm certs renew all         ── writes new files to /etc/kubernetes/pki/
  2️⃣ systemctl restart kubelet       ── static pods reload certs from disk
      (or move manifests out of /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ and back)
  3️⃣ kubeadm init phase kubeconfig admin  ── refresh admin.conf, then
      cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Step 1 alone changes nothing observable — the apiserver process already has the OLD cert loaded in memory and keeps serving it until restarted. Step 3 is the most commonly forgotten: if your admin kubeconfig embedded the old client cert, <code>kubectl</code> itself starts failing with an x509 error even though the server side is fine.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where do the cluster's certificates live on disk? Name two files and what signs them.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Signed by</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt</code></td><td>self-signed (it IS the root)</td><td>Cluster CA — signs everything else</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt</code></td><td>cluster CA</td><td>apiserver serving cert</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</code></td><td>etcd's OWN CA (<code>etcd/ca.crt</code>)</td><td>etcd serving cert</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Everything hangs off <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> — but that directory contains TWO trust chains, not one: the cluster CA (apiserver, kubelet, scheduler, controller-manager, admins) and etcd's own separate CA in the <code>etcd/</code> subdirectory.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does a raw X.509 client certificate become a Kubernetes RBAC identity? Is there a "User" object you create?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>There is <strong>no User object</strong> anywhere in the Kubernetes API. The apiserver reads the client cert's Subject field directly: <strong>CN → username</strong>, each <strong>O → a group</strong>. RBAC's Role/ClusterRole bindings then authorize that (user, groups) tuple.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Certificate field</th><th>Becomes in Kubernetes</th><th>Example (from §26.3)</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>Subject CN</code></td><td>Username</td><td><code>CN=dev</code> → every request presenting this cert is treated as user <code>dev</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Subject O</code></td><td>Group (repeatable — one cert can carry multiple <code>O=</code> entries)</td><td><code>O=easypj-devs</code> → member of group <code>easypj-devs</code>, matched by a RoleBinding's <code>subjects</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><em>(nothing — no API object)</em></td><td>Identity IS the certificate</td><td>revoke access by expiring/deleting the cert or removing the RoleBinding — never by deleting a "user"</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Insight:</strong> issuing a certificate <em>is</em> creating a user — there's no second "create user" API call anywhere.</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This surprises people coming from systems with a Users database. In Kubernetes, identity for cert-auth is entirely encoded in the cert you were handed — revoking access means revoking/expiring the cert or removing the RoleBindings, not deleting a "user row" anywhere.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Compare typical CA validity vs leaf-certificate validity, and explain why the difference matters operationally.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Certificate</th><th>Typical validity</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Cluster CA (<code>ca.crt</code>)</td><td>~10 years</td><td>Rotating a CA is disruptive — every trusting party must get the new one</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Leaf certs (apiserver, kubelet, etc.)</td><td>~1 year</td><td>Short lifetime limits blast radius if a component cert leaks</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>An expired leaf breaks ONLY that component, even though the CA underneath is still perfectly valid — which is exactly why <code>kubeadm certs renew</code> only touches leaves, never the CA. Rotating the CA itself is a much bigger, separate operation (see the UTH section below).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You renewed all certs, but <code>kubectl</code> and inter-component calls still fail with x509 errors. What's the most likely mistake?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The affected process was never restarted, so it's still serving the OLD certificate it loaded into memory at startup — renewing the files on disk changes nothing until the process re-reads them.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Renewed on disk ≠ renewed in the running process</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  BEFORE restart                            AFTER restart
  ┌──────────────────────────┐              ┌──────────────────────────┐
  │ disk:    apiserver.crt NEW │             │ disk:    apiserver.crt NEW│
  │ process: apiserver.crt OLD │  --restart-->│ process: apiserver.crt NEW│
  │          (loaded once at   │             │          (re-read from    │
  │           startup, cached) │             │           disk on boot)   │
  │ result:  x509 error STILL  │             │ result:  TLS handshake OK │
  └──────────────────────────┘              └──────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="info-box danger">
                            <h5>🚨 The #1 certificate gotcha</h5>
                            <p>"Renewed but not restarted" is indistinguishable from "not renewed" from the outside — both produce the exact same x509 error. Always follow renewal with a restart in the SAME breath.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Restart the specific static pod (move its manifest out of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and back) or bounce the kubelet (<code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>) to force all static pods to reload. Also re-check the admin kubeconfig — a stale embedded client cert produces the same symptom on the <code>kubectl</code> side.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does etcd having its own separate CA matter operationally?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>etcd's cert chain is independent of the cluster CA that signs the apiserver/kubelet/scheduler certs. That means: (1) renewing the cluster CA does <strong>not</strong> fix expired etcd certs, and (2) an expired etcd cert takes down the datastore — and because everything else depends on etcd, it takes down the WHOLE cluster, even though the apiserver's own cert might be perfectly fine.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Chain</th><th>Signs</th><th>If it breaks</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Cluster CA (<code>ca.crt</code>)</td><td>apiserver, kubelet, scheduler, controller-manager, admins</td><td>Those components fail to authenticate — etcd itself stays healthy</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>etcd CA (<code>etcd/ca.crt</code>)</td><td>etcd server + peer certs</td><td>etcd becomes unreachable → apiserver can't read/write state → the WHOLE cluster is down, even with a perfectly valid apiserver cert</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Insight:</strong> "certificate expired" errors during troubleshooting always require asking WHICH cert — apiserver's own, apiserver's client cert to etcd, or etcd's server cert to itself — because the fix (<code>kubeadm certs renew &lt;name&gt;</code>) targets one at a time.</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubeadm certs renew etcd-server</code> and <code>kubeadm certs renew etcd-peer</code> are the specific commands for etcd's own certs — <code>renew all</code> covers them too, but knowing they're a separate chain helps you target a fix fast under exam time pressure.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The entire control plane is down: every <code>kubectl</code> command returns <code>x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid</code>. Outline your recovery, in order.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">kubectl is USELESS here — you must work ON the node</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  1. ssh &lt;control-plane-node&gt;             ← kubectl can't reach a dead apiserver
  2. sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration   ← confirm which certs actually expired
  3. sudo kubeadm certs renew all          ← regenerate every expired leaf cert
  4. sudo systemctl restart kubelet        ← forces static pods to reload new certs
  5. sudo kubeadm init phase kubeconfig admin
     cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config
  6. kubectl get nodes                     ← confirm recovery
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The key insight tested here: you cannot <code>kubectl</code> your way out of an expired apiserver certificate — the API server itself is unreachable over TLS. Recovery MUST happen at the node/filesystem level with <code>kubeadm</code>/<code>openssl</code>, then <code>kubectl</code> becomes usable again only after the admin kubeconfig is refreshed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

