                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>SANs define which hostnames/IPs the cert is valid for. The API server cert must include: <code>kubernetes</code>, <code>kubernetes.default</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local</code>, the ClusterIP of the kubernetes service (usually 10.96.0.1), and the node's IP. On the CKA, if you add a load balancer or new DNS name, you must regenerate the API server cert with updated SANs using <code>--apiserver-cert-extra-sans</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a CertificateSigningRequest (CSR) for a new user <code>devuser</code> in Kubernetes and approve it.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate private key</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate private key</span>
openssl genrsa -out devuser.key 2048

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create CSR (CN=username, O=group)</span>
openssl req -new -key devuser.key \
-subj "/CN=devuser/O=developers" -out devuser.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Base64 encode the CSR</span>
cat devuser.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n'

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create K8s CSR object</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
name: devuser-csr
spec:
request: $(cat devuser.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n')
signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
expirationSeconds: 86400    <span class="comment"># 24 hours</span>
usages:
- client auth
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Approve the CSR</span>
kubectl certificate approve devuser-csr

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Extract the signed certificate</span>
kubectl get csr devuser-csr \
-o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > devuser.crt

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify</span>
openssl x509 -in devuser.crt -noout -subject
<span class="comment"># subject= /CN=devuser/O=developers</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The CSR workflow is a HIGH-WEIGHT CKA task. Key details: (1) <code>CN</code> becomes the username, <code>O</code> becomes the group, (2) <code>signerName</code> must be <code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code> for user certs, (3) <code>usages</code> must include <code>client auth</code>, (4) the <code>request</code> field must be base64-encoded with no newlines (<code>tr -d '\n'</code>). After approval, extract the cert from <code>.status.certificate</code>. Common mistake: forgetting to base64-encode the CSR or using the wrong signerName.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you renew all kubeadm certificates? What happens to control plane components?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">renew all certificates at once</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Renew ALL certificates at once:</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># [renew] Reading configuration from the cluster...</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate embedded in admin.conf renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate front-proxy-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ... all renewed</span>

<span class="comment"># Renew specific certificate:</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver-kubelet-client

<span class="comment"># CRITICAL: Restart control plane after renewal!</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm static pods auto-restart on cert change?</span>
<span class="comment"># NO — you must restart them:</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 1: Move and restore manifests</span>
cd /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
sudo mv kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># Wait ~20s for kubelet to stop the pod</span>
sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml .
<span class="comment"># Repeat for controller-manager and scheduler</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Restart kubelet (easier)</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Method 3: If using crictl</span>
sudo crictl pods --name kube-apiserver -q | xargs sudo crictl stopp

<span class="comment"># Verify new certs:</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># ← Should show dates ~1 year from now</span>

<span class="comment"># Also update admin kubeconfig:</span>
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) ~/.kube/config</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> renews every certificate. CRITICAL: after renewal, restart the control plane components so they pick up new certs. Static pods read certs from disk — if you restart kubelet, it detects the manifest changes and recreates the pods. Don't forget to update <code>~/.kube/config</code> (copy the new <code>admin.conf</code>). On the CKA, cert renewal + restart + kubeconfig update is a common multi-step question. Certs renewed via kubeadm inherit the same 1-year validity.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a kubeconfig file for user <code>devuser</code> using a signed certificate.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">assuming you have: devuser.key, devuser.crt, ca.crt</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Assuming you have: devuser.key, devuser.crt, ca.crt</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Set cluster</span>
kubectl config set-cluster my-cluster \
--certificate-authority=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
--server=https://192.168.1.10:6443 \
--embed-certs=true \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Set credentials</span>
kubectl config set-credentials devuser \
--client-certificate=devuser.crt \
--client-key=devuser.key \
--embed-certs=true \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Set context</span>
kubectl config set-context devuser-context \
--cluster=my-cluster \
--user=devuser \
--namespace=dev \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Use context</span>
kubectl config use-context devuser-context \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Test</span>
kubectl get pods \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig -n dev
<span class="comment"># Error (Forbidden) ← Need RBAC!</span>
<span class="comment"># This is expected — cert gives identity, RBAC gives permissions</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubeconfig creation is a 4-step process: set-cluster, set-credentials, set-context, use-context. <code>--embed-certs=true</code> embeds certs directly in the kubeconfig (portable). On the CKA, the order matters: cluster → credentials → context → use-context. After creating the kubeconfig, the user still needs RBAC (Role+RoleBinding) to do anything. Without RBAC, they authenticate successfully but all requests return Forbidden.</p>
