<span class="comment"># Step 3: Restart API server (static pod)</span>
mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># Wait for pod to stop (~10 seconds)</span>
sleep 10
mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># Wait for restart</span>
sleep 30

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify new expiry</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout \
  | grep "Not After"
<span class="comment"># Not After : (should be ~1 year from today)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify API server is running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver</code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 26.5 — Diagnose a CN/O Mismatch Denying Access</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Scenario</h5>
                        <p>A teammate says their signed certificate "isn't working" — <code>kubectl --context=dev get pods -n jobpost</code> returns <code>Forbidden</code>, even though the cert was approved and issued successfully. There's a RoleBinding granting <code>get/list</code> on pods in <code>jobpost</code> to group <code>easypj-devs</code>.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Decode the presented certificate and inspect its Subject (CN/O)</li>
                            <li>Compare it against the RoleBinding's subject (kind: Group, name: easypj-devs)</li>
                            <li>Identify the mismatch and explain why authentication succeeded but authorization failed</li>
                            <li>Fix it the right way — reissue a correct cert, don't edit the RoleBinding to match a typo</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> "Forbidden" (403) after a cert was successfully approved means authentication passed — the API server knows exactly who you are. The problem is always downstream, in RBAC, comparing what the cert SAYS against what the RoleBinding EXPECTS.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Decode the cert the user is actually presenting</span>
openssl x509 -in dev.crt -noout -subject
<span class="comment"># subject=CN = dev, O = easypj-dev      ← note: "easypj-dev" not "easypj-devs"!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check what the RoleBinding actually expects</span>
kubectl get rolebinding -n jobpost -o yaml | grep -A3 subjects
<span class="comment"># subjects:</span>
<span class="comment"># - kind: Group</span>
<span class="comment">#   name: easypj-devs        ← trailing "s" — does NOT match the cert's O field</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Confirm it authenticated fine (whoami-style check)</span>
kubectl --context=dev auth whoami
<span class="comment"># ATTRIBUTE   VALUE</span>
<span class="comment"># Username    dev</span>
<span class="comment"># Groups      [easypj-dev system:authenticated]   ← wrong group, confirmed</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: FIX — regenerate the CSR with the CORRECT O= field, re-approve</span>
openssl req -new -key dev.key -subj "/CN=dev/O=easypj-devs" -out dev-fixed.csr
<span class="comment"># ... submit as a new CSR, approve, extract, rebuild kubeconfig ...</span>
kubectl --context=dev get pods -n jobpost
<span class="comment"># NAME               READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE      ← now works</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 26 Visual Summary — the certificate web of a kubeadm cluster</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
                              🏛️ CLUSTER CA (~10y)
                          /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
        ┌──────────┬───────────┴────────┬──────────────────┐
   📡 apiserver  🤖 kubelet        🔀 front-proxy CA    👩‍💻 admins/users
  (server+client)  (client+serving)  (separate chain —   CN=user, O=group
   ~1y leaf certs   ~1y leaf certs    aggregation only)   → RBAC identity
                                                              │
                                          🗄️ etcd — OWN CA (etcd/ca.crt)
                                          peer + server certs, ~1y leaves
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  🔑 ONBOARD:  openssl genrsa+req ─► kubectl apply CSR ─► certificate approve
               ─► extract .status.certificate ─► embed in kubeconfig
  ⏰ MONITOR:  kubeadm certs check-expiration   (every kubeadm cert + CA, one table)
  🔄 RENEW:    kubeadm certs renew all  ─►  RESTART control plane / kubelet
               ─►  refresh admin.conf (kubeadm init phase kubeconfig admin)
  ⚠️  renew-without-restart = still broken.  etcd's CA ≠ cluster CA.
      identity = CN+O in the cert, never a stored "User" object.
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> one cluster CA (~10 years) sits at the center and signs leaf certs (~1 year) for the apiserver (both server AND client roles), the kubelet (client to the apiserver, serving for exec/logs), the controller-manager, the scheduler, and every human admin — whose Subject (CN=username, O=group) IS their entire RBAC identity, since Kubernetes has no separate "User" object anywhere. etcd hangs off its own, completely separate CA, and the front-proxy CA serves only the API aggregation layer (extension apiservers like metrics-server) — three distinct trust chains living under one directory.</p>
                    <p>To onboard a new person you generate a key and CSR with <code>openssl</code>, submit it as a CertificateSigningRequest naming the correct <code>signerName</code>, get it approved, extract the signed cert from <code>.status.certificate</code>, and embed it in a kubeconfig — at which point they can authenticate, though RBAC still decides what they're allowed to actually do.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> to keep the cluster alive over time you periodically run <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code>, then <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> when leaves approach expiry — but renewal on disk is meaningless until you RESTART the affected component (and refresh any kubeconfig that embedded the old cert). Skipping the restart is the single most common certificate mistake in production and on the exam alike.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
