            <section class="section-block" id="s27-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.1 Pod Security Standards (PSS)</h3>
                <p>Pod Security Standards define three progressively restrictive security profiles that cover the most common pod security needs.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Three Pod Security Standards — Increasing Restriction</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │          POD SECURITY STANDARDS (PSS) — THREE LEVELS                    │
  │                                                                         │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐       │
  │  │   🔓 PRIVILEGED │   │   🔒 BASELINE   │   │   🔐 RESTRICTED │       │
  │  │                 │   │                 │   │                 │       │
  │  │ • No restrict.  │   │ • Blocks known  │   │ • Strictest     │       │
  │  │ • Full access   │   │   escalations   │   │ • Hardened pods │       │
  │  │ • Host PID/Net  │   │ • No hostPID    │   │ • Non-root only │       │
  │  │ • Privileged    │   │ • No hostNet    │   │ • Drop ALL caps │       │
  │  │   containers OK │   │ • No privileged │   │ • No privilege  │       │
  │  │                 │   │   containers    │   │   escalation    │       │
  │  │ Use: system     │   │ • No hostPath   │   │ • Read-only FS  │       │
  │  │ components,     │   │                 │   │ • Seccomp       │       │
  │  │ kube-system     │   │ Use: general    │   │                 │       │
  │  │                 │   │ workloads       │   │ Use: sensitive  │       │
  │  │                 │   │                 │   │ workloads       │       │
  │  │  ◀─── OPEN ────│── ├── MODERATE ────│── ├── STRICT ──▶   │       │
  │  └─────────────────┘   └─────────────────┘   └─────────────────┘       │
  │                                                                         │
  │  💡 Choose based on workload needs:                                     │
  │     kube-system → Privileged | apps → Baseline | PII/finance → Restric │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Check</th>
                                <th>Privileged</th>
                                <th>Baseline</th>
                                <th>Restricted</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Privileged containers</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>❌ Blocked</td><td>❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Host namespaces (PID/IPC/Net)</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>❌ Blocked</td><td>❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>hostPath volumes</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>❌ Blocked</td><td>❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Host ports (<code>containers[].ports[].hostPort</code>)</td><td>✅ Any value allowed</td><td>⚠️ Must be undefined or <code>0</code> — any explicit non-zero value blocked</td><td>❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>runAsNonRoot</td><td>Not required</td><td>Not required</td><td>✅ Required</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Privilege escalation</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>❌ Must be false</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Capabilities</td><td>Any</td><td>Safe 13-capability list only (see below)</td><td>Drop ALL, add back only <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Seccomp profile (<code>seccompProfile.type</code>)</td><td>Any value, incl. <code>Unconfined</code></td><td>⚠️ <code>Unconfined</code> explicitly blocked (v1.19+); undefined/<code>RuntimeDefault</code>/<code>Localhost</code> all still pass</td><td>Same rule as Baseline — undefined, <code>RuntimeDefault</code>, or <code>Localhost</code> (in practice, always set one explicitly on the exam)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 PSS vs PodSecurityPolicy</h5>
                    <p>PodSecurityPolicy (PSP) was deprecated in K8s 1.21 and <strong>removed in 1.25</strong>. Pod Security Standards + Pod Security Admission (PSA) replaced it — PSA reached its own stable/GA status in that same 1.25 release. The CKA tests PSS/PSA, NOT PSP.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Lesser-known Baseline control</th><th>What it restricts</th><th>Allowed values</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Capabilities safe list</td><td><code>containers[].securityContext.capabilities.add</code></td><td>Only these 13 may be added under baseline: <code>AUDIT_WRITE</code>, <code>CHOWN</code>, <code>DAC_OVERRIDE</code>, <code>FOWNER</code>, <code>FSETID</code>, <code>KILL</code>, <code>MKNOD</code>, <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code>, <code>SETFCAP</code>, <code>SETGID</code>, <code>SETPCAP</code>, <code>SETUID</code>, <code>SYS_CHROOT</code>. Anything else (e.g. <code>NET_ADMIN</code>, <code>SYS_ADMIN</code>) is blocked even under baseline.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>AppArmor</td><td><code>securityContext.appArmorProfile.type</code> (pod &amp; container)</td><td>Undefined, <code>RuntimeDefault</code>, or <code>Localhost</code> — <code>Unconfined</code> is blocked. Same shape as the seccomp control, one Linux MAC (mandatory access control) system disallowing the other.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>SELinux</td><td><code>securityContext.seLinuxOptions.type</code> (pod &amp; container); <code>.user</code>/<code>.role</code> must stay unset</td><td>Only <code>container_t</code>, <code>container_init_t</code>, <code>container_kvm_t</code>, or <code>container_engine_t</code> (added v1.31) — a custom SELinux type or a non-empty <code>user</code>/<code>role</code> is blocked.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Sysctls</td><td><code>securityContext.sysctls[].name</code></td><td>A short namespaced "safe" list only: <code>kernel.shm_rmid_forced</code>, <code>net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range</code>, <code>net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start</code>, <code>net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies</code>, <code>net.ipv4.ping_group_range</code>, plus several TCP-keepalive sysctls added in later releases. Anything else (e.g. <code>kernel.panic</code>) is blocked.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>/proc mount type</td><td><code>containers[].securityContext.procMount</code></td><td>Undefined or <code>Default</code> only — <code>Unmasked</code> (which exposes extra host <code>/proc</code> entries) is blocked.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Host probes / lifecycle hooks (v1.34+)</td><td><code>livenessProbe</code>/<code>readinessProbe</code>/<code>startupProbe</code>/lifecycle-hook <code>httpGet.host</code> &amp; <code>tcpSocket.host</code></td><td>Undefined or empty string — pointing a probe at an explicit host (e.g. <code>127.0.0.1</code>) to reach outside the pod is blocked. A newer control; verify against your cluster's actual minor version before relying on it.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>HostProcess containers (Windows only, v1.26+)</td><td><code>securityContext.windowsOptions.hostProcess</code></td><td>Undefined or <code>false</code> — the Windows analogue of a privileged container; irrelevant on Linux-only clusters, but know it exists if your cluster mixes Windows node pools.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why this matters beyond the exam</h5>
                    <p>Baseline is NOT "just blocks host access and privileged containers" — it also quietly requires a sane seccomp/AppArmor/SELinux posture and blocks unsafe sysctls, even though it never forces <code>runAsNonRoot</code> or capability-dropping the way restricted does. In production, a pod that mysteriously fails admission in a <code>baseline</code>-enforced namespace despite having no obvious privilege-escalation fields is very often tripped up by one of these lesser-known controls — the rejection message will name the exact field, same as any other PSA violation.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>PSA Admission Decision Flow</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Pod Created / Updated</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>kubectl apply -f pod.yaml</code> → API server receives the request</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Check Namespace Labels</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>PSA reads <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce</code>, <code>/warn</code>, <code>/audit</code> labels on the target namespace</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>Evaluate Against Standard</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Compares pod spec fields (securityContext, capabilities, hostNetwork, etc.) against the configured PSS level</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Apply Mode Action</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>enforce</strong> → REJECT pod | <strong>warn</strong> → allow + show warning | <strong>audit</strong> → allow + log to audit</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §27.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s27-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.2 Pod Security Admission (PSA)</h3>
                <p>Pod Security Admission is the <strong>built-in admission controller</strong> that enforces Pod Security Standards. It's configured entirely through <strong>namespace labels</strong> — no YAML files needed.</p>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Mental model: PSS is the rulebook, PSA is the referee</h5>
                    <p>Pod Security Standards (27.1) is just a <em>spec</em> — three named profiles describing what "privileged," "baseline," and "restricted" mean, on paper, independent of any cluster. Pod Security Admission is the <em>enforcer</em> — the actual running code inside kube-apiserver that reads a namespace's labels and holds every incoming pod up against whichever profile that namespace selected. You never "install" or "create" PSA; it ships built into the apiserver. You only ever choose a level (PSS) and flip it on (PSA), via a label.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">PSA Enforcement via Namespace Labels</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │           POD SECURITY ADMISSION — HOW IT WORKS                         │
  │                                                                         │
  │   kubectl apply -f pod.yaml                                             │
  │          │                                                              │
  │          ▼                                                              │
  │   ┌──────────────┐                                                      │
  │   │  API Server   │                                                      │
  │   └──────┬───────┘                                                      │
  │          │ intercepts                                                    │
  │          ▼                                                              │
  │   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                          │
  │   │  PSA Admission Controller                 │                          │
  │   │                                           │                          │
  │   │  Checks namespace labels:                 │                          │
  │   │  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=X      │                          │
  │   │  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=X         │                          │
  │   │  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=X        │                          │
  │   └──────┬───────────┬──────────┬────────────┘                          │
  │          │           │          │                                        │
  │      ENFORCE       WARN      AUDIT                                      │
  │          │           │          │                                        │
  │     ❌ REJECT   ⚠️ Warning   📝 Log                                    │
  │     the pod     but ALLOW    but ALLOW                                  │
  │                                                                         │
  │   💡 You can combine modes: enforce=baseline + warn=restricted          │
  │      → Blocks baseline violations, warns about restricted violations    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">psa-namespace-labels.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Apply PSA labels to namespaces ──

<span class="comment"># Production: enforce restricted (reject non-compliant pods)</span>
kubectl label ns production \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest

<span class="comment"># Staging: enforce baseline, warn about restricted violations</span>
kubectl label ns staging \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted

<span class="comment"># Development: warn only (don't block anything)</span>
kubectl label ns development \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=baseline \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=baseline

<span class="comment"># Verify labels</span>
kubectl get ns production --show-labels
<span class="comment"># NAME         STATUS   AGE   LABELS</span>
<span class="comment"># production   Active   5d    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted,...</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Test: What happens when you deploy a non-compliant pod? ──</span>

<span class="comment"># This pod runs as root — violates "restricted"</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n production
<span class="comment"># Error: pods "test" is forbidden: violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest"</span>

<span class="comment"># Same pod in dev namespace — just a warning</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n development
<span class="comment"># Warning: would violate PodSecurity "baseline:latest"</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/test created  ← allowed!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div>
                        <h5>enforce</h5>
                        <p><strong>Rejects</strong> pods that violate the standard. Use in production to prevent insecure workloads from deploying.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>warn</h5>
                        <p><strong>Warns</strong> the user but allows the pod. Perfect for migration — see what would break before enforcing.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div>
                        <h5>audit</h5>
                        <p><strong>Logs</strong> the violation to the audit log silently. Pods still run. Use for monitoring compliance.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §27.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s27-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.3 Security Contexts</h3>
                <p>SecurityContext lets you set fine-grained security settings at the pod and container level. These are the fields that PSA actually checks against the Pod Security Standards.</p>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy: a SecurityContext is a building pass with the lowest clearance that still lets you do your job</h5>
                    <p>No master key, no propping the fire door, no promoting yourself to admin while inside. Every field is a small, specific grant — this UID, this one capability, read access here but not write there — never a blanket "trust this container." The restricted profile is simply the strictest version of that pass: no privilege escalation, no spare capabilities, no writable floor plan (read-only rootfs), and a guard checking every action against a fixed rulebook (seccomp).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Field</th>
                                <th>Level</th>
                                <th>What It Does</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>runAsUser</code></td><td>Pod / Container</td><td>Set the UID the process runs as</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>runAsGroup</code></td><td>Pod / Container</td><td>Set the primary GID</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>runAsNonRoot</code></td><td>Pod / Container</td><td>Reject if image would run as root (UID 0)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>fsGroup</code></td><td>Pod only</td><td>Supplemental group for mounted volumes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Make container filesystem read-only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Block setuid/setgid binaries</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>capabilities.drop</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Remove Linux capabilities</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>capabilities.add</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Add back specific capabilities</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>privileged</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Full host access (avoid!)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>seccompProfile</code></td><td>Pod / Container</td><td>System call filtering profile</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">restricted-compliant-pod.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># This pod passes the "restricted" PSS — use this as template</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secure-app
  namespace: production     <span class="comment"># namespace with enforce=restricted</span>
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true      # ◀── Required by restricted
    runAsUser: 1000
    runAsGroup: 3000
    fsGroup: 2000
    seccompProfile:         # ◀── Required by restricted
      type: RuntimeDefault
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # ◀── Required by restricted
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true     # ◀── Best practice
      capabilities:
        drop: ["ALL"]                  # ◀── Required by restricted
    resources:
      limits:
        memory: "128Mi"
        cpu: "250m"
    volumeMounts:
    - name: tmp
      mountPath: /tmp       # ◀── writable dir via emptyDir
  volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}            <span class="comment"># use emptyDir for writable paths</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod-Level vs Container-Level SecurityContext</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   POD-LEVEL securityContext          applies to ALL containers  │
  │   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
  │   │  runAsUser: 1000                                       │     │
  │   │  runAsGroup: 3000                                      │     │
  │   │  fsGroup: 2000                                         │     │
  │   │  runAsNonRoot: true                                    │     │
  │   │  seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault                        │     │
  │   └───────────┬───────────────────────┬────────────────────┘     │
  │               │                       │                          │
  │               ▼                       ▼                          │
  │   ┌───────────────────┐   ┌───────────────────┐                  │
  │   │ CONTAINER-LEVEL   │   │ CONTAINER-LEVEL   │                  │
  │   │ (overrides pod)   │   │ (inherits pod)    │                  │
  │   │                   │   │                   │                  │
  │   │ runAsUser: 2000   │   │ (uses 1000 from   │                  │
  │   │ ← overrides 1000  │   │  pod-level)       │                  │
  │   │                   │   │                   │                  │
  │   │ capabilities:     │   │ capabilities:     │                  │
  │   │   drop: ["ALL"]   │   │   drop: ["ALL"]   │                  │
  │   │                   │   │                   │                  │
  │   │ allowPrivilegeEsc │   │ readOnlyRootFS:   │                  │
  │   │ : false           │   │ true              │                  │
  │   └───────────────────┘   └───────────────────┘                  │
  │     container: app          container: sidecar                   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Pattern: Restricted-Compliant Pod</h5>
                    <p>To make ANY pod pass the "restricted" standard, add these 4 things: <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, <code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code>, and <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code>. Missing any one ➔ rejected.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Production misconfiguration</th><th>Why it bites</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Set <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> but forgot volumes for every path the app writes to</td><td>Container starts, then crashes/CrashLoopBackOffs the instant it tries to write a log, temp file, or cache — the error is buried in application logs, not the Kubernetes event stream</td><td>Run the image once WITHOUT read-only rootfs, <code>strace -f -e trace=open,openat</code> or check the app's docs for every writable path, then mount an <code>emptyDir</code> at each one before flipping the flag back on</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>fsGroup set on the pod, but the volume is NFS or a CSI driver that doesn't support <code>fsGroupChangePolicy</code></td><td>Files inside the volume are still owned by the image's baked-in UID, not the pod's <code>runAsUser</code> — the non-root process gets <code>Permission denied</code> even though fsGroup "should" have fixed it</td><td>Check the CSI driver's docs for fsGroup support; if unsupported, either chown the export at the storage layer once, or accept a privileged one-time init step in a namespace that isn't restricted</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Namespace left at <code>warn</code>+<code>audit</code> indefinitely, enforce never flipped on</td><td>Warnings scroll past in CI logs and get ignored forever — the namespace LOOKS hardened in a `kubectl get ns --show-labels` glance but blocks nothing, a false sense of security</td><td>Track a hard migration deadline; alert on any namespace where <code>warn</code>/<code>audit</code> is set but <code>enforce</code> is absent or looser, and revisit weekly during rollout</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Copy-pasted a <code>kube-system</code>-style privileged SecurityContext into an application namespace "to be safe"</td><td>Grants far more than the app needs (often full <code>capabilities</code>, sometimes <code>hostNetwork</code>) — turns a routine app namespace into a high-value target if the container is ever compromised</td><td>Start from <code>drop: ["ALL"]</code> and add back only what a real error message demands — never start from a privileged template "just in case"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>enforce-version</code> left unset (defaults to <code>latest</code>)</td><td>A cluster upgrade can silently add NEW restricted checks; a pod that passed admission last month can suddenly fail the same manifest after a version bump, breaking CI/CD with no code change</td><td>Pin <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version</code> to the current minor version explicitly, and bump it deliberately (with testing) alongside planned cluster upgrades</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">volume-permissions-under-restricted.yaml</span></div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Common real-world snag: the app needs a volume owned/writable by its non-root
# UID, but a privileged "chown" initContainer is ITSELF rejected under restricted
# (privileged/root init containers fail the exact same PSA checks as the main one).
# The fix is almost always fsGroup, NOT a privileged init step:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-worker
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 1000
    fsGroup: 2000                    <span class="comment"># ← kubelet chowns/chmods the volume to this GID
                                      #   for every container BEFORE they start —
                                      #   no privileged step required at all</span>
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault
  containers:
  - name: worker
    image: easypj:v1
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      capabilities:
        drop: ["ALL"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: media
      mountPath: /app/media          <span class="comment"># ← owned by GID 2000, writable by UID 1000</span>
  volumes:
  - name: media
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: easypj-media-pvc</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why not just add <code>CHOWN</code> to an initContainer?</h5>
                    <p>Because an initContainer is a container too — under <code>enforce=restricted</code> it must pass the SAME nine checks as the main container. A <code>chown</code>-only initContainer typically wants to run as root with the <code>CHOWN</code> capability, which restricted forbids outright. <code>fsGroup</code> is the sanctioned escape hatch: the kubelet performs the ownership fix-up itself, outside the container's process entirely, so no container ever needs elevated capabilities just to fix permissions.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §27.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s27-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.4 Admission Controllers</h3>
                <p>Admission Controllers are plugins that intercept API requests <strong>after authentication and authorization</strong> but <strong>before the object is persisted to etcd</strong>. They can validate, mutate, or reject requests.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Request Flow Through Admission Controllers</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                API REQUEST LIFECYCLE                                          │
  │                                                                              │
  │   kubectl apply ──▶ API Server                                               │
  │                        │                                                     │
  │                   ┌────▼────┐                                                │
  │                   │  AuthN  │ ── Who are you? (certs, tokens)                │
  │                   └────┬────┘                                                │
  │                   ┌────▼────┐                                                │
  │                   │  AuthZ  │ ── Are you allowed? (RBAC)                     │
  │                   └────┬────┘                                                │
  │                   ┌────▼──────────────────────────────────┐                  │
  │                   │  ADMISSION CONTROLLERS                │                  │
  │                   │                                       │                  │
  │                   │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  │                  │
  │                   │  │  MUTATING    │  │  VALIDATING  │  │                  │
  │                   │  │              │  │              │  │                  │
  │                   │  │ • Defaults   │  │ • Policy     │  │                  │
  │                   │  │ • Inject     │  │ • Rules      │  │                  │
  │                   │  │   sidecars   │  │ • Quotas     │  │                  │
  │                   │  │ • Labels     │  │ • PSA        │  │                  │
  │                   │  │ • Webhooks   │  │ • Webhooks   │  │                  │
  │                   │  └──────┬───────┘  └──────┬───────┘  │                  │
  │                   │         │ modify           │ check    │                  │
  │                   └─────────┼──────────────────┼──────────┘                  │
  │                             │                  │                             │
  │                             └────────┬─────────┘                             │
  │                                      │ if ALL pass                           │
  │                                 ┌────▼────┐                                  │
  │                                 │  etcd   │ ── Object persisted              │
  │                                 └─────────┘                                  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Admission Controller</th>
                                <th>Type</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>NamespaceLifecycle</code></td><td>Validating</td><td>Prevents creating resources in terminating/non-existent namespaces</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>LimitRanger</code></td><td>Mutating</td><td>Applies default resource limits from LimitRange objects</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>ResourceQuota</code></td><td>Validating</td><td>Enforces resource quotas per namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>ServiceAccount</code></td><td>Mutating</td><td>Auto-mounts SA tokens onto pods</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>PodSecurity</code></td><td>Validating</td><td>Enforces Pod Security Standards (PSA)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>DefaultStorageClass</code></td><td>Mutating</td><td>Assigns default StorageClass to PVCs without one</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>MutatingAdmissionWebhook</code></td><td>Mutating</td><td>Calls external webhooks to modify objects</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</code></td><td>Validating</td><td>Calls external webhooks to validate objects</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">admission-controllers.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Check which admission controllers are enabled ──
<span class="comment"># Look at API server manifest on control plane node</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml \
  | grep enable-admission-plugins
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,...</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Enable/disable admission controllers ──</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit the API server static pod manifest:</span>
vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># Add to the command args:</span>
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,ResourceQuota</span>
<span class="comment"># --disable-admission-plugins=DefaultStorageClass</span>

<span class="comment"># ── After editing, API server auto-restarts (static pod) ──</span>

<span class="comment"># ── List default enabled controllers ──</span>
kube-apiserver -h | grep enable-admission-plugins
<span class="comment"># Default: CertificateApproval,CertificateSigning,</span>
<span class="comment"># CertificateSubjectRestriction,DefaultIngressClass,</span>
<span class="comment"># DefaultStorageClass,DefaultTolerationSeconds,LimitRanger,</span>
<span class="comment"># MutatingAdmissionWebhook,NamespaceLifecycle,PodSecurity,</span>
<span class="comment"># ResourceQuota,ServiceAccount,StorageObjectInUseProtection,</span>
<span class="comment"># ValidatingAdmissionPolicy,ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA: Editing API Server Manifest</h5>
                    <p>On the exam, you may need to enable an admission controller by editing <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. After saving, the kubelet auto-restarts the API server pod. Wait ~30 seconds and verify with <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code>. If the API server doesn't come back, check your YAML syntax.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ DO</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Use PSA labels for namespace-level enforcement</li>
                            <li>Start with <code>warn</code> mode before switching to <code>enforce</code></li>
                            <li>Set <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> for all application pods</li>
                            <li>Drop ALL capabilities, add only what's needed</li>
                            <li>Use <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> + emptyDir for writable paths</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>❌ DON'T</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Run containers as root without a compelling reason</li>
                            <li>Use <code>privileged: true</code> for application workloads</li>
                            <li>Skip seccomp profiles in restricted namespaces</li>
                            <li>Forget to restart API server after editing admission plugins</li>
                            <li>Confuse PodSecurityPolicy (removed) with Pod Security Standards</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Security Context Quick Reference — CKA Cheat Sheet</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │        SECURITY CONTEXT CHEAT SHEET — WHAT TO SET WHERE                      │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │  POD-LEVEL                       │  │  CONTAINER-LEVEL                 │ │
  │  │  spec.securityContext            │  │  spec.containers[].securityCtx   │ │
  │  │                                  │  │                                  │ │
  │  │  ✅ runAsUser: 1000              │  │  ✅ allowPrivilegeEscalation     │ │
  │  │  ✅ runAsGroup: 3000             │  │  ✅ readOnlyRootFilesystem       │ │
  │  │  ✅ runAsNonRoot: true           │  │  ✅ capabilities.drop/add       │ │
  │  │  ✅ fsGroup: 2000                │  │  ✅ privileged (avoid!)         │ │
  │  │  ✅ seccompProfile               │  │  ✅ runAsUser (overrides pod)   │ │
  │  │  ✅ supplementalGroups           │  │  ✅ runAsNonRoot (overrides)    │ │
  │  │                                  │  │  ✅ seccompProfile (overrides)  │ │
  │  │  ❌ NOT available:               │  │                                  │ │
  │  │     capabilities                 │  │  ❌ NOT available:               │ │
  │  │     readOnlyRootFilesystem       │  │     fsGroup                      │ │
  │  │     allowPrivilegeEscalation     │  │     supplementalGroups           │ │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────┘  └──────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                                                                              │
  │  💡 Rule: Container-level OVERRIDES pod-level for shared fields             │
  │     Pod sets runAsUser: 1000, Container sets runAsUser: 2000                │
  │     → Container runs as UID 2000                                            │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

            <!-- ─── Pod Security Verification Checklist ─── -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>Pod Security Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get ns NAME --show-labels</code> → verify PSA labels are applied</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl label ns NAME pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted --dry-run=server</code> → preview label effect</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f pod.yaml</code> → test pod against PSA before deploying</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- id</code> → confirm runAsUser/runAsGroup</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- touch /test</code> → verify readOnlyRootFilesystem (should fail)</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver</code> → verify apiserver restarted after admission controller changes</li>
                    <li><code>cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep enable-admission</code> → check enabled admission controllers</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

                
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Speed Tip: PSA Labels</h5>
                    <p>The fastest way to apply PSA is a single <code>kubectl label</code> command. No YAML needed!<br><code>kubectl label ns my-ns pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest</code></p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 What PSA Does NOT Protect Against</h5>
                    <p>Restricted PSA is a pod-spec gate, not a complete security program. Treat it as one layer, not the whole strategy:</p>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Threat</th><th>Does restricted PSA stop it?</th><th>What actually stops it</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>A container image with a known CVE or malware baked in</td><td>❌ No — PSA never inspects image contents/layers</td><td>Image scanning in CI (Trivy, Grype) + an admission webhook/VAP that blocks unscanned or non-allowlisted registries</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>An app that leaks its own database password via a log line</td><td>❌ No — PSA doesn't see application logic or env values</td><td>Secret management discipline (Ch 23), least-privilege DB accounts, log scrubbing</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A compromised pod scanning/attacking other pods over the network</td><td>❌ No — PSA has no opinion on east-west traffic</td><td>NetworkPolicy (Ch 17) default-deny + explicit allow rules</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A pod consuming unbounded CPU/memory and starving neighbors</td><td>❌ No — PSA doesn't check <code>resources.limits</code></td><td>ResourceQuota + LimitRange per namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A pod running as root because <code>runAsNonRoot</code> was never set</td><td>✅ Yes — this is exactly what restricted blocks</td><td>PSA is the right tool here</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A container escaping via a kernel exploit reachable ONLY without seccomp</td><td>✅ Partially — RuntimeDefault seccomp closes the most common escape syscalls</td><td>PSA (seccomp) + keeping nodes/kernel patched + runtime detection (Falco) for anything novel</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Defense in depth, not a single gate</h5>
                    <p>The exam-favorite mental model is "PSA = the whole security story," but production reality is a stack: image scanning (before the cluster) → RBAC (Ch 24, who can act) → PSA/SecurityContext (Ch 27, what the pod spec is allowed to declare) → NetworkPolicy (Ch 17, what it can talk to) → resource quotas (what it can consume) → runtime detection (what it does after admission, when a static check can no longer help). PSA earns its keep at exactly one link in that chain — declaring it "done" after labeling a namespace restricted is the single most common false sense of security in real clusters.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production nuance: enforce only ever touches Pods, not their parent workload</h5>
                    <p><code>enforce</code> is evaluated against the final Pod object only — a Deployment, StatefulSet, Job, or CronJob whose pod template violates the standard is still created successfully by <code>kubectl apply</code>; it's the ReplicaSet/Job controller's subsequent attempt to create Pods that gets rejected, surfacing as a stuck rollout (<code>0/N</code> ready) with a <code>FailedCreate</code> event, not an apply-time error. <code>warn</code> and <code>audit</code>, by contrast, ARE evaluated directly against the workload resource itself at apply-time (so their warnings appear immediately, before any Pod exists) — this is precisely why the recommended rollout order is warn/audit first: they catch the same violation earlier and more visibly than enforce ever will.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Observing PSA at scale: three Prometheus metrics</h5>
                    <p>Beyond reading individual rejection messages, kube-apiserver exposes PSA's own health/activity as metrics, useful for alerting on a fleet of namespaces rather than debugging one pod at a time: <code>pod_security_evaluations_total</code> (every policy check PSA actually performed), <code>pod_security_exemptions_total</code> (requests that skipped evaluation entirely due to an exemption), and <code>pod_security_errors_total</code> (evaluations that errored rather than passed/failed cleanly — worth alerting on, since an error usually means a malformed namespace label or an internal bug, not a legitimate policy decision).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>Ch 24 RBAC</h5>
                        <p>RBAC decides <strong>who</strong> may submit the pod create request at all; PSA then inspects <strong>what</strong> that pod spec declares, regardless of who submitted it. A cluster-admin with full RBAC rights is still blocked by a restricted namespace if their pod spec is insecure — the two controls are evaluated at different pipeline stages and neither substitutes for the other.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>Ch 25 ServiceAccounts</h5>
                        <p>A pod's ServiceAccount identity is what RBAC/PSA exemptions can key off (<code>usernames</code> exemption = <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>). Disabling <code>automountServiceAccountToken</code> shrinks the pod's attack surface the same way <code>capabilities.drop:["ALL"]</code> does — both are "grant nothing by default, add back only what's needed."</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>Ch 26 TLS Certificates</h5>
                        <p>The apiserver's own admission pipeline — including PSA — only runs AFTER the caller's mTLS client cert or SA token has been authenticated. A CSR approval flow (Ch 26) provisions the identity; PSA never touches certs directly, but every PSA decision happens downstream of a successful TLS handshake and AuthN check.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>Ch 41 Policies Deep Dive</h5>
                        <p>PSA covers exactly 3 fixed profiles with zero custom logic. Anything org-specific — "images must come from our registry," "every Deployment needs a <code>cost-center</code> label" — needs <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> (CEL, in-process) or a policy engine like Kyverno/OPA Gatekeeper (external webhook). Both stack alongside PSA at the same validating-admission stage; see §27's Lab Q8 and Ch 41.5.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Pod Security Admission Enforces Standards: SecurityContext Resolution, Capability Dropping, and seccomp Profiles</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Pod Security Admission (PSA) — Replacing PSP</h5>
            <p>PSA is a built-in admission controller that enforces security standards at the namespace level. It replaces the deprecated PodSecurityPolicy (PSP). It's simpler, faster, and checked at pod creation time.</p>
