                    <h5>① The restricted profile, field-by-field — the exact checklist PSA runs</h5>
                    <p>"restricted" isn't one abstract idea — it's a fixed, ordered list of concrete field checks the PodSecurity admission controller walks through on every pod. Knowing the literal list is what lets you read a rejection message and go straight to the fix instead of guessing.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   PSA's restricted-profile checklist (evaluated top to bottom, pod + every container):

   1. spec.securityContext.runAsNonRoot            must be true (or a non-zero runAsUser)
   2. spec.hostNetwork / hostPID / hostIPC          must all be false/unset
   3. spec.volumes[].hostPath                       must be absent (safe volume types only)
   4. containers[].securityContext.privileged        must be false/unset
   5. containers[].securityContext.allowPrivilegeEscalation   must be false
   6. containers[].securityContext.capabilities.drop must include "ALL"
   7. containers[].securityContext.capabilities.add  only NET_BIND_SERVICE permitted
   8. securityContext.seccompProfile.type            must be RuntimeDefault or Localhost
   9. containers[].securityContext.runAsUser         if set, must be non-zero

   ── first failing check on ANY container → the whole pod is rejected ──</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Baseline requires?</th><th>Restricted requires?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>No privileged containers</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>No hostNetwork/hostPID/hostIPC</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>No hostPath volumes</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>runAsNonRoot: true</td><td>❌ not required</td><td>✅ required</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</td><td>❌ not required</td><td>✅ required</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</td><td>❌ default set allowed</td><td>✅ required (only NET_BIND_SERVICE addable)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>seccompProfile set</td><td>❌ not required</td><td>✅ required (RuntimeDefault/Localhost)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The rejection message IS the checklist</strong> — PSA names every single failing control by field name (e.g. <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation != false</code>), in the same order as this table. Read it top to bottom and fix each one; there's no partial credit, ALL controls must pass simultaneously.</div>

                    <h5>② PSA exemptions — how system pods legally bypass restricted</h5>
                    <p>If every namespace were forced through restricted with zero exceptions, core cluster components would never start — a CNI plugin needs hostNetwork, a CSI node driver needs hostPath, a monitoring DaemonSet needs to read host /proc. Exemptions are how the cluster admin carves precise, auditable holes without loosening the whole namespace.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   PSA evaluation order — exemptions are checked BEFORE the profile itself:

   incoming pod create/update
        │
        ▼
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ Is the request exempt?                    │
   │  • username matches AdmissionConfig list?  │──yes──▶  ✅ SKIP PSA entirely
   │  • pod's runtimeClassName is exempted?     │          (no profile check at all)
   │  • pod's namespace is in the exempt list?  │
   └──────────────────┬──────────────────────┘
                       │ no
                       ▼
   normal PSS evaluation proceeds (①'s checklist)</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Exemption type</th><th>Granularity</th><th>Real cluster example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>usernames</code></td><td>one identity, any namespace</td><td><code>system:serviceaccount:kube-system:calico-node</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>runtimeClasses</code></td><td>any pod using that RuntimeClass</td><td>a gVisor/Kata sandbox that's already isolated at the runtime level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>namespaces</code></td><td>the whole namespace, regardless of labels</td><td><code>kube-system</code>, <code>kube-node-lease</code>, <code>kube-public</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Exemptions live cluster-wide</strong> in the apiserver's <code>AdmissionConfiguration</code> file, NOT in namespace labels — that's a deliberate design choice so a namespace owner can't grant themselves an exemption just by editing their own namespace's labels.</div>

                    <h5>③ Cluster-wide defaults via AdmissionConfiguration — secure by default</h5>
                    <p>Labeling namespaces one by one doesn't scale, and it means every NEW namespace starts unrestricted until someone remembers to label it. The fix is a cluster-wide default wired through the apiserver's <code>--admission-control-config-file</code> flag — new namespaces inherit a safe baseline automatically.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   PRECEDENCE — most specific wins:

   ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 1. namespace's OWN pod-security.kubernetes.io/* labels     │  ◀── highest priority
   ├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 2. cluster-wide "defaults" in AdmissionConfiguration        │  ◀── applies if #1 absent
   ├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 3. PSA's own built-in default = privileged (unrestricted)   │  ◀── last resort
   └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   new namespace "easypj-staging" created, NO labels yet
        │
        ▼
   PSA checks: any namespace label? NO → fall through to cluster default
        │
        ▼
   cluster default says enforce=baseline, warn=restricted → applied automatically</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field in AdmissionConfiguration</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>defaults.enforce</code> / <code>warn</code> / <code>audit</code></td><td>cluster-wide fallback levels, same 3 modes as namespace labels</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>defaults.enforce-version</code></td><td>pins the cluster-wide default's version too</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>exemptions</code></td><td>the usernames/runtimeClasses/namespaces carve-outs from ②</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Cluster-wide defaults are a real-world pattern (every new namespace is safe on day one) but rarely the exam's focus — the exam almost always wants the fast one-liner <code>kubectl label ns &lt;ns&gt; pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code>. Know both, reach for labels first under time pressure.</div>

                    <h5>④ The NET_BIND_SERVICE capability — the one hole in drop:["ALL"]</h5>
                    <p>Traditional Linux only lets root bind a "privileged" port (below 1024). Capabilities let you grant that ONE specific power back to a non-root process without granting anything else — <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> is the capability that does exactly that, and it's the only one the restricted profile allows you to add back.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Linux capability bounding set for a container process:

   drop: ["ALL"]  ──▶  starts with ZERO capabilities (CapBnd: 0000000000000000)
                        │
   add: ["NET_BIND_SERVICE"]
                        ▼
   CapBnd: 0000000000000400   ◀── exactly ONE bit set, everything else stays zero

   effect: bind() syscall on port 80 succeeds even though the process is UID 1000,
           because the kernel checks the capability, not the UID, for that syscall.
           EVERY other privileged operation (mount, ptrace, raw sockets, chown) is
           still denied — the container gained NOTHING else.</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Capabilities held</th><th>Passes restricted?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Run as root (no securityContext)</td><td>ALL (~40 capabilities)</td><td>❌ No — runAsNonRoot fails</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>drop ALL, add NET_ADMIN</td><td>1 capability, but the wrong one</td><td>❌ No — restricted only permits NET_BIND_SERVICE</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>drop ALL, add NET_BIND_SERVICE</td><td>1 narrow capability</td><td>✅ Yes — the one sanctioned exception</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>drop ALL, listen on 8080 + Service remaps to 80</td><td>0 capabilities</td><td>✅ Yes — smallest possible surface</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> covers ALL of ports 0–1023, not just 80 — so it also covers 22, 25, 443, etc. When in doubt, prefer remapping the Service's port instead of adding the capability; it needs zero securityContext changes to the container at all.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ seccomp filtering — what RuntimeDefault actually blocks, and when to go custom</h5>
                    <p>seccomp (secure computing mode) is a kernel feature that filters which syscalls a process is allowed to make. Kubernetes exposes it as a pod/container field, but the interesting part is what the RuntimeDefault filter is actually doing underneath, and why you'd ever need a custom one.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Every syscall a container process makes passes through the seccomp filter first:

   container process ──▶ syscall (e.g. open(), read(), write())
                              │
                              ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │  SECCOMP BPF FILTER  │   (installed at container start,
                    │  (per-syscall table) │    can NEVER be loosened afterward)
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                     ALLOWED   │   BLOCKED
                       │       │       │
                       ▼       │       ▼
                syscall runs   │   process killed / EPERM returned
                normally       │   (e.g. reboot(), mount(), ptrace(), kexec_load())

   RuntimeDefault = containerd/CRI-O's built-in list (~300 of ~350 syscalls allowed,
                    blocks the dangerous ~50 like reboot, mount, kernel module load)
   Localhost      = YOUR OWN JSON list, for cases RuntimeDefault is wrong for</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Right seccomp choice</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Standard easypj Django web pod</td><td>RuntimeDefault — blocks dangerous syscalls, needs zero setup</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>An app that legitimately calls a syscall RuntimeDefault blocks (rare)</td><td>Localhost profile explicitly allowing that one syscall, nothing more</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A sandboxed/gVisor RuntimeClass pod</td><td>often exempted (isolation happens at the runtime layer instead)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>No seccomp field set at all</td><td>Unconfined — fails restricted, and is a real security gap even outside the exam</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> the filter is loaded once at container start and the kernel guarantees it can only get MORE restrictive over the container's life, never looser — so there's no runtime bypass once it's applied. On the exam, always reach for <code>RuntimeDefault</code>; only mention <code>Localhost</code> if the scenario explicitly demands a syscall RuntimeDefault blocks.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ Webhook admission internals — the AdmissionReview round-trip, failurePolicy, and why one bad webhook can freeze the whole cluster</h5>
                    <p>PSA is a built-in plugin, but §27.4's other big category — <code>MutatingAdmissionWebhook</code> and <code>ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</code> — works completely differently: the apiserver doesn't run any logic itself, it makes a network call to an external service (usually a Deployment fronted by a ClusterIP Service, e.g. a cert-manager or Kyverno/OPA Gatekeeper webhook pod) and waits for an answer. Understanding that round-trip is what separates "webhooks are magic" from being able to debug a stuck cluster at 3am.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Every webhook-intercepted request is a synchronous HTTPS call, not an in-process check:

   kubectl apply -f easypj-web.yaml
        │
        ▼
   apiserver matches the request against every Mutating/ValidatingWebhookConfiguration's
   .webhooks[].rules[] (apiGroups/apiVersions/resources/operations) that could apply
        │
        ▼
   apiserver builds an AdmissionReview JSON object:
     { "request": { "uid", "kind", "resource", "namespace", "operation",
                     "userInfo", "object": {...the Pod being created...},
                     "oldObject": {...on UPDATE only...}, "dryRun": false } }
        │
        ▼   POST https://<webhook-service>.<ns>.svc:<port>/<path>   (mTLS, caBundle-verified)
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  WEBHOOK SERVER  (e.g. Kyverno, OPA Gatekeeper, cert-manager,│
   │  a custom sidecar-injector)                                  │
   │  runs its own logic against request.object, decides:        │
   │    allowed: true/false  (+ patch, base64 JSONPatch, if       │
   │    mutating)                                                  │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
        │  AdmissionReview RESPONSE, within timeoutSeconds (default 10s, max 30s)
        ▼
   response received in time?  ──NO──▶  failurePolicy decides:
        │ YES                              Fail    → reject the whole request
        ▼                                  Ignore  → admit as if webhook didn't exist
   allowed:true → apiserver applies response.patch (if any), continues pipeline
   allowed:false → request rejected with the webhook's message, pipeline stops</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Webhook field</th><th>What it controls</th><th>Production nuance</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>failurePolicy</code></td><td><code>Fail</code> (reject on error/timeout) or <code>Ignore</code> (admit on error/timeout)</td><td><code>Fail</code> is safer for security-critical webhooks but a crashed/unreachable webhook Pod then blocks EVERY matching request cluster-wide — this is a real, common self-inflicted outage</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>timeoutSeconds</code></td><td>how long the apiserver waits (1–30s, default 10s)</td><td>keep it low (1–5s) for webhooks in the hot path of every pod create — a slow webhook makes every <code>kubectl apply</code> feel hung</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>namespaceSelector</code> / <code>objectSelector</code></td><td>narrows which requests are even sent to the webhook</td><td>always exclude <code>kube-system</code>/<code>kube-node-lease</code> here, or a webhook outage can take down core cluster components too</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>reinvocationPolicy</code></td><td><code>Never</code> (default) or <code>IfNeeded</code> for MUTATING webhooks only</td><td><code>IfNeeded</code> re-runs this webhook if a LATER mutating webhook changed the object after it ran — needed when two mutating webhooks can interact (e.g. a sidecar injector running after a defaulting webhook)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sideEffects</code></td><td>declares whether the webhook has side effects outside the API request</td><td>must be <code>None</code> or <code>NoneOnDryRun</code> for the webhook to be safely called during <code>--dry-run=server</code>; otherwise dry-run skips it entirely</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Real-world nuance beyond the exam:</strong> PSA can never cause a cluster-wide outage — it's compiled into the apiserver binary and always answers instantly. A custom <code>ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</code> (Kyverno, OPA Gatekeeper, a home-grown policy service) is a whole extra network hop with its own uptime, and a misconfigured <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> pointed at a webhook Service with zero healthy endpoints is one of the most common ways teams accidentally lock themselves out of their own cluster — <em>every</em> matching <code>kubectl apply</code> starts failing, including the fix for the webhook itself, unless you scoped <code>namespaceSelector</code> to exclude <code>kube-system</code> in advance. Production teams pair <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> with a PodDisruptionBudget and multiple replicas for the webhook Deployment, and always carve out an escape hatch namespace.</div>

                    <h5>⑦ Troubleshooting workflow — "my pod won't start," diagnosed layer by layer</h5>
                    <p>A pod that fails to appear or fails to run can be blocked at any one of four completely different layers, and each produces a different symptom. Guessing wastes exam minutes and production incident time alike — this is the fixed diagnostic order that separates them fast.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   "kubectl apply -f easypj-web.yaml" produced no Running pod — where in the
   pipeline did it stop? Check in THIS order, each step has a distinct signature:

   1. RBAC (authorization) blocked the request
      symptom: kubectl itself prints "Error from server (Forbidden): ...
                cannot create resource \"pods\" in API group \"\" "
      check:   kubectl auth can-i create pods --as=&lt;user&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;
      → this is a Ch 24 problem, not a Ch 27 problem — no object was ever built

   2. PodSecurity admission (PSA) rejected the object
      symptom: "Error from server (Forbidden): pods \"x\" is forbidden:
                violates PodSecurity \"restricted:latest\": ..." — the pod
                object was NEVER created (no entry in `kubectl get pods` at all)
      check:   the error message itself lists every failing control by name
      → fix the securityContext fields it names, re-apply

   3. kubelet refused to START the (already-admitted) container
      symptom: pod EXISTS (`kubectl get pods` shows it) but STATUS is
                CreateContainerConfigError or CrashLoopBackOff
      check:   kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;  → Events section
                "container has runAsNonRoot and image will run as root"
                = admission passed (field was present) but the IMAGE's actual
                UID resolves to 0 — a PSA/kubelet split-check mismatch (see ①)
      → set an explicit runAsUser that matches a real non-root UID in the image

   4. The container started but the APP failed inside it
      symptom: STATUS shows Running briefly then CrashLoopBackOff, or Running
                but the app is unhealthy — readOnlyRootFilesystem or a dropped
                capability the app needed silently broke it at the code level
      check:   kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous   → application's own error
                (e.g. "Errno 30: Read-only file system" from Python itself)
      → this is an application-level consequence of §27.3's hardening —
        add the missing emptyDir mount or the missing capability</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Layer</th><th>Fastest confirmation</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>Forbidden</code>, no pod object created, mentions a verb/resource</td><td>RBAC</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i create pods -n &lt;ns&gt; --as=&lt;user&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Forbidden</code>, no pod object created, mentions <code>PodSecurity</code></td><td>PSA (admission)</td><td>read the error text — it names every violated control</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod EXISTS, <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code></td><td>kubelet runtime check</td><td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → Events</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod Running then <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>, app-level error in logs</td><td>Application vs. hardening interaction</td><td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> layers 1-2 never create a pod object at all (nothing to <code>describe</code> or <code>logs</code> — the rejection is the ENTIRE signal), while layers 3-4 always do. That single fact — "does <code>kubectl get pods</code> show anything?" — is the fastest triage question and instantly cuts your search space in half.</div>

            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Chapter 27 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Pod Security Standards define three levels:</strong> privileged (anything goes — for kube-system/CNI), baseline (blocks the obvious escapes: privileged containers, hostNetwork/hostPID/hostIPC, hostPath), and restricted (everything baseline blocks, plus non-root, drop ALL capabilities, seccomp, no privilege escalation).</li>
                    <li><strong>Pod Security Admission (PSA) enforces PSS per NAMESPACE via labels</strong>, in three independent modes: <code>enforce</code> (rejects the pod), <code>warn</code> (stderr message, still admits), <code>audit</code> (log entry, still admits). Roll out with warn/audit first, then flip to enforce.</li>
                    <li><strong>SecurityContext is where you actually harden a pod:</strong> <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, <code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code>, <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code> — those four fields are exactly what the restricted profile checks (add <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> too as a best practice — it hardens the pod further but isn't itself one of PSA's checks).</li>
                    <li><strong>PSA is admission-time only</strong> — it does NOT evict already-running pods, and it complements (never replaces) RBAC and NetworkPolicy. A pod that was compliant when created keeps running even if the namespace's policy later tightens; only recreate/update re-evaluates it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Admission control runs AFTER authn/authz and BEFORE etcd:</strong> mutating webhooks/plugins execute first (they may rewrite the object), then validating plugins run (PodSecurity is a validating plugin) — a single failed control under <code>enforce</code> rejects the whole pod.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pin <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version</code></strong> so a cluster upgrade never silently tightens the restricted definition and breaks admissions that used to pass.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">Container SecurityContext inherits all pod-level settings</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">Container SecurityContext <strong>overrides</strong> pod-level for overlapping fields</span></li>
                    <li>PSA labels use the format <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/MODE=LEVEL</code> — three modes (enforce/warn/audit) × three levels (privileged/baseline/restricted)</li>
                    <li>PSA <code>enforce</code> mode <strong>rejects</strong> pods. <code>warn</code> shows a warning but allows. <code>audit</code> logs only</li>
                    <li><code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> checks the <strong>image USER directive</strong> — if the image has no USER set, the pod is rejected even if the process would run as non-root</li>
                    <li><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> blocks writes to the container filesystem — use emptyDir volumes for writable directories</li>
                    <li><code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code> then <code>add</code> specific ones = principle of least privilege</li>
                    <li>The <code>restricted</code> PSS requires: runAsNonRoot, no privilege escalation, drop ALL capabilities, seccomp profile — missing ANY of these causes rejection</li>
                </ul>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>3 PSA modes</strong> — <code>enforce</code> (reject violations), <code>warn</code> (allow but show warning), <code>audit</code> (log only). The exam usually asks for <code>enforce</code>. Apply via namespace labels, not cluster config</li>
                        <li><strong>3 PSS levels</strong> — <code>privileged</code> (no restrictions), <code>baseline</code> (blocks known escalations), <code>restricted</code> (hardened). Restricted requires <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, drop ALL capabilities, and <code>seccompProfile</code></li>
                        <li><strong>SecurityContext at pod vs container level</strong> — <code>spec.securityContext</code> sets defaults for ALL containers. <code>spec.containers[].securityContext</code> overrides per container. Container-level wins</li>
                        <li><strong><code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> fails if image uses root</strong> — The container must have a non-root USER in Dockerfile, or set <code>runAsUser: 1000</code> explicitly. Otherwise the pod is rejected at startup</li>
                        <li><strong>Admission controllers are API server flags</strong> — <code>--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</code>. To add one, edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. The API server auto-restarts</li>
                        <li><strong>A pod can pass <code>warn</code> and still be <code>enforce</code>-rejected</strong> — if you set <code>warn</code> to a looser level than <code>enforce</code> (e.g. <code>warn=baseline</code> but <code>enforce=restricted</code>), a pod can sail past the warning check yet still get hard-rejected by enforce. Keep <code>warn</code>/<code>audit</code> aligned with (or stricter than) <code>enforce</code> during rollout so the warning actually previews what enforce will do.</li>
                        <li><strong><code>enforce</code> only ever blocks the resulting Pod object, never the parent workload resource</strong> — <code>kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml</code> against a restricted namespace <em>succeeds</em> even if its pod template violates the standard; the Deployment object is created, but its ReplicaSet then fails to create Pods (visible as 0/N ready replicas and a <code>FailedCreate</code> event on the ReplicaSet, not an error from <code>kubectl apply</code> itself). <code>warn</code>/<code>audit</code>, by contrast, ARE evaluated directly against Deployments/Jobs/etc. at apply-time for early detection — this is why the warning can appear before you even create a single Pod.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — SecurityContext skeleton (memorize):</strong><br>
                <code>securityContext:</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>runAsUser: 1000</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>runAsNonRoot: true</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>capabilities: { drop: ["ALL"] }</code><br>
                This covers 90% of SecurityContext questions on the exam.</p>
                <p><strong>Roll-out order, every time:</strong> set <code>warn</code>+<code>audit</code>=restricted FIRST, watch the warnings/audit log for a while, fix the flagged pods, and only THEN flip <code>enforce</code>=restricted. Going straight to enforce on a live namespace is how you take down a Deployment you didn't realize was non-compliant.</p>
                <p><strong><code>kubectl get ns easypj --show-labels</code></strong> is the single fastest way to read a namespace's current PSA level and mode — faster than <code>kubectl describe ns</code>, and it's the first command to run when a rejection doesn't make sense.</p>
                <p><strong>Test before you commit:</strong> <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f pod.yaml -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> evaluates the manifest against the namespace's live PSA labels without actually creating anything — the rejection message (if any) names the exact violated control, so you can fix the YAML before it ever touches the cluster.</p>
                <p><strong>The one-liner to memorize:</strong> <code>kubectl label ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted --overwrite</code> — add <code>--overwrite</code> whenever the label might already exist (safe to include even when it doesn't).</p>
            </div>

