            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 27 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Deeper recall of PSA's place in the pipeline, exemptions, cluster-wide defaults, and the kernel mechanics behind SecurityContext — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where does Pod Security Admission sit relative to RBAC in request handling? Are they redundant?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Two different questions, two different stages</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   kubectl apply -f easypj-web.yaml
        │
        ▼
   AUTHENTICATION  ──  "who are you?"            (cert / SA token)
        │
        ▼
   AUTHORIZATION (RBAC)  ──  "may THIS SUBJECT create a Pod at all?"
        │  (only reached if allowed)
        ▼
   ADMISSION — Pod Security  ──  "is the POD'S SPEC itself safe?"
        │  (checks runAsNonRoot, capabilities, seccomp... on the OBJECT, not the caller)
        ▼
   💾 etcd
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <p>Not redundant — they answer different questions. RBAC (authorization) decides whether the <em>subject</em> (a user or ServiceAccount) may perform the <code>create</code> verb on <code>pods</code> at all. PSA (admission) then inspects the <em>object itself</em> — its securityContext, hostNetwork flag, capabilities — regardless of who submitted it. A cluster admin with full RBAC rights can still be blocked by PSA if their pod spec violates the namespace's restricted standard.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Defense in depth: RBAC alone can't stop an authorized user from submitting an insecure pod spec, and PSA alone can't stop an unauthorized user from trying. They are complementary controls layered on the same request, evaluated at different pipeline stages.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A cluster admin can exempt certain workloads from restricted enforcement. What can you exempt by, and why would you need to?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Exemption dimension</th><th>What it matches</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>usernames</code></td><td>a specific authenticated identity (user or SA)</td><td>a cluster controller/operator that legitimately needs privileged pods</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>runtimeClasses</code></td><td>pods using a named RuntimeClass</td><td>a gVisor/Kata sandboxed runtime that's inherently isolated differently</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>namespaces</code></td><td>an entire namespace, regardless of its labels</td><td><code>kube-system</code> — CNI/CSI/monitoring daemonsets need host access</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">admission-config-psa-exemptions.yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: AdmissionConfiguration
plugins:
- name: PodSecurity
  configuration:
    apiVersion: pod-security.admission.config.k8s.io/v1
    kind: PodSecurityConfiguration
    defaults:
      enforce: baseline
    exemptions:
      usernames: ["system:serviceaccount:kube-system:calico-node"]
      runtimeClasses: ["gvisor-sandbox"]
      namespaces: ["kube-system"]</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Without exemptions, cluster-critical system components (CNI plugins that need hostNetwork, node agents that need hostPath) would be permanently blocked the moment you set any restrictive PSA default. Exemptions are cluster-wide (in the apiserver's AdmissionConfiguration), not per-namespace labels — they carve precise, auditable holes instead of leaving a whole namespace unlabeled.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Instead of labeling every namespace by hand, how do you set a cluster-wide default PSA level, and do per-namespace labels still win?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Reference an <code>AdmissionConfiguration</code> file from the apiserver's <code>--admission-control-config-file</code> flag. It sets cluster-wide <code>defaults</code> (enforce/warn/audit + version) for every namespace that has NO conflicting label — a namespace's own <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/*</code> labels always override the cluster default for that namespace.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">wire-cluster-wide-psa-default.sh</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Write the AdmissionConfiguration on the control-plane node</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF &gt; /etc/kubernetes/pki/admission-config.yaml
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: AdmissionConfiguration
plugins:
- name: PodSecurity
  configuration:
    apiVersion: pod-security.admission.config.k8s.io/v1
    kind: PodSecurityConfiguration
    defaults:
      enforce: baseline
      enforce-version: latest
      audit: restricted
      warn: restricted
    exemptions:
      namespaces: [kube-system, kube-public, kube-node-lease]
EOF

<span class="comment"># 2. Point the apiserver at it (edit the static pod manifest)</span>
vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># add: --admission-control-config-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/admission-config.yaml
# and mount that file into the apiserver static pod's volumes/volumeMounts</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Cluster-wide defaults keep every future namespace safe-by-default without a human remembering to label it. Precedence order is: namespace label &gt; cluster-wide default &gt; PSA's own built-in default (privileged, unrestricted). This is a real production pattern — new namespaces are "secure by default" the moment they're created.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You wrote <code>capabilities: {drop: ["ALL"], add: ["NET_BIND_SERVICE"]}</code> for easypj-web. Can that container still bind port 22 (SSH)? Why or why not?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Yes — <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> allows binding <strong>any</strong> port below 1024, not just 80/443. Port 22 is &lt;1024, so it's covered too. The capability grants a whole numeric range, not a specific port.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Port range</th><th>Needs NET_BIND_SERVICE?</th><th>easypj example</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>0 – 1023 (privileged)</td><td>✅ Yes — non-root can't bind without it</td><td>80 (HTTP), 443 (HTTPS), 22 (SSH)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>1024 – 65535 (unprivileged)</td><td>❌ No — any UID can bind these</td><td>8080 (Gunicorn/Django dev server)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="info-box tip">
                            <h5>💡 Preferred pattern</h5>
                            <p>Most teams avoid granting <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> entirely: run easypj-web on port <strong>8080</strong> as a normal unprivileged process, then let the Kubernetes <code>Service</code> map external port <strong>80</strong> → <code>targetPort: 8080</code>. That keeps <code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code> with nothing added back — the smallest possible attack surface.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Linux capabilities are coarse-grained by design — <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> is a single on/off switch for "can bind &lt;1024," not a per-port ACL. If you truly need &lt;1024 binding, add only that one capability; otherwise, remap the port in the Service and add nothing.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your image's Dockerfile has no <code>USER</code> instruction (so it defaults to root/UID 0). You set <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>. What exactly happens at pod startup?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">runAsNonRoot is checked TWICE — once at admission, once by the kubelet</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   1. PSA (API server, at apply-time)
      → only checks that the FIELD runAsNonRoot: true is PRESENT in the spec.
      → it does NOT know what UID the image actually resolves to.
      → passes admission — the pod is created.
              │
              ▼
   2. kubelet (on the node, at container-start-time)
      → inspects the IMAGE's metadata (or the resolved runAsUser) for its real UID.
      → if the effective UID would be 0 → REFUSES to start the container.
              │
              ▼
   kubectl get pod → STATUS: CreateContainerConfigError
   kubectl describe pod → "container has runAsNonRoot and image will run as root"
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PSA/admission validates the <em>declared intent</em> in the YAML; the kubelet enforces the <em>actual runtime identity</em> when it starts the container. This two-stage check is why a pod can be admitted successfully and still fail to start — always pair <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> with an explicit <code>runAsUser: 1000</code> (or a Dockerfile <code>USER</code> line) so both checks agree.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">When would you use a <code>Localhost</code> seccomp profile instead of <code>RuntimeDefault</code>, and where does the profile file have to live?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Source of the filter</th><th>When to use</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>RuntimeDefault</code></td><td>the container runtime's (containerd/CRI-O) built-in profile</td><td>the default — good enough for 95% of workloads, satisfies "restricted"</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Localhost</code></td><td>a custom JSON file you author and ship to every node</td><td>RuntimeDefault is too permissive (extra-hardened) OR too strict (blocks a syscall your app legitimately needs)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Unconfined</code></td><td>none — no syscall filtering at all</td><td>never, in a restricted namespace — this is what "no seccomp set" effectively means</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">localhost-seccomp-pod.yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-worker
spec:
  securityContext:
    seccompProfile:
      type: Localhost
      localhostProfile: profiles/easypj-worker.json
      <span class="comment"># resolves to /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/easypj-worker.json
      # on EVERY node the pod could schedule to — ship it via a
      # DaemonSet init step, host provisioning, or a CSI driver.</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>localhostProfile</code> path is relative to the kubelet's seccomp root, <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> — the JSON file must exist there on every node the pod can be scheduled to, or the container fails to start. This is why <code>RuntimeDefault</code> is the default recommendation: it needs zero file distribution and already satisfies the restricted PSS.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> actually stop an attack, at the kernel level?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">no_new_privs — the kernel bit that closes the setuid escape hatch</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   WITHOUT allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ container runs as UID 1000 (non-root)                       │
   │  → attacker finds a setuid-root binary (e.g. /usr/bin/sudo, │
   │    a misconfigured setuid helper shipped in the image)      │
   │  → executes it → kernel grants it EUID 0 during exec        │
   │  → attacker is effectively root inside the container         │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   WITH allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ kubelet sets the process's no_new_privs bit (prctl syscall) │
   │  → the kernel now REFUSES to honor setuid/setgid bits, or   │
   │    any file capability, for that process AND its children   │
   │  → the same setuid-root binary silently runs as UID 1000,   │
   │    it can no longer escalate — no_new_privs wins             │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>no_new_privs</code> is a per-process, inheritable Linux kernel flag (set via the <code>prctl(PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS)</code> syscall) that permanently disables privilege gains through <code>execve()</code> — no setuid binary, no file capability, no securebits trick can raise privileges once it's set, and children inherit it too. This is why the restricted PSS requires it: it neutralizes an entire class of "found a setuid binary in the image" container-breakout techniques.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL-based, covered in depth in Ch 41) relate to Pod Security Admission — do they compete?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Pod Security Admission (PSA)</th><th>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (VAP)</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Scope</td><td>fixed — only the 3 built-in PSS profiles</td><td>arbitrary — any rule you can express in CEL</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Configuration</td><td>namespace labels</td><td>a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> + binding object</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Extensibility</td><td>none — can't add custom checks</td><td>fully custom (e.g. "image must come from our registry")</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Needs a webhook server?</td><td>no — built into the apiserver</td><td>no — CEL runs in-process in the apiserver too</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p>They stack, not compete — both are validating-admission-stage checks that run side by side. Use PSA for the standard, well-known pod-hardening baseline (fast, zero config, exam-favorite); use VAP for anything PSA can't express, like "images must be from <code>registry.easypj.internal</code>" or "Deployments must have a <code>team</code> label."</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PSA is deliberately narrow and opinionated (only the 3 PSS levels) so it stays simple and fast to reason about. VAP fills the gap for organization-specific policy without needing an external webhook server (unlike the older ValidatingAdmissionWebhook pattern) — CEL expressions are compiled and evaluated in-process. Both sit at the same "validating" stage of the admission pipeline from §27.4.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why was PodSecurityPolicy (PSP) removed in Kubernetes 1.25, and what should you never do on the exam?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>PodSecurityPolicy (PSP) — removed v1.25</th><th>PSA / PSS — current</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Object type</td><td>a cluster-scoped API resource you create</td><td>no object — just namespace labels</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Applies via</td><td>RBAC bindings to the PSP itself (confusing, indirect)</td><td>direct namespace labels — one line, obvious scope</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>fully custom fields, but very complex to reason about</td><td>3 fixed profiles — simpler, but less granular</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Status</td><td>❌ deprecated 1.21, REMOVED 1.25 — does not exist anymore</td><td>✅ current, GA since 1.25</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="info-box danger">
                            <h5>🚨 Exam trap</h5>
                            <p>Never write <code>kind: PodSecurityPolicy</code> on a current-version exam cluster — it isn't a registered API kind anymore and <code>kubectl apply</code> will fail outright. The CKA exam runs a recent Kubernetes version; it tests <strong>PSA labels</strong> (<code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=...</code>), never the old PSP object.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PSP was removed because its RBAC-binding-to-a-policy-object model was confusing and error-prone (a pod's effective policy depended on which PSP the creating SA/user happened to be bound to, not the namespace) — PSA's namespace-label model is simpler, more auditable, and impossible to accidentally leave unbound.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the fastest way to discover exactly which admission plugins are enabled on a running kubeadm cluster, without editing anything?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">discover-admission-plugins.sh</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Fastest — read the static pod manifest directly (no exec needed):</span>
grep enable-admission-plugins /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># Equally fast, from the live running Pod spec:</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod -l component=kube-apiserver \
  -o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ' ' '\n' | grep admission

<span class="comment"># Baseline defaults compiled into any modern (v1.25+) apiserver:</span>
kube-apiserver -h | grep -A2 "enable-admission-plugins"
<span class="comment"># CertificateApproval,CertificateSigning,CertificateSubjectRestriction,
# DefaultIngressClass,DefaultStorageClass,DefaultTolerationSeconds,
# LimitRanger,MutatingAdmissionWebhook,NamespaceLifecycle,PodSecurity,
# ResourceQuota,ServiceAccount,StorageObjectInUseProtection,
# ValidatingAdmissionPolicy,ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The static pod manifest is the source of truth on a kubeadm cluster — it's what the kubelet actually launches the apiserver container with. <code>PodSecurity</code> has shipped enabled-by-default since v1.25, so on any current exam cluster you can assume PSA is already active without checking, but knowing how to verify it (and how to add a missing plugin) is a fast, high-confidence exam point.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

