                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> a namespace labeled <code>enforce=restricted</code> turns Pod Security Admission into a gate at the door of the API server, sitting in the validating-admission stage right before etcd. The hardened <code>easypj-web</code> pod sets all four restricted controls PSA actually checks — non-root, no privilege escalation, drop ALL capabilities, seccomp RuntimeDefault — plus the best-practice read-only rootfs (not itself a PSA check, but good hardening on top) — so PSA admits it and it reaches etcd, then the scheduler. A stock nginx image running as root sets none of them, so PSA rejects it outright, and the rejection message names every single violated control by name.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> pick a Pod Security Standard (privileged/baseline/restricted) for the workload's sensitivity, enforce it per namespace with a one-line <code>kubectl label</code> — roll out with warn+audit first, then flip to enforce — and harden every production pod's SecurityContext with the same four PSA-checked fields plus read-only rootfs every time. Already-running pods are untouched by a new label until they are recreated, and PSA is a checkpoint that complements RBAC and NetworkPolicy, never a replacement for either. Next: Part 8 turns from securing the cluster to keeping it alive — upgrades, backups, and node maintenance.</p>
