            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🏨 Real-World Analogy: Renovating a Fully-Booked Hotel</h5>
                <p>A busy hotel can't close its doors to renovate — guests are checked in and the front desk stays open. So the crew works floor by floor: finish floor 3 completely and confirm it's solid before starting floor 4, never skipping ahead. Before touching anything, they photocopy the guest ledger and lock it in a fireproof safe — if a renovation goes wrong, that ledger is the only way to reconstruct who's staying where. And when a floor is actually under the drill, its guests are politely relocated to open rooms elsewhere in the hotel — never all evicted onto the street at once, and never below the hotel's minimum-occupancy promise to the rest of the building. That's exactly the discipline Part 8 teaches: patch the cluster one component and one node at a time, keep a tested backup of the source of truth, and move workloads off a node safely before you touch it.</p>
<pre>  RENOVATING A BUSY HOTEL                       CLUSTER MAINTENANCE
  ───────────────────────                       ──────────────────
