            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Upgrading Kubernetes means bumping every node to the new version at once."</td>
                            <td><code>kubeadm upgrade</code> is strictly sequential: the primary control-plane node first, then any additional control-plane nodes, then workers one at a time — and never skipping a minor version. The version skew policy (kubelet up to 3 minors behind the API server, kubectl ±1) exists precisely because a mid-upgrade cluster is briefly running mixed versions on purpose.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"My YAML manifests in Git are my real backup — etcd is just an implementation detail."</td>
                            <td>etcd is the <em>only</em> complete record of cluster state: current Pod-to-node bindings, Secrets, live status, leases, and everything your controllers have reconciled — not just the desired state you wrote. Lose etcd with no snapshot and you lose the ability to reconstruct exactly what was actually running, even if every manifest is safely in Git.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"<code>kubectl drain</code> force-kills every Pod on the node immediately."</td>
                            <td>Drain calls the Eviction API, which checks every matching PodDisruptionBudget first. If evicting a Pod would push a Deployment below its <code>minAvailable</code>, that eviction is rejected and retried with backoff — drain can visibly "hang" waiting for a replacement Pod to become Ready elsewhere, which is the PDB doing its job, not a bug.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
