                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production Nuance — Preview Before You Commit, and Know the Alternative Strategy</h5>
                    <p>Everything in this chapter is the <strong>in-place upgrade</strong> model: the same node's binaries get replaced one version at a time. This is exactly what the CKA exam tests, and it is how you must operate any self-managed kubeadm cluster (bare metal, on-prem VMs, or a hand-rolled cloud cluster). Two things worth knowing beyond the exam script:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Dry-run before you commit control plane changes:</strong> <code>kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0 --dry-run</code> renders the new static pod manifests and prints exactly what would change — without writing anything to disk or restarting a single container. On a production control plane you don't get to practice on, running the dry-run first (in addition to <code>upgrade plan</code>, which only diffs versions, not manifest content) is the closer you'd want to the "what does the actual YAML look like after this?" question.</li>
                        <li><strong>Managed Kubernetes (EKS/GKE/AKS) does NOT do this dance.</strong> Those platforms upgrade the control plane via their own control-plane-as-a-service API (you click/API-call "upgrade to 1.31", the provider swaps its own control plane infrastructure behind the scenes — you never SSH in or touch <code>apt-mark</code>). For <em>worker nodes</em> on managed platforms, the far more common real-world pattern isn't "drain, upgrade in place, uncordon" at all — it's <strong>immutable node replacement</strong>: spin up brand-new nodes already running the target kubelet version (new AMI/image), drain and cordon the old ones, let the scheduler move pods to the new nodes, then terminate the old nodes entirely. Nothing is ever "upgraded in place" on the node itself.</li>
                        <li><strong><code>--etcd-upgrade=false</code> decouples etcd from the control-plane bump:</strong> if your etcd runs outside kubeadm's normal lifecycle expectations, or you simply want to schedule its upgrade separately from the Kubernetes components, run <code>kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0 --etcd-upgrade=false</code> — this upgrades only the API server/controller-manager/scheduler manifests and leaves etcd's image and config completely untouched, to be upgraded on its own schedule per its own compatibility matrix. Note <code>--etcd-upgrade</code> defaults to <code>true</code> — you only ever pass it explicitly to turn etcd upgrading <em>off</em>.</li>
                        <li><strong>On a 3-node HA control plane, addon upgrades wait for the LAST control-plane node.</strong> kubeadm checks whether every control-plane instance has been upgraded before it touches the CoreDNS/kube-proxy addons — so if you upgrade cp-1 and cp-2 but leave cp-3 on the old minor for days, the addon bump (and its accompanying image pull) simply doesn't happen yet. Either upgrade all control-plane nodes back-to-back in one sitting, or accept that CoreDNS/kube-proxy stay on the old version until the very last CP node finishes.</li>
                        <li><strong>Minimizing etcd downtime during a stacked-etcd upgrade:</strong> when <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> bumps a stacked etcd member, in-flight requests to the API server on that node briefly stall while the etcd static pod restarts. To shrink that window, gracefully stop the API server first so it finishes in-flight requests and closes its connections cleanly: <code>sudo killall -s SIGTERM kube-apiserver && sleep 20</code> immediately before running <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>/<code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> — the kubelet will restart the API server as a normal part of the upgrade regardless.</li>
                        <li><strong>A failed/interrupted <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> is safe to just re-run.</strong> The command is designed to be idempotent — if it errors out partway (network blip pulling an image, a transient etcd health hiccup), fix the underlying cause and run the exact same <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>/<code>upgrade node</code> command again; kubeadm re-evaluates the cluster's actual current state and only performs the steps still needed to reach the target version, rather than assuming a clean slate.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Why does this matter if the exam only tests kubeadm? Because as an administrator you will very likely operate both kinds of clusters across your career, and conflating the two models is a real production mistake — e.g. trying to <code>apt-mark hold kubelet</code> on an EKS-managed worker node accomplishes nothing, because that node's AMI is baked and replaced wholesale, not patched live.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> vs Reconfiguring a Cluster — Two Different Jobs</h5>
                    <p><code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>/<code>upgrade node</code> also accept a <code>--config</code> file (the <code>UpgradeConfiguration</code> API type), but don't confuse "supply config during an upgrade" with "change my cluster's configuration." <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> is exclusively for moving to a new Kubernetes <em>version</em> — it deliberately does <strong>not</strong> support reconfiguring an already-running cluster (e.g. changing the pod CIDR, swapping the certificate SANs, or altering <code>ClusterConfiguration</code> fields unrelated to the version bump). If what you actually need is to change existing cluster configuration rather than its version, that's a separate, dedicated workflow (<code>kubeadm init phase</code> / the reconfiguration docs) — don't reach for <code>upgrade</code> to do it.</p>
                </div>

