            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Cluster Upgrade — The Complete Sequence</h5>
            <p><code>kubeadm upgrade</code> doesn't upgrade running containers in-place. It updates static pod manifests AND upgrades kubelet/kubectl binaries. Understanding the exact sequence prevents catastrophic upgrade failures.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CLUSTER UPGRADE — Full Sequence (1.30.0 → 1.31.0)                           │
│                                                                              │
│  PHASE 1: UPGRADE CONTROL PLANE NODE (cp-node-1)                             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. DRAIN the node (move workloads to other nodes):                   │   │
│  │    $ kubectl drain cp-node-1 --ignore-daemonsets                      │   │
│  │    → All pods evicted to other nodes                                  │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ 2. UPGRADE kubeadm binary:                                            │   │
│  │    $ sudo apt-mark unhold kubeadm                                     │   │
│  │    $ sudo apt-get update && apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.0-*     │   │
│  │    $ sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm                                       │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ 3. UPGRADE control plane (kubeadm updates static pod manifests):     │   │
│  │    $ sudo kubeadm upgrade plan   <span class="comment">← SEE what will change              </span>│   │
│  │    $ sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0                               │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │    kubeadm upgrade apply DOES:                                        │   │
│  │    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │   │
│  │    │ • Update /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml     │    │   │
│  │    │   → image: registry.k8s.io/kube-apiserver:v1.30.0          │    │   │
│  │    │                        ↓                                   │    │   │
│  │    │   → image: registry.k8s.io/kube-apiserver:v1.31.0          │    │   │
│  │    │                                                              │    │   │
│  │    │ • Same for: kube-controller-manager.yaml, kube-scheduler.yaml│    │   │
│  │    │ • Update kubeadm-config + kubelet-config ConfigMaps         │    │   │
│  │    │ • Renew ALL managed certs unconditionally                   │    │   │
│  │    │ • Add new API resources for v1.31 (if any)                  │    │   │
│  │    │                                                              │    │   │
│  │    │ ⚡ KUBELET DETECTS manifest changes → restarts static pods  │    │   │
│  │    │ ⚡ New API Server starts with v1.31.0 image                 │    │   │
│  │    └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ 4. UPGRADE kubelet + kubectl on this node:                           │   │
│  │    $ sudo apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.0-* kubectl=1.31.0-*     │   │
│  │    $ sudo systemctl daemon-reload                                     │   │
│  │    $ sudo systemctl restart kubelet                                   │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ 5. UNCORDON the node:                                                 │   │
│  │    $ kubectl uncordon cp-node-1                                        │   │
│  │    → Node is schedulable again                                         │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ 6. VERIFY:                                                             │   │
│  │    $ kubectl get nodes                                                │   │
│  │    cp-node-1   Ready   control-plane   v1.31.0 ✅                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  PHASE 2: UPGRADE WORKER NODES (for EACH worker)                             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. DRAIN worker: kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets           │   │
│  │ 2. Upgrade kubeadm: apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.0-*              │   │
│  │ 3. Upgrade node: sudo kubeadm upgrade node                            │   │
│  │    → Updates kubelet-config, NOT static pod manifests (worker)       │   │
│  │ 4. Upgrade kubelet: apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.0-*              │   │
│  │ 5. Restart kubelet: sudo systemctl restart kubelet                    │   │
│  │ 6. Uncordon: kubectl uncordon worker-1                                │   │
│  │ 7. Repeat for each worker                                             │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  VERSION SKEW POLICY (CKA Critical):                                         │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ • kube-apiserver is the version baseline for every component         │   │
│  │ • kubelet/kube-proxy: up to 3 minor behind (N-3 to N), never ahead   │   │
│  │ • kubectl: X-1 to X+1 (1 minor version skew, either direction)       │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ Upgrade ORDER (MUST follow):                                          │   │
│  │   1. Control Plane FIRST                                              │   │
│  │   2. Workers AFTER (one at a time, drain before each)                │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ NEVER skip minor versions: 1.30 → 1.31 → 1.32 (NOT 1.30 → 1.32)     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Cluster upgrade is a GUARANTEED CKA exam question. Memorize the sequence: drain→upgrade kubeadm→upgrade apply (cp) or upgrade node (worker)→upgrade kubelet→restart→uncordon. Common traps: forgetting to hold packages after upgrade, draining without --ignore-daemonsets (blocks on DaemonSet pods), and skipping version skew policy (always upgrade one minor at a time).
            </div>

                    <h5>① <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> — the exact preflight checks it runs before you touch anything</h5>
                    <p>"Just run the upgrade and see what happens" is not a CKA-safe strategy, and it's not a production-safe one either. <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> exists specifically so you never find out about a blocking problem <em>during</em> the upgrade — it's a fully read-only dry run that surfaces every reason the real <code>apply</code> could fail.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   $ sudo kubeadm upgrade plan   — what it checks, in order:

   1. Reads the LIVE cluster version from the kubeadm-config ConfigMap (kube-system namespace)
      — NOT from a local file, so it always reflects reality even if someone hand-edited a manifest

   2. Runs the SAME preflight checks as `kubeadm init`:
        • Is the container runtime reachable via the CRI socket?
        • Can the node pull the target images from the configured registry?
        • Are required ports free, swap disabled, kernel modules loaded?

   3. Queries for available STABLE versions it's allowed to suggest
      (patch releases for your current minor + the very next minor, never further)

   4. Diffs current vs. target for EVERY control-plane component and prints a table:
        COMPONENT                  CURRENT   TARGET
        kube-apiserver              v1.30.0   v1.31.0
        kube-controller-manager     v1.30.0   v1.31.0
        kube-scheduler              v1.30.0   v1.31.0
        kube-proxy                  v1.30.0   v1.31.0
        CoreDNS                     v1.11.1   v1.11.3
        etcd                        3.5.12-0  3.5.15-0

   5. Prints WARNINGS for anything that needs manual action (e.g. a custom
      admission webhook config referencing a removed API version)

   ── kubeadm upgrade plan changes NOTHING on disk or in etcd — it is purely diagnostic ──</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Read the etcd and CoreDNS rows too.</strong> Most people only look at the Kubernetes component versions in the plan output, but etcd and CoreDNS get bumped by <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> as well — if either pull fails (private registry, no internet egress), the whole apply can stall partway through. Confirming image pull access <em>before</em> running apply is exactly what plan is for.</div>

                    <h5>② Static pod manifest swap — how <code>upgrade apply</code> changes the control plane without "restarting a service"</h5>
                    <p>There's no <code>systemctl restart kube-apiserver</code> — the API server isn't a systemd service, it's a static pod. So how does its container image actually change? kubeadm never edits the live manifest in place; it writes a complete new file to a staging location and only then does an atomic move, so the kubelet either sees the fully-old file or the fully-new one, never a half-written one.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0 — the manifest swap, step by step:

   1. kubeadm BACKS UP the current manifests first (safety net, not optional):
        /etc/kubernetes/tmp/kubeadm-backup-manifests-&lt;timestamp&gt;/kube-apiserver.yaml
        /etc/kubernetes/tmp/kubeadm-backup-etcd-&lt;timestamp&gt;/                (etcd data dir, if local)

   2. kubeadm renders the NEW manifest into a TEMP file first:
        /etc/kubernetes/tmp/kubeadm-upgraded-manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
             image: registry.k8s.io/kube-apiserver:v1.30.0  →  v1.31.0

   3. kubeadm does an ATOMIC rename (not an in-place edit) into the real path:
        mv /etc/kubernetes/tmp/kubeadm-upgraded-manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
           /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
        ── an atomic rename means the kubelet NEVER sees a partially-written file ──

   4. kubelet's file watcher (fsnotify on /etc/kubernetes/manifests/) fires almost
      instantly — it does NOT wait for its usual ~20s sync-loop interval for this

   5. kubelet KILLS the old static pod's container and starts a brand-new one
      from the new manifest — this is a real container restart, not a live patch,
      which is exactly why the API server has a few seconds of downtime here
      — technically, kubelet recomputes a hash of the pod spec on every sync;
      ANY change to the manifest (not just the image tag) changes that hash,
      which is what actually triggers the restart

   6. Repeat for kube-controller-manager.yaml and kube-scheduler.yaml (etcd.yaml
      too, if etcd is stacked on this node)</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>If it fails here…</th><th>Recovery path</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Backup (1)</td><td>Upgrade aborts before touching anything</td><td>Nothing changed — just re-run once the underlying issue (usually disk space) is fixed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Render new file (2)</td><td>Temp file is incomplete/invalid</td><td>Real manifest untouched (still old file) — API server keeps running on old version</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Atomic rename (3)</td><td>Cannot fail partway — rename is a single filesystem operation</td><td>N/A by design</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>kubelet restart (4-5)</td><td>New image can't be pulled, or crashes on start</td><td>Restore from the backup path in step 1, or fix the image/registry issue and let kubelet retry</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The backup step is why upgrades are recoverable.</strong> If <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> fails midway, you can manually copy the backed-up manifests from <code>/etc/kubernetes/tmp/kubeadm-backup-manifests-&lt;timestamp&gt;/</code> back into <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> to roll back to the exact pre-upgrade state — this is your emergency exit even without an etcd restore.</div>

                    <h5>③ How <code>kubectl drain</code> actually evicts pods — the Eviction API, not a raw delete</h5>
                    <p>It's tempting to think <code>drain</code> just deletes every pod on a node. It doesn't — it goes through a dedicated sub-resource that checks PodDisruptionBudgets first, which is exactly why a drain can "hang" waiting on a budget instead of just tearing everything down immediately.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
        │
        ▼
   1. cordon worker01 first (taint: node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable=NoSchedule)
      → scheduler will never place a NEW pod here, but existing pods are untouched so far
        │
        ▼
   2. list every pod bound to worker01 via the API server
        │
        ├─ DaemonSet-owned pod?  → --ignore-daemonsets set → SKIP (can't be evicted, node-bound by design)
        ├─ pod using emptyDir?   → --delete-emptydir-data set → allowed to proceed (data is deleted)
        └─ everything else       → proceed to eviction
        │
        ▼
   3. for each remaining pod, POST to the EVICTION sub-resource (NOT a DELETE call):
        POST /api/v1/namespaces/&lt;ns&gt;/pods/&lt;pod&gt;/eviction
        │
        ▼
   4. the API server checks: would this eviction violate ANY matching PodDisruptionBudget?
        ├─ NO  → eviction proceeds, pod gets a normal graceful-shutdown SIGTERM
        └─ YES → API returns HTTP 429 Too Many Requests
                  drain CLIENT retries this pod with backoff, keeps trying
                  (this is why a drain can look "stuck" — it's respecting your PDB, not broken)
        │
        ▼
   5. once ALL evictable pods are gone (or DaemonSet-skipped), drain reports success
      → node is now safe to reboot / upgrade kubelet on</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A "stuck" drain is usually a PDB doing its job.</strong> If <code>kubectl drain</code> hangs, check <code>kubectl get pdb -A</code> first — a PDB with <code>minAvailable</code> set higher than your currently-schedulable capacity will block eviction forever until you either free up capacity elsewhere or (last resort) add <code>--disable-eviction</code> to force a raw delete, bypassing the PDB.</div>

                    <h5>④ Certificate renewal — the other thing <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> quietly does for you</h5>
                    <p><code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> (and <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code>) isn't only about swapping container images — by default it also <strong>unconditionally renews every kubeadm-managed certificate</strong> on that node, entirely automatically, regardless of how much time is actually left before they'd expire. This matters because a cluster that goes a year or more between upgrades can otherwise walk straight into an expired-certificate outage — but it also means every upgrade is a certificate-rotation event whether you asked for one or not.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   During "kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0", kubeadm ALSO:

   1. Renews every cert under /etc/kubernetes/pki/ that it manages —
        apiserver.crt, apiserver-kubelet-client.crt, front-proxy-client.crt, etc.
      — UNCONDITIONALLY, not only ones close to expiry, signed by the
      existing CA (ca.crt / ca.key), no separate command needed

   2. Opt OUT of this behavior with a flag if your org treats cert rotation
      as its own controlled change (e.g. strict change-management policy):
        $ sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0 --certificate-renewal=false
      — this upgrades binaries/manifests only and leaves existing certs alone

   3. Certs are backed up before renewal:
        /etc/kubernetes/tmp/kubeadm-backup-manifests-&lt;timestamp&gt;/pki-backup/

   4. To check current expiry status WITHOUT upgrading (any time):
        $ sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration
      — shows expiration date + residual time for every managed cert
      — default leaf-cert validity is 365 days; the CA itself defaults to 3650 days (10 years)

   5. To force a renewal manually, WITHOUT waiting for an upgrade:
        $ sudo kubeadm certs renew all
        $ sudo systemctl restart kubelet
        $ sudo systemctl daemon-reload   (if the kubelet client cert changed)</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Regular upgrades are a free certificate-hygiene habit — by default, not by luck.</strong> Because <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> renews every managed cert unconditionally (not just near-expiry ones), and the CKA and most production clusters upgrade every few months, cert expiry rarely bites — the upgrade itself keeps resetting the clock. Clusters that go untouched for a year+ are the ones that hit a surprise "x509: certificate has expired" outage; run <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> proactively if you ever suspect a cluster has been neglected, and reach for <code>--certificate-renewal=false</code> only if you deliberately want an upgrade that does NOT touch certs.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ Auditing for deprecated/removed APIs BEFORE you upgrade — the check most admins skip</h5>
                    <p><code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> happily bumps the API server to the new minor version — but it does <strong>not</strong> check whether your own manifests, Helm charts, or CI pipelines still reference an API version that the new minor just removed. That check is entirely on you, and skipping it is how a routine upgrade turns into a Monday-morning incident: every <code>kubectl apply</code> or Deployment rollout that targets a dropped <code>apiVersion</code> starts failing the instant the new API server comes up.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   BEFORE any minor upgrade — audit for deprecated/removed APIs:

   1. Kubernetes deprecates an API version for AT LEAST one full year (or 3
      releases, whichever is longer) before removing it — but once it's gone,
      it's gone. A common historical example: batch/v1beta1 CronJob was
      deprecated in 1.21 and REMOVED in 1.25 — any manifest still using it
      would be rejected outright by a 1.25+ API server.

   2. Run a static scan of everything you might apply, BEFORE the upgrade:

        $ pluto detect-files -d ./k8s-manifests/          # scans YAML on disk
        $ pluto detect-helm -n easypj-production           # scans installed Helm releases
        $ kubent --target-versions 1.31.0                  # scans LIVE cluster objects

   3. For anything flagged, convert the manifest to the current apiVersion:

        $ kubectl convert -f old-cronjob.yaml \
            --output-version batch/v1 &gt; new-cronjob.yaml
        # kubectl convert is a plugin — install via:
        #   kubectl krew install convert

   4. Re-apply the converted manifest and re-run your CI's `kubectl apply
      --dry-run=server` against a staging cluster already on the TARGET
      minor, so removed-API errors surface before the real upgrade — not
      during it.

   5. This step is invisible in `kubeadm upgrade plan` output — plan only
      diffs COMPONENT versions (apiserver, etcd, CoreDNS…), it does NOT
      scan your workload manifests for removed API versions. That's a
      separate, manual responsibility every single upgrade.</pre></div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Scans</th><th>When to run</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>pluto detect-files</code></td><td>YAML manifests on disk (Git repo, Helm chart templates)</td><td>In CI, on every PR touching manifests</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>pluto detect-helm</code></td><td>Already-installed Helm releases in the live cluster</td><td>Before upgrading, against the TARGET version</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubent</code> (kube-no-trouble)</td><td>Live objects actually stored in etcd right now</td><td>Before upgrading — catches drift that manifests-on-disk scans miss</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl convert</code></td><td>N/A — fixes a single manifest's <code>apiVersion</code></td><td>After a scan flags a specific file</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 "It upgraded fine" ≠ "my workloads still work."</strong> A successful <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> only proves the CONTROL PLANE came up on the new version. It says nothing about whether your easypj Deployment/CronJob manifests, Helm charts, or a CI pipeline's <code>kubectl apply</code> step still reference an apiVersion that just got removed. Run <code>pluto</code>/<code>kubent</code> against the TARGET version as a pre-upgrade gate, not a post-upgrade surprise.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ What <code>kubeadm</code> does NOT upgrade for you — external etcd, CNI plugins, and multi-CP quorum</h5>
                    <p>It's easy to assume <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> is a one-command upgrade of "the whole cluster." It isn't. It upgrades the Kubernetes control-plane components and the two addons it manages directly (CoreDNS, kube-proxy) — and stops there. Three things routinely surprise people who think that's the whole story.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   THINGS kubeadm upgrade apply DOES NOT TOUCH:

   1. EXTERNAL etcd (not a kubeadm-managed static pod):
        If your cluster's etcd runs as its own systemd service / external
        cluster (common in larger production setups, NOT the CKA exam's
        default stacked-etcd layout), `kubeadm upgrade apply` skips it
        entirely — no etcd image bump, no version check against the new
        apiserver's compatibility matrix. YOU must upgrade external etcd
        yourself, checking the compatibility matrix for the target minor,
        BEFORE bumping the API server (an apiserver too new for its etcd
        can fail to start or misbehave on newer API object schemas).

   2. CNI PLUGIN (Calico, Cilium, Flannel, etc.):
        Pod networking is installed as its OWN DaemonSet/Deployment,
        completely outside kubeadm's control. A Kubernetes minor bump can
        require a matching CNI plugin bump (check ITS release notes for
        Kubernetes-version compatibility) — kubeadm will never do this
        for you, and an incompatible CNI can silently break pod-to-pod
        networking right after an otherwise "successful" upgrade.

   3. MULTI-CONTROL-PLANE QUORUM (HA clusters, e.g. cp-1/cp-2/cp-3):
        etcd requires a quorum (majority) of members to be healthy to
        accept writes. Upgrading more than one control-plane node's
        stacked-etcd member AT THE SAME TIME can drop below quorum and
        wedge the ENTIRE cluster — reads and writes both stop. This is
        why §28.2's "one node at a time" rule isn't just about avoiding
        app downtime — for a 3-node control plane it's the difference
        between an upgrade and an outage.
        # for a 3-member etcd, quorum = 2 — you can safely lose 1, never 2</pre></div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Upgraded by kubeadm?</th><th>Your responsibility</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Stacked etcd (kubeadm static pod)</td><td>✅ Yes — image bumped by <code>upgrade apply</code></td><td>Nothing extra — just back it up first (Ch.29)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>External etcd (own systemd unit/cluster)</td><td>❌ No</td><td>Upgrade it yourself per the compatibility matrix, BEFORE the apiserver</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>CoreDNS / kube-proxy</td><td>✅ Yes — bundled addon bump</td><td>Nothing extra</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>CNI plugin (Calico/Cilium/Flannel)</td><td>❌ No</td><td>Check ITS release notes for K8s-version compatibility; upgrade separately</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Multi-CP etcd quorum</td><td>N/A — kubeadm won't stop you</td><td>Upgrade CP nodes strictly one at a time; never drop below majority</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 kubeadm upgrades are largely forward-only — plan for that.</strong> There's no <code>kubeadm downgrade</code>. Your real rollback path is the etcd snapshot from Chapter 29 (STEP 0 of every upgrade) plus the pre-upgrade static-pod manifest backups kubeadm writes to <code>/etc/kubernetes/tmp/</code> (see subsection ②). On a 3-node HA control plane, treat "one CP node at a time" as sacred — losing etcd quorum mid-upgrade is one of the few ways a routine version bump becomes a full cluster outage.</div>

                    <h5>⑦ kubeadm's Built-In Guardrails — why <code>upgrade apply</code> can refuse to run at all</h5>
                    <p>Everything above describes what happens when an upgrade succeeds. Just as important is what kubeadm does <strong>before</strong> it lets an upgrade start: it doesn't just document the version-skip and cluster-health rules in a README — it actively enforces them in code, aborting with zero changes made if a precondition isn't met. Knowing these guardrails exist (and what to do when one fires) turns a scary red error message into a routine troubleshooting step.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   GUARDRAIL 1 — the version-skip block (enforced BEFORE any file is touched):

   $ sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.32.0        <span class="comment"># cluster is currently v1.30.x</span>
   [upgrade/version] FATAL: this version of kubeadm can only upgrade to
   a version that is at most one minor version newer than the currently
   installed version (v1.30.x) — v1.32.0 is two minor versions ahead.
   Upgrade to v1.31.x first.
        │
        ▼
   kubeadm compares your TARGET against the LIVE version recorded in the
   kubeadm-config ConfigMap — not a local file — so this check can't be
   fooled by a stale manifest. Nothing is written to disk; the abort is
   pure validation.

   GUARDRAIL 2 — the etcd health precheck (runs every single time, HA or not):

   $ sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0
   [upgrade/etcd] Etcd cluster health check failed: unhealthy members found
   [upgrade] FATAL: fix the etcd cluster before proceeding with the upgrade
        │
        ▼
   Before rewriting a single static-pod manifest, kubeadm calls the etcd
   member-list + health endpoint on EVERY member it knows about. One
   unreachable/unhealthy member is enough to abort — kubeadm refuses to
   layer a control-plane version bump on top of an already-degraded
   datastore, because a failed member during the upgrade's brief apiserver
   restart could tip a 3-node cluster below quorum.

   THE ESCAPE HATCH (last resort only):

   $ sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0 --force
   <span class="comment"># skips confirmation prompts AND some of these safety checks —</span>
   <span class="comment"># NOT a routine flag; only reach for it once you understand and have</span>
   <span class="comment"># deliberately accepted exactly which guardrail you're bypassing</span></pre></div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Guardrail</th><th>What it checks</th><th>If it fails</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Version-skip block</td><td>Target minor vs. LIVE cluster minor (from the kubeadm-config ConfigMap)</td><td>Aborts instantly — no manifest touched, no etcd contacted</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>etcd health precheck</td><td>Health endpoint of every known etcd member</td><td>Aborts before any static-pod manifest is rewritten</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Local kubelet-ahead check</td><td>Is this node's CURRENT kubelet already newer than the requested target?</td><td>Aborts — you can't "upgrade" to an older version this way</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A guardrail firing is kubeadm doing its job, not a bug to work around.</strong> If <code>upgrade apply</code> refuses to run, resist the urge to reach for <code>--force</code> first — read the error, fix the actual condition (heal the unhealthy etcd member, pick a valid one-minor-away target), and only re-run normally. <code>--force</code> exists for genuine edge cases (e.g. a known-safe etcd blip you've already verified), not as a way to silence an inconvenient check.</div>
