            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Upgrade order is rigid</strong> — Always: kubeadm → control plane components (<code>upgrade apply</code>) → kubelet + kubectl → worker nodes (one at a time). Never upgrade workers before the control plane. Never skip minor versions (1.30→1.32 is NOT allowed, must go 1.30→1.31→1.32).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>apply</code> vs <code>node</code></strong> — <code>kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0</code> runs ONLY on the first control-plane node and upgrades cluster-wide components. Every other node — additional control planes AND all workers — uses <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code>, which only refreshes local kubelet config.</li>
                    <li><strong>Drain before upgrading any node</strong> — <code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code>. Forgetting <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> causes the drain to fail (DaemonSet pods can't be evicted). Forgetting to drain at all means workloads crash mid-restart instead of gracefully rescheduling.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>apt-mark hold</code> after every install</strong> — Always hold kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl once installed: <code>apt-mark hold kubeadm kubelet kubectl</code>. This prevents a routine <code>apt upgrade</code> from silently jumping your cluster to an untested version.</li>
                    <li><strong>Control plane static pods restart automatically</strong> — After <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> rewrites the static pod manifests, the API server, controller-manager, scheduler, and etcd restart on their own (kubelet watches the manifest directory). There's a brief disruption to the API — the exam expects you to know this is normal, not a failure.</li>
                    <li><strong>Version skew policy (current, since K8s 1.25)</strong> — kubelet and kube-proxy must never be <em>newer</em> than the API server, and can each be up to <strong>3 minor versions behind</strong> it (N-3 to N); kube-proxy is additionally allowed up to 3 minor versions older <em>or</em> newer than the specific kubelet it runs alongside on the same node. kubectl can be ±1 minor version. Control-plane components (controller-manager, scheduler, cloud-controller-manager) are expected to match the API server's minor version but may run up to 1 minor version older. The exam tests whether you can judge if a mixed-version cluster is still valid.</li>
                    <li><strong>Back up etcd before every upgrade</strong> (Chapter 29) — an upgrade that goes wrong with no snapshot on hand is often unrecoverable. This is standard practice both for the exam and in production.</li>
                    <li><strong>daemon-reload is not optional</strong> — after upgrading the kubelet binary, run <code>systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; systemctl restart kubelet</code>. Skipping <code>daemon-reload</code> leaves systemd running the old cached unit file.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">Skip from 1.30 to 1.32 directly</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">Upgrade one minor version at a time: 1.30 → 1.31 → 1.32</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">Upgrade workers before control plane</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">Always upgrade control plane FIRST, then workers</span></li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting daemon-reload</strong> — Wrong: <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> (only). Correct: <code>systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; systemctl restart kubelet</code>. After upgrading the kubelet binary, systemd still has the old unit file cached. Without <code>daemon-reload</code>, the restart may use the old configuration or fail silently.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>apply</code> vs <code>node</code> on workers</strong> — Using <code>apply</code> on a worker node will fail or cause unexpected behavior. Only the <strong>first</strong> control-plane node uses <code>apply</code>. All other nodes (additional control-planes AND workers) use <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> is REQUIRED on drain — DaemonSet pods can't be evicted</li>
                    <li>Check the package version format — some providers use <code>=1.31.0-1.1</code> not <code>=1.31.0-00</code></li>
                    <li><strong>"kubelet can only be 1 minor version behind."</strong> Outdated — since Kubernetes 1.25 the policy allows kubelet (and kube-proxy) to be up to <strong>3</strong> minor versions behind the API server, not 1 (clusters older than 1.25 allowed only 2). Don't panic if you see a cluster with, say, API server 1.31 and a kubelet at 1.29 — that's still within policy.</li>
                    <li><strong>"kube-proxy must be the exact same version as kubelet."</strong> Not required — kube-proxy may be up to 3 minor versions older <em>or newer</em> than the kubelet on the same node, though in practice most clusters keep them in lockstep since they're upgraded together.</li>
                    <li><strong>"An HA control plane can have any mix of API server versions."</strong> Not true — across a multi-control-plane cluster, the newest and oldest <code>kube-apiserver</code> instances must be within <strong>1 minor version</strong> of each other. This is exactly why an HA control-plane upgrade must run to completion (every CP node landed on the new minor) in a reasonable window — leaving cp-1 on v1.31 and cp-3 on v1.29 for an extended period is out of policy, not just untidy.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting that the control-plane node is ALSO a node.</strong> <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> only bumps cluster-wide components (API server, controller-manager, scheduler, etcd) — it does NOT touch that node's own kubelet. You still owe cp-1 the full drain → upgrade kubelet/kubectl → restart → uncordon sequence, exactly like a worker. Skip it and <code>kubectl get nodes</code> keeps showing the control plane on the OLD version even though <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> reported success.</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming the container runtime upgrades itself.</strong> kubeadm/apt upgrades kubeadm, kubelet, and kubectl — never containerd or CRI-O. A newer kubelet occasionally expects a minimum CRI socket/API version; an aged container runtime after a kubelet bump can surface as cryptic <code>RunPodSandbox</code> or CRI-version-mismatch errors in <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> that have nothing to do with kubeadm and everything to do with the runtime being stale.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

