            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 28 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Concept-level recall — the rules and reasoning behind the upgrade, not just the commands (those get their own drills below).</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between a minor release and a patch release, and can you skip either one?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Minor (e.g. 1.30 → 1.31)</th><th>Patch (e.g. 1.31.0 → 1.31.3)</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Contains</td><td>New features, API changes</td><td>Bug fixes, security patches only</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Cadence</td><td>~Every 4 months</td><td>Roughly monthly</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Can you skip?</td><td>❌ No — must upgrade one at a time</td><td>✅ Yes — jump straight to the newest patch</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Minor versions can introduce breaking API changes, so <code>kubeadm</code> refuses to jump more than one minor version at a time (1.30 → 1.32 directly is rejected). Patch versions are pure bug/security fixes with no API surface change, so skipping from 1.31.0 straight to 1.31.5 is completely safe and common.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">State the current kubelet version skew policy relative to the API server — exactly, not "roughly."</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>kubelet must <strong>never be newer</strong> than the API server, and may be <strong>up to 3 minor versions older</strong> (N-3 to N). This was widened from N-2 to N-3 in Kubernetes 1.25. kube-proxy follows the same "never newer, up to N-3 older" rule relative to the API server, and is separately allowed to sit up to 3 minor versions older <em>or</em> newer than the specific kubelet it runs alongside.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">If the API server is v1.31…</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  kubelet supported at:   v1.28   v1.29   v1.30   v1.31          ← all OK (N-3 to N)
  kubelet NOT supported:  v1.27 (too far behind)   v1.32 (ahead — never allowed)
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Many older study materials still say "kubelet can be 1 minor version behind" — that was never the policy even pre-1.25 (it was N-2, not N-1), and is outdated now regardless. On the exam, if you're shown a cluster with a wide version gap between nodes, don't assume it's broken — check the actual N-3 window before declaring it invalid.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why must you always upgrade the control plane before any worker node — what specifically breaks if you do it backwards?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because kubelet can never be <em>newer</em> than the API server. If you upgraded a worker first, its kubelet would report a version ahead of the still-old API server — a state the skew policy forbids outright, and one that can cause the API server to reject or misinterpret that node's status updates.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Backwards order — the forbidden state</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ❌ Worker upgraded FIRST:      kubelet v1.31  ──▶  apiserver v1.30   (kubelet AHEAD — invalid)
  ✅ Control plane upgraded FIRST: apiserver v1.31 ──▶ kubelet v1.30   (kubelet BEHIND — valid, N-3 window)
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The API server is always the "anchor" version — every other component's validity is defined <em>relative to it</em>. Upgrading it first guarantees every node you haven't touched yet is automatically within the allowed "behind" window, never in the forbidden "ahead" state.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>apt-mark hold</code> actually do, and why is it required immediately after installing kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>apt-mark hold &lt;package&gt;</code> tells <code>apt</code> to exclude that package from any future <code>apt-get upgrade</code> or <code>apt upgrade</code> — the package stays pinned at its current version until explicitly <code>unhold</code>-ed.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Without hold</th><th>With hold</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>A routine <code>apt upgrade</code> (e.g. a cron job, or an admin patching the OS) can silently bump kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl to a newer minor version</td><td>Kubernetes packages stay exactly where you put them — only an explicit, deliberate <code>apt-mark unhold</code> + reinstall changes them</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Risk: a mismatched version skew appears without anyone touching Kubernetes directly</td><td>Version drift only happens on purpose, during a planned upgrade</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Because Kubernetes is version-sensitive (skew policy, API compatibility), you never want the OS package manager silently upgrading it as a side effect of unrelated maintenance. <code>hold</code> is the safety catch; you deliberately <code>unhold</code> only when you're intentionally starting an upgrade.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> and <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Changes anything?</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code></td><td>❌ Read-only</td><td>Preflight checks + shows available target versions and what will change — safe to run any time</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0</code></td><td>✅ Mutates the cluster</td><td>Rewrites static pod manifests, updates ConfigMaps, unconditionally renews all managed certs (unless <code>--certificate-renewal=false</code>) — actually performs the upgrade</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Always run <code>plan</code> before <code>apply</code> — it's your dry-run. It tells you the exact target version string, flags any preflight problems (unreachable image registry, insufficient permissions, etc.), and gives you a chance to abort before anything actually changes. On the exam, skipping straight to <code>apply</code> without checking <code>plan</code> first is a common way to get surprised.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You drain <code>worker01</code>, which is running 2 of your 3 <code>easypj-web</code> replicas, and you have a PodDisruptionBudget of <code>minAvailable: 2</code>. Walk through exactly what happens.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Drain with a PDB in play</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  BEFORE drain:  worker01 [easypj-web-a, easypj-web-b]   worker02 [easypj-web-c]   (3/3 up)
       │
       ▼ kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
       │
  1. drain calls the EVICTION API for each pod on worker01 (not a raw delete)
  2. eviction checks the PDB BEFORE allowing it: would evicting drop availability below minAvailable:2?
  3. Deployment controller notices the gap and schedules 2 replacements — only worker02 is drainable, so both land there
  4. once BOTH replacements are Running, drain evicts easypj-web-b next (never both at once — that would go below minAvailable:2)
       │
       ▼
  AFTER drain:  worker01 [empty, cordoned]   worker02 [easypj-web-a', easypj-web-b', easypj-web-c]   (3/3 up throughout)
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl drain</code> doesn't force-delete pods — it calls the Eviction API, which is PDB-aware. If evicting a pod would push the Deployment below <code>minAvailable: 2</code>, that specific eviction is rejected (HTTP 429) and drain retries with backoff until the replacement pod is ready elsewhere. This is exactly why draining a node during a rolling upgrade doesn't cause a customer-visible outage for easypj.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How long is a Kubernetes minor version supported, and how often does a new minor ship?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A new minor version ships roughly every 4 months (about 3 per year). The project maintains the <strong>3 most recent</strong> minor release branches at any time, and each minor (v1.19+) gets approximately <strong>1 year</strong> of patch support before it drops out of the supported window.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Timeline (from a minor's release)</th><th>Support status</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Month 0 — v1.31.0 released</td><td>✅ Supported (newest)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Month 4 — v1.32.0 released</td><td>✅ v1.31 still supported (2nd newest)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Month 8 — v1.33.0 released</td><td>✅ v1.31 still supported (3rd newest — last covered slot)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Month 12 — v1.34.0 released</td><td>❌ v1.31 drops out — only v1.32/v1.33/v1.34 covered now</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Do the arithmetic: 3 supported minors × ~4 months between releases ≈ 12 months of coverage for any given minor, counting from its own release until a 4th newer minor pushes it out. This is why staying "one minor behind latest" is a completely normal, supported posture — you only fall out of support if you let TWO newer minors ship without upgrading.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your cluster runs etcd as its own external service, not as a kubeadm-managed static pod. Does <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> upgrade it for you?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No. <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> only upgrades the etcd static pod it directly manages (the default "stacked etcd" layout). If etcd runs externally — its own systemd unit or its own cluster outside kubeadm's control — kubeadm skips it entirely: no image bump, no version check against the new API server's compatibility matrix. You are responsible for upgrading external etcd yourself, checking the compatibility matrix for your target Kubernetes minor, and doing it <strong>before</strong> you bump the API server.</p>
<pre><code># Check whether etcd is stacked (kubeadm-managed) or external
kubectl -n kube-system get pods -l component=etcd
# A hit here = stacked etcd (kubeadm upgrades it automatically)
# No hit here = external etcd (you must upgrade it yourself, out-of-band)

kubectl -n kube-system get pod etcd-cp-1 -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].image}'
# registry.k8s.io/etcd:3.5.15-0   <- kubeadm-managed image tag it will bump on "upgrade apply"</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is a common trap for anyone who's only ever practiced on the CKA exam's default single-node stacked-etcd layout: production HA clusters frequently run etcd as a separate, externally-managed cluster for isolation and independent scaling, and that etcd is completely invisible to <code>kubeadm upgrade</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Before a minor version upgrade, what should you audit in your manifests and Helm charts — and why doesn't <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> catch it for you?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Audit for deprecated/removed <code>apiVersion</code>s — API versions that graduated (e.g. old betas replaced by GA) or were dropped entirely in the target minor. <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> only diffs component versions (apiserver, etcd, CoreDNS…) — it never scans your workload YAML, Helm releases, or CI pipeline manifests, so a removed <code>apiVersion</code> is invisible to it. Use <code>pluto detect-files</code>/<code>detect-helm</code> or <code>kubent</code> against the TARGET version before upgrading, and fix flagged manifests with <code>kubectl convert</code>.</p>
<pre><code>$ pluto detect-files -d easypj-manifests/ --target-versions k8s=v1.31.0
NAME             KIND        VERSION           REPLACEMENT   REMOVED   DEPRECATED
easypj-web       Ingress     networking.k8s.io/v1beta1   networking.k8s.io/v1   true      true
easypj-cron      CronJob     batch/v1beta1               batch/v1               true      true
# "REMOVED: true" on the target version = this manifest will be REJECTED by the new API server, not just warned about</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A "successful" <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> only proves the control plane came up on the new version — it says nothing about whether your easypj Deployment/CronJob manifests still reference an apiVersion the new API server just rejected outright. This audit is a separate, manual responsibility every single upgrade, not something kubeadm does for you.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">On a 3-node HA control plane (cp-1/cp-2/cp-3 with stacked etcd), why must you upgrade the nodes strictly one at a time rather than in parallel?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>To protect etcd quorum. A 3-member etcd cluster needs a majority (2 of 3) healthy to accept writes at all. Upgrading more than one control-plane node's stacked-etcd member simultaneously can drop the cluster below quorum mid-upgrade — at that point etcd stops accepting writes entirely, which stalls the ENTIRE cluster (not just the nodes being upgraded), including the API servers you haven't touched yet.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>etcd members (N)</th><th>Quorum needed (majority)</th><th>Members that can be down/upgrading at once</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>3 (cp-1/cp-2/cp-3)</td><td>2</td><td>1 at a time — a 2nd simultaneous outage loses quorum</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>5</td><td>3</td><td>2 at a time — still never more, and never during an active upgrade window</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is a stronger constraint than the "upgrade workers one at a time to keep the app available" rule from earlier — for a multi-CP control plane, "one node at a time" isn't just about avoiding app downtime, it's the difference between a routine version bump and a full cluster outage. Additionally, remember the HA-specific rule: the newest and oldest <code>kube-apiserver</code> instances in the cluster must stay within 1 minor version of each other, so don't let the last CP node lag for an extended period either.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

