                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to running workloads during a control plane upgrade?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">during kubeadm upgrade apply</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># During kubeadm upgrade apply:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ Running pods on worker nodes → UNAFFECTED</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ Already-scheduled pods keep running</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ API server briefly unavailable (seconds)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ Cannot create/delete/modify resources during restart</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ kubectl commands may timeout temporarily</span>

<span class="comment"># What restarts:</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver         → brief downtime</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager → auto-recovers</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler         → auto-recovers</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd                   → auto-recovers (if single node)</span>

<span class="comment"># What does NOT restart:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet (until you manually upgrade and restart it)</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy (DaemonSet, updated via rolling update)</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS (Deployment, updated via rolling update)</span>

<span class="comment"># Impact on workloads:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Existing pods: keep running (kubelet manages them)</span>
<span class="comment"># - New scheduling: paused during API server restart</span>
<span class="comment"># - Services/networking: unaffected (kube-proxy still runs)</span>
<span class="comment"># - DNS: unaffected (CoreDNS still runs)</span>

<span class="comment"># For zero-downtime upgrades:</span>
<span class="comment"># Use HA control plane (multiple API servers)</span>
<span class="comment"># Upgrade one control plane at a time</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Control plane upgrade causes brief API server downtime but does NOT affect running pods. Workers keep running independently. On the CKA, understand: existing pods survive, new scheduling pauses, kubectl commands fail briefly. For HA clusters, upgrade one control plane at a time for zero-downtime. The kubelet continues managing pods locally even without API server connectivity.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to upgrade a cluster but <code>apt-cache madison kubeadm</code> doesn't show the target version. What's wrong?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the apt repository is versioned by minor release</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The apt repository is versioned by minor release</span>
<span class="comment"># Each minor version has its own repo URL</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current repo:</span>
cat /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list
<span class="comment"># deb https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.30/deb/ /</span>
<span class="comment">#                                        ^^^^ Current minor</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Update to target minor version repo:</span>
sudo vi /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list
<span class="comment"># Change v1.30 → v1.31:</span>
<span class="comment"># deb https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.31/deb/ /</span>

<span class="comment"># OR use sed:</span>
sudo sed -i 's/v1.30/v1.31/' \
/etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list

<span class="comment"># Then update:</span>
