                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> A cluster upgrade is really three layers of the same idea. §28.1 gave you the <em>rules</em>: minor releases arrive every ~4 months and can never be skipped, while kubelet and kube-proxy are allowed to lag the API server by up to <strong>3</strong> minor versions (never ahead) and kubectl tolerates ±1. §28.2 turned those rules into a <em>plan</em>: control plane always goes first (because kubelet can never be ahead of the API server), then workers are upgraded one at a time so easypj-web never drops below its PodDisruptionBudget's <code>minAvailable</code>.</p>
                    <p>§28.3 and §28.4 are that same plan typed out as literal commands. Every node — control plane or worker — follows the same five-beat rhythm: <strong>drain → upgrade kubeadm → run the upgrade command → upgrade kubelet/kubectl → uncordon</strong>. The only thing that changes is which upgrade command: the very first control-plane node uses <code>kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0</code>, and every other node in the cluster — additional control planes AND all workers — uses <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code>.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> if you can recite "drain, kubeadm, apply-or-node, kubelet, uncordon" from memory and you know why control plane goes first, you've internalized the single most heavily-tested hands-on task on the CKA exam.</p>
