│  snapshot" before any   │    │  runs. If the upgrade later   │    │  discipline: cordon →   │
│  manifest/binary is     │    │  corrupts a component, §29.3  │    │  wait-for-drain → patch │
│  touched.               │    │  restore IS the rollback plan.│    │  → uncordon.            │
└────────────────────────┘    └──────────────────────────────┘    └────────────────────────┘

  ⚠️  Shared mechanic: Ch29's `etcdctl defrag` (§UTH③) and Ch30's node drain are BOTH
      "one member/node at a time in HA" operations — draining two control-plane nodes
      at once loses etcd quorum exactly like defragging two members at once would risk.
</pre>
</div>
            </div>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Why This Matters Across Part 8</h5>
                <p>Chapters 28-30 aren't three independent skills — they're one continuous safety discipline. Chapter 28 never starts a <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> without this chapter's snapshot in hand first; if the upgrade goes wrong, the restore procedure you just learned in §29.3 is the actual rollback plan, not a hypothetical. And when Chapter 30 drains a control-plane node for OS patching, it's relying on the same one-at-a-time discipline this chapter's Under-the-Hood section ⑥ teaches for HA etcd membership — take down more than one control-plane member at once, in either chapter's procedure, and you lose Raft quorum the same way either time.</p>
            </div>


            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 29.1 — Why etcd Backup is Critical                 -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §29.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s29-1">                    <h3 class="section-h">29.1 Why etcd Backup is Critical</h3>

                <p>etcd is a distributed key-value store that holds the <strong>entire state</strong> of your Kubernetes cluster. Without etcd, the API server has no data to serve — your cluster effectively doesn't exist.</p>

