                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Real-World Disaster Scenarios</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Disk failure</strong> on the etcd node — all cluster state lost</li>
                        <li><strong>Accidental deletion</strong> — someone deletes all namespaces</li>
                        <li><strong>Botched upgrade</strong> — etcd data corrupted during cluster upgrade</li>
                        <li><strong>Security breach</strong> — attacker modifies RBAC rules and secrets</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>In every case, a recent etcd snapshot lets you restore the cluster to a known-good state.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 easypj — What's at Stake</h5>
                    <p>Your <strong>easypj</strong> job board has: 3 Deployment replicas, a Service, ConfigMaps with database URLs, Secrets with credentials, RBAC roles for the team, and NetworkPolicies. Without etcd backup, a disk failure means manually recreating ALL of this from scratch — assuming you even remember every configuration.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 The Classic Trap — a Backup That Was Never Protecting What You Thought</h5>
                    <p>A team snapshots etcd every night without fail. One day easypj's PostgreSQL volume corrupts, and they calmly say "no problem, we restore from backup" — then discover the etcd snapshot never contained a single row of postgres data. etcd faithfully backed up the <code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code> <em>object</em> (the claim pointing at storage) every single night; it never once touched the <em>bytes</em> the claim was pointing to. The nightly backup routine gave the team false confidence for months, right up until the one day it actually mattered.</p>
                    <p>This is precisely why §29.5 (Velero) exists as a mandatory pairing, not an optional extra: an etcd-only backup strategy is a backup of the cluster's <em>shape</em>, not its <em>substance</em>.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>📖 Key Terms — RPO and RTO Drive Your Whole Backup Strategy</h4>
                <p>Two acronyms decide <strong>how often</strong> you back up and <strong>how fast</strong> you must recover. Every backup schedule, every retention policy, and every "is this good enough?" conversation with your team traces back to these two numbers.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⏪</div>
                        <h5>RPO — Recovery Point Objective</h5>
                        <p>The maximum amount of data you can afford to <strong>LOSE</strong>, measured in time. It drives how <em>often</em> you snapshot. A 6-hour RPO means snapshots at least every 6 hours — because in the worst case, you'll lose everything written since the last one.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⏱️</div>
                        <h5>RTO — Recovery Time Objective</h5>
                        <p>The maximum <strong>DOWNTIME</strong> you can tolerate before the cluster must be back. It drives how <em>fast</em> your restore must run — and therefore how well-rehearsed your runbook needs to be before disaster strikes.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">⏪⏱️ RPO vs RTO on a timeline</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
   ── time ──▶
   ...──📸 snap──────────────📸 snap────────💥 DISASTER────✅ restored, serving again
              │◀──── RPO window ────▶│                    │◀── RTO window ──▶│
              │  (data written here  │                    │  (how long it    │
              │   is what you'd LOSE │                    │   takes to be    │
              │   if disaster hits   │                    │   back up)       │
              │   right before the   │                    │                  │
              │   NEXT snapshot)     │                    │                  │

   RPO answers: "How much can I afford to lose?"   → drives backup FREQUENCY
   RTO answers: "How long can I be down?"          → drives restore SPEED / rehearsal
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 easypj example — turning RPO/RTO into a real policy</h5>
                    <p>If the team decides easypj can tolerate losing at most <strong>6 hours</strong> of API-object changes (RPO = 6h), the CronJob in §29.4 must run at least every 6 hours. If the team also decides easypj can be down for at most <strong>10 minutes</strong> during a control-plane disaster (RTO = 10min), that number is exactly what the timed Practice Drills at the end of this chapter are rehearsing — you don't get a fast, confident restore on exam day (or in production) unless you've already done it under a clock.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

