                <!-- Finding Certificate Paths -->
                <h4>Step 1: Find Certificate Paths</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">find-etcd-certs.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: From the etcd pod spec</span>
kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Look for these lines in the Command section:</span>
<span class="comment"># --cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</span>
<span class="comment"># --trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --listen-client-urls=https://127.0.0.1:2379,...</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: From the etcd static pod manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca|listen-client'

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Quick grep for just the values</span>
kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system -o yaml | grep -E 'cert|key|ca\.' </code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Backup Command -->
                <h4>Step 2: Take the Snapshot</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">etcd-backup.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Take etcd snapshot backup</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># ✅ Output: Snapshot saved at /opt/etcd-backup.db</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Verify Backup -->
                <h4>Step 3: Verify the Backup</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">verify-backup.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Verify the snapshot is valid</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table

<span class="comment"># Example output:</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | 5d16a713 |    38504 |       1234 |     2.1 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>

<span class="comment"># Also check file exists and has reasonable size</span>
ls -lh /opt/etcd-backup.db</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>⚡ Speed Tip — Set the Flags Once, Then Forget Them</h5>
                    <p>Typing all four TLS flags on every single <code>etcdctl</code> invocation is slow and error-prone under exam pressure. Export the API version once and wrap the repeated flags in a shell alias for the rest of the session — every subsequent command becomes a short, low-error one-liner:</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">alias-once.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Set once per shell session:</span>
export ETCDCTL_API=3
alias e='etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379   --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt   --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt   --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key'

<span class="comment"># Now every command is short:</span>
e snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db
e snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table
e member list --write-out=table
e endpoint health</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Production Reality — Your Backup File IS Every Secret in the Cluster</h5>
                    <p>An etcd snapshot is not just "configuration" — it's a complete, offline copy of <strong>every <code>Secret</code> object in the cluster</strong>: easypj's database password, TLS private keys, image-pull credentials, ServiceAccount tokens, all of it. Kubernetes Secrets are only <em>base64-encoded</em>, not encrypted, unless the cluster has encryption-at-rest configured for the API server — and even with encryption-at-rest enabled, whoever holds the snapshot file plus the encryption key can decrypt everything.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Common mistake</th><th>Why it's dangerous</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Snapshot saved to a world-readable path like <code>/tmp</code></td><td>Any user on the node can read every Secret in the cluster from the file, no <code>kubectl</code> RBAC required</td><td><code>chmod 600</code> the snapshot immediately after <code>snapshot save</code>, owned by root</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Snapshot shipped to object storage without server-side encryption</td><td>Anyone with bucket read access (or a misconfigured public bucket) gets every Secret</td><td>Enable bucket-level encryption + strict IAM policy on the backup bucket, same rigor as a database dump</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Treating the backup file like a config file when sharing with teammates</td><td>Pasting a path into Slack/email/a ticket effectively shares production credentials</td><td>Treat the <code>.db</code> file with the same handling rules as the Secrets it contains — never attach it casually</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>The takeaway: the access-control discipline you'd apply to a database credentials dump applies identically to an etcd snapshot — because that's exactly what it is, plus everything else in the cluster.</p>
                </div>

