                <!-- Restore Commands -->
                <h4>Step 1: Restore the Snapshot</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">etcd-restore.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Restore snapshot to a NEW data directory</span>
<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Do NOT restore to /var/lib/etcd — use a NEW path!</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># Verify the restored directory exists</span>
ls -la /var/lib/etcd-restored/
<span class="comment"># Should show: member/ directory</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>etcdctl</code> vs <code>etcdutl</code> — Which Binary Should Restore?</h5>
                    <p>Every command so far used <code>etcdctl</code>, and on most exam images and existing kubeadm clusters that still works fine for <code>snapshot restore</code>. But newer etcd releases split the tool in two: <strong><code>etcdctl</code></strong> stays the day-to-day client for talking to a <em>live</em> etcd (backups, member list, health checks — anything over the network), while <strong><code>etcdutl</code></strong> is the dedicated <em>offline</em> data-file tool, and it is now the officially recommended binary specifically for <code>snapshot restore</code> — a purely local, offline file operation, exactly the kind of job <code>etcdutl</code> was split out to own.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Talks to</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>etcdctl</code></td><td>A live, running etcd (network, needs TLS for backup)</td><td><code>snapshot save</code>, <code>member list</code>, <code>endpoint health</code>, <code>defrag</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>etcdutl</code></td><td>Nothing — reads/writes local files only, no network at all</td><td><code>snapshot restore</code> (recommended), <code>snapshot status</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>The flag is identical either way — <code>--data-dir</code> — so swapping <code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code> for <code>etcdutl snapshot restore</code> in any command in this chapter is a drop-in change. On the exam, try <code>etcdutl</code> first (<code>which etcdutl</code> to confirm it's installed); if the image is older and only ships <code>etcdctl</code>, that still works identically for restore. <strong>Version compatibility gotcha:</strong> a snapshot taken by one etcd minor version can fail to restore — or restore into a subtly incompatible state — on a binary from a very different version; always restore using an <code>etcdctl</code>/<code>etcdutl</code> that matches (or is adjacent to) the version that took the snapshot, and check <code>etcdctl version</code> on both ends after any cluster upgrade.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 2: Update the etcd Static Pod Manifest</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
