                <h4>🧪 The Full DR Drill — Backup → Disaster → Restore → Validate, End to End</h4>
                <p>Everything above assumes etcd is still <em>running</em> while you restore. In a real disaster drill (and in some exam tasks), you deliberately <strong>stop</strong> the control plane first — because restoring while the API server and etcd are both live risks the kubelet or a controller re-writing objects mid-restore. The safe pattern is: prove a marker object exists, delete it to simulate data loss, stop the control plane, restore, repoint, restart the control plane, then confirm the marker is back.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🧪 Full DR Drill — 8 steps, backup to validated recovery</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  FULL DR DRILL — backup → disaster → restore → validate          │
│                                                                  │
│  ① BACKUP        e snapshot save /backup/snap.db                 │
│                  e snapshot status /backup/snap.db --write-table │
│       ▼                                                          │
│  ② MARKER        kubectl create cm dr-marker -n default          │
│                  (a proof object you will delete &amp; expect back)  │
│       ▼                                                          │
│  ③ DISASTER      kubectl delete cm dr-marker -n default          │
│                  (simulate the data loss)                        │
│       ▼                                                          │
│  ④ STOP CP       move kube-apiserver.yaml &amp; etcd.yaml OUT of     │
│                  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ (kubelet stops them) │
│       ▼                                                          │
│  ⑤ RESTORE       etcdutl snapshot restore /backup/snap.db \      │
│                    --data-dir /var/lib/etcd-restored             │
│       ▼                                                          │
│  ⑥ REPOINT       edit etcd.yaml hostPath → /var/lib/etcd-restored│
│       ▼                                                          │
│  ⑦ START CP      move etcd.yaml + kube-apiserver.yaml back        │
│       ▼                                                          │
│  ⑧ VALIDATE      kubectl get cm dr-marker -n default  ✅ it's    │
│                  back!  Record the RTO you actually achieved.    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">full-dr-drill.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ① BACKUP — snapshot, then verify it's readable</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/backup.db --write-table

<span class="comment"># ② MARKER — a proof object you will delete and expect back</span>
kubectl create configmap dr-marker -n default --from-literal=proof=easypj

<span class="comment"># ③ DISASTER — simulate data loss</span>
kubectl delete configmap dr-marker -n default

<span class="comment"># ④ STOP CONTROL PLANE — kubelet stops static pods when their manifest disappears</span>
sudo mkdir -p /tmp/manifests-parked
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/manifests-parked/
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml           /tmp/manifests-parked/

<span class="comment"># ⑤ RESTORE — offline, no certs needed, to a brand-new directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdutl snapshot restore /opt/backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># ⑥ REPOINT — edit the parked etcd.yaml BEFORE moving it back</span>
sudo sed -i 's|/var/lib/etcd|/var/lib/etcd-restored|g' /tmp/manifests-parked/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># ⑦ START CONTROL PLANE — move both manifests back; kubelet recreates the static pods</span>
sudo mv /tmp/manifests-parked/etcd.yaml           /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
sudo mv /tmp/manifests-parked/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

<span class="comment"># ⑧ VALIDATE — the marker created BEFORE the backup should be back;</span>
<span class="comment">#              anything created AFTER the backup (there was none here) would be gone</span>
watch kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=etcd
kubectl get configmap dr-marker -n default   <span class="comment"># ✅ back — timing this end-to-end IS your real-world RTO</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Gotcha — never restore into a LIVE, running etcd's data-dir</h5>
                    <p>Restoring while etcd is still running against the same directory corrupts the store. Always restore to a NEW directory, and for a true DR drill, stop etcd (park its static pod manifest) before flipping the hostPath — then start it again. This is exactly what the <strong>④ STOP CP / ⑦ START CP</strong> steps above are for; skipping them is fine for a quick single-object restore test, but not for a rehearsal that's meant to mirror a real total-control-plane-loss incident.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 29.4 — etcd Backup Automation                      -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §29.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s29-4">                    <h3 class="section-h">29.4 etcd Backup Automation</h3>

                <p>In production, you should automate etcd backups using a CronJob or external tooling. Here's a Kubernetes CronJob that takes automatic etcd snapshots on a schedule.</p>

                <!-- CronJob YAML -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">etcd-backup-cronjob.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: etcd-backup
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  schedule: "0 */6 * * *"          <span class="comment"># Every 6 hours</span>
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          hostNetwork: true
          containers:
          - name: etcd-backup
            image: registry.k8s.io/etcd:3.5.12-0
            command:
            - /bin/sh
            - -c
            - |
              ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save \
                /backup/etcd-snapshot-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).db \
                --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
                --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
                --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
                --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
            volumeMounts:
            - name: etcd-certs
              mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd
              readOnly: true
            - name: backup-dir
              mountPath: /backup
          volumes:
          - name: etcd-certs
            hostPath:
              path: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd
          - name: backup-dir
            hostPath:
              path: /opt/etcd-backups
              type: DirectoryOrCreate
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          nodeSelector:
            node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: ""
          tolerations:
          - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
            effect: NoSchedule</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🧭 Decision Guide — CronJob vs a systemd Timer on the Host</h4>
                <p>The CronJob above is the Kubernetes-native answer, but it has one structural weakness: it depends on a healthy control plane to even <em>fire</em>. If the very thing you're protecting against — a broken control plane — is already happening, the CronJob may never run. A <strong>systemd timer</strong> on the control-plane node's own OS runs independently of Kubernetes health, which is exactly when you most need a backup to succeed.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">☸️</div>
                        <h5>CronJob (in-cluster)</h5>
                        <p>Runs <strong>inside</strong> the cluster, scheduled by the control plane itself. Self-documenting, portable across clusters, and shows up in <code>kubectl get cronjob</code> — but it depends on a healthy API server/scheduler to fire. If the control plane is sick, the very backup you need may not run.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🖥️</div>
                        <h5>systemd timer (on the host)</h5>
                        <p>Runs on the control-plane node's OS, completely independent of Kubernetes. Fires even when the API server is down or etcd itself is unhealthy — precisely the scenario where you most need a fresh snapshot. Preferred for the control-plane node's own etcd backups in production.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">/etc/systemd/system/etcd-backup.timer + .service</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># /usr/local/bin/etcd-backup.sh — the actual backup logic (save + verify + ship off-node)</span>
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail
STAMP=$(date +%F-%H%M)
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /var/backups/etcd/etcd-$STAMP.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /var/backups/etcd/etcd-$STAMP.db --write-table
scp /var/backups/etcd/etcd-$STAMP.db backup-host:/srv/etcd-backups/   <span class="comment"># ship off-node</span>

<span class="comment"># /etc/systemd/system/etcd-backup.service</span>
[Unit]
Description=etcd snapshot backup
[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/etcd-backup.sh

<span class="comment"># /etc/systemd/system/etcd-backup.timer</span>
[Unit]
Description=Run etcd-backup.service every 6 hours
[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 00/6:00:00
Persistent=true
[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target

<span class="comment"># Enable it:</span>
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now etcd-backup.timer
systemctl list-timers | grep etcd-backup</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Retention Policy</h5>
                    <p>A <strong>retention policy</strong> is the rule for how much snapshot history you keep before pruning old files. It must keep enough history to satisfy your RPO <em>and</em> to recover from a problem discovered <strong>late</strong> (e.g. a corruption bug that only surfaces days after it was introduced) — while still pruning old snapshots often enough that storage doesn't grow unbounded. A common pattern: keep every snapshot for 7 days, then only the daily snapshot for 30 days, then delete.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 A Real Production Failure Mode — The Backup That Takes Down the Control Plane</h5>
                    <p>A surprisingly common incident has nothing to do with restore mechanics at all: a CronJob (or systemd timer) is set up correctly, fires every 6 hours exactly as designed — and nobody ever adds the rotation/off-node-shipping step. Months later, the control-plane node's <code>/opt/etcd-backups/</code> directory has silently grown to gigabytes of never-deleted <code>.db</code> files.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
  ⏰ CronJob fires every 6h, snapshot saved, NEVER pruned, NEVER shipped off-node
        │
        ▼
  📈 /opt/etcd-backups/ grows unbounded — weeks of 6-hourly snapshots pile up
        │
        ▼
  💥 Node disk crosses the kubelet's `nodefs.available` eviction threshold
        │
        ▼
  🏷️  kubelet taints the node  node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure:NoSchedule
        │
        ▼
  ⚠️  kubelet starts EVICTING pods to reclaim space — on a CONTROL-PLANE node,
      that can include etcd's own static pod's ability to write new WAL segments
        │
        ▼
  🚨 The exact backup job meant to PROTECT the cluster is now the thing
     causing a control-plane outage — self-inflicted disk pressure
</pre></div>
                    <p>The fix is exactly what the checklist below already prescribes — ship snapshots off-node and prune old ones — but this incident is why "store backups externally" and "rotate old snapshots" are not optional nice-to-haves. An unrotated local backup directory is a slow-motion, self-inflicted <code>DiskPressure</code> incident, and it gets discovered at the worst possible time: usually right when someone finally needs to <em>use</em> one of those old snapshots and the node hosting them is already unhealthy.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Backup Checklist -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>✅ etcd Backup Best Practices Checklist</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Practice</th><th>Why</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>✅ Backup before every cluster upgrade</td><td>Rollback point if upgrade fails</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Store backups externally (not on same node)</td><td>Node failure won't destroy backups too</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Test restore procedure regularly</td><td>Untested backups are unreliable backups</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Document backup/restore steps for team</td><td>Anyone on-call should be able to restore</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Include timestamp in backup filename</td><td>Identify which backup to restore from</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Verify backup with <code>snapshot status</code></td><td>Confirm backup is not corrupted</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Rotate / prune old snapshots (retention policy)</td><td>Keeps storage bounded while preserving enough history to meet RPO — see the disk-pressure incident above for what happens when this is skipped</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Pin a known-good etcd/etcdctl minor version for the backup image</td><td>A snapshot taken by one etcd minor version can restore into a subtly incompatible state on a very different version — match or stay adjacent</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- etcd Restore Troubleshooting -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>🛠️ etcd Restore Troubleshooting — Common Failures</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Problem</th>
                                <th>Symptom</th>
                                <th>Fix</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>etcd pod won't start after restore</strong></td>
                                <td><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> for etcd pod</td>
                                <td>Check BOTH <code>--data-dir</code> and <code>hostPath.path</code> in manifest point to restored directory</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Forgot to update hostPath</strong></td>
                                <td>etcd starts but uses old data (restore appears to fail)</td>
                                <td>Edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> — change <code>volumes[].hostPath.path</code> too</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Permission denied on restored dir</strong></td>
                                <td><code>permission denied</code> in etcd logs</td>
                                <td><code>chown -R root:root /var/lib/etcd-restored</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>kubectl not responding after restore</strong></td>
                                <td>All <code>kubectl</code> commands hang or timeout</td>
                                <td>Wait 30-60s for kubelet to detect manifest change — use <code>crictl ps | grep etcd</code> instead</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Wrong backup file used</strong></td>
                                <td>Cluster restored but resources are from wrong point in time</td>
                                <td>Verify backup with <code>etcdctl snapshot status</code> before restoring — check revision count</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>etcd restore command fails</strong></td>
                                <td><code>Error: data-dir already exists</code></td>
                                <td>Always restore to a NEW directory — never overwrite <code>/var/lib/etcd</code> directly</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🩺 Troubleshooting Workflow — <code>snapshot save</code> Hangs or Fails Outright</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  $ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db --cacert=... --cert=... --key=...
  ...command just hangs, or errors immediately. Work through in order:

  1. Does it fail INSTANTLY with a TLS/cert error?
     └─▶ Wrong cert path, or ETCDCTL_API=3 missing. Re-grep the manifest:
         grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

  2. Does it fail INSTANTLY with "connection refused"?
     └─▶ Wrong --endpoints. Confirm etcd is actually listening:
         grep listen-client-urls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
         crictl ps | grep etcd     (is the etcd container even Running?)

  3. Does it HANG for a long time, then time out?
     └─▶ Likely a network partition between the node running etcdctl and the
         etcd member, OR clock skew breaking TLS certificate validation
         (etcd certs are time-bound — check `date` on both sides, then `chronyc
         tracking` / `timedatectl` for clock drift > a few seconds).

  4. Does it fail with "no space left on device" / "database space exceeded"?
     └─▶ Disk pressure on the etcd node itself (see the failure mode above) —
         free space first, THEN retry the snapshot; a snapshot attempted
         during active NOSPACE alarms can itself fail or be truncated.

  5. Does the file get created but `snapshot status` reports a hash mismatch?
     └─▶ The save was interrupted mid-write (killed process, node reboot,
         disk error). Delete the partial file and re-run — never trust a
         snapshot you have not verified with `snapshot status --write-table`.
</pre>
</div>
