            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How etcd Backup and Restore Actually Works: Snapshot Internals, Restoration Mechanics, and Disaster Recovery Scenarios</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ etcd Snapshot Internals — What's Inside a Backup File</h5>
            <p>An etcd snapshot is NOT just a file copy. It's a point-in-time, consistent dump of the entire key space that resets cluster membership — allowing the backup to be restored to a completely different cluster.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ETCD SNAPSHOT INTERNALS                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  $ etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  SNAPSHOT FILE CONTENTS:                                                      │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  /opt/backup.db (single file, SQLite/BoltDB format)                   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │  HEADER:                                                     │      │   │
│  │  │  • etcd version (e.g., 3.5.x)                               │      │   │
│  │  │  • Cluster ID (unique to this cluster)                       │      │   │
│  │  │  • Member ID                                                │      │   │
│  │  │  • Snapshot revision (e.g., 105432)                          │      │   │
│  │  │  • Total key count                                          │      │   │
│  │  │  • Total size                                               │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │  KEY SPACE (ALL keys at snapshot revision):                  │      │   │
│  │  │                                                               │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/namespaces/default            → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/namespaces/easypj-prod        → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/pods/default/nginx            → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/deployments/easypj-dev/web    → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/services/easypj-dev/web-svc   → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/secrets/easypj-dev/db-creds   → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  ... (ALL other K8s objects)                                  │      │   │
│  │  │  ... (ALL leases, endpoints, events, etc.)                    │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  RESTORE MECHANICS — What Actually Happens:                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  $ etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup.db \                          │   │
│  │      --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                                │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  RESTORE DOES:                                                         │   │
│  │  1. Creates NEW BoltDB from snapshot data                             │   │
│  │  2. Creates NEW WAL files                                             │   │
│  │  3. Creates NEW member directory                                       │   │
│  │  4. RESETS cluster membership (new Cluster ID, new Member ID)          │   │
│  │  5. Makes this member the SINGLE member of a new cluster               │   │
│  │  6. Does NOT touch existing /var/lib/etcd/data                         │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  AFTER RESTORE, YOU MUST:                                              │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │ 1. EDIT etcd.yaml static pod manifest:                      │      │   │
│  │  │       --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored  <span class="comment">← CHANGE PATH     </span>│      │   │
│  │  │                                                            │      │   │
│  │  │ 2. UPDATE volume mount in etcd.yaml:                       │      │   │
│  │  │       volumes:                                              │      │   │
│  │  │       - hostPath:                                           │      │   │
│  │  │           path: /var/lib/etcd-restored  <span class="comment">← CHANGE PATH     </span>│      │   │
│  │  │                                                            │      │   │
│  │  │ 3. WAIT for kubelet to detect manifest change               │      │   │
│  │  │    → kubelet restarts etcd with NEW data-dir               │      │   │
│  │  │    → etcd starts as single-node cluster                     │      │   │
│  │  │                                                            │      │   │
│  │  │ 4. VERIFY pods are back:                                    │      │   │
│  │  │    kubectl get pods -A (may take 30-60 seconds)            │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  DISASTER RECOVERY — When ALL etcd Is Lost:                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Scenario: All 3 etcd nodes corrupted, no quorum possible              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ Recovery (requires backup):                                            │   │
│  │  1. Stop all etcd instances                                            │   │
│  │  2. Pick ONE node to restore to                                        │   │
│  │  3. Restore snapshot to a fresh data-dir on that node                  │   │
│  │  4. Update etcd.yaml manifest on THAT node                             │   │
│  │  5. Start etcd → it runs as SINGLE-node cluster                       │   │
│  │  6. API Server connects → cluster is back (from snapshot point)       │   │
│  │  7. Rebuild remaining etcd nodes from this single member               │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  ⚠️  ALL changes after the snapshot time are LOST!                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> etcd backup/restore is guaranteed on the CKA. The restore changes the cluster to a single-node etcd — all HA control plane nodes except the restore target must be removed from the etcd cluster. After restore, you MUST update --data-dir in BOTH the command AND the hostPath volume mount in etcd.yaml. Forgetting the volume mount is the #1 mistake — etcd starts but uses the old (empty) data directory.
            </div>

            <h5>① How etcd Actually Persists Data — WAL, BoltDB, and What a Snapshot Really Captures</h5>
            <p>"etcd is a key-value store" hides the two-file design that makes both durability AND fast snapshots possible. Every write hits an append-only <strong>Write-Ahead Log (WAL)</strong> first, and is periodically compacted into a <strong>BoltDB</strong> B+tree file — and it's that BoltDB file <code>snapshot save</code> actually reads from.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ON-DISK LAYOUT OF /var/lib/etcd/member/                                 │
│                                                                          │
│  Every write (e.g. `kubectl apply`) does TWO things, in this order:      │
│                                                                          │
│   1. 📝 APPEND to the WAL   (wal/0000000000000000-0000000000000000.wal) │
│      ──▶ sequential, append-only, fsync'd before the write is ACKed     │
│      ──▶ this is what makes etcd durable across a crash mid-write       │
│                                                                          │
│   2. 🌳 APPLY to the BoltDB B+Tree  (snap/db)                            │
│      ──▶ the actual key→value data, indexed for fast reads              │
│      ──▶ periodically SNAPSHOTTED to snap/ so the WAL can be truncated   │
│                                                                          │
│  `etcdctl snapshot save` reads a CONSISTENT COPY of snap/db —            │
│  it does NOT replay the WAL — which is why the backup is instant        │
│  and doesn't block writes.                                               │
│                                                                          │
│  If etcd crashes between WAL-append and BoltDB-apply:                   │
│    on restart, etcd REPLAYS any WAL entries newer than the last          │
│    BoltDB snapshot — so nothing acknowledged to a client is ever lost.   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Backup relevance</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>member/wal/*.wal</code></td><td>Append-only log of every write, for crash durability</td><td>NOT included in a <code>snapshot save</code> — irrelevant once the snapshot is taken</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>member/snap/db</code></td><td>BoltDB B+tree — the actual current key-value data</td><td>THIS is what <code>snapshot save</code> copies out</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is why backup is cheap and restore is a full replace.</strong> <code>snapshot save</code> only needs a consistent read of the BoltDB file — it never blocks on the WAL, so backups don't slow down your cluster. But <code>snapshot restore</code> builds a brand-new WAL and BoltDB pair from scratch — there is no "replay forward" from the restore point, which is exactly why anything written after the snapshot's revision cannot survive a restore.</div>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📖 The Rarely-Used Alternative — Copying <code>member/snap/db</code> Directly</h5>
                <p>etcd's own operations guide documents a second, more primitive backup path: instead of running <code>snapshot save</code> against a live member, you can simply copy the <code>member/snap/db</code> file straight off disk while etcd is stopped (or accept the small risk of copying it live). This skips the network/TLS round-trip entirely — but the copy has no built-in integrity hash, and it "might lose data that has not been written yet, but is included in the WAL folder," since it bypasses the consistent-snapshot machinery <code>snapshot save</code> normally handles for you. If you ever restore such a raw copy, <code>etcdutl snapshot restore</code> needs the <code>--skip-hash-check</code> flag (§UTH⑦) because there is no hash to verify. In practice, <code>snapshot save</code> is almost always the right choice — this raw-copy path exists mainly as a documented fallback when etcdctl/etcdutl themselves are unavailable. Kubernetes' own etcd documentation names a third approach too — a storage-layer <strong>volume snapshot</strong> of the disk/volume backing etcd's data directory (the same idea as a cloud-disk snapshot) — as another supported option; whichever method you pick, the same rule applies: an untested backup is not a real backup.</p>
            </div>

            <h5>② Raft Leader Election — Why a 3-Node etcd Cluster Can Briefly Freeze Writes</h5>
            <p>etcd's HA guarantee comes from <strong>Raft</strong> consensus: at any moment, exactly one member is the <em>leader</em>, and it's the only one that accepts writes. When the leader disappears, the remaining followers must elect a new one before the cluster can accept writes again — and understanding that gap explains a real production symptom: "kubectl apply just hangs for a few seconds" during a control-plane node failure.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  RAFT LEADER ELECTION — what happens when the leader dies                │
│                                                                          │
│  BEFORE:                                                                 │
│    etcd-1 (LEADER) ◀──heartbeat every ~100ms── etcd-2, etcd-3 (followers)│
│                                                                          │
│  💥 etcd-1 crashes / network-partitioned                                 │
│                                                                          │
│  1. etcd-2 and etcd-3 stop receiving heartbeats                          │
│  2. After "election timeout" (randomized, ~1-2s) elapses with no        │
│     heartbeat, a follower (say etcd-2) becomes a CANDIDATE               │
│  3. etcd-2 requests votes from etcd-3                                    │
│  4. etcd-3 grants its vote (it hasn't voted this term & etcd-2's log     │
│     is at least as up-to-date)                                          │
│  5. etcd-2 gets a MAJORITY (2 of 3 — itself + etcd-3) → becomes LEADER   │
│  6. etcd-2 starts sending heartbeats; cluster resumes accepting writes   │
│                                                                          │
│  ⏱️  DURING steps 1-5: NO writes are acknowledged (read-your-writes      │
│      consistency would break otherwise) — this is the brief "freeze"    │
│      an admin sees as slow kubectl commands during a control-plane      │
│      node failure.                                                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Election timeout</td><td>Randomized per-node delay before a follower with no heartbeat starts an election — randomization prevents every node from starting one at the same instant</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Term</td><td>A logical "epoch" number that increases with every election, used to detect and reject stale leaders</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Split vote</td><td>Two candidates tie in an even-sized cluster — a NEW election starts with a fresh randomized timeout, which is why odd member counts are recommended</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is why odd etcd member counts (1, 3, 5) are standard</strong> — going from an odd count to the next even one buys you nothing: 4 members still need quorum 3, tolerating only 1 failure, exactly like 3 members — you'd be paying for an extra node's resources for zero extra fault tolerance. Always size etcd clusters at odd numbers.</div>

            <h5>③ Compaction &amp; Defragmentation — Why etcd Fills Up, and the Day-2 Admin Fix</h5>
            <p>etcd never overwrites old data in place — every update to a key creates a new MVCC revision, keeping the old one around so in-flight <code>watch</code> streams can still read it. Left unchecked, this makes the on-disk file grow forever. <strong>Compaction</strong> prunes old revisions; <strong>defragmentation</strong> reclaims the disk space compaction frees up. Both are real Day-2 operations, as promised back in Chapter 1's control-loop deep dive.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  COMPACTION vs DEFRAGMENTATION — two different problems                  │
│                                                                          │
│  Every write to a key keeps the OLD revision too (MVCC):                 │
│    key=easypj-replicas   rev=100 → "3"                                   │
│    key=easypj-replicas   rev=104 → "5"   (rev 100's value still on disk!) │
│    key=easypj-replicas   rev=109 → "3"   (rev 104's value still on disk!) │
│                                                                          │
│  1️⃣ COMPACTION — logically discard revisions older than a cutoff:       │
│     $ etcdctl compact 105        (discards everything before rev 105)   │
│     → the B+tree no longer POINTS to revisions 100-104                  │
│     → but the disk PAGES they occupied are not yet returned to the OS   │
│                                                                          │
│  2️⃣ DEFRAGMENTATION — physically reclaim that freed disk space:        │
│     $ etcdctl defrag --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 ...             │
│     → rewrites the BoltDB file, compacting free pages                   │
│     → BLOCKS that member while running — do ONE member at a time in HA  │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚠️  Symptom of skipping both: `etcdctl endpoint status` shows a large   │
│      DB SIZE and eventually etcd raises an "mvcc: database space         │
│      exceeded" ALARM and goes read-only until you compact + defrag.      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it does</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>etcdctl compact &lt;revision&gt;</code></td><td>Discards old MVCC revisions before a given revision</td><td>kubeadm clusters auto-compact every 5 min by default</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>etcdctl defrag</code></td><td>Reclaims disk space compaction freed, one member at a time</td><td>Manual — run during a maintenance window, one node at a time in HA</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>etcdctl alarm list</code></td><td>Shows active alarms, e.g. <code>NOSPACE</code></td><td>Check when etcd unexpectedly rejects writes</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>etcdctl alarm disarm</code></td><td>Clears a resolved <code>NOSPACE</code> alarm after compact+defrag</td><td>After freeing space, to let writes resume</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A "database space exceeded" alarm is NOT fixed by a bigger disk alone.</strong> You must <code>compact</code> (logically prune) THEN <code>defrag</code> (physically reclaim) THEN <code>alarm disarm</code> — skipping compact means defrag has nothing to reclaim, and skipping disarm leaves etcd read-only even after space is freed. This 3-step sequence is exactly what Chapter 1 meant by "a real Day-2 admin task."</div>

            <h5>④ Velero Backup Mechanics — restic/node-agent (File-Level) vs CSI Snapshots (Block-Level)</h5>
            <p>Velero needs a way to actually capture the <em>bytes</em> inside a PersistentVolume, and it has two fundamentally different mechanisms for doing that. Understanding the trade-off explains why a production team might run one, the other, or both.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  VELERO'S TWO VOLUME-BACKUP MECHANISMS                                    │
│                                                                          │
│  1️⃣ restic / node-agent — FILE-LEVEL backup                            │
│     ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │
│     │ A DaemonSet-style agent runs on each node, reads the        │      │
│     │ actual FILES inside the mounted volume, and streams them    │      │
│     │ (chunked + deduplicated) to object storage.                 │      │
│     │                                                              │      │
│     │ easypj's postgres PV  ──files──▶  node-agent  ──▶  S3 bucket │      │
│     └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │
│     ✅ Works on ANY storage backend (portable — no CSI driver needed)   │
│     ⚠️  Slower — walks the filesystem tree file by file               │
│                                                                          │
│  2️⃣ CSI volume snapshots — BLOCK-LEVEL backup                          │
│     ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │
│     │ Velero asks the storage driver itself (via the CSI          │      │
│     │ VolumeSnapshot API) to snapshot the underlying disk/volume   │      │
│     │ at the STORAGE layer — no file walking at all.               │      │
│     │                                                              │      │
│     │ easypj's postgres PV  ──CSI API──▶  storage driver snapshot  │      │
│     └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │
│     ✅ Much FASTER, crash-consistent at the block level                │
│     ⚠️  Tied to a CSI driver that actually supports snapshots           │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>How it captures data</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>restic / node-agent</td><td>Reads files at the filesystem level, chunks + dedupes to object storage</td><td>Any storage backend, especially when no CSI snapshot support exists</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>CSI volume snapshot</td><td>Calls the storage driver's native snapshot API — no file walking</td><td>Fast, large volumes on a CSI driver that supports snapshots (most cloud disks)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Pick the mechanism to match your storage, not the other way around.</strong> If easypj's PostgreSQL PV lives on a cloud disk with CSI snapshot support, prefer CSI snapshots for speed. If it's on storage without CSI snapshot support (or you need one backup mechanism that works identically across mixed storage backends), restic/node-agent is the portable fallback — Velero can even be configured to use both, per-volume, in the same backup.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Blast Radius — Namespace Restore vs Full etcd Restore, and DR via Migration</h5>
            <p>Both etcd restore and Velero restore can "bring the cluster back" — but they bring back very different <em>amounts</em> of the cluster, and picking the smaller blast radius that actually fixes the problem is a real production skill, not just an exam fact.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  BLAST RADIUS — how much of the cluster does each restore touch?         │
│                                                                          │
│  etcd snapshot restore:                                                  │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  ██████████████████████████████████████████████████████████    │    │
│  │  ENTIRE CLUSTER — every namespace, every object, all at once     │    │
│  │  ALL-OR-NOTHING: you cannot restore just "easypj" this way        │    │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                          │
│  velero restore --include-namespaces easypj:                             │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  ██████  ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░    │    │
│  │  other namespaces  easypj   other namespaces                     │    │
│  │  UNTOUCHED          ▲ RESTORED — smallest blast radius that fixes│    │
│  │                       the actual problem                         │    │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                          │
│  🌐 CROSS-CLUSTER MIGRATION — same tool, different goal:                 │
│     velero backup create easypj-daily      (on cluster A)               │
│     velero restore create --from-backup easypj-daily   (on cluster B)   │
│     → moves easypj + its PV data to a NEW cluster entirely               │
│                                                                          │
│  📄 GitOps re-apply as a THIRD option:                                   │
│     git repo (Deployments/Services/ConfigMaps as YAML) ──▶ kubectl apply │
│     → rebuilds API OBJECTS from source control, but NEVER the PV bytes  │
│     → must still be paired with a volume-level backup for real DR       │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Smallest-blast-radius tool</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Total control-plane loss, ALL namespaces affected</td><td>etcd snapshot restore</td><td>Only tool that restores the whole cluster state at once</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Just easypj's namespace got corrupted/deleted</td><td>Velero restore <code>--include-namespaces easypj</code></td><td>Leaves every other namespace completely untouched</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Rebuilding a cluster from source control</td><td>GitOps re-apply + separate volume restore</td><td>Recreates API objects declaratively, but needs a paired data-restore step</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Moving easypj to a new cluster (migration, not disaster)</td><td>Velero backup/restore across clusters</td><td>Portable via object storage — no manual re-authoring of manifests</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 "Which tool is correct?" is really "how big does the blast radius need to be?"</strong> An etcd restore is a blunt instrument — reaching for it to fix a single deleted namespace needlessly reverts EVERY OTHER namespace to the snapshot's revision too, destroying anything created anywhere else since that snapshot. Always ask "what's the smallest restore that actually solves this incident?" before running anything all-or-nothing.</div>

            <h5>⑥ HA etcd Restore — What Happens to Cluster Membership, and How to Safely Rejoin Peers</h5>
            <p>Everything so far assumed a single etcd instance. Production clusters run etcd as a <strong>3-node (or 5-node) Raft cluster</strong> for HA, and that changes what "restore" actually means. The earlier diagram noted restore "resets cluster membership" — this is the part of the exam AND real production incidents that trips people up: a restore doesn't rejoin the old cluster, it <em>replaces</em> it with a brand-new one of size 1, and the other two members must be deliberately reconciled or you get split-brain.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  HA ETCD RESTORE — MEMBERSHIP BEFORE / DURING / AFTER                     │
│                                                                          │
│  BEFORE (healthy 3-node Raft cluster):                                   │
│    etcd-1 (LEADER)  ◀──raft──▶  etcd-2 (follower)  ◀──raft──▶  etcd-3    │
│    Cluster ID: 7a91f3   Member IDs: 1a, 2b, 3c   Quorum = 2 of 3          │
│                                                                          │
│  💥 DISASTER — all 3 members corrupted (or you're rehearsing total loss) │
│                                                                          │
│  RESTORE (on ONE chosen node, say etcd-1's host):                        │
│    $ etcdutl snapshot restore snap.db --data-dir /var/lib/etcd-restored \│
│        --initial-cluster etcd-1=https://10.0.0.1:2380 \                 │
│        --initial-cluster-token etcd-cluster-dr-1 \                      │
│        --initial-advertise-peer-urls https://10.0.0.1:2380 \            │
│        --name etcd-1                                                    │
│    → generates a NEW Cluster ID (NOT 7a91f3 — a fresh one)               │
│    → generates a NEW Member ID for etcd-1                                │
│    → etcd-1 becomes the ONLY member — quorum = 1 of 1                    │
│                                                                          │
│  AFTER — if you just start etcd-2 and etcd-3 pointing at their OLD data: │
│    ❌ SPLIT BRAIN: etcd-2/etcd-3 still believe they belong to Cluster    │
│       7a91f3 with 3 members — they will NEVER agree with the new         │
│       single-member cluster on etcd-1. Two disagreeing "truths" exist.  │
│                                                                          │
│  ✅ CORRECT: wipe etcd-2 and etcd-3's OLD data directories completely,   │
│     then re-run `etcdutl snapshot restore` on EACH of them too, with     │
│     --initial-cluster listing ALL THREE new peer URLs, so all three      │
│     restores agree on the SAME new Cluster ID and rejoin as one cluster. │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Restore approach</th><th>What happens to membership</th><th>Safe?</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Restore on 1 node only; start other 2 on their OLD data-dirs</td><td>2 old members (old Cluster ID) vs 1 new single-member cluster (new Cluster ID) — permanent disagreement</td><td>❌ Never — this is split-brain</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Restore on 1 node; wipe + manually re-<code>join</code> the other 2 as fresh members via <code>member add</code></td><td>1 restored member + 2 brand-new (empty) members that sync FROM the restored one via Raft replication</td><td>✅ Safe — this is the standard kubeadm HA restore runbook</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Restore independently on all 3 nodes with matching <code>--initial-cluster</code></td><td>All 3 agree on the same new Cluster ID from the start — no replication catch-up needed</td><td>✅ Safe — faster, used when you can restore all 3 in one maintenance window</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A snapshot restore is a NEW cluster wearing the old cluster's data — not a resurrection of the old one.</strong> This is precisely why kubeadm's official HA restore runbook says to stop kube-apiserver on ALL control-plane nodes, restore ONLY ONE etcd member with <code>--initial-cluster</code>/<code>--initial-cluster-token</code>/<code>--initial-advertise-peer-urls</code>/<code>--name</code> set correctly, then either wipe-and-<code>member add</code> the other two peers or restore them in lockstep with identical flags — mixing "restored" and "not-yet-touched" peers in the same running cluster is the #1 cause of a real production etcd split-brain during a botched DR drill. <code>--initial-cluster-token</code> gives the new cluster its own unique token so it can never be mistaken for — or accidentally join — the OLD cluster it was restored from.</div>

            <h5>⑦ Kubernetes-Specific Restore Gotchas — Revision Bumping, Hash Checks, and the <code>--force-new-cluster</code> Trap</h5>
            <p>Three etcd/etcdutl flags rarely mentioned in generic etcd tutorials matter <em>specifically</em> because Kubernetes controllers watch etcd through the API server's <strong>informers</strong> — local caches that stay in sync via long-lived watch streams keyed to a monotonically increasing revision number. A careless restore can quietly break that assumption.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  THREE FLAGS THAT MATTER MORE IN KUBERNETES THAN IN GENERIC etcd USE      │
│                                                                          │
│  1️⃣ --skip-hash-check  (etcdutl snapshot restore)                       │
│     Use ONLY if the "snapshot" was copied directly from a data          │
│     directory's member/snap/db file (not produced by `snapshot save`)   │
│     — such a copy has no integrity hash to check, so the normal         │
│     verification would simply fail. Never pass it as a "just in case"  │
│     habit — it disables the check that protects you from a truncated   │
│     or corrupted file.                                                  │
│                                                                          │
│  2️⃣ --bump-revision N  +  --mark-compacted  (etcdutl snapshot restore)   │
│     A restore resets etcd's revision counter to (roughly) where the     │
│     snapshot left off. If ANY informer/controller cached a HIGHER       │
│     revision from before the restore, it will never see updates at or   │
│     below that number again — a silent, stuck-cache bug that's brutal   │
│     to diagnose in production.                                         │
│       --bump-revision 1000000000   (bump comfortably past anything a    │
│                                     controller could have cached)        │
│       --mark-compacted             (tell etcd to refuse watches for     │
│                                     revisions from before the restore,   │
│                                     instead of serving stale/wrong data) │
│                                                                          │
│  3️⃣ --force-new-cluster  (etcdctl/etcdutl — DANGEROUS, avoid)           │
│     Forces a running member to become a brand-new single-member         │
│     cluster in place. The docs call this "strongly discouraged" —       │
│     it PANICS if other members from the previous cluster are still      │
│     alive and reachable. Prefer the restore-to-new-data-dir workflow    │
│     this chapter teaches; reach for --force-new-cluster only as a       │
│     last-resort, fully-isolated recovery of a single surviving member.  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>When to use it</th><th>Risk if misused</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>--skip-hash-check</code></td><td>Restoring a raw <code>snap/db</code> file copied directly off disk, not a <code>snapshot save</code> output</td><td>Masks real corruption in a normal snapshot if used out of habit</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--bump-revision</code> / <code>--mark-compacted</code></td><td>Any Kubernetes restore, so controllers' cached informer revisions can't collide with the restored revision range</td><td>Skipping it risks controllers silently missing updates because they're still watching a now-invalid revision</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--force-new-cluster</code></td><td>Essentially never in a routine runbook — only an isolated, no-other-members-alive emergency</td><td>Panics/crashes if other old members are still reachable; can fracture the cluster further</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 "It restored fine" is not the same as "it's safe for Kubernetes to use."</strong> A restore that skips revision bumping can pass every etcd-level health check while still leaving kube-controller-manager or a custom operator's informer cache silently stuck — because from etcd's point of view nothing is wrong, and from the controller's point of view it's still waiting for a revision number that will never come again. When restoring a live Kubernetes cluster (not just a bare etcd for practice), always bump the revision comfortably past what any controller could have observed.</div>
            </div>
