            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>etcd is the single source of truth</strong> — every Pod, Deployment, Service, Secret, RBAC rule, and ConfigMap exists only because it's a key in etcd. Lose etcd without a backup and you lose the cluster's entire memory, not just some of it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Backup needs 3-4 TLS flags, restore needs none</strong> — <code>snapshot save</code> talks to a live etcd over TLS, so it needs <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code> (and usually <code>--endpoints</code>). <code>snapshot restore</code> works entirely offline against the snapshot file, so no certs are required.</li>
                    <li><strong>Restore always targets a NEW <code>--data-dir</code></strong> — never overwrite the live directory in place. This is what makes a restore safely reversible if you picked the wrong backup.</li>
                    <li><strong>Two manifest edits, not one</strong> — after restoring, both the <code>--data-dir</code> command argument AND the <code>hostPath.path</code> volume in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> must point at the new directory. Forgetting the volume is the single most common restore mistake.</li>
                    <li><strong>A restore is a full time-machine, not a merge</strong> — every object created after the snapshot's revision is gone; every object that existed at snapshot time comes back exactly as it was.</li>
                    <li><strong>Automate it, but still verify it</strong> — a <code>CronJob</code> removes the "forgot to back up" risk, but an untested backup is not a real backup; periodically prove a snapshot restores cleanly.</li>
                    <li><strong>etcd backup ≠ full disaster recovery</strong> — snapshots capture desired-state <em>objects</em> only. PersistentVolume data and container images live outside etcd and need their own DR story (Velero, cloud-disk snapshots, an image registry).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>❌ Wrong</th><th>✅ Correct</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Forgetting <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></td><td>Always prefix with <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> or <code>export ETCDCTL_API=3</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Missing any of the 4 cert flags</td><td>Include <code>--endpoints</code>, <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Restoring to the same <code>--data-dir</code></td><td>Restore to a NEW directory, then update etcd manifest</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Using wrong cert paths</td><td>Check <code>kubectl describe pod etcd-*</code> for actual paths</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Not waiting for etcd pod restart</td><td>After manifest change, wait for kubelet to restart etcd</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Forgetting to update hostPath volume</td><td>Both <code>--data-dir</code> arg AND <code>hostPath</code> must point to new dir</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Assuming the etcd snapshot backs up PersistentVolume data</td><td>It only backs up the PV/PVC <em>objects</em> — back up volume bytes separately (Velero, §29.5)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Keeping the only copy of a snapshot on the same node as etcd</td><td>Ship every snapshot OFF the node immediately — a node loss must not also destroy its own backups</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Restoring while kube-apiserver/etcd are still live</td><td>Stop the control plane first (park the static pod manifests) — a live kubelet/controller can rewrite objects mid-restore</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>3 of the 4 flags are strictly non-negotiable</strong> — <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code> authenticate you to etcd; omit any one and the command fails outright. <code>--endpoints</code> defaults to <code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code> (fine when running directly on the control-plane node), but always include it explicitly so the command still works from elsewhere. Find the cert paths from: <code>kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system</code> or from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code></li>
                        <li><strong><code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> must be set</strong> — Either export it or prefix every command. Without it, etcdctl defaults to API v2 which uses different syntax and fails silently</li>
                        <li><strong>After restore, etcd restarts automatically</strong> — The kubelet watches <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. Changing <code>etcd.yaml</code> triggers a restart. Wait 30-60 seconds before verifying</li>
                        <li><strong>Backup path matters</strong> — The exam specifies WHERE to save the backup (e.g., <code>/opt/cluster-backup.db</code>). Use the exact path given or you lose marks</li>
                </ul>
                <h5>⚠️ Exam Trap: The Two Changes You Must Make</h5>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Change</th><th>Where</th><th>What to Change</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>1. Command arg</strong></td>
                            <td><code>spec.containers[0].command</code></td>
                            <td><code>--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</code> → <code>--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>2. Volume hostPath</strong></td>
                            <td><code>spec.volumes[0].hostPath.path</code></td>
                            <td><code>/var/lib/etcd</code> → <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>If you only change the <code>--data-dir</code> but not the <code>hostPath</code>, etcd will mount the <strong>old</strong> data directory into the container and ignore your restored data!</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — etcd backup one-liner (memorize):</strong><br>
                <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db \<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code><br>
                <strong>Restore:</strong><br>
                <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code><br>
                Then edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> → change <code>--data-dir</code> and <code>hostPath.path</code></p>
                <p><strong>Set the cert flags once per shell:</strong> <code>export ETCDCTL_API=3</code> then <code>alias e='etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=... --cert=... --key=...'</code> — every command after that is just <code>e snapshot save ...</code> / <code>e snapshot status ...</code>.</p>
                <p><strong>To "stop" the control plane fast for a full DR drill:</strong> move <code>etcd.yaml</code> and <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> OUT of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (the kubelet stops the static pods instantly); move them back to restart. No <code>systemctl</code> or <code>kubectl</code> needed for either direction.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-commands"></span>
