            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 29 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Understand the <em>why</em> behind etcd backup/restore — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your container images and PersistentVolume data still exist on disk. Why is losing etcd without a backup still "losing the entire cluster"?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because etcd is where every <em>object definition</em> lives — the images and PV data are useless without the Deployments, Services, Secrets, and RBAC rules that tell Kubernetes how to use them.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>✅ Stored IN etcd</th><th>❌ NOT stored in etcd</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Pod/Deployment/Service specs</td><td>Container images (in the registry)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Secrets, ConfigMaps, RBAC rules</td><td>PersistentVolume <em>data</em> (on disk/cloud storage)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Namespaces, CRDs, NetworkPolicies</td><td>Container logs (on the node)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Node registrations, PV/PVC objects</td><td>Running container process state</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The registry still has your <code>easypj:v2</code> image and the cloud disk still has your PostgreSQL data — but with etcd gone, nothing tells Kubernetes those things should exist, be scheduled, or be connected together. You'd have to recreate every Deployment, Service, Secret, and RBAC rule from scratch, by memory. That's why etcd backup is treated as backing up "the whole cluster," even though it never touches the data those objects point to.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> require 3-4 TLS flags, while <code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code> needs none?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Backup talks to a <em>live, running</em> etcd member over an authenticated TLS connection. Restore never talks to etcd at all — it reads the snapshot file and writes a new local data directory, entirely offline.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Backup is a network call · Restore is a local file operation</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  snapshot save  →  etcdctl ──TLS/mTLS──▶ 🗄️ live etcd member :2379
                     (needs --cacert/--cert/--key to prove who's asking)

  snapshot restore →  etcdctl ──reads──▶ 📄 backup.db (local file)
                                  ──writes──▶ 📁 new --data-dir (local disk)
                     (no network hop, no server to authenticate to)
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is a common exam trap: candidates paste the same <code>--cacert/--cert/--key</code> flags onto a <code>restore</code> command out of habit and the command still works (etcdctl silently ignores flags it doesn't need) — but memorizing that restore is a pure offline file operation makes the procedure much faster to reason about under time pressure.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why must a restore always target a brand-new <code>--data-dir</code> instead of overwriting the live one in place?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Restoring in-place would destroy the running data directory before you've confirmed the restore succeeded — if anything goes wrong, you'd have no way back. Restoring to a new directory keeps the original untouched until you deliberately repoint etcd at the new one.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>❌ Restore in-place</th><th>✅ Restore to new dir</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>If the wrong backup was used</td><td>Original data already gone — unrecoverable</td><td>Original data-dir untouched — just don't switch the manifest</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>If restore itself fails/corrupts</td><td>Nothing left to fall back to</td><td>Live etcd keeps running on the old dir the whole time</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Rollback</td><td>Not possible</td><td>Just revert the manifest's two paths</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code> actually refuses to run if the target <code>--data-dir</code> already contains data — it errors with "data-dir already exists" precisely to force this safer pattern. The switchover only becomes real the moment you edit the static pod manifest, which is a much smaller, easily-reversible action than a destructive in-place restore.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the two changes required in <code>etcd.yaml</code> after a restore, and explain what happens if you make only one of them.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) the <code>--data-dir</code> command argument, and (2) the <code>hostPath.path</code> of the volume that gets mounted into the container. If you change only the command flag, the container still <em>mounts</em> the old host directory — so etcd runs against stale (or empty) data even though the flag looks correct.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Change made</th><th>What actually happens</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Only <code>--data-dir</code> updated</td><td>Container still mounts the OLD hostPath — etcd silently uses stale data; restore appears to have "failed"</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Only <code>hostPath.path</code> updated</td><td>etcd's own <code>--data-dir</code> flag still points at the old path inside the container — mount is irrelevant, same stale result</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Both updated</td><td>The restored directory is both what etcd asks for AND what's mounted — correct behavior</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The command flag tells the etcd <em>process</em> which path to open; the <code>hostPath</code> volume tells the <em>container runtime</em> which host directory to expose at that path. They're two independent settings that happen to need the same value. This mismatch is the single most common real-world and exam mistake in etcd restore — always <code>grep -A2 hostPath /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> to confirm both changed together.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You back up etcd at 10:00, create a namespace at 10:30, then restore the 10:00 backup at 11:00. Is the namespace deleted, or does it stay because it existed at restore time?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It is deleted. A restore is a <strong>full time-machine to the snapshot's exact revision</strong>, not a merge with what currently exists — anything created after the backup, no matter how long ago the restore itself runs, disappears.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Restore reverts to the SNAPSHOT moment, ignoring everything since</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  10:00 ──snapshot──▶ 📸 backup.db (captures state AS OF 10:00)
  10:30 ── create namespace "test-ns" ── (this only exists in LIVE etcd, not in the snapshot)
  11:00 ── restore backup.db ──▶ live etcd is now REPLACED by the 10:00 snapshot
                                  └─▶ "test-ns" (created at 10:30) is GONE — it was never in the file
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Restore doesn't "go back and undo changes since 10:00" — it replaces the entire key space with exactly what was captured at 10:00. Anything written between the snapshot and the restore simply never makes it into the new data directory. This is why regular, frequent backups matter: the gap between your last snapshot and "now" is exactly how much work you'd lose in a real disaster.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">In a 3-node etcd cluster, how many members can fail before the cluster loses quorum — and why does that matter for backups?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Only 1. etcd uses Raft consensus, which needs a <strong>majority</strong> of members to agree before any write commits. With 3 members, quorum is 2 — losing 2 of 3 leaves only 1 member, which is not a majority, so the cluster goes read-only (the API server can no longer write, meaning it can no longer schedule, evict, or update anything).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Cluster size</th><th>Quorum (majority)</th><th>Failures tolerated</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1 (dev/test only)</td><td>1</td><td>0 — any failure is total loss</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3 (typical HA)</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>5 (large HA)</td><td>3</td><td>2</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is why a backup matters even in an HA etcd cluster: quorum protects you from a <em>single</em> node failure, but it does nothing against a correlated failure (bad upgrade rolled to all 3, a shared storage fault, an operator <code>kubectl delete</code> mistake at the object level, or 2 nodes failing before the third can be replaced). A recent snapshot is the only defense against losing a <em>majority</em> at once — quorum and backups solve different failure classes, and the CKA/production world needs both.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is an untested backup considered unreliable, and what's the fastest way to prove one actually restores?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>snapshot status</code> only proves the file's internal hash is self-consistent — it does NOT prove the file will restore cleanly into a usable etcd data directory. The fastest real test is a throwaway dry-run restore to a scratch directory.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">test-restore.sh</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># This does NOT touch the live cluster — it's a disposable directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/tmp/etcd-restore-test

ls /tmp/etcd-restore-test/
<span class="comment"># member/  ← restore succeeded, the backup is genuinely usable</span>

rm -rf /tmp/etcd-restore-test   <span class="comment"># cleanup, no manifest was ever touched</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>"Untested backups are unreliable backups" applies to etcd exactly as it does to any other backup system — a corrupted, truncated, or wrong-version snapshot can pass a superficial file-size check and still fail to restore. Since <code>snapshot restore</code> never touches the live cluster (it only writes to whatever <code>--data-dir</code> you give it), you can safely rehearse the full restore into a scratch directory on a schedule, without any risk to production.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why doesn't a routine etcd backup protect your PersistentVolume data, and what closes that gap?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>etcd only stores the <code>PersistentVolume</code>/<code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code> <em>objects</em> (the metadata describing size, storage class, and binding) — never the actual bytes written by your application. A tool like <strong>Velero</strong> is what backs up the real volume contents (and can also back up/restore the Kubernetes objects themselves as a convenient alternative to etcd snapshots).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Backed up by</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Cluster desired-state objects</td><td>Deployments, Services, PV/PVC <em>definitions</em>, RBAC, Secrets</td><td>etcd snapshot (this chapter)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Volume data</td><td>The PostgreSQL rows, uploaded files — actual bytes on disk/cloud storage</td><td>Velero (with a storage-provider plugin) or the storage layer's own snapshot feature</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Container images</td><td>The built <code>easypj:v2</code> image layers</td><td>The image registry's own retention/backup policy</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A restored etcd snapshot will happily bring back a <code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code> object that points at a volume — but if the underlying disk was never separately backed up, the claim binds to empty (or missing) storage. Full disaster recovery in production always combines etcd snapshots (for the cluster's "shape") with a volume-level backup tool (for the data inside that shape) — that's the gap a tool like Velero exists to close.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Define RPO and RTO, and explain which one decides your backup <em>schedule</em> versus which one decides how much you <em>rehearse</em>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>RPO</strong> (Recovery Point Objective) = the maximum data loss you can tolerate, measured in time — it decides backup <em>frequency</em>. <strong>RTO</strong> (Recovery Time Objective) = the maximum downtime you can tolerate — it decides how fast (and therefore how well-rehearsed) your restore procedure must be.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Answers</th><th>Drives</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>RPO</td><td>"How much data can I afford to lose?"</td><td>How OFTEN you snapshot (a 6h RPO needs snapshots ≥ every 6h)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>RTO</td><td>"How long can I be down?"</td><td>How FAST and well-rehearsed your restore runbook must be</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>These two numbers turn "back up sometimes and restore eventually" into an actual, testable policy. A CronJob every 6 hours is a concrete answer to an RPO of 6 hours; a target of "under 8 minutes" in this chapter's timed Practice Drills is a concrete answer to an RTO of 8 minutes. Neither number matters if you never write it down and never rehearse against it — that's why §29.1 introduces them before you touch a single <code>etcdctl</code> command.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Give the full order of operations for a DR runbook after TOTAL control-plane loss (all control-plane nodes gone, only a verified etcd snapshot and the workers survive).</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Provision a new control-plane node → restore the latest verified etcd snapshot → repoint the etcd static pod at the restored data-dir → bring up the rest of the control plane (API server, controller-manager, scheduler) → restore PersistentVolume data with Velero → validate workloads → document the actual RPO/RTO achieved.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">DR Runbook — total control-plane loss, in order</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  1. 🖥️  Provision a new control-plane node (or repair the existing hardware)
  2. 📸  Restore the latest VERIFIED etcd snapshot to a fresh --data-dir
  3. 🔗  Repoint etcd.yaml (--data-dir AND hostPath) at the restored directory
  4. ☸️  Bring up kube-apiserver / controller-manager / scheduler static pods
  5. 🗄️  Restore PersistentVolume data (Velero) — etcd alone never had the bytes
  6. ✅  Validate: kubectl get all -A, confirm easypj serves traffic correctly
  7. 📝  Document the RPO you actually lost and the RTO you actually achieved
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Order matters: you cannot repoint etcd before it's restored, and you cannot usefully validate workloads before the API server is back up. Step 5 is easy to forget under pressure — a perfectly executed etcd restore still leaves easypj's PostgreSQL PersistentVolumeClaim bound to whatever storage exists (possibly empty), so a real total-loss runbook always pairs the etcd restore with a Velero (or storage-layer) volume restore. Step 7 closes the loop back to Q9 — a DR event is exactly when you find out whether your declared RPO/RTO were realistic.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

