                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that an etcd backup is valid and not corrupted?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">snapshot status (recommended)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: snapshot status (recommended)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db \
--write-table
<span class="comment"># +--------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |  HASH  | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +--------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | 7c2...  |    15234 |       1456 |     5.2 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +--------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># ← Hash, revision, key count, size all shown</span>

<span class="comment"># If corrupted:</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /bad-backup.db
<span class="comment"># Error: expected sha256 ...</span>
<span class="comment"># ← Hash mismatch = corrupted backup!</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: JSON output for scripting</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db \
--write-table=false
<span class="comment"># Shows: hash, revision, totalKey, totalSize</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: File size check</span>
ls -la /opt/etcd-backup.db
<span class="comment"># Should be non-zero (typically several MB)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Dry-run restore</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
--data-dir=/tmp/etcd-test
ls /tmp/etcd-test/
<span class="comment"># member/  ← Restore succeeded</span>
rm -rf /tmp/etcd-test  <span class="comment"># Cleanup</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>etcdctl snapshot status</code> verifies the backup's integrity hash. If the hash doesn't match, the backup is corrupted. On the CKA, always verify after taking a backup — the exam may ask you to confirm the backup's validity. The <code>--write-table</code> flag gives human-readable output. A zero-size file or hash mismatch means you need to retake the backup.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The etcd pod is not starting after a restore. What should you check?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if manifest was updated correctly</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check if manifest was updated correctly</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep data-dir
<span class="comment"># --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored ← Correct?</span>

cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep -A2 hostPath
<span class="comment"># hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#   path: /var/lib/etcd-restored ← Must match --data-dir!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check the restored directory exists</span>
ls -la /var/lib/etcd-restored/
<span class="comment"># member/ ← Should exist</span>

<span class="comment"># Check ownership (must be root or etcd user):</span>
ls -la /var/lib/ | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># drwx------ 3 root root etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check kubelet logs</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -l | grep etcd | tail -20

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check container status</span>
crictl ps -a | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># If restarting: check crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Common issues after restore:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. hostPath not updated → etcd starts with old data</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. --data-dir not updated → path mismatch, crash</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Permission denied → chown the restored directory</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Both changes not saved → yaml syntax error</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. Wrong restore path → directory doesn't exist</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix example:</span>
sudo chown -R root:root /var/lib/etcd-restored
sudo chmod 700 /var/lib/etcd-restored</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Post-restore etcd failures: (1) <code>--data-dir</code> and <code>hostPath</code> mismatch — BOTH must point to the restored directory, (2) restored directory doesn't exist — re-run the restore, (3) permission issues — chown to root, (4) YAML syntax error in the manifest — validate the file. On the CKA, the most common mistake is updating only one of the two settings. Always verify both the command flag AND the volume path.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

