  │  --cert=...  --key=...  │          │  (NO certs needed)         │         │  + retention rule  │
  └────────────────────────┘          └─────────────────────────┘          └───────────────────┘
           │                                     │                                    │
           ▼                                     ▼                                    ▼
   ✅ verify: snapshot status         ⚠️ edit etcd.yaml — BOTH:              ✅ verify: snapshot status
      (hash/revision/keys/size)          --data-dir=...-restored                on every scheduled run
                                          hostPath.path=...-restored
                                       ⏱️ wait 30-60s for kubelet restart
                                       ⚠️ everything AFTER the snapshot's
                                          revision is gone — no merge, only
                                          a full time-travel to that moment
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Backing up etcd is a live, authenticated network call — <code>snapshot save</code> needs <code>--endpoints</code>, <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, and <code>--key</code> to talk to the running etcd member over TLS, and <code>snapshot status --write-table</code> proves the resulting file is hash-valid. Restoring is the opposite: a pure offline file operation with <strong>no certs at all</strong>, but it must always target a brand-new <code>--data-dir</code>, never the live one in place.</p>
                    <p>The step people skip is the one that actually breaks things: after <code>snapshot restore</code> finishes, you still have to <strong>manually repoint the etcd static pod manifest</strong> at the new directory — and that means changing <em>both</em> the <code>--data-dir</code> command argument and the <code>hostPath.path</code> volume. Forgetting either one leaves etcd quietly running on stale data while every command <em>looks</em> like it succeeded. The kubelet then auto-restarts etcd once it sees the manifest change, and 30-60 seconds later the cluster reflects exactly the state that existed at snapshot time — nothing more recent survives.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> production teams don't do this by hand every time — a scheduled <code>CronJob</code> takes the snapshot automatically, but the discipline that matters most is treating an <em>untested</em> backup as no backup at all: periodically prove a snapshot restores cleanly, and remember that etcd backup alone doesn't cover PersistentVolume data — that's a job for a tool like Velero.</p>
