                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The "Why" Behind <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> — DaemonSet Pods Were Never Scheduled in the First Place</h5>
                    <p>It's tempting to think of <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> as just a flag that silences an error. The real reason runs deeper: DaemonSet pods are never placed by the <strong>kube-scheduler</strong> at all. The <strong>DaemonSet controller</strong> creates one pod per matching node directly and binds it to that node itself — there's no scheduling decision to redo elsewhere, because the entire point of a DaemonSet is "exactly one copy, on every node, permanently." If drain deleted a DaemonSet pod anyway, the DaemonSet controller would simply notice the missing pod and recreate an identical one on the <em>same</em> (now-cordoned) node a moment later — accomplishing nothing except a brief gap in that node's kube-proxy or CNI coverage while it restarts. <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> isn't a workaround for a limitation; it's the technically correct instruction: "don't bother evicting this pod, it has nowhere useful to go and will just come right back."</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production Tip: You Can Drain Multiple Nodes in Parallel — a Shared PDB Still Protects You Globally</h5>
                    <p>Nothing about <code>kubectl drain</code> requires you to run it one terminal at a time. On a large fleet it's common to fire off several drains concurrently against <em>different</em> nodes to shrink a maintenance window:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">parallel-drain.sh</span>
                        </div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Multiple drain commands can run concurrently against different nodes</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data &amp;
kubectl drain worker02 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data &amp;
kubectl drain worker03 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data &amp;
wait</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>This is safe precisely because a PDB's <code>disruptionsAllowed</code> counter is <strong>global</strong>, not per-drain-invocation — the disruption controller recalculates it off the live pod count regardless of which command triggered an eviction. If <code>easypj-web-pdb</code> has <code>minAvailable: 2</code> across 3 replicas spread over these three nodes, the three parallel drains will collectively evict at most 1 pod at a time no matter how many <code>kubectl drain</code> processes are racing to do it — every eviction attempt still goes through the same Eviction API check against the same PDB object. The only thing you gain by parallelizing is not waiting for node N to finish before starting node N+1's SSH/patch/reboot steps; the PDB's math doesn't care how many drains are in flight.</p>
                </div>

            </section>

