            <!-- ─────────── §30.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s30-2">                    <h3 class="section-h">30.2 PodDisruptionBudgets (PDB)</h3>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>

                <p>A PodDisruptionBudget controls how many pods can be unavailable during <strong>voluntary disruptions</strong> like drain, upgrades, and node scaling. It prevents drain from killing too many pods at once, ensuring your application stays available.</p>

                <!-- PDB concept diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🛡️ PDB — Protecting Applications During Disruptions</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│          PodDisruptionBudget — How It Works                               │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  Deployment: easypj (3 replicas)                                         │
│  PDB: minAvailable: 2                                                    │
│                                                                          │
│  WITHOUT PDB:                        WITH PDB:                           │
│  ┌────────────────────────┐         ┌────────────────────────┐           │
│  │ kubectl drain worker01 │         │ kubectl drain worker01 │           │
│  │                        │         │                        │           │
│  │ Pod A ─── EVICTED ❌   │         │ Pod A ─── EVICTED ❌   │           │
│  │ Pod B ─── EVICTED ❌   │         │ Pod B ─── WAIT ⏸️     │           │
│  │ Pod C ─── running ✅   │         │          (PDB blocks   │           │
│  │                        │         │           until Pod A   │           │
│  │ ⚠️ Only 1 replica!    │         │           reschedules)  │           │
│  │ Possible downtime!     │         │ Pod B ─── EVICTED ❌   │           │
│  │                        │         │ Pod C ─── running ✅   │           │
│  │                        │         │ Pod A' ── running ✅   │           │
│  │                        │         │ ✅ Always ≥2 replicas  │           │
│  └────────────────────────┘         └────────────────────────┘           │
│                                                                          │
│  PDB guarantees minimum availability during voluntary disruptions        │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <!-- Two types comparison -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 minAvailable vs maxUnavailable — Choose ONE</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Field</th>
                                <th>Meaning</th>
                                <th>Example (3 replicas)</th>
                                <th>Max Disrupted</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>minAvailable: 2</code></td>
                                <td>At least N pods must stay running</td>
                                <td>2 must run → only 1 can be disrupted</td>
                                <td>1 pod</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>minAvailable: "67%"</code></td>
                                <td>Percentage must stay running</td>
                                <td>67% of 3 = 2 must run → 1 disrupted</td>
                                <td>1 pod</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>maxUnavailable: 1</code></td>
                                <td>At most N pods can be down</td>
                                <td>Max 1 down → 2 must run</td>
                                <td>1 pod</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>maxUnavailable: "33%"</code></td>
                                <td>Max percentage can be down</td>
                                <td>33% of 3 = 1 can be down</td>
                                <td>1 pod</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Percentages Always Round UP — <code>maxUnavailable</code> Can Silently Allow MORE Disruption Than You Typed</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes rounds a percentage <strong>up</strong> to the nearest whole pod for BOTH fields — but "round up" helps you with <code>minAvailable</code> and hurts you with <code>maxUnavailable</code>, because rounding up the number of pods that <em>must stay available</em> is conservative, while rounding up the number that <em>may be taken down</em> is permissive.</p>
                    <div class="comparison-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>7 Replicas, Value</th><th>Raw Math</th><th>Rounded (actual behavior)</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>minAvailable: "50%"</code></td><td>50% of 7</td><td>3.5</td><td>rounds UP → <strong>4 must stay up</strong></td><td>Safer than you typed (protects 1 extra pod)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>maxUnavailable: "30%"</code></td><td>30% of 7</td><td>2.1</td><td>rounds UP → <strong>3 may be disrupted</strong></td><td>Looser than you typed (1 more pod at risk than the raw 30%)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>On a small replica count this margin is easy to overlook until an incident review asks "why did 3 pods go down when we set 30%?" — the honest answer is that the rounding rule, not a bug, allowed it. If a workload's availability math is tight, prefer an exact integer <code>minAvailable</code>/<code>maxUnavailable</code> over a percentage so the disruption ceiling is unambiguous.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Two More Selector Traps: an Empty Selector, and Multiple PDBs on One Pod</h5>
                    <p><strong>Empty <code>selector</code>:</strong> on the stable <code>policy/v1</code> API (what this guide always uses), an <em>empty</em> <code>selector: {}</code> matches <strong>every pod in the namespace</strong> — the opposite trap from the typo'd selector described next, which matches zero. An accidentally-empty selector silently puts a disruption floor under pods it was never meant to protect (or, worse, under pods from an unrelated app sharing the namespace).</p>
                    <p><strong>Multiple PDBs matching the same pod:</strong> if more than one PDB's selector matches a given pod, Kubernetes enforces the <strong>most restrictive</strong> one — not an average, not the first one created. Two loosely-scoped PDBs that each look reasonable in isolation can combine into a drain that never proceeds, because the tighter of the two always wins.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Common Misconfiguration: a PDB Selector That Matches ZERO Pods Gives FALSE Confidence</h5>
                    <p>A PDB with a typo'd or stale <code>selector.matchLabels</code> — say, <code>app: easypj</code> when the Deployment's pods are actually labeled <code>app: easypj-web</code> — creates and applies without any error. <code>kubectl get pdb</code> will happily show it existing with <code>MIN AVAILABLE: 2</code>, and it LOOKS like protection is in place. It is not: the PDB simply matches zero pods, so it never gates anything, and a drain will evict every one of those pods with no resistance whatsoever. This is more dangerous than having no PDB at all, because the team believes they're protected. Always confirm the match with <code>kubectl describe pdb &lt;name&gt;</code> — check the <strong>Status</strong> block's pod count, or cross-check <code>kubectl get pods -l &lt;same-selector&gt;</code> actually returns your workload's pods before trusting the PDB in a maintenance runbook.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- PDB YAML examples -->
                <h4>PDB with minAvailable (easypj Example)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-pdb-minavailable.yaml</span>
