                <!-- Voluntary vs Involuntary Disruptions Detail -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 Voluntary vs Involuntary Disruptions — Complete Reference</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Disruption Type</th>
                                <th>Examples</th>
                                <th>PDB Honored?</th>
                                <th>Graceful?</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Voluntary</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl drain</code></td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Voluntary</strong></td>
                                <td>Cluster autoscaler scale-down</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Voluntary</strong></td>
                                <td>Deployment rolling update</td>
                                <td>⚠️ Not directly*</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Involuntary</strong></td>
                                <td>Node hardware failure / crash</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Involuntary</strong></td>
                                <td>Node kernel panic</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Involuntary</strong></td>
                                <td>OOM kill (out of memory)</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Bypass</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl delete pod</code></td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Bypass</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl drain --disable-eviction</code></td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p><small>* A Deployment's own <code>rollingUpdate</code> strategy is <strong>not gated by the PDB</strong> — the Deployment controller deletes old pods directly per its own <code>maxUnavailable</code>/<code>maxSurge</code>, not via the Eviction API, and the rollout proceeds even if it would push the PDB below its floor. What IS true: pods that are down for the rollout count against the PDB's live <code>currentHealthy</code> math, which can starve <code>disruptionsAllowed</code> to 0 and block a <em>separate</em>, unrelated <code>kubectl drain</code> sharing the same PDB — see "Two Processes, One Budget" below. Don't confuse "the rollout affects the PDB's numbers" with "the PDB controls the rollout"; it only does the former.</small></p>
                </div>

                <!-- PDB YAML Skeleton Quick Reference -->
                <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                    <h4>📋 PDB — YAML Skeleton (Exam Quick Reference)</h4>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
