                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Production Nuance: cluster-autoscaler and <code>safe-to-evict: "false"</code> Are NOT the Same Protection as a PDB</h5>
                    <p>Cluster-autoscaler scale-down uses the exact same Eviction API as <code>kubectl drain</code>, so it honors your PDBs too — but some pods shouldn't be evicted just because a node <em>looks</em> underutilized: a local cache that takes minutes to re-warm, or a pod holding expensive in-memory state. For those, annotate the pod template with <code>cluster-autoscaler.kubernetes.io/safe-to-evict: "false"</code> to tell the autoscaler "never scale down a node hosting this pod." The trap: teams often assume this annotation ALSO protects the pod from <code>kubectl drain</code> during planned maintenance — it does not. The annotation is autoscaler-specific; drain has never heard of it and will evict the pod exactly as normal, gated only by any PDB that happens to match it.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 What Actually Gates Each Disruption Mechanism</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What It's Trying To Do</th><th>Honors <code>safe-to-evict: "false"</code>?</th><th>Honors a PDB?</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl drain</code></td><td>Planned maintenance on a specific node</td><td>❌ No — the annotation is invisible to drain</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>cluster-autoscaler scale-down</td><td>Remove underutilized nodes to save cost</td><td>✅ Yes — skips annotated pods entirely</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

            </section>

