            <!-- Section 30.3 — OS Upgrades and Kernel Patches              -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §30.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s30-3">                    <h3 class="section-h">30.3 OS Upgrades and Kernel Patches</h3>

                <p>When a node needs an OS upgrade or kernel patch, you follow the drain → upgrade → uncordon pattern. The key concern is what happens if you <strong>skip</strong> the drain — if a node goes down without being drained first, Kubernetes falls back to its slower, taint-based eviction path (a <code>NoExecute</code> taint plus <code>tolerationSeconds</code>, default <strong>300s</strong>) and takes roughly <strong>5m40s total</strong> (the 40s <code>node-monitor-grace-period</code> plus the 300s <code>tolerationSeconds</code>) before it even starts rescheduling pods, versus the seconds a planned drain takes.</p>

                <!-- OS Upgrade Procedure Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🖥️ OS Upgrade Procedure — Step by Step</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    OS UPGRADE / KERNEL PATCH PROCEDURE                    │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  Step 1: DRAIN the node                                                  │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data│  │
│  │ Pods gracefully shut down and reschedule to other nodes           │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                          │
│                               ▼                                          │
│  Step 2: PERFORM the OS upgrade / kernel patch                           │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ (SSH into the node)                                               │   │
│  │ $ apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y                            │   │
│  │ $ reboot     (if kernel patch requires reboot)                    │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                          │
│                               ▼                                          │
│  Step 3: VERIFY the node is healthy                                      │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Wait for node to come back online after reboot                    │   │
│  │ $ systemctl status kubelet       (is kubelet running?)            │   │
│  │ $ kubectl get nodes              (is node Ready?)                 │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                          │
│                               ▼                                          │
│  Step 4: UNCORDON the node                                               │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl uncordon worker01                                       │   │
│  │ Node is now schedulable — new pods can be placed here             │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                          │
│  ⏱️ Skip the drain and this whole sequence still happens — just slower   │
│  If a node goes down WITHOUT drain, the node controller marks it NotReady│
│  after node-monitor-grace-period (40s), adds a NoExecute taint at the    │
│  same moment, and pods without a matching toleration are only evicted    │
│  once tolerationSeconds (default 300s) expires — roughly 5m40s total.    │
│  Always DRAIN first to avoid this delay!                                 │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <!-- Complete OS upgrade commands -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">os-upgrade-workflow.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># === COMPLETE OS UPGRADE WORKFLOW ===

<span class="comment"># FROM CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Drain the node</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># 2. SSH to worker and perform upgrade</span>
ssh worker01

<span class="comment"># ON WORKER NODE:</span>
apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y
<span class="comment"># If kernel upgrade:</span>
reboot

<span class="comment"># Wait for node to come back...</span>

<span class="comment"># FROM CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Verify node is healthy</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker01 should show Ready,SchedulingDisabled</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify kubelet is running</span>
ssh worker01 systemctl status kubelet

<span class="comment"># 5. Make node schedulable again</span>
kubectl uncordon worker01

<span class="comment"># 6. Final verification</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># All nodes should show Ready (no SchedulingDisabled)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Eviction Timeout comparison -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 Planned Drain vs Unplanned Node Failure</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Scenario</th>
                                <th>Pod Rescheduling</th>
                                <th>Downtime</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Planned: <code>kubectl drain</code> first</strong></td>
                                <td>Immediate — pods evicted and rescheduled instantly</td>
                                <td>Minimal (graceful shutdown period only)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Unplanned: Node crashes</strong></td>
                                <td>After <code>node-monitor-grace-period</code> (40s) + <code>tolerationSeconds</code> (default <strong>300s</strong>)</td>
                                <td>~5m40s — pods stuck in Unknown state</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Unplanned: Node power off</strong></td>
                                <td>After node becomes NotReady + taint-based <code>tolerationSeconds</code> countdown</td>
                                <td>~5m40s — no graceful shutdown</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway</h5>
                    <p>Always <code>kubectl drain</code> before maintenance. It ensures pods are gracefully rescheduled immediately instead of waiting out the ~5m40s taint-based eviction path that only kicks in when a node disappears unannounced. Combined with a PDB, your application maintains availability throughout the process.</p>
                </div>

