                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 Draining a Worker vs Draining an HA Control-Plane Node</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Worker Node</th><th>Control-Plane Node (HA, 3+ members)</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>What's at risk</td><td>Only the app pods scheduled there</td><td>etcd raft quorum + apiserver capacity for the <strong>whole cluster</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Safe concurrency</td><td>Can drain multiple workers if replicas/PDBs allow it</td><td><strong>ONE control-plane node at a time, always</strong> — never two simultaneously</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>What protects you</td><td>PodDisruptionBudget on your Deployment</td><td>etcd's own raft majority rule ((N/2)+1) — <strong>there is no PDB for etcd members</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Client-facing impact if done wrong</td><td>Reduced app capacity, possible brief 5xx</td><td>Cluster-wide API writes hang or fail (reads may still work from a surviving member's local cache)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Static pods (apiserver/scheduler/controller-manager)</td><td>N/A</td><td>Are NOT evicted by drain — they're kubelet-managed, not scheduler-managed; only user workloads on that node get drained</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pre-check before draining</td><td><code>kubectl get pdb</code></td><td><code>etcdctl endpoint health --cluster</code> and confirm the OTHER 2 members report healthy first</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
