                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Production Rule: Never Drain a Second Control-Plane Node Until the First Has Fully Rejoined</h5>
                    <p>A drain does not touch etcd membership by itself — etcd keeps running as a static pod (or systemd service) on a cordoned node and stays part of the raft group. The real danger is <strong>stacking maintenance</strong>: patching/rebooting cp-node-1 while its etcd member is offline, then starting on cp-node-2 before cp-node-1's etcd has rejoined and caught up. For a few seconds you have only 1 of 3 members reachable — below the majority — and every write across the <em>entire cluster</em> (not just easypj) blocks until quorum is restored. The fix is procedural, not technical: uncordon and verify <code>etcdctl endpoint health --cluster</code> shows all members healthy again before you touch the next control-plane node. This is exactly why Chapter 29's etcd backup discipline and this chapter's one-node-at-a-time rule are two halves of the same production practice.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Rolling Update + Drain collision — real production failure mode -->
                <p>One more failure mode catches teams off guard because it involves TWO separate, unrelated-looking operations quietly sharing the same budget: draining a node while a <code>Deployment</code> rollout is <em>also</em> in progress against the same pods.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">⚔️ Two Processes, One Budget — Rolling Update + Node Drain Can Deadlock Each Other</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
