            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Node Draining and PodDisruptionBudgets Work: Graceful Eviction, PDB Enforcement, and the Cordon/Drain Distinction</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Node Maintenance — The Complete Drain Lifecycle</h5>
            <p>Draining a node is more than just deleting pods. It respects PDBs, honors graceful termination, and can stall indefinitely if not understood properly.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NODE DRAIN — Internal Sequence                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  $ kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data        │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: CORDON the node (prevent new pods)                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ kubectl cordon worker-1                                               │   │
│  │ → Sets node.spec.unschedulable = true                                 │   │
│  │ → Node condition: SchedulingDisabled                                  │   │
│  │ → EXISTING pods continue running                                       │   │
│  │ → NEW pods CANNOT be scheduled to this node                            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ CORDON ONLY: Pods stay, no new pods. Useful for quick isolation.      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: EVICT pods (one by one, respecting constraints)                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ For each pod on the node (except DaemonSets + mirror pods):            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  a) CHECK PodDisruptionBudget:                                         │   │
│  │     → Does evicting this pod violate any PDB?                          │   │
│  │     → PDB .status.disruptionsAllowed must be >= 1                      │   │
│  │     → If NOT allowed → DRAIN BLOCKS (waits, retries)                   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  b) SEND SIGTERM to pod (graceful shutdown):                           │   │
│  │     → Pod receives SIGTERM                                             │   │
│  │     → App has terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30s) to cleanup  │   │
│  │     → If pod doesn't exit → SIGKILL after grace period                 │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  c) REMOVE pod from node:                                              │   │
│  │     → API Server deletes the pod object                                │   │
│  │     → ReplicaSet controller creates replacement pod                    │   │
│  │     → Replacement scheduled to a DIFFERENT node (this one is cordoned) │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  d) REPEAT for next pod                                                 │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  CORDON vs DRAIN:                                                             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ ┌──────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │   │
│  │ │ CORDON   │ Marks node unschedulable. Existing pods STAY.        │   │   │
│  │ │          │ Use: quick "do not use this node" flag.             │   │   │
│  │ ├──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤   │   │
│  │ │ DRAIN    │ Cordons + EVICTS all pods. Node becomes empty.       │   │   │
│  │ │          │ Use: maintenance, decommissioning, upgrades.         │   │   │
│  │ └──────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ DRAIN FLAGS:                                                           │   │
│  │  --ignore-daemonsets    <span class="comment">← Skip DaemonSet pods (they restart anyway)  </span>│   │
│  │  --delete-emptydir-data <span class="comment">← Delete emptyDir volumes (data loss warning)</span>│   │
│  │  --force                 <span class="comment">← Force even if pod not managed by controller</span>│   │
│  │  --timeout=5m           <span class="comment">← Max time to wait for drain to complete     </span>│   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>

            <h5>① How the Eviction API Gates Each Pod — PodDisruptionBudgets From the Inside</h5>
            <p>Drain doesn't delete pods directly — it calls the <strong>Eviction API</strong> (<code>POST /api/v1/namespaces/&lt;ns&gt;/pods/&lt;pod&gt;/eviction</code>), a permission-checked request the apiserver can refuse. Every PDB maintains a live <code>.status.disruptionsAllowed</code> counter, continuously recalculated by the <strong>disruption controller</strong>, and the Eviction API consults that counter before honoring each request.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  PODDISRUPTIONBUDGET — Protecting Application Availability:                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  apiVersion: policy/v1                                                │   │
│  │  kind: PodDisruptionBudget                                             │   │
│  │  metadata:                                                             │   │
│  │    name: easypj-pdb                                                    │   │
│  │  spec:                                                                 │   │
│  │    minAvailable: 2    <span class="comment">← At least 2 pods must be running               </span>│   │
│  │    selector:                                                           │   │
│  │      matchLabels: {app: easypj}                                       │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  PDB MATH:                                                             │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │ Deployment: easypj-web (replicas=5)                         │      │   │
│  │  │ PDB: minAvailable=2                                          │      │   │
│  │  │                                                               │      │   │
│  │  │ disruptionsAllowed = total pods - minAvailable               │      │   │
│  │  │                    = 5 - 2 = 3                                │      │   │
│  │  │                                                               │      │   │
│  │  │ → Up to 3 pods can be disrupted simultaneously               │      │   │
│  │  │ → At least 2 pods ALWAYS running during maintenance          │      │   │
│  │  │                                                               │      │   │
│  │  │ DRAIN with PDB: Drains pods until disruptionsAllowed == 0,  │      │   │
│  │  │ then blocks until pods on other nodes are healthy again.     │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> If drain hangs with no error message (as opposed to an immediate error about DaemonSets or emptyDir), it's almost always a PDB with <code>disruptionsAllowed: 0</code>. Check with <code>kubectl get pdb -A</code>. The fix is to scale the Deployment up (creating headroom) or wait for an in-flight eviction's replacement to become Ready — never <code>--disable-eviction</code>, which bypasses the PDB and can cause the very outage it exists to prevent.
            </div>

            <h5>② Graceful Termination — The Exact 30-Second Shutdown Sequence</h5>
            <p>Once the Eviction API allows a pod to go, it isn't simply killed — it goes through a precise, ordered shutdown so in-flight requests aren't dropped. This same sequence runs for <em>every</em> pod deletion, not just drain — rolling updates and manual <code>kubectl delete pod</code> trigger it too.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  GRACEFUL TERMINATION — The Full 30-Second Sequence:                         │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  1. Pod status set to "Terminating"                                  │   │
│  │  2. Pod removed from ALL Service Endpoints (stops receiving traffic)  │   │
│  │  3. SIGTERM sent to container (PID 1)                                 │   │
│  │  4. Container runs preStop hook (if defined)                          │   │
│  │  5. App has terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30s) to exit       │   │
│  │  6. After grace period: SIGKILL sent (force kill)                     │   │
│  │  7. Pod deleted from API Server                                       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Steps 2 and 3 happen <strong>in parallel</strong>, not sequentially — the pod is pulled from Service endpoints at the same instant SIGTERM fires, so a well-behaved app that finishes its in-flight requests within the grace period drops zero traffic. If your app ignores SIGTERM, it eats the full <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> before SIGKILL — tune this value (<code>spec.terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code>) rather than shortening drain's <code>--grace-period</code>, which only overrides the wait, not the app's actual shutdown behavior.
            </div>

            <h5>③ The Involuntary Path — What Happens Without Drain (NoExecute Taints)</h5>
            <p>Everything above describes the <em>voluntary</em> path — drain asking nicely via the Eviction API. If a node just disappears (crash, power loss, network partition) with no drain involved, Kubernetes falls back to a slower, taint-based mechanism instead, because nobody told it maintenance was coming.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  INVOLUNTARY NODE LOSS — Taint-Based Eviction (no drain involved)             │
│                                                                              │
│  t=0s     kubelet stops renewing its Lease object (heartbeat)                │
│  t=40s    node-monitor-grace-period expires                                  │
│           → Node Controller sets condition Ready=Unknown                     │
│           → kubectl get nodes shows STATUS: NotReady                         │
│  t=40s    Node Controller adds taint:                                        │
│           node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute                           │
│           (or ...not-ready:NoExecute if kubelet reported unhealthy, not gone)│
│  t=40s+   For each pod on the node WITHOUT a matching toleration:             │
│           → tolerationSeconds counts down (default 300s, i.e. 5 more min)    │
│           → pods WITH an explicit toleration for this taint are NEVER evicted│
│             this way (e.g. DaemonSet pods tolerate NoExecute by default)     │
│  t=5m40s  tolerationSeconds expires → pod object deleted (no graceful        │
│           SIGTERM handshake was possible — the kubelet that would run it     │
│           is unreachable) → ReplicaSet creates a replacement elsewhere       │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚡ Compare: drain triggers this in ~seconds via the Eviction API.           │
│     A silent crash costs you the full ~5m40s before Kubernetes even starts   │
│     moving your pods — this is the entire reason planned maintenance never  │
│     just powers a node off.                                                 │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Node maintenance is a CKA staple. Always drain before maintenance: <code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code>. If drain hangs: check PDBs (<code>kubectl get pdb -A</code>), check for pods not managed by controllers (<code>kubectl get pods -o wide | grep &lt;node&gt;</code> — bare pods block drain unless --force). After maintenance: <code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code> to allow scheduling again.
            </div>

            <h5>④ Eviction API vs Raw Pod Deletion — Why <code>kubectl delete pod</code> Isn't a Substitute for Drain</h5>
            <p>It's tempting to think "draining a node just deletes its pods," and to reach for <code>kubectl delete pod</code> as a shortcut. It is NOT the same operation. <code>kubectl delete pod</code> talks to a completely different API path than eviction — one that has no concept of a PodDisruptionBudget at all. Understanding the two separate code paths inside the API server explains why one is safe for production maintenance and the other is not.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  TWO PATHS TO "REMOVE THIS POD" — Only One Consults the PDB                   │
│                                                                              │
│  PATH A: kubectl delete pod easypj-web-7cd9  (raw deletion)                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ DELETE /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods/easypj-web-7cd9                │   │
│  │   → apiserver removes the Pod object immediately                     │   │
│  │   → NO PodDisruptionBudget check performed — none exists on this path│   │
│  │   → kubelet is notified, runs normal graceful-termination (SIGTERM), │   │
│  │     but nothing GATED the decision to remove the pod in the first place│   │
│  │   → If this pod was the last one keeping easypj-web above its budget,│   │
│  │     too bad — the deletion still happens                             │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  PATH B: kubectl drain <node>  (eviction, used by drain + descheduler)       │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ POST /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods/easypj-web-7cd9/eviction          │   │
│  │   → apiserver runs the Eviction sub-resource handler                 │   │
│  │   → Handler looks up every PDB whose selector matches this pod        │   │
│  │   → Checks disruptionsAllowed for each matching PDB                  │   │
│  │   → disruptionsAllowed == 0 for any match?  → HTTP 429 TooManyRequests│   │
│  │     "Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod's disruption budget"│   │
│  │   → disruptionsAllowed >= 1 for all matches? → deletion proceeds,     │   │
│  │     and the PDB's disruptionsAllowed is atomically decremented        │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  drain and the descheduler ALWAYS use Path B. Nothing else in the ecosystem  │
│  (kubectl delete, a crashing kubelet, an OOM-killed container) uses Path B — │
│  they all bypass the PDB because they are not asking permission, they are   │
│  reporting a fact ("this pod is gone") or making an unconditional request.   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>API Called</th><th>Consults PDB?</th><th>Can Be Rejected (429)?</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt;</code></td><td>Eviction sub-resource</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>✅ Yes — retries automatically</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Descheduler rebalancing a pod</td><td>Eviction sub-resource</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Cluster-autoscaler scale-down</td><td>Eviction sub-resource</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubectl delete pod</code></td><td>Core Pod DELETE</td><td>❌ No</td><td>❌ No — always succeeds</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Node crash / OOM kill</td><td>N/A (no API call — node just disappears)</td><td>❌ No</td><td>❌ No — nothing to reject</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> If a task says "safely remove this pod without risking availability," the answer is always eviction (drain, or a direct <code>POST .../eviction</code> if you're scripting it), never <code>kubectl delete pod</code>. On the exam, a distractor answer that uses <code>kubectl delete pod</code> to "test the PDB" is always wrong — delete has no PDB awareness whatsoever, so it can never demonstrate PDB behavior; only <code>kubectl drain</code> or a raw eviction API call can.
            </div>

            <h5>⑤ PDB <code>unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy</code> — Stopping a Broken Pod From Wedging Your Drain</h5>
            <p>A subtle trap: what happens when one of the pods a PDB protects is already <strong>unhealthy</strong> (CrashLoopBackOff, failing readiness) — not because of your drain, but from some unrelated bug? By default, Kubernetes still counts that broken pod as part of the calculation, which can wedge a drain that should obviously be allowed to proceed. Since Kubernetes 1.26, PDBs expose a field specifically to fix this: <code>unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy</code> — official guidance recommends setting it to <code>AlwaysAllow</code> on every PDB precisely so a single crash-looping pod can never wedge maintenance.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy: IfHealthyBudget (DEFAULT) vs AlwaysAllow         │
│                                                                              │
│  Setup: easypj-web, 3 replicas, PDB minAvailable: 2                          │
│  Pod-A: Running ✅   Pod-B: Running ✅   Pod-C: CrashLoopBackOff ❌ (broken) │
│                                                                              │
│  DEFAULT — unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy: IfHealthyBudget                       │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ currentHealthy = 2 (only Pod-A, Pod-B count — Pod-C isn't Ready)      │   │
│  │ minAvailable = 2  →  disruptionsAllowed = 2 - 2 = 0                   │   │
│  │ You try to evict Pod-C (the ALREADY BROKEN one, to unstick it)        │   │
│  │ → REJECTED (429)! The budget treats Pod-C as if evicting it would     │   │
│  │   drop you below 2 healthy — even though Pod-C ISN'T contributing to  │   │
│  │   availability in the first place. Drain WEDGES here.                │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  FIX — unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy: AlwaysAllow                               │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Pod-C is unhealthy → it is ALWAYS eligible for eviction, regardless   │   │
│  │ of disruptionsAllowed, because removing a pod that already isn't      │   │
│  │ serving traffic can never make availability WORSE.                    │   │
│  │ → Pod-C evicted immediately, drain proceeds to Pod-A / Pod-B normally │   │
│  │   (still gated by the budget, since THOSE pods are healthy)           │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Policy Value</th><th>Unhealthy Pods Count Toward Budget?</th><th>Risk</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>IfHealthyBudget</code> (default)</td><td>✅ Yes — treated like a healthy pod for the math</td><td>A cluster full of already-broken pods can wedge a drain forever</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>AlwaysAllow</code></td><td>❌ No — always evictable regardless of <code>disruptionsAllowed</code></td><td>None for availability — an unhealthy pod contributes zero capacity anyway</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Add this to your drain-troubleshooting checklist: if <code>kubectl get pdb</code> shows <code>ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS: 0</code> but <code>kubectl get pods</code> shows one of the "counted" pods is actually CrashLoopBackOff or stuck in a non-Ready state, the fix isn't necessarily to scale up — check whether the PDB's <code>spec.unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy</code> is set to <code>AlwaysAllow</code>. Without it, a single already-broken pod can permanently block every future drain on the pods it shares a PDB with.
            </div>

            <h5>⑥ Drain Timeouts, <code>--grace-period</code>, and Surge Capacity — Why Some Drains Finish in Seconds and Others Stall for Minutes</h5>
            <p>Two drains against identical PDBs can behave completely differently depending on cluster headroom. This is the piece most people miss: a drain isn't slow because of the PDB math itself — it's slow because the REPLACEMENT pod has nowhere to go yet. Understanding surge capacity explains both the fast case and the stalled case.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  FAST DRAIN (spare capacity exists)     vs     SLOW / STALLED DRAIN (no room) │
│                                                                              │
│  Cluster: 4 worker nodes, plenty of free CPU/memory on worker02-04           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ t=0s   Evict Pod-A from worker01 (allowed — disruptionsAllowed≥1)│       │
│  │ t=1s   Scheduler IMMEDIATELY places Pod-A' on worker03 (room free)│       │
│  │ t=8s   Pod-A' passes readiness probe → becomes Ready              │       │
│  │ t=8s   disruptionsAllowed recalculated back to 1 → evict Pod-B    │       │
│  │ ... repeats quickly, whole node drains in well under a minute     │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                                                              │
│  Cluster: nodes running near 100% CPU/memory request capacity               │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ t=0s   Evict Pod-A from worker01 (allowed)                        │       │
│  │ t=1s   Scheduler tries to place Pod-A' — NO node has enough        │       │
│  │        allocatable CPU/memory → Pod-A' stays Pending               │       │
│  │ t=1s+  disruptionsAllowed stays at 0 (Pod-A' never becomes Ready) │       │
│  │        → drain CANNOT evict Pod-B → appears to "hang"             │       │
│  │ t=Xm   --timeout expires (if set) → drain reports failure         │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                                                              │
│  RELEVANT FLAGS ON kubectl drain:                                            │
│  --timeout=5m      caps TOTAL time drain will wait before giving up          │
│  --grace-period=30 OVERRIDES each pod's terminationGracePeriodSeconds        │
│                     for THIS drain only (does not edit the pod spec)         │
│  --force            allows deleting pods with no controller (data loss risk) │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Root Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Drain evicts pods quickly, one after another</td><td>Cluster has spare CPU/memory — replacements schedule &amp; become Ready fast</td><td>Nothing to do — this is the healthy case</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Drain stalls after the first eviction, no error</td><td>Replacement pod stuck <code>Pending</code> — no node has capacity</td><td>Free capacity elsewhere, or scale the cluster/add a node before draining</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Drain fails after several minutes with a timeout error</td><td><code>--timeout</code> expired while waiting on a stuck eviction</td><td>Diagnose the underlying stall (capacity or PDB) — don't just raise the timeout</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pod takes the full grace period to terminate every time</td><td>App ignores SIGTERM / has a long <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code></td><td>Fix the app's shutdown handler, or accept the wait — <code>--grace-period</code> only caps THIS drain's patience, it doesn't speed up the app</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> "The drain is hanging" has TWO distinct root causes that are easy to conflate: (1) a PDB with <code>disruptionsAllowed: 0</code> because of the availability math (Subsection ①), or (2) a healthy PDB that simply can't be satisfied because the replacement pod has no node to schedule onto. Always check <code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> for a replacement stuck in <code>Pending</code> before you assume the PDB itself is misconfigured — scaling the Deployment won't help if the real problem is that the cluster is out of room.
            </div>

            <h5>⑦ What <code>cordon</code> Actually Writes to the API — <code>spec.unschedulable</code> AND a Mirrored Taint</h5>
            <p>Most people describe <code>cordon</code> as "a flag the scheduler checks," and stop there. There are actually TWO separate mechanisms working together, and knowing both explains why some older tooling and some newer scheduler-plugin-based systems behave slightly differently around a cordoned node.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  kubectl cordon worker-1 — TWO THINGS HAPPEN, NOT ONE                        │
│                                                                              │
│  MECHANISM 1 (legacy, still authoritative):                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ PATCH node/worker-1                                                   │   │
│  │   spec.unschedulable: true                                            │   │
│  │ → kubectl get nodes shows STATUS: Ready,SchedulingDisabled            │   │
│  │ → The default scheduler's PodFitsCondition-style check reads this     │   │
│  │   field DIRECTLY and refuses to consider the node — this is the       │   │
│  │   original, pre-taint mechanism and it is what "cordon" has always    │   │
│  │   meant since early Kubernetes.                                       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  MECHANISM 2 (modern, taint-based, layered on top):                          │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ The node lifecycle controller MIRRORS spec.unschedulable into a taint:│   │
│  │   node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable:NoSchedule                         │   │
│  │ → kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep Taints                        │   │
│  │   Taints: node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable:NoSchedule                 │   │
│  │ → The scheduler's TaintToleration plugin ALSO rejects this node,      │   │
│  │   independently of Mechanism 1 — because modern kube-scheduler is     │   │
│  │   built around a unified taint/toleration model for ALL exclusions.   │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  WHY BOTH EXIST: spec.unschedulable predates taints in Kubernetes history.   │
│  Rather than break every tool that reads that field, the taint was added    │
│  ALONGSIDE it so the newer plugin-based scheduler framework has a single,   │
│  consistent taint/toleration code path for every kind of scheduling         │
│  exclusion (node conditions, cordoning, node.kubernetes.io/not-ready, etc).  │
│  `kubectl cordon` / `kubectl uncordon` manage BOTH atomically — you never    │
│  set spec.unschedulable by hand and expect the taint to appear on its own.  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
