                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>What You Inspect</th><th>Command</th><th>What It Shows</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>spec.unschedulable</code> field</td><td><code>kubectl get node worker-1 -o jsonpath='{.spec.unschedulable}'</code></td><td><code>true</code> once cordoned, absent/omitted when schedulable</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Human-readable status</td><td><code>kubectl get nodes</code></td><td><code>Ready,SchedulingDisabled</code> in the STATUS column</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The mirrored taint</td><td><code>kubectl describe node worker-1</code></td><td><code>Taints: node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable:NoSchedule</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Never try to "cordon" a node by manually adding a custom taint of your own (e.g. <code>kubectl taint nodes worker-1 mytaint=true:NoSchedule</code>) — that's a DIFFERENT taint that pods must separately tolerate, and it does nothing to <code>spec.unschedulable</code>, so <code>kubectl get nodes</code> will keep showing plain <code>Ready</code>, not <code>SchedulingDisabled</code>. Always use <code>kubectl cordon</code>/<code>kubectl uncordon</code> so both the field and the built-in taint stay in sync — that's exactly why the exam expects the dedicated subcommand instead of a raw <code>kubectl patch</code> or a hand-rolled taint.
            </div>

            <h5>⑧ Why a Drained StatefulSet Pod With Local Storage Can Get Stuck Pending — Node Affinity From <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code></h5>
            <p>Drain treats StatefulSet pods like any other controller-managed pod: it evicts them and trusts the StatefulSet controller to recreate a replacement. That assumption holds fine for a Deployment's interchangeable pods — but a StatefulSet pod's PVC (from <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code>) can be bound to a <strong>specific node</strong> when the underlying storage is local (a <code>local</code> PV, or a cloud zonal disk), and drain has no idea that's true.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STATEFULSET + LOCAL STORAGE — WHY THE REPLACEMENT CAN'T MOVE                │
│                                                                              │
│  Setup: StatefulSet postgres-easypj, pod postgres-easypj-0 on worker-1       │
│  PVC "data-postgres-easypj-0" is bound to a LOCAL PV that only worker-1 has  │
│                                                                              │
│  $ kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets                               │
│    │                                                                        │
│    ▼ Eviction API evicts postgres-easypj-0 (no PDB blocks it — say none set)│
│    │                                                                        │
│    ▼ StatefulSet controller creates REPLACEMENT postgres-easypj-0           │
│      (SAME name — StatefulSet pods keep stable identity, unlike Deployment) │
│    │                                                                        │
│    ▼ Scheduler tries to place it:                                           │
│      → PVC "data-postgres-easypj-0" still exists, still bound to the        │
│        LOCAL PV that physically lives on worker-1's disk                    │
│      → nodeAffinity baked into that PV says "this volume = worker-1 ONLY"   │
│      → worker-1 is CORDONED (unschedulable) → no other node qualifies       │
│      → Pod sits in Pending: "0/N nodes are available: 1 node(s) had volume  │
│        node affinity conflict"                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  RESULT: drain "succeeds" (pod evicted) but the app is DOWN until worker-1  │
│  is uncordoned again — the exact opposite of what drain is supposed to buy  │
│  you. This is invisible in the drain output; you only see it in            │
│  `kubectl get pods` afterward.                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Storage Type Behind the PVC</th><th>Node-Locked?</th><th>Drain Behavior</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>local</code> PV / <code>hostPath</code></td><td>✅ Yes — one specific node only</td><td>Replacement stuck Pending until that exact node is uncordoned</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Cloud zonal block storage (most EBS/PD/Azure Disk classes)</td><td>Zone-locked, not node-locked</td><td>Replacement can land on any OTHER node in the same zone</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Network/shared storage (NFS, most CSI "RWX" drivers, distributed block stores)</td><td>❌ No</td><td>Replacement schedules freely on any node — this is the safe default for StatefulSets you plan to drain routinely</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Before draining a node, always check for StatefulSet pods with node-local storage first: <code>kubectl get pods -o wide | grep &lt;node&gt;</code> then cross-check their PVC's StorageClass. If you find one, the safe order is DIFFERENT from a stateless drain — either accept a maintenance window for that specific pod (it genuinely cannot move), or migrate the workload to zone/cluster-wide storage BEFORE you make node draining routine. On the exam this rarely comes up directly, but "why is my pod stuck Pending after I drained the node" is exactly the kind of real troubleshooting scenario Chapter 34 builds on.
            </div>

            <h5>⑨ Cordon Isn't a Perfect Seal — <code>nodeName</code>-Pinned Pods and Custom Tolerations Can Still Land There</h5>
            <p>Subsection ⑦ showed that <code>cordon</code> sets <strong>both</strong> <code>spec.unschedulable</code> and a mirrored <code>node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable:NoSchedule</code> taint. Both mechanisms are enforced entirely by the <strong>default kube-scheduler</strong> — which means anything that places a pod WITHOUT going through the scheduler's normal fit-checking is invisible to cordon and can still land on your "sealed" node.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  TWO WAYS A POD CAN STILL LAND ON A CORDONED NODE                             │
│                                                                              │
│  NORMAL PATH (what cordon actually blocks):                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ New pod → kube-scheduler evaluates candidate nodes                    │   │
│  │ → sees spec.unschedulable=true / TaintToleration plugin sees the      │   │
│  │   node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable:NoSchedule taint                   │   │
│  │ → node is FILTERED OUT of scheduling candidates                       │   │
│  │ ✅ This is the path cordon is designed to stop                        │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  BYPASS 1 — pod manifest sets spec.nodeName directly:                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ spec:                                                                  │   │
│  │   nodeName: worker-1        ← skips the scheduler ENTIRELY            │   │
│  │ → kubelet on worker-1 sees a pod bound to it and just runs it —       │   │
│  │   there was no scheduling DECISION for cordon to influence            │   │
│  │ ⚠️ Rare in hand-written YAML, but some custom schedulers / operators   │   │
│  │   bind pods this way, and it silently ignores cordon                  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  BYPASS 2 — pod carries a toleration for the unschedulable taint:            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ tolerations:                                                           │   │
│  │ - key: node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable                               │   │
│  │   operator: Exists                                                     │   │
│  │   effect: NoSchedule       ← this pod tolerates the cordon taint       │   │
│  │ → TaintToleration plugin no longer filters the node out for THIS pod  │   │
│  │ → DaemonSet pods carry this toleration BY DEFAULT (so they can still  │   │
│  │   be reconciled onto a cordoned node — by design, and harmless)       │   │
│  │ ⚠️ A regular Deployment pod with this toleration copy-pasted from a   │   │
│  │   DaemonSet spec will ALSO land on cordoned nodes — almost never      │   │
│  │   what you want                                                        │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Pod Type</th><th>Blocked by cordon?</th><th>Why</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Ordinary scheduler-placed pod (Deployment, StatefulSet, Job)</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Goes through kube-scheduler, which honors <code>spec.unschedulable</code> and the taint</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>DaemonSet pod</td><td>❌ No (by design)</td><td>Tolerates <code>node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable</code> by default so it can still be reconciled everywhere</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pod with <code>spec.nodeName</code> set directly</td><td>❌ No</td><td>Bypasses the scheduler entirely — nothing ever checks the taint or the field</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pod with a hand-added toleration for the taint</td><td>❌ No</td><td>Explicitly opts out of the one filter cordon relies on</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> If pods keep appearing on a node you just cordoned, don't assume cordon is broken — check for a <code>tolerations</code> entry matching <code>node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable</code> on the offending pod template (<code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.tolerations}'</code>) or a controller that sets <code>spec.nodeName</code> directly instead of letting the scheduler place it. Never copy a DaemonSet's tolerations onto a Deployment template "just to be safe" — that toleration is exactly what lets DaemonSet pods ignore cordon, and a Deployment doing the same defeats the entire point of taking a node out of service.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>drain = cordon + evict</strong> — <code>kubectl drain</code> marks the node <code>SchedulingDisabled</code> AND evicts every non-DaemonSet pod. <code>cordon</code> alone only stops new scheduling; existing pods keep running.</li>
                    <li><strong>Almost every drain needs <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code></strong> — DaemonSet pods (kube-proxy, CNI agents, log shippers) can't be evicted; the flag tells drain to skip them and let the DaemonSet controller manage them.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> is required whenever emptyDir volumes are in play</strong> — without it, drain refuses and warns that local (ephemeral) data would be lost.</li>
                    <li><strong>A PodDisruptionBudget (PDB) caps voluntary disruptions</strong> — <code>minAvailable</code> or <code>maxUnavailable</code> (never both) tells drain, rolling updates, and the cluster autoscaler how many pods may be down at once.</li>
                    <li><strong>PDBs do NOT protect against involuntary disruptions</strong> — node crashes, kernel panics, and OOM kills bypass the PDB entirely; only planned, API-driven evictions honor it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Always <code>uncordon</code> as the last step</strong> — forgetting it leaves the node schedulable-blocked forever; uncordon does NOT rebalance existing pods back onto the node automatically.</li>
                    <li><strong>Planned drain beats an unplanned crash by minutes</strong> — a drained node reschedules pods in seconds; an unannounced node failure waits out <code>node-monitor-grace-period</code> (40s, when the <code>NoExecute</code> taint also lands) + <code>tolerationSeconds</code> (default 300s) — roughly 5m40s total — before Kubernetes even starts moving pods. (<code>--pod-eviction-timeout</code> is the legacy pre-taint flag name; the taint + <code>tolerationSeconds</code> path is what actually runs today.)</li>
                    <li><strong>The safe OS-upgrade pattern is always the same four steps</strong> — drain → patch/reboot → verify (<code>kubectl get nodes</code>, <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>) → uncordon — repeated one node at a time so the PDB keeps the app available throughout.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>❌ Wrong</th><th>✅ Correct</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>kubectl drain node01</code> (no flags)</td><td><code>kubectl drain node01 --ignore-daemonsets</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Forgetting to uncordon after maintenance</td><td><code>kubectl uncordon node01</code> after work is done</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Draining node with local storage pods (fails)</td><td>Add <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> flag</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Thinking cordon evicts pods</td><td>Cordon only stops NEW scheduling; existing pods stay</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>PDB with both minAvailable AND maxUnavailable</td><td>Use only ONE — they are mutually exclusive</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>Drain fails without flags</strong> — DaemonSet pods can't be evicted (use <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code>). Pods with emptyDir data (use <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code>). Unmanaged pods without a controller (use <code>--force</code> — but data is lost)</li>
                        <li><strong>PDB blocks drain</strong> — If a PodDisruptionBudget would be violated, drain hangs. Check PDBs: <code>kubectl get pdb</code>. If ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS is 0, you must wait or scale up first</li>
                        <li><strong>PDB spec: <code>minAvailable</code> vs <code>maxUnavailable</code></strong> — Use one OR the other, not both. <code>minAvailable: 2</code> means at least 2 pods must stay up. <code>maxUnavailable: 1</code> means at most 1 pod can be down</li>
                        <li><strong>Percentages round UP for both fields</strong> — a <code>maxUnavailable: "30%"</code> on 7 replicas rounds up to 3 pods allowed down, not 2.1 — the ceiling can be looser than the raw percentage suggests</li>
                        <li><strong>Multiple PDBs matching the same pod</strong> — Kubernetes enforces the MOST restrictive one, not an average or the first one applied</li>
                        <li><strong>A scaled-to-zero Deployment's PDB protects nothing</strong> — with 0 desired replicas there are no healthy pods to protect, so the PDB allows all disruptions by definition; don't rely on a PDB alone to prevent a scale-to-zero mistake</li>
                </ul>
                <h5>🚨 CKA Exam Gotchas — Node Maintenance</h5>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Trap</th><th>What Happens</th><th>Correct Approach</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Using <code>--disable-eviction</code> to unstick a hanging drain</td>
                            <td>Bypasses the PDB check entirely — can drop a Deployment below <code>minAvailable</code> and cause the exact outage the PDB exists to prevent</td>
                            <td>Scale the Deployment up to create headroom, or wait for the in-flight eviction's replacement to become Ready — never disable eviction enforcement</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Using <code>kubectl delete node</code> instead of drain</td>
                            <td>Node removed from cluster — must rejoin with kubeadm</td>
                            <td>Use <code>kubectl drain</code> for maintenance, never delete</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Draining two HA control-plane nodes at the same time</td>
                            <td>etcd raft quorum can drop below (N/2)+1 — apiserver can't persist ANY write cluster-wide, not just for one app</td>
                            <td>Drain control-plane nodes ONE at a time; verify <code>etcdctl endpoint health --cluster</code> shows all remaining members healthy before touching the next one</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Draining control plane without thought</td>
                            <td>If single control plane, you lose API server access!</td>
                            <td>Control plane static pods can't be drained anyway — only user workloads</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

