                <h4>🧠 Chapter 30 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick concept checks before you move to the hands-on scenario drills below.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">True or false: <code>kubectl cordon worker01</code> will terminate the pods currently running on <code>worker01</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>cordon</code> terminates existing pods</td><td>❌ False</td><td><code>cordon</code> only sets <code>node.spec.unschedulable = true</code> — it blocks NEW pods from landing there. Pods already running keep running untouched.</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Remember:</strong> if you want existing pods gone too, you need <code>drain</code>, not <code>cordon</code>. Drain internally cordons first, then evicts.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment has 4 replicas and a PDB with <code>minAvailable: 3</code>. How many pods can be disrupted at once (<code>ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS</code>)?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Total Pods</th><th>minAvailable</th><th>Allowed Disruptions</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>4</td><td>3</td><td><strong>1</strong> (4 − 3 = 1)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><code>disruptionsAllowed = currentHealthy − minAvailable</code>. With 4 healthy pods and <code>minAvailable: 3</code>, exactly 1 pod may be evicted at a time; drain must wait for that pod's replacement to become Ready before evicting the next one.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which of these events are honored by a PodDisruptionBudget? <code>kubectl drain</code>, node kernel panic, cluster-autoscaler scale-down, OOM kill, <code>kubectl delete pod</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Type</th><th>PDB Honored?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>kubectl drain</code></td><td>Voluntary</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Cluster-autoscaler scale-down</td><td>Voluntary</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Node kernel panic</td><td>Involuntary</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>OOM kill</td><td>Involuntary</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kubectl delete pod</code></td><td>Direct bypass</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p>Only requests that go through the <strong>Eviction API</strong> (drain, descheduler, autoscaler) consult the PDB. Anything that kills a pod directly — a crash, an OOM kill, or a manual <code>delete</code> — bypasses it entirely.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubectl drain worker01</code> with no flags and it fails immediately. What is the single most likely cause on a freshly-installed cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Typical failure:</span>
error: cannot delete DaemonSet-managed Pods: kube-system/kube-proxy-xyz, kube-flannel-ds-abc

<span class="comment"># Fix:</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets</code></pre>
                        <p>Every cluster runs DaemonSets in <code>kube-system</code> (kube-proxy, CNI agent). Drain refuses to touch them by default because deleting a DaemonSet pod doesn't actually remove it — the DaemonSet controller immediately recreates it on the same node. <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> tells drain to leave them alone instead of erroring out.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After <code>kubectl uncordon worker01</code>, will the pods that were evicted from it during the earlier drain automatically move back?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Evicted pods automatically return to worker01 after uncordon</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p>Kubernetes never reschedules <em>running</em> pods on its own — the scheduler only places <strong>new</strong> pods. <code>uncordon</code> just makes the node eligible again. To rebalance, you need a new scheduling event: <code>kubectl rollout restart deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code>, scaling up/down, or deleting individual pods so their controller recreates them.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

