                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>kubectl cordon</code> and <code>kubectl drain</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Command</th>
                                        <th>Marks Unschedulable</th>
                                        <th>Evicts Pods</th>
                                        <th>Use Case</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>cordon</code></td>
                                        <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                        <td>❌ No — existing pods stay</td>
                                        <td>Prevent new pods while existing finish</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>drain</code></td>
                                        <td>✅ Yes (cordons first)</td>
                                        <td>✅ Yes — evicts all pods</td>
                                        <td>Full node evacuation for maintenance</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cordon</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Cordon:</span>
kubectl cordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 cordoned</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker-1  Ready,SchedulingDisabled  ← Exists pods stay</span>

<span class="comment"># Drain:</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># Cordons + evicts all non-DaemonSet pods</span>

<span class="comment"># Uncordon (same for both):</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 uncordoned</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>cordon</code> = soft block (no new pods, existing stay). <code>drain</code> = hard block (cordons + evicts everything). On the CKA, "remove all workloads from a node" = <code>drain</code>. "Prevent new pods on a node" = <code>cordon</code>. Both show <code>SchedulingDisabled</code> in node status. <code>uncordon</code> reverses both. Drain includes cordon, so you only need <code>uncordon</code> after drain.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Drain a node safely. What flags do you need and why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">full drain command</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Full drain command:</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 \
--ignore-daemonsets \
--delete-emptydir-data \
--force

<span class="comment"># Flag breakdown:</span>
<span class="comment"># --ignore-daemonsets  → DaemonSet pods can't be evicted</span>
<span class="comment">#   (they're managed by the DaemonSet controller)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Without this: "cannot delete DaemonSet-managed Pods"</span>

<span class="comment"># --delete-emptydir-data → Pods with emptyDir volumes</span>
<span class="comment">#   (data in emptyDir is lost on eviction)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Without this: "cannot delete Pods with local storage"</span>

<span class="comment"># --force → Pods not managed by a controller</span>
<span class="comment">#   (standalone pods with no ReplicaSet/Deployment)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Without this: "cannot delete Pods not managed by..."</span>
<span class="comment">#   WARNING: forced pods are DELETED, not rescheduled!</span>

<span class="comment"># Best practice (CKA safe combo):</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># Timeout (if drain hangs):</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets \
--delete-emptydir-data --timeout=60s</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Without flags, drain fails on: (1) DaemonSet pods (<code>--ignore-daemonsets</code>), (2) pods with emptyDir (<code>--delete-emptydir-data</code>), (3) unmanaged pods (<code>--force</code>). On the CKA, <code>--ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code> is the standard combo. Use <code>--force</code> only when told to — it permanently deletes standalone pods. DaemonSet pods are NOT evicted (they're recreated by the DS controller if the node becomes schedulable).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a PodDisruptionBudget that ensures at least 2 pods of a deployment stay available during disruptions.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative (fastest)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative (fastest):</span>
kubectl create pdb web-pdb \
--selector=app=web \
--min-available=2

<span class="comment"># YAML (for reference):</span>
apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
name: web-pdb
spec:
minAvailable: 2           <span class="comment"># At least 2 pods must stay up</span>
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: web

<span class="comment"># Alternative: use maxUnavailable:</span>
apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
name: web-pdb
spec:
maxUnavailable: 1         <span class="comment"># At most 1 pod can be down</span>
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: web

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pdb
<span class="comment"># NAME      MIN AVAILABLE  MAX UNAVAILABLE  ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># web-pdb   2              N/A              1                    10s</span>

<span class="comment"># Test: drain a node with pods matching the selector</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets
<span class="comment"># If draining would violate PDB → drain HANGS until safe</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PDBs protect against voluntary disruptions (drain, upgrades). Use <code>minAvailable</code> OR <code>maxUnavailable</code>, not both. <code>minAvailable: 2</code> means drain will only evict pods if at least 2 will remain. On the CKA, the imperative <code>kubectl create pdb</code> is fastest. The <code>ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS</code> column shows how many pods can currently be disrupted. If it's 0, drain will hang. PDBs only affect voluntary disruptions — node crashes still kill pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A <code>kubectl drain</code> command hangs and won't complete. What could be wrong?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cause 1: pdb blocks the drain</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Cause 1: PDB blocks the drain</span>
kubectl get pdb -A
<span class="comment"># NAME      MIN AVAILABLE  ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-pdb   2              0  ← ZERO allowed!</span>
<span class="comment"># If deployment has exactly 2 replicas and minAvailable=2,</span>
<span class="comment"># NO pods can be evicted</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Scale up first</span>
kubectl scale deploy web --replicas=3
<span class="comment"># Now ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS becomes 1</span>
<span class="comment"># Drain can proceed (one at a time)</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 2: Missing flags</span>
kubectl drain worker-1
<span class="comment"># Error: cannot delete DaemonSet-managed Pods</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: --ignore-daemonsets</span>
<span class="comment"># Error: cannot delete Pods with local storage</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: --delete-emptydir-data</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 3: Pod termination takes too long</span>
<span class="comment"># Pods have long graceful shutdown (terminationGracePeriodSeconds)</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Add timeout</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --timeout=120s

<span class="comment"># Cause 4: Unmanaged pods</span>
<span class="comment"># Standalone pods with no controller</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --force
<span class="comment"># WARNING: these pods will be DELETED permanently</span>

<span class="comment"># Debug: See what's blocking</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --dry-run=client
<span class="comment"># Shows what WOULD be evicted without doing it</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Drain hangs because: (1) PDB won't allow disruption (ALLOWED=0), (2) missing flags for DaemonSets/emptyDir, (3) pods taking too long to terminate, (4) unmanaged standalone pods. On the CKA, check PDBs first with <code>kubectl get pdb</code>. If ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS is 0, scale up the deployment or delete the PDB (if appropriate). Use <code>--dry-run=client</code> to preview what drain would do without executing it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Perform a complete OS upgrade on a worker node without losing workloads.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create pdb for critical workloads (optional)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create PDB for critical workloads (optional)</span>
kubectl create pdb web-pdb --selector=app=web --min-available=2

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Drain the node (from control plane)</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 \
--ignore-daemonsets \
--delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># All pods evicted and rescheduled to other nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: SSH to the worker</span>
ssh worker-1

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Perform OS upgrade</span>
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get upgrade -y
<span class="comment"># Or: sudo apt-get dist-upgrade -y</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Reboot if kernel was updated</span>
sudo reboot

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Wait for node to come back online</span>
<span class="comment"># From control plane:</span>
kubectl get nodes -w
<span class="comment"># worker-1  NotReady → Ready (after reboot)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Uncordon the node</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1

<span class="comment"># Step 8: Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker-1  Ready  ← Schedulable again!</span>

kubectl get pods -o wide
<span class="comment"># Pods will gradually be scheduled back to worker-1</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>OS upgrade workflow: drain → SSH → upgrade → reboot → uncordon. Draining first ensures workloads move to other nodes. PDBs protect against too many pods being evicted. On the CKA, this is a multi-step task: you must drain, perform maintenance, then uncordon. After uncordon, pods won't automatically move back — they stay on other nodes until rescheduled (by scale-up, rolling update, or pod restart).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to pods when a node becomes <code>NotReady</code> for more than 5 minutes?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">timeline when a node goes down</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Timeline when a node goes down (modern taint-based eviction):</span>
<span class="comment"># 0s:      Node stops sending heartbeats</span>
<span class="comment"># 40s:     Node status → Unknown (default node-monitor-grace-period),</span>
<span class="comment">#          AND a NoExecute taint (node.kubernetes.io/unreachable or</span>
<span class="comment">#          .../not-ready) is added at this SAME moment</span>
<span class="comment"># 40s+:    tolerationSeconds countdown starts (default 300s) for pods</span>
<span class="comment">#          with no matching toleration to that taint</span>
<span class="comment"># ~5m40s:  tolerationSeconds expires → pods deleted, rescheduled to</span>
<span class="comment">#          healthy nodes (40s + 300s, NOT a separate 5-minute step)</span>

<span class="comment"># Controller-manager flags (kube-controller-manager.yaml):</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml
<span class="comment"># --node-monitor-grace-period=40s      ← Default; also when the taint lands</span>
<span class="comment"># --node-monitor-period=5s             ← How often to check</span>
<span class="comment"># NOTE: --pod-eviction-timeout is a LEGACY, pre-taint-based flag — modern</span>
<span class="comment"># clusters use the NoExecute taint + tolerationSeconds (default 300s)</span>
<span class="comment"># path shown above instead. The old flag name still appears on the exam.</span>

<span class="comment"># Check node conditions:</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 Conditions
<span class="comment"># Ready    Unknown    ... kubelet stopped posting status</span>

<span class="comment"># Check pod status:</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide | grep worker-1
<span class="comment"># web-xxx  1/1  Terminating  ← Being evicted</span>
<span class="comment"># After eviction, controller creates replacement on other nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># DaemonSet pods: NOT evicted (rescheduled when node returns)</span>
<span class="comment"># StatefulSet pods: Wait for node to return (no auto-reschedule)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The node controller marks the node NotReady and adds a <code>NoExecute</code> taint at <code>node-monitor-grace-period</code> (40s) — the SAME moment, not two separate steps. Pods without a matching toleration are then deleted once their <code>tolerationSeconds</code> (default 300s) expires, roughly <strong>5m40s</strong> after the node first went dark, and the Deployment/ReplicaSet controller reschedules them to a healthy node. DaemonSet pods tolerate this taint by default and are skipped. StatefulSet pods are NOT auto-rescheduled (stable identity + often node-locked storage). The old <code>--pod-eviction-timeout</code> flag name is a legacy holdover from the pre-taint mechanism and still shows up as exam trivia, but the taint + <code>tolerationSeconds</code> path is what actually runs. This is why planned maintenance uses <code>drain</code> (immediate, controlled) instead of just powering off the node.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>minAvailable</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code> in a PDB?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">minavailable: minimum pods that must stay running</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># minAvailable: MINIMUM pods that must stay running</span>
<span class="comment"># Scenario: 5 replicas, minAvailable: 3</span>
<span class="comment"># Allowed disruptions: 5 - 3 = 2 (can evict 2 at a time)</span>

<span class="comment"># maxUnavailable: MAXIMUM pods that can be down</span>
<span class="comment"># Scenario: 5 replicas, maxUnavailable: 2</span>
<span class="comment"># Allowed disruptions: 2 (can evict 2 at a time)</span>

<span class="comment"># They're equivalent in this case!</span>

<span class="comment"># Percentage support:</span>
apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
name: web-pdb
spec:
minAvailable: "50%"       <span class="comment"># At least 50% must stay up</span>
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: web
<span class="comment"># 10 replicas → at least 5 must stay up</span>

<span class="comment"># Edge cases:</span>
<span class="comment"># minAvailable: 0   → ALL pods can be disrupted (useless PDB)</span>
<span class="comment"># maxUnavailable: 0 → NO pods can be disrupted (drain always hangs!)</span>
<span class="comment"># minAvailable: 100% → Same as maxUnavailable: 0 (nothing can be evicted)</span>

<span class="comment"># You can ONLY use ONE — not both:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ minAvailable + maxUnavailable = ERROR</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ minAvailable only</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ maxUnavailable only</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Both control the same thing from different angles. <code>minAvailable</code> = floor ("at least N must run"), <code>maxUnavailable</code> = ceiling ("at most N can be down"). Can be integer or percentage. Use only ONE. On the CKA, <code>maxUnavailable: 1</code> is often preferred — it's intuitive and works regardless of replica count. Setting <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> or <code>minAvailable: 100%</code> prevents all voluntary disruptions — drain will hang forever.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After uncordoning a node, pods don't move back to it. Is this expected?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yes — this is expected behavior!</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># YES — this is expected behavior!</span>

<span class="comment"># After uncordon:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ Node becomes schedulable again</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ NEW pods CAN be scheduled there</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ Existing pods do NOT automatically move back</span>

<span class="comment"># Why? Kubernetes doesn't reschedule running pods.</span>
<span class="comment"># The scheduler only places NEW pods.</span>

<span class="comment"># To rebalance pods manually:</span>
<span class="comment"># Method 1: Delete pods (controller recreates them)</span>
kubectl delete pod web-app-xxxxx
<span class="comment"># ReplicaSet creates new pod → scheduler may pick the empty node</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Rolling restart</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment/web-app
<span class="comment"># All pods are recreated → rebalanced across nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Use Descheduler (not built-in)</span>
<span class="comment"># Third-party tool that automatically rebalances</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify pods are now on the uncordoned node:</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide | grep worker-1
<span class="comment"># web-app-new-xxx  1/1  Running  worker-1 ← Rebalanced!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubernetes does NOT auto-rebalance after uncordon. Running pods stay where they are — only NEW pods consider the uncordoned node. On the CKA, this is expected behavior. To force rebalancing: <code>kubectl rollout restart</code> (graceful) or delete individual pods. The scheduler considers resource availability, so the "empty" node is likely to get new pods. The Descheduler is a community project — NOT on the CKA.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you remove a node from the cluster permanently?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">drain all workloads</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Drain all workloads</span>
kubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Delete the node object</span>
kubectl delete node worker-3
<span class="comment"># node "worker-3" deleted</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: On the worker node, reset kubeadm</span>
ssh worker-3
sudo kubeadm reset -f
<span class="comment"># Clears: kubelet config, PKI certs, etcd data</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Clean up network (on the worker)</span>
sudo iptables -F && sudo iptables -t nat -F
sudo ipvsadm --clear  <span class="comment"># if using IPVS</span>
sudo rm -rf /etc/cni/net.d

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify from control plane</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker-3 should be GONE</span>

<span class="comment"># To re-join later:</span>
<span class="comment"># On control plane, generate new token:</span>
kubeadm token create --print-join-command
<span class="comment"># On worker: run the join command output</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Permanent removal: drain → delete node → kubeadm reset on worker → clean up networking. <code>kubeadm reset</code> removes all cluster configuration from the node. On the CKA, this is less common but may appear in cluster maintenance scenarios. After reset, the node can rejoin with a new <code>kubeadm join</code> command. Always drain first to gracefully move workloads.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

