                    <p>Your easypj deployment has 3 replicas with label <code>app: easypj</code>. Create a PodDisruptionBudget named <code>easypj-pdb</code> that ensures at least 2 pods are always available during voluntary disruptions.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Create a PDB YAML with <code>minAvailable: 2</code></li>
                        <li>Apply the PDB to the cluster</li>
                        <li>Verify the PDB shows correct allowed disruptions</li>
                        <li>Test by draining a node — observe the PDB in action</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> The PDB's <code>selector.matchLabels</code> must match the pod labels from your Deployment. Use <code>apiVersion: policy/v1</code> and choose either <code>minAvailable</code> or <code>maxUnavailable</code> — not both.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj-pdb.yaml</span>
apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-pdb
  namespace: default
spec:
  minAvailable: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj</code></pre>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-pdb.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get pdb
<span class="comment"># NAME         MIN AVAILABLE   MAX UNAVAILABLE   ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-pdb   2               N/A               1                     10s</span>

<span class="comment"># Test — drain will respect PDB</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets
<span class="comment"># Drain will evict pods ONE AT A TIME, waiting for each to reschedule</span>
<span class="comment"># before evicting the next, ensuring at least 2 are always running</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 30.4 — Complete OS Upgrade Workflow</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 5 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Worker node <code>worker01</code> needs a kernel upgrade. Perform the complete maintenance workflow: drain, upgrade, verify, and restore service.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Drain worker01</li>
                        <li>SSH to worker01 and simulate an OS upgrade</li>
                        <li>Verify kubelet is running and the node is healthy</li>
                        <li>Uncordon and verify pods can be scheduled again</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> After a reboot, kubelet should start automatically (it's a systemd service). If it doesn't, check with <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> and start it with <code>systemctl start kubelet</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># From control plane:</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># SSH to worker:</span>
ssh worker01
apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y
<span class="comment"># If kernel upgrade: reboot</span>
<span class="comment"># Wait for node to come back...</span>

<span class="comment"># From control plane — verify:</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker01 should show Ready,SchedulingDisabled</span>

<span class="comment"># Check kubelet</span>
ssh worker01 systemctl status kubelet

<span class="comment"># Uncordon</span>
kubectl uncordon worker01

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># All nodes Ready, no SchedulingDisabled</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 30.5 — Diagnose Why Drain Won't Complete</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 5 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Your on-call teammate ran <code>kubectl drain worker02 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code> ten minutes ago from the control-plane node. It is still hanging with no error message. Diagnose the cause and unblock it <strong>without</strong> disabling PDB enforcement.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Check whether any PDB matching pods on <code>worker02</code> has <code>ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS: 0</code></li>
                        <li>Check whether the blocked Deployment can be safely scaled up to create headroom</li>
                        <li>Re-run the drain and confirm it now proceeds pod-by-pod</li>
                        <li>Uncordon once maintenance is complete and verify node status</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> A hanging drain with no error (as opposed to an immediate error about DaemonSets or emptyDir) almost always means a PDB is blocking eviction — <code>kubectl get pdb -A</code> and look for <code>ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS: 0</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Identify what's blocking — check PDBs cluster-wide</span>
kubectl get pdb -A
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE   NAME         MIN AVAILABLE   ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># default     easypj-pdb   3               0                     2h   ← the culprit</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Confirm: the Deployment has exactly 3 replicas, PDB requires minAvailable=3</span>
kubectl get deploy easypj-web
<span class="comment"># NAME         READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web   3/3     3            3           2h</span>
<span class="comment"># 3 healthy - minAvailable(3) = 0 allowed disruptions → drain can NEVER evict a pod here</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Fix WITHOUT bypassing the PDB — scale up to create headroom</span>
kubectl scale deploy easypj-web --replicas=4
kubectl get pdb easypj-pdb
<span class="comment"># ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS is now 1 — drain can proceed</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Re-run drain (it will resume automatically once disruptions are allowed,</span>
<span class="comment">#    but re-issuing the command is safe and idempotent)</span>
kubectl drain worker02 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># 5. After maintenance, uncordon and (optionally) scale back down</span>
kubectl uncordon worker02
kubectl scale deploy easypj-web --replicas=3
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                        <p><strong>Why not <code>--disable-eviction</code>?</strong> That flag bypasses the PDB entirely and can drop you below <code>minAvailable</code>, causing exactly the outage the PDB exists to prevent. Scaling up (or waiting for the in-flight eviction to finish rescheduling) is always the safer fix.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 30 Visual Summary — from "node needs maintenance" to zero-downtime patch</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  🛡️ BEFORE                🚿 DURING MAINTENANCE                    ✅ AFTER
  ┌──────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐
  │ Create PDB        │    │ kubectl drain worker01            │    │ kubectl uncordon  │
  │ minAvailable: 2   │───▶│  --ignore-daemonsets              │───▶│ worker01          │
  │ (or maxUnavail: 1)│    │  --delete-emptydir-data           │    │ Node schedulable  │
  └──────────────────┘    │                                    │    │ again (pods do    │
                           │ ① cordon (SchedulingDisabled)      │    │ NOT auto-rebalance)│
                           │ ② evict pods one-by-one,           │    └──────────────────┘
                           │    checking PDB before each        │
                           │ ③ SSH in → patch/upgrade → reboot  │
                           │ ④ verify kubelet + node Ready       │
                           └─────────────────────────────────┘
   drain = cordon + evict         PDB caps disruptions in-flight        pods stay put until
   (DaemonSets skipped)           (never below minAvailable)            a new scheduling event
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Planned drain reschedules pods in ~seconds. An unannounced crash instead waits out
   node-monitor-grace-period (40s, when the NoExecute taint lands) + tolerationSeconds
   (default 300s) — ~5m40s total — before Kubernetes reacts. That's exactly why every
   node touch on the exam and in production starts with drain.
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Before touching a node, put a <strong>PodDisruptionBudget</strong> in front of the workloads it hosts — <code>minAvailable</code> or <code>maxUnavailable</code>, never both. Then run <code>kubectl drain</code>, which is really two operations glued together: it <strong>cordons</strong> the node (blocks new scheduling) and then <strong>evicts</strong> every non-DaemonSet pod one at a time, checking the PDB before each eviction so the app never drops below its guaranteed minimum.</p>
                    <p>With the node empty, you SSH in, apply the OS patch or kernel upgrade, reboot if required, and verify from the control plane that <code>kubectl get nodes</code> shows <code>Ready</code> again and <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> is healthy on the node itself.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> <code>kubectl uncordon</code> is always the last step — and it only makes the node eligible again, it does not pull pods back onto it. Repeat this drain → patch → uncordon cycle one node at a time across your fleet and, with a correctly-sized PDB, your application never drops below its availability floor — that's the entire point of planned maintenance over an unplanned crash.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article><!-- end ch30 -->

