        <section class="part-cover" id="part9">
            <span class="part-kicker">🔴 Part 9 · Troubleshooting</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Fix It Fast</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 31–34 · CKA 30% · <em>"The biggest exam domain"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Troubleshooting is the single largest slice of the CKA — and the heart of the job. Part 9 builds a methodical fault-finding muscle: debugging pods and containers by status and events, diagnosing NotReady nodes and a sick control plane, reading logs and metrics, and — in Chapter 34 — a 12-scenario capstone playbook (Node NotReady, Pod Pending, CrashLoopBackOff, DNS, etcd, certs, NetworkPolicy, PVC, kubeconfig, scheduler-down, ImagePullBackOff) that drills every layer above, not just Services/networking, on the easypj cluster.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> read a pod’s status → events → logs to root-cause a failure; recover a NotReady node and a down control-plane component with <code>crictl</code>/<code>journalctl</code>; surface usage with the metrics server; and walk the Service/DNS/NetworkPolicy playbook under time pressure.</div>
            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🩺 Real-World Analogy: The Emergency-Room Doctor, Not the Detective Novelist</h5>
                <p>A detective novelist works backwards from the crime scene — theorizing about whodunit, chasing whichever clue feels most interesting, sometimes guessing before checking a single fact. An ER doctor does the opposite. No matter what's wheeled through the door, they take <strong>vitals in a fixed order</strong> — pulse, blood pressure, temperature, oxygen — and let the readings point at the problem instead of guessing first and testing second. Part 9 trains you to be the doctor, never the novelist. Every broken easypj pod, node, control-plane component, or network path gets the exact <em>same seven-step exam</em>, every time, regardless of what looks broken: <strong>get</strong> (take vitals) → <strong>describe</strong> (read the chart — Events, always, first) → <strong>logs</strong> (check history; <code>--previous</code> for the crash that already happened) → <strong>exec</strong> (physically examine the patient) → <strong>isolate</strong> the failing organ system (which layer) → <strong>fix</strong> (treat the cause, not the symptom) → <strong>verify</strong> (confirm the patient is actually stable before you walk away). The cluster is the body; Pod, Node, Control Plane, and Network are its organ systems — a symptom at one layer (a Pod that won't run) very often has its root cause <em>one layer down</em> (a sick Node, a struggling control plane, or a severed network path), exactly like chest pain that turns out to be a heart problem, not a rib.</p>
<pre>  ER DOCTOR — FIXED VITALS ORDER                 CLUSTER TROUBLESHOOTING METHOD
  ────────────────────────────────                ──────────────────────────────
  1️⃣ Take vitals (pulse, BP, temp) ───────────▶ GET      kubectl get &lt;obj&gt; -o wide — what STATE is it in?
  2️⃣ Read the chart / triage notes ───────────▶ DESCRIBE kubectl describe — read the Events block FIRST
  3️⃣ Check patient history ───────────────────▶ LOGS     kubectl logs [--previous] — what already happened
  4️⃣ Physical examination ────────────────────▶ EXEC     kubectl exec -it -- sh — poke inside the patient
  5️⃣ Identify the failing organ system ───────▶ ISOLATE  which layer: pod → node → control-plane → network
  6️⃣ Treat ────────────────────────────────────▶ FIX      the smallest change that addresses the ROOT cause
  7️⃣ Confirm the patient is stable ───────────▶ VERIFY   re-run GET — never declare victory unread</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"The error message always tells you the real cause."</td>
                            <td>The Events block names the <em>symptom's mechanism</em> — <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>, <code>FailedScheduling</code>, <code>Unhealthy</code> — not always the human root cause behind it. <code>ErrImagePull</code> tells you the pull failed; it doesn't tell you whether that's a typo'd tag, a private registry with no <code>imagePullSecret</code>, or a registry that's simply down. Reading Events is <strong>step 2 of 7</strong>, not the whole exam — it narrows where to look next, it rarely hands you the finished diagnosis.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Restarting the pod fixes the root cause."</td>
                            <td>A restart only resets the <em>process</em>; it does nothing to the condition that killed it. If the memory limit is genuinely too low, the container OOM-kills again at the same line of code. If a mounted Secret is missing, the new container hits the same missing file. Deleting the pod to "fix" a CrashLoopBackOff without reading <code>--previous</code> logs first just restarts the backoff clock on an unsolved problem — often making the exam clock the only thing you actually fixed.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"CrashLoopBackOff always means the same thing."</td>
                            <td><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> is a <strong>generic symptom label</strong>, not a diagnosis — it only means "this container keeps exiting and Kubernetes keeps backing off before retrying." The actual cause could be an app-level exception (exit 1), an OOM kill (exit 137), a missing ConfigMap/Secret key, a failing startup probe killing an app that just needs more boot time, or a bad command/entrypoint. Two pods can show the identical status and need completely different fixes — which is exactly why <code>logs --previous</code> and <code>describe</code> Events, not the status word itself, are what you diagnose from.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
