            <!-- ─────────── §31.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s31-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.1 Pod Lifecycle and Status Codes</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Understanding pod phases and container states is the foundation of all Kubernetes troubleshooting. When something breaks, the status tells you <strong>where to look</strong>.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Pod Lifecycle — From Creation to Termination</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                        POD LIFECYCLE FLOW                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  kubectl apply ─▶ API Server ─▶ Scheduler ─▶ kubelet ─▶ Container Runtime   │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────┐     ┌─────────┐     ┌─────────┐     ┌───────────┐             │
│  │ Pending │────▶│ Running │────▶│Succeeded│     │  Failed   │             │
│  │         │     │         │──┐  │ (exit 0)│     │(exit ≠ 0) │             │
│  └─────────┘     └─────────┘  │  └─────────┘     └───────────┘             │
│       │               │      │                        ▲                     │
│       │               │      └────────────────────────┘                     │
│       ▼               ▼                                                     │
│  ┌─────────┐     ┌──────────────┐                                          │
│  │ Unknown │     │CrashLoopBack │  ← Container keeps crashing              │
│  │(node    │     │    Off       │     and Kubernetes keeps restarting       │
│  │ lost)   │     │              │     it with exponential backoff           │
│  └─────────┘     └──────────────┘     (10s, 20s, 40s, ... 5min max)        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Pod Phases</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Phase</th>
                                <th>Meaning</th>
                                <th>What To Do</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Pending</code></td>
                                <td>Accepted by API server but not yet running — scheduling, image pull, or resource wait</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → check Events</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Running</code></td>
                                <td>At least one container is running, starting, or restarting</td>
                                <td>Check if pod is <strong>Ready</strong> (readiness probe passing)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Succeeded</code></td>
                                <td>All containers terminated with exit code 0</td>
                                <td>Normal for Jobs and CronJobs</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Failed</code></td>
                                <td>All containers terminated, at least one with non-zero exit</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Unknown</code></td>
                                <td>Cannot determine pod state — node communication lost</td>
                                <td>Check node status: <code>kubectl get nodes</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Container States — What Each Status Means</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Status</th>
                                <th>Common Cause</th>
                                <th>Debug Command</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: ContainerCreating</code></td>
                                <td>Image being pulled or volume mounting</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → Events</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: ErrImagePull</code></td>
                                <td>Wrong image name, tag doesn't exist, or registry auth failed</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → check image field</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: ImagePullBackOff</code></td>
                                <td>Same as ErrImagePull but after repeated failures (with backoff)</td>
                                <td>Fix image reference or add <code>imagePullSecrets</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: CrashLoopBackOff</code></td>
                                <td>Container starts then crashes repeatedly</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: CreateContainerError</code></td>
                                <td>SecurityContext issue, ConfigMap/Secret missing</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → Events</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Running</code> but not Ready</td>
                                <td>Readiness probe failing — app not serving traffic</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → Conditions section</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Terminated: OOMKilled</code></td>
                                <td>Container exceeded memory limit (exit code 137)</td>
                                <td>Increase <code>resources.limits.memory</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Terminated: Error</code></td>
                                <td>Container exited with non-zero code (app crash)</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Exit Code Quick Reference</h5>
                    <p><strong>Exit 0</strong> = Success (normal completion). <strong>Exit 1</strong> = Application error. <strong>Exit 137</strong> = OOM Killed (128 + signal 9). <strong>Exit 139</strong> = Segmentation fault. <strong>Exit 143</strong> = Graceful termination (128 + signal 15 SIGTERM).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Restart Policies — How Kubernetes Handles Failures</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Restart Policy</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>Used By</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Always</code> (default)</td>
                                <td>Restart container regardless of exit code — with exponential backoff (10s, 20s, 40s, ... 5min max)</td>
                                <td>Deployments, StatefulSets, DaemonSets</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>OnFailure</code></td>
                                <td>Restart only if exit code ≠ 0. Exit 0 = done.</td>
                                <td>Jobs (retry on failure)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Never</code></td>
                                <td>Never restart, regardless of exit code</td>
                                <td>Jobs (one-shot), debug pods</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Restart Backoff Timeline — CrashLoopBackOff Explained</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  restartPolicy: Always — Exponential Backoff                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Time:  0s     10s     30s      70s       150s        310s       600s       │
│         │       │       │        │          │           │          │         │
│  Run:   ▓▓▓X   ▓▓X    ▓▓▓X    ▓▓X       ▓▓▓X       ▓▓X        ▓▓▓        │
│         crash   crash  crash   crash     crash      crash      success?    │
│                                                                              │
│  Wait:  ──10s── ─20s── ──40s── ───80s─── ──160s──── ──300s────             │
│                                                                              │
│  Status:  CrashLoopBackOff ────────────────────────────▶  Running           │
│                                                                              │
│  💡 Max backoff = 5 minutes (300s). Resets after pod runs for 10+ minutes.  │
│  💡 The STATUS alternates between "CrashLoopBackOff" and "Error".           │
│  💡 Restart count = shown in kubectl get pods RESTARTS column.              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Pods Stuck in <code>Terminating</code> — The Status the Lifecycle Table Doesn't Show</h4>
                <p>A pod that won't <em>go away</em> is just as common a production incident as one that won't come up. <code>Terminating</code> isn't one of the five formal phases — the phase stays <code>Running</code> internally — but <code>kubectl get pods</code> displays it the moment a delete is issued, and if it lingers for more than a few seconds something downstream of the API is blocking it.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Why a Pod Gets Stuck Terminating</div>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  kubectl delete pod easypj-web-xxxx                                          │
│       │                                                                     │
│       ▼                                                                     │
│  1. API server sets deletionTimestamp (pod is NOT removed from etcd yet)     │
│       │                                                                     │
│       ▼                                                                     │
│  2. kubelet sees deletionTimestamp → sends preStop hook (if defined)         │
│       │                                                                     │
│       ▼                                                                     │
│  3. kubelet sends SIGTERM → waits terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30s)│
│       │                                                                     │
│       ▼                                                                     │
│  4. If still alive at grace period end → kubelet sends SIGKILL               │
│       │                                                                     │
│       ▼                                                                     │
│  5. kubelet reports back to API server → pod object is DELETED from etcd     │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️ STUCK if any step never completes:                                      │
│    • Step 2: preStop hook hangs (e.g. calling an unreachable service)        │
│    • Step 3: process ignores SIGTERM and grace period is set very high       │
│    • Step 5: a metadata.finalizer on the pod is never removed by its         │
│      controller — the API server refuses to fully delete the object         │
│      while ANY finalizer string remains in metadata.finalizers               │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>How to confirm</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Slow/hanging <code>preStop</code> hook</td><td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> — still shows the old container as terminating well past its grace period</td><td>Fix the hook (e.g. add a timeout inside it) or reduce <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>App ignores SIGTERM</td><td>Pod stays exactly <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> then finally goes away via SIGKILL</td><td>Add a signal handler in the app, or accept the SIGKILL fallback if that's fine for the workload</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Stuck volume unmount (common with network/CSI volumes)</td><td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> Events: <code>"Unable to attach or mount volumes"</code>; node's kubelet logs show unmount retries</td><td>Check the CSI driver/node plugin health; a node reboot or CSI driver restart often clears it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Finalizer never removed</td><td><code>kubectl get pod X -o jsonpath='{.metadata.finalizers}'</code> shows a non-empty list</td><td>Find and fix the controller that owns the finalizer; only as a last resort, patch it out (see warning below)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code>/<code>MutatingWebhookConfiguration</code> blocks the finalizer-removing UPDATE</td><td><code>kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations,mutatingwebhookconfigurations -o yaml</code> — look for one whose <code>rules</code> match <code>UPDATE</code> on <code>pods</code>; the webhook's own logs/events show it rejecting or timing out on that call</td><td>Fix or exempt the webhook for finalizer-removal updates on pods, or disable it if it's a third-party webhook that shouldn't be touching pod UPDATEs at all</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose a stuck-Terminating pod</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. How long has it been stuck, and what's its grace period?</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.metadata.deletionTimestamp} {.spec.terminationGracePeriodSeconds}{"
"}'

<span class="comment"># 2. Does it have a finalizer holding it open?</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.metadata.finalizers}{"
"}'

<span class="comment"># 3. Check Events for a volume/attach clue</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj | sed -n '/Events/,$p'

<span class="comment"># 4. LAST RESORT ONLY — force-delete (skips graceful shutdown entirely)</span>
kubectl delete pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj --grace-period=0 --force</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ <code>--grace-period=0 --force</code> Is a Last Resort, Not a Habit</h5>
                    <p>Force-deleting removes the pod object from the API immediately without waiting for the kubelet to confirm the container actually stopped. If the underlying container process is still running on the node (common when the kubelet itself is unresponsive), you can end up with <strong>two instances of the same workload running simultaneously</strong> for a moment — the old one still alive on its node, and a new one starting from the ReplicaSet/StatefulSet controller. For a normal Deployment pod this is usually harmless; for a StatefulSet pod with a volume that can only attach to one node at a time (e.g. an EBS-backed PostgreSQL pod), this can cause a "multi-attach" error or worse, split-brain data corruption. Always try to find and fix the real blocker (Step 2–4 above) before reaching for force-delete.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §31.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s31-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.2 The Debugging Toolkit</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>When a pod isn't working, follow this decision tree systematically. <strong>Never guess — always gather evidence first.</strong></p>

                <h4>Systematic Debug Sequence</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Get Status</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> — What phase is the pod in? How many restarts? Which node?</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Describe Pod</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> — Check Events at the bottom for scheduling/pulling/startup errors.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Check Logs</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> — For CrashLoopBackOff, <code>--previous</code> shows the last crash output.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Shell In</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code> — If pod is Running, interactively inspect files, network, processes.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Check Events</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code> — Cluster-wide timeline showing all recent events.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Exit Code Quick Reference</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Exit 0 — Success</h5>
                        <p>Container completed normally. Expected for Jobs/CronJobs. For long-running pods, this means the process quit unexpectedly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div>
                        <h5>Exit 1 — App Error</h5>
                        <p>Application crashed (unhandled exception, config error, missing dependency). Check <code>kubectl logs --previous</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💀</div>
                        <h5>Exit 137 — OOM Killed</h5>
                        <p>Container exceeded memory limit (128 + SIGKILL=9). Fix: increase <code>resources.limits.memory</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛑</div>
                        <h5>Exit 143 — SIGTERM</h5>
                        <p>Graceful termination (128 + SIGTERM=15). Normal during pod deletion/rollout. Not an error.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔍 Pod Debugging Decision Tree</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                 POD NOT WORKING? Follow this tree:                         │
│                                                                            │
│  $ kubectl get pods -o wide                                                │
│       │                                                                    │
│       ├── STATUS: Pending?                                                 │
│       │    └── $ kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;                               │
│       │         ├── "Insufficient cpu/memory"                              │
│       │         │    → Scale cluster or adjust resource requests           │
│       │         ├── "no nodes match pod's node selector"                   │
│       │         │    → Check nodeSelector/nodeAffinity labels              │
│       │         ├── "node(s) had taint... pod didn't tolerate"             │
│       │         │    → Add toleration to pod spec OR remove taint          │
│       │         ├── "persistentvolumeclaim not found"                      │
│       │              → Create the missing PVC or fix PVC name              │
│       │         └── hostPort already bound on every eligible node          │
│       │              → Use a Service/NodePort instead of hostPort          │
│       │                                                                    │
│       ├── STATUS: CrashLoopBackOff?                                        │
│       │    └── $ kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous                             │
│       │         ├── Application error / stack trace                        │
│       │         │    → Fix app code or configuration                       │
│       │         ├── "connection refused" / "no such host"                  │
│       │         │    → Dependent service not ready (DB, Redis)             │
│       │         ├── Missing config/secret reference                        │
│       │         │    → Check ConfigMap/Secret exists &amp; mounted correctly   │
│       │         └── Permission denied                                      │
│       │              → Check SecurityContext &amp; file permissions            │
│       │                                                                    │
│       ├── STATUS: ImagePullBackOff?                                        │
│       │    └── $ kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;                               │
│       │         ├── Wrong image name/tag                                   │
│       │         │    → Fix spec.containers[].image                         │
│       │         └── Private registry auth failed                           │
│       │              → Add imagePullSecrets to pod spec                    │
│       │                                                                    │
│       └── STATUS: Running but not working?                                 │
│            ├── $ kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;           ← application logs           │
│            ├── $ kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh  ← shell in            │
│            ├── $ kubectl port-forward &lt;pod&gt; 8080:80 ← test locally        │
│            └── Check readiness/liveness probes in describe output          │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h4>⚠️ Common Debugging Mistakes</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Immediately editing the pod YAML without checking logs first</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Always run <code>describe</code> → <code>logs</code> → <code>events</code> before making changes</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Using <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code> for a crashed container</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Using <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> to see the last crashed container's output</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Forgetting to check the Events section at the bottom of <code>describe</code></li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Scroll to the very bottom — Events show scheduling/pulling/startup timeline</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Where <code>kubectl logs</code> Actually Comes From — The Cluster's Logging Architecture</h4>
                <p><code>kubectl logs</code> is not a query against some central database — it is a live read of a file that the <strong>kubelet</strong> on that specific node has been writing to. Understanding this one fact explains why logs vanish when a node dies, and why every production cluster runs a log-shipping agent.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes Logging Architecture — From Container to Central Store</div>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Worker Node 1                          Worker Node 2 (same pattern)
  ┌───────────────────────┐              ┌───────────────────────┐
  │ easypj-web (pod A)    │              │ easypj-web (pod C)    │
  │  stdout/stderr ──┐    │              │  stdout/stderr ──┐    │
  └───────────────────┼────┘              └───────────────────┼────┘
                       ▼                                      ▼
  /var/log/containers/*.log            /var/log/containers/*.log
  /var/log/pods/&lt;ns&gt;_&lt;pod&gt;/...          /var/log/pods/&lt;ns&gt;_&lt;pod&gt;/...
                       │                                      │
                       ▼                                      ▼
          ┌────────────────────┐                 ┌────────────────────┐
          │ 📋 DaemonSet         │                 │ 📋 DaemonSet         │
          │ Log Agent (1/node)  │                 │ Log Agent (1/node)  │
          │ fluentd/filebeat/   │                 │ fluentd/filebeat/   │
          │ fluent-bit          │                 │ fluent-bit          │
          └──────────┬──────────┘                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                     │                                        │
                     └──────────────────┬─────────────────────┘
                                         ▼
                     ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
                     │   🗄️  CENTRAL LOG STORE               │
                     │   Elasticsearch / Loki / CloudWatch   │
                     │                ▼                       │
                     │   Kibana / Grafana (visualization)     │
                     └─────────────────────────────────────┘

  Quick debug (no central logging needed — direct kubelet read):
    kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;              ← current container's file, live
    kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous   ← the crashed instance's file
    kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -f           ← kubelet streams the file as it grows

  ⚠️  Node deleted / replaced / crashed → the on-disk log file is GONE.
      kubectl logs for that pod's history is unrecoverable UNLESS a
      DaemonSet agent already shipped it off-node before the node died.
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Production Reality — "It Worked Yesterday, the Logs Are Gone Today"</h5>
                    <p>This is one of the most common production incidents: a pod crashed overnight, the cluster autoscaler scaled the node group down (or a spot/preemptible node was reclaimed), and by the time an engineer investigates in the morning, <code>kubectl logs --previous</code> returns nothing — not because the app didn't log, but because the <em>node itself</em> no longer exists. The only way to have those logs is if a DaemonSet log agent (fluentd/filebeat/fluent-bit) had already shipped them to Elasticsearch/Loki/CloudWatch <em>before</em> the node was reclaimed. This is why "we'll add centralized logging later" is a decision that costs real incident-response time — set it up from day one on any cluster that autoscales or uses spot instances.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Common Misdiagnoses — What Costs You Time Under Exam Pressure</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>What it looks like</th><th>Wrong instinct</th><th>Right move</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code></td><td>Treat the status itself as the diagnosis and start editing YAML blindly</td><td>It's only the backoff <em>wait state</em> — the cause is in <code>logs --previous</code> or Events, always</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod shows <code>1/1 Running</code></td><td>Assume the pod is healthy and look elsewhere (Service, Ingress, DNS) for the real problem</td><td>Check the READY column value itself and the container count — <code>0/1</code> or <code>2/3</code> means something inside isn't ready yet, even though STATUS says Running</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Exit code 137</td><td>Assume the app has a bug and start reading application code</td><td>137 = OOMKilled, a <em>resource limit</em> problem — the fix is <code>resources.limits.memory</code>, not a code change</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code> is empty</td><td>Conclude the app never logged anything and give up on logs as a lead</td><td>You forgot <code>--previous</code> — the current (post-restart) container hasn't logged yet; the crash's own output is in the previous instance</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Multi-container pod, one <code>logs</code> command "just doesn't work"</td><td>Assume <code>kubectl logs</code> is broken or the pod has no output</td><td>Missing <code>-c &lt;container&gt;</code> — the CLI refuses to guess which of several containers you meant</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pending pod with plenty of visible cluster capacity</td><td>Assume the scheduler is malfunctioning</td><td>Read Events for the <em>actual</em> reason — usually a taint without toleration, a nodeSelector/affinity mismatch, or an unbound PVC, not a capacity problem at all</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod is <code>Running</code> and Ready, but behaves as if a field you set was never applied</td><td>Assume Kubernetes ignored your change, or start editing the live object directly to "force" it</td><td>A misspelled key (<code>commnd</code> instead of <code>command</code>, wrong indentation nesting a field under the wrong parent) is <strong>silently dropped</strong> by the API's schema validation — it never errors, it just isn't there. Diff what you meant to apply against what's actually running</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>The previous row's failure mode is common enough that it deserves its own quick check — Kubernetes' API validation drops unknown fields rather than rejecting the whole object, so a typo'd key can silently vanish instead of producing an error you'd notice.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">catch silently-dropped fields — validate &amp; diff</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Client-side schema validation catches gross typos before they're even sent</span>
kubectl apply --validate=true -f easypj-web.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. The real test: diff your source YAML against what the API server actually stored.</span>
<span class="comment">#    If a key was misspelled or nested wrong, it simply won't appear on the right side.</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -o yaml > /tmp/live.yaml
diff easypj-web.yaml /tmp/live.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Spot-check one field directly instead of reading the whole diff</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].command}'</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ A Typo Is Not Always an Error — Sometimes It's Silence</h5>
                    <p>Unlike a bad image name or a missing Secret (which produce a loud, specific Event), a misspelled field name like <code>commnd</code> instead of <code>command</code>, or a field nested one level too deep, is simply <strong>not part of the Pod schema</strong> — the API server accepts the object, silently drops what it doesn't recognize, and your pod runs with whatever the <em>correct</em> fields say (often the image's default <code>CMD</code>). No Event, no error, no restart. If a pod is "Running fine" but a specific override you made doesn't seem to be taking effect, this — not a Kubernetes bug — is the first thing to suspect.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §31.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s31-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.3 Essential Debug Commands</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>These are the commands you'll use on <strong>every single troubleshooting task</strong> in the CKA exam. Memorize them.</p>

                

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Essential Debug Commands — Complete Reference</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># 1. GET — Overview of pod status</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl get pods                           <span class="comment"># All pods in current namespace</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide                   # + Node, IP columns
kubectl get pods -A                        <span class="comment"># All namespaces</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels             <span class="comment"># See pod labels</span>
kubectl get pods -w                        <span class="comment"># Watch for changes in real-time</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. DESCRIBE — Detailed pod information + Events</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;                <span class="comment"># Full details + Events at bottom</span>
<span class="comment"># KEY SECTIONS TO CHECK:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Status / Conditions     → Is it ready? Why not?</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Containers → State      → Waiting/Running/Terminated + reason</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Events (bottom)         → Scheduling, pulling, startup timeline</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. LOGS — Application output</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;                         <span class="comment"># Current container stdout</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous              <span class="comment"># Previous crashed container ⭐</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -f                      <span class="comment"># Follow / stream live</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --tail=100              <span class="comment"># Last 100 lines only</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --since=1h              <span class="comment"># Last hour only</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;          <span class="comment"># Specific container (multi-container)</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --all-containers        <span class="comment"># All containers in the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. EXEC — Shell into a running container</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh          <span class="comment"># Interactive shell (Alpine/BusyBox)</span>
kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/bash        <span class="comment"># Bash shell (Ubuntu/Debian)</span>
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- cat /etc/resolv.conf <span class="comment"># Run single command</span>
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- env                  <span class="comment"># Check environment variables</span>
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- wget -qO- http://svc <span class="comment"># Test connectivity from inside</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. EVENTS — Cluster-wide timeline</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl get events --sort-by='.metadata.creationTimestamp'
kubectl get events --field-selector reason=Failed
kubectl get events -n kube-system          <span class="comment"># Control plane events</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 6. RESOURCE USAGE — CPU and memory</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl top pods                           <span class="comment"># CPU/memory per pod</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=cpu             <span class="comment"># Sort by CPU usage</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=memory          <span class="comment"># Sort by memory usage</span>
kubectl top nodes                          <span class="comment"># Node-level resource usage</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-verify">
                    <h4>✅ Verify Your Debug Skills</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Can you get previous container logs? → <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></li>
                        <li>Can you check events sorted by time? → <code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.metadata.creationTimestamp'</code></li>
                        <li>Can you shell into a specific container? → <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt; -- /bin/sh</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Ephemeral Debug Containers — Debugging Distroless Images</h4>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 When <code>kubectl exec</code> Won't Work</h5>
                    <p>Many production images are <strong>distroless</strong> — they don't include a shell (<code>/bin/sh</code>), <code>curl</code>, or any debug tools. Ephemeral containers let you inject a debug container into a running pod without restarting it.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Ephemeral Debug Containers</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Attach a debug container to a running pod</span>
kubectl debug -it &lt;pod-name&gt; --image=busybox:1.36 --target=&lt;container&gt;

<span class="comment"># Debug with a full toolkit (nicolaka/netshoot has curl, dig, tcpdump, etc.)</span>
kubectl debug -it &lt;pod-name&gt; --image=nicolaka/netshoot --target=app

<span class="comment"># Create a copy of the pod with a debug container (doesn't affect original)</span>
kubectl debug &lt;pod-name&gt; -it --image=busybox --copy-to=debug-pod

<span class="comment"># Debug a node directly (creates a pod with host namespaces)</span>
kubectl debug node/&lt;node-name&gt; -it --image=busybox</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Ephemeral Containers vs kubectl exec</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                              │
│  REGULAR CONTAINER (distroless)        EPHEMERAL DEBUG CONTAINER             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────┐          ┌─────────────────────────┐           │
│  │  Your App (Go binary)   │          │  Debug Tools            │           │
│  │                         │          │  • /bin/sh              │           │
│  │  ❌ No /bin/sh          │  ◄──────▶│  • curl, wget           │           │
│  │  ❌ No curl, dig        │  shares  │  • dig, nslookup        │           │
│  │  ❌ No package manager  │  process │  • tcpdump, strace      │           │
│  │                         │  space   │  • ps, netstat          │           │
│  └─────────────────────────┘          └─────────────────────────┘           │
│                                                                              │
│  💡 The ephemeral container shares the same PID namespace, network,         │
│     and IPC namespace — you can see the app's processes, files, and         │
│     network connections without modifying the original container.            │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  Ephemeral containers CANNOT be removed once added — they stay          │
│     until the pod is deleted. They are never restarted.                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Multi-Container Pods: Finding WHICH Container Isn't Ready</h4>
                <p>A pod's READY column shows a single fraction like <code>2/3</code> — but it never tells you <em>which</em> of the three containers is the odd one out. Scrolling a long <code>describe</code> output line-by-line to find it is slow; jsonpath gets there in one command.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">identify the non-ready container by name</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pod shows 2/3 — which container is the problem?</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME              READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
# easypj-web-xxxx   2/3     Running   0          4m</span>

<span class="comment"># One jsonpath call: prints "name=ready" pairs for every container</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj   -o jsonpath='{range .status.containerStatuses[*]}{.name}{"="}{.ready}{"
"}{end}'
<span class="comment"># web=true
# sidecar-envoy=false     ← this is the one blocking readiness
# metrics-exporter=true</span>

<span class="comment"># Now target only that container's logs and readiness reason:</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -c sidecar-envoy --tail=50
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj | grep -A8 "sidecar-envoy:"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Insight — Readiness Is an AND, Not an OR</h5>
                    <p>A pod's overall <code>Ready</code> <strong>condition</strong> (visible via <code>kubectl get pod -o wide</code>'s conditions, or <code>describe</code>'s Conditions section) is the logical AND of every container's individual readiness — and by extension, of every configured <code>readinessGate</code> too. One unready sidecar holds the <em>entire pod</em> out of every Service's Endpoints, even if the main application container is perfectly healthy. This is the single most common cause of "my app container is fine but the pod still isn't receiving traffic" in a service-mesh (Istio/Linkerd sidecar) environment.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                <div class="scenario-box positive">
                    <h5>✅ kubectl exec — Standard Debugging</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Works when container has a shell</li>
                        <li>Direct access to running process</li>
                        <li>Can modify files, check network</li>
                        <li>Best for: standard images (nginx, python, node)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔍 kubectl debug — Advanced Debugging</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox</code></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Works on <strong>distroless</strong> images (no shell)</li>
                        <li>Injects a sidecar with debug tools</li>
                        <li>Shares process/network namespace</li>
                        <li>Best for: Go binaries, scratch images, production pods</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §31.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s31-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.4 Debugging easypj — Common Scenarios</h3>
                <p>Let's apply the debugging toolkit to real-world scenarios using our easypj Django application. Each scenario follows the <strong>identify → gather → analyze → fix → verify</strong> flow.</p>

                <h4>Scenario 1: easypj Pod in CrashLoopBackOff</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔴 CrashLoopBackOff — Database Connection Refused</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP-BY-STEP WALKTHROUGH                                               │
│                                                                         │
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     NAME                  READY   STATUS             RESTARTS   AGE     │
│     easypj-web-7d4f8c5    0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5          10m     │
│     easypj-db-0           1/1     Running            0          10m     │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Check logs from the PREVIOUS crash:                        │
│     $ kubectl logs easypj-web-7d4f8c5 -n easypj --previous             │
│                                                                         │
│     django.db.utils.OperationalError:                                   │
│       could not connect to server: Connection refused               ◄── │
│       Is the server running on host "easypj-db" and                     │
│       accepting TCP/IP connections on port 5432?                        │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE:                                                            │
│     → The web pod starts before the DB pod is ready                     │
│     → Django tries to connect to PostgreSQL → fails → crashes           │
│     → Kubernetes restarts it → same thing → CrashLoopBackOff            │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Add an init container that waits for DB:                      │
│     initContainers:                                                     │
│     - name: wait-for-db                                                 │
│       image: busybox                                                    │
│       command: ['sh', '-c',                                             │
│         'until nc -z easypj-db 5432; do sleep 2; done']                 │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj -w                                     │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   0/1   Init:0/1   0   5s    ← init running        │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   1/1   Running    0   15s   ← ✅ success          │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Scenario 2: easypj Pod Stuck in Pending</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🟡 Pending — Insufficient Memory</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP-BY-STEP WALKTHROUGH                                               │
│                                                                         │
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     NAME                  READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE              │
│     easypj-web-xxxx       0/1     Pending   0          5m               │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj                    │
│     ...                                                                 │
│     Events:                                                             │
│       Warning  FailedScheduling  0/2 nodes available:                   │
│         2 Insufficient memory.  ◄── The clue is always in Events!       │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE:                                                            │
│     → Pod requests 2Gi memory but no node has that much free            │
│     → Check current requests vs capacity:                               │
│       $ kubectl describe node worker01 | grep -A5 "Allocated"           │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Reduce the request to fit available resources:                │
│     resources:                                                          │
│       requests:                                                         │
│         memory: "512Mi"    ← was 2Gi, reduced to fit                    │
│         cpu: "250m"                                                     │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   1/1   Running   0   30s   ← ✅ scheduled         │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
