                <h4>Scenario 4: easypj Pod in ImagePullBackOff</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🟠 ImagePullBackOff — Bad Tag or Missing Registry Credentials</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP-BY-STEP WALKTHROUGH                                               │
│                                                                         │
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     NAME                  READY   STATUS             RESTARTS   AGE     │
│     easypj-web-9c2a1b0    0/1     ImagePullBackOff    0          2m      │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — describe always names the exact pull failure:              │
│     $ kubectl describe pod easypj-web-9c2a1b0 -n easypj                 │
│     ...                                                                 │
│     Events:                                                             │
│       Warning  Failed   Failed to pull image "easypj:latset":           │
│         rpc error: manifest unknown              ◄── typo'd tag         │
│       -- OR, for a private registry --                                  │
│       Warning  Failed   Failed to pull image "registry.internal/easypj":│
│         unauthorized: no basic auth credentials  ◄── missing secret     │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE:                                                            │
│     → Case A: "easypj:latset" is a typo of "easypj:1.0" — the registry  │
│       correctly reports the tag/manifest does not exist.                │
│     → Case B: the image reference is correct, but the node's kubelet    │
│       has no credentials for that private registry, so the pull is      │
│       rejected with 401/403 before it even checks the tag.              │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Case A, correct the tag:                                       │
│     spec:                                                               │
│       containers:                                                       │
│       - name: web                                                       │
│         image: easypj:1.0        ← was easypj:latset (typo)             │
│                                                                         │
│     FIX — Case B, create and attach a registry credential:               │
│     $ kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \                   │
│         --docker-server=registry.internal \                             │
│         --docker-username=deploy --docker-password=$REG_PASS \          │
│         -n easypj                                                       │
│     spec:                                                               │
│       imagePullSecrets:                                                 │
│       - name: regcred                                                   │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj -w                                     │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   0/1   ContainerCreating   0   3s   ← pulling now  │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   1/1   Running             0   9s   ← ✅ success  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why This Happens in Production</h5>
                    <p>ImagePullBackOff almost never means "Kubernetes is broken" — it means the request Kubernetes sent to the registry was rejected, and the registry always says why in plain text. A CI/CD pipeline that pushes to a private registry (ECR, GCR, Harbor, a self-hosted registry) but forgets to rotate the cluster's <code>imagePullSecret</code> when credentials expire is the #1 real-world cause — it works fine at build time (the pipeline has fresh creds) and only breaks when the <em>node</em> tries to pull, days or weeks later on a rollout or node replacement.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Scenario 5: easypj Pod in CreateContainerConfigError</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🟣 CreateContainerConfigError — Missing ConfigMap/Secret Reference</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP-BY-STEP WALKTHROUGH                                               │
│                                                                         │
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     NAME                  READY   STATUS                     RESTARTS  │
│     easypj-web-5f7e3d2    0/1     CreateContainerConfigError  0         │
│     ← Note: NOT CrashLoopBackOff — the container never even started.    │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl describe pod easypj-web-5f7e3d2 -n easypj                 │
│     ...                                                                 │
│     Events:                                                             │
│       Warning  Failed   Error: configmap "easypj-config" not found      │
│       -- OR --                                                          │
│       Warning  Failed   Error: couldn't find key DB_PASSWORD in         │
│         Secret easypj/easypj-secret                                     │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE:                                                            │
│     → The Deployment's envFrom/env references a ConfigMap or Secret     │
│       (or a specific key inside one) that does not exist in this        │
│       namespace. This fails at the KUBELET level before the container   │
│       runtime is even invoked — it can never produce app logs, because  │
