                    <h5>① Container Exit Codes &amp; the 128+Signal Rule — Reading the Container's Last Words</h5>
                    <p>Every terminated container leaves an exit code in <code>status.containerStatuses[].lastState.terminated.exitCode</code>. Codes 0-125 are the app's own choice; anything ≥128 means the kernel killed the process with a signal, and the rule is fixed: <strong>signal number = exit code − 128</strong>. Memorizing this turns a bare number into a diagnosis.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Container process ──▶ exits on its own (0-125)         ──▶ read as-is: app's own exit status
                     └──▶ killed BY THE KERNEL (≥128)      ──▶ signal = code − 128

   exit 137 = 128 + 9  (SIGKILL)   ──▶ almost always the OOM killer enforcing memory.limit
   exit 143 = 128 + 15 (SIGTERM)   ──▶ graceful stop request (rollout, scale-down, drain)
   exit 134 = 128 + 6  (SIGABRT)   ──▶ the app aborted itself (assertion failure, panic)
   exit 139 = 128 + 11 (SIGSEGV)   ──▶ segmentation fault — a real application/runtime bug

   describe pod also surfaces terminationMessagePath (/dev/termination-log) —
   a container can write its OWN last words there, and describe prints them verbatim.</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Exit Code</th><th>128 + Signal</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical Root Cause</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>Clean exit</td><td>Normal completion (Jobs/init containers)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>1</td><td>—</td><td>App error</td><td>Unhandled exception, bad config, missing dependency</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>134</td><td>128+6 SIGABRT</td><td>Aborted</td><td>Assertion failure, Python/Go panic</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>137</td><td>128+9 SIGKILL</td><td>OOMKilled</td><td>Exceeded <code>resources.limits.memory</code> — a limits problem, not a bug</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>139</td><td>128+11 SIGSEGV</td><td>Segfault</td><td>Native crash — real application/runtime bug</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>143</td><td>128+15 SIGTERM</td><td>Graceful stop</td><td>Rolling update, scale-down, node drain — usually normal</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Never treat 137 as an application bug — it is the kernel enforcing a cgroup memory ceiling, and the fix is a <code>resources.limits.memory</code> edit (or fixing a real leak), never a code change. Conversely, 143 during a rollout is expected — only chase it if it fires outside a deliberate rollout/scale/drain.</div>

                    <p><code>terminationMessagePath</code> only writes to <code>/dev/termination-log</code> by default — but whether <code>describe</code> actually shows anything there is controlled by a second field, <code>terminationMessagePolicy</code>, which most easypj manifests never set explicitly and therefore silently get the default.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>terminationMessagePolicy</th><th>Default?</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>File</code></td><td>✅ default</td><td>Only reads whatever the container itself wrote to <code>terminationMessagePath</code>; if the file is empty, the message is empty</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>FallbackToLogsOnError</code></td><td>—</td><td>If the message file is empty <em>and</em> the container exited with an error, uses the last chunk of container log output instead (capped at 2048 bytes / 80 lines, whichever is smaller)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Set <code>terminationMessagePolicy: FallbackToLogsOnError</code> on easypj-web so a crash surfaces its last log lines directly in <code>status.containerStatuses[].lastState.terminated.message</code> — visible straight from <code>kubectl describe pod</code> — even if the app never wrote anything to <code>/dev/termination-log</code> itself. Also know the size caps: <strong>4096 bytes</strong> per container (kubelet truncates longer messages), and a <strong>12KiB total</strong> ceiling shared across all containers/init containers in the pod.</div>

                    <h5>② CrashLoopBackOff — The Kubelet's Exponential Backoff Engine</h5>
                    <p>CrashLoopBackOff is not a Kubernetes "state" stored anywhere — it is the kubelet's own restart-throttling behavior, reported back as a container waiting reason. The kubelet tracks a per-container restart counter and multiplies the wait time so a broken container can never hammer the node.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   KUBELET'S PER-CONTAINER RESTART BACKOFF (restartPolicy: Always)

   attempt 1 fails ─▶ wait 10s  ─▶ attempt 2 fails ─▶ wait 20s  ─▶ attempt 3 fails ─▶ wait 40s
                                                                                          │
   ◀────────────────────────────── doubles each time, capped at 300s ──────────────────────┘
                                                                                          │
                        attempt 4 → 80s → attempt 5 → 160s → attempt 6+ → 300s (5min cap)

   RESET RULE: the backoff counter resets to zero only after the container has stayed
   Running continuously for ~10 minutes — a single successful restart does NOT reset it.

   Status column alternates:  CrashLoopBackOff (waiting) ⇄ Error/Terminated (the crash itself)</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Where to see it</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>RESTARTS column</td><td><code>kubectl get pods</code></td><td>total restart count for the pod's first container</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Last State</td><td><code>kubectl describe pod</code></td><td>the PREVIOUS crash's Reason + Exit Code</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Back-off event</td><td>describe Events</td><td>"Back-off restarting failed container" — confirms the kubelet is throttling</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> CrashLoopBackOff is a symptom of this timer, never the root cause — the cause lives in <code>logs --previous</code> or in Events. If a candidate pod has been crashing for a while, expect the NEXT retry to be minutes away; don't wait for it — go straight to <code>--previous</code> logs while it's in the wait window.</div>

                    <div class="info-box note">
                        <h5>📖 The 10s→300s Curve Is Evolving — Newer Kubernetes Feature Gates</h5>
                        <p>The classic 10s/20s/40s.../300s curve above is the long-standing default, but recent Kubernetes releases add two feature gates that let clusters tune it: <code>ReduceDefaultCrashLoopBackOffDecay</code> shortens the <em>default</em> curve to start at 1s and double up to a 60s cap (instead of 10s→300s) — a much faster retry cadence for clusters that opt in. <code>KubeletCrashLoopBackOffMax</code> goes further, exposing a per-kubelet <code>maxContainerRestartPeriod</code> config field (still 10s start, still doubling, but capped anywhere from 1s to 300s that <em>you</em> choose) instead of a hardcoded 300s. Since these are newer, gated features, don't assume they're active on any given cluster — check <code>kubectl version</code> and the node's kubelet config before treating a faster-than-expected backoff as a bug rather than a deliberately tuned cluster.</p>
                    </div>

                    <h5>③ Probes: Liveness vs Readiness — Different Failures, Different Symptoms</h5>
                    <p>Both probes run the same kind of check (HTTP/TCP/exec), but a failure sends the pod down two completely different paths. Confusing them is one of the most common CKA and real-world misdiagnoses.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   livenessProbe FAILS                         readinessProbe FAILS
   ────────────────────                        ─────────────────────
   kubelet decides the container is DEAD       kubelet decides the container is NOT READY
        │                                            │
        ▼                                            ▼
   KILLS the container (SIGTERM→SIGKILL)       LEAVES it running, untouched
        │                                            │
        ▼                                            ▼
   restartPolicy takes over → restart          pod pulled OUT of Service Endpoints
        │                                            │
        ▼                                            ▼
   symptom: RESTARTS count climbs,             symptom: pod stays "1/1 Running" but
   status cycles Running→CrashLoopBackOff       READY shows 0/1 — traffic silently stops

   readinessGates extend this further: a pod can define EXTRA custom conditions
   (beyond the readinessProbe) that ALL must be True before it counts as Ready.</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Probe</th><th>On failure</th><th>Visible symptom</th><th>Fix target</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>livenessProbe</td><td>container killed &amp; restarted</td><td>RESTARTS climbing, CrashLoopBackOff</td><td>probe path/port/timing, or the app's health endpoint</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>readinessProbe</td><td>container left running</td><td>READY 0/1, empty Service endpoints, no restart</td><td>probe path/port, or app startup dependencies</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>startupProbe</td><td>blocks the other two probes</td><td>slow-starting apps not liveness-killed prematurely</td><td>set generous <code>failureThreshold × periodSeconds</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> If RESTARTS is climbing, suspect livenessProbe. If RESTARTS is 0 but the Service isn't reaching the pod, suspect readinessProbe — check <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> for an empty list. A <code>startupProbe</code> exists specifically to protect slow boots (like Django running migrations) from a trigger-happy livenessProbe.</div>

                    <h5>④ Ephemeral Debug Containers — A Real Pod Sub-Resource, Not a Spec Edit</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl debug</code> doesn't work by editing the pod's YAML — pod specs are largely immutable after creation. Ephemeral containers are their own dedicated API sub-resource (<code>pods/ephemeralcontainers</code>), injected at runtime into a running pod without a restart.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl debug -it easypj-web --image=busybox --target=web -- sh
        │
        ▼
   PATCH /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods/easypj-web/ephemeralcontainers
        │  (a dedicated sub-resource — NOT a normal pod spec PATCH)
        ▼
   kubelet adds the ephemeral container to the RUNNING pod sandbox
        │  • shares the pod's network namespace (same IP, can curl localhost)
        │  • with --target: shares that container's PID namespace too (sees its processes)
        │  • does NOT restart existing containers
        ▼
   busybox tools now see 'web' container's filesystem view, network stack, and (with
   --target) its running processes — all without ever touching the original image.

   ⚠️ Ephemeral containers CANNOT be removed — they persist until the pod itself dies,
      and they are never restarted if they exit.</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>API surface</th><th>Restarts existing containers?</th><th>Removable?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Editing pod spec</td><td><code>spec.containers</code> (mostly immutable)</td><td>would require delete+recreate</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl debug --target</code></td><td><code>pods/ephemeralcontainers</code> sub-resource</td><td>no — added live, in place</td><td>no — lives until pod deletion</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl debug --copy-to</code></td><td>creates a NEW pod (copy)</td><td>original pod untouched</td><td>delete the copy pod</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> This is why <code>kubectl debug</code> works even on a pod you can't <code>exec</code> into (distroless) or one that keeps crashing (CrashLoop) — it's not modifying the container you care about, it's attaching a fresh, disposable one beside it via a purpose-built API, then riding its shared namespaces to see inside.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ Process Namespace Sharing &amp; Node-Level Debugging</h5>
                    <p><code>shareProcessNamespace: true</code> and <code>kubectl debug node/</code> extend the same idea — shared namespaces — to two more scopes: sibling containers in a pod, and a whole misbehaving node.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   POD-LEVEL: shareProcessNamespace: true (set at pod creation)
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  container "web" (PID 1 inside)              │
   │  container "sidecar" (PID 1 inside, DEFAULT) │ ◀── isolated: sidecar's ps only shows itself
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
             │  WITH shareProcessNamespace: true
             ▼
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  ONE shared PID namespace for the whole pod  │
   │  ps aux (from ANY container) sees ALL procs  │ ◀── sidecar can now signal/inspect web's PID
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   NODE-LEVEL: kubectl debug node/worker01 -it --image=busybox
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  privileged pod scheduled ON that node       │
   │  host filesystem mounted at /host            │
   │  chroot /host  ──▶ full node access:         │
   │    journalctl -u kubelet, /var/log, df -h    │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Command / Field</th><th>Use case</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Pod (all containers)</td><td><code>shareProcessNamespace: true</code></td><td>a sidecar needs to signal/inspect the main container's process</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod (one container)</td><td><code>kubectl debug --target=&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>ephemeral debug tool inspects one specific container's processes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Whole node</td><td><code>kubectl debug node/&lt;node&gt;</code></td><td>kubelet health, disk pressure, host logs — no SSH needed</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>kubectl debug node/</code> is the escape hatch when a cluster has no SSH access configured (common in exam environments) and the problem is bigger than one pod — disk pressure, a wedged kubelet, or corrupted <code>/var/log</code>. It uses the exact same ephemeral-container plumbing, just aimed at a node instead of a pod.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ OOM Forensics — Reading cgroup Memory Signals Beyond "OOMKilled"</h5>
                    <p>"OOMKilled" tells you memory was the cause, but not always whether it was YOUR container's limit or the whole node running out. Going one level deeper into the cgroup filesystem confirms which.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl describe pod → Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137
        │
        ▼
   Was it MY container's limit, or NODE-WIDE memory pressure?  Check the cgroup:

   cgroup v2 (unified hierarchy, default on current distros/kubelet):
   /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max          ← this container's hard ceiling
   /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events       ← "oom_kill 1" confirms IT was killed
   /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.current      ← usage at time of read

   cgroup v1 (older distros/kernels — different filenames, same idea):
   .../memory/memory.limit_in_bytes   ← hard ceiling      .../memory.usage_in_bytes ← usage
   .../memory/memory.failcnt          ← times the limit was hit (roughly equivalent to oom_kill)

   node-wide signal (kubelet's OWN eviction, different from a container OOM):
   kubectl describe node worker01 | grep -A3 Conditions
     MemoryPressure   True   ← kubelet is evicting PODS to protect the node itself
                              (starts with BestEffort pods, lowest priority first)</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Scope</th><th>What it confirms</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Exit code 137 + Reason OOMKilled</td><td>one container</td><td>THIS container hit its own <code>resources.limits.memory</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>memory.events</code> → <code>oom_kill</code></td><td>one container's cgroup</td><td>cgroup-level confirmation of the same event</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Node condition <code>MemoryPressure: True</code></td><td>whole node</td><td>kubelet is evicting pods node-wide — a capacity problem, not one container's limit</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Don't stop at "OOMKilled" — if it keeps happening across DIFFERENT pods on the same node, check node Conditions for <code>MemoryPressure</code>, not just individual container limits. That's a capacity/scheduling problem (add nodes, tighten requests) rather than a single easypj-web limits fix.</div>

                    <h5>⑦ The kubectl logs / exec Call Chain — API Server → Kubelet → CRI → Container Runtime</h5>
                    <p>Neither <code>kubectl logs</code> nor <code>kubectl exec</code> talks to the container runtime directly — <code>kubectl</code> never has a direct network path to a node's container engine. Both commands travel through the API server and the target node's kubelet, which then delegates to the Container Runtime Interface (CRI) to actually reach the container. Knowing this chain explains every "it works from one node but not another" and "exec hangs / times out" mystery.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   $ kubectl exec -it easypj-web -- sh          $ kubectl logs easypj-web -f
        │                                            │
        ▼                                            ▼
   kubectl builds a SPDY/WebSocket           kubectl opens a streaming GET to
   upgrade request to the API server         the API server's /log subresource
        │                                            │
        ▼                                            ▼
   API SERVER: authenticates + authorizes (RBAC on pods/exec or pods/log),
   then PROXIES the streaming connection to the target node's KUBELET
   (not to the pod directly — the apiserver has no route to pod IPs)
        │                                            │
        ▼                                            ▼
   KUBELET (on that node) receives the proxied request on its own
   read-only/HTTPS port, and calls its LOCAL Container Runtime Interface (CRI)
   gRPC endpoint (containerd's or CRI-O's unix socket) —
        │  exec  ──▶ CRI  ExecSync/Exec RPC        │  logs ──▶ kubelet just
        ▼           (attaches to the container's   ▼         tail -f's the on-disk
   containerd/CRI-O  namespace via runc/crun,       log file kubelet already
   shim execs into    streams stdin/stdout/stderr   owns already — CRI is NOT
   the container's    back up through the same      re-invoked for plain logs
   PID/mount/network  chain to your terminal)
   namespaces

   ⚠️ This is why: exec/logs fail cluster-wide if the API server can't reach a
      node's kubelet port (firewall/NetworkPolicy on port 10250); exec hangs if
      the CRI socket or shim is wedged even though the API server + kubelet are
      both healthy; and RBAC denials on 'pods/exec' or 'pods/log' block the
      FIRST hop even before the kubelet or CRI are ever contacted.</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Hop</th><th>Component</th><th>Protocol / mechanism</th><th>Fails if…</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>1</td><td>kubectl → API server</td><td>HTTPS, SPDY/WebSocket upgrade for exec; streaming GET for logs</td><td>kubeconfig/RBAC wrong, or no <code>pods/exec</code> / <code>pods/log</code> permission</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2</td><td>API server → kubelet</td><td>API server proxies to the node's kubelet HTTPS port (10250)</td><td>NetworkPolicy/firewall blocks apiserver→node:10250, or kubelet cert issues</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3</td><td>kubelet → CRI runtime</td><td>gRPC over a local unix socket (<code>containerd.sock</code>/<code>crio.sock</code>)</td><td>containerd/CRI-O service down or CRI socket unresponsive</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>4</td><td>CRI → container</td><td>shim calls <code>runc</code>/<code>crun</code> to enter the container's namespaces</td><td>container's PID namespace gone (already exited) or corrupted overlay filesystem</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>kubectl logs</code> is cheaper than <code>kubectl exec</code> in this chain — for plain (non-<code>-f</code>) logs the kubelet just reads the file it already owns and never calls the CRI at all, which is why <code>logs</code> keeps working even when a container's shim is unhealthy but its log file survives. <code>exec</code> always calls all four hops live, which is why a wedged CRI shim breaks <code>exec</code>/<code>attach</code>/<code>cp</code> but may leave <code>logs</code> unaffected — a genuinely useful diagnostic split when triaging "logs work but exec doesn't."</div>

                    <h5>⑧ The Part 9 Layer Stack — Where Chapter 31 Ends and Chapters 32/33/34 Begin</h5>
                    <p>Every technique in this chapter assumes the <strong>pod's own layer</strong> is where the fault lives — a bad image, a bad probe, a missing ConfigMap. But a pod is only ever a symptom surface: the actual root cause frequently sits one or two layers below, on the node or in the control plane, and the tools you use to <em>observe</em> any of these layers are shared across all of them. Knowing which chapter owns which layer stops you from debugging the wrong thing for twenty minutes on the exam.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Ch31 — POD / CONTAINER LAYER  (this chapter)                       │
   │  Symptom here: CrashLoopBackOff, ImagePullBackOff, OOMKilled,        │
   │  Pending, Running-but-not-Ready, stuck Terminating                   │
   └───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘
                                    │  "the pod says Pending / Unknown /
                                    │   everything on this node is unhealthy"
                                    ▼
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Ch32 — NODE / CONTROL-PLANE LAYER                                   │
   │  Root causes here: kubelet down, disk/memory pressure, NotReady      │
   │  node, control-plane component (apiserver/etcd/scheduler) unhealthy  │
   └───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘
                                    │  "I need PROOF, not just a guess —
                                    │   show me the metric/log/event"
                                    ▼
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Ch33 — OBSERVABILITY LAYER  (the instruments, used at EVERY layer) │
   │  kubectl logs / top / events / metrics-server feed the diagnosis    │
   │  for Ch31 AND Ch32 AND Ch34 — it is not a separate failure domain,  │
   │  it is the toolset that proves what layer you're actually in         │
   └───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘
                                    │  "the pod AND the node are both fine,
                                    │   but traffic still doesn't arrive"
                                    ▼
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Ch34 — SERVICE / NETWORKING LAYER                                   │
   │  Root causes here: no Endpoints, wrong selector, NetworkPolicy       │
   │  deny, DNS resolution failure, kube-proxy/CNI misconfiguration       │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   🧭 THE ROUTING RULE: if `kubectl describe pod` Events name a pod-local
      cause (bad image, bad probe, missing secret) → stay in Ch31. If
      Events point at the NODE ("node not ready", "disk pressure", no
      Events at all and the pod is stuck Pending/Unknown with a healthy
      spec) → jump to Ch32. If the pod is 1/1 Ready but nothing can reach
      it → jump to Ch34. Ch33's tools ride along at every single step.</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>You see…</th><th>It's actually a…</th><th>Go to</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Pod <code>Pending</code>, Events: "Insufficient cpu/memory" or "node(s) had taint"</td><td>Pod-layer scheduling constraint — the pod spec vs. the node's real capacity/taints</td><td>Stay in Ch31 (this chapter's §31.1/§31.2 decision tree)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod <code>Pending</code>/<code>Unknown</code>, NO useful Events, node shows <code>NotReady</code></td><td>Node-layer failure — kubelet crashed, node lost network, disk/memory pressure</td><td>Ch32 (Troubleshooting Nodes &amp; Control Plane)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod <code>1/1 Running</code> and Ready, but the app is slow or returns errors intermittently</td><td>Needs metrics/logs correlation to prove WHERE the slowness is (app, node, network)</td><td>Ch33 (Observability: Logs, Events &amp; Metrics) — the toolkit, not a separate root cause</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod <code>1/1 Ready</code>, Service has no Endpoints or DNS doesn't resolve</td><td>Networking-layer failure — selector mismatch, NetworkPolicy, CoreDNS, kube-proxy/CNI</td><td>Ch34 (Troubleshooting Services &amp; Networking)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the real exam, the biggest time sink isn't lacking a command — it's staying in the wrong chapter's toolkit for too long. If you've run <code>describe</code>, checked Events, checked logs, and the pod spec genuinely looks correct, STOP debugging the pod. Pivot immediately to "is the NODE healthy?" (Ch32) or "can anything reach this pod at all?" (Ch34) — most CKA "impossible" pod tasks are actually one layer removed from where you're looking.</div>

            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Read the pod <strong>STATE</strong> first, then let it route you: <code>Pending</code> (scheduling), <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> (image/secret), <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code> (missing ConfigMap/Secret), <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> (app/config), Running-but-bad (probes/network).</li>
                    <li><strong>THE METHOD</strong>: <code>describe</code> (read Events!) → <code>logs --previous</code> → <code>exec</code> → fix → verify. Skipping Events is the #1 wasted-time mistake on the exam.</li>
                    <li><strong>Exit codes tell the story</strong>: 0 success, 1 app error, 137 OOMKilled (memory), 143 graceful SIGTERM. 137 is a <em>limits</em> problem, not a code bug — never treat it as an application crash.</li>
                    <li><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> is a <strong>symptom</strong> of the kubelet's backoff timer, never the root cause — the cause is always in <code>--previous</code> logs or the describe Events section.</li>
                    <li>For crashed or distroless containers, use <code>logs --previous</code> and <code>kubectl debug</code> ephemeral containers — you cannot <code>exec</code> into a container that has no shell or won't stay up.</li>
                    <li>A failing <strong>readinessProbe</strong> causes silent "no traffic" (pod stays Running, just pulled from Service endpoints); a failing <strong>livenessProbe</strong> causes restart loops. Know which symptom maps to which probe.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl logs</code> reads the on-node log file <em>via the kubelet</em> — it is not talking to a central log store. Central logging (a DaemonSet log agent shipping to Elasticsearch/Loki/CloudWatch) is what survives a dead node.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>Pod status decode</strong> — <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> = wrong image name/tag or no registry access. <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> = container starts then exits (check logs + exit code). <code>Pending</code> = scheduling issue (resources, taints, nodeSelector). <code>Init:Error</code> = init container failed</li>
                        <li><strong><code>kubectl logs</code> shows CURRENT container</strong> — If the container crashed and restarted, use <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> to see the LAST crash output. Without <code>--previous</code> you see the new (maybe empty) logs</li>
                        <li><strong>Multi-container pods need <code>-c</code></strong> — <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code>. Without it, you get an error if there are multiple containers. Use <code>kubectl describe pod</code> to find container names</li>
                        <li><strong>Exit codes matter</strong> — Exit 0 = success (Completed). Exit 1 = application error. Exit 137 = OOMKilled (needs more memory). Exit 139 = segfault. The exam expects you to interpret these</li>
                        <li><strong><code>kubectl debug</code> for distroless images</strong> — <code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox --target=&lt;container&gt;</code> attaches a debug container sharing the process namespace. Use this when the pod has no shell</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

