            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 31 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the pod-debugging mental model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between a pod's <strong>PHASE</strong> and a container's <strong>STATE</strong>, and why do you need both?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>PHASE (<code>Pending</code>/<code>Running</code>/<code>Succeeded</code>/<code>Failed</code>/<code>Unknown</code>) is the pod-level summary. STATE (<code>Waiting</code>/<code>Running</code>/<code>Terminated</code>, each with a reason) is per-container and is where the real diagnosis lives — a pod can show <code>Running</code> while one of its containers is actually <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> underneath.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>Tells you</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Phase</td><td><code>status.phase</code></td><td>Triage colour — is the pod broadly OK?</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Container State</td><td><code>status.containerStatuses[0].state</code></td><td>Exact reason — ErrImagePull, OOMKilled, etc.</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>🧠 ANALOGY: the PHASE is the patient's triage colour (red/yellow/green); the STATE and Events are the chart that tells you what is actually wrong. Always read both — <code>kubectl get pods</code> for phase, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> for container state.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A container exits with code <strong>143</strong>. Decode it — is it a problem?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>143 = 128 + 15 (SIGTERM) — a <strong>graceful shutdown</strong>, e.g. during a rolling update, scale-down, or node drain. It is normal and is <em>not</em> an error by itself.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">The 128+signal rule</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  exit code &gt;= 128  -&gt;  process was killed BY A SIGNAL  -&gt;  signal = code - 128
  137 = 128 + 9  (SIGKILL)  -&gt; OOMKilled, a limits problem
  143 = 128 + 15 (SIGTERM)  -&gt; graceful stop, usually normal
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Don't panic at every non-zero code. 143 during a Deployment rollout or <code>kubectl delete pod</code> is expected. Only investigate 143 if it happens <em>unexpectedly</em> (e.g. no rollout was in progress) — that could mean an external process or the kubelet is killing the pod (liveness probe failure sends SIGTERM too).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>CreateContainerConfigError</code> — what causes it, and how do you fix it for easypj?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The pod references a ConfigMap/Secret (or a specific key inside one) that doesn't exist. For easypj this is almost always <code>easypj-config</code> (DB host) or <code>easypj-secret</code> (SECRET_KEY, DB password) missing or misspelled.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">fix — create the missing objects</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create configmap easypj-config --from-literal=DB_HOST=easypj-db
kubectl create secret generic easypj-secret \
  --from-literal=SECRET_KEY=&lt;value&gt; --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=&lt;value&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Unlike <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> (network/registry problem) this error is purely local config — the object simply isn't there, or a key referenced under <code>envFrom</code>/<code>valueFrom.configMapKeyRef</code> doesn't match. <code>kubectl describe pod</code> Events names the exact missing object/key.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is <code>Running</code> but the Service sends it <strong>no traffic</strong>. What's the single most likely cause?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A failing <strong>readinessProbe</strong>. The container stays up and Running, but Kubernetes pulls it out of the Service's Endpoints because it isn't marked Ready — traffic silently stops flowing, with no restart and no obvious error in <code>kubectl get pods</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Probe fails</th><th>Container action</th><th>Traffic effect</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>readinessProbe</td><td>stays running, not killed</td><td>removed from Service endpoints — silent</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>livenessProbe</td><td>killed &amp; restarted</td><td>restart loop, visible in RESTARTS column</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the sneakiest failure mode because <code>kubectl get pods</code> looks fine (<code>1/1 Running</code> can mask a <code>0/1</code> READY column if you don't look closely). Always check the READY column and <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — an empty endpoints list confirms it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod crashes every ~30 seconds after running fine each cycle. What specific feature is the likely culprit?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A failing <strong>livenessProbe</strong> killing the container on a fixed interval. Check the probe's <code>path</code>/<code>port</code>/<code>initialDelaySeconds</code>/<code>periodSeconds</code> — a common mistake is setting <code>initialDelaySeconds</code> too low for a slow-starting Django app, so the probe starts failing (and killing the container) before the app is even ready.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">loosen the probe</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">livenessProbe:
  httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 8000 }
  initialDelaySeconds: 20   <span class="comment"># give Django/Gunicorn time to boot</span>
  periodSeconds: 10
  failureThreshold: 3</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The regular cadence (30s, 60s, …) is the tell — it matches the probe's <code>periodSeconds × failureThreshold</code>, not the app's actual crash behaviour. Compare this to <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>'s exponential 10/20/40/80/160/300s pattern, which comes from the kubelet's own restart backoff, not a probe.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where do container logs actually come from when you run <code>kubectl logs</code>, and why do they disappear if the node is deleted?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs</code> asks the <strong>kubelet</strong> on that node to serve the container's stdout/stderr, which the kubelet has been writing to a file under <code>/var/log/containers/</code> / <code>/var/log/pods/</code>. It does <em>not</em> read from any central store.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Where kubectl logs actually reads from</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  kubectl logs pod  --&gt;  API Server  --&gt;  kubelet (on that node)  --&gt;  /var/log/containers/*.log
                                                                              |
                                                    node deleted -------------+  log file GONE
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>If the node is deleted (scaled down, replaced, crashed), the on-disk log file goes with it — <code>kubectl logs</code> for that pod is gone forever. This is exactly why production clusters run a DaemonSet log agent (fluentd/filebeat/fluent-bit) that ships every node's logs to a central store (Elasticsearch/Loki/CloudWatch) <em>before</em> the node can disappear.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The whole <strong>node</strong> looks unhealthy, not just one pod. How do you get a debug shell on it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl debug node/&lt;node-name&gt; -it --image=busybox</code> — this schedules a privileged pod on that node with the host filesystem mounted at <code>/host</code>, so you can inspect kubelet logs, <code>/var/log</code>, and disk pressure without SSH access.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node-level debug</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl debug node/worker01 -it --image=busybox
<span class="comment"># inside the debug pod:</span>
chroot /host                        <span class="comment"># full node filesystem access</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -n 100        <span class="comment"># recent kubelet logs</span>
df -h                                <span class="comment"># disk pressure check</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the node-scoped sibling of the pod-scoped <code>kubectl debug &lt;pod&gt; --target=&lt;container&gt;</code>. Both use the same ephemeral-container mechanism, just targeting a node instead of a pod — invaluable when the cluster has no SSH access configured (a common exam-environment constraint).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>shareProcessNamespace: true</code>, and how does it pair with <code>kubectl debug --target</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>shareProcessNamespace: true</code> on a pod spec lets every container in that pod see every other container's processes (a single shared PID namespace). <code>kubectl debug --target=&lt;container&gt;</code> uses the same mechanism at runtime — the ephemeral debug container joins the target container's process namespace so tools like <code>ps</code>, <code>strace</code>, and <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/</code> can see and inspect it.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Set where</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>shareProcessNamespace: true</code></td><td>pod spec, at creation</td><td>all containers in the pod share one PID namespace</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kubectl debug --target=X</code></td><td>runtime, ephemeral</td><td>debug container joins container X's PID namespace only</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Without either mechanism, each container is isolated — <code>ps aux</code> inside one container shows only its own process (PID 1 = the app). With <code>--target</code>, an ephemeral busybox container can run <code>ps aux</code> and see the easypj Gunicorn workers, letting you inspect a distroless app's process tree without ever modifying its image.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is <code>Pending</code> because of an <strong>unbound PVC</strong>. What's the fix path?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Events show <code>"persistentvolumeclaim ... not found"</code> or the PVC itself stays <code>Pending</code>. Check the PVC's own status, then its StorageClass, then whether a matching PV exists (for static provisioning) or a provisioner is running (for dynamic).</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose the PVC chain</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pvc -n easypj                    <span class="comment"># is the PVC itself Bound or Pending?</span>
kubectl describe pvc easypj-data -n easypj   <span class="comment"># Events: no PV matches / no provisioner</span>
kubectl get storageclass                      <span class="comment"># does the referenced StorageClass exist?</span>
kubectl get pv                                <span class="comment"># any Available PV for static binding?</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A pod referencing a PVC that doesn't exist yet, or a PVC that can't bind (wrong StorageClass name, no provisioner installed, insufficient capacity on any PV), leaves the pod <code>Pending</code> forever — describe the <em>PVC</em>, not just the pod, to see the real reason.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the fastest one-line command to jump straight to a pod's Events without scrolling through the whole <code>describe</code> output?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe pod X | sed -n '/Events/,$p'</code> — prints everything from the line containing "Events" to the end of the output, skipping straight past Labels/Annotations/Conditions/Volumes.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">speed tip</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe pod easypj-web | sed -n '/Events/,$p'</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On the timed CKA exam, every second counts — <code>describe</code> output can be 60+ lines, and Events is always last. This one-liner (or <code>| tail -20</code> as a rougher equivalent) gets you to the answer immediately instead of scrolling.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

