                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 31 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Fast pod &amp; container debugging recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> state. Walk through the debugging process.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check pod status</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check pod status</span>
kubectl get pod crash-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  STATUS            RESTARTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># crash-pod  0/1    CrashLoopBackOff  5         3m</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check PREVIOUS container logs</span>
kubectl logs crash-pod --previous
<span class="comment"># Error: connection refused to database at db:5432</span>
<span class="comment"># ← App started but couldn't connect to DB</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check current logs (may be empty if crash is instant)</span>
kubectl logs crash-pod
<span class="comment"># (empty — container crashed before logging)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check describe for events and exit code</span>
kubectl describe pod crash-pod
<span class="comment"># Last State:  Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#   Exit Code: 1          ← App error (non-zero exit)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Reason:    Error</span>
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Back-off restarting failed container</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Decode exit code</span>
<span class="comment"># 0   = Success (Completed)</span>
<span class="comment"># 1   = Generic app error</span>
<span class="comment"># 137 = OOMKilled (need more memory)</span>
<span class="comment"># 139 = Segfault</span>
<span class="comment"># 143 = SIGTERM (graceful termination)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Fix based on cause</span>
<span class="comment"># Exit 1 → Fix app config, env vars, dependencies</span>
<span class="comment"># Exit 137 → Increase memory limits</span>
<span class="comment"># Exit 139 → Fix application bug</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CrashLoopBackOff = container starts, crashes, Kubernetes restarts it with exponential backoff. Key: use <code>--previous</code> to see the LAST crash's logs (current container may not have logged anything yet). Exit codes tell the story: 1 = app error, 137 = OOM (out of memory), 139 = segfault. On the CKA, always check describe for events AND exit code, then logs with <code>--previous</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is stuck in <code>Pending</code> state. What are the possible causes and how do you diagnose?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">describe the pod — check events</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Describe the pod — check Events</span>
kubectl describe pod pending-pod | tail -20
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment"># Warning  FailedScheduling  ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 1: Insufficient resources</span>
<span class="comment"># "0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu"</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Lower resource requests OR add nodes</span>
kubectl get pod pending-pod -o yaml | grep -A5 resources

<span class="comment"># Cause 2: NodeSelector doesn't match</span>
<span class="comment"># "0/3 nodes are available: 3 node(s) didn't match Pod's node selector"</span>
kubectl get pod pending-pod -o yaml | grep -A3 nodeSelector
kubectl get nodes --show-labels | grep &lt;label&gt;
<span class="comment"># Fix: Add label to node OR fix nodeSelector</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 3: Taints with no toleration</span>
<span class="comment"># "0/3 nodes are available: 3 node(s) had taint {key: NoSchedule}"</span>
kubectl describe nodes | grep Taints
<span class="comment"># Fix: Add tolerations OR remove taint</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 4: PVC not bound</span>
<span class="comment"># "persistentvolumeclaim "data-pvc" not found" OR PVC Pending</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># Fix: Create PV or fix StorageClass</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 5: SA doesn't exist</span>
<span class="comment"># "serviceaccount "missing-sa" not found"</span>
kubectl get sa -n &lt;namespace&gt;
<span class="comment"># Fix: Create the ServiceAccount</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick diagnosis:</span>
kubectl get events --field-selector type=Warning \
--sort-by=.lastTimestamp | head -10</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pending = scheduler can't place the pod. Five main causes: insufficient resources, nodeSelector mismatch, taints without toleration, unbound PVC, missing ServiceAccount. On the CKA, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> Events section ALWAYS explains why. The FailedScheduling message lists: how many nodes exist, why each was rejected. Read this message carefully — it's your diagnosis.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod shows <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. How do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">describe to find the exact error</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Describe to find the exact error</span>
kubectl describe pod img-pod | grep -A5 "Events"
<span class="comment"># Warning  Failed  ...  Failed to pull image "ngnix:latest"</span>
<span class="comment"># Warning  Failed  ...  rpc error: pull access denied</span>

<span class="comment"># Diagnosis by error message:</span>
<span class="comment"># "manifest unknown"     → Wrong tag (e.g., ngnix instead of nginx)</span>
<span class="comment"># "pull access denied"   → Private registry, no credentials</span>
<span class="comment"># "no such host"         → Wrong registry URL</span>
<span class="comment"># "i/o timeout"          → Network issue</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 1: Wrong image name/tag</span>
kubectl set image pod/img-pod app=nginx:1.25
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
kubectl edit pod img-pod  <span class="comment"># Fix image name</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 2: Private registry credentials</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
--docker-server=registry.example.com \
--docker-username=user \
--docker-password=pass + then add imagePullSecrets

<span class="comment"># Fix 3: Check if image exists</span>
<span class="comment"># From a node with network:</span>
crictl pull nginx:1.25
<span class="comment"># If this works, the image name is correct</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick fix: delete and recreate with correct image</span>
kubectl delete pod img-pod
kubectl run img-pod --image=nginx:1.25</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ImagePullBackOff = can't download the container image. Three main causes: (1) typo in image name/tag (most common on CKA), (2) private registry without credentials, (3) network issues. On the CKA, the fix is usually a simple typo: <code>ngnix</code> → <code>nginx</code>, or wrong tag. <code>kubectl set image</code> is the fastest fix. Check the Events section for the exact error message — it tells you exactly what failed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you use <code>kubectl debug</code> to troubleshoot a pod with a distroless image (no shell)?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">problem: pod has no shell (distroless/scratch image)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Problem: Pod has no shell (distroless/scratch image)</span>
kubectl exec -it app-pod -- sh
<span class="comment"># OCI runtime exec failed: exec failed: unable to start</span>
<span class="comment"># container process: exec: "sh": executable file not found</span>

<span class="comment"># Solution: Ephemeral debug container</span>
kubectl debug -it app-pod \
--image=busybox \
--target=app-container
<span class="comment"># Attaches a busybox container that shares:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Process namespace (can see app processes)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Network namespace (same IP, ports)</span>

<span class="comment"># Inside the debug container:</span>
ps aux  <span class="comment"># See app processes</span>
netstat -tlnp  <span class="comment"># See listening ports</span>
wget -O- localhost:8080  <span class="comment"># Test app endpoint</span>
cat /proc/1/environ | tr '\0' '\n'  <span class="comment"># See env vars</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: Copy pod for debugging</span>
kubectl debug app-pod -it \
--image=busybox \
--copy-to=debug-pod \
--share-processes
<span class="comment"># Creates a copy of the pod with debug container added</span>

<span class="comment"># Debug a node:</span>
kubectl debug node/worker-1 -it --image=ubuntu
<span class="comment"># Creates a privileged pod on the node</span>
<span class="comment"># Root filesystem at /host</span>
chroot /host  <span class="comment"># Full node access</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl debug</code> adds an ephemeral container to a running pod without restarting it. Use <code>--target=</code> to share the process namespace with a specific container. On the CKA, this is the way to debug distroless images. The debug container sees the app's processes and network. <code>kubectl debug node/</code> creates a privileged pod on a node for node-level debugging. The <code>--copy-to</code> option creates a copy, preserving the original pod.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check why a container was OOMKilled and fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">identify oomkilled</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Identify OOMKilled:</span>
kubectl describe pod oom-pod
<span class="comment"># Containers:</span>
<span class="comment">#   app:</span>
<span class="comment">#     Last State:  Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#       Reason:    OOMKilled    ← Out of Memory!</span>
<span class="comment">#       Exit Code: 137          ← 128 + 9 (SIGKILL)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current memory usage:</span>
kubectl top pod oom-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME      CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># oom-pod   50m         490Mi  ← Close to limit!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check memory limits:</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -o yaml | grep -A3 limits
<span class="comment"># limits:</span>
<span class="comment">#   memory: 512Mi  ← 490Mi usage / 512Mi limit = 95%!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Increase memory limit</span>
kubectl patch pod oom-pod -p \
'{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"app","resources":{"limits":{"memory":"1Gi"}}}]}}'
<span class="comment"># Error: Pod spec changes not allowed (immutable field)</span>

<span class="comment"># Must delete and recreate:</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -o yaml > oom-fix.yaml
<span class="comment"># Edit: Change memory limit from 512Mi to 1Gi</span>
vi oom-fix.yaml
kubectl delete pod oom-pod
kubectl apply -f oom-fix.yaml

<span class="comment"># For Deployments (easier):</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/web \
--limits=memory=1Gi --requests=memory=512Mi
<span class="comment"># Triggers rolling update with new limits</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>OOMKilled = container exceeded its memory limit. Exit code 137 = 128 + 9 (SIGKILL). On the CKA: (1) identify via <code>describe</code> (Reason: OOMKilled), (2) check current usage with <code>kubectl top</code>, (3) compare to limits, (4) increase memory limit. Pod resource specs are immutable — you must delete and recreate. For Deployments, use <code>kubectl set resources</code> which triggers a rolling update. Common CKA trap: confusing memory requests (scheduling) with limits (enforcement).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you get logs from all containers in a multi-container pod?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list containers in the pod</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List containers in the pod:</span>
kubectl get pod multi-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'
<span class="comment"># app sidecar</span>

<span class="comment"># Logs from specific container:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod -c app
kubectl logs multi-pod -c sidecar

<span class="comment"># Logs from ALL containers:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod --all-containers=true

<span class="comment"># Stream ALL containers:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod --all-containers=true -f

<span class="comment"># Previous crash logs for specific container:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod -c app --previous

<span class="comment"># Last N lines from all containers:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod --all-containers --tail=50

<span class="comment"># Logs from all pods matching a label:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web --all-containers
<span class="comment"># (max 5 pods by default)</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web --all-containers --max-log-requests=20

<span class="comment"># Init container logs:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod -c init-container-name

<span class="comment"># Check which container is failing:</span>
kubectl describe pod multi-pod | grep -A10 "Container.*:"</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Multi-container pods require <code>-c</code> to specify which container's logs to view. <code>--all-containers=true</code> shows logs from every container. On the CKA, if you see "a]]container name must be specified" error, the pod has multiple containers — use <code>-c</code>. Init container logs: same syntax with <code>-c init-name</code>. <code>-l</code> flag retrieves logs from multiple pods by label selector. Default limit is 5 concurrent pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is running but the application inside isn't responding. How do you debug?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pod shows running but app doesn't work</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pod shows Running but app doesn't work:</span>
kubectl get pod app-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME      READY  STATUS   RESTARTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># app-pod   1/1    Running  0         10m</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check app logs</span>
kubectl logs app-pod
<span class="comment"># [ERROR] Failed to bind to port 8080: address already in use</span>
<span class="comment"># ← App started but errored internally</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Exec into the pod</span>
kubectl exec -it app-pod -- sh
<span class="comment"># Inside:</span>
curl localhost:8080  <span class="comment"># Test from inside</span>
netstat -tlnp        <span class="comment"># Check listening ports</span>
ps aux               <span class="comment"># Verify process is running</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check readiness probe</span>
kubectl describe pod app-pod | grep -A5 Readiness
<span class="comment"># Readiness probe failed: connection refused</span>
<span class="comment"># READY shows 0/1 → Service won't send traffic</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check liveness probe</span>
kubectl describe pod app-pod | grep -A5 Liveness
<span class="comment"># If liveness fails → pod gets restarted</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check environment variables</span>
kubectl exec app-pod -- env | sort
<span class="comment"># Missing DB_HOST? Wrong PORT?</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check if service endpoints are populated</span>
kubectl get endpoints service-name
<span class="comment"># If empty → selector doesn't match pod labels</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix common issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># Wrong port in probe → kubectl edit pod</span>
<span class="comment"># Missing env var → fix ConfigMap/Secret</span>
<span class="comment"># App crash silent → check app-level config</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Running ≠ working. A pod can be Running but the app inside may be broken. On the CKA: check logs first (app-level errors), then exec in to test connectivity, then check probes (readiness/liveness), then verify environment variables. If READY shows 0/1, the readiness probe is failing — the Service won't send traffic to this pod. Common CKA scenario: probe targets wrong port or path.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you find all non-running pods across all namespaces?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">field selector (fastest)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: Field selector (fastest)</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE   NAME          READY  STATUS            RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># default     broken-pod    0/1    CrashLoopBackOff  8</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-system missing-img   0/1    ImagePullBackOff  0</span>
<span class="comment"># prod        pending-pod   0/1    Pending           0</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Include Succeeded (completed jobs)</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running,status.phase!=Succeeded

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Custom columns for more detail</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running \
-o custom-columns=\
'NS:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,STATUS:.status.phase,NODE:.spec.nodeName'

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Sort by restart count (find crashers)</span>
kubectl get pods -A --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'

<span class="comment"># Method 5: Events for all failing pods</span>
kubectl get events -A --field-selector type=Warning \
--sort-by=.lastTimestamp | head -20

<span class="comment"># Quick health check:</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running | grep -v Completed</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>--field-selector=status.phase!=Running</code> finds all non-running pods instantly. On the CKA, start troubleshooting by finding what's broken. <code>-A</code> for all namespaces. Sort by restart count to find pods that keep crashing. The events command with <code>type=Warning</code> shows what's going wrong cluster-wide. Quick grep: <code>| grep -v Running | grep -v Completed</code> filters to only problem pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check why a container exited with a specific exit code?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Exit Code</th>
                                        <th>Meaning</th>
                                        <th>Common Cause</th>
                                        <th>Fix</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>0</td>
                                        <td>Success</td>
                                        <td>Process completed normally</td>
                                        <td>Expected for Jobs/init containers</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>1</td>
                                        <td>Generic error</td>
                                        <td>App crash, missing config</td>
                                        <td>Check logs</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>126</td>
                                        <td>Cannot execute</td>
                                        <td>Permission denied</td>
                                        <td>Check command/entrypoint</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>127</td>
                                        <td>Command not found</td>
                                        <td>Wrong command in spec</td>
                                        <td>Fix command/args</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>137</td>
                                        <td>SIGKILL (128+9)</td>
                                        <td>OOMKilled or manual kill</td>
                                        <td>Increase memory limits</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>139</td>
                                        <td>SIGSEGV (128+11)</td>
                                        <td>Segmentation fault</td>
                                        <td>Fix app bug</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>143</td>
                                        <td>SIGTERM (128+15)</td>
                                        <td>Graceful termination</td>
                                        <td>Normal during rollouts</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">get exit code</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Get exit code:</span>
kubectl describe pod crash-pod | grep "Exit Code"
<span class="comment">#   Exit Code:    137</span>

<span class="comment"># OR via jsonpath:</span>
kubectl get pod crash-pod -o jsonpath=\
'{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.exitCode}'
<span class="comment"># 137</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Exit codes above 128 mean the process was killed by a signal (exit code = 128 + signal number). On the CKA: 137 = OOMKilled (SIGKILL), 143 = graceful shutdown (SIGTERM), 1 = app error. Use <code>kubectl describe</code> or jsonpath to get the exit code. Combined with <code>--previous</code> logs, the exit code tells you exactly what happened and guides the fix.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod's init container is failing. How do you diagnose and fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">identify: pod status shows init:error or…</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Identify: Pod status shows Init:Error or Init:CrashLoopBackOff</span>
kubectl get pod init-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  STATUS         RESTARTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># init-pod   0/1    Init:Error     3         2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Find init container name</span>
kubectl describe pod init-pod | grep -A10 "Init Containers"
<span class="comment"># Init Containers:</span>
<span class="comment">#   wait-for-db:</span>
<span class="comment">#     State:    Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#     Reason:   Error</span>
<span class="comment">#     Exit Code: 1</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check init container logs</span>
kubectl logs init-pod -c wait-for-db
<span class="comment"># Connection refused: db-service:5432</span>
<span class="comment"># ← The database isn't ready yet</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Common init container issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Waiting for a service that doesn't exist</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong service name or port</span>
<span class="comment"># - DNS not resolving (service in different namespace)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing ConfigMap/Secret referenced by init container</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Fix</span>
<span class="comment"># Check if the dependency service exists:</span>
kubectl get svc db-service
<span class="comment"># If missing: create it</span>
<span class="comment"># If wrong namespace: use FQDN (db-service.other-ns.svc.cluster.local)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify init container succeeds</span>
kubectl get pod init-pod -w
<span class="comment"># Init:0/1 → PodInitializing → Running ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Remember: Init containers run SEQUENTIALLY</span>
<span class="comment"># If init-1 fails, init-2 never starts</span>
<span class="comment"># Main containers don't start until ALL inits succeed</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Init containers run before main containers and must complete successfully. Status shows <code>Init:Error</code> or <code>Init:CrashLoopBackOff</code>. Use <code>-c init-container-name</code> to get init container logs. On the CKA, init containers typically wait for a dependency (database, config). If the dependency doesn't exist, the init container loops. Fix the dependency → init succeeds → main containers start. Init containers run sequentially — if one fails, the rest don't execute.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            
