            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 31.2 — ImagePullBackOff &amp; Private Registry</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Scenario</h5>
                        <p>The easypj-web Deployment references <code>easypj:1.0</code> but was mistyped as <code>easypj:latset</code> and pushed to a private registry <code>registry.example.com</code> without credentials. Diagnose both problems and fix them.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Deploy: <code>kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=registry.example.com/easypj:latset -n easypj</code></li>
                            <li>Confirm the pod status and read the exact Events error.</li>
                            <li>Fix the image tag AND add the missing registry credentials.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> A typo'd tag and a missing <code>imagePullSecrets</code> often show up together in real incidents — describe Events will show either "manifest unknown" (bad tag) or "pull access denied" / "no basic auth credentials" (missing secret), sometimes both in sequence as you fix one and hit the next.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: confirm the failure</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-xxxx   0/1   ImagePullBackOff   0   2m</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj | sed -n '/Events/,$p'
<span class="comment"># Warning  Failed  ...  Failed to pull image "registry.example.com/easypj:latset":
#   rpc error: manifest unknown          <- bad tag
# Warning  Failed  ...  pull access denied for registry.example.com/easypj,
#   repository does not exist or may require 'docker login': denied  <- missing secret</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: create the registry credential</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred -n easypj \
  --docker-server=registry.example.com \
  --docker-username=&lt;user&gt; --docker-password=&lt;pass&gt;

<span class="comment"># Step 3: fix the tag AND attach the secret</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web \
  easypj=registry.example.com/easypj:1.0 -n easypj
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -n easypj -p \
  '{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"imagePullSecrets":[{"name":"regcred"}]}}}}'

<span class="comment"># Step 4: verify</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
kubectl get pods -n easypj   <span class="comment"># 1/1 Running</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 31.3 — OOMKilled Under Load</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Scenario</h5>
                        <p>easypj-web is deployed with <code>resources.limits.memory: 128Mi</code>, but Gunicorn with 4 workers under real traffic needs closer to 400Mi. Reproduce the OOMKilled crash, diagnose the exit code, and fix it without over-provisioning.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Deploy easypj-web with <code>limits.memory: 128Mi</code> and generate load (or simulate with a memory-hungry busybox: <code>kubectl run oomtest --image=polinux/stress --limits=memory=128Mi -- stress --vm 1 --vm-bytes 200M</code>).</li>
                            <li>Watch the pod get OOMKilled and check the exit code + Reason via <code>describe</code>.</li>
                            <li>Right-size the memory limit and requests based on what you now know the app needs.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>kubectl top pod</code> shows current usage vs. limit; <code>describe</code>'s <code>Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137</code> confirms it's a memory ceiling, not an app bug — the fix is a resources edit, never a code change.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: reproduce and observe</span>
kubectl run oomtest --image=polinux/stress --restart=Never \
  --limits=memory=128Mi -- stress --vm 1 --vm-bytes 200M --vm-hang 0

<span class="comment"># Step 2: diagnose</span>
kubectl describe pod oomtest | grep -A4 "Last State"
<span class="comment"># Last State:  Terminated
#   Reason:    OOMKilled
#   Exit Code: 137          <- 128 + 9 (SIGKILL), memory limit hit</span>
kubectl top pod oomtest   <span class="comment"># (if it's still up momentarily) usage approaching limit</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: right-size — for easypj-web specifically</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/easypj-web -n easypj \
  --requests=memory=256Mi,cpu=200m \
  --limits=memory=512Mi,cpu=500m
<span class="comment"># Triggers a rolling update with the new resource envelope</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: verify no more OOMKilled restarts</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj -w
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj | grep -A2 "Last State"</code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 31.4 — Silent Readiness Failure (No Traffic, No Restart)</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Scenario</h5>
                        <p>easypj-web is <code>1/1 Running</code> with 0 restarts, but the Service <code>easypj-svc</code> returns connection errors. Nothing in <code>kubectl logs</code> looks wrong. Find the silent cause.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Deploy easypj-web with a readinessProbe pointed at the wrong path (<code>/healthz</code> when the app only serves <code>/health/</code>).</li>
                            <li>Confirm the pod shows <code>Running</code> but check the READY column and the Service's endpoints.</li>
                            <li>Fix the probe path and confirm traffic flows again.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>kubectl get pods</code>'s READY column (e.g. <code>0/1</code>) and <code>kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc</code> (empty) are the two fastest tells — <code>kubectl logs</code> won't show a readinessProbe failure because the app itself never errors; the probe target is simply wrong.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: confirm the symptom</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME              READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
# easypj-web-xxxx   0/1     Running   0          5m   <- READY 0/1, not restarting!</span>

kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME         ENDPOINTS   AGE
# easypj-svc   &lt;none&gt;      5m         <- empty! Service has nowhere to send traffic</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: confirm it's the readinessProbe</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj | grep -A5 Readiness
<span class="comment"># Readiness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 404
# (probe hits /healthz, app only serves /health/)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: fix the probe path</span>
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -n easypj --type=json -p \
  '[{"op":"replace","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/readinessProbe/httpGet/path","value":"/health/"}]'

<span class="comment"># Step 4: verify</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj            <span class="comment"># READY 1/1</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc -n easypj   <span class="comment"># now lists the pod IP:port</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 31.5 — Debugging a Distroless Image With Ephemeral Containers</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Scenario</h5>
                        <p>An easypj sidecar runs a distroless Go binary with no shell. It's Running but you suspect it isn't listening on the port the docs claim. You cannot <code>exec</code> in — there's no <code>/bin/sh</code>. Use an ephemeral debug container instead.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Try <code>kubectl exec -it distroless-pod -- sh</code> and observe the failure.</li>
                            <li>Attach a <code>kubectl debug</code> ephemeral container targeting the app container.</li>
                            <li>From inside the debug container, confirm which port the app is actually listening on.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox --target=&lt;container&gt;</code> attaches a container that shares the target's network and (with <code>--target</code>) process namespace — <code>netstat -tlnp</code> or <code>ss -tlnp</code> from inside it reveals the real listening port without ever touching the original image.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: exec fails — no shell in the image</span>
kubectl exec -it distroless-pod -- sh
<span class="comment"># OCI runtime exec failed: exec: "sh": executable file not found in $PATH</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: attach an ephemeral debug container instead</span>
kubectl debug -it distroless-pod --image=busybox:1.36 --target=app -- sh

<span class="comment"># Step 3: inside the debug container — shares app's network namespace</span>
netstat -tlnp
<span class="comment"># tcp   0   0 :::9090   :::*   LISTEN   1/app     <- actually 9090, not 8080!</span>
wget -qO- localhost:9090/health
<span class="comment"># {"status":"ok"}   <- confirms the real port</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: fix downstream — correct the Service/probe port to 9090</span>
kubectl patch svc distroless-svc -p \
  '{"spec":{"ports":[{"port":80,"targetPort":9090}]}}'</code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>


