│  ├─ Pending                  ► describe Events: no resources? taint?       │
│  │                             unbound PVC? nodeSelector mismatch?          │
│  ├─ ImagePullBackOff         ► describe: wrong image:tag / missing         │
│  │                             imagePullSecret                             │
│  ├─ CreateContainerConfigErr ► missing ConfigMap/Secret/key                 │
│  ├─ CrashLoopBackOff         ► logs --previous: app error? OOM(137)?       │
│  │                             bad config? DB refused?                     │
│  ├─ Running but bad          ► logs + exec -it + check readiness/          │
│  │                             liveness probes + Service endpoints         │
│  └─ Completed/Error          ► was it a Job? check the exit code           │
│                                                                            │
│  🔁 ALWAYS: describe FIRST (read Events) → logs --previous → exec →       │
│             fix → verify. Never skip Events. Never guess.                  │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>
                <p class="vs-prose">Debugging is a <strong>fixed order, not a guess</strong>: the pod's STATE and the <code>describe</code> Events section tell you exactly where to look, and exit codes (137 OOM, 143 SIGTERM) finish the diagnosis. Read the pod STATE, branch to the matching cause — Pending routes to scheduling, ImagePullBackOff to image/secret problems, CreateContainerConfigError to a missing ConfigMap/Secret, CrashLoopBackOff to app/config issues via <code>--previous</code> logs, and Running-but-bad to probes or networking — then <strong>always</strong> run <code>describe</code> and read Events before moving on to <code>logs</code> → <code>exec</code> → fix → verify. Skipping the Events section is the single most expensive mistake on the CKA exam: it almost always names the exact cause (FailedScheduling, Failed to pull image, OOMKilled, FailedMount) in plain English, so read it before you theorize.</p>
            </div>
        </article><!-- end ch31 -->

