            <section class="section-block" id="s32-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.1 Node Not Ready — Diagnosis</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>A node in <code>NotReady</code> state means the kubelet has stopped reporting to the API server. Your job is to find out <strong>why</strong> and fix it.</p>

                <h4>Common NotReady Causes</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛑</div>
                        <h5>kubelet Stopped</h5>
                        <p>The most common cause. kubelet crashed or was stopped. Fix: <code>systemctl start kubelet</code> and <code>systemctl enable kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔌</div>
                        <h5>CNI Plugin Missing</h5>
                        <p>No network plugin installed or CNI binary missing. Pods can't get IPs. Fix: Install/reinstall the CNI plugin (Calico, Flannel, etc.).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>Swap Enabled</h5>
                        <p>kubelet refuses to start if swap is on (default). Fix: <code>swapoff -a</code> and remove swap entry from <code>/etc/fstab</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>containerd Down</h5>
                        <p>Container runtime crashed. No containers can be created. Fix: <code>systemctl restart containerd</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Systematic Node NotReady Fix</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>SSH to Node</h5>
                        <p><code>ssh worker01</code> — You can't fix node issues remotely via kubectl. You must be on the node.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Check kubelet</h5>
                        <p><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> — Is it active? If dead: <code>systemctl start kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Read Logs</h5>
                        <p><code>journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50</code> — Error messages reveal the root cause.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Fix &amp; Restart</h5>
                        <p>Fix the underlying issue (config, certs, swap, containerd) then <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Verify</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get nodes</code> — Wait for node to transition from NotReady to Ready (may take 30-60s).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔍 Node Troubleshooting Flowchart</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  $ kubectl get nodes                                                         │
│  NAME           STATUS     ROLES           AGE   VERSION                     │
│  controlplane   Ready      control-plane   30d   v1.31.0                    │
│  worker01       NotReady   &lt;none&gt;          30d   v1.31.0  ◄── PROBLEM      │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ▼                                                                      │
│  $ kubectl describe node worker01                                            │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ├── Conditions Section:                                                │
│       │   ┌──────────────────┬────────┬──────────────────────────────┐       │
│       │   │ Condition        │ Status │ What It Means                │       │
│       │   ├──────────────────┼────────┼──────────────────────────────┤       │
│       │   │ Ready            │ False  │ kubelet not reporting        │       │
│       │   │ MemoryPressure   │ True   │ Node running out of RAM     │       │
│       │   │ DiskPressure     │ True   │ Node running out of disk    │       │
│       │   │ PIDPressure      │ True   │ Too many processes          │       │
│       │   │ NetworkUnavail.  │ True   │ CNI plugin not working      │       │
│       │   └──────────────────┴────────┴──────────────────────────────┘       │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ▼                                                                      │
│  SSH into the node: $ ssh worker01                                           │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ├── $ systemctl status kubelet                                         │
│       │    ├── active (running) → kubelet is up, check network              │
│       │    └── inactive (dead)  → kubelet crashed or was stopped            │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ├── $ journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50                      │
│       │    └── Look for error messages about certificates, config, etc.     │
│       │                                                                      │
│       └── Common Fixes:                                                      │
│            ├── Kubelet stopped   → systemctl start kubelet                   │
│            ├── Kubelet disabled  → systemctl enable --now kubelet            │
│            ├── Container runtime → systemctl restart containerd              │
│            ├── Swap enabled      → swapoff -a                                │
│            └── Wrong config      → check /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml       │
│                                                                              │
│  VERIFY: $ kubectl get nodes   → worker01 should show Ready                 │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                    <h5>⚠️ Node Conditions vs Node Status</h5>
                    <p><strong>Status</strong> (Ready/NotReady) is the overall health. <strong>Conditions</strong> (MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, etc.) tell you <em>why</em> a node is unhealthy. A node is Ready only when ALL pressure conditions are False and kubelet is communicating with the API server.</p>
                    <div class="info-box note">
                        <h5>📖 Three Values, Not Two — <code>True</code> / <code>False</code> / <code>Unknown</code></h5>
                        <p>Every Condition (including <code>Ready</code>) actually has THREE possible <code>status</code> values, not just two, and the difference matters diagnostically: <code>True</code>/<code>False</code> mean the kubelet is alive and actively REPORTING that state (e.g. <code>Ready=False</code> because it explicitly said so). <code>Unknown</code> means something different and more urgent — the kubelet has <strong>stopped posting status entirely</strong> and the node controller can no longer tell whether the node is fine, overloaded, or dead; <code>kubectl describe node</code> shows a message like <code>"Kubelet stopped posting node status"</code> in this case. A <code>Ready=False</code> node is talking to you and telling you what's wrong (pressure, CNI, etc.); a <code>Ready=Unknown</code> node has gone silent — you're debugging a communications blackout (network partition, kubelet crashed hard, or the node is powered off), not a reported problem.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Node Diagnosis Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Get node status overview</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Describe the problematic node</span>
kubectl describe node worker01

<span class="comment"># Key areas to check in the output:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Conditions     → Ready, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure</span>
<span class="comment"># - Capacity       → Total CPU, memory, pods</span>
<span class="comment"># - Allocatable    → Available for pods</span>
<span class="comment"># - Allocated      → Currently used by pods</span>
<span class="comment"># - Events         → Recent node events</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: SSH to node and check kubelet</span>
ssh worker01
systemctl status kubelet
journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -l | tail -50

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check container runtime</span>
systemctl status containerd
crictl ps -a                    <span class="comment"># List all containers on the node</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check disk and memory</span>
df -h                           <span class="comment"># Disk usage</span>
free -m                         <span class="comment"># Memory usage</span>
top                             <span class="comment"># Process and CPU overview</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 No SSH Access? Use <code>kubectl debug node/</code> Instead</h5>
                    <p>Every diagnostic step above assumes <code>ssh worker01</code> works. On a managed cluster (a fleet where SSH keys are locked down, or a bastion-only network) that's often not true — but as long as <code>kubectl</code> can still reach an apiserver, you don't need SSH at all: <code>kubectl debug node/worker01 -it --image=ubuntu</code> creates an auto-named debugging Pod (e.g. <code>node-debugger-worker01-pdx84</code>) ON that node, running in the host's IPC, Network, and PID namespaces, with the node's entire root filesystem mounted at <code>/host</code>.</p>
                    <div class="diagram-container">
                        <div class="diagram-title">⚠️ It Is NOT Privileged By Default — <code>chroot /host</code> Will Fail</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
kubectl debug node/worker01 -it --image=ubuntu
     │
     ▼
 debugging Pod created — but SECURITY CONTEXT IS UNPRIVILEGED by default
     │
     ├─ ls /host, cat /host/var/log/kubelet.log        ← works fine (plain file reads)
     │
     └─ chroot /host                                    ← FAILS — needs superuser access
                                                            this debug pod doesn't have

 FIX — request a privileged pod explicitly:
     kubectl debug node/worker01 -it --image=ubuntu --profile=sysadmin
     (or hand-craft the Pod spec yourself with securityContext.privileged: true)
     → NOW chroot /host, systemctl status kubelet, journalctl -u kubelet all work
</pre>
</div>
                    </div>
                    <p>So the honest workflow is: without <code>--profile=sysadmin</code>, you can still read files under <code>/host</code> directly (e.g. <code>cat /host/var/log/kubelet.log</code>, <code>cat /host/etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml</code>) — genuinely useful for a quick look. But <code>systemctl</code>/<code>journalctl</code> commands and <code>chroot /host</code> need the privileged profile. This only fails entirely if kubelet itself can't schedule a new pod on that node (e.g. it's already NotReady and unreachable, or the kubelet process itself is dead) — at that point SSH (or cloud-provider console access) is genuinely the only way in. Clean up afterward with a normal <code>kubectl delete pod node-debugger-worker01-pdx84 --now</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §32.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s32-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.2 Troubleshooting kubelet</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>kubelet is the <strong>only component that runs as a systemd service</strong> (not a static pod). It's the node agent — if kubelet is down, the node goes NotReady.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>kubelet — The Node Agent Architecture</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────── Worker Node ─────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │                    kubelet (systemd service)                 │        │
│  │                                                             │        │
│  │  📡 Reports to API Server every 10s (node heartbeat)        │        │
│  │  📋 Watches for pod assignments from Scheduler              │        │
│  │  🔄 Manages pod lifecycle via Container Runtime             │        │
│  │  💉 Runs health probes (liveness, readiness, startup)       │        │
│  │  📊 Collects metrics for Metrics Server                     │        │
│  │                                                             │        │
│  │  Config: /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml                       │        │
│  │  Certs:  /var/lib/kubelet/pki/                              │        │
│  │  Logs:   journalctl -u kubelet                              │        │
│  └────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                           │                                              │
│                           ▼                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐                       │
│  │         Container Runtime (containerd)        │                       │
│  │                                              │                       │
│  │  ┌─────┐  ┌─────┐  ┌─────┐  ┌─────┐        │                       │
│  │  │Pod A│  │Pod B│  │Pod C│  │Pod D│        │                       │
│  │  └─────┘  └─────┘  └─────┘  └─────┘        │                       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘                       │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Common kubelet Issues and Fixes</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Problem</th>
                                <th>Symptom</th>
                                <th>Diagnosis</th>
                                <th>Fix</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>kubelet stopped</td>
                                <td>Node goes NotReady</td>
                                <td><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> → inactive</td>
                                <td><code>systemctl start kubelet</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>kubelet disabled</td>
                                <td>Doesn't start after reboot</td>
                                <td><code>systemctl is-enabled kubelet</code> → disabled</td>
                                <td><code>systemctl enable kubelet</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Wrong config</td>
                                <td>kubelet crashes repeatedly</td>
                                <td><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> → config errors</td>
                                <td>Fix <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Certificate expired</td>
                                <td>"x509: certificate has expired"</td>
                                <td><code>openssl x509 -in cert -noout -dates</code></td>
                                <td><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Cannot reach API server</td>
                                <td>"connection refused" in kubelet logs</td>
                                <td>Check network, firewall, API server IP</td>
                                <td>Fix network/firewall or API server address in kubelet config</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Swap enabled</td>
                                <td>kubelet refuses to start</td>
                                <td><code>free -m</code> shows swap</td>
                                <td><code>swapoff -a</code> and remove from <code>/etc/fstab</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">kubelet Troubleshooting — Step by Step</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══ On the problematic node (SSH in first) ═══

<span class="comment"># 1. Check kubelet status</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Look for: active (running) vs inactive (dead) vs failed</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check kubelet logs for errors</span>
journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50
journalctl -u kubelet -f                    <span class="comment"># Follow live</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. If kubelet is stopped, start it</span>
systemctl start kubelet
systemctl enable kubelet                    <span class="comment"># Persist across reboots</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Check kubelet configuration</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
<span class="comment"># Verify: clusterDNS, clusterDomain, authentication/authorization</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Check kubelet certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-current.pem \
  -noout -text | grep -A2 "Validity"

<span class="comment"># 6. Restart kubelet after any config change</span>
systemctl daemon-reload                     <span class="comment"># If service file changed</span>
systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># 7. Verify from control plane</span>
kubectl get nodes                           <span class="comment"># Should show Ready</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h4>⚠️ kubelet Gotchas on the CKA Exam</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Looking for kubelet in <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> — kubelet is NOT a pod</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Check kubelet via <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> on the node itself</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Forgetting <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> after editing service files</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Always run <code>daemon-reload</code> before <code>restart</code> if you modified a systemd unit</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Restarting kubelet without checking WHY it crashed</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Read <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> first to understand the root cause</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §32.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s32-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.3 Troubleshooting Control Plane Components</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Control plane components run as <strong>static pods</strong> managed by kubelet. Their manifests live in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. If a component is broken, check its manifest and logs.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Control Plane Components — Where They Live</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────── Control Plane Node ──────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                          │
│  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/           ← kubelet watches this directory   │
│  ├── kube-apiserver.yaml              ← API Server static pod           │
│  ├── kube-controller-manager.yaml     ← Controller Manager static pod   │
│  ├── kube-scheduler.yaml              ← Scheduler static pod            │
│  └── etcd.yaml                        ← etcd static pod                 │
│                                                                          │
│  How static pods work:                                                   │
│  ┌──────────┐    watches    ┌────────────────────┐                      │
│  │ kubelet  │ ──────────▶  │ /etc/kubernetes/   │                      │
│  │          │    dir for    │    manifests/      │                      │
│  │          │    changes    │                    │                      │
│  └────┬─────┘               └────────────────────┘                      │
│       │ creates/restarts                                                 │
│       ▼                                                                  │
│  ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐                   │
│  │API Server│ │Controller│ │Scheduler │ │  etcd   │                   │
│  │   Pod    │ │Mgr Pod   │ │   Pod    │ │   Pod   │                   │
│  └──────────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘                   │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚠️ If kubelet is down, static pods don't restart!                      │
│  ⚠️ If manifest has syntax error, static pod won't start!              │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Control Plane Troubleshooting Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># 1. CHECK — Are control plane pods running?</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Expected: kube-apiserver, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager, etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. CHECK — Control plane pod logs</span>
kubectl logs kube-apiserver-controlplane -n kube-system
kubectl logs kube-scheduler-controlplane -n kube-system
kubectl logs kube-controller-manager-controlplane -n kube-system
kubectl logs etcd-controlplane -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. IF API SERVER IS DOWN — kubectl won't work!</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Use container runtime directly:</span>
crictl ps -a                               <span class="comment"># List all containers</span>
crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver         <span class="comment"># Find API server container</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;                 <span class="comment"># Read its logs</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. CHECK — Static pod manifests</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml

<span class="comment"># 5. FIX — Edit manifest, kubelet auto-restarts the pod</span>
vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects change → restarts the static pod automatically</span>

<span class="comment"># 6. VERIFY</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
kubectl get componentstatuses             <span class="comment"># Quick cluster health check (deprecated since v1.19 -- prefer: kubectl get --raw=/readyz?verbose)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Pro Tip: When API Server Is Down</h5>
                    <p>If the API server won't start, <code>kubectl</code> doesn't work at all. You must use <code>crictl</code> (the container runtime CLI) to check container status and logs directly. This is a <strong>common CKA exam scenario</strong> — they break the API server manifest and you need to fix it.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ When kubectl Works</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n kube-system</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>🚨 When kubectl Is Dead</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>crictl ps -a | grep kube</code></li>
                            <li><code>crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;</code></li>
                            <li><code>journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager</code></li>
                            <li><code>cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Which Symptom Points at Which Static Pod</h4>
                <p>Four different control-plane pods can be down, and each one produces a <strong>distinct, recognizable symptom</strong> — learning to read the symptom backward to the broken component turns a vague "the cluster feels broken" into a five-second diagnosis:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Component that's actually down</th><th>Fastest confirming check</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Every <code>kubectl</code> command times out or says "connection refused"</td><td><code>kube-apiserver</code></td><td><code>crictl ps -a | grep apiserver</code>; also check etcd — apiserver depends on it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pods stay <code>Pending</code> forever, never get a <code>nodeName</code> assigned</td><td><code>kube-scheduler</code></td><td><code>crictl logs &lt;scheduler-container&gt;</code>; check <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml</code> for a bad flag</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>New Deployments never create Pods; deleted nodes' Pods never get cleaned up</td><td><code>kube-controller-manager</code></td><td><code>crictl logs &lt;controller-manager-container&gt;</code>; check its manifest</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>apiserver responds to reads, but every write ("apply", "create") fails or is flaky</td><td><code>etcd</code></td><td><code>crictl logs &lt;etcd-container&gt;</code>; <code>etcdctl endpoint health</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>You edited a manifest and the pod never reappears — kubectl shows NOTHING useful</td><td>a typo in the manifest itself (not a real component outage)</td><td><code>journalctl -u kubelet -e | grep &lt;component&gt;</code> — the kubelet logs the parse/run failure locally</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why Existing Pods Keep Running When the Control Plane Is Down</h5>
                    <p>A common misconception: "if the apiserver is down, my application pods crash." They don't — at least not immediately. The kubelet on each worker node already has the pod specs it needs locally (from the last sync) and keeps the containers running via the container runtime with zero apiserver involvement. What breaks immediately is anything that needs a <em>fresh decision</em>: new Pods can't be created, no rescheduling happens if a node dies, <code>kubectl</code> reads/writes fail, and Services stop getting <em>new</em> endpoint updates (though existing iptables/IPVS rules keep routing traffic that's already flowing). This is exactly why a CKA "the apiserver is down" scenario is graded on <strong>recovery speed</strong>, not on whether the easypj <code>jobpost</code> app crashed the instant you broke it — it almost never does, right away.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §32.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s32-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.4 Troubleshooting Networking</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Network problems manifest as pods that can't reach services, DNS failures, or external traffic not arriving. Systematic diagnosis is key.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Network Troubleshooting — Layer by Layer</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NETWORKING TROUBLESHOOTING LAYERS                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Layer 1: POD-TO-POD                                                         │
│  ┌──────────┐              ┌──────────┐                                     │
│  │  Pod A   │──── CNI ────▶│  Pod B   │                                     │
│  │ 10.244.1 │   network    │ 10.244.2 │                                     │
│  └──────────┘              └──────────┘                                     │
│  Debug: kubectl exec podA -- ping &lt;podB-IP&gt;                                 │
│  Fix:   Check CNI plugin pods (Calico/Weave/Flannel) running in kube-system │
│                                                                              │
│  Layer 2: POD-TO-SERVICE                                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐  ┌───────────────┐  ┌──────────┐                             │
│  │  Pod A   │─▶│  Service      │─▶│  Pod B   │                             │
│  │          │  │  (ClusterIP)  │  │(endpoint)│                             │
│  └──────────┘  └───────────────┘  └──────────┘                             │
│  Debug: kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;   ← Are there endpoints?               │
│  Fix:   Service selector must match pod labels                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Layer 3: DNS RESOLUTION                                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────────┐                              │
│  │  Pod A   │─▶│ CoreDNS  │─▶│ Service IP   │                              │
│  │nslookup  │  │(kube-dns)│  │  resolved    │                              │
│  └──────────┘  └──────────┘  └──────────────┘                              │
│  Debug: kubectl exec pod -- nslookup &lt;svc-name&gt;                             │
│  Fix:   Check CoreDNS pods, ConfigMap, and kube-dns Service                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Layer 4: EXTERNAL ACCESS                                                    │
│  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐                   │
│  │ External │─▶│ NodePort │─▶│ Service  │─▶│  Pod     │                   │
│  │  Client  │  │  :30080  │  │ ClusterIP│  │(backend) │                   │
│  └──────────┘  └──────────┘  └──────────┘  └──────────┘                   │
│  Debug: curl http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:&lt;nodeport&gt;                                    │
│  Fix:   Check firewall rules, kube-proxy, service type                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Network Debugging Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══ SERVICE NOT RESPONDING ═══
<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check endpoints — are pods registered?</span>
kubectl get endpoints &lt;service-name&gt;
<span class="comment"># If &lt;none&gt;: service selector doesn't match any pod labels!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Compare service selector with pod labels</span>
kubectl get svc &lt;service-name&gt; -o yaml | grep -A5 selector
kubectl get pods --show-labels

<span class="comment"># ═══ DNS NOT WORKING ═══</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 1: Test DNS from inside a pod</span>
kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox --rm -it --restart=Never -- \
  nslookup kubernetes.default

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check CoreDNS pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check CoreDNS ConfigMap for typos</span>
kubectl get cm coredns -n kube-system -o yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kube-dns Service ClusterIP</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># This IP should match /etc/resolv.conf inside pods</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ CNI PLUGIN ISSUES ═══</span>
<span class="comment"># Check CNI plugin pods (Calico, Weave, Flannel, etc.)</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -i "calico\|weave\|flannel\|cilium"
kubectl get ds -n kube-system            <span class="comment"># CNI typically runs as DaemonSet</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ KUBE-PROXY ISSUES ═══</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep kube-proxy
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy manages iptables rules for Service → Pod routing</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-verify">
                    <h4>✅ Verify Your Node &amp; Cluster Troubleshooting Skills</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Can you check a node's kubelet status? → <code>ssh node; systemctl status kubelet</code></li>
                        <li>Can you view static pod manifests? → <code>ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></li>
                        <li>Can you debug when API server is down? → <code>crictl ps -a; crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code></li>
                        <li>Can you test DNS from a pod? → <code>kubectl exec pod -- nslookup svc-name</code></li>
                        <li>Can you check if service has endpoints? → <code>kubectl get endpoints svc-name</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>kube-proxy Modes — How Service Routing Works</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Mode</th>
                                <th>How It Works</th>
                                <th>Performance</th>
                                <th>Check Command</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>iptables</code> (default)</td>
                                <td>Creates iptables rules for each Service → Pod mapping. Kernel-level packet routing.</td>
                                <td>Good — random pod selection. Scales to ~5K services.</td>
                                <td><code>iptables -t nat -L -n | grep &lt;service-clusterip&gt;</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ipvs</code> (deprecated in v1.35)</td>
                                <td>Uses IPVS kernel module with hash tables. More efficient routing algorithms.</td>
                                <td>Better than iptables at scale — round-robin, least-conn, etc. Scales to 10K+ services.</td>
                                <td><code>ipvsadm -Ln</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>nftables</code> (beta v1.31, GA/stable v1.33)</td>
                                <td>Successor to iptables using the nftables kernel subsystem. More efficient rule processing than either predecessor.</td>
                                <td>Best — modern kernel-level routing with better rule optimization; the officially-recommended path going forward.</td>
                                <td><code>nft list ruleset</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 IPVS Is Being Retired — <code>nftables</code> Is the Future</h5>
                    <p>As of Kubernetes v1.35, the <code>ipvs</code> proxy mode is officially <strong>deprecated</strong> in favor of <code>nftables</code> (which went alpha in v1.29, beta in v1.31, and reached GA/stable in v1.33). If your cluster still runs an older kube-proxy, <code>iptables</code> remains the default and <code>ipvs</code> still works fine — but any "use IPVS for large clusters" advice you encounter (including in older CKA prep material) should now be read as "use <code>nftables</code>." The exam may still test whichever mode a given cluster ships with, so know how to <em>verify</em> the active mode (<code>kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | grep mode</code>) rather than assuming one.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Diagnosing kube-proxy Issues</h5>
                    <p>If services exist but traffic doesn't reach pods (and endpoints ARE populated), the problem may be kube-proxy. Check: <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy</code>. If kube-proxy is down, iptables rules become stale and services stop routing to new pods.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Fourth Rung Nobody Checks: NetworkPolicy</h5>
                    <p>The three-rung ladder above (CNI → endpoints → DNS → kube-proxy) covers connectivity mechanics, but there's a silent fifth possibility that looks IDENTICAL to a broken network: a <strong>NetworkPolicy</strong> quietly blocking the traffic. For the easypj app, imagine <code>jobpost-web</code> pods can reach the <code>easypj-db</code> Service by name (DNS resolves fine, endpoints are populated, kube-proxy is healthy) — yet every connection to PostgreSQL times out. If a NetworkPolicy in the namespace sets a default-deny posture (an empty <code>podSelector: {}</code> with no matching allow rule for the DB's port), every one of the earlier checks passes and traffic STILL doesn't arrive. Always run <code>kubectl get networkpolicy -n easypj-production</code> as a final check before concluding "it must be the CNI" — a missing allow-rule after a recent policy rollout is a far more common real-world cause than an actual CNI bug.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Complete Networking Ladder — Five Rungs, Not Three</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  1. CNI healthy?        kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|flannel|cilium'
        │ yes
        ▼
  2. Endpoints populated? kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;         ← empty = selector/readiness, not network
        │ yes
        ▼
  3. DNS resolves?        kubectl exec debug -- nslookup &lt;svc&gt;
        │ yes
        ▼
  4. kube-proxy routing?  iptables -t nat -L | grep &lt;clusterIP&gt;   (or ipvsadm -Ln)
        │ yes                                                       ← everyone stops here!
        ▼
  5. NetworkPolicy allowing it?  kubectl get networkpolicy -n &lt;ns&gt; -o yaml
        │                        ← the rung most candidates forget even exists
        ▼
  ✅ traffic actually reaches the pod
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §32.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s32-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.5 Cluster-Level Failures &amp; Systematic Recovery</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Beyond a single node or a single component, whole-<strong>cluster</strong> failures cluster around a small set of root causes. Work them top-down in the same fixed order every time and you can recover almost any broken <code>kubeadm</code> cluster — including the easypj production cluster after a bad night.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Start Wide Before You Start Deep: <code>kubectl cluster-info dump</code></h5>
                    <p>Before diving layer-by-layer, if <code>kubectl</code> still responds at all, one command captures a comprehensive snapshot of overall cluster health in a single shot: <code>kubectl cluster-info dump</code>. It's noisy (redirect it to a file — <code>kubectl cluster-info dump &gt; /tmp/cluster-dump.txt</code>) but it pulls node status, every pod across every namespace, and control-plane component state into one place, which is useful for spotting a pattern (e.g. "every node in one AZ is affected") before you commit to the top-down §32.5 recovery order below. It only works, of course, as long as the apiserver itself is still answering — once <code>kubectl</code> is fully dead, skip straight to <code>crictl</code>/<code>journalctl</code> on the control-plane node.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Four Cluster-Level Failure Modes</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗄️</div>
                        <h5>[A] etcd Unhealthy</h5>
                        <p>The apiserver is only as healthy as etcd underneath it. Watch for disk too slow ("apply entries took too long"), lost quorum, or a database space-exceeded (<code>NOSPACE</code>) alarm.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>[B] Expired Certificates</h5>
                        <p>x509 errors everywhere — every component that talks TLS starts failing at once. <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> confirms it in one line.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💽</div>
                        <h5>[C] Full Disk on Control-Plane Node</h5>
                        <p>DiskPressure, etcd "no space", apiserver crashlooping — all the same root cause. Free space and etcd/apiserver recover on their own.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>[D] Port Conflicts / Firewall</h5>
                        <p>A blocked required port (6443, 2379/2380, 10250, 10257/10259) LOOKS exactly like a NotReady node or a dead apiserver.</p>
                    </div>
