                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">[A] etcd Health Check</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># etcd is the source of truth — nothing works if it's unhealthy</span>
crictl ps -a | grep etcd
crictl logs &lt;etcd-container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># The authoritative health check (needs the etcd peer certs):</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health \
    --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
    --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
    --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
    --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Watch for in the logs:</span>
<span class="comment">#   "apply entries took too long"   → disk too slow for etcd's fsync</span>
<span class="comment">#   "lost quorum"                   → too many etcd members down</span>
<span class="comment">#   "database space exceeded" (NOSPACE) → defrag / compact the db</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
    --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
    --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
    --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
    endpoint status --write-out=table</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">💡 Why a Slow Disk Under etcd Cascades Into "Everything Feels Broken"</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  etcd's fsync gets slower (noisy neighbor, cheap cloud disk, disk nearly full)
        │
        ▼
  every etcd WRITE (Raft commit) now takes longer  ── "apply entries took too long" in logs
        │
        ▼
  apiserver's WRITES (create/update/apply) slow down or start timing out
        │        (apiserver READS from its local watch cache — largely unaffected at first,
        │         which is WHY "kubectl get" can look fine while "kubectl apply" hangs)
        ▼
  controller-manager's work queue backs up (it does read-then-write reconcile loops)
        │
        ▼
  new Pods/Deployments/Jobs stop being created; NEW replicas don't spawn on node failure
        │
        ▼
  scheduler still runs, but has nothing NEW to schedule — LOOKS "idle/broken" but isn't
        │
        ▼
  ⚠️  SYMPTOM AT THE TOP: "the scheduler seems stuck" / "apply is slow" — ROOT CAUSE
      at the BOTTOM: etcd's disk. This is exactly why §32.5's recovery order checks
      etcd FIRST — every layer above it is a symptom, not a cause.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">[B] Expired Certificates — x509 Errors Everywhere</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Symptom: components suddenly fail with "x509: certificate has expired"</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Jul 03, 2026 09:00 UTC   -2h  ← EXPIRED!</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Jul 03, 2026 09:00 UTC   -2h  ← EXPIRED!</span>

<span class="comment"># Renew everything in one shot:</span>
kubeadm certs renew all

<span class="comment"># Then RESTART the affected static pods (move manifests out/in — the</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet deletes then recreates the pod with the fresh certs mounted):</span>
mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

<span class="comment"># And restart the kubelet itself:</span>
systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># NOTE: the kubelet's OWN client cert usually auto-rotates on its own —</span>
<span class="comment"># it's the STATIC POD certs (apiserver, etcd, controller-manager,</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler, admin.conf) that need the manual "renew all" + restart.</span></code></pre>
