                    <h4>🚑 Systematic Recovery Order — Broken Cluster, Top-Down</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SYSTEMATIC RECOVERY ORDER — a broken kubeadm cluster, worked top-down        │
│                                                                              │
│  1. etcd healthy?              ── nothing works without it (§A above)       │
│         │ yes                                                               │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  2. apiserver up?              ── check via crictl, NOT kubectl (§32.3)     │
│         │ yes                                                               │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  3. kubelet up on EACH node?   ── systemctl status kubelet (§32.2)          │
│         │ yes                                                               │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  4. controller-mgr + scheduler up? ── kubectl get pods -n kube-system       │
│         │ yes                                                               │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  5. CNI / kube-proxy healthy?  ── DaemonSets Running on every node (§32.4)  │
│         │ yes                                                               │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  6. certs valid? disk free? ports open? ── kubeadm certs check-expiration,  │
│         df -h, ss -tlnp (§B, §C, §D above)                                  │
│         │                                                                   │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  ✅ CLUSTER HEALTHY — work this list in order; do not skip ahead to guess.  │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>
                    </div>
                    <h5>💡 Why This Order, Not Another</h5>
                    <p>Each layer's health check is meaningless if the layer above it is broken: checking "is the scheduler working?" is pointless if the apiserver can't even accept the request, and checking the apiserver is pointless if etcd itself is down. Working <strong>top-down, one gate at a time</strong>, means you never waste exam minutes debugging a symptom whose real cause is one layer further down.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>If you skip ahead and debug this layer first…</th><th>…without confirming the layer below it is healthy, here's what actually happens</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Scheduler ("why aren't pods scheduling?")</td><td>Every <code>kubectl</code> command you run to inspect the scheduler is failing for the SAME underlying reason (a dead/slow apiserver) — you end up debugging two problems as if they were one, confusingly</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>apiserver ("why is kubectl slow/erroring?")</td><td>You "fix" it by restarting the apiserver pod — it comes back healthy for a few seconds, then degrades again, because etcd underneath it never got fixed</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 The etcd-First Rule</h5>
                    <p>On the CKA exam, a "the whole cluster is broken" scenario is very often <strong>etcd</strong> underneath, disguised as an apiserver problem. Before you spend time reading apiserver flags or certs, run <code>crictl ps -a | grep etcd</code> and an <code>etcdctl endpoint health</code> check first — it's the fastest way to rule out (or confirm) the deepest layer.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🩺 Common Misdiagnoses — What Costs You Exam Minutes</h4>
                <p>Every one of these LOOKS like the obvious diagnosis under time pressure, and every one is wrong. Recognizing the pattern in one second saves five exam minutes chasing the wrong layer.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>What you see</th><th>Tempting (WRONG) diagnosis</th><th>Actual cause / correct check</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Node STATUS column shows <code>Ready,SchedulingDisabled</code></td><td>"The node is unhealthy / NotReady"</td><td>Someone ran <code>kubectl cordon</code> — the node is perfectly healthy, just marked unschedulable. Check <code>kubectl describe node | grep Taints</code> for <code>node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable</code>, not <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pods stuck <code>Pending</code> with event <code>0/3 nodes are available: 3 node(s) had taint</code></td><td>"A control-plane component (scheduler) is down"</td><td>The scheduler is working FINE — it correctly refuses to place the pod because of a taint/toleration mismatch (Ch 16), not a node-health or control-plane problem at all.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>An HA cluster still answers every <code>kubectl</code> command</td><td>"The whole control plane must be healthy"</td><td>A load balancer in front of 3 apiservers only needs ONE healthy backend to keep <code>kubectl</code> working — always check <code>kubectl get nodes</code> and each control-plane node's static pods individually; one or two can be silently down.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>You <code>kubectl delete pod kube-scheduler-cp1 -n kube-system</code> "to force a restart"</td><td>"This is how you restart a broken component"</td><td>It's a mirror pod — kubelet recreates it from the manifest almost instantly regardless, so the delete accomplished NOTHING diagnostic. Edit or move the actual manifest file if you need a real restart.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>One node flaked once and recovered</td><td>"Nothing to fix, it's back to Ready — ignore it"</td><td>A single transient blip is often an early signal (disk nearing a pressure threshold, network flakiness) — check <code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code> for a HISTORY of flips before assuming it's a one-off (see the flapping-node deep dive below).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
