            <h5>① The node-lease heartbeat — why NotReady takes exactly ~40 seconds, never instant</h5>
            <p>The kubelet doesn't have ONE heartbeat — it has <strong>two</strong>, and confusing them is why candidates misjudge how fast a node should flip to NotReady. The full <code>NodeStatus</code> (Conditions, Capacity, Images) is expensive to compute and push, so it's sent rarely. A second, much cheaper heartbeat — a <code>Lease</code> object in the <code>kube-node-lease</code> namespace — is renewed constantly, and it's the ONLY thing the node controller actually watches for liveness.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  TWO HEARTBEATS, ONE NODE                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  kubelet on worker-1                                                         │
│   │                                                                          │
│   ├─▶ (1) full NodeStatus  ── every node-status-update-frequency (10s)      │
│   │       Conditions, Capacity, Allocatable, Images  ── EXPENSIVE, rare      │
│   │       $ kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A6 Conditions            │
│   │                                                                          │
│   └─▶ (2) Lease renewal    ── every ~10s, MUCH cheaper (just a timestamp)   │
│           kube-node-lease namespace, one Lease object per node               │
│           $ kubectl -n kube-node-lease get lease worker-1 -o yaml           │
│                                                                              │
│  NODE CONTROLLER watches (2), NOT (1):                                       │
│    last Lease renewTime &gt; node-monitor-grace-period (default 40s)          │
│           │                                                                  │
│           ▼                                                                  │
│    node marked NotReady  ── THIS is why it's ~40s, not instant                │
│           │                                                                  │
│           │ NoExecute taint + tolerationSeconds (default 300s) later…    │
│           ▼                                                                  │
│    pods on that node are evicted &amp; rescheduled elsewhere                    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Heartbeat</th><th>Object</th><th>Frequency</th><th>Who watches it</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Full NodeStatus</td><td><code>Node.status</code> (Conditions/Capacity)</td><td>~10s (throttled if unchanged)</td><td>You — via <code>kubectl describe node</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Lease renewal</td><td><code>Lease</code> in <code>kube-node-lease</code></td><td>~10s, cheap timestamp bump</td><td>node-controller — for the NotReady decision</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A stale Lease = NotReady, full stop.</strong> If you ever see a node NotReady but the kubelet LOOKS healthy from the outside, check the actual Lease timestamp — <code>kubectl -n kube-node-lease get lease &lt;node&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.renewTime}'</code> — a Lease that stopped updating (even with the kubelet process technically alive) is the node-controller's entire NotReady trigger.</div>

            <h5>② kubelet flags vs config.yaml — precedence when the two disagree</h5>
            <p>The kubelet reads settings from TWO places at once: the systemd unit / drop-in (command-line flags) and <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> (the structured config file). When you see behavior that doesn't match what you edited, you edited the wrong one — or forgot that flags always win.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  kubelet STARTUP — where its settings actually come from                     │
│                                                                              │
│  /etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/10-kubeadm.conf   (the DROP-IN)      │
│    ExecStart=/usr/bin/kubelet $KUBELET_KUBEADM_ARGS ...                     │
│                    │                                                        │
│                    │  command-line FLAGS  ── ALWAYS WIN if both set a value │
│                    ▼                                                        │
│  /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml                            (the CONFIG FILE) │
│    cgroupDriver: systemd                                                     │
│    clusterDNS: [10.96.0.10]                                                  │
│    clusterDomain: cluster.local                          ── the DEFAULTS,   │
│                                                              overridden by    │
│                                                              any matching flag│
│                                                                              │
│  RULE: edit config.yaml   → restart kubelet is enough                        │
│        edit the unit/drop-in → daemon-reload BEFORE restart, or it's IGNORED │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>You changed…</th><th>Correct fix sequence</th><th>Common mistake</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>config.yaml</code> only</td><td><code>systemctl restart kubelet</code></td><td>none needed — this one's simple</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>systemd unit / drop-in</td><td><code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> THEN <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code></td><td>skipping daemon-reload — systemd silently keeps serving the OLD unit</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Both, and they conflict</td><td>the FLAG wins — remove it from the drop-in if you want config.yaml's value to apply</td><td>editing config.yaml and wondering why nothing changed — a flag was overriding it</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Check BOTH when behavior is wrong.</strong> kubeadm-managed clusters put most settings in <code>config.yaml</code> and only a few in the drop-in (like <code>--container-runtime-endpoint</code>) — but on the exam you must verify by reading both files, not assume based on convention.</div>

            <h5>③ The static-pod silent-failure loop — how a manifest typo hides from kubectl entirely</h5>
            <p>A typo in a static-pod manifest is one of the CKA's favorite traps precisely because <code>kubectl</code> shows you NOTHING useful. Understanding the kubelet's manifest-watch loop tells you exactly where the truth actually lives.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  THE STATIC-POD RECOVERY LOOP — works even with kubectl completely DEAD      │
│                                                                              │
│  kubelet ──watches (fsnotify)──▶ /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml           │
│      │                                                                       │
│      │ file changed (edit / typo / move out+in)                             │
│      ▼                                                                       │
│  kubelet parses the manifest LOCALLY (no apiserver round-trip needed)        │
│      │                                                                       │
│      ├─ VALID   → creates/recreates the container via the CRI (containerd)  │
│      │              → ALSO posts a read-only "mirror pod" to the apiserver  │
│      │                 so `kubectl get pods -n kube-system` can SEE it       │
│      │                                                                       │
│      └─ INVALID (typo, bad flag, bad YAML) → kubelet logs the parse/run     │
│                     error to its OWN journal — kubectl shows NOTHING         │
│                     $ journalctl -u kubelet -e | grep -i &lt;component&gt;        │
│                     $ crictl ps -a | grep &lt;component&gt;   ← Exited container? │
│                     $ crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;         ← the real error   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>Where the truth actually is</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> — pod missing or stuck</td><td>a read-only <strong>mirror pod</strong>; kubectl can't create/manage/fix it directly</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>No error from <code>kubectl apply</code> (you don't even use apply here)</td><td>the kubelet's own parse step — <code>journalctl -u kubelet -e</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Container "not starting"</td><td><code>crictl ps -a</code> shows an <code>Exited</code> container with a real exit reason</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Fix loop needs no restart command at all.</strong> Because the kubelet re-parses on every file change, the fix is simply: edit the manifest correctly and save — the kubelet detects the write and recreates the pod within seconds. Moving the file out (<code>mv … /tmp/</code>) and back in also works and is often faster to reason about mid-incident.</div>

            <h5>④ node-problem-detector — the DaemonSet that sees hardware faults the kubelet doesn't</h5>
            <p>The kubelet's own Conditions (Memory/Disk/PID pressure, Ready) only cover what the kubelet itself measures. Real production clusters run <strong>node-problem-detector (NPD)</strong>, a DaemonSet that watches the kernel log and system daemons for faults the kubelet has no visibility into — and surfaces them as additional Node Conditions and Events, the exact same channels you already read.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NODE-PROBLEM-DETECTOR — extending "is this node healthy?" beyond kubelet    │
│                                                                              │
│  Sources NPD watches on each node:                                           │
│    • /var/log/kern.log / dmesg  ── kernel deadlocks, OOM-killer events       │
│    • Hardware error counters    ── ECC memory errors, corrupted filesystem   │
│    • NTP / clock sync           ── clock drift beyond tolerance              │
│                                                                              │
│              │  detects a fault                                             │
│              ▼                                                              │
│  Publishes ──▶ a NEW Node Condition (e.g. "KernelDeadlock: True")            │
│           └──▶ a Node Event (visible in `kubectl describe node`)             │
│                                                                              │
│  $ kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A10 Conditions                    │
│    KernelDeadlock       False                                                │
│    ReadonlyFilesystem   False                                                │
│    FrequentKubeletRestart  False        ← all from NPD, not core kubelet    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Without NPD</th><th>With NPD</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Kernel deadlock → node just silently gets slow/weird, no clear signal</td><td>surfaces as a real Node Condition you can alert on</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Corrupted filesystem → cryptic pod failures, hard to trace to root cause</td><td>a <code>ReadonlyFilesystem</code> Condition points straight at it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Detection lives only in "day-2 tribal knowledge"</td><td>codified, DaemonSet-deployed, same interface as core Conditions</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>NPD reporting choice</th><th>When it's used</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Node <strong>Condition</strong></td><td>a PERMANENT problem (the fault persists until resolved)</td><td>shows up every time you <code>describe node</code>, stays until fixed — good for alerting rules</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Node <strong>Event</strong></td><td>a TEMPORARY, one-off problem</td><td>visible in <code>kubectl describe node</code>'s Events section, ages out — good for post-mortem timelines, not standing alerts</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>NPD deploys as a DaemonSet you apply yourself (some cloud providers pre-install it as a managed add-on instead), and its detection rules are overridable via a <code>ConfigMap</code> mounted into the pod — the built-in rule set is only a starting point, not a fixed list. One limitation worth knowing on the exam: if your cloud provider installs NPD as a cluster Add-on (rather than a plain DaemonSet you deployed), you generally can't override its config via ConfigMap — the Add-on manager doesn't support that path.</p>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Day-2/Beyond:</strong> the exam tests the manual, kubectl/journalctl version of diagnosis; production clusters ADD node-problem-detector, alert on Lease renewal gaps, and monitor etcd db size + fsync latency automatically — NPD is the tool that turns "the node feels off" into an actual, alertable Condition.</div>

            <h5>⑤ Reading logs and containers without the API server — /var/log/pods, crictl inspect, crictl exec</h5>
            <p>When <code>kubectl logs</code> is unreachable (apiserver down) you still have TWO independent ways to see the exact same data: the raw files the kubelet already writes to disk, and the CRI socket directly via <code>crictl</code>.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  READING CONTAINER STATE WITHOUT THE API SERVER                             │
│                                                                              │
│  `kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;`  ── needs: you ──▶ apiserver ──▶ kubelet ──▶ CRI      │
│                                     ✗ BROKEN if apiserver is down            │
│                                                                              │
│  Path 1 — read the kubelet's OWN on-disk logs (no apiserver hop at all):    │
│    /var/log/pods/&lt;namespace&gt;_&lt;pod&gt;_&lt;uid&gt;/&lt;container&gt;/0.log                │
│    /var/log/containers/&lt;pod&gt;_&lt;namespace&gt;_&lt;container&gt;-&lt;id&gt;.log  (symlink)   │
│                                                                              │
│  Path 2 — talk to the CRI socket directly (no apiserver, no kubelet even):  │
│    $ crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt;   ── full runtime state as JSON          │
│    $ crictl exec -it &lt;container-id&gt; sh  ── shell in, bypassing kubectl exec │
│    $ crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;      ── same log content as `kubectl logs`  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Needs apiserver?</th><th>Needs kubelet?</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>kubectl logs</code> / <code>exec</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Normal operation</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>cat /var/log/pods/…</code></td><td>No</td><td>No (just reads the file)</td><td>apiserver AND kubelet both down, node still up</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>crictl logs</code> / <code>inspect</code> / <code>exec</code></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Deepest-layer debugging — talks straight to containerd via the CRI socket</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Three independent paths to the same truth.</strong> On the exam, if <code>kubectl logs</code> times out, don't assume you're stuck — <code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> reads the identical log stream directly from the runtime, and <code>/var/log/pods/</code>/<code>/var/log/containers/</code> are readable with plain <code>cat</code> even if BOTH the apiserver and the kubelet are unresponsive.</div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📖 systemd vs Flat Log Files — Know Which One Your Distro Uses</h5>
                <p>Everything in this chapter assumes <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>, because kubeadm clusters on systemd-based Linux distros (Ubuntu, Debian, RHEL/CentOS — essentially every CKA exam node) run the kubelet as a systemd unit and its logs live only in the journal. Some non-systemd or custom installs instead write plain flat files you can <code>tail</code>/<code>grep</code> directly: <code>/var/log/kubelet.log</code>, <code>/var/log/kube-proxy.log</code>, and on control-plane nodes <code>/var/log/kube-apiserver.log</code>, <code>/var/log/kube-scheduler.log</code>, <code>/var/log/kube-controller-manager.log</code> — plus <code>/var/log/containerd.log</code> for the runtime itself. If <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> ever comes back completely empty on a real system (not the exam's standard kubeadm lab), check for these flat files before assuming logging is broken.</p>
            </div>

            <h5>⑥ cgroup-driver mismatch — the silent kubelet/containerd disagreement that flaps nodes</h5>
            <p>Both the kubelet and the container runtime must agree on which cgroup driver manages resource limits — <code>systemd</code> or the older <code>cgroupfs</code>. When they disagree, pods fail to start with cryptic runtime errors, and worse, the node itself can flap NotReady because the kubelet's own cgroup bookkeeping conflicts with what containerd expects.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CGROUP DRIVER MISMATCH — two components, one shared resource, one truth    │
│                                                                              │
│  kubelet's view:            /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml                    │
│    cgroupDriver: systemd    ◀── must MATCH the runtime's                    │
│                                                                              │
│  containerd's view:         /etc/containerd/config.toml                     │
│    [plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runc.options]   │
│      SystemdCgroup = true   ◀── must MATCH the kubelet's                    │
│                                                                              │
│  MISMATCH SYMPTOM:                                                           │
│    pods fail with runtime errors referencing cgroup paths                   │
│    node may flap Ready/NotReady under load                                  │
│    both kubelet AND containerd process-level health can look "fine"         │
│                                                                              │
│  FIX: set BOTH to "systemd" (the modern default), then:                     │
│    systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; systemctl restart containerd kubelet         │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Command</th><th>Must show</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>kubelet's cgroup driver</td><td><code>grep cgroupDriver /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></td><td><code>systemd</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>containerd's cgroup driver</td><td><code>grep SystemdCgroup /etc/containerd/config.toml</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Restart order after fixing</td><td><code>systemctl restart containerd</code> then <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code></td><td>runtime first, then the agent that talks to it</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Both must say "systemd."</strong> This is a favorite hidden CKA trap: everything LOOKS configured correctly (kubelet running, containerd running, manifests fine) yet pods won't start or the node intermittently flaps — the actual bug is one config line disagreeing between two files that most candidates never think to cross-check.</div>

            <h5>⑦ Flapping nodes — when Ready↔NotReady oscillates instead of staying down</h5>
            <p>Everything covered so far assumes a node fails and STAYS failed until you fix it. Production has a nastier variant: the node flips Ready → NotReady → Ready → NotReady, sometimes for hours, before anyone notices. This needs a different diagnostic instinct — you're not looking for "why is it down," you're looking for "why does it keep almost-dying."</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  A FLAPPING NODE — timeline, not a snapshot                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Ready ──40s──▶ NotReady ──90s──▶ Ready ──40s──▶ NotReady ──▶ Ready ──▶ ...  │
│                                                                              │
│  A single `kubectl get nodes` snapshot shows "Ready" — LOOKS fine!           │
│  The problem only appears in HISTORY:                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  $ kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | grep worker-1              │
│    NodeNotReady   worker-1   (x14 over 2h)   ◄── the real signal            │
│                                                                              │
│  Common root causes of FLAPPING (vs. steady failure):                        │
│    • Network flakiness between kubelet and apiserver — Lease renewal        │
│      occasionally misses the grace window, then catches up                  │
│    • Node right at a pressure THRESHOLD — memory/disk hovers exactly        │
│      around the eviction limit, tips over and recovers repeatedly           │
│    • Clock skew (NTP not synced) — confuses Lease renewTime comparisons     │
│    • kubelet itself OOM-killed and auto-restarted by systemd in a loop      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Where to look</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Flip count visible in Events, node otherwise looks normal</td><td><code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp -A | grep -i notready</code></td><td>establish a pattern (time of day, correlates with a batch job?) before touching anything</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Flips correlate with memory/disk near a threshold</td><td><code>kubectl describe node | grep -A6 Allocated</code>, historical <code>kubectl top node</code></td><td>add headroom or lower requests — a resource sitting AT the threshold will always flap</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Flips correlate with kubelet restarts</td><td><code>journalctl -u kubelet | grep -i "systemd\[1\]: kubelet.service"</code> for start/stop lines</td><td>find why kubelet dies (often OOM) — <code>dmesg | grep -i "killed process.*kubelet"</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Flips at regular intervals, clock looks "off"</td><td><code>timedatectl</code> / <code>chronyc tracking</code> on the node</td><td>fix NTP sync — clock skew corrupts Lease timestamp comparisons</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A single "Ready" snapshot proves nothing.</strong> Before declaring a node healthy, always check its EVENT HISTORY, not just its current status — a node that flapped 14 times in the last 2 hours and happens to be Ready right now is a ticking incident, not a closed one.</div>

            <h5>⑧ The Part 9 layer-stack — how Chapters 31, 32 &amp; 33 fit into ONE mental model</h5>
            <p>Chapter 31 taught you to read a <em>Pod's</em> symptoms. This chapter (32) teaches you that the Pod's symptom often has a root cause one or two layers BELOW the Pod. Chapter 33 (next) teaches the instruments — logs and metrics — you use to look at EVERY layer in this stack, not just one. Seeing all three chapters as ONE tool, not three separate skills, is what makes troubleshooting fast instead of a random walk.</p>
