            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Diagnose a NotReady node FROM the node</strong> — <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> → <code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code> → read the error → fix → restart. <code>kubectl</code> can only TELL you it's NotReady; it can never fix it.</li>
                    <li><strong>The kubelet is a systemd service, NOT a pod</strong> — its config is <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>; after editing the systemd UNIT or a drop-in, <code>daemon-reload</code> BEFORE <code>restart</code> or the change is silently ignored.</li>
                    <li><strong>Control-plane components are STATIC PODS</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. A typo there fails SILENTLY through kubectl — when the apiserver is down, use <code>crictl ps -a</code> and <code>crictl logs</code>, not kubectl.</li>
                    <li><strong>Common NotReady causes to memorize</strong> — kubelet down, CNI missing/unhealthy, swap left on, container runtime down, disk/memory/PID pressure, expired certs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Node Conditions name the failed subsystem</strong> — MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, PIDPressure, NetworkUnavailable — read them with <code>kubectl describe node</code> or jsonpath on <code>.status.conditions</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Two heartbeats, not one</strong> — the cheap, frequent <code>kube-node-lease</code> Lease renewal is what the node-controller actually watches for NotReady; the full NodeStatus (Conditions) is separate and rarer.</li>
                    <li><strong>Network troubleshooting has a fixed ladder</strong> — CNI healthy? → Service has endpoints? → DNS resolves? → kube-proxy healthy? — walk it top to bottom, never skip a rung.</li>
                    <li><strong>Recover a broken cluster top-down, always in this order</strong> — etcd → apiserver → kubelet (each node) → controller-manager/scheduler → CNI/kube-proxy → certs/disk/ports.</li>
                    <li><strong>cgroup-driver must match</strong> — kubelet's <code>cgroupDriver</code> and containerd's <code>SystemdCgroup</code> setting must BOTH say "systemd," or pods fail with cryptic runtime errors and the node can flap.</li>
                    <li><strong>A single "Ready" snapshot doesn't rule out flapping</strong> — check <code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code> for a NotReady flip history before declaring a node healthy.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ch 31 → Ch 32 → Ch 33 is ONE investigation, not three skills</strong> — a Pod symptom (Ch 31) with no Pod-level cause almost always points one layer down to the node/control plane (this chapter), inspected with the instruments in Ch 33.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>Node NotReady top causes</strong> — (1) kubelet not running: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>. (2) kubelet config error: <code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code>. (3) CNI plugin missing: <code>ls /etc/cni/net.d/</code>. (4) Container runtime down: <code>systemctl status containerd</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Static pod manifests location</strong> — <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. If kube-apiserver.yaml has a typo, the API server crashes. Fix the YAML, kubelet auto-restarts the pod. The exam loves this pattern</li>
                        <li><strong><code>crictl</code> not <code>docker</code></strong> — Modern clusters use containerd. Debug with <code>crictl ps</code>, <code>crictl logs</code>, <code>crictl inspect</code>. On the CKA exam, docker CLI is NOT available</li>
                        <li><strong>kubelet runs as systemd service</strong> — <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> to restart. <code>journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -l</code> for full logs. Config file at <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Control plane component logs</strong> — <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt;</code> for API server. Same pattern for controller-manager, scheduler. If API server is down, use <code>crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;</code></li>
                </ul>
                <ul>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Reaching for <code>kubectl</code> when the apiserver itself is down — it can't help you; it depends on the very thing that's broken</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Switch to <code>crictl</code> / <code>journalctl</code> directly on the control-plane node</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Assuming empty Service endpoints means a CNI/network bug</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Empty endpoints are USUALLY a label-selector or readiness-probe problem, not the network — check <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> and the pod's labels/readiness first</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Blaming the apiserver when the whole control plane is flaky</li>
                        <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Check etcd FIRST — a slow or full disk under etcd silently degrades the entire control plane, and the apiserver is only ever as healthy as etcd underneath it</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Node NotReady systematic fix:</strong><br>
                1. SSH into the node<br>
                2. <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> — is it running?<br>
                3. <code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code> — what's the error?<br>
                4. Common fixes: <code>systemctl enable --now kubelet</code>, fix <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>, reinstall CNI<br>
                5. <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> after any fix</p>
                <p><strong>More speed tips:</strong><br>
                • <code>journalctl -u kubelet -e</code> jumps straight to the END of the log — the real error is almost always there, not at the top.<br>
                • Editing (or moving out/in) a file in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> makes the kubelet recreate that static pod within seconds — no restart command needed.<br>
                • <code>crictl ps -a | grep apiserver</code> then <code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> reads the apiserver's own logs the instant <code>kubectl</code> is dead.<br>
                • <code>kubectl describe node &lt;n&gt; | sed -n '/Conditions/,/Events/p'</code> jumps straight to the Conditions block, skipping the noisy Capacity/Allocatable sections.<br>
                • <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> answers "is it the certs?" in one line — always run it before chasing anything else when you see x509 errors.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 32)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>node status + versions + IPs</td><td><code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>conditions, capacity, taints, events</td><td><code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>jump straight to Conditions block</td><td><code>kubectl describe node &lt;n&gt; | sed -n '/Conditions/,/Events/p'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>conditions as raw JSON</td><td><code>kubectl get node &lt;n&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>node heartbeat leases</td><td><code>kubectl get leases -n kube-node-lease</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>recent kubelet logs</td><td><code>ssh &lt;node&gt; journalctl -u kubelet --since "5 min ago"</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>stream the kubelet log live</td><td><code>journalctl -u kubelet -f --no-pager</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>jump to the END of the kubelet log</td><td><code>journalctl -u kubelet -e</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>reload systemd after editing the kubelet unit</td><td><code>systemctl daemon-reload</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>all containers including exited ones</td><td><code>ssh &lt;node&gt; crictl ps -a</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>read a container's logs sans apiserver</td><td><code>crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>full runtime state of a container</td><td><code>crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>free disk: remove unused images</td><td><code>crictl rmi --prune</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>the static-pod manifests</td><td><code>ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>disable / verify swap</td><td><code>swapoff -a</code> ; <code>swapon --show</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>check / renew all cluster certs</td><td><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> ; <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>etcd health (needs etcd certs)</td><td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=… --cert=… --key=…</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>disk usage + listening ports</td><td><code>df -h</code> ; <code>ss -tlnp</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>control-plane + CNI + kube-proxy pods</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>empty = selector/readiness problem</td><td><code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;service-name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>CoreDNS pods</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>(deprecated but still works) quick health check</td><td><code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>NotReady flip history — is this node flapping?</td><td><code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp -A | grep -i notready</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>no-SSH node access (managed clusters)</td><td><code>kubectl debug node/&lt;node&gt; -it --image=ubuntu</code> — add <code>--profile=sysadmin</code> before <code>chroot /host</code> will work</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>comprehensive cluster diagnostics dump</td><td><code>kubectl cluster-info dump</code></td></tr>
