            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 32 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the node / control-plane mental model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">In one sentence: why can't <code>kubectl</code> ever FIX a NotReady node, only report it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because <code>kubectl</code> only talks to the apiserver, which only stores what the kubelet already REPORTED — the fix (restarting kubelet, clearing swap, fixing CNI, renewing a cert) can only happen on the node itself, where the actual problem lives.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Where the report lives vs where the fix lives</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  kubectl get nodes  ──▶  apiserver  ──▶  etcd     (READ-ONLY view of a report)
                                            ▲
                                            │ kubelet PUSHED this status
                                            │
                                       worker-1 (the ACTUAL problem: kubelet/CNI/swap/cert)
                                       ── only SSH + systemctl/journalctl fixes it here ──
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the single most important mental model in the chapter: kubectl is a MIRROR of node state, never a REMOTE CONTROL for it. Every NotReady fix ends with an SSH session.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the FIVE most common root causes of a NotReady node, in the order you should suspect them.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Cause</th><th>First check</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1</td><td>kubelet stopped/crashed</td><td><code>systemctl status kubelet</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2</td><td>CNI plugin missing/unhealthy</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3</td><td>swap left enabled</td><td><code>swapon --show</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>4</td><td>container runtime down</td><td><code>systemctl status containerd</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>5</td><td>disk/memory/PID pressure, or expired cert</td><td><code>df -h</code>/<code>free -h</code>, cert expiry check</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The exam draws from exactly this list. Memorizing it turns "why is this node NotReady?" from an open investigation into a five-item checklist you run in order.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubectl</code> times out entirely — every command hangs or refuses the connection. What's your very first command, and why NOT kubectl?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>SSH to the control-plane node and run <code>crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver</code>, then <code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code>. Never reach for another <code>kubectl</code> command — it depends on the exact component that's down.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">apiserver-down triage</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver
<span class="comment"># CONTAINER   IMAGE   STATE     NAME</span>
<span class="comment"># a1b2c3      ...     Exited    kube-apiserver   ← crashing</span>
crictl logs a1b2c3</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Also check etcd FIRST if apiserver logs look clean — the apiserver is only as healthy as etcd underneath it, and a crashlooping apiserver is very often an etcd symptom in disguise.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml</code> and it never comes back — no kubectl error at all. What actually happened, and how do you find out?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Almost certainly a YAML/flag typo. Static-pod failures are SILENT through kubectl — read <code>journalctl -u kubelet -e | grep scheduler</code> and <code>crictl ps -a | grep scheduler</code> for the real parse/run error.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Silent failure path</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  vi kube-scheduler.yaml (typo) ──▶ kubelet notices the file change
        │
        ▼
  kubelet tries to parse/run it ──▶ FAILS
        │                              │
        ▼                              ▼
  kubectl get pods -n kube-system   journalctl -u kubelet -e   ← the truth is HERE
  shows the pod missing/stuck,     crictl ps -a | grep scheduler
  with NO explanatory error         crictl logs &lt;id&gt;
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Because static pods are managed by the kubelet reading a LOCAL file, not by the apiserver processing an <code>apply</code>, there is no admission/validation pipeline to reject the change with a friendly message — the kubelet just fails locally.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">x509 errors start appearing across multiple components at once. What do you check first, and what's the full fix sequence?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> confirms it in one line. Fix: <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code>, then restart the affected static pods (move the manifest out/in) and the kubelet.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1. Confirm</td><td><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2. Renew</td><td><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3. Restart static pods</td><td><code>mv kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/ &amp;&amp; mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml .</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>4. Restart kubelet</td><td><code>systemctl restart kubelet</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The kubelet's OWN client cert usually auto-rotates; it's the static-pod certs (apiserver, etcd, controller-manager, scheduler, admin.conf) that need this manual sequence.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The kubelet log shows <code>"running with swap on ... failed"</code>. What's the complete, PERSISTENT fix (not just for right now)?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>swapoff -a</code> turns it off NOW, but you must also remove the swap entry from <code>/etc/fstab</code> or it comes back on the next reboot — then restart the kubelet.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">persistent swap-off</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">swapoff -a
sed -i '/swap/d' /etc/fstab      <span class="comment"># remove the swap line so it stays OFF after reboot</span>
systemctl restart kubelet</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A common exam trap: candidates run <code>swapoff -a</code>, see the node go Ready, and stop — but forget fstab, so a later reboot silently re-breaks the node.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node shows <code>DiskPressure: True</code>. What does the kubelet do AUTOMATICALLY, and what two commands clear it fastest?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The kubelet taints the node <code>NoSchedule</code> and starts evicting pods (BestEffort first). Clear it with <code>crictl rmi --prune</code> (unused images) and <code>journalctl --vacuum-size=200M</code> (trim the journal).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Eviction threshold (default)</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>nodefs.available &lt; 10%</code></td><td>root filesystem running low</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>imagefs.available &lt; 15%</code></td><td>image storage running low</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pressure clears automatically once space is freed — you never need to manually clear the Condition or remove the taint; the kubelet re-evaluates and does it for you.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Kubelet log says <code>"cni plugin not initialized"</code>. What's broken and what's the fix?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The CNI isn't installed or its DaemonSet pod isn't healthy on that node. Check <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E 'calico|flannel|cilium'</code>, then re-apply the CNI manifest or fix the crashing pod — once it's Running, the kubelet reports Ready.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">CNI is a Ready-condition dependency</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  CNI DaemonSet pod  NOT Running on worker-1
        │
        ▼
  kubelet on worker-1 reports:  NetworkUnavailable: True
        │
        ▼
  node stays NotReady even though kubelet ITSELF is up
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is a favorite exam sequence: a node joins the cluster, kubelet starts fine, but it's still NotReady because no CNI plugin has been applied to the cluster yet.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Give the two commands to read a node's Conditions, and what five values you're looking for.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe node &lt;n&gt;</code> (Conditions section) or <code>kubectl get node &lt;n&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}'</code>. Looking for: <code>Ready</code>, <code>MemoryPressure</code>, <code>DiskPressure</code>, <code>PIDPressure</code>, <code>NetworkUnavailable</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">fast conditions read</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe node worker-1 | sed -n '/Conditions/,/Events/p'</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>sed</code> one-liner skips the noisy Capacity/Allocatable/Non-terminated-Pods blocks and jumps straight to the five values you actually need under exam time pressure.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Service for the easypj 'jobpost' app returns no response, but <code>kubectl get pods</code> shows the backing pods as Running. Trace the full diagnosis order.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl get endpoints jobpost</code> first — if EMPTY, it's a label/readiness problem, not the network. If endpoints exist, check CoreDNS (nslookup), then kube-proxy health, then a default-deny NetworkPolicy.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">The fixed diagnostic ladder</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  kubectl get endpoints jobpost
     │
     ├─ EMPTY  → selector/labels mismatch, or pods failing readiness
     │
     └─ populated → kubectl exec debug -- nslookup jobpost
                        │
                        ├─ fails → CoreDNS pods/config
                        │
                        └─ resolves → check kube-proxy, then NetworkPolicy default-deny
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>"Running pods but no response" is a classic exam trap that LOOKS like a network bug but is overwhelmingly a Service-selector or readiness-probe mismatch — always check endpoints before touching CNI or NetworkPolicy.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>


