            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 32 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Fast node &amp; control-plane troubleshooting recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node shows <code>NotReady</code>. Walk through the troubleshooting process.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">identify which node is notready</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Identify which node is NotReady</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS     ROLES    AGE  VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2   NotReady   &lt;none&gt;   30d  v1.31.0</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Describe the node for conditions</span>
kubectl describe node worker-2 | grep -A20 Conditions
<span class="comment"># MemoryPressure    False</span>
<span class="comment"># DiskPressure      False</span>
<span class="comment"># PIDPressure       False</span>
<span class="comment"># Ready             False  ← kubelet is NOT reporting</span>
<span class="comment">#   Last Heartbeat: 5 minutes ago</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: SSH to the node</span>
ssh worker-2

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kubelet status</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Active: inactive (dead)  ← Kubelet is stopped!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check kubelet logs for errors</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager
<span class="comment"># "failed to load kubelet config file"</span>
<span class="comment"># OR "unable to connect to API server"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Common fixes:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet stopped → systemctl start kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet crashing → check config: /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># cert expired → check /var/lib/kubelet/pki/</span>
<span class="comment"># wrong API server → check /etc/kubernetes/kubelet.conf</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Start and enable kubelet</span>
systemctl start kubelet
systemctl enable kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 8: Verify from control plane</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker-2   Ready   &lt;none&gt;   30d  v1.31.0 ✓</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>NotReady = kubelet stopped sending heartbeats. On the CKA: SSH to the node, check <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>, check <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> for errors, fix the issue, restart kubelet. The most common CKA scenario: kubelet is stopped or has a config error. Always check the config file path and API server address. After fixing, verify with <code>kubectl get nodes</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The kubelet is running but keeps restarting. How do you diagnose the config issue?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check kubelet status</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check kubelet status</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Active: activating (auto-restart)</span>
<span class="comment"># ← kubelet keeps crashing and systemd restarts it</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check journal logs</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -n 100 --no-pager
<span class="comment"># Look for error messages:</span>

<span class="comment"># Error A: "failed to load kubelet config file"</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
<span class="comment"># Check YAML syntax, missing fields</span>

<span class="comment"># Error B: "unable to load client CA file"</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
<span class="comment"># Check if cert file exists and is readable</span>

<span class="comment"># Error C: "server.key: no such file or directory"</span>
<span class="comment"># Wrong cert/key path in kubelet config</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check kubelet systemd unit</span>
cat /etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/10-kubeadm.conf
<span class="comment"># Look for wrong flags or paths</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify kubelet config</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
<span class="comment"># Common issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong clusterDNS IP</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong clusterDomain</span>
<span class="comment"># - Invalid YAML syntax</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix and restart</span>
vi /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml  <span class="comment"># Fix the issue</span>
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify</span>
systemctl status kubelet  <span class="comment"># Should show "active (running)"</span>
kubectl get nodes          <span class="comment"># Node should be Ready</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>When kubelet keeps restarting (activating/auto-restart), it's usually a configuration problem. On the CKA, check <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> for the specific error message. Common causes: broken kubelet config YAML, missing/wrong cert paths, wrong systemd flags. Fix the config file, <code>daemon-reload</code>, restart kubelet. Always do <code>daemon-reload</code> if you changed the systemd unit file.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A static pod isn't starting on a node. How do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find static pod manifest directory</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find static pod manifest directory</span>
<span class="comment"># Check kubelet config:</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath
<span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># OR check kubelet flags:</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep -- --pod-manifest-path

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check the manifest exists</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml  kube-apiserver.yaml  kube-controller-manager.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler.yaml  my-static-pod.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Validate the manifest YAML</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/my-static-pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check for syntax errors</span>
<span class="comment"># Common issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong indentation</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing required fields (apiVersion, kind, spec)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong image name</span>
<span class="comment"># - Invalid port numbers</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check kubelet logs for static pod errors</span>
journalctl -u kubelet | grep "my-static-pod"
<span class="comment"># "failed to parse static pod manifest: ..."</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Fix the manifest</span>
vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/my-static-pod.yaml
<span class="comment"># Kubelet auto-detects changes — no restart needed!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep my-static-pod
<span class="comment"># my-static-pod-worker-1   1/1  Running  0  10s</span>

<span class="comment"># Remember: Static pod names have node name appended</span>
<span class="comment"># Static pods are managed by kubelet, NOT the scheduler</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Static pods are managed directly by the kubelet (not the API server). They read manifests from <code>staticPodPath</code> (default: <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>). On the CKA: if a static pod isn't working, check the YAML in the manifest directory. Kubelet watches the directory and auto-restarts when manifests change — no need to restart kubelet. Static pod names have the node name appended (e.g., <code>etcd-master</code>). Control plane components (etcd, apiserver, scheduler, controller-manager) are static pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

