                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node shows <code>MemoryPressure</code> or <code>DiskPressure</code>. What happens and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check node conditions</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check node conditions:</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A20 Conditions
<span class="comment"># Type              Status</span>
<span class="comment"># MemoryPressure    True    ← Node running out of memory!</span>
<span class="comment"># DiskPressure      True    ← Node running out of disk!</span>
<span class="comment"># PIDPressure       False</span>
<span class="comment"># Ready             True    (can be True with pressure)</span>

<span class="comment"># What happens automatically:</span>
<span class="comment"># MemoryPressure → kubelet starts evicting pods (BestEffort first)</span>
<span class="comment"># DiskPressure   → kubelet reclaims dead containers, unused images</span>
<span class="comment"># Both           → Node gets tainted: node.kubernetes.io/memory-pressure:NoSchedule</span>

<span class="comment"># Check memory on the node:</span>
ssh worker-1
free -h
<span class="comment"># total: 4Gi  used: 3.8Gi  available: 200Mi ← Very low!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check disk:</span>
df -h /
<span class="comment"># /dev/sda1  50G  48G  2G  96% /  ← 96% full!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix MemoryPressure:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Find memory-hungry pods:</span>
kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=memory | head -10
<span class="comment"># 2. Delete or reduce limits on heavy consumers</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Add memory to the node (if possible)</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix DiskPressure:</span>
ssh worker-1
<span class="comment"># Clean up container logs:</span>
journalctl --vacuum-size=100M
<span class="comment"># Remove unused images:</span>
crictl rmi --prune
<span class="comment"># Clear unused containers:</span>
crictl rm $(crictl ps -a -q --state exited)

<span class="comment"># Eviction thresholds (defaults):</span>
<span class="comment"># memory.available &lt; 100Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># nodefs.available &lt; 10%</span>
<span class="comment"># imagefs.available &lt; 15%</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Node pressure conditions trigger automatic eviction. The kubelet evicts pods in order: BestEffort (no limits) first, then Burstable, then Guaranteed last. Pressure also adds a <code>NoSchedule</code> taint so new pods won't be scheduled. On the CKA: check with <code>describe node</code>, SSH to the node to check <code>free -h</code> and <code>df -h</code>, fix by removing unused resources or moving pods. The kubelet's eviction thresholds are configurable in <code>kubelet config.yaml</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The <code>kube-proxy</code> DaemonSet isn't running on a node. How do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">symptom: services don't work on one specific node</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Symptom: Services don't work on one specific node</span>
<span class="comment"># Pods can ping other pods but Service ClusterIP fails</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check kube-proxy pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME              READY  STATUS   NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-abc12  1/1    Running  worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-def34  0/1    Error    worker-2  ← Problem!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check logs</span>
kubectl logs kube-proxy-def34 -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># "failed to load config: couldn't read file"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check kube-proxy ConfigMap</span>
kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | head -30

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kube-proxy DaemonSet</span>
kubectl get ds kube-proxy -n kube-system
kubectl describe ds kube-proxy -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check iptables/ipvs rules on the node</span>
ssh worker-2
iptables -L -t nat | grep -i kube
<span class="comment"># If empty → kube-proxy isn't creating rules</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Delete the erroring pod (DaemonSet recreates)</span>
kubectl delete pod kube-proxy-def34 -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># If still broken, check kube-proxy mode:</span>
kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | grep mode
<span class="comment"># mode: "iptables" (default) or "ipvs"</span>

<span class="comment"># Restart ALL kube-proxy pods:</span>
kubectl rollout restart ds kube-proxy -n kube-system</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>kube-proxy runs as a DaemonSet (one pod per node) and maintains network rules (iptables/ipvs) for Service routing. If kube-proxy is broken on a node, Services won't work on that node. On the CKA: check the kube-proxy pod logs, delete the broken pod (DaemonSet auto-recreates), or <code>rollout restart</code> the DaemonSet. Config comes from the <code>kube-proxy</code> ConfigMap in <code>kube-system</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
