                            <li>Watch <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -w</code> — the scheduler pod restarts/crashes</li>
                            <li>Diagnose the failure from the crashed container's logs</li>
                            <li>Fix the manifest and confirm the kubelet auto-recovers it</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> Static pods are recreated by the kubelet the moment it notices the manifest file in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> change — you never need to <code>kubectl apply</code> or restart anything manually.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Break the scheduler — invalid flag value</span>
sudo sed -i 's/--leader-elect=true/--leader-elect=BROKEN/' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -w   <span class="comment"># scheduler pod restarts, then crashes</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Diagnose</span>
kubectl logs kube-scheduler-controlplane -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Error message points to the invalid flag value: "BROKEN" is not a valid bool</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Fix — revert the manifest</span>
sudo sed -i 's/--leader-elect=BROKEN/--leader-elect=true/' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -w   <span class="comment"># kubelet detects the change and restarts the scheduler automatically</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 32.3 — Diagnose Expired Certificates</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Multiple control-plane components start throwing x509 errors simultaneously on the easypj production cluster. Confirm the cause and recover.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Run <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> to confirm which certs expired</li>
                        <li>Renew all certificates in one command</li>
                        <li>Restart the affected static pods so they pick up the new certs</li>
                        <li>Restart the kubelet and verify the cluster is healthy again</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Renewing certs alone isn't enough — the running static-pod containers already loaded the OLD cert files into memory. You must force the kubelet to recreate them (move the manifest out/in, or just restart the kubelet).
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE   EXPIRES    RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver     ...        -1h  ← expired</span>

kubeadm certs renew all

<span class="comment"># Force the static pods to reload with fresh certs:</span>
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

sudo systemctl restart kubelet

kubectl get pods -n kube-system   <span class="comment"># control plane pods back to Running</span>
kubectl get nodes                 <span class="comment"># cluster responsive again</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 32.4 — Diagnose an Empty-Endpoints Service</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>The easypj <code>jobpost</code> Service stops responding. Pods are Running, but requests time out. Find the root cause using the fixed diagnostic ladder.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Check whether the Service has any endpoints at all</li>
                        <li>If endpoints are empty, compare the Service selector against the pod's actual labels</li>
                        <li>Fix the mismatch (relabel the pods or correct the Service selector)</li>
                        <li>Confirm endpoints populate and the Service responds again</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> An empty <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> output means the selector matched ZERO Ready pods — this is almost always a label typo or a readiness probe that's failing, never a CNI bug.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get endpoints jobpost
<span class="comment"># NAME       ENDPOINTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># jobpost    &lt;none&gt;      10m   ← empty!</span>

kubectl get svc jobpost -o yaml | grep -A3 selector
<span class="comment"># selector:</span>
<span class="comment">#   app: jobpost-web</span>

kubectl get pods --show-labels | grep jobpost
<span class="comment"># jobpost-7d9f8   1/1  Running   app=jobpost   ← MISMATCH! "jobpost" not "jobpost-web"</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: relabel the pods (or fix the Deployment's pod template + Service selector)</span>
kubectl label pod jobpost-7d9f8 app=jobpost-web --overwrite

kubectl get endpoints jobpost   <span class="comment"># now populated</span>
curl http://jobpost.easypj-production.svc.cluster.local   <span class="comment"># responds</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 32.5 — Full Systematic Cluster Recovery</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 10 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>You arrive to a completely unresponsive easypj cluster: <code>kubectl</code> hangs, and you have no idea which layer failed. Apply the top-down systematic recovery order from §32.5 to find and fix the root cause.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Check etcd health first (before touching anything else)</li>
                        <li>If etcd is healthy, check the apiserver via crictl</li>
                        <li>If apiserver is healthy, check kubelet on each node</li>
                        <li>Continue down the list (controllers → CNI/kube-proxy → certs/disk/ports) until you find the broken layer</li>
                        <li>Fix that layer only, then re-verify the layers ABOVE it also recovered</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Resist the urge to jump straight to the layer you suspect — a full disk on the etcd node can produce symptoms that look exactly like a dead apiserver. Always start at the top of the recovery order, not your best guess.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. etcd healthy?</span>
crictl ps -a | grep etcd
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># unhealthy: "database space exceeded"  ← ROOT CAUSE FOUND at layer 1</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Free etcd's disk (etcd data dir usually /var/lib/etcd)</span>
df -h /var/lib/etcd
crictl rmi --prune
journalctl --vacuum-size=200M

<span class="comment"># 3. Re-check etcd, THEN re-verify every layer above it recovered too</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 ...
kubectl get pods -n kube-system     <span class="comment"># apiserver, scheduler, controller-manager Running?</span>
kubectl get nodes                    <span class="comment"># all nodes Ready?</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running  <span class="comment"># anything still broken?</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 32 Visual Summary — The Node / Control-Plane Diagnostic Ladder</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ kubectl works?                                                       │
│   ├─ NO  → apiserver down → crictl ps -a | grep apiserver → logs     │
│   │        (check etcd first — apiserver is only as healthy as etcd) │
│   └─ YES → kubectl get nodes                                         │
│             └─ NotReady? → ssh node → systemctl status kubelet       │
│                  └─ journalctl -u kubelet -f → read the error:       │
│                       swap / cert / CNI / runtime / pressure → fix   │
│                       → restart kubelet (daemon-reload if unit edit) │
│                                                                       │
│  Components up but pods can't talk?                                  │
│    CNI healthy? → Service has endpoints? → DNS resolves? → kube-proxy│
│                                                                       │
│  Whole cluster broken?                                                │
│    etcd → apiserver → kubelet(each node) → controllers → CNI/proxy   │
│    → certs / disk / ports  — work this list top-down, never skip     │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="vs-prose">Diagnosis flows top-down. First ask whether <code>kubectl</code> itself works: if not, the apiserver (or the etcd behind it) is down and you debug with <code>crictl</code> and <code>journalctl</code> on the control-plane node, never <code>kubectl</code>. If <code>kubectl</code> works, list the nodes; a NotReady node sends you onto that node where the kubelet — a systemd service, not a pod — is interrogated with <code>systemctl</code> and <code>journalctl</code>. The streamed kubelet error names the real cause (swap, expired cert, missing CNI, dead runtime, or a pressure condition); you fix that, then restart the kubelet (running <code>daemon-reload</code> first if you changed its unit). Control-plane components are static pods in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> that the kubelet recreates on file change and that fail SILENTLY through kubectl — so <code>crictl</code> and <code>journalctl</code>, not <code>kubectl</code>, are the tools of last resort. When the symptom is networking rather than a dead component, walk the fixed ladder: CNI healthy, then Service endpoints populated, then DNS resolving, then kube-proxy routing — in that order, never skipping a rung. And when it's not one node or one component but the whole cluster, recover top-down: etcd first (nothing works without it), then the apiserver, then kubelet on every node, then the controller-manager and scheduler, then CNI/kube-proxy, and finally certs, disk, and ports — the same six-step method whether you're facing a single broken pod or a cluster reduced to silence.</p>
            </div>
        </article><!-- end ch32 -->

