            <section class="section-block" id="s33-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">33.1 Application Logging</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Containers write to <strong>stdout/stderr</strong>, and the container runtime captures these streams as log files on the node. <code>kubectl logs</code> retrieves this output.</p>

                <h4>kubectl logs — Debugging Flow</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Basic Logs</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code> — Start with current container output. If pod is running, this shows real-time stdout/stderr.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Previous Crash</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> — If CrashLoopBackOff, this shows output from the <strong>last crashed</strong> container instance.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Specific Container</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code> — Multi-container pods require specifying which container. Use <code>--all-containers</code> for everything.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Label Selector</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs -l app=name --tail=50</code> — Check logs across ALL replicas of a Deployment at once.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Application Logging Examples</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Basic logs</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Previous crashed container ⭐ (CKA critical)</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj --previous

<span class="comment"># Follow live (useful during testing)</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -f

<span class="comment"># Multi-container pod: specify the container</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -c django
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -c nginx-sidecar

<span class="comment"># All containers at once</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj --all-containers

<span class="comment"># Logs from all pods with label app=easypj</span>
kubectl logs -l app=easypj -n easypj --tail=50

<span class="comment"># Combine flags: last 100 lines from last hour, follow live</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx --tail=100 --since=1h -f</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Where Do Container Logs Actually Live?</h5>
                    <p>On the <strong>node</strong>, container logs are stored at:<br>
                    <code>/var/log/containers/&lt;pod-name&gt;_&lt;namespace&gt;_&lt;container&gt;-&lt;id&gt;.log</code><br>
                    <code>/var/log/pods/&lt;namespace&gt;_&lt;pod-name&gt;_&lt;uid&gt;/&lt;container&gt;/</code><br>
                    These are symlinks managed by the container runtime. <code>kubectl logs</code> reads these files via kubelet.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — "Output Streams"</h5>
                    <p><strong>Output streams</strong> means the application's <strong>stdout</strong> (normal output) and <strong>stderr</strong> (errors). The 12-factor convention is: log to these streams and let the <em>platform</em> handle capture, rotation, and shipping — never write logs to a file inside the container, which is invisible to the entire cluster log pipeline (see the sidecar workaround in §33.2 if easypj's Django app insists on file-based logging).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Insight — <code>-l</code> Fans Out to Every Matching Pod</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl logs</code> does <strong>not</strong> search across pods by itself — except via <code>-l</code> (label selector). <code>kubectl logs -l app=easypj --all-containers --tail=50</code> fans out to <em>every</em> pod matching that label, printing all their containers' recent output in one command. This is the fastest way to read an entire easypj Deployment's logs at once — no need to loop over each replica by name.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Misdiagnosis — "My Logs Are Empty But easypj Is Clearly Serving Requests"</h5>
                    <p>Python (and by extension Django/gunicorn) buffers stdout when it detects the output stream isn't an interactive terminal — which is exactly the case inside a container. Result: <code>kubectl logs -f</code> shows <strong>nothing</strong> for minutes at a time even though easypj is actively handling traffic, because Python is holding lines in a memory buffer instead of flushing them line-by-line. Candidates burn real time assuming the logging pipeline itself is broken when it's actually a Python buffering default.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Fix: force unbuffered stdout</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Option 1 — env var on the Deployment's container spec:</span>
env:
- name: PYTHONUNBUFFERED
  value: "1"

<span class="comment"># Option 2 — pass -u directly on the interpreter (Dockerfile CMD/entrypoint):</span>
<span class="comment"># CMD ["python", "-u", "manage.py", "runserver", "0.0.0.0:8000"]</span>

<span class="comment"># Either forces Python to flush stdout after every write, so</span>
<span class="comment"># `kubectl logs -f` shows lines as they happen, not in delayed bursts.</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Gotcha — "error: a container name must be specified"</h5>
                    <p>Multi-container pods (easypj-web with a <code>django</code> + <code>nginx-sidecar</code>) have no implicit "primary" container as far as <code>kubectl logs</code> is concerned. Omit <code>-c</code> and kubectl refuses outright rather than silently guessing:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">$ kubectl logs easypj-web-7d9f-abcde -n easypj
<span class="comment">error: a container name must be specified for pod easypj-web-7d9f-abcde, choose one of: [django nginx-sidecar]</span></code></pre>
                    <p>Under exam time pressure it's easy to mistake this for a broken pod rather than an ambiguous command. Two fixes: always pass <code>-c &lt;container&gt;</code> explicitly, OR annotate the Pod template with <code>kubectl.kubernetes.io/default-container: django</code> so bare <code>kubectl logs</code> (and <code>kubectl exec</code>) both default to that container automatically.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §33.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s33-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">33.2 Cluster-Level Logging</h3>
                <p>Node-level logging only keeps logs on individual nodes. For production, you need a <strong>centralized logging architecture</strong> — a DaemonSet log agent that ships logs to a central store.</p>

                <h4>The Logging Patterns</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Node-Level Agent (Recommended)</h5>
                        <p>DaemonSet runs 1 log agent per node (fluentd, filebeat). Agent reads <code>/var/log/containers/</code> and ships to central store. Lightweight, no pod modification needed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔀</div>
                        <h5>Sidecar Streaming</h5>
                        <p>Sidecar container reads app log files and streams to its own stdout. Node agent then picks up the sidecar's output. Use when app writes to files instead of stdout.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📡</div>
                        <h5>Sidecar with Agent</h5>
                        <p>Sidecar runs its own logging agent (fluentd) that ships directly to the backend. More flexible but resource-heavy — each pod gets its own agent.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📤</div>
                        <h5>Exposing Logs Directly From the Application</h5>
                        <p>The app itself pushes logs straight to a backend (e.g. calls an Elasticsearch/Loki API from inside Django) instead of writing to stdout or a file at all. Kubernetes and the kubelet are completely out of the loop — the application owns collection and shipping end-to-end. Rarely worth it for easypj: it couples app code to a specific logging backend and bypasses <code>kubectl logs</code> entirely (nothing reaches the node's log files), so debugging a broken shipping pipeline means debugging the app itself, not a sidecar or DaemonSet.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💡</div>
                        <h5>CKA Focus</h5>
                        <p>Know the <strong>architecture concepts</strong> and <code>kubectl logs</code> flags. The exam won't ask you to deploy EFK/Loki stacks — but may ask you to create a sidecar logging container.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

