                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Scope: Logging</h5>
                    <p>The CKA exam tests <code>kubectl logs</code> commands, not deploying full logging stacks. Know the <strong>architecture concepts</strong> (DaemonSet agent, node-level logs, sidecar patterns, plus the rarer "app ships its own logs directly" pattern above) but focus your practice on the <code>kubectl logs</code> flags in section 33.1.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy — The Security Camera vs the Off-Site Recorder</h5>
                    <p>Node-level logs are like a security camera that <strong>overwrites its own tape</strong> every few hours (that's log rotation — <code>containerLogMaxSize</code>/<code>containerLogMaxFiles</code>). If you don't check the footage before it loops back to the start, it's gone forever. The DaemonSet log agent is the <strong>off-site recorder</strong> — it copies every frame to a vault (Loki/Elasticsearch) before the local tape gets overwritten, so you can review it days or weeks later, and it survives even if the camera itself (the node) is destroyed.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Sidecar Logging Pattern — When Apps Don't Log to stdout</h4>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why Sidecar Logging?</h5>
                    <p>Some applications write logs to <strong>files</strong> instead of stdout. Since <code>kubectl logs</code> only captures stdout/stderr, you need a sidecar container that reads the log file and streams it to its own stdout — making it visible via <code>kubectl logs</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">sidecar-logging.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  containers:
  <span class="comment"># Main application — writes logs to a file</span>
  - name: django
    image: easypj:v1
    volumeMounts:
    - name: log-volume
      mountPath: /var/log/django

  <span class="comment"># Sidecar — streams the log file to stdout</span>
  - name: log-streamer
    image: busybox:1.36
    args: ['sh', '-c', 'tail -F /var/log/django/app.log']
    volumeMounts:
    - name: log-volume
      mountPath: /var/log/django

  volumes:
  - name: log-volume
    emptyDir: {}

<span class="comment"># Now you can read the app logs via:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl logs easypj-web -c log-streamer</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Container Log Rotation</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Setting</th>
                                <th>Default</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>containerLogMaxSize</code></td>
                                <td>10Mi</td>
                                <td>Max size of a single log file before rotation</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>containerLogMaxFiles</code></td>
                                <td>5</td>
                                <td>Max number of rotated log files kept per container</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>containerLogMaxWorkers</code></td>
                                <td>1</td>
                                <td>How many rotations can run concurrently — tune upward on nodes with many containers so rotation doesn't fall behind</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>containerLogMonitorInterval</code></td>
                                <td>10s</td>
                                <td>How often the kubelet checks whether a container log needs rotating</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Log Rotation Config</h5>
                    <p>These are set in the <strong>kubelet configuration</strong> (<code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>). Default: each container can use up to <strong>50Mi of disk</strong> for logs (10Mi × 5 files). If a node has disk pressure, check if log rotation is properly configured. <code>containerLogMaxWorkers</code>/<code>containerLogMonitorInterval</code> are less commonly tuned but matter on busy nodes running dozens of containers, where a single rotation worker checking only every 10s can fall behind a burst of fast-growing logs.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ A Subtle Rotation Gotcha — <code>kubectl logs</code> Only Ever Reads the LATEST Rotated File</h5>
                    <p>Log rotation isn't only a "logs disappear after a restart" problem — it can bite a container that never restarted at all. If easypj-web writes logs fast enough to hit <code>containerLogMaxSize</code> mid-run, the kubelet rotates to a new file, and <code>kubectl logs</code> can only ever show the contents of the <strong>current (latest) log file</strong> for that instance — content in the older rotated segments (<code>0.log.1</code>, <code>0.log.2</code>…) is invisible to <code>kubectl logs</code> entirely, even though the container never crashed. This is separate from <code>--previous</code> (which handles crash/restart history) — it's about within-instance rotation. The only fix for a chatty container is a shipper (§ above) that ingests every rotated segment before it's deleted, not a <code>kubectl logs</code> flag.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Where <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> Works — and Where It Doesn't</h5>
                    <p>On a <strong>systemd</strong>-managed Linux node (the default for kubeadm, and virtually every CKA lab), the kubelet and container runtime write their own component logs to the systemd journal by default — that's why <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> and <code>journalctl -u containerd</code> work. On a node WITHOUT systemd, these components instead write plain <code>.log</code> files under <code>/var/log</code> (optionally via the <code>kube-log-runner</code> helper), and <code>journalctl</code> simply has nothing to show. This is a different code path from container application logs (<code>/var/log/pods/</code>, read via <code>kubectl logs</code>) — don't confuse "my container's app logs" with "the kubelet's own process logs" when deciding which tool to reach for.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §33.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s33-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">33.3 Monitoring with Metrics Server</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Metrics Server is a lightweight, in-cluster component that collects CPU and memory usage from kubelet on each node. It powers <code>kubectl top</code> and is <strong>required for HPA</strong> (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
