            <section class="section-block" id="s33-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">33.4 Monitoring easypj Resource Usage</h3>
                <p>Let's apply monitoring to our easypj project — find resource-hungry pods, identify OOM risks, and set appropriate limits.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Monitoring easypj — Finding Resource Issues</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Which pods are using the most CPU?</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj --sort-by=cpu
<span class="comment"># NAME                          CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-7d4f8c5-abc12      250m         128Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-db-0                   100m         512Mi     ← DB uses most memory</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-worker-xxxx            50m          64Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># Which pods are using the most memory?</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj --sort-by=memory

<span class="comment"># Per-container breakdown (useful for sidecar pods)</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj --containers
<span class="comment"># NAME                  CONTAINER   CPU    MEMORY</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-xxx        django      200m   100Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-xxx        nginx       50m    28Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># Node-level — is any node under pressure?</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           CPU(cores)  CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)  MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># controlplane   350m        17%    1200Mi         62%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker01       800m        40%    3200Mi         80%     ← ⚠️ High memory!</span>

<span class="comment"># Compare against resource requests/limits</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj | grep -A8 "Requests\|Limits"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Resource Monitoring — Requests vs Limits vs Actual Usage</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  easypj-web pod: CPU usage over time                                     │
│                                                                          │
│  CPU (millicores)                                                        │
│  500m ┤                                                                  │
│       │                                                                  │
│  400m ┤                              ╔═══════════════╗ ← LIMIT (400m)   │
│       │                        ┌─────╨───┐           ║                   │
│  300m ┤                   ┌────┘         └───┐       ║                   │
│       │              ┌────┘                  │       ║                   │
│  250m ┤─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─│─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─│─ ─ ─ ─║─ ← REQUEST (250m)│
│       │         ┌────┘                       └───┐   ║                   │
│  200m ┤    ┌────┘                                └───║──┐                │
│       │────┘                                         ║  └───             │
│  100m ┤                                              ║                   │
│       │                                              ║                   │
│    0m ┤──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶ Time  │
│       │                                                                  │
│  REQUEST = guaranteed minimum (scheduler uses this for placement)        │
│  LIMIT   = maximum allowed (container is throttled/killed above this)    │
│  ACTUAL  = the wavy line (what kubectl top shows)                        │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚠️ If ACTUAL exceeds MEMORY LIMIT → OOMKilled (exit 137)              │
│  ⚠️ If ACTUAL exceeds CPU LIMIT → Throttled (not killed)               │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Interpreting the Signal — What Each Usage Pattern Means</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Signal</th><th>What It Means / What To Do</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>usage ≈ request</code></td><td>Healthy. The scheduler's estimate matches reality.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>usage &lt;&lt; request</code></td><td><strong>Over-provisioned.</strong> Requests too high → wasted node capacity / poor bin-packing. Lower the request.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>usage &gt;&gt; request</code></td><td><strong>Under-provisioned.</strong> Scheduler overcommits the node; neighbors may starve. Raise the request.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>usage near limit</code> (memory)</td><td><strong>At risk</strong> — crossing the memory limit means OOMKill. Raise the limit or fix the leak.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>usage &gt; limit</code> (CPU)</td><td>Impossible for memory (kernel OOMKills first); for CPU it is simply <strong>throttled</strong> to the limit (no kill).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>top row, sorted</td><td>The "hog" — start your investigation here.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Must-Remember — Requests Schedule, Limits Enforce, <code>top</code> Reveals Reality</h5>
                    <p><strong>Requests</strong> drive SCHEDULING (where a pod lands and the node's committed capacity). <strong>Limits</strong> drive ENFORCEMENT (throttle for CPU, OOMKill for memory). <code>kubectl top</code> shows ACTUAL usage. Compare all three to right-size a workload — a pod using far more than its request but well under its limit is a scheduling risk (may starve neighbors), while one near its memory limit is an OOMKill risk.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🎯 Common Misdiagnosis — Three Different "% Used" Numbers, Don't Conflate Them</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Number</th><th>Where It Comes From</th><th>What It Actually Measures</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl top nodes</code> MEMORY%</td><td>cAdvisor reading the node's real cgroup/kernel memory counters</td><td>REAL physical RAM in use right now — OS, kubelet, container runtime, AND every pod's actual usage, all combined</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl describe node</code> "Allocated resources"</td><td>Sum of every scheduled pod's <code>resources.requests</code></td><td>What the SCHEDULER has committed on paper — a reservation, not a measurement</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl top pods</code> summed across a node</td><td>cAdvisor per-pod actual usage</td><td>What workloads alone are using — closer to node top% but still excludes OS/kubelet/runtime overhead</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why These Three Numbers Almost Never Match</h5>
                    <p>A classic exam-time-pressure mistake: seeing <code>kubectl top nodes</code> report 80% memory and assuming pods must be over-requesting, then wasting minutes hunting for a phantom over-provisioned Deployment. In reality, "Allocated resources" (requests sum) and node top% (actual usage) measure completely different things — a node can show LOW allocated-requests% while its ACTUAL top% is high, because system daemons (kubelet, containerd, kube-proxy, journald) and kernel page cache consume real RAM that never appears in any pod's <code>resources.requests</code>. Check all three before concluding "pods are over-provisioned" or "the node is undersized" — they answer three different questions.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🏢 Day-2: Beyond metrics-server — Prometheus &amp; Grafana</h4>
                <p>metrics-server is intentionally minimal: in-memory, no history, only CPU/memory, only for <code>top</code> and the HPA. For dashboards, alerting, and historical or custom metrics, production teams run a fuller observability stack.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📈</div>
                        <h5>Prometheus</h5>
                        <p>Scrapes <code>/metrics</code> endpoints on a schedule and stores results as a <strong>time series</strong> (unlike metrics-server's in-memory-only snapshot). Powers alerting via Alertmanager and historical queries via PromQL.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📊</div>
                        <h5>Grafana</h5>
                        <p>Visualization layer on top of Prometheus (or Loki, or any data source) — dashboards, graphs over time, and threshold-based alert rules with a friendly UI.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>easypj Application Metrics</h5>
                        <p>Expose custom metrics — request latency, queue depth, job-posting throughput — on a <code>/metrics</code> endpoint in the Django app (e.g. via <code>django-prometheus</code>) and let Prometheus scrape them.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>Custom/External Metrics for HPA</h5>
                        <p>The HPA can consume Prometheus-sourced metrics through the <strong>Custom Metrics API</strong> (via Prometheus Adapter) — scaling easypj on request latency or queue depth, not just CPU/memory.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>metrics-server</th><th>Prometheus + Grafana</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Storage</td><td>In-memory only, no history</td><td>Persistent time-series database</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Scope</td><td>CPU/memory only</td><td>Any exposed metric (app, infra, custom)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Powers</td><td><code>kubectl top</code>, basic HPA</td><td>Dashboards, alerting, custom/external-metric HPA</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>CKA depth</td><td>Install + <code>top</code> flags — 🎯 exam topic</td><td>Know the shape — 🏢 day-2/beyond, not deep-config tested</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Know the Shape, Not the Deep Config</h5>
                    <p>The CKA exam tests <code>kubectl top</code> and metrics-server installation, <strong>not</strong> deploying/configuring Prometheus and Grafana. Understand the concept — "metrics-server = live snapshot for <code>top</code>/HPA; Prometheus = historical time-series for dashboards/alerting/custom-metric autoscaling" — and move on.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🔗 Closing the Loop — Which Ch33 Tool Answers Which Ch31/Ch32 Symptom</h4>
                <p>Part 9's layer-stack mental model says Ch33's logs and metrics are "the instruments you read at every layer." Concretely, that means:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Symptom (Ch31/Ch32)</th><th>Layer</th><th>Ch33 Instrument</th><th>What It Reveals</th></tr>
