            <h5>① Container Runtime Log Capture — How stdout/stderr <em>Really</em> Becomes a File</h5>
            <p>When easypj's Django process writes to stdout, no Kubernetes component is "listening" in the way you'd expect. The <strong>container runtime</strong> (containerd or CRI-O) attaches directly to the container's stdout/stderr file descriptors — a Linux-level pipe — and every line that crosses it gets tagged with a timestamp and stream name (stdout/stderr), then appended to a JSON-per-line log file on the node's disk. <code>kubectl logs</code> never touches your container directly; it asks the kubelet to read that file.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  FROM print() IN DJANGO TO YOUR TERMINAL — THE FULL HOP-BY-HOP PATH           │
│                                                                              │
│  🐍 Django process:  print("Job posted: Senior Backend Engineer")            │
│         │  (writes to file descriptor 1 = stdout)                            │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  🐋 containerd/CRI-O — attached to that fd, captures every write             │
│         │  wraps each line: {"log":"Job posted...\n","stream":"stdout",      │
│         │                    "time":"2026-07-04T10:15:03.552Z"}               │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  📄 /var/log/pods/easypj_easypj-web-7d9f-abcde_&lt;uid&gt;/django/0.log            │
│         │  (kubelet creates a friendlier symlink alongside it)               │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  🔗 /var/log/containers/easypj-web-7d9f-abcde_easypj_django-&lt;id&gt;.log         │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  👤 kubectl logs easypj-web-7d9f-abcde -c django                              │
│      → apiserver → kubelet on that node → reads the file → streams it back  │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️ NO agent, NO central store, NO network hop is involved in this basic    │
│     path — it is a pure node-local file read via the kubelet.               │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Because this is a plain file read, if a node goes down or the Pod is deleted, the file — and the logs — are gone forever. The same is true for <strong>eviction</strong>: when the kubelet evicts a Pod (e.g. under <code>DiskPressure</code>/<code>MemoryPressure</code>, see Ch32), its containers and their log files are removed right along with it — eviction is not gentler than deletion when it comes to log survival. There is no "replay from the cluster" for a deleted-or-evicted pod's logs unless a DaemonSet agent already shipped them off-node (§33.2). This is exactly why <code>--previous</code> matters so much: it's your only window into a crashed container's last words before the kubelet eventually garbage-collects that file.
            </div>

            <h5>② The Metrics Pipeline Internals — cAdvisor, the Summary API &amp; the Aggregation Layer</h5>
            <p><code>kubectl top</code> feels instant, but three components cooperate behind it. Every kubelet embeds <strong>cAdvisor</strong>, which reads Linux cgroup accounting files directly — the same kernel counters that track CPU cycles and memory pages per container. metrics-server polls this data from every node on a fixed interval and republishes it as a proper Kubernetes API. (Ch43 §43.2 revisits this exact pipeline at SRE depth — the raw API response's working-set memory nuance and the CRI Container Metrics requirement — once Prometheus is in the picture too.)</p>
