            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>kubectl top</code> and the HPA are BOTH just clients of <code>metrics.k8s.io</code> — that's why a broken metrics-server breaks autoscaling silently too (HPA shows <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code> for current metrics). If HPA scaling seems "stuck," always check <code>kubectl top</code> first — if that's broken, the HPA has no data to scale on.
            </div>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📖 Reading the Raw API Response — Nanocores and Kibibytes</h5>
                <p>Run <code>kubectl get --raw /apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1/nodes/&lt;node&gt;</code> and the raw JSON reports CPU as a string like <code>"487558164n"</code> — that trailing <code>n</code> means <strong>nanocores</strong> (billionths of a CPU core), the unit the Metrics API actually uses internally; <code>kubectl top</code> just converts it to the friendlier millicore (<code>m</code>) display for you. Memory is reported in Ki (kibibytes) as <code>"732212Ki"</code>, not Mi — another silent unit conversion <code>kubectl top</code> performs before printing. Each response also carries a <code>"window"</code> field (typically <code>"30s"</code>) showing the averaging period the CPU rate was computed over — useful context if a number looks like a spike rather than a sustained value.</p>
            </div>

            <h5>③ The Three Metrics APIs — Resource vs Custom vs External</h5>
