            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📖 The Resource Metrics API Has THREE Consumers, Not Two</h5>
                <p>It's easy to name <code>kubectl top</code> and the HPA as the Resource Metrics API's clients and stop there — but the <strong>Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA)</strong> is a third consumer of the exact same <code>metrics.k8s.io</code> data, using it to recommend (or automatically apply) better <code>resources.requests</code>/<code>limits</code> values instead of changing replica count. This is why a broken metrics-server silently breaks THREE things at once: <code>kubectl top</code>, HPA scaling, AND VPA recommendations — not just autoscaling. See the Autoscaling chapter for VPA's own update modes and the "never run HPA and VPA on the same metric" rule.</p>
            </div>

            <h5>④ klog Verbosity &amp; Structured Logging — Reading Control-Plane Component Logs</h5>
            <p>Kubernetes' own components (apiserver, scheduler, controller-manager, kubelet) don't use Django-style logging — they use <strong>klog</strong>, Kubernetes' internal logging library. Understanding its verbosity levels helps you read control-plane logs when troubleshooting the cluster itself, not just your workloads.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  klog VERBOSITY — THE -v=N FLAG ON EVERY CONTROL-PLANE COMPONENT             │
│                                                                              │
│  kube-apiserver --v=2   (default in most distros)                           │
│  kube-scheduler --v=4   (more detail while debugging scheduling decisions)    │
│  kubelet        --v=2                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  -v=0  ┤ Always visible — critical failures only                            │
│  -v=1  ┤ Reasonable default for production                                  │
│  -v=2  ┤ Steady-state info — service startup, config, etc. (COMMON DEFAULT) │
│  -v=3  ┤ Extended info about changes                                        │
│  -v=4  ┤ Debug-level — useful when scheduling/admission is misbehaving      │
│  -v=6+ ┤ Very verbose — request/response tracing (rarely used in prod)      │
│                                                                              │
│  Reading them: journalctl -u kubelet -f    (kubeadm clusters, systemd unit) │
│                kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-scheduler-controlplane       │
│                crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;   (static pods, kubectl can't    │
│                                                see them in kube-system)      │
│                                                                              │
│  NEWER components increasingly emit STRUCTURED logs (key="value" pairs or   │
│  JSON) instead of free text — easier for log-shipping pipelines (§33.2) to  │
│  parse and index automatically.                                             │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>-v level</th><th>What You'll See</th><th>When To Raise It</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>0-1</td><td>Critical errors, minimal noise</td><td>Production steady state</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2</td><td>Startup, config, routine state changes</td><td>Common default — good baseline</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>4</td><td>Debug detail on decisions (e.g. scheduler scoring)</td><td>Diagnosing "why won't this pod schedule?"</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>6+</td><td>Full request/response tracing</td><td>Deep API-server debugging only — very noisy</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Control-plane components running as static pods (apiserver, scheduler, controller-manager, etcd) are NOT reachable via <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n kube-system</code> if the apiserver itself is down — that's a chicken-and-egg problem. When kubectl itself is broken, SSH to the control-plane node and use <code>crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;</code> directly against the container runtime — no API server required.
            </div>

            <h5>⑤ Day-2 Observability Architecture — Where Prometheus Fits Under the Hood</h5>
            <p>metrics-server's "no history, CPU/memory only" limitation is a deliberate design choice, not an oversight — it keeps the in-cluster component tiny and fast for the one job it must never fail at (feeding the HPA). Production teams layer a separate, heavier observability stack alongside it for everything metrics-server intentionally doesn't do.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  DAY-2 OBSERVABILITY STACK — HOW IT SITS ALONGSIDE metrics-server            │
│                                                                              │
│  📦 easypj-web  📦 easypj-worker  📦 postgres         (your workloads)       │
│       │  exposes /metrics (Prometheus text format, via django-prometheus)    │
│       ▼                                                                     │
│  📈 Prometheus  ──(scrapes on interval, e.g. 30s)──►  stores as TIME SERIES  │
│       │                                              (survives restarts,    │
│       │                                               queryable via PromQL) │
│       ├──► 🔔 Alertmanager   (fires alerts: "easypj p99 latency > 2s")       │
│       └──► 📊 Grafana         (dashboards: request rate, error rate, queue   │
│                                depth, over DAYS/WEEKS — not just "now")      │
│                                                                              │
│  MEANWHILE, completely separately:                                           │
│  🤖 kubelet/cAdvisor ──► 📊 metrics-server ──► kubectl top / basic HPA       │
│  (still running — the two stacks coexist, they don't replace each other)     │
│                                                                              │
│  🔗 BRIDGE: Prometheus Adapter exposes selected Prometheus metrics through   │
│     custom.metrics.k8s.io so the HPA can scale on THEM too (see ③).         │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> If an exam question (or a real production incident) asks "why did easypj slow down 3 hours ago," metrics-server cannot answer it — it has no history. That question needs Prometheus + Grafana's time-series data. Know this boundary cold: metrics-server = "what's happening RIGHT NOW" for `top`/HPA; Prometheus = "what happened, and can I alert on it" for everything else.
            </div>

            <h5>⑥ OOMKilled — What Actually Happens Inside the Kernel When easypj Crosses Its Memory Limit</h5>
            <p>§33.4 tells you WHAT to watch for (usage near the memory limit); this is WHY it ends in exit code 137 instead of a graceful warning. A container's memory limit isn't enforced by Kubernetes or even by the container runtime — it's enforced by a Linux <strong>cgroup</strong>, and the actual kill decision is made by the kernel's OOM (Out-Of-Memory) killer, a subsystem that predates containers entirely and doesn't know or care that "easypj-web" is a Kubernetes Pod.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  FROM "resources.limits.memory: 512Mi" TO "OOMKilled, exit 137" — THE PATH    │
│                                                                              │
│  1️⃣ kubelet creates the container via CRI, passing the limit to the        │
│     runtime (containerd/CRI-O)                                              │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  2️⃣ containerd writes the limit into a cgroup file for that container:     │
│     cgroup v2: /sys/fs/cgroup/.../memory.max         = 536870912 (512Mi)    │
│     cgroup v1: /sys/fs/cgroup/memory/.../memory.limit_in_bytes = same       │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  3️⃣ Django process allocates memory normally — the KERNEL tracks every     │
│     page charged to that cgroup, completely invisibly to the app            │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  4️⃣ Allocation would push the cgroup's charged memory PAST memory.max      │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  5️⃣ Kernel's OOM killer wakes up SCOPED TO THAT CGROUP ONLY — it picks      │
│     the process inside the cgroup with the highest oom_score (usually the  │
│     biggest resident-memory consumer) and sends it SIGKILL                  │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  6️⃣ containerd observes the process exited via signal 9 → reports exit     │
│     code 137 (128 + SIGKILL's signal number 9) to the kubelet               │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  7️⃣ kubectl describe pod shows: Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, │
│     Exit Code: 137 — restart policy then creates a NEW container instance   │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️ NEIGHBOR PODS ARE SAFE: the cgroup boundary means the OOM killer never  │
│     touches processes OUTSIDE easypj-web's own cgroup — one leaking Pod     │
│     cannot OOM-kill an unrelated Pod on the same node (unless the NODE      │
│     itself runs out of memory — see the system-level OOM row below).       │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>OOM Scope</th><th>Who Decides</th><th>What Gets Killed</th><th>Signal to easypj</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Container-level (normal case)</td><td>Kernel OOM killer, scoped to the container's cgroup</td><td>The one process inside that cgroup nearest its memory.max</td><td>Pod stays Running; container restarts per <code>restartPolicy</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Node-level (rare, dangerous)</td><td>Kernel OOM killer, scoped to the WHOLE node</td><td>Whatever process the kernel scores worst cluster-wide — can be ANY pod, even ones with plenty of headroom in their own limit</td><td>Node shows <code>MemoryPressure</code>; kubelet starts evicting pods to prevent this</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>kubelet eviction (proactive)</td><td>kubelet itself, BEFORE the kernel is forced to act</td><td>Lowest-priority / most-over-request pods first (soft eviction thresholds)</td><td>Pod shows <code>Evicted</code>, not <code>OOMKilled</code> — a softer, Kubernetes-native shutdown</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>OOMKilled</code> (exit 137) and <code>Evicted</code> look similar but are NOT the same event: OOMKilled means the KERNEL had to forcibly kill a process because a cgroup limit was crossed (reactive, hard, instant); Evicted means the KUBELET proactively removed a whole Pod because the NODE was running low on memory/disk, BEFORE the kernel was forced to intervene (softer, has a grace period). If you see repeated OOMKills, raise that container's memory limit or fix the leak. If you see Evicted pods, the NODE itself is under-provisioned — check <code>kubectl describe node | grep -A3 Pressure</code> for <code>MemoryPressure</code>/<code>DiskPressure</code> conditions.
            </div>

            <h5>⑦ Working Set vs RSS vs Page Cache — Why a Pod Can Get OOMKilled Even Though <code>kubectl top</code> Showed Headroom</h5>
            <p>§33.4's "usage near limit" rule of thumb assumes <code>kubectl top</code>'s number and the kernel's OOM-decision number are the same thing. They're close, but not identical — and the gap explains a genuinely confusing production incident: a container OOMKilled while its last <code>kubectl top</code> reading, taken seconds earlier, was comfortably under the limit.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  THREE DIFFERENT "MEMORY USED" NUMBERS FOR THE SAME CONTAINER                  │
│                                                                              │
│  RSS (Resident Set Size)                                                    │
│    Anonymous memory the process directly allocated (heap, stack) — memory   │
│    that CANNOT be reclaimed without killing something.                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Page Cache                                                                  │
│    Kernel-cached file reads/writes attributed to the container's cgroup —   │
│    e.g. Django reading static files, SQLite/Postgres page reads. RECLAIMABLE│
│    under memory pressure (the kernel can drop it for free, in theory).      │
│                                                                              │
│  Working Set = RSS + (Page Cache the kernel judges "hard to reclaim")        │
│    THIS is the number cAdvisor reports and kubectl top displays —           │
│    NOT raw RSS, and not raw cgroup memory.current either.                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️ THE GAP: a sudden burst of file I/O (e.g. Django serving a large static │
│     asset, or a Postgres checkpoint) can spike a container's TRUE cgroup    │
│     memory.current — which is what memory.max/the OOM killer compares      │
│     against — well above what the LAST kubectl top scrape (up to 15s       │
│     stale) reported as "working set." The OOM killer fires on the live     │
│     cgroup number, not on metrics-server's slightly-stale, cache-adjusted  │
│     working-set number.                                                    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
            </pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Includes Page Cache?</th><th>What Decides OOMKill</th><th>Shown by <code>kubectl top</code>?</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>RSS</td><td>No</td><td>Not directly used</td><td>No</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>cgroup <code>memory.current</code></td><td>Yes (all of it)</td><td><strong>Yes — the kernel compares this to <code>memory.max</code></strong></td><td>No</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Working Set (cAdvisor)</td><td>Partially — only "hard to reclaim" cache</td><td>No — informational only</td><td><strong>Yes — this is the number in <code>kubectl top</code></strong></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Don't treat a <code>kubectl top</code> reading as a hard guarantee of OOM safety — it's an up-to-15-second-stale, cache-adjusted approximation, not the live number the kernel enforces against. For memory-sensitive workloads (a Django worker doing bulk exports, or Postgres under heavy checkpoint I/O), leave real headroom between the observed working set and the limit — don't set the limit to "just barely above what <code>top</code> showed."
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Container output streams (stdout/stderr) become node log files (<code>/var/log/containers</code>, <code>/var/log/pods</code>); <code>kubectl logs</code> reads them. Logs vanish when the pod is deleted — ship them off-node for retention.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl logs</code> flags are your scalpel: <code>--previous</code> (crashed instance), <code>-f</code> (follow), <code>--tail</code> / <code>--since</code> / <code>--since-time</code> (windowing), <code>-c</code> / <code>--all-containers</code> (multi-container), <code>-l</code> (by label across pods).</li>
                    <li>For a CRASHED container the current log may be empty — the crash reason is in <code>--previous</code>. Grab it before the kubelet cleans it up.</li>
                    <li>Cluster logging = a node-agent DaemonSet (or sidecars) forwarding to a store (EFK / Loki); node-level rotation is <code>containerLogMaxSize</code>/<code>containerLogMaxFiles</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl top</code> (nodes/pods) needs metrics-server installed; it scrapes the kubelet/cAdvisor summary API and serves <code>metrics.k8s.io</code>. Use it to compare requests vs limits vs ACTUAL usage and right-size workloads.</li>
                    <li>metrics-server also powers the HPA. No metrics-server → no <code>top</code>, no CPU/memory autoscaling.</li>
                    <li>Requests drive scheduling; limits drive enforcement (CPU throttle / memory OOMKill); <code>top</code> shows reality. All three together tell you what to fix.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

