            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Expecting <code>kubectl top</code> to work without Metrics Server installed</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Install Metrics Server first — it's not included by default in kubeadm clusters</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Thinking Metrics Server stores historical data</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Metrics Server provides <strong>current snapshot only</strong> — for history use Prometheus</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Forgetting <code>--kubelet-insecure-tls</code> in lab environments</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Add this flag in kubeadm/kind/minikube — otherwise Metrics Server can't scrape kubelet</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Assuming <code>kubectl top</code>'s "Metrics API not available" error right after install means something is broken</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Wait ~15-30s for the first scrape cycle, then retry — it's a warm-up delay, not a failure</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Assuming an app that logs to a file inside the container is visible to <code>kubectl logs</code></li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> File-based logs are INVISIBLE to the cluster log pipeline unless a sidecar tails the file to stdout</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Assuming CPU and memory fail the same way when usage crosses the limit</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> CPU over-limit is <strong>throttled</strong> (no kill); MEMORY over-limit is <strong>OOMKilled</strong> — different failure modes for the same "near limit" reading</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Assuming an empty <code>kubectl logs -f</code> output means the log pipeline is broken</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Python/Django buffers stdout by default when it isn't attached to a TTY — set <code>PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1</code> before assuming anything else is wrong</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Treating <code>kubectl top nodes</code>'s memory% as the same number as "Allocated resources" in <code>describe node</code></li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Top% is REAL usage (incl. OS/kubelet/runtime overhead); Allocated% is the SUM OF REQUESTS — they answer different questions and rarely match</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Trusting a <code>kubectl top</code> reading as a hard guarantee against OOMKill</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> <code>top</code> shows an up-to-15s-stale "working set"; the kernel's OOM decision uses the LIVE cgroup <code>memory.current</code>, which can spike faster than the last scrape shows</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

