                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the actual difference between <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code> and <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead><tr><th>Container Instance</th><th><code>kubectl logs</code> (no flag)</th><th><code>kubectl logs --previous</code></th></tr></thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td>Instance #1 (crashed)</td><td>Not shown</td><td><strong>Shown</strong> — this is what "previous" means</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>Instance #2 (current, just restarted)</td><td><strong>Shown</strong> (often nearly empty)</td><td>Not shown</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Each container restart creates a NEW instance with its own log stream. The default <code>kubectl logs</code> always reads the CURRENT (latest) instance — which for a freshly-crashed-and-restarted container is often nearly empty, because it just started. The crash reason lives in the PRIOR instance's stream, only reachable via <code>--previous</code>. This is the single most-forgotten flag on the CKA exam.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's Django app is configured to write logs to <code>/var/log/django/app.log</code> instead of stdout. Why does <code>kubectl logs</code> show nothing, and how do you fix it without changing the app's code?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  WITHOUT SIDECAR                    WITH SIDECAR              │
│  📦 django → app.log (file)         📦 django → app.log       │
│       ✗ never touches stdout             │ (shared emptyDir)  │
│       ✗ kubectl logs = empty             ▼                    │
│                                     📦 log-streamer sidecar    │
│                                        tail -F app.log         │
│                                            │                   │
│                                            ▼ stdout            │
│                                     ✓ kubectl logs -c          │
│                                       log-streamer  WORKS      │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                            </pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl logs</code> ONLY reads stdout/stderr captured by the container runtime — it never inspects arbitrary files inside a container's filesystem. Since the app writes to a file, that file is invisible to the entire cluster log pipeline. The fix without touching app code: add a sidecar container that shares an <code>emptyDir</code> volume with the app and runs <code>tail -F app.log</code>, streaming the file's content to ITS OWN stdout — which <code>kubectl logs -c log-streamer</code> can then read.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the three Kubernetes metrics APIs used for autoscaling, and identify which one <code>kubectl top</code> and a plain CPU-based HPA both use.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead><tr><th>API</th><th>Served By</th><th>Used By <code>kubectl top</code>?</th></tr></thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td><code>metrics.k8s.io</code> (Resource Metrics API)</td><td>metrics-server</td><td>✅ Yes — this is the one</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><code>custom.metrics.k8s.io</code> (Custom Metrics API)</td><td>Prometheus Adapter</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><code>external.metrics.k8s.io</code> (External Metrics API)</td><td>Cloud-provider adapter</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Only the Resource Metrics API (<code>metrics.k8s.io</code>, served by metrics-server) powers <code>kubectl top</code> and basic CPU/memory HPA scaling. The other two APIs let the HPA scale on app-specific metrics (queue depth, request rate) or metrics from outside the cluster — but BOTH require an additional adapter component that metrics-server alone does not provide.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You just installed metrics-server. <code>kubectl top pods</code> returns "Metrics API not available." Is this a real problem?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   t=0s    kubectl apply -f metrics-server.yaml
   t=5s    Pod scheduled, image pulling…
   t=15s   Pod Running, but 0 scrapes completed yet   ← "Metrics API not available"
   t=20s   First scrape cycle completes (--metric-resolution=15s)
   t=21s   kubectl top pods  →  ✅ real numbers appear
                            </pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Not necessarily. Right after install, metrics-server needs one full scrape interval (~15-30s) before it has ANY data to serve. "Metrics API not available" during that window is expected, not a failure. Wait for <code>kubectl -n kube-system rollout status deploy metrics-server</code> to confirm the Pod is Ready, then retry — only investigate further (TLS errors, missing flag) if it's STILL failing after ~1 minute.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj-web is using 480Mi of memory. Its request is 256Mi and its limit is 512Mi. Is this pod healthy, over-provisioned, or at risk?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Number</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td>Request</td><td>256Mi</td><td>Scheduling guarantee — usage is 1.9× this, so it's under-provisioned relative to request</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td class="gotcha-wrong">Actual (top)</td><td>480Mi</td><td>93.75% of the limit — <strong>AT RISK</strong></td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>Limit</td><td>512Mi</td><td>Hard ceiling — crossing it triggers OOMKill</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>480Mi is dangerously close to the 512Mi memory limit — this pod is at real risk of being OOMKilled under a small additional load spike. Separately, usage (480Mi) far exceeding the request (256Mi) means the SCHEDULER under-reserved for this pod — raise both the request (to reflect reality and protect neighbors) and the limit (to give headroom before OOMKill).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does a container that exceeds its CPU limit get throttled, while one that exceeds its memory limit gets killed outright?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Nature</th><th>Over-Limit Behavior</th><th>Enforced By</th></tr></thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td>CPU</td><td>Compressible — can be rationed over time</td><td><strong>Throttled</strong> (process keeps running, just slower)</td><td>CFS cgroup quota</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>Memory</td><td>Incompressible — can't "wait" for free RAM</td><td><strong>OOMKilled</strong> (exit code 137)</td><td>Linux kernel OOM killer via cgroup</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CPU is a compressible resource — the kernel can simply give a process fewer CPU cycles per time slice (throttling) without breaking anything, so it never needs to kill the process. Memory is incompressible — a process holding memory it needs cannot be "slowed down" into using less; the only enforcement mechanism available to the kernel is to kill it (OOMKill, exit code 137) once it crosses the cgroup's memory limit. This is why "near limit" reads completely differently for the two resources.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A worker node's kubelet crashes. What happens to <code>kubectl top pods</code> output for pods on that node?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  worker02: kubelet DOWN                                          │
│                                                                    │
│  📦 easypj-web-xxx  ──✗──▶  cAdvisor (part of dead kubelet)       │
│                                    │                              │
│                                    ✗  metrics-server can't scrape │
│                                       worker02's /stats/summary   │
│                                    │                              │
│  kubectl top pods -n easypj                                       │
│  NAME              CPU    MEMORY                                   │
│  easypj-web-xxx    &lt;unknown&gt;   &lt;unknown&gt;   ← this node's pods    │
│  easypj-web-yyy    142m   310Mi                ← other nodes fine │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                            </pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>metrics-server scrapes each kubelet independently. If worker02's kubelet is down, cAdvisor (which lives INSIDE the kubelet process) is also unreachable, so metrics-server cannot get data for that node's pods — they show <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code> in <code>kubectl top</code> output while pods on healthy nodes still report correctly. This is a good signal: partial <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code> readings point you straight at a node-level problem, not a metrics-server problem.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the two cluster-level logging architecture patterns. When would you choose a sidecar over the recommended DaemonSet agent?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>How Many Agents?</th><th>Choose When…</th></tr></thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td>Node-agent DaemonSet</td><td>1 per node (all pods share it)</td><td><strong>Default choice</strong> — app already logs to stdout, zero app changes needed</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>Sidecar</td><td>1 per Pod (extra overhead)</td><td>App insists on file-based logging and you can't change its code — sidecar tails the file to stdout so the node agent (or kubectl logs) can still see it</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The DaemonSet node-agent pattern (Fluentd/Fluent Bit/Promtail, 1 per node) is the default and most resource-efficient — it requires zero changes to your Pods. The sidecar pattern costs an extra container PER Pod (more resource overhead cluster-wide) and is reserved for the specific case where an application cannot be made to log to stdout directly — e.g. a legacy component that only supports file-based logging.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod has restarted 8 times. You run <code>kubectl logs pod --previous</code> and get "previous terminated container not found." Why, and what should you have done differently?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  RESTART TIMELINE — --previous SEES ONLY THE LAST INSTANCE      │
│                                                                 │
│  Restart 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 6 → 7 → 8 (current)                │
│                              └──┬──┘                            │
│                     --previous reaches ONLY here                │
│                                                                 │
│  Instances #1-6 may or may not still be on disk — that is       │
│  kubelet container GC, NOT containerLogMaxSize/Files (which     │
│  only rotates ONE instance's OWN growing log — see §33.2).      │
│  Either way, --previous can NEVER reach past #7 — it always     │
│  means "the ONE instance immediately before the current one."   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                            </pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>--previous</code> only ever shows the SINGLE most recent prior instance — not a full history of all 8 restarts. That "instance history" pruning is done by the kubelet's container garbage collection, a <em>separate</em> mechanism from the <code>containerLogMaxSize</code>/<code>containerLogMaxFiles</code> rotation covered in §33.2 (that setting only rotates a single running instance's own growing log file, it does not govern how many past crashed instances survive). If "previous terminated container not found" appears, either that immediately-prior instance was already garbage-collected (rare, but happens under disk pressure or very rapid restart loops), or the container had already been fully removed by the runtime. The lesson: grab <code>--previous</code> logs IMMEDIATELY after the first restart is observed — don't wait, because each subsequent restart pushes the useful evidence further out of reach, and <code>--previous</code> never reaches back further than one instance regardless.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">When would you reach for Prometheus + Grafana instead of just metrics-server for easypj?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>metrics-server</th><th>Prometheus + Grafana</th></tr></thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td>"What's easypj-web using RIGHT NOW?"</td><td>✅ Perfect fit</td><td>Overkill</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>"Why did latency spike 3 hours ago?"</td><td>❌ No history at all</td><td>✅ Time-series query (PromQL)</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>"Alert me if error rate &gt; 5%"</td><td>❌ No alerting</td><td>✅ Alertmanager</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>"Scale on Celery queue depth, not CPU"</td><td>❌ CPU/memory only</td><td>✅ via Custom Metrics API</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>metrics-server is deliberately minimal — a live snapshot for <code>kubectl top</code> and basic HPA, nothing more. The moment you need HISTORY (what happened over time), ALERTING (notify humans automatically), or CUSTOM APPLICATION METRICS (queue depth, request latency, business KPIs) as an autoscaling signal, you need Prometheus's time-series storage plus Grafana's dashboards — the two stacks run side-by-side, they don't replace each other.</p>
