                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you get the last 100 lines of logs from a pod and stream new logs in real time?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">last 100 lines</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Last 100 lines:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --tail=100

<span class="comment"># Stream (follow) logs in real time:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod -f

<span class="comment"># Combine: last 100 lines THEN stream:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --tail=100 -f

<span class="comment"># Logs since a specific time:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --since=1h     <span class="comment"># Last 1 hour</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --since=30m    <span class="comment"># Last 30 minutes</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --since=5s     <span class="comment"># Last 5 seconds</span>

<span class="comment"># Logs since a timestamp:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --since-time="2024-01-15T10:00:00Z"

<span class="comment"># Add timestamps to log lines:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --timestamps=true
<span class="comment"># 2024-01-15T10:30:00Z  [INFO] Server started on port 8080</span>

<span class="comment"># Previous container logs (after crash/restart):</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --previous

<span class="comment"># Combine for exam speed:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --tail=50 --timestamps -f</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>--tail=N</code> shows the last N lines (avoids flooding). <code>-f</code> follows/streams new logs. <code>--since</code> accepts durations (1h, 30m) or timestamps. <code>--timestamps</code> prepends timestamps to each line. On the CKA, use <code>--tail=50 -f</code> to quickly see recent logs and watch for new errors. <code>--previous</code> gets logs from the previous container instance (crucial after crashes).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a sidecar container that reads the main application's log file and streams it to stdout.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: app-with-sidecar
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: busybox
    command: ['sh', '-c',
      'while true; do echo "$(date) - Processing request" >> /var/log/app.log; sleep 5; done']
    volumeMounts:
    - name: log-volume
      mountPath: /var/log

  - name: log-sidecar
    image: busybox
    command: ['sh', '-c', 'tail -f /var/log/app.log']
    volumeMounts:
    - name: log-volume
      mountPath: /var/log

  volumes:
  - name: log-volume
    emptyDir: {}

<span class="comment"># Now you can view app logs via kubectl:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl logs app-with-sidecar -c log-sidecar</span>
<span class="comment"># 2024-01-15 10:30:00 - Processing request</span>
<span class="comment"># 2024-01-15 10:30:05 - Processing request</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Sidecar logging pattern: the main app writes logs to a file, the sidecar reads that file with <code>tail -f</code> and outputs to stdout. Both containers share a volume (<code>emptyDir</code>). This makes file-based logs accessible via <code>kubectl logs</code>. On the CKA, you may need to add a sidecar container to stream logs from a file. Key: shared volume, <code>tail -f</code> in the sidecar, and matching <code>mountPath</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

