                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A cluster has multiple pods with high restart counts. How do you investigate?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find pods with high restart counts</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find pods with high restart counts</span>
kubectl get pods -A --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'
<span class="comment"># Shows ascending — look at the bottom for highest</span>

<span class="comment"># Reverse sort trick (show highest first):</span>
kubectl get pods -A --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount' \
-o custom-columns=\
'NS:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,RESTARTS:.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount,STATUS:.status.phase'
<span class="comment"># NS        NAME         RESTARTS  STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># default   crash-app    45        Running  ← Lots of restarts!</span>
<span class="comment"># prod      buggy-api    23        Running</span>
<span class="comment"># default   web-app      0         Running</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check WHY it's restarting</span>
kubectl describe pod crash-app | grep -A5 "Last State"
<span class="comment"># Last State:  Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#   Reason:    OOMKilled    ← Memory issue</span>
<span class="comment">#   Exit Code: 137</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check recent restarts with events</span>
kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=crash-app \
--sort-by=.lastTimestamp

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check liveness probe</span>
kubectl describe pod crash-app | grep -A5 Liveness
<span class="comment"># If liveness probe fails → pod gets killed and restarted</span>

<span class="comment"># Common restart causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># OOMKilled (137) → Increase memory limit</span>
<span class="comment"># Exit Code 1     → App error, check logs --previous</span>
<span class="comment"># Liveness failed → Fix probe path/port/threshold</span>
<span class="comment"># Readiness fail  → Doesn't cause restart, but indicates issues</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Sort by restart count to find the most unstable pods. <code>--sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'</code> sorts ascending (highest at bottom). Check <code>describe pod</code> for "Last State: Terminated" with the reason. Top causes of restarts: OOMKilled (exit 137), liveness probe failures, and app errors (exit 1). Note: high restart count + Running status = the pod keeps crashing and recovering.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you monitor cluster-wide resource usage and identify capacity issues?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node-level overview</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Node-level overview:</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       CPU(cores)  CPU%  MEMORY(bytes)  MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># master     250m        12%   1500Mi         75%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   1800m       90%   6000Mi         80%  ← High!</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2   100m        5%    400Mi          20%</span>

<span class="comment"># Identify overloaded node:</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1 at 90% CPU and 80% memory → capacity issue!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check what's on the overloaded node:</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector spec.nodeName=worker-1 \
-o custom-columns='NS:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,CPU:.spec.containers[0].resources.requests.cpu'

<span class="comment"># Check if pods are unevenly distributed:</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o wide | awk '{print $8}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
<span class="comment">#  15 worker-1    ← Too many pods!</span>
<span class="comment">#   3 worker-2</span>
<span class="comment">#   2 master</span>

<span class="comment"># Check node allocations:</span>
for node in $(kubectl get nodes -o name); do
echo "=== $node ==="
kubectl describe $node | grep -A8 "Allocated resources"
done

<span class="comment"># Check for resource quotas:</span>
kubectl get resourcequotas -A

<span class="comment"># Check for limit ranges:</span>
kubectl get limitranges -A

<span class="comment"># Quick cluster health:</span>
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running | grep -v Completed
kubectl top nodes
kubectl get events -A --field-selector type=Warning --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | head -10</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl top nodes</code> gives CPU% and Memory% per node — look for nodes above 80%. Check pod distribution with <code>-o wide</code> and count per node. If pods are unevenly distributed, it might indicate affinity rules or the scheduler choosing based on available resources. <code>describe node</code> shows allocated vs allocatable, which tells you what the scheduler sees. ResourceQuotas and LimitRanges control resource usage at the namespace level.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

