            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 33.5 — End-to-End Metrics Server Lab Fix</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Scenario</h5>
                        <p>You installed metrics-server on a fresh kubeadm cluster. Ten minutes later, <code>kubectl top nodes</code> STILL returns "Metrics API not available." Diagnose and fix it end-to-end.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Confirm metrics-server's Pod status and check its logs</li>
                            <li>Identify the root cause from the log output</li>
                            <li>Apply the fix and verify <code>kubectl top</code> works</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> After 10 minutes, this is NOT the normal warm-up delay — something else is wrong. kubeadm clusters use self-signed kubelet certs by default.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check pod status and logs</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep metrics-server
<span class="comment"># metrics-server-xxx   1/1   Running   0   10m   ← Pod IS running</span>

kubectl logs -n kube-system deploy/metrics-server
<span class="comment"># E0704 ... unable to fetch metrics: x509: certificate signed by</span>
<span class="comment"># unknown authority   ← ROOT CAUSE: TLS verification failure</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Root cause = kubelet's self-signed cert fails verification</span>
<span class="comment"># (this is expected in kubeadm/kind/minikube — no shared CA by default)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Apply the fix — add --kubelet-insecure-tls</span>
kubectl patch deployment metrics-server -n kube-system --type='json' \
  -p='[{"op":"add","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/args/-","value":"--kubelet-insecure-tls"}]'

<span class="comment"># Wait for rollout, then verify</span>
kubectl -n kube-system rollout status deploy metrics-server
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           CPU(cores)   CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)   MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># controlplane   250m         12%    1500Mi          75%    ← WORKING</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
