   🔑 Requests (scheduling) vs Limits (enforcement) vs kubectl top (reality) —
      compare all three to right-size any easypj workload.
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Chapter 33 is two independent pipelines that both answer "is easypj healthy?" The LOGS pipeline captures every container's stdout/stderr into a node-local file that <code>kubectl logs</code> reads directly through the kubelet — no agent required for the basic path, though a DaemonSet log agent (or sidecar, for file-based loggers) can ship copies to a central store like Loki or Elasticsearch before the node-local copy rotates away.</p>
                    <p>The METRICS pipeline is built on cAdvisor (embedded in every kubelet), scraped by metrics-server into an in-memory snapshot served as the <code>metrics.k8s.io</code> API — the single source both <code>kubectl top</code> and the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler read from. Neither logs nor metrics survive a Pod's deletion or a metrics-server restart on their own; that permanence has to be engineered in (log shippers, or Prometheus for metrics history).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> <code>--previous</code> is your first move on any crash, <code>--sort-by=cpu|memory</code> is your first move on any "what's the hog" question, and comparing requests vs limits vs actual usage tells you whether a workload is healthy, wasteful, or one bad day away from an OOMKill. Master these two pipelines and you can triage almost any broken or slow easypj workload inside the 30%-weight Troubleshooting domain.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article><!-- end ch33 -->

