                <h3 class="section-h">34.1 Scenario Collection — 12 Must-Practice Scenarios</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Each scenario presents a broken cluster state and walks you through the exact commands and thinking process to diagnose and fix it. These mirror real CKA exam tasks.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Where the deep networking internals live</h5>
                    <p>This chapter is the <strong>synthesis and drill layer</strong> for the whole Troubleshooting domain — it assumes you already know the protocol-level material and focuses on turning it into fast, repeatable fixes. If a scenario below mentions Services, DNS, or NetworkPolicy and you want the full "how it works" deep dive (EndpointSlices, the Corefile, iptables/IPVS translation, Flannel vs Calico), that content lives in <a href="#ch18">Chapter 18 (Services Deep Dive)</a>, <a href="#ch19">Chapter 19 (DNS &amp; CoreDNS)</a>, and <a href="#ch20">Chapter 20 (Ingress &amp; Network Policies)</a>. Nothing here duplicates those chapters — it drills the symptom-to-fix reflex on top of them.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🚑 The "First 60 Seconds" Triage Bundle — when you truly don't know what's broken</h4>
                <p>Before you commit to any one of the 12 scenarios below, run one bundle that answers "how big is the blast radius?" A whole-cluster fault (control plane, DNS) needs a completely different first move than a single broken pod — and guessing wrong here is the #1 way candidates burn 10 minutes chasing the wrong layer.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">run this FIRST, every time, before you hypothesize anything</span></div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># One copy-pasted bundle — read top to bottom, don't skip ahead:</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
kubectl get pods -A -o wide | grep -v Running
kubectl get events -A --sort-by='''.lastTimestamp''' | tail -20

<span class="comment"># Interpreting the output:</span>
<span class="comment">#  1. Any node NotReady?              → Scenario 1. Fix it FIRST — everything</span>
<span class="comment">#     else may be a downstream symptom of this one root cause.</span>
<span class="comment">#  2. Non-Running pods in kube-system → control plane/DNS (Scenarios 5,6,7,11)</span>
<span class="comment">#     Non-Running pods in your app ns → workload issue (Scenarios 2,4,9,12)</span>
<span class="comment">#  3. Recent Events mentioning "FailedScheduling"/"BackOff"/"Unhealthy" name</span>
<span class="comment">#     the exact broken object — jump straight to its scenario, skip guessing.</span>
<span class="comment">#  4. Nodes Ready, all pods Running, but "can't connect" reported anyway?</span>
<span class="comment">#     → this bundle won't show a Service-layer bug. Go straight to the</span>
<span class="comment">#     4-Layer Network Diagnostic Ladder below (Scenarios 3, 5, 8).</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The 4-Layer Network Diagnostic Ladder</h4>
                <p>Most "it can't connect" tickets are actually layered: a request from <code>easypj-web</code> to <code>easypj-db</code> crosses the pod, the Service, DNS, and the NetworkPolicy/kube-proxy dataplane before it reaches the other container — and any one of those four layers can silently swallow it. Isolate top-down, never bottom-up, and never jump straight to "the app is broken."</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>❓ "easypj-web can't reach easypj-db" — isolate top-down</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  L1 POD      Are both pods Running &amp; Ready?                             │
│              $ kubectl get pod -o wide                                  │
│                    │ Ready? ──▶ yes, go to L2 │ no ──▶ Ch31 pod triage   │
│                    ▼                                                     │
│  L2 SERVICE  Does the Service have ENDPOINTS?                            │
│              $ kubectl get endpoints easypj-db                          │
│              (empty = selector/label mismatch OR pod not Ready)          │
│                    │ populated? ──▶ yes, go to L3 │ no ──▶ fix selector  │
│                    ▼                                                     │
│  L3 DNS      Does the name resolve?                                      │
│              $ kubectl exec t -- nslookup easypj-db.easypj              │
│              (fail = CoreDNS down / wrong namespace / ndots trap)        │
│                    │ resolves? ──▶ yes, go to L4 │ no ──▶ Ch19 DNS fix   │
│                    ▼                                                     │
│  L4 POLICY   Is a NetworkPolicy blocking port 5432?                     │
│              $ kubectl get netpol; kubectl exec t -- curl -m3 easypj-db:5432│
│                    │ still broken? ──▶ kube-proxy mode/health, MTU,      │
│                    │                    conntrack (see Under-the-Hood)   │
│                                                                          │
│  🔁 Method everywhere: GATHER ► READ(events/logs) ► ISOLATE layer        │
│                        ► FIX ► VERIFY                                    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy — the postal trace</h5>
                    <p>Debugging a connection is like tracing a letter through the mail. L1 asks: is the sender/recipient even at home (pod Running/Ready)? L2 asks: is there a valid street address routed to them (Service endpoints)? L3 asks: does the name in the address book resolve to that street (DNS)? L4 asks: is there a checkpoint refusing the parcel (NetworkPolicy/kube-proxy)? You never rewrite the letter (the app code) until you've ruled out the address book and the checkpoint.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The 5-Step Troubleshooting Method</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>IDENTIFY</h5>
                        <p>What's broken? <code>kubectl get pods/nodes/svc</code> — find the failing resource and its status.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>GATHER</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe</code> + <code>kubectl logs</code> — collect Events, conditions, and error messages.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>ANALYZE</h5>
                        <p>Read the error messages. Match symptoms to known causes. Narrow down root cause.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>FIX</h5>
                        <p>Apply the fix — edit YAML, restart service, add missing resource. Make ONE change at a time.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>VERIFY</h5>
                        <p>Confirm the fix worked. <code>kubectl get</code> + <code>kubectl describe</code> — status should be healthy.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Troubleshooting Priority Layers</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏗️</div>
                        <h5>Infrastructure Layer</h5>
                        <p>Nodes NotReady, kubelet down, etcd unhealthy, certificates expired, kubeconfig issues. Fix infrastructure <strong>first</strong> — nothing else works if the cluster is broken.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Workload Layer</h5>
                        <p>Pod Pending, CrashLoopBackOff, PVC not binding, resource limits. Most common exam tasks — fix pod/deployment configs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>Networking Layer</h5>
                        <p>Service not accessible, DNS failing, NetworkPolicy blocking. Hardest to debug — check selectors, endpoints, kube-proxy, CoreDNS.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⏱️</div>
                        <h5>CKA Time Strategy</h5>
                        <p>Easy tasks first (2-3 min each), hard tasks last. Skip if stuck &gt;8 minutes. Return to skipped tasks at the end. <strong>Never zero a question.</strong></p>
