│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc -n easypj                        │
│     ENDPOINTS: 10.244.1.5:8000   ← ✅ Pod is now connected!            │
│                                                                         │
│     $ curl http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:30080                                       │
│     → ✅ easypj responds!                                               │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- ── Scenario 4: CrashLoopBackOff ── -->
                <h4>Scenario 4: CrashLoopBackOff</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Problem</h5>
                    <p><code>easypj-db</code> pod keeps crashing — restart count is climbing and backoff interval increasing.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔴 CrashLoopBackOff — Missing Environment Variable</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     NAME         READY   STATUS             RESTARTS      AGE           │
│     easypj-db-0  0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   7 (3m ago)    15m           │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Logs from the PREVIOUS crash:                              │
│     $ kubectl logs easypj-db-0 -n easypj --previous                     │
│                                                                         │
│     Error: Database is uninitialized and superuser password              │
│     is not specified. You must specify POSTGRES_PASSWORD to a    ◄──    │
│     non-empty value for the superuser.                                  │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE — Check pod spec:                                           │
│     $ kubectl describe pod easypj-db-0 -n easypj                        │
│     Containers:                                                         │
│       postgres:                                                         │
│         Image: postgres:15                                              │
│         Environment: &lt;none&gt;    ◄── No env vars set! Missing            │
│                                    POSTGRES_PASSWORD                     │
│     Last State: Terminated (Exit Code 1)  ← App error                   │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Add the required env var via Secret:                          │
│     $ kubectl create secret generic db-creds \                          │
│         --from-literal=POSTGRES_PASSWORD=securepass123 -n easypj        │
│                                                                         │
│     Then patch the StatefulSet:                                          │
│     $ kubectl edit statefulset easypj-db -n easypj                       │
│     env:                                                                │
│     - name: POSTGRES_PASSWORD                                           │
│       valueFrom:                                                        │
│         secretKeyRef:                                                   │
│           name: db-creds                                                │
│           key: POSTGRES_PASSWORD                                        │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj -w                                     │
│     easypj-db-0   1/1   Running   0   20s   ← ✅ No more crashes!      │
│     $ kubectl logs easypj-db-0 -n easypj                                │
│     → "database system is ready to accept connections"                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>🔢 CrashLoopBackOff Exit-Code Decoder — don't assume "app bug"</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Exit Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Root Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Process exited cleanly</td><td>The main process finished successfully — but <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> keeps restarting it. This is a <strong>workload-type</strong> bug, not an app bug.</td><td>Use a Job/CronJob for run-to-completion work, or fix the entrypoint to keep running in the foreground</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Generic application error</td><td>Uncaught exception, missing config, failed startup check</td><td><code>kubectl logs --previous</code> — read the actual stack trace/error text</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>137</code></td><td>SIGKILL (128+9)</td><td><strong>OOMKilled</strong> — container exceeded its memory limit</td><td>Raise <code>resources.limits.memory</code>, or find the leak with <code>kubectl top pod --containers</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>143</code></td><td>SIGTERM (128+15)</td><td>Graceful shutdown requested but the app didn't exit — kubelet escalated to SIGKILL after the grace period</td><td>Handle SIGTERM in the app (close connections, exit promptly) or raise <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Common Misdiagnosis: "It's CrashLoopBackOff, must be an app bug"</h5>
                    <p>The single most common candidate mistake under time pressure: seeing <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> and immediately assuming the application code is broken. <strong>Exit 0</strong> means the opposite — the process worked and finished cleanly; the real bug is the Kubernetes <em>workload type</em> (a batch script deployed as a Deployment instead of a Job). <strong>Exit 137</strong> means the app never even got to run its own error-handling — the kernel killed it for memory. Read the exit code in <code>kubectl describe pod</code> (Last State) BEFORE reading <code>--previous</code> logs; it tells you which of three completely different fixes you need before you've read a single log line.</p>
                </div>

