                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Not one of the 12, but a common exam sighting: Init containers</h5>
                    <p>A pod stuck at <code>0/1 Init:0/1</code> (or worse, <code>Init:Error</code>/<code>Init:CrashLoopBackOff</code>) is a 13th pattern worth recognizing on sight: <strong>init containers run sequentially and must ALL complete successfully before any main container starts</strong> — a single failing init container blocks the pod forever, and it's a completely different root cause than an ordinary <code>Pending</code> or <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>. The full walkthrough (reading <code>status.initContainerStatuses</code>, using <code>-c &lt;init-name&gt;</code> with <code>logs</code>/<code>describe</code>, and the exact status-string meanings) already lives in <a href="#ch31">Chapter 31</a> — this callout exists purely so you recognize the <code>Init:</code> prefix instantly during the exam instead of mistaking it for a plain scheduling or crash failure.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Common Gotcha: Multiple Symptoms, One Root Cause</h5>
                    <p>On the CKA exam, a single failure (e.g., kubelet down) can produce <strong>many symptoms at once</strong> — Pods in Terminating state, Services returning errors, DNS failing. <strong>Always start at the infrastructure layer</strong> (nodes → control plane → workloads) and work upward. Fixing the root cause often resolves several visible symptoms simultaneously.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

