                <h4>🚧 Common Misdiagnoses Under Time Pressure</h4>
                <p>These four are the actual, repeated mistakes candidates make on the real exam — not textbook traps. Each one costs 5-10 minutes because the candidate has to backtrack after discovering the "fix" didn't fix anything.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗑️</div>
                        <h5>Deleting the crashing pod first</h5>
                        <p>Deleting a <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> pod before reading <code>--previous</code> logs destroys your only evidence of the last crash. The new pod just repeats the same crash — you spent time and gained nothing. Always <code>describe</code> + <code>logs --previous</code> BEFORE deleting anything.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div>
                        <h5>Forgetting the -n flag</h5>
                        <p>Running <code>kubectl get pods</code> with no namespace and seeing nothing is not "the pod doesn't exist" — it's usually "you're in the wrong namespace." Read the question's namespace FIRST, then <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;</code> once, for the whole question.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🩹</div>
                        <h5>Patching the symptom, not the root cause</h5>
                        <p>Increasing replica count to "fix" a Pending Deployment when the real issue is a resource-starved node just creates MORE Pending pods. If the symptom doesn't fully clear after step 4 (FIX), you patched a symptom — go back to GATHER, don't pile on more changes.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📄</div>
                        <h5>Assuming a saved file applies itself</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl edit</code> on a live object applies immediately — but editing a static-pod manifest or a local YAML you haven't run <code>kubectl apply -f</code> on changes NOTHING until kubelet notices it (static pods) or you explicitly apply it. Confirm with a "configured"/"edited" message or a fresh <code>get</code>, don't just trust that saving a file did something.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

