            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Multi-Layer Troubleshooting Scenarios Work: The Systematic Debugging Methodology, Dependency Chain Analysis, and the CKA Troubleshooting Mindset</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ The CKA Troubleshooting Methodology — Systematic Root Cause Analysis</h5>
            <p>Complex CKA scenarios involve multiple failing components. The key is a systematic top-down approach that isolates the root cause layer by layer.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SYSTEMATIC TROUBLESHOOTING METHODOLOGY                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  LAYER MODEL (debug from top down):                                           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ LAYER 1: APPLICATION (logs, exit codes, probes)                       │   │
│  │   └── LAYER 2: POD (status, events, describe, resource usage)         │   │
│  │       └── LAYER 3: CONTROLLER (Deployment/RS/DaemonSet status)        │   │
│  │           └── LAYER 4: SERVICE/NETWORK (endpoints, DNS, NetworkPolicy)│   │
│  │               └── LAYER 5: NODE (kubelet, containerd, disk, memory)   │   │
│  │                   └── LAYER 6: CONTROL PLANE (API, etcd, scheduler)   │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  SCENARIO: "Deployment is running but unreachable"                           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. APP: kubectl logs → app listening on correct port? ✅              │   │
│  │ 2. POD: kubectl describe → probes passing? ready? ✅                  │   │
│  │ 3. CONTROLLER: kubectl get deploy,rs → desired=ready? ✅              │   │
│  │ 4. SERVICE: kubectl get svc → selector matches pod labels?           │   │
│  │    kubectl get endpoints → ENDPOINTS is &lt;none&gt;? ❌                   │   │
│  │    → Service selector doesn't match pod labels!                      │   │
│  │    → Fix: update Service selector or pod labels                      │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ 5. VERIFY: curl &lt;svc-ip&gt;:&lt;port&gt; → works now ✅                       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  DEPENDENCY CHAIN ANALYSIS — Finding the Root Cause:                         │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ When multiple things are broken, find the DEPENDENCY that broke first│   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Chain: DNS down → Services unreachable → Pods can't talk → app dead  │   │
│  │  ROOT CAUSE: CoreDNS pods crashed (check kube-system)                 │   │
│  │  FIX: Restart CoreDNS → everything recovers                            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Chain: Node disk full → kubelet can't write → node NotReady           │   │
│  │  → pods evicted → Deployment shows fewer replicas                      │   │
│  │  ROOT CAUSE: DiskPressure on node (check df -h)                       │   │
│  │  FIX: Clean disk → node recovers                                       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CKA TROUBLESHOOTING MINDSET — Rules for the Exam:                           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. ALWAYS check namespace first: -n &lt;ns&gt; (most common mistake)       │   │
│  │ 2. ALWAYS check context: kubectl config current-context               │   │
│  │ 3. DESCRIBE before LOGS: describe gives events + state; logs gives    │   │
│  │    application-level details                                          │   │
│  │ 4. CHECK EVENTS: kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp          │   │
│  │ 5. WHEN STUCK: Move up one layer. Pod broken? Check node. Node        │   │
│  │    broken? Check kubelet. Kubelet broken? Check disk/containerd.      │   │
│  │ 6. VERIFY AFTER FIXING: Don't assume. Run a test command.             │   │
│  │ 7. ONE FIX AT A TIME: Fix one thing, verify, then fix the next.       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Troubleshooting is 30% of the CKA — the largest single domain. Exam questions often have 2-3 cascading failures. Fix the ROOT CAUSE first (not the symptoms). Common root causes: wrong namespace, wrong context, label mismatch, missing ConfigMap/Secret, insufficient resources, expired certs. After every fix, verify with a test command before moving on. Don't fix-and-forget.
            </div>

            <h5>① Why a Service connection fails at EACH layer — the mechanics behind the ladder</h5>
            <p>The 4-Layer Network Diagnostic Ladder in §34.1 tells you WHERE to look. This subsection explains WHY each layer can independently swallow a packet — the mechanism, not just the checklist.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  WHY EACH LAYER CAN SILENTLY EAT A PACKET                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  L1 POD      A pod can be Running but NOT Ready. Readiness GATES endpoint    │
│              membership — a failing probe quietly removes a perfectly       │
│              running pod from the Service without ever touching the pod.    │
│                                                                              │
│  L2 SERVICE  The ClusterIP is VIRTUAL — no host, no process listens there.  │
│              It only "works" because kube-proxy programs node-level rules   │
│              that DNAT the VIP to a backend pod IP:targetPort.              │
│              No endpoints ⇒ no DNAT target ⇒ the packet is simply dropped.  │
│                                                                              │
│  L3 DNS      CoreDNS is itself a Deployment sitting behind the kube-dns     │
│              Service. Pods find it via the nameserver line in               │
│              /etc/resolv.conf, then append search domains up to ndots.      │
│              If CoreDNS's own pod is unhealthy, EVERY name lookup in the    │
│              cluster degrades at once — it looks like "the app is broken"   │
│              in a dozen unrelated places simultaneously.                    │
│                                                                              │
│  L4 POLICY   NetworkPolicy is compiled by the CNI into that SAME            │
│              iptables/eBPF dataplane kube-proxy already uses. A deny is a   │
│              DROP rule the packet hits before it ever reaches the backend — │
│              from the app's point of view it looks identical to L2 being   │
│              broken (both just "hang" / "connection timed out").           │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What's actually broken</th><th>What it LOOKS like from the app</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>L1 Pod</td><td>Readiness probe failing</td><td>"Service times out" (pod never even appears in endpoints)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>L2 Service</td><td>No DNAT target (empty endpoints) or wrong targetPort</td><td>"Connection refused" or timeout</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>L3 DNS</td><td>CoreDNS unhealthy or wrong namespace in name</td><td>"Unknown host" — looks like a typo, is often infra</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>L4 Policy</td><td>A DROP rule in the dataplane before the backend</td><td>Identical symptom to L2 — timeout, no error message</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> L2 (no endpoints) and L4 (policy DROP) produce the EXACT SAME symptom from the client's side — a hang or timeout with no distinguishing error text. The only way to tell them apart is to check them in order: endpoints first (cheap, instant), then policy (requires reading YAML). Never guess which layer based on the symptom alone; the ladder exists precisely because the symptoms are indistinguishable.</div>

            <h5>② Reading the dataplane by hand — when <code>kubectl get</code> isn't enough</h5>
            <p>Sometimes every <code>kubectl</code> object looks correct — Service, endpoints, NetworkPolicy — yet traffic still fails. At that point you stop trusting the API and read the actual rules kube-proxy and the CNI installed on the node.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  READING THE DATAPLANE — three modes, three tools                            │
│                                                                              │
│  iptables mode (default on many clusters):                                  │
│    $ iptables-save | grep &lt;clusterIP&gt;                                     │
│    → shows the DNAT/SNAT chains kube-proxy wrote for this exact Service     │
│                                                                              │
│  IPVS mode (higher-performance, used at scale):                              │
│    $ ipvsadm -Ln                                                            │
│    → shows virtual servers and their real-server (pod) weight/health        │
│                                                                              │
│  Either mode — live connection state:                                       │
│    $ conntrack -L                                                           │
│    → shows in-flight connections; a stuck/stale entry can wedge a retry     │
│                                                                              │
│  eBPF CNIs (Cilium) replace iptables/IPVS entirely:                          │
│    $ hubble observe --pod easypj-web                                        │
│    → shows ALLOWED/DENIED flows live, no manual rule-reading required       │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Dataplane</th><th>Inspect with</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>iptables</td><td><code>iptables-save | grep &lt;IP&gt;</code></td><td>Small/medium clusters, the CKA exam default</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>IPVS</td><td><code>ipvsadm -Ln</code></td><td>Large clusters — O(1) lookups vs iptables' linear scan</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>conntrack</td><td><code>conntrack -L</code></td><td>Diagnosing stuck/stale connections after a Service or pod IP changed</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>eBPF (Cilium)</td><td><code>hubble observe</code></td><td>Live allow/deny visibility without reading raw rules at all</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Reading the dataplane by hand is a LAST RESORT — reach for it only after <code>kubectl get endpoints</code>, <code>describe netpol</code>, and <code>nslookup</code> have all come back looking correct. On the exam this is rare, but knowing <code>iptables-save | grep</code> exists (and what it proves) separates "I'm stuck" from "I can keep isolating."</div>

            <h5>③ Ephemeral debug containers — troubleshooting distroless and shell-less images</h5>
            <p>easypj's production images are increasingly built distroless (no shell, no <code>curl</code>, no <code>nslookup</code> inside the container) for a smaller attack surface. When that pod misbehaves, <code>kubectl exec</code> has nothing to run — <code>kubectl debug</code> attaches a temporary, fully-equipped container into the SAME pod's namespaces instead.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  EPHEMERAL DEBUG CONTAINERS — troubleshoot a pod with NO shell                │
│                                                                              │
│  $ kubectl exec -it easypj-web-xyz -- sh                                     │
│  → OCI runtime exec failed: exec: "sh": executable file not found  ❌       │
│                                                                              │
│  $ kubectl debug -it easypj-web-xyz \                                       │
│      --image=nicolaka/netshoot --target=easypj-web                         │
│  → attaches netshoot (full of curl/dig/tcpdump/nslookup) into the SAME      │
│    pod, sharing its NETWORK namespace (--target)                           │
│  → now: curl localhost:8000, dig easypj-db, tcpdump -i eth0 all WORK       │
│                                                                              │
│  The ephemeral container is added to the pod's spec temporarily and never   │
│  restarts the original containers — a debug session leaves no lasting       │
│  change to the workload once you delete the pod or it's rolled.             │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Distroless app pod, need a shell</td><td><code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox --target=&lt;container&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Need network tools (curl/dig/tcpdump)</td><td><code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=nicolaka/netshoot --target=&lt;container&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Node itself needs inspecting (not a pod)</td><td><code>kubectl debug node/&lt;node&gt; -it --image=busybox</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>--target=&lt;container&gt;</code> is what makes this useful for networking bugs — it shares the TARGET container's network namespace, so <code>curl localhost:&lt;port&gt;</code> from inside the debug container reaches the app exactly as the app's own process would see traffic. Without <code>--target</code>, the ephemeral container gets its OWN network namespace and can't help diagnose connectivity.</div>

            <h5>④ Node-level bisection when the API server itself is down</h5>
            <p>Every technique so far assumes <code>kubectl</code> works. When the apiserver is the thing that's broken, you lose that lens entirely and must debug the control plane as what it actually is: a handful of static pods managed directly by kubelet, one node at a time.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  WHEN kubectl IS DOWN — bisect on the node itself                            │
│                                                                              │
│  ssh master-01                                                              │
│  $ systemctl status kubelet         ← is kubelet even running?              │
│  $ crictl ps -a                     ← list ALL containers (not just kubectl)│
│  $ crictl ps -a | grep apiserver    ← is the apiserver container present?   │
│  $ crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;       ← read its logs directly, no kubectl   │
│  $ cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml   ← the SOURCE of truth│
│                                                                              │
│  kubelet watches this directory. Editing the YAML here and saving IS the    │
│  fix — there is no "apply" step; kubelet detects the file change and        │
│  recreates the static pod automatically within seconds.                     │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Replaces</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>crictl ps -a</code></td><td><code>kubectl get pods</code></td><td>apiserver unreachable</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs</code></td><td>need a control-plane container's logs directly</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code></td><td>—</td><td>kubelet itself won't start static pods at all</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl apply</code></td><td>fixing a static pod — there is no apply, only the file</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Static pods have NO Deployment, NO controller, and NO <code>kubectl apply</code> workflow — kubelet reads the manifest directory directly. This is exactly Scenario 11 and Scenario 6's technique: fix the file, kubelet notices the change, the pod restarts on its own. Never try to <code>kubectl edit</code> a static pod through the API when the apiserver itself is the thing that's broken.</div>

            <h5>⑤ The subtle traps — MTU, asymmetric routing &amp; DNS ndots</h5>
            <p>The final class of network bugs doesn't show up as an obvious "down" state at all — everything reports Running/Ready/healthy, yet requests are slow, flaky, or fail only under specific conditions. These are the hardest bugs precisely because every layer LOOKS fine.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  TRAPS THAT LOOK LIKE "EVERYTHING IS FINE" BUT AREN'T                        │
│                                                                              │
│  MTU mismatch:                                                              │
│    Small requests (a health check ping) succeed; LARGE responses (an        │
│    easypj API response with a big JSON payload) silently hang.              │
│    Cause: an overlay network (VXLAN) adds encapsulation overhead; if the    │
│    resulting packet exceeds the path MTU, it's dropped instead of           │
│    fragmented cleanly. Symptom: "small stuff works, big stuff hangs."       │
│                                                                              │
│  DNS ndots:5 amplification:                                                 │
│    A pod calling an EXTERNAL domain (api.stripe.com) with the default       │
│    ndots:5 tries up to 5 search-domain-appended lookups (all of which       │
│    FAIL) before finally trying the bare name that succeeds — turning one    │
│    external call into 5-6 DNS queries and measurable added latency.        │
│                                                                              │
│  Asymmetric routing:                                                        │
│    A request's OUTBOUND path differs from its RETURN path (common after    │
│    a manual iptables change or a multi-NIC node). Connection tracking       │
│    (conntrack) sees a reply it never tracked the request for, and drops it. │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Trap</th><th>Tell-tale symptom</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>MTU too large</td><td>Small requests work, large responses hang</td><td>Lower the CNI's MTU below the physical path MTU minus encapsulation overhead</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>High ndots</td><td>External DNS lookups are slow/flaky, internal ones aren't</td><td>Set <code>dnsConfig.options: [{name: ndots, value: "2"}]</code> on latency-sensitive pods</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Asymmetric routing</td><td>conntrack drops replies that "should" work</td><td>Ensure consistent routing/SNAT on multi-homed nodes</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> These three traps are why "systematic binary-search isolation" matters more than intuition once the obvious layers (pod/Service/DNS/policy) all check out clean. Prove each layer up or down with a real command before ever blaming "the network is just flaky" — that phrase usually means the search stopped one layer too early.</div>

            <h5>⑥ The Root-Cause Decision Tree — one flowchart that ties every layer together</h5>
            <p>Every subsection above explains ONE layer in isolation. But the real exam (and real production) doesn't tell you which layer is broken — it just shows you a symptom. This final subsection is the synthesis: a single decision tree that takes ANY "it's broken" report and routes you, branch by branch, to the exact layer and the exact next command, without you having to guess which of the 12 scenarios you're even looking at.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  THE UNIVERSAL ROOT-CAUSE DECISION TREE                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  START: "Something is broken" — kubectl get pods,nodes,svc -A               │
│    │                                                                        │
│    ├─ Are ALL nodes/pods across the WHOLE cluster affected at once?         │
│    │     │                                                                  │
│    │    YES ──▶ Suspect CONTROL PLANE or DNS (one shared dependency)        │
│    │             ├─ kubectl commands hang/refuse entirely?                  │
│    │             │    ──▶ apiserver/etcd down → crictl on master (S6,S7)    │
│    │             └─ kubectl works but names don't resolve anywhere?         │
│    │                  ──▶ CoreDNS down → check kube-system pods (S5)        │
│    │                                                                        │
│    └─ NO, only ONE pod / node / namespace is affected                       │
│          │                                                                  │
│          ├─ Is it a single NODE (kubectl get nodes shows NotReady)?          │
│          │     ──▶ SSH + systemctl status kubelet/containerd (S1)          │
│          │                                                                  │
│          ├─ Is the POD not even scheduled (Pending)?                        │
│          │     ├─ Events show FailedScheduling? ──▶ resources/taint (S2)   │
│          │     └─ Events are EMPTY?              ──▶ scheduler down (S11)  │
│          │                                                                  │
│          ├─ Is the POD scheduled but restarting/crashing?                   │
│          │     ├─ CrashLoopBackOff ──▶ logs --previous (S4)                │
│          │     └─ ImagePullBackOff ──▶ describe → Events (S12)             │
│          │                                                                  │
│          ├─ Is the POD Running, but something can't REACH it?               │
│          │     ──▶ walk the 4-layer network ladder top-down:                │
│          │         L1 pod Ready? → L2 endpoints? → L3 DNS? → L4 policy?     │
│          │         (S3 endpoints / S5 DNS / S8 NetworkPolicy)               │
│          │                                                                  │
│          └─ Is a VOLUME the problem (pod stuck waiting on storage)?         │
│                ──▶ kubectl get pvc,sc,pv (S9)                               │
│                                                                              │
│  AT EVERY BRANCH: one wrong "yes/no" answer sends you down the wrong path — │
│  so CONFIRM with a command, never assume from memory or a hunch.            │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>First triage question</th><th>If YES, go to…</th><th>If NO, go to…</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Is the WHOLE cluster affected, not just one workload?</td><td>Control plane / etcd / CoreDNS (Scenarios 5-7)</td><td>Narrow to node → pod → network (below)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Is one specific NODE NotReady?</td><td>kubelet/containerd on that node (Scenario 1)</td><td>Check the pod itself</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Is the pod stuck Pending?</td><td>Events present → scheduling constraint (S2); Events empty → scheduler down (S11)</td><td>Check if it's Running but unreachable</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Is the pod Running but unreachable?</td><td>Walk L1→L2→L3→L4 (Scenarios 3, 5, 8)</td><td>Check storage — pod stuck waiting on a volume (Scenario 9)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Notice the tree ALWAYS asks "blast radius" first (whole cluster vs one workload) — that single question eliminates half the scenario playbook in one command (<code>kubectl get nodes; kubectl get pods -A</code>). Candidates who skip this and dive straight into <code>describe</code> on the one broken-looking pod often miss that the REAL root cause is one layer up (the node under it, or the control plane under that). Always widen the lens before you narrow it.</div>

            <h5>⑦ The Cross-Part Layer Stack — how Ch31→32→33→34 fit together as ONE mental model</h5>
            <p>The 12 scenarios in this chapter don't live in isolation — they're the synthesis of three chapters you've already studied. Ch31 taught you to read a POD's own symptoms; Ch32 taught you the NODE/control-plane root causes that often sit underneath those symptoms; Ch33 gave you the INSTRUMENTS (logs, events, metrics) you reach for at every single layer. This chapter is what happens when you run all three at once, at exam speed.</p>
