            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ <strong>ONE method, every scenario:</strong> IDENTIFY → GATHER → ANALYZE → FIX → VERIFY (equivalently: GATHER → READ Events/logs → ISOLATE the layer → FIX → VERIFY). Never skip VERIFY — the exam checks end state, not intent.</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>"Can't connect" is layered — isolate top-down:</strong> L1 pod (Running/Ready?) → L2 Service (has endpoints?) → L3 DNS (name resolves?) → L4 NetworkPolicy/kube-proxy (traffic allowed/programmed?). Never jump straight to the app.</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Empty endpoints</strong> = selector/label mismatch OR no Ready pod. A Service can "exist" with a ClusterIP yet route nowhere — always confirm <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> before suspecting the network.</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Zero scheduling Events ≠ FailedScheduling Events.</strong> A pod stuck Pending with Events present (Scenario 2) means the scheduler is running and rejecting it (resources/taint/affinity). Zero Events at all (Scenario 11) means the scheduler itself isn't running — check <code>kube-system</code>, not the pod spec.</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Pod statuses decode the fault directly:</strong> CrashLoopBackOff (app crashes repeatedly, check <code>--previous</code>), ImagePullBackOff (wrong image/tag or missing pull secret), Pending (no node/resources/PVC/toleration), Error (container exited non-zero).</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Work the layers for cluster faults too:</strong> pod → node/kubelet (<code>systemctl</code>, <code>journalctl</code>) → control plane (<code>crictl</code>/<code>journalctl</code> when the apiserver itself is down) → network. Control-plane components are static pods in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — editing the file IS the fix; kubelet auto-restarts them.</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>etcd restore always uses a NEW data-dir</strong> — never restore in-place — and you must update <em>both</em> the <code>--data-dir</code> flag and the <code>hostPath</code> volume in the static-pod manifest, with the API server stopped during the restore.</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Multiple symptoms, one root cause</strong> is common on the exam: a NotReady node can look like a Pending pod AND a Service with no endpoints simultaneously. Always start at the infrastructure layer and work upward — fixing the root cause often resolves several visible symptoms at once.</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>EXAM:</strong> Troubleshooting is 30% — the single biggest domain. Memorize the 12-scenario playbook (symptom → first command) until you can recite it cold; speed of the FIRST command beats deep analysis every time.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>Always VERIFY after fixing</strong> — The exam checks the end state. If you fix a broken service but don't verify with <code>curl</code> or <code>kubectl get endpoints</code>, you might have missed something. Follow the 5-step method: IDENTIFY → GATHER → ANALYZE → FIX → VERIFY</li>
                        <li><strong>Multiple issues per question</strong> — A single scenario may have 2-3 things broken (wrong label + wrong port + missing service). Fix one, test, then check for more. Don't assume one fix solves everything</li>
                        <li><strong>Read the question TWICE</strong> — "Ensure the pod runs on node01" could mean nodeSelector, nodeName, or taint toleration. "Fix the deployment" could mean image, resources, selectors, or mount issues. Misreading costs 5-10 minutes</li>
                        <li><strong>Use <code>kubectl explain</code> as your API reference</strong> — <code>kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources --recursive</code>. Faster than searching docs. Works offline (exam environment)</li>
                        <li><strong>Time management</strong> — 17 questions in 2 hours = ~7 min per question. If stuck for 8 minutes, flag it and move on. Come back with fresh eyes. Easy questions first</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

