            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 34 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the 4-layer network ladder and the 12-scenario playbook — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Service returns no traffic; <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> shows <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>. What are the two possible causes, and how do you fix each?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The selector matches no <strong>Ready</strong> pod. Either the Service selector doesn't match the pod's labels at all, or it matches but the pod's readiness probe is failing.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>How to confirm</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Selector/label mismatch</td><td><code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code> vs <code>svc.spec.selector</code></td><td>Align one side with the other</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Pod not Ready</td><td><code>kubectl get pods</code> shows <code>0/1</code></td><td>Fix the readiness probe / health endpoint</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A not-Ready pod is <strong>pulled from endpoints by design</strong>, even if its labels match perfectly — readiness gates endpoint membership. This is why "endpoints empty" is always L2, checked before DNS or policy (see the 4-Layer Network Diagnostic Ladder in §34.1).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>easypj-web</code> can't resolve <code>easypj-db</code>. Walk the diagnostic order.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">DNS diagnostic order — prove DNS itself before blaming the name</div>
                            <div class="diagram-box">
                            <pre>
$ kubectl run t --rm -it --image=busybox -- nslookup kubernetes.default
    → FAILS  ⇒ DNS itself is broken. Check CoreDNS:
       kubectl -n kube-system get pods -l k8s-app=kube-dns
       kubectl -n kube-system logs -l k8s-app=kube-dns
    → WORKS  ⇒ DNS is up; your NAME is wrong. Try:
       nslookup easypj-db          (same namespace only)
       nslookup easypj-db.easypj   (cross-namespace / explicit)
                            </pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Never assume DNS is broken because one name fails — prove it with a known-good name (<code>kubernetes.default</code>) first. That single test splits the problem in two: "DNS is down" (go check CoreDNS pods/logs) vs "DNS works, my name/namespace is wrong" (go check the FQDN).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>easypj-web → easypj-db</code> worked yesterday; today it's blocked, right after a teammate added NetworkPolicies. What's the likely cause and the fix?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">NetworkPolicy block — diagnose &amp; fix</div>
                            <div class="diagram-box">
                            <pre>
1. kubectl get netpol -A                          → list all policies
2. kubectl describe netpol &lt;name&gt; -n easypj       → does Ingress allow "from" web?
3. kubectl get pods --show-labels -n easypj       → do web pods carry the expected label?
4. kubectl exec web-pod -n easypj -- curl -m3 easypj-db:5432   → confirm still blocked
5. kubectl apply -f fixed-netpol.yaml             → add the missing ingress allow rule
6. repeat step 4                                  → VERIFY
                            </pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A NetworkPolicy selecting <code>easypj-db</code> without an ingress rule allowing <code>easypj-web</code> (or a blanket default-deny with no allow at all) is the classic exam trap. Confirm the web pods actually carry the label the policy's <code>from.podSelector</code> expects — a label typo on either side produces the identical symptom.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You suspect kube-proxy itself is broken. What exactly do you check?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>DaemonSet pods Running?</td><td><code>kubectl -n kube-system get pods -l k8s-app=kube-proxy</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Its logs</td><td><code>kubectl -n kube-system logs ds/kube-proxy</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Its mode (iptables vs IPVS)</td><td>check the kube-proxy ConfigMap</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Dataplane rules on the node</td><td><code>iptables-save | grep &lt;clusterIP&gt;</code> or <code>ipvsadm -Ln</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A broken kube-proxy breaks <strong>Service VIPs</strong> while pod IPs still work — that split (Q5 below) is the diagnostic tell that points you at kube-proxy specifically instead of DNS or NetworkPolicy.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Service's ClusterIP is unreachable, but curling the pod's IP directly works fine. What does this tell you?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the diagnostic split</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash">curl &lt;podIP&gt;:5432       <span class="comment"># works → the app is fine</span>
curl &lt;serviceVIP&gt;:5432  <span class="comment"># fails → the SERVICE layer is broken</span>

<span class="comment"># Narrow it down:</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-db          <span class="comment"># empty?</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-db -o yaml        <span class="comment"># targetPort correct?</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pods -l k8s-app=kube-proxy   <span class="comment"># running?</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The app and the pod are proven fine the moment the pod IP responds. The bug lives entirely in the Service layer: empty endpoints, a wrong <code>targetPort</code>, or a broken kube-proxy not programming the VIP's DNAT rule. Never touch application code for this symptom.</p>
                            <p class="mb-0">🔬 <strong>Rare edge case worth recognizing:</strong> a pod can sometimes fail to reach its <em>own</em> Service VIP even when every other pod behind that Service reaches it fine — a hairpin/loopback NAT quirk in the node's dataplane, not a config bug. If only ONE specific pod (typically the Service's own backend calling itself) fails while all its siblings succeed, suspect this before re-editing YAML that already looks correct.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Connections are refused even though the Service exists and has healthy endpoints. What's the most common config bug?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th><th>easypj-db value</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>port</code></td><td>What clients hit on the Service VIP</td><td>5432</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>targetPort</code></td><td>What the Service forwards to on the pod</td><td>must equal <strong>5432</strong> — the real listening port</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>containerPort</code></td><td>What the container actually listens on</td><td>5432</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>If <code>targetPort</code> doesn't match the real listening port, you get "connection refused" even with populated endpoints — the Service correctly forwards the packet, but nothing is listening on that port inside the pod. Fix by matching <code>targetPort</code> to the container's actual listening port.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">From namespace <code>web</code>, you need to reach a Service <code>db</code> that lives in namespace <code>data</code>. What name do you use, and why does a bare <code>db</code> fail?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cross-namespace dns</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash">curl db.data:5432                          <span class="comment"># ✅ works — explicit namespace</span>
curl db.data.svc.cluster.local:5432        <span class="comment"># ✅ works — full FQDN</span>
curl db:5432                               <span class="comment"># ❌ fails from namespace "web" — searches web.svc.cluster.local first</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A bare name only resolves within the CALLER's own namespace search domain (<code>web.svc.cluster.local</code> for a pod in <code>web</code>). To reach a Service in another namespace you must qualify it: <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;</code> or the full FQDN. This is the single most common "works in one namespace, not another" DNS trap.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An Ingress for easypj isn't routing any traffic. What do you check first?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Ingress controller installed &amp; Running?</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx</code> (or your controller's namespace)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Ingress has an ADDRESS?</td><td><code>kubectl get ingress easypj-ing -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>host/path/backend correct?</td><td><code>kubectl describe ingress easypj-ing -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Backend Service has endpoints?</td><td><code>kubectl get endpoints easypj-web -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>An Ingress object is completely inert without a running controller to implement it — creating the YAML alone does nothing. After confirming the controller is up, work down: ADDRESS populated → host/path/<code>ingressClassName</code> correct → backend Service has endpoints. Full Ingress internals live in Ch20.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you peek at the actual dataplane rules implementing a Service, on the node itself?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">read the dataplane by hand</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># iptables mode:</span>
iptables-save | grep &lt;clusterIP&gt;

<span class="comment"># IPVS mode:</span>
ipvsadm -Ln

<span class="comment"># live connections (either mode):</span>
conntrack -L

<span class="comment"># eBPF CNIs (Cilium) — use Hubble instead of raw iptables:</span>
hubble observe --pod easypj-web</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>kube-proxy programs the Service VIP into one of these dataplanes — the ClusterIP itself has no host or process, it only "works" because these rules DNAT it to a backend pod IP. When endpoints look correct in <code>kubectl</code> but traffic still fails, reading the actual rules on the node is the last resort before assuming a kube-proxy bug.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Every new pod sticks at <code>Pending</code>, but <code>kubectl describe</code> shows NO scheduling Events at all — not even a "FailedScheduling". What's different about this from an ordinary Pending pod, and where do you look?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Events present vs Events absent — two different Pending bugs</div>
                            <div class="diagram-box">
                            <pre>
Pending WITH Events           →  scheduler IS running, and is REJECTING the pod
  "Insufficient cpu"              (taint, resources, affinity — fix the pod spec)
  "didn't tolerate taint"

Pending with ZERO Events      →  scheduler ISN'T running to make any decision
                                  → kubectl -n kube-system get pods | grep scheduler
                                  → crictl logs &lt;scheduler-container-id&gt;
                                  → fix /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
                            </pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>"No Events at all" is the tell that the scheduler component itself is down or CrashLooping — the fault is in the control plane, not the pod's spec. This is Scenario 11 in §34.1: check the scheduler's static pod in <code>kube-system</code>, inspect its manifest and container logs with <code>crictl</code>, fix the typo/bad flag, and let kubelet auto-restart it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
