                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment's pods are running but the Service returns no response. Walk through the troubleshooting methodology.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">systematic 5-step approach</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Systematic 5-step approach:</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check pods are running and ready</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=web
<span class="comment"># NAME        READY  STATUS   RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc12   1/1    Running  0        ← Looks good</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check Service exists and has correct selector</span>
kubectl get svc web-svc
kubectl describe svc web-svc
<span class="comment"># Selector: app=web</span>
<span class="comment"># Port:     80/TCP</span>
<span class="comment"># TargetPort: 8080</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check Endpoints (MOST CRITICAL STEP)</span>
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME      ENDPOINTS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-svc   &lt;none&gt;     ← NO ENDPOINTS! Selector mismatch!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Compare selector with pod labels</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels
<span class="comment"># NAME        LABELS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc12   app=webapp    ← "webapp" not "web"!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix the selector</span>
kubectl edit svc web-svc
<span class="comment"># Change selector from app: web → app: webapp</span>

<span class="comment"># OR fix the pod labels:</span>
kubectl label pod web-abc12 app=web --overwrite

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># web-svc   10.244.1.5:8080   ← Endpoints populated!</span>

<span class="comment"># Test:</span>
kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -- wget -qO- web-svc
<span class="comment"># &lt;html&gt;...&lt/html&gt; ✓</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The #1 CKA troubleshooting pattern: Service → Endpoints → Pods. If Endpoints shows <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>, the Service selector doesn't match any pod labels. Always: (1) check pods are Running+Ready, (2) check Service selector, (3) check Endpoints, (4) compare labels, (5) fix. This scenario appears in almost every CKA exam. The <code>targetPort</code> (container port) must match what the app listens on.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment isn't rolling out new pods after an image update. What's wrong?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check rollout status</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check rollout status:</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment web
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "web" rollout to finish:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment"># ← Stuck! New pods aren't becoming ready</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the new ReplicaSet:</span>
kubectl get rs
<span class="comment"># NAME          DESIRED  CURRENT  READY</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc123    3        3        3     ← OLD (still running)</span>
<span class="comment"># web-def456    1        1        0     ← NEW (0 ready!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the new pod:</span>
kubectl get pods | grep web-def456
<span class="comment"># web-def456-xyz   0/1   ImagePullBackOff   0   2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Root cause: Wrong image name</span>
kubectl describe pod web-def456-xyz | grep Image
<span class="comment"># Image: nginxx:latest  ← Typo! "nginxx"</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix:</span>
kubectl set image deployment/web web=nginx:latest
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment web

<span class="comment"># If rollout was paused:</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment web
<span class="comment"># "deployment is paused" ← Someone paused it!</span>
kubectl rollout resume deployment web

<span class="comment"># If maxUnavailable=0 and cluster is at capacity:</span>
<span class="comment"># New pod can't schedule → old pod can't terminate → deadlock</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Increase maxSurge or reduce replicas temporarily</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment web
<span class="comment"># deployment "web" successfully rolled out ✓</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Stuck rollouts happen when new pods can't become Ready. Common causes: (1) wrong image name/tag (ImagePullBackOff), (2) readiness probe fails, (3) resource constraints, (4) rollout paused. On the CKA: check `kubectl rollout status`, then check the new ReplicaSet's pods. <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> is the fastest way to revert. Deadlock scenario: maxUnavailable=0 + maxSurge=0 = can't add new or remove old pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">DNS resolution isn't working inside pods. How do you debug?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">test dns from inside a pod</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Test DNS from inside a pod</span>
kubectl run dns-test --rm -it --image=busybox -- nslookup kubernetes
<span class="comment"># Server:    10.96.0.10</span>
<span class="comment"># nslookup: can't resolve 'kubernetes'  ← DNS broken!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check CoreDNS pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># NAME                      READY  STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-5d78c9869d-abc12  0/1    CrashLoopBackOff  ← Problem!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check CoreDNS logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check CoreDNS ConfigMap</span>
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for syntax errors in Corefile</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check kube-dns Service</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Should be ClusterIP 10.96.0.10</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check pod DNS config</span>
kubectl exec dns-test -- cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># nameserver 10.96.0.10     ← Should point to kube-dns</span>
<span class="comment"># search default.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># Common fixes:</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS crash → Check ConfigMap for syntax errors</span>
<span class="comment"># Wrong nameserver → Check kubelet --cluster-dns flag</span>
<span class="comment"># Loop detected → Remove "loop" plugin from Corefile</span>
<span class="comment"># Upstream DNS → Check forward . directive in Corefile</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix CoreDNS ConfigMap:</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Then restart:</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>DNS is CoreDNS running in <code>kube-system</code>. Pods get the DNS IP (10.96.0.10) from kubelet config. On the CKA: test with <code>nslookup kubernetes</code>, check CoreDNS pods, check the Corefile ConfigMap. Common issue: loop detection plugin causes crashes on some setups — fix by editing the ConfigMap. The <code>kube-dns</code> Service name is kept for backward compatibility, but it routes to CoreDNS pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A user reports they can't create pods in a namespace. How do you check RBAC permissions?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check what the user can do</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check what the user CAN do</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n dev --as=user1
<span class="comment"># no  ← User can't create pods!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check all permissions for the user</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=user1 -n dev
<span class="comment"># Resources  Verbs</span>
<span class="comment"># (nothing relevant)  ← No roles bound!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check existing RoleBindings</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -n dev
<span class="comment"># NAME       ROLE              SUBJECTS</span>
<span class="comment"># (empty)    ← No bindings!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check ClusterRoleBindings</span>
kubectl get clusterrolebindings | grep user1
<span class="comment"># (nothing)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Create the necessary RBAC</span>
<span class="comment"># Option A: Create Role + RoleBinding</span>
kubectl create role pod-manager -n dev \
--verb=create,get,list,delete \
--resource=pods

kubectl create rolebinding user1-pods -n dev \
--role=pod-manager \
--user=user1

<span class="comment"># Option B: Use existing ClusterRole</span>
kubectl create rolebinding user1-edit -n dev \
--clusterrole=edit \
--user=user1

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n dev --as=user1
<span class="comment"># yes ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Debug: Check which role is blocking</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n dev --as=user1 -v=5
<span class="comment"># Shows API calls and authorization decisions</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> tests permissions. <code>--as=user1</code> impersonates the user. <code>--list</code> shows all allowed actions. On the CKA: if a user can't do something, check RoleBindings in the namespace and ClusterRoleBindings. The fix is creating a Role + RoleBinding. Built-in ClusterRoles (view, edit, admin, cluster-admin) save time. <code>-v=5</code> shows verbose authorization debugging.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A NetworkPolicy is blocking expected traffic. How do you debug it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">symptom: pod a can't reach pod b</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Symptom: Pod A can't reach Pod B, but it worked before</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check NetworkPolicies in the namespace</span>
kubectl get networkpolicies -n prod
<span class="comment"># NAME           POD-SELECTOR  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># deny-all       app=api       5m   ← Recently created!</span>
<span class="comment"># allow-web      app=web       30d</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Examine the blocking policy</span>
kubectl describe networkpolicy deny-all -n prod
<span class="comment"># PodSelector: app=api</span>
<span class="comment"># Allowing ingress traffic: &lt;none&gt;   ← Blocks ALL ingress!</span>
<span class="comment"># Allowing egress traffic:  &lt;none&gt;   ← Blocks ALL egress!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify traffic is actually blocked</span>
kubectl exec -n prod web-pod -- curl -s --max-time 3 api-svc:8080
<span class="comment"># curl: (28) Connection timed out  ← Blocked!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Fix — Add allow rule for needed traffic</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-web-to-api
  namespace: prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: api
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: web
    ports:
    - port: 8080

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify</span>
kubectl exec -n prod web-pod -- curl -s api-svc:8080
<span class="comment"># {"status": "ok"} ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Key rules:</span>
<span class="comment"># - If ANY NetworkPolicy selects a pod, only allowed traffic works</span>
<span class="comment"># - No NetworkPolicy = all traffic allowed</span>
<span class="comment"># - Multiple policies are ADDITIVE (union of all allow rules)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>NetworkPolicies are default-deny once applied. If a pod has ANY NetworkPolicy selecting it, only explicitly allowed traffic passes. On the CKA: check which policies select the target pod (via <code>podSelector</code>), verify with <code>curl</code>/<code>wget</code> from source pod, then add an allow rule if needed. Multiple NetworkPolicies are additive — the union of all rules determines what's allowed. Common trap: creating a deny-all without corresponding allow rules.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You have 5 minutes left on the CKA exam and 3 questions remaining. What's your strategy?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Strategy</th>
                                        <th>Action</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Triage</strong></td>
                                        <td>Check weights — do highest % question first</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Imperative First</strong></td>
                                        <td>Use <code>kubectl run/create/expose</code> not YAML</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Skip Stuck</strong></td>
                                        <td>If stuck &gt;60s, flag and move on</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Partial Credit</strong></td>
                                        <td>Create the resource even if incomplete — partial credit exists</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Context!</strong></td>
                                        <td>Always check <code>kubectl config use-context</code> — wrong context = 0 points</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">speed aliases (should already be set)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Speed aliases (should already be set):</span>
export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"
export now="--force --grace-period 0"

<span class="comment"># Fast pod creation:</span>
k run web --image=nginx --port=80 $do > pod.yaml
vi pod.yaml  <span class="comment"># Quick edit</span>
k apply -f pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Fast service:</span>
k expose pod web --port=80 --target-port=80

<span class="comment"># Fast delete and recreate:</span>
k delete pod broken $now
k run broken --image=nginx</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Time management is critical on the CKA. With 5 minutes left: (1) do the highest-weight question, (2) use imperative commands exclusively, (3) don't debug — create from scratch if faster, (4) always verify the context with <code>kubectl config use-context</code>. Partial credit exists, so creating an incomplete resource is better than skipping entirely. The <code>$do</code> and <code>$now</code> variables save significant time.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod can't resolve an external domain (e.g., <code>google.com</code>) but can resolve cluster services. What's the issue?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">verify internal dns works</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Verify internal DNS works</span>
kubectl exec test-pod -- nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># Name:   kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.0.1  ← Internal DNS works!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Test external DNS</span>
kubectl exec test-pod -- nslookup google.com
<span class="comment"># ** server can't find google.com: SERVFAIL ← External fails!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check CoreDNS forward config</span>
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml
<span class="comment"># Corefile:</span>
<span class="comment">#   .:53 {</span>
<span class="comment">#     forward . /etc/resolv.conf    ← Should forward to upstream DNS</span>
<span class="comment">#   }</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check the node's /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS forwards external queries to the node's DNS</span>
ssh worker-1
cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># nameserver 127.0.0.53  ← systemd-resolved (might cause loops!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix — Point CoreDNS to a real DNS server</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Change: forward . /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># To:     forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Restart CoreDNS</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify</span>
kubectl exec test-pod -- nslookup google.com
<span class="comment"># Name:   google.com</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 142.250.80.46 ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: Check NetworkPolicy blocking DNS egress</span>
kubectl get networkpolicies -n &lt;namespace&gt;
<span class="comment"># If egress restricted, must allow UDP port 53 to kube-dns</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Internal DNS works (CoreDNS resolves <code>.cluster.local</code>) but external fails = upstream DNS forwarding issue. CoreDNS forwards external queries using the <code>forward</code> directive. If the node uses <code>systemd-resolved</code> (127.0.0.53), CoreDNS may loop. Fix by pointing to real upstream DNS (8.8.8.8). Also check NetworkPolicies — if egress is restricted, UDP port 53 to kube-dns must be explicitly allowed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to fix a broken cluster where <code>kubectl</code> commands timeout. Where do you start?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl timeout = api server unreachable</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># kubectl timeout = API server unreachable</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: SSH to the master node</span>
ssh master

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check kubelet (manages static pods)</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># If stopped → systemctl start kubelet</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check API server container</span>
crictl ps | grep kube-apiserver
<span class="comment"># If not listed → Check static pod manifest</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check API server manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong image</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong port</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing/wrong cert paths</span>
<span class="comment"># - Syntax errors</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check API server logs</span>
crictl logs $(crictl ps -a | grep apiserver | awk '{print $1}')
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
journalctl -u kubelet | grep apiserver

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check etcd (API server depends on it)</span>
crictl ps | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># If etcd is down → API server can't work</span>
<span class="comment"># Check: /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Check certificates</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -noout -dates
<span class="comment"># notAfter=... ← Expired?</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix sequence:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Fix manifests in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. kubelet auto-restarts static pods</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Wait 30-60 seconds</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Test: kubectl get nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># Common CKA causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong port in kube-apiserver.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># - Cert file path typo</span>
<span class="comment"># - etcd endpoint wrong</span>
<span class="comment"># - Image tag wrong</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>kubectl timeout = API server down. On the CKA: SSH to master, use <code>crictl</code> (not kubectl!), check kubelet, check static pod manifests. API server is a static pod managed by kubelet. Common exam scenario: a deliberate error is placed in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. Fix the manifest → kubelet detects the change → API server restarts. Check etcd too — if etcd is down, API server won't work even if its config is correct.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you use <code>kubectl explain</code> to quickly find the correct YAML field paths during the exam?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">basic: what fields does a pod spec have?</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Basic: What fields does a Pod spec have?</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec
<span class="comment"># Lists all fields with descriptions</span>

<span class="comment"># Recursive: ALL nested fields</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive
<span class="comment"># Shows the full tree without descriptions</span>
<span class="comment"># Perfect for finding the exact path</span>

<span class="comment"># Specific field:</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources
<span class="comment"># Shows: limits, requests sub-fields</span>

<span class="comment"># Common exam lookups:</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.volumes --recursive
<span class="comment"># Shows all volume types and their fields</span>

kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.volumeMounts
<span class="comment"># mountPath, name, subPath, readOnly</span>

kubectl explain pod.spec.tolerations
<span class="comment"># effect, key, operator, tolerationSeconds, value</span>

kubectl explain pod.spec.affinity --recursive
<span class="comment"># nodeAffinity, podAffinity, podAntiAffinity</span>

kubectl explain deployment.spec.strategy
<span class="comment"># type, rollingUpdate (maxSurge, maxUnavailable)</span>

kubectl explain pv.spec --recursive
<span class="comment"># All PV fields: capacity, accessModes, hostPath, nfs...</span>

<span class="comment"># CKA shortcut: Use with aliases</span>
alias kx="kubectl explain"
kx pod.spec.securityContext --recursive
kx pod.spec.initContainers

<span class="comment"># Can't remember the field name? Use recursive + grep:</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive | grep -i "toleration"
<span class="comment">#   tolerations  &lt;[]Toleration&gt;</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl explain</code> is your built-in documentation during the CKA — it's faster than searching docs. <code>--recursive</code> shows the full field tree. Use it when you can't remember the exact YAML path (e.g., "is it <code>spec.containers.resources.limits</code> or <code>spec.resources.limits</code>?"). <code>grep</code> with recursive output helps find field names. On the CKA, alias it as <code>kx</code> for speed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A multi-issue scenario: pods are Pending, a Service has no endpoints, and a node is NotReady. Solve it systematically.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">multi-issue diagnosis order</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Multi-issue diagnosis order: Infrastructure → Workloads → Networking</span>

<span class="comment"># PHASE 1: FIX INFRASTRUCTURE (Node)</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS     ROLES          AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># master     Ready      control-plane  30d</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   NotReady   &lt;none&gt;         30d  ← Fix this first!</span>

ssh worker-1
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Active: inactive (dead)</span>
systemctl start kubelet
systemctl enable kubelet
exit
<span class="comment"># Wait: kubectl get nodes → worker-1 Ready ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># PHASE 2: FIX WORKLOADS (Pending Pods)</span>
kubectl get pods
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  STATUS   RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc    0/1    Pending  0</span>
kubectl describe pod web-abc | tail -10
<span class="comment"># "0/1 nodes available: 1 node had taint {NoSchedule}"</span>
<span class="comment"># (Before we fixed the node, no node was available)</span>
<span class="comment"># After node fix → pod auto-schedules!</span>
kubectl get pods  <span class="comment"># web-abc → Running ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># PHASE 3: FIX NETWORKING (Service)</span>
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># web-svc   &lt;none&gt;  ← Still no endpoints!</span>

kubectl get svc web-svc -o yaml | grep -A2 selector
<span class="comment"># selector:</span>
<span class="comment">#   app: website      ← Wrong label!</span>

kubectl get pods --show-labels
<span class="comment"># web-abc   app=web   ← Label is "web" not "website"</span>

kubectl patch svc web-svc -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"web"}}}'
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># web-svc   10.244.1.5:80 ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># VERIFY ALL:</span>
kubectl get nodes        <span class="comment"># All Ready ✓</span>
kubectl get pods         <span class="comment"># All Running ✓</span>
kubectl get endpoints    <span class="comment"># All populated ✓</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Multi-issue scenarios test your ability to prioritize. Fix order: Infrastructure (nodes) → Workloads (pods) → Networking (services). Fixing the node first often auto-resolves the Pending pods (they were waiting for a schedulable node). Then fix the Service selector to populate endpoints. On the CKA, issues are often cascading — one root cause creates multiple symptoms. Fix the root cause first and check if downstream issues self-resolve before spending time on them.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            
