            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 34.2 — etcd Backup &amp; Restore Fire Drill</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 15 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Your easypj cluster's etcd has just started returning <code>failed to commit</code> errors and <code>kubectl</code> is intermittently timing out. You have a snapshot from an hour ago at <code>/opt/backups/etcd-snapshot.db</code>. Restore the cluster to a healthy state using that snapshot — without losing the ability to start the API server afterward.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Never restore in-place over the existing <code>--data-dir</code>. Stop the API server first (move its static-pod manifest out of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>) so it isn't writing while etcd is down, restore to a NEW directory, then repoint <strong>both</strong> the <code>--data-dir</code> flag AND the <code>hostPath</code> volume in <code>etcd.yaml</code> before moving the API server manifest back.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Stop the API server so nothing writes to etcd mid-restore</span>
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/

<span class="comment"># 2. Restore the snapshot to a FRESH data-dir (never restore in-place)</span>
sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backups/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># 3. Point etcd's static-pod manifest at the new data-dir (BOTH places!)</span>
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd-restored   ← was /var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># the container's --data-dir flag must match</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Bring the API server back — kubelet restarts both static pods</span>
sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

<span class="comment"># 5. VERIFY</span>
kubectl get nodes
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># → "is healthy" and kubectl responds normally</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 34.3 — The 4-Layer Network Speed-Run</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 10 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p><code>easypj-web</code> pods are <code>Running</code> and <code>1/1 Ready</code>, but <code>curl easypj-db:5432</code> from inside a web pod hangs and times out. Nothing else has visibly changed. Walk the 4-layer ladder from this chapter's §34.1 top to bottom and name the single layer that's actually broken before you touch any YAML.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Since the pod itself is Ready, L1 is already ruled out — start at L2 (<code>kubectl get endpoints easypj-db</code>). Don't skip straight to NetworkPolicy just because that's the "interesting" layer; prove L2 and L3 first.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># L1 POD — already Running/Ready, ruled out. Confirm anyway:</span>
kubectl get pod -l app=easypj-db -o wide

<span class="comment"># L2 SERVICE — does it have endpoints?</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-db -n easypj
<span class="comment"># easypj-db   10.244.2.9:5432   ← populated, L2 is fine, move on</span>

<span class="comment"># L3 DNS — does the name resolve from inside the cluster?</span>
kubectl run t --rm -it --image=nicolaka/netshoot -n easypj -- nslookup easypj-db.easypj
<span class="comment"># Name: easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local   Address: 10.96.44.10  ← resolves fine</span>

<span class="comment"># L4 POLICY — is something blocking port 5432?</span>
kubectl get netpol -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME              POD-SELECTOR   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># db-lockdown       app=easypj-db  2m   ← applied 2 minutes ago!</span>
kubectl describe netpol db-lockdown -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Ingress allows: podSelector app=easypj-worker   ← NOT app=easypj-web!</span>

<span class="comment"># ROOT CAUSE: a NetworkPolicy applied minutes ago selects easypj-db and only</span>
<span class="comment"># allows ingress from "easypj-worker", silently dropping easypj-web's traffic.</span>

<span class="comment"># FIX: add web to the allowed ingress selector</span>
kubectl patch netpol db-lockdown -n easypj --type=json -p \
  '[{"op":"add","path":"/spec/ingress/0/from/-","value":{"podSelector":{"matchLabels":{"app":"easypj-web"}}}}]'

<span class="comment"># VERIFY</span>
kubectl exec -n easypj deploy/easypj-web -- curl -m3 easypj-db:5432</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
