        <section class="part-cover" id="part10">
            <span class="part-kicker">🔴 Part 10 · Production and Beyond</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Run It in Production</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 35–36 · Advanced · <em>"No single point of failure, anywhere"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">A single-control-plane cluster is a demo, not production. Part 10 makes the cluster itself resilient — an HA control plane with etcd quorum and load-balanced apiservers — then shows how those same self-managed skills map onto managed cloud Kubernetes (EKS/GKE/AKS), where the provider runs the control plane for you.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> design an HA control plane (stacked vs external etcd, quorum math, leader election); and map self-managed concepts onto EKS/GKE/AKS so you know what the provider took over.</div>
            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🌉 Real-World Analogy: One Bridge Into the City, or a Bridge Authority That Owns Them All</h5>
                <p>A city with a single bridge into it is one accident away from being cut off — every ambulance, delivery truck, and commuter depends on that one span, and a single closure strands the whole city. There are two production-grade fixes, not one. The city can build two more bridges itself, hand traffic direction to a control-tower authority that spreads cars across whichever bridges are open, and design the bridges so any single one can close for the night without stranding anyone — that's Ch35: <strong>you</strong> still own, build, and maintain every bridge (control-plane node), you've just made sure no single one is load-bearing alone. Or the city can hand the entire bridge network to a regional highway authority that already runs redundant bridges across the whole state as its full-time job — the city just designs the roads (workloads) that connect to it and stops worrying about bridge maintenance at all — that's Ch36: a cloud provider (EKS/GKE/AKS) runs the control plane, and easypj's job shifts to designing workloads that connect to it well. Either way, "one bridge, one crash, city cut off" stops being possible.</p>
<pre>  ONE BRIDGE INTO THE CITY                        EASYPJ'S CONTROL PLANE
  ─────────────────────────                        ───────────────────────
  🌉 One bridge — a crash cuts off the city ─────▶  Baseline — 1 control-plane node, 1 etcd member
  🌉🌉🌉 3 city-built bridges + traffic tower ────▶  Ch 35 — 3 stacked control planes + LB + etcd quorum
  🏛️ Hand the whole network to a highway authority ▶ Ch 36 — managed control plane (EKS/GKE/AKS)
  🚦 The tower only directs traffic — it doesn't ──▶  A load balancer only routes to :6443 — etcd
     pour concrete or maintain the bridges itself      quorum, leader election, and correct kubeadm
                                                        join steps still have to be built right</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"3 control-plane nodes means I can lose any 2 and be fine."</td>
                            <td>etcd needs a <strong>majority (quorum)</strong> to accept writes, not just "at least one survivor." With 3 members, quorum is 2 — losing 1 node still leaves 2, which is a majority, so the cluster keeps working. But losing 2 of 3 leaves only 1 member, which is <em>not</em> a majority: etcd stops accepting writes and the cluster goes read-only/unavailable, even though a control-plane node is technically still running.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"The load balancer makes HA automatic."</td>
                            <td>The load balancer only does one job: route client traffic (kubectl, kubelets, controllers) to whichever apiserver is healthy behind <code>:6443</code>. It does nothing for etcd's Raft replication, quorum math, or leader election for the scheduler/controller-manager — those still have to be set up correctly (odd member count, <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> pointed at the LB <em>before</em> the first <code>kubeadm init</code>) or the LB is just evenly distributing requests to a cluster that was never actually resilient.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Managed cloud Kubernetes means I have nothing left to manage."</td>
                            <td>EKS/GKE/AKS take the control plane — apiserver HA, etcd operation, and its patching — off your plate. You still own everything downstream of it: IAM/identity for pods (IRSA / Workload Identity / AAD Pod Identity), node group sizing and upgrades, RBAC, NetworkPolicy, StorageClasses/CSI, workload reliability (PDBs, anti-affinity, autoscaling), and the cloud bill all of that generates. "Managed control plane" is not "managed cluster."</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
