│  │  │┌─────────┐│  │┌─────────┐│  │┌─────────┐│  │┌─────────┐│       │     │
│  │  ││ easypj  ││  ││ easypj  ││  ││ easypj  ││  ││ jobpost ││       │     │
│  │  ││ web-1   ││  ││ web-2   ││  ││ db-0    ││  ││ worker  ││       │     │
│  │  │└─────────┘│  │└─────────┘│  │└─────────┘│  │└─────────┘│       │     │
│  │  │ kubelet   │  │ kubelet   │  │ kubelet   │  │ kubelet   │       │     │
│  │  │ kube-proxy│  │ kube-proxy│  │ kube-proxy│  │ kube-proxy│       │     │
│  │  └───────────┘  └───────────┘  └───────────┘  └───────────┘       │     │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 CKA Exam: Know the Components</h5>
                    <p>You must know what runs on control plane nodes vs worker nodes. <strong>API server</strong> is active-active (all serve requests). <strong>Scheduler</strong> and <strong>controller-manager</strong> are active-passive (only the leader acts). <strong>etcd</strong> uses Raft consensus (all peers participate but one is the Raft leader).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🧠 In Plain English — the Cockpit Crew Analogy</h5>
                    <p>Think of the control plane like an airline cockpit crew. The <strong>apiservers</strong> are the gate agents — many of them, all serving passengers simultaneously behind one "Departures" sign (the load balancer); it doesn't matter which gate agent checks you in, they all have access to the same reservation system. The <strong>scheduler</strong> is the captain: only one flies the plane at any moment, but a fully-qualified co-pilot (the standby instance on another control-plane node) is strapped in and instrument-current, ready to take the yoke the instant the captain is incapacitated — that's the ~15 second Lease failover. Nobody wants two captains fighting over the controls at once, which is exactly why leader election exists instead of letting every scheduler instance act simultaneously.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>Component Activity Model — at a Glance</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>How many are active?</th><th>Coordination</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>kube-apiserver</td><td>ALL nodes, simultaneously</td><td>None needed — stateless, LB round-robins</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>kube-scheduler</td><td>ONE (the elected leader)</td><td>Lease object in <code>kube-system</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>kube-controller-manager</td><td>ONE (the elected leader)</td><td>Lease object in <code>kube-system</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>etcd</td><td>ALL nodes are Raft peers</td><td>Internal Raft leader (write ordering only)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.2 Stacked vs External etcd</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>There are two ways to deploy etcd in an HA cluster. The choice affects fault tolerance, complexity, and cost. You must understand both for the CKA exam.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Stacked etcd Topology — etcd on Control Plane Nodes</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STACKED etcd TOPOLOGY (Default with kubeadm)                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Each control plane node runs BOTH Kubernetes components AND etcd            │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌───────────────┐    ┌───────────────┐    ┌───────────────┐                │
│  │  CP Node 1    │    │  CP Node 2    │    │  CP Node 3    │                │
│  │ ┌───────────┐ │    │ ┌───────────┐ │    │ ┌───────────┐ │                │
│  │ │API Server │ │    │ │API Server │ │    │ │API Server │ │                │
│  │ │Scheduler  │ │    │ │Scheduler  │ │    │ │Scheduler  │ │                │
│  │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │    │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │    │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │                │
│  │ ├───────────┤ │    │ ├───────────┤ │    │ ├───────────┤ │                │
│  │ │  etcd  📀 │ │    │ │  etcd  📀 │ │    │ │  etcd  📀 │ │                │
│  │ └───────────┘ │    │ └───────────┘ │    │ └───────────┘ │                │
│  └───────┬───────┘    └───────┬───────┘    └───────┬───────┘                │
│          │                    │                    │                          │
│          └─────── Raft Replication ───────────────┘                          │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ Simpler setup — fewer nodes to manage                                   │
│  ✅ Default kubeadm topology                                                │
│  ⚠️ Risk: If a node dies, you lose both CP and an etcd member              │
│  📊 Minimum: 3 nodes (for etcd quorum)                                     │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>External etcd Topology — etcd on Dedicated Servers</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  EXTERNAL etcd TOPOLOGY                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  etcd runs on SEPARATE dedicated nodes — decoupled from control plane        │
│                                                                              │
│  Control Plane Tier:                                                         │
│  ┌───────────────┐    ┌───────────────┐    ┌───────────────┐                │
│  │  CP Node 1    │    │  CP Node 2    │    │  CP Node 3    │                │
│  │ ┌───────────┐ │    │ ┌───────────┐ │    │ ┌───────────┐ │                │
│  │ │API Server │ │    │ │API Server │ │    │ │API Server │ │                │
│  │ │Scheduler  │ │    │ │Scheduler  │ │    │ │Scheduler  │ │                │
│  │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │    │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │    │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │                │
│  │ └───────────┘ │    │ └───────────┘ │    │ └───────────┘ │                │
│  └───────┬───────┘    └───────┬───────┘    └───────┬───────┘                │
│          └────────────┬───────┘                    │                          │
│                       │  Connect via TLS            │                          │
│          ┌────────────┼────────────────────────────┘                          │
│          ▼            ▼            ▼                                          │
│  ┌───────────┐  ┌───────────┐  ┌───────────┐                                │
│  │ etcd-1 📀 │  │ etcd-2 📀 │  │ etcd-3 📀 │  ← Dedicated etcd cluster    │
│  └─────┬─────┘  └─────┬─────┘  └─────┬─────┘                                │
│        └───── Raft Replication ───────┘                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ etcd survives control plane failures                                    │
│  ✅ Can scale etcd independently                                            │
│  ⚠️ More complex — requires 6+ nodes minimum (3 CP + 3 etcd)               │
│  ⚠️ More certs to manage (API→etcd TLS)                                    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Topology Comparison</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Aspect</th>
                                <th>Stacked (default)</th>
                                <th>External etcd</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>etcd location</strong></td>
                                <td>On control plane node</td>
                                <td>Separate dedicated servers</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Setup complexity</strong></td>
                                <td>Easier — <code>kubeadm init</code> handles it</td>
                                <td>Harder — manual etcd cluster setup</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Fault isolation</strong></td>
                                <td>⚠️ CP failure = etcd member lost too</td>
                                <td>✅ etcd survives CP node failure</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Minimum nodes</strong></td>
                                <td>3 (combined CP + etcd)</td>
                                <td>6 (3 CP + 3 etcd)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Cost</strong></td>
                                <td>Lower — fewer servers</td>
                                <td>Higher — 2× the infrastructure</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>CKA Exam</strong></td>
                                <td>Most tested topology ✅</td>
                                <td>Conceptual understanding needed</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Best for</strong></td>
                                <td>Most production clusters</td>
                                <td>Critical workloads with strict SLAs</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Decision Guide — Which Topology Should easypj Actually Use?</h5>
                    <p>Choose <strong>stacked</strong> when: you want simplicity, have fewer machines to work with, are running a standard production workload, or are preparing for the CKA exam. Losing one control-plane node also removes one etcd member — but with 3 nodes that's still fine, because quorum (2 of 3) survives.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0">Choose <strong>external</strong> when: you want the datastore fully decoupled from control-plane churn, you rebuild apiserver nodes frequently (e.g. an automated pipeline that reimages control-plane VMs weekly), or you need to scale/secure etcd independently of the API servers. The cost is roughly double the machines and twice the operational surface.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h4>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: Which Topology Is on the Exam?</h4>
                    <p>The CKA exam uses <strong>stacked etcd</strong> topology (kubeadm default). When asked to backup/restore etcd, the etcd data dir is on the control plane node at <code>/var/lib/etcd</code>. The API server connects to etcd on <code>localhost:2379</code>. Know both topologies conceptually, but practice stacked for the exam.</p>
                    <p>⚠️ <strong>Either way, it's the etcd member COUNT — not the topology — that determines fault tolerance.</strong> A stacked 3-CP cluster and an external 3-etcd cluster both tolerate exactly 1 member loss; going external doesn't buy you more quorum headroom by itself, only independence between CP and etcd failure domains.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.3 Setting Up HA with kubeadm</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Setting up an HA cluster with kubeadm follows a specific sequence: configure the load balancer first, initialize the first control plane, then join additional control planes and workers.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>HA Setup Sequence — Step by Step</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    HA CLUSTER SETUP SEQUENCE                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Step 1          Step 2           Step 3           Step 4                    │
│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐              │
│  │ Setup    │    │ Init 1st │    │ Join     │    │ Join     │              │
│  │ Load     │───▶│ Control  │───▶│ More CP  │───▶│ Worker   │              │
│  │ Balancer │    │ Plane    │    │ Nodes    │    │ Nodes    │              │
│  └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘              │
│  HAProxy/nginx    kubeadm init    kubeadm join    kubeadm join              │
│  on :6443         --control-      --control-      (no flag)                 │
│                   plane-endpoint  plane                                      │
│                   =LB_IP:6443                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Step 5          Step 6           Step 7                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐                               │
│  │ Install  │    │ Verify   │    │ Test     │                               │
│  │ CNI      │───▶│ etcd     │───▶│ Failover │                               │
│  │ Plugin   │    │ Health   │    │          │                               │
│  └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘                               │
│  Calico/Cilium    etcdctl         Kill a CP node                             │
│                   endpoint        → verify LB                                │
│                   health          reroutes                                    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 1: HAProxy Configuration</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">/etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># HAProxy configuration for Kubernetes API Server HA</span>
frontend kubernetes-api
    bind *:6443
    mode tcp
    option tcplog
    default_backend kubernetes-api-backend

backend kubernetes-api-backend
    mode tcp
    option tcp-check
    balance roundrobin
    <span class="comment"># Health check: API server /healthz endpoint</span>
    server cp-1 10.0.0.1:6443 check fall 3 rise 2
    server cp-2 10.0.0.2:6443 check fall 3 rise 2
    server cp-3 10.0.0.3:6443 check fall 3 rise 2</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 2: Initialize First Control Plane</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — cp-1 (first control plane)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Initialize first control plane — point to the load balancer!</span>
$ sudo kubeadm init \
    --control-plane-endpoint "10.0.0.100:6443" \
    --upload-certs \
    --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16

<span class="comment"># Output includes TWO join commands:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. For additional control plane nodes (has --control-plane flag)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. For worker nodes (no --control-plane flag)</span>

<span class="comment"># Copy kubeconfig</span>
$ mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
$ sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
$ sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ The <code>--certificate-key</code> Expires in 2 Hours</h5>
                    <p>The <code>kubeadm-certs</code> Secret that <code>--upload-certs</code> creates is deliberately short-lived: <strong>kubeadm deletes it 2 hours after upload</strong> (the certificate key grants access to cluster-sensitive data, so it isn't left lying around indefinitely). If you don't finish joining every control-plane node inside that window, re-run <code>sudo kubeadm init phase upload-certs --upload-certs</code> on an already-joined control-plane node to get a fresh certificate-key without repeating the whole <code>init</code>. You can also pre-generate your own key with <code>kubeadm certs certificate-key</code> and pass it explicitly via <code>--certificate-key &lt;key&gt;</code> on <code>init</code> if you want a known value ahead of time.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Steps 3 &amp; 4: Join Additional Nodes</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — cp-2 and cp-3</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Join as additional control plane (note --control-plane flag!)</span>
$ sudo kubeadm join 10.0.0.100:6443 \
    --token &lt;token&gt; \
    --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;hash&gt; \
    --control-plane \
    --certificate-key &lt;cert-key&gt;</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — worker nodes</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Join as worker node (NO --control-plane flag)</span>
$ sudo kubeadm join 10.0.0.100:6443 \
    --token &lt;token&gt; \
    --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;hash&gt;</code></pre>
                </div>

