            <!-- ─────────── §35.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.4 etcd Quorum &amp; Fault Tolerance</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>etcd uses the <strong>Raft consensus algorithm</strong>. A majority of etcd members must agree before any write is committed. This majority is called the <strong>quorum</strong>. Understanding quorum math is essential for CKA.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>etcd Quorum Formula: (N/2) + 1</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    ETCD QUORUM — THE MATH                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Formula: Quorum = floor(N/2) + 1                                            │
│  Tolerated Failures = N - Quorum                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────┬────────┬─────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ Members │ Quorum │ Tolerated Failures  │ Recommendation               │  │
│  ├─────────┼────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤  │
│  │    1    │   1    │         0           │ ❌ Dev/test only              │  │
│  │    2    │   2    │         0           │ ❌ NEVER — worse than 1!     │  │
│  │    3    │   2    │         1           │ ✅ Standard production       │  │
│  │    4    │   3    │         1           │ ❌ Same tolerance as 3!      │  │
│  │    5    │   3    │         2           │ ✅ Large-scale production    │  │
│  │    6    │   4    │         2           │ ❌ Same tolerance as 5!      │  │
│  │    7    │   4    │         3           │ ⚠️ Rare — more latency       │  │
│  └─────────┴────────┴─────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                              │
│  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: Even numbers are WASTEFUL!                                 │
│  ─────────────────────────────────────────                                   │
│  3 members → tolerate 1 failure                                              │
│  4 members → STILL only tolerate 1 failure (need 3 for quorum)              │
│  → You added a node but got ZERO extra fault tolerance!                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ALWAYS USE ODD NUMBERS: 1, 3, 5, 7                                        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Quorum in Action — What Happens During Failures</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  3-MEMBER CLUSTER FAILURE SCENARIOS                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Normal (3/3 alive):         1 Node Down (2/3 alive):                        │
│  ┌────┐  ┌────┐  ┌────┐     ┌────┐  ┌────┐  ┌────┐                        │
│  │ E1 │  │ E2 │  │ E3 │     │ E1 │  │ E2 │  │ ╳╳ │  ← Node 3 down       │
│  │ ✅ │  │ ✅ │  │ ✅ │     │ ✅ │  │ ✅ │  │ ❌ │                        │
│  └────┘  └────┘  └────┘     └────┘  └────┘  └────┘                        │
│  Quorum: 2 of 3 = ✅         Quorum: 2 of 3 = ✅ (still works!)            │
│  Writes: ✅                  Writes: ✅                                      │
│  Reads:  ✅                  Reads:  ✅                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  2 Nodes Down (1/3 alive):   Split Brain (network partition):                │
│  ┌────┐  ┌────┐  ┌────┐     ┌────┐  │  ┌────┐  ┌────┐                     │
│  │ E1 │  │ ╳╳ │  │ ╳╳ │     │ E1 │  │  │ E2 │  │ E3 │                     │
│  │ ✅ │  │ ❌ │  │ ❌ │     │ ✅ │  │  │ ✅ │  │ ✅ │                     │
│  └────┘  └────┘  └────┘     └────┘  │  └────┘  └────┘                     │
│  Quorum: 1 of 3 = ❌         Side A: 1 (no quorum ❌)                       │
│  Writes: ❌ CLUSTER DOWN!    Side B: 2 (has quorum ✅)                       │
│  Reads:  ❌ (stale reads     → Side B continues serving                      │
│             may be possible)  → Side A becomes read-only                     │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 CKA Exam Quick Recall</h5>
                    <p>If asked "how many etcd node failures can a 5-member cluster tolerate?" — the answer is <strong>2</strong>. Formula: <code>floor(5/2) + 1 = 3</code> needed for quorum, so <code>5 - 3 = 2</code> failures tolerated. <strong>Always use odd numbers for etcd cluster size.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why Even Numbers Are Dangerous</h5>
                    <p>A 4-member etcd cluster tolerates only <strong>1 failure</strong> — the same as a 3-member cluster — but uses more resources. Worse, with 4 members a network partition can create two equal groups (2 vs 2), and <strong>neither has quorum</strong>, causing a total outage. Odd numbers eliminate this symmetric split risk.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Production Incident Pattern: Slow Disks Trigger False Leader Elections</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Why etcd Cares About Disk fsync Latency, Not Just "Is the Disk Up"</div>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  THE CASCADE: shared/slow disk → missed heartbeats → spurious re-elections     │
│                                                                              │
│  1. etcd fsyncs EVERY write to disk before acknowledging it (durability)    │
│     — this is not optional; it's how Raft guarantees no data loss           │
│  2. Cluster runs on network-attached or noisy-neighbor-shared storage       │
│     (common mistake: "just use the same SSD pool as everything else")      │
│  3. A neighboring workload spikes disk I/O → etcd's fsync latency jumps    │
│     from ~2ms to 200ms+                                                     │
│  4. The Raft heartbeat/leader-lease deadline is missed (default ~1s)        │
│  5. Followers assume the leader is dead → trigger a NEW ELECTION           │
│  6. Election causes a brief write stall cluster-wide → apiserver requests  │
│     queue up → kubectl/controllers appear to "hang" for a few seconds      │
│  7. Once the disk spike passes, a new leader stabilizes — but if the noisy │
│     neighbor keeps spiking, this repeats ("leader flapping")               │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  This looks like a NETWORK problem (leader keeps changing!) but the     │
│      root cause is almost always DISK, not network.                        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely root cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Frequent <code>"leader changed"</code> log lines with no node actually failing</td><td>Disk fsync latency spikes (shared/network storage)</td><td>Move etcd to dedicated local SSD/NVMe; never share disks with noisy workloads</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>etcdctl check perf</code> or <code>wal_fsync_duration_seconds</code> metric shows high p99</td><td>Under-provisioned or contended disk I/O</td><td>Provision IOPS headroom; monitor <code>etcd_disk_wal_fsync_duration_seconds</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Cluster-wide write stalls lasting a few seconds, correlated across all 3 CP nodes</td><td>Network partition OR clock skew (see below), not disk</td><td>Check NTP sync status across all control-plane nodes first</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Clock Skew Is the Other Silent Killer of Raft Stability</h5>
                    <p>Raft's leader lease and election timers assume all members' clocks are reasonably in sync. If NTP/chrony isn't running (or drifts) on one control-plane node, that node's view of "has the lease expired yet?" disagrees with the others — causing the same symptom as disk latency: unnecessary re-elections. <strong>Always verify <code>chronyc tracking</code> or <code>timedatectl</code> shows synced clocks on every control-plane node</strong> before chasing a "network flakiness" ghost that's actually a clock or a disk.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>etcd Backup &amp; Restore in HA — Critical CKA Skill</h4>
                <p>Backing up and restoring etcd is a <strong>guaranteed CKA exam topic</strong>. In an HA cluster, you must back up from <strong>one member</strong> and restore to <strong>all members</strong>.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — etcd backup &amp; restore (CKA exam critical)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># STEP 1: BACKUP — Take snapshot from any healthy member</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /tmp/etcd-backup.db \
    --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
    --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
    --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
    --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify the backup</span>
$ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /tmp/etcd-backup.db --write-table
+----------+----------+------------+------------+
|   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |
+----------+----------+------------+------------+
| abc12def |   150234 |       1847 |     4.2 MB |
+----------+----------+------------+------------+

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># STEP 2: RESTORE — Stop API server, restore, restart</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># On EACH control plane node:</span>
$ mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/

<span class="comment"># Restore with a NEW data directory (don't overwrite!)</span>
$ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /tmp/etcd-backup.db \
    --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored \
    --name=cp-1 \
    --initial-cluster="cp-1=https://10.0.0.1:2380,cp-2=https://10.0.0.2:2380,cp-3=https://10.0.0.3:2380" \
    --initial-advertise-peer-urls=https://10.0.0.1:2380

<span class="comment"># Update etcd manifest to point to new data dir</span>
$ sed -i 's|/var/lib/etcd|/var/lib/etcd-restored|g' \
    /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># Restart API server</span>
$ mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>etcd Backup Strategy — When &amp; Where</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  etcd BACKUP BEST PRACTICES                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐               │
│  │  BACKUP SCHEDULE:                                         │               │
│  │                                                           │               │
│  │  CronJob every 30 min ──▶ /backups/etcd-{timestamp}.db   │               │
│  │  Keep last 48 backups (24 hours of 30-min snapshots)      │               │
│  │  Store offsite: S3 / Azure Blob / GCS bucket ✅           │               │
│  │  NEVER store backups only on the etcd nodes! ❌            │               │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘               │
│                                                                              │
│  RESTORE SCENARIOS:                                                          │
│  ┌───────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐               │
│  │ Scenario          │ Action                               │               │
│  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤               │
│  │ 1 etcd member     │ Remove &amp; re-add member (simpler)   │               │
│  │ lost              │ etcdctl member add                   │               │
│  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤               │
│  │ Quorum lost       │ Full restore from snapshot on ALL    │               │
│  │ (majority down)   │ remaining members                    │               │
│  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤               │
│  │ Data corruption   │ Full restore from known-good         │               │
│  │                   │ snapshot + verify cluster health      │               │
│  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤               │
│  │ Disaster recovery │ Restore snapshot to new cluster +    │               │
│  │                   │ update certificates if needed         │               │
│  └───────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘               │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  REMEMBER: etcd restore creates a NEW cluster ID — all members          │
│     must restore from the SAME snapshot to form a consistent cluster!        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Common Mistake: Forgetting TLS Flags</h5>
                    <p>The #1 reason <code>etcdctl</code> commands fail on the CKA exam: missing the <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, and <code>--key</code> flags. These certs are always at <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code>. Also remember to set <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> — version 2 has different commands!</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 How This Connects: HA Doesn't Replace Backups, It Changes the Blast Radius</h5>
                    <p>Chapters 11 and 29 already taught the mechanics of <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code>/<code>restore</code> on a single-node cluster — that procedure is unchanged here. What HA changes is the <strong>blast radius and coordination</strong>: on a single-node cluster a bad restore only affects that one node; on a 3-member HA cluster, restoring from a snapshot creates a brand-new cluster ID, so <strong>every</strong> member must restore from the identical snapshot with matching <code>--initial-cluster</code> flags, or you end up with etcd peers that refuse to agree with each other. Treat HA etcd restore as a full-cluster maintenance operation, never a single-node op you happen to repeat three times.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>Backup/Restore Quick Reference — Single-Node vs HA</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Single control-plane (Ch11/Ch29)</th><th>HA cluster (this chapter)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Snapshot source</td><td>The one etcd instance</td><td>Any ONE healthy member</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Restore target</td><td>The one etcd instance</td><td>ALL members, same snapshot, matching <code>--initial-cluster</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>New cluster ID risk</td><td>N/A — only one member exists</td><td>Every member must agree, or they refuse to form quorum</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.5 Leader Election Deep Dive</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>In an HA cluster, only <strong>one instance</strong> of the kube-scheduler and kube-controller-manager is actively making decisions at any time. This is managed through <strong>leader election</strong> using the Kubernetes Lease API.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Leader Election — How It Works</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  LEADER ELECTION MECHANISM                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Component        │ Active Instances │ Election Method                       │
│  ─────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────               │
│  kube-apiserver   │ ALL (active-     │ No election needed —                  │
│                   │ active)          │ stateless, LB distributes             │
│  ─────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────               │
│  kube-scheduler   │ ONE (leader)     │ Lease object in kube-system          │
│                   │                  │ --leader-elect=true                   │
│  ─────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────               │
│  kube-controller  │ ONE (leader)     │ Lease object in kube-system          │
│  -manager         │                  │ --leader-elect=true                   │
│  ─────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────               │
│  etcd             │ ALL (Raft        │ Raft protocol — internal             │
│                   │ consensus)       │ leader for write ordering            │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│  │  LEASE-BASED LEADER ELECTION FLOW:                          │            │
│  │                                                              │            │
│  │  1. All instances try to acquire a Lease object              │            │
│  │  2. Winner writes its identity + timestamp to Lease          │            │
│  │  3. Leader renews Lease well within the 15s duration (~10s deadline) │            │
│  │  4. Other instances watch — if Lease expires, they compete   │            │
│  │  5. Fastest to write wins → new leader in ~15 seconds        │            │
│  │                                                              │            │
│  │  Timeline:                                                   │            │
│  │  ─────────── Leader CP-1 ──────────── ╳ (dies)              │            │
│  │  ───── standby CP-2 ────────────────── ★ new leader ──────  │            │
│  │  ───── standby CP-3 ────────────────── standby ───────────  │            │
│  │                               ~15s gap                       │            │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Inspecting Leader Election</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — check current leaders</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all leases in kube-system</span>
$ kubectl get lease -n kube-system
NAME                      HOLDER       AGE
kube-scheduler            cp-1_xxx     3d
kube-controller-manager   cp-1_xxx     3d

<span class="comment"># Describe scheduler lease — see who holds it</span>
$ kubectl describe lease kube-scheduler -n kube-system
Name:         kube-scheduler
Namespace:    kube-system
Spec:
  Holder Identity:    cp-1_xxxxxxxx-xxxx  ← THIS is the active leader
  Lease Duration:     15s
  Renew Time:         2024-03-15T10:45:32Z

<span class="comment"># Check leader-elect flags in scheduler manifest</span>
$ cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml | grep leader
    - --leader-elect=true                    ← Enables leader election (default)
    - --leader-elect-lease-duration=15s       ← how long a lease stays valid
    - --leader-elect-renew-deadline=10s       ← leader must renew within this
    - --leader-elect-retry-period=2s          ← how often standbys retry acquiring</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 What Happens During Leader Failover</h5>
                    <p>When the leader dies, there's a brief gap (~15 seconds) before a new leader is elected. During this time, <strong>no new pods are scheduled</strong> and <strong>no controller actions happen</strong> (like scaling Deployments). Existing pods continue running — they don't depend on the scheduler or controller-manager once scheduled.</p>
                </div>

                
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.6 Load Balancer Configuration for HA</h3>
                <p>The load balancer is the <strong>front door</strong> to your HA cluster. All kubectl commands, worker node registrations, and internal API calls go through it. If the LB fails, the entire cluster is unreachable — making the LB itself another critical HA component.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Load Balancer Health Check Flow</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  LOAD BALANCER HEALTH CHECK &amp; ROUTING                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐                                        │
│  │        kubectl / kubelet        │                                        │
│  │        All API requests         │                                        │
│  └───────────────┬─────────────────┘                                        │
│                  │                                                            │
│                  ▼ :6443                                                      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐                                        │
│  │        LOAD BALANCER            │      Health Check:                      │
│  │      (HAProxy / nginx)          │      GET /healthz on :6443              │
│  │                                 │      Every 5 seconds                    │
│  │   Rules:                        │                                        │
│  │   - TCP mode (Layer 4)          │      Response 200 → Backend UP ✅      │
│  │   - Round-robin distribution    │      No response  → Remove from pool ❌│
│  │   - 3 failed checks → remove   │                                        │
│  │   - 2 passed checks → re-add   │                                        │
│  └──┬──────────┬──────────┬───────┘                                        │
│     │          │          │                                                  │
│     ▼          ▼          ▼                                                  │
│  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐                                              │
│  │CP-1  │  │CP-2  │  │CP-3  │                                              │
│  │:6443 │  │:6443 │  │:6443 │                                              │
│  │ ✅   │  │ ✅   │  │ ❌   │  ← CP-3 failed health check                 │
│  └──────┘  └──────┘  └──────┘    → removed from pool                       │
│  ▲ 50%     ▲ 50%     (0%)       → traffic split between CP-1 &amp; CP-2       │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>/healthz</code> vs <code>/readyz</code> — What Does kubeadm's Own Reference Config Actually Check?</h5>
                    <p>The apiserver exposes several health endpoints — the original <code>/healthz</code>, plus the more granular <code>/livez</code> and <code>/readyz</code> split out since Kubernetes v1.16. It's tempting to assume the newest, most specific endpoint (<code>/readyz</code>) is the "correct" one to health-check, but the official kubeadm HA reference material (the <code>keepalived</code>/HAProxy examples in the kubeadm project's own <code>ha-considerations.md</code>) checks <strong><code>GET /healthz</code></strong> over HTTPS — for example its reference <code>keepalived</code> health-check script runs <code>curl -sfk --max-time 2 https://localhost:6443/healthz -o /dev/null</code>. Either endpoint is a legitimate choice; what actually matters is that the check is an <strong>HTTP-level GET request that expects a 200</strong>, not a bare TCP-connect-only check (which only proves the port is open, not that the apiserver behind it is behaviorally healthy).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0">The kubeadm HA doc itself is even more minimal by default: it describes the baseline load-balancer health check as simply <strong>"a TCP check on the port the kube-apiserver listens on (default <code>:6443</code>)"</strong> — an HTTP GET to <code>/healthz</code> or <code>/readyz</code> is an upgrade over that baseline, not a requirement the exam will test you on. Use whichever endpoint your LB config already uses consistently; don't treat this as a hard "readyz-only" rule.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>nginx Stream Proxy Configuration (Alternative to HAProxy)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">/etc/nginx/nginx.conf — stream proxy for K8s API</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-nginx">stream {
    upstream kubernetes_api {
        <span class="comment"># Round-robin between control plane nodes</span>
        server 10.0.0.1:6443 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
        server 10.0.0.2:6443 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
        server 10.0.0.3:6443 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
    }

    server {
        listen 6443;
        proxy_pass kubernetes_api;
        proxy_timeout 10m;
        proxy_connect_timeout 1s;
    }
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ LB Single Point of Failure?</h5>
                    <p>Yes — a single load balancer is itself a SPOF. In production, use <strong>keepalived + VRRP</strong> for LB failover (two LB hosts sharing a virtual IP), <strong>kube-vip</strong> (a single static pod combining VIP management and load balancing), or use a cloud-managed load balancer (AWS NLB, Azure LB, GCP TCP LB) which is inherently HA.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>keepalived + VRRP — Making the Load Balancer HA</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  LOAD BALANCER HIGH AVAILABILITY WITH keepalived                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Problem: Single LB = Single Point of Failure                                │
│  Solution: Two LBs sharing a Virtual IP (VIP) via VRRP protocol             │
│                                                                              │
│                     ┌──────────────┐                                        │
│   kubectl ────▶     │  VIP:        │     ← Clients connect to VIP           │
│   kubelet           │  10.0.0.100  │        NOT individual LB IPs            │
│                     └──────┬───────┘                                        │
│                            │                                                 │
│              ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐                                  │
│              │                           │                                   │
│     ┌────────▼────────┐        ┌────────▼────────┐                          │
│     │  LB-1 (MASTER)  │  VRRP  │  LB-2 (BACKUP)  │                          │
│     │  10.0.0.10      │◄──────▶│  10.0.0.11       │                          │
│     │  Owns VIP ✅    │ health  │  Standby ⏳      │                          │
│     │  Priority: 101  │ checks  │  Priority: 100   │                          │
│     └────────┬────────┘        └────────┬─────────┘                          │
│              │                           │                                   │
│              └─────────┬─┬───────────────┘                                  │
│                        │ │ │                                                 │
│                   CP-1 CP-2 CP-3                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Failover Flow:                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│  │  1. LB-1 sends VRRP advertisements every 1 second          │            │
│  │  2. LB-2 monitors — if no VRRP from LB-1 for 3 seconds    │            │
│  │  3. LB-2 claims the VIP → sends gratuitous ARP             │            │
│  │  4. Traffic redirects to LB-2 in &lt;5 seconds ✅            │            │
│  │  5. When LB-1 recovers → reclaims VIP (preempt mode)       │            │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>kube-vip — a Modern, Single-Component Alternative to keepalived + HAProxy</h4>
                <p>The official kubeadm project documents a third option alongside "keepalived + HAProxy" and "cloud provider load balancer": <strong>kube-vip</strong>. Instead of running two separate pieces of software (a VIP manager and a TCP load balancer), kube-vip combines VIP management AND API-server load balancing into a single static pod that every control-plane node runs — simplifying the LB tier from two moving parts down to one.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>keepalived + HAProxy</th><th>kube-vip</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Moving parts</td><td>Two separate daemons per LB node</td><td>One static pod per control-plane node</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>VIP mechanism</td><td>VRRP (keepalived)</td><td>ARP-based (same-subnet leader election) or BGP-based (all nodes advertise the VIP)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Where it runs</td><td>Dedicated LB host(s), separate from CP nodes</td><td>On the control-plane nodes themselves, as a static pod in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Bootstrap dependency</td><td>LB must exist before <code>kubeadm init</code></td><td>Needs access to the apiserver during cluster init — <code>admin.conf</code> provides the initial authentication</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 kube-vip in Practice</h5>
                    <p>Because kube-vip runs as a static pod, it can be deployed before the cluster even exists — kubeadm-style bootstrapping still works because static pods are started by the kubelet directly, with no apiserver dependency for scheduling. In <strong>ARP mode</strong>, control-plane nodes on the same IP subnet elect a leader that answers ARP requests for the VIP (conceptually similar to keepalived's VRRP). In <strong>BGP mode</strong>, every node advertises the VIP via a routing protocol and the network fabric handles path selection — useful when nodes span L3 boundaries where ARP-based VIP floating won't work.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Production Incident Pattern: The Reconnect "Thundering Herd"</h4>
                <p>A subtler LB failure mode isn't the LB going down — it's a control-plane node briefly <strong>flapping</strong> (failing one health check, then recovering seconds later). Every client that was holding a long-lived watch connection to that apiserver gets disconnected at once and immediately reconnects to whichever apiservers remain healthy.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One Flapping Node Can Overload the Survivors</div>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  THUNDERING HERD — a brief flap on cp-2 hammers cp-1 and cp-3                │
│                                                                              │
│  Normal: watches spread ~evenly        cp-2 flaps for 8s (fails healthcheck)│
│  cp-1: 400 watches                     cp-1: 400 → 600 watches (reconnects) │
│  cp-2: 400 watches                     cp-2: 400 → 0 (pulled from LB pool)  │
│  cp-3: 400 watches                     cp-3: 400 → 600 watches (reconnects) │
│                                                                              │
│  cp-2 recovers 8s later and rejoins the pool — but the RECONNECT STORM     │
│  itself (all clients re-establishing watches simultaneously) can spike CPU  │
│  and etcd read load on cp-1/cp-3 harder than the original flap did,        │
│  occasionally causing a SECOND, larger apiserver slowdown right after the  │
│  "fix."                                                                     │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Don't Chase the Flap Alone — Watch for the Echo</h5>
                    <p>When investigating a brief control-plane blip, check apiserver CPU/memory a few seconds <em>after</em> the flap resolves, not just during it — the reconnect storm from redistributed watches is often the bigger, second-order symptom. Client-side, <code>kube-apiserver</code>'s built-in watch-bookmark and priority-and-fairness (APF) features exist specifically to smooth this kind of reconnect spike; don't disable APF on an HA cluster without understanding this trade-off.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

