                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 PDB vs Anti-Affinity — They Protect Against Different Kinds of Disruption</h5>
                    <p>These two mechanisms are complementary, not redundant. The <strong>PodDisruptionBudget</strong> protects easypj during <strong>voluntary</strong> disruptions — a <code>kubectl drain</code>, a node upgrade, or the cluster autoscaler removing a node — by making the eviction API refuse any eviction that would drop the Deployment below <code>minAvailable</code>. The <strong>podAntiAffinity</strong> rule protects against <strong>involuntary</strong> disruptions — a node crashing outright — because no single node hosts all the replicas in the first place, so one node dying can never take down 100% of easypj-web at once. Skip either one and you still have a real gap: a PDB alone doesn't stop 3 replicas from all landing on one node that then crashes; anti-affinity alone doesn't stop an over-eager drain from evicting every remaining replica one at a time.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Looking Ahead: HA Is the "You Run It" Answer — Chapter 36 Is the "Someone Else Runs It" Answer</h5>
                    <p>Everything in this chapter — the load balancer, the etcd quorum math, the leader-election Lease, the <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> indirection — exists to answer one question: <em>how do I stop the control plane being a single point of failure?</em> Chapter 36 answers the exact same question a completely different way: hand the control plane to a managed provider (EKS/GKE/AKS) so apiserver HA and etcd operation are no longer your job at all. Notice what does <strong>not</strong> change between the two: the ServiceAccount and RBAC model from Chapter 25 still governs how easypj's pods authenticate to the Kubernetes API either way — the only thing that changes in Ch36 is that pod identity <em>also</em> gets federated outward to the cloud provider's IAM (IRSA on EKS, Workload Identity on GKE, AAD Pod Identity on AKS) so pods can reach cloud services like S3 or Cloud SQL without long-lived cloud credentials baked into a Secret. HA and managed control planes are two different answers to the same availability question; ServiceAccount-based identity is the one piece that spans both unchanged.</p>
                </div>

