                    <div class="uth-callout">
                    <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> The exam environment uses stacked etcd. When you back up/restore etcd on the exam, the data directory is on the control-plane node itself (<code>/var/lib/etcd</code>), and the apiserver reaches it at <code>localhost:2379</code> — because on stacked topology, "local" IS where etcd lives.
                    </div>

                    <h5>② etcd Quorum &amp; Raft Consensus — the Math That Decides Everything</h5>
                    <p>Every HA decision ultimately reduces to one formula. etcd uses the <strong>Raft</strong> consensus protocol: a write is only committed once a <strong>majority</strong> of members acknowledge it. This single rule is why odd counts matter, why "just add a node" doesn't always help, and why losing too many members doesn't corrupt data — it just stops accepting writes.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ETCD QUORUM — How Many Failures Can Your Cluster Survive?                   │
│  Formula:  quorum = floor(N/2) + 1     tolerated failures = floor(N/2)       │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌───────┬──────────┬──────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ Nodes │ Quorum   │ Max Failures │ Notes                                │  │
│  ├───────┼──────────┼──────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤  │
│  │   1   │    1     │      0       │ No HA — single point of failure      │  │
│  │   2   │    2     │      0       │ WORSE than 1 — needs BOTH alive      │  │
│  │   3   │    2     │      1       │ ✅ Minimum for production HA         │  │
│  │   4   │    3     │      1       │ ❌ Same tolerance as 3 — wasted node │  │
│  │   5   │    3     │      2       │ ✅ Large-scale production            │  │
│  │   7   │    4     │      3       │ ⚠️  Rare — Raft round-trips add up   │  │
│  └───────┴──────────┴──────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                              │
│  RULE: ALWAYS odd (3, 5, 7). Even numbers add cost/latency for ZERO extra   │
│  tolerance, AND they can split evenly (e.g. 2-vs-2) so NEITHER half has a   │
│  majority — a failure mode odd counts structurally cannot hit.              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                    <div class="uth-callout">
                    <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> "With N etcd members, how many failures are tolerated?" is a guaranteed question style. Answer with floor(N/2) instantly: 3→1, 5→2, 7→3. Below quorum, etcd does not silently elect a leader from the minority (that would risk two diverging "leaders") — it simply refuses new writes until a majority is restored.
                    </div>

                    <h5>③ API Server Load Balancing — Why There's No apiserver "Leader"</h5>
                    <p>It's tempting to assume every control-plane component works the same way, but the apiserver is fundamentally different from the scheduler and controller-manager: it is completely <strong>stateless</strong> per-request, so there is nothing to elect a leader over.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  API SERVER LOAD BALANCING — stateless, so ALL instances are active          │
│                                                                              │
│   kubectl ────▶ haproxy/nginx LB (10.0.1.200:6443)                          │
│                        │                                                     │
│              ┌─────────┼─────────┐                                          │
│              ▼         ▼         ▼                                          │
│         CP-Node1   CP-Node2   CP-Node3      (ALL active, ALL serving)       │
│         :6443      :6443      :6443                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Any apiserver can serve reads directly from its own watch cache.           │
│  Any apiserver can accept writes — it just forwards them to etcd, and       │
│  Raft (inside etcd) handles ordering. No apiserver-to-apiserver election.   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Active instances</th><th>Coordination mechanism</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>kube-apiserver</td><td>ALL (active-active)</td><td>None needed — stateless, LB distributes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>kube-scheduler</td><td>ONE (leader)</td><td>Lease object in kube-system</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>kube-controller-manager</td><td>ONE (leader)</td><td>Lease object in kube-system</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>etcd</td><td>ALL (Raft peers)</td><td>Internal Raft leader for write ordering only</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout">
                    <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Don't confuse etcd's internal Raft leader with the scheduler/controller-manager Lease leader — they're unrelated concepts that happen to share the word "leader." etcd's Raft leader is invisible to you operationally; the Lease leader is the one you check with <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code>.
                    </div>

                    <h5>④ etcd Learner Members — Growing a Cluster Without Risking Quorum</h5>
                    <p>Adding a brand-new etcd member as a full voting member immediately is riskier than it looks: until the new member fully catches up on the Raft log, it can't meaningfully vote, yet it already counts toward the quorum denominator — briefly making the cluster MORE fragile, not less. etcd solves this with <strong>learner members</strong>.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ETCD LEARNER MEMBERS — safely growing a cluster                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Step 1: Add as LEARNER (non-voting)                                       │
│    etcdctl member add cp-4 --peer-urls=https://10.0.0.14:2380 --learner     │
│    ┌────┐  ┌────┐  ┌────┐   ┌──────────┐                                   │
│    │ E1 │  │ E2 │  │ E3 │   │E4 LEARNER│ ← catching up, does NOT count      │
│    │vote│  │vote│  │vote│   │(no vote) │   toward quorum yet                │
│    └────┘  └────┘  └────┘   └──────────┘                                   │
│    Quorum still = 2 of 3 voting members — UNCHANGED, no risk added          │
│                                                                              │
│  Step 2: Once caught up, PROMOTE to voting member                          │
│    etcdctl member promote &lt;learner-id&gt;                                     │
│    ┌────┐  ┌────┐  ┌────┐   ┌────┐                                         │
│    │ E1 │  │ E2 │  │ E3 │   │ E4 │  ← NOW a full voter                      │
│    │vote│  │vote│  │vote│   │vote│                                         │
│    └────┘  └────┘  └────┘   └────┘                                         │
│    Quorum recalculated = 3 of 4 (or plan for 5 total to stay odd)           │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                    <div class="uth-callout">
                    <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> A learner member replicates the log but has no vote and cannot become Raft leader — it exists purely so a slow-joining member can't stall writes or accidentally tip quorum math mid-catch-up. Always add via <code>--learner</code> then <code>member promote</code> when scaling a production etcd cluster; going straight to a voting member is the riskier path.
                    </div>

                    <h5>⑤ The Stable Endpoint Indirection &amp; Certificate SANs</h5>
                    <p>The single design idea that makes an HA control plane possible is <strong>indirection</strong>: no client, kubeconfig, or kubelet ever talks to a control-plane node directly by IP — they all talk to one name, and that name is free to point anywhere.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  THE ENDPOINT INDIRECTION — why a CP node is disposable                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ~/.kube/config, every kubelet.conf, every static pod manifest:             │
│    server: https://lb.easypj:6443    ← ONE name, baked in at kubeadm init   │
│                                                                              │
│                lb.easypj:6443 (LB / keepalived VIP)                        │
│               ╱          │          ╲                                       │
│           CP-1         CP-2        CP-3   ← any one can die, rebuild, or    │
│      (rebuilt today)  (original)  (moved AZ)  move — the NAME never changes │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  certSANs requirement: the apiserver's TLS cert must list              │
│      "lb.easypj" (or the VIP) in its Subject Alternative Names.            │
│      kubeadm adds --control-plane-endpoint to certSANs automatically       │
│      at init time — but if you front an EXISTING cluster with a NEW LB     │
│      name later, you must regenerate the apiserver cert with that SAN     │
│      or every client's TLS handshake will be rejected.                     │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                    <div class="uth-callout">
                    <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> This is exactly why <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> cannot be bolted on after a single-node <code>kubeadm init</code> — the certificate was never issued with that name in its SANs, and every already-joined component was configured to trust a different address. Set it on the very first init, even for a cluster you intend to start single-node.
                    </div>

                    <h5>⑥ Recovering a Failed Control-Plane Node &amp; Leader-Election Tuning Trade-offs</h5>
                    <p>Two operational realities that separate "read about HA" from "run HA in production": how to safely replace a dead control-plane node, and how the Lease timing constants trade failover speed against false-positive flapping.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  RECOVERING A FAILED CP NODE — the correct order of operations              │
│                                                                              │
│  ❌ WRONG: kubeadm join --control-plane  (on the replacement, immediately)   │
│     → the dead node's OLD etcd member entry is still in the member list →   │
│       member list becomes corrupted / inconsistent                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ RIGHT:                                                                  │
│    1. etcdctl member remove &lt;dead-member-id&gt;   (from a HEALTHY node)       │
│    2. kubeadm reset -f                          (on the replacement node)   │
│    3. kubeadm join ... --control-plane --certificate-key ...  (re-join)     │
│    4. etcdctl member list -w table              (confirm quorum restored)  │
│                                                                              │
│  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │
│  LEASE TUNING — failover speed vs false-positive risk                      │
│    --leader-elect-lease-duration=15s   ← how long a lease is valid          │
│    --leader-elect-renew-deadline=10s   ← leader must renew within this      │
│    --leader-elect-retry-period=2s      ← how often standbys retry acquiring │
│    Shorter values = faster failover, but a leader under brief network       │
│    jitter can lose its lease and trigger an unnecessary failover ("flap").  │
│                                                                              │
│  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │
│  MULTI-AZ LATENCY — spread for resilience, not distance                    │
│    etcd is latency-sensitive (a Raft round-trip on every write). Spread     │
│    members across AZs within ONE region for resilience — cross-region      │
│    etcd members add enough round-trip latency to cripple write throughput. │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                    <div class="uth-callout">
                    <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Never re-join a failed control-plane node without first removing its stale etcd member — this exact mistake corrupts the member list and can leave a cluster stranded below quorum even though "enough" machines are technically running. Remove, reset, then re-join, in that order, every time.
                    </div>

                    <h5>⑦ Planned Maintenance — Rolling Patches/Reboots Without Losing Quorum</h5>
                    <p>Failure recovery (point ⑥) is reactive; production clusters also need a <strong>proactive</strong> runbook for routine OS patching, kernel upgrades, and kubeadm version upgrades that doesn't accidentally recreate the exact outage HA was built to prevent. The rule is simple to state and easy to violate under time pressure: touch one control-plane node at a time, and verify quorum is fully restored before touching the next.</p>
                    <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ROLLING CONTROL-PLANE MAINTENANCE — the safe order of operations            │
│                                                                              │
│  ❌ WRONG: patch cp-1 and cp-2 back-to-back because "the maintenance window │
│     is short" — the instant cp-2 reboots, only cp-3 is left (1 of 3), and   │
│     etcd has LOST QUORUM mid-patch. Total outage, self-inflicted.           │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ RIGHT, per node, in sequence:                                           │
│    1. etcdctl endpoint health --cluster   → confirm ALL 3 members healthy   │
│       BEFORE starting (never begin maintenance on an already-degraded       │
│       cluster)                                                              │
│    2. kubectl cordon cp-N                  → stop new work landing here     │
│       (mostly relevant if workloads are scheduled on CP nodes too)          │
│    3. Patch OS / reboot / upgrade kubeadm on cp-N                           │
│    4. Wait for cp-N's kubelet AND etcd member to report healthy again:      │
│       kubectl get nodes  +  etcdctl endpoint health --cluster               │
│    5. kubectl uncordon cp-N                                                 │
│    6. ONLY THEN move to cp-N+1 and repeat from step 1                      │
│                                                                              │
│  The load balancer's health check (/healthz or /readyz) naturally routes   │
│  traffic away from cp-N while it's down for step 3 — that's the whole      │
│  point of the LB, but it does NOT protect you from stacking two            │
│  simultaneous outages.                                                     │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Maintenance style</th><th>Nodes touched at once</th><th>Quorum risk</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Rolling, one at a time, health-gated</td><td>1 of 3</td><td>None — quorum (2) always intact</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Fast" batch of 2 to save time</td><td>2 of 3</td><td>Quorum LOST — self-inflicted outage</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout">
                    <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> The exam won't time you on a maintenance runbook, but production incident post-mortems very often trace back to exactly this mistake — someone patched "just one more" node to finish the maintenance window early, without re-checking quorum first. Always gate the next node on a green health check of the current one, never on the clock.
                    </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch35-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>HA = ≥3 control-plane nodes behind a load balancer</strong> (the stable <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> on :6443) plus a quorum etcd cluster. Always use ODD counts.</li>
                    <li><strong>etcd quorum is N/2+1</strong> — 3 members tolerate 1 failure, 5 tolerate 2. Lose quorum and the cluster goes read-only/unavailable until it's restored.</li>
                    <li><strong>apiservers are ACTIVE-ACTIVE</strong> (the LB spreads traffic across all of them); <strong>scheduler and controller-manager are ACTIVE-PASSIVE</strong>, electing one leader via a Lease object in <code>kube-system</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubeadm builds it with two flags</strong>: <code>kubeadm init --control-plane-endpoint &lt;LB&gt; --upload-certs</code>, then join the other control-plane nodes with <code>--control-plane --certificate-key</code>, then join workers normally.</li>
                    <li><strong>Stacked etcd (default) is simpler and most exam-tested</strong>; external etcd decouples the datastore for resilience at higher ops cost. Either way, the etcd member COUNT — not the topology — sets fault tolerance.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify leaders</strong> with <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code>; <strong>verify etcd</strong> with <code>etcdctl member list -w table</code> and <code>etcdctl endpoint health --cluster</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>App-level HA still matters</strong> — spread easypj with <code>podAntiAffinity</code> + a PodDisruptionBudget + multi-AZ storage so no single failure drops all replicas. A resilient control plane does nothing if easypj runs as one replica on one node.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch35-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <p>The CKA exam uses <strong>stacked etcd</strong> topology (kubeadm default). When asked to backup/restore etcd, the etcd data dir is on the control plane node at <code>/var/lib/etcd</code>. The API server connects to etcd on <code>localhost:2379</code>. Know both topologies conceptually, but practice stacked for the exam.</p>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>Stacked vs External etcd</strong> — Stacked: etcd runs on control plane nodes (simpler, fewer machines). External: etcd on dedicated nodes (better isolation, harder to set up). The CKA uses stacked topology. Know both for conceptual questions</li>
                        <li><strong>etcd quorum formula</strong> — <code>(N/2) + 1</code> members must be alive. 3 nodes = tolerate 1 failure. 5 nodes = tolerate 2. Always use ODD numbers. Even numbers waste a node without improving fault tolerance</li>
                        <li><strong>Leader election</strong> — Only ONE controller-manager and ONE scheduler are active at a time (the leader). Others are standby. Check leader: <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code>. If the leader dies, a new one is elected in ~15s</li>
                        <li><strong><code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> is critical</strong> — Must point to a load balancer, not a single node IP. Without this, adding control plane nodes later fails. Set during <code>kubeadm init</code> and it CANNOT be changed after</li>
                        <li><strong>Pod anti-affinity for HA workloads</strong> — Without <code>podAntiAffinity</code>, all replicas could land on the same node. If that node dies, 100% downtime. Always spread critical workloads across nodes</li>
                        <li><strong>Forgetting <code>--upload-certs</code></strong> — without it, additional control-plane nodes can't fetch the CA/certs from the <code>kubeadm-certs</code> Secret and the <code>kubeadm join --control-plane</code> command fails partway through</li>
                        <li><strong>LB health check &amp; certSANs</strong> — the load balancer should use an HTTP-level health check (kubeadm's own reference configs check <code>/healthz</code>; <code>/readyz</code> is an equally valid, more granular alternative) rather than a bare TCP-only connect, and the apiserver's certificate must list the LB name/VIP in its <code>certSANs</code> or TLS clients reject the connection</li>
                        <li><strong>Re-joining a failed CP node without cleanup</strong> — if you just re-run <code>kubeadm join --control-plane</code> on a node that already has a stale etcd member entry, the member list becomes corrupted. Always <code>etcdctl member remove</code> the stale member FIRST, then <code>kubeadm reset</code>, then re-join</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch35-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — HA concepts to know:</strong><br>
                <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list -w table</code> (with the etcd certs) — the FIRST check on any HA etcd issue, shows the member set and who's healthy<br>
