            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 35 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of HA fundamentals — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How many control-plane nodes do you need for HA, and why must the count be odd?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>At least <strong>3</strong>, and always an <strong>odd</strong> number. etcd needs a majority (quorum) of its members to agree before committing a write. Odd counts maximize fault tolerance per machine — even counts add a node and add split-brain risk but grant ZERO extra tolerance.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Why 4 nodes is strictly worse than 3</div>
                            <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  3 nodes: quorum=2, tolerates 1 failure   ✅ good ratio
  4 nodes: quorum=3, tolerates 1 failure   ❌ same tolerance, 1 more box to buy/patch/break
  5 nodes: quorum=3, tolerates 2 failures  ✅ next real step up
</pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A 4-member cluster can also split 2-vs-2 in a network partition — neither half has a majority, so the WHOLE cluster goes read-only, something a 3 or 5-member cluster structurally cannot do (one side always has the extra vote). This is why kubeadm HA guides and the CKA blueprint both insist on odd counts.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Stacked vs external etcd — what's the trade-off, and which does the exam use?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Stacked (kubeadm default)</th><th>External etcd</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Machines</td><td>3 total</td><td>3 CP + 3 etcd = 6</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Simplicity</td><td>Easier — one <code>kubeadm init</code></td><td>Harder — separate etcd bootstrap</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Fault coupling</td><td>CP node loss = etcd member loss too</td><td>Decoupled — etcd survives CP loss</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>CKA exam</td><td>✅ what you'll actually see</td><td>Understand conceptually only</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Stacked is simpler and uses fewer machines but couples etcd's fate to control-plane node health; external isolates the datastore for resilience at roughly double the machines and ops burden. The CKA exam environment is stacked — but you must still reason about external topologies conceptually.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">With 5 etcd members, how many node failures can the cluster tolerate?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>2</strong>. Quorum = floor(5/2)+1 = 3, so 5 − 3 = 2 members can be down and the remaining 3 still form a majority.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>N</th><th>Quorum (⌊N/2⌋+1)</th><th>Tolerated failures</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>5</td><td>3</td><td><strong>2</strong></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>7</td><td>4</td><td>3</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>General rule: tolerated failures = floor(N/2). Memorize 3→1, 5→2, 7→3 cold — this exact question format ("with N members, how many failures?") is a favorite CKA phrasing.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> actually provide, and when must it be set?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It gives every component, kubelet, and kubeconfig ONE stable address (the load balancer or VIP) to trust — instead of a single control-plane node's IP. It <strong>must be set on the very first <code>kubeadm init</code></strong>; it cannot be added cleanly afterward.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">The indirection that makes a CP node disposable</div>
                            <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  every client/kubeconfig  ──▶  lb.easypj:6443 (stable name, never changes)
                                       │
                          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
                          ▼            ▼            ▼
                        CP-1         CP-2         CP-3   (any can die/rebuild)
</pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Without this flag, kubeadm defaults to the first master's own IP — every kubeconfig and worker join then hard-codes a single point of failure. Set it up front even if you start with one control-plane node you plan to grow later; rebuilding the cluster is the only way to add it after the fact.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do additional control-plane nodes get the certificates they need to join?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># On the FIRST init, --upload-certs stores certs in a Secret:</span>
sudo kubeadm init --control-plane-endpoint "lb.easypj:6443" --upload-certs

<span class="comment"># init prints a certificate-key; additional CP nodes use it:</span>
sudo kubeadm join lb.easypj:6443 \
  --token &lt;token&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;hash&gt; \
  --control-plane --certificate-key &lt;certificate-key&gt;

<span class="comment"># Forgot --upload-certs? Re-upload without a full re-init:</span>
sudo kubeadm init phase upload-certs --upload-certs</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>--upload-certs</code> places the shared CA/certs into a <code>kubeadm-certs</code> Secret in <code>kube-system</code>, encrypted with the printed <code>--certificate-key</code>. Any node joining with <code>--control-plane</code> and that key downloads and installs them automatically — no manual cert copying required.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How are the scheduler and controller-manager made highly available if only one instance can act at a time?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>They run on <strong>every</strong> control-plane node but operate <strong>active-passive</strong>: only the elected leader (holding a <code>Lease</code> object in <code>kube-system</code>) does work, and the rest sit as warm standbys.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Active-passive failover via Lease</div>
                            <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  CP-1 scheduler (LEADER, renews Lease well within its 15s duration)
  CP-2 scheduler (standby, watching renewTime)
  CP-3 scheduler (standby, watching renewTime)

  CP-1 dies ──▶ renewTime goes stale ──▶ CP-2 acquires Lease ──▶ CP-2 is new leader
</pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the same Lease-based leader-election pattern that custom controllers/operators use to run multiple replicas safely — only one replica ever reconciles at a time, even though all replicas are running.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What exactly is the load balancer's job in an HA cluster, and what's the risk of running just one?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>It fronts all the apiservers on :6443, health-checks each one (an HTTP GET such as <code>/healthz</code> or <code>/readyz</code>), and gives every client one stable endpoint. Running a single LB instance makes the LB itself a new SPOF — pair two LBs with <strong>keepalived/VRRP</strong> (or run <strong>kube-vip</strong>) sharing a floating VIP.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">LB health-check loop</div>
                            <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  client ──▶ LB:6443 ──health-check /healthz (or /readyz) every Ns──▶ CP-1 / CP-2 / CP-3
              │
              └─ unhealthy backend removed from pool automatically
</pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A bare TCP connect check can pass even when the apiserver is up but not truly ready (e.g. etcd unreachable from it) — kubeadm's own reference HAProxy/keepalived configs use an HTTP-level check against <code>/healthz</code>; <code>/readyz</code> is an equally valid, more granular alternative some environments prefer. Either beats a bare TCP-only check, which can't tell "port open" from "server healthy."</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">2 of 3 control-plane nodes are down. What is the cluster's actual state, and what's the priority action?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>etcd has <strong>lost quorum</strong> (only 1 of 3 members alive, need 2) → the API server's writes fail and the API becomes effectively <strong>read-only/unavailable</strong>. The priority is restoring/replacing a member to regain a majority — NOT adding workers or anything else.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Alive members</th><th>Quorum (need 2)?</th><th>Cluster state</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>3/3</td><td>✅</td><td>Fully healthy</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2/3</td><td>✅</td><td>Degraded but functional</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>1/3</td><td>❌</td><td>Read-only / unavailable — fix NOW</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>etcd (Raft) refuses to commit any write without a majority acknowledging it — this is a deliberate safety property, not a bug. Reads of already-committed data from the surviving member may still work, but nothing mutates until quorum is restored.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check the etcd member list and their health on an HA cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list -w table \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health --cluster -w table \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Both commands need the etcd CA/cert/key from <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code> — forgetting these TLS flags is the #1 reason <code>etcdctl</code> commands fail on the exam. <code>member list</code> shows the roster; <code>endpoint health --cluster</code> confirms each one is actually reachable and responsive.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

