--token abc123 \
--discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:xyz789 \
--control-plane \
--certificate-key abc123def456

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Update load balancer</span>
<span class="comment"># Add new node's IP to the LB backend pool</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS  ROLES          VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># master-1   Ready   control-plane  v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># master-2   Ready   control-plane  v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># master-3   Ready   control-plane  v1.31.0  ← New!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check etcd is healthy:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># etcd-master-1  1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-master-2  1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-master-3  1/1  Running  ← New etcd member!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Adding a control plane node uses <code>kubeadm join</code> with the <code>--control-plane</code> flag and <code>--certificate-key</code> to download shared certificates. The cluster must have been initialized with <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code>. On the CKA, know this workflow conceptually. The key steps: generate token, upload certs, join with control-plane flag, update load balancer. Stacked etcd automatically adds a new member when a control plane node joins.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a PodDisruptionBudget and how does it protect HA during maintenance?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create pdb imperatively</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create PDB imperatively:</span>
kubectl create pdb web-pdb --selector=app=web --min-available=2

<span class="comment"># OR declaratively:</span>
apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
name: web-pdb
spec:
minAvailable: 2          <span class="comment"># At least 2 pods must be running</span>
<span class="comment"># OR: maxUnavailable: 1  # At most 1 pod can be down</span>
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: web

<span class="comment"># What PDB protects against:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ kubectl drain (node maintenance)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Cluster autoscaler removing nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ kubeadm upgrade</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ Does NOT protect against: node crash, OOMKill, pod deletion</span>

<span class="comment"># Without PDB (3 replicas, draining 2 nodes):</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --delete-emptydir-data --ignore-daemonsets
kubectl drain worker-2 --delete-emptydir-data --ignore-daemonsets
<span class="comment"># Both succeed → 0 pods running → DOWNTIME!</span>

<span class="comment"># With PDB (minAvailable: 2):</span>
kubectl drain worker-1  <span class="comment"># Succeeds (2 pods remain on worker-2,3)</span>
kubectl drain worker-2  <span class="comment"># BLOCKS! "Cannot evict pod: would violate PDB"</span>
<span class="comment"># Service stays up with 2 pods ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Check PDB status:</span>
kubectl get pdb
<span class="comment"># NAME     MIN-AVAILABLE  MAX-UNAVAILABLE  ALLOWED-DISRUPTIONS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># web-pdb  2              N/A              1                    5m</span>
<span class="comment"># ALLOWED-DISRUPTIONS: 1 = can safely evict 1 more pod</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PDB sets limits on voluntary disruptions (drain, upgrades, autoscaler). <code>minAvailable</code> = minimum pods that must stay running. <code>maxUnavailable</code> = maximum pods that can be down. PDB blocks drain commands that would violate the budget. On the CKA, create PDBs to protect critical workloads during node maintenance. <code>kubectl create pdb</code> is the fastest imperative method. PDB does NOT prevent involuntary disruptions (node crashes, OOMKill).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An HA cluster has 3 control plane nodes and one goes down. What happens?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">3 cp nodes → quorum = 2 → can tolerate 1 failure ✓</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 3 CP nodes → Quorum = 2 → Can tolerate 1 failure ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># What happens when master-2 goes down:</span>

<span class="comment"># API Server:</span>
<span class="comment"># master-1 API server: ✅ Still serving requests</span>
<span class="comment"># master-3 API server: ✅ Still serving requests</span>
<span class="comment"># Load balancer routes to remaining 2 nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl commands still work (via LB)</span>

<span class="comment"># etcd:</span>
<span class="comment"># 3 members, 1 down → 2 alive → Quorum (2) met ✅</span>
<span class="comment"># Reads work, writes work</span>
<span class="comment"># "etcdctl member list" shows member as unreachable</span>

<span class="comment"># Controller Manager:</span>
<span class="comment"># If master-2 was the leader → new leader elected (~15s)</span>
<span class="comment"># If master-2 was standby → no impact</span>
kubectl get lease kube-controller-manager -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># HOLDER changes to master-1 or master-3</span>

<span class="comment"># Scheduler:</span>
<span class="comment"># Same as controller manager — leader election kicks in</span>
kubectl get lease kube-scheduler -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Impact on workloads:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Existing pods keep running (kubelet is independent)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ New pods can be created (scheduler on remaining CP)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Self-healing works (controller-manager re-elected)</span>
<span class="comment"># ⚠️ But now: ONE MORE failure = total cluster loss!</span>

<span class="comment"># Action: Fix/replace master-2 ASAP</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Fix the node if recoverable</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. OR remove and re-add: kubeadm reset + re-join</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>With 3 CP nodes and 1 down: quorum is still met (2/3 alive), so the cluster functions normally. API server (via LB), etcd, controller-manager, and scheduler all continue. Leader election replaces the lost leader in ~15 seconds. Critical: you now have ZERO fault tolerance. If another node fails, you lose quorum and the cluster becomes read-only (no writes to etcd). Fix the failed node immediately.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

