    default-server inter 5s fall 3 rise 2
    server cp1 10.0.0.11:6443 check check-ssl verify none
    server cp2 10.0.0.12:6443 check check-ssl verify none
    server cp3 10.0.0.13:6443 check check-ssl verify none

<span class="comment"># To remove the LB itself as a SPOF: run TWO HAProxy hosts and float a</span>
<span class="comment"># Virtual IP (VIP) between them with keepalived (VRRP) — the</span>
<span class="comment"># control-plane-endpoint DNS name points at the VIP, so an LB failure</span>
<span class="comment"># triggers a sub-5-second VIP failover that clients never notice.</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 35.5 — Recover a Failed Control-Plane Node</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Scenario</h5>
                        <p><code>cp-2</code> in your 3-node HA cluster suffered unrecoverable disk corruption. You have a replacement machine ready with the same hostname/IP freed up. Walk through the correct recovery sequence — a naive "just re-join it" will corrupt the etcd member list.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> The dead node's OLD etcd member entry is still in the member list even though the machine is gone — you must remove it before anything else.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: From a HEALTHY control-plane node, find the dead member's ID</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list -w table \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Remove the stale member BEFORE touching the new machine</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member remove &lt;dead-member-id&gt; \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Step 3: On the replacement machine, reset any stale local state</span>
sudo kubeadm reset -f

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Generate a fresh join command and certificate-key</span>
kubeadm token create --print-join-command
kubeadm init phase upload-certs --upload-certs

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Re-join as a control-plane node</span>
sudo kubeadm join lb.easypj:6443 --token &lt;token&gt; \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;hash&gt; \
  --control-plane --certificate-key &lt;certificate-key&gt;

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify quorum is 3/3 again</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list -w table --cacert=... --cert=... --key=...
kubectl get nodes -l node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
<span class="comment"># Never skip Step 2 — re-joining with a stale member still listed</span>
<span class="comment"># corrupts the member list and can strand the cluster without quorum.</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 35 Visual Summary — from single point of failure to no single point of failure</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
                    🌐 control-plane-endpoint (LB/VIP, :6443)
                   ╱            │            ╲
            CP-1 active    CP-2 active   CP-3 active   (apiservers ALL active-active)
            etcd-0 ◄──────► etcd-1 ◄──────► etcd-2     (Raft quorum = 2 of 3)
                   ╲            │            ╱
                       workers: easypj pods (podAntiAffinity + PDB)

  scheduler &amp; controller-manager: ONE Lease-elected leader, rest are warm standbys
  quorum = floor(N/2)+1  ·  3 tolerates 1  ·  5 tolerates 2  ·  ALWAYS odd
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> An HA cluster removes every single point of failure. Three (odd) control-plane nodes sit behind one stable load-balancer endpoint — the <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> — that every client and kubeconfig trusts. The apiservers are all active simultaneously and the load balancer spreads traffic across them, while the scheduler and controller-manager run active-passive with exactly one Lease-elected leader at a time.</p>
                    <p>etcd replicates via Raft and stays writable as long as a majority (N/2+1) of its members survive — 3 members tolerate 1 loss, 5 tolerate 2, and even member counts are forbidden because they add cost and split-brain risk without adding any tolerance. <code>kubeadm</code> assembles the whole thing with <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> plus <code>--upload-certs</code> on the first init, then control-plane and worker joins.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> a resilient control plane is only half the job — the easypj app itself earns its own HA on top via <code>podAntiAffinity</code>, a <code>PodDisruptionBudget</code>, and multi-AZ storage, so that no single node, zone, or control-plane failure ever takes the whole system down.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
