                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🟠</div>
                        <h5>AWS EKS</h5>
                        <p><strong>Control plane:</strong> $0.10/hr. <strong>Nodes:</strong> EC2 or Fargate (serverless). <strong>Identity:</strong> IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts). <strong>Storage:</strong> EBS CSI. <strong>Best for:</strong> AWS-heavy orgs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔵</div>
                        <h5>Google GKE</h5>
                        <p><strong>Control plane:</strong> Free (standard). <strong>Nodes:</strong> Standard or Autopilot. <strong>Identity:</strong> Workload Identity. <strong>Storage:</strong> PD CSI. <strong>Best for:</strong> K8s-native features first.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔷</div>
                        <h5>Azure AKS</h5>
                        <p><strong>Control plane:</strong> Free! <strong>Nodes:</strong> VMSS pools. <strong>Identity:</strong> Managed Identity. <strong>Storage:</strong> Azure Disk CSI. <strong>Best for:</strong> Azure/enterprise shops.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>🔧 Self-Managed (kubeadm)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ Full control over every component</li>
                            <li>✅ Deep understanding of K8s internals</li>
                            <li>✅ No vendor lock-in</li>
                            <li>❌ You manage etcd, certs, upgrades, HA</li>
                            <li>❌ Responsible for node provisioning</li>
                            <li>❌ Must run your own monitoring/alerting</li>
                            <li><strong>CKA:</strong> This is what the exam tests</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>☁️ Cloud-Managed (EKS/GKE/AKS)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ Control plane fully managed &amp; HA</li>
                            <li>✅ Auto-rotating certs, auto-backup etcd</li>
                            <li>✅ 1-click upgrades, cluster autoscaler</li>
                            <li>✅ Integrated identity, storage, networking</li>
                            <li>❌ Less visibility into control plane</li>
                            <li>❌ Vendor-specific tooling and patterns</li>
                            <li><strong>CKA:</strong> Where you’ll apply exam skills</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p>One managed-Kubernetes detail that trips up almost everyone the first time they hit it: <strong>where does the apiserver endpoint itself actually live on the network?</strong> On a kubeadm cluster you built the apiserver yourself, so you already know it's just a process bound to a node IP. On EKS/GKE/AKS, the provider gives you a choice of <strong>endpoint access mode</strong> — and picking wrong is a common source of "why can't I even <code>kubectl get nodes</code>" incidents.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Endpoint mode</th><th>Who can reach the apiserver</th><th>Typical use</th><th>The catch</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Public</strong></td><td>Anyone on the internet (auth still required — this is network reachability, not auth bypass)</td><td>Dev/test clusters, quick demos</td><td>Bigger attack surface; still protected by RBAC + auth, but reachable at all is itself a risk</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Public + private</strong> (hybrid)</td><td>Internet AND in-VPC clients, simultaneously</td><td>Most real production clusters — CI/CD runners in-VPC, admins from laptops over internet</td><td>You must whitelist source CIDRs on the public side or it's effectively fully public</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Private only</strong></td><td>Only clients inside the VPC (or peered/VPN'd into it)</td><td>Regulated environments, banks, anything with a "no control plane on the public internet" policy</td><td><code>kubectl</code> from your laptop fails outright unless you're on a VPN, bastion, or Cloud Shell that's inside the VPC</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The #1 "cluster is broken" false alarm: private-endpoint lockout</h5>
                    <p>If someone flips easypj-prod's EKS/GKE/AKS cluster to <strong>private-only</strong> endpoint access and then tries <code>kubectl get pods</code> from a laptop at home, they'll get a timeout or "no route to host" — <strong>not</strong> an auth error. This looks exactly like a broken cluster but is actually 100% expected behavior: the request never reaches the apiserver's network interface at all, so there's nothing for RBAC to even evaluate. The fix is always network-layer (VPN into the VPC, use a bastion host, or use the cloud provider's browser-based Cloud Shell that runs inside the VPC) — never a kubeconfig or RBAC change. Diagnosing "is this an auth problem or a network problem" starts with: can I even <code>curl -k https://&lt;apiserver-endpoint&gt;:6443/version</code> from here? If that hangs/times out, it's network, not auth.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 "The provider backs up etcd" does NOT mean "I have a restore button for my own mistakes"</h5>
                    <p>Chapters 11 and 29 drilled <code>etcdctl snapshot save/restore</code> as a CKA skill because on a kubeadm cluster, YOU are the disaster-recovery plan. On EKS/GKE/AKS, the provider does take periodic etcd snapshots — but those exist so <em>the provider</em> can recover from an infrastructure-level failure (a control-plane node dying, a storage fault). They are <strong>not</strong> a customer-facing "restore my cluster to 10 minutes ago" self-service tool on any of the three clouds. If someone runs <code>kubectl delete namespace easypj-production</code> by accident, there is no button in the AWS/GCP/Azure console that undoes it — the managed etcd backup protects the provider's infrastructure SLA, not your fat-fingered <code>kubectl delete</code>.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Protects against</th><th>Who runs it</th><th>Covered in</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>etcd backup/restore</td><td>Losing the control plane's brain (infra failure)</td><td>You (kubeadm) / Provider (managed cloud)</td><td>Ch11, Ch29</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>HA control plane</td><td>A single control-plane node dying</td><td>You (kubeadm 3-node) / Provider (managed cloud)</td><td>Ch35</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>App-aware backup (Velero)</td><td>Human/CI accidents deleting YOUR objects or data</td><td>You — always, on every cluster type</td><td>This chapter (Ch36)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>This is exactly why Chapter 35's HA control-plane chapter and this chapter are NOT redundant: HA control plane (Ch35) and managed etcd (this chapter) both protect against <strong>infrastructure</strong> failure. Neither protects against <strong>application-level</strong> accidents — an admin error, a bad Helm rollback, a compromised CI pipeline deleting resources. For that you still need an application-aware backup tool (Velero is the standard: it snapshots Kubernetes object manifests AND triggers cloud disk snapshots for PVs, then can selectively restore a single namespace). The mental model: <strong>etcd backup (yours on kubeadm, the provider's on cloud) protects the cluster's brain; Velero-style backup protects your data and objects</strong> — you need the second one on every cluster, managed or not.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.2 CKA Concepts in Cloud Kubernetes</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>The good news: <strong>most CKA skills transfer directly</strong> to cloud Kubernetes. Your kubectl commands, YAML manifests, and troubleshooting skills work everywhere. Here's exactly what changes and what stays the same.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Self-Managed vs Cloud-Managed — What Changes?</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
