│  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────          │
│  kubeadm init/join         You do it ✋      Not needed ☁️                   │
│  etcd backup/restore       You do it ✋      Auto-managed ☁️                 │
│  Certificate rotation      You do it ✋      Auto-rotated ☁️                 │
│  Cluster upgrades          kubeadm ✋       Cloud console / CLI ☁️          │
│  Node scaling              Manual ✋         Cluster Autoscaler ☁️           │
│  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────          │
│  kubectl commands          Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  YAML manifests            Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  RBAC                      Same ✅           Same ✅ + Cloud IAM             │
│  NetworkPolicies           Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  PV / PVC                  Same ✅           Same ✅ + Cloud Disks           │
│  Troubleshooting           Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  Pod scheduling            Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  Services / Ingress        Same ✅           Same ✅ + Cloud LB              │
│  Resource limits           Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────          │
│                                                                              │
│     KEY INSIGHT: ~80% of CKA skills are IDENTICAL on cloud K8s.           │
│      The 20% that differ are infrastructure setup tasks that cloud          │
│      providers handle FOR you. That's WHY companies use managed K8s.        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA to Cloud Translation</h5>
                    <p>When you see a CKA task like "backup etcd" — on EKS/GKE/AKS, this is handled automatically. But the <strong>concept</strong> of "etcd holds cluster state, and losing it means losing the cluster" still applies. The CKA teaches you <strong>why</strong> things matter; cloud just automates the <strong>how</strong>.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.3 Cloud-Native Storage and Networking</h3>
                <p>Cloud providers use <strong>CSI (Container Storage Interface)</strong> drivers to connect Kubernetes PersistentVolumes to cloud block storage. You write the same PVC YAML — only the <code>StorageClass</code> provisioner changes.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>CSI Architecture — How Cloud Storage Plugs In</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CSI (Container Storage Interface)                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────────┐      ┌──────────────────┐              │
│  │  Pod      │      │  Kubernetes  │      │  Cloud Provider  │              │
│  │  (easypj)│─────▶│  PVC → PV    │─────▶│  CSI Driver      │              │
│  └──────────┘      └──────────────┘      └────────┬─────────┘              │
│                                                    │                         │
│                                           ┌────────▼─────────┐              │
│                                           │  Cloud Disk API  │              │
│                                           │  (EBS / PD / AD) │              │
│                                           └──────────────────┘              │
│                                                                              │
│  Your YAML (same pattern):       StorageClass (cloud-specific):             │
│  ┌──────────────────────┐       ┌────────────────────────────────┐          │
│  │ apiVersion: v1        │       │ provisioner:                   │          │
│  │ kind: PVC             │       │   AWS:   ebs.csi.aws.com      │          │
│  │ spec:                 │       │   GCP:   pd.csi.storage.gke.io│          │
│  │   storageClass: fast  │       │   Azure: disk.csi.azure.com   │          │
│  │   resources:          │       │ parameters:                    │          │
│  │     requests:         │       │   type: gp3 / pd-ssd / Premium│          │
│  │       storage: 20Gi   │       └────────────────────────────────┘          │
│  └──────────────────────┘                                                   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>StorageClass Configurations by Cloud Provider</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">storageclass-aws.yaml — AWS EBS StorageClass</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-ebs
provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com      <span class="comment"># AWS EBS CSI driver</span>
parameters:
  type: gp3                        <span class="comment"># General Purpose SSD (gp3)</span>
  fsType: ext4                      <span class="comment"># Filesystem the node plugin formats on first use</span>
  encrypted: "true"                <span class="comment"># Encrypt at rest</span>
reclaimPolicy: Delete
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer  <span class="comment"># Schedule-aware binding</span>
allowVolumeExpansion: true</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">storageclass-azure.yaml — Azure Disk StorageClass</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-azure
provisioner: disk.csi.azure.com    <span class="comment"># Azure Disk CSI driver</span>
parameters:
  skuName: Premium_LRS             <span class="comment"># Premium SSD</span>
  fsType: ext4
  cachingmode: ReadOnly
reclaimPolicy: Delete
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
allowVolumeExpansion: true</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">storageclass-gcp.yaml — GCE Persistent Disk StorageClass</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-gce
provisioner: pd.csi.storage.gke.io  <span class="comment"># GCE PD CSI driver</span>
parameters:
  type: pd-ssd                       <span class="comment"># SSD persistent disk</span>
reclaimPolicy: Delete
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
allowVolumeExpansion: true</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 CKA Exam Relevance</h5>
                    <p>The CKA tests you on <code>PersistentVolume</code>, <code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code>, and <code>StorageClass</code> concepts — the YAML structure is identical across all providers. Know the <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code> pattern — it ensures the volume is created in the same zone as the pod. Also know the field's DEFAULT if you omit it entirely: <code>Immediate</code>, not <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> — every StorageClass in this chapter sets it explicitly precisely to avoid that default.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>allowedTopologies</code> — the older, <code>Immediate</code>-mode way to pin a zone</h5>
                    <p>Before <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code> existed (and still today, on any StorageClass that intentionally keeps <code>volumeBindingMode: Immediate</code> for other reasons), a StorageClass can carry an <code>allowedTopologies</code> field to restrict which zones a volume is allowed to be provisioned into — independent of where the Pod ends up. With <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> this field is normally unnecessary (the scheduler already picks the zone first), but it's worth recognizing on sight since older manifests and some vendor docs still use it:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: easypj-fast-us-east
provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com
volumeBindingMode: Immediate       <span class="comment"># provisions right away, so the zone must be pinned</span>
allowedTopologies:
- matchLabelExpressions:
  - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
    values:
    - us-east-1a
    - us-east-1b</code></pre>
                    <p>Two mechanisms, same goal (don't create a disk in a zone the Pod can never reach): <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> solves it by delaying provisioning; <code>allowedTopologies</code> solves it by narrowing the zone list up front. You'll typically only need one of the two — reach for <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> first, and only add <code>allowedTopologies</code> if something else in your pipeline requires <code>Immediate</code> binding.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Cloud-Native Networking — LoadBalancer Services &amp; Annotations</h4>
                <p>Storage is half of §36.3's promise — the other half is <strong>networking</strong>. A <code>Service</code> of <code>type: LoadBalancer</code> triggers the cloud-controller-manager to provision a real cloud load balancer (AWS ALB/NLB, GCP TCP/HTTP LB, Azure LB/App Gateway) and write its public address into <code>status.loadBalancer.ingress</code>, which shows up as the <code>EXTERNAL-IP</code> column in <code>kubectl get svc</code>. An Ingress (or the newer Gateway API) then routes HTTP traffic for easypj behind that LB.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-web-service.yaml — exposed via a cloud LoadBalancer</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  annotations:
    <span class="comment"># AWS: request an NLB instead of the classic ELB</span>
    service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: "nlb"
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer      <span class="comment"># the ONLY portable, cloud-agnostic part</span>
  selector:
    app: easypj
    tier: web
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>What the annotation controls</th><th>AWS EKS</th><th>Google GKE</th><th>Azure AKS</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>LB product choice</td><td><code>aws-load-balancer-type: "nlb"</code> (else classic ELB)</td><td>GCP always provisions its native LB; product picked by Ingress class</td><td>Azure LB (L4) vs Application Gateway Ingress Controller (L7) — separate controller</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Internal vs internet-facing</td><td><code>aws-load-balancer-internal: "true"</code></td><td><code>networking.gke.io/load-balancer-type: "Internal"</code></td><td><code>service.beta.kubernetes.io/azure-load-balancer-internal: "true"</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Health-check path</td><td><code>aws-load-balancer-healthcheck-path</code></td><td>Auto-derived from readiness probe, or BackendConfig</td><td><code>service.beta.kubernetes.io/port_80_health-probe_request-path</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Where the cloud-specific intent actually lives</h5>
                    <p>Notice the <code>spec</code> block above is 100% portable — it's the exact same shape you'd write for a kubeadm cluster. Every cloud-specific decision (which LB product, internal vs public, health-check paths, subnet selection) is expressed as <strong>annotations</strong>, never as new <code>spec</code> fields. This is deliberate: it keeps the Service API generic across every Kubernetes cluster on Earth, while still giving each cloud controller room to accept vendor-specific configuration through a mechanism (annotations) the API was already designed to ignore if unrecognized. When a LoadBalancer Service on easypj-web "isn't behaving as expected" in production, <code>kubectl get svc easypj-web -o yaml</code> and read the <code>annotations</code> block first — that's where the answer almost always is.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.4 Cloud Identity Integration</h3>
                <p>In cloud Kubernetes, pods can <strong>assume cloud IAM roles</strong> without storing credentials. This is the production-grade way to give your application access to cloud resources like databases, storage buckets, and message queues.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Pod → ServiceAccount → Cloud IAM Role → Cloud Resource</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CLOUD IDENTITY INTEGRATION — How Pods Access Cloud Resources               │
│                                                                              │
│  ❌ BAD: Hardcoded credentials (Secret with access keys)                    │
│  ┌──────┐   Secret: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID      ┌──────────┐                    │
│  │ Pod  │──────────────────────────────────▶│ S3 Bucket│  ← Key leaked!    │
│  └──────┘                                   └──────────┘                    │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ GOOD: IAM Role via ServiceAccount (no credentials stored)              │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌─────────────┐    ┌──────────┐          │
│  │ Pod  │───▶│ K8s Service  │───▶│ Cloud IAM   │───▶│ Cloud    │          │
│  │      │    │ Account      │    │ Role         │    │ Resource │          │
│  └──────┘    └──────────────┘    └─────────────┘    └──────────┘          │
│                                                                              │
│  How Each Cloud Does It:                                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │  AWS (IRSA):                                                     │       │
│  │  Pod → ServiceAccount → IAM Role via OIDC → AWS Resources       │       │
│  │  (annotation: eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn)                        │       │
│  │                                                                  │       │
│  │  Azure (Workload Identity):                                      │       │
│  │  Pod → ServiceAccount → Managed Identity → Azure Resources      │       │
│  │  (annotation: azure.workload.identity/client-id)                │       │
│  │                                                                  │       │
│  │  GCP (Workload Identity):                                        │       │
│  │  Pod → KSA → GSA binding → GCP Resources                        │       │
│  │  (annotation: iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account)                   │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                                                              │
│  easypj Example:                                                             │
│  Pod (easypj-web) → SA (easypj-sa) → IAM Role → Cloud Database Access     │
│  No passwords in YAML! No secrets to rotate! ✅                              │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why This Matters for easypj</h5>
                    <p>Instead of storing database passwords in Kubernetes Secrets, easypj pods can authenticate to a cloud-managed PostgreSQL database using IAM roles. The ServiceAccount annotation is the only config needed — no credentials ever appear in your YAML manifests or container environment.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>ServiceAccount Annotations — Per Cloud Provider</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">serviceaccount.yaml — Cloud IAM integration (all 3 providers)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># AWS EKS — IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    <span class="comment"># This links the K8s SA to an AWS IAM Role</span>
    eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/easypj-db-role
---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Azure AKS — Workload Identity</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    azure.workload.identity/client-id: "abc-def-123-456"
  labels:
    azure.workload.identity/use: "true"    <span class="comment"># Required label!</span>
---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># GCP GKE — Workload Identity Federation</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account: easypj-app@project-id.iam.gserviceaccount.com</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deployment.yaml — Using the annotated ServiceAccount</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Reference the SA in your Deployment — that's it!</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: easypj-sa    # ← Pod inherits cloud IAM role
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: easypj/web:v2.1
        env:
        - name: DATABASE_URL
          <span class="comment"># No password needed — IAM auth handles it!</span>
          value: "postgresql://easypj-db.cluster-xyz.us-east-1.rds.amazonaws.com/easypj"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 CKA Focus — ServiceAccounts</h5>
                    <p>The CKA exam tests <code>serviceAccountName</code> in pod specs. While cloud IAM annotations are provider-specific and not directly tested, understanding <strong>how ServiceAccounts bind to pods</strong> and that <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> restricts API access is core CKA knowledge.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.5 Deploying easypj to Cloud Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>The same Kubernetes manifests you use locally work on cloud. The differences are <strong>how you create the cluster</strong> and <strong>how you connect kubectl</strong>. Here's the complete walkthrough for all three clouds.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Cloud Deployment Flow — 4 Steps on Any Cloud</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  DEPLOYMENT FLOW (same for all clouds)                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Step 1               Step 2               Step 3              Step 4       │
│  ┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐   ┌───────────┐ │
│  │ Create      │     │ Configure   │     │ Apply same   │   │ Expose    │ │
│  │ Managed     │────▶│ kubectl     │────▶│ YAML as      │──▶│ via Cloud │ │
│  │ Cluster     │     │ Context     │     │ local env    │   │ LB/Ingress│ │
│  └─────────────┘     └─────────────┘     └──────────────┘   └───────────┘ │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 1 &amp; 2: Create Cluster + Configure kubectl</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — create cluster and connect (all 3 clouds)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># AWS EKS</span>
<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ eksctl create cluster --name easypj-prod --region us-east-1 --nodes 3
$ aws eks update-kubeconfig --name easypj-prod --region us-east-1

<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Azure AKS</span>
<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ az aks create --resource-group easypj-rg --name easypj-prod \
    --node-count 3 --generate-ssh-keys
$ az aks get-credentials --resource-group easypj-rg --name easypj-prod

<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Google GKE</span>
<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ gcloud container clusters create easypj-prod --zone us-central1-a --num-nodes 3
$ gcloud container clusters get-credentials easypj-prod --zone us-central1-a

<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Verify connection (same for ALL clouds!)</span>
<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ kubectl get nodes
NAME                  STATUS   ROLES   AGE   VERSION
node-pool-xxxx-001    Ready    &lt;none&gt;  2m    v1.29.0
node-pool-xxxx-002    Ready    &lt;none&gt;  2m    v1.29.0
node-pool-xxxx-003    Ready    &lt;none&gt;  2m    v1.29.0</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 3 &amp; 4: Deploy easypj (Same YAML, Any Cloud)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — deploy easypj to cloud cluster</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Same commands whether on EKS, AKS, or GKE!</span>
$ kubectl create namespace easypj
$ kubectl apply -f easypj-deployment.yaml -n easypj
$ kubectl apply -f easypj-service.yaml -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Expose via cloud LoadBalancer (auto-provisions cloud LB)</span>
$ kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --type=LoadBalancer \
    --port=80 --target-port=8000 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Wait for external IP</span>
$ kubectl get svc -n easypj -w
NAME          TYPE           CLUSTER-IP    EXTERNAL-IP      PORT(S)
easypj-web    LoadBalancer   10.100.0.52   52.186.xxx.xxx   80:31234/TCP

<span class="comment"># Test it! 🎉</span>
$ curl http://52.186.xxx.xxx/
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;&lt;html&gt;...easypj is running!...</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 Focus on CKA Core Concepts</h5>
                    <p>The CKA exam won't ask you to create an EKS/AKS/GKE cluster. But it <strong>will</strong> test Deployments, Services, PVCs, and troubleshooting — which are <strong>identical</strong> on cloud and on-prem. Master the core concepts, and they transfer everywhere.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                    <h5>⚡ SPEED TIP — One Kustomize Overlay Per Cloud</h5>
                    <p>Don't hand-edit <code>storageClassName</code>, LB annotations, or ServiceAccount identity annotations every time easypj moves cloud. Keep ONE base (<code>deployment.yaml</code>, <code>service.yaml</code>, <code>serviceaccount.yaml</code> with no cloud-specific fields) and THREE thin Kustomize overlays that patch only the glue:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">easypj/
├── base/
│   ├── kustomization.yaml
│   ├── deployment.yaml        <span class="comment"># no storageClassName, no LB annotations</span>
│   ├── service.yaml            <span class="comment"># type: LoadBalancer, no annotations</span>
│   └── serviceaccount.yaml     <span class="comment"># no cloud annotations</span>
└── overlays/
    ├── aws/kustomization.yaml   <span class="comment"># patches: ebs.csi.aws.com SC + IRSA annotation</span>
    ├── gcp/kustomization.yaml   <span class="comment"># patches: pd.csi.storage.gke.io SC + Workload ID annotation</span>
    └── azure/kustomization.yaml <span class="comment"># patches: disk.csi.azure.com SC + Workload ID annotation</span>

<span class="comment"># Deploy to any cloud with one command:</span>
kubectl apply -k overlays/aws/</code></pre>
                    <p>This is the pattern every Lab Q&amp;A and drill answer in this chapter that says "keep it in a Kustomize overlay" is pointing back to — the base manifests never change; only the three overlay directories differ.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.6 Cluster Autoscaler</h3>
                <p>The Cluster Autoscaler <strong>automatically adjusts the number of worker nodes</strong> in your cluster. When pods can't be scheduled (no node has enough resources), it adds nodes. When nodes are underutilized for 10+ minutes, it removes them.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
