            <h5>① The cloud-controller-manager reconcile loop — the exact seam where a portable object becomes a billable cloud resource</h5>
            <p>§36.3 said "a LoadBalancer Service triggers a cloud LB." Under the hood this is just <em>another controller running the same OBSERVE → DIFF → ACT loop</em> you learned in Chapter 1 — except its "ACT" step calls an external cloud API instead of writing to etcd. Per the current upstream Kubernetes architecture, the cloud-controller-manager (CCM) implements exactly <strong>3 shared sub-controllers</strong> — Node, Route, and Service — each watching one Kubernetes object type and reconciling it against a cloud resource. (Older Kubernetes releases also shipped a fourth, volume-related controller inside the in-tree cloud provider; that responsibility has since fully migrated OUT of the CCM into the CSI sidecar ecosystem, which is why storage is a separate handshake in §④ below, not a CCM sub-controller at all.)</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kube-controller-manager (in-cluster objects)     cloud-controller-manager (cloud-facing, 3 sub-controllers)
   ┌───────────────────────────┐                  ┌────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ Deployment/ReplicaSet ctrl   │                  │ 🔀 Service controller                  │
   │ Job/CronJob ctrl             │                  │   WATCH Service type=LoadBalancer      │
   │ Node lifecycle ctrl (basic)  │                  │   ACT: call cloud LB API, write        │
   └───────────────────────────┘                  │        status.loadBalancer.ingress[]   │
                                                      │                                        │
   easypj-web Service (type: LoadBalancer) ─────▶│ 🗺️ Route controller                    │
   created via kubectl apply                          │   ACT: create cloud VPC routes so      │
                                                      │        pod CIDRs reach each other      │
                                                      │                                        │
   Node goes NotReady for 5+ min ────────────▶│ 🖥️ Node controller                     │
                                                      │   ACT: remove node from cloud LB pool, │
                                                      │        eventually terminate the VM     │
                                                      └────────────────────────────────────┘
                                                      (PVCs are NOT watched by the CCM — see ④:
                                                       the CSI external-provisioner sidecar
                                                       watches them directly, independent of CCM)</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>CCM sub-controller</th><th>Watches</th><th>Calls out to</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Service controller</td><td>Services of type <code>LoadBalancer</code></td><td>Cloud LB API (ELB/ALB/NLB, GCP LB, Azure LB)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Route controller</td><td>Node pod-CIDR allocations</td><td>Cloud VPC routing tables (so pod-to-pod traffic crosses nodes)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Node controller</td><td>Node health/lifecycle</td><td>Cloud compute API (deregister from LB, verify/terminate the VM)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> This is why a <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service is portable YAML but produces different results per cluster: the object itself is generic, but the controller reconciling it is cloud-specific. On kubeadm there is <strong>no</strong> cloud-controller-manager running at all, which is exactly why <code>EXTERNAL-IP</code> stays <code>&lt;pending&gt;</code> forever — nothing is watching. Note the asymmetry with storage: a PVC's cloud disk is provisioned by the CSI external-provisioner sidecar, a completely separate process from the CCM — this is why some clusters run a CCM but no CSI driver (LBs work, storage doesn't) or vice versa.</div>

            <h5>② IRSA / Workload Identity — the full OIDC token-exchange handshake, step by step</h5>
            <p>§36.4 showed the ServiceAccount annotation. The mechanism behind it is a genuine OIDC federation flow — no long-lived key ever touches the pod, only a chain of short-lived, audience-scoped tokens.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   1. Cluster's OIDC provider issues its own discovery doc (the cluster IS an identity provider)
                              │
   2. easypj-web Pod starts → kubelet PROJECTS a ServiceAccount token as a volume
      /var/run/secrets/eks.amazonaws.com/serviceaccount/token
      (JWT: aud=sts.amazonaws.com, sub=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa, exp=~1h)
                              │
   3. AWS SDK inside the container auto-detects AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE env var
                              │
   4. SDK calls AWS STS: AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity(role-arn, jwt)
                              │
   5. STS validates: (a) JWT signature against cluster's OIDC public keys
                       (b) role's TRUST POLICY allows this OIDC provider + this SA subject
                              │
   6. STS returns SHORT-LIVED (~1h) AWS credentials (AccessKeyId/SecretKey/SessionToken)
                              │
   7. SDK calls S3/RDS with those temp creds → auto-refreshes before they expire</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What could go wrong here</th><th>Symptom</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>2 — token projection</td><td>SA not referenced by the pod (<code>serviceAccountName</code> missing)</td><td>SDK falls back to instance-role creds (or none) — wrong identity used</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>4 — SDK auto-detect</td><td>Old SDK version without web-identity support</td><td>SDK never even attempts the exchange</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>5 — trust policy</td><td>Role's trust policy doesn't list this OIDC provider/namespace/SA</td><td><code>AccessDenied</code> from STS — the #1 IRSA gotcha</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>6 — expiry</td><td>Long-running pod, SDK doesn't refresh</td><td>Works for ~1h then starts failing with expired-token errors</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Every cloud's "Workload Identity" is the same shape — cluster OIDC provider → projected token → cloud STS/token-exchange endpoint → trust policy check → short-lived creds. Learn the AWS flow once and GKE Workload Identity Federation / Azure Workload Identity map onto it almost 1:1, just with different annotation keys (§36.4's ServiceAccount code block).</div>

            <h5>③ Cloud LB annotations — the one knob that carries ALL cloud-specific intent</h5>
            <p>The Kubernetes <code>Service</code>/<code>Ingress</code> spec stays deliberately generic. Every cloud-specific decision — internal vs internet-facing, which LB product, which subnets, which health check — is expressed as <strong>annotations</strong>, not spec fields. This keeps the core API portable while still exposing full cloud LB power.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   apiVersion: v1
   kind: Service
   metadata:
     name: easypj-web
     annotations:
       # ── AWS: pick NLB over classic ELB ──
       service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: "nlb"
       # ── AWS: make it INTERNAL (no public IP) ──
       service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-internal: "true"
       # ── GCP: internal LB ──
       # networking.gke.io/load-balancer-type: "Internal"
       # ── Azure: internal LB ──
       # service.beta.kubernetes.io/azure-load-balancer-internal: "true"
   spec:
     type: LoadBalancer   # ← the ONLY portable, cloud-agnostic part</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Intent</th><th>AWS annotation</th><th>Azure annotation</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Internal-only LB</td><td><code>aws-load-balancer-internal: "true"</code></td><td><code>azure-load-balancer-internal: "true"</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>LB product choice</td><td><code>aws-load-balancer-type: "nlb"</code></td><td>(App Gateway Ingress uses a separate controller)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Health-check path</td><td><code>aws-load-balancer-healthcheck-path</code></td><td><code>azure-load-balancer-health-probe-request-path</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> If a Service/Ingress "isn't behaving as expected" on a specific cloud, <code>kubectl get svc -o yaml</code> and read the <code>annotations</code> block first — that's where 100% of cloud-specific LB behavior is configured, never in <code>spec</code>.</div>

            <h5>④ Per-cloud CSI drivers — what actually happens between <code>kubectl apply -f pvc.yaml</code> and a mounted disk</h5>
            <p>Dynamic provisioning is a 3-actor handshake between the external-provisioner sidecar (watches PVCs), the CSI driver's controller plugin (calls the cloud API), and the CSI driver's node plugin (formats + mounts on the actual node) — all hidden behind the single <code>provisioner:</code> field you set in §36.3.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl apply -f easypj-postgres-pvc.yaml (storageClassName: easypj-fast)
        │
        ▼
   external-provisioner sidecar (runs alongside the CSI controller pod)
   sees new PVC → calls CSI CreateVolume RPC
        │
        ▼
   CSI controller plugin ──▶ cloud API: "create a 20Gi gp3 EBS volume"
        │                        (ebs.csi.aws.com / pd.csi.storage.gke.io / disk.csi.azure.com)
        ▼
   Cloud returns volume-id → a PersistentVolume object is created, bound to the PVC
        │
        ▼
   Pod scheduled on Node X ──▶ CSI node plugin on Node X:
        ControllerPublishVolume (attach disk to Node X)  →  NodeStageVolume (format if new)
        →  NodePublishVolume (bind-mount into the pod's volume path)</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>CSI RPC call</th><th>Runs where</th><th>Does what</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>CreateVolume</code></td><td>CSI controller plugin</td><td>Calls the cloud API to create the actual disk</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>ControllerPublishVolume</code></td><td>CSI controller plugin</td><td>Attaches the disk to the target node (cloud API)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NodeStageVolume</code></td><td>CSI node plugin (on the node)</td><td>Formats the block device (first use) and stages it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NodePublishVolume</code></td><td>CSI node plugin (on the node)</td><td>Bind-mounts the staged volume into the pod's path</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code> (used in every StorageClass in §36.3) delays <code>CreateVolume</code> until a Pod actually claims the PVC — this is what guarantees the disk is created in the SAME zone the scheduler picked, avoiding the classic "PVC bound, but Pod stuck Pending: volume node affinity conflict" error.</div>

            <p><strong>Worked example — what that error actually looks like on easypj-postgres:</strong> this bug is common enough (usually from an old StorageClass created with <code>volumeBindingMode: Immediate</code> instead of <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code>) that it's worth recognizing the exact output on sight instead of re-deriving the theory under pressure:</p>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">real symptom: volume node affinity conflict</span></div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash">$ kubectl get pods -n easypj
easypj-postgres-0   0/1   Pending   0   4m

$ kubectl describe pod easypj-postgres-0 -n easypj | tail -6
Events:
  Type     Reason            Message
  ----     ------            -------
  Warning  FailedScheduling  0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had volume
                              node affinity conflict, 2 node(s) didn't match
                              pod anti-affinity rules.

<span class="comment"># Translation: the EBS/PD/Azure Disk was already created in AZ "us-east-1a"</span>
<span class="comment"># (because the StorageClass used volumeBindingMode: Immediate, so the disk</span>
<span class="comment"># was provisioned the instant the PVC was created — BEFORE the scheduler</span>
<span class="comment"># ever picked a node). The scheduler now wants to place the pod in a zone</span>
<span class="comment"># where the disk does NOT exist, and a disk can't cross zones. Neither side</span>
<span class="comment"># will move: the disk can't follow the pod, the pod can't ignore its PVC.</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: volumeBindingMode is IMMUTABLE on an existing StorageClass object,</span>
<span class="comment"># so a patch can't change it — delete and recreate the SC itself instead:</span>
kubectl delete sc easypj-fast
kubectl apply -f easypj-fast-storageclass.yaml   <span class="comment"># same manifest, now with WaitForFirstConsumer</span>

<span class="comment"># Then delete the stuck PVC (and the orphaned disk it created) and recreate</span>
<span class="comment"># it — now the disk waits for the Pod to be scheduled first, so it's always</span>
<span class="comment"># born in the right zone:</span>
kubectl delete pvc easypj-postgres-data -n easypj
kubectl apply -f easypj-postgres-pvc.yaml -n easypj</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h5>⑤ Managed node pools — two independent upgrade tracks and how a rolling node replace actually protects easypj</h5>
            <p>A node pool upgrade is NOT a control-plane upgrade — the provider drives one, you drive the other, and they can be at different Kubernetes minor versions temporarily (within the provider's supported skew, usually ±2 minor versions).</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Node pool "general-purpose" rolling upgrade (v1.28 → v1.29):
   ┌─────────┐        ┌─────────┐        ┌─────────┐
   │ Node A     │        │ Node B     │        │ Node C     │   ← old nodes (v1.28)
   │ 4 easypj   │        │ 4 easypj   │        │ 4 easypj   │
   └─────────┘        └─────────┘        └─────────┘
        │  1. cordon Node A (no new pods scheduled here)
        │  2. drain Node A  (respects PodDisruptionBudget — same as Ch35's HA drains!)
        │  3. terminate Node A, provision a NEW node (v1.29)
        │  4. new node joins, easypj pods reschedule onto it
        ▼
   ┌─────────┐        ┌─────────┐        ┌─────────┐
   │ Node A'    │        │ Node B     │        │ Node C     │   ← A upgraded, B/C still old
   │ (v1.29)    │        │ (v1.28)    │        │ (v1.28)    │
   └─────────┘        └─────────┘        └─────────┘
        repeat for Node B, then Node C — one at a time, never all at once</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Upgrade track</th><th>Who drives it</th><th>Protects workloads via</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Control plane</td><td>Provider (button/CLI)</td><td>HA control plane (Ch35) — no workload impact expected</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Node pool</td><td>You (or a managed "auto-upgrade" schedule)</td><td>Cordon → drain → replace, one node at a time, honoring PDBs</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> This is the exact same cordon/drain sequence you'd run manually on a kubeadm node (Chapter 30, Node Maintenance &amp; OS Upgrades) — managed node-pool upgrades are just that sequence automated by the provider. A missing or too-tight PodDisruptionBudget on easypj-web can still cause an outage during a node-pool upgrade, exactly as it would during manual maintenance.</div>

            <h5>⑥ Cluster Autoscaler vs Karpenter — two different algorithms for "add a node"</h5>
            <p>The classic Cluster Autoscaler (CA) and AWS's newer Karpenter solve the same problem — a Pending pod needs a node — with fundamentally different algorithms. Knowing the difference explains why Karpenter provisions faster and more cheaply on AWS.</p>
            <pre class="uth-diagram">
   CLASSIC CLUSTER AUTOSCALER                    KARPENTER (AWS, newer)
   ┌─────────────────────────┐           ┌─────────────────────────┐
   │ Pod Pending (needs 2 CPU/4Gi)     │           │ Pod Pending (needs 2 CPU/4Gi)     │
   │        │                          │           │        │                          │
   │        ▼                          │           │        ▼                          │
   │ "Which EXISTING node group has   │           │ "What is the CHEAPEST instance    │
   │  room, or can I scale ONE UP     │           │  type across the ENTIRE fleet     │
   │  by adding an identical node?"   │           │  catalog that fits this pod?"     │
   │        │                          │           │        │                          │
   │        ▼                          │           │        ▼                          │
   │ Adds a node from the SAME         │           │ Provisions a RIGHT-SIZED node     │
   │ pre-defined node-group shape      │           │ directly — no pre-defined groups  │
   │ (fixed instance type)             │           │ (picks from 100s of instance types)│
   └─────────────────────────┘           └─────────────────────────┘</pre>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Cluster Autoscaler</th><th>Karpenter</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Scaling unit</td><td>Pre-defined node group (fixed instance type)</td><td>Individual right-sized node from a broad pool</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Bin-packing efficiency</td><td>Lower — group shape is fixed upfront</td><td>Higher — picks the best-fit instance per pod</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Availability</td><td>All clouds (EKS/GKE/AKS)</td><td>AWS-focused (open-source, growing multi-cloud support)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Config surface</td><td>Node-group min/max</td><td>"Provisioner"/"NodePool" CRD with flexible constraints</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Both are conceptually "the thing that reacts to Pending pods by calling the cloud API to add capacity" — for the CKA, the concept (HPA scales pods, node autoscaling scales nodes) is what's tested; Karpenter vs classic CA is real-world nuance for when you're actually running EKS in production. §44.2's Under-the-Hood deep dive revisits Karpenter's just-in-time provisioning at fleet/capacity-planning scale and adds automatic consolidation — this section is the concept-level introduction, that one is the deeper dive.</div>

            <h5>⑦ The node's IAM role is a backdoor around IRSA — why §36.4's identity model exists as a SECURITY boundary, not just a convenience</h5>
            <p>§36.4 framed IRSA/Workload Identity as "how a pod gets cloud credentials without a Secret." The deeper reason it exists is defensive: every cloud VM already has its OWN identity (an EC2 instance profile, a GCE/Azure VM service identity), reachable by ANY process on that VM — including every pod on it — via the Instance Metadata Service (IMDS) at the link-local address <code>169.254.169.254</code>. Without IRSA/Workload Identity explicitly scoping credentials per-pod, a pod inherits the NODE's permissions by default, which is almost always far broader than that one pod needs. This ties directly back to Chapter 25's ServiceAccount/RBAC model: RBAC controls what a pod can do INSIDE the Kubernetes API; IMDS access controls what it can do OUTSIDE it, in the cloud account — and RBAC has zero visibility into that second door.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
   ❌ WITHOUT pod-level identity (IRSA/Workload Identity NOT configured):
   easypj-web Pod ──curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/...──▶ NODE's IAM role
                     (works from ANY container on the node — no auth needed for the metadata call itself)
                     └──▶ if the node's IAM role has S3 admin, ANY compromised pod on that node has S3 admin

   ✅ WITH IRSA/Workload Identity configured + IMDS access blocked for pods:
   easypj-web Pod ──projected SA token──▶ STS/OIDC exchange ──▶ scoped role (S3 read-only, ONE bucket)
                     easypj-worker Pod (different SA, no annotation) ──▶ IMDS BLOCKED ──▶ no cloud creds at all
</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it restricts</th><th>Typical implementation</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>IRSA / Workload Identity annotation</td><td>Which pods get WHICH cloud role</td><td>ServiceAccount annotation + IAM trust policy (§36.4)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Block pod → IMDS at the network layer</td><td>Whether a pod can reach the node's OWN identity at all</td><td>AWS: require IMDSv2 + hop-limit 1 (blocks non-host callers); GKE: metadata concealment / GKE Metadata Server; NetworkPolicy egress-deny to <code>169.254.169.254</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Least-privilege node IAM role</td><td>The "floor" every pod on the node can reach if IMDS isn't blocked</td><td>Keep the node/instance role's permissions minimal — assume every pod can reach it</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> This is a genuine, commonly-exploited real-world escalation path (it's a well-known pattern in cloud security write-ups, not a theoretical one) — a single overprivileged pod without network egress restrictions to the metadata endpoint can pivot into full node-level cloud access. It isn't CKA exam content, but it's the single most important "why" behind everything §36.4 teaches: IRSA/Workload Identity isn't just cleaner than Secrets, it's what makes a compromised easypj-web pod's blast radius ONE scoped role instead of the whole node's IAM identity.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Managed K8s owns the control plane</strong> — apiserver/etcd/scheduler/controller-manager, its upgrades, HA, backups, and certs are the provider's job; you still own workloads, RBAC, NetworkPolicy, PV/PVC, and ingress/gateway.</li>
                    <li><strong>Your CKA skills transfer directly</strong> — the same easypj manifests apply on EKS/GKE/AKS. Only the GLUE changes: <code>storageClass</code> → cloud CSI, <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service → cloud LB, ServiceAccount → cloud IAM (IRSA / Workload Identity).</li>
                    <li><strong>Same kubectl, mostly the same YAML, everywhere</strong> — the real differences are networking (CNI/LB), storage provisioners, and IAM — not the Kubernetes API surface itself.</li>
                    <li><strong>Two different autoscalers, one team</strong> — the HPA scales PODS on metrics; the Cluster Autoscaler (or Karpenter) scales NODES when pods can't be scheduled or nodes sit idle.</li>
                    <li><strong>etcd backups, apiserver upgrades, certificate rotation, and control-plane HA become the provider's job</strong> — but you still upgrade node pools and drive the easypj rollout yourself.</li>
                    <li><strong>CSI makes storage vendor-agnostic</strong> — the cloud installs the driver, and a StorageClass's <code>provisioner</code> field targets the cloud disk so PVCs are dynamically provisioned, exactly like the on-prem mechanism you already drilled.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>CKA is cloud-agnostic</strong> — The exam tests vanilla Kubernetes (kubeadm clusters), NOT EKS/GKE/AKS specifics. But understanding cloud vs self-managed helps you know WHAT the CKA is actually testing (the control plane parts cloud providers hide)</li>
                    <li><strong>StorageClass differences</strong> — Cloud clusters have default StorageClasses (gp2, standard, default). CKA kubeadm clusters may NOT have a default. Always check: <code>kubectl get sc</code>. If no <code>(default)</code> annotation, PVCs without <code>storageClassName</code> stay Pending</li>
                    <li><strong>LoadBalancer Service type</strong> — Only works on cloud. On kubeadm/bare-metal, <code>type: LoadBalancer</code> stays <code>Pending</code> with no EXTERNAL-IP. Use <code>NodePort</code> or install MetalLB. The CKA exam uses NodePort</li>
                    <li><strong>Cloud IAM vs Kubernetes RBAC</strong> — Cloud providers use their own IAM (AWS IAM, GCP IAM, Azure AD) to control WHO can access the cluster. Inside the cluster, Kubernetes RBAC controls WHAT they can do. Both layers matter</li>
                    <li><strong>Cluster Autoscaler vs HPA</strong> — HPA scales pods. CA scales nodes (when pods are Pending due to insufficient resources). They work together: HPA creates more pods → pods go Pending → CA adds nodes → pods get scheduled</li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy needs a policy-capable CNI</strong> — writing a <code>NetworkPolicy</code> object never fails, even on a CNI that can't enforce it. Some default cloud CNIs silently accept the object but enforce NOTHING until a feature flag is turned on (GKE's default dataplane needs "Dataplane V2" / Calico enabled; Azure's default kubenet needs Azure NPM or Calico added; AWS VPC CNI needs its NetworkPolicy add-on installed). The failure mode is the worst kind: <code>kubectl apply</code> succeeds, <code>kubectl describe networkpolicy</code> shows the rule, and traffic that should be blocked flows anyway — always verify enforcement with a real connectivity test (<code>kubectl exec ... -- curl --max-time 2 &lt;blocked-target&gt;</code>), never trust the object's mere existence</li>
                    <li><strong>Control-plane auto-upgrade can outrun your manifests</strong> — GKE release channels (and similar auto-upgrade settings on EKS/AKS) can bump the apiserver's minor version on a schedule you don't control. If that version removes a deprecated API your easypj manifests still use (a classic one: <code>policy/v1beta1</code> PodDisruptionBudget, removed in 1.25), the NEXT <code>kubectl apply</code> — not the upgrade itself — is what fails, often days later and looking like an unrelated CI break. Pin/monitor the release channel and run <code>kubectl convert</code> or <code>pluto</code>/<code>kube-no-trouble</code> against your manifests before any control-plane minor bump</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — CKA skills that transfer to cloud:</strong><br>
                ✅ RBAC, NetworkPolicies, PV/PVC, ConfigMaps/Secrets — identical across all platforms<br>
                ✅ Deployments, Services, Ingress, DaemonSets — same YAML everywhere<br>
                ✅ Troubleshooting (logs, describe, exec) — universal toolkit<br>
                ❌ kubeadm, etcd backup, static pods, certificate management — cloud providers handle these<br>
                <strong>The CKA tests everything including what cloud hides from you.</strong></p>
                <p><strong>Production Speed Tips — keeping easypj portable and cheap:</strong><br>
                📦 Keep cloud-specific bits (<code>storageClass</code>, LB annotations, IRSA/Workload Identity annotations) in a <strong>Kustomize overlay per cloud</strong> — the base <code>easypj-deployment.yaml</code>/<code>easypj-service.yaml</code> stay portable across EKS/GKE/AKS and your local kubeadm cluster<br>
                💰 Add the internal-LB annotation (<code>aws-load-balancer-internal: "true"</code> / GKE <code>Internal</code> / Azure <code>azure-load-balancer-internal: "true"</code>) to any Service that doesn't need to be public — cuts both attack surface and the public-LB bill<br>
                📈 Right-size with HPA (pods) + Cluster Autoscaler/Karpenter (nodes) instead of over-provisioning fixed capacity "just in case" — let the two autoscalers do the math<br>
                🖥️ Use the provider CLI (<code>eksctl</code>/<code>gcloud</code>/<code>az</code>) only for cluster and node-pool lifecycle; use plain <code>kubectl</code> for everything inside the cluster — mixing the two unnecessarily is how tutorials get confusing</p>
            </div>

