                    <div class="eq-question">What exact problem do IRSA and Workload Identity solve for an easypj pod?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Projected SA token → STS/OIDC exchange → short-lived cloud creds</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  easypj Pod ──uses──▶ ServiceAccount (annotated with role-arn / client-id)
                              │  kubelet mounts a projected, audience-scoped JWT
                              ▼
                  Cloud STS / OIDC endpoint verifies + swaps the token
                              │
                              ▼
                  Short-lived cloud credentials ──▶ RDS / S3 / Key Vault
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>They bind a Kubernetes ServiceAccount to a cloud IAM identity so the pod never carries a static access key. The SA annotation alone is not sufficient — the cloud-side trust policy (IAM role trust relationship / federated credential) must also be configured, or the exchange fails silently and the pod can't reach the cloud resource (see the GOTCHAS rail).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj-web's HPA just scaled from 5 to 12 replicas, but 4 of the new pods are stuck Pending. What fixes it, and which controller is responsible?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Controller</th><th>Scales</th><th>Trigger</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>HPA</td><td>Pod replicas</td><td>CPU/memory/custom metric thresholds</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Cluster Autoscaler / Karpenter</td><td>Worker nodes</td><td>Pods stuck Pending with "insufficient cpu/memory"</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Nothing is broken — this is the expected two-level scaling loop from §36.6. HPA created the demand (12 replicas); the existing nodes ran out of room, so the pods went Pending; the Cluster Autoscaler (not the HPA) is responsible for adding a node so the scheduler can place them. Give it 2-5 minutes for the new node to join.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You have easypj's Deployment/Service/Ingress YAML working on a local kubeadm cluster. What actually needs to change to deploy the SAME app to EKS/GKE/AKS?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>easypj object</th><th>Managed-cloud equivalent</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Deployment easypj-web</td><td>IDENTICAL — runs unchanged</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Service type LoadBalancer</td><td>Cloud LB (ALB/NLB · GCP LB · Azure LB)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>PVC + StorageClass</td><td>Cloud CSI disk (EBS · PD · Azure Disk)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>ServiceAccount easypj</td><td>IRSA / Workload Identity → cloud IAM</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Ingress easypj</td><td>Cloud Ingress / Gateway controller</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>RBAC Role/RoleBinding</td><td>IDENTICAL (+ optional cloud IAM mapping)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Only three pieces of GLUE change: the <code>storageClass</code>, the LoadBalancer/Ingress annotations, and the ServiceAccount↔IAM identity. The Deployment, RBAC objects, and NetworkPolicy stay byte-for-byte the same — which is precisely why keeping the cloud-specific bits in a Kustomize overlay (§36.5 SPEED TIP) keeps the base manifests portable.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How are upgrades split between the provider and you on a managed cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Two independent upgrade tracks</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ☁️ CONTROL-PLANE TRACK               🔧 NODE-POOL TRACK
  provider drives it (button/CLI)      you drive it (rolling replace)
  apiserver/etcd/scheduler/CM bumped   node pool AMI/image bumped
  no workload disruption expected      pods drain + reschedule per node
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The provider upgrades the control plane (often a one-click / one-CLI-call operation with no direct workload impact). You still upgrade node pools yourself — usually a managed rolling replace — and you're responsible for validating easypj's workloads survive the node churn (readiness probes + PDBs matter here exactly as they did in Ch35's HA chapter).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a "node pool" (EKS: managed node group; GKE/AKS: node pool), and why does it matter for easypj's capacity planning?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node pool = a managed set of identical workers</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># A node pool is a managed set of IDENTICAL worker nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># (same instance type, same size, its own autoscaling policy)</span>
<span class="comment"># that you can scale/upgrade INDEPENDENTLY of other pools</span>
<span class="comment"># and independently of the control plane.</span>

<span class="comment"># easypj could run two pools:</span>
<span class="comment">#   general-purpose pool  → web/API pods (easypj-web)</span>
<span class="comment">#   memory-optimized pool → PostgreSQL StatefulSet pods</span>

kubectl get nodes -L cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool
kubectl get nodes -L eks.amazonaws.com/nodegroup
kubectl get nodes -L kubernetes.azure.com/agentpool</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Node pools let you right-size hardware per workload shape instead of running one giant homogeneous fleet — e.g. a cheap general-purpose pool for stateless easypj-web pods and a memory-optimized pool for PostgreSQL, each with its own Cluster Autoscaler min/max bounds.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the biggest cost trap unique to running easypj on managed Kubernetes, and two levers to control cloud spend.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="info-box warning">
                            <h5>⚠️ The trap</h5>
                            <p>A stray <code>type: LoadBalancer</code> Service or an orphaned dynamically-provisioned disk keeps billing <strong>even if nothing uses it</strong> — unlike a kubeadm/bare-metal cluster where those objects simply do nothing.</p>
                        </div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Lever</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Right-size requests + HPA/Cluster Autoscaler</td><td>avoids paying for idle node capacity</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Internal LB annotations for private Services</td><td>avoids provisioning a public, billable LB unnecessarily</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Delete unused <code>LoadBalancer</code> Services and PVCs promptly, prefer <code>ClusterIP</code>/internal LB for anything that doesn't need public exposure, and let the two autoscalers (HPA for pods, CA for nodes) do the right-sizing instead of over-provisioning fixed capacity "just in case."</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

