                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A PVC stays in <code>Pending</code> state on a kubeadm cluster. How does this differ from cloud and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check pvc status</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check PVC status:</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># NAME     STATUS   VOLUME  CAPACITY  ACCESS  SC       AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># data     Pending                            (none)   5m</span>

<span class="comment"># Why it's Pending:</span>
kubectl describe pvc data | tail -10
<span class="comment"># "no persistent volumes available for this claim"</span>

<span class="comment"># Cloud vs kubeadm differences:</span>
<span class="comment"># Cloud: Default StorageClass auto-provisions PVs</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm: NO default StorageClass → PVCs without SC stay Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check StorageClasses</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># (empty) ← No StorageClass!</span>

<span class="comment"># On cloud, you'd see:</span>
<span class="comment"># gp2 (default)  OR  standard (default)</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 1: Create a PV manually</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: data-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 1Gi
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  hostPath:
    path: /data/pv
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 2: Specify storageClassName in PVC</span>
<span class="comment"># and create a matching StorageClass</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 3: Create PV + PVC with matching labels</span>
<span class="comment"># PV: storageClassName: manual</span>
<span class="comment"># PVC: storageClassName: manual</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># NAME  STATUS  VOLUME   CAPACITY  SC      AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># data  Bound   data-pv  1Gi       manual  1m  ✓</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On cloud, StorageClasses with dynamic provisioning auto-create PVs. On kubeadm/bare-metal, you usually need to create PVs manually. CKA uses kubeadm, so expect to create PVs with <code>hostPath</code>. Common trap: PVC with no <code>storageClassName</code> looks for the default SC — if none exists, it stays Pending forever. Always check <code>kubectl get sc</code> first.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you create a HorizontalPodAutoscaler (HPA) and what prerequisites does it need?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prerequisite 1: metrics server must be installed</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Prerequisite 1: Metrics Server must be installed</span>
kubectl top pods  <span class="comment"># If this works, metrics-server is ready</span>

<span class="comment"># Prerequisite 2: Pods must have resource REQUESTS defined</span>
<span class="comment"># HPA compares actual usage vs requested</span>

<span class="comment"># Create HPA imperatively:</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment web \
--min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70
<span class="comment"># Scales up when avg CPU > 70% of requests</span>
<span class="comment"># Scales down when avg CPU < 70%</span>

<span class="comment"># Check HPA:</span>
kubectl get hpa
<span class="comment"># NAME  REFERENCE        TARGETS        MINPODS  MAXPODS  REPLICAS</span>
<span class="comment"># web   Deployment/web   45%/70%        2        10       3</span>
<span class="comment">#                        ↑ current/target</span>

<span class="comment"># Declarative:</span>
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: web
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: web
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 70

<span class="comment"># If TARGETS shows &lt;unknown&gt;/70%:</span>
<span class="comment"># → Metrics Server not installed</span>
<span class="comment"># OR → Pod has no resource requests defined!</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Add requests to pod spec</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: 100m
    memory: 128Mi</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>HPA requires: (1) Metrics Server installed, (2) pods have CPU/memory requests defined. Without requests, HPA can't calculate percentage. <code>kubectl autoscale</code> is the imperative shortcut. If TARGETS shows <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code>, check Metrics Server and resource requests. On the CKA, HPA is a practical topic — know how to create it imperatively and what the prerequisites are.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service shows <code>Pending</code> EXTERNAL-IP on a kubeadm cluster. Why and what's the alternative?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check service</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check Service:</span>
kubectl get svc web-lb
<span class="comment"># NAME     TYPE          CLUSTER-IP    EXTERNAL-IP  PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># web-lb   LoadBalancer  10.96.50.100  &lt;pending&gt;    80:31234/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Why Pending?</span>
<span class="comment"># LoadBalancer type requires a cloud provider's LB controller</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm has NO cloud LB controller → stays Pending forever</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative 1: Use NodePort</span>
kubectl expose deployment web --type=NodePort --port=80
kubectl get svc web
<span class="comment"># NAME  TYPE      CLUSTER-IP   EXTERNAL-IP  PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># web   NodePort  10.96.1.100  &lt;none&gt;       80:30080/TCP</span>
<span class="comment"># Access: http://&lt;any-node-ip&gt;:30080</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative 2: Convert existing LB to NodePort</span>
kubectl patch svc web-lb -p '{"spec":{"type":"NodePort"}}'

<span class="comment"># Alternative 3: Install MetalLB (bare-metal LB)</span>
<span class="comment"># MetalLB assigns real IPs to LoadBalancer services</span>
<span class="comment"># Not CKA scope but useful to know</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative 4: Use Ingress (for HTTP/HTTPS)</span>
<span class="comment"># Ingress controller (nginx) with NodePort</span>
<span class="comment"># Routes traffic by hostname/path</span>

<span class="comment"># CKA exam: NodePort is the expected answer</span>
<span class="comment"># Port range: 30000-32767 (default)</span>
kubectl create svc nodeport web --tcp=80:80 --node-port=30080</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>type: LoadBalancer</code> needs a cloud provider's load balancer integration. On kubeadm/bare-metal, it stays Pending permanently. The CKA expects you to use <code>NodePort</code> instead (port range 30000-32767). NodePort makes the service accessible on every node's IP at the allocated port. MetalLB can provide LoadBalancer functionality on bare-metal but is not CKA scope.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

