        <section class="part-cover" id="part11">
            <span class="part-kicker">🔴 Part 11 · Packaging, Delivery &amp; Extensibility</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Package It, Extend It, Ship It</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 37–39 · Advanced · <em>"Helm, Kustomize, CRDs — the 2025 exam additions"</em></p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 3 Chapters</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 CKA 2025</span>
            </div>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Hand-applying YAML doesn't scale past one cluster, one environment, or one engineer. Part 11 covers the official 2025 exam additions — Helm &amp; Kustomize for templated and overlaid installs, and CRDs &amp; operators for extending Kubernetes itself — then the Day-2 delivery layer (CI/CD &amp; GitOps) that ships easypj automatically and safely.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you'll be able to do by the end:</strong> package easypj as a Helm chart and a Kustomize overlay; extend Kubernetes with a Custom Resource + operator; and deliver changes continuously via a CI/CD + GitOps pipeline.</div>
            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🧠 Real-World Analogy: The Hand-Written Letter, the Mail-Merge Template, and the Clerk Who Never Stops</h5>
                <p>A raw Kubernetes manifest is a hand-written letter — perfectly fine to send once, painful the moment you owe a hundred people the same letter with slightly different names and addresses. Helm is a <strong>mail-merge template</strong>: you write the letter once with placeholders (<code>{{ .Values.replicaCount }}</code>, <code>{{ .Values.image.tag }}</code>) and a values sheet (<code>values.yaml</code>) fills them in per recipient — dev gets one set of values, prod gets another, and the letter itself never changes. Kustomize is a different answer to the same problem: a <strong>base letter plus sticky notes</strong> — you keep one plain, template-free base manifest and layer small, explicit patches ("cross out 1 replica, write 3"; "cross out SQLite, write PostgreSQL") on top per environment, with nothing hidden behind template syntax. A CRD/operator is neither of those — it's <strong>hiring a clerk</strong> who doesn't just send a letter once, but keeps checking the recipient's actual situation against what you asked for and keeps re-sending corrected letters, forever, until reality matches your intent. That's the difference between a one-time templated write (Helm/Kustomize, which run once at install/apply time) and a continuous reconciliation loop (a CRD's controller, which runs forever). Chapter 37 teaches the first two letter-writing styles; Chapter 38 hires the clerk; Chapter 39 puts the entire mailroom on autopilot so a <code>git push</code> is the only human action left — the GitOps controller becomes a clerk-of-clerks that watches the git repo itself and keeps the whole cluster's mail matching what's filed there.</p>
<pre>  THE LETTER ANALOGY                              EASYPJ'S PACKAGING &amp; DELIVERY STACK
  ────────────────────                              ───────────────────────────────────
  ✉️ Hand-written letter, one-off ────────────────▶ Baseline — hand-edited YAML, `kubectl apply -f`
  📋 Mail-merge template + values sheet ──────────▶ Ch 37a — Helm chart + values.yaml (dev/prod)
  🗒️ Base letter + sticky-note patches ───────────▶ Ch 37b — Kustomize base + overlays/dev, overlays/prod
  🧑‍💼 A clerk who re-sends corrected letters ───▶ Ch 38 — CRD (JobPostBackup) + operator reconcile loop
  🏤 The whole mailroom on autopilot ─────────────▶ Ch 39 — GitOps (ArgoCD/Flux): git push is the only step
  ⚠️ A clerk who was never hired ────────────────▶ Gotcha — a CRD with no controller just stores data;
                                                       nothing reconciles it until an operator exists</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Helm and Kustomize are competitors — pick one and stick with it."</td>
                            <td>They solve different problems and real shops run <strong>both</strong>, often on the same cluster. Helm is for software you <em>consume</em> from someone else — Prometheus, ingress-nginx, cert-manager — where you want versioned releases, a values API, and one-command rollback. Kustomize is for YAML you <em>own</em> and just need to vary per environment — your own easypj base manifests, patched for dev vs prod — with zero templating syntax to learn or hide bugs behind. Choosing "Helm vs Kustomize" as a one-time, cluster-wide decision is the beginner mistake; the expert question is "for THIS manifest, am I installing someone else's chart or patching my own base?"</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Installing a CRD is the same as installing an operator — now Kubernetes understands my new resource."</td>
                            <td>A CRD alone only teaches the API server a new <strong>schema</strong> — it lets you <code>kubectl apply</code> and store <code>JobPostBackup</code> objects, and that's <em>all</em> it does. Nothing watches them, nothing acts on them, nothing reconciles a spec against reality. Without an operator (the controller process running a reconcile loop against that CRD), a custom resource is inert data sitting in etcd — indistinguishable, functionally, from a ConfigMap with extra steps. The operator is what turns "Kubernetes knows this shape exists" into "Kubernetes actively makes this shape true."</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"GitOps just means storing your YAML in a git repo instead of a shared folder."</td>
                            <td>Storing manifests in git is necessary but not sufficient — plenty of teams did that for years while still running <code>kubectl apply -f</code> by hand or from CI, which is still <em>push</em>-based deployment, not GitOps. The defining feature of GitOps is an <strong>active controller inside the cluster</strong> (ArgoCD, Flux) that continuously <em>pulls</em> from git and reconciles live cluster state to match it — on a loop, forever — so that manual <code>kubectl edit</code> drift gets silently reverted back to what git says, and the only way to change the cluster is to change git. Git-as-storage is a filing cabinet; GitOps is the clerk who checks the filing cabinet every few seconds and corrects the world to match it.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
