                    <tr><td><a href="#s37-1"><strong>37.1</strong> Helm Basics</a></td><td>What is a chart, release, and value?</td><td>Templates + values → a rendered, revisioned release.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s37-2"><strong>37.2</strong> Chart Anatomy</a></td><td>What files make up a chart?</td><td>Chart.yaml + values.yaml + templates/ + _helpers.tpl + charts/.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s37-3"><strong>37.3</strong> Kustomize Basics</a></td><td>How do template-free overlays work?</td><td>A base + overlays, applied with <code>kubectl apply -k</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s37-4"><strong>37.4</strong> Patches &amp; Generators</a></td><td>How does an overlay actually change the base?</td><td>Strategic-merge / JSON6902 patches + config/secret generators.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s37-5"><strong>37.5</strong> Helm vs Kustomize</a></td><td>Which tool, when?</td><td>Helm for 3rd-party/releases; Kustomize for your own manifests/GitOps.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.1 Helm Basics — Chart, Release, Values, Repo</h3>
                <p>Helm is Kubernetes' package manager. It renders a chart's templates with a set of values into final YAML, then applies that YAML to the cluster and remembers the result as a numbered <strong>release</strong>. Because every install/upgrade is a revision, you get one-command rollbacks — the same superpower <code>apt</code>/<code>yum</code> give you for OS packages, but for whole Kubernetes applications.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Terms</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>CHART</strong> — a versioned package of templated Kubernetes manifests (a directory or a <code>.tgz</code>).</li>
                        <li><strong>RELEASE</strong> — an installed <em>instance</em> of a chart in a namespace, tracked by name and revision number.</li>
                        <li><strong>VALUES</strong> — the parameters that fill the templates: defaults live in <code>values.yaml</code>, overridable with <code>-f</code> files and <code>--set</code> flags.</li>
                        <li><strong>REPO</strong> — an HTTP server hosting packaged charts (e.g. Bitnami, ingress-nginx, the Helm Hub).</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">From chart to running easypj — the Helm install flow</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   📦 chart/               🎛️ values (defaults + -f + --set)
   ├ Chart.yaml               replicas: 3
   ├ values.yaml               image.tag: v2
   └ templates/                  │
       deployment.yaml ◀─────────┘  (fills {{ .Values.x }})
       service.yaml
              │
              │  helm install easypj ./chart -n easypj -f prod.yaml
              ▼
    🖨️  RENDERED, plain YAML (client-side — the cluster never sees a template)
              │
              │  applied via the Kubernetes API
              ▼
    ☸️  RUNNING RESOURCES  (Deployment, Service…)   +   📜 RELEASE "easypj" rev.1
                                                          (tracked, rollback-able)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <p>Every lifecycle command below operates on that same tracked release — installing easypj, tuning it, undoing a bad change, and eventually removing it entirely:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">the full easypj release lifecycle</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add a chart repo (for 3rd-party components) and refresh its index</span>
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
helm repo update

<span class="comment"># Install easypj as release "easypj", using a prod values file</span>
helm install easypj ./chart -n easypj -f prod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Upgrade the release — new values file AND/OR an inline override</span>
helm upgrade easypj ./chart -f prod.yaml --set replicas=4

<span class="comment"># Preview what would be applied — renders locally, touches nothing</span>
helm template ./chart -f prod.yaml

<span class="comment"># See every revision + its status (deployed/superseded/failed)</span>
helm history easypj

<span class="comment"># A bad upgrade? Revert straight back to revision 1</span>
helm rollback easypj 1

<span class="comment"># Remove the release entirely (deletes its resources)</span>
helm uninstall easypj -n easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th><th>Touches the cluster?</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>helm install</code></td><td>Renders + applies a chart as a brand-new release</td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>helm upgrade</code></td><td>Re-renders with new values/chart version, applies the diff, bumps the revision</td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>helm rollback &lt;rev&gt;</code></td><td>Re-applies a previously-stored revision's manifests</td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>helm uninstall</code></td><td>Deletes the release's resources and its history</td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>helm template</code></td><td>Renders the chart to stdout only</td><td>❌ no — safe preview</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>helm history</code></td><td>Lists every revision and its status</td><td>❌ no — read-only</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Flags Worth Knowing on Every Lifecycle Command</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>On</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>--keep-history</code></td><td><code>helm uninstall</code></td><td>Keeps the release's revision records (its Secrets) even after uninstalling, so <code>helm history</code>/<code>helm status</code> still work afterward — WITHOUT it, uninstall deletes the release's history too, matching the table above.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--no-hooks</code></td><td>install / upgrade / rollback / uninstall</td><td>Skips running that command's hooks entirely — useful to isolate whether a failure is in the hook itself or in the main release resources.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--timeout</code></td><td>any command that waits</td><td>A Go duration to wait for Kubernetes operations to finish; defaults to <code>5m0s</code> if not set.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                    <h5>⚡ Speed Tip</h5>
                    <p><code>helm upgrade --install easypj ./chart</code> is <strong>idempotent</strong> — it installs if the release is absent, upgrades if present. One command covers both first-run and re-runs, which is exactly what you want in CI pipelines and under exam time pressure.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🏢 Day-2 Operations</h5>
                    <p>In production, release history <em>is</em> your audit log and your undo button. <code>helm history easypj</code> shows every revision, roughly when it happened, and its status; <code>helm rollback easypj &lt;rev&gt;</code> reverts a bad ship in seconds — no need to remember or reconstruct the previous YAML by hand.</p>
                </div>
            
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Skip the Manual Recovery: <code>--atomic</code></h5>
                    <p>Scenario Drill Q1 in this chapter (below) shows how to manually recover from a stuck <code>pending-upgrade</code> release with <code>helm rollback</code>. In production, prevent the stuck state entirely with <strong><code>--atomic</code></strong>: if the upgrade fails, Helm automatically rolls back for you — no manual "which revision was last good?" investigation under pressure.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">self-healing upgrade — no stuck pending-upgrade</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># --atomic: on failure, Helm rolls back automatically (implies --wait)</span>
helm upgrade easypj ./chart --set replicas=4 --atomic --timeout 5m

<span class="comment"># --cleanup-on-fail: also removes new resources a FAILED install created</span>
helm install easypj ./chart --atomic --cleanup-on-fail</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>Without <code>--atomic</code>, a failed upgrade leaves the release in <code>pending-upgrade</code> and the old AND new resources potentially half-mixed until someone runs <code>helm rollback</code> by hand. <code>--atomic</code> makes that rollback part of the same command — the exact fix CI pipelines should bake in by default.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📦 Modern Chart Repos: OCI Registries</h5>
                    <p>Classic chart REPOs (an HTTP index served by <code>helm repo add</code>) aren't the only option since Helm 3.8. Charts can also be pushed to and pulled from an <strong>OCI registry</strong> — the same kind of registry that stores container images (Docker Hub, ECR, ACR, GHCR, Harbor). This is increasingly the 2025 production default because a team already runs one registry for both images and charts, with one auth/RBAC model.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">OCI-based chart repo — no `helm repo add` needed</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Package and push easypj's chart straight to an OCI registry</span>
helm package ./chart -d ./dist
helm push ./dist/easypj-1.4.0.tgz oci://ghcr.io/easypj-org/charts

<span class="comment"># Install directly from the OCI reference — no repo add/update step</span>
helm install easypj oci://ghcr.io/easypj-org/charts/easypj --version 1.4.0 -f prod.yaml</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>An <code>oci://</code> reference is versioned and pulled the same way an image tag is — no separate <code>helm repo update</code> to keep an index fresh, and the registry's existing image-pull auth (imagePullSecrets-style credentials) usually covers the chart too.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔍 Find &amp; Inspect a Chart BEFORE Installing It</h5>
                    <p>Before you <code>helm install</code> a third-party component you've never used (exactly the CKA task pattern for ingress-nginx/cert-manager), two read-only commands tell you what you're about to get:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">search before you install, show before you set values</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Search Artifact Hub (all public repos) for a chart by keyword</span>
helm search hub ingress-nginx

<span class="comment"># Search only the repos YOU'VE already `helm repo add`-ed locally</span>
helm search repo bitnami

<span class="comment"># Print every value a chart exposes, WITH its default — before you write -f/--set</span>
helm show values bitnami/postgresql</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>helm search hub</code></td><td>"Does a public chart for X exist anywhere?" (queries Artifact Hub)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>helm search repo</code></td><td>"What charts/versions are in the repos I've already added locally?"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>helm show values &lt;chart&gt;</code></td><td>"What CAN I override, and what's the default?" — read this before guessing at <code>-f</code>/<code>--set</code> keys</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 <code>--set</code> Syntax Cheat Sheet — Beyond Simple <code>key=value</code></h5>
                    <p><code>--set</code> parses a compact dotted/bracketed syntax that mirrors nested YAML — knowing its edge cases saves you from writing a throwaway <code>-f</code> file for a one-off override:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Syntax</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Simple value</td><td><code>--set replicas=4</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Multiple values</td><td><code>--set replicas=4,image.tag=v2</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Nested object</td><td><code>--set image.repo=easypj,image.tag=v2</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>List</td><td><code>--set env={DEBUG,ALLOWED_HOSTS}</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Array index</td><td><code>--set containers[0].image=easypj:v2</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Null / empty</td><td><code>--set nodeSelector=null,tags={}</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Literal comma/dot in a key or value</td><td><code>--set nodeSelector."kubernetes\.io/role"=master</code> (escape the dot); <code>--set name=v1\,v2</code> (escape the comma)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <h5>⚠️ Gotcha</h5>
                        <p>A dotted key like <code>kubernetes.io/role</code> looks like nesting to <code>--set</code>'s parser — without the backslash-escaped dot (<code>kubernetes\.io/role</code>), Helm tries to nest into a non-existent <code>kubernetes</code> object with an <code>io/role</code> field instead of setting the single literal key you meant. When a <code>--set</code> override "does nothing," check for an un-escaped dot or comma first.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
</section>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.2 Helm Chart Anatomy — Packaging easypj</h3>
                <p>A chart is just a directory tree with a fixed shape. Here's easypj packaged as a chart:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj's chart directory tree</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   chart/
    ├ Chart.yaml          # name/version/appVersion (chart metadata)
    ├ values.yaml         # defaults: replicas: 2, image: easypj:v1
    ├ templates/
    │   ├ deployment.yaml # spec.replicas: {{ .Values.replicas }}
    │   ├ service.yaml
    │   └ _helpers.tpl    # named templates (labels, fullname)
    └ charts/             # subchart dependencies (e.g. a postgresql chart)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>File / dir</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Chart.yaml</code></td><td>The chart's identity card. <code>version</code> is the <strong>chart</strong> version; <code>appVersion</code> is the <strong>app's</strong> version (e.g. the easypj image tag). They're independent — you can ship chart <code>1.4.0</code> that deploys <code>appVersion: "v2"</code>.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>values.yaml</code></td><td>The default parameters. Everything a consumer might want to tune — replicas, image, resources, env — lives here so it can be overridden without editing templates.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>templates/</code></td><td>The manifests, with Go-template directives. A field reads a value with <code>{{ .Values.replicas }}</code>. Files starting with <code>_</code> (like <code>_helpers.tpl</code>) are NOT rendered as manifests — they hold reusable named templates such as a standard label block or a "fullname" helper.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>charts/</code></td><td>Vendored <strong>subcharts</strong> (dependencies). Declaring a PostgreSQL subchart here lets easypj's chart pull in its database chart.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">templates/deployment.yaml — a real template fragment</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: {{ include "easypj.fullname" . }}
  labels: {{- include "easypj.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
spec:
  replicas: {{ .Values.replicas }}
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: web
          image: "{{ .Values.image.repo }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}"
          env:
            - name: DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE
              value: easypj.settings.{{ .Values.env }}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Named Templates via <code>_helpers.tpl</code></h5>
                    <p>The <code>{{ include "easypj.labels" . }}</code> call pulls a named template defined in <code>_helpers.tpl</code>, so every resource in the chart carries <strong>identical</strong> labels without copy-paste. <code>nindent 4</code> re-indents the included block to fit correctly inside the surrounding YAML.</p>
                </div>
            
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔍 Validate Before You Package: <code>helm lint</code> &amp; <code>--debug</code></h5>
                    <p>A chart with a YAML/template mistake often fails silently at render time with a confusing Go-template error pointing at the wrong line. Two commands catch problems BEFORE you ever run <code>helm install</code>:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">lint the chart, then render with full error context</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Static checks: required fields present, values referenced exist, YAML is valid</span>
helm lint ./chart -f prod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Render AND print the values actually used + full stack trace on failure</span>
helm template ./chart -f prod.yaml --debug

<span class="comment"># Validate the rendered YAML against the live API's OpenAPI schema too</span>
helm install easypj ./chart -f prod.yaml --dry-run --debug</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Catches</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>helm lint</code></td><td>Missing <code>Chart.yaml</code> fields, obviously broken templates, undocumented values</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>helm template --debug</code></td><td>Template rendering errors, WITH the exact line and the merged values that produced them</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>helm install --dry-run --debug</code></td><td>Everything above, PLUS whether the API server itself would accept the rendered YAML (schema validation) — without creating anything</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>Run all three in a chart's CI pipeline before it's ever packaged — catching a broken <code>{{ .Values.replicas }}</code> reference in lint is far cheaper than debugging a failed rollout in staging.</p>
                </div>
</section>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.3 Kustomize Basics — Base + Overlays</h3>
                <p>Kustomize is template-<strong>FREE</strong>. You keep plain, valid manifests in a <strong>base</strong>, then create <strong>overlays</strong> that reference the base and patch it per environment. It ships built into <code>kubectl</code>, so you apply with <code>kubectl apply -k</code> — no extra binary to install.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Terms</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>KUSTOMIZATION</strong> — a <code>kustomization.yaml</code> file (plus the directory containing it) that declares resources, generators, and transformers.</li>
                        <li><strong>BASE</strong> — a kustomization referred to by another kustomization (i.e. one that other overlays build on top of).</li>
                        <li><strong>OVERLAY</strong> — a kustomization that depends on (references) another kustomization as its base.</li>
                        <li><strong>VARIANT</strong> — the actual outcome in a cluster of applying one overlay to its base (e.g. easypj's "dev variant" vs its "prod variant").</li>
                        <li><strong>GENERATOR</strong> — produces new resources from scratch (e.g. <code>configMapGenerator</code> builds a ConfigMap object that didn't exist in the base).</li>
                        <li><strong>TRANSFORMER</strong> — modifies resources that already exist (e.g. the <code>replicas</code>/<code>images</code> transformers rewrite fields on the base's Deployment).</li>
                        <li><strong>PATCH</strong> — a general instruction to modify a resource (strategic-merge or JSON6902); patches are one specific kind of transformer.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy</h5>
                    <p>The base is a master document; each overlay is a "track changes" layer. The original is never edited — Kustomize merges the layer on the way out.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj as a Kustomize base + prod overlay</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   base/kustomization.yaml:
     resources: [deployment.yaml, service.yaml]

   overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml:
     resources: ["../../base"]
     replicas: [{ name: easypj-web, count: 3 }]
     images:   [{ name: easypj, newTag: v2 }]
     patches:  [{ path: prod-env.yaml }]      # DEBUG=False, ALLOWED_HOSTS

   # apply:    kubectl apply -k overlays/prod
   # preview:  kubectl kustomize overlays/prod
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <p>How it flows, step by step:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td>Canonical base</td><td>The base holds the canonical, deployable manifests (<code>deployment.yaml</code>, <code>service.yaml</code>) — they apply fine on their own, with no Kustomize involved.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2</td><td>Overlay declares transforms</td><td>Each overlay's <code>kustomization.yaml</code> lists the base under <code>resources:</code>, then declares transformations: bump <code>replicas</code>, retag <code>images</code>, and apply <code>patches</code> that override env vars (<code>DEBUG</code>, <code>ALLOWED_HOSTS</code>).</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3</td><td>Build &amp; apply</td><td><code>kubectl apply -k overlays/prod</code> builds the final resource list and applies it. <code>kubectl kustomize overlays/prod</code> renders it to stdout first so you can eyeball it.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Gotcha</h5>
                    <p>An overlay <strong>must</strong> reference the base under <code>resources:</code>. Forget it and Kustomize builds an empty or partial set — your patches have nothing to patch. Paths are relative to the <code>kustomization.yaml</code> that contains them.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">build &amp; apply the prod overlay</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Preview the fully-rendered manifests — no cluster touched</span>
kubectl kustomize overlays/prod

<span class="comment"># Apply them for real</span>
kubectl apply -k overlays/prod

<span class="comment"># Same for dev — same base, different overlay</span>
kubectl apply -k overlays/dev</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.4 Patches &amp; Generators</h3>
                <p>Kustomize changes the base in three main ways: strategic-merge patches, JSON6902 patches, and generators/transformers. All three are declared in an overlay's <code>kustomization.yaml</code> — none of them touch the base files themselves.</p>

                <h4>Strategic-Merge Patch (the default)</h4>
                <p>A <strong>partial</strong> manifest that Kustomize merges field-by-field into the matching base resource. You write only the fields you want to change; Kustomize finds the resource by kind/name and deep-merges. Example <code>prod-env.yaml</code> that flips <code>DEBUG</code> off on easypj's web container:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">overlays/prod/prod-env.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: web
          env:
            - name: DJANGO_DEBUG
              value: "False"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>JSON6902 Patch</h4>
                <p>A precise RFC-6902 operation list (<code>op</code>/<code>path</code>/<code>value</code>) for surgical edits — useful when a strategic merge can't express the change (e.g. replacing an item at a specific list index):</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">a JSON6902 patch in kustomization.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">patches:
  - target: { kind: Deployment, name: easypj-web }
    patch: |-
      - op: replace
        path: /spec/replicas
        value: 3</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Gotcha: Older Blogs/Docs Use Different Field Names</h5>
                    <p>Kustomize used to require separate <code>patchesStrategicMerge:</code> and <code>patchesJson6902:</code> lists in <code>kustomization.yaml</code>. Both were formally <strong>deprecated in Kustomize v5.0.0</strong> in favor of a single unified <code>patches:</code> list, where Kustomize auto-detects the patch style from its shape (a full/partial resource → strategic-merge; an <code>op</code>/<code>path</code>/<code>value</code> list → JSON6902). The deprecated fields are never being removed from the older <code>kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1</code> API (so existing overlays keep building), but they are dropped from the newer <code>v1</code> Kustomization API.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Older syntax (deprecated, still works)</th><th>Current unified syntax</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>patchesStrategicMerge: [prod-env.yaml]</code></td><td><code>patches: [{ path: prod-env.yaml }]</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>patchesJson6902: [{ target: {...}, path: patch.yaml }]</code></td><td><code>patches: [{ target: {...}, path: patch.yaml }]</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>If you copy a <code>kustomization.yaml</code> snippet from an older tutorial and see <code>patchesStrategicMerge</code>/<code>patchesJson6902</code>, it will still build correctly — but write new overlays with the unified <code>patches:</code> key so you're not maintaining two different patch lists for what is conceptually one thing. The standalone <code>kustomize</code> binary can even migrate an old overlay for you: <code>kustomize edit fix</code> rewrites deprecated fields to their current equivalents in place.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Generators &amp; Transformers</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>configMapGenerator</code> / <code>secretGenerator</code></td><td>Build a ConfigMap/Secret from literals or files <strong>and</strong> append a content HASH to the name. When the config changes, the name changes, which forces a rolling update automatically — no more "I edited the ConfigMap but the pods kept the old value."</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>images: [{ name, newTag }]</code></td><td>Retag an image across all resources without touching the base.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>replicas: [{ name, count }]</code></td><td>Set replica counts per environment.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">configMapGenerator — why the hash suffix triggers a rollout</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   kustomization.yaml:
     configMapGenerator:
       - name: easypj-config
         literals: [DJANGO_DEBUG=False, ALLOWED_HOSTS=easypj.example.com]

   BUILD OUTPUT:
     ConfigMap "easypj-config-8f24bc6b2"   ◀── name = base name + content hash
                    │
     Deployment's podTemplate references "easypj-config-8f24bc6b2"
                    │
     change a literal ──▶ hash changes ──▶ NEW ConfigMap name ──▶ podTemplate
                                             changes ──▶ Deployment rolls pods
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Gotcha</h5>
                    <p>The content-hash suffix on generated ConfigMaps/Secrets is a <strong>feature</strong>, not a bug — it's what makes config rollouts atomic. If you need a stable name (e.g. another controller references it by a fixed name), set <code>generatorOptions: { disableNameSuffixHash: true }</code> — but accept that you then lose the automatic rollout.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.5 Helm vs Kustomize — Decision Guide</h3>
                <p>Two ways to ship easypj per environment — pick by the job, not by dogma.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Helm vs Kustomize — two ways to ship easypj per environment</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   HELM (templating + releases)          KUSTOMIZE (overlays, no templates)
   chart/                                base/
    ├ Chart.yaml                          ├ deployment.yaml  service.yaml
    ├ values.yaml  (defaults)             └ kustomization.yaml
    └ templates/  {{ .Values.replicas }} overlays/
   helm install easypj ./chart \          ├ dev/   (patch: replicas=1, DEBUG)
     -f prod.yaml   (override per env)     └ prod/  (patch: replicas=3, image)
                                          kubectl apply -k overlays/prod
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Is…</th><th>Best for…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Helm</strong></td><td>templated, versioned releases with rollbacks</td><td>installing 3rd-party components (charts)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Kustomize</strong></td><td>template-FREE base + overlays/patches</td><td>your own manifests, env overlays, GitOps-friendly</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Preview First, Always</h5>
                    <p><code>helm template ./chart</code> and <code>kubectl kustomize overlays/prod</code> render the final YAML without touching the cluster — run one of these before every apply, no exceptions.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🧭 Decision Guide</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <h5>⚓ Reach for HELM when…</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>You're installing a <strong>third-party component</strong> shipped as a chart (ingress-nginx, cert-manager, a database).</li>
                            <li>You need <strong>versioned releases</strong> with one-command rollback.</li>
                            <li>You want a single package others can <code>helm install</code> with their own values.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>🧱 Reach for KUSTOMIZE when…</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>The manifests are <strong>your own</strong>; you want template-free, readable YAML.</li>
                            <li>You're doing <strong>GitOps</strong> — Argo CD and Flux both render Kustomize natively.</li>
                            <li>Environments differ only by a few patched fields (replicas, image, env).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 COMPOSE Both When Convenient</h5>
                    <p><code>helm template ./chart -f prod.yaml | kubectl kustomize</code> (or feed a chart's rendered output into a Kustomize base) lets a Helm chart provide the manifests and Kustomize apply final org-wide overlays/patches on top. Neither tool is "better" — the exam wants you fluent in both, and to know when to reach for each (or compose them).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 A Third Way to Compose: <code>helmCharts</code> Natively in <code>kustomization.yaml</code></h5>
                    <p>Rather than piping <code>helm template</code> into Kustomize by hand, a <code>kustomization.yaml</code> can declare a chart directly under <code>helmCharts:</code> and let Kustomize inflate (render) it as part of the build itself — one command instead of two:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">kustomization.yaml — inflate a Helm chart in-build</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">helmCharts:
  - name: easypj
    repo: oci://ghcr.io/easypj-org/charts
    version: 1.4.0
    releaseName: easypj
    namespace: easypj
    valuesFile: prod.yaml

<span class="comment"># build/apply — this flag is required, and it fetches a real chart at build time</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl kustomize --enable-helm .</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <h5>⚠️ Gotcha</h5>
                        <p>Kustomize's own docs describe <code>helmCharts</code> as offering only "limited support for helm chart inflation," covering roughly what <code>helm template</code>'s own flags cover — it deliberately does NOT support private-registry authentication or other security-sensitive chart features. It also must be explicitly opted into with <strong><code>--enable-helm</code></strong> (standalone <code>kustomize build</code>) — without that flag the <code>helmCharts</code> block is ignored. For anything beyond a simple public chart, the manual <code>helm template | kubectl kustomize</code> pipe from above remains the more flexible, more widely-supported option.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how Helm renders, where releases live, and how Kustomize actually builds</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Everything in §37.1–37.5 works because of a small set of mechanics: Helm renders <strong>entirely client-side</strong> (the cluster never sees a template), and Kustomize builds a resource list with a well-defined transformer order. Understanding these mechanics is what lets you diagnose "why didn't my override apply?" instead of guessing.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌─────────────── HELM (client) ───────────────┐   ┌────── KUSTOMIZE (client) ──────┐
   │ chart + values ──▶ Go-template engine        │   │ base + overlay ──▶ transformer  │
   │              ──▶ plain YAML                  │   │              ──▶ plain YAML     │
   └───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘   └───────────────┬─────────────────┘
                           │  kubectl / Helm SDK applies via the API           │
                           ▼                                                  ▼
                     ☸️  KUBERNETES API SERVER  (only ever receives FINISHED, template-free YAML)
</pre>

                    <h5>① Client-Side Rendering &amp; Release Storage — where Helm's "memory" actually lives</h5>
                    <p>Helm 3 removed the old client/server split (Helm 2's cluster-side "Tiller" component) so that the Helm client renders templates <strong>entirely client-side</strong>, producing plain YAML, then applies it via the Kubernetes API directly. The cluster never sees a template — only finished manifests. So where does <code>helm history</code> get its data from?</p>
                    <div class="info-box note">
                        <h5>📖 A Newer Major Version Exists Too</h5>
                        <p>Helm has since shipped a Helm 4 major version on top of this same Helm-3 foundation — official docs describe it as "a significant evolution from v3, introducing breaking changes, new architectural patterns, and enhanced functionality while maintaining backwards compatibility for charts." The client-side rendering model, release-as-Secrets storage, values precedence, hooks, and the CRD install-once behavior covered in this chapter all carry over unchanged; Helm 4's headline shift is treating OCI registries as the primary chart distribution method (including installing a chart by content digest for supply-chain security) rather than replacing any of the mechanics taught here. Confirm your target environment's Helm major version with <code>helm version</code> before assuming CLI flag parity.</p>
                    </div>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   helm install easypj ./chart -f prod.yaml
        │
        ▼
   🖥️  YOUR MACHINE (Helm client)
        │  1. reads Chart.yaml + values.yaml + templates/
        │  2. renders Go-templates with merged values  →  plain YAML
        ▼
   📡  kube-apiserver  ──▶  applies the rendered Deployment/Service/etc.
        │
        │  Helm ALSO writes a second object:
        ▼
   🗄️  Secret  type=helm.sh/release.v1
       name: sh.helm.release.v1.easypj.v1     (one per revision!)
       data: { rendered manifests (gzip+base64), values used, chart metadata }
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Reads from</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>helm history easypj</code></td><td>Lists every <code>helm.sh/release.v1</code> Secret for "easypj" in the namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>helm rollback easypj 1</code></td><td>Reads revision 1's stored manifests and re-applies them verbatim</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>helm get manifest easypj</code></td><td>Prints the current revision's rendered YAML straight from its Secret</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Release state is <em>data</em>, stored as ordinary Secrets in the release's own namespace — one per revision. That's why <code>helm history</code>/<code>helm rollback</code> keep working across Helm client restarts or a different laptop entirely, and why deleting those Secrets by hand (e.g. a careless <code>kubectl delete secret -n easypj --all</code>) silently destroys your rollback history with no warning.</div>

                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <h5>⚠️ Security: Your --set/-f Values Are ALSO in That Secret, Forever</h5>
                        <p>Because the release Secret stores the values used to render each revision, ANY sensitive value passed via <code>--set dbPassword=...</code> or a <code>-f secrets.yaml</code> file is now sitting — base64-encoded, NOT encrypted — inside every past revision's Secret, even after you rotate the credential. <code>helm get values easypj --all</code> or <code>kubectl get secret sh.helm.release.v1.easypj.v3 -o json</code> hands anyone with read access to that namespace the old password in plain text.</p>
                        <p>Never pass real secrets through Helm values. Instead, reference an existing Kubernetes <code>Secret</code> object (created separately by a tool like <strong>Sealed Secrets</strong>, <strong>External Secrets Operator</strong>, or SOPS) from your templates, and let the chart mount/env-inject it by name — the secret's actual content never touches <code>values.yaml</code>, <code>--set</code>, or the release history at all.</p>
                    </div>


                    <h5>② Values Precedence — the exact resolution chain</h5>
                    <p>"Just override the value" hides a precise merge order. Get this wrong and you ship the wrong config while believing your override "should have worked":</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   LOWEST precedence                                          HIGHEST precedence
   chart's values.yaml  ──▶  -f file 1  ──▶  -f file 2  ──▶  --set flags
   (baked-in defaults)      (first override) (later WINS      (wins over
                                              over file 1)      everything)

   helm upgrade easypj ./chart -f base.yaml -f prod.yaml --set replicas=4
                                    │             │                │
                                 applied first  applied second   applied LAST
                                 (can be beaten) (beats base.yaml) (always wins)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Precedence</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>values.yaml</code> (chart default)</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Sane, safe defaults shipped with the chart</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>-f file.yaml</code> (multiple allowed)</td><td>Middle — later files win over earlier ones</td><td>Per-environment values files (dev.yaml, prod.yaml)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--set key=value</code></td><td>Highest</td><td>One-off overrides, CI pipeline injections, quick tests</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Mnemonic: <em>"set beats files, later files beat earlier files, files beat defaults."</em> If a value "isn't taking," check for a <code>--set</code> flag or a later <code>-f</code> file quietly overriding it — <code>helm get values easypj</code> shows the effective merged values so you never have to guess.</div>

                    <h5>③ Helm Hooks &amp; Weights — automating the release lifecycle</h5>
                    <p>Annotate a resource with <code>helm.sh/hook</code> (e.g. <code>pre-install</code>, <code>post-upgrade</code>) and Helm runs it at a specific point around the release lifecycle instead of as an ordinary resource — perfect for a Django migration Job that must run <em>before</em> easypj's new pods start serving traffic.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   helm upgrade easypj ./chart
        │
        ▼
   🪝 pre-upgrade hooks run FIRST (weight order: lower number runs earlier)
       Job "easypj-migrate" (hook-weight: "0")  →  runs `manage.py migrate`
        │  Helm WAITS for the hook Job to complete successfully
        ▼
   📦 normal release resources applied/upgraded (Deployment, Service…)
        │
        ▼
   🪝 post-upgrade hooks run LAST
       Job "easypj-smoke-test" (hook-weight: "5")  →  hits /healthz
</pre>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">templates/migrate-job.yaml — a pre-upgrade hook</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-migrate
  annotations:
    "helm.sh/hook": pre-upgrade,pre-install
    "helm.sh/hook-weight": "0"
    "helm.sh/hook-delete-policy": before-hook-creation
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
        - name: migrate
          image: "{{ .Values.image.repo }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}"
          command: ["python", "manage.py", "migrate"]</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> A hook resource is NOT tracked as part of the release's normal resource list — it's a side-channel Helm manages itself, run in <code>hook-weight</code> order (same-weight hooks fall back to normal resource install order, not guaranteed to be stable). <code>hook-delete-policy: before-hook-creation</code> deletes the previous run's Job before creating a new one, so re-running <code>helm upgrade</code> doesn't collide with a leftover completed Job of the same name — and this exact policy is Helm's DEFAULT if you omit the annotation entirely, so a bare <code>pre-upgrade</code> hook Job already self-cleans on every run.</div>

                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>All 8 lifecycle hook types + <code>test</code></th><th>Runs…</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>pre-install</code> / <code>post-install</code></td><td>Before / after resources are created on <code>helm install</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>pre-upgrade</code> / <code>post-upgrade</code></td><td>Before / after resources are updated on <code>helm upgrade</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>pre-rollback</code> / <code>post-rollback</code></td><td>Before / after resources are reverted on <code>helm rollback</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>pre-delete</code> / <code>post-delete</code></td><td>Before / after resources are removed on <code>helm uninstall</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>test</code></td><td>ONLY on explicit <code>helm test</code> — never automatic</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th><code>hook-delete-policy</code> value</th><th>Deletes the previous hook resource…</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>before-hook-creation</code> (the DEFAULT if unset)</td><td>Right before the new hook resource is created</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>hook-succeeded</code></td><td>After the hook resource completes successfully</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>hook-failed</code></td><td>After the hook resource fails</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <h5>⚠️ Gotcha: Hook Resources Survive <code>helm uninstall</code></h5>
                        <p>Resources a hook creates are "currently not tracked or managed as part of the release" — so <code>helm uninstall</code> does NOT clean them up. A one-shot hook Job normally finishes and gets deleted by its own <code>hook-delete-policy</code> on the next run, but if a release is uninstalled before that next run ever happens, the completed hook Job/Pod is left behind in the namespace and must be cleaned up manually (or via <code>ttlSecondsAfterFinished</code> on the Job spec).</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-box tip">
                        <h5>💡 A Ninth Hook Type: <code>test</code></h5>
                        <p>Beyond the eight lifecycle hooks above (<code>pre</code>/<code>post-install</code>, <code>pre</code>/<code>post-upgrade</code>, <code>pre</code>/<code>post-rollback</code>, <code>pre</code>/<code>post-delete</code>), Helm supports a <code>test</code> hook — a Job/Pod that does NOT run automatically during install/upgrade, only when you explicitly ask for it with <code>helm test</code>. It's the natural place for a post-deploy smoke check.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">templates/tests/smoke-test.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-smoke-test
  annotations:
    "helm.sh/hook": test
spec:
  containers:
    - name: curl
      image: curlimages/curl
      command: ["curl", "--fail", "http://easypj-web/healthz"]
  restartPolicy: Never</code></pre>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">helm install easypj ./chart -f prod.yaml
helm test easypj                 <span class="comment"># runs the test hook Pod NOW, on demand</span></code></pre>
                        <p>Because it's opt-in rather than automatic, a <code>test</code> hook is safe to make slow or thorough (hit multiple endpoints, check a DB migration applied) without adding latency to every single install/upgrade.</p>
                    </div>


                    <h5>④ Subcharts &amp; Dependencies — composing charts</h5>
                    <p>Real applications rarely stand alone — easypj needs PostgreSQL. Rather than hand-writing a Deployment/StatefulSet for Postgres, declare it as a <strong>subchart</strong> dependency and let its own chart (e.g. Bitnami's) manage it.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   chart/Chart.yaml:
     dependencies:
       - name: postgresql
         version: "13.2.0"
         repository: "https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami"
         condition: postgresql.enabled

   helm dependency update ./chart      # pulls postgresql-13.2.0.tgz into charts/

   chart/
    ├ Chart.yaml
    ├ values.yaml            postgresql:            ◀── keyed block overrides
    │                          auth: { database: easypj }   the SUBCHART's values
    ├ charts/
    │   └ postgresql-13.2.0.tgz     # vendored subchart, untouched
    └ templates/               (easypj's own Deployment/Service)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>helm dependency update</code></td><td>Downloads/pins each declared subchart into <code>charts/</code> as a <code>.tgz</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Keyed override</td><td>Parent's <code>values.yaml</code> can set subchart values under a block named after the subchart (<code>postgresql: {...}</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>condition</code></td><td>A values.yaml boolean (<code>postgresql.enabled</code>) that turns the whole subchart on/off</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Subcharts let you reuse battle-tested charts (a Postgres chart maintained by experts) instead of reinventing StatefulSets, PVCs, and backup CronJobs yourself — you only own the <em>parent</em> chart's overrides, not the subchart's internals.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ CRDs &amp; Helm — the install-once caveat</h5>
                    <p>Helm installs CRDs from a chart's <code>crds/</code> directory <strong>ONCE</strong> on first install and does NOT upgrade or delete them with the release. This is a deliberate safety choice — CRDs are cluster-scoped and shared, so Helm refuses to silently mutate or remove something other releases/tools might depend on.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   helm install my-operator ./chart          (chart has a crds/ directory)
        │
        ▼
   1️⃣ FIRST install:  crds/*.yaml applied  →  CRD registered in the API   ✅
        │
   helm upgrade my-operator ./chart --version 2.0.0   (crds/ changed in v2!)
        │
        ▼
   2️⃣ UPGRADE:  crds/ directory is SKIPPED  →  old CRD schema still active ⚠️
        │
   helm uninstall my-operator
        │
        ▼
   3️⃣ UNINSTALL:  CRDs are NOT deleted  →  custom resources survive          ✅ (by design)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>What Helm does with <code>crds/</code></th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Install</td><td>Applies once</td><td>Registers the type so the chart's resources can be created</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Upgrade</td><td>Ignored entirely</td><td>Avoids silently breaking existing custom resources with a schema change</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Uninstall</td><td>Left in place</td><td>Deleting a CRD cascade-deletes ALL its custom resources cluster-wide — too destructive to automate</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> After bumping a chart version that changes its CRDs, you must <code>kubectl apply -f</code> the new CRD YAML yourself — <code>helm upgrade</code> silently will not do it. This exact gap is a favorite CKA/real-world trap: "I upgraded the operator chart but the new CRD field is rejected" almost always means the CRD itself was never updated.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ How Kustomize Builds — the transformer pipeline</h5>
                    <p>Kustomize builds a <strong>resource list</strong> with NO templating — it reads the base, applies transformers/patches in a defined order, and emits final YAML. <code>kubectl</code> embeds a version of this; the standalone <code>kustomize</code> binary adds newer features on top.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl kustomize overlays/prod
        │
        ▼
   1. LOAD   resources: ["../../base"]         →  read every base manifest
   2. GENERATE   configMapGenerator/secretGenerator  →  build new Config/Secret objects
   3. TRANSFORM  namePrefix/nameSuffix/commonLabels/commonAnnotations/namespace
   4. IMAGES     images: [{name, newTag}]          →  rewrite image references
   5. REPLICAS   replicas: [{name, count}]          →  rewrite replica counts
   6. PATCH      strategic-merge patches, THEN JSON6902 patches
   7. EMIT       final, plain YAML  →  (kubectl apply -k applies it; else printed)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Available in</th><th>What it adds</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Bases / overlays / patches / generators</td><td><code>kubectl</code> built-in AND standalone <code>kustomize</code></td><td>The core feature set covered in §37.3–37.4</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Components</strong></td><td>Standalone <code>kustomize</code> (newer <code>kubectl</code> too)</td><td>Reusable, <em>opt-in</em> overlay fragments an overlay can mix in — e.g. a "debug-logging" component toggled per overlay</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Replacements</strong></td><td>Standalone <code>kustomize</code></td><td>Copy a value from one field to many others (replaces the older, more limited <code>vars</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Custom transformers</td><td>Standalone <code>kustomize</code></td><td>Plug in your own transformation logic beyond the built-ins</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> The ORDER matters: generators run before patches, so a <code>configMapGenerator</code>'s hash-suffixed name is what your strategic-merge patch must target if it references that ConfigMap. And because there's no templating step at all, every value in the final YAML came from either the base file or an explicit transformer — nothing is ever "computed" the way a Helm <code>{{ if }}</code> can be.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Helm packages manifests as a templated, versioned CHART</strong>; installing it creates a RELEASE you can upgrade/rollback. <code>values.yaml</code> (+ <code>-f</code>/<code>--set</code> overrides) parameterize per environment.</li>
                    <li><strong>A chart</strong> = <code>Chart.yaml</code> (metadata) + <code>values.yaml</code> (defaults) + <code>templates/</code> (manifests with <code>{{ .Values }}</code>) + <code>_helpers.tpl</code> (named templates) + <code>charts/</code> (subcharts).</li>
                    <li><strong>Kustomize is template-FREE</strong> — a base + overlays that patch it (replicas, images, env). Apply with <code>kubectl apply -k</code> — it's built into <code>kubectl</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Use Helm</strong> to install 3rd-party components (charts) and when you need releases/rollbacks; <strong>use Kustomize</strong> for your own manifests and GitOps. They can even compose (<code>helm template | kustomize</code>).</li>
                    <li><strong>Values precedence</strong>: <code>--set</code> &gt; <code>-f</code> values file &gt; chart defaults (<code>values.yaml</code>); later <code>-f</code> files override earlier ones.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>configMapGenerator</code>/<code>secretGenerator</code></strong> append a content hash so config changes trigger automatic rolling updates.</li>
                    <li><strong>Always PREVIEW before applying</strong>: <code>helm template ./chart</code> and <code>kubectl kustomize overlays/prod</code> render the final YAML without touching the cluster.</li>
                    <li><strong>Helm's release state lives in Secrets</strong> (<code>helm.sh/release.v1</code>), one per revision — that's what powers <code>helm history</code>/<code>helm rollback</code>, and why deleting them breaks both.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Helm release-name collisions</strong> in a namespace fail the install — release names are unique per namespace, just like object names.</li>
                    <li><strong>An overlay's kustomization must reference the base</strong> under <code>resources:</code>; forgetting it produces an empty/partial build with nothing for your patches to target.</li>
                    <li><strong>Helm CRDs in the <code>crds/</code> dir</strong> install once and aren't upgraded or deleted with the release — manage CRD upgrades separately with a manual <code>kubectl apply</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>--set</code> overrides win over <code>-f</code> values</strong>, which win over chart defaults; mix them up under time pressure and you ship the wrong config.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deleting a release's <code>helm.sh/release.v1</code> Secrets</strong> silently breaks <code>helm history</code>/<code>helm rollback</code> — there is no warning, and no way to reconstruct history afterward.</li>
                    <li><strong>The generated ConfigMap/Secret name-hash suffix</strong> means anything that references the resource by a fixed name will break — disable the hash only when you truly need a stable name, and accept you lose automatic rollouts.</li>
                    <li><strong>"<code>helm install</code> is idempotent like <code>kubectl apply</code>."</strong> It is NOT — a second <code>helm install</code> of the same release name errors. Use <code>helm upgrade --install</code> for a repeatable, idempotent command.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Kustomize patches apply if they're close enough."</strong> No — a strategic-merge or JSON6902 patch's target must match kind/name/namespace <em>exactly</em>, or it silently no-ops with no error.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong><code>helm template</code> / <code>kubectl kustomize</code></strong> to eyeball the rendered manifests is the fastest way to catch a templating/patch mistake before it ever touches the cluster.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>helm history easypj</code></strong> then <strong><code>helm rollback easypj &lt;rev&gt;</code></strong> reverts a bad upgrade in one step — no need to reconstruct the previous YAML.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>helm upgrade --install</code></strong> is idempotent — installs if absent, upgrades if present. Use it by default in scripts and CI.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>helm install ... --create-namespace</code></strong> makes the target namespace in the same command — handy for one-shot component installs like ingress-nginx.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>--dry-run</code></strong> on <code>helm install</code>/<code>upgrade</code> renders AND validates against the API without applying — cheaper than a full preview-then-apply cycle.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>helm get values easypj</code></strong> shows the effective merged values for a running release — the fastest way to confirm precedence actually did what you expected.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

