            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 37 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of Helm and Kustomize fundamentals — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Install, upgrade, then roll back an easypj Helm release.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>helm install easypj ./chart -f prod.yaml</code> → <code>helm upgrade easypj ./chart --set replicas=4</code> → <code>helm rollback easypj 1</code>. Verify with <code>helm history easypj</code> at each step.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the full lifecycle</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">helm install easypj ./chart -f prod.yaml
helm upgrade easypj ./chart --set replicas=4
helm history easypj                    <span class="comment"># now shows 2 revisions</span>
helm rollback easypj 1
helm history easypj                    <span class="comment"># revision 3 = a copy of revision 1's state</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Each install/upgrade is a new numbered revision. <code>rollback</code> doesn't delete history — it creates a <em>new</em> revision whose content matches the target revision, so the trail stays linear and auditable.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Override a chart value per environment. What is the precedence?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use a <code>-f</code> values file or <code>--set</code> on the command line. Precedence: <code>--set</code> &gt; <code>-f</code> values file &gt; chart defaults (<code>values.yaml</code>); later <code>-f</code> files override earlier ones.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Wins over</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>--set replicas=4</code></td><td>Everything else</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>-f prod.yaml</code> (2nd file)</td><td><code>-f base.yaml</code> (1st file)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>values.yaml</code></td><td>Nothing — it's the floor</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Mnemonic: "set beats files, later files beat earlier files, files beat defaults." <code>helm get values easypj</code> shows what actually won.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Describe a Helm chart's anatomy.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>Chart.yaml</h5><p>Metadata: name/version/appVersion</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>values.yaml</h5><p>Default parameters</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>templates/</h5><p>Manifests with <code>{{ .Values }}</code> directives</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>_helpers.tpl + charts/</h5><p>Named templates + subchart dependencies</p></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>version</code> (chart) and <code>appVersion</code> (app) in <code>Chart.yaml</code> are independent — a chart bump doesn't require an app bump and vice versa.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Kustomize overlay that sets prod replicas and image tag.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml</code> with <code>resources: ["../../base"]</code>, <code>replicas: [{ name: easypj-web, count: 3 }]</code>, <code>images: [{ name: easypj, newTag: v2 }]</code>. Apply with <code>kubectl apply -k overlays/prod</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">resources: ["../../base"]
replicas:
  - name: easypj-web
    count: 3
images:
  - name: easypj
    newTag: v2</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>No templating occurred — Kustomize rewrote the <em>existing</em> base fields in place during the build, using the <code>replicas</code>/<code>images</code> transformers.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a strategic-merge patch?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A partial manifest merged field-by-field into the matching base resource (e.g. set env or resources on a container) — the default Kustomize patch style. You write only the fields you want to change.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Strategic merge — partial in, full out</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   base Deployment (full)     +     patch (partial: just env)     =    merged result
   spec.replicas: 2                 spec.template.spec.containers      spec.replicas: 2 (untouched)
   containers: [web]                  [{name: web, env: [DEBUG=False]}] containers: [web + new env]
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kustomize matches the patch to the base resource by <code>kind</code>+<code>metadata.name</code>, then deep-merges — fields you don't mention are left exactly as the base defined them.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>configMapGenerator</code> do?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Generates a ConfigMap from literals/files and appends a content hash to its name, so changing the config produces a new name and triggers a rolling update automatically.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Without generator</th><th>With <code>configMapGenerator</code></th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Edit ConfigMap in place — same name</td><td>New hashed name on every content change</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Running pods keep the OLD value (no restart trigger)</td><td>Deployment's podTemplate reference changes → rollout happens</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubernetes doesn't restart pods just because a mounted ConfigMap changed — the hash-suffix trick forces a podTemplate change, which the Deployment controller <em>does</em> react to.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Set replicas differently for dev vs prod without templating.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use Kustomize overlays — the base defines the Deployment, and each overlay patches replicas (and image/env) for its environment.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">two overlays, one base</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># overlays/dev/kustomization.yaml</span>
resources: ["../../base"]
replicas: [{ name: easypj-web, count: 1 }]

<span class="comment"># overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml</span>
resources: ["../../base"]
replicas: [{ name: easypj-web, count: 3 }]</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The base file itself never changes — each overlay only expresses its <em>delta</em> from the base, keeping environments readable and diffable in Git.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Preview what Helm/Kustomize will apply.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>helm template ./chart -f prod.yaml</code> or <code>kubectl kustomize overlays/prod</code> — renders the final YAML without applying it.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">preview, then apply</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">helm template ./chart -f prod.yaml | less
kubectl kustomize overlays/prod | less
<span class="comment"># satisfied? now actually apply:</span>
kubectl apply -k overlays/prod</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Neither preview command contacts etcd for a write — they're pure client-side rendering, making them completely safe to run repeatedly while debugging.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Helm vs Kustomize — when each?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Helm for templated/versioned releases and 3rd-party charts; Kustomize for template-free overlays of your own manifests and GitOps workflows. They can compose via <code>helm template | kustomize</code>.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Reach for</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Install ingress-nginx / cert-manager</td><td>Helm</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Deploy easypj's own manifests to dev/staging/prod</td><td>Kustomize</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Need one-command rollback</td><td>Helm</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Argo CD / Flux GitOps pipeline</td><td>Kustomize</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Neither is "better" — the CKA wants fluency in both and a reasoned choice, not dogma.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
