            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 37 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — 10 drills that mirror how the CKA actually asks: a broken state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You ran <code>helm upgrade easypj ./chart --set replicas=4</code> and it failed halfway through. <code>helm status easypj</code> now shows <code>pending-upgrade</code> and every further <code>helm upgrade</code> errors with "another operation is in progress." How do you recover?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Roll back to the last known-good revision to clear the stuck state, THEN retry the upgrade.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">clear a stuck release</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">helm history easypj                 <span class="comment"># find the last "deployed" revision, e.g. 2</span>
helm rollback easypj 2               <span class="comment"># clears pending-upgrade, restores rev 2</span>
helm status easypj                   <span class="comment"># should now read "deployed"</span>
helm upgrade easypj ./chart --set replicas=4   <span class="comment"># retry the original change</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Helm locks a release while an operation runs; a crash/timeout mid-upgrade can leave that lock (and a <code>pending-upgrade</code> status) behind. <code>helm rollback</code> to any completed revision clears it — there's no separate "unlock" command.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubectl apply -k overlays/prod</code> runs with no errors, but the Deployment still shows the base's 2 replicas and old image tag — the overlay seems to have no effect. What's the most likely cause, and how do you confirm it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The overlay's <code>kustomization.yaml</code> is missing (or misreferencing) the base under <code>resources:</code> — Kustomize built an empty/partial resource list, so your <code>replicas:</code>/<code>images:</code> transformers had nothing to patch, or you applied the wrong path entirely.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">confirm before touching anything</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># See EXACTLY what Kustomize would build — no guessing</span>
kubectl kustomize overlays/prod | grep -A2 replicas

<span class="comment"># If replicas/image look wrong here, the overlay's resources: is the bug</span>
cat overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>"Applied with no error" does not mean "applied what you expected" — Kustomize won't fail loudly on a broken <code>resources:</code> reference. Always <code>kubectl kustomize</code> preview first; this is exactly the recurring gotcha from §37.3.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You edited a plain <code>ConfigMap</code> (not generated by Kustomize) referenced by easypj's Deployment. The ConfigMap shows the new value with <code>kubectl get cm -o yaml</code>, but the running pods are still using the OLD value. Why, and what's the fix — both a one-off fix and a permanent one?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Kubernetes does not restart pods just because a referenced ConfigMap changed. One-off: force a rollout manually. Permanent: switch to a <code>configMapGenerator</code> so the name itself changes with the content, which DOES trigger a rollout automatically.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">one-off fix vs permanent fix</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># One-off: force a rollout now</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment/easypj-web -n easypj</code></pre>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Permanent fix — overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml</span>
configMapGenerator:
  - name: easypj-config
    literals:
      - DJANGO_DEBUG=False</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the exact "I edited the ConfigMap but the pods kept the old value" trap from §37.4 — the generator's content-hash suffix is the durable fix, not a one-time restart.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You run <code>helm upgrade easypj ./chart -f base.yaml -f prod.yaml -f hotfix.yaml --set replicas=5</code>. <code>prod.yaml</code> sets <code>replicas: 3</code> and <code>hotfix.yaml</code> sets <code>replicas: 10</code>. What replica count actually gets applied, and why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>5</strong> — the <code>--set</code> flag always wins over every <code>-f</code> file, regardless of file order.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Value</th><th>Wins?</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>base.yaml</code></td><td>(unset, or a default)</td><td>❌ lowest</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>prod.yaml</code></td><td>3</td><td>❌ beaten by hotfix.yaml</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>hotfix.yaml</code> (last -f)</td><td>10</td><td>❌ beaten by --set</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>--set replicas=5</code></td><td>5</td><td>✅ WINS</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>File order only matters <em>among</em> <code>-f</code> files (last one wins there); <code>--set</code> is a separate, always-higher tier. This exact trap is why teams standardize on either all-files or all-<code>--set</code>, not mixing casually under exam pressure.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You bump a third-party operator chart from v1.2.0 to v2.0.0. The new version's CRD adds a required <code>spec.backupSchedule</code> field, but creating a custom resource with that field fails with a schema validation error. What went wrong, and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Helm never upgrades CRDs shipped in a chart's <code>crds/</code> directory — the old (v1.2.0) CRD schema is still registered, so it rejects the new field as unknown. Apply the new CRD manually.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">manually update the CRD</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Extract the new chart version's CRD and apply it directly</span>
helm pull operator/operator-chart --version 2.0.0 --untar
kubectl apply -f operator-chart/crds/

<span class="comment"># NOW the upgrade's custom resources will validate</span>
helm upgrade my-operator operator/operator-chart --version 2.0.0</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the §37's UTH ⑤ CRD install-once caveat in the wild — a very common real-world (and CKA-style) trap when adopting operator charts.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A strategic-merge patch can't express what you need: replacing the <em>third</em> container in a Pod spec's <code>containers</code> list with a completely different one. Which patch type solves this, and what does it look like?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A JSON6902 patch — it targets exact list indices, which a strategic merge can't reliably do.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">patches:
  - target: { kind: Deployment, name: easypj-web }
    patch: |-
      - op: replace
        path: /spec/template/spec/containers/2
        value:
          name: sidecar-v2
          image: easypj-sidecar:v2</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>path: /spec/template/spec/containers/2</code> addresses the list by zero-based index — exactly the kind of surgical, unambiguous edit RFC-6902 exists for.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>helm install easypj ./chart -n easypj</code> fails with <code>cannot re-use a name that is still in use</code>. What's going on, and what are your two legitimate options?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A release named "easypj" already exists in that namespace — release names are unique per namespace. Either upgrade the existing release, or use a different release name / namespace.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">two legitimate fixes</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Option 1 — you meant to update it, not create it again</span>
helm upgrade --install easypj ./chart -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Option 2 — you genuinely want a second, independent instance</span>
helm install easypj-staging ./chart -n easypj-staging --create-namespace</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is exactly why <code>helm upgrade --install</code> is the recommended default in CI — it never hits this collision because it upgrades when the name already exists.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Your org mandates that every environment's final manifests go through a common Kustomize overlay (adding a company-wide label and annotation) — but easypj is packaged as a Helm chart by another team. How do you get Helm's chart through that mandatory Kustomize layer?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Compose the two tools: render the chart with <code>helm template</code>, feed the output into a Kustomize base as a resource (or pipe it through <code>kubectl kustomize</code>), and apply the combined result.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Helm renders, Kustomize finishes the job</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Render the chart to plain YAML</span>
helm template easypj ./chart -f prod.yaml &gt; rendered.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. Reference it as a resource in the org-wide overlay</span>
echo 'resources: [rendered.yaml]' &gt; kustomization.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Build (adds the company label/annotation) and apply</span>
kubectl apply -k .</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Composing lets a Helm chart supply the manifests while Kustomize applies final org-wide policy on top — the exact "COMPOSE both" pattern from §37.5.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's chart has a <code>pre-upgrade</code> hook Job annotated <code>"helm.sh/hook-delete-policy": hook-succeeded</code>. The FIRST upgrade's migration failed (a bad migration file), and now every subsequent <code>helm upgrade</code> errors with <code>Error: Job "easypj-migrate" already exists</code>. Why did the default cleanup not save you here, and what's the fix?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Helm's DEFAULT delete policy is already <code>before-hook-creation</code> — but this chart explicitly overrode it to <code>hook-succeeded</code>, which only deletes the previous hook resource if it completed SUCCESSFULLY. The first run FAILED, so the failed Job was never cleaned up and blocks every later attempt to create a Job of the same name (Jobs are immutable once created). Fix: delete the stuck Job once by hand, then change (or add back) the policy to <code>before-hook-creation</code> so a failure can never leave a lingering blocker again.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">one-time cleanup, then the durable fix</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># One-time: remove the stuck failed Job so the next upgrade can create it</span>
kubectl delete job easypj-migrate -n easypj</code></pre>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># templates/migrate-job.yaml — durable fix</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj-migrate
  annotations:
    "helm.sh/hook": pre-upgrade,pre-install
    "helm.sh/hook-weight": "0"
    "helm.sh/hook-delete-policy": before-hook-creation</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is UTH ③'s hook mechanics in practice: <code>before-hook-creation</code> cleans up regardless of whether the previous run succeeded or failed, which is exactly why it's the safer default — <code>hook-succeeded</code>/<code>hook-failed</code> are narrower, opt-in policies for when you deliberately want to inspect a failed hook's logs before Helm removes it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A separate monitoring tool references easypj's ConfigMap by the FIXED name <code>easypj-config</code> in its own scrape config — but your <code>configMapGenerator</code> keeps producing hash-suffixed names like <code>easypj-config-8f24bc6b2</code>, breaking that integration on every change. How do you resolve this, and what do you give up?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Set <code>generatorOptions: { disableNameSuffixHash: true }</code> to keep the name stable — but you give up the automatic rolling update on config change; you'd need <code>kubectl rollout restart</code> manually afterward.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kustomization.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">generatorOptions:
  disableNameSuffixHash: true
configMapGenerator:
  - name: easypj-config
    literals:
      - DJANGO_DEBUG=False</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is a real trade-off, not a free fix — a stable name is convenient for external references but reintroduces the "edited config, pods didn't notice" problem the hash suffix was solving in the first place.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

