                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>helm template</code> writes plain YAML you can treat as an ordinary Kustomize <code>resources:</code> entry; <code>commonLabels</code> is the transformer that stamps a label onto everything.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Helm renders, Kustomize labels</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Render the chart — plain YAML, no templates left</span>
helm template easypj ./chart -f prod.yaml &gt; rendered.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. A tiny overlay that just adds a common label</span>
cat &gt; kustomization.yaml &lt;&lt;'EOF'
resources:
  - rendered.yaml
commonLabels:
  team: platform
EOF

<span class="comment"># 3. Build (preview) then apply</span>
kubectl kustomize . | grep -A1 "team:"
kubectl apply -k .</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> every resource in the build output now carries <code>team: platform</code> — including ones the chart's own templates never mentioned that label at all. This is the "COMPOSE both" pattern from §37.5 done for real: Helm owns the app manifests, Kustomize owns the org-wide policy layer.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 37 Visual Summary — two routes from raw YAML to a running easypj</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
      raw YAML
         │
         ├──▶  Helm chart  ──(values + -f/--set)──▶  rendered manifests
         │       templates/{{.Values}}                     │
         │                                                  ▼
         │                                            RELEASE (revisioned,
         │                                            rollback-able)  ──┐
         │                                                              │
         └──▶  Kustomize base ──(overlay patches)──▶ rendered manifests │
                 deployment.yaml   replicas/images/env                  │
                                                          │             │
                                                          ▼             ▼
                                                   ┌──────────────────────┐
                                                   │     KUBERNETES        │
                                                   │       CLUSTER         │
                                                   └──────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Both tools turn raw YAML into what the cluster actually runs, by opposite routes. Helm <strong>TEMPLATES</strong> the manifests: you parameterize fields with <code>{{ .Values }}</code>, supply per-environment values via <code>-f</code> files and <code>--set</code> flags (precedence <code>--set</code> &gt; <code>-f</code> &gt; defaults), and each install/upgrade becomes a numbered, rollback-able RELEASE stored as Secrets.</p>
                    <p>Kustomize <strong>OVERLAYS</strong> the manifests: a template-free base is patched per environment (replicas, images, env) and applied with <code>kubectl apply -k</code>, with no templates and no release objects — just a deterministic build pipeline (load → generate → transform → patch → emit).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> use Helm for third-party charts and versioned releases, Kustomize for your own manifests and GitOps — and preview either with <code>helm template</code> or <code>kubectl kustomize</code> before it ever touches the cluster. Neither replaces the other; the strongest pipelines often compose them (<code>helm template | kustomize</code>).</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
