            <!-- ─────────── §38.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s38-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.1 Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs)</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>A <strong>Custom Resource Definition (CRD)</strong> lets you create entirely new resource types in Kubernetes. Once registered, your custom resources behave just like built-in ones — you can <code>kubectl get</code>, <code>describe</code>, <code>delete</code>, and <code>apply</code> them.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>💡 Why CRDs Matter</h5>
                    <p>Every popular Kubernetes tool uses CRDs: cert-manager creates <code>Certificate</code> resources, Prometheus Operator creates <code>ServiceMonitor</code> resources, ArgoCD creates <code>Application</code> resources. Understanding CRDs is understanding how the K8s ecosystem works.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>CRD vs Built-in Resources</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │                    Kubernetes API Server                  │
 ├──────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┤
 │   BUILT-IN RESOURCES │       CUSTOM RESOURCES (CRDs)     │
 │   ─────────────────  │       ───────────────────────     │
 │   Pod          v1    │       Certificate   cert-manager  │
 │   Deployment   apps  │       ServiceMonitor prometheus   │
 │   Service      v1    │       Application   argocd        │
 │   ConfigMap    v1    │       DjangoApp     easypj (ours!)│
 │   Secret       v1    │       VirtualService istio        │
 │                      │                                   │
 │   API Group: core,   │       API Group: custom defined   │
 │   apps, networking   │       e.g. apps.easypj.io/v1     │
 └──────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Creating a CRD — easypj DjangoApp Example</h4>
                <p>Let's create a custom <code>DjangoApp</code> resource type that describes a Django application deployment:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">djangoapp-crd.yaml — Custom Resource Definition</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
kind: CustomResourceDefinition
metadata:
  name: djangoapps.apps.easypj.io    <span class="comment"># Must match: plural.group</span>
spec:
  group: apps.easypj.io              <span class="comment"># API group for our custom resources</span>
  versions:
    - name: v1                        <span class="comment"># Version of our API</span>
      served: true                    <span class="comment"># Enable this version</span>
      storage: true                   <span class="comment"># Store objects in this version</span>
      schema:
        openAPIV3Schema:              <span class="comment"># Validation schema (required!)</span>
          type: object
          properties:
            spec:
              type: object
              required: ["image", "replicas"]
              properties:
                image:
                  type: string
                  description: "Container image for the Django app"
                replicas:
                  type: integer
                  minimum: 1
                  maximum: 20
                database:
                  type: object
                  properties:
                    engine:
                      type: string
                      enum: ["postgresql", "mysql", "sqlite3"]
                    host:
                      type: string
                port:
                  type: integer
                  default: 8000
            status:
              type: object
              properties:
                availableReplicas:
                  type: integer
                phase:
                  type: string
      subresources:
        status: {}                    <span class="comment"># Enable /status subresource</span>
      additionalPrinterColumns:       <span class="comment"># Custom columns for kubectl get</span>
        - name: Image
          type: string
          jsonPath: .spec.image
        - name: Replicas
          type: integer
          jsonPath: .spec.replicas
        - name: Phase
          type: string
          jsonPath: .status.phase
        - name: Age
          type: date
          jsonPath: .metadata.creationTimestamp
  scope: Namespaced                   <span class="comment"># Namespaced (vs Cluster)</span>
  names:
    plural: djangoapps                <span class="comment"># kubectl get djangoapps</span>
    singular: djangoapp               <span class="comment"># kubectl get djangoapp</span>
    kind: DjangoApp                   <span class="comment"># kind: DjangoApp in YAML</span>
    shortNames:
      - dja                           <span class="comment"># kubectl get dja</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Registering and Using the CRD</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — CRD lifecycle</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Register the CRD</span>
kubectl apply -f djangoapp-crd.yaml
<span class="comment"># customresourcedefinition.apiextensions.k8s.io/djangoapps.apps.easypj.io created</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify CRD is registered</span>
kubectl get crd djangoapps.apps.easypj.io
<span class="comment"># NAME                          CREATED AT</span>
<span class="comment"># djangoapps.apps.easypj.io     2026-04-02T10:00:00Z</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: The new resource type is now available!</span>
kubectl api-resources | grep djangoapp
<span class="comment"># djangoapps   dja   apps.easypj.io/v1   true   DjangoApp</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: kubectl explain works on CRDs too!</span>
kubectl explain djangoapp.spec
<span class="comment"># KIND:     DjangoApp</span>
<span class="comment"># VERSION:  apps.easypj.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># FIELD:    spec</span>
<span class="comment"># FIELDS:</span>
<span class="comment">#   database     Object   ...</span>
<span class="comment">#   image        string   Container image for the Django app</span>
<span class="comment">#   replicas     integer  ...</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Creating a Custom Resource Instance</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-djangoapp.yaml — Custom Resource instance</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps.easypj.io/v1
kind: DjangoApp
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
  replicas: 3
  database:
    engine: postgresql
    host: postgres-svc.easypj.svc.cluster.local
  port: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — managing custom resources</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create the custom resource</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-djangoapp.yaml

<span class="comment"># List custom resources (short name works!)</span>
kubectl get dja -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME         IMAGE                  REPLICAS   PHASE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web   easypj/jobpost:v2.1    3                  5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Describe it — just like any built-in resource</span>
kubectl describe djangoapp easypj-web -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Edit it</span>
kubectl edit djangoapp easypj-web -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Delete it</span>
kubectl delete djangoapp easypj-web -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Delete the CRD (WARNING: deletes ALL custom resources of this type!)</span>
kubectl delete crd djangoapps.apps.easypj.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CRD Deletion Warning</h5>
                    <p>Deleting a CRD deletes <strong>ALL custom resources</strong> of that type across <strong>all namespaces</strong>. This is a cluster-wide destructive operation. Always back up custom resources before deleting a CRD: <code>kubectl get djangoapps --all-namespaces -o yaml > backup.yaml</code></p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>📛 Naming Rule #2 — Both the CRD and Its Instances Must Be Valid DNS Subdomain Names</h5>
                    <p>Beyond the <code>plural.group</code> rule for the CRD object's own <code>metadata.name</code>, every <strong>custom resource instance</strong> you create (like <code>easypj-web</code> above) also has its <code>metadata.name</code> validated as a DNS subdomain name — lowercase alphanumerics, <code>-</code>, and <code>.</code>, max 253 characters, same rule as every other Kubernetes object name. Nothing exotic here, but it's worth stating explicitly: an operator that tries to auto-generate resource names from arbitrary user input (e.g. a Slack channel name with spaces/underscores/uppercase) will get a rejected CRD instance unless it first sanitizes the name.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>When a CRD Is the Wrong Tool — ConfigMap vs Custom Resource</h4>
                <p>Not every new "thing you want to store" deserves a CRD. Kubernetes' own docs draw a specific line: if you're describing an existing, well-known configuration file format that a program in a Pod just reads (an <code>nginx.conf</code>, a <code>my.cnf</code>), a plain <strong>ConfigMap</strong> is the right call — a CRD is for when you want Kubernetes-native tooling (<code>kubectl get</code>, watches, automation, RBAC on the type itself) built around your own object.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>📄 Reach for a ConfigMap When</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>You're wrapping an existing, well-documented config file format (<code>nginx.conf</code>, <code>pom.xml</code>)</li>
                            <li>The whole config fits naturally as one key's value</li>
                            <li>The only consumer is a program inside a Pod reading a file/env var</li>
                            <li>A rolling update via the owning Deployment is all the "automation" you need</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>🗂️ Reach for a Custom Resource When</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>You want <code>kubectl get/describe &lt;kind&gt;</code> and client-library support out of the box</li>
                            <li>Something must WATCH for changes and react (automation, an operator)</li>
                            <li>The object should follow K8s conventions — <code>.spec</code>/<code>.status</code>/<code>.metadata</code>, RBAC on the type</li>
                            <li>It abstracts a whole collection of other resources (a DjangoApp owning a Deployment+Service+ConfigMap)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Anti-Pattern — Don't Store Application Data in a Custom Resource</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes' own extension-model guidance is explicit on this: custom resources are for <strong>declarative configuration objects</strong> — not for your application's end-user data, uploaded files, or high-volume monitoring/metrics data. A design that leans on the K8s API (and etcd) as your app's database indicates too-tight coupling to the cluster; it also inherits etcd's size/throughput limits (etcd is not built for high-write-rate or multi-MB objects), and it means your app can't run/test/restore without a live Kubernetes API. Keep <strong>DjangoApp</strong> describing "what should exist and how it's configured" — job postings, applicants, and uploaded resumes stay in PostgreSQL, exactly where they already live, never in a custom resource.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §38.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s38-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.2 The Operator Pattern</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <p>An <strong>Operator</strong> = CRD + Custom Controller. The CRD defines <em>what</em> your custom resource looks like, and the controller watches for those resources and <em>takes action</em> to make reality match the desired state.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Operator Pattern — How It Works</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │                     THE OPERATOR PATTERN                     │
 ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
 │                                                              │
 │  YOU create:        DjangoApp YAML                          │
 │       │              (desired state)                         │
 │       ▼                                                      │
 │  ┌──────────┐     ┌────────────────────┐                    │
 │  │ API      │────▶│ Controller (Watch)  │                    │
 │  │ Server   │     │                    │                    │
 │  │ (stores  │     │ "DjangoApp created" │                    │
 │  │  CRD)    │     │  → Create Deployment│                    │
 │  └──────────┘     │  → Create Service   │                    │
 │                   │  → Create ConfigMap  │                    │
 │                   │  → Create Ingress    │                    │
 │                   │  → Run migrations    │                    │
 │                   │  → Update status     │                    │
 │                   └────────────────────┘                    │
 │                           │                                  │
 │                           ▼                                  │
 │             ┌─────────────────────────┐                     │
 │             │    RECONCILIATION LOOP   │                     │
 │             │  Watch → Compare → Act   │                     │
 │             │  (desired vs actual)     │                     │
 │             └─────────────────────────┘                     │
 └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Real-World Operators You Already Use</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>CRD It Creates</th><th>What It Automates</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>cert-manager</strong></td><td><code>Certificate</code>, <code>ClusterIssuer</code></td><td>TLS certificate provisioning and renewal via Let's Encrypt</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Prometheus Operator</strong></td><td><code>ServiceMonitor</code>, <code>PrometheusRule</code></td><td>Monitoring target discovery and alerting rules</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>PostgreSQL Operator</strong></td><td><code>PostgresCluster</code></td><td>Database provisioning, backups, failover, upgrades</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ArgoCD</strong></td><td><code>Application</code>, <code>AppProject</code></td><td>GitOps deployment sync and health monitoring</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Ingress NGINX</strong></td><td><code>IngressClass</code></td><td>HTTP routing based on Ingress resource specs</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — exploring Operators in your cluster</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List ALL CRDs in the cluster — see what Operators installed</span>
kubectl get crd
<span class="comment"># NAME                                      CREATED AT</span>
<span class="comment"># certificates.cert-manager.io              2026-01-01T00:00:00Z</span>
<span class="comment"># clusterissuers.cert-manager.io            2026-01-01T00:00:00Z</span>
<span class="comment"># applications.argoproj.io                  2026-01-15T00:00:00Z</span>
<span class="comment"># servicemonitors.monitoring.coreos.com     2026-02-01T00:00:00Z</span>

<span class="comment"># List custom resources for a specific CRD</span>
kubectl get certificates --all-namespaces
kubectl get servicemonitors --all-namespaces

<span class="comment"># Check which controller manages a CRD</span>
kubectl get deployment -n cert-manager
<span class="comment"># NAME                      READY   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># cert-manager              1/1     90d</span>
<span class="comment"># cert-manager-cainjector   1/1     90d</span>
<span class="comment"># cert-manager-webhook      1/1     90d</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>What Operators Actually Automate — the Full Capability List</h4>
                <p>The official Kubernetes definition of the pattern is broader than "creates a Deployment when a CR appears." An Operator exists to capture the knowledge a <strong>human operator</strong> would otherwise apply by hand — which covers a lot more than day-1 provisioning:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>What it looks like for easypj</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Deploy an application on demand</td><td>Create a <code>DjangoApp</code> → operator provisions Deployment/Service/ConfigMap</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Take and restore backups of application state</td><td>A <code>PostgresCluster</code> CR triggers scheduled <code>pg_dump</code> Jobs and a restore path</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Handle upgrades, including schema/config changes together</td><td>Bumping <code>spec.version</code> on a DjangoApp runs Django migrations <em>before</em> rolling the new image</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Publish a Service to applications that don't speak the K8s API</td><td>An external legacy billing system gets a stable endpoint the operator maintains on its behalf</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Simulate failure to test resilience (chaos engineering)</td><td>An operator that periodically kills a random easypj Pod to prove the Deployment self-heals</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Provide leader election for apps with no built-in mechanism</td><td>A legacy singleton batch job that was never written to coordinate itself gets one via the operator</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Operator vs Controller</h5>
                    <p>All Operators are controllers, but not all controllers are Operators. The built-in Deployment controller is a controller (it reconciles Deployments), but it's not called an "Operator" because it manages generic workloads. An Operator specifically manages a <strong>complex, stateful application</strong> with domain-specific knowledge (e.g., how to back up PostgreSQL, how to rotate TLS certificates).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📜 Where the Term Comes From</h5>
                    <p>"Operator" was coined by CoreOS in a 2016 blog post describing how they ran etcd and Prometheus on Kubernetes, and was later formalized in a joint CNCF Operator White Paper. It's not a Kubernetes API kind — there's no <code>kind: Operator</code> anywhere — it's a naming convention for "a CRD plus a controller that encodes operational knowledge," which is why you'll see it applied loosely (some tools call themselves an operator even though they're a fairly simple CRD+controller pair, others do genuinely sophisticated, stateful-application-aware automation).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔄 Operator Reconciliation Loop</h5>
                    <p>The controller continuously watches for changes and reconciles desired state with actual state — the same pattern Kubernetes uses internally for Deployments, Services, etc.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🍽️ Analogy — The CRD Is the Menu, the Operator Is the Chef</h5>
                    <p>The CRD is a new entry on a restaurant's menu — <code>"DjangoApp"</code>. Writing it on the menu changes nothing on its own; a customer can "order" one (create the custom resource) and the kitchen still does nothing until someone reads the ticket. The <strong>Operator is the chef</strong>: it reads the order (spec), checks what's already plated (actual cluster state), cooks the difference (creates/updates the Deployment/Service), and keeps the dish topped up if a diner takes a bite mid-meal (drift correction). WATCH → COMPARE → ACT → repeat, forever — a menu item with no chef is just print on paper, exactly like a CRD with no controller is just a row in etcd.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Watch</h5>
                        <p>Controller subscribes to CRD events via the API server — create, update, delete notifications for DjangoApp resources</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Compare</h5>
                        <p>Read the DjangoApp spec (desired: 3 replicas, v2.1 image) and check current cluster state (actual: 2 replicas, v2.0)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Act</h5>
                        <p>Create/update child resources — scale Deployment to 3, update image to v2.1, run database migrations</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Update Status</h5>
                        <p>Write back to DjangoApp <code>.status</code> — set phase to "Running", availableReplicas to 3</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Repeat</h5>
                        <p>Loop back to Watch — any drift (pod crash, manual change) triggers re-reconciliation automatically</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §38.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s38-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.3 Installing &amp; Configuring Operators</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Operators ship as a bundle: their CRDs, a controller Deployment, and the RBAC the controller needs to run. There are three common install paths — Helm, raw manifests, and the Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM). Whichever path you use, the <strong>order</strong> of operations is what the exam (and production) actually cares about.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Install path</th><th>Command pattern</th><th>When to use</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Helm</strong></td><td><code>helm repo add jetstack https://charts.jetstack.io</code><br><code>helm install cert-manager jetstack/cert-manager --set installCRDs=true -n cert-manager --create-namespace</code></td><td>Most common in production — versioned, upgradeable, configurable via <code>values.yaml</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Raw manifests</strong></td><td><code>kubectl apply -f https://.../cert-manager.yaml</code></td><td>One big YAML bundling CRDs + Deployment + RBAC — fast, but no built-in upgrade/rollback story</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>OLM</strong> (Operator Lifecycle Manager)</td><td>install OLM once, then apply a <code>Subscription</code> / <code>OperatorGroup</code></td><td>A <code>Subscription</code> pulls the operator from a catalog and manages its upgrades for you — the productionized install path, common on OpenShift and increasingly vanilla K8s</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>The Golden Install-and-Verify Sequence — Memorize the ORDER</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │  1. INSTALL the operator (its CRDs + controller Deployment)               │
 │     $ kubectl apply -f cert-manager.yaml                                  │
 │                            │                                              │
 │                            ▼                                              │
 │  2. VERIFY the CRDs landed — BEFORE creating any custom resource!         │
 │     $ kubectl get crd | grep cert-manager.io                             │
 │                            │                                              │
 │                            ▼                                              │
 │  3. CONFIRM the controller pod is Running                                 │
 │     $ kubectl -n cert-manager get pods                                    │
 │                            │                                              │
 │                            ▼                                              │
 │  4. NOW create custom resources to configure it                           │
 │     $ kubectl apply -f my-clusterissuer.yaml                              │
 └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — install cert-manager, then configure it</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. install the operator (its CRDs + controller)</span>
kubectl apply -f cert-manager.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. VERIFY the CRDs landed BEFORE you create any custom resource</span>
kubectl get crd | grep cert-manager.io
<span class="comment"># certificates.cert-manager.io</span>
<span class="comment"># certificaterequests.cert-manager.io</span>
<span class="comment"># clusterissuers.cert-manager.io</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. confirm the controller is running</span>
kubectl -n cert-manager get pods
<span class="comment"># cert-manager-7d...           1/1     Running</span>
<span class="comment"># cert-manager-cainjector-...  1/1     Running</span>
<span class="comment"># cert-manager-webhook-...     1/1     Running</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. NOW create custom resources to configure it (for easypj's TLS)</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-clusterissuer.yaml</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Creating a Custom Resource Before Its CRD Exists</h5>
                    <p>If you create a custom resource before its CRD is registered, the apply fails with:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">error: unable to recognize "my-clusterissuer.yaml": no matches for kind "ClusterIssuer" in version "cert-manager.io/v1"</code></pre>
                    <p>Always verify the CRDs first (<code>kubectl get crd | grep &lt;operator&gt;</code>) — this is the single most common install-order mistake on real clusters and in exam-style tasks alike.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §38.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s38-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.4 API Aggregation Layer</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <p>The <strong>API Aggregation Layer</strong> allows you to extend the Kubernetes API by registering additional API servers. Unlike CRDs (which store data in etcd via the main API server), aggregated APIs run their own backend — useful for resources that need custom storage or complex logic.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>CRD vs API Aggregation — When to Use Each</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌──────────────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────────┐
 │     CRD APPROACH          │    │   API AGGREGATION         │
 ├──────────────────────────┤    ├──────────────────────────┤
 │ ✅ Simple to create       │    │ ✅ Custom storage backend │
 │ ✅ No extra server needed │    │ ✅ Custom validation logic│
 │ ✅ Stored in etcd         │    │ ✅ Protocol translation   │
 │ ❌ Limited validation     │    │ ❌ Complex to build       │
 │ ❌ No custom storage      │    │ ❌ Requires running server│
 │                          │    │                          │
 │ USE FOR:                  │    │ USE FOR:                  │
 │ Most custom resources     │    │ metrics-server            │
 │ App configuration         │    │ Custom metrics API        │
 │ Operator CRDs             │    │ Complex sub-resources     │
 └──────────────────────────┘    └──────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p>The most common example you interact with daily is <strong>metrics-server</strong> — it registers an aggregated API at <code>/apis/metrics.k8s.io</code> so that <code>kubectl top pods</code> and <code>kubectl top nodes</code> work. It doesn't store data in etcd; it collects real-time metrics from kubelets.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>📋 Use CRDs When</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>You need simple CRUD operations on custom types</li>
                            <li>Data fits naturally in etcd (configs, specs, state)</li>
                            <li>You want easy setup with just a YAML file</li>
                            <li>Validation via OpenAPI schema is sufficient</li>
                            <li>Examples: cert-manager, ArgoCD, Prometheus Operator</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>🔌 Use API Aggregation When</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>You need a custom storage backend (not etcd)</li>
                            <li>Data is real-time/ephemeral (metrics, logs)</li>
                            <li>You need complex protocol translation</li>
                            <li>Sub-resources need custom behavior</li>
                            <li>Examples: metrics-server, custom metrics API</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — check registered API services</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all registered API services</span>
kubectl get apiservice | head -20
<span class="comment"># NAME                       SERVICE                      AVAILABLE</span>
<span class="comment"># v1.                        Local                        True</span>
<span class="comment"># v1.apps                    Local                        True</span>
<span class="comment"># v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io     kube-system/metrics-server   True  ← aggregated!</span>

<span class="comment"># "Local" = built-in API, handled by main API server</span>
<span class="comment"># A service reference = aggregated API, handled by separate server</span>

<span class="comment"># Check a specific aggregated API</span>
kubectl get apiservice v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io -o yaml
<span class="comment"># spec:</span>
<span class="comment">#   group: metrics.k8s.io</span>
<span class="comment">#   service:</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: metrics-server</span>
<span class="comment">#     namespace: kube-system</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Real Failure Mode — <code>kubectl top</code> Says "metrics not available yet"</h5>
                    <p>The single most common metrics-server failure isn't a CRD/RBAC problem — it's <strong>TLS</strong>. metrics-server verifies the kubelet's serving certificate by default; on kind/minikube/self-signed test clusters that certificate usually isn't signed by a CA the aggregation layer trusts, so the pod runs (<code>1/1 Running</code>) but its <code>APIService</code> flips to <code>Available: False</code> and every <code>kubectl top</code> fails with <code>error: metrics not available yet</code>. The fix for non-production clusters is adding <code>--kubelet-insecure-tls</code> to the metrics-server container args (never do this in production — it disables kubelet certificate verification cluster-wide). Diagnose with <code>kubectl get apiservice v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io -o yaml</code> and check the <code>status.conditions</code> message, plus <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system deploy/metrics-server</code> for the TLS handshake error.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §38.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s38-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.5 kubectl Plugins and krew</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>

                <p>kubectl supports a <strong>plugin mechanism</strong> — any executable named <code>kubectl-PLUGINNAME</code> in your PATH becomes a kubectl subcommand. <strong>krew</strong> is the plugin package manager (like apt/yum for kubectl).</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — kubectl plugins</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List installed plugins</span>
kubectl plugin list

<span class="comment"># Install krew (the plugin manager)</span>
<span class="comment"># See: https://krew.sigs.k8s.io/docs/user-guide/setup/install/</span>

<span class="comment"># Install useful plugins via krew</span>
kubectl krew install ctx       <span class="comment"># Fast context switching</span>
kubectl krew install ns        <span class="comment"># Fast namespace switching</span>
kubectl krew install neat      <span class="comment"># Clean up kubectl output (remove managed fields)</span>
kubectl krew install tree      <span class="comment"># Show resource ownership hierarchy</span>
kubectl krew install who-can   <span class="comment"># RBAC analysis — who can do what?</span>

<span class="comment"># Usage examples</span>
kubectl ctx production         <span class="comment"># Switch to production context</span>
kubectl ns easypj              <span class="comment"># Switch to easypj namespace</span>
kubectl neat get pod web-0 -o yaml   <span class="comment"># Clean YAML output</span>
kubectl tree deployment easypj-web   <span class="comment"># Show: Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods</span>
kubectl who-can create pods -n easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 CKA Note</h5>
                    <p>kubectl plugins (krew) are <strong>NOT available on the CKA exam</strong>. However, you should understand how the plugin mechanism works conceptually: any executable named <code>kubectl-&lt;name&gt;</code> in your PATH is automatically discovered as a plugin. This is relevant for understanding how organizations extend kubectl for their workflows.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §38.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s38-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.6 Admission Webhooks</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>After a request passes authentication and authorization, it goes through <strong>admission control</strong>. Kubernetes has two types of dynamic admission webhooks:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>API Request Flow — Where Webhooks Fit</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 kubectl apply ──▶ API Server
                      │
            ┌─────────┼─────────┐
            ▼         ▼         ▼
       Authentication  Authorization  Admission Control
       (Who are you?)  (Can you do    ┌─────────────────┐
                        this?)        │ 1. Mutating      │
                                     │    Webhooks       │
                                     │    (modify req)   │
                                     │                   │
                                     │ 2. Schema         │
                                     │    Validation     │
                                     │                   │
                                     │ 3. Validating     │
                                     │    Webhooks       │
                                     │    (accept/reject)│
                                     └─────────────────┘
                                            │
                                            ▼
                                       etcd (persist)
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>MutatingWebhookConfiguration</th><th>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>When it runs</strong></td><td>First — before validation</td><td>After mutation + schema validation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Can modify request?</strong></td><td>✅ Yes — can add/change/remove fields</td><td>❌ No — can only accept or reject</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Use case</strong></td><td>Inject sidecars, add labels, set defaults</td><td>Enforce policies, block insecure configs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Example</strong></td><td>Istio sidecar injection</td><td>Deny pods without resource limits</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Example: Validating Webhook — Require Labels on Pods</h4>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🎯 Admission Control — The API Server Gateway</h5>
                    <p>Every resource mutation passes through admission control. Mutating webhooks run first (can change the request), then validating webhooks (can only accept/reject). This is your last chance to enforce policies before data hits etcd.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">validating-webhook.yaml — require "team" label on all pods</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingWebhookConfiguration
metadata:
  name: require-team-label
webhooks:
  - name: require-team-label.easypj.io
    admissionReviewVersions: ["v1"]
    sideEffects: None
    clientConfig:
      service:
        name: webhook-server          <span class="comment"># Your webhook service</span>
        namespace: webhook-system
        path: /validate-pods
      caBundle: &lt;base64-encoded-CA&gt;   <span class="comment"># CA cert for TLS</span>
    rules:
      - apiGroups: [""]
        apiVersions: ["v1"]
        resources: ["pods"]
        operations: ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]
    namespaceSelector:                <span class="comment"># Only in labeled namespaces</span>
      matchLabels:
        webhook-enabled: "true"
    failurePolicy: Fail               <span class="comment"># Reject if webhook unreachable</span>
    timeoutSeconds: 5</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ failurePolicy: Fail vs Ignore</h5>
                    <p><strong>Fail</strong> (default) — if the webhook is down, ALL matching requests are rejected. Safe but can break your cluster if the webhook crashes. <strong>Ignore</strong> — if the webhook is down, requests pass through without validation. Less safe but prevents cluster lockout. For production, use <code>Fail</code> with high availability for the webhook server.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Production Failure Mode — The Self-Deadlocking Webhook</h5>
                    <p>A <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code>/<code>MutatingWebhookConfiguration</code> with no <code>namespaceSelector</code> matches <strong>every</strong> namespace — including <code>kube-system</code> and the webhook's own namespace. If the webhook Pod ever crashes and needs to be recreated (new node, OOM-kill, rolling restart) and <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> is set, the very act of the ReplicaSet trying to create the replacement webhook Pod can be rejected by... the now-dead webhook it's trying to replace, because <code>CREATE</code> on that Pod matches its own rules. The cluster locks itself out of fixing the problem. <strong>Always exclude</strong> <code>kube-system</code> and the webhook's own namespace with a <code>namespaceSelector</code> using the <code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name</code> label's <code>NotIn</code> operator, e.g.:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">namespaceSelector:
  matchExpressions:
    - key: kubernetes.io/metadata.name
      operator: NotIn
      values: ["kube-system", "webhook-system"]</code></pre>
                    <p>This is the exact reason cert-manager, Istio, and most production admission webhooks ship this exclusion by default — without it, one bad deploy of the webhook itself can require a manual <code>kubectl delete validatingwebhookconfiguration</code> from an admin with cluster-admin access just to unblock the API server.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>💡 <code>sideEffects</code> and <code>--dry-run=server</code></h5>
                    <p>The CKA (and real CI pipelines) lean heavily on <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server</code> to validate manifests without actually persisting them. Admission webhooks run during dry-run too — unless <code>sideEffects</code> tells the API server it's safe to skip them. Set <code>sideEffects: None</code> only if the webhook makes zero external calls (no Slack notification, no ticket creation, no billing side-effect); if the webhook has effects that must be skipped specifically during a dry run but are fine for real requests, use <code>sideEffects: NoneOnDryRun</code> instead. Getting this wrong means <code>--dry-run=server</code> either silently skips validation it shouldn't, or triggers a real side-effect (like paging someone) for a request that was never actually going to happen.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §38.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s38-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.7 ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL-Based)</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p><strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> (GA in Kubernetes v1.30) lets you write validation rules using <strong>Common Expression Language (CEL)</strong> — no external webhook server needed! This is the future of in-cluster policy enforcement.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">require-replicas-policy.yaml — No single-replica deployments</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Define the policy</span>
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-minimum-replicas
spec:
  failurePolicy: Fail
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
      - apiGroups: ["apps"]
        apiVersions: ["v1"]
        operations: ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]
        resources: ["deployments"]
  validations:
    - expression: "object.spec.replicas >= 2"
      message: "Deployments must have at least 2 replicas for high availability"
    - expression: "has(object.spec.template.spec.containers) && object.spec.template.spec.containers.all(c, has(c.resources) && has(c.resources.limits))"
      message: "All containers must have resource limits defined"
---
<span class="comment"># Step 2: Bind the policy to namespaces</span>
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: require-minimum-replicas-binding
spec:
  policyName: require-minimum-replicas
  validationActions: [Deny]          <span class="comment"># Deny, Warn, or Audit</span>
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        environment: production       <span class="comment"># Only enforce in production</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — testing the policy</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Label namespace for policy enforcement</span>
kubectl label namespace easypj environment=production

<span class="comment"># Apply the policy</span>
kubectl apply -f require-replicas-policy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Test — this should be DENIED</span>
kubectl create deployment test --image=nginx --replicas=1 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Error: Deployments must have at least 2 replicas for high availability</span>

<span class="comment"># This should SUCCEED</span>
kubectl create deployment test --image=nginx --replicas=3 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/test created</span>

<span class="comment"># List policies</span>
kubectl get validatingadmissionpolicies
kubectl get validatingadmissionpolicybindings</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CEL Expression Cheat Sheet</h5>
                    <p><code>object</code> = the resource being created/updated. <code>oldObject</code> = the previous version (for UPDATE). <code>has(object.spec.field)</code> = check if field exists. <code>object.spec.containers.all(c, expr)</code> = check all items. <code>object.metadata.labels.exists(k, k == "team")</code> = check labels.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §38.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s38-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">38.8 RBAC for Custom Resources</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Custom resources follow the same RBAC model as built-in resources. You need to grant access to the CRD's API group and resource name.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">crd-rbac.yaml — RBAC for DjangoApp CRD</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Role for managing DjangoApp custom resources</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: djangoapp-manager
  namespace: easypj
rules:
  - apiGroups: ["apps.easypj.io"]     <span class="comment"># CRD API group</span>
    resources: ["djangoapps"]          <span class="comment"># CRD plural name</span>
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "create", "update", "patch", "delete"]
  - apiGroups: ["apps.easypj.io"]
    resources: ["djangoapps/status"]   <span class="comment"># Status subresource</span>
    verbs: ["get", "update", "patch"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: djangoapp-manager-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: easypj-operator
    namespace: easypj
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: djangoapp-manager
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔤 One Resource, Two Spellings — YAML Plural vs CLI <code>plural.group</code></h5>
                    <p>Notice the RBAC rule above writes <code>resources: ["djangoapps"]</code> — the <strong>plain plural</strong>, because <code>apiGroups: ["apps.easypj.io"]</code> already disambiguates it in the same rule. On the <strong>command line</strong>, though, there's no separate <code>apiGroups</code> field to disambiguate with, so you spell the resource as <code>&lt;plural&gt;.&lt;group&gt;</code> instead — e.g. <code>kubectl auth can-i create djangoapps.apps.easypj.io</code> or <code>kubectl create clusterrole eapp-admin --resource=djangoapps.apps.easypj.io</code>. Mixing these up (typing the dotted form inside a YAML <code>resources:</code> list, or the bare plural on the CLI when two CRDs across different groups share the same plural name) is a common source of "why does this Role/can-i check not match what I expect" confusion.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-verify">
                    <h4>Verify RBAC for Custom Resources</h4>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check if a service account can access CRDs</span>
kubectl auth can-i get djangoapps --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-operator -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete djangoapps --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-operator -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

<span class="comment"># Note: to CREATE CRDs themselves (not instances), you need cluster-level access:</span>
kubectl auth can-i create customresourcedefinitions --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-operator
<span class="comment"># no  ← only cluster admins should create/delete CRDs</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 38 — How Kubernetes API Extensions Work ═══ -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch38-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Kubernetes API Extensions Actually Work: CRD Schema Validation, Operator Reconciliation Loop, Admission Webhook Flow, and API Aggregation Architecture</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ One API Server, Four Extension Points — Where Each Mechanism Actually Plugs In</h5>
            <p>§38.1–38.8 each introduced one extension mechanism in isolation. Under the hood they all plug into the exact same request pipeline at different points — a CRD teaches the apiserver a new noun, an Operator's controller watches that noun over the same watch/informer machinery every built-in controller uses, and webhooks/ValidatingAdmissionPolicy sit directly in the request path before anything reaches etcd. Seeing all four on one timeline is what makes troubleshooting "why didn't my custom resource do what I expected" tractable instead of mysterious.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ONE REQUEST, FOUR EXTENSION POINTS                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  kubectl apply -f my-djangoapp.yaml                                          │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  (A) REST PATH EXISTS? ── registered once, when the CRD (§38.1) was applied  │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  (C) ADMISSION CHAIN — mutating webhook → schema validation → validating     │
│     webhook / ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (§38.6–38.7) → can edit/reject here  │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  PERSIST to etcd under the CRD's registered key prefix                       │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                    │
│  (B) OPERATOR'S CONTROLLER (§38.2) wakes on the watch stream, reconciles     │
│     desired vs actual, writes real Deployments/Services, updates .status     │
│                                                                              │
│  (D) API AGGREGATION (§38.4) is the alternate universe: no etcd row at all — │
│   the main apiserver just proxies to a separate apiserver it registered      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <h5>① CRD Internals — How Custom Resources Become Part of the API</h5>
            <p>A CRD is not just a YAML file — it registers a new resource type in the Kubernetes API, creates an etcd key prefix, and enables standard kubectl operations on custom objects.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CRD LIFECYCLE — Registration to Usable Resource                              │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: CREATE CRD                                                           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl apply -f djangoapp-crd.yaml                                 │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1                                   │   │
│  │ kind: CustomResourceDefinition                                         │   │
│  │ metadata:                                                              │   │
│  │   name: djangoapps.apps.easypj.io  <span class="comment">← Must be: &lt;plural&gt;.&lt;group&gt;        </span>│   │
│  │ spec:                                                                  │   │
│  │   group: apps.easypj.io                                                │   │
│  │   versions:                                                            │   │
│  │   - name: v1                                                           │   │
│  │     served: true                                                       │   │
│  │     storage: true                                                      │   │
│  │     schema:                                                            │   │
│  │       openAPIV3Schema:  <span class="comment">← Defines structure + validation              </span>│   │
│  │         type: object                                                   │   │
│  │         properties:                                                    │   │
│  │           spec:                                                        │   │
│  │             type: object                                               │   │
│  │             properties:                                                │   │
│  │               replicas: {type: integer, minimum: 1}                   │   │
│  │               image: {type: string}                                    │   │
│  │   scope: Namespaced  <span class="comment">← Namespaced or Cluster                           </span>│   │
│  │   names:                                                               │   │
│  │     plural: djangoapps                                                 │   │
│  │     singular: djangoapp                                                │   │
│  │     kind: DjangoApp                                                    │   │
│  │     shortNames: [dja]                                                  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: API SERVER REGISTERS THE CRD                                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. API Server detects new CRD in etcd                                 │   │
│  │ 2. Registers new REST endpoint:                                       │   │
│  │    /apis/apps.easypj.io/v1/namespaces/{ns}/djangoapps                 │   │
│  │ 3. Creates etcd key prefix:                                            │   │
│  │    /registry/apps.easypj.io/djangoapps/                                │   │
│  │ 4. OpenAPI schema updated → kubectl explain DjangoApp works!          │   │
│  │ 5. kubectl api-resources now shows: djangoapps dja apps.easypj.io/v1 true│   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: CREATE CUSTOM RESOURCE INSTANCES                                    │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl apply -f my-djangoapp.yaml                                   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ apiVersion: apps.easypj.io/v1                                         │   │
│  │ kind: DjangoApp                                                        │   │
│  │ metadata:                                                              │   │
│  │   name: easypj-web                                                     │   │
│  │ spec:                                                                  │   │
│  │   replicas: 3                                                          │   │
│  │   image: ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1                                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ CRD instances are just like any other K8s resource:                   │   │
│  │ $ kubectl get djangoapps                                               │   │
│  │ $ kubectl describe djangoapp easypj-web                                │   │
│  │ $ kubectl edit djangoapp easypj-web                                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Registration step</th><th>What actually happens</th><th>How you'd observe it</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>1. CRD applied</td><td>apiserver writes the CRD object to etcd like any resource</td><td><code>kubectl get crd djangoapps.apps.easypj.io</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2. REST path mounted</td><td>New path <code>/apis/apps.easypj.io/v1/.../djangoapps</code> registered — NO apiserver restart</td><td><code>kubectl api-resources | grep djangoapp</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>3. etcd prefix created</td><td>Instances persist at <code>/registry/apps.easypj.io/djangoapps/&lt;ns&gt;/&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>(internal — not directly queryable via kubectl)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>4. OpenAPI schema live</td><td>Validation + docs available exactly like a built-in type</td><td><code>kubectl explain djangoapp.spec</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> CRD registration is DYNAMIC — no apiserver restart, no recompile. The moment <code>kubectl get crd</code> shows your CRD, every standard verb (<code>get/create/describe/edit/delete</code>) already works on its instances, because the apiserver treats a custom type exactly like a built-in one once it's registered.</div>

            <h5>② Operator Pattern — Custom Controller Reconciliation</h5>
            <p>An Operator = CRD + Custom Controller. The controller watches CRD instances and reconciles actual state with desired state — the same pattern used by built-in controllers:</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  OPERATOR RECONCILIATION LOOP                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  1. WATCH: DjangoApp CRD instances (via K8s API watch)                │   │
│  │     → djangoapp/easypj-web created                                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  2. RECONCILE:                                                         │   │
│  │     Desired (CRD spec):        Actual (cluster):                       │   │
│  │     ┌──────────────────┐       ┌──────────────────┐                   │   │
│  │     │ replicas: 3       │       │ No Deployment yet │                   │   │
│  │     │ image: easypj:v2.1│  vs   │ No Service yet    │                   │   │
│  │     │ env: [DB_HOST,...] │       │ No ConfigMap yet  │                   │   │
│  │     └──────────────────┘       └──────────────────┘                   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  3. ACT:                                                               │   │
│  │     → Create ConfigMap (from spec.env)                                 │   │
│  │     → Create Deployment (spec.replicas, spec.image)                    │   │
│  │     → Create Service (ClusterIP, port 8000)                            │   │
│  │     → Update CRD status: phase=Running, url=easypj-svc:8000           │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  4. LOOP: Watch for changes → reconcile again                          │   │
│  │     → User changes replicas: 3→5 → controller scales Deployment       │   │
│  │     → User updates image → controller updates Deployment → rollout    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  OPERATOR FRAMEWORKS (simplify controller development):                      │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ • Operator SDK (Go/Ansible/Helm) — Red Hat's framework                │   │
│  │ • Kubebuilder — Official k8s SIG project, Go-based                   │   │
│  │ • KUDO — Declarative operator building                                │   │
│  │ • Kopf — Python operator framework                                     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Framework</th><th>Language</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Operator SDK</td><td>Go / Ansible / Helm</td><td>Red Hat / OpenShift ecosystems, multiple skill levels</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Kubebuilder</td><td>Go</td><td>Official k8s SIG project — most idiomatic controller-runtime code</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>KUDO</td><td>Declarative YAML</td><td>Teams who want an operator with almost no code</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Kopf</td><td>Python</td><td>Python shops — fastest to prototype an easypj-style operator</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Every built-in Kubernetes controller — Deployment, Job, even kube-scheduler — is this exact same loop. An Operator is just YOU writing one of those loops for your own domain (a DjangoApp, a Backup, a database cluster) instead of Kubernetes' own built-ins.</div>

            <h5>③ Admission Webhooks — Mutating + Validating Request Interception</h5>
            <p>Webhooks let external services intercept and modify/validate API requests BEFORE they're persisted to etcd:</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ADMISSION WEBHOOK FLOW                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  kubectl create pod → API Server                                              │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  MUTATING WEBHOOKS (run FIRST, in order):                             │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │   │
│  │  │ 1. API Server sends AdmissionReview (JSON) to webhook URL     │    │   │
│  │  │ 2. Webhook modifies the object (e.g., inject sidecar,        │    │   │
│  │  │    add labels, set defaults)                                   │    │   │
│  │  │ 3. Webhook returns AdmissionResponse with JSON patch           │    │   │
│  │  │ 4. API Server applies the patch                                │    │   │
│  │  │                                                               │    │   │
│  │  │  Example: Istio injects sidecar container into every pod      │    │   │
│  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  VALIDATING WEBHOOKS (run AFTER mutating):                            │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │   │
│  │  │ 1. API Server sends AdmissionReview to webhook URL            │    │   │
│  │  │ 2. Webhook validates the object (policy checks)              │    │   │
│  │  │ 3. Returns: allowed: true OR allowed: false + message        │    │   │
│  │  │ 4. If false → API Server rejects the request (403 Forbidden) │    │   │
│  │  │                                                               │    │   │
│  │  │  Example: Require all pods to have resource limits           │    │   │
│  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│                          PERSIST TO etcd                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  WEBHOOK REQUIREMENTS:                                                       │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ • Must be accessible via HTTPS (TLS required)                         │   │
│  │ • Runs as a Service inside the cluster                                │   │
│  │ • Requires caBundle in webhook config (CA cert for TLS validation)    │   │
│  │ • Timeout: default 10s (webhook must respond in time)                 │   │
│  │ • Failure policy: Ignore (allow request) or Fail (reject request)     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Config field</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Gotcha</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>admissionReviewVersions</code></td><td>Which AdmissionReview API version the webhook speaks</td><td>Must match what your webhook server implements or requests are rejected</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>sideEffects</code></td><td>Whether the webhook has side effects (for dry-run compat)</td><td>Set <code>None</code> unless you truly mutate external state</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>failurePolicy</code></td><td>Fail (reject) or Ignore (allow) when the webhook is unreachable</td><td>Fail + a crashed webhook = cluster-wide lockout on matching resources</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>timeoutSeconds</code></td><td>How long the apiserver waits for a response</td><td>Default 10s; every matching request pays this latency on a slow webhook</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>matchPolicy</code></td><td><code>Equivalent</code> (default) matches a resource regardless of which API version the client requested; <code>Exact</code> matches only the literal <code>apiVersions</code> listed</td><td>A webhook rule listing only <code>apps/v1</code> with <code>matchPolicy: Exact</code> silently misses a Deployment created via an older/aliased API version — <code>Equivalent</code> avoids that trap and is why it's the default</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>reinvocationPolicy</code></td><td><em>Mutating webhooks only.</em> <code>Never</code> (default) or <code>IfNeeded</code> — whether this webhook is called AGAIN if a later mutating webhook changes the object after it already ran</td><td>With two mutating webhooks that both touch overlapping fields, <code>Never</code> means the first webhook's view can be stale by the time the second one finishes — set <code>IfNeeded</code> if your webhook's mutation depends on fields another webhook might also change</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> While CRDs and Operators are not heavily tested on the CKA, understanding the extension model helps with troubleshooting. Key points: (1) CRDs create new API endpoints — <code>kubectl api-resources</code> shows them, (2) CRD schema validation is OpenAPI v3 based, (3) Admission webhooks intercept ALL matching requests — a broken webhook with failurePolicy=Fail can BLOCK all pod creation, (4) Always check webhook configurations if pods suddenly can't be created: <code>kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations,mutatingwebhookconfigurations</code>.
            </div>

            <h5>④ Finalizers — Why a Custom Resource Refuses to Die</h5>
            <p>Deleting a custom resource doesn't always delete it immediately. A string in <code>metadata.finalizers</code> tells the apiserver "don't actually remove this object until the owner clears me" — the object gets a <code>deletionTimestamp</code> and shows as <code>Terminating</code>, but stays in etcd until every finalizer is gone.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  FINALIZER-BLOCKED DELETION                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  $ kubectl delete djangoapp easypj-web -n easypj                              │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│  apiserver sets metadata.deletionTimestamp = now()                           │
│  object STAYS in etcd — status shows "Terminating"                           │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│  Operator's controller sees deletionTimestamp is set:                        │
│    → runs CLEANUP: deregister external DB, release cloud LB, etc.            │
│    → once cleanup succeeds, REMOVES its finalizer from the list              │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│  metadata.finalizers is now EMPTY → apiserver finally deletes the object     │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️ If the operator crashes/is deleted BEFORE removing its finalizer:         │
│     the DjangoApp is stuck in Terminating FOREVER — patch the finalizer      │
│     out manually only as a LAST RESORT (may leak external resources)         │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>CR stuck <code>Terminating</code> for hours</td><td>Operator crashed/was deleted before finishing cleanup</td><td>Restart/reinstall the operator so it can finish and remove its finalizer</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>CR stuck even with operator healthy</td><td>Cleanup target (external DB, cloud LB) is unreachable</td><td>Check operator logs; fix the external dependency first</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Need it gone NOW (non-prod only)</td><td>—</td><td><code>kubectl patch &lt;kind&gt; &lt;name&gt; --type=json -p='[{"op":"remove","path":"/metadata/finalizers"}]'</code> — last resort, may leak resources</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> A finalizer is a promise, not a lock — it exists so the operator gets a guaranteed LAST CHANCE to clean up before the object disappears. Stripping it manually breaks that promise; always try to fix/restart the operator first.</div>

            <h5>⑤ CRD Versioning, Conversion Webhooks &amp; Production-Grade Schemas</h5>
            <p>Real CRDs evolve. A field gets renamed, a type gets stricter, a new version ships — and old clients need to keep working. Kubernetes solves this with multiple served versions plus a few schema-authoring features that make CRDs safe in production. One prerequisite underlies all of it: every version's <code>openAPIV3Schema</code> must be a <strong>structural schema</strong> — every field's <code>type</code> must be specified (not left to be inferred), and the schema must not mix <code>type</code> with unrestricted <code>additionalProperties</code>/logic keywords in ways the apiserver can't statically reason about. A non-structural schema silently disables pruning, defaulting, <code>/status</code> and <code>/scale</code> subresources, and CEL validation — a structural schema became <strong>mandatory</strong> the moment a CRD uses <code>apiextensions.k8s.io/v1</code> (GA since v1.16; under the older, now-removed <code>v1beta1</code> API it was only optional). Since <code>v1beta1</code> CRDs stopped being served entirely as of v1.22, every CRD you can create on a modern cluster is structural by construction — this mostly bites people copy-pasting very old CRD examples from pre-2019 blog posts that still show <code>apiextensions.k8s.io/v1beta1</code>.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  MULTI-VERSION CRD + CONVERSION WEBHOOK                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  spec.versions:                                                              │
│    - name: v1alpha1   served: true    storage: false   (old clients)         │
│    - name: v1         served: true    storage: true    (current — ONE only)  │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│  Old client requests v1alpha1 object ──► apiserver calls the CONVERSION      │
│  webhook ──► translates v1 (stored) ⇄ v1alpha1 (requested) on the fly        │
│                                                                              │
│  PRODUCTION SCHEMA HARDENING (all live under openAPIV3Schema):               │
│  • x-kubernetes-preserve-unknown-fields — allow pass-through extra fields     │
│  • x-kubernetes-int-or-string           — accept int OR string (like ports)  │
│  • x-kubernetes-validations (CEL)       — cross-field rules schema can't do  │
│  • additionalPrinterColumns             — custom `kubectl get` columns       │
│  • subresources: status / scale         — split ownership, enable HPA        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Problem it solves</th><th>easypj example</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Conversion webhook</td><td>Old and new API clients must coexist while you migrate schemas</td><td>Old CI scripts still POST <code>v1alpha1</code> DjangoApps while new ones use <code>v1</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>x-kubernetes-validations</code> (CEL)</td><td>Basic OpenAPI types can't express cross-field rules</td><td>"if <code>spec.database.engine == postgresql</code> then <code>spec.database.host</code> is required"</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>additionalPrinterColumns</code></td><td>Default <code>kubectl get</code> output is just NAME/AGE</td><td>Show REPLICAS, PHASE, IMAGE as real columns (already used in §38.1)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>/status</code> and <code>/scale</code> subresources</td><td>Prevent spec/status write races; let HPA and <code>kubectl scale</code> work</td><td>Operator updates <code>.status.phase</code> without racing a user's <code>spec.replicas</code> edit</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>OLM (Operator Lifecycle Manager)</td><td>Manual CRD/controller upgrades don't scale across many operators</td><td>A <code>Subscription</code> to a catalog auto-upgrades cert-manager/ArgoCD for you</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Storage version migration</td><td>Flipping <code>storage: true</code> from <code>v1alpha1</code> to <code>v1</code> does NOT rewrite existing objects — old ones stay serialized in etcd as <code>v1alpha1</code> until touched</td><td>An old <code>djangoapps</code> object created a year ago can silently fail conversion-webhook round-tripping until something writes to it again</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Production Gotcha — Changing the Storage Version Doesn't Rewrite Old Objects</h5>
                <p>When you promote a new version to <code>storage: true</code>, Kubernetes does <strong>not</strong> retroactively re-encode objects already sitting in etcd — they stay stored in whatever version was <code>storage: true</code> at the time they were last written, and get transparently converted on read. This is invisible until (a) you eventually DROP the old version entirely — now-orphaned objects that were never rewritten can become unreadable, or (b) your conversion webhook has a bug that only shows up on the old version's exact field shape. The fix is a <strong>storage version migration</strong>: after flipping <code>storage: true</code>, do a no-op write to every existing object (<code>kubectl get &lt;kind&gt; -o yaml | kubectl replace -f -</code> per object, or the <code>StorageVersionMigrator</code> controller/CRD if installed) so etcd is fully re-encoded in the new version BEFORE you ever remove the old one from <code>spec.versions</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> "Exactly one version has <code>storage: true</code>" is the rule the exam loves to test — but production CRDs often SERVE several versions at once for backward compatibility, with a conversion webhook translating between them. If you see multiple <code>served: true</code> versions on a CRD, that's normal; multiple <code>storage: true</code> versions is what actually breaks the apply.</div>

            <h5>⑥ Informers, Workqueues &amp; the client-go Controller Machinery</h5>
            <p>"The controller watches and reconciles" hides a specific piece of engineering nearly every operator (and every built-in K8s controller) is built from: the <strong>client-go</strong> Informer/Workqueue pattern. This is the actual code shape Kubebuilder and Operator SDK generate for you — knowing it explains why operators are cheap on the API server, resilient to missed events, and never race each other on the same object.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CLIENT-GO MACHINERY — from "kubectl apply" to a reconcile() call             │
│                                                                              │
│  1. LIST + WATCH (once, at startup, then a long-lived stream)                 │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  Reflector: LIST all DjangoApp objects → seed a local cache            │   │
│  │             then WATCH for adds/updates/deletes (one TCP stream)        │   │
│  │             → NOT a poll loop — the apiserver PUSHES changes            │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│  2. DELTAFIFO → SHARED CACHE (Indexer)                                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  Every add/update/delete becomes a "Delta" in a FIFO queue             │   │
│  │  The Informer applies each Delta to a thread-safe in-memory Indexer    │   │
│  │  (a local mirror of every DjangoApp — reconcile NEVER calls the        │   │
│  │  apiserver just to "read current state", it reads the local cache)     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│  3. EVENT HANDLERS enqueue a KEY (not the object!) onto the WORKQUEUE          │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  OnAdd/OnUpdate/OnDelete → workqueue.Add("easypj/easypj-web")          │   │
│  │  Queue is DE-DUPED: 5 rapid edits to the same DjangoApp = 1 queue entry│   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                               │
│                               ▼                                               │
│  4. WORKER POOL pops a key, re-fetches the LATEST object from the Indexer,     │
│     and calls YOUR reconcile(namespace, name) function                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  reconcile() returns:                                                  │   │
│  │    nil            → success, item removed from queue                  │   │
│  │    error           → workqueue.AddRateLimited(key) → retry with        │   │
│  │                      EXPONENTIAL BACKOFF (5ms → 1000s cap, typical)     │   │
│  │  Every ~10h (resyncPeriod) the Informer also re-enqueues EVERY key —    │   │
│  │  a safety net that heals any silently missed watch event                │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Job</th><th>Why it matters for your operator</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Reflector</td><td>LIST once, then WATCH a long-lived stream from the apiserver</td><td>One TCP connection per watched type per controller — not a polling loop, so it scales to thousands of DjangoApps cheaply</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>DeltaFIFO + Indexer</td><td>Turns the watch stream into an ordered queue of changes applied to a local thread-safe cache</td><td>Your <code>reconcile()</code> reads the Indexer, never the apiserver directly — near-zero read load on etcd/apiserver even with a busy controller</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Workqueue</td><td>De-duplicated, rate-limited queue of object <em>keys</em> (namespace/name), not full objects</td><td>Explains why editing a DjangoApp 10× in one second only triggers one reconcile, and why a failing reconcile retries with backoff instead of hot-looping</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>resyncPeriod</td><td>Periodic full re-list of every cached key even with no real change</td><td>The reason a "self-healing" operator eventually corrects manual/out-of-band drift even if the watch event was somehow lost</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> This is why the reconcile loop is called <strong>level-triggered, not edge-triggered</strong>: <code>reconcile()</code> is handed a namespace/name key, not "what changed" — it always re-reads full current state (spec + cluster) and decides what to do from scratch. That's exactly why a missed event, a controller restart mid-reconcile, or a manual <code>kubectl edit</code> on a child Deployment all self-heal on the next pass — nothing depends on remembering history, only on comparing current desired vs current actual.</div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch38-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>A CRD registers a brand-new API type</strong> — group/version/kind + an OpenAPIv3 schema. Once applied, <code>kubectl get/create/describe/edit/delete &lt;kind&gt;</code> work exactly like a built-in resource, and instances persist in etcd.</li>
                    <li><strong>The naming rule is absolute</strong> — <code>metadata.name</code> MUST equal <code>&lt;plural&gt;.&lt;group&gt;</code> (e.g. <code>djangoapps.apps.easypj.io</code>), and exactly ONE version may set <code>storage: true</code> (multiple storage versions = invalid CRD).</li>
                    <li><strong>An Operator = CRD + Controller</strong> running the reconcile loop: WATCH the custom resources → COMPARE desired spec vs live cluster state → ACT (create/update Deployments/Services/…) → update <code>.status</code> → repeat, forever. This is level-triggered, not edge-triggered, which is why operators self-heal missed events.</li>
                    <li><strong>Real operators you already use</strong> — cert-manager, Prometheus Operator, the PostgreSQL Operator, and ArgoCD are all CRD+controller pairs; install via Helm/manifests/OLM, then <strong>verify the CRDs exist BEFORE creating custom resources</strong> or the apply fails with "no matches for kind".</li>
                    <li><strong>API Aggregation is the other extension path</strong> — used when you need a custom apiserver with its own storage or computed/real-time responses (metrics-server is the canonical example); CRDs cover ~95% of needs, aggregation is for the rest.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubectl plugins extend the CLI itself</strong> — any executable named <code>kubectl-&lt;name&gt;</code> on PATH becomes a subcommand; krew is the plugin package manager. Not exam content, but explains how teams customize kubectl.</li>
                    <li><strong>Admission webhooks intercept requests before etcd</strong> — mutating webhooks run first and can edit the object; validating webhooks run after and can only accept or reject. A webhook with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> that goes down can block ALL matching requests cluster-wide.</li>
                    <li><strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (GA in v1.30)</strong> enforces CEL-based policy in-process — no external webhook server, lower latency, one less moving part to keep highly available.</li>
                    <li><strong>Custom resources obey normal RBAC</strong> — grant verbs on the CRD's <code>apiGroup</code> + plural (and <code>/status</code>) or subjects get <code>Forbidden</code>; use <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> to prove it.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

