                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get crd
kubectl api-resources --api-group=apps.easypj.io</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl get crd</code> is cluster-scoped and lists every CRD regardless of namespace. <code>kubectl api-resources --api-group=&lt;group&gt;</code> is the faster way to see just the kinds (and short names) for one operator/group without scrolling past unrelated CRDs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain the Operator pattern end to end — what does "CRD + Controller" actually mean in terms of a running loop?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">The reconcile loop, in one line each</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  WATCH DjangoApp CRs  ──►  COMPARE spec (desired) vs cluster (actual)
        ▲                              │
        │                              ▼
   requeue/resync            ACT: create/update Deployment, Service, ...
        │                              │
        └──────────────────  UPDATE .status (observedGeneration, phase)
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A CRD alone is inert storage — creating a DjangoApp writes a row in etcd and nothing happens. The Operator's controller is the missing half: it WATCHes those resources, COMPARES desired spec to live cluster state, ACTs (creates/updates real objects), and writes status — level-triggered so a missed event just self-heals on the next resync.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

