                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">CRD vs API Aggregation — when would you use each, and why does metrics-server use aggregation instead of a CRD?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                            <div class="info-card">
                                <h5>📋 Use a CRD when</h5>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Declarative object K8s should store in etcd</li>
                                    <li>OpenAPI schema validation is enough</li>
                                    <li>Examples: DjangoApp, ClusterIssuer</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                            <div class="info-card">
                                <h5>🧩 Use API Aggregation when</h5>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>You need a custom apiserver with its own storage/logic</li>
                                    <li>Data is computed/real-time (no etcd row needed)</li>
                                    <li>Example: metrics-server (<code>metrics.k8s.io</code>)</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>metrics-server serves live CPU/memory pulled from kubelets on every request — storing that in etcd would be wasteful and immediately stale. Aggregation lets it run its OWN apiserver and have the main apiserver simply proxy <code>/apis/metrics.k8s.io/...</code> to it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code> with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> is blocking ALL pod creation because its webhook service crashed. Give the fastest safe mitigation and the proper fix.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># fastest mitigation — temporarily let requests through</span>
kubectl patch validatingwebhookconfiguration &lt;name&gt; \
  --type='json' -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/webhooks/0/failurePolicy","value":"Ignore"}]'

<span class="comment"># proper fix — restore the webhook service</span>
kubectl get pods -n webhook-system
kubectl logs -n webhook-system &lt;webhook-pod&gt;</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> is the safer DEFAULT, but it means an unreachable webhook rejects every matching request cluster-wide. Patch <code>failurePolicy</code> to <code>Ignore</code> only as a temporary unblock, then fix and restore the webhook server and flip it back to <code>Fail</code>.</p>
