                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A <code>DjangoApp</code> custom resource is stuck in <code>Terminating</code> and won't go away. Diagnose and fix it.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get djangoapp easypj-web -n easypj -o yaml
<span class="comment"># look under metadata.finalizers — a stuck finalizer blocks deletion</span>
<span class="comment"># fix/restart the operator so it finishes cleanup and removes the finalizer</span>
<span class="comment"># LAST RESORT ONLY — manually strip it:</span>
kubectl patch djangoapp easypj-web -n easypj --type=json \
  -p='[{"op":"remove","path":"/metadata/finalizers"}]'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A finalizer string in <code>metadata.finalizers</code> blocks deletion — the object gets a <code>deletionTimestamp</code> but is NOT removed until the operator finishes cleanup (drain external resources, deregister) and removes the finalizer itself. The correct fix is to repair/restart the operator, not to blindly strip the finalizer, which can leak the external resources the operator was supposed to clean up.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You just applied the <code>djangoapps.apps.easypj.io</code> CRD and immediately run <code>kubectl get djangoapp</code> — it returns <code>error: the server doesn't have a resource type "djangoapp"</code> (a "no matches for kind" style error). What are the three things you check, in order?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Command</th><th>What it rules out</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1. Did the CRD actually register?</td><td><code>kubectl get crd djangoapps.apps.easypj.io</code></td><td>Registration is near-instant but not synchronous with <code>apply</code> returning — a script racing straight to <code>get</code> can lose this race by milliseconds</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2. Is the short name/kind spelled right, and does it show up as an API resource?</td><td><code>kubectl api-resources --api-group=apps.easypj.io</code></td><td>A typo'd kind/plural, or querying a CRD that failed validation and never fully registered (check <code>kubectl describe crd</code> for a <code>NamesAccepted: False</code> condition)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3. Are you querying the right scope/namespace?</td><td><code>kubectl get djangoapp -A</code> (all namespaces) vs a Cluster-scoped CRD queried with <code>-n</code></td><td>A Namespaced CRD instance that lives in a different namespace than your current context, or a Cluster-scoped CRD queried as if it were namespaced</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>"No matches for kind" right after applying a CRD is almost always a <strong>timing or scope</strong> issue, not a broken cluster: either the apiserver hasn't finished mounting the new REST path yet (rare, usually sub-second), the kind/short-name/API-group was mistyped, or the query is looking in the wrong namespace/scope for how the CRD defined <code>spec.scope</code>. Confirm registration with <code>kubectl get crd</code> first — if that already shows the CRD, the problem is almost certainly scope or a typo, not a race.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

