            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 38.3 — Install-Order Drill: Don't Skip the Verify Step</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 5 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>You <code>kubectl apply -f cert-manager.yaml</code> and immediately <code>kubectl apply -f my-clusterissuer.yaml</code> in the same script, no pause in between. It fails. Explain why, and rewrite the script with the correct 4-step order.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> CRD registration is fast but not synchronous with the apply command returning.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f cert-manager.yaml
<span class="comment"># wait/verify BEFORE proceeding</span>
until kubectl get crd clusterissuers.cert-manager.io &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; do sleep 2; done
kubectl -n cert-manager rollout status deploy/cert-manager
kubectl apply -f my-clusterissuer.yaml</code></pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the failure is <code>error: no matches for kind "ClusterIssuer"</code> — the CRD from the same manifest bundle hasn't finished registering when the second apply races ahead. Real pipelines poll/wait for the CRD (or the controller's rollout status) before creating dependent custom resources — never assume "applied" means "usable" a millisecond later.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 38.4 — Prove (and Fix) a Forbidden on a Custom Resource</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>ServiceAccount <code>easypj-operator</code> gets <code>Forbidden</code> when it tries to update a <code>DjangoApp</code>'s status. Prove the RBAC gap with one command, then write the minimal fix.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the resource name for RBAC checks on the CLI uses the full <code>plural.group</code> form.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Prove the gap</span>
kubectl auth can-i update djangoapps.apps.easypj.io/status \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-operator -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no</span></code></pre>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># 2. Fix — add the missing status verbs</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: djangoapp-status-writer
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: ["apps.easypj.io"]
  resources: ["djangoapps/status"]
  verbs: ["get","update","patch"]</code></pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> re-run the same <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> command after binding the Role — it must now say <code>yes</code>. Forgetting to include <code>/status</code> as its own <code>resources</code> entry is the most common reason an otherwise-correct operator RBAC setup still gets <code>Forbidden</code> on status writes.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 38.5 — Choose the Right Extension Mechanism</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>For each requirement below, name the correct Chapter 38 mechanism (CRD, Operator, API Aggregation, Admission Webhook, or ValidatingAdmissionPolicy): (a) store a declarative "Backup schedule" object; (b) auto-provision a Postgres instance whenever a CR appears; (c) serve live GPU utilization with no etcd row; (d) inject a sidecar into every pod in a namespace; (e) reject Deployments missing resource limits, with zero extra pods to run.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> ask "does K8s store this?", "does something need to DO something?", "is this real-time/computed?", "does the request need to be CHANGED or just ACCEPT/REJECT?"
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>(a) declarative Backup schedule object</td><td>CRD</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(b) auto-provision Postgres from a CR</td><td>Operator (CRD + controller)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(c) live GPU utilization, no etcd row</td><td>API Aggregation Layer</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(d) inject a sidecar into every pod</td><td>Mutating Admission Webhook</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(e) reject missing resource limits, no extra pods</td><td>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL, in-process)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> (e) is the trap most people miss — a webhook would ALSO work, but it requires running and keeping HA a whole webhook server; ValidatingAdmissionPolicy does the same job with zero extra pods, which is why it's preferred whenever the rule is CEL-expressible.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 38 Visual Summary — teaching Kubernetes new nouns, brains &amp; guardrails</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   apply CRD          create CR              OPERATOR reconciles           ADMISSION governs
  ┌──────────┐      ┌─────────────┐        ┌────────────────────┐      ┌─────────────────────┐
  │ kind:    │      │ DjangoApp   │        │ WATCH DjangoApp     │      │ Mutating webhook     │
  │ Django-  │ ───► │ replicas: 3 │ ─────► │   ▼                │ ───► │  (inject/default)    │
  │ App CRD  │      │ image: v2.1 │  etcd  │ COMPARE spec/cluster│      │ Schema validation     │
  └──────────┘      └─────────────┘        │   ▼                │      │ Validating webhook /  │
   new API type      stored object         │ ACT: Deploy+Service │      │  ValidatingAdmission-  │
   (dja)             in /registry           │   ▼                │      │  Policy (CEL)          │
                                            │ UPDATE status ─►loop│      │   ▼ persist to etcd   │
                                            └────────────────────┘      └─────────────────────┘
   RBAC gate: every step above is subject to apiGroup + plural (+/status) verbs — no implicit access
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Chapter 38 is the story of Kubernetes teaching itself new vocabulary. A CRD adds a brand-new noun to the API — <code>kubectl get dja</code> becomes a first-class command the instant <code>metadata.name</code> correctly reads <code>&lt;plural&gt;.&lt;group&gt;</code> and the OpenAPIv3 schema is defined. On its own that noun is inert: an Operator (CRD + controller) is what brings it to life, running the eternal WATCH → COMPARE → ACT → update-status loop that real tools like cert-manager, Prometheus Operator, and ArgoCD all use — always installed, verified, THEN configured, never the reverse.</p>
                    <p>When etcd-backed storage isn't enough — live, computed data like <code>kubectl top</code>'s metrics — the API Aggregation Layer registers an entirely separate apiserver instead. Around all of this sit the guardrails: mutating webhooks that can edit a request before it's saved, validating webhooks and CEL-based ValidatingAdmissionPolicies that can only accept or reject it, and kubectl plugins (via krew) that extend the CLI experience itself without touching the API at all.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> every new noun, brain, and guardrail this chapter adds is still governed by the SAME RBAC model as Pods and Deployments — grant verbs on the CRD's <code>apiGroup</code> + plural (and <code>/status</code>), or the shiniest operator in the world gets <code>Forbidden</code> the moment it tries to act.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
