                <h4>Canary Deployments — Progressive Rollout with Traffic Splitting</h4>
                <p>Instead of replacing all pods at once (risky!), a <strong>canary deployment</strong> sends a small percentage of traffic to the new version first. If it's healthy, gradually increase. If not, rollback instantly:</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ A Plain Kubernetes <code>Service</code> Cannot Do "10% Traffic" On Its Own</h5>
                    <p>This matters because it's a common misconception: a vanilla <code>Service</code> load-balances round-robin (or random, via <code>kube-proxy</code>) across <strong>every matching Endpoint equally</strong> — it has no concept of "send 10% here, 90% there." There are exactly two ways to get the percentages shown in the diagram below:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>How the % is actually achieved</th><th>Precision</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Native — replica-ratio canary</strong> (the plain-Kubernetes pattern: a shared <code>app</code> label the Service selects on, plus a <code>track: stable</code> / <code>track: canary</code> label the Service does <em>not</em> select on)</td><td>Run 9 stable pods + 1 canary pod under the SAME Service selector → each pod gets roughly 1/10 of requests simply because <code>kube-proxy</code> spreads connections evenly across all matched endpoints</td><td>Approximate — snapped to whatever ratio your replica counts allow (1/10, not an exact 10.0%); no extra tooling required</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Mesh/Ingress/Argo Rollouts — weighted split</strong></td><td>An Ingress controller, service mesh (Istio, Linkerd), or Argo Rollouts' <code>canary.steps[].setWeight</code> controls traffic percentage directly, independent of how many pods exist on each side</td><td>Exact, arbitrary percentages (1%, 37%, whatever) — requires the extra component</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">The step-by-step diagram below shows the CONCEPT (traffic shifting gradually to the new version); on a plain cluster with no mesh/Argo Rollouts installed, achieving that requires controlling it via replica counts (native canary) — you cannot just add a <code>weight: 10</code> field to a stock <code>Service</code>.</p>
                </div>

