                <h4>Blue-Green Deployments — Instant Rollback by Switching Services</h4>
                <p>Blue-Green runs TWO complete environments side by side and switches traffic instantly by updating a Service selector:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Blue-Green Deployment — easypj example</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># BLUE (current production) — labeled version: blue</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-blue
  labels:
    app: easypj
    version: blue
spec:
  replicas: 3
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
        version: blue        <span class="comment"># Service currently points to "blue"</span>
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.0

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># GREEN (new version) — labeled version: green</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-green
  labels:
    app: easypj
    version: green
spec:
  replicas: 3
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
        version: green       <span class="comment"># Not receiving traffic yet</span>
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1  <span class="comment"># NEW version</span>

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># SERVICE — switch between blue and green</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  selector:
    app: easypj
    version: blue            # ← Change to "green" to switch!

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># SWITCHING TRAFFIC (zero downtime):</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Deploy green alongside blue (both running)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Test green: kubectl port-forward deploy/easypj-green 8080:8000</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Smoke test green internally</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Switch Service: kubectl patch svc easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"version":"green"}}}'</span>
<span class="comment">#    → All traffic now goes to green INSTANTLY</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. PROBLEM? Switch back:</span>
<span class="comment">#    kubectl patch svc easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"version":"blue"}}}'</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Instant rollback! (sub-second, no pod restarts)</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># 6. Green stable for 24h → delete blue deployment</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ROLLING UPDATE vs BLUE-GREEN vs CANARY:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ┌──────────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┐</span>
<span class="comment"># │                  │ Rolling      │ Blue-Green   │ Canary       │</span>
<span class="comment"># ├──────────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────┤</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Rollback speed   │ Minutes      │ Seconds      │ Seconds      │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Resource usage   │ Same         │ 2× (both env)│ 1.1×         │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Risk             │ Medium       │ Low          │ Lowest       │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Complexity        │ Low          │ Medium       │ High         │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ K8s native?      │ ✅ Built-in  │ ⚙️ Manual    │ ⚙️ Manual    │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Best for          │ Most apps    │ Critical     │ High-traffic │</span>
<span class="comment"># └──────────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┘</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Argo Rollouts — Progressive Delivery with Automated Analysis</h4>
                <p>Argo Rollouts is a Kubernetes controller that makes canary and blue-green deployments <strong>first-class resources</strong>, with automated metric analysis:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-rollout.yaml — Argo Rollouts with automated canary</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Rollout                          <span class="comment"># Replaces Deployment</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  replicas: 5
  strategy:
    canary:                            <span class="comment"># Canary strategy</span>
      steps:
      - setWeight: 10                  <span class="comment"># 10% traffic to canary</span>
        pause: {duration: 5m}          <span class="comment"># Wait 5 minutes</span>
      - setWeight: 30                  <span class="comment"># 30% traffic</span>
        pause: {duration: 10m}         <span class="comment"># Wait 10 minutes</span>
      - setWeight: 50                  <span class="comment"># 50% traffic</span>
        pause: {duration: 10m}
      - setWeight: 100                 <span class="comment"># Full promotion</span>
      
      <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════</span>
      <span class="comment"># AUTOMATED ANALYSIS (Prometheus metrics)</span>
      <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════</span>
      analysis:
        templates:
        - templateName: canary-health-check
        startingStep: 1                <span class="comment"># Start analysis at 10% step</span>
        
        args:
        - name: service-name
          value: easypj-web

  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ANALYSIS TEMPLATE — Automated pass/fail criteria</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: AnalysisTemplate
metadata:
  name: canary-health-check
spec:
  metrics:
  - name: error-rate
    interval: 30s
    failureLimit: 3                   <span class="comment"># Fail if 3 consecutive bad checks</span>
    successCondition: result[0] < 0.01 <span class="comment"># Error rate must be < 1%</span>
    provider:
      prometheus:
        address: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090
        query: |
          rate(http_requests_total{
            service="{{args.service-name}}",
            status=~"5.."
          }[1m]) / rate(http_requests_total{
            service="{{args.service-name}}"
          }[1m])
  
  - name: latency-p99
    interval: 30s
    failureLimit: 2
    successCondition: result[0] < 0.5  <span class="comment"># p99 < 500ms</span>
    provider:
      prometheus:
        query: |
          histogram_quantile(0.99,
            rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{
              service="{{args.service-name}}"
            }[1m]))

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># WHAT HAPPENS:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Rollout starts → 10% pods get v2.1</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. AnalysisTemplate runs every 30s</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. If error-rate stays below 1% AND p99 < 500ms → continue</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. If either metric fails → AUTOMATIC ROLLBACK to v2.0</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. No human intervention needed!</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># This is the gold standard of production deployments.</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>Argo Rollouts vs Flagger — Two Progressive-Delivery Controllers</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Argo Rollouts</th><th>Flagger</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Resource model</td><td>Replaces <code>Deployment</code> with its own <code>Rollout</code> CRD</td><td>Wraps an EXISTING <code>Deployment</code> — no CRD swap needed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Traffic splitting</td><td>Needs a supported ingress/mesh (NGINX, Istio, Linkerd, ALB, SMI)</td><td>Same requirement — Istio, Linkerd, App Mesh, Gloo, NGINX, Traefik</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Ecosystem fit</td><td>Native pairing with ArgoCD (same project, shares UI/CLI conventions)</td><td>Native pairing with Flux and service meshes (grew up in the Flux/CNCF mesh world)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Migration cost</td><td>Higher — every <code>Deployment</code> manifest becomes a <code>Rollout</code></td><td>Lower — your existing Deployment YAML is untouched; Flagger observes it</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Which One for easypj?</h5>
                    <p>If easypj is already GitOps'd with ArgoCD, Argo Rollouts is the natural pairing — same vendor, same UI shows both sync status AND canary progress. If the platform team standardizes on Flux instead, Flagger fits the same role without forcing a <code>Deployment</code> → <code>Rollout</code> migration. Both solve the identical problem (automated, metric-gated progressive traffic shifting); pick based on which GitOps controller and service mesh you've already committed to, not on any feature Argo Rollouts/Flagger uniquely has.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §39.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s39-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">39.7 Helm/Kustomize in GitOps</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <p>GitOps doesn't replace Helm or Kustomize — it <strong>consumes</strong> them. An ArgoCD <code>Application</code> (or a Flux <code>Kustomization</code>/<code>HelmRelease</code>) doesn't point at raw, pre-rendered YAML sitting in Git — it points at a <strong>source path</strong>, and the GitOps controller itself runs <code>helm template</code> or <code>kustomize build</code> on every single sync, then diffs and applies the rendered output. This is why ArgoCD's Phase 1 ("Fetch Desired State" — see the reconciliation loop in Under the Hood) explicitly lists "if Helm: <code>helm template</code> → render YAML" and "if Kustomize: <code>kustomize build</code> → render YAML" as steps <em>inside</em> the sync loop, not something a human does beforehand.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>GitOps Source Types — What ArgoCD/Flux Can Point At</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  GITOPS SOURCE TYPES — one Application, three possible source shapes         │
│                                                                              │
│           ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│           │           GIT REPO: easypj-k8s-manifests            │           │
│           │                                                     │           │
│           │  overlays/production/    (Kustomize)   ─┐           │           │
│           │  easypj-chart/  + values-prod.yaml (Helm)─┼──▶ ArgoCD/Flux      │
│           │  raw-manifests/           (plain YAML)  ─┘   CONTROLLER        │
│           │                                              renders + applies │
│           └────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
│                                                                              │
│  PER-ENVIRONMENT = just a different path or values file, SAME Application:  │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  spec.source.path: overlays/dev          → renders DEV manifests    │    │
│  │  spec.source.path: overlays/staging      → renders STAGING manifests│    │
│  │  spec.source.path: overlays/production   → renders PROD manifests  │    │
│  │  (all three Kustomize builds read the SAME base/, only patches vary)│    │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚙️ WHAT RUNS INSIDE THE CONTROLLER ON EVERY SYNC (not once, at commit-time):│
│  1. Clone/pull the Git repo at targetRevision                               │
│  2. If source is Kustomize → run `kustomize build &lt;path&gt;`                   │
│  3. If source is Helm      → run `helm template &lt;chart&gt; -f &lt;values&gt;`       │
│  4. If source is plain YAML → read the files as-is                          │
│  5. Result: a flat list of rendered K8s resources — THIS is what gets diffed│
│     against the live cluster (never the un-rendered chart/overlay itself)  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>ArgoCD Application Pointed at a Helm Chart (with Values)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-argocd-app-helm.yaml — ArgoCD rendering a Helm chart</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application
metadata:
  name: easypj-helm
  namespace: argocd
spec:
  project: default
  source:
    repoURL: https://github.com/youruser/easypj-chart   <span class="comment"># Points at a CHART, not raw YAML</span>
    targetRevision: main
    path: .                                              <span class="comment"># Chart.yaml lives at repo root</span>
    helm:
      valueFiles:
      - values-prod.yaml               <span class="comment"># Per-env values file, same chart</span>
      parameters:
      - name: image.tag
        value: v2.1-stable             <span class="comment"># Equivalent to --set image.tag=v2.1-stable</span>
  destination:
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
    namespace: easypj
  syncPolicy:
    automated:
      prune: true
      selfHeal: true</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Source Type</th><th>ArgoCD renders with</th><th>Per-Environment Config</th><th>Best Use Case</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Kustomize</td><td><code>kustomize build overlays/&lt;env&gt;</code></td><td>Different overlay <strong>path</strong> per env</td><td>easypj's own manifests (this guide's default)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Helm</td><td><code>helm template chart -f values.yaml</code></td><td>Different <strong>values file</strong>, same chart/path</td><td>3rd-party charts (Prometheus, ingress-nginx)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Plain YAML</td><td>Read files as-is (no render step)</td><td>Usually one path per env (no reuse)</td><td>Tiny apps, quick prototypes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Config Management Plugin (CMP)</td><td>A custom tool you register with ArgoCD (e.g. <code>jsonnet</code>, <code>ytt</code>, or an in-house script) — ArgoCD shells out to it instead of Kustomize/Helm</td><td>Whatever the plugin's own inputs are</td><td>Escape hatch for templating tools ArgoCD doesn't natively speak</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📘 Config Management Plugins — the Fourth Source Type</h5>
                    <p>Kustomize, Helm, and plain YAML cover almost everything, but ArgoCD's ApplicationSource actually recognizes a fourth "Tool" — a <strong>Config Management Plugin (CMP)</strong>: an arbitrary executable ArgoCD invokes to turn a directory of files into Kubernetes manifests. You'd reach for this only if your team standardized on something ArgoCD doesn't understand out of the box (Jsonnet, ytt, cdk8s, or an internal generator script) — everything else about the sync loop (diff, apply, prune, selfHeal) works identically once the plugin hands back rendered YAML.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The Render Step Is Where Diffing Actually Happens</h5>
                    <p>A common misconception: people think ArgoCD diffs your <code>values.yaml</code> or <code>kustomization.yaml</code> against the cluster. It doesn't — those are just <em>inputs</em>. ArgoCD diffs the <strong>rendered output</strong> (actual Deployment/Service/ConfigMap YAML) against what's live. This is why changing a Helm chart's template logic (not just values) still triggers a normal sync — the rendered bytes changed, even though "your config" conceptually didn't.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Flux's Equivalent — <code>HelmRelease</code> and <code>Kustomization</code> CRDs</h4>
                <p>Flux (the other major GitOps controller, alongside ArgoCD) uses two separate CRDs instead of ArgoCD's single <code>Application</code>: a <code>HelmRelease</code> for Helm sources and a <code>Kustomization</code> for Kustomize/plain-YAML sources. Both still render inside the controller on every reconcile:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Flux CRDs — HelmRelease and Kustomization for easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># FLUX: HelmRelease — renders a Helm chart</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: helm.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v2beta1
kind: HelmRelease
metadata:
  name: easypj
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  chart:
    spec:
      chart: ./easypj-chart
      sourceRef:
        kind: GitRepository
        name: easypj-chart-repo
  values:
    image:
      tag: v2.1-stable
  interval: 5m                       <span class="comment"># Reconcile every 5 minutes</span>

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># FLUX: Kustomization — renders a Kustomize overlay</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: Kustomization
metadata:
  name: easypj
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  sourceRef:
    kind: GitRepository
    name: easypj-manifests-repo
  path: ./overlays/production
  prune: true                        <span class="comment"># Equivalent to ArgoCD's prune: true</span>
  interval: 5m</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                    <p><strong>Speed Tip — reasoning about "what will actually apply":</strong> whenever you're handed an ArgoCD/Flux app pointed at a chart or overlay, run the SAME render command locally first — <code>helm template ./chart -f values-prod.yaml</code> or <code>kubectl kustomize overlays/production/</code> — before touching the cluster. That output IS what the controller will apply; reviewing it locally catches template/patch bugs in seconds instead of waiting for a failed sync.</p>
                </div>
            </section>



                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 39 — How GitOps Reconciliation and Controller Patterns Work ═══ -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch39-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How GitOps Reconciliation and Controller Patterns Actually Work: ArgoCD Sync Loop Internals, Helm Storage Secrets, and Kustomize Strategic Merge Algorithm</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ GitOps Reconciliation — The Pull-Based Deployment Model</h5>
            <p>Unlike traditional CI/CD that pushes to clusters, GitOps tools like ArgoCD continuously reconcile cluster state with Git. This provides drift detection, auto-healing, and a complete audit trail.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  GITOPS RECONCILIATION LOOP — ArgoCD Internals                               │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │                         GIT REPO (source of truth)                     │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │   │
│  │  │  easypj-k8s-manifests/                                        │    │   │
│  │  │  ├── overlays/dev/                                             │    │   │
│  │  │  ├── overlays/staging/                                         │    │   │
│  │  │  └── overlays/production/  <span class="comment">← ArgoCD watches this              </span>│    │   │
│  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                 │                                            │
│                    Every 3 min (or webhook):                                  │
│                                 │                                            │
│                                 ▼                                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  ARGOCD APPLICATION CONTROLLER                                        │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  1. FETCH DESIRED STATE:                                              │   │
│  │     Git clone → kustomize build overlays/production → YAML resources │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  2. FETCH LIVE STATE:                                                 │   │
│  │     kubectl get &lt;resource&gt; -n easypj-prod → actual resources        │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  3. COMPARE (three-way diff):                                         │   │
│  │     desired YAML vs live YAML vs last-applied-configuration           │   │
│  │     → Synced (matches) or OutOfSync (diff detected)                   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  4. ACT (if auto-sync enabled):                                       │   │
│  │     kubectl apply desired resources → drive cluster to Git state      │   │
│  │     kubectl delete resources not in Git (if prune=true)               │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
