            <h5>① Helm 2 vs Helm 3 — Why Tiller Was Removed</h5>
            <p>"Helm renders templates and applies them" hides a huge architectural shift between Helm's two major versions. Helm 2 ran a server-side component called <strong>Tiller</strong> inside your cluster with cluster-admin-like privileges; Helm 3 removed it entirely. Knowing why explains a whole category of "why does my old Helm tutorial not match reality" confusion:</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  HELM 2 (Tiller) vs HELM 3 (Tiller-less) — ARCHITECTURE CHANGE                │
│                                                                              │
│  HELM 2:                                                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐  gRPC   ┌──────────────────────┐  kubeconfig  ┌─────────────┐ │
│  │ helm CLI │ ──────▶│ 🖥️ Tiller (in-cluster)│ ───────────▶│ API server  │ │
│  │ (local)  │         │  runs as a Deployment  │  (Tiller's   │             │ │
│  └──────────┘         │  in kube-system, with  │   OWN SA,    └─────────────┘ │
│                        │  broad RBAC by default │   often      ▲              │
│                        └──────────────────────┘   cluster-admin│              │
│                                                                 │              │
│  ⚠️ PROBLEM: any user who can talk to Tiller inherits TILLER'S permissions,  │
│     not their own RBAC — a classic privilege-escalation vector.             │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│                                                                              │
│  HELM 3:                                                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐  talks DIRECTLY using YOUR kubeconfig  ┌─────────────┐        │
│  │ helm CLI │ ─────────────────────────────────────▶│ API server  │        │
│  │ (local)  │        (your own RBAC applies)          │             │        │
│  └──────────┘                                          └─────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ No Tiller = no separate privileged component to secure or patch.        │
│  ✅ Whatever YOU can kubectl apply, Helm can install — nothing more.         │
│  ✅ Release state moved from a ConfigMap in kube-system (Helm 2) to a        │
│     Secret in the RELEASE'S OWN namespace (Helm 3) — better isolation.      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Helm 2 (2016–2019)</th><th>Helm 3 (2019–now)</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Server-side component</td><td>Tiller (a Deployment in <code>kube-system</code>)</td><td>None — client talks straight to the API server</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Permissions used</td><td>Tiller's own ServiceAccount (often over-privileged)</td><td>YOUR kubeconfig's RBAC — no escalation</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Release storage</td><td>ConfigMap, in <code>kube-system</code> (shared namespace)</td><td>Secret, in the release's OWN namespace</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Namespaces</td><td>2-way merge (chart vs cluster)</td><td>3-way merge (chart, cluster, AND last-release state)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>CRD handling</td><td>Installed via a hook (fragile)</td><td>Native <code>crds/</code> directory (installed once, not upgraded)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 If you ever see a tutorial mention "installing Tiller" or "helm init", it's Helm 2 — obsolete since 2020.</strong> Every command in this guide (<code>helm install</code>, <code>helm upgrade</code>, <code>helm rollback</code>) is Helm 3 syntax, which talks straight to the API server with your own kubeconfig. This also means Helm 3 security = your RBAC; there's no separate Tiller ServiceAccount to lock down.</div>

            <h5>② App-of-Apps — Bootstrapping an Entire Cluster from One Commit</h5>
            <p>So far, every ArgoCD example in this chapter manages ONE Application (easypj). Real platform teams manage dozens of Applications — ingress-nginx, cert-manager, Prometheus, easypj, and more. Manually creating each <code>Application</code> object doesn't scale. The <strong>App-of-Apps</strong> pattern solves this: one parent <code>Application</code> whose "manifests" ARE other <code>Application</code> objects.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  APP-OF-APPS PATTERN — Bootstrap a Whole Cluster from ONE Commit             │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  Git repo: platform-apps/                                          │    │
│  │  ├── root-app.yaml           <span class="comment">← the ONE Application you create by hand</span>│    │
│  │  └── apps/                   <span class="comment">← everything else lives here as YAML</span>       │    │
│  │      ├── ingress-nginx-app.yaml    (Application → Helm chart)      │    │
│  │      ├── cert-manager-app.yaml     (Application → Helm chart)      │    │
│  │      ├── prometheus-app.yaml       (Application → Helm chart)      │    │
│  │      └── easypj-app.yaml           (Application → Kustomize overlay)│    │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼   ArgoCD syncs root-app                  │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  root-app's "manifests" are just MORE Application objects           │    │
│  │  → ArgoCD applies apps/*.yaml → creates 4 child Applications        │    │
│  │  → each child Application then syncs ITS OWN source (chart/overlay)│    │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐            │
│  │ingress-nginx│  │cert-manager│  │ Prometheus │  │  easypj    │            │
│  │  Synced ✅  │  │  Synced ✅  │  │  Synced ✅  │  │  Synced ✅  │            │
│  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘            │
│                                                                              │
│  RESULT: adding a 5th tool = commit one new YAML file to apps/ — nobody     │
│  ever runs `argocd app create` by hand again.                                │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
