            <h5>③ GitOps Secret Management — Sealed Secrets vs External Secrets</h5>
            <p>Every code example in this chapter that touches a password says "in real life, use sealed-secrets or external-secrets" — because committing a raw Kubernetes <code>Secret</code> (base64 is NOT encryption) to Git is a critical security failure: anyone with repo read access can decode it instantly. GitOps needs the desired state in Git, but secrets can't safely live there in plaintext. Two competing solutions:</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  TWO WAYS TO PUT SECRETS IN GIT SAFELY                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  APPROACH A: SEALED SECRETS (Bitnami) — encrypt, THEN commit                │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  1. $ kubeseal --cert cluster-public-cert.pem < secret.yaml \       │    │
│  │       > sealed-secret.yaml                                          │    │
│  │  2. sealed-secret.yaml is SAFE to commit (asymmetrically encrypted, │    │
│  │     only the in-cluster controller's PRIVATE key can decrypt it)   │    │
│  │  3. git commit sealed-secret.yaml &amp;&amp; git push                       │    │
│  │  4. ArgoCD/Flux applies it → sealed-secrets-controller (in-cluster) │    │
│  │     decrypts → creates a real Secret object                         │    │
│  │                                                                       │    │
│  │  ✅ Fully GitOps-native: the encrypted form IS the Git artifact      │    │
│  │  ⚠️ Losing the cluster's private key = can never decrypt old secrets│    │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  APPROACH B: EXTERNAL SECRETS OPERATOR — Git stores a REFERENCE only        │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  1. Real secret value lives OUTSIDE the cluster: AWS Secrets        │    │
│  │     Manager / Vault / Azure Key Vault (never touches Git at all)   │    │
│  │  2. Git commits only an ExternalSecret object — a POINTER:          │    │
│  │       apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1beta1                       │    │
│  │       kind: ExternalSecret                                          │    │
│  │       spec:                                                         │    │
│  │         secretStoreRef: {name: aws-sm, kind: SecretStore}          │    │
│  │         target: {name: easypj-db-creds}                            │    │
│  │         data: [{secretKey: password, remoteRef: {key: easypj/db}}] │    │
│  │  3. external-secrets-operator (in-cluster) polls AWS/Vault, PULLS  │    │
│  │     the real value, materializes a normal Secret object            │    │
│  │                                                                       │    │
│  │  ✅ Real values NEVER touch Git, even encrypted                     │    │
│  │  ✅ Central rotation in Vault/AWS SM propagates automatically       │    │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Sealed Secrets</th><th>External Secrets Operator</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>What's in Git</td><td>Encrypted ciphertext (safe to commit)</td><td>A reference/pointer only, no secret data</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Where the real value lives</td><td>Decrypted in-cluster from the sealed blob</td><td>External vault (AWS SM, Vault, Azure KV) — never in Git</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Rotation</td><td>Re-seal + re-commit a new encrypted blob</td><td>Rotate in the vault; cluster auto-picks up on next poll</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Key management risk</td><td>Losing the controller's private key is catastrophic</td><td>Depends on the external vault's own HA/backup</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Best for easypj</td><td>Small teams, no existing vault infra</td><td>Teams that already run Vault/AWS SM for other secrets</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The CKA doesn't test either tool by name, but the underlying rule IS tested:</strong> Secrets are base64-<em>encoded</em>, not encrypted — anyone with <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code> access (or Git read access, if you commit raw Secrets) can decode them instantly. On the exam, if you're asked to create a Secret, that's fine — the exam cluster's RBAC IS the security boundary. In production GitOps, sealed-secrets or external-secrets close the gap that plain Secrets-in-Git would otherwise leave wide open.</div>

            <h5>④ Image Automation — How a New Tag Reaches Git Without a Human</h5>
            <p>Every workflow shown so far has a human (or CI job) editing a manifest to bump an image tag, then committing. <strong>Image automation</strong> tools remove even that step — they watch a registry for new tags and commit the bump to Git themselves, so the ONLY human action left is merging (or not) a bot-authored pull request.</p>
