            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 39.4 — Helm Chart Install, Upgrade &amp; Rollback</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Package easypj as a Helm chart and practice the full release lifecycle: install with prod values, upgrade the image tag, then roll back a broken release.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Install the easypj chart with <code>values-prod.yaml</code> into the <code>easypj</code> namespace</li>
                        <li>Verify the release and check what values are active</li>
                        <li>Upgrade to a new (broken) image tag</li>
                        <li>Notice pods are CrashLoopBackOff</li>
                        <li>Roll back to the previous working revision</li>
                        <li>Confirm the rollback via <code>helm history</code></li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Every <code>helm upgrade</code> — including a bad one — creates a NEW numbered revision; nothing is overwritten. <code>helm rollback &lt;release&gt; &lt;N&gt;</code> just re-applies a stored prior revision's rendered manifest, the same way <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> re-applies an old ReplicaSet.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Install</span>
helm install easypj ./easypj-chart --values values-prod.yaml -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify</span>
helm list -n easypj
helm get values easypj -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Upgrade to a broken tag</span>
helm upgrade easypj ./easypj-chart --values values-prod.yaml   --set image.tag=v2.2-broken -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Notice the problem</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-xxxxx   0/1   CrashLoopBackOff   4 (30s ago)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Roll back</span>
helm rollback easypj 1 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Rollback was a success!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Confirm</span>
helm history easypj -n easypj
<span class="comment"># REVISION  STATUS      DESCRIPTION</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         superseded  Install complete</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         superseded  Upgrade "easypj" failed</span>
<span class="comment"># 3         deployed    Rollback to 1</span>

kubectl get pods -n easypj
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-yyyyy   1/1   Running   0</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 39.5 — Diagnose: Is It CI, CD, or the Cluster?</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>easypj's pods are stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> after a "routine" deploy. Practice the triage flow that separates a CI problem, a CD/manifest problem, and a cluster problem — a realistic troubleshooting drill that combines this entire chapter.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Confirm what image tag the Deployment is actually requesting</li>
                        <li>Check whether that tag exists in the registry (a CI-side question)</li>
                        <li>Check whether ArgoCD's Application is Synced against Git (a CD-side question)</li>
                        <li>Check <code>imagePullSecrets</code> and events for an auth failure (a cluster-side question)</li>
                        <li>Identify the root cause and fix it at the RIGHT layer</li>
                        <li>Verify the fix restores <code>Running</code> pods without needing a manual <code>kubectl</code> patch that GitOps would just revert</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> has three completely different root causes that look identical from <code>kubectl get pods</code> — a CI job that never actually pushed the tag, a manifest that references the wrong tag, or a missing/expired <code>imagePullSecrets</code>. Read <code>kubectl describe pod</code>'s Events section — it names which one it is.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: What tag is the Deployment requesting?</span>
kubectl get deploy easypj-web -n easypj-production   -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'
<span class="comment"># ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.3-typo   ← suspicious tag</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2 (CI-side): Does that tag exist in the registry?</span>
<span class="comment"># If CI's build job failed silently, the tag was never pushed —</span>
<span class="comment"># this is a CI problem: check the GitHub Actions run logs.</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3 (CD-side): Is ArgoCD in sync with Git?</span>
argocd app get easypj
<span class="comment"># Sync Status: Synced   ← means Git ALSO says v2.3-typo (a manifest-repo typo)</span>
<span class="comment"># This narrows it: not a drift problem, the WRONG tag was committed.</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4 (cluster-side): rule out an auth problem too</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxxx -n easypj-production | grep -A5 Events
<span class="comment"># Failed to pull image "ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.3-typo": manifest unknown</span>
<span class="comment"># ("manifest unknown" = the TAG doesn't exist — confirms it's a CI/tag problem,</span>
<span class="comment">#  NOT an imagePullSecrets/auth problem, which would say "unauthorized" instead)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix at the RIGHT layer — commit the correct tag to Git</span>
<span class="comment"># (NOT `kubectl set image`, which GitOps selfHeal would just revert!)</span>
git -C easypj-k8s-manifests checkout -b fix-image-tag
sed -i 's/v2.3-typo/v2.3/' overlays/production/kustomization.yaml
git -C easypj-k8s-manifests commit -am "Fix easypj image tag typo: v2.3-typo -> v2.3"
git -C easypj-k8s-manifests push

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Let ArgoCD sync the fix, then verify</span>
argocd app sync easypj
kubectl get pods -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-zzzzz   1/1   Running   0   ← fixed, and it'll STAY fixed</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

