            <span class="part-kicker">🔴 Part 12 · Hardening &amp; Policy</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Lock It Down</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 40–42 · Advanced · <em>"Beyond-exam depth + admission/policy mastery"</em></p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 3 Chapters</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🏢 Day-2</span>
            </div>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Security isn't one switch; it's layers. This part stacks them: harden the cluster surface (apiserver flags, audit, supply chain), then enforce rules automatically with Pod Security Admission and layered policy engines — and shows the admission-webhook plumbing that powers it all.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you'll be able to do by the end:</strong> apply a security-hardening checklist across RBAC, secrets, audit, runtime, and supply chain; enforce Pod Security Standards and resource governance; and explain the admission-webhook machinery underneath it.</div>
            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🏢 Real-World Analogy: An Airport's Three Layers of Security</h5>
                <p>Think of easypj-production as an airport terminal. Chapter 40 is the <strong>perimeter</strong>: the fence, the badge-only doors, the cameras — RBAC, encrypted secrets, audit logging, and a locked-down API server that decide who's even allowed on the property. Chapter 41 is the <strong>checkpoint</strong>: every single passenger (every Pod, every request) is screened against a published rulebook before being let through — Pod Security Standards, OPA/Gatekeeper, Kyverno, and CEL ValidatingAdmissionPolicy. Chapter 42 is the <strong>X-ray machine itself</strong> — the actual mechanism the checkpoint runs on: the AdmissionReview wire format, the mutating-then-validating webhook chain, TLS certs and failure policies that decide what happens when the scanner breaks. You can have a fence and a checkpoint, but if you don't understand the machine underneath, you can't debug it when it jams — and a jammed admission webhook with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> can wedge an entire cluster shut, exactly like a broken scanner backing up an entire terminal.</p>
<pre>  AIRPORT SECURITY                            KUBERNETES CLUSTER (Part 12)
  ─────────────────                            ──────────────────
  🚧 Perimeter fence, badge doors    ──────▶   Ch 40 Hardening (RBAC, secrets, audit, runtime, supply chain)
  🛂 Checkpoint rulebook              ──────▶   Ch 41 Policies (Pod Security Standards, OPA/Kyverno, CEL VAP)
  🖥️ The X-ray scanner's internals    ──────▶   Ch 42 Admission webhooks (AdmissionReview, mutate→validate, TLS)
  🚨 Scanner jams, no backup lane     ──────▶   failurePolicy: Fail + webhook down = cluster writes wedged</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Admins Get Wrong About Cluster Security</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Admins Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"RBAC is the whole security model — if roles are tight, we're safe."</td>
                            <td>RBAC only answers "who can call the API." It says nothing about what a Pod can do once it's running (seccomp/AppArmor/capabilities), whether the image was tampered with (supply chain), or whether the workload itself meets a security baseline (Pod Security Standards). RBAC is one ring in a defense-in-depth stack, not the whole wall.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Pod Security Standards / OPA / Kyverno are optional extras — the cluster works fine without them."</td>
                            <td>Without an admission-time policy layer, ANY authenticated, authorized user can deploy a privileged, host-mounting, root container — RBAC lets them create the object, it just can't judge whether the object's spec is dangerous. Policies are what turn "technically allowed" into "actually safe," and they run automatically on every create/update, not just at review time.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Admission webhooks are internal plumbing — I'll never need to touch them."</td>
                            <td>Gatekeeper, Kyverno, cert-manager, Istio sidecar injection, and most service meshes ALL run as admission webhooks under the hood. When one of them misbehaves (bad TLS cert, wrong <code>failurePolicy</code>, unreachable endpoint), it can silently block every Pod creation cluster-wide — understanding the AdmissionReview flow from Ch 42 is what lets you diagnose and recover from that outage instead of guessing.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
