            <!-- ─────────── §40.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.1 The Kubernetes Security Hardening Checklist</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Use this as a reference checklist when securing a cluster. Each item links to the detailed section below.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #ef4444;">1</div>
                        <h5>Infrastructure Layer</h5>
                        <p>API server flags, etcd encryption at rest, audit logging, component TLS — secure the foundation</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #f59e0b;">2</div>
                        <h5>Auth & RBAC</h5>
                        <p>Least-privilege roles, disable default SA token mounting, no wildcard permissions, audit bindings</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #eab308;">3</div>
                        <h5>Admission Control</h5>
                        <p>Pod Security Standards (restricted), ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, image registry allowlisting</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #22c55e;">4</div>
                        <h5>Workload Hardening</h5>
                        <p>SecurityContext (runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem), drop ALL capabilities, seccomp profiles</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #60a5fa;">5</div>
                        <h5>Network Isolation</h5>
                        <p>Default-deny NetworkPolicy per namespace, egress filtering, DNS rules, mTLS with service mesh</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #8b5cf6;">6</div>
                        <h5>Data Protection</h5>
                        <p>Secrets encrypted at rest, mount as files (not env), external secret stores, credential rotation</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Security Layers — Defense in Depth</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │                  DEFENSE IN DEPTH                            │
 ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 1: CLUSTER INFRASTRUCTURE                            │
 │  ├── API server flags (--anonymous-auth=false)              │
 │  ├── etcd encryption at rest                                │
 │  ├── Audit logging enabled                                  │
 │  └── Component TLS (kubelet, etcd)                          │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 2: AUTHENTICATION & AUTHORIZATION                    │
 │  ├── RBAC with least privilege                              │
 │  ├── Disable default SA token auto-mount                    │
 │  ├── No cluster-admin for workloads                         │
 │  └── Audit who has wildcard permissions                     │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 3: ADMISSION CONTROL                                 │
 │  ├── Pod Security Admission (restricted)                    │
 │  ├── ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)                        │
 │  ├── Image allowlisting                                     │
 │  └── Deny privileged containers                              │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 4: WORKLOAD SECURITY                                 │
 │  ├── SecurityContext (runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem) │
 │  ├── Resource limits (CPU/memory)                           │
 │  ├── seccomp / AppArmor profiles                            │
 │  └── No hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC                       │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 5: NETWORK SECURITY                                  │
 │  ├── Default-deny NetworkPolicy                             │
 │  ├── Egress filtering                                       │
 │  ├── Namespace isolation                                    │
 │  └── mTLS (service mesh)                                    │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 6: DATA SECURITY                                     │
 │  ├── Secrets encrypted at rest                              │
 │  ├── External secret management (Vault, etc.)               │
 │  ├── No secrets in environment variables                    │
 │  └── Rotate credentials regularly                           │
 │                                                              │
 └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Decision Guide — Which Layer Does a Given Control Belong To?</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Ask Yourself...</th><th>Layer</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Is it about the host/node itself?</td><td>1 — 🏗️ Infra</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Who can call the API?</td><td>2 — 🔐 AuthN/Z</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Can this pod even be created?</td><td>3 — 🧪 Admission</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>What can the pod do once it's running?</td><td>4 — 📦 Workload</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Who can it talk to over the network?</td><td>5 — 🧯 Network</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Is the data safe at rest / in the log?</td><td>6 — 💾 Data</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why This Matters</h5>
                    <p>New hires (and exam-takers under time pressure) often waste minutes deciding WHERE a new requirement belongs — "does <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> go under RBAC or Workload?" This decision guide collapses that hesitation into one lookup: ask what the control is actually deciding at the moment it fires. A ServiceAccount token mount is decided at pod-creation time by the kubelet reading the pod spec, so it's Layer 4 (Workload), even though it's ABOUT API access — the RBAC rules that token is scoped to are the separate Layer 2 control.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.2 RBAC Hardening Deep Dive</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <h4>Dangerous Permissions to Avoid</h4>
                <p>These RBAC rules grant far more access than they appear to. Treat them as red flags during security reviews.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Dangerous Permission</th><th>Why It's Dangerous</th><th>What to Do Instead</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>verbs: ["*"]</code> on any resource</td><td>Grants ALL operations including delete</td><td>List only the verbs you need: <code>["get", "list", "watch"]</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resources: ["*"]</code></td><td>Access to ALL resource types in the API group</td><td>List specific resources: <code>["pods", "services"]</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiGroups: ["*"]</code></td><td>Access across ALL API groups</td><td>Specify: <code>["", "apps", "networking.k8s.io"]</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>create</code> on <code>pods</code></td><td>Can exec into nodes, mount secrets, escape</td><td>Grant <code>create</code> on <code>deployments</code> instead</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>escalate</code> on <code>roles</code></td><td>Can grant themselves cluster-admin</td><td>Never grant to workload service accounts</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>bind</code> on <code>clusterroles</code></td><td>Can bind any role to any subject</td><td>Limit to specific roles with resourceNames</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>impersonate</code> on <code>users/groups</code></td><td>Can act as any user including cluster-admin</td><td>Never grant unless absolutely required</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>RBAC Best Practices — easypj Example</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">rbac-hardened.yaml — least-privilege for easypj app</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ❌ BAD — overly permissive
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: bad-example-do-not-use
rules:
  - apiGroups: ["*"]
    resources: ["*"]
    verbs: ["*"]           <span class="comment"># This is basically cluster-admin!</span>
---
<span class="comment"># ✅ GOOD — least privilege for easypj web app</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-role
  namespace: easypj
rules:
  <span class="comment"># Read-only access to ConfigMaps (for Django settings)</span>
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["configmaps"]
    resourceNames: ["easypj-config"]  <span class="comment"># Only THIS specific ConfigMap</span>
    verbs: ["get", "watch"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Read-only access to Secrets (for DB credentials)</span>
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["secrets"]
    resourceNames: ["easypj-db-secret"]
    verbs: ["get"]
  
  <span class="comment"># No access to pods, services, etc. — the app doesn't need it</span>
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: easypj-web-sa
    namespace: easypj
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-web-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Audit Existing RBAC Permissions</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — RBAC audit commands</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Find all ClusterRoleBindings that reference cluster-admin</span>
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.roleRef.name=="cluster-admin") |
  .metadata.name + " → " + (.subjects[]? | .kind + "/" + .name)'

<span class="comment"># Check what a specific ServiceAccount can do</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Find roles with wildcard permissions</span>
kubectl get roles,clusterroles -A -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.rules[]? | .verbs[]? == "*") |
  .kind + "/" + .metadata.name'

<span class="comment"># Verify no default SA has extra bindings</span>
kubectl get rolebindings,clusterrolebindings -A -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.subjects[]? | .name == "default") |
  .metadata.namespace + "/" + .metadata.name'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🩺 Troubleshooting — "It Only Works With cluster-admin"</h5>
                    <p>If a workload only functions after you bind it to <code>cluster-admin</code>, that is a <strong>smell, not a fix</strong>. It means you never identified the exact permissions the app needs — you just kept escalating until the 403s stopped. The correct fix is always the same three-step loop: (1) bind the workload to a deliberately narrow Role first, (2) run <code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa -n easypj</code> and reproduce the failure to see exactly which verb/resource pair is missing, (3) add ONLY that rule and re-test. Repeat until it works — you'll end up with a Role that grants precisely what the app uses, which is both the CKA-correct answer and the production-correct one. Never take the shortcut of granting <code>cluster-admin</code> "temporarily" — temporary bindings are the ones nobody remembers to revoke.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h4>RBAC Gotchas on the CKA</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>❌ <strong>Role vs ClusterRole</strong> — Role is namespace-scoped, ClusterRole is cluster-wide. Use Role unless you need cross-namespace access</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong>RoleBinding to ClusterRole</strong> — this is valid! It scopes the ClusterRole's permissions to a single namespace</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong>Forgetting <code>apiGroup</code></strong> — core resources (pods, services) use <code>""</code>, apps resources use <code>"apps"</code></li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Use <code>resourceNames</code></strong> — when possible, restrict access to specific named resources</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.3 Secrets Management Best Practices</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Kubernetes Secrets are Base64-encoded, <strong>not encrypted</strong> by default. Anyone with RBAC access to Secrets or etcd can read them. Here's how to harden Secrets properly.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Critical Security Truth</h5>
                    <p><code>echo "cGFzc3dvcmQ=" | base64 -d</code> → <code>password</code>. Base64 is encoding, NOT encryption. Without additional measures, Secrets in etcd are plaintext-equivalent. NEVER assume Secrets are safe by default.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Enable Encryption at Rest</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">encryption-config.yaml — encrypt secrets in etcd</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
  - resources:
      - secrets
    providers:
      - aescbc:                      <span class="comment"># AES-CBC encryption</span>
          keys:
            - name: key1
              secret: &lt;base64-encoded-32-byte-key&gt;
      - identity: {}                 <span class="comment"># Fallback: read unencrypted (for migration)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — enable encryption at rest</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Generate a 32-byte encryption key</span>
head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64

<span class="comment"># 2. Add --encryption-provider-config flag to kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit: /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># Add to spec.containers[0].command:</span>
<span class="comment">#   --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Mount the encryption config into the API server pod</span>
<span class="comment"># Add volume and volumeMount to kube-apiserver.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Re-encrypt existing Secrets (they're still unencrypted!)</span>
kubectl get secrets --all-namespaces -o json | kubectl replace -f -

<span class="comment"># 5. Verify encryption is working</span>
<span class="comment"># Read a Secret directly from etcd — should be encrypted</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/easypj/easypj-db-secret \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt | hexdump -C | head</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Secrets Anti-Patterns vs Best Practices</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Anti-Pattern ❌</th><th>Best Practice ✅</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Secrets in env vars</td><td>Mount as files via <code>volumeMount</code></td><td>Env vars appear in logs, process listings, and crash dumps</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Secrets in Git repos</td><td>Use SealedSecrets or external secret stores</td><td>Git history is permanent — secrets can never be truly removed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Using the <code>default</code> SA</td><td>Create dedicated ServiceAccounts</td><td>Default SA is shared by all pods in a namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Auto-mounting SA tokens</td><td>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></td><td>Most pods don't need API server access</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Hardcoding secrets in YAML</td><td>Use <code>kubectl create secret</code> or external tool</td><td>Keeps secret values out of manifest files</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">How should I provide secrets to my pod?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div>
                            <div class="decision-branch-label">Need K8s-native?</div>
                            <div class="decision-node yes">✅ Use K8s Secret mounted as volume file</div>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <div class="decision-branch-label">Production/enterprise?</div>
                            <div class="decision-node yes">✅ External secret store (Vault, AWS SM)</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">Never use these approaches:</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div>
                            <div class="decision-node no">❌ Environment variables — leak in logs/crashes</div>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <div class="decision-node no">❌ Hardcoded in YAML — committed to Git history</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-secure-pod.yaml — proper secret handling</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-web-sa
  automountServiceAccountToken: false   # ✅ Don't mount SA token
  containers:
    - name: django
      image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
      volumeMounts:
        - name: db-creds
          mountPath: /etc/secrets/db     # ✅ Mount as files
          readOnly: true                 # ✅ Read-only mount
  volumes:
    - name: db-creds
      secret:
        secretName: easypj-db-secret
        defaultMode: 0400                # ✅ Restrictive permissions</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>External Secret Stores — How Vault Actually Gets Wired Into a Pod</h4>
                <p>"Use an external secret store" isn't a single mechanism — there are two genuinely different K8s-native ways to connect Vault/AWS Secrets Manager/Azure Key Vault to easypj, and they trade off differently.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>How It Works</th><th>Creates a K8s Secret?</th><th>Best For</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Secrets Store CSI Driver</td><td>A CSI volume plugin pulls the value directly from Vault/cloud SM at pod-mount time and presents it as a file — no controller, no polling</td><td>No (opt-in <code>syncSecret</code> can mirror it, but by default nothing persists in etcd)</td><td>Highest security bar — the value never rests in the K8s API at all</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>External Secrets Operator (ESO)</td><td>A controller polls the external store on an interval and writes/refreshes a real <code>Secret</code> object in-cluster</td><td>Yes — a normal <code>Secret</code>, refreshed automatically</td><td>Teams that want normal <code>envFrom</code>/volumeMount ergonomics with rotation handled for them</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 Which One Does easypj Use?</h5>
                    <p>For the database credential, easypj uses the <strong>Secrets Store CSI Driver</strong> — the DB password never becomes a K8s <code>Secret</code> object, so it never appears in <code>kubectl get secrets</code>, never gets etcd-encrypted-at-rest as a fallback (it was never AT rest in etcd to begin with), and an RBAC rule granting <code>get</code> on Secrets in the namespace grants nothing extra. The trade-off: if Vault is unreachable during a pod restart, the mount fails and the pod can't start — ESO's cached copy would have kept the last-known-good value available. Choose CSI when leak-surface matters most; choose ESO when availability during a Vault outage matters most.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.4 API Server & Control Plane Security</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>The API server is the front door to your cluster. Hardening it is your highest priority.</p>

                <h4>Critical API Server Flags</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Recommended Value</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>--anonymous-auth</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Disable unauthenticated access to the API</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--enable-admission-plugins</code></td><td>Include <code>NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</code></td><td>Restrict node self-modification, enforce pod security</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-path</code></td><td><code>/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code></td><td>Enable audit logging for all API requests</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-policy-file</code></td><td>Point to policy YAML</td><td>Define what events to record and at what level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--profiling</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Disable profiling endpoints (information disclosure)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--insecure-port</code></td><td><code>0</code> (disabled)</td><td>No HTTP access — HTTPS only (deprecated, removed in 1.24)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--encryption-provider-config</code></td><td>Point to config file</td><td>Encrypt secrets at rest in etcd</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Beyond the API Server — Kubelet, etcd &amp; Cloud Metadata Are Front Doors Too</h4>
                <p>"Harden the front door" is usually read as "harden the API server," but the API server isn't the ONLY component with its own authentication surface. Three more components need the same scrutiny, and each is a genuinely different attack surface than RBAC.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Hardening</th><th>Why It's a Separate Concern From API-Server RBAC</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Kubelet</td><td><code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> and <code>--authorization-mode=Webhook</code> on <em>every kubelet</em> (its own flags, not the API server's); pair with the <code>NodeRestriction</code> admission plugin</td><td>The kubelet exposes its own HTTPS API on port 10250 (exec, logs, portforward, stats). Without kubelet-level auth, anyone reaching that port on the node network can run commands in ANY pod on that node — no API server, no RBAC check, involved at all.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>etcd</td><td>Bind etcd only to the control-plane network (client/peer TLS with mutually-verified certs); never expose the etcd client port to worker nodes or the internet</td><td>etcd holds every Secret, every object, cluster-wide. <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> (§40.3) protects the VALUE of a Secret once someone reads etcd — it does nothing to stop them reading it in the first place if network access to etcd itself isn't restricted.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Cloud metadata API</td><td>A NetworkPolicy egress rule (or <code>iptables</code>/CNI mechanism) that blocks pods from reaching <code>169.254.169.254</code> (the AWS/Azure/GCP instance metadata IP) unless a workload specifically needs it</td><td>A compromised pod that can still reach the node's cloud metadata endpoint can often retrieve the NODE's IAM/service-identity credentials — turning a container escape into a full cloud-account compromise, bypassing every Kubernetes-level control entirely.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ A Locked-Down API Server Can Still Leave These Wide Open</h5>
                    <p><code>--anonymous-auth=false</code> on the <strong>kube-apiserver</strong> says nothing about the <strong>kubelet's own</strong> <code>--anonymous-auth</code> flag — they are two independent settings on two independent HTTPS servers. A cluster hardened perfectly at the API-server layer but with an un-hardened kubelet is still one curl command away from arbitrary code execution on any pod, from anywhere that can route to port 10250. This is why the CKA/CKS "securing a cluster" checklist always lists kubelet auth as its own line item, not a sub-bullet of API-server hardening.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Audit Logging — Tracking Who Did What</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">audit-policy.yaml — sample audit policy</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
rules:
  <span class="comment"># Log Secret access at Metadata level (don't log Secret content!)</span>
  - level: Metadata
    resources:
      - group: ""
        resources: ["secrets"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Log all write operations at RequestResponse level</span>
  - level: RequestResponse
    verbs: ["create", "update", "patch", "delete"]
    resources:
      - group: ""
        resources: ["pods", "services", "configmaps"]
      - group: "apps"
        resources: ["deployments", "statefulsets", "daemonsets"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Log RBAC changes at RequestResponse level</span>
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
      - group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io"
        resources: ["roles", "rolebindings", "clusterroles", "clusterrolebindings"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Don't log read-only requests to known non-sensitive endpoints</span>
  - level: None
    resources:
      - group: ""
        resources: ["events"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Catch-all: log everything else at Metadata level</span>
  - level: Metadata</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                    <p><strong>CKA Exam</strong> — You may be asked to configure audit logging. Remember the four audit levels: <code>None</code> (no logging), <code>Metadata</code> (log request metadata only), <code>Request</code> (log request body), <code>RequestResponse</code> (log both request and response body). Never log Secret content — use <code>Metadata</code> level for Secrets.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.5 Runtime Security — seccomp & AppArmor</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <h4>seccomp Profiles</h4>
                <p><strong>seccomp</strong> (Secure Computing Mode) restricts which <strong>Linux system calls</strong> a container can make. By blocking dangerous syscalls, you reduce the attack surface if a container is compromised.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-seccomp.yaml — seccomp profile for Django app</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-hardened
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  securityContext:
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault          # ✅ Use container runtime's default profile
  containers:
    - name: django
      image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
      securityContext:
        runAsNonRoot: true           # ✅ Never run as root
        runAsUser: 1000
        readOnlyRootFilesystem: true # ✅ Prevent filesystem writes
        allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # ✅ Block setuid/setgid
        capabilities:
          drop: ["ALL"]              # ✅ Drop ALL Linux capabilities
      volumeMounts:
        - name: tmp
          mountPath: /tmp            <span class="comment"># Django needs a writable temp dir</span>
  volumes:
    - name: tmp
      emptyDir: {}                   # ✅ Provide writable tmp via emptyDir</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>AppArmor Profiles</h4>
                <p><strong>AppArmor</strong> is a Linux Security Module that restricts a program's capabilities — file access, network access, and raw socket creation.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">apparmor-pod.yaml — apply AppArmor profile</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-apparmor
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  containers:
    - name: django
      image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
      securityContext:
        appArmorProfile:
          type: RuntimeDefault       <span class="comment"># Use runtime default AppArmor profile</span>
          <span class="comment"># type: Localhost           # Or use a custom profile:</span>
          <span class="comment"># localhostProfile: easypj-web-profile</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The <code>appArmorProfile</code> Field Is Newer Than You Might Assume</h5>
                    <p>Before <strong>Kubernetes v1.30</strong>, there was no <code>securityContext.appArmorProfile</code> field at all — AppArmor could ONLY be attached via a pod annotation: <code>container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/&lt;container-name&gt;: runtime/default</code> (or <code>localhost/&lt;profile-name&gt;</code>). The structured field shown above became available in v1.30 and reached <strong>stable/GA in v1.31</strong>. The old annotation still works for backward compatibility, but new manifests should use the field — if you're troubleshooting a cluster running an older Kubernetes version (or reading an older exam-prep source), don't be surprised to see the annotation form instead.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 seccomp vs AppArmor</h5>
                    <p><strong>seccomp</strong> restricts <em>system calls</em> (kernel-level operations). <strong>AppArmor</strong> restricts <em>file and network access</em> (path-based). They are complementary — use both for defense in depth. The <code>RuntimeDefault</code> profile for both is a good starting point that blocks obviously dangerous operations without breaking most applications.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>🔒 seccomp (System Call Filter)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Layer:</strong> Kernel syscalls</li>
                            <li><strong>Blocks:</strong> mount, reboot, ptrace, raw sockets</li>
                            <li><strong>Config:</strong> <code>securityContext.seccompProfile</code></li>
                            <li><strong>Types:</strong> RuntimeDefault, Localhost, Unconfined</li>
                            <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Preventing kernel exploits</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>🛡️ AppArmor (Application Armor)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Layer:</strong> File/network access paths</li>
                            <li><strong>Blocks:</strong> /etc/shadow reads, raw network, specific file writes</li>
                            <li><strong>Config:</strong> <code>securityContext.appArmorProfile</code></li>
                            <li><strong>Types:</strong> RuntimeDefault, Localhost, Unconfined</li>
                            <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Preventing file-based attacks</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What the <code>restricted</code> Pod Security Standard ACTUALLY Requires</h4>
                <p>Every hardened field shown above isn't just a "best practice" — most of them are the exact, enumerated requirements the built-in <code>restricted</code> Pod Security Standard checks at admission time. Knowing the PRECISE allowed values (not just "drop capabilities, generally") is what separates a Pod that merely looks hardened from one that will actually pass admission under <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted</code>.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Restricted-tier control</th><th>Exact allowed values</th><th>Why easypj's manifests already satisfy it</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Capabilities to <strong>add</strong></td><td>undefined/nil, or <strong>only</strong> <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> — every other capability in Linux is rejected if listed under <code>add</code></td><td>easypj never adds capabilities back, so <code>drop: ["ALL"]</code> alone already satisfies this</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Capabilities to <strong>drop</strong></td><td>must include <code>ALL</code></td><td><code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code> in every §40.5 example</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Volume types</td><td>only <code>configMap</code>, <code>csi</code>, <code>downwardAPI</code>, <code>emptyDir</code>, <code>ephemeral</code>, <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code>, <code>projected</code>, <code>secret</code> — <strong><code>hostPath</code> is completely disallowed</strong></td><td>easypj's writable <code>/tmp</code> uses <code>emptyDir</code>, never <code>hostPath</code> — this is WHY every example in this chapter reaches for <code>emptyDir</code> and not a node-local mount</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>seccompProfile.type</code></td><td>must be set (not undefined) to <code>RuntimeDefault</code> or <code>Localhost</code> — <code>Unconfined</code> is rejected</td><td><code>type: RuntimeDefault</code> in every hardened pod spec</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code></td><td>must be explicitly <code>false</code> (Linux pods only)</td><td>set explicitly in every §40.5 container securityContext</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>runAsNonRoot</code></td><td>must be explicitly <code>true</code> (at pod or container level)</td><td>set at the pod level in every example</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Host namespaces</td><td><code>hostNetwork</code>, <code>hostPID</code>, <code>hostIPC</code> must all be undefined or <code>false</code></td><td>easypj's Pods never set any of the three</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The One Field This Chapter Recommends That PSS Does NOT Actually Require</h5>
                    <p><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> — despite being one of the most repeated hardening recommendations in this chapter — is <strong>not</strong> a field the built-in <code>restricted</code> Pod Security Standard checks at all. PSS's field list stops at capabilities/volumes/seccomp/privilege-escalation/non-root/host-namespaces/SELinux. A Pod can pass <code>restricted</code> admission with a fully writable root filesystem. Read-only-rootfs is still exactly the right thing to do (it's real defense in depth against a compromised process persisting malware), but if you need it ENFORCED rather than merely recommended, you need an extra layer — a Kyverno/Gatekeeper policy or a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> that checks it explicitly, because PSS alone won't catch a Pod that omits it.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Preventive vs Detective: What Catches an Exploit That Bypasses seccomp/AppArmor?</h4>
                <p>seccomp and AppArmor are <strong>preventive</strong> — they block a forbidden syscall or file access before it happens. But a sufficiently novel attack (an application-layer RCE using only ALLOWED syscalls, like reading a file the app was already permitted to read) sails right through both without triggering anything. That's the gap <strong>runtime threat detection</strong> tools like <strong>Falco</strong> close.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 PREVENTIVE (seccomp/AppArmor)              DETECTIVE (Falco)
 ─────────────────────────────              ──────────────────
 Decides BEFORE the action:                 Watches AFTER the action:
   "is this syscall/path allowed?"            "does this BEHAVIOR look wrong?"
        │                                          │
        ▼                                          ▼
  ALLOW or SIGKILL/EPERM                    eBPF hooks stream every syscall
  (binary, pre-defined rules)               to a rules engine in real time:
                                               • shell spawned inside a container
                                               • outbound connection to a new IP
                                               • write to /etc/shadow or /root/.ssh
                                               • a process reading env vars then
                                                 opening a raw socket (exfil pattern)
                                                      │
                                                      ▼
                                              ALERT → Slack/PagerDuty/SIEM
                                              (Falco does NOT block by default —
                                               it's an alarm, not a lock)
                        </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The Complete Picture</h5>
                    <p>seccomp/AppArmor answer "what CAN happen" (a fixed allowlist, enforced by the kernel). Falco answers "what IS happening RIGHT NOW that looks suspicious" (a live behavioral signal, evaluated against known-bad patterns). Neither replaces the other: a well-hardened pod still runs Falco because the hardening only blocks KNOWN-dangerous syscalls, and an attacker operating entirely within the allowed syscall set (which includes basically everything a normal app needs — read, write, connect) would be invisible to seccomp but would very likely trip a Falco rule like "shell spawned in a container that never spawns shells." This is why the Chapter 40.1 checklist calls hardening "defense in depth" rather than "defense" — Falco is effectively an unlisted seventh layer that watches the other six for signs they were beaten.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.6 Supply Chain Security</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <p>Securing the container image supply chain prevents compromised or vulnerable images from running in your cluster.</p>

                <div class="icon-feature-list">
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">📌</span><div><h5>Pin Image Versions</h5><p>Use specific tags (<code>v2.1</code>) or SHA digests — never <code>:latest</code></p></div></div>
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">🔍</span><div><h5>Scan Images</h5><p>Run vulnerability scanners (Trivy, Grype) in CI/CD before deployment</p></div></div>
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">✍️</span><div><h5>Sign Images</h5><p>Use cosign/Notation to verify image provenance and integrity</p></div></div>
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">🏢</span><div><h5>Allowlist Registries</h5><p>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy to restrict image sources to trusted repos only</p></div></div>
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">📦</span><div><h5>Minimal Base Images</h5><p>Use <code>-slim</code> or distroless images — fewer packages = fewer vulnerabilities</p></div></div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">image-security-best-practices</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ✅ Best Practice 1: Use specific image tags (NEVER use :latest)
containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1        # ✅ Pinned version
    <span class="comment"># image: easypj/jobpost:latest     # ❌ Mutable, unpredictable</span>
    <span class="comment"># image: easypj/jobpost             # ❌ Defaults to :latest</span>

<span class="comment"># ✅ Best Practice 2: Use image digests for maximum immutability</span>
containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj/jobpost@sha256:abc123...  # ✅ Content-addressable

<span class="comment"># ✅ Best Practice 3: Set imagePullPolicy</span>
containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
    imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent     <span class="comment"># or Always for security-critical apps</span>

<span class="comment"># ✅ Best Practice 4: Use minimal base images</span>
<span class="comment"># Dockerfile:</span>
<span class="comment"># FROM python:3.13-slim              # ✅ Minimal — fewer vulnerabilities</span>
<span class="comment"># FROM python:3.13                    # ❌ Full — larger attack surface</span>
<span class="comment"># FROM ubuntu:24.04                   # ❌ General purpose — bloated</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Restrict Image Sources with ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">restrict-registries.yaml — only allow trusted registries</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
metadata:
  name: restrict-image-registries
spec:
  failurePolicy: Fail
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
      - apiGroups: [""]
        apiVersions: ["v1"]
        operations: ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]
        resources: ["pods"]
  validations:
    - expression: >-
        object.spec.containers.all(c,
          c.image.startsWith('easypj/') ||
          c.image.startsWith('registry.example.com/') ||
          c.image.startsWith('gcr.io/distroless/'))
      message: "Images must come from approved registries: easypj/, registry.example.com/, gcr.io/distroless/"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Keyless Signing — Why Modern cosign Doesn't Ask You to Store a Private Key</h4>
                <p>Early image-signing setups generated a long-lived cosign keypair and stored the private key in a CI secret — which just relocates the "one leaked credential compromises everything" problem instead of solving it. Sigstore's <strong>keyless signing</strong> removes the standing key entirely.</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>OIDC identity (e.g. GitHub Actions job token)</td><td>Proves "this signature came from CI workflow X on commit Y" — no password, short-lived token issued per job run</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Fulcio</td><td>A certificate authority that, given a valid OIDC token, issues a code-signing certificate valid for ~10 minutes — long enough to sign one build, gone before it could ever be stolen and reused</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Rekor</td><td>A public, tamper-evident transparency log — every signature is recorded permanently, so anyone (including you, during an incident) can later prove exactly which identity signed which digest and when</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>⚡ Speed Tip</h5>
                    <p><code>cosign sign registry.example.com/easypj@sha256:&lt;digest&gt;</code> with no <code>--key</code> flag triggers keyless mode automatically in CI (it detects the OIDC token from the CI environment). Verification is <code>cosign verify --certificate-identity=... --certificate-oidc-issuer=... registry.example.com/easypj@sha256:&lt;digest&gt;</code> — you pin WHO was allowed to sign (a specific GitHub repo/workflow), not a key file that could go stale or leak.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 ImagePolicyWebhook — the Built-In Alternative to Kyverno/Gatekeeper</h5>
                    <p>Everything above (ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, Kyverno, Gatekeeper) is either built directly into the API server (VAP) or a separate policy-engine Deployment you install. <code>ImagePolicyWebhook</code> is a third option: a built-in admission plugin (<code>--enable-admission-plugins=...,ImagePolicyWebhook</code>) that calls out to an EXTERNAL HTTP service you write, sending it the image reference and letting that service return allow/deny. It predates OPA/Kyverno and is rarely the first choice for new clusters today (CEL-based ValidatingAdmissionPolicy covers most of the same ground without a separate service to run) — but it's still the answer when an org already has a central image-approval service and just needs Kubernetes to consult it.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.7 Multi-Tenancy & Namespace Isolation</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>In multi-tenant clusters, namespaces are the primary isolation boundary. But namespaces alone aren't enough — you need RBAC, ResourceQuota, LimitRange, NetworkPolicy, and Pod Security working together. Each blocks a DIFFERENT attack: RBAC stops "read the neighbor's secrets," ResourceQuota + LimitRange stop "starve the neighbor" (a noisy-neighbor / denial-of-service), NetworkPolicy stops "reach the neighbor's pods," and Pod Security stops "run a privileged pod that escapes to the node." Remove any one control and that specific attack class reopens — this is the same defense-in-depth thesis from §40.1, applied per-namespace instead of cluster-wide.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Multi-Tenancy Isolation Stack</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │              MULTI-TENANCY ISOLATION STACK                 │
 ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
 │                                                            │
 │  Namespace: team-a        │  Namespace: team-b             │
 │  ┌──────────────────┐    │  ┌──────────────────┐          │
 │  │ RBAC: team-a-role │    │  │ RBAC: team-b-role │          │
 │  │ (only team-a      │    │  │ (only team-b      │          │
 │  │  resources)       │    │  │  resources)       │          │
 │  ├──────────────────┤    │  ├──────────────────┤          │
 │  │ ResourceQuota:    │    │  │ ResourceQuota:    │          │
 │  │  CPU: 4 cores     │    │  │  CPU: 8 cores     │          │
 │  │  Memory: 8Gi      │    │  │  Memory: 16Gi     │          │
 │  ├──────────────────┤    │  ├──────────────────┤          │
 │  │ NetworkPolicy:    │    │  │ NetworkPolicy:    │          │
 │  │  deny-all +       │    │  │  deny-all +       │          │
 │  │  allow internal   │    │  │  allow internal   │          │
 │  ├──────────────────┤    │  ├──────────────────┤          │
 │  │ Pod Security:     │    │  │ Pod Security:     │          │
 │  │  restricted       │    │  │  restricted       │          │
 │  └──────────────────┘    │  └──────────────────┘          │
 │                           │                                │
 └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">tenant-namespace.yaml — complete tenant isolation</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># 1. Namespace with Pod Security Standard</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: easypj
  labels:
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
    <span class="comment"># pin the enforced version so a cluster upgrade can't silently change</span>
    <span class="comment"># which checks "restricted" means — see the callout below</span>
    <span class="comment"># pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version: v1.31</span>
---
<span class="comment"># 2. Default deny-all NetworkPolicy</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: ["Ingress", "Egress"]
---
<span class="comment"># 3. Allow internal communication within namespace</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-internal
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: ["Ingress", "Egress"]
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector: {}            <span class="comment"># Allow from same namespace</span>
  egress:
    - to:
        - podSelector: {}            <span class="comment"># Allow to same namespace</span>
    - to:                            <span class="comment"># Allow DNS resolution</span>
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              k8s-app: kube-dns
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
---
<span class="comment"># 4. ResourceQuota to limit resource consumption</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "4"
    requests.memory: 8Gi
    limits.cpu: "8"
    limits.memory: 16Gi
    pods: "20"
    services: "10"
    secrets: "20"
    configmaps: "20"
---
<span class="comment"># 5. LimitRange — per-pod/container defaults so ResourceQuota can't be exhausted by one giant Pod</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-limits
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container
    default:                 <span class="comment"># applied if a container omits limits</span>
      cpu: 500m
      memory: 256Mi
    defaultRequest:          <span class="comment"># applied if a container omits requests</span>
      cpu: 100m
      memory: 128Mi
    max:                     <span class="comment"># hard ceiling — a single container can never exceed this</span>
      cpu: "2"
      memory: 2Gi
    min:                     <span class="comment"># floor — rejects a container requesting less (misconfiguration guard)</span>
      cpu: 50m
      memory: 64Mi</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 ResourceQuota vs LimitRange — Different Scopes, Same Goal</h5>
                    <p><strong>ResourceQuota</strong> caps the NAMESPACE total (e.g. "this namespace may never request more than 4 CPU cores across every Pod combined"). <strong>LimitRange</strong> caps a single CONTAINER/Pod and fills in defaults for ones that omit <code>resources:</code> entirely. They compose: without a LimitRange, one greedy Pod with no resource requests set could consume the ENTIRE namespace's ResourceQuota by itself (since an unset request often means "unbounded" or falls back to a very permissive scheduler default); the LimitRange's <code>defaultRequest</code>/<code>default</code> values ensure every Pod gets a sane, bounded footprint even if its author forgot to set one.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Pin the Pod Security Standard Version, Don't Just the Level</h5>
                    <p>The <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code> label alone always resolves to "whatever the CURRENT Kubernetes minor version defines as restricted" — and that definition legitimately changes between releases (new fields get added to the standard as Kubernetes itself grows new security-relevant fields). Add <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version: v1.31</code> alongside it to pin the exact rule-set, so a routine cluster upgrade can't silently start rejecting previously-compliant Pods the moment the control plane's default "latest" resolves to a newer, stricter definition. Omitting the version label is fine for a lab/exam environment; pin it in production so PSA behavior only changes when YOU decide to bump the version label, not automatically on upgrade day.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch40-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — the machinery behind every hardening control</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Every control in this chapter is a config flag or a YAML field on the surface — but each one sits on top of a real subsystem: an audit pipeline, a key hierarchy, a syscall filter table, a signature store. Understanding the machinery is what turns "I copied this YAML" into "I know exactly what breaks if I get it wrong."</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌─────────── WHERE EACH CONTROL ACTUALLY LIVES ───────────┐
   │                                                          │
   │  RBAC verbs        ──▶  authorization webhook chain      │
   │  Encryption config  ──▶  apiserver storage layer (etcd)  │
   │  Audit policy       ──▶  apiserver request/response tap  │
   │  seccomp/AppArmor   ──▶  container runtime → kernel LSM  │
   │  Image signature    ──▶  OCI registry + admission plugin │
   │                                                          │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   Six sections of "what to configure." One diagram of "where it runs."
                    </pre>

                    <h5>① The Audit Pipeline — Stages, Levels &amp; Where the Log Actually Comes From</h5>
                    <p>An audit policy doesn't log "events" in the abstract — every API request passes through four well-defined <strong>stages</strong>, and your policy's <code>level</code> decides how much of EACH stage gets written. Missing this distinction is why audit configs silently under- or over-log.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl delete secret easypj-db-secret -n easypj
        │
        ▼
   📡 kube-apiserver audit tap
        │
        ├─ RequestReceived  ── request parsed, not yet authorized (often omitted — noisy)
        ├─ ResponseStarted  ── long-running/watch calls only (list --watch, exec, port-forward)
        ├─ ResponseComplete ── the request finished          ◀── policy LEVEL decided HERE
        └─ Panic            ── the apiserver crashed mid-request (always captured if possible)
        │
        ▼
   audit policy evaluates rules TOP-TO-BOTTOM, first match wins:
        rule 1: secrets      → level: Metadata          (who/verb/when, NOT the body)
        rule 2: rbac objects → level: RequestResponse    (full body — high value, low risk)
        rule 3: catch-all    → level: Metadata
        │
        ▼
   /var/log/kube-audit.log  (or a webhook backend — SIEM, Falco, etc.)
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Captures</th><th>Use For</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>None</code></td><td>nothing — the rule matches and drops it</td><td>noisy read-only chatter (health checks, <code>events</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Metadata</code></td><td>who / verb / resource / timestamp — NOT bodies</td><td>Secrets, ConfigMaps — anything whose body is sensitive</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Request</code></td><td>Metadata + the request body</td><td>rarely used alone — usually you want the response too</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>RequestResponse</code></td><td>Metadata + request AND response bodies</td><td>RBAC objects — high forensic value, no sensitive payload</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Rules are evaluated top-to-bottom and the <em>first</em> match wins — that's why the narrowest, most sensitive rules (Secrets at Metadata) must be listed BEFORE any broad catch-all, or the catch-all will win and could log at a more verbose level than you intended.</div>

                    <h5>② RBAC's Dangerous Verbs — How <code>escalate</code>/<code>bind</code>/<code>impersonate</code> Actually Work</h5>
                    <p>These verbs aren't just "more permissions" — each one lets a subject reach OUTSIDE its own granted rules to gain new authority. Seeing the mechanism makes it obvious why they're red flags in any RBAC review.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Subject: easypj-ci-sa   (has: create/update on roles, NO explicit pod-exec rights)
        │
        │ normally, the API server's RBAC authorizer would REJECT granting
        │ a Role with MORE power than the requester already holds...
        ▼
   ...UNLESS the requester has the "escalate" verb on roles/clusterroles.
        │
        ▼
   PATCH role/some-role  { rules: [{resources:["pods/exec"], verbs:["create"]}] }
        │                  ▲ this rule exceeds easypj-ci-sa's own permissions —
        │                  │  "escalate" is the ONE verb that bypasses that check
        ▼
   easypj-ci-sa now controls a Role granting pod-exec.
   Pair it with "bind" (create a RoleBinding to ANY role, even ones it can't grant)
   and the SA has just handed itself a permission it never legitimately had.
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>What it bypasses</th><th>Real-world trigger</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>escalate</code></td><td>the "can't grant more than you hold" rule on Role/ClusterRole writes</td><td>a CI/CD service account with broad <code>roles</code> write access</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>bind</code></td><td>the same rule, but for RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding creation</td><td>a "helper" ClusterRole meant only for namespace admins</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>impersonate</code></td><td>identity itself — <code>--as=</code> literally becomes another user/SA</td><td>debugging tooling left with a standing impersonation grant</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>*</code> (wildcard)</td><td>every check above, at once, within its scope</td><td>"just make it work" RBAC copy-pasted from a tutorial</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> <code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa</code> is the exam-fast way to prove a subject does NOT have these verbs before you sign off on a Role. Absence of evidence in a quick manual read is not evidence of absence — always run the command.</div>

                    <h5>③ Encryption at Rest — aescbc vs KMS v2, and Why Provider ORDER Is Load-Bearing</h5>
                    <p><code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> isn't a single on/off switch — it's an ordered chain of providers the apiserver tries in sequence, and the ordering decides both what gets WRITTEN and what can still be READ during a migration.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   WRITE path (kubectl apply/create a Secret)
        │
        ▼
   apiserver storage layer ──▶ tries providers TOP-TO-BOTTOM, uses the FIRST ONE to WRITE
        providers: [ aescbc, identity ]
                       ▲
                       └── this one wins for every new write — "identity" never gets used to write
                            as long as it's listed AFTER a real provider

   READ path (kubectl get a pre-existing Secret)
        │
        ▼
   apiserver tries EVERY provider in order until ONE successfully decrypts
        aescbc fails to decrypt plaintext-shaped bytes → falls through
        identity succeeds on old plaintext data           → returns it
   This is exactly how migration works: old data still readable via identity,
   new data written encrypted via aescbc — until you re-`kubectl replace` everything.
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Key model</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>aescbc</code></td><td>one static 32-byte key, stored in the config file on disk</td><td>simplest to set up; rotating means editing the file and re-encrypting everything</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kms</code> (v2)</td><td>a remote Key Management Service issues per-object Data Encryption Keys (DEKs), wrapped by one root Key Encryption Key (KEK)</td><td>faster rotation (rotate the KEK, DEKs are cheap to regenerate), key never touches local disk</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>identity</code></td><td>none — reads/writes plaintext</td><td>MUST be last when enabling, first when disabling (reverse the list to decrypt everything)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Enabling encryption only changes behavior going forward. Every Secret written BEFORE the flag was set stays plaintext in etcd until you force a rewrite: <code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code>. This is one of the most commonly-missed steps in real audits.</div>

                    <h5>④ seccomp &amp; AppArmor — From YAML Field to Kernel Enforcement</h5>
                    <p>Setting <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code> is one line of YAML, but three different components have to cooperate before a single syscall is actually blocked.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   securityContext.seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault  (in the Pod spec)
        │
        ▼
   kubelet reads the field when creating the container
        │
        ▼
   CRI call to the container runtime (containerd/CRI-O) includes the profile
        │
        ▼
   runtime loads a BPF syscall-filter program into the kernel for that
   container's process tree — RuntimeDefault = the runtime's own curated
   blocklist (ptrace, mount, reboot, kernel-module loading, etc.)
        │
        ▼
   syscall arrives at the kernel ──▶ BPF filter checks it ──▶ ALLOW or SIGKILL/EPERM
   AppArmor runs in PARALLEL as a separate Linux Security Module (LSM) hook,
   checking file paths and network operations against a loaded profile —
   independent of seccomp, which only sees syscall NUMBERS, not paths.
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Sees</th><th>Blocks</th><th>Sandboxes further with</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>seccomp</td><td>syscall number + args</td><td><code>ptrace</code>, <code>mount</code>, <code>reboot</code>, raw sockets</td><td>custom JSON profiles in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>AppArmor</td><td>file paths, network ops</td><td>reading <code>/etc/shadow</code>, unexpected outbound binds</td><td>profiles loaded on every node, referenced by name</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>RuntimeClass (gVisor/Kata)</td><td>the ENTIRE syscall surface</td><td>kernel exploits — the "kernel" the container sees is emulated/virtualized</td><td><code>runtimeClassName: gvisor</code> on the Pod spec</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> seccomp and AppArmor are independent LSM/BPF mechanisms — enabling one does NOT enable the other. The restricted Pod Security Standard requires seccomp <code>RuntimeDefault</code> (or better) but does not mandate AppArmor, since AppArmor profiles must be pre-loaded per-node and aren't portable across distros the way seccomp is.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ Supply-Chain Attestation — How cosign Signatures &amp; SBOMs Are Actually Stored and Checked</h5>
                    <p>"Sign the image" sounds abstract until you see WHERE the signature lives and HOW admission finds it. cosign doesn't modify your image bytes at all — it publishes a second, linked artifact next to it in the same registry.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   registry.example.com/easypj@sha256:abc123...           ← the image manifest (unchanged)
   registry.example.com/easypj:sha256-abc123....sig        ← cosign PUSHES a sibling tag
   registry.example.com/easypj:sha256-abc123....sbom       ← optional: attached SBOM
        │
        ▼
   Pod admission (Kyverno/Gatekeeper verify-images policy):
        1. resolve the Pod's image digest
        2. GET the .sig artifact from the SAME registry
        3. verify the signature against your configured public key
        4. ALLOW only if verification succeeds — else reject at admission time
        │
        ▼
   SLSA provenance (optional, stored the same way) answers a DIFFERENT
   question than the signature: not "is this untampered" but "which CI
   pipeline / which source commit produced these exact bytes" — a forged
   build from a compromised laptop fails provenance even with a valid sig.
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Artifact</th><th>Answers</th><th>Fails to catch</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Digest pin</td><td>"is this the exact bytes I tested?"</td><td>whether those bytes were ever malicious to begin with</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>cosign signature</td><td>"was this published by a key I trust?"</td><td>vulnerabilities inside an otherwise legitimately-signed image</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>SBOM</td><td>"what packages/versions are inside?"</td><td>nothing by itself — it's an index, pair it with a CVE feed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>SLSA provenance</td><td>"which CI run, which commit built this?"</td><td>vulnerabilities already present in the source code</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Each artifact answers a narrow question — stack all four (digest + signature + SBOM + provenance) rather than treating any single one as "the" supply-chain control. This mirrors the chapter's whole thesis: no single layer is ever enough.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ NodeRestriction — Why a Compromised Kubelet Can't Take Over the Cluster</h5>
                    <p>Every kubelet authenticates to the API server as <code>system:node:&lt;nodename&gt;</code> and, without NodeRestriction, could technically edit ANY Node or Pod object — including a different node's. NodeRestriction is the admission plugin that closes that gap.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubelet on node-3 authenticates as: system:node:node-3
        │
        │  WITHOUT NodeRestriction admission plugin enabled:
        │  RBAC alone (system:node ClusterRole) still permits writes to
        │  labels/taints on ANY Node object — including node-1, node-2...
        ▼
   WITH --enable-admission-plugins=...,NodeRestriction,...
        │
        ▼
   NodeRestriction admission checks: "is the object being modified
   node-3's OWN Node object, or a Pod bound to node-3?"
        │
        ├─ YES → allow (a kubelet must update its own Node status/Pods)
        └─ NO  → reject, even though RBAC alone would have said yes
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> NodeRestriction is a Layer-1 (Infra) control that most RBAC reviews never think to check, because RBAC itself looks correct — the restriction is enforced entirely inside admission, one level deeper than <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> will show you.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch40-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Security is DEFENSE IN DEPTH across 6 layers</strong> — infra, authN/Z, admission, workload, network, data. A breach must defeat EVERY layer; one control alone is never enough.</li>
                    <li><strong>RBAC hardening = least privilege + watching the dangerous verbs</strong> (<code>escalate</code>, <code>bind</code>, <code>impersonate</code>, and wildcard <code>*</code>) that let a subject grant itself more power than it started with. Over-broad RBAC (wildcards, cluster-admin "just to make it work") is the single most common real-world misconfiguration — audit bindings regularly.</li>
                    <li><strong>Secrets are base64-encoded in etcd by default, NOT encrypted</strong> — encrypt at rest with <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> (aescbc or KMS v2), prefer volume mounts over env vars, disable token automount, and consider external stores (Vault, cloud SM) for production.</li>
                    <li><strong>The API server is the front door</strong> — <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>, the right admission plugins, and an audit policy that uses <code>Metadata</code> for Secrets (never leak bodies) and <code>RequestResponse</code> for RBAC objects (full forensic detail, no sensitive payload).</li>
                    <li><strong>Runtime controls box in a compromised process</strong> — <code>runAsNonRoot</code>, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>, <code>drop: ["ALL"]</code> capabilities, and <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code> together mean an exploited container can barely move even after the exploit succeeds.</li>
                    <li><strong>Supply-chain security stacks four independent checks</strong> — pin by digest (reproducibility), scan in CI (known CVEs), sign with cosign (provenance/trust), and allowlist registries (source control) — <code>:latest</code> silently defeats all four at once.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>resourceNames</code> narrows <code>get</code>/<code>update</code>/<code>delete</code>/<code>patch</code> but NOT <code>list</code>/<code>watch</code>/<code>create</code></strong> — the object name isn't known yet for those verbs, so keep list/watch rules as tight as the resource type otherwise allows.</li>
                    <li><strong>Multi-tenancy is a STACK, not a single boundary</strong> — RBAC + ResourceQuota + LimitRange + default-deny NetworkPolicy + PSA=restricted, applied together per namespace. Namespaces alone are a soft boundary; untrusted tenants need sandboxing (gVisor/Kata) or separate clusters.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch40-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <p class="section-lead">RBAC-specific gotchas (Role vs ClusterRole, <code>apiGroup</code>, <code>resourceNames</code>) are covered inline in <a href="#s40-2">§40.2's RBAC Gotchas box</a> — this list covers the rest of the chapter.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Audit at <code>RequestResponse</code> on Secrets logs their BODIES</strong> into the audit log — a plaintext credential leak in a file you ship to a SIEM. Always cap Secrets at <code>Metadata</code> level.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong><code>:latest</code> (or any tag with no digest)</strong> makes deployments non-reproducible AND bypasses scan/sign guarantees — the tag can be retagged out from under you after it was scanned. Pin to digests in production.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Leaving <code>automountServiceAccountToken</code> on for every pod</strong> needlessly exposes live API credentials to any process — or attacker — running inside it, even pods that never call the API.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong><code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> only protects NEW writes</strong> — Secrets written before you enabled it stay plaintext in etcd until you force a rewrite with <code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code>.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Namespaces are NOT a hard security boundary</strong> on their own — kernel and node resources are still shared. Untrusted tenants need RuntimeClass sandboxing (gVisor/Kata) or separate clusters, not just separate namespaces.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>A read-only root filesystem breaks apps that write to disk</strong> unless you add explicit <code>emptyDir</code>/PVC mounts for their writable paths (e.g. <code>/tmp</code>, upload directories, cache dirs).</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Labeling a namespace <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code> does NOT touch pods already running</strong> — Pod Security Admission only evaluates objects at CREATE/UPDATE time. Existing non-compliant pods keep running untouched right after you apply the label; the surprise hits later, on the next rollout, scale-up, or manual pod delete, when the replacement suddenly gets rejected. Audit with <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted</code> and <code>kubectl get events</code> BEFORE flipping to <code>enforce</code>, so you find every non-compliant workload on your own schedule instead of during an unrelated deploy.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch40-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Exam</strong> — You may be asked to configure audit logging. Remember the four audit levels: <code>None</code> (no logging), <code>Metadata</code> (log request metadata only), <code>Request</code> (log request body), <code>RequestResponse</code> (log both request and response body). Never log Secret content — use <code>Metadata</code> level for Secrets.</p>
                <p><strong>Audit what a subject can really do:</strong> <code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa -n easypj</code>. Install the krew <code>who-can</code> plugin to invert the question — <code>kubectl who-can delete pods -n easypj</code> lists every subject with that permission in one shot.</p>
                <p><strong>Stack per-namespace tenancy controls in one command block:</strong> RBAC + ResourceQuota + LimitRange + NetworkPolicy + <code>kubectl label ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code> — that one-liner is the fastest way to turn on the strongest built-in pod hardening during the exam.</p>
                <p><strong>Verify encryption-at-rest by reading the raw etcd value:</strong> a <code>k8s:enc:aescbc</code> prefix in the hexdump means it worked; readable plaintext means it didn't. Scan images in CI (Trivy/Grype) and fail the build on CRITICAL/HIGH — far cheaper than catching it in the running cluster.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch40-commands"></span>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 40)</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>audit what a ServiceAccount can do</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>find risky cluster-admin / wildcard bindings</td><td><code>kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep -i cluster-admin</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>invert the question — who can do X (krew plugin)</td><td><code>kubectl who-can delete pods -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>turn on restricted Pod Security on a namespace</td><td><code>kubectl label ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>re-encrypt all existing secrets after enabling encryption-at-rest</td><td><code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>verify a secret is encrypted directly in etcd</td><td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/easypj/easypj-db-secret --cacert=... --cert=... --key=... | hexdump -C | head</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>supply-chain scan in CI (fail on criticals)</td><td><code>trivy image easypj/jobpost:v2.1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>sign an image at build time</td><td><code>cosign sign registry.example.com/easypj@sha256:&lt;digest&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>create a least-privilege Role imperatively</td><td><code>kubectl create role secret-reader -n easypj --verb=get,list --resource=secrets</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>bind a Role to a ServiceAccount imperatively</td><td><code>kubectl create rolebinding secret-reader-bind -n easypj --role=secret-reader --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-web-sa</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>API-server hardening flags (static-pod manifest)</td><td><code>--anonymous-auth=false --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit/policy.yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>check for wildcard permissions across all roles</td><td><code>kubectl get roles,clusterroles -A -o json | jq -r '.items[] | select(.rules[]?.verbs[]? == "*")'</code></td></tr>
