            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 40 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the hardening checklist's mental models — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Give easypj's ServiceAccount the minimum it needs — read its own ConfigMap, nothing else. How do you scope it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="comparison-table">
                            <table>
                                <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Verbs</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td>get on the ConfigMap</td><td><code>get</code></td><td><code>resourceNames: ["easypj-config"]</code> — narrowed to ONE object</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>list/watch on configmaps</td><td><code>list</code>, <code>watch</code></td><td>type-wide — <code>resourceNames</code> cannot scope these verbs</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A Role scoped to <code>get</code> on that ConfigMap (with <code>resourceNames</code>) plus <code>list</code>/<code>watch</code> on configmaps generally, bound to the SA — no wildcards, nothing cluster-wide. <code>resourceNames</code> narrows <code>get</code>/<code>update</code>/<code>delete</code>/<code>patch</code> but list/watch can't be name-scoped because the object name isn't known at request time for those verbs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Enable encryption at rest for Secrets, end to end. What are the steps and what's the one step people forget?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Write /etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml with an aescbc (or kms) provider, identity LAST</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Add --encryption-provider-config to the kube-apiserver static-pod manifest</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Mount the config file into the apiserver pod (hostPath volume + volumeMount)</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. THE STEP PEOPLE FORGET — re-encrypt what already exists:</span>
kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Configure an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> (aescbc or KMS) and point the apiserver at it with <code>--encryption-provider-config</code>. List a real provider first and <code>identity</code> last so new writes ARE encrypted. Then re-<code>replace</code> existing secrets so they're re-encrypted too — the config only protects new writes going forward, never retroactively.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List every control that hardens a workload (Pod) itself, not the cluster around it.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>Identity</h5><p><code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, explicit <code>runAsUser</code></p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>Filesystem</h5><p><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> + emptyDir for writable paths</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>Capabilities</h5><p><code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code>, add back only what's proven needed</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>Syscalls</h5><p><code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code></p></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>runAsNonRoot</code>, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, drop ALL capabilities, seccomp <code>RuntimeDefault</code>, and <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>. Together these mean a successfully exploited process is still non-root, can't write to the image, can't escalate, can't make dangerous syscalls, and has no live API token to abuse.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an audit policy that captures useful forensic data but never leaks a Secret's contents.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
rules:
- level: Metadata            <span class="comment"># Secrets: who/what/when, NEVER the body</span>
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["secrets", "configmaps"]
- level: RequestResponse     <span class="comment"># RBAC: full detail, no sensitive payload</span>
  resources:
  - group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io"
    resources: ["roles","rolebindings","clusterroles","clusterrolebindings"]
- level: Metadata             <span class="comment"># catch-all</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Log Secrets (and ConfigMaps, which often carry near-secret config) at <code>Metadata</code> level only. Log sensitive RBAC objects at <code>RequestResponse</code> for full forensic value — their bodies aren't sensitive the way a Secret's value is. Rules are evaluated top-to-bottom with first-match-wins, so order the specific rules before the catch-all.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code> actually provide, and is it required by the restricted Pod Security Standard?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault   <span class="comment"># required baseline for pod-security.../enforce=restricted</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>It's the container runtime's default syscall filter, blocking a curated list of dangerous syscalls (<code>ptrace</code>, <code>mount</code>, <code>reboot</code>, etc.) at the kernel level via a BPF filter program. Yes — it's a required baseline for the <code>restricted</code> Pod Security standard, though the equivalent AppArmor control is NOT mandated since AppArmor profiles must be pre-loaded per node.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why use a read-only root filesystem, and how does easypj's Django app still write its temp files?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">securityContext:
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
volumeMounts:
- name: tmp
  mountPath: /tmp             <span class="comment"># the ONLY writable panel</span>
volumes:
- name: tmp
  emptyDir: {}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>It prevents tampering/persistence by an attacker — malware can't be written into the image's layers. Apps that must write (Django's cache, upload temp files) get explicit <code>emptyDir</code> or PVC mounts for just those paths. Analogy: it's like welding the engine bay shut — the car still runs off the image, but nobody can swap parts while it's moving; anything that needs to write gets one deliberately unlocked panel.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Restrict which container registries easypj images may be pulled from. What enforces it and when?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">validations:
- expression: >-
    object.spec.containers.all(c,
      c.image.startsWith('registry.example.com/easypj/'))
  message: "Images must come from the approved easypj registry"</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>An admission-time policy — Kyverno, Gatekeeper (OPA), or a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> (CEL, built-in) — that allowlists approved registries and rejects everything else. It runs at Pod CREATE/UPDATE, before the object is ever persisted, so an unapproved image never even makes it to the scheduler.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Reduce the attack surface of easypj's default ServiceAccount without breaking pods that DO need API access.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
spec:
  automountServiceAccountToken: false   <span class="comment"># most pods don't call the API at all</span>
  serviceAccountName: easypj-web-sa      <span class="comment"># dedicated SA, not "default"</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> at the pod (or ServiceAccount) level so no pod silently inherits a live API token. For the pods that genuinely need it (an operator, a controller), leave automount on and pair it with a tightly-scoped Role — never the shared <code>default</code> SA.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Isolate two cooperating teams sharing one cluster. Which objects go into each team's namespace?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>Blocks "read the neighbor"</h5><p>Scoped RBAC (Role + RoleBinding, no cluster verbs)</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>Blocks "starve the neighbor"</h5><p>ResourceQuota + LimitRange</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>Blocks "reach the neighbor"</h5><p>Default-deny NetworkPolicy</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card"><h5>Blocks "escape to the node"</h5><p>PSA <code>enforce=restricted</code></p></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Scoped RBAC, ResourceQuota + LimitRange (fair share), a default-deny NetworkPolicy, and PSA=restricted — stacked together in each namespace. Each control blocks a DIFFERENT attack class; remove any one and that class reopens. This is soft multi-tenancy for cooperating teams — untrusted tenants need node-level or cluster-level isolation instead.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Find every risky cluster-wide binding in the cluster before an audit. Which commands?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep -i cluster-admin
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa
kubectl who-can delete pods -n easypj              <span class="comment"># krew plugin, inverts the question</span>
kubectl get roles,clusterroles -A -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.rules[]?.verbs[]? == "*")'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Audit <code>ClusterRoleBindings</code> for <code>cluster-admin</code> and wildcard subjects, and specifically look for the <code>escalate</code>/<code>bind</code>/<code>impersonate</code> verbs on any Role. <code>kubectl auth can-i --list</code> asks "what can THIS subject do"; the krew <code>who-can</code> plugin inverts it to "who can do THIS action" — both are exam-fast and audit-useful.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
