                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 40 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (5)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of hardening checklist items — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Role in the <code>easypj</code> namespace called <code>secret-reader</code> that allows only <code>get</code> and <code>list</code> on Secrets. Bind it to a ServiceAccount called <code>easypj-app</code>. Then verify the binding works.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create the SA</span>
kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-app -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Create the role</span>
kubectl create role secret-reader -n easypj \
--verb=get,list \
--resource=secrets

<span class="comment"># Create the binding</span>
kubectl create rolebinding secret-reader-binding -n easypj \
--role=secret-reader \
--serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-app

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i get secrets -n easypj \
--as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-app
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete secrets -n easypj \
--as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-app
<span class="comment"># no   ← correct! delete is not granted</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The imperative <code>kubectl create role</code> command is fastest for the CKA. The <code>--verb=get,list</code> flag sets allowed verbs, <code>--resource=secrets</code> sets the resource type. A RoleBinding connects the role to the service account. Verify with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> using the <code>--as</code> flag to impersonate the service account. Note the ServiceAccount format: <code>namespace:name</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Enable encryption at rest for Secrets. Create an EncryptionConfiguration using <code>aescbc</code> provider. Then update the API server to use it.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Generate an encryption key</span>
head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64
<span class="comment"># Output: Bz4w1F...==</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Create the encryption config</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF &gt; /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
  - resources:
      - secrets
    providers:
      - aescbc:
          keys:
            - name: key1
              secret: Bz4w1F...==
      - identity: {}
EOF

<span class="comment"># 3. Edit kube-apiserver manifest</span>
vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># Add to command args:</span>
<span class="comment">#   --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># Add volume:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - name: encryption-config</span>
<span class="comment">#     hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#       path: /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#       type: File</span>
<span class="comment"># Add volumeMount:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - name: encryption-config</span>
<span class="comment">#     mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#     readOnly: true</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. API server restarts automatically (static pod)</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. Re-encrypt existing secrets</span>
kubectl get secrets --all-namespaces -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The EncryptionConfiguration lists providers in order — the <strong>first</strong> provider is used for new writes, remaining providers are tried for reads (enabling migration). The <code>identity: {}</code> fallback allows reading pre-existing unencrypted Secrets. After enabling encryption, you must re-encrypt existing Secrets with <code>kubectl get ... | kubectl replace</code>. This is a common CKA task — remember to mount the config file into the API server pod.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Pod in the <code>easypj</code> namespace that follows all security best practices: non-root user, read-only filesystem, drop all capabilities, seccomp profile, no SA token mounting.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-hardened
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  automountServiceAccountToken: false
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 1000
    runAsGroup: 3000
    fsGroup: 2000
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault
  containers:
    - name: django
      image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
      ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
      securityContext:
        allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
        readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
        capabilities:
          drop: ["ALL"]
      resources:
        limits:
          cpu: 500m
          memory: 256Mi
        requests:
          cpu: 100m
          memory: 128Mi
      volumeMounts:
        - name: tmp
          mountPath: /tmp
  volumes:
    - name: tmp
      emptyDir:
        sizeLimit: 100Mi</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This pod follows all CKA security best practices: <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> ensures no root processes, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> prevents filesystem writes (except mounted volumes), <code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code> removes all Linux capabilities, <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code> restricts syscalls, <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> prevents API access, and resource limits prevent resource abuse. The <code>emptyDir</code> volume provides a writable <code>/tmp</code> directory since the root filesystem is read-only.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Configure audit logging for the API server. The policy should: log all Secret access at Metadata level, log RBAC changes at RequestResponse level, and skip logging events and health checks.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml
apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
rules:
  <span class="comment"># Skip health checks and events</span>
  - level: None
    nonResourceURLs: ["/healthz*", "/readyz*", "/livez*"]
  - level: None
    resources:
      - group: ""
        resources: ["events"]

  <span class="comment"># Log Secrets at Metadata only (never log content!)</span>
  - level: Metadata
    resources:
      - group: ""
        resources: ["secrets"]

  <span class="comment"># Log RBAC changes at full detail</span>
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
      - group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io"
        resources: ["roles", "rolebindings", "clusterroles", "clusterrolebindings"]

  <span class="comment"># Catch-all</span>
  - level: Metadata</code></pre>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add to kube-apiserver.yaml:</span>
<span class="comment"># --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit-policy.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># --audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</span>
<span class="comment"># --audit-log-maxage=30</span>
<span class="comment"># --audit-log-maxbackup=10</span>
<span class="comment"># --audit-log-maxsize=100</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># Mount both the policy file and log directory as volumes</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Audit policy rules are evaluated in order — the <strong>first matching rule</strong> wins. That's why the <code>None</code> rules (skip) come first. For Secrets, use <code>Metadata</code> level to avoid logging Secret values in the audit trail. RBAC changes need <code>RequestResponse</code> level for full forensic detail. The API server needs both <code>--audit-policy-file</code> and <code>--audit-log-path</code> flags, plus volume mounts for both the policy file and the log output directory.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Set up complete namespace isolation for the <code>easypj</code> namespace: enforce the <code>restricted</code> Pod Security Standard, create a default-deny NetworkPolicy, and set a ResourceQuota limiting to 4 CPU cores and 8Gi memory.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Label namespace for Pod Security Standard</span>
kubectl label namespace easypj \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted

<span class="comment"># 2. Default deny NetworkPolicy</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: ["Ingress", "Egress"]
EOF

<span class="comment"># 3. ResourceQuota</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "4"
    requests.memory: 8Gi
    limits.cpu: "4"
    limits.memory: 8Gi
    pods: "20"
EOF

<span class="comment"># Verify all three</span>
kubectl describe namespace easypj
kubectl get networkpolicy -n easypj
kubectl describe resourcequota -n easypj</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Complete namespace isolation requires three components: <strong>Pod Security Standards</strong> via namespace labels (enforce=restricted blocks privileged containers, host networking, etc.), <strong>NetworkPolicy</strong> with empty podSelector and both Ingress/Egress policyTypes creates a default-deny rule, and <strong>ResourceQuota</strong> limits total resource consumption. On the CKA, you'll often need to combine these. After default-deny, remember to create allow-rules for legitimate traffic (DNS, ingress controller, internal communication).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            
