            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 40.1 — Least-Privilege RBAC From Scratch</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>easypj's CI pipeline needs a ServiceAccount that can read (but not modify) Deployments and Pods in the <code>easypj</code> namespace only — nothing cluster-wide. Build it, then PROVE it's minimal.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Create ServiceAccount <code>easypj-ci-sa</code> in namespace <code>easypj</code></li>
                        <li>Create a Role granting only <code>get</code>/<code>list</code>/<code>watch</code> on <code>deployments</code> and <code>pods</code></li>
                        <li>Bind it with a RoleBinding</li>
                        <li>Verify it CAN list deployments</li>
                        <li>Verify it CANNOT delete deployments</li>
                        <li>Verify it CANNOT do anything outside the <code>easypj</code> namespace</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> <code>kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;resource&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; --as=system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;sa&gt;</code> is how you PROVE a permission boundary, not just assume it from reading the YAML.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-ci-sa -n easypj

kubectl create role easypj-ci-reader -n easypj \
  --verb=get,list,watch --resource=deployments,pods

kubectl create rolebinding easypj-ci-reader-bind -n easypj \
  --role=easypj-ci-reader --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-ci-sa

<span class="comment"># Positive check</span>
kubectl auth can-i list deployments -n easypj \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-ci-sa
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

<span class="comment"># Negative check — must be "no"</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete deployments -n easypj \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-ci-sa
<span class="comment"># no</span>

<span class="comment"># Negative check — cross-namespace, must be "no"</span>
kubectl auth can-i list deployments -n kube-system \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-ci-sa
<span class="comment"># no</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 40.2 — Enable and Verify Encryption at Rest</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Enable encryption-at-rest for Secrets on a static-pod control plane, then PROVE — by reading raw etcd, not just trusting the config — that an existing Secret is actually encrypted.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Generate a 32-byte key and write an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code></li>
                        <li>Wire it into the kube-apiserver static-pod manifest</li>
                        <li>Re-encrypt the existing <code>easypj-db-secret</code></li>
                        <li>Read the raw etcd value and confirm the <code>k8s:enc:aescbc</code> prefix</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the kube-apiserver static pod restarts automatically the moment kubelet notices the manifest file changed — no manual restart command needed, but you DO need to add both the volume AND the volumeMount, or the flag will point at a path that doesn't exist inside the container.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64
<span class="comment"># save output as &lt;KEY&gt; below</span>

cat &lt;&lt;EOF &gt; /etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
- resources: ["secrets"]
  providers:
  - aescbc:
      keys:
      - name: key1
        secret: &lt;KEY&gt;
  - identity: {}
EOF

<span class="comment"># Edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml:</span>
<span class="comment">#  add --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml to command</span>
<span class="comment">#  add a hostPath volume + volumeMount for /etc/kubernetes/enc</span>

kubectl get secret easypj-db-secret -n easypj -o json | kubectl replace -f -

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/easypj/easypj-db-secret \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key | hexdump -C | head
<span class="comment"># look for "k8s:enc:aescbc:v1:key1" — that's proof it worked</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
