            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 40.3 — Audit Policy That Never Leaks a Secret</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Write and wire in an audit policy for the easypj cluster: Secrets at Metadata only, RBAC objects at RequestResponse, health checks skipped entirely, everything else at Metadata as a catch-all.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Write the policy YAML with rules in the CORRECT order (first-match-wins)</li>
                        <li>Wire <code>--audit-policy-file</code> and <code>--audit-log-path</code> into the apiserver manifest</li>
                        <li>Generate a Secret read and confirm the log entry has no Secret body</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> rule order matters — a catch-all listed FIRST will shadow your more specific Secrets/RBAC rules and log everything at the same level, silently defeating the whole point of a tiered policy.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/kubernetes/audit/policy.yaml
apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
rules:
- level: None
  nonResourceURLs: ["/healthz*", "/readyz*", "/livez*"]
- level: Metadata
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["secrets"]
- level: RequestResponse
  resources:
  - group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io"
    resources: ["roles","rolebindings","clusterroles","clusterrolebindings"]
- level: Metadata</code></pre>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"># kube-apiserver.yaml command additions:
#   --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit/policy.yaml
#   --audit-log-path=/var/log/kube-audit.log

kubectl get secret easypj-db-secret -n easypj
grep '"resource":"secrets"' /var/log/kube-audit.log | tail -1
# confirm: no "requestObject"/"responseObject" key present — Metadata level only</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 40.4 — Fully Hardened easypj Pod</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Deploy easypj's Django container with every runtime hardening control from §40.5 applied at once, and confirm the Pod actually starts (a common trap: read-only rootfs without a writable <code>/tmp</code> crashes Django on first write).</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>non-root, dropped capabilities, seccomp RuntimeDefault, read-only rootfs</li>
                        <li>a writable <code>emptyDir</code> for <code>/tmp</code></li>
                        <li>automount token disabled</li>
                        <li>verify with <code>kubectl exec</code> that the container truly cannot write outside <code>/tmp</code></li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> if the Pod goes <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> immediately after adding <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code>, the first thing to check is whether the app tried to write somewhere you didn't mount an <code>emptyDir</code> for — Django's default cache/session paths are common culprits.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-hardened
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  automountServiceAccountToken: false
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 10001
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: registry.example.com/easypj/jobpost@sha256:abc123...
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
      capabilities:
        drop: ["ALL"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: tmp
      mountPath: /tmp
  volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}</code></pre>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl exec -it easypj-web-hardened -n easypj -- touch /tmp/ok      # succeeds
kubectl exec -it easypj-web-hardened -n easypj -- touch /app/ok      # Read-only file system</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 40.5 — End-to-End Namespace Hardening (Multi-Tenancy Stack)</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Stand up the FULL tenancy stack for the <code>easypj</code> namespace in one pass: restricted Pod Security, a default-deny NetworkPolicy, and a ResourceQuota — then verify a privileged Pod is actually rejected.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Label the namespace <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code></li>
                        <li>Apply a default-deny-all NetworkPolicy</li>
                        <li>Apply a ResourceQuota capping CPU/memory/object counts</li>
                        <li>Attempt to create a privileged Pod and confirm it's REJECTED at admission</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the rejection for a PSA violation happens at admission time with a clear message naming the violated control (e.g. "privileged" or "allowPrivilegeEscalation") — read the message, don't guess which control tripped.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted

cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: ["Ingress", "Egress"]
EOF

cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "4"
    requests.memory: 8Gi
    pods: "20"
EOF

<span class="comment"># Attempt a privileged pod — should be REJECTED by PSA at admission time</span>
kubectl run bad-pod -n easypj --image=nginx --privileged
<span class="comment"># Error: pods "bad-pod" is forbidden: violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest":
#   privileged (container "bad-pod" must not set securityContext.privileged=true)</span></code></pre>
