        │
        ▼
   🧯 NETWORK      default-deny NetworkPolicy, egress control
        │
        ▼
   💾 DATA         Secrets encrypted at rest, audit logged, signed+scanned
                    images from allowlisted registries      ◀── easypj JobPost
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   a breach must defeat EVERY layer — one is never enough.
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> easypj's security is six independent rings — Infra, AuthN/Z, Admission, Workload, Network, Data — wrapped around the JobPost workload and its data. Each ring answers a different question (whose host? who can call the API? can this pod even be created? what can it do once running? who can it reach over the network? is the data safe at rest?) and stops a different class of attack. RBAC enforces least privilege and guards the escalate/bind/impersonate verbs; Secrets are encrypted at rest, mounted as volumes, and have automount off; the API server refuses anonymous auth and audits at the right level per resource; the workload runs non-root with a read-only rootfs, dropped capabilities and seccomp; NetworkPolicy defaults to deny; and images are scanned, signed, and pinned by digest from allowlisted registries.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> no single control in this chapter is load-bearing on its own — that's the entire point of defense in depth. A stolen easypj-web-sa token can do almost nothing (RBAC), a compromised process can barely move (workload hardening), and even a full etcd leak doesn't yield plaintext credentials (encryption at rest). Chapter 41 builds on this foundation with policy engines that enforce these rules automatically instead of by convention.</p>
