            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 Policies and the CKA Exam</h5>
                <p>The CKA tests your ability to <strong>enforce security policies</strong> at the namespace and cluster level. Pod Security Admission via namespace labels is the most likely exam topic. LimitRange and ResourceQuota questions also appear frequently. Know the difference between them and when to use each.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Must Know</strong> — Pod Security Standards (3 levels), LimitRange, ResourceQuota</li>
                    <li><strong>Should Know</strong> — ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL), PodDisruptionBudget</li>
                    <li><strong>Awareness</strong> — OPA Gatekeeper, Kyverno (not on CKA but used in production)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📖 Why This Matters — Policy Replaces "Please Be Careful" With "The API Server Refuses It"</h5>
                <p>Policy is how a cluster enforces its standards <strong>automatically</strong> instead of trusting every human to remember the rules. Without it: one developer ships a root container because nobody caught it in review; another forgets resource requests entirely and a single runaway pod starves an otherwise-healthy node of memory; a third deploys a single-replica "production" Deployment that quietly dies the moment the node it happens to land on is drained for maintenance. Every layer in this chapter moves one of those failure modes from "please be careful" to "the API server will refuse the request." For the <strong>easypj</strong> platform specifically, stacking Pod Security Admission + LimitRange + ResourceQuota + a replica-count ValidatingAdmissionPolicy means the jobpost workload (Django + Gunicorn talking to PostgreSQL) is <strong>forced</strong> to run non-root, with capped resources, with a sane replica floor — guaranteed at admission time, before a single bad pod ever schedules.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 The Airport Security Analogy</h5>
                <p>Think of admission as <strong>airport security for pods</strong>. <strong>Pod Security Admission</strong> is the metal detector — no weapons, no root. <strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> is the gate agent checking your boarding-pass rules ("you must travel with at least one companion" → <code>replicas &gt;= 2</code>). <strong>LimitRange</strong> is the helpful clerk who fills in the blank fields on your form (default cpu/mem) so you're not turned away for an incomplete application. <strong>ResourceQuota</strong> is the flight's total weight limit — the whole cabin can only carry so much. And <strong>NetworkPolicy + PodDisruptionBudget</strong> are the in-flight rules once you're already aboard — who you may talk to, and how many seats must stay filled if turbulence (a node drain) hits. None of these checkpoints replaces another; a boarding pass doesn't excuse you from the metal detector, and a pod that clears PSA still has to clear the gate agent's CEL rule right after.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <h4>🧭 Decision Guide — Which Policy Tool Do I Reach For?</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Need…</th><th>Reach for…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Block root / privileged / dangerous capabilities</td><td>Pod Security Admission (namespace labels)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Inject default cpu/mem on bare pods</td><td>LimitRange</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Cap a namespace's TOTAL cpu/mem/object counts</td><td>ResourceQuota</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Stop fork bombs / runaway processes</td><td><code>podPidsLimit</code> (PID limit)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Force even spread across zones/nodes</td><td><code>topologySpreadConstraints</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>A simple, cluster-native rule (no webhook)</td><td>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Rich validate + MUTATE + GENERATE rules in plain YAML</td><td>Kyverno</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Rego-based org-wide policy with reusable constraints</td><td>OPA Gatekeeper</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Who-may-talk-to-whom at runtime</td><td>NetworkPolicy</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Survive node drains / voluntary disruption</td><td>PodDisruptionBudget</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
