            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §41.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s41-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">41.1 Pod Security Standards — The Three Levels</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Pod Security Standards define three progressive security levels. They replaced the deprecated PodSecurityPolicy in Kubernetes v1.25.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Description</th><th>What It Blocks</th><th>Use Case</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Privileged</strong></td>
                                <td>No restrictions</td>
                                <td>Nothing — everything allowed</td>
                                <td>System/infrastructure namespaces (<code>kube-system</code>)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>
                                <td>Minimally restrictive</td>
                                <td>Privileged containers, hostNetwork/hostPID/hostIPC, <strong>hostPath volumes (forbidden entirely</strong> — not merely "limited"; any pod mounting a hostPath is rejected outright), and capabilities beyond a small allowed set (e.g. <code>CHOWN</code>, <code>KILL</code>, <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code>)</td>
                                <td>General workloads with basic security</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Restricted</strong></td>
                                <td>Heavily restricted</td>
                                <td>Everything in baseline + must run as non-root (<code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>), privilege escalation forbidden (<code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>), ALL capabilities dropped, <code>seccompProfile.type</code> must be <code>RuntimeDefault</code> or <code>Localhost</code> (never unset, never <code>Unconfined</code>), and only 8 approved volume types are permitted at all</td>
                                <td>Security-critical and multi-tenant workloads</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 3px solid #ef4444;">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Privileged</h5>
                        <p>No restrictions. Use for <code>kube-system</code> and infrastructure components only.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 3px solid #f59e0b;">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🟡</div>
                        <h5>Baseline</h5>
                        <p>Blocks known escalations. Good starting point for general workloads.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight" style="border-left: 3px solid #22c55e;">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🟢</div>
                        <h5>Restricted</h5>
                        <p>Maximum security. Required for production and multi-tenant clusters.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Pod Security Admission — Enforcement Modes</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Label Key</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>enforce</strong></td><td>Block pods that violate the policy</td><td><code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>warn</strong></td><td>Allow but show warning to user</td><td><code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>audit</strong></td><td>Allow but record in audit log</td><td><code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — applying Pod Security Standards</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply restricted level with all three modes</span>
kubectl label namespace easypj \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted

<span class="comment"># Verify labels</span>
kubectl get namespace easypj --show-labels

<span class="comment"># Test — this pod violates restricted (runs as root)</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Error from server (Forbidden): pods "test" is forbidden:</span>
<span class="comment">#   violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest":</span>
<span class="comment">#   allowPrivilegeEscalation != false</span>
<span class="comment">#   unrestricted capabilities</span>
<span class="comment">#   runAsNonRoot != true</span>

<span class="comment"># Pin to a specific K8s version for consistent behavior</span>
kubectl label namespace easypj \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.30</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Pod Security Standard Decision Tree</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 Is it a system namespace (kube-system, etc.)?
 ├── YES → Use "privileged" (system components need elevated privileges)
 └── NO
     ├── Does it run trusted, well-known images only?
     │   ├── YES → Use "baseline" (prevents obvious attacks)
     │   └── NO  → Use "restricted" (maximum security)
     └── Multi-tenant cluster?
         └── ALWAYS → Use "restricted" + "warn" on next level
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h4>Pod Security Admission Gotchas</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>❌ <strong>Labels apply at namespace level only</strong> — you cannot apply PSA to individual pods, only entire namespaces</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong>Existing pods aren't evicted</strong> — enforce mode only blocks NEW pods. Existing violating pods continue running</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong>"Read-only root filesystem" is NOT actually a PSS check</strong> — a common misconception. The <code>restricted</code> profile validates <code>runAsNonRoot</code>, dropped capabilities, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, and <code>seccompProfile</code> — it never inspects <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>. A read-only root filesystem is excellent hardening practice, but PSA will happily admit a <code>restricted</code>-compliant pod with a writable root filesystem.</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Use warn + audit first</strong> — apply warning mode before enforce to discover violations without breaking workloads</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Check with dry-run</strong> — <code>kubectl label --dry-run=server --overwrite ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code> shows what would be blocked</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Quick Hands-On Proof: Same Pod, Two Verdicts</h5>
                    <p>The fastest way to internalize what a level actually blocks is to watch ONE pod get rejected, then accepted, purely because the namespace label changed underneath it — nothing about the pod spec itself changes.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1) Namespace labelled baseline — a privileged pod is rejected</span>
kubectl label ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline --overwrite
kubectl run priv-test --image=nginx -n easypj \
  --overrides='{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"priv-test","image":"nginx","securityContext":{"privileged":true}}]}}'
<span class="comment"># Error from server (Forbidden): pods "priv-test" is forbidden:</span>
<span class="comment">#   violates PodSecurity "baseline:latest": privileged (container "priv-test"</span>
<span class="comment">#   must not set securityContext.privileged=true)</span>

<span class="comment"># 2) Relabel the SAME namespace to privileged — the identical pod now succeeds</span>
kubectl label ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=privileged --overwrite
kubectl run priv-test --image=nginx -n easypj \
  --overrides='{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"priv-test","image":"nginx","securityContext":{"privileged":true}}]}}'
<span class="comment"># pod/priv-test created</span></code></pre>
                    <p>Nothing about <code>priv-test</code> changed between the two attempts — only the namespace's <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce</code> label did. This is the single clearest demonstration that PSA is a per-namespace GATE, evaluated fresh on every admission, not a property baked into the pod itself.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §41.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s41-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">41.2 LimitRange vs ResourceQuota — Complete Comparison</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>These two resources work together but solve different problems. Understanding their differences is critical for the CKA.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 LimitRange + ResourceQuota = Complete Resource Governance</h5>
                    <p>LimitRange sets guardrails for <em>individual</em> pods (defaults, min, max). ResourceQuota caps the <em>total</em> namespace consumption. Use both together — LimitRange provides defaults so pods satisfy ResourceQuota's requirement that all pods have limits.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="lifecycle-chain">
                    <div class="lc-pill active">Pod Created</div>
                    <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="lc-pill">LimitRange Injects Defaults</div>
                    <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="lc-pill">LimitRange Validates Min/Max</div>
                    <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="lc-pill">ResourceQuota Checks Totals</div>
                    <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="lc-pill active">Pod Scheduled</div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>LimitRange</th><th>ResourceQuota</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>Per-container / per-pod defaults</td><td>Namespace-wide totals</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>What it controls</strong></td><td>Default, min, max for individual pods</td><td>Total aggregate consumption across all pods</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Applies when</strong></td><td>Pod is created (sets defaults)</td><td>Namespace hits capacity limits</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>If not set</strong></td><td>Pods get no default limits</td><td>No cap on total namespace usage</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Primary purpose</strong></td><td>Prevent one pod from being too large/small</td><td>Prevent one team from consuming all cluster resources</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">limitrange-vs-quota.yaml — both working together</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># LimitRange — defaults and bounds for INDIVIDUAL pods/containers</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-limits
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  limits:
    - type: Container
      default:              <span class="comment"># Applied if container doesn't specify limits</span>
        cpu: 200m
        memory: 256Mi
      defaultRequest:       <span class="comment"># Applied if container doesn't specify requests</span>
        cpu: 100m
        memory: 128Mi
      max:                  <span class="comment"># No container can exceed these</span>
        cpu: "2"
        memory: 2Gi
      min:                  <span class="comment"># No container can request less than these</span>
        cpu: 50m
        memory: 64Mi
      maxLimitRequestRatio:  <span class="comment"># limit can be at most 4x the request — caps overcommit per container</span>
        cpu: "4"
        memory: "2"
    - type: Pod
      max:                  <span class="comment"># Total pod limits (sum of all containers)</span>
        cpu: "4"
        memory: 4Gi
    - type: PersistentVolumeClaim  <span class="comment"># LimitRange isn't just compute — it bounds PVC storage requests too</span>
      max:
        storage: 10Gi
      min:
        storage: 1Gi
---
<span class="comment"># ResourceQuota — aggregate limits for the ENTIRE namespace</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "8"         <span class="comment"># Total CPU requests across all pods</span>
    requests.memory: 16Gi
    limits.cpu: "16"
    limits.memory: 32Gi
    pods: "30"                <span class="comment"># Max 30 pods in namespace</span>
    services: "10"
    persistentvolumeclaims: "10"
    secrets: "20"
    configmaps: "20"
    replicationcontrollers: "10"
    services.loadbalancers: "2"
    services.nodeports: "5"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — checking limits and quotas</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View LimitRange settings</span>
kubectl describe limitrange easypj-limits -n easypj

<span class="comment"># View ResourceQuota usage</span>
kubectl describe resourcequota easypj-quota -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Name:            easypj-quota</span>
<span class="comment"># Resource         Used   Hard</span>
<span class="comment"># --------         ---    ----</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.cpu       600m   16</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.memory    768Mi  32Gi</span>
<span class="comment"># pods             3      30</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.cpu     300m   8</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.memory  384Mi  16Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Test: try to create a pod that exceeds LimitRange max</span>
kubectl run big --image=nginx -n easypj \
  --overrides='{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"big","image":"nginx","resources":{"limits":{"cpu":"5"}}}]}}'
<span class="comment"># Error: cpu max constraint (2) exceeded</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                    <p><strong>CKA Quick Reference</strong> — When a ResourceQuota exists in a namespace, ALL pods in that namespace MUST specify resource requests and limits. If they don't and no LimitRange provides defaults, the pod will be rejected. This is why LimitRange and ResourceQuota are often used together: LimitRange provides defaults so pods without explicit limits still get created.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h4>LimitRange Gotchas the Docs Explicitly Call Out</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>❌ <strong>LimitRange does NOT sanity-check its own defaults</strong> — nothing stops you from setting a <code>default</code> (limit) LOWER than <code>defaultRequest</code>, or from a container's own explicit <code>requests</code> exceeding the LimitRange's injected <code>default</code> limit. Either produces a hard rejection: <code>must be less than or equal to cpu limit</code>. If you set an explicit <code>requests.cpu: 700m</code> on a container but the LimitRange only defaults <code>limits.cpu</code> to <code>500m</code> and you didn't ALSO set an explicit <code>limits.cpu</code>, the pod is rejected — always set both request and limit explicitly once either one is close to a LimitRange boundary.</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong>Two or more LimitRange objects in the same namespace = undefined behavior</strong> — the docs are explicit that "it is not deterministic which default value will be applied." Keep exactly ONE LimitRange per namespace, or merge them.</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong>Not retroactive</strong> — like PSA, a LimitRange only validates at pod ADMISSION. Adding or editing a LimitRange never touches pods that are already running in that namespace.</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>LimitRange also bounds storage, not just compute</strong> — a <code>type: PersistentVolumeClaim</code> entry sets <code>min</code>/<code>max</code> on the <code>storage</code> request of every PVC in the namespace, exactly like <code>type: Container</code> does for cpu/memory.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §41.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s41-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">41.3 PID Limits — Preventing Fork Bombs</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <p><strong>PID limits</strong> restrict the number of process IDs a pod can use, preventing fork bombs and runaway process creation from destabilizing the entire node.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">kubelet-config.yaml — PID limits</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Kubelet configuration — set per node</span>
apiVersion: kubelet.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: KubeletConfiguration
podPidsLimit: 1024           <span class="comment"># Max PIDs per pod</span>
<span class="comment"># System reserved PIDs are configured separately:</span>
<span class="comment"># --system-reserved=pid=1000</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — checking PID usage</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check current PID allocation on a node</span>
kubectl get --raw /api/v1/nodes/&lt;node-name&gt;/proxy/stats/summary | \
  jq '.pods[] | select(.podRef.name | startswith("easypj")) |
  {name: .podRef.name, pids: .process_stats.process_count}'

<span class="comment"># Check kubelet PID limit setting</span>
kubectl get --raw /api/v1/nodes/&lt;node-name&gt;/proxy/configz | \
  jq '.kubeletconfig.podPidsLimit'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 PID Limits and the CKA</h5>
                    <p>PID limits are configured at the kubelet level, not via YAML manifests. On the CKA, you may see this in troubleshooting scenarios where pods are evicted due to PID pressure. Check <code>kubectl describe node</code> for the <code>PIDPressure</code> condition.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §41.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s41-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">41.4 Topology Spread Constraints</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p><strong>Topology Spread Constraints</strong> ensure pods are evenly distributed across failure domains (nodes, zones, regions). This prevents all replicas landing on one node or in one availability zone.</p>

                <!-- Anatomy card: 4 key fields -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 4px solid #e74c3c;">
                        <h5>🎯 maxSkew</h5>
                        <p>Maximum allowed pod count difference between topology domains. Lower = more even. <code>1</code> means perfect balance.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 4px solid #3498db;">
                        <h5>🗺️ topologyKey</h5>
                        <p>Node label that defines domains: <code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code> (per-node) or <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code> (per-zone).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 4px solid #f39c12;">
                        <h5>⚖️ whenUnsatisfiable</h5>
                        <p><code>DoNotSchedule</code> (hard — block) or <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> (soft — best-effort). Choose per constraint.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 4px solid #2ecc71;">
                        <h5>🏷️ labelSelector</h5>
                        <p>Selects which pods to count for skew calculation. Must match the pod template's own labels.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">topology-spread.yaml — even distribution for easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 6
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-web
    spec:
      topologySpreadConstraints:
        <span class="comment"># Spread evenly across zones</span>
        - maxSkew: 1
          topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
          whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
          labelSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj-web
        <span class="comment"># Spread evenly across nodes within each zone</span>
        - maxSkew: 1
          topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
          whenUnsatisfiable: ScheduleAnyway
          labelSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj-web
      containers:
        - name: django
          image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th><th>Values</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>maxSkew</code></td><td>Maximum difference between zone/node pod counts</td><td>Integer ≥ 1 (lower = more even)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>topologyKey</code></td><td>Node label to group topology domains</td><td><code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code>, <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>whenUnsatisfiable</code></td><td>What to do if constraint can't be met</td><td><code>DoNotSchedule</code> (hard), <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> (soft)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>labelSelector</code></td><td>Which pods to count in the topology calculation</td><td>Must match the pod template labels</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Topology Spread Visualization</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 Without Topology Spread          With maxSkew=1, by zone
 ┌────────────────────┐          ┌────────────────────┐
 │ Zone A             │          │ Zone A             │
 │ ┌──────┐ ┌──────┐ │          │ ┌──────┐ ┌──────┐ │
 │ │Node1 │ │Node2 │ │          │ │Node1 │ │Node2 │ │
 │ │██████│ │      │ │          │ │██    │ │██    │ │
 │ │██████│ │      │ │          │ └──────┘ └──────┘ │
 │ └──────┘ └──────┘ │          │ 2 pods             │
 │ 6 pods             │          ├────────────────────┤
 ├────────────────────┤          │ Zone B             │
 │ Zone B             │          │ ┌──────┐ ┌──────┐ │
 │ ┌──────┐          │          │ │Node3 │ │Node4 │ │
 │ │Node3 │          │          │ │██    │ │██    │ │
 │ │      │          │          │ └──────┘ └──────┘ │
 │ └──────┘          │          │ 2 pods             │
 │ 0 pods             │          ├────────────────────┤
 ├────────────────────┤          │ Zone C             │
 │ Zone C             │          │ ┌──────┐          │
 │ ┌──────┐          │          │ │Node5 │          │
 │ │Node5 │          │          │ │██    │          │
 │ │      │          │          │ └──────┘          │
 │ └──────┘          │          │ 2 pods             │
 │ 0 pods             │          └────────────────────┘
 └────────────────────┘          Evenly distributed!
  All eggs in one basket!
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §41.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s41-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">41.5 ValidatingAdmissionPolicy — In-Cluster CEL Rules</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p><strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (VAP)</strong> is a built-in, in-cluster admission policy that evaluates <strong>CEL</strong> (Common Expression Language) expressions against incoming objects — with <strong>no external webhook</strong> to deploy, secure, or keep alive. Before VAP, even a trivial rule like "Deployments must have at least 2 replicas" meant standing up a validating admission webhook: a service, a TLS certificate, a deployment to keep healthy, and a single point of failure sitting in the admission path. VAP runs the same logic <em>in-process</em>, inside the API server itself.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</h5>
                    <p>A cluster-native admission rule expressed in CEL. It has <strong>two objects</strong>: the <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> itself (the rule) and a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding</code> (where the rule applies and what action to take when it fails). Neither object requires a webhook server, a Deployment, or a Service — the CEL expression is compiled and evaluated directly by <code>kube-apiserver</code>. The API is <code>admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1</code> and reached <strong>General Availability (stable) in Kubernetes v1.30</strong> — on any current cluster it's a first-class, fully-supported feature, not an experimental one.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">vap-min-replicas.yaml — require ≥2 replicas on Deployments in easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># The POLICY — defines the rule and what it matches</span>
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-min-replicas
spec:
  failurePolicy: Fail            <span class="comment"># if CEL errors, REJECT (Ignore = let it through)</span>
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
      - apiGroups:   ["apps"]
        apiVersions: ["v1"]
        operations:  ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]
        resources:   ["deployments"]
  validations:
    - expression: "object.spec.replicas >= 2"
      message: "easypj Deployments must have at least 2 replicas"
---
<span class="comment"># The BINDING — WHERE the policy applies + what action to take</span>
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: require-min-replicas-binding
spec:
  policyName: require-min-replicas
  validationActions: ["Deny"]    <span class="comment"># Deny | Warn | Audit — can combine Warn+Audit</span>
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        kubernetes.io/metadata.name: easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>VAP vs a Traditional Validating Webhook</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 Traditional Validating Webhook              ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)
 ┌─────────────────────────────┐             ┌─────────────────────────────┐
 │ kube-apiserver               │             │ kube-apiserver               │
 │   │ HTTPS call OUT            │             │   │ CEL evaluated IN-PROCESS  │
 │   ▼                          │             │   ▼ (no network hop)         │
 │ 🌐 webhook Service            │             │ ✅ accept / ❌ reject         │
 │   │ needs: Deployment, TLS    │             │                             │
 │   │ cert, HA, health checks   │             │ nothing to deploy, patch,    │
 │   ▼                          │             │ or keep alive — it's data    │
 │ 🖥️ webhook Pod (your code)    │             │ stored in etcd, evaluated    │
 │   runs your validation logic │             │ by the apiserver itself      │
 └─────────────────────────────┘             └─────────────────────────────┘
  extra moving part in the        zero extra moving parts — the rule
  critical admission path         IS the object, no server to run
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>CEL Variable</th><th>What It Holds</th><th>Typical Use</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>object</code></td><td>The resource being submitted (the new/updated version)</td><td><code>object.spec.replicas &gt;= 2</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>oldObject</code></td><td>The prior version, present only on UPDATE</td><td>Diff-based rules — e.g. block changing an immutable field</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>request</code></td><td>Admission metadata — operation, userInfo, namespace</td><td><code>request.userInfo.username</code> for user-based rules</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>params</code></td><td>An optional bound parameter resource (a CRD/ConfigMap)</td><td>Parameterize one policy differently per binding (e.g. per-namespace <code>minReplicas</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>variables</code></td><td>Named sub-expressions reused across multiple <code>validations</code></td><td>Compute something once, reference it in several rules</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Extra <code>spec</code> Field</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Why It Matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>matchConditions</code></td><td>An ADDITIONAL list of CEL expressions evaluated BEFORE <code>validations</code>, purely to decide whether this request should be evaluated at all — independent of <code>matchConstraints</code>' GVK/operation matching</td><td>Skip evaluating the policy entirely for requests that don't need it (e.g. <code>"!('skip-vap' in object.metadata.labels)"</code>) — cheaper than writing the exclusion into every <code>validations</code> expression, and it's the exact same mechanism (and field name) used by mutating/validating webhook configurations in Chapter 42</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>auditAnnotations</code></td><td>A CEL <code>messageExpression</code>-style expression that, when a validation is evaluated, attaches a custom key/value pair to the request's audit log entry — independent of whether the request was allowed or denied</td><td>Lets you record WHY a decision was made (e.g. the actual replica count that triggered a Warn) directly in the cluster's audit trail, without needing an external PolicyReport-style system</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Parameterizing One Policy Per Namespace — <code>params</code> in Practice</h4>
                <p>The <code>params</code> row above is easy to skim past, but it's what turns ONE ValidatingAdmissionPolicy into a reusable rule instead of a hardcoded constant. Declare a <code>paramKind</code> (any resource — often a ConfigMap or a small CRD), then each <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding</code> points at a DIFFERENT instance of that kind via <code>paramRef</code>. The result: <code>easypj-staging</code> and <code>easypj-production</code> can enforce different minimums from the exact same compiled CEL expression — no copy-pasted policy per environment.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">vap-params-example.yaml — one policy, per-namespace minimums via params + a dynamic message</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># The POLICY — generic, reads its threshold from params instead of a literal</span>
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-configurable-min-replicas
spec:
  failurePolicy: Fail
  paramKind:                     <span class="comment"># WHAT KIND of object supplies the parameter</span>
    apiVersion: v1
    kind: ConfigMap
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
      - apiGroups:   ["apps"]
        apiVersions: ["v1"]
        operations:  ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]
        resources:   ["deployments"]
  validations:
    - expression: "object.spec.replicas >= int(params.data.minReplicas)"
      messageExpression: >-
        "replicas must be >= " + params.data.minReplicas +
        ", got " + string(object.spec.replicas)
---
<span class="comment"># The PARAMETER — a plain ConfigMap, one per environment</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-production-min-replicas
  namespace: easypj
data:
  minReplicas: "3"               <span class="comment"># production requires 3; staging could bind a "1" instead</span>
---
<span class="comment"># The BINDING — WHICH param instance applies WHERE</span>
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: require-min-replicas-production
spec:
  policyName: require-configurable-min-replicas
  validationActions: ["Deny"]
  paramRef:
    name: easypj-production-min-replicas
    namespace: easypj
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        kubernetes.io/metadata.name: easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 <code>messageExpression</code> — Build the Rejection Message From the ACTUAL Values</h5>
                    <p>A static <code>message</code> field always shows the same string no matter what violated it. <code>messageExpression</code> replaces it with a CEL expression evaluated ONLY when the rule fails, so the developer sees the real numbers involved — <code>"replicas must be >= 3, got 1"</code> — instead of a generic "not enough replicas." Combined with <code>params</code>, this is how a single ValidatingAdmissionPolicy scales to an entire fleet of namespaces, each with its own threshold and its own precisely-worded rejection message, without a single line of CEL being duplicated.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — apply and inspect a VAP</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f vap.yaml

<span class="comment"># List policies and bindings</span>
kubectl get validatingadmissionpolicy
kubectl get validatingadmissionpolicybinding

<span class="comment"># Test — this Deployment violates the rule (only 1 replica)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj/jobpost:v2.1 \
  --replicas=1 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Error from server: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy 'require-min-replicas' with</span>
<span class="comment">#   binding 'require-min-replicas-binding' denied request:</span>
<span class="comment">#   easypj Deployments must have at least 2 replicas</span>

<span class="comment"># Test WITHOUT creating anything — --dry-run=server runs the FULL admission</span>
<span class="comment"># chain (including this VAP) but never persists the object to etcd</span>
kubectl create deployment probe --image=easypj/jobpost:v2.1 \
  --replicas=1 -n easypj --dry-run=server
<span class="comment"># same "denied request" error, and NO Deployment object is left behind —</span>
<span class="comment"># the safest way to validate a manifest against every admission layer at once</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 <code>--dry-run=server</code> Exercises the WHOLE Admission Chain, Not Just VAP</h5>
                    <p><code>--dry-run=client</code> never leaves your machine — it can't know about PSA, VAP, LimitRange, or ResourceQuota at all. <code>--dry-run=server</code> sends the request all the way to <code>kube-apiserver</code> and runs it through every admission stage from §UTH's opening diagram (mutating, schema, validating), then stops just before the etcd write. It's the single fastest way to check "would this manifest survive the ENTIRE policy stack" — PSA, this VAP, LimitRange injection, and ResourceQuota — without creating, and then having to clean up, a real object.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h4>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy Gotchas</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>❌ <strong>Validation only</strong> — VAP cannot mutate/change the incoming object, only accept or reject it. Need to inject a sidecar or set a default? You still need a mutating webhook, Kyverno, or Gatekeeper.</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong>A CEL error can fail-open or fail-closed silently</strong> — a syntax error, or a reference to a field that doesn't exist on the object, makes the expression error at evaluation time. <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> means an evaluation error REJECTS the request; <code>Ignore</code> means it's allowed through. Always test the expression before relying on it.</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Two objects, always</strong> — a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> with no <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding</code> does nothing; the Policy defines the rule, the Binding turns it on for a scope.</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>validationActions can combine</strong> — bind with <code>["Warn", "Audit"]</code> to preview a rule's impact before switching to <code>["Deny"]</code>, exactly like the PSA warn/audit-before-enforce pattern in §41.1.</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong><code>matchResources</code> isn't limited to <code>namespaceSelector</code></strong> — a Binding can also scope by <code>objectSelector</code> (labels on the object itself, e.g. only Deployments carrying <code>tier: critical</code>), the same field name used by webhook configurations in Chapter 42. Combine both to bind a rule to "this namespace AND these specific objects."</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong><code>Deny</code> and <code>Warn</code> cannot be combined in the same Binding</strong> — <code>validationActions</code> allows <code>["Deny"]</code> alone, or <code>["Warn", "Audit"]</code> together, but never <code>["Deny", "Warn"]</code>; a denied request is already rejected, so a redundant warning makes no sense and the API server rejects the Binding itself.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §41.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s41-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">41.6 External Policy Engines — OPA Gatekeeper & Kyverno</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <p>For complex policy requirements beyond what ValidatingAdmissionPolicy and Pod Security Standards provide, organizations use external policy engines. These are <strong>not on the CKA exam</strong> but are critical for production environments.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>OPA Gatekeeper</th><th>Kyverno</th><th>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Policy language</strong></td><td>Rego (custom DSL)</td><td>YAML (native K8s)</td><td>CEL (simple expressions)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Learning curve</strong></td><td>Steep (learn Rego)</td><td>Low (YAML-based)</td><td>Low (CEL expressions)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Mutation support</strong></td><td>Yes (added as a beta feature starting around v3.7)</td><td>Yes (native)</td><td>No (validation only)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Generation support</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Yes (generate resources)</td><td>No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Report/audit mode</strong></td><td>Yes (audit constraint)</td><td>Yes (audit mode)</td><td>Yes (Audit action)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Requires webhook?</strong></td><td>Yes (runs as webhook)</td><td>Yes (runs as webhook)</td><td>No (built into API server)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKA exam?</strong></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (may appear)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- Decision tree: which policy engine? -->
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">Do you need only validation (no mutation/generation)?</div>
                    <div class="decision-node yes">YES → Is it a simple per-resource rule (e.g., require labels, enforce limits)?
                        <div class="decision-node yes">YES → Use <strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> (built-in, no extra components)</div>
                        <div class="decision-node no">NO → Need cross-resource or complex logic? → <strong>OPA Gatekeeper</strong></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node no">NO → Do you need to mutate resources or auto-generate defaults?
                        <div class="decision-node yes">YES → Use <strong>Kyverno</strong> (YAML-native, supports validate + mutate + generate)</div>
                        <div class="decision-node no">NO → Combine <strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> (simple rules) + external engine (complex rules)</div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — PolicyReport / ClusterPolicyReport</h5>
                    <p>Both Gatekeeper and Kyverno publish their findings as a standardized <code>PolicyReport</code> (namespaced) or <code>ClusterPolicyReport</code> (cluster-scoped) custom resource — a machine-readable, queryable audit trail of every pass/fail decision, independent of whether the policy is set to <code>enforce</code>/<code>Enforce</code> or merely <code>audit</code>/<code>Audit</code>. This is the mechanism that lets you preview the blast radius of a brand-new policy across the ENTIRE cluster before you ever flip it to blocking mode — without grepping through webhook pod logs one namespace at a time.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — reading policy audit results before enforcing anything</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># One PolicyReport per namespace, aggregating every engine's findings</span>
kubectl get policyreport -A

<span class="comment"># Drill into what a specific report actually caught</span>
kubectl get policyreport -n easypj -o yaml
<span class="comment"># summary:</span>
<span class="comment">#   pass: 12</span>
<span class="comment">#   fail: 2</span>
<span class="comment"># results:</span>
<span class="comment"># - policy: require-team-label</span>
<span class="comment">#   result: fail</span>
<span class="comment">#   resources:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - kind: Pod</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: easypj-web-7d4f9c-x2k9p</span>
<span class="comment">#     namespace: easypj</span>

<span class="comment"># Cluster-scoped violations, e.g. a Gatekeeper constraint matching Namespaces</span>
kubectl get clusterpolicyreport -o yaml</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>OPA Gatekeeper Example — ConstraintTemplate</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">gatekeeper-example.yaml — require labels (Rego)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ConstraintTemplate defines the policy logic in Rego</span>
apiVersion: templates.gatekeeper.sh/v1
kind: ConstraintTemplate
metadata:
  name: k8srequiredlabels
spec:
  crd:
    spec:
      names:
        kind: K8sRequiredLabels
      validation:
        openAPIV3Schema:
          properties:
            labels:
              type: array
              items:
                type: string
  targets:
    - target: admission.k8s.gatekeeper.sh
      rego: |
        package k8srequiredlabels
        violation[{"msg": msg}] {
          provided := {label | input.review.object.metadata.labels[label]}
          required := {label | label := input.parameters.labels[_]}
          missing := required - provided
          count(missing) > 0
          msg := sprintf("Missing required labels: %v", [missing])
        }
---
<span class="comment"># Constraint applies the template with specific parameters</span>
apiVersion: constraints.gatekeeper.sh/v1beta1
kind: K8sRequiredLabels
metadata:
  name: require-team-label
spec:
  match:
    kinds:
      - apiGroups: [""]
        kinds: ["Namespace"]
  parameters:
    labels: ["team", "environment"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kyverno Example — ClusterPolicy</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">kyverno-example.yaml — require labels (YAML)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-team-label
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce
  rules:
    - name: check-team-label
      match:
        any:
          - resources:
              kinds:
                - Pod
      validate:
        message: "All pods must have a 'team' label"
        pattern:
          metadata:
            labels:
              team: "?*"         <span class="comment"># Must exist and be non-empty</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Which Policy Engine Should You Choose?</h5>
                    <p><strong>Start with ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> (built-in, no extra components). If you need mutation or resource generation, add <strong>Kyverno</strong> (easiest to learn). If your organization already uses OPA or needs complex cross-resource policies, use <strong>Gatekeeper</strong>. In practice, many teams combine ValidatingAdmissionPolicy for simple rules with an external engine for complex ones.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §41.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s41-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">41.7 Combining Policies — The Complete Stack</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>In production, you use multiple policy types together. Here's the complete policy stack for the easypj namespace:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Complete Policy Stack for easypj Namespace</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │              easypj NAMESPACE — POLICY STACK                │
 ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
 │                                                              │
 │  1. Pod Security Admission (enforce=restricted)             │
 │     └─ Blocks: privileged, hostNetwork, root, no seccomp    │
 │                                                              │
 │  2. ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)                         │
 │     ├─ Require minimum 2 replicas                           │
 │     ├─ Only approved image registries                       │
 │     └─ All containers need resource limits                  │
 │                                                              │
 │  3. LimitRange                                              │
 │     ├─ Default: 200m CPU / 256Mi memory                     │
 │     ├─ Max per container: 2 CPU / 2Gi memory                │
 │     └─ Min per container: 50m CPU / 64Mi memory             │
 │                                                              │
 │  4. ResourceQuota                                           │
 │     ├─ Total: 8 CPU / 16Gi memory                           │
 │     ├─ Max 30 pods                                          │
 │     └─ Max 2 LoadBalancers                                  │
 │                                                              │
 │  5. NetworkPolicy                                           │
 │     ├─ Default deny-all                                     │
 │     ├─ Allow internal namespace traffic                     │
 │     └─ Allow DNS egress to kube-system                      │
 │                                                              │
 │  6. PodDisruptionBudget                                     │
 │     └─ minAvailable: 2 (at least 2 pods during disruptions) │
 │                                                              │
 └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Scenario box: policy stack enforcement order -->
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🎬 Scenario: What Happens When easypj Deploys a Pod?</h5>
                    <div class="lifecycle-chain">
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #e74c3c; color: white;">1. PSA Gate</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #e67e22; color: white;">2. Admission Policy (CEL)</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #f1c40f; color: #333;">3. LimitRange Defaults</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #2ecc71; color: white;">4. ResourceQuota Check</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #3498db; color: white;">5. Schedule + NetPolicy</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #9b59b6; color: white;">6. PDB Protected</div>
                    </div>
                    <p style="margin-top:12px;"><strong>If the pod runs <code>privileged: true</code></strong> → blocked at step 1 (PSA). <strong>If it has no resource limits</strong> → LimitRange injects defaults at step 3. <strong>If the namespace is over quota</strong> → rejected at step 4. Each layer adds defense-in-depth.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — verify the policy stack</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">echo "=== Pod Security Standards ==="
kubectl get namespace easypj -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels}' | jq .

echo "=== LimitRange ==="
kubectl get limitrange -n easypj

echo "=== ResourceQuota ==="
kubectl describe resourcequota -n easypj

echo "=== NetworkPolicies ==="
kubectl get networkpolicy -n easypj

echo "=== PodDisruptionBudgets ==="
kubectl get pdb -n easypj

echo "=== ValidatingAdmissionPolicies ==="
kubectl get validatingadmissionpolicybindings -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.spec.matchResources.namespaceSelector.matchLabels | 
  to_entries[]? | .value == "easypj" or .key == "kubernetes.io/metadata.name") |
  .metadata.name'</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch41-uth"></span>
