                <summary>Under the Hood — How Each Policy Layer Actually Executes Inside the API Server</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Every section above showed you the YAML. This is the machinery underneath: <strong>where</strong> in the request pipeline each layer runs, <strong>what</strong> it costs, and <strong>why</strong> the order (LimitRange → PSA → VAP → ResourceQuota) is fixed rather than arbitrary.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ONE incoming "create pod" request for easypj — the full admission trip:

   kubectl apply -f pod.yaml ─▶ 📡 kube-apiserver
        │
        ├─ 1. 🔐 AuthN / 🛂 AuthZ (RBAC)     — who are you, are you allowed?
        ├─ 2. 🧬 Mutating admission          — LimitRange injects defaults HERE
        ├─ 3. 📋 Schema validation           — is the YAML well-formed?
        ├─ 4. ✅ Validating admission         — PSA, VAP, ResourceQuota ALL run here
        └─ 5. 💾 Persist to etcd             — only if every gate above said yes
   easypj example: a bare pod with no resources gets its cpu/mem FILLED IN at
   stage 2, THEN gets checked against PSA + the namespace quota at stage 4 —
   which is exactly why LimitRange must inject BEFORE ResourceQuota can count.
                    </pre>

                    <h5>① Pod Security Admission — a stateless, in-process gate with no CRD</h5>
                    <p>Unlike RBAC (which reads Role/RoleBinding objects) or a webhook (which calls out to a Pod), <strong>Pod Security Admission has no API object of its own</strong>. It is a built-in admission plugin compiled into <code>kube-apiserver</code> that reads exactly one thing: the three <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/*</code> labels on the target namespace. There is nothing to install, nothing to keep alive, and nothing that can crash independently of the API server itself.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Pod create request for namespace "easypj"
        │
        ▼
   PSA plugin reads:  kubectl get ns easypj -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels}'
        │              pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
        │              pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version: v1.30
        ▼
   PSA evaluates the pod's securityContext / capabilities / host-namespace
   fields against the "restricted" policy definition PINNED to v1.30 rules
        │
   ┌────┴────┐
   │ PASS    │──▶ request continues to the next admission stage (VAP, quota…)
   │ FAIL    │──▶ 403 Forbidden, request REJECTED before it ever reaches etcd
   └─────────┘
   note: warn/audit labels run the SAME evaluation but never set the FAIL path
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>PSA (built-in)</th><th>A hand-rolled validating webhook</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>What defines the rule</td><td>Fixed, versioned profile (privileged/baseline/restricted)</td><td>Whatever code you deploy</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Extra components to run</td><td>None — compiled into the apiserver</td><td>A Service + Deployment + TLS cert</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Configured via</td><td>Namespace labels</td><td>A <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code> object</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Can it mutate?</td><td>No — validate only</td><td>No (a <em>mutating</em> webhook can; this is validating)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Because PSA is namespace-label-driven and stateless, it is the cheapest, most reliable policy layer in the whole stack — nothing to keep healthy, nothing to upgrade separately from the cluster itself. This is exactly why it's the first VALIDATING gate: reject the obviously dangerous pod before spending any more validation work on it. (LimitRange, being MUTATING, has already run by this point — see ② below and UTH's opening admission-trip diagram.)</div>

                    <h5>② LimitRange's real job: it is a MUTATING admission plugin, not a validator</h5>
                    <p>Most administrators think of LimitRange purely as "a bound." Its more important job happens earlier and more quietly: LimitRange is implemented as the <strong>LimitRanger</strong> admission plugin, and it runs during the <em>mutating</em> phase — meaning it can rewrite the incoming pod object before anything downstream (like ResourceQuota) ever sees it.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   bare pod submitted (no resources block):
     spec.containers[0] = { name: django, image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1 }
        │
        ▼  LimitRanger admission plugin (MUTATING stage)
     reads LimitRange "easypj-limits": default cpu=200m/mem=256Mi
                                        defaultRequest cpu=100m/mem=128Mi
        │
        ▼  object is REWRITTEN in-flight:
     spec.containers[0].resources = {
       limits:   { cpu: 200m, memory: 256Mi },   ◀── injected
       requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi } }   ◀── injected
        │
        ▼  ONLY NOW does the object reach the VALIDATING stage, where
           ResourceQuota can finally see non-empty requests to count against
           the namespace total. Without this step, ResourceQuota would see
           an empty resources block and reject the pod outright.
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> "LimitRange fills in the blanks so ResourceQuota can count them" is not a metaphor — it's the literal admission-pipeline order. Mutating admission (LimitRange) always runs BEFORE validating admission (ResourceQuota), by design. This single fact explains the entire "pair LimitRange with ResourceQuota" rule from §41.2.</div>

                    <h5>③ CEL evaluation inside ValidatingAdmissionPolicy — compiled, sandboxed, cheap</h5>
                    <p>A CEL expression like <code>object.spec.replicas >= 2</code> is not interpreted line-by-line like a shell script. The API server <strong>compiles</strong> each expression into an internal representation once (when the policy is created/updated), then evaluates the compiled form per matching request — a fraction of the cost of a network round-trip to a webhook.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ValidatingAdmissionPolicy "require-min-replicas" is created
        │
        ▼  kube-apiserver COMPILES the CEL expression ONCE:
     "object.spec.replicas >= 2"  ──▶  compiled CEL program (cached in memory)
        │
        ▼  for EVERY matching Deployment create/update afterward:
     bind "object" = the incoming Deployment  ──▶  run compiled program
        │
   ┌────┴────┐
   │ true     │──▶ validation PASSES, request continues
   │ false    │──▶ validationActions fires: Deny / Warn / Audit
   │ ERROR    │──▶ failurePolicy decides: Fail = reject, Ignore = allow
   └─────────┘
   cost per request: microseconds, in-process — no HTTP call, no TLS handshake
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Where Evaluation Happens</th><th>Network Hop?</th><th>New Failure Modes</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)</td><td>Inside <code>kube-apiserver</code> process</td><td>No</td><td>CEL compile/runtime error only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Validating webhook (Gatekeeper/Kyverno)</td><td>External Pod, reached over HTTPS</td><td>Yes</td><td>DNS, TLS, timeout, webhook Pod crash/OOM, network partition</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> "No webhook to deploy" isn't just an operational convenience — it removes an ENTIRE CLASS of failure modes (timeouts, cert rotation, webhook Pod crashes) from your admission critical path. This is why VAP is the "should know" item on the exam: it's the modern, lower-risk default for simple rules.</div>

                    <h5>④ How Gatekeeper and Kyverno actually intercept a request (the webhook mechanics VAP skips)</h5>
                    <p>When a rule genuinely needs mutation, generation, or Rego, the request takes a fundamentally different path: the API server makes an <strong>outbound HTTPS call</strong> to a webhook Service, which proxies to the policy engine's own Pods. This is more powerful but introduces exactly the operational surface VAP was designed to avoid.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml ─▶ 📡 kube-apiserver
        │
        ├─ mutating admission ─▶ 🌐 gatekeeper-webhook Service (ClusterIP)
        │                             │  HTTPS + mTLS, must present a valid cert
        │                             ▼
        │                        🖥️ gatekeeper-controller-manager Pod(s)
        │                             │  evaluates Rego ConstraintTemplates
        │                             ▼  admission review response: allow/deny/patch
        │                        ⏱️ timeoutSeconds (default 10s) — if it doesn't
        │                            answer in time, failurePolicy decides
        ▼
   request continues (or is rejected) based on the webhook's verdict
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Every webhook-based engine (Gatekeeper, Kyverno, and any custom mutating/validating webhook) adds a <code>timeoutSeconds</code> and a <code>failurePolicy</code> knob. A slow or crashed webhook Pod with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> can block ALL matching admissions cluster-wide — a real, exam-relevant production outage pattern, and the reason production Gatekeeper/Kyverno installs always run multiple replicas behind their webhook Service.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ PID limits under the hood — the cgroup <code>pids.max</code> controller</h5>
                    <p><code>podPidsLimit</code> is not a Kubernetes-level counter — it's a direct pass-through to the Linux kernel's cgroup <code>pids</code> controller. Every pod gets its own cgroup, and the kubelet writes the configured limit straight into that cgroup's <code>pids.max</code> file when the pod is created.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubelet creates pod cgroup:  /sys/fs/cgroup/pids/kubepods/.../pod-&lt;uid&gt;/
        │
        ▼  kubelet writes the configured limit:
     echo 1024 &gt; .../pod-&lt;uid&gt;/pids.max
        │
        ▼  process inside the pod tries to fork() past the limit:
     fork() ──▶ kernel checks pids.current against pids.max ──▶ EAGAIN
                "Resource temporarily unavailable" — returned to the OFFENDING
                pod's process ONLY. Every other pod's cgroup is untouched.
                    </pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> This is why a pod hitting its PID limit logs "fork: retry: Resource temporarily unavailable" or "cannot allocate memory" while the NODE still has plenty of free RAM — the kernel is refusing new processes, not running out of memory. Recognizing this log signature instantly rules out a memory investigation and points straight at <code>podPidsLimit</code>.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ Gatekeeper's compilation pipeline — how a ConstraintTemplate becomes an enforced Rego policy</h5>
                    <p>§41.6 showed you a ConstraintTemplate and a Constraint as two separate YAML files. What actually happens between "I applied the template" and "a violating pod gets rejected" is a compile step most administrators never see: the Gatekeeper controller turns your Rego source into a brand-new, dynamically-registered API type.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ConstraintTemplate "k8srequiredlabels" applied via kubectl:
     spec.crd.spec.names.kind: K8sRequiredLabels        ◀── the NEW Kind name
     spec.targets[0].rego: |
       violation[{"msg": msg}] { ... }                   ◀── the RULE body
        │
        ▼  Gatekeeper controller reconciles the ConstraintTemplate:
     1. registers a NEW CRD "K8sRequiredLabels" (group constraints.gatekeeper.sh)
     2. compiles the embedded Rego into the OPA constraint-framework library,
        keyed by that Kind — a ONE-TIME compile, cached in the controller's memory
        │
        ▼  admin creates a Constraint (an INSTANCE of the new Kind):
     kind: K8sRequiredLabels
     metadata: { name: must-have-team-label }
     spec: { match: {kinds:[{kinds:["Namespace"]}]}, parameters: {labels:["team"]} }
        │
        ▼  admission review arrives for a new Namespace:
     webhook looks up every Constraint of every Kind whose "match" fits this object
        │
        ▼  for EACH matching Constraint: run the ALREADY-COMPILED Rego for its Kind,
           binding input.review = AdmissionReview, input.parameters = spec.parameters
        │
   ┌────┴────┐
   │ no violation   │──▶ allow
   │ violation found│──▶ deny (or warn, per enforcementAction) — msg returned verbatim
   └─────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>ConstraintTemplate</th><th>Constraint</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Role</td><td>Defines the RULE (Rego) + registers a new CRD Kind</td><td>An INSTANCE of that Kind — supplies parameters + match criteria</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Analogy</td><td>Class / schema</td><td>Object / instance</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Rego compiled</td><td>Once, when the template is created/updated</td><td>Never — every Constraint reuses the template's compiled Rego</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Reusable?</td><td>N/A — it's the definition</td><td>Yes: many Constraints can share ONE ConstraintTemplate with different <code>parameters</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> ConstraintTemplate/Constraint is a class/instance split for the same reason CustomResourceDefinition/CustomResource is: write the Rego ONCE, then stamp out many parameterized policies (require label <code>team</code> in one namespace, require label <code>cost-center</code> in another) without touching Rego again. It also explains a real production gotcha: deleting a ConstraintTemplate cascades and garbage-collects every Constraint instance of the Kind it defined, because the CRD itself disappears with it.</div>

                    <h5>⑦ Topology spread under the hood — the scheduler's two-phase Filter/Score handling of maxSkew</h5>
                    <p><code>maxSkew</code> isn't one global calculation the scheduler runs once — it's implemented as TWO separate scheduler-framework plugin hooks, and which one actually runs depends entirely on whether <code>whenUnsatisfiable</code> is <code>DoNotSchedule</code> (hard) or <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> (soft).</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   scheduler receives Pod #4 of Deployment easypj-spread (3 already placed):
     node-1: 2 pods    node-2: 1 pod    node-3: 1 pod      (current skew = 1)
        │
        ▼  PreFilter: build a per-topology-domain pod COUNT map ONCE per cycle
     {node-1: 2, node-2: 1, node-3: 1}   ◀── cached, reused by every node's Filter call
        │
        ▼  Filter phase (runs ONLY if whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule):
     candidate node-1: would placing here make count=3?
       skew = 3 - min(2,1,1) = 2  &gt;  maxSkew(1)  ──▶ node-1 REMOVED from feasible set
     candidate node-2: skew = 2 - 1 = 1  ≤  maxSkew(1)  ──▶ node-2 stays feasible
        │
        ▼  Score phase (runs ONLY if whenUnsatisfiable: ScheduleAnyway):
     nodes are NOT removed — instead, nodes that would WORSEN skew get a LOWER
     score, so the pod prefers the least-skewed node but can still land anywhere
        │
        ▼  final bind: pod scheduled to node-2 or node-3 (whichever wins Score)
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>DoNotSchedule</th><th>ScheduleAnyway</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Scheduler-framework phase</td><td>Filter (hard exclude)</td><td>Score (soft preference)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Node that would break maxSkew</td><td>Removed from the feasible set entirely</td><td>Still eligible, just scored lower</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>If NO node satisfies maxSkew</td><td>Pod stays <code>Pending</code></td><td>Pod still gets scheduled somewhere</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Exam-safe default</td><td>Yes — deterministic, gradeable outcome</td><td>No — "success" can still be skewed</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> A Pending pod right after adding <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> almost always means <code>DoNotSchedule</code> made every remaining node infeasible — confirm with <code>kubectl describe pod</code> and look for a "didn't satisfy PodTopologySpreadConstraint" event, then either add capacity in the underfilled domain or relax to <code>ScheduleAnyway</code>. The exam favors <code>DoNotSchedule</code> because it's deterministic and gradeable; production rollouts often START with <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> to avoid stranding day-one capacity, then tighten to <code>DoNotSchedule</code> once headroom is proven.</div>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> the whole chapter is really one idea applied five different ways — a request or a resource declaration hits a <em>gate</em>, and the gate either accepts, rejects, mutates, or (for topology spread) constrains WHERE the object may land. Whether that gate is a stateless namespace-label check (PSA), an in-process compiled expression (VAP), a cgroup write (PID limits), a scheduler predicate (topology spread), or a full external webhook (Gatekeeper/Kyverno), the mental model is identical: <strong>find the gate, know its order relative to the others, know what makes it fail.</strong></p>
                </div>
