            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch41-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Policy is layered, not singular</strong> — LimitRange (inject defaults, MUTATING) → PSA (pod security) → ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL, in-cluster, no webhook) → ResourceQuota (namespace totals) → then NetworkPolicy + PodDisruptionBudget at runtime.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pod Security Standards</strong> have three levels (privileged / baseline / restricted) applied in three modes (enforce / warn / audit) via namespace labels, and can be version-pinned so a cluster upgrade never silently tightens the rules.</li>
                    <li><strong>PSA is per-namespace and never evicts running pods</strong> — it only checks NEW or UPDATED pods. Always roll out warn/audit before flipping to enforce.</li>
                    <li><strong>LimitRange sets per-container defaults and min/max bounds; ResourceQuota caps the namespace's TOTAL requests/limits and object counts.</strong> They're designed to work together: a ResourceQuota on requests/limits forces every pod to declare them, and LimitRange injects defaults so bare pods still pass that quota.</li>
                    <li><strong>podPidsLimit protects a node from fork bombs</strong> by capping processes per pod via the cgroup <code>pids.max</code> controller — the blast radius is contained to the offending pod only.</li>
                    <li><strong>topologySpreadConstraints (maxSkew + DoNotSchedule) enforce even distribution</strong> across zones/nodes as a reliability/governance control, not just a scheduling nicety.</li>
                    <li><strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy runs CEL expressions in-process</strong> — a Policy (the rule) plus a Binding (where it applies + validationActions) with no external webhook to deploy, secure, or keep alive.</li>
                    <li><strong>External engines add power via webhooks</strong> — OPA Gatekeeper uses Rego (ConstraintTemplates + Constraints); Kyverno uses native YAML and additionally supports mutate/generate/verifyImages. Both register admission webhooks, so a down/slow webhook with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> can block admissions cluster-wide.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pick the least-powerful tool that solves the problem</strong> — start with ValidatingAdmissionPolicy for simple native rules, add Kyverno for mutate/generate, reach for Gatekeeper if your org already uses Rego or needs complex cross-resource logic.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch41-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Labels apply at namespace level only</strong> — you cannot apply PSA to individual pods, only entire namespaces, and PSA never evicts already-running pods (only NEW/UPDATED ones are checked)</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Rolling straight to <code>enforce</code> without <code>warn</code>/<code>audit</code> first instantly blocks legitimate deploys</strong> — a real, self-inflicted production outage pattern. Always preview with warn/audit, fix manifests, THEN flip to enforce.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>A CEL syntax error, or a reference to a field that doesn't exist on the object, makes a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy misbehave silently</strong> — <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> rejects on evaluation error, <code>Ignore</code> lets it through. Test the expression (e.g. in the CEL playground) before trusting it in production.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>A too-tight ResourceQuota with NO LimitRange defaults rejects every pod that omits requests/limits</strong> — the error looks like a quota bug but the fix is always a LimitRange, never editing the quota.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Not version-pinning the PSS label (<code>enforce-version</code>) means a future cluster upgrade can silently tighten "restricted"</strong> and start rejecting pods that were previously valid — a delayed-onset incident with no obvious trigger.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Gatekeeper/Kyverno are webhooks sitting in the critical admission path</strong> — a down or slow webhook Pod with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> can block ALL matching admissions cluster-wide, not just the ones the policy was meant to catch.</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Use warn + audit first, always</strong> — apply warning mode before enforce to discover violations without breaking workloads</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Check with dry-run</strong> — <code>kubectl label --dry-run=server --overwrite ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code> shows what would be blocked</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch41-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Quick Reference</strong> — Memorize the three Pod Security Standards levels cold: <strong>Privileged</strong> (no restrictions), <strong>Baseline</strong> (blocks privileged containers, hostNetwork/hostPID/hostIPC, hostPath volumes, dangerous capabilities), <strong>Restricted</strong> (baseline + must run as non-root, capabilities dropped, seccomp required). They replaced the deprecated PodSecurityPolicy (removed in v1.25) — expect exam questions on the <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce</code> namespace label, not the old PSP API.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>⚡ Reach for a <strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> for any simple cluster-native rule BEFORE standing up a full Gatekeeper/Kyverno install — it's built-in, nothing extra to deploy.</li>
                    <li>⚡ A LimitRange with both <code>default</code> AND <code>defaultRequest</code> means bare pods (no resources block at all) still schedule sanely under a ResourceQuota — set both, not just one.</li>
                    <li>⚡ <code>kubectl get resourcequota -n easypj -o yaml</code> (or <code>describe</code>) shows <code>used</code> vs <code>hard</code> side by side — instantly see which resource is exhausted instead of guessing why pods are Pending or rejected.</li>
                    <li>⚡ Label a namespace <code>warn</code> + <code>audit</code> at the TARGET PSS level first — this previews every breakage with zero blocking, before you ever touch <code>enforce</code>.</li>
                    <li>⚡ <code>kubectl create quota &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; --hard=cpu=4,memory=8Gi,pods=20</code> is the fastest imperative path to a namespace cap under exam time pressure — no YAML file needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

