            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 41 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the policy mental model — 11 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Apply the <code>restricted</code> Pod Security Standard to the <code>easypj</code> namespace, safely.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Roll out <code>warn</code> + <code>audit</code> first to observe breakage with zero blocking, fix any offending manifests, THEN flip to <code>enforce</code>.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1) observe only — nobody is blocked</span>
kubectl label ns easypj \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted

<span class="comment"># 2) fix any manifests that would violate (drop caps, runAsNonRoot, etc.)</span>

<span class="comment"># 3) commit to the hard block</span>
kubectl label ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted --overwrite</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PSA is namespace-labeled and stateless — there's no separate object to create. The safe-rollout pattern (warn/audit → fix → enforce) is the single most exam- and production-relevant workflow in this chapter, because jumping straight to <code>enforce</code> on a busy namespace can instantly block legitimate deploys.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between LimitRange and ResourceQuota, and why are they almost always used together?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>LimitRange</th><th>ResourceQuota</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Scope</td><td>Per-container / per-pod</td><td>Entire namespace (aggregate)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Main job</td><td>Inject defaults, enforce min/max</td><td>Cap the SUM across all pods + object counts</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>They're paired because a ResourceQuota that constrains <code>requests.cpu</code>/<code>requests.memory</code> forces EVERY pod to declare those fields — a bare pod with no resources block would be rejected. A LimitRange with <code>default</code>/<code>defaultRequest</code> injects those fields automatically (during mutating admission, see UTH ②), so bare pods still pass the quota check.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ResourceQuota capping the <code>easypj</code> namespace at 4 CPU requests, 8Gi memory requests, and 20 pods, using the fastest exam-appropriate method.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create quota easypj-quota -n easypj \
  --hard=requests.cpu=4,requests.memory=8Gi,pods=20

kubectl describe resourcequota easypj-quota -n easypj</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The imperative <code>kubectl create quota</code> command with <code>--hard=</code> is the fastest path under exam time pressure — no YAML file needed. <code>kubectl describe resourcequota</code> shows <code>Used</code> next to <code>Hard</code> so you instantly see which resource, if any, is exhausted.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are PID limits for, and what log signature tells you a pod hit one?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>podPidsLimit</code> caps the number of processes a single pod may fork, protecting the NODE's kernel PID table from a fork bomb or runaway process loop in one misbehaving pod. A pod that hits its limit logs something like <code>fork: retry: Resource temporarily unavailable</code> — <strong>while the node still has free RAM</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                            <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧬</div><h5>Real memory pressure</h5><p>Node RAM is actually exhausted — OOM killer activity, MemoryPressure condition true</p></div>
                            <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧯</div><h5>PID limit hit</h5><p>Plenty of free RAM, but <code>fork()</code> fails with EAGAIN — the cgroup <code>pids.max</code> ceiling, not memory</p></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This distinction is a classic troubleshooting trap: the error message LOOKS like a memory problem but is actually the kernel's <code>pids</code> cgroup controller refusing to allow more processes. Checking <code>free -m</code> on the node (plenty free) alongside that log line is the tell.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a topology spread constraint that forces easypj-web pods to spread evenly across zones, with a hard guarantee.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">topologySpreadConstraints:
  - maxSkew: 1
    topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
    whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
    labelSelector:
      matchLabels:
        app: easypj-web</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule</code> is what makes this a hard "policy," not a soft preference — a pod that would break the maxSkew stays <strong>Pending</strong> rather than scheduling somewhere lopsided. <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> would make it best-effort instead. The <code>labelSelector</code> MUST match the pod template's own labels, or the scheduler can't count existing pods correctly.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy that requires all Deployments in easypj to have at least 2 replicas — no webhook allowed.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-min-replicas
spec:
  failurePolicy: Fail
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
    - apiGroups: ["apps"]
      apiVersions: ["v1"]
      operations: ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]
      resources: ["deployments"]
  validations:
  - expression: "object.spec.replicas >= 2"
    message: "easypj Deployments must have at least 2 replicas"
---
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: require-min-replicas-binding
spec:
  policyName: require-min-replicas
  validationActions: ["Deny"]
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        kubernetes.io/metadata.name: easypj</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Two objects are always required: the <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> (the rule) and a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding</code> (where it applies + the <code>validationActions</code>). Because CEL runs in-process, there's no Service, Deployment, or TLS certificate to create — this is the entire solution.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Compare OPA Gatekeeper and Kyverno — when would you reach for each?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Choose</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Org already knows Rego / uses OPA elsewhere</td><td>OPA Gatekeeper</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Want native YAML, fast onboarding, mutate + generate + verifyImages</td><td>Kyverno</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Just one simple cluster-native rule</td><td>Neither — use ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Gatekeeper's authoring model is ConstraintTemplate (Rego) + Constraint (parameters). Kyverno expresses validate/mutate/generate/verifyImages directly in a single YAML <code>ClusterPolicy</code> — no new DSL. Both register as admission webhooks and publish results as <code>PolicyReport</code> CRDs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is rejected with an error like "must specify cpu" the moment a ResourceQuota is added to a namespace. Diagnose and fix.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The ResourceQuota constrains <code>requests.cpu</code> (and/or <code>requests.memory</code>), so every pod in the namespace must now declare that field — this pod's manifest omits it. Fix by adding a LimitRange with a matching <code>defaultRequest</code>, so the missing value is injected automatically.</p>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-defaults
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container
    defaultRequest:
      cpu: 100m
      memory: 128Mi</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the single most common ResourceQuota "gotcha" — the error message looks like a quota bug, but the real cause is a missing LimitRange. It's the same interplay tested in §41.2 and UTH ②, just experienced from the failure side instead of the working side.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Do PSA, ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, LimitRange, and ResourceQuota conflict with each other on the same namespace?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — they COMPOSE. Each layer guards a different aspect of the request, and a single create-pod request must satisfy every layer that applies to it, in order.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Composition, not conflict</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
 request ──▶ 📏 LimitRange ──▶ 🛡️ PSA ──▶ 🧮 VAP ──▶ 📊 ResourceQuota ──▶ scheduled
             (inject default)  (identity)  (CEL rule)  (namespace total)
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Understanding this as a PIPELINE rather than a set of competing rules is the key mental shift of the chapter — see §41.7 and UTH's opening admission-trip diagram for the full five-stage order.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you stop a cluster upgrade from silently tightening a Pod Security Standard under you?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label ns easypj \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.30 --overwrite</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Version-pinning the label evaluates the standard against the RULES of that specific Kubernetes version forever, regardless of what version the cluster is later upgraded to. Without a pin, a future upgrade could introduce new restrictions into "restricted" that start rejecting pods that were previously valid — a subtle, delayed-onset production incident.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the exact admission enforcement order a write request goes through, and where do LimitRange and ResourceQuota each sit in it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
authN → authZ (RBAC) → MUTATING admission webhooks/plugins → schema
validation → VALIDATING admission (PSA / VAP / validating webhooks) → persist to etcd
                        </pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>LimitRange is a MUTATING admission plugin (the LimitRanger), so it runs BEFORE schema validation and before any validating stage. ResourceQuota, PSA, and ValidatingAdmissionPolicy are all VALIDATING admission, running AFTER mutation. That ordering is exactly why LimitRange can inject default <code>cpu</code>/<code>memory</code> into a bare container and have ResourceQuota see a fully-specified request right after — defaults are always applied before totals are ever checked. See UTH's opening admission-trip diagram for the full five-stage picture.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

