pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.30 \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=baseline \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn-version=v1.30 \
--overwrite

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get namespace easypj --show-labels</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>You can mix enforcement levels and modes. Here, <code>restricted</code> is enforced (blocks violations), while <code>baseline</code> in <code>warn</code> mode shows warnings for baseline violations that pass restricted enforcement. Pinning to a version (e.g., <code>v1.30</code>) ensures consistent behavior even after cluster upgrades. Use <code>--overwrite</code> if labels already exist.</p>
