            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 41.1 — Roll Out Restricted PSA Without an Outage</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>The <code>easypj</code> namespace currently has NO Pod Security Standard applied and 3 running pods, one of which (a legacy debug pod) sets <code>privileged: true</code>. Roll out the <code>restricted</code> standard using the safe, exam-correct sequence — without breaking the running pods or blindly enforcing on your first attempt.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> label with <code>warn</code> + <code>audit</code> first, then check what would break, THEN enforce.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1) observe only — nothing is blocked yet</span>
kubectl label ns easypj \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted

<span class="comment"># 2) trigger a re-evaluation to see who'd fail (e.g. dry-run against the debug pod's spec)</span>
kubectl label --dry-run=server --overwrite ns easypj \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted
<span class="comment"># Warning: existing pods in namespace "easypj" violate "restricted": debug-pod</span>
<span class="comment">#   (and 1 more): privileged != false</span>

<span class="comment"># 3) fix or remove the offending pod, THEN commit to enforce</span>
kubectl delete pod debug-pod -n easypj
kubectl label ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted --overwrite</code></pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the existing legacy pod is NOT auto-evicted by <code>enforce</code> — PSA only checks new/updated pods. If you skipped straight to <code>enforce</code> without checking, you'd have blocked the NEXT legitimate deploy the moment it happened, not the pod that's already running. That delayed-failure gap is exactly what the warn/audit-first workflow is designed to prevent.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 41.2 — Diagnose a Quota Rejection That Isn't a Quota Bug</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A developer runs <code>kubectl run test --image=nginx -n easypj</code> and gets: <code>Error from server (Forbidden): pods "test" is forbidden: failed quota: easypj-quota: must specify limits.cpu,requests.cpu</code>. There is a ResourceQuota named <code>easypj-quota</code> in the namespace. Diagnose the real cause and fix it WITHOUT editing the ResourceQuota.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the fix is a completely different object type from the one in the error message.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># The ResourceQuota is doing its job correctly — there's simply no LimitRange</span>
<span class="comment"># to inject defaults into a bare pod. Add one:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-defaults
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container
    default:
      cpu: 200m
      memory: 256Mi
    defaultRequest:
      cpu: 100m
      memory: 128Mi</code></pre>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f limitrange.yaml
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n easypj   <span class="comment"># now succeeds</span></code></pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the ResourceQuota's error message names the FIELDS that are missing, not the OBJECT that fixes them — a common exam trap. Never edit the ResourceQuota to solve this; the correct fix is always adding (or fixing) a LimitRange so bare pods get defaults injected during mutating admission.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 41.3 — Distinguish a PID-Limit Log from a Real Memory Problem</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>The <code>easypj-worker</code> pod's logs show <code>fork: retry: Resource temporarily unavailable</code> repeatedly. <code>kubectl top node</code> shows the node at only 40% memory usage. A teammate insists on doubling the pod's memory limit. Explain why that fix is wrong, and give the correct diagnostic + fix.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the log line is about processes, not bytes.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
1. RULE OUT memory:  kubectl top node        → 40% used, plenty free
2. SUSPECT PIDs:     kubectl get --raw /api/v1/nodes/&lt;node&gt;/proxy/configz \
                       | jq '.kubeletconfig.podPidsLimit'    → 256
3. CONFIRM:          the worker forks a new process per job with no pool/
                     reuse, and legitimately needs &gt;256 concurrent PIDs
4. FIX:              raise podPidsLimit in the KubeletConfiguration on that
                     node (requires a kubelet restart), OR fix the worker to
                     reuse a process pool instead of forking unboundedly
                        </pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> raising the memory <code>limit</code> would do nothing — the kernel's <code>pids.max</code> cgroup controller rejects the fork() call regardless of how much RAM is available. Recognizing "plenty of free RAM + fork error" as a PID-limit signature (not a memory signature) is the core skill this drill tests.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 41.4 — Fix a Topology Spread That Silently Isn't Working</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>The <code>easypj-web</code> Deployment has a <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> block with <code>maxSkew: 1</code> and <code>topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code>, yet <code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> shows all 6 replicas piled onto nodes in a single zone. Find the misconfiguration and fix it.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the constraint's own <code>labelSelector</code> has to match something specific.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># BROKEN — labelSelector doesn't match the pod template's labels</span>
topologySpreadConstraints:
- maxSkew: 1
  topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
  whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
  labelSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web            <span class="comment"># ← pod template actually has app: easypj-web</span>

<span class="comment"># FIXED — matches the real pod template labels</span>
topologySpreadConstraints:
- maxSkew: 1
  topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
  whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
  labelSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web</code></pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> a mismatched <code>labelSelector</code> means the scheduler counts ZERO existing pods for the skew calculation on every placement decision — every new pod "sees" an empty domain and happily schedules wherever, silently defeating the constraint with no error or warning. Always verify <code>labelSelector</code> matches <code>spec.template.metadata.labels</code> exactly.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 41.5 — Choose the Right Policy Tool for Five Requirements</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>For each requirement, name the single best Chapter 41 mechanism: (a) block any container from running as root, cluster-default; (b) guarantee no bare pod skips resource requests without extra YAML per pod; (c) require Deployments to carry a <code>team</code> label AND auto-inject it if missing; (d) require Deployments to have ≥3 replicas, with zero extra pods to run; (e) stop a single pod's fork loop from starving its node.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> ask "does this need to MUTATE the object?" — that single question eliminates two of the five options immediately.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>(a) block root containers, cluster-default</td><td>Pod Security Admission — <code>restricted</code>, enforce</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(b) bare pods still get resource requests</td><td>LimitRange (<code>defaultRequest</code>)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(c) require + auto-inject a <code>team</code> label</td><td>Kyverno (needs MUTATION, not just validation)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(d) require ≥3 replicas, zero extra pods</td><td>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL, in-process)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>(e) stop a fork loop from starving a node</td><td><code>podPidsLimit</code> (PID limit)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> (c) is the trap most people miss — a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy can only ACCEPT or REJECT, it cannot inject the missing label. Any requirement containing the word "inject," "default," or "auto-add" needs a MUTATING mechanism (LimitRange for resources, or Kyverno/a mutating webhook for arbitrary fields) — CEL-based VAP is validation-only.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 41.6 — Stack the Whole Policy Pipeline and Watch One Bad Pod Get Caught Layer by Layer</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 10 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>On the <code>easypj</code> namespace, stack ALL FOUR admission-time layers at once: PSA <code>enforce=restricted</code>, a LimitRange with default cpu/mem, a ResourceQuota, and the "replicas &gt;= 2" ValidatingAdmissionPolicy from §41.5. Then attempt to create a 1-replica Deployment whose pod template explicitly sets <code>runAsUser: 0</code> (root) and omits a resources block. Predict — and then observe — which layer rejects it, and in what order the layers would have been checked if the first one hadn't already stopped it.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> mutating admission (LimitRange) always finishes before ANY validating admission (PSA, VAP, ResourceQuota) begins — but among the validating layers themselves, think about which one is cheapest/statelessness-first.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1) stack every layer</span>
kubectl label ns easypj pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted --overwrite
kubectl apply -f limitrange.yaml     <span class="comment"># default cpu/mem</span>
kubectl apply -f resourcequota.yaml
kubectl apply -f vap-min-replicas.yaml   <span class="comment"># Policy + Binding from §41.5</span>

<span class="comment"># 2) attempt the non-compliant Deployment</span>
kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-bad
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector: { matchLabels: { app: easypj-bad } }
  template:
    metadata: { labels: { app: easypj-bad } }
    spec:
      securityContext: { runAsUser: 0 }
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
EOF
<span class="comment"># Error from server (Forbidden): pods "easypj-bad-xxxxx" is forbidden:</span>
<span class="comment">#   violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest": runAsNonRoot != true</span>
<span class="comment">#   (container "django" must not set runAsNonRoot=false or runAsUser=0)</span></code></pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the request is rejected by Pod Security Admission (<code>runAsUser: 0</code> fails <code>restricted</code>'s <code>runAsNonRoot</code> requirement) — but that is only ONE of at least two violations present (the 1-replica count is the other). Admission STOPS at the first validating rejection; it does not continue checking every remaining layer once one has already said no. PSA runs FIRST among the validating layers (see UTH ①) precisely because it is the cheapest, stateless check, so it catches the root-user violation before the ValidatingAdmissionPolicy ever gets a chance to evaluate the replica count. If you fix the pod's <code>securityContext</code> and resubmit, the NEXT rejection surfaces — the ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, for <code>replicas &lt; 2</code>. LimitRange, being MUTATING, would have already injected default cpu/mem in the background on the very first attempt (mutating admission always finishes before validating admission runs), so ResourceQuota is never even the layer that catches this particular pod. The lesson: fixing one rejection often just reveals the next one underneath — always re-check ALL layers after any fix, not just re-run the same command once.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 41 Visual Summary — the layered policy stack over easypj</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   admission-time layers                              runtime layers
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────┐
  │ 📏 LimitRange (MUTATING — injects defaults)  │    │ 🌐 NetworkPolicy     │
  │    fills cpu/mem so bare pods aren't bare    │    │   who may talk to    │
  │        ▼                                    │    │   whom, default-deny │
  │ 🛡️ PSA (enforce=restricted)                  │    ├─────────────────────┤
  │    blocks: root, privileged, hostNetwork    │    │ 🛟 PodDisruptionBudget│
  │        ▼                                    │    │   minAvailable: 2 —  │
  │ 🧮 ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)           │    │   survives drains    │
  │    e.g. object.spec.replicas &gt;= 2            │    └─────────────────────┘
  │        ▼                                    │
  │ 📊 ResourceQuota (namespace totals)          │     🧩 external engines
  │    rejects if the namespace total is exceeded│    (when CEL isn't enough)
  └────────────────────────────────────────────┘    OPA Gatekeeper (Rego)
                    ▼ scheduled                       Kyverno (YAML, mutate+
              ✅ pod runs, governed end to end          generate+verifyImages)
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Chapter 41 shows that "policy" in Kubernetes is not one API object — it's a layered pipeline where each layer guards a different aspect of a request. A pod-create request for easypj first hits LimitRange, which quietly MUTATES the request, injecting default cpu/memory into any container that omitted them — mutating admission always finishes before validating admission runs. Only then does the now fully-specified pod reach the validating gates: Pod Security Admission (blocks root/privileged outright), a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy CEL rule with no webhook to deploy, and finally ResourceQuota, which — thanks to LimitRange's earlier mutation — can now count a fully-specified request instead of rejecting a bare one.</p>
                    <p>Two more controls apply once the pod is running: NetworkPolicy governs who it may talk to, and a PodDisruptionBudget guarantees a minimum number of replicas survive voluntary disruptions like node drains. When native CEL rules aren't expressive enough — you need mutation, generation, or complex cross-resource logic — you reach for an external engine: OPA Gatekeeper (Rego, ConstraintTemplates) or Kyverno (native YAML, validate+mutate+generate+verifyImages), both wired in as admission webhooks.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> master the ORDER (LimitRange → PSA → VAP → ResourceQuota → NetworkPolicy/PDB) and which layers can MUTATE vs only VALIDATE, and every policy question — on the exam or in production — becomes "which layer, and why did it act the way it did?" instead of a memorization exercise.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
