            <!-- ─────────── §42.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s42-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">42.1 Mutating vs Validating Webhooks</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-advanced">🏢 PRODUCTION</span>

                <p>Dynamic admission control hooks the API server out to YOUR HTTP endpoints at write time. Every Deployment, Service, PVC, and NetworkPolicy you have ever applied to easypj was inspected, possibly rewritten, and either accepted or rejected before it ever touched etcd. There are two kinds of hook, registered by two different configuration objects, and they run in a <strong>fixed order</strong>.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 KEY TERM — MutatingWebhookConfiguration</h5>
                    <p>Registers webhook endpoints that may <strong>CHANGE</strong> the incoming object (via a JSON Patch) before it is stored. Examples: inject an Envoy/Istio sidecar into an easypj-web pod, set a default <code>securityContext</code>, add labels/annotations, or set defaults the OpenAPI schema can't express.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 KEY TERM — ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</h5>
                    <p>Registers endpoints that may only say <strong>YES or NO</strong>. They CANNOT change the object. Examples: reject <code>:latest</code> images, reject images from non-allowlisted registries, require resource limits. OPA Gatekeeper and Kyverno are validating-webhook engines.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Mutating Webhook</th><th>Validating Webhook</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Config object</strong></td><td><code>MutatingWebhookConfiguration</code></td><td><code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Can change the object?</strong></td><td>✅ YES — returns a JSON Patch</td><td>❌ NO — accept / reject only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Runs</strong></td><td>FIRST</td><td>AFTER mutating + schema validation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Typical use</strong></td><td>sidecar injection, defaulting</td><td>policy enforcement, denial</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Response shape</strong></td><td><code>allowed</code> + <code>patch</code> + <code>patchType: JSONPatch</code></td><td><code>allowed</code> + <code>status.message</code> (no patch)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Real engines</strong></td><td>Istio/Linkerd sidecar injector, cert-manager</td><td>OPA Gatekeeper, Kyverno</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 INSIGHT — why mutating runs BEFORE validating</h5>
                    <p>A mutating webhook may add the very field a validating webhook checks for. If a sidecar injector adds a container, and a validating policy requires every container to have resource limits, validation must see the <strong>FINAL (mutated)</strong> object — otherwise you'd validate something that won't actually be stored. So the pipeline mutates to a final shape, THEN validates that shape, THEN persists.</p>
                </div>

                <p>Both webhook types speak the exact same JSON envelope: an <strong>AdmissionReview</strong>. The API server POSTs an AdmissionReview <em>request</em> to your webhook; your webhook returns an AdmissionReview <em>response</em>.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">AdmissionReview — the REQUEST the apiserver sends to a webhook</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "apiVersion": "admission.k8s.io/v1",
  "kind": "AdmissionReview",
  "request": {
    "uid": "705ab-c3",              <span class="comment">// echo this back in the response</span>
    "operation": "CREATE",          <span class="comment">// CREATE / UPDATE / DELETE / CONNECT</span>
    "userInfo": {
      "username": "easypj-deployer",
      "groups": ["system:authenticated"]
    },
    "resource": { "group": "", "version": "v1", "resource": "pods" },
    "object":    { "...": "the new easypj-web pod object..." },
    "oldObject": null,              <span class="comment">// populated on UPDATE/DELETE</span>
    "dryRun":    false
  }
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">AdmissionReview — the RESPONSE a MUTATING webhook returns</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "apiVersion": "admission.k8s.io/v1",
  "kind": "AdmissionReview",
  "response": {
    "uid": "705ab-c3",              <span class="comment">// MUST match the request uid</span>
    "allowed": true,
    "patchType": "JSONPatch",
    "patch": "&lt;base64 of a JSON-Patch array&gt;"   <span class="comment">// e.g. add a sidecar</span>
  }
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">AdmissionReview — the RESPONSE a VALIDATING webhook returns</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "apiVersion": "admission.k8s.io/v1",
  "kind": "AdmissionReview",
  "response": {
    "uid": "705ab-c3",
    "allowed": false,               <span class="comment">// reject</span>
    "status": {
      "code": 403,
      "message": "image must not use the :latest tag"
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>What the base64 JSON Patch actually contains</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
 A mutating webhook's "patch" field is base64-encoded RFC-6902 JSON Patch:

 [ { "op":   "add",
     "path": "/spec/containers/-",
     "value": { "name": "envoy", "image": "envoy:v1.30", "...": "..." } } ]

 The API server decodes it, applies it to the easypj-web pod object, and the
 PATCHED object continues down the admission pipeline — schema validation and
 every validating webhook after this point sees the sidecar as if it had
 always been there.
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h4>Webhook Type Gotchas</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>❌ <strong>A validating webhook cannot fix what it rejects</strong> — it can only say no. If you need to auto-correct, you need a MUTATING webhook instead (or in addition).</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong>Forgetting the response <code>uid</code></strong> — if your webhook's response <code>uid</code> doesn't match the request's, the API server treats the response as invalid.</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Order is not configurable per-webhook</strong> — ALL mutating webhooks run (in a stable but not user-controlled relative order among themselves) before ANY validating webhook runs.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §42.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s42-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">42.2 The Admission Flow</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Every write request (CREATE/UPDATE/DELETE) the API server receives walks the SAME chain. Reads (GET/LIST/WATCH) skip admission entirely — this is why a broken webhook never stops you from reading the cluster to diagnose it.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>The Full Admission Pipeline — Annotated</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
 ┌──────────┐   who are you?        ┌──────────┐   are you allowed?
 │  authN   │ ────────────────────► │  authZ   │ ──────────────────►
 └──────────┘  (certs, tokens,      └──────────┘  (RBAC / Node / ABAC
                OIDC, webhook)                      / webhook)
                                                        │
      ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
      ▼
 ┌──────────────────────────────┐  built-in mutating plugins (e.g.
 │  MUTATING admission           │  DefaultStorageClass, ServiceAccount)
 │  • built-in plugins           │  run, THEN your registered
 │  • MutatingWebhookConfig(s)   │  MutatingWebhookConfigurations.
 └──────────────────────────────┘  Object may be REWRITTEN here.
      ▼
 ┌──────────────────────────────┐  decode → validate against the
 │  OBJECT SCHEMA VALIDATION     │  OpenAPI schema / CRD structural
 │  (+ defaulting)               │  schema. Bad fields rejected here.
 └──────────────────────────────┘
      ▼
 ┌──────────────────────────────┐  built-in validating plugins
 │  VALIDATING admission         │  (ResourceQuota, PodSecurity / PSA,
 │  • built-in plugins           │  LimitRanger checks), then
 │  • ValidatingAdmissionPolicy  │  ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL),
 │  • ValidatingWebhookConfig(s) │  then your validating webhooks.
 └──────────────────────────────┘  Accept / REJECT only — no changes.
      ▼
 ┌──────────────────────────────┐
 │  PERSIST to etcd 🗄️           │  only now is the object durable.
 └──────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Runs</th><th>Can reject?</th><th>Can change the object?</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>authN</td><td>certs, tokens, OIDC, webhook</td><td>✅ (401 if identity unresolved)</td><td>❌</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>authZ</td><td>RBAC / Node / ABAC / webhook</td><td>✅ (403 Forbidden)</td><td>❌</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Mutating admission</td><td>built-ins, then MutatingWebhookConfigs</td><td>✅ (if <code>allowed:false</code>)</td><td>✅ (JSON Patch)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Schema validation</td><td>OpenAPI / CRD structural schema</td><td>✅ (400 Bad Request)</td><td>❌</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Validating admission</td><td>built-ins, VAP (CEL), ValidatingWebhookConfigs</td><td>✅ (403 Forbidden)</td><td>❌</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Persist</td><td>write to etcd</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 ANALOGY — admission is airport security</h5>
                    <p><strong>authN</strong> = passport check (who you are). <strong>authZ</strong> = boarding pass check (are you allowed on this flight). <strong>Mutating</strong> = the agent who makes you take off your belt and put your laptop in a bin — they REARRANGE your stuff. <strong>Schema check</strong> = the scanner that only understands certain shapes. <strong>Validating</strong> = the final officer who can wave you through or pull you aside — but can't repack your bag. Only after all of it do you reach the gate (etcd).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — watching the chain reject an easypj deploy</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># A validating webhook rejecting a deploy looks exactly like this</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-web-deploy.yaml
<span class="comment"># Error from server: error when creating "easypj-web-deploy.yaml":</span>
<span class="comment"># admission webhook "policy.easypj.example.com" denied the request:</span>
<span class="comment">#   image must not use the :latest tag</span>

<span class="comment"># Reads are NEVER blocked by admission — this always still works:</span>
kubectl get deployment easypj-web -n easypj-production</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ GOTCHA — reinvocationPolicy and matchPolicy</h5>
                    <p><code>reinvocationPolicy: IfNeeded</code> — if a LATER mutating webhook changes the object, EARLIER mutating webhooks may be re-invoked so they can react to the change (default <code>Never</code> means each mutating webhook is called once). <code>matchPolicy: Equivalent</code> (default) matches a resource even when the client used a different but equivalent API version (e.g. <code>apps/v1beta1</code> vs <code>apps/v1</code>); <code>Exact</code> matches only the literal group/version/resource you listed. <code>Equivalent</code> is almost always what you want so version skew doesn't silently bypass your webhook.</p>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Default / value</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>namespaceSelector</code> / <code>objectSelector</code></td><td>Scope WHICH objects the webhook sees</td><td>none — always exclude <code>kube-system</code> explicitly</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>matchConditions</code> (CEL)</td><td>Fine-grained pre-filter — must ALSO evaluate true, in-process, before the webhook is called</td><td>none by default; beta since v1.28, enabled by default (see UTH §③)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>sideEffects</code></td><td>Declares whether the webhook mutates external state</td><td><code>None</code> / <code>NoneOnDryRun</code> / <code>Some</code> (deprecated) / <code>Unknown</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>timeoutSeconds</code></td><td>How long the apiserver waits for a response</td><td>default 10, max 30</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 INSIGHT — the latency budget: mutating webhooks chain SEQUENTIALLY, validating webhooks fan out in PARALLEL</h5>
                    <p>The two webhook types don't share the same execution model. Mutating webhooks of the same type are called ONE AT A TIME, in sequence — each one must see the object AFTER the previous one's patch was applied (that's the whole reason <code>reinvocationPolicy</code> talks about "earlier" and "later" webhooks in UTH §③). So three mutating webhooks are roughly ADDITIVE latency, not the slowest-one-wins. Validating webhooks, by contrast, only accept or reject — they don't need to see each other's output — so the apiserver CAN call multiple matching validating webhooks concurrently, making that stage's latency closer to the slowest single validator rather than the sum. Either way, the MUTATING stage as a whole still finishes entirely before the VALIDATING stage starts, so worst case for easypj-web is roughly <code>sum(mutating webhook latencies) + schema check + max(validating webhook latencies)</code>. This is why ONE slow mutating webhook (near its <code>timeoutSeconds</code> ceiling) drags down every <code>kubectl apply</code> even when the other nine registered webhooks respond instantly — check <code>kubectl get events</code> or webhook pod latency metrics before assuming the apiserver itself is slow.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h4>Admission Flow Gotchas</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>❌ <strong>No <code>None</code>/<code>NoneOnDryRun</code> on <code>sideEffects</code></strong> — breaks <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server</code> and <code>kubectl diff</code>, because the apiserver won't call a webhook that might cause real side effects during a dry run.</li>
                        <li>❌ <strong>Reads NEVER go through admission</strong> — a broken webhook can block writes but you can always still <code>kubectl get</code>/<code>describe</code> to diagnose it.</li>
                        <li>✅ <strong>Order among webhooks of the SAME type is not guaranteed</strong> — don't build logic that depends on one mutating webhook running before another unless you explicitly test it.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §42.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s42-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">42.3 Failure Policy &amp; TLS</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-important">🔴 CRITICAL</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>A webhook is an out-of-process HTTP service. It can be slow, down, or unreachable. <code>failurePolicy</code> tells the API server what to do then — and getting this wrong is the single most common way admission webhooks turn into cluster outages.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>failurePolicy Decision</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  webhook unreachable / times out / errors ...

   failurePolicy: Fail   ──►  REJECT the request  (default; safe-by-
                               default for SECURITY policies)

   failurePolicy: Ignore ──►  ALLOW the request   (availability-by-
                               default; webhook is "best effort")
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🧭 Decision Guide — Fail or Ignore?</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
 Is the webhook enforcing a SECURITY/COMPLIANCE invariant
 that MUST hold (e.g. block privileged pods)?
   ├─ YES ─► failurePolicy: Fail  (better to block than to leak),
   │        BUT scope it tightly (objectSelector / namespaceSelector)
   │        and EXCLUDE kube-system so you can still recover.
   └─ NO, it's a convenience/defaulting/injection nicety?
            └─► failurePolicy: Ignore  (don't let it wedge writes).
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 THE CLASSIC WEDGE</h5>
                    <p><code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> + a broadly-scoped webhook + its backing pod is <code>Pending</code>/<code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> = EVERY matching CREATE/UPDATE is rejected, <strong>including attempts to scale up or fix the webhook's own Deployment</strong>. The easypj-production cluster appears "frozen" — every <code>kubectl apply</code> fails with an admission error. Recovery is to DELETE the offending webhook configuration (see Commands below), then redeploy it.</p>
                </div>

                <p>The API server calls webhooks over HTTPS <strong>ONLY</strong>. It must trust the webhook's serving certificate. The <code>clientConfig</code> of each webhook carries a <code>caBundle</code> (base64 CA cert) the apiserver uses to verify the webhook's TLS cert.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-injector-webhook.yaml — service-backed clientConfig</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: MutatingWebhookConfiguration
metadata:
  name: easypj-injector
webhooks:
- name: inject.easypj.example.com
  clientConfig:
    service:
      name: easypj-injector
      namespace: easypj-system
      path: /mutate
      port: 443
    caBundle: &lt;base64 CA cert&gt;      <span class="comment"># apiserver trusts certs signed by this</span>
  rules:
  - apiGroups:   [""]
    apiVersions: ["v1"]
    operations:  ["CREATE"]
    resources:   ["pods"]
  namespaceSelector:                <span class="comment"># scope: only easypj namespaces</span>
    matchLabels: { easypj-injection: enabled }
  failurePolicy: Ignore             <span class="comment"># don't wedge pod creation if down</span>
  sideEffects: None
  admissionReviewVersions: ["v1"]
  timeoutSeconds: 5</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ GOTCHA — caBundle rotation</h5>
                    <p>When the webhook's serving cert is rotated, the <code>caBundle</code> must still chain to it. cert-manager's ca-injector or a controller usually keeps <code>caBundle</code> in sync. A stale <code>caBundle</code> → TLS handshake failure → with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code>, requests blocked.</p>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Symptom in apiserver logs</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>What decides allow vs reject</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>x509: certificate signed by unknown authority</code></td><td><code>caBundle</code> wrong/stale, or cert rotated without updating <code>caBundle</code></td><td><code>failurePolicy</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>context deadline exceeded</code> / <code>i/o timeout</code></td><td>webhook slow or unreachable; check the Service/Endpoints and the webhook pod</td><td><code>failurePolicy</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>everything suddenly rejected</td><td>a broadly-scoped webhook with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> and a down backing pod</td><td>list webhook configs, find the culprit</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — the emergency unwedge</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Confirm a webhook is the culprit</span>
kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations,mutatingwebhookconfigurations

<span class="comment"># 2. Inspect the suspect</span>
kubectl get mutatingwebhookconfiguration easypj-injector -o yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Emergency recovery — delete the offending config</span>
kubectl delete mutatingwebhookconfiguration easypj-injector

<span class="comment"># 4. Fix the root cause (restart the backing pod, fix caBundle), then redeploy the webhook config</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                    <p><strong>CKA Quick Reference</strong> — If the cluster suddenly rejects everything, suspect a webhook FIRST, before you start debugging RBAC or resource quotas. <code>kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations,mutatingwebhookconfigurations</code> takes 5 seconds and rules out (or confirms) the whole category of outage.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §42.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s42-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">42.4 kubectl Plugins &amp; krew</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-reference">🏢 PRODUCTION</span>

                <p>You can extend <code>kubectl</code> ITSELF. Any executable on your PATH named <code>kubectl-foo</code> becomes the subcommand <code>kubectl foo</code>. krew is the plugin MANAGER that installs and updates these for you.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 KEY TERM — krew</h5>
                    <p>The package manager for kubectl plugins. Installs plugins into <code>~/.krew/bin</code> (add it to PATH). <code>kubectl krew install &lt;name&gt;</code> is the install command.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>ctx</code></td><td>switch kube-context fast</td><td><code>kubectl ctx easypj-prod</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>ns</code></td><td>switch current namespace fast</td><td><code>kubectl ns easypj-production</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>neat</code></td><td>strip <code>managedFields</code>/status noise</td><td><code>kubectl neat -f po.yaml</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>tree</code></td><td>show ownership tree (Deploy→RS→Pod)</td><td><code>kubectl tree deploy easypj-web</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>who-can</code></td><td>reverse-RBAC: who can do X</td><td><code>kubectl who-can get pods -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>access-matrix</code></td><td>show what the CURRENT user can do</td><td><code>kubectl access-matrix</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>stern</code></td><td>multi-pod log tailing</td><td><code>stern easypj-web</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — installing and using krew plugins on easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Install krew itself (one-time), then plugins via it</span>
kubectl krew install ns ctx neat tree who-can

<span class="comment"># Fast context/namespace switching</span>
kubectl ctx easypj-prod
kubectl ns easypj-production

<span class="comment"># What owns this pod? (Deploy → ReplicaSet → Pod)</span>
kubectl tree deployment easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Reverse RBAC — who can create pods in easypj-production?</span>
kubectl who-can create pods -n easypj-production

<span class="comment"># Strip noise before re-applying a fetched object</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web-abc123 -o yaml | kubectl neat</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 INSIGHT — neat and tree are lifesavers</h5>
                    <p><code>neat</code> is a lifesaver in exams/audits: it turns a server-fetched object back into something you can re-apply, dropping <code>status</code>, <code>managedFields</code>, <code>creationTimestamp</code>, <code>resourceVersion</code>, <code>uid</code>, etc. <code>tree</code> instantly answers "what owns this pod?". <code>who-can</code>/<code>access-matrix</code> reverse RBAC — instead of "what can this subject do?", they ask "who can do THIS?".</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Client-side only</h5>
                    <p>Plugins run on YOUR machine, not in the cluster. They are never required by the CKA — the exam environment may not have krew installed at all — but they shave real seconds in daily operations. Never build a script that depends on a plugin being present on someone else's machine.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 KEY TERM — plugin naming and discovery rules</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl plugin list</code> scans every directory in your <code>PATH</code> for executables named <code>kubectl-&lt;something&gt;</code>, warns about non-executable matches, and warns if two plugins with the same name overshadow each other (the one found FIRST in <code>PATH</code> order wins). Dashes in the filename become spaces in the subcommand — <code>kubectl-foo-bar</code> becomes <code>kubectl foo bar</code> — and kubectl uses LONGEST-NAME matching, so if both <code>kubectl-foo-bar</code> and <code>kubectl-foo-bar-baz</code> exist, <code>kubectl foo bar baz</code> invokes the more specific one. If your subcommand name itself needs a literal dash, use an underscore in the filename (<code>kubectl-foo_bar</code> → invocable as either <code>kubectl foo-bar</code> or <code>kubectl foo_bar</code>).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ GOTCHA — plugins can never override or extend a BUILT-IN kubectl command</h5>
                    <p>A plugin named <code>kubectl-version</code> is silently ignored — the built-in <code>kubectl version</code> always wins. Likewise a plugin can't bolt a new subcommand onto an existing one (a <code>kubectl-get-custom</code> plugin does NOT give you <code>kubectl get custom</code>). The one deliberate exception is <code>kubectl create</code>: plugins named <code>kubectl-create-&lt;x&gt;</code> DO extend it, giving you <code>kubectl create &lt;x&gt;</code>. Outside that one exception, if you need to shadow a built-in verb, you'll need a different plugin name entirely.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §42.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s42-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">42.5 API Aggregation (recap)</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Webhooks extend BEHAVIOR on existing types. To add NEW types or NEW API surface, you have two tools — and telling them apart is a common exam-adjacent distinction. Chapter 38 §38.4 covered API Aggregation in the context of building your own operator; here's the recap focused on what it means for the admission chain.</p>

