            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch42-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — The AdmissionReview Wire Format, Mutate-Before-Validate Rationale, Webhook Scoping, TLS/caBundle Rotation, and Webhook vs ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Five Moving Parts, One Pipeline — Where Each One Actually Lives</h5>
            <p>§42.1–42.5 each introduced one piece of the admission/extension machinery in isolation. Under the hood they are all facets of the SAME request pipeline: one JSON envelope (AdmissionReview) carried between two webhook stages, gated by scoping fields and a failure policy, secured by TLS, and ultimately competing with a CEL-native alternative (ValidatingAdmissionPolicy) for the validating slot. Seeing all of it on one timeline is what turns "the cluster suddenly rejects everything" from a mystery into a 2-minute diagnosis.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ONE REQUEST, THE FULL ADMISSION MACHINERY                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  kubectl apply -f easypj-web-deploy.yaml                                     │
│         │                                                                    │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  ① ADMISSIONREVIEW built by apiserver, POSTed to each matching webhook       │
│         │                                                                   │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  ② MUTATING webhooks run first — final (mutated) shape is what's validated  │
│         │                                                                   │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  ③ matchPolicy/reinvocationPolicy decide WHICH webhooks fire, how often     │
│         │                                                                   │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  ④ namespaceSelector/objectSelector/sideEffects decide WHAT they can see    │
│         │                                                                   │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  ⑤ TLS handshake using caBundle — every webhook call, every time            │
│         │                                                                   │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  ⑥ VALIDATING stage: your webhook competes with VAP (CEL) for the same job  │
│         │                                                                   │
│         ▼                                                                   │
│  PERSIST to etcd — only if every stage said yes                             │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <h5>① The AdmissionReview Wire Format — Request/Response Anatomy</h5>
            <p>Every webhook — mutating or validating — is just an HTTP server that receives a POST body shaped like an AdmissionReview and must reply with one. Knowing the exact fields is what separates "I know webhooks exist" from "I could write one."</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ADMISSIONREVIEW REQUEST → YOUR WEBHOOK → ADMISSIONREVIEW RESPONSE            │
│                                                                              │
│  REQUEST carries:                                                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ uid          — correlation id, MUST be echoed back exactly            │  │
│  │ operation    — CREATE / UPDATE / DELETE / CONNECT                     │  │
│  │ userInfo     — who's making the request (username, groups, uid)       │  │
│  │ resource     — {group, version, resource} being written               │  │
│  │ object       — the NEW object (post-mutation-so-far state)            │  │
│  │ oldObject    — populated on UPDATE/DELETE only, else null             │  │
│  │ dryRun       — true if --dry-run=server; obey sideEffects here        │  │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                              │
│  RESPONSE must carry (at minimum):                                          │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ uid          — echoed from the request, EXACT match required          │  │
│  │ allowed      — true/false                                             │  │
│  │ patch        — base64 JSON Patch (mutating only, only if allowed)     │  │
│  │ patchType    — always "JSONPatch" if patch is set                     │  │
│  │ status       — {code, message} explaining a rejection (validating)    │  │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Present on</th><th>Trap if you get it wrong</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>uid</code></td><td>request &amp; response</td><td>Mismatch → apiserver treats the ENTIRE response as invalid, request errors out</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>object</code></td><td>CREATE/UPDATE requests</td><td><code>null</code> on DELETE — a webhook that unconditionally reads <code>object.spec</code> panics on every DELETE (only <code>oldObject</code> is populated then)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>oldObject</code></td><td>UPDATE/DELETE requests</td><td><code>null</code> on CREATE — a webhook that unconditionally reads <code>oldObject.spec</code> panics on every CREATE</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>dryRun</code></td><td>every request</td><td>Ignoring it and mutating external state anyway breaks <code>kubectl diff</code>/<code>--dry-run=server</code> silently</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>patch</code></td><td>mutating response only</td><td>Must be base64; a raw (non-base64) JSON Patch string is rejected</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>auditAnnotations</code></td><td>response, either webhook type</td><td>a <code>map[string]string</code> your webhook can attach to the request's audit event (e.g. <code>"easypj.io/injected-sidecar": "envoy-v1.30"</code>) — invisible unless you go looking, so document that your webhook uses it or nobody will know to check audit logs for its decisions</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>warnings</code></td><td>response, either webhook type, ALLOWED or not</td><td>a list of human-readable strings surfaced directly to the caller as <code>Warning:</code> lines on <code>kubectl apply</code> — unlike <code>status.message</code> (only shown on rejection), warnings can accompany an ALLOWED response too, e.g. "sidecar injected using deprecated Envoy image tag"</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> You will never need to hand-write this JSON for the exam, but recognizing it in a troubleshooting scenario (e.g. reading webhook server logs, or an error message that echoes back an AdmissionReview) tells you immediately which stage rejected the request — a <code>status.message</code> means a VALIDATING decision; a missing/malformed <code>patch</code> field points at a broken MUTATING webhook.</div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">webhook_server.py — the minimal shape of a real validating webhook (illustrative, not exam material)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-python"><span class="comment"># A validating webhook is just an HTTPS server that speaks AdmissionReview.
# This is the entire request/response cycle for "reject :latest images",
# stripped to its essentials — TLS termination and cert loading omitted.</span>
from flask import Flask, request, jsonify
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/validate", methods=["POST"])
def validate():
    review = request.get_json()
    req = review["request"]
    pod = req["object"]
    uid = req["uid"]                      <span class="comment"># MUST echo this back exactly</span>

    bad = any(c["image"].endswith(":latest")
              for c in pod["spec"]["containers"])

    response = {"uid": uid, "allowed": not bad}
    if bad:
        response["status"] = {"code": 403,
            "message": "image must not use the :latest tag"}

    return jsonify({"apiVersion": "admission.k8s.io/v1",
                     "kind": "AdmissionReview",
                     "response": response})

<span class="comment"># app.run(ssl_context=(cert_path, key_path), port=443)  # HTTPS is mandatory</span></code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> This is Day-2/SRE depth, not exam material — but seeing the ~15 real lines behind the AdmissionReview JSON demystifies it: a webhook is not magic, it is any HTTPS server that reads <code>request.object</code>, decides, and echoes <code>uid</code> back. The exact same shape, with a <code>patchType</code>/<code>patch</code> added to the response, is a mutating webhook.</div>

            <h5>② Why Mutating Runs Before Validating — the Final-Shape Rule</h5>
            <p>This ordering is not arbitrary — it is the ONLY ordering that makes admission coherent. If validation ran first, a validating webhook checking "does every container have a resource limit" would see the object BEFORE a sidecar-injecting mutating webhook added its container — and would validate something that isn't actually what gets persisted.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  WHY ORDER MATTERS — sidecar injection + limit-enforcement on easypj-web      │
│                                                                              │
│  WRONG ORDER (hypothetical): validate THEN mutate                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ pod has 1 container (easypj-web) with limits set        → PASSES     │  │
│  │ THEN Envoy sidecar injected, NO limits on it              → but now   │  │
│  │ the persisted object has an unvalidated container!                    │  │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                              │
│  ACTUAL ORDER: mutate THEN validate                                          │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ Envoy sidecar injected FIRST (2 containers now)                       │  │
│  │ THEN validation checks BOTH containers for limits         → catches   │  │
│  │ the sidecar's missing limits, rejects correctly                       │  │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Stage order</th><th>Sees</th><th>Guarantee it provides</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>1. Mutating</td><td>the object as submitted (may be re-run per reinvocationPolicy)</td><td>final shape is settled before anything downstream inspects it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2. Schema validation</td><td>the fully-mutated object</td><td>structurally valid before any policy webhook wastes a network call</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>3. Validating</td><td>the fully-mutated, schema-valid object</td><td>policy decisions are made on what will ACTUALLY be persisted</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> "Mutating runs before validating" is one of the single most-tested one-liners about admission control across CKA/CKS-adjacent material — it comes up disguised as "why would a Deployment that looks like it violates policy still get created?" (answer: a mutating webhook fixed it before validation ever ran).</div>

            <h5>③ matchPolicy &amp; reinvocationPolicy — Version Skew and Multi-Webhook Chains</h5>
            <p>Two configuration fields decide WHICH webhooks fire and HOW OFTEN — get either wrong and a webhook that looks correctly configured silently never runs, or runs against stale data.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  matchPolicy: Equivalent vs Exact                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Webhook rule lists: apiVersions: ["apps/v1"]                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Client sends a request via an EQUIVALENT older API version                  │
│  (e.g. an old CI script still POSTs apps/v1beta1)                            │
│                                                                              │
│    matchPolicy: Equivalent (default) → apiserver recognizes v1beta1 IS      │
│      equivalent to v1 for this resource → webhook FIRES                     │
│    matchPolicy: Exact → only the LITERAL listed version matches             │
│      → webhook is SILENTLY SKIPPED for the v1beta1 request                  │
│                                                                              │
│  reinvocationPolicy (mutating only):                                         │
│    Never (default)   → each mutating webhook called exactly ONCE            │
│    IfNeeded           → if a LATER webhook changes the object, EARLIER      │
│                          mutating webhooks may be re-invoked to react        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>matchPolicy</code></td><td><code>Equivalent</code> (default)</td><td>matches version-equivalent resources too — safest default</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>matchPolicy</code></td><td><code>Exact</code></td><td>only the literal <code>apiVersions</code> listed — can silently miss requests</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>reinvocationPolicy</code></td><td><code>Never</code> (default)</td><td>called once; may see a stale view if another webhook mutates after it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>reinvocationPolicy</code></td><td><code>IfNeeded</code></td><td>re-run if a later webhook changed the object — use when your mutation depends on fields another webhook might also touch</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> A webhook that "should have fired but didn't" is very often a <code>matchPolicy: Exact</code> combined with a client using a slightly different (but equivalent) API version — check this before assuming the webhook itself is broken.</div>

            <p><code>matchPolicy</code> and <code>rules</code> decide WHICH resources/versions a webhook is eligible for — but <code>matchConditions</code> (beta since v1.28, enabled by default) goes one step further and lets you write a CEL expression that must ALSO evaluate to <code>true</code> before the apiserver bothers calling your webhook at all. It's a pre-filter that runs entirely inside the apiserver, for free, before any network call.</p>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">easypj-injector-webhook.yaml — matchConditions pre-filter (CEL)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">webhooks:
- name: inject.easypj.example.com
  matchConditions:
  - name: "exclude-leases"       <span class="comment"># skip a whole noisy resource type</span>
    expression: >-
      !(request.resource.group == "coordination.k8s.io" &&
        request.resource.resource == "leases")
  - name: "skip-easypj-system"   <span class="comment"># don't ever call the injector on itself</span>
    expression: "object.metadata.namespace != 'easypj-system'"</code></pre>
            </div>

            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Runs where</th><th>Why it's worth using</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>rules</code> / <code>matchPolicy</code></td><td>apiserver, before matchConditions</td><td>coarse filter — which GVR/operations even reach CEL evaluation</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>matchConditions</code> (CEL)</td><td>apiserver, in-process</td><td>fine-grained filter — cuts the NUMBER of network calls to your webhook, which is real latency and load saved for free (no HTTP round-trip to reject a request the webhook would've ignored anyway)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Before <code>matchConditions</code> existed, "only run on objects with label X and NOT in kube-system" required an <code>objectSelector</code>/<code>namespaceSelector</code> combination that couldn't express richer logic (e.g. "only if <code>spec.replicas &gt; 2</code>"). <code>matchConditions</code> can express that — it's the same CEL engine ValidatingAdmissionPolicy uses, just applied as a gate in front of a webhook instead of as the decision itself. Multiple <code>matchConditions</code> entries are ANDed: ALL must evaluate <code>true</code> for the webhook to be called.</div>

            <h5>④ Scoping a Webhook — namespaceSelector/objectSelector, sideEffects &amp; Dry-Run</h5>
            <p>An unscoped webhook is a loaded gun pointed at the whole cluster — including <code>kube-system</code>. Scoping fields are not optional polish; they are what keeps a broken webhook from locking out the control plane itself.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SCOPING A WEBHOOK — easypj-injector example                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  webhooks:                                                                    │
│  - name: inject.easypj.example.com                                           │
│    namespaceSelector:                                                        │
│      matchExpressions:                                                       │
│      - key: kubernetes.io/metadata.name                                      │
│        operator: NotIn                                                       │
│        values: [kube-system, kube-node-lease, kube-public]  ◄── ALWAYS       │
│                                                                 exclude these │
│    objectSelector:                                                           │
│      matchLabels: { easypj-injection: enabled }              ◄── opt-in,     │
│                                                                   not opt-out │
│    sideEffects: None                                          ◄── required   │
│                                                                   for dry-run │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Selector</th><th>Scopes by</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>namespaceSelector</code></td><td>labels on the Namespace object</td><td>the #1 way to exclude <code>kube-system</code> so a broken webhook can't lock out control-plane writes</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>objectSelector</code></td><td>labels on the object being admitted</td><td>opt-in webhooks — only pods labeled <code>easypj-injection: enabled</code> get a sidecar</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>sideEffects: None</code></td><td>declares no external side effects</td><td>required for <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server</code> and <code>kubectl diff</code> to still call the webhook</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Every production webhook config should have a <code>namespaceSelector</code> excluding <code>kube-system</code> as a non-negotiable baseline — it's the difference between "a broken webhook blocks my app deploys" (recoverable) and "a broken webhook blocks the CoreDNS fix I need to recover" (a genuine outage).</div>

            <h5>⑤ TLS, caBundle &amp; Certificate Rotation</h5>
            <p>Every single admission-webhook call is a real HTTPS connection from the apiserver to your webhook Service — there is no insecure fallback. The <code>caBundle</code> is the ONE field standing between "works" and "x509: certificate signed by unknown authority" the moment a cert rotates.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  caBundle ROTATION — what breaks when it drifts                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Day 0:  webhook serving cert issued, caBundle in webhook config matches      │
│          → TLS handshake succeeds on every call                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Day 90: cert-manager (or manual process) rotates the serving cert           │
│          IF caBundle is NOT updated in lockstep:                             │
│            apiserver → webhook TLS handshake FAILS                           │
│            "x509: certificate signed by unknown authority"                   │
│            failurePolicy decides: Fail = every matching write REJECTED       │
│                                     Ignore = requests silently bypass the     │
│                                     webhook (policy is NOT enforced!)         │
│                                                                              │
│  FIX: cert-manager's ca-injector annotation keeps caBundle in sync           │
│       automatically — this is why production webhooks almost always use     │
│       cert-manager rather than hand-rolled certs                             │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>caBundle stays in sync?</th><th>Operational cost</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Manual cert + manual caBundle patch</td><td>❌ only if someone remembers, every rotation</td><td>high — a forgotten rotation is an outage waiting to happen</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>cert-manager + ca-injector annotation</td><td>✅ automatic, every rotation</td><td>low — set up once, self-maintaining</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Self-signed cert baked into a Helm chart</td><td>❌ static — breaks the day the cert expires</td><td>medium — needs a manual re-issue + redeploy on every expiry</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> <code>failurePolicy: Ignore</code> on a TLS failure is a silent security hole, not a safe fallback — the webhook simply stops being enforced with no error surfaced to the person applying manifests. That's why security-critical webhooks lean <code>Fail</code> even though it risks a wedge: an invisible policy bypass is often worse than a loud outage.</div>

            <h5>⑥ Webhook vs ValidatingAdmissionPolicy — the Production Trade-off</h5>
            <p>Everything in §42.1–42.3 — the HTTP server, the TLS cert, the failurePolicy wedge risk — exists because a webhook is an EXTERNAL process. ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (VAP) removes that entire category of operational risk for validation logic that CEL can express, by running INSIDE the apiserver.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  VALIDATING WEBHOOK vs ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)                        │
│                                                                              │
│  VALIDATING WEBHOOK                     VALIDATINGADMISSIONPOLICY (VAP)      │
│  ┌────────────────────────┐             ┌────────────────────────┐         │
│  │ external HTTP server    │             │ CEL expression,         │         │
│  │ needs: Deployment, Svc, │             │ evaluated IN-PROCESS    │         │
│  │ TLS cert, caBundle       │    vs      │ needs: nothing extra —  │         │
│  │ failurePolicy wedge risk│             │ no pod, no TLS, no HA   │         │
│  │ network latency per call│             │ near-zero latency       │         │
│  └────────────────────────┘             └────────────────────────┘         │
│                                                                              │
│  CHOOSE VAP when: the rule is expressible in CEL (field comparisons,        │
│    string checks, cross-field logic) — e.g. "replicas >= 2"                 │
│  CHOOSE A WEBHOOK when: you need EXTERNAL DATA (call another service),      │
│    MUTATION (VAP is validate-only), or logic CEL genuinely can't express    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Validating Webhook</th><th>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Runs</td><td>external HTTP server (your pod)</td><td>inside the apiserver process</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Needs TLS/caBundle?</td><td>✅ yes</td><td>❌ no</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>failurePolicy wedge risk?</td><td>✅ real risk if pod is down</td><td>❌ no external dependency to go down</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Can call external services?</td><td>✅ yes</td><td>❌ no — CEL only sees the object</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Can mutate?</td><td>only if also a mutating webhook</td><td>❌ validate-only by design</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>GA since</td><td>beta in v1.9, GA in v1.16</td><td>beta v1.28, GA v1.30</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> The exam and real production both reward the same instinct: reach for ValidatingAdmissionPolicy first, and drop to a validating webhook only when you genuinely need external data or the CEL expression would be unreadable. Gatekeeper/Kyverno remain relevant for their policy-authoring ergonomics and MUTATING capability (which VAP doesn't have) — not because webhooks are inherently better.</div>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📖 AWARENESS — MutatingAdmissionPolicy closes the "VAP can't mutate" gap</h5>
                <p>The row above says ValidatingAdmissionPolicy is validate-only "by design" — that's still true of THAT specific API kind. Kubernetes also ships a sibling <strong>MutatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> (CEL-based, in-apiserver mutation, no webhook pod) that reached GA well after ValidatingAdmissionPolicy did. Treat this the same way you'd treat any very recent GA: check your target cluster's version before assuming it's available, and don't expect the current CKA syllabus to test it yet — but recognize the name if you see it, because it means "the mutating equivalent of VAP," not a typo for ValidatingAdmissionPolicy.</p>
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch42-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The admission chain:</strong> authN → authZ → MUTATING webhooks → schema validation → VALIDATING webhooks → persist. Mutating runs BEFORE validating so the final (mutated) object is what gets validated.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mutating webhooks MODIFY objects</strong> (inject sidecars, set defaults); validating webhooks only ACCEPT or REJECT. Both speak the AdmissionReview request/response JSON, and the response <code>uid</code> must echo the request <code>uid</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>failurePolicy</code> decides what happens when the webhook is unreachable:</strong> <code>Fail</code> (block the request) or <code>Ignore</code> (allow it). <code>Fail</code> + a down webhook can wedge the whole cluster — including the requests you'd use to fix it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Webhooks require TLS with a <code>caBundle</code></strong> the apiserver trusts. Reads skip admission entirely; only writes (CREATE/UPDATE/DELETE) walk the chain.</li>
                    <li><strong>Prefer ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL, in-apiserver)</strong> over a validating webhook when you can — no pod, no TLS, no wedge risk.</li>
                    <li><strong>krew extends kubectl ITSELF</strong> (ctx, ns, neat, tree, who-can) — client-side convenience, never required by the exam.</li>
                    <li><strong>Extend the API:</strong> CRD = new type stored by K8s (+ a controller); aggregation = a separate apiserver behind an APIService (e.g. metrics-server). Webhooks change/validate EXISTING objects, they don't add new ones.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch42-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <strong><code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> + the webhook pod down</strong> = every matching request is rejected — including the ones you'd use to fix it. Scope match narrowly and consider <code>Ignore</code> for non-critical webhooks.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>A stale/incorrect <code>caBundle</code></strong> (cert rotation) breaks the webhook with TLS "certificate signed by unknown authority" errors.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Webhook ORDER matters</strong> — a mutating webhook's output feeds validation; design for it (and use <code>reinvocationPolicy</code> if webhooks chain on the same fields).</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Webhooks that don't declare <code>sideEffects</code>/dry-run support</strong> break <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server</code> and <code>kubectl diff</code>.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>A webhook NOT excluding <code>kube-system</code></strong> can lock out control-plane writes — always <code>namespaceSelector</code>-exclude system namespaces.</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>A down metrics-server</strong> makes its APIService not <code>Available</code>, which is why <code>kubectl top</code> fails — that's the aggregation layer, not a webhook.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch42-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>If the cluster suddenly rejects everything</strong>, suspect a webhook first: <code>kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations,mutatingwebhookconfigurations</code> and delete/fix the offender. Prefer ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL) over a validating webhook when you can — no pod to keep alive, no TLS to rotate. Use <code>namespaceSelector</code>/<code>objectSelector</code> to scope a webhook so it can't affect <code>kube-system</code> and lock you out. <code>kubectl get apiservices | grep -v Local</code> finds the aggregated APIs fast; a <code>False</code>/not-Available row points at a down backend. <code>kubectl neat</code> before saving an object you fetched, so it re-applies cleanly; <code>kubectl tree</code> to see what owns a pod.</p>
            </div>

