                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Mutating vs validating webhook — what's the purpose of each, and why does mutating run first?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Mutating</th><th>Validating</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Can change the object?</td><td>✅ YES (JSON Patch)</td><td>❌ NO — accept/reject only</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Runs</td><td>FIRST</td><td>AFTER mutating + schema</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Mutating MODIFIES the object (defaults, sidecar injection); validating only ACCEPTS or REJECTS it. Mutating runs first because validation must see the FINAL (mutated) object — otherwise you'd validate something that isn't what actually gets persisted.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Give the full admission order for a write request to easypj-web, and say what happens to a read request.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">authN → authZ → mutating webhooks → schema validation → validating
webhooks (built-ins + VAP + your ValidatingWebhookConfigs) → persist to etcd

Reads (GET/LIST/WATCH) skip admission ENTIRELY.</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Only writes (CREATE/UPDATE/DELETE) walk the admission chain. This is why a broken webhook that blocks writes never stops you from <code>kubectl get</code>ting the cluster to diagnose it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> do when the webhook is down, and how can it become an outage?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                            <div class="info-card">
                                <h5>failurePolicy: Fail</h5>
                                <p>Matching requests are REJECTED (blocked) when the webhook is unreachable.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="info-card">
                                <h5>failurePolicy: Ignore</h5>
                                <p>Matching requests are ALLOWED THROUGH when the webhook is unreachable.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>Fail</code> can wedge the cluster if the webhook is broadly scoped and its backing pod is down — including the very requests you'd use to fix it (e.g. scaling the webhook's own Deployment back up).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why do webhooks need TLS/caBundle, and what breaks when caBundle goes stale?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>TLS (HTTPS only)</td><td>the apiserver calls webhooks ONLY over HTTPS, never plain HTTP</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>caBundle</code></td><td>base64 CA cert in <code>clientConfig</code> that the apiserver trusts to verify the webhook's serving cert</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A stale <code>caBundle</code> after cert rotation breaks the TLS handshake with <code>x509: certificate signed by unknown authority</code>. With <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code>, this blocks every matching request; with <code>Ignore</code>, it silently stops enforcing policy — arguably worse, since nothing alerts you.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The cluster rejects all creates after a webhook broke. Walk through the recovery.</div>
