<span class="comment"># NAME                     SERVICE                      AVAILABLE</span>
<span class="comment"># v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io   kube-system/metrics-server   False (FailedDiscoveryCheck)</span>

kubectl -n kube-system get pods -l k8s-app=metrics-server
<span class="comment"># metrics-server pod is CrashLoopBackOff or Pending</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>No — this is the API AGGREGATION layer, not an admission webhook. <code>kubectl top</code> depends on the <code>metrics.k8s.io</code> APIService being <code>Available</code>. A down metrics-server backend shows <code>Available: False</code>, which is exactly why <code>top</code> fails — distinguishing this from a webhook outage is the point of §42.5.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a MutatingWebhookConfiguration snippet for an easypj sidecar injector that is properly scoped to avoid ever locking out <code>kube-system</code>, and explain each safety field you included.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">webhooks:
- name: inject.easypj.example.com
  namespaceSelector:
    matchExpressions:
    - key: kubernetes.io/metadata.name
      operator: NotIn
      values: [kube-system, kube-node-lease, kube-public]
  objectSelector:
    matchLabels: { easypj-injection: enabled }
  failurePolicy: Ignore
  sideEffects: None
  timeoutSeconds: 5</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>namespaceSelector</code> excludes system namespaces so a broken webhook can't lock out control-plane writes. <code>objectSelector</code> makes injection opt-in (fewer pods affected = smaller blast radius). <code>failurePolicy: Ignore</code> is appropriate here because sidecar injection is a convenience, not a security invariant — a down injector shouldn't block deploys. <code>sideEffects: None</code> keeps <code>--dry-run=server</code>/<code>diff</code> working.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Compare: would you implement "every Deployment must have at least 2 replicas" as a validating webhook or a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy? Justify with the operational trade-offs.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <table>
                                <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Needs</th><th>Wedge risk</th></tr></thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td>Validating webhook</td><td>a running pod, Service, TLS cert, caBundle</td><td>real — if the pod goes down and failurePolicy is Fail</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)</td><td><code>expression: "object.spec.replicas >= 2"</code>, nothing else</td><td>none — runs inside the apiserver itself</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This rule is a simple field comparison, fully expressible in CEL — the textbook case for ValidatingAdmissionPolicy. Choosing a webhook here would add an operational dependency (a whole HTTP server + TLS lifecycle) for zero added capability. Reserve webhooks for rules VAP genuinely can't express: calling external services, or mutation.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

