                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 42 Visual Summary — the X-ray machine every write to easypj passes through</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   REQUEST              authN/authZ            MUTATING → SCHEMA → VALIDATING          PERSIST
  ┌──────────┐        ┌──────────────┐        ┌─────────────────────────────────┐   ┌──────────┐
  │ kubectl  │  ───►  │ who are you? │  ───►  │ 🪝 rewrite (sidecar, defaults)   │──►│ 🗄️ etcd  │
  │ apply    │        │ are you      │        │ 📋 schema check                  │   │ durable  │
  │ easypj-  │        │ allowed?     │        │ ✅ accept/reject (VAP + webhooks)│   │          │
  │ web.yaml │        └──────────────┘        └─────────────────────────────────┘   └──────────┘
  └──────────┘                                          ▲
                                            failurePolicy: Fail + webhook DOWN
                                            = every matching write REJECTED
                                            (needs TLS + caBundle, always)
   🧰 krew (client-side): ctx · ns · neat · tree · who-can — never in this pipeline
   🧩 CRD (new TYPE, etcd) vs API Aggregation (2nd apiserver, e.g. metrics-server)
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> A write to easypj enters at the left as a raw API request. authN confirms WHO is calling, authZ confirms they're ALLOWED. Then the request hits MUTATING admission, where built-in mutators and your MutatingWebhookConfigurations may rewrite it — injecting sidecars or setting defaults. The rewritten object is checked against its SCHEMA, then passed to VALIDATING admission (built-in checks, ValidatingAdmissionPolicy CEL rules, and your ValidatingWebhookConfigurations) which can only accept or reject. Survive all of that and the object is PERSISTED to etcd.</p>
                    <p>The danger spot: any webhook with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> whose pod is down rejects every matching request — scope webhooks narrowly, exclude <code>kube-system</code>, and know the emergency <code>kubectl delete ...webhookconfiguration</code> unwedge. Every webhook call is real HTTPS, so TLS/caBundle drift is a silent failure mode of its own.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> krew extends the TOOLING around this pipeline (client-side only, never inside it), while CRDs and API aggregation extend the API SURFACE the pipeline governs — a CRD is a new stored type, aggregation is a whole second apiserver. Prefer ValidatingAdmissionPolicy over a validating webhook whenever the rule is CEL-expressible: it does the same job with none of the pod/TLS/wedge risk this chapter spent five sections explaining.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
