            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🧠 Real-World Analogy: The Smoke Detector and the Security Camera</h5>
                <p>Observability is the difference between a smoke detector and a security camera. <code>kubectl get pods</code> showing a green "Running" is the smoke detector — it's cheap, it's always there, and it tells you something is on fire only once flames are already visible and smoke has filled the room. A real observability stack is the camera, the motion sensor, and the thermostat working together: it shows you the room heating up degree by degree, names the exact corner the heat started in, and pages the fire department while the fire is still a spark on a countertop. Chapter 43 installs that camera system for easypj — Prometheus watching the temperature (metrics), Loki recording exactly what everyone in the room said and did (logs), and Jaeger tracing the path the spark took from outlet to curtain (traces) — then wires Alertmanager to actually call someone before the room is ash. Chapter 44 asks the next question: you don't own one room, you own a whole building of them (multiple clusters, multiple regions) — how do you keep a camera in every room without the security budget exceeding the building's rent? <strong>Observability</strong> is the property of being able to ask ANY question about the system's internal state from the outside, using only the signals it already emits, without shipping new code to add a probe — contrast this with "monitoring," which only watches a PRE-DEFINED set of checks. You monitor for the failures you predicted; you need observability for the ones you didn't. <strong>Day-2 operations</strong> is everything that happens after Day 0 (design) and Day 1 (install/deploy): upgrades, scaling, incident response, capacity planning, cost control. Most of a cluster's life — and most of its risk — lives in Day 2, which is exactly where this part lives.</p>
<pre>  THE ANALOGY                                        EASYPJ'S DAY-2 SRE STACK
  ─────────────                                        ──────────────────────
  🚨 Smoke detector — green light, then screams  ───▶ `kubectl get pods` — tells you AFTER it's on fire
  📹 Camera + motion sensor + thermostat  ──────────▶ Ch 43 — Prometheus + Loki + Jaeger/Tempo + Grafana
  🌡️ Room heating up, corner it started in  ────────▶ Ch 43 — golden signals + SLO burn-rate pinpoint WHERE/HOW BAD
  📟 Pages the fire dept. while it's still a spark ─▶ Ch 43 — Alertmanager → on-call, before users notice
  🏢 A whole building, not one room  ────────────────▶ Ch 44 — the same stack across N clusters/regions on a budget</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Admins Get Wrong About Observability</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Admins Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"<code>kubectl top</code> gives me an observability stack — I can already see CPU and memory."</td>
                            <td><code>kubectl top</code> needs metrics-server installed and gives you ONLY the most recent CPU/memory sample — no history, no latency, no error rate, no way to ask "was this bad an hour ago too?" It's a flashlight, not a dashboard. The exam may use it for a quick check; do not mistake it for the metrics/logs/traces stack Chapter 43 builds.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"I have metrics dashboards — that's the whole observability story, logs and traces are nice-to-haves."</td>
                            <td>None of the three pillars is a superset of the others, and a real incident needs all three: metrics tell you a problem EXISTS and is getting worse (latency p95 climbing), logs tell you WHAT happened on one specific broken request (the stack trace), and traces tell you WHERE in the request path the time or the error went (which downstream hop). Build all three so the jump from alert → root cause is one click, not one ticket.</td>
