            <section class="section-block" id="s43-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">43.1 The Three Pillars</h3>
                <p>Every observability tool boils down to one of three signal types, each answering a different question about easypj running in production. Get comfortable naming which one you need <em>before</em> you go looking — it's the single fastest way to stop wasting time in the wrong tool.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Observability</h5>
                    <p><strong>Observability</strong> is the property of a system that lets you ask <strong>arbitrary</strong> questions about its internal state from the <strong>outside</strong>, without shipping new code. <strong>Monitoring</strong> answers known questions ("is CPU high?"); observability lets you answer <em>unknown</em> ones ("why is THIS user's <code>/jobs/</code> request slow, only on Tuesdays, only on node-3?"). You monitor for the failures you predicted; you need observability for the ones you didn't.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Three Pillars — observing easypj in production</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   📈 METRICS (Prometheus)    what &amp; how much?     rate · p95 latency · error %
       └─ numeric, time-series, cheap to store, great for trends &amp; alerts

   📜 LOGS    (Loki / EFK)    what happened?         structured stdout/stderr
       └─ discrete events, high detail, expensive at scale, great for "why"

   🔗 TRACES  (OpenTelemetry) where did the time go?  a /jobs/ request, end-to-end
       └─ one request stitched across web→db→cache spans, finds bottlenecks

                              ▼
                🚨 ALERTS (Alertmanager) on SLOs / symptoms ─▶ error budget

   📖 RED (Rate / Errors / Duration)     for SERVICES
   📖 USE (Utilization / Saturation / Errors)  for RESOURCES (nodes / disks)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>Decision Guide — Which Pillar Answers Which Question?</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Question you're asking</th><th>Pillar</th><th>Tool / signal</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>"Is easypj up &amp; how fast?"</td><td>Metrics</td><td>Prometheus rate / latency series</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"What % of <code>/jobs/</code> is failing?"</td><td>Metrics</td><td>error-rate PromQL</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"WHY did THIS request 500?"</td><td>Logs</td><td><code>kubectl logs</code> / Loki query</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"What did the worker print?"</td><td>Logs</td><td>stdout/stderr → log shipper</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"WHERE did the 900ms go?"</td><td>Traces</td><td>OTel spans (web → db → cache)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Is the NODE saturated?"</td><td>Metrics</td><td>USE: node CPU / mem / disk</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Should I get paged?"</td><td>Alerts</td><td>SLO burn-rate rule</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Insight — Complementary, Not Redundant</h5>
                    <p>The pillars are complementary, not redundant. Metrics tell you <strong>something</strong> is wrong (error rate up). Traces tell you <strong>where</strong> (the DB span). Logs tell you <strong>why</strong> (deadlock / missing index / null author). A mature workflow flows <code>alert → metric → trace → log</code>, narrowing each step. Modern stacks link them with <strong>exemplars</strong> — a metric data point that carries a <code>trace_id</code> — so you click a latency spike and jump straight to the slow trace.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Pillar Economics — Why You Don't Log Everything</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Cost model</th><th>What you get</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>📈 Metrics</td><td>Pre-aggregated numbers; roughly constant cost per series — cheapest</td><td>You query the <em>shape</em> over time, not the individual event</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>📜 Logs</td><td>One line per event; cost scales with traffic — pricey at scale</td><td>The richest "what exactly happened" detail, one line at a time</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🔗 Traces</td><td>Usually <strong>sampled</strong> (e.g. 1%) — full tracing of every request is expensive</td><td>Enough sampled requests to find patterns and slow outliers</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Logs in Practice — the Sidecar Shipper &amp; a LogQL Query</h4>
                <p>Metrics and traces got full worked examples above; logs deserve the same treatment. In easypj, a container never talks to Loki directly — it just writes JSON lines to <code>stdout</code>, and a <strong>sidecar log shipper</strong> (a small helper container running alongside <code>easypj</code> in the same pod) does the actual forwarding. This is the "beyond-exam" tool built on the exam-relevant primitive: <code>kubectl logs</code> reads exactly the same stream the sidecar is tailing.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Sidecar log shipper — how stdout becomes a queryable log in Loki</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────── easypj-web POD ─────────────────────────┐
   │  CONTAINER: easypj              SIDECAR: fluent-bit               │
   │   writes JSON lines               tails /var/log/containers/...   │
   │   to stdout ──────────┐           parses JSON, adds pod labels    │
   │                       ▼                        │                  │
   │              [ shared log volume ] ────────────┘                  │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────┘
                                                       ▼
                                          forwards to Loki (or EFK's
                                          Fluentd → Elasticsearch)

   `kubectl logs` reads the SAME container stdout stream directly —
   no sidecar needed for that. The sidecar exists so the log OUTLIVES
   the pod (searchable after a CrashLoop) and is centrally queryable
   across every easypj-web replica at once.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">structured log line + LogQL — correlating a log to its trace</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-json"><span class="comment">// easypj emits structured JSON, not free text — this is what makes it queryable</span>
{"level":"error","msg":"db query failed","view":"JobPost.list",
 "duration_ms":790,"trace_id":"ab12cd34","status":500,
 "path":"/jobs/","pod":"easypj-web-7d9f-x2k4l"}</code></pre>
<pre><code><span class="comment"># LogQL (Loki's query language) — find every ERROR log carrying that exact trace_id:</span>
{app="easypj"} | json | trace_id="ab12cd34" | level="error"

<span class="comment"># Broader: error rate FROM LOGS, cross-checked against the Prometheus metric:</span>
sum(rate({app="easypj"} | json | level="error" [5m]))</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">💡 <strong>Structured logging is what makes exemplars work end to end.</strong> Because easypj logs JSON with a <code>trace_id</code> field, and the OTel span for that same request carries the identical <code>trace_id</code>, and the Prometheus histogram bucket sample is tagged with it too — one click in Grafana chains metric → trace → log without ever grepping raw text. Free-text logging ("DB query failed for job 4471") breaks this chain; you'd be back to manual string search.</p>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── §43.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s43-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">43.2 Prometheus</h3>
                <p>Prometheus is the metrics collector at the center of the stack. Understanding its <strong>pull</strong> model — the opposite of how most logging pipelines work — explains almost everything else in this section.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Prometheus</h5>
                    <p>Prometheus is an open-source, <strong>pull-based</strong> metrics system. It <strong>scrapes</strong> HTTP endpoints (<code>/metrics</code>) on a schedule, stores the numbers in a local time-series database (TSDB), and lets you query them with <strong>PromQL</strong>. The pull model means Prometheus decides who to scrape and when — targets just expose <code>/metrics</code> and stay dumb; they never need to know Prometheus exists.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Prometheus pull model — scraping easypj</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   ┌────────────────┐  scrape every 15s   ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │  PROMETHEUS     │ ───── GET /metrics ─▶│ easypj pod   :8000/metrics    │
   │  (server + TSDB)│ ◀──── 200 text ──────│  http_requests_total ...      │
   │                 │                      │  http_request_duration_... │
   │  service        │ ───── GET /metrics ─▶│ kube-state-metrics            │
   │  discovery ─────┤ ───── GET /metrics ─▶│ node-exporter (USE / node)    │
   │   (k8s API)     │ ───── GET /metrics ─▶│ cAdvisor / kubelet (per-pod)  │
   └────────┬────────┘                      └──────────────────────────────┘
            │ targets resolved from ServiceMonitor / Pod annotations
            ▼
   ┌────────────────┐    PromQL     ┌──────────┐   alerts  ┌──────────────┐
   │  TSDB (15d def) │ ◀──── query ──│ Grafana  │           │ Alertmanager │
   │                 │ ───────────── │ Explore  │  rules ──▶│ route/dedup  │
   └────────────────┘               └──────────┘           └──────┬───────┘
                                                     page/Slack ◀──┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔌</div>
                        <h5>Exporter</h5>
                        <p>A small process that translates some system's stats into Prometheus <code>/metrics</code> text. <strong>node-exporter</strong> (node CPU/mem/disk), <strong>kube-state-metrics</strong> (k8s object state: deployments, pods, replicas), <strong>postgres-exporter</strong> (easypj's PostgreSQL), and easypj's own client library (<code>django-prometheus</code>) all expose <code>/metrics</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>ServiceMonitor</h5>
                        <p>A Prometheus-Operator CRD that declaratively tells Prometheus which Services to scrape. Instead of hand-editing Prometheus config, you create a ServiceMonitor whose selector matches the easypj Service; the Operator regenerates scrape config for you automatically.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">servicemonitor.yaml — scrape easypj-web</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ServiceMonitor — Prometheus Operator</span>
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: ServiceMonitor
metadata:
  name: easypj
  namespace: easypj-production
  labels:
    release: kube-prometheus-stack   <span class="comment"># matched by the Prometheus CR</span>
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                    <span class="comment"># matches the easypj-web Service labels</span>
  endpoints:
    - port: web                      <span class="comment"># the named port on the Service</span>
      path: /metrics
      interval: 15s</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">📖 With the Operator, the Prometheus custom resource carries a <code>serviceMonitorSelector</code>. Each matching ServiceMonitor is compiled into a scrape job; Prometheus then uses Kubernetes service discovery to enumerate the Endpoints behind that Service and scrapes each <strong>pod IP directly</strong> — not the ClusterIP — so you get per-pod series. Pod annotations (<code>prometheus.io/scrape: "true"</code>) are the older, non-Operator way to achieve the same thing. Full depth in Under the Hood ①.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>PromQL Primer — the Four Functions You Actually Use</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Function / pattern</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>a raw <strong>counter</strong>, e.g. <code>http_requests_total</code></td><td>only ever increases; you almost never graph it raw</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>rate(counter[5m])</code></td><td>per-second average increase over a 5-minute window — turns a counter into a "requests per second" line</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sum(...)</code> / <code>sum by (label)(...)</code></td><td>aggregate across pods, or group by a label like <code>status</code> or <code>path</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>histogram_quantile(0.95, ...)</code></td><td>estimate the 95th-percentile latency from a histogram's bucket counters</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">basic PromQL on easypj</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code><span class="comment"># Request rate (req/s) across all easypj pods:</span>
sum(rate(http_requests_total{app="easypj"}[5m]))

<span class="comment"># Error rate (fraction of 5xx) — the canonical query, memorize this one:</span>
sum(rate(http_requests_total{app="easypj",status=~"5.."}[5m]))
  / sum(rate(http_requests_total{app="easypj"}[5m]))

<span class="comment"># p95 request latency (seconds):</span>
histogram_quantile(0.95,
  sum by (le)(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{app="easypj"}[5m])))

<span class="comment"># Per-path request rate (group by the path label):</span>
sum by (path)(rate(http_requests_total{app="easypj"}[5m]))

<span class="comment"># Quick, NO-Prometheus alternative for live CPU/mem (needs metrics-server):</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj-production</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Insight — Counters vs Gauges vs Histograms</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Counter</strong> — monotonic up only (requests, errors, bytes). Always wrap it in <code>rate()</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Gauge</strong> — goes up and down (in-flight requests, queue depth, temperature). Use it raw, or with <code>avg()</code>/<code>max()</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Histogram</strong> — buckets of observations (request durations) plus <code>_sum</code>/<code>_count</code>. Feed the <code>_bucket</code> series to <code>histogram_quantile()</code> for percentiles.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Why rate() survives a pod restart — counter resets, explained</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   A pod redeploy resets its in-memory counter back to 0. Naive graphing
   of the raw counter looks like traffic crashed to zero, then a fresh
   climb — but rate() is built to see through this:

   raw counter:   ...480, 495, 510,  0,  8, 22, 40...   ◀── pod restarted here
                                     └┬┘
                          rate() DETECTS the decrease (impossible for a
                          real counter) and treats it as a RESET, counting
                          the post-restart increase (0→40) on its own —
                          it does NOT report a nonsense negative rate.

   result:        rate() line stays a smooth "requests/sec" curve through
                   the restart — a small dip at most, never a cliff to
                   a negative number.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Troubleshooting — <code>kubectl top</code> returns "error: Metrics API not available"</h5>
                    <p><strong>metrics-server</strong> is not installed or not Ready. metrics-server is the <strong>lightweight, in-cluster</strong> source for <code>kubectl top</code> and the HPA — it is <strong>not</strong> Prometheus and stores no history (one live sample, no time-series). Install it and verify with <code>kubectl get deploy metrics-server -n kube-system</code>. This is one of the highest-value distinctions in this whole chapter — don't confuse the two.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Metrics API Deep Dive — What's Actually Behind <code>kubectl top</code></h4>
                <p><code>kubectl top</code> and the HPA don't talk to metrics-server directly — they talk to the <strong><code>metrics.k8s.io</code></strong> API, which metrics-server registers via Kubernetes' <strong>API aggregation layer</strong> so it looks like a native part of the API server. Following that call end to end explains exactly what "the Metrics API" means and why it can never replace Prometheus. (Ch33's Under-the-Hood ② first introduced this cAdvisor→kubelet→metrics-server pipeline; here we go one level deeper into the raw response and the fields most people misread.)</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Resource Metrics Pipeline — cAdvisor to <code>kubectl top</code></div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   ┌───────────┐  /metrics/resource   ┌────────────────┐   polls every node,  ┌──────────────┐
   │ cAdvisor  │ ───── per-node ─────▶│    KUBELET      │◀── ~15s (default) ───│ metrics-server│
   │(in kubelet)│      cgroup stats    │   :10250        │                      │ (cluster addon)│
   └───────────┘                       └────────────────┘                      └───────┬────────┘
                                                                                          │ registers as an
                                                                              API AGGREGATION LAYER extension
                                                                                          ▼
                                              GET /apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1/nodes/&lt;name&gt;
                                              GET /apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1/namespaces/&lt;ns&gt;/pods/&lt;pod&gt;
                                                                                          │
                                          ┌───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┤
                                          ▼                       ▼                       ▼
                                    kubectl top                  HPA                     VPA

   `/metrics/resource` is the CURRENT kubelet endpoint (metrics-server v0.6.0+); the
   older `/stats/summary` endpoint still exists but is legacy. Either way, this whole
   pipeline holds ONE live sample per resource — no history, which is exactly why it
   cannot answer "was CPU high an hour ago?" the way Prometheus can.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">raw Metrics API call — the exact data kubectl top formats for you</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># same data `kubectl top pod` shows, straight from the API — useful when kubectl's</span>
<span class="comment"># own formatting hides a field you need, or you're scripting against it directly</span>
kubectl get --raw "/apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1/namespaces/easypj-production/pods/easypj-web-7d9f-x2k4l" | jq .</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-json"><span class="comment">// response shape (trimmed) — note "window" and the "n"/"Ki" unit suffixes</span>
{
  "kind": "PodMetrics",
  "timestamp": "2026-07-09T14:03:00Z",
  "window": "30s",
  "containers": [
    { "name": "easypj", "usage": { "cpu": "9559630n", "memory": "22244Ki" } }
  ]
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Field</th><th>What it actually means</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>usage.cpu</code> (e.g. <code>9559630n</code>)</td><td>nanocores — a <strong>rate</strong> derived from the kernel's cumulative CPU-time counter, not an instantaneous snapshot</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>usage.memory</code> (e.g. <code>22244Ki</code>)</td><td><strong>working set</strong> bytes — in-use memory that <strong>cannot</strong> be freed under memory pressure. This is <em>not</em> the same number as RSS or total allocated memory, and it's why a pod's <code>kubectl top</code> memory can differ from what a tool inside the container reports</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>window</code></td><td>the sampling interval the usage figure was averaged over (commonly <code>30s</code>) — always read this field before comparing two samples, since it isn't fixed across every cluster/version</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">📖 The container runtime has to implement the <strong>CRI Container Metrics</strong> interface for cAdvisor to report anything at all — true by default for containerd and CRI-O, so this is rarely the culprit, but it's WHY a heavily customized or sandboxed runtime can leave metrics-server running fine yet reporting nothing for a specific node.</p>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── §43.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s43-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">43.3 Grafana</h3>
                <p>Prometheus stores numbers; Grafana makes them <em>legible</em>. Once easypj's metrics exist, the next question is what to actually put on a screen — and RED vs USE answers that instantly.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Grafana</h5>
                    <p>Grafana is the visualization layer. It queries data sources — Prometheus for metrics, Loki for logs, Tempo/Jaeger for traces — and renders <strong>dashboards</strong> (collections of panels), plus its own Explore view and its own alerting engine. Grafana stores nothing itself; it asks Prometheus fresh on every refresh.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Use it for</th><th>What you dashboard</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>RED</strong></td><td>request-driven SERVICES (the easypj web tier)</td><td><strong>R</strong>ate (req/s) · <strong>E</strong>rrors (% 5xx) · <strong>D</strong>uration (p50/p95/p99 latency)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>USE</strong></td><td>RESOURCES (nodes, disks, easypj's PostgreSQL)</td><td><strong>U</strong>tilization (% busy) · <strong>S</strong>aturation (queued work) · <strong>E</strong>rrors (device/error counts)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj "Service Overview" dashboard (RED)</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   ┌───────────────────────┐ ┌───────────────────────┐ ┌───────────────────────┐
   │ Rate (req/s)          │ │ Error % (5xx/total)   │ │ Latency p50/95/99     │
   │      ╱╲    ╱╲╱        │ │  0.3% ── SLO 1% ────  │ │   p99 ╱╲              │
   │  ___╱  ╲__╱    ╲___   │ │  ▁▁▂▁▁▁▁▃▁▁ (under)   │ │   p95 ___╱╲___        │
   └───────────────────────┘ └───────────────────────┘ └───────────────────────┘
   ┌───────────────────────┐ ┌───────────────────────┐ ┌───────────────────────┐
   │ Pods Ready (4/4)      │ │ Restarts (last 1h)    │ │ DB conns / pool %     │
   └───────────────────────┘ └───────────────────────┘ └───────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>⚡ Speed Tip — Don't Hand-Build Node Dashboards</h5>
                    <p>The <code>kube-prometheus-stack</code> Helm chart ships ready-made Grafana dashboards for nodes, pods, namespaces, and the control plane. Import the community "Node Exporter Full" (ID 1860) and "Kubernetes / Compute Resources / Namespace (Pods)" and you have USE coverage in minutes. Spend your own effort on the easypj RED dashboard — that's the one only you can write, because only you know what <code>/jobs/</code> means to a real user.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">💡 A dashboard without a reference line is just decoration. Draw the <strong>SLO threshold</strong> — the 1% error line, the 300ms p95 line — directly on the panel so a glance tells you "inside budget" vs "burning budget," which is exactly what §43.4 turns into a page.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §43.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s43-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">43.4 Alerting &amp; SLOs</h3>
                <p>A dashboard nobody watches at 3 AM doesn't help anyone. Alerting turns a threshold into a page — and SLOs turn an arbitrary threshold into a principled, negotiated one.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th><th>easypj example</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>SLI</strong> (Indicator)</td><td>a <em>measured</em> number that reflects user experience</td><td>request-success ratio, or p95 latency</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SLO</strong> (Objective)</td><td>the <em>target</em> for an SLI over a window</td><td>"99.9% of <code>/jobs/</code> requests succeed over 30 days"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SLA</strong> (Agreement)</td><td>the <em>contract</em> with consequences (refunds) if the SLO is missed — SLA ≤ SLO, you set the SLO stricter than the SLA</td><td>a customer contract promising 99.5% uptime</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Error budget</strong></td><td><code>1 − SLO</code> — the allowable failure you may "spend"</td><td>at 99.9% SLO the budget is 0.1% ≈ 43 minutes of "allowed" failure per 30 days</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">💡 You <strong>spend</strong> the error budget on risk — deploys, experiments, chaos testing. When it's exhausted, the team freezes risky changes until it recovers. This turns "should we ship today?" from a political argument into a number anyone can check.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Alerting pipeline — symptom in, page out</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   Prometheus ── evaluates ALERTING RULES (PromQL + `for:`) ──┐
      (e.g. error rate &gt; 1% for 5m → fires "EasypjHighErrorRate")
                                                              ▼
                                                ┌───────────────────────────┐
                                                │  ALERTMANAGER              │
    group similar alerts ◀── route by labels ──┤  dedupe · group · throttle │
    inhibit (mute B if A firing)               │  silence · inhibit         │
                                                └────────┬──────────────────┘
                                  receivers ──────────────┼──────▶ PagerDuty
                                                          ├──────▶ Slack
                                                          └──────▶ email
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Key Principle — Alert on Symptoms, Not Causes</h5>
                    <p>A <strong>symptom</strong> is user-visible (error rate, latency, budget burn). A <strong>cause</strong> is an internal condition (CPU high, a pod restarted, a disk 80% full). Symptom alerts (1) map directly to user pain, so every page is worth waking up for; (2) catch <strong>unknown</strong> failure modes — you didn't predict the cause, but the symptom still fires; and (3) stay few in number, which means less alert fatigue. Causes belong on dashboards for diagnosis, not on the pager.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 War Story — The On-Call Rotation That Muted PagerDuty</h5>
                    <p>A team wires <strong>every</strong> cause into Alertmanager: CPU &gt; 70%, memory &gt; 80%, any pod restart, any disk &gt; 75%, connection pool &gt; 60%. Before/after, once easypj switched to symptom/SLO-based paging:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Cause-based (before)</th><th>Symptom/SLO-based (after)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Pages per on-call week</td><td>~140</td><td>~6</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pages that needed real action</td><td>~8 (6%)</td><td>~5 (83%)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Real outage caught?</td><td>❌ missed — buried under noise, on-call had muted the channel by week 2</td><td>✅ caught — the one page that fired WAS the incident</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">By week 2, the on-call engineer had muted the PagerDuty service entirely — not maliciously, just from exhaustion. When easypj's real error-rate incident hit two weeks later, nobody was paged for 40 minutes because the muted channel ate that page too. The fix isn't "try harder to keep up with 140 pages" — it's deleting nearly all of them and keeping only the ones tied to an SLO.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-rules.yaml — PrometheusRule</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: PrometheusRule
metadata:
  name: easypj-rules
  namespace: easypj-production
  labels:
    release: kube-prometheus-stack
spec:
  groups:
    - name: easypj.slo
      rules:
        <span class="comment"># --- recording rule: pre-compute the error ratio once ---</span>
        - record: easypj:request_error_ratio:rate5m
          expr: |
            sum(rate(http_requests_total{app="easypj",status=~"5.."}[5m]))
              / sum(rate(http_requests_total{app="easypj"}[5m]))
        <span class="comment"># --- symptom alert: high error rate (fast burn) ---</span>
        - alert: EasypjHighErrorRate
          expr: easypj:request_error_ratio:rate5m &gt; 0.01
          for: 5m
          labels: {severity: page}
          annotations:
            summary: "easypj 5xx error rate above 1% for 5m"
            description: "Error ratio is {{ $value | humanizePercentage }}"
        <span class="comment"># --- symptom alert: slow p95 latency ---</span>
        - alert: EasypjHighLatency
          expr: |
            histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le)(
              rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{app="easypj"}[5m])
            )) &gt; 0.3
          for: 10m
          labels: {severity: ticket}
          annotations:
            summary: "easypj p95 latency above 300ms"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Burn-Rate Alerting</h5>
                    <p>Rather than "error rate &gt; 1% for 5m," the modern Google-SRE approach alerts on how <strong>fast</strong> you are consuming the error budget. A burn rate of <strong>1</strong> means you'll exactly exhaust the 30-day budget in 30 days; a burn rate of <strong>14.4</strong> means you'd burn the whole month's budget in ~2 days — page now. Fast-burn (page) + slow-burn (ticket) multi-window rules give urgency proportional to severity, expanded fully in Under the Hood ⑥.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">💡 <strong>Recording rules vs alerting rules.</strong> A <strong>recording</strong> rule pre-computes a frequently-used PromQL expression into a new series, named with the <code>metric:operation:window</code> convention — <code>easypj:request_error_ratio:rate5m</code> above. Dashboards and alerts then reference the cheap pre-computed series instead of re-evaluating an expensive query on every scrape. <strong>Alerting</strong> rules evaluate a condition and, when true for the <code>for:</code> duration, emit an alert to Alertmanager.</p>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── §43.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s43-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">43.5 Tracing</h3>
                <p>Metrics said something is wrong. Now: where, exactly? Tracing follows one real request through every service it touches and shows precisely where its time went.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>Distributed Tracing</h5>
                        <p>Follows a <strong>single</strong> request as it fans out across services, stitching the work into one <strong>trace</strong> made of <strong>spans</strong>. Each span is one unit of work (an HTTP handler, a DB query, a cache lookup) with a start time, duration, and parent. A propagated <strong>trace ID</strong> ties them together so you see exactly where a slow <code>/jobs/</code> request spent its time.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📡</div>
                        <h5>OpenTelemetry (OTel)</h5>
                        <p>The vendor-neutral standard + SDKs for emitting metrics, logs, <em>and</em> traces. You instrument easypj with the OTel SDK (or auto-instrumentation), which exports spans to an OTel <strong>Collector</strong>; the collector processes/batches them and forwards to a backend (Jaeger, Tempo, etc.). One standard, swappable backends.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Trace of a slow /jobs/ request (easypj) — trace_id=ab12…</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   span: GET /jobs/            ████████████████████████████████████████  920ms
    ├─ middleware/auth          ██                                           30ms
    ├─ view: JobPost.list       ░░░░████████████████████████████████████    840ms ⚠️
    │   ├─ db: SELECT jobpost   ░░░░██                                        40ms
    │   └─ db: SELECT skills    ░░░░░░████████████████████████████████ ◀── 790ms 🔴
    │         (N+1 join lost its index — the bottleneck)
    └─ template render          █                                            20ms

   ▶ Metrics said "p95 up."  The TRACE says WHERE: the skills query.
   ▶ Logs for that trace_id then say WHY: "Seq Scan on jobpost_skills".
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>Span Anatomy — What's Actually Inside One Box in That Diagram</h4>
                <p>The "skills SELECT" bar above isn't just a label and a width — it's a structured record with a fixed set of fields. Knowing them is what separates "I can read a trace waterfall" from "I can actually instrument easypj's own spans correctly."</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Span field</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Name</td><td>the operation label, e.g. <code>db: SELECT skills</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>trace_id</code> / <code>span_id</code></td><td>identifiers correlating this span to its whole trace, and to itself uniquely</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>parent_span_id</code></td><td>empty for the root span; otherwise the calling span's id — this is what builds the parent/child tree in the waterfall</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Start / end timestamps</td><td>wall-clock duration of just this span (the bar's width)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Attributes</td><td>key-value metadata, e.g. <code>http.method</code>, <code>db.statement</code> — standardized names come from OTel's <strong>semantic conventions</strong> so a Django span and a Go span label the same concept identically</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Events</td><td>timestamped log entries <strong>inside</strong> this span's own duration, e.g. "cache miss at 14:03:00.402"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Links</td><td>a causal reference to a span in a <strong>different</strong> trace — e.g. a nightly batch job that fans out 50 independent async traces links back to the trigger instead of forcing everything into one giant trace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Status</td><td><code>Unset</code> (default — no explicit success/failure verdict), <code>Ok</code> (explicitly marked successful), or <code>Error</code> (explicitly marked failed)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Kind</td><td><code>Client</code> / <code>Server</code> / <code>Internal</code> / <code>Producer</code> / <code>Consumer</code> — hints to the backend how to assemble and render the trace, detailed below</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📤</div>
                        <h5>Client / Server</h5>
                        <p>A synchronous remote call, seen from both ends: <strong>Client</strong> is the outgoing side (easypj-web calling PostgreSQL), <strong>Server</strong> is the incoming side (easypj-web receiving the browser's HTTP request). Most spans in a typical web-request trace are one of these two.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚙️</div>
                        <h5>Internal</h5>
                        <p>Work that happens entirely inside one process, with no network hop — e.g. template rendering or in-memory serialization. No "other side" span exists for it to pair with.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📮</div>
                        <h5>Producer</h5>
                        <p>Creates work for <strong>asynchronous</strong> processing — e.g. easypj-web enqueuing a "send application-confirmation email" Celery task. The Producer span ends as soon as the task is queued, long before the work actually runs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📥</div>
                        <h5>Consumer</h5>
                        <p>Processes that queued work — the Celery worker picking up the email task. Its span starts <strong>after</strong> the Producer span has already ended, which is exactly why async work needs Span Links rather than a simple parent/child edge.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <p class="mb-0">💡 This is the full loop in one picture. The Prometheus p95 <strong>alert</strong> fired (symptom). The OTel <strong>trace</strong> localized the 790ms to the skills SELECT (where). The <strong>log</strong> for that <code>trace_id</code> revealed the missing index (why). Three pillars, one investigation. <strong>Exemplars</strong> make this clickable: the latency histogram bucket carries a <code>trace_id</code>, so you jump from the spike straight to the offending trace.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch43-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — target discovery, cardinality, and where all these metrics actually go</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Everything above works because of a handful of mechanisms most dashboards never show you: how Prometheus actually finds a pod to scrape, why one careless label can take down the whole TSDB, and what happens to a metric between "scraped" and "paged." Six deep dives, in the order a metric's life actually happens.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   easypj pod exists ──▶ ① discovered ──▶ scraped ──▶ ② stored (cardinality-bounded)
        ──▶ ③ recorded/alerted ──▶ ④ queried as a percentile ──▶ ⑤ exported as a trace
        ──▶ ⑥ burned against an SLO, then shipped to long-term storage
   Every arrow above is one of the six deep dives below.
                    </pre>

                    <h5>① Target Discovery — How Prometheus Finds easypj's Pods</h5>
                    <p>§43.2 showed a ServiceMonitor and said "the Operator compiles it into scrape config." Here's the actual mechanism: the Prometheus custom resource carries a <code>serviceMonitorSelector</code> that watches for matching ServiceMonitor objects, and each one becomes a scrape job that resolves to real pod IPs — never the ClusterIP.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   1. You create a ServiceMonitor (selector: app=easypj)
   2. Prometheus Operator's `serviceMonitorSelector` matches it
   3. Operator compiles it into a scrape job inside Prometheus's own config
   4. Prometheus uses Kubernetes SD (watches the EndpointSlice for the
      easypj-web Service) to enumerate every backing POD IP
   5. Prometheus scrapes EACH POD IP directly on port "web" → /metrics
      (NOT the ClusterIP — a ClusterIP would randomly load-balance across
       pods and silently under-count some of them)
   6. Pod restarts / scales? EndpointSlice updates → SD re-resolves targets
      automatically, no Prometheus restart needed.
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Discovery method</th><th>How Prometheus finds the target</th><th>When it's used</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>ServiceMonitor (Operator)</td><td>selector matches a Service → K8s SD resolves its pod IPs</td><td>the standard, declarative way — used throughout this chapter</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>PodMonitor (Operator)</td><td>selector matches Pods directly, no Service needed</td><td>pods without a fronting Service (batch jobs, headless workloads)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pod annotations (legacy)</td><td><code>prometheus.io/scrape: "true"</code> read by a hand-configured scrape job</td><td>pre-Operator / non-CRD Prometheus installs</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Because scraping targets pod IPs directly, you get <strong>per-pod</strong> series — <code>sum()</code> in your PromQL is what re-aggregates them back into one number. If a ServiceMonitor's label selector is even slightly off (a typo, or a missing <code>release:</code> label — the <code>kube-prometheus-stack</code> Helm chart configures the Prometheus CR's <code>serviceMonitorSelector</code> to require one by default), Prometheus silently scrapes zero targets — no error, just an empty dashboard. Always check <code>Status → Targets</code> in the Prometheus UI first.</div>

                    <h5>② Cardinality — the Silent TSDB Killer</h5>
                    <p>Total series held in memory ≈ (number of metric names) × (the product of every label's distinct value count). Prometheus keeps every active series' recent samples in RAM — an unbounded label doesn't just add rows to a table, it multiplies the whole series count.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   SAFE:   http_requests_total{app="easypj", status="200", path="/jobs/"}
           3 labels, each with ~5-20 distinct values → thousands of series. Fine.

   DANGEROUS: http_requests_total{app="easypj", user_id="8827", request_id="a1b2c3"}
           user_id: millions of values × request_id: unique EVERY request
           = essentially INFINITE series → Prometheus OOMs, ingestion stalls

   THE FIX — relabel_configs DROPS the dangerous label before it's ever stored:
     metricRelabelings:
       - sourceLabels: [request_id]
         action: labeldrop
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Cardinality</th><th>Safe to keep?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>status</code> (200/404/500…)</td><td>~10 values</td><td>✅ yes — this is exactly what you group by</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>path</code> (route pattern, not raw URL)</td><td>tens of values</td><td>✅ yes, IF it's the route template (<code>/jobs/&lt;id&gt;</code>), not the raw URL</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>pod</code> name</td><td>bounded by replica count</td><td>✅ yes, usually fine at normal fleet sizes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>user_id</code> / <code>request_id</code> / raw query string</td><td>unbounded, effectively infinite</td><td>❌ never — drop with <code>relabel_configs</code> at scrape time</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>War story, the kind that shows up in a postmortem: a well-intentioned "add <code>user_id</code> to easypj's request metric so we can debug per-customer issues" PR merges cleanly, passes review, and ships. Nothing breaks that day.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Week 1:  50,000 easypj users active → 50,000× multiplier on EVERY metric
            that carries the new label. Series count: 40k → 6.2M. Prometheus
            RAM: 1.5GB → 11GB. Still "working," just slower dashboards.

   Week 3:  user base grows, request_id-style values start appearing too
            (a second dev copies the pattern onto a different metric).
            Series count: 6.2M → 40M+. Prometheus OOMKilled. Scrapes start
            failing cluster-wide — even UNRELATED teams' dashboards go dark.
            remote_write to Thanos also backs up, and the LONG-TERM storage
            bill spikes because every one of those series gets shipped and
            retained too, not just held in local RAM.

   THE FIX: labeldrop at scrape time (immediate) + revert the app-level
   label (permanent) + add a cardinality budget/alert on `count by
   (__name__)({__name__=~".+"})` so the NEXT one is caught in code review,
   not by an OOMKill three weeks later.
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Cardinality bugs are almost always introduced by a well-meaning developer adding "just one more useful label" to an app's own metrics — <code>django-prometheus</code> won't stop you from labeling by raw path or user. Code review your metric labels the same way you'd review a schema migration; a cardinality explosion is an outage, not a cosmetic issue — and unlike most outages, it can take down OTHER teams' unrelated dashboards on the same shared Prometheus.</div>

                    <h5>③ Recording vs Alerting Rules — Precompute Once, Reuse Everywhere</h5>
                    <p>§43.4's PrometheusRule had both kinds in one file. The distinction matters because they run on different schedules and serve different consumers.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Prometheus evaluation loop, every rule_evaluation_interval (default 30s/1m):

   RECORDING RULE                          ALERTING RULE
   ┌─────────────────────────┐             ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ evaluate expensive PromQL │            │ evaluate a boolean condition   │
   │ e.g. error ratio          │            │ e.g. error ratio &gt; 0.01        │
   │ store result as a NEW     │            │ true for `for:` duration?      │
   │ series: name:op:window    │            │   NO  → nothing happens        │
   │                            │            │   YES → fire to Alertmanager   │
   └─────────────────────────┘             └──────────────────────────────┘
        ▲ read by dashboards                        ▲ read by on-call humans
        AND by alerting rules (cheap reuse)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Recording rule</th><th>Alerting rule</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Output</td><td>a new stored time series</td><td>an alert (state: inactive/pending/firing)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Consumer</td><td>Grafana panels, other rules</td><td>Alertmanager → humans</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Naming convention</td><td><code>level:metric:operations</code> (e.g. <code>easypj:request_error_ratio:rate5m</code>)</td><td>PascalCase, e.g. <code>EasypjHighErrorRate</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Point BOTH the dashboard panel and the alert at the same recording rule. If they instead each hand-write their own copy of the error-ratio expression, they can silently drift (different window lengths, a forgotten label filter) and disagree about whether you're in an incident — a real, recurring production bug.</div>

                    <h5>④ Histogram Quantiles — Why p95 Is an Estimate, Not a Fact</h5>
                    <p><code>histogram_quantile()</code> doesn't know individual request durations — it only has bucket <em>counts</em> (how many requests were ≤100ms, ≤300ms, ≤1s…) and linearly interpolates within the bucket that crosses the 95th percentile. Bad bucket boundaries produce a p95 that's technically computed correctly and practically meaningless.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Buckets:  le="0.1"=800   le="0.3"=950   le="1.0"=1000   (1000 total requests)

   p95 target = the 950th request (95% of 1000)
   950 requests fall at or under le=0.3 exactly → histogram_quantile interpolates
   INSIDE the (0.1, 0.3] bucket for the 950th value → estimate ≈ 0.3s

   ⚠️ If your SLO is "p95 &lt; 300ms" but your buckets jump 0.1 → 0.3 → 1.0,
      you cannot tell 305ms from 295ms — TOO COARSE near the number you actually
      care about. Add an extra bucket at 0.25 or 0.3 exactly on your SLO line.
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it is</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Fixed buckets</td><td>you declare boundaries up front (e.g. 0.1, 0.3, 1, 3s)</td><td>coarse buckets near your SLO give a meaningless percentile — put extra buckets THERE</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Exemplars</td><td>a bucket sample carries a <code>trace_id</code></td><td>click a latency spike in Grafana, jump straight to the actual slow trace (§43.5's loop, made clickable)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Native (sparse) histograms</td><td>Prometheus feature: auto-scaling, high-resolution buckets instead of fixed ones</td><td>accurate quantiles at a fraction of the series count — no more manual bucket tuning</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> "The p95 dashboard says 250ms but users are complaining about 2-second loads" is very often a bucket-boundary problem, not a lie — check <code>django-prometheus</code>'s configured buckets against your actual SLO number before you trust the graph.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ The OTel Collector Pipeline — Receivers → Processors → Exporters</h5>
                    <p>§43.5 said "the collector processes/batches" spans. The Collector is itself a small pipeline, and understanding its three stages is what lets you control tracing <em>cost</em> without losing the traces that actually matter.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   easypj (OTel SDK) ──OTLP──▶ ┌───────────── OTel COLLECTOR ─────────────┐
                               │ RECEIVERS   → PROCESSORS   → EXPORTERS   │
                               │ otlp/grpc     batch            otlp→Tempo │
                               │ otlp/http     attributes       otlp→Jaeger│
                               │               tail-sampling ◀── keeps    │
                               │                                 errors & │
                               │                                 slow ones│
                               └───────────────────────────────────────────┘
   tail-sampling decides AFTER seeing the whole trace: keep it if it has an
   error OR is a latency outlier — discard routine fast/successful ones.
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Job</th><th>easypj example</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Receiver</td><td>accept incoming telemetry</td><td><code>otlp</code> receiver on gRPC/HTTP from the Django OTel SDK</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Processor</td><td>transform / filter / batch</td><td><code>batch</code> (efficiency), <code>tail_sampling</code> (keep errors &amp; p99 outliers, drop the rest)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Exporter</td><td>ship to a backend</td><td><code>otlp</code> exporter → Tempo or Jaeger</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Sampling 1% of ALL requests would randomly miss most errors, since errors are rare by definition. <strong>Tail sampling</strong> fixes this: it waits until the whole trace is complete, THEN decides — so it can guarantee "keep every trace with an error" while still discarding most of the boring, fast, successful traffic. That's why tracing backends stay affordable even at high request volume.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ SLO Burn-Rate Alerts &amp; Long-Term Storage</h5>
                    <p>§43.4 introduced burn-rate alerting; here's the full multi-window shape real SRE teams run, plus where your 15 days of local Prometheus data goes after that.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   MULTI-WINDOW / MULTI-BURN-RATE (Google SRE workbook pattern):

   burn 14.4× sustained over BOTH 1h AND 5m  ──▶ 🚨 PAGE  (~2 days to exhaust the month)
   burn  6×   sustained over BOTH 6h AND 30m ──▶ 🎫 TICKET (5 days to exhaust)

   requiring BOTH a long AND a short window match avoids paging on a 2-minute
   blip that a single 5m window alone would trigger on.

   ── after 15 days, Prometheus's local TSDB rotates out old data: ──
   Prometheus (15d local) ── remote_write ──▶ Thanos / Mimir / Cortex
                                               (long-term, downsampled,
                                                globally queryable across
                                                every cluster, cheap storage)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Window pair</th><th>Burn rate</th><th>Time to exhaust budget</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>1h + 5m</td><td>14.4×</td><td>~2 days</td><td>page immediately — fast, severe</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>6h + 30m</td><td>6×</td><td>~5 days</td><td>ticket — slower, still worth tracking</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3d + 6h (optional 3rd window)</td><td>1×</td><td>~30 days (exactly the SLO window)</td><td>weekly review, not a page</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>War story, the cost-surprise kind (not just an outage — a bill): a team sets <code>remote_write</code> retention to "keep everything, forever, full resolution" because storage seems cheap, then never revisits it as easypj's fleet grows from 1 to 12 clusters.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Retention choice</th><th>Monthly cost driver</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Full-resolution, kept forever, on all 12 clusters</td><td>storage grows UNBOUNDED — this is a straight line up, forever, not a plateau</td><td>set an explicit retention window (e.g. 13 months) — "forever" is rarely an actual requirement, just an unset default</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>No downsampling on old data</td><td>a 2-year-old data point costs the same to store as a 2-minute-old one, even though nobody queries it at full resolution</td><td>downsample after N days (Thanos compactor: 5m→1h resolution after 40 days) — same trend visibility, a fraction of the bytes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Every cluster's cardinality problem (UTH ②) ALSO gets shipped &amp; retained long-term</td><td>a local RAM problem becomes a recurring STORAGE bill, every month, until someone notices</td><td>fix cardinality at the source — remote-write cost is a multiplier on top of it, not a separate problem</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Prometheus itself retains roughly 15 days locally — it is <strong>not</strong> designed as a long-term archive. <code>remote_write</code> streams every sample out to Thanos/Mimir/Cortex, which adds downsampling and cross-cluster global queries — the piece that makes Chapter 44's multi-cluster fleet observable from one place instead of N separate 15-day silos. But "cheap long-term storage" is still a per-cluster, per-series, per-month LINE ITEM — treat retention/downsampling policy as a cost decision, not a default to leave untouched; Chapter 44 picks this up as a whole-fleet budgeting problem.</div>
                </div>
            </details>
            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch43-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Three pillars, one loop:</strong> Metrics (Prometheus — what &amp; how much: rate/latency/errors), Logs (Loki/EFK — what happened), Traces (OpenTelemetry — where the time went across services). Together they feed Alerts.</li>
                    <li><strong>The pillars are complementary:</strong> alert → metric (something's wrong) → trace (where) → log (why). Exemplars link a metric spike directly to a trace.</li>
                    <li><strong>Prometheus is pull-based:</strong> it scrapes exporters/ServiceMonitors on a schedule into a local TSDB. Core PromQL: <code>rate()</code> turns counters into per-second rates; <code>histogram_quantile()</code> estimates p95/p99 latency.</li>
                    <li><strong>A ServiceMonitor</strong> (Operator CRD) tells Prometheus which Services to scrape by label selector; <strong>metrics-server</strong> is the lightweight, history-less source for <code>kubectl top</code> and the HPA — not Prometheus.</li>
                    <li><strong>Use RED</strong> (Rate/Errors/Duration) for SERVICES and <strong>USE</strong> (Utilization/Saturation/Errors) for RESOURCES/nodes to decide what to dashboard.</li>
                    <li><strong>Alert on symptoms/SLOs</strong> (user-visible: error rate, latency, budget burn), NOT raw causes — and watch metric cardinality so Prometheus stays healthy.</li>
                    <li><strong>SLI</strong> = measured indicator; <strong>SLO</strong> = its target; <strong>error budget</strong> = 1 − SLO, the failure you may "spend." Burn-rate alerts page faster when the budget drains faster.</li>
                    <li><strong>Recording rules</strong> pre-compute hot queries; <strong>alerting rules</strong> evaluate a condition + <code>for:</code> and hand firing alerts to Alertmanager (dedupe, group, route, silence, inhibit).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch43-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>High-cardinality labels</strong> (user id, request id, full URL with query string) explode Prometheus memory — keep label sets bounded; drop offenders with relabeling at scrape time.</li>
                    <li><strong>Alerting on every cause</strong> produces alert fatigue; alert on a few meaningful SLO/symptom conditions instead.</li>
                    <li><strong>Dashboards without SLOs</strong> are pretty but don't tell you if you're OK — define objectives and draw the threshold line, not just graphs.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>histogram_quantile</code> needs sensible bucket boundaries;</strong> bad buckets give meaningless p95s. Put extra buckets near your latency SLO.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl top</code> failing</strong> usually means metrics-server isn't installed/Ready — not a Prometheus problem. Don't reach for Prometheus to debug a metrics-server outage.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>rate()</code> on a GAUGE is nonsense</strong> — <code>rate()</code> is for monotonic COUNTERS only; likewise don't <code>sum()</code> a gauge across pods blindly (that's a sum of percentages, meaningless).</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting the <code>for:</code></strong> on an alert makes it flap on every transient spike; too long a <code>for:</code> delays paging on real outages — tune it deliberately, don't leave it default.</li>
                    <li><strong>A ServiceMonitor with the wrong <code>release:</code> label</strong> silently scrapes nothing — the Prometheus CR's <code>serviceMonitorSelector</code> (set by the <code>kube-prometheus-stack</code> chart to require that label by default) has to match it; no error is raised, the dashboard just stays empty.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
