                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Design an SLO-based alert for easypj: define the SLO, write the PromQL, and add a recording rule so the dashboard and the alert never disagree.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">groups:
  - name: easypj.slo
    rules:
      - record: easypj:request_error_ratio:rate5m
        expr: |
          sum(rate(http_requests_total{app="easypj",status=~"5.."}[5m]))
            / sum(rate(http_requests_total{app="easypj"}[5m]))
      - alert: EasypjHighErrorRate
        expr: easypj:request_error_ratio:rate5m &gt; 0.01
        for: 5m
        labels: {severity: page}
        annotations:
          summary: "easypj 5xx error rate above 1% for 5m"</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><strong>SLO:</strong> 99% success rate → 1% error budget. Both the Grafana panel AND the alert reference <code>easypj:request_error_ratio:rate5m</code> — the SAME recording rule — so they can never silently disagree about the current error ratio, even if someone edits one query later.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Two easypj alerts fire at once: <code>EasypjHighErrorRate</code> and <code>EasypjPodCrashLooping</code> for the same pod. Without Alertmanager grouping, on-call gets two separate pages. How does Alertmanager fix this, and what's the config idea?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">route:
  group_by: ['alertname', 'namespace']
  group_wait: 30s
  group_interval: 5m
  repeat_interval: 4h
  routes:
    - match: {severity: page}
      receiver: pagerduty
inhibit_rules:
  - source_match: {alertname: 'EasypjPodCrashLooping'}
    target_match: {alertname: 'EasypjHighErrorRate'}
    equal: ['namespace']</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><strong>Grouping</strong> (<code>group_by</code>) bundles related alerts into ONE notification instead of one-per-alert. <strong>Inhibition</strong> goes further: if the crashloop alert is already firing, the resulting error-rate alert is EXPECTED and gets muted — the on-call engineer sees one root-cause page, not a flood of downstream symptoms.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Compute the burn rate for easypj: SLO is 99.9% (30-day window), and over the last hour the error ratio has been 1.5%. Should this page immediately?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">error budget = 1 − 0.999 = 0.001 (0.1%)
observed error ratio (1h window) = 0.015 (1.5%)

burn rate = observed / budget = 0.015 / 0.001 = 15×

15× ≈ the 14.4× "page now" threshold (exhausts the 30-day budget in ~2 days
                                        if it keeps burning at this rate)
→ YES, this should page immediately.</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Burn rate = how many times FASTER than sustainable you're consuming the budget. A rate of 1× exactly exhausts it in exactly 30 days; 15× would exhaust it in roughly 30 ÷ 15 = 2 days if sustained. That's fast enough to page immediately — but only after confirming over BOTH a long window (1h) AND a short window (5m), which is exactly why real rules require both to agree before paging, so a single noisy minute doesn't trigger it alone.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A developer adds <code>user_id</code> as a label on a new easypj metric to "make debugging easier." Two days later Prometheus is OOMKilled. Diagnose and fix.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Diagnose — check series count and find the worst offender</span>
<span class="comment"># PromQL in Prometheus's own Explore view:</span>
<span class="comment">#   topk(10, count by (__name__)({__name__=~".+"}))</span>
<span class="comment"># reveals one metric name with millions of series — the user_id-labeled one</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix — drop the dangerous label AT SCRAPE TIME via the ServiceMonitor</span>
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: ServiceMonitor
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  endpoints:
    - port: web
      path: /metrics
      metricRelabelings:
        - sourceLabels: [user_id]
          action: labeldrop</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Total series ≈ metric names × the product of every label's distinct value count — an unbounded label like <code>user_id</code> multiplies series without limit and eventually OOMs the TSDB. The real fix is at the SOURCE (stop emitting the label in the app) but <code>metricRelabelings</code> / <code>labeldrop</code> is the immediate, no-redeploy mitigation. See Under the Hood ②.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
