                <p>A cluster is a <strong>shipping container</strong>; pods are <strong>boxes</strong>; a pod's resource <strong>request</strong> is the box's <strong>dimensions</strong>, written on the side so the yard crew knows how to stack it. <strong>Capacity planning</strong> is deciding how many containers to rent and how to stack the boxes inside them. <strong>Cost</strong> is the rental bill for every container you're paying for, full or not. Lie about a box's dimensions — say it's smaller than it really is — and it doesn't fit where the crew placed it (an <strong>eviction</strong>). Say it's bigger than it really is, "just to be safe," and you ship half-empty containers across the ocean at full price (<strong>wasted spend</strong>). A <strong>fleet</strong> is simply a whole yard of these containers — a dev yard, a staging yard, a production yard, maybe one per region — and nobody sane manages a real shipping yard by walking to every container by hand. They work from one master manifest and let dispatchers reconcile it. That master manifest, for a Kubernetes fleet, is a Git repository.</p>
