            <section class="section-block" id="s44-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">44.1 Multi-Cluster Patterns</h3>
                <p>One cluster is the exam. Production is a <strong>fleet</strong>. The first design decision a platform team makes is what each cluster boundary actually <em>buys</em> you — because a cluster boundary is the strongest isolation Kubernetes offers, far stronger than a namespace, and it isn't free: every extra cluster is another control plane to upgrade, another etcd to back up, another set of RBAC and NetworkPolicies to maintain.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Fleet</h5>
                    <p>A <strong>fleet</strong> is a managed set of clusters treated as <em>one logical estate</em>, configured from a single source of truth (Git) rather than by hand, cluster by cluster. The word matters: you don't operate "17 clusters," you operate "one fleet of 17" — the distinction is whether a human clicks through each one, or a control plane reconciles all of them from the same declared state.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Three Dominant Patterns (Often Combined)</h4>
                <p>Real platforms rarely pick just one. A company might run per-environment clusters (dev/stage/prod) <em>and</em> per-region clusters within prod <em>and</em> a dedicated per-tenant cluster for one demanding enterprise customer — all at once. Each pattern answers a different question about <em>why</em> you're splitting:</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌗</div>
                        <h5>Per-Environment</h5>
                        <p><strong>Driver:</strong> blast-radius containment + independent upgrade cycles.</p>
                        <p><strong>easypj:</strong> break <code>dev</code> freely — test a risky Django migration, crash the DB, wipe a namespace — and <code>prod</code> never feels it. Upgrade the <code>dev</code> control plane to a new Kubernetes minor version weeks before touching <code>prod</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌍</div>
                        <h5>Per-Region</h5>
                        <p><strong>Driver:</strong> latency (locality) + disaster recovery.</p>
                        <p><strong>easypj:</strong> serve job listings from <code>us-east</code>, <code>eu-west</code>, and <code>ap-south</code> clusters so users get a nearby, fast <code>/jobs/</code> — and if one region has an outage, the others keep serving traffic.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏢</div>
                        <h5>Per-Tenant</h5>
                        <p><strong>Driver:</strong> hard isolation + compliance + a clean cost boundary.</p>
                        <p><strong>easypj:</strong> a large enterprise customer's contract demands a <em>dedicated</em> control plane — no shared etcd, no shared API server — so a noisy or compromised neighbor can never touch their data.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>🧭 Decision Guide — Separate CLUSTER vs Separate NAMESPACE?</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Choose…</th><th>When…</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>A <strong>namespace</strong></td><td>Soft isolation is enough: a shared control plane is fine, you want RBAC/quota/NetworkPolicy boundaries within one team's estate, and you accept a shared blast radius (one noisy neighbor's control-plane load can still touch you).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A <strong>cluster</strong></td><td>You need a <em>hard</em> boundary: separate control planes / blast radius, independent upgrade timing, regulatory isolation, or a clean per-tenant cost &amp; security line that a shared control plane can never fully guarantee.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Insight — Why a Namespace Isn't Enough for Hard Isolation</h5>
                    <p>A namespace shares <em>one</em> API server, <em>one</em> etcd, <em>one</em> scheduler with every other namespace in the cluster. A noisy or compromised tenant in a namespace can still affect neighbors through that shared control plane and shared nodes — a runaway controller hammering the API server slows down <em>everyone's</em> <code>kubectl</code>, and a kernel-level container-escape bug threatens every namespace on the box. A separate cluster gives each tenant its own control plane — true blast-radius separation — at the real cost of more clusters to operate, patch, and back up.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Fleet Management — GitOps + a Control Plane</h4>
                <p>You do <strong>not</strong> click through 30 clusters by hand, applying the same YAML 30 times and hoping you didn't fat-finger cluster #17. You declare desired state once, in Git, and let a fleet control plane reconcile <em>every</em> cluster to it — the exact same reconciliation idea from Chapter 1's control loop, just aimed at a whole fleet instead of one cluster's objects.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Fleet = GitOps + a control plane, reconciling easypj into every cluster</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
        ┌─────────────┐   reconciles    ┌──────────────────────────────┐
        │  Git repo   │  ─────────────► │  cluster: dev   (easypj v1)   │
        │ (desired    │                 ├──────────────────────────────┤
        │  state for  │  ─────────────► │  cluster: stage (easypj v1)   │
        │  the whole  │                 ├──────────────────────────────┤
        │  fleet)     │  ─────────────► │  cluster: prod  (easypj v1)   │
        └─────────────┘                 └──────────────────────────────┘
               ▲
               │ Argo CD / Flux / Rancher Fleet pull &amp; apply per cluster
     Kustomize OVERLAYS hold per-env differences (replicas / requests / env vars)

   base/                       overlays/prod/                overlays/dev/
   ├── deployment.yaml         ├── kustomization.yaml         ├── kustomization.yaml
   ├── service.yaml            │   patches: replicas: 6       │   patches: replicas: 1
   └── kustomization.yaml      │            requests: 500m    │            requests: 50m
                                └── (references ../../base)    └── (references ../../base)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🔧 Under the Hood — How Fleet Tools Actually Reconcile</h5>
                    <p>Tools like <strong>Argo CD</strong>, <strong>Flux</strong>, and <strong>Rancher Fleet</strong> register each target cluster's API server credentials, then run the same OBSERVE → DIFF → ACT loop from Chapter 1 — just pointed at a Git repository instead of a single object. They continuously diff the <em>live</em> state of each registered cluster against the <em>desired</em> state in Git, and re-apply anything that has drifted. The <strong>same base manifests</strong> — the easypj Deployment, Service, Ingress, and the JobPost/Author/Location/Skills schema migration Job — are shared across every cluster; <strong>Kustomize overlays</strong> inject only the per-environment <em>differences</em>. Prod gets more replicas and bigger requests; dev gets one replica and tiny requests. Nobody hand-edits YAML per cluster — they edit one overlay file and let the fleet tool push it everywhere that overlay applies.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip mb-0">
                    <h5>⚡ Speed Tip</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">Keep <strong>one base</strong> and <strong>per-env Kustomize overlays</strong> so the exact same easypj base scales from a laptop <code>kind</code> cluster to a 200-node production cluster by changing only the overlay — never by forking the manifest.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §44.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s44-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">44.2 Capacity Planning</h3>
                <p>Capacity planning answers one question: <strong>how many nodes, of what size, do I need to run my workloads — with room to spare?</strong> The unit of that math is the resource <strong>request</strong>, because the scheduler places pods by their requests against each node's <strong>allocatable</strong> capacity. This is the single most important sentence in the whole chapter, so it's worth restating precisely: a <strong>limit</strong> caps what a pod is <em>allowed</em> to use; a <strong>request</strong> <em>reserves</em> capacity for it before it ever runs a single instruction. The scheduler has never once looked at a limit when deciding where a pod fits — only the request.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Allocatable</h5>
                    <p>A node's total capacity <em>minus</em> what is reserved for the kubelet, the OS, and eviction thresholds (<code>system-reserved</code> + <code>kube-reserved</code> + <code>eviction-hard</code>). This is the <em>real</em> pool the scheduler can hand out as requests — never the raw <code>kubectl describe node</code> "Capacity" number, which overstates what's actually schedulable.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Bin-Packing</h5>
                    <p>The scheduler's job of fitting pods (boxes sized by their requests) into nodes (bins sized by allocatable) so that as little space as possible is wasted. A perfect bin-packer would use the fewest nodes possible with zero slack; real clusters trade some packing efficiency for resilience (topology spread) and headroom (spare capacity for spikes).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Capacity = Σ requests + headroom, bin-packed</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   Step 1   Sum the REQUESTS of everything you must run:
            Σ(cpu requests)   and   Σ(memory requests)

   Step 2   Add HEADROOM for spikes + node failure (e.g. N+1 — survive losing one node).

   Step 3   Pick node SIZE/TYPE so requests bin-pack to a sane utilization
            target (e.g. ~60–75% of allocatable — not 100%, not 30%).

            nodes ≈  ceil( (Σ requests + headroom) / node allocatable )

   ── worked example: easypj-production ──────────────────────────────────
   easypj-web    ×6 replicas  ×  (cpu 250m, mem 384Mi)  =  1500m, 2304Mi
   celery-worker ×3 replicas  ×  (cpu 500m, mem 512Mi)  =  1500m, 1536Mi
   postgres      ×1 replica   ×  (cpu 1000m, mem 2048Mi) = 1000m, 2048Mi
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Σ requests                                            = 4000m, 5888Mi
   + headroom (N+1, ~30%)                                = 1200m, 1766Mi
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   TOTAL needed                                          ≈ 5200m, 7654Mi

   node type: 4 vCPU / 8GiB, allocatable ≈ 3800m / 6900Mi (after reservations)
   nodes ≈ ceil(5200m / 3800m) = 2   AND   ceil(7654Mi / 6900Mi) = 2  →  take the MAX = 2
   (in practice you'd round up further for real headroom — this is the floor, not the target)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Headroom</h5>
                    <p><strong>Headroom</strong> is spare capacity <em>deliberately</em> reserved so the cluster can absorb a traffic spike or survive losing a node <em>without</em> immediately producing Pending pods. Headroom that accumulates by accident — nobody ever right-sized, so every workload quietly runs 3× oversized "just in case" — is pure waste. Headroom sized on purpose — e.g. explicitly provisioning for N+1 (survive losing one node) — is insurance you chose to buy. Same spare capacity on a graph; very different intent, and only one of them is defensible in a cost review.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Gotcha — Size by the BINDING Resource, Not Just One</h5>
                    <p>Notice the worked example computes node count from <em>both</em> CPU and memory and takes the larger result. A workload can be CPU-bound on one node type and memory-bound on another — easypj's <code>celery-worker</code> pods might be memory-hungry while <code>easypj-web</code> is CPU-hungry. Always compute both and size to whichever one runs out first; sizing by only one resource silently under-provisions the other.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Reading a Node's Current Allocation</h4>
                <p>Before adding any new workload, read how full a node's bin already is. This single command block is the fastest capacity sanity-check available on any cluster, exam or production:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title"><code>kubectl describe node</code> — "Allocated resources"</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Allocated resources:
    (Total limits may be over 100 percent, i.e. overcommitted.)
    Resource           Requests        Limits
    --------           --------        ------
    cpu                1450m (72%)     3200m (160%)
    memory             2400Mi (60%)    4096Mi (102%)

  ◄ Requests % = how full the bin is for SCHEDULING — a new pod needing
    600m CPU will NOT schedule here even though usage might be near-zero,
    because the scheduler only ever looks at requests already reserved.
  ◄ Limits over 100% = overcommit. Fine (even healthy) for CPU, because CPU
    is compressible — a pod just gets throttled under contention. DANGEROUS
    for memory, which is incompressible — contention there means OOMKills.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Gotcha — Pods Without Requests Are Invisible to This Math</h5>
                    <p>A pod with <strong>no</strong> resource requests is treated by the scheduler as needing <em>almost nothing</em> — it can land on a node the "Allocated resources" table already calls 90% full, because 90% full by requests still has room for a request of effectively zero. That pod then wrecks both bin-packing <em>and</em> cost accounting: it packs onto a node that's genuinely full of real workloads, and at runtime it fights those workloads for the actual CPU/memory that was never reserved for it. <strong>Always set requests</strong> — a Deployment with no requests is not "flexible," it's a scheduling landmine.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🔬 Deeper — Topology-Aware Bin-Packing and Just-In-Time Provisioning</h4>
                <p>A naive bin-packer minimizes node count above everything else. But you often want pods <strong>spread</strong> across zones for availability — <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> — which deliberately trades a little packing efficiency for resilience: if a whole zone goes dark, you don't lose every replica of <code>easypj-web</code> at once. Modern provisioners like <strong>Karpenter</strong> take this further with "just-in-time" node provisioning: instead of picking from a handful of pre-defined node-group sizes, they look at the <em>actual shape</em> of currently-Pending pods and launch a node sized to fit them — then <em>consolidate</em> (repack pods onto fewer nodes and retire the emptied ones) automatically as usage drops. This section's Under the Hood block (§44's closing block, subsections ① and ②) goes deep on both mechanisms.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Optimizes for</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Naive bin-packing</td><td>Fewest nodes possible</td><td>All replicas can land on one zone/node — a single failure takes out everything</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Topology-spread bin-packing</td><td>Fewest nodes <em>while</em> spreading across zones/hosts</td><td>Slightly more nodes than the naive minimum, in exchange for real resilience</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Just-in-time provisioning (Karpenter)</td><td>Right-sized nodes for the <em>actual</em> Pending pods, provisioned on demand</td><td>Requires cloud API access to launch instances directly — more moving parts than a fixed node group</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §44.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s44-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">44.3 Right-Sizing &amp; Cost</h3>
                <p>Requests are the unit of <strong>both</strong> scheduling and cost. That single overlap is the whole cost game: keep requests close to real usage, and keep nodes busy. Every dollar of cloud spend traces back to a node that exists because <em>something's</em> requests said it needed to.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Requests = the unit of both scheduling AND cost</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   requests too HIGH ─►  poor bin-packing, idle $$$      (over-provisioned)
   requests too LOW  ─►  evictions, noisy neighbors       (under-provisioned)

              🧭 right-size from REAL usage
                 (kubectl top  /  VPA recommendations)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Right-Sizing Method</h4>
                <p>Compare declared requests against <em>measured</em> usage and move requests toward reality. This is the single highest-leverage cost exercise on any cluster, and it takes under five minutes per workload:</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">right-size easypj-web</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. What is easypj-web actually using?</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj-production -l app=easypj-web
<span class="comment"># NAME                       CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-7d9c-abcde      120m         180Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. What did we DECLARE it needs?</span>
kubectl get deploy easypj-web -n easypj-production \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}'
<span class="comment"># {"requests":{"cpu":"500m","memory":"512Mi"},"limits":{"cpu":"1","memory":"1Gi"}}</span>

<span class="comment"># declared requests:  cpu 500m  ·  memory 512Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># measured usage:     cpu 120m  ·  memory 180Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># → OVER-PROVISIONED: paying for 500m, using 120m</span>
<span class="comment"># new requests ≈ usage + buffer:  cpu 200m  ·  memory 256Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># (keep a sane limit; don't pin requests BELOW real steady-state usage)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>⚠️ Symptoms of OVER-Provisioning</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>🔴 Low node utilization across the fleet</li>
                            <li>🔴 Requests <code>&gt;&gt;</code> usage in every <code>kubectl top</code> vs <code>describe</code> comparison</li>
                            <li>🔴 Idle nodes the Cluster Autoscaler <em>cannot</em> remove, because the reserved requests still "look used" to it</li>
                            <li>🔴 You pay for capacity you never touch, month after month</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>⚠️ Symptoms of UNDER-Provisioning</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>🔴 OOMKills and pod evictions under normal load</li>
                            <li>🔴 Noisy neighbors stealing CPU from a pod that under-declared its need</li>
                            <li>🔴 Pods stuck <code>Pending</code> the moment real load arrives</li>
                            <li>🔴 Cheap on paper, expensive in 3 AM incidents and post-mortems</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Four Cost Levers, in Order of Bang-for-Effort</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The four cost levers</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   1. RIGHT-SIZE requests ......... stop reserving capacity you don't use.
   2. AUTOSCALE ..................... remove idle nodes (Cluster Autoscaler).
   3. SPOT / PREEMPTIBLE nodes ...... much cheaper, but reclaimable anytime.
   4. MEASURE allocation ............ OpenCost / Kubecost map spend to
                                       namespace / team / workload.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Insight — The Lever This List Leaves Out: Committing for Your Baseline</h5>
                    <p>The four levers above all shrink or reshuffle spend on <em>on-demand</em> pricing. Real cloud bills add a fifth move: <strong>commit ahead of time for the load you know won't go away.</strong> Reserved Instances, Savings Plans, and Committed Use Discounts (naming varies by cloud provider) trade a 1–3 year usage commitment for a steep discount — often comparable to spot, but with none of the reclamation risk. The production pattern is "<strong>baseline on commitment, burst on-demand or spot</strong>": look at 90 days of <code>kubectl top nodes</code> / cost-tool history to find the floor your fleet never drops below (easypj-production's steady 6 <code>easypj-web</code> replicas plus <code>postgres</code>, say), commit to that floor, and let the Cluster Autoscaler add uncommitted on-demand or spot capacity only for the variable part above it. Committing for headroom you don't reliably use is the opposite mistake — a locked-in discount on capacity that then sits idle is still money spent, just paid up front instead of monthly.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Spot / Preemptible Nodes</h5>
                    <p>Deeply discounted nodes (often 60–90% cheaper) the cloud provider can <strong>reclaim</strong> with little or no notice — usually a 30-second to 2-minute interruption warning, not a graceful drain on your schedule. Great for interruption-tolerant work; dangerous for anything that must stay continuously up.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🧭 Decision Guide — Can This Workload Run on Spot?</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Answer</th><th>Condition</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>YES</strong>, if…</td><td>it tolerates restarts: stateless <code>easypj-web</code> replicas behind a Service, <code>celery-worker</code> batch/queue processors, CI runners — <em>combined with</em> a PodDisruptionBudget + topology spread so losing a spot node doesn't take the whole Deployment down at once.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>NO</strong>, if…</td><td>it is singleton, stateful, or latency-critical: the easypj PostgreSQL database, leader-elected controllers, anything holding an in-memory session you can't rebuild instantly — keep these on on-demand nodes; use spot only as overflow capacity, never as the primary home.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Gotcha — Spot Nodes Can Vanish With Short Notice</h5>
                    <p>Protect every spot-eligible workload with a <strong>PodDisruptionBudget</strong> and <strong>topology spread</strong>, and keep genuinely critical pods — the database, leader-elected controllers, anything with irreplaceable in-flight state — on on-demand nodes. A spot interruption isn't a bug; it's the entire deal you signed up for by taking the discount, and the platform (not the cloud provider) is responsible for surviving it gracefully.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Insight — Correlated Reclamation: Don't Bet Everything on One Spot Pool</h5>
                    <p>Spot capacity is reclaimed per <strong>pool</strong> — a specific instance type in a specific availability zone. A team that runs all of <code>celery-worker</code> on a single spot pool (say, one instance type in one zone) can lose <em>every</em> replica in the same few minutes when that pool's price spikes or capacity tightens, because the interruptions aren't independent events — they share the same underlying cause. The production fix is <strong>diversification</strong>: spread spot-eligible workloads across several instance types and zones (Karpenter does this automatically by evaluating many types/AZs per launch, exactly as shown in this chapter's Under the Hood ①), so a reclamation wave in one pool only ever costs a fraction of the fleet, never all of it at once.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🔬 Deeper — Cost Allocation with OpenCost / Kubecost</h4>
                <p>These tools read pod requests/usage and node prices, then <strong>allocate</strong> the bill back to namespaces, labels, and teams — turning one opaque cloud invoice into "easypj-production's web tier costs $X/mo, the database costs $Y/mo, the staging environment costs $Z/mo." <strong>OpenCost</strong> is a CNCF <strong>Incubating</strong> open-source project (originally built and donated by the Kubecost team); <strong>Kubecost</strong> is the commercial product built on top of it with extra reporting and governance features. Allocation is what makes "right-size" <em>actionable</em> instead of a vague good intention — you can see exactly which workload is over-provisioned and precisely what fixing it would save, in real currency, not just in millicores. Karpenter's consolidation (§44's Under the Hood, subsection ①) is the automated complement to this measurement — it continuously repacks pods onto fewer nodes and retires the empties to cut idle spend without a human running a report first.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it is</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>OpenCost</strong></td><td>CNCF Incubating open-source cost-allocation engine</td><td>Teams that want the raw allocation data, self-hosted, no license cost</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Kubecost</strong></td><td>Commercial product built on OpenCost</td><td>Teams that want dashboards, budgets/alerts, and governance out of the box</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §44.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s44-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">44.4 Autoscaling Layers</h3>
                <p>Three autoscalers operate on <strong>three different axes</strong>. They are not rivals competing for the same job — they are layers, meant to be used <em>together</em>. Confusing them, or letting two of them act on the same signal, is the single classic mistake in this whole chapter.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Three autoscalers, three different axes</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   📈 HPA  ─ more/fewer PODS          (on CPU / memory / custom metrics)
   📐 VPA  ─ bigger/smaller REQUESTS  (right-size one pod's own resources)
   🖥️ Cluster Autoscaler ─ more/fewer NODES  (when pods can't fit anywhere)

   easypj-web:  HPA scales 3→10 replicas on CPU · VPA tunes each pod's
                requests · Cluster Autoscaler adds nodes if the new pods
                from HPA's scale-up go Pending for lack of room.

   ⚠️ HPA + VPA acting on the SAME metric conflict — split their signals.
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Three Layers Chain Together Under a Traffic Surge</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj-web under a traffic surge — the three layers in motion</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   load rises ─► CPU per pod climbs
        │
        ▼
   📈 HPA: scale replicas 3 → 10   (target CPU utilization against requests)
        │
        ▼  new pods may not FIT on the CURRENT nodes
   🖥️ Cluster Autoscaler: pods sit Pending → ADD a node → pods schedule
        │
        ▼  (separately, over time, on steady-state usage — not tied to this spike)
   📐 VPA recommender: "your requests are 500m but you actually use 120m" →
        right-sizes requests so the HPA/CA math above stays HONEST
        │
   load drops ─► HPA scales replicas back down ─► nodes sit idle ─►
   🖥️ Cluster Autoscaler removes the now-empty node  (saves $$$)
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Autoscaler</th><th>Axis it controls</th><th>Reacts to</th><th>easypj example</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>📈 HPA</td><td>Pod <strong>count</strong></td><td>CPU / memory / custom metrics (requests-per-second, queue depth)</td><td>3 → 10 <code>easypj-web</code> replicas on CPU utilization</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>📐 VPA</td><td>Request <strong>size</strong></td><td>Historical usage, via the recommender</td><td>Suggests <code>easypj-web</code> drop from 500m to 200m CPU requests</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🖥️ Cluster Autoscaler</td><td>Node <strong>count</strong></td><td>Pending pods (scale up) / empty nodes (scale down)</td><td>Adds a node when HPA's new replicas can't schedule</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Production War Story — "Why Won't the Cluster Autoscaler Remove This Idle Node?"</h5>
                    <p>Right-sizing worked, the node shows 8% CPU usage on <code>kubectl top nodes</code>, and yet it sits there for hours, refusing to scale down. The Cluster Autoscaler will silently refuse to remove a node if <em>any</em> pod on it hits one of these blockers — and it rarely tells you which one:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Blocker</th><th>Why it stops scale-down</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Pod with local storage (<code>emptyDir</code>, <code>hostPath</code>)</td><td>The Autoscaler treats losing that data as unsafe by default</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A bare Pod with no owning controller</td><td>No Deployment/ReplicaSet means it can never be rescheduled elsewhere if evicted</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A PodDisruptionBudget that would be violated</td><td>Same protection meant for voluntary drains — it also blocks CA's node removal</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>cluster-autoscaler.kubernetes.io/safe-to-evict: "false"</code> annotation</td><td>An explicit opt-out some workloads (e.g. certain monitoring agents) set on purpose</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A <code>kube-system</code> pod without a matching PDB</td><td>CA is conservative around system pods it doesn't recognize as safely rescheduled</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">The fix is almost never "force it" — it's finding <em>which</em> pod on that specific node is the blocker (<code>kubectl describe node</code> plus the CA's own logs, which name the blocking pod explicitly) and deciding deliberately: move that workload's storage off <code>hostPath</code>, add a PDB, or accept the node is pinned and stop counting it as reclaimable idle capacity.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 The Conflict — HPA vs VPA on the SAME Signal</h5>
                    <p>HPA scales pod <strong>count</strong> based on (say) CPU utilization measured against requests. VPA <strong>changes those requests</strong>. If both act on CPU at once: VPA shrinks the requests → the utilization percentage HPA computes spikes (same usage, smaller denominator) → HPA adds more pods → VPA, seeing steady-state usage per pod drop further, shrinks requests again — and the two <strong>oscillate</strong> forever, neither one ever settling.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🧭 How to Avoid It</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Strategy</th><th>How it works</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>VPA in recommend-only mode (safest, most common)</strong></td><td>Set <code>updateMode: Off</code>. VPA computes and shows target requests but never applies them; a human (or a CI pipeline) reviews and applies the suggestion on its own schedule. HPA handles all live scaling.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Split signals</strong></td><td>HPA scales on a <em>custom</em> metric (requests-per-second, queue depth) that VPA never touches, while VPA owns CPU/memory requests in <code>Recreate</code> or <code>InPlaceOrRecreate</code> mode. Neither one's action changes the other's input.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Never do this</strong></td><td>Run VPA in an applying mode (<code>Recreate</code>/<code>InPlaceOrRecreate</code>, formerly called <code>Auto</code>) <em>and</em> HPA on the same CPU or memory metric, on the same workload, at the same time.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Insight — Where a "Custom Metric" Like Queue Depth Actually Comes From: KEDA</h5>
                    <p>The split-signals row above says "HPA scales on requests-per-second or queue depth" as if that's simple — in practice, the HPA's <code>autoscaling/v2</code> API only understands Resource metrics (CPU/memory) and External/Object metrics served through the <strong>Custom Metrics API</strong>, and wiring a real external signal like "messages waiting in a queue" into that API by hand is real plumbing. <strong>KEDA</strong> (Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling, a CNCF-graduated project) is the standard way production clusters close that gap: it ships 60+ pre-built <em>scalers</em> for sources like Redis, RabbitMQ, Kafka, PostgreSQL, and cron schedules, and under the hood it simply creates and manages an HPA object for you, feeding it the external metric. For easypj, a <code>ScaledObject</code> pointed at <code>celery-worker</code>'s Redis queue length is the realistic way to scale worker replicas on <em>actual backlog</em> instead of CPU — CPU can stay low even while a queue backs up if jobs are I/O-bound, which is exactly the blind spot a queue-depth signal fixes.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>🩺 Troubleshooting — "HPA Shows Targets &lt;unknown&gt; and Won't Scale"</h4>
                <p>This is one of the most common real-world autoscaling failures, and it has exactly two usual suspects — the same missing-requests root cause Ch 13 §13.3 first introduced, revisited here alongside its metrics-server sibling and extended one layer further into the Cluster Autoscaler. Work through them in this order:</p>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Cause</th><th>How to confirm</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>metrics-server missing or unhealthy</td><td><code>kubectl top pods</code> — if this itself errors, metrics-server is the problem, not the HPA</td><td>Check <code>kubectl get deploy metrics-server -n kube-system</code>; look at its logs for the classic self-signed-cert issue on kubelet TLS</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>No resource requests on the container</td><td><code>kubectl get deploy easypj-web -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}'</code> — empty or missing <code>requests</code></td><td>Set <code>resources.requests.cpu</code> — HPA computes utilization as a <em>percentage of requests</em>, and with no request there's no denominator to compute against</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0">
                    <h5>⚠️ Gotcha — A Third, Rarer Cause</h5>
                    <p class="mb-0">If <code>kubectl top pods</code> works and requests are set but the HPA <em>still</em> won't add nodes when it scales pod count up, the problem has moved one layer down: check that the Cluster Autoscaler isn't blocked by a node group already at its configured max size, and that the new Pending pods are genuinely unschedulable for lack of capacity — not blocked by a taint, node affinity, or PodDisruptionBudget that has nothing to do with capacity at all.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch44-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how requests actually drive both the scheduler and the bill</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Every section above leaned on one claim: <strong>requests drive both scheduling and cost.</strong> Here's precisely why. The scheduler's <strong>filter</strong> phase eliminates any node whose allocatable, minus the sum of requests already reserved on it, cannot fit this pod's requests — and the <strong>score</strong> phase then ranks the surviving nodes (by default, generally preferring the <em>least</em>-utilized ones, spreading load). So inflated requests fragment your bins into idle, paid-for capacity that nothing can use, and tiny requests overcommit the node, risking eviction under real contention. Node utilization is simply <code>Σ requests / allocatable</code> — the entire cost game is pushing that ratio to a healthy target without crossing into eviction territory.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   THE SCHEDULER'S FILTER PHASE — requests vs allocatable, per node
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  node-2   allocatable: 3800m cpu / 6900Mi mem                  │
   │  already reserved (existing pods' requests): 3400m / 6200Mi    │
   │  remaining room:                              400m / 700Mi     │
   │                                                                │
   │  new pod requests: cpu 250m, mem 384Mi   →  FITS  ✅            │
   │  new pod requests: cpu 500m, mem 384Mi   →  cpu doesn't fit ❌   │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   The scheduler NEVER looks at current USAGE here — only requests already
   reserved vs allocatable. A node "full" of idle, over-requested pods looks
   exactly as full to the scheduler as one genuinely saturated with real load.
                    </pre>

                    <h5>① Karpenter Just-In-Time Provisioning &amp; Consolidation</h5>
                    <p>A fixed node group forces a choice made in advance: pick one or two instance sizes and hope Pending pods fit them well. <strong>Karpenter</strong> (and similar just-in-time provisioners) skip that guess entirely — they watch for Pending pods, read their <em>actual</em> requests, and launch a node <em>shaped</em> to fit them, right then.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   TRADITIONAL NODE GROUP                    KARPENTER (just-in-time)
   ┌──────────────────────────┐             ┌───────────────────────────────┐
   │ fixed pool of, say,       │             │ pod goes Pending: needs        │
   │ m5.xlarge instances       │             │   cpu 1800m, mem 3Gi           │
   │                            │             │      │                        │
   │ pod needs cpu 1800m/3Gi   │             │      ▼ picks the CHEAPEST      │
   │ → scales the WHOLE group  │             │   instance type that fits      │
   │   by one m5.xlarge, even  │             │   (maybe a c5.xlarge, maybe    │
   │   if only 40% is used     │             │   a spot m5a.xlarge — Karpenter│
   └──────────────────────────┘             │   compares many types/AZs)     │
                                             └───────────────────────────────┘
   CONSOLIDATION (both, but automated only by Karpenter):
      usage drops → Karpenter finds it can move pods off 2 half-empty nodes
      onto 1 more-full node → drains &amp; terminates the emptied nodes
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Fixed node group</th><th>Karpenter (just-in-time)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Instance selection</td><td>Pre-chosen sizes, decided in advance</td><td>Chosen per Pending pod's actual shape, at launch time</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Idle capacity</td><td>Common — a whole instance added for one pod</td><td>Minimized — the node is sized to the workload</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Consolidation</td><td>Manual / scripted, if done at all</td><td>Continuous and automatic</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> Karpenter is not a replacement for the Cluster Autoscaler's <em>concept</em> — it still adds nodes when pods are Pending and removes them when idle — it's a more efficient <em>implementation</em> of that same idea, because it provisions per-pod-shape instead of per-fixed-group. Both ultimately react to the same signal this chapter has repeated all along: unschedulable Pending pods. §36.6 first introduced this Cluster-Autoscaler-vs-Karpenter distinction at the concept level; this deep dive adds the piece that section didn't cover — automatic consolidation.</div>

                    <h5>② Topology-Aware Bin-Packing — Spread vs Pack</h5>
                    <p>A pure bin-packer's goal is fewest nodes. But <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> asks it to also respect zone/host boundaries — a real trade-off between efficiency and survivability that every capacity plan for easypj-production must make deliberately, not by accident.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ❌ PURE BIN-PACKING (fewest nodes, ignores zones)
   zone-a: [easypj-web-1] [easypj-web-2] [easypj-web-3] [easypj-web-4]
   zone-b: (empty — nothing scheduled here, wasted zone redundancy)
   → lose zone-a → ALL 4 replicas gone at once 💥

   ✅ TOPOLOGY-SPREAD BIN-PACKING (topologySpreadConstraints: zone, maxSkew: 1)
   zone-a: [easypj-web-1] [easypj-web-2]
   zone-b: [easypj-web-3] [easypj-web-4]
   → lose zone-a → 2 of 4 replicas survive, Service still serves traffic ✅
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> unset</td><td>Scheduler is free to pack all replicas into one zone/node if that's most efficient — cheapest, least resilient</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>maxSkew: 1</code>, <code>whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule</code></td><td>Hard requirement: replica count between zones may differ by at most 1 — resilient, may leave a pod Pending if perfectly even placement isn't possible</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>whenUnsatisfiable: ScheduleAnyway</code></td><td>Best-effort spread — prefers even distribution but still schedules the pod somewhere if it can't be perfectly even</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Spread costs a little packing efficiency on purpose.</strong> Every zone-spread replica set uses slightly more total capacity than a perfectly packed one — that's not waste, it's the insurance premium for surviving a zone outage. Size capacity planning (§44.2) with this constraint <em>already applied</em>, not as an afterthought.</div>

                    <h5>③ Spot / Preemptible Interruption Handling</h5>
                    <p>"Spot nodes can vanish" is easy to say and easy to get wrong in practice. The real mechanism is a short-lived interruption <em>notice</em>, and the platform's job is to react to it before the hard deadline, not after.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   CLOUD PROVIDER'S SPOT RECLAMATION SEQUENCE
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  T-120s (or less, provider-dependent): interruption notice      │
   │           published (instance metadata endpoint / EventBridge)  │
   │                          │                                      │
   │                          ▼                                      │
   │  node-termination-handler (DaemonSet) SEES the notice:           │
   │    1. cordon the node  (no new pods scheduled here)               │
   │    2. drain gracefully (respects PodDisruptionBudgets!)           │
   │    3. new replicas scheduled elsewhere BEFORE the hard cutoff     │
   │                          │                                      │
   │  T-0: instance reclaimed, whatever wasn't drained is just gone   │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   Without a PDB: drain can evict ALL easypj-web replicas on this node
   at once if that's every remaining replica — PDB caps how many can go.
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Cloud interruption notice</td><td>Short warning (often ~2 min) published before reclamation, polled from the instance metadata endpoint</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Node-termination-handler (DaemonSet)</td><td>Watches for the notice, cordons + gracefully drains the node ahead of the hard deadline</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>PodDisruptionBudget</td><td>Caps how many replicas of a Deployment can be down at once <em>during that drain</em> — without it, a drain can legally evict every replica simultaneously</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 A PDB alone does nothing without something to respect it.</strong> The PDB is a promise ("never take down more than N at once"); the node-termination-handler + graceful drain is the mechanism that actually <em>honors</em> that promise during a spot reclamation. Ship both together, or the PDB is decoration.</div>

                    <h5>④ VPA Internals — Recommender, Updater &amp; Admission Controller</h5>
                    <p>The VPA is not one component — it's three, each with a distinct job, and understanding the split explains exactly why the classic eviction-based VPA mode is disruptive and why recommend-only mode is so popular.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   VPA — THREE COOPERATING PIECES
   ┌───────────────┐    watches historical      ┌──────────────────────┐
   │ 📊 RECOMMENDER │───usage, emits target/────▶│ VerticalPodAutoscaler │
   │ (reads metrics)│    lowerBound/upperBound   │ .status.recommendation│
   └───────────────┘                            └──────────┬─────────┘
                                                             │  (if updateMode: Recreate)
                            ┌────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┐
                            ▼                                                  ▼
                  ┌──────────────────┐                          ┌──────────────────────┐
                  │ 🔄 UPDATER        │  evicts a pod running    │ 🧬 ADMISSION CONTROLLER│
                  │ decides WHEN to   │  outdated requests  ────▶│ intercepts the         │
                  │ apply a new size  │                          │ RECREATED pod, injects │
                  └──────────────────┘                          │ the new requests        │
                                                                 └──────────────────────┘
   updateMode: "Off"              → Recommender only, nothing auto-applied (recommend-only)
   updateMode: "Initial"          → applied ONLY at pod creation, never mid-life
   updateMode: "Recreate"         → Updater evicts + Admission Controller resizes on recreate
   updateMode: "InPlaceOrRecreate" → tries an in-place resize first, falls back to Recreate
   updateMode: "InPlace" (alpha)  → in-place only, NEVER falls back to eviction
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th><code>updateMode</code></th><th>Behavior</th><th>Disruptive?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>Off</code></td><td>Recommender computes targets; nothing is ever auto-applied — you read <code>.status.recommendation</code> and act by hand</td><td>No — safest, most common alongside HPA</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Initial</code></td><td>New requests only take effect for pods created <em>after</em> the recommendation — existing pods untouched</td><td>No pod eviction, but slow to apply</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>Recreate</code></td><td>Updater evicts running pods so they get recreated with the new (admission-injected) requests</td><td>Yes — a full pod restart, disruptive to a live workload</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>InPlaceOrRecreate</code></td><td>Updater resizes CPU/memory on the <em>running</em> pod using the in-place resize API first; only evicts if the in-place attempt isn't possible for that change</td><td>Usually no — falls back to eviction only when it must</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="info-box note">
                        <h5>📖 Key Term — In-Place Pod Resize</h5>
                        <p>Kubernetes gained a native <code>resize</code> subresource that lets the kubelet change a running container's CPU/memory requests and limits <em>without</em> tearing the pod down — no new Pod UID, no reschedule. This primitive (feature gate <code>InPlacePodVerticalScaling</code>) graduated to <strong>stable, on by default</strong> in Kubernetes v1.35. It's what VPA's newer <code>InPlaceOrRecreate</code> mode and alpha <code>InPlace</code> mode are built on. Two practical caveats: not every resize can be done in place (some changes still force a restart, which is exactly why <code>InPlaceOrRecreate</code> keeps eviction as a fallback), and <code>InPlace</code> mode alone is still alpha — most production clusters are not yet running VPA with it as the sole mechanism.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <h5>⚠️ Naming Note — "Auto" Mode Is Deprecated</h5>
                        <p>Older VPA tutorials (and some still-running production YAML) use <code>updateMode: "Auto"</code> — this name is <strong>deprecated as of VPA 1.4.0</strong> and is now just an alias for <code>Recreate</code>, with identical (eviction-based) behavior. If you see <code>Auto</code> in existing manifests it still works exactly as this section describes for <code>Recreate</code>; write new manifests with the explicit <code>Recreate</code> or <code>InPlaceOrRecreate</code> name instead.</p>
                    </div>
                    <p>One more practical wrinkle: the recommender builds its target from a rolling <strong>history window</strong> (commonly several days), not the last five minutes. That's deliberate — it smooths out normal spikes — but it means the recommendation <em>lags</em> behind a real, permanent shift. If easypj-web's traffic genuinely doubles this week and stays there, don't expect the VPA recommendation to reflect that on day one; it takes time to accumulate enough history to trust the new baseline. Treat a fresh recommendation right after a real usage-pattern change with a healthy amount of skepticism, and re-check it again in a few days before acting on it.</p>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Recommend-only is still the dominant real-world pattern, even though in-place resize now exists.</strong> The <code>Recreate</code> mode (formerly called "Auto") is inherently disruptive because it evicts to resize. The newer <code>InPlaceOrRecreate</code> mode narrows that gap significantly by resizing live containers whenever the kubelet can, but it's newer and its fallback still evicts sometimes. Until in-place resize is uniformly available and battle-tested everywhere you run, <code>updateMode: Off</code> paired with a live HPA remains the safest default: you get VPA's sizing insight with zero disruption risk, and HPA still owns all live scaling.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ Cost Allocation Internals — How OpenCost/Kubecost Attribute Spend</h5>
                    <p>"It reads requests/usage and node prices" hides a real allocation algorithm. Shared costs (a node running pods from three different teams) can't just be split evenly — they need to be apportioned by actual consumption.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ONE NODE, THREE TENANTS — how the bill gets split
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  node-7   list price: $140/month                                │
   │  ┌────────────────┐ ┌────────────────┐ ┌────────────────┐       │
   │  │ easypj-web       │ │ easypj-worker    │ │ other-team-app  │       │
   │  │ requests: 40%    │ │ requests: 35%    │ │ requests: 25%    │       │
   │  └────────────────┘ └────────────────┘ └────────────────┘       │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
        allocated cost:      $56/mo             $49/mo            $35/mo
   (allocation is by REQUESTS, not usage — a pod that requests 40% of the
    node pays for 40% of the node, whether it uses 5% or 100% of that request)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Allocation basis</th><th>Why requests, not usage</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Requests</td><td>Requests are what <em>reserved</em> the capacity — the scheduler placed this pod because of its request, so it's fair to bill it for what it reserved, not just what it happened to use this hour</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Usage (shown alongside, not billed on)</td><td>Displayed for context — "you're paying for 500m but using 120m" is exactly the right-sizing signal from §44.3, now denominated in real currency</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>The worked example above conveniently sums to exactly 100% — real clusters rarely do. When requests only fill, say, 80% of a node's allocatable, OpenCost/Kubecost don't just silently split that leftover 20% across the tenants who happen to be present; they report it as its own line item, and a second one alongside it for infrastructure that serves everyone at once:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Cost bucket</th><th>What it captures</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Idle cost</strong></td><td>Allocatable capacity nobody requested — the direct, visible price tag of under-utilization from §44.3, now shown as its own number instead of hiding inside someone else's bill</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Shared / system cost</strong></td><td><code>kube-system</code> pods, cluster networking overhead, the monitoring stack itself — infrastructure that serves every tenant and is typically apportioned evenly or pro-rata, not billed to whichever team happened to be scheduled nearby</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is why right-sizing is directly a cost fix, not just a scheduling nicety.</strong> Since allocation bills by request, shrinking <code>easypj-web</code>'s over-declared request from 500m to 200m doesn't just improve bin-packing — it directly shrinks that workload's line item on next month's cost report. And watching the <em>idle</em> bucket shrink fleet-wide as more teams right-size is the single clearest sign the whole exercise is working, independent of any one team's number.</div>

                    <h5>⑥ Fleet GitOps Internals — Reconciliation Across Many Clusters</h5>
                    <p>§44.1 showed the high-level fleet diagram; here's what a tool like Argo CD is actually doing under that arrow from Git to each cluster, continuously, for every registered target.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ARGO CD's PER-CLUSTER RECONCILE LOOP (runs independently, per cluster)
   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  1. 👁️ OBSERVE   — read the LIVE state of this cluster's easypj    │
   │                    objects via that cluster's own API server      │
   │  2. 📖 READ       — render the DESIRED state: base + this          │
   │                    cluster's Kustomize overlay, from Git           │
   │  3. 🧮 DIFF       — compare live vs desired, object by object       │
   │  4. 🛠️ ACT        — apply ONLY the objects that drifted             │
   │  5. repeat forever, or immediately on a Git webhook push           │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   Run this loop 30 times — once per cluster in the fleet — and you get
   30 independently-reconciled clusters from ONE Git repository, no human
   ever running `kubectl apply` against any of them by hand.
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Failure mode</th><th>What the fleet tool shows you</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Someone <code>kubectl edit</code>'d a live object directly</td><td>"OutOfSync" — the tool detects drift on its next reconcile pass and can auto-heal it back to Git's version</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A cluster is unreachable</td><td>That one cluster's status goes stale/unknown; the other 29 keep reconciling independently — no fleet-wide outage from one dead cluster</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>An overlay has a real error (bad YAML, invalid patch)</td><td>"Degraded" on that specific cluster only — the base + other overlays are unaffected</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 It's the SAME reconciliation pattern as Chapter 1, one level up.</strong> Chapter 1's controllers reconcile one object against etcd; a fleet GitOps tool reconciles one <em>cluster's</em> objects against Git; running it per-cluster reconciles the whole <em>fleet</em>. Same OBSERVE → DIFF → ACT shape, different scope — which is exactly why "GitOps" isn't a new idea, it's Kubernetes' own core idea applied one layer higher.</div>

                    <h5>⑦ Building Real Headroom — the Pause-Pod / PriorityClass Trick</h5>
                    <p>§44.2 defined headroom as "spare capacity reserved on purpose." That's the intent — but Kubernetes has no <code>spec.headroom</code> field. The standard, official pattern for actually <em>building</em> that spare capacity is to reserve it with disposable placeholder pods that get shoved out of the way the instant a real pod needs the room.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   THE PAUSE-POD OVERPROVISIONING PATTERN
   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  1. Create a PriorityClass with a very LOW (negative) value:          │
   │       value: -1000                                                    │
   │  2. Run a small Deployment of trivial "pause" pods using it, sized     │
   │     to the headroom you want (5 pods × 100m/200Mi ≈ 500m/1000Mi).     │
   │  3. Normal easypj-web/celery-worker pods use the DEFAULT priority      │
   │     (0), which is higher than -1.                                     │
   │                                                                        │
   │  Under a traffic spike, a new easypj-web pod is unschedulable ──►      │
   │     the scheduler PREEMPTS (evicts) enough pause pods to fit it        │
   │     instead of leaving the real pod Pending for a full CA scale-up     │
   │  The evicted pause pods go back to Pending themselves ──►              │
   │     THAT triggers the Cluster Autoscaler to add a node ──►             │
   │     once it lands, the pause pods reschedule, headroom is restored     │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">overprovisioning PriorityClass + placeholder</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: overprovisioning
value: -1000                  <span class="comment"># lower than every real workload's default (0)</span>
globalDefault: false
description: "Placeholder pods that reserve headroom and get preempted first."
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: overprovisioning-headroom
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  replicas: 5                 <span class="comment"># tune replica count × request size = target headroom</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels: {run: overprovisioning}
  template:
    metadata:
      labels: {run: overprovisioning}
    spec:
      priorityClassName: overprovisioning
      containers:
        - name: pause
          image: registry.k8s.io/pause:3.9
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "100m", memory: "200Mi"}</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Without this pattern</th><th>With it</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>A traffic spike's new pods go Pending and wait the FULL Cluster Autoscaler cycle (node boot + join, often 1-3+ minutes) before they schedule</td><td>New pods preempt pause pods and schedule almost <em>immediately</em>, while the Autoscaler backfills the displaced headroom in the background</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Headroom" is just an aspiration in a capacity spreadsheet</td><td>Headroom is a real, observable Deployment you can scale up/down like any other — <code>kubectl scale deploy overprovisioning-headroom --replicas=10</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is the concrete mechanism behind an abstract idea.</strong> "Reserve headroom" sounds like a policy; the pause-pod pattern is the actual Kubernetes primitive that implements it — negative-priority pods that exist purely to be preempted, converting a slow "wait for a whole new node" spike response into a fast "steal capacity, then quietly replace it" one. Size the placeholder Deployment's total requests to match the N+1 (or larger) headroom target from §44.2's capacity formula.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch44-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Multi-cluster patterns split by environment, region, or tenant</strong> — dev/stage/prod for blast-radius, per-region for latency/DR, per-tenant for isolation/compliance; fleets are managed with GitOps + a control plane (Argo CD / Flux / Rancher Fleet), never by clicking through each cluster by hand.</li>
                    <li><strong>Per-environment and per-tenant clusters give stronger isolation than namespaces</strong> — separate control planes and blast radius, independent upgrade timing, and clean cost/security boundaries a shared control plane can't fully guarantee.</li>
                    <li><strong>Requests are the unit of both scheduling and cost</strong> — too high wastes money on idle, unused reservation (bad bin-packing); too low invites evictions and noisy neighbors. Right-size from real, measured usage.</li>
                    <li><strong>Capacity ≈ Σ requests + deliberate headroom</strong>, bin-packed to a sane utilization target (not 100%, not 30%); pick node size/type to fit both the CPU and memory totals, whichever binds first.</li>
                    <li><strong>Three autoscalers, three different axes</strong> — HPA scales pod COUNT (on metrics), VPA right-sizes a pod's REQUESTS, Cluster Autoscaler scales NODE COUNT (when pods can't fit) — used together, never as rivals, and never both acting on the same metric at once.</li>
                    <li><strong>Four cost levers, in order of leverage</strong> — right-size requests, autoscale away idle nodes, use spot/preemptible nodes with PDBs, and measure allocation with OpenCost/Kubecost so "right-size" is backed by real numbers, not guesses.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch44-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Pods without requests wreck both scheduling and cost accounting.</strong> The scheduler treats a request-less pod as needing almost nothing, so it packs onto "full" nodes and then fights for real capacity at runtime — always set requests.</li>
                    <li><strong>HPA and VPA fighting on the same metric.</strong> VPA changes requests while HPA scales on utilization computed against those requests, so acting on the same signal at once causes oscillation — separate the signals or run VPA in <code>updateMode: Off</code> (recommend-only).</li>
                    <li><strong>Spot/preemptible nodes can vanish with short notice.</strong> Protect workloads with PodDisruptionBudgets and topology spread, and keep critical singleton/stateful pods (the database, leader-elected controllers) on on-demand nodes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Over-provisioned headroom feels safe but is pure cost.</strong> Size it deliberately (e.g. N+1) — never let it accumulate by accident from repeatedly "rounding up" requests.</li>
                    <li><strong>Idle nodes full of over-sized requests can't be removed by the Cluster Autoscaler.</strong> The reserved requests still "look used" to it even if real usage is near zero — right-size first, then let autoscaling remove the resulting idle capacity.</li>
                    <li><strong>metrics-server missing/unhealthy or no requests set → HPA shows <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code></strong> and silently never scales — always confirm <code>kubectl top pods</code> works and requests are declared before debugging anything more exotic.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cost allocation bills by request, not usage.</strong> A pod requesting 40% of a node's capacity is billed 40% of that node's cost even if it only ever uses 5% — which is precisely why right-sizing requests is a direct, measurable cost fix, not just a scheduling nicety.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch44-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Compare <code>kubectl top pods</code> to declared requests</strong> to spot over/under-provisioning instantly — this single comparison is the fastest cost audit available on any cluster.</li>
                    <li><strong>Let VPA's recommender suggest requests even if you don't auto-apply them.</strong> <code>updateMode: Off</code> gives you the insight with zero disruption risk.</li>
                    <li><strong>Put per-env differences (replicas / requests) in Kustomize overlays</strong> so the exact same easypj base scales cleanly from a laptop <code>kind</code> cluster to a 200-node production fleet.</li>
                    <li><strong>Read <code>kubectl describe node</code> "Allocated resources"</strong> to see how full a bin already is, by request percentage, before adding new workloads.</li>
                    <li><strong>Label nodes by instance-type and zone</strong> (<code>node.kubernetes.io/instance-type</code>, <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code>) so you can see spot vs on-demand and topology spread at a glance with <code>kubectl get nodes -L</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

