            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch44-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 44 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (12)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the multi-cluster, capacity, and cost mental model — 12 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name a multi-cluster pattern and the reason a team would choose it.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Any one of: <strong>per-environment</strong> (dev/stage/prod, driven by blast-radius containment and independent upgrade timing), <strong>per-region</strong> (driven by latency/locality and disaster recovery), or <strong>per-tenant</strong> (driven by hard isolation and compliance).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Driver</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Per-environment</td><td>Blast-radius + independent upgrades</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Per-region</td><td>Latency/locality + disaster recovery</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Per-tenant</td><td>Hard isolation + compliance + cost boundary</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Real fleets often combine all three at once — per-env clusters, each region-replicated, with one tenant carved out onto its own dedicated cluster. Each pattern is chosen for a different <em>reason</em>, not interchangeably.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you estimate how many nodes a cluster needs?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Sum the pod <strong>requests</strong> (the scheduling unit) for everything that must run, add deliberate <strong>headroom</strong>, then size node count/type so those requests bin-pack to your utilization target.</p>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">nodes ≈ ceil( (Σ requests + headroom) / node allocatable )</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Compute this separately for CPU and memory and take the larger result — a workload can be CPU-bound on one node type and memory-bound on another, and sizing by only one resource silently under-provisions the other.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Right-size easypj-web given usage 120m CPU / 180Mi memory and declared requests 500m CPU / 512Mi memory. What's the method and the new numbers?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Compare <code>kubectl top</code> actual usage against declared requests/limits (or use VPA recommendations), and set requests near real usage plus a sane buffer — never pin requests exactly at, or below, real steady-state usage.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>CPU</th><th>Memory</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Declared requests</td><td>500m</td><td>512Mi</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Measured usage</td><td>120m</td><td>180Mi</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>New requests (usage + buffer)</td><td>≈200m</td><td>≈256Mi</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This workload was over-provisioned by roughly 4× on CPU — paying for 500m while using 120m. Dropping requests to ~200m keeps a safety buffer above measured usage while releasing the wasted reservation for other pods to bin-pack into.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the distinct roles of HPA, VPA, and the Cluster Autoscaler?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>HPA = more/fewer <strong>pods</strong> (on metrics). VPA = bigger/smaller pod <strong>requests</strong>. Cluster Autoscaler = more/fewer <strong>nodes</strong>, when pods are unschedulable or nodes sit idle.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Three axes, one team</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   📈 HPA  → pod COUNT       📐 VPA  → request SIZE       🖥️ CA  → node COUNT
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Each controls a different resource dimension, so they can — and should — run simultaneously without conflict, as long as no two of them act on the exact same metric.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the trade-offs of spot/preemptible nodes?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Much cheaper (often 60–90% off), but reclaimable by the provider at any time with only a short interruption notice — safe only for interruption-tolerant workloads protected by PodDisruptionBudgets and topology spread, with an on-demand fallback for anything critical.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Good fit for spot</th><th>Keep on on-demand</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Stateless <code>easypj-web</code> replicas</td><td>The easypj PostgreSQL database</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Batch/queue workers (<code>celery-worker</code>)</td><td>Leader-elected controllers</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>CI runners</td><td>Anything with irreplaceable in-flight state</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The discount is real, but it's a trade for reliability, not a free lunch — the platform, not the cloud provider, is responsible for surviving the interruption gracefully via PDBs and a node-termination handler.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why do resource requests drive bin-packing?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The scheduler's filter phase places a pod only on a node whose allocatable, minus the sum of requests already reserved there, can still fit this pod's requests — never on usage or limits.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Inflated vs tiny requests</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  inflated requests ─► wasted, idle, paid-for capacity (bad bin-packing)
  tiny requests      ─► overcommit ─► real contention ─► evictions
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A node "full" of over-requested but idle pods looks exactly as full to the scheduler as one genuinely saturated with real load — which is why "just add more requests to be safe" quietly wastes real money.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Give two concrete cost levers for a Kubernetes fleet, in rough order of leverage.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Any two of: <strong>right-size requests</strong> (stop reserving unused capacity), <strong>autoscale</strong> away idle nodes with the Cluster Autoscaler, use <strong>spot/preemptible</strong> nodes where appropriate, and <strong>measure allocation</strong> with OpenCost/Kubecost so the other three levers are backed by real data.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Lever</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>1</td><td>Right-size requests</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>2</td><td>Autoscale away idle nodes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>3</td><td>Spot/preemptible nodes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>4</td><td>Measure allocation (OpenCost/Kubecost)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Right-sizing is listed first because it's usually the single highest-leverage, lowest-effort fix — a few <code>kubectl top</code> checks can uncover requests set 3-4× higher than real usage.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is headroom, and why is it worth having deliberately?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Spare capacity deliberately reserved so the cluster can absorb a traffic spike or survive losing a node without immediately producing Pending pods.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Headroom = insurance you pay for on purpose</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  Σ requests  +  HEADROOM (e.g. N+1)  =  total capacity you provision
  headroom feels like waste — until the node you lose is the one that mattered
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Headroom that accumulates by accident (nobody ever right-sized) is pure waste; headroom sized deliberately (e.g. "we can survive losing one node") is insurance. Same spare capacity, very different intent.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The HPA for easypj-web shows targets <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code> and never scales. Diagnose.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Usually one of two causes: <strong>metrics-server</strong> is missing or unhealthy (verify <code>kubectl top pods</code> works at all), or the container has <strong>no resource requests set</strong>, so HPA has no baseline denominator to compute a utilization percentage against.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>If it fails</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>kubectl top pods</code></td><td>metrics-server problem, not an HPA problem — check its Deployment/logs</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>container's <code>resources.requests</code></td><td>empty/missing → set it; HPA computes % of the request, not an absolute value</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Install/repair metrics-server and set requests before assuming anything more exotic (custom metrics adapter, PromQL adapter) is broken — these two account for the overwhelming majority of real <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code> cases.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why can running VPA in an applying mode (<code>Recreate</code>/<code>InPlaceOrRecreate</code>, formerly called <code>Auto</code>) alongside HPA on the same CPU metric cause a problem, and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>VPA changes requests while HPA scales on utilization computed against those same requests, so the two <strong>oscillate</strong>: VPA shrinks requests → utilization % spikes → HPA adds pods → VPA shrinks requests further, and so on.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Fix: split the signals, or make VPA advisory-only</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ✅ VPA updateMode: Off  (recommend-only) + HPA handles all live scaling
  ✅ HPA on a CUSTOM metric (RPS/queue depth) + VPA owns CPU/memory requests
  ❌ VPA Recreate/InPlaceOrRecreate mode + HPA, BOTH on same CPU/mem metric — oscillates
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Recommend-only VPA (<code>updateMode: Off</code>) alongside a live HPA is the dominant real-world pattern — you get VPA's sizing insight with zero disruption risk, and HPA remains the sole live-scaling actor.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the symptoms of an over-provisioned fleet, and why can't the Cluster Autoscaler fix that on its own?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Low node CPU/memory <strong>usage</strong> alongside high node <strong>request</strong> allocation, requests far above measured usage in every <code>kubectl top</code> comparison, and idle nodes that never get reclaimed even though nothing on them is doing real work.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Requests 85%, usage 25% on <code>describe node</code></td><td>Over-provisioned, not overloaded — the gap is pure reserved-but-unused capacity</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Idle nodes the Cluster Autoscaler won't remove</td><td>The reserved requests still "look used" to the scheduler's math, even at near-zero real usage</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Steady, unchanging cloud bill despite low load</td><td>You're paying for capacity by request, not by usage — see §44's Under the Hood ⑤</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The Cluster Autoscaler only ever reacts to <em>requests</em>, never real usage — a node "full" of over-declared but idle pods is, by its math, exactly as full as one genuinely saturated with load. Right-sizing has to happen first, by a human or VPA recommendation; only after that does the resulting idle capacity become something the Autoscaler can actually remove.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why might a team choose per-environment clusters over per-environment namespaces on one shared cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Separate clusters give a <strong>hard</strong> boundary — separate control planes and blast radius, independent upgrade timing, and a clean cost/security line — that a shared control plane can never fully guarantee even with strict RBAC, ResourceQuotas, and NetworkPolicies.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Boundary</th><th>Namespace</th><th>Cluster</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Control plane / etcd</td><td>Shared with every other namespace</td><td>Fully separate</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Upgrade timing</td><td>All namespaces upgrade together</td><td>Independent per cluster — <code>dev</code> weeks ahead of <code>prod</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Blast radius of a control-plane bug or compromise</td><td>Whole shared cluster, every namespace</td><td>Just that one cluster</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Namespaces are the right call when soft isolation is enough and you want to avoid the operational overhead of another control plane to patch and back up. Clusters are the right call the moment you need a boundary that survives even a compromised or badly-behaved neighbor — because a namespace still shares the one API server and etcd that neighbor is hammering.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

            </div>

            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch44-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 44 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (6)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Hands-on scenarios that mirror how a real platform-team incident actually unfolds — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Pull easypj-web's actual usage, compare it to its declared requests/limits, and set right-sized requests. Then add an HPA on top.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">right-size, then autoscale</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Compare usage vs declared</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj-production -l app=easypj-web
kubectl get deploy easypj-web -n easypj-production \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}{"\n"}'

<span class="comment"># 2. Patch in right-sized requests (usage + buffer)</span>
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -n easypj-production --type=json -p='[
  {"op":"replace","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/resources/requests/cpu","value":"200m"},
  {"op":"replace","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/resources/requests/memory","value":"256Mi"}
]'

<span class="comment"># 3. Add an HPA on the now-honest request baseline</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment easypj-web -n easypj-production \
  --cpu-percent=70 --min=3 --max=10

kubectl get hpa easypj-web -n easypj-production</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Right-sizing <em>before</em> adding the HPA matters: if requests are inflated, HPA's 70% target is computed against a fictional baseline and never triggers a scale-up until real usage is absurdly high. Fix the denominator first, then let HPA scale on top of it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Under sustained load, HPA scales easypj-web from 3 to 10 replicas but 4 new pods stay Pending. Diagnose and predict when the Cluster Autoscaler intervenes.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Confirm WHY they're Pending — capacity, not something else</span>
kubectl describe pod &lt;pending-pod&gt; -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># Events: "0/6 nodes are available: 6 Insufficient cpu"</span>

<span class="comment"># Confirm current node capacity is genuinely exhausted</span>
kubectl describe node &lt;node&gt; | sed -n '/Allocated resources/,/Events/p'

<span class="comment"># Cluster Autoscaler sees the SAME Pending pods and adds a node — watch it happen:</span>
kubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/cluster-autoscaler --tail=50 -f</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The exact trigger the Cluster Autoscaler reacts to is <strong>Pending pods that are unschedulable for lack of capacity</strong> — the same "Insufficient cpu/memory" Event a human would see in <code>describe</code>. It provisions a new node sized (or picked, with Karpenter) to fit them, and once they schedule the Pending state clears on its own.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A teammate enabled VPA in <code>Auto</code> mode on easypj-web, which already has an HPA scaling on CPU. Replica count is bouncing between 4 and 9 every few minutes with no traffic change. Diagnose and fix.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Diagnosis: VPA (Auto) and HPA are BOTH acting on the CPU metric —</span>
<span class="comment"># classic oscillation. Fix: switch VPA to recommend-only.</span>
apiVersion: autoscaling.k8s.io/v1
kind: VerticalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  targetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj-web
  updatePolicy:
    updateMode: "Off"   <span class="comment"># was "Auto" — now recommend-only</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>With <code>updateMode: "Off"</code>, the VPA recommender keeps computing suggested requests (readable via <code>kubectl get vpa -o yaml</code>) but never evicts pods to apply them — HPA becomes the sole live-scaling actor and the oscillation stops immediately. Apply the VPA's suggestions by hand later, on your own schedule.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Finance flags that the cluster's bill doubled last month. <code>kubectl top nodes</code> shows most nodes around 25% CPU utilization. Walk through the diagnosis and fix.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Confirm it's a REQUESTS problem, not a real usage problem</span>
kubectl describe node &lt;node&gt; | sed -n '/Allocated resources/,/Events/p'
<span class="comment"># requests 85% but usage (kubectl top) only 25% → over-provisioned, not overloaded</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Find the worst offenders across the fleet</span>
kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu
<span class="comment"># cross-reference against each pod's declared requests</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Right-size the worst offenders (see Q1), THEN let the Cluster Autoscaler remove nodes that are now genuinely idle:</span>
kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,\
AGE:.metadata.creationTimestamp</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Low node CPU <em>usage</em> alongside high node <em>request</em> allocation is the signature of over-provisioning, not under-load — the Cluster Autoscaler can't remove those nodes on its own because the reserved (if unused) requests still look "used" to it. Right-sizing has to happen first; removal follows automatically once it does.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj-web runs partly on spot nodes. A spot reclamation wave takes out 3 nodes at once, and every replica on them goes down simultaneously — an outage. What was missing, and how do you prevent a repeat?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-pdb
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  minAvailable: 70%
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
---
<span class="comment"># AND spread replicas across zones so one reclamation wave can't hit them all:</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      topologySpreadConstraints:
        - maxSkew: 1
          topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
          whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
          labelSelector:
            matchLabels: {app: easypj-web}</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A PodDisruptionBudget alone doesn't stop a spot reclamation — cloud-initiated terminations aren't voluntary evictions a PDB can block. What it (combined with topology spread) DOES prevent is <em>concentration</em>: with replicas spread across zones and a PDB capping simultaneous voluntary disruption, a single reclamation wave in one zone can no longer take every replica down at once.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Someone <code>kubectl edit</code>'d easypj-web directly in the <code>stage</code> cluster to hotfix an incident, bypassing Git. A day later the fleet GitOps tool silently reverted it back to the old (broken) config. Explain why, and the correct fix.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The GitOps tool is doing exactly its job: it reconciles LIVE state</span>
<span class="comment"># back to DESIRED state in Git on every pass. A live-only edit is just</span>
<span class="comment"># "drift" from its point of view — expected to be corrected, not honored.</span>

<span class="comment"># Correct fix: commit the hotfix to the SAME place the tool reads from</span>
git add overlays/stage/patch-easypj-web.yaml
git commit -m "hotfix: bump easypj-web memory limit for stage incident"
git push
<span class="comment"># the next reconcile pass now sees Git and live IN agreement — no more revert</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the GitOps discipline in one incident: <strong>Git is the only place a change is considered "real."</strong> A live edit that skips Git isn't a fix, it's a temporary illusion the next reconcile pass erases — exactly the same behavior Chapter 1's reconciliation loop exhibits on a single object, just applied at fleet scale.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

            </div>

