                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> metrics-server needs a minute or two to collect its first samples after install — <code>kubectl top pods</code> errors with "metrics not available yet" until then, which is normal, not a bug.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">right-size end to end</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Deploy with intentionally-oversized requests</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=nginx --replicas=2
kubectl set resources deployment easypj-web \
  --requests=cpu=500m,memory=256Mi --limits=cpu=1,memory=512Mi

<span class="comment"># 2. Install metrics-server (Minikube: enable the addon; otherwise apply components.yaml)</span>
minikube addons enable metrics-server
<span class="comment"># wait ~60s, then:</span>
kubectl top pods -l app=easypj-web

<span class="comment"># 3. Compare and patch to real usage + buffer</span>
kubectl set resources deployment easypj-web \
  --requests=cpu=100m,memory=64Mi --limits=cpu=250m,memory=128Mi

<span class="comment"># 4. Add HPA on the now-honest baseline</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment easypj-web --cpu-percent=70 --min=2 --max=6
kubectl get hpa easypj-web</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> before the patch, <code>kubectl top</code> usage should be well below the 500m/256Mi requests — proving the workload was over-provisioned. After patching to a realistic baseline and adding the HPA, <code>kubectl describe hpa</code> should show a real percentage (not <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code>), because requests are now set and metrics-server is healthy.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 44.2 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 44.2 — Compute Node Count From Requests, by Hand</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>easypj-production must run: <code>easypj-web</code> ×8 replicas at (300m CPU, 400Mi memory) each, <code>celery-worker</code> ×4 replicas at (600m CPU, 768Mi memory) each, and <code>postgres</code> ×1 at (1500m CPU, 3Gi memory). Add 25% headroom. Given a node type with 3600m CPU / 7000Mi memory allocatable, compute the minimum node count — <em>by hand, without a calculator</em> — for BOTH CPU and memory, and state which one binds.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Sum each resource separately first, multiply the sum by 1.25 for headroom, THEN divide by that node type's allocatable and round up — don't average CPU and memory together.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
CPU:     easypj-web  8 × 300m  = 2400m
         celery       4 × 600m  = 2400m
         postgres     1 × 1500m = 1500m
         ────────────────────────────
         Σ                      = 6300m   × 1.25 headroom = 7875m
         nodes = ceil(7875m / 3600m) = 3

MEMORY:  easypj-web  8 × 400Mi  = 3200Mi
         celery       4 × 768Mi  = 3072Mi
         postgres     1 × 3072Mi = 3072Mi   (3Gi = 3072Mi)
         ────────────────────────────
         Σ                      = 9344Mi  × 1.25 headroom = 11680Mi
         nodes = ceil(11680Mi / 7000Mi) = 2

RESULT:  take the MAX(3, 2) = 3 nodes — CPU is the binding resource here.
</pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> if you computed both totals independently and took the larger node count (3, bound by CPU) rather than averaging or only checking one resource, you avoided the exact under-provisioning trap §44.2's gotcha warns about.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 44.3 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 44.3 — Diagnose &amp; Fix an HPA/VPA Conflict Live</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 7 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>On a cluster with the VPA CRDs and controller installed, create an HPA and a VPA both targeting the same Deployment, on the same CPU metric, with the VPA in <code>Auto</code> mode. Watch replica count and requests both change over several minutes without any real traffic change. Then apply the correct fix and confirm it stabilizes.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> You don't need real traffic to see the oscillation — the VPA recommender reacting to its own resize-triggered restarts is enough to create visible churn within a few minutes on a small cluster.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">reproduce, then fix, the conflict</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Reproduce: HPA + VPA(Recreate) on the same target/metric</span>
<span class="comment"># ("Recreate" is the current explicit name; older docs call this "Auto" — deprecated alias, same behavior)</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment easypj-web --cpu-percent=50 --min=2 --max=8
kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;EOF
apiVersion: autoscaling.k8s.io/v1
kind: VerticalPodAutoscaler
metadata: {name: easypj-web}
spec:
  targetRef: {apiVersion: apps/v1, kind: Deployment, name: easypj-web}
  updatePolicy: {updateMode: "Recreate"}
EOF

<span class="comment"># 2. Watch it churn</span>
watch -n5 'kubectl get hpa easypj-web; kubectl get pods -l app=easypj-web'

<span class="comment"># 3. Fix: flip VPA to recommend-only</span>
kubectl patch vpa easypj-web --type=merge -p \
  '{"spec":{"updatePolicy":{"updateMode":"Off"}}}'

<span class="comment"># 4. Confirm it stabilizes — replica count should settle within a couple minutes</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> after the patch, <code>kubectl get vpa easypj-web -o yaml</code> should still show live recommendations under <code>.status.recommendation</code> — you kept the insight, you just stopped it from auto-applying and fighting the HPA.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 44.4 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 44.4 — Design a Kustomize Overlay Set for a 3-Cluster Fleet</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Design (on paper, or as real files) a Kustomize <code>base/</code> for easypj-web plus three overlays — <code>dev</code>, <code>stage</code>, <code>prod</code> — that differ ONLY in replica count and resource requests. List the exact file layout and what each overlay patches.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> the base holds everything IDENTICAL across environments (image name, ports, labels, probes); each overlay's <code>kustomization.yaml</code> references the base and patches only the fields that differ.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
base/
├── deployment.yaml        (replicas: 1, requests: cpu 50m/mem 64Mi — placeholder)
├── service.yaml
└── kustomization.yaml     (resources: [deployment.yaml, service.yaml])

overlays/dev/
└── kustomization.yaml     resources: [../../base]
                            patches: replicas → 1, requests unchanged (tiny)

overlays/stage/
└── kustomization.yaml     resources: [../../base]
                            patches: replicas → 2, requests → cpu 150m/mem 192Mi

overlays/prod/
└── kustomization.yaml     resources: [../../base]
                            patches: replicas → 6, requests → cpu 200m/mem 256Mi

deploy any environment with:  kubectl apply -k overlays/prod/
</pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> only replicas and requests differ between overlays — image, ports, probes, and labels all come from the untouched base. If you found yourself duplicating the whole Deployment per environment, that's exactly the anti-pattern this drill is meant to catch.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL 44.5 ─────────── -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 44.5 — Allocate a Shared Node's Cost by Hand</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 4 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A node costs $180/month. It runs three workloads whose combined requests are: <code>easypj-web</code> 2000m CPU, <code>celery-worker</code> 1500m CPU, and an unrelated team's app at 500m CPU (total 4000m, matching the node's allocatable exactly). Compute each workload's allocated monthly cost the way OpenCost/Kubecost would — by request share, not by usage.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> each workload's share of the bill equals its fraction of the TOTAL requests on that node, applied to the node's total price — usage never enters this calculation.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre class="uth-diagram">
easypj-web:      2000m / 4000m = 50%   →  $180 × 0.50 = $90.00/mo
celery-worker:   1500m / 4000m = 37.5% →  $180 × 0.375 = $67.50/mo
other-team-app:   500m / 4000m = 12.5% →  $180 × 0.125 = $22.50/mo
                                         ─────────────────────────
                                         total = $180.00/mo ✅ (fully accounted)
</pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> the three allocations sum back to exactly $180 — no cost was double-counted or lost. This is exactly why right-sizing easypj-web's requests directly lowers ITS line item next month, even if usage never changes: shrink the request share, shrink the bill share.</p>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 44 Visual Summary — easypj across clusters, with the three autoscalers</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   Git (desired state)  ──►  dev cluster ─ stage cluster ─ prod cluster
        per-env Kustomize overlays: replicas + requests differ

   In EACH cluster, easypj-web is governed by three axes:
     📈 HPA  → pod COUNT      (3→10 on CPU)
     📐 VPA  → request SIZE   (right-size from usage, recommend-only alongside HPA)
     🖥️ CA  → node COUNT     (add when Pending / remove when idle)

   REQUESTS tie it all together — the ONE field that is simultaneously:
     • what the SCHEDULER bin-packs by (§44.2)
     • what the BILL is allocated by  (§44.3)
     • the DENOMINATOR the HPA computes utilization against (§44.4)

   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Get requests right → tight bin-packing, honest autoscaling, fair cost.
   Get requests wrong → evictions OR idle nodes the autoscaler can't touch.
</pre>
</div>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> one Git repository holds the desired state for the whole easypj fleet, and a GitOps control plane reconciles it into each cluster — dev, stage, prod — with Kustomize overlays supplying only the per-environment differences, such as replica counts and resource requests. Inside every cluster, the easypj-web workload is shaped on three independent axes: the HPA changes how <em>many</em> pods run based on live metrics, the VPA right-sizes how <em>big</em> each pod's requests are based on real historical usage (best run recommend-only, alongside a live HPA), and the Cluster Autoscaler changes how many <em>nodes</em> exist — adding one when HPA's new pods go Pending, and removing one when nodes fall idle.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the single field that links scheduling and cost across all of this is the resource <strong>request</strong>. Set it from measured usage, give yourself deliberate headroom, and you can run easypj across many clusters both reliably and affordably — which is the whole difference between "I can operate a cluster" (the exam) and "I can operate a fleet, well, on a budget" (the job).</p>
                </div>
            </div>
