  ──────────────                                      ─────────────────────────
  🏊 Swim, bike, run — trained separately   ──────▶  Parts 1–13 — Pods, Services, storage, security, etc.
  🔄 Transition zone (wetsuit → bike → run) ──────▶  §45.1 Setup ritual — alias/autocomplete/context in &lt;60s
  ⏱️ Pacing the whole race, not one leg     ──────▶  §45.2 3-pass time strategy across the 2-hour clock
  🚫 Fumbled buckle = lost seconds, not DQ  ──────▶  §45.3/45.4 Do's/Don'ts + Top-10 Point-Losers
  🏁 Race-day dress rehearsal beats reading ──────▶  §45.8 Full 20-task timed simulator on easypj</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Candidates Get Wrong About the CKA Exam</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Candidates Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Knowing more Kubernetes than the exam requires guarantees a pass — depth of knowledge is the whole game."</td>
                            <td>Time management fails genuinely competent engineers constantly. The most common failure pattern isn't "didn't know how" — it's arriving at the correct answer to question 12 at minute 130, after the clock stopped at minute 120. The exam scores what you FINISH and VERIFY inside 120 minutes, not what you could eventually produce with unlimited time. Chapter 45's 3-pass strategy exists precisely to convert knowledge you already have into points you actually collect.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"I should perfect every answer before moving on — a half-finished task is worse than a slow, flawless one."</td>
                            <td>Partial credit plus disciplined time triage beats perfectionism almost every time. Spending 25 minutes polishing one 4%-weighted networking task while a 13%-weighted RBAC task sits untouched is a losing trade even if the polished answer is flawless. The 3-pass strategy (harvest the easy/high-weight first, tackle the hard middle second, salvage and sweep last) is specifically designed to prevent this — flag it, move on, come back with fresh eyes in Pass 3.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Hand-writing YAML from memory shows real mastery, and it's what the exam expects."</td>
                            <td>The exam grades the live cluster state, not how the manifest was produced. <code>kubectl create deployment ... $do</code> (imperative-command-then-edit) is faster, less error-prone, and scores identically to hand-typed YAML — a single misplaced indent in hand-written YAML can silently invalidate an entire answer with zero partial credit, while the generator never gets indentation wrong. Chapter 45's Do's/Don'ts make "generate first, edit second, verify always" the default reflex, not an occasional shortcut.</td>
