            <!-- ─────────── §45.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s45-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">45.1 The First 5 Minutes — The Setup Ritual</h3>
                <p>Before you touch a single question, invest <strong>5 minutes</strong> building your tooling. This is the single highest-leverage 5 minutes of the entire exam — every minute you spend here pays itself back on <em>every one</em> of the 15–20 tasks that follow. Skip it, and you retype the same flags, fight the same indentation bugs, and hunt the same field names two dozen times over.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Four Things, In This Order</h5>
                    <p>The ritual has exactly four moving parts, and they build on each other: (1) alias <code>kubectl</code> down to one letter with working tab-completion, (2) export two environment variables that turn imperative commands into YAML generators and instant deletes, (3) make vim safe for pasting indented YAML, (4) know where your one allowed documentation tab is going to point. Do all four before you read task 1.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">~/.bashrc — run this first, every exam, every practice session</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ~/.bashrc  (then load it:  source ~/.bashrc )</span>
<span class="keyword">alias</span> k=kubectl
<span class="keyword">source</span> &lt;(kubectl completion bash)
complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k     <span class="comment"># completion works on `k` too</span>

<span class="keyword">export</span> do=<span class="string">"--dry-run=client -o yaml"</span>          <span class="comment"># k create deploy x $do &gt; x.yaml</span>
<span class="keyword">export</span> now=<span class="string">"--force --grace-period=0"</span>        <span class="comment"># k delete pod x $now (fast, no hang)</span>

<span class="keyword">alias</span> kgp=<span class="string">'kubectl get pods -o wide'</span>
<span class="keyword">alias</span> kn=<span class="string">'kubectl config set-context --current --namespace'</span>

<span class="comment"># ~/.vimrc</span>
<span class="keyword">set</span> tabstop=2 shiftwidth=2 expandtab
<span class="keyword">set</span> number autoindent
<span class="comment">" paste-safe YAML: type  :set paste  BEFORE pasting an indented block,</span>
<span class="comment">" :set nopaste afterward so normal typing still auto-indents</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <p>Each piece removes a specific, measurable time cost. Here's exactly what each one buys you and why it matters more than it looks:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Ritual piece</th><th>What it does</th><th>What it saves you from</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>alias k=kubectl</code> + completion</td><td>one keystroke instead of eight, with tab-complete on resource names, flags, and even pod names</td><td>literally hundreds of keystrokes across 15–20 tasks; completion also prevents typos in resource names</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>export do=...</code></td><td>turns any <code>k create ... $do &gt; x.yaml</code> into a perfectly-indented YAML scaffold you edit instead of write from scratch</td><td>the <strong>#1</strong> source of lost points: hand-typed YAML indentation errors</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>export now=...</code></td><td>instantly force-deletes a pod instead of waiting through a 30-second graceful termination</td><td>minutes wasted watching a stuck <code>Terminating</code> pod during troubleshooting tasks</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>~/.vimrc</code> 2-space expandtab</td><td>pasted YAML lands with real 2-space indentation, not tabs or a cascading auto-indent mess</td><td>the #2 most common YAML-generation failure — corrupted indentation from a raw paste</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Doc bookmarks (mental, not literal)</td><td>you already know which kubernetes.io page has the NetworkPolicy / Ingress / Gateway API skeleton before you need it</td><td>minutes lost mid-task searching instead of pasting-and-editing</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Insight</h5>
                    <p><code>$do</code> is the single highest-leverage export in the whole ritual — it turns any imperative <code>create</code> into a perfectly-indented YAML scaffold you can edit, eliminating the #1 source of lost points on the entire exam: indentation errors in hand-written YAML.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Gotcha — Paste Without <code>:set paste</code> Corrupts YAML</h5>
                    <p>In vim, pasting a YAML block without first running <code>:set paste</code> cascades the <code>autoindent</code> setting — every line gets progressively more indented than the last, silently invalidating the manifest. Toggle <code>:set paste</code> immediately before every paste, and <code>:set nopaste</code> immediately after so normal typing still auto-indents correctly.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §45.1b ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s45-1b">
                <h3 class="section-h">45.1b Verify Env Each Task — Context &amp; Namespace</h3>
                <p>This is the single most expensive mistake on the entire exam, and it is 100% avoidable. The CKA switches <strong>clusters</strong> per question — each task tells you exactly which context to use. Do flawless work in the wrong cluster, and the auto-grader finds nothing there to check: you score <strong>zero</strong>, no partial credit, no matter how correct your YAML was.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The exam's cluster-switching trap — and the 3-line fix</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   ❌ THE TRAP                                    ✅ THE FIX — every task, no exceptions
   ┌─────────────────────────────┐               ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ Task 7 says:                │               │ kubectl config get-contexts          │
   │ "context: cluster2-admin"   │               │   → list what's available            │
   │                              │               │ kubectl config use-context cluster2… │
   │ You keep working in         │  ──instead──▶ │   → COPY the exact string from the   │
   │ cluster1 (last task's       │               │     question, don't type from memory │
   │ context) out of habit.      │               │ kubectl config set-context --current │
   │                              │               │   --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;                    │
   │ Result: perfect YAML,       │               │                                       │
   │ WRONG cluster → 0 points    │               │ Result: work lands where the grader   │
   └─────────────────────────────┘               │ is actually looking → full credit     │
                                                   └─────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">the first 3 lines of EVERY task, no exceptions</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config get-contexts                  <span class="comment"># list what's available</span>
kubectl config use-context &lt;context&gt;         <span class="comment"># the task gives you this line — copy it</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Must-Remember</h5>
                    <p>The first command of every question is <code>use-context</code>. Copy the exact context string from the question prompt character-for-character — never type it from memory, and never assume the previous task's context still applies.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §45.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s45-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">45.2 The 3-Pass Time Strategy</h3>
                <p>Do <strong>not</strong> solve questions in the order they're presented. Triage them into three passes by effort so you bank guaranteed points first and never let one hard task consume your whole exam. This is the single biggest lever separating candidates who know the material but run out of time from candidates who pass comfortably with knowledge to spare.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The 120-minute battle plan — maximize points per minute, not perfection</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
   ┌──────────────── THE 120-MINUTE BATTLE PLAN ────────────────┐
   │ maximize POINTS PER MINUTE, not perfection                 │
   │                                                            │
   │ ⚡ Setup  (5m)  ████  aliases · completion · vim · bookmarks │
   │ 🟢 Pass 1 (35m) ████████████        every task &lt; 4 min      │
   │                  (quick wins — banked points)              │
   │ 🟡 Pass 2 (50m) ██████████████████  5–8 min tasks           │
   │                  (the BULK of your score)                  │
   │ 🔴 Pass 3 (30m) ██████████          hardest + VERIFY all    │
   │                                                            │
   │ Rules:  switch CONTEXT every question · generate YAML with │
   │ $do · NEVER camp &gt;10 min on one task · 66% to pass ·       │
   │ NEVER leave a question blank (partial credit is real).     │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
</div>
                </div>

                <p>Each pass has a different job. Read every question once at the very start — before solving anything — so you know what's coming and can triage accurately:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Pass</th><th>Budget</th><th>What goes here</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Pass 1 — Quick Wins</strong></td><td>≈35 min</td><td>Anything solvable in under 4 minutes. Solve immediately, verify, move on. Flag everything else.</td><td>RBAC creates, expose a Service, scale a Deployment, apply a taint, add a label</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Pass 2 — The Bulk</strong></td><td>≈50 min</td><td>5–8 minute tasks — most of your total score lives here. Generate YAML, adjust, verify.</td><td>Ingress, NetworkPolicy, PVC + mount, HPA, rolling update</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Pass 3 — Hardest + Verify</strong></td><td>≈30 min</td><td>The hardest multi-step tasks, plus a final verification sweep across <em>everything</em> you've already answered.</td><td>etcd restore, kubeadm upgrade, multi-step troubleshooting chains</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>⚡ Speed Tip</h5>
                    <p>A 4%-weighted question and a 13%-weighted question both deserve the same 4-minute effort in Pass 1. Don't over-invest in low-weight tasks just because they happen first — read the weight before you decide how much time it's worth.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 The 10-Minute Rule</h5>
                    <p>If you have spent 10 minutes on one task without a working answer, <strong>stop</strong>. Leave your best-effort partial answer (never delete what you've built so far), flag the task, and move on. Come back in Pass 3 with fresh eyes and — critically — with the rest of your guaranteed points already banked. Camping on one hard task while three easy ones sit untouched is the most common way a genuinely competent engineer fails this exam.</p>
                </div>

                <p>Why exactly 10 minutes, and not 5 or 15? Because it's the point where the <strong>expected value</strong> of continuing flips negative — the math below is the same triage logic professional engineers use for production incidents, applied to an exam clock:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Minute mark on a stuck task</th><th>Points still likely available here</th><th>What those same minutes buy elsewhere</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>0–4 min</td><td>High — you're probably still on the right track, keep going</td><td>—</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>5–8 min</td><td>Medium — a wrong turn is now plausible; re-read the task once, then continue</td><td>—</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>9–10 min</td><td>Low and dropping — sunk-cost thinking starts to feel like progress even when it isn't</td><td>1–2 untouched Pass-1 tasks worth 2–4% combined, guaranteed if attempted now</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>10+ min</td><td>Marginal — every extra minute here is a minute NOT spent on a task you could solve in 4</td><td>The rest of Pass 1 and Pass 2 — where most of your total score actually lives</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The Real Reason This Works</h5>
                    <p>A stuck 8%-weighted task still sitting at 0/3 sub-checks after 10 minutes is not obviously worth less than three untouched 3%-weighted tasks — until you account for <em>certainty</em>. The three quick tasks are near-certain wins in 4 minutes each (12 minutes for ~9% banked); the stuck task's remaining value is uncertain and may still be 0% after another 10 minutes. Triage by <strong>certain points per minute</strong>, not by raw task weight — that's the entire justification for the rule, and it's why Pass 3 exists to mop up the genuinely hard tasks once every certain point is already banked.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §45.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s45-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">45.3 Imperative vs Declarative on the Clock</h3>
                <p>Speed comes from imperative commands. Correctness on complex, multi-field objects comes from declarative YAML — usually YAML <em>generated by</em> those same imperative commands, not hand-typed from memory. The exam-winning move is almost never "type YAML from scratch" and almost never "figure out every flag imperatively" — it's picking the fastest <em>correct</em> path per object type.</p>

                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Decision Guide — How to Produce Each Object Type</div>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Need</th><th>Fastest correct path</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Pod / Deployment / Job</td><td><code>k create ... $do &gt; x.yaml</code> → edit → <code>apply</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Service (expose)</td><td><code>k expose ...</code> — pure imperative, no YAML needed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>RBAC (sa / role / binding)</td><td><code>k create sa|role|rolebinding ...</code> — imperative, every piece</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>ConfigMap / Secret</td><td><code>k create cm|secret ... --from-literal</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>NetworkPolicy / Ingress</td><td>COPY a skeleton from kubernetes.io docs → edit selectors/ports</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Gateway API HTTPRoute</td><td>COPY a skeleton from docs → edit parentRefs/rules</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>A field name you forgot</td><td><code>kubectl explain &lt;kind&gt;.spec --recursive</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">the hybrid workflow — imperative speed, declarative safety</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Generate — imperative command, but stop before it creates anything</span>
k create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v2 --replicas=3 $do &gt; d.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. Edit — add the 2-3 fields the imperative flags can't express</span>
vim d.yaml    <span class="comment"># :set paste first if pasting a block</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Apply — declarative, idempotent, safe to re-run</span>
k apply -f d.yaml

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify — never skip this step</span>
k get deploy easypj-web -o wide</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Insight</h5>
                    <p>NetworkPolicy, Ingress and Gateway API are too fiddly to author correctly from memory — one wrong indent in a <code>podSelector</code> block silently changes which pods it matches. The exam <strong>allows</strong> a small whitelist of documentation sites — <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code>, plus <code>gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io</code> for HTTPRoute specifically (full list in Under the Hood ⑤): copy a known-good skeleton and adjust selectors/ports/hosts. Adjusting beats authoring, every single time.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §45.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s45-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">45.4 Domain-by-Domain Strategy</h3>
                <p>How to attack each of the five weighted domains, with a concrete command kit per domain and the exact chapters where you already built this knowledge. Drill each box until the commands are reflexive — you should not have to think about syntax, only about the specific task in front of you.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>🏗️ Cluster Architecture / Install <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></h5>
                        <p><strong>Built in:</strong> Ch 3 (Architecture), Ch 9 (kubeadm install), Ch 10 (kubeconfig), Ch 11 (etcd), Ch 12 (control plane), Ch 24 (RBAC), Ch 28 (upgrades)</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>RBAC:</strong> <code>k create sa|role|clusterrole|rolebinding|clusterrolebinding ...</code></li>
                            <li><strong>kubeadm upgrade:</strong> drain → apt upgrade kubeadm → <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> → upgrade kubelet/kubectl → uncordon (control plane first, then workers)</li>
                            <li><strong>etcd:</strong> <code>etcdctl snapshot save/restore</code> with <code>--cacert</code>/<code>--cert</code>/<code>--key</code></li>
                            <li><strong>Static pods:</strong> drop YAML in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>; kubelet auto-runs it</li>
                            <li><strong>CSR:</strong> <code>kubectl get csr</code> → <code>kubectl certificate approve &lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>📦 Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></h5>
                        <p><strong>Built in:</strong> Ch 13 (Deployments/scaling), Ch 14 (scheduling/node mgmt), Ch 15 (resource mgmt), Ch 16 (Jobs/CronJobs/DaemonSets/StatefulSets)</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Rolling update/rollback:</strong> <code>k set image</code> / <code>k rollout undo</code></li>
                            <li><strong>HPA:</strong> <code>k autoscale deploy ... --min --max --cpu-percent</code></li>
                            <li><strong>Placement:</strong> <code>k taint nodes</code> / tolerations + nodeSelector / affinity</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>🌐 Services &amp; Networking <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></h5>
                        <p><strong>Built in:</strong> Ch 17 (networking fundamentals), Ch 18 (Services), Ch 19 (DNS/CoreDNS), Ch 20 (Ingress &amp; NetworkPolicies), Ch 20b (Gateway API)</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Expose:</strong> <code>k expose ... --type=NodePort|ClusterIP --port --target-port</code></li>
                            <li><strong>NetworkPolicy:</strong> default-deny + allow (copy from docs)</li>
                            <li><strong>Ingress / Gateway API:</strong> HTTPRoute (copy from docs, set host/path/backend)</li>
                            <li><strong>DNS debug:</strong> <code>kubectl run tmp --image=busybox -it --rm -- nslookup &lt;svc&gt;</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>💾 Storage <span class="cka-weight">10%</span></h5>
                        <p><strong>Built in:</strong> Ch 21 (volumes &amp; persistent storage), Ch 22 (PVs, PVCs &amp; StorageClasses)</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Dynamic provisioning:</strong> PVC referencing a StorageClass</li>
                            <li><strong>Mount:</strong> <code>volumes[].persistentVolumeClaim</code> + matching <code>volumeMounts</code> in the pod</li>
                            <li><strong>Check binding:</strong> <code>kubectl get pvc,pv</code> ; <code>describe pvc</code> for the events</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card" style="margin-top:18px;">
