            <!-- ─────────── §45.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s45-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">45.6 Scoring &amp; Partial Credit</h3>
                <p>Understanding exactly how you're scored changes how you should spend your time. This isn't a philosophical exercise — it directly informs the 3-pass triage from §45.2.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>📊 How Grading Actually Works</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Tasks are <strong>WEIGHTED</strong> — a multi-step task is worth more than a one-liner, and the weight is shown on each question</li>
                            <li>Grading is <strong>AUTOMATED</strong> against live cluster state — a script reads the object, not a human reading your intent</li>
                            <li>Exact names, namespaces and labels matter because the script matches strings, not meaning</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ What This Means for Your Strategy</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>PARTIAL CREDIT is real</strong> — a task with 3 checks can earn 2/3 even if the last step fails</li>
                            <li>Always leave a best-effort answer, even an incomplete one</li>
                            <li>Let the weight set your effort — don't spend 10 minutes polishing a 2%-weighted task</li>
                            <li>You need <strong>66%</strong> to pass. Aim to bank ~75% of available points and verify the rest — a perfect score is not the goal</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Insight</h5>
                    <p>Because grading reads <em>live cluster state</em> and awards <em>partial credit per sub-check</em>, the highest-EV strategy is never "perfect one task, skip the rest" — it's "get every task to at least a partial, correct state, then use leftover time to polish." A half-finished RBAC task (SA created, Role missing) can still earn real points; a perfect answer submitted after the clock stops earns none.</p>
                </div>

                <p>Here's the arithmetic made concrete, using Task 1 from §45.8 (a 6%-weighted, 3-sub-check RBAC task) as the worked example — this is exactly how a partial attempt converts into partial points:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Sub-check</th><th>Your cluster state</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1. ServiceAccount <code>jobpost-reader</code> exists in <code>easypj-production</code></td><td>Created correctly</td><td>✅ pass</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2. Role <code>pod-reader</code> grants exactly <code>get,list,watch</code> on pods</td><td>Created, but you typed <code>--verb=get,list</code> and forgot <code>watch</code></td><td>❌ fail</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3. RoleBinding correctly binds the Role to the ServiceAccount</td><td>Created correctly</td><td>✅ pass</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <p class="mb-0">📖 <strong>Result:</strong> 2 of 3 sub-checks pass → you bank <strong>2/3 × 6% ≈ 4%</strong> of the total exam score from this one task — not zero, even though the Role was technically wrong. Multiply this pattern across 15–20 tasks and it's clear why "leave a best-effort answer on everything" consistently outscores "leave three tasks completely blank to spend extra time perfecting two others."</p>
                </div>
            </section>

