                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🎯 Task 1 — Bootstrap: Namespace + RBAC</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ ~5 min · 🎯 Weight ~6%</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Task</h5>
                        <p>Create the namespace <code>easypj-production</code> if it does not already exist. Inside it, create a ServiceAccount <code>jobpost-reader</code> that can <code>get</code>/<code>list</code>/<code>watch</code> pods (and nothing else). Verify the permission with <code>auth can-i</code> before moving on — this ServiceAccount and namespace are the foundation every later task in this simulator builds on.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> Three imperative commands, one verify. No YAML needs to be hand-written for any of this.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution &amp; Verification</summary>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bootstrap-task1.sh</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Namespace (idempotent — safe even if it already exists)</span>
k create namespace easypj-production --dry-run=client -o yaml | k apply -f -
k config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-production

<span class="comment"># 2. ServiceAccount + Role + RoleBinding — all imperative</span>
k create sa jobpost-reader -n easypj-production
k create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n easypj-production
k create rolebinding jobpost-reader-rb --role=pod-reader \
  --serviceaccount=easypj-production:jobpost-reader -n easypj-production

<span class="comment"># 3. Verify BEFORE moving on</span>
k auth can-i list pods -n easypj-production \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-production:jobpost-reader     <span class="comment"># expect: yes</span>
k auth can-i delete pods -n easypj-production \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj-production:jobpost-reader     <span class="comment"># expect: no (never granted)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Verify:</strong> both <code>auth can-i</code> checks return the expected answer — the first proves the grant works, the second proves it's correctly <em>scoped</em> (not accidentally over-privileged).</p>
                            <p><strong>Why:</strong> All three pieces are imperative — no YAML to hand-write, no indentation to get wrong. The <code>auth can-i --as</code> check is the single most reliable way to prove an RBAC binding before you trust it, and it's the pattern every later RBAC task in this guide reuses.</p>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🎯 Task 2 — kubeadm Cluster Upgrade</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ ~8 min · 🎯 Weight ~5%</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Task</h5>
                        <p>Upgrade the control-plane node from the current minor version to the next available minor version using <code>kubeadm</code>. The exam gives you the exact target version string — assume <code>&lt;ver&gt;</code> below is that string.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> Order is everything: drain, upgrade the kubeadm binary, run <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>, THEN upgrade kubelet/kubectl, then uncordon. Control-plane nodes always go before workers.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution &amp; Verification</summary>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">upgrade-task2.sh</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Drain first — evict workloads gracefully</span>
kubectl drain &lt;cp-node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets

<span class="comment"># 2. Upgrade the kubeadm tool itself, then plan + apply</span>
apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y kubeadm=&lt;ver&gt;
kubeadm upgrade plan
kubeadm upgrade apply v&lt;ver&gt;

<span class="comment"># 3. THEN upgrade kubelet + kubectl (order matters)</span>
apt-get install -y kubelet=&lt;ver&gt; kubectl=&lt;ver&gt;
systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># 4. Bring the node back into service</span>
kubectl uncordon &lt;cp-node&gt;

<span class="comment"># 5. Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide     <span class="comment"># VERSION column shows the new minor</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Verify:</strong> <code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code> shows the target version under <code>VERSION</code>, and the node's <code>STATUS</code> reads <code>Ready</code> again after uncordon.</p>
                            <p><strong>Why:</strong> Upgrading kubelet <em>before</em> kubeadm apply would leave the node running a kubelet the control plane doesn't yet recognize. The sequence — drain, upgrade the tool, apply the upgrade, upgrade the agent, uncordon — is the only order the exam accepts full credit for.</p>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🎯 Task 3 — etcd Backup + Restore</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ ~8 min · 🎯 Weight ~6%</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Task</h5>
                        <p>Take a snapshot of etcd, then restore it into a <strong>new</strong> data directory and repoint the static-pod manifest so the restored data becomes live. This is one of the highest-stakes tasks on the real exam — get the order wrong and you can break the cluster.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> Never restore over the live data directory. Restore to a fresh path, then edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> to point at it — the kubelet handles the restart.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution &amp; Verification</summary>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">etcd-backup-restore-task3.sh</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Snapshot (with certs — always required)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/snap.db \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># 2. Sanity-check the snapshot before trusting it</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/snap.db

<span class="comment"># 3. Restore to a NEW data-dir (never the live one)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/snap.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-new

<span class="comment"># 4. Repoint the static-pod manifest at the new dir</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># change hostPath.path from /var/lib/etcd to /var/lib/etcd-new</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. kubelet notices the manifest change and recreates the pod — verify</span>
watch crictl ps        <span class="comment"># etcd container restarts</span>
kubectl get pods -A    <span class="comment"># objects are back once etcd is healthy again</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>Verify:</strong> <code>kubectl get ns,deploy -A</code> returns the objects that existed at snapshot time; <code>etcdctl snapshot status</code> before restoring confirmed the file isn't corrupt.</p>
                            <p><strong>Why:</strong> Restoring over the live directory risks a half-written, corrupt state with no way back. A new directory plus a manifest edit is safe, reversible (just point back at the old path), and matches exactly how the exam's grading script checks your work.</p>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🎯 Task 4 — Static Pod</h4>
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ ~4 min · 🎯 Weight ~4%</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Task</h5>
                        <p>Schedule a static pod named <code>jobpost-cache</code> (image <code>redis:7</code>) on a worker node, managed directly by that node's kubelet — not by the scheduler.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">
                        <strong>Hint:</strong> Generate the pod manifest with <code>$do</code>, then copy the file to the node's manifests directory — the kubelet takes it from there.
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>View Solution &amp; Verification</summary>
