                    <h5>③ tmux Panes — Watching State Change While You Edit</h5>
                    <p>A split terminal is not a nice-to-have on the exam — it's how you avoid re-typing <code>kubectl get pods</code> forty times. Splitting panes with <code>tmux</code> also protects you from a specific, real failure mode: if your SSH-into-a-node session drops, a detached tmux session on that node survives and you reattach instead of losing your place.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌─────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┐
   │ PANE 1 (left)                │ PANE 2 (right)               │
   │                              │                              │
   │ $ vim jobpost-web.yaml       │ $ watch -n2 'kubectl get      │
   │   … editing the manifest …   │      pods -n easypj-prod'    │
   │                              │                              │
   │                              │ jobpost-web-7d..  0/1 Pend.  │
   │                              │ jobpost-web-7d..  1/1 Runn.  │  ◀── you SEE
   │                              │                              │      the apply
   │ :wq  → back to shell        │                              │      land, live
   └─────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┘
   Ctrl+b then %   → split vertically      Ctrl+b then "   → split horizontally
   Ctrl+b then arrow-key → move between panes
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>tmux action</th><th>Keys</th><th>Why it's worth learning for exam day</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Split vertically</td><td><code>Ctrl+b %</code></td><td>Edit YAML in one pane, watch <code>get pods -w</code> in the other — no context switching</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Split horizontally</td><td><code>Ctrl+b "</code></td><td>Stack a log tail under your main working pane</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Detach</td><td><code>Ctrl+b d</code></td><td>Leave a session running; reattach with <code>tmux attach</code> if your node SSH drops</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The specific payoff.</strong> A dropped SSH session mid-troubleshooting task (Tasks 15–20's exact scenario) would normally cost you your shell history and any in-progress edits. A detached tmux session on that node survives the drop — you reattach and keep going instead of starting over.</div>

                    <h5>④ The vimrc That Saves You From Indentation Hell</h5>
                    <p>§45.1's <code>~/.vimrc</code> looked like three throwaway lines. It is actually the single fix for the #2 most common way candidates lose points: YAML that looks correct on screen but parses wrong because of mixed tabs/spaces or a cascading auto-indent.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   WITHOUT the vimrc fix                    WITH tabstop=2 shiftwidth=2 expandtab
   ┌──────────────────────────┐             ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ spec:                    │             │ spec:                        │
   │ \tcontainers:             │   pastes    │   containers:                │
   │ \t\t- name: jobpost        │   as REAL   │   - name: jobpost             │
   │ \t\t  image: v2             │   tabs →   │     image: v2                 │
   │                            │   YAML      │                              │
   │ ❌ error converting YAML:  │   parser    │ ✅ parses cleanly, indentation │
   │    found character that   │   REJECTS   │    is real 2-space blocks     │
   │    cannot start any token │   tabs      │                              │
   └──────────────────────────┘             └──────────────────────────────┘
   PLUS: pasting without ":set paste" first cascades autoindent — each line gets
   MORE indented than the line before it, corrupting even correctly-typed YAML.
                    </pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>vimrc setting</th><th>What it fixes</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>set expandtab</code></td><td>Tab key inserts spaces, not a literal tab character — YAML's parser rejects tabs outright</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>set tabstop=2 shiftwidth=2</code></td><td>Matches Kubernetes YAML's own 2-space convention, so pasted and typed content line up</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>:set paste</code> / <code>:set nopaste</code></td><td>Suspends autoindent for exactly the duration of a paste, so a pasted block keeps its original indentation instead of cascading</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Do this once, benefit on every task.</strong> The vimrc is set up once in §45.1 and then silently prevents a whole category of point loss on every single YAML-editing task for the rest of the exam — Tasks 6, 7, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14 in the simulator alone.</div>

                    <h5>⑤ The Allowed Documentation — Four Named Sites, Not a Free-for-All</h5>
