            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch45-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>"Skipping the setup ritual saves time."</strong> Backwards — skipping it costs minutes on <em>every single task afterward</em>. The 5-minute investment has one of the highest returns of anything in this entire guide.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting <code>kubectl config use-context</code> (or the namespace).</strong> You do flawless work in the WRONG cluster and score zero — the grader has no way to know you meant the right one.</li>
                    <li><strong>Perfectionism.</strong> Polishing one task to 100% while three easy ones sit completely untouched is a losing trade almost every time — read §45.6's scoring model again if this tempts you.</li>
                    <li><strong>Hand-writing YAML</strong> instead of generating it with <code>$do</code> — typos and indentation errors are the #1 and #2 point-losers on the entire exam, and both are eliminated by generating instead of typing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting <code>$now</code> on deletes</strong>, or using deprecated API versions (<code>extensions/v1beta1</code> instead of <code>apps/v1</code>) — both silently cost time or fail outright on modern clusters.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pasting YAML into vim without <code>:set paste</code></strong> corrupts the indentation via cascading autoindent — see Under the Hood ④ for exactly why.</li>
                    <li><strong>etcd restore over the LIVE data directory</strong> instead of a fresh one, or forgetting to repoint the static-pod manifest afterward — this is the single highest-stakes mistake in the entire simulator (Task 3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing killer.sh's difficulty with the real exam's.</strong> killer.sh is deliberately harder — a 60–70% there is a healthy signal, not a warning sign (Under the Hood ②).</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming a PVC is shared network storage by default.</strong> <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> means one node at a time, not automatically shared across every pod (Task 14) — a common storage-domain misconception.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

