                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're 90 minutes into the exam with 30 minutes left and 6 tasks remaining, including one 13%-weighted etcd restore task and five smaller ones totaling roughly 20%. How do you allocate the last 30 minutes?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Attempt the five smaller tasks first if they're each under 4 minutes — that's ~20 minutes for ~20% of the remaining points, leaving 10 minutes for the etcd task. If the etcd task alone would take your full remaining time and you're not confident, a partial etcd attempt (snapshot save + status, even without a full restore) still earns partial credit under §45.6's rules — better than skipping it and gambling everything on the five small tasks.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Expected outcome</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Small tasks first, then etcd</td><td>Banks ~20% guaranteed, uses remaining time on the highest-weight item</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>etcd first, small tasks if time remains</td><td>Riskier — a stuck etcd restore can eat all 30 minutes and leave zero time for anything else</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is §45.2's Pass 3 logic in a concrete scenario — quick wins first even at the very end of the clock, because a stuck hard task has unbounded downside while a quick task has a hard time ceiling.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're not 100% sure which cluster context is currently active before starting a new task. What's the fastest way to confirm, without wasting time?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl config current-context</code> — one command, instant answer. Never assume based on what you were just working on; always confirm explicitly before running anything that could mutate state.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">confirm before you act</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config current-context
kubectl config get-contexts | grep '^\*'   <span class="comment"># the asterisk marks the active one</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This one-liner is cheap insurance against the exam's single most expensive mistake (§45.1b) — make checking it as reflexive as checking your namespace.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You realize you just edited a Deployment named <code>jobpost-web</code> in the wrong namespace — there happen to be two Deployments with that exact name in different namespaces. How do you recover?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>First confirm the damage: <code>kubectl get deploy jobpost-web -n &lt;wrong-ns&gt; -o yaml</code> to see what changed. If you have the original values (or can regenerate them with <code>$do</code>), reapply the correct spec to the wrong-namespace object to restore it, then redo your intended edit in the CORRECT namespace this time.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Recovery sequence</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  1. confirm which namespace you actually edited (get -n &lt;ns&gt; -o yaml)
  2. revert that namespace's object (regenerate original spec, re-apply)
  3. redo the SAME edit in the CORRECT namespace, with -n explicit this time
  4. verify BOTH namespaces are now in their intended state
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is exactly why §45.1b insists on setting the namespace explicitly per task rather than relying on the "current" one — same-name objects across namespaces are a real, exam-realistic trap.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A task says "using the kubeconfig at <code>/root/exam/config</code>, perform the following…". How do you work with it without disturbing your default kubeconfig or your setup ritual's aliases?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Point <code>--kubeconfig</code> at the given file explicitly on every command for that task, or export <code>KUBECONFIG</code> for the duration of just that task and unset it afterward — don't overwrite <code>~/.kube/config</code> itself.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">scoped kubeconfig usage</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Option A: per-command flag (safest, no cleanup needed)</span>
k --kubeconfig=/root/exam/config get nodes

<span class="comment"># Option B: scoped export, then unset when the task is done</span>
export KUBECONFIG=/root/exam/config
k get nodes
unset KUBECONFIG    <span class="comment"># back to default for the NEXT task</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Your <code>k</code> alias and completion still work unchanged either way — only the target cluster changes. Forgetting to <code>unset KUBECONFIG</code> is a realistic way to accidentally carry a task-specific config into the NEXT task, so make unsetting it part of your task-completion routine.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You pasted a block of YAML into vim and you're not sure whether it landed as real 2-space indentation or got corrupted with tabs / cascading autoindent. How do you check quickly, without leaving vim?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Run <code>:set list</code> in vim — it reveals tab characters and trailing whitespace visually (tabs show as <code>^I</code>). If you see them, undo the paste, run <code>:set paste</code>, and paste again.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Symptom in <code>:set list</code></th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>^I</code> characters</td><td>real tabs — YAML's parser will reject these</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Indentation that increases every line</td><td>cascading autoindent from a paste without <code>:set paste</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Clean, consistent 2-space blocks</td><td>correctly pasted — safe to save and apply</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Catching a corrupted paste before you <code>apply</code> it saves a full round-trip of "apply, get a cryptic error, debug, retry" — <code>:set list</code> is a 2-second check with a real payoff.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A task asks you to create a StatefulSet with a specific <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> entry. Can you generate this imperatively with <code>$do</code> the way you would a Deployment?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>No — <code>kubectl create</code> has no imperative subcommand for StatefulSets with volume claim templates. The fastest correct path is generating a <em>Deployment's</em> container-spec fields with <code>$do</code> for the boilerplate you DO need (image, ports, env), then hand-adapting <code>kind</code>, adding <code>serviceName</code>, and hand-writing the <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> block, which has no imperative equivalent at all.</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">Where imperative generation stops helping</div>
<div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
  ✅ CAN generate imperatively:  image, ports, env, replicas, labels
  ❌ CANNOT generate imperatively: volumeClaimTemplates, serviceName,
                                    envFrom, probes, NetworkPolicy rules
                                    → these are ALWAYS a hand-edit step
</pre>
</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Knowing exactly which fields <code>$do</code> can and can't produce (this exact list) is what separates "I tried the imperative command and it didn't have that flag, now I'm stuck" from "I expected to hand-edit this part, here it is."</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You finish Pass 1 with 40 minutes elapsed instead of the planned 35, and you've banked 8 of 15 quick-win tasks. Do you adjust your Pass 2/3 budget, and how?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Yes — shave the extra 5 minutes off Pass 2 (drop it from ~50 to ~45 minutes) rather than off Pass 3's verify sweep. Pass 3's final verification is what catches silent mistakes across everything you've already answered; it's the wrong place to cut.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Where to absorb overrun</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>✅ Pass 2 (the bulk)</td><td>Still has the most total minutes to flex; a 5-minute trim rarely costs a whole task</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>❌ Pass 3 (verify sweep)</td><td>Cutting this defeats the entire purpose of a 3-pass strategy — unverified answers are exactly what §45.5's #4 point-loser warns about</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The 3-pass budget in §45.2 is a guideline, not a rigid law — the actual invariant to protect is "verify everything before time runs out," not "hit exactly 35/50/30."</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You scored 62% on a full simulator run — just under the 66% pass line and below this chapter's 75% readiness gate. What's the recommended next step?</div>
                    <details>
