            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Short Names Save Time</h5>
                <p>Use short names on the exam: <code>po</code> (pods), <code>svc</code> (services), <code>deploy</code> (deployments), <code>cm</code> (configmaps), <code>ns</code> (namespaces), <code>no</code> (nodes), <code>pv</code> (persistentvolumes), <code>pvc</code> (persistentvolumeclaims), <code>sa</code> (serviceaccounts), <code>netpol</code> (networkpolicies), <code>ing</code> (ingresses).</p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ <code>--minify</code> vs a Bare <code>config view</code></h5>
                <p>Plain <code>kubectl config view</code> dumps <strong>every</strong> cluster/user/context your kubeconfig knows about — on a multi-cluster exam that's a wall of noise. <code>--minify</code> trims it down to just the current context's entries, which is almost always what you actually want to verify. Combine with <code>-o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.server}'</code> to grab just the API server URL in one line.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.2: Node Management ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.2 Node Management</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Node management is a core CKA skill — you'll cordon, drain, and uncordon nodes during upgrades and maintenance.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Node Maintenance Lifecycle</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    NODE MAINTENANCE WORKFLOW                            │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                         │
  │   ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────┐     │
  │   │  NORMAL  │    │ CORDON   │    │    DRAIN     │    │ UNCORDON │     │
  │   │          │───▶│          │───▶│              │───▶│          │     │
  │   │ Schedul- │    │ No new   │    │ Evict all    │    │ Allow    │     │
  │   │ able ✅  │    │ pods ⛔  │    │ pods 🚀      │    │ pods ✅  │     │
  │   └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────────┘    └──────────┘     │
  │                                          │                              │
  │                                   ┌──────┴──────┐                      │
  │                                   │  PERFORM    │                      │
  │                                   │  MAINTENANCE│                      │
  │                                   │  (upgrade,  │                      │
  │                                   │   reboot)   │                      │
  │                                   └─────────────┘                      │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Node Management Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Effect</th><th>CKA Context</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code></td>
                            <td>List nodes with IP, OS, kernel, container runtime</td>
                            <td>First check during troubleshooting</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Full node details: conditions, capacity, pods, taints</td>
                            <td>Diagnose NotReady, resource pressure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Mark unschedulable (existing pods stay)</td>
                            <td>Pre-maintenance — stop new pods</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code></td>
                            <td>Cordon + evict all pods (except DaemonSets)</td>
                            <td>Safely evacuate before maintenance</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Mark schedulable again</td>
                            <td>After maintenance — resume scheduling</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node&gt; key=value:NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>Add taint — only pods with matching toleration can schedule</td>
                            <td>Isolate nodes for specific workloads</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node&gt; key=value:NoSchedule-</code></td>
                            <td>Remove that taint (note the trailing <code>-</code>)</td>
                            <td>Reopen a node to general scheduling</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl label nodes &lt;node&gt; disk=ssd</code></td>
                            <td>Add label — used with nodeSelector for scheduling</td>
                            <td>Target pods to specific nodes</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl label nodes &lt;node&gt; disk-</code></td>
                            <td>Remove that label (same trailing-<code>-</code> convention as taints)</td>
                            <td>Undo a mis-applied label without retyping the whole command</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get node &lt;node&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.capacity}'</code></td>
                            <td>Raw allocatable capacity: cpu, memory, pods, ephemeral-storage</td>
                            <td>Confirm a node actually has room before scheduling a big workload</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — node maintenance for easypj</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check current node status</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS   ROLES           VERSION   INTERNAL-IP   OS-IMAGE</span>
<span class="comment"># cp-1       Ready    control-plane   v1.31.0   10.0.0.10     Ubuntu 22.04</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   Ready    &lt;none&gt;          v1.31.0   10.0.0.11     Ubuntu 22.04</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2   Ready    &lt;none&gt;          v1.31.0   10.0.0.12     Ubuntu 22.04</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Prepare worker-1 for maintenance</span>
kubectl cordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 cordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Drain it (move easypj pods to worker-2)</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># evicting pod easypj/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-xk2vn</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-xk2vn evicted</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify pods moved</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME                          READY   NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q   1/1     worker-2  ← moved here</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Perform maintenance (upgrade kubelet, etc.)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Bring node back</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 uncordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Bonus: taint/label lifecycle (add, inspect, remove)</span>
kubectl taint nodes worker-1 dedicated=easypj:NoSchedule
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 tainted</span>
kubectl get node worker-1 -o jsonpath='{.status.capacity}'
<span class="comment"># {"cpu":"4","ephemeral-storage":"103080204Ki","memory":"8025168Ki","pods":"110"}</span>
kubectl taint nodes worker-1 dedicated=easypj:NoSchedule-
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 untainted   ← trailing "-" removes it</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧭 Cordon vs Drain — Don't Confuse Them</h5>
                <p><code>cordon</code> = "no <strong>NEW</strong> pods here" — existing pods are left running untouched. <code>drain</code> = <code>cordon</code> <strong>+</strong> evict every existing pod gracefully, so it's the stronger operation you use right before maintenance/an upgrade/a reboot. A common exam trap: expecting <code>cordon</code> alone to empty a node — it never does. And <code>uncordon</code> is the mirror of <code>cordon</code> only, not of <code>drain</code>: it re-enables scheduling but does nothing to bring back the pods that were evicted (their controllers already recreated them elsewhere).</p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Drain Fails? Common Causes</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Unmanaged pods</strong> — Pods not managed by a controller (bare pods). Fix: add <code>--force</code> flag (pods will be deleted, not rescheduled!)</li>
                    <li><strong>PodDisruptionBudget violated</strong> — PDB prevents eviction. Fix: check PDB with <code>kubectl get pdb -A</code>, adjust <code>minAvailable</code></li>
                    <li><strong>emptyDir data loss</strong> — Need <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> flag to acknowledge data loss</li>
                    <li><strong>DaemonSet pods</strong> — Always need <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> (they're present on every node)</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgot the trailing <code>-</code></strong> — <code>kubectl taint nodes worker-1 key=value:NoSchedule</code> run a second time does NOT remove the taint, it errors that the taint already exists for that key+effect. Removal requires the exact key/effect plus a trailing <code>-</code>, e.g. <code>key=value:NoSchedule-</code>. To instead <strong>change</strong> an existing taint's value in place (not remove it), add <code>--overwrite</code>: <code>kubectl taint nodes worker-1 key=newvalue:NoSchedule --overwrite</code>. The same "already has a value, need <code>--overwrite</code>" behavior applies to <code>kubectl label</code> — it's not taint-specific.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.3: Workload Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.3 Workload Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Deployment lifecycle commands — create, scale, update, rollback. These are your bread and butter on the CKA exam.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Deployment Rollout Lifecycle</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  CREATE ──▶ SCALE ──▶ UPDATE ──▶ ROLLBACK
    │          │         │          │
    ▼          ▼         ▼          ▼
  kubectl    kubectl   kubectl    kubectl
  create     scale     set image  rollout undo
  deploy     deploy    deploy/    deploy/
  --image    --rep=5   name       name
    │          │         │          │
    ▼          ▼         ▼          ▼
  1 replica   5 reps   Rolling    Previous
  created     running  update     revision
                       started    restored

  📊 MONITOR: kubectl rollout status deploy/name
  📜 HISTORY: kubectl rollout history deploy/name
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Deployment &amp; Workload Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Effect</th><th>CKA Speed Tip</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3</code></td>
                            <td>Create deployment with 3 replicas</td>
                            <td>Fastest way — no YAML needed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5</code></td>
                            <td>Scale to 5 replicas</td>
                            <td>Instant — no rolling update</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web web=easypj:v2</code></td>
                            <td>Update container image (triggers rolling update)</td>
                            <td>Container name must match spec</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout status deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Watch rollout progress</td>
                            <td>Blocks until complete or failed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Show revision history</td>
                            <td>See all past versions</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web --revision=2</code></td>
                            <td>Show the exact pod template (image, env, resources) that revision 2 used</td>
                            <td>Confirm precisely what you'd be rolling back to before running <code>rollout undo --to-revision=2</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Rollback to previous revision</td>
                            <td>Instant rollback — saves exam time</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web --to-revision=2</code></td>
                            <td>Rollback to specific revision</td>
                            <td>When previous isn't enough</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl autoscale deployment easypj-web --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=80</code></td>
                            <td>Create a HorizontalPodAutoscaler targeting 80% average CPU</td>
                            <td>Fastest way to wire up an HPA — no separate manifest needed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout restart deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Roll every pod, same image (new pod hashes/creation timestamps)</td>
                            <td>Force-refresh pods after a ConfigMap/Secret change (no image bump needed)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout pause deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Freeze the rollout — no new ReplicaSet is created</td>
                            <td>Batch multiple <code>set image</code>/<code>set resources</code> edits into ONE rollout</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout resume deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Un-freeze — all batched changes roll out together</td>
                            <td>Pairs with <code>pause</code> above</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl set resources deploy/easypj-web --limits=cpu=500m,memory=256Mi</code></td>
                            <td>Patch container resource limits without a full YAML edit</td>
                            <td>Fast fix for OOMKilled/CPU-throttled pods</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl patch deploy/easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}'</code></td>
                            <td>Merge an arbitrary partial object into the live resource (default strategy: strategic merge)</td>
                            <td>The general-purpose escape hatch when no <code>set</code>/<code>scale</code>/<code>rollout</code> subcommand covers the field you need to change</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl wait --for=condition=Available deploy/easypj-web --timeout=90s</code></td>
                            <td>Block until a resource reports a given condition (or hits the timeout)</td>
                            <td>Script-friendly alternative to polling <code>rollout status</code> by hand — works on any resource with <code>status.conditions</code>, not just Deployments</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — easypj deployment lifecycle</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create deployment imperatively</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Generate YAML without creating (exam trick!)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Scale up for traffic</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Update to new version (triggers rolling update)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web web=easypj:v2 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Watch the rollout</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "easypj-web" rollout to finish: 2 of 5 updated...</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment "easypj-web" successfully rolled out</span>

<span class="comment"># Something wrong? Rollback!</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web rolled back</span>

<span class="comment"># Autoscale for traffic spikes (needs metrics-server)</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment easypj-web --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=80 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># horizontalpodautoscaler.autoscaling/easypj-web autoscaled</span>

<span class="comment"># Batch several edits into one rollout instead of triggering N rollouts</span>
kubectl rollout pause deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web web=easypj:v3 -n easypj
kubectl set resources deployment/easypj-web --limits=cpu=500m,memory=256Mi -n easypj
<span class="comment"># neither edit above triggered a rollout yet — deployment is paused</span>
kubectl rollout resume deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NOW both changes roll out together, as a single revision</span>

<span class="comment"># ConfigMap changed but the Deployment spec itself didn't — force a refresh</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web restarted</span>

<span class="comment"># patch: default strategy is a strategic merge — good enough for most one-field edits</span>
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -n easypj -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}'
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web patched</span>

<span class="comment"># patch: --type=json for surgical array edits (RFC 6902) — e.g. append one env var</span>
<span class="comment"># without touching any of the others already in the array</span>
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -n easypj --type=json \
  -p '[{"op":"add","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/env/-","value":{"name":"LOG_LEVEL","value":"debug"}}]'

<span class="comment"># wait: block a script until the rollout actually finishes, instead of polling</span>
kubectl wait --for=condition=Available deployment/easypj-web -n easypj --timeout=90s
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web condition met</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p>The <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> combo is your best friend. Use it to generate YAML for any resource, then edit as needed. With the <code>$do</code> alias: <code>k create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; deploy.yaml</code></p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Rollouts That Don't Trigger a Rollout</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>ConfigMap/Secret edits are NOT tracked</strong> by the Deployment spec unless you mount them via a hash-suffixed name or use a tool like Reloader — editing a ConfigMap in place does <em>not</em> automatically roll pods. Use <code>kubectl rollout restart</code> to force it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Paused deployments silently swallow changes</strong> — if a Deployment is stuck <code>paused</code> (often left over from a previous task or a bad script), <code>set image</code>/<code>set resources</code> DO update the spec but nothing rolls out until you <code>rollout resume</code>. Check with <code>kubectl get deploy easypj-web -o jsonpath='{.spec.paused}'</code> when a change "isn't taking effect."</li>
                    <li><strong><code>scale</code> vs <code>autoscale</code></strong> — <code>scale</code> sets a fixed replica count and stays there. <code>autoscale</code> creates an HPA that will override any manual <code>scale</code> command on its next reconcile loop — don't fight the HPA with manual scaling once one exists.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl patch</code>'s three strategies aren't interchangeable</strong> — the default <strong>strategic merge patch</strong> (Kubernetes-aware; knows how to merge lists like <code>containers</code> by name) only works on native API types, not CRDs. A plain <strong>JSON merge patch</strong> (<code>--type=merge</code>, RFC 7386) replaces whole arrays wholesale — patch one env var wrong and you can silently wipe every other one in that list. <strong>JSON Patch</strong> (<code>--type=json</code>, RFC 6902) is the most surgical — explicit <code>add</code>/<code>remove</code>/<code>replace</code> operations by exact array index or path — but a <code>replace</code> against a path/index that doesn't exist yet fails outright instead of creating it.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.4: Debugging Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.4 Debugging Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Debugging is <strong>30% of the CKA exam</strong>. Master these commands — they're your diagnostic toolkit.</p>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p>🩺 <strong>The first three commands on ANY broken pod</strong> — run them in this order, every time, before you think about anything else:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li><code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod&gt; -o wide</code> — state, node, restart count, pod IP</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code> — the <strong>Events</strong> section at the bottom tells you why</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; [-p]</code> — the application's own error message / crash reason</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Pod Debugging Decision Tree</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
                        🔍 Pod Not Working?
                        ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
                                │
               ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
               ▼                ▼                ▼
          Pod Status?      Container        Can't reach
               │           crashing?        the service?
               │                │                │
    ┌──────────┼──────┐        ▼                ▼
    ▼          ▼      ▼   kubectl logs     kubectl get
  Pending  CrashLoop  Image  pod -c ctr    endpoints svc
    │      BackOff    Pull     │                │
    ▼        ▼       BackOff   ▼                ▼
  describe  logs     describe  Fix app      Empty? →
  pod →     pod →    pod →     crash or     Labels don't
  check     check    check     missing      match between
  events    app      image/    config       pod &amp; service
            error    secret
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Debug &amp; Inspect Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Shows</th><th>Debug Scenario</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>Full pod details, events, conditions</td>
                            <td>First stop for ANY pod issue</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>Container stdout/stderr</td>
                            <td>App crash, startup failure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                            <td>Logs from previous (crashed) container</td>
                            <td>CrashLoopBackOff — see WHY it crashed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Logs from specific container in multi-container pod</td>
                            <td>Sidecar / init container issues</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs -l app=easypj-web --all-containers</code></td>
                            <td>Logs from every pod matching a label, every container in each</td>
                            <td>Tail all replicas of a Deployment at once instead of naming pods one by one</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code></td>
                            <td>Open shell inside container</td>
                            <td>Check files, env vars, network from inside</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt; -- env</code></td>
                            <td>One-shot command in a specific container, no interactive shell</td>
                            <td>Fast env/config check without the overhead of <code>-it</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox --target=&lt;container&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Attach an ephemeral debug container sharing the target's process namespace</td>
                            <td><strong>Distroless / no-shell images</strong> — the only way in when the app image has no <code>sh</code>/<code>bash</code> at all</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl debug node/&lt;node&gt; -it --image=busybox</code></td>
                            <td>Launch a privileged debug pod on the node, chroot-able into its filesystem</td>
                            <td>Investigate the node itself (kubelet/containerd state, disk) without SSH</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl port-forward pod/&lt;pod&gt; 8080:8000</code></td>
                            <td>Forward local 8080 → pod 8000</td>
                            <td>Test pod directly without Service</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl port-forward svc/easypj-web 8080:80</code></td>
                            <td>Forward local 8080 → the Service's ClusterIP:80 (Service picks the backing pod)</td>
                            <td>Test the Service's load-balancing / selector without exposing it externally</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=&lt;pod&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Events for specific pod only</td>
                            <td>Targeted event search</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;resource&gt; --as=&lt;user&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Check RBAC permissions</td>
                            <td>Forbidden errors — who can do what?</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod&gt; -o yaml | less</code></td>
                            <td>The pod's complete live spec + status, pageable</td>
                            <td>When <code>describe</code>'s summarized view hides the exact field you need (e.g. a full <code>status.conditions</code> list)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl cp &lt;pod&gt;:/path/in/container ./local/path -c &lt;container&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Copy a file/directory out of (or into) a running container over an <code>exec tar</code> pipe</td>
                            <td>Pull a crash dump, log file, or config off a pod without a shared volume; requires <code>tar</code> to exist inside the container</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl attach -it &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Attach to the container's own PID 1 stdin/stdout/stderr stream (does NOT start a new process, unlike <code>exec</code>)</td>
                            <td>Watch/interact with an app that reads stdin directly, or when the container has no shell for <code>exec</code> to launch</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl debug &lt;pod&gt; -it --copy-to=&lt;pod&gt;-debug --container=&lt;c&gt; --image=busybox -- sh</code></td>
                            <td>Clone the pod, swap one container's image/command in the copy, leave the original untouched</td>
                            <td>Debug a container whose image/command is wrong at the root (crash-on-start) — <code>--target</code>'s ephemeral container can't help if the main container never comes up at all</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl proxy --port=8080</code></td>
                            <td>Open an authenticated local HTTP proxy to the API server (no manual bearer token/cert handling)</td>
                            <td>Hit any API path by hand for debugging, e.g. <code>curl localhost:8080/api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods</code></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — debugging easypj pods</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pod stuck in CrashLoopBackOff — investigate</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  BackOff  kubelet  Back-off restarting failed container</span>

<span class="comment"># Get logs from the crashed container</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash -n easypj --previous
<span class="comment"># ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'easypj.settings'</span>

<span class="comment"># Shell into a running pod to investigate</span>
kubectl exec -it easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q -n easypj -- /bin/sh
<span class="comment"># $ env | grep DB          ← check environment variables</span>
<span class="comment"># $ cat /app/manage.py     ← check files exist</span>
<span class="comment"># $ python manage.py check ← test Django</span>
<span class="comment"># $ exit</span>

<span class="comment"># Test pod connectivity directly</span>
kubectl port-forward pod/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q 8080:8000 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Then: curl http://localhost:8080/jobs/</span>

<span class="comment"># Test the SERVICE instead of one pod (verifies selector + load-balancing)</span>
kubectl port-forward svc/easypj-web 8080:80 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Then: curl http://localhost:8080/jobs/</span>

<span class="comment"># Check who can create pods in easypj namespace</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n easypj --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

<span class="comment"># easypj-web's image is distroless — no shell to exec into! Use an ephemeral debug container instead</span>
kubectl debug -it easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q -n easypj \
  --image=busybox --target=web
<span class="comment"># Defaulting debug container name to debugger-8xqfz.</span>
<span class="comment"># If you don't see a command prompt, try pressing enter.</span>
<span class="comment"># / # ps aux            ← shares the target container's process namespace</span>
<span class="comment"># / # wget -qO- localhost:8000/healthz</span>

<span class="comment"># Node itself won't respond — debug the node directly, no SSH needed</span>
kubectl debug node/worker-1 -it --image=busybox
<span class="comment"># root@worker-1:/# chroot /host   ← now you're inside the node's real filesystem</span>
<span class="comment"># root@worker-1:/# systemctl status kubelet</span>

<span class="comment"># Pull the app's crash dump off a pod for offline inspection (needs tar in-container)</span>
kubectl cp easypj/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash:/var/log/app/crash.log ./crash.log -c web

<span class="comment"># The main container never even starts (bad ENTRYPOINT) — ephemeral debug can't help</span>
<span class="comment"># here because there's no running "web" process to --target. Debug a COPY instead:</span>
kubectl debug easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash -n easypj -it \
  --copy-to=easypj-web-debug --container=web --image=busybox -- sh
<span class="comment"># original pod is untouched; easypj-web-debug is a disposable clone — delete it when done</span>
kubectl delete pod easypj-web-debug -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Hit the API server directly through an authenticated local proxy, no token wrangling</span>
kubectl proxy --port=8080 &amp;
curl -s localhost:8080/api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods | jq '.items[].metadata.name'</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Ephemeral Debug Container — How <code>kubectl debug</code> Attaches</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              POD: easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q                        │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │   ┌───────────────────┐         ┌───────────────────────────────┐   │
  │   │  container: web    │         │  ephemeral: debugger-8xqfz     │   │
  │   │  image: distroless  │◀───────│  image: busybox                │   │
  │   │  (no shell, no ps)  │ shares │  (has ps, wget, sh, curl...)   │   │
  │   │                     │  PID   │  --target=web                  │   │
  │   │  still running,     │  ns    │  can "see" web's processes,    │   │
  │   │  never restarted    │        │  hit its localhost ports       │   │
  │   └───────────────────┘         └───────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                                     │
  │   ⚠️ Ephemeral containers CANNOT be removed once added — they exist  │
  │      until the pod itself is deleted (this is a known K8s limitation)│
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Log &amp; Debug Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Multi-container pods</strong> — If a pod has multiple containers (sidecar pattern), <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code> will fail with <strong>"a container name must be specified"</strong>. Always use <code>-c &lt;container-name&gt;</code>. List containers with: <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Distroless / minimal images have no shell</strong> — <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code> fails with "exec: \"sh\": executable file not found" on hardened production images. This is by design (smaller attack surface). <code>kubectl debug --target=</code> is the sanctioned workaround — it doesn't modify the running container at all.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ephemeral containers can't be removed</strong> — once you <code>kubectl debug</code> a running pod, that debug container's record stays in <code>pod.spec.ephemeralContainers</code> for the pod's lifetime. It's not a problem functionally, but don't be surprised seeing it in <code>describe pod</code> output later.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>port-forward svc/</code> vs <code>port-forward pod/</code></strong> — forwarding to the Service proves the Service's <code>selector</code> correctly matches pod labels and that its <code>targetPort</code> is right; forwarding straight to a pod skips the Service entirely and only proves the container itself is listening. Use both when you're not sure whether the bug is in the app or in the Service wiring.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl cp</code> needs <code>tar</code> inside the container</strong> — it works by running <code>tar</code> in the container and streaming the result, not by any special kubelet API. A distroless/scratch image with no <code>tar</code> binary makes <code>cp</code> fail exactly like <code>exec -- sh</code> does; fall back to <code>kubectl debug --copy-to</code> with a sidecar that has <code>tar</code>, or mount a shared volume instead.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>--copy-to</code> pods are real, separate objects</strong> — unlike an ephemeral debug container, a <code>kubectl debug --copy-to</code> clone is its own Pod with its own name; it is NOT auto-deleted when you exit the shell. Clean it up yourself (<code>kubectl delete pod &lt;name&gt;-debug</code>) or it keeps consuming node resources indefinitely — an easy thing to forget mid-exam.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.5: RBAC Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.5 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-5">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.5 RBAC Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) commands for managing who can do what in your cluster. These are frequently tested on the CKA exam.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>RBAC Components — The Permission Chain</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    RBAC PERMISSION MODEL                           │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                    │
  │   WHO (Subject)         BINDING              WHAT (Role)           │
  │   ──────────────    ─────────────────    ──────────────────        │
  │                                                                    │
  │   👤 User            RoleBinding           Role                    │
  │   👥 Group           (namespace-scoped)    (namespace-scoped)      │
  │   🤖 ServiceAccount  ─────────────────    ──────────────────       │
  │                      ClusterRoleBinding    ClusterRole              │
  │                      (cluster-wide)        (cluster-wide)          │
  │                                                                    │
  │   Example for easypj:                                              │
  │   ┌────────────┐  ┌─────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐       │
  │   │ easypj-sa  │──│ easypj-binding  │──│ easypj-role      │       │
  │   │ (SA)       │  │ (RoleBinding)   │  │ get,list pods    │       │
  │   └────────────┘  └─────────────────┘  │ in ns: easypj   │       │
  │                                         └──────────────────┘       │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>RBAC Imperative Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Creates</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create role easypj-role --verb=get,list --resource=pods -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>Namespace-scoped Role</td>
                            <td>easypj namespace only</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create clusterrole node-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=nodes</code></td>
                            <td>Cluster-wide ClusterRole</td>
                            <td>All namespaces</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create rolebinding dev-binding --role=easypj-role --user=jane -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>RoleBinding (links a human User → Role)</td>
                            <td>easypj namespace — same command shape as the SA version, just <code>--user=</code> instead of <code>--serviceaccount=</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create rolebinding easypj-binding --role=easypj-role --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>RoleBinding (links SA → Role)</td>
                            <td>easypj namespace</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create clusterrolebinding admin-binding --clusterrole=cluster-admin --user=admin</code></td>
                            <td>ClusterRoleBinding</td>
                            <td>Cluster-wide</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=&lt;user&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Lists all permissions for that user</td>
                            <td>Debug RBAC issues for a human account</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa</code></td>
                            <td>Lists all permissions for that SA</td>
                            <td>Debug RBAC issues for a workload identity</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl auth can-i create deployments --namespace dev --as dev-user</code></td>
                            <td>Single yes/no answer for one specific verb+resource+namespace+identity combo</td>
                            <td>Fastest possible RBAC check when you already know exactly what to test</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧭 Role vs ClusterRole &nbsp;/&nbsp; RoleBinding vs ClusterRoleBinding</h5>
                <p><code>Role</code> = a set of permissions, valid in <strong>one namespace only</strong>. <code>ClusterRole</code> = a set of permissions that is either <strong>cluster-wide</strong> (e.g. permissions on nodes, which aren't namespaced at all) <em>or</em> a reusable definition you intend to bind per-namespace. <code>RoleBinding</code> = grants a subject a Role's (or a ClusterRole's!) permissions, but the grant itself only applies <strong>inside one namespace</strong>. <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code> = grants a ClusterRole's permissions <strong>everywhere</strong>. The trick to remember: a <code>ClusterRole</code> bound by a <code>RoleBinding</code> is a completely valid and common pattern — e.g. reusing a built-in <code>view</code> ClusterRole but only in the <code>easypj</code> namespace, instead of writing a namespace-specific Role from scratch.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — RBAC setup for easypj</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ServiceAccount for easypj app</span>
kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Create Role: can read pods and services</span>
kubectl create role easypj-role \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=pods,services \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Bind the role to the ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-binding \
  --role=easypj-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Verify permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods -n easypj \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n easypj \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa
<span class="comment"># no  ← correct! We only granted get,list,watch</span>

<span class="comment"># List ALL permissions for the SA</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Bind the SAME Role to a human user (developer) too — identical pattern, --user instead of --serviceaccount</span>
kubectl create rolebinding jane-binding \
  --role=easypj-role \
  --user=jane \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># One-shot check: can dev-user create deployments in namespace "dev"?</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments --namespace dev --as dev-user
<span class="comment"># no</span>

<span class="comment"># Reuse a built-in ClusterRole, but scope the grant to ONE namespace via a RoleBinding</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-viewers \
  --clusterrole=view \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Now easypj-sa can view (get/list/watch) almost everything, but ONLY inside the easypj namespace —</span>
<span class="comment"># even though "view" is a ClusterRole, binding it with a RoleBinding contains its scope.</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ RBAC Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>ClusterRole + RoleBinding = scoped grant</strong> — this combination is easy to misread as "cluster-wide" because the word ClusterRole is right there. It is NOT: a RoleBinding always contains the grant to its own namespace, no matter what kind of Role it references.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>--user=</code> creates no actual User object</strong> — Kubernetes has no native User resource. RBAC subjects for humans are just strings that must match whatever your authentication method (client cert CN, OIDC claim, webhook) presents. <code>kubectl create rolebinding --user=jane</code> only creates the binding; "jane" must already be an authenticated identity from some other mechanism (see A.6 for the certificate-based path).</li>
                    <li><strong>RBAC is additive-only, deny rules don't exist</strong> — you cannot write a Role that subtracts a permission granted elsewhere. If a subject is over-permissioned via one binding, the fix is to remove/narrow that binding, not to add a "deny" rule.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.6: Certificate Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.6 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-6">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.6 Certificate Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Kubernetes uses TLS certificates extensively. Certificate management — checking expiry, renewing, and inspecting certs — is a core CKA topic.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Kubernetes Certificate Architecture</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              KUBERNETES PKI — /etc/kubernetes/pki/                  │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                     │
  │  📁 /etc/kubernetes/pki/                                            │
  │  ├── ca.crt + ca.key               ← Cluster CA (root of trust)    │
  │  ├── apiserver.crt + apiserver.key  ← API Server cert               │
  │  ├── apiserver-kubelet-client.crt   ← API → kubelet auth           │
  │  ├── front-proxy-ca.crt            ← Aggregation layer CA          │
  │  ├── sa.key + sa.pub               ← ServiceAccount signing        │
  │  └── etcd/                                                          │
  │      ├── ca.crt + ca.key           ← etcd CA (separate!)           │
  │      ├── server.crt + server.key   ← etcd server cert              │
  │      ├── peer.crt + peer.key       ← etcd peer-to-peer             │
  │      └── healthcheck-client.crt    ← etcd health check             │
  │                                                                     │
  │  ⏰ Default validity: 1 year (except CA: 10 years)                  │
  │  🔄 kubeadm certs renew all ← renews all 1-year certs              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Certificate Management Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Does</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></td>
                            <td>Show expiry dates for all certs</td>
                            <td>Regular health check / before renewal</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></td>
                            <td>Renew all kubeadm-managed certificates</td>
                            <td>Before expiry or after upgrade</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubeadm certs renew apiserver</code></td>
                            <td>Renew just ONE named certificate</td>
                            <td>Faster, more surgical fix when only one cert is near expiry — see <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> for the exact name to pass</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>openssl x509 -in &lt;cert&gt; -text -noout</code></td>
                            <td>Inspect certificate details (CN, SANs, dates)</td>
                            <td>Verify specific cert properties</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>openssl x509 -in &lt;cert&gt; -noout -dates</code></td>
                            <td>Just the <code>notBefore</code>/<code>notAfter</code> dates — no other output</td>
                            <td>Fast expiry check without wading through the full <code>-text</code> dump</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>openssl req -in &lt;csr&gt; -text -noout</code></td>
                            <td>Decode a Certificate Signing Request (not a cert — the request that precedes one)</td>
                            <td>Verify the requested CN/O (which map to RBAC username/group) before approving it</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get csr</code></td>
                            <td>List pending/approved/denied CertificateSigningRequest objects</td>
                            <td>See who is asking the cluster CA to sign them a client cert</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl certificate approve &lt;csr-name&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Approve a CSR — the cluster CA signs it, producing a usable client cert</td>
                            <td>Onboard a new user's kubectl access without hand-running <code>openssl ca</code></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — certificate management</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check expiration dates for ALL certs</span>
sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client      Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager.conf    Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-healthcheck-client    Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-peer                  Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-server                Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client         Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf             Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># CA                         Dec 12, 2035 10:30 UTC   10y  ← CA lasts 10 years</span>

<span class="comment"># Renew all certificates</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># [renew] Reading configuration from the cluster...</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver-kubelet-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Only ONE cert is close to expiring — renew just that one instead of all 10</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>

<span class="comment"># IMPORTANT: static Pods don't reload certs on their own — force a restart.</span>
<span class="comment"># Dynamic cert reload isn't supported for the API server/controller-manager/</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler, and kubectl can't delete a static Pod (kubelet owns it, not the</span>
<span class="comment"># API server). Move its manifest out and back to force kubelet to recreate it:</span>
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># wait ~20s for kubelet to notice the Pod is gone, then move it back</span>
sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># repeat for kube-controller-manager.yaml and kube-scheduler.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Equivalent alternative: delete the running container directly via crictl —</span>
<span class="comment"># the kubelet notices it's gone and recreates it from the (now-renewed) manifest</span>
sudo crictl ps --name kube-apiserver -q | xargs -r sudo crictl rm -f

<span class="comment"># Inspect a specific certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A2 "Validity"
<span class="comment">#     Validity</span>
<span class="comment">#         Not Before: Dec 15 10:30:00 2025 GMT</span>
<span class="comment">#         Not After : Dec 15 10:30:00 2026 GMT</span>

<span class="comment"># Same info, one line, no grep needed</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -noout -dates
<span class="comment"># notBefore=Dec 15 10:30:00 2025 GMT</span>
<span class="comment"># notAfter=Dec 15 10:30:00 2026 GMT</span>

<span class="comment"># Check Subject Alternative Names (SANs)</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A1 "Subject Alternative"
<span class="comment">#     X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:</span>
<span class="comment">#         DNS:cp-1, DNS:kubernetes, DNS:kubernetes.default, IP:10.96.0.1, IP:10.0.0.10</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ After Renewing Certs</h5>
                <p>You <strong>must manually restart</strong> the static control-plane Pods after renewing certs, because dynamic certificate reload isn't supported for the API server, controller-manager, or scheduler — and <code>kubectl delete pod</code> won't work on them since the local <strong>kubelet</strong> owns static Pods, not the API server. Force a restart either by moving each Pod's manifest out of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and back (kubelet notices the file disappear/reappear and recreates the Pod), or by removing its running container directly with <code>crictl rm</code> (kubelet recreates it from the still-present manifest). Merely running <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> is <strong>not</strong> a reliable substitute — on restart the kubelet typically finds the containers already running and leaves them alone, so the old (expired) certs can stay loaded in memory. Also refresh any <em>copied</em> kubeconfigs: <code>kubelet.conf</code>'s client cert auto-rotates on its own, but <code>admin.conf</code>, <code>controller-manager.conf</code>, and <code>scheduler.conf</code> do not — their embedded certs only get new bytes on disk from <code>kubeadm certs renew</code>, so any file you already copied out (e.g. to <code>~/.kube/config</code>) is now stale and must be re-copied.</p>
            </div>

            <p>Beyond renewing the cluster's own certs, the CKA exam also tests <strong>onboarding a new user</strong> via the Kubernetes CertificateSigningRequest (CSR) API — the built-in way to get a client cert signed by the cluster CA without ever touching the CA's private key yourself.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>CSR Onboarding Workflow — New User Gets kubectl Access</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  1. USER generates a keypair + CSR locally (never sends the private key) │
  │     openssl genrsa -out jane.key 2048                                    │
  │     openssl req -new -key jane.key -out jane.csr -subj "/CN=jane/O=dev"  │
  │                                    │                                     │
  │                                    ▼                                    │
  │  2. ADMIN wraps the CSR in a CertificateSigningRequest object            │
  │     and submits it to the API server                                     │
  │                                    │                                     │
  │                                    ▼                                    │
  │  3. kubectl get csr           →   STATUS: Pending                        │
  │                                    │                                     │
  │                                    ▼                                    │
  │  4. kubectl certificate approve jane-csr                                 │
  │                                    │                                     │
  │                                    ▼  cluster CA signs it                │
  │  5. kubectl get csr -o jsonpath='{...status.certificate}' | base64 -d    │
  │     → jane.crt  (a real, cluster-trusted client certificate)             │
  │                                    │                                     │
  │                                    ▼                                    │
  │  6. jane.crt + jane.key added to jane's kubeconfig — RBAC (A.5) then     │
  │     decides what CN=jane is actually allowed to DO                      │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — CSR-based onboarding for a new easypj developer</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Jane generates her own keypair + CSR (private key never leaves her machine)</span>
openssl genrsa -out jane.key 2048
openssl req -new -key jane.key -out jane.csr -subj "/CN=jane/O=easypj-dev"

<span class="comment"># Decode the CSR to double-check what's actually being requested</span>
openssl req -in jane.csr -text -noout | grep Subject:
<span class="comment"># Subject: CN=jane, O=easypj-dev</span>

<span class="comment"># Admin wraps it in a CertificateSigningRequest object and applies it</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
  name: jane-csr
spec:
  request: $(cat jane.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n')
  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
  usages: [client auth]
EOF

<span class="comment"># See it waiting for a decision</span>
kubectl get csr
<span class="comment"># NAME       AGE   SIGNERNAME                            REQUESTOR      CONDITION</span>
<span class="comment"># jane-csr   5s    kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client    kubernetes-admin   Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># Approve it — the cluster CA signs the CSR</span>
kubectl certificate approve jane-csr
<span class="comment"># certificatesigningrequest.certificates.k8s.io/jane-csr approved</span>

<span class="comment"># Pull out the signed certificate</span>
kubectl get csr jane-csr -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d &gt; jane.crt

<span class="comment"># Add it to Jane's kubeconfig — RBAC (Role/RoleBinding, see A.5) decides what she can DO</span>
kubectl config set-credentials jane --client-certificate=jane.crt --client-key=jane.key</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ CSR Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Approving a CSR ≠ granting permissions</strong> — <code>kubectl certificate approve</code> only proves identity (the cert is now cluster-trusted). It grants ZERO permissions on its own. You must still create a Role/RoleBinding (A.5) for that CN before the user can do anything beyond authenticate.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>spec.request</code> must be base64-encoded WITHOUT line breaks</strong> — a raw multi-line base64 blob (the default output of <code>base64</code> on some systems wraps at 76 chars) fails validation. Always pipe through <code>tr -d '\n'</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>The default signer expires client certs in 1 year and does NOT auto-renew</strong> — unlike a kubelet's own client cert, a CSR-issued user cert is a fire-and-forget artifact. Track its expiry the same way you'd track any other cert with <code>openssl x509 -noout -dates</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.7: etcd Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.7 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-7">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.7 etcd Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>etcd backup and restore is <strong>guaranteed on the CKA exam</strong>. Every command requires TLS flags — memorize the cert paths.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>etcd Backup &amp; Restore Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                 etcd BACKUP &amp; RESTORE WORKFLOW                     │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                     │
  │  📸 BACKUP                          🔄 RESTORE                      │
  │  ──────────                         ─────────                       │
  │                                                                     │
  │  etcdctl snapshot save              etcdctl snapshot restore        │
  │       │                                   │                         │
  │       ▼                                   ▼                         │
  │  /opt/backup/                        /var/lib/etcd-restored/        │
  │  etcd-snapshot.db                    (new data dir)                 │
  │       │                                   │                         │
  │       ▼                                   ▼                         │
  │  etcdctl snapshot                    Update etcd pod manifest:      │
  │  status (verify)                     --data-dir=/var/lib/           │
  │                                      etcd-restored                  │
  │                                           │                         │
  │                                           ▼                         │
  │  ⚠️ ALL COMMANDS REQUIRE:            Restart: etcd auto-restarts    │
  │  --cacert, --cert, --key             from static pod manifest       │
  │  + ETCDCTL_API=3                                                    │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>etcd Admin Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th><th>CKA Exam Note</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save &lt;file&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Create backup snapshot</td>
                            <td>Always specify full path: <code>/opt/backup/snapshot.db</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore &lt;file&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Restore from snapshot</td>
                            <td>Use <code>--data-dir</code> to set new directory</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status &lt;file&gt; --write-table</code></td>
                            <td>Verify backup integrity</td>
                            <td>Shows hash, revision, total keys</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list</code></td>
                            <td>List etcd cluster members</td>
                            <td>Verify HA etcd cluster health</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list --write-out=table</code></td>
                            <td>Same member list, rendered as an aligned ASCII table</td>
                            <td>Much easier to scan than the raw default output on a 3+ member cluster</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health</code></td>
                            <td>Quick "is this endpoint alive?" check (healthy/unhealthy + latency)</td>
                            <td>Fastest first command when etcd itself is suspected unhealthy — faster than a full <code>member list</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint status --write-out=table</code></td>
                            <td>Per-endpoint detail: leader flag, raft term/index, DB size, errors</td>
                            <td>Find which member is the current Raft leader, or spot one that's fallen behind</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — etcd backup &amp; restore (CKA exam)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══ STEP 1: BACKUP ═══
<span class="comment"># The TLS flags are MANDATORY — memorize these paths!</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379
<span class="comment"># Snapshot saved at /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ STEP 2: VERIFY BACKUP ═══</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --write-table
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | 3c5d8a2f |    48291 |       1043 |     4.1 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ STEP 3: RESTORE ═══</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># ═══ STEP 4: UPDATE ETCD MANIFEST ═══</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit the static pod manifest:</span>
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># Change: --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># To:     --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># ALSO update the hostPath volume:</span>
<span class="comment"># path: /var/lib/etcd → path: /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># etcd will auto-restart with restored data</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ VERIFY ═══</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># Shows: member ID, name, peer URLs, client URLs</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ BONUS: HA ETCD CLUSTER HEALTH CHECKS ═══</span>
<span class="comment"># NOTE: this is a SEPARATE hypothetical 3-control-plane HA cluster, not the</span>
<span class="comment"># single-CP cp-1/worker-1/worker-2 cluster used elsewhere in this appendix —</span>
<span class="comment"># cp-1/cp-2/cp-3 below are three control-plane nodes each running stacked etcd,</span>
<span class="comment"># never worker nodes (etcd only ever runs on control-plane nodes in-tree)</span>
<span class="comment"># Fast alive/dead check across all endpoints</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health \
  --endpoints=https://10.0.0.20:2379,https://10.0.0.21:2379,https://10.0.0.22:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># https://10.0.0.20:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 12ms</span>
<span class="comment"># https://10.0.0.21:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 14ms</span>
<span class="comment"># https://10.0.0.22:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 11ms</span>

<span class="comment"># Which member is the Raft leader? Detailed per-endpoint status</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint status --write-out=table \
  --endpoints=https://10.0.0.20:2379,https://10.0.0.21:2379,https://10.0.0.22:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># +----------------------+------------------+---------+---------+-----------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |       ENDPOINT       |        ID        | VERSION | DB SIZE | IS LEADER |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------------------+------------------+---------+---------+-----------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | https://10.0.0.20:2379 | 8211f1d0f64f3269 | 3.5.9   | 4.2 MB  |    true   |</span>
<span class="comment"># | https://10.0.0.21:2379 | 91bc3c398fb3c146 | 3.5.9   | 4.2 MB  |   false   |</span>
<span class="comment"># | https://10.0.0.22:2379 | fd422379fda50e48 | 3.5.9   | 4.2 MB  |   false   |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------------------+------------------+---------+---------+-----------+</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ The 3 Cert Flags — Don't Forget Any!</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</code> — etcd CA certificate</li>
                    <li><code>--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</code> — etcd server certificate</li>
                    <li><code>--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code> — etcd server private key</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Also: prefix commands with <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> out of habit. On any etcdctl shipped with etcd <strong>3.4 or later</strong> (every current kubeadm cluster, including the 3.5.x used in this appendix's examples) the v3 API is already the default, so it's technically a no-op — but it's still the CKA curriculum's documented convention, costs nothing to type, and protects you on the rare older/custom image where v2 is still the default. What genuinely <em>will</em> break your command if you get it wrong is the <strong>cert flags</strong> above — those are never optional.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Restore Creates a NEW Data Directory</h5>
                <p>Never restore into the existing <code>/var/lib/etcd</code> directory. Always use a new path like <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>, then update the etcd static pod manifest to point to the new directory. This prevents data corruption and allows you to keep the old data as a safety net.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.8: Helm & Kustomize ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.8 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-8">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.8 Helm &amp; Kustomize</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>The CKA exam curriculum lists Helm and Kustomize under "Understand and use both Helm and Kustomize to install cluster components." You will not be asked to author a chart from scratch — you'll be asked to <strong>install, upgrade, roll back, and inspect</strong> a package that's already given to you, and to render or apply a Kustomize overlay. Both tools solve the same underlying problem — "don't hand-write 15 near-identical YAML files per environment" — but they solve it very differently.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Helm vs Kustomize — Two Philosophies for the Same Problem</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    PACKAGING &amp; CONFIG-MANAGEMENT MODELS                  │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                         │
  │   HELM (templating + release lifecycle)                                │
  │   ─────────────────────────────────────                                │
  │   Chart.yaml + values.yaml + templates/*.yaml (Go templates)           │
  │        │                                                               │
  │        ▼  helm install/upgrade                                        │
  │   Tiller-free (Helm 3) → API server ──▶ a tracked "Release"            │
  │        │                                                               │
  │        ▼                                                               │
  │   Release history (helm history) ──▶ helm rollback &lt;rev&gt;              │
  │                                                                         │
  │   KUSTOMIZE (template-free overlays, built into kubectl -k)            │
  │   ─────────────────────────────────────                                │
  │   base/  (deployment.yaml, service.yaml, kustomization.yaml)           │
  │        │                                                               │
  │        ▼  overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml patches the base            │
  │   patchesStrategicMerge / patches / images / replicas / namePrefix     │
  │        │                                                               │
  │        ▼  kubectl apply -k ./overlays/prod                             │
  │   Plain YAML applied straight to the API server — NO release object    │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Helm Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Effect</th><th>CKA / Real-World Note</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm repo add &lt;name&gt; &lt;url&gt; &amp;&amp; helm repo update</code></td>
                            <td>Register a chart repository and refresh its index</td>
                            <td>Always <code>repo update</code> right after <code>repo add</code> — a stale index is the #1 "chart not found" cause</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm search repo &lt;keyword&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Search chart names/versions in added repos</td>
                            <td>Find the exact chart name before <code>install</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm install &lt;release&gt; &lt;chart&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; --create-namespace</code></td>
                            <td>Install a chart as a named Release</td>
                            <td><code>--create-namespace</code> saves a separate <code>kubectl create ns</code> step</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm install &lt;release&gt; &lt;chart&gt; -f values.yaml --set image.tag=1.1</code></td>
                            <td>Install with overridden values (file + CLI flag)</td>
                            <td><code>--set</code> wins over <code>-f values.yaml</code> when both touch the same key</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm upgrade &lt;release&gt; &lt;chart&gt; --reuse-values --set replicas=3</code></td>
                            <td>Upgrade, keeping all previously-set values except the one overridden</td>
                            <td>Without <code>--reuse-values</code>, an upgrade RESETS every value to chart defaults</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm upgrade --install &lt;release&gt; &lt;chart&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Install if absent, upgrade if present — idempotent</td>
                            <td>The exam-safe / GitOps-safe pattern — never fails on "already exists"</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm rollback &lt;release&gt; &lt;revision&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Revert the release to a prior numbered revision</td>
                            <td>Mirrors <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> but at the release level (all resources the chart owns)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm history &lt;release&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>List every revision with status &amp; description</td>
                            <td>Find the revision number to roll back to</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm list -A</code></td>
                            <td>List every release in every namespace</td>
                            <td>"What's installed on this cluster?" — first troubleshooting step</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm uninstall &lt;release&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Delete the release and (by default) all its resources</td>
                            <td>Add <code>--keep-history</code> to keep the revision record for a possible reinstall</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm template &lt;release&gt; &lt;chart&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Render the chart to plain YAML — does NOT talk to the cluster</td>
                            <td>Safe dry-run to review output before install; great for diffing chart versions</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>helm get manifest &lt;release&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Show the exact YAML currently deployed by a release</td>
                            <td>"What did Helm actually apply?" — debugging drift between chart source and live state</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — installing easypj with Helm</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add the chart repo and refresh the index</span>
helm repo add easypj-charts https://charts.easypj.example.com
helm repo update

<span class="comment"># Search for the chart</span>
helm search repo easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME                    CHART VERSION   APP VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-charts/easypj   2.3.0           1.0</span>

<span class="comment"># Install into its own namespace, overriding the image tag</span>
helm install easypj-web easypj-charts/easypj \
  -n easypj --create-namespace \
  --set image.tag=1.0 --set replicaCount=3
<span class="comment"># NAME: easypj-web</span>
<span class="comment"># STATUS: deployed</span>
<span class="comment"># REVISION: 1</span>

<span class="comment"># Upgrade to a new image, keeping every other value as-is</span>
helm upgrade easypj-web easypj-charts/easypj \
  -n easypj --reuse-values --set image.tag=1.1

<span class="comment"># Something's wrong with 1.1 — roll back to revision 1</span>
helm history easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># REVISION   STATUS       DESCRIPTION</span>
<span class="comment"># 1          superseded   Install complete</span>
<span class="comment"># 2          deployed     Upgrade complete</span>
helm rollback easypj-web 1 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Rollback was a success!</span>

<span class="comment"># See exactly what's running right now</span>
helm get manifest easypj-web -n easypj | head -20</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Kustomize Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Effect</th><th>CKA / Real-World Note</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl apply -k ./overlays/prod</code></td>
                            <td>Build the overlay and apply the result to the cluster</td>
                            <td>The exam almost always tests <code>-k</code> directly, not the standalone <code>kustomize</code> binary</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl kustomize ./overlays/prod</code></td>
                            <td>Render the final YAML to stdout — does NOT apply</td>
                            <td>Preview before applying, or pipe into <code>kubectl diff -f -</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl delete -k ./overlays/prod</code></td>
                            <td>Delete every resource the overlay would produce</td>
                            <td>Clean teardown of an environment in one command</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">easypj/ — a minimal base + overlay layout</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash">easypj/
├── base/
│   ├── deployment.yaml
│   ├── service.yaml
│   └── kustomization.yaml        <span class="comment"># resources: [deployment.yaml, service.yaml]</span>
└── overlays/
    ├── dev/
    │   └── kustomization.yaml    <span class="comment"># bases: [../../base]  replicas: 1  namePrefix: dev-</span>
    └── prod/
        └── kustomization.yaml    <span class="comment"># bases: [../../base]  replicas: 5  images: [easypj:1.1]</span>

<span class="comment"># Preview the prod overlay before touching the cluster</span>
kubectl kustomize ./overlays/prod

<span class="comment"># Apply it for real</span>
kubectl apply -k ./overlays/prod
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/prod-easypj-web created</span>
<span class="comment"># service/prod-easypj-web created</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Helm &amp; Kustomize Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Forgot <code>helm repo update</code></strong> — <code>helm install</code> fails with "chart not found" even though you just added the repo. The local index cache is stale until you update it.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>helm upgrade</code> without <code>--reuse-values</code></strong> — silently resets every value you previously overrode with <code>--set</code> back to the chart's defaults. Always pair <code>upgrade</code> with either <code>--reuse-values</code> or a full <code>-f values.yaml</code> that has everything you need.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl apply -k</code> vs a plain directory</strong> — <code>kubectl apply -f ./overlays/prod</code> (no <code>-k</code>) tries to apply the raw <code>kustomization.yaml</code> as if it were a resource and fails. The <code>-k</code> flag is mandatory for Kustomize.</li>
                    <li><strong>No release tracking with Kustomize</strong> — unlike Helm, there is no "rollback" command. Kustomize output is plain declarative YAML; your rollback strategy is <code>git revert</code> the overlay + re-apply, or <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> on the individual Deployment it produced.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 When to Reach for Which</h5>
                <p>Installing a third-party component (ingress-nginx, cert-manager, Prometheus stack) → <strong>Helm</strong> — it ships as a chart with a real release lifecycle. Managing your <em>own</em> app's per-environment differences (dev has 1 replica, prod has 5; dev uses a debug image, prod doesn't) → <strong>Kustomize</strong> — no templating language to learn, and it's already built into <code>kubectl</code> (no extra binary required on the exam's bastion host).</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.9: Speed Kit ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.9 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-9">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.9 Speed Kit (Aliases, $do, $now, Completion, JSONPath)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>The CKA is a <strong>timed, hands-on</strong> exam — roughly 15-20 tasks in 2 hours, which means under 7 minutes per task on average. You are not scored on typing speed directly, but every second spent typing <code>kubernetes</code> instead of <code>k</code> is a second not spent verifying your answer. This section is the single highest-leverage 5 minutes you can spend before the exam clock starts.</p>

            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Set These Up FIRST — Before Reading Question 1</h5>
                <p>The exam terminal is a fresh shell every time. Nothing here persists between sessions unless you put it in your own <code>~/.bashrc</code> ahead of time (which the exam permits, since it's your own environment/VM, not the remote cluster). Muscle memory means typing this setup block takes under 30 seconds — do it before you even open the question list.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — the exam-day speed kit</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Short alias + tab-completion for it</span>
alias k=kubectl
complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k

<span class="comment"># Generate-manifest and force-delete shortcuts</span>
export do='--dry-run=client -o yaml'
export now='--force --grace-period=0'

<span class="comment"># Make `kubectl edit` / `k edit` open in vim, not the default (often nano/ed)</span>
export KUBE_EDITOR=vim

<span class="comment"># Load kubectl's own bash completion (needed for `k` completion above to work)</span>
source &lt;(kubectl completion bash)</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>The $do Pattern — Generate, Don't Hand-Write</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │           GENERATE-THEN-EDIT  (fast, correct, exam-safe)             │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                      │
  │   k run nginx --image=nginx $do &gt; pod.yaml                          │
  │        │                                                            │
  │        ▼  kubectl builds the FULL valid manifest for you             │
  │   apiVersion, kind, metadata, spec — zero indentation typos          │
  │        │                                                            │
  │        ▼  open pod.yaml, add ONLY what the generator can't infer     │
  │   e.g. resources.limits, env vars, volumeMounts, securityContext     │
  │        │                                                            │
  │        ▼                                                            │
  │   kubectl apply -f pod.yaml                                          │
  │                                                                      │
  │   ── vs ──                                                          │
  │                                                                      │
  │   HAND-WRITING YAML FROM MEMORY  (slow, typo-prone, exam-risky)      │
  │   One bad indent → "error validating data" → wasted minutes          │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — generate-then-edit for easypj resources</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># A bare pod (rarely what you want, but the fastest smoke test)</span>
k run nginx --image=nginx $do &gt; pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># The real pattern: a Deployment for easypj</span>
k create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj/jobpost:1.0 --replicas=3 $do &gt; deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># A ClusterIP Service exposing it — generators exist for services too</span>
k create svc clusterip easypj-web --tcp=80:8080 $do &gt; svc.yaml

<span class="comment"># Instantly kill a stuck pod (skips the graceful termination period)</span>
k delete pod easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash $now
<span class="comment"># pod "easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash" force deleted</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>JSONPath &amp; Custom-Columns — Common Exam Outputs</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>All node internal IPs</td>
                            <td><code>k get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}'</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Pod → Node mapping</td>
                            <td><code>k get pods -o custom-columns=POD:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>All images used in a namespace</td>
                            <td><code>k get pods -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.spec.containers[*].image}{"\n"}{end}'</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Sort pods by restart count</td>
                            <td><code>k get pods --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Container ready states</td>
                            <td><code>k get pods -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.containerStatuses[*].ready}'</code></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 JSONPath Cheat-Sheet</h5>
                <p><code>{.items[*]}</code> = every item in a list &nbsp;•&nbsp; <code>{.metadata.name}</code> = one field &nbsp;•&nbsp; <code>{range …}{end}</code> = loop over a list, emitting a template per item &nbsp;•&nbsp; <code>{"\n"}</code> / <code>{"\t"}</code> = literal newline/tab (jsonpath emits nothing by default between items) &nbsp;•&nbsp; <code>[?(@.type=="InternalIP")]</code> = filter an array by a field's value. Always <strong>single-quote</strong> the whole expression in bash — double quotes let the shell eat the inner <code>"</code> characters.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — context / namespace fast-switch</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Set the namespace once, stop typing -n on every command</span>
k config set-context --current --namespace=easypj

<span class="comment"># Switch clusters (every question tells you which context to use)</span>
k config use-context k8s-cluster-1

<span class="comment"># List all available contexts + see which is current</span>
k config get-contexts
<span class="comment"># CURRENT   NAME              CLUSTER           AUTHINFO</span>
<span class="comment"># *         k8s-cluster-1     k8s-cluster-1     admin</span>
<span class="comment">#           k8s-cluster-2     k8s-cluster-2     admin</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>Set the namespace once with <code>set-context --current --namespace=X</code> instead of typing <code>-n X</code> on every single command for the rest of the task.</li>
                    <li><code>k explain pod.spec.containers --recursive</code> recalls any field path with <strong>zero internet access</strong> — this is your offline schema reference when you blank on an exact field name (e.g. is it <code>livenessProbe.httpGet.path</code> or <code>.httpProbe.path</code>?).</li>
                    <li><code>$do</code> + a generator beats hand-typed YAML on both speed <em>and</em> accuracy — reserve hand-editing for the 1-2 fields no generator flag covers (probes, securityContext, volumeMounts, resource limits).</li>
                    <li>Tab-completion after <code>alias k=kubectl</code> only works once you've also run <code>complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k</code> AND sourced completion — do both, in that order, or <code>k &lt;TAB&gt;</code> silently does nothing.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Speed Kit Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Aliases don't survive a new terminal tab</strong> — if the exam's terminal spawns a fresh shell (some proctoring setups do), re-source your setup block. Keep it in a scratch file you can <code>source</code> quickly rather than relying purely on memory.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>$now</code> is destructive</strong> — <code>--force --grace-period=0</code> skips graceful shutdown entirely (no <code>preStop</code> hook execution, no SIGTERM grace window). Only use it on pods you've already confirmed are stuck (e.g. node gone, pod stuck <code>Terminating</code>), never as a routine delete.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>--dry-run=client</code> vs <code>--dry-run=server</code></strong> — <code>client</code> never contacts the API server (pure local render, what <code>$do</code> uses). <code>server</code> DOES contact the API server and runs admission control/validation without persisting — useful to catch a webhook rejection before you commit, but slower. Know the difference; the exam sometimes specifically asks for one or the other.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

