            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                <div class="eq-question">A node must be patched. Safely move all easypj pods off it, then return it to service after the reboot.</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cordon-free node maintenance</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># ...perform the reboot/patch...</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>From A.2: <code>drain</code> implicitly cordons first (blocks new scheduling), then evicts every pod not backed by a DaemonSet, giving controllers a chance to reschedule them elsewhere. <code>uncordon</code> only re-enables scheduling — it does NOT move the evicted pods back; that's a one-way trip by design.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                <div class="eq-question">The <code>easypj-web</code> rollout just introduced a bad image. Roll back to the previous revision and confirm the rollback succeeded.</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">rollback trio</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj-web</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>A.3's core rollback trio: <code>undo</code> reverts to the previous <code>ReplicaSet</code> revision, <code>status</code> blocks until the rolling update finishes (or reports failure), and <code>history</code> confirms the revision count moved forward (a rollback is itself a new revision, not a deletion of history).</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                <div class="eq-question">Prove whether ServiceAccount <code>easypj-sa</code> in namespace <code>easypj</code> may create Deployments.</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">RBAC impersonation check</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl auth can-i create deployments \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj
<span class="comment"># or, for the FULL permission set: --list instead of a single verb/resource</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>From A.5: <code>--as=system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code> is the impersonation syntax for a ServiceAccount (contrast with <code>--as=&lt;username&gt;</code> for a regular user). This is the fastest RBAC debugging command on the exam — no need to trace Role → RoleBinding → Subject by hand.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                <div class="eq-question">Take a verified etcd snapshot on a kubeadm control-plane node.</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">snapshot save + verify</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
  snapshot save /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db

<span class="comment"># then verify:</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db --write-table</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>From A.7: all four TLS flags plus <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> are mandatory — miss any one and you get a connection or "unknown flag" error. <code>snapshot status --write-table</code> is what makes the backup "verified" rather than just "taken": it decodes the file and prints hash/revision/key-count, proving it isn't corrupt or empty.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                <div class="eq-question">Control-plane certs expire in 5 days. Renew them and bring the cluster back cleanly.</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">renew + force-reload control plane</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration
sudo kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># force the static control-plane pods to pick up the new certs — dynamic reload</span>
<span class="comment"># isn't supported, so move each manifest out and back (or crictl rm the container):</span>
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/ &amp;&amp; sleep 20 &amp;&amp; \
  sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># repeat for kube-controller-manager.yaml and kube-scheduler.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># refresh admin.conf / any copies of it used by your kubeconfig:</span>
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>From A.6: <code>renew all</code> writes new cert files to disk, but the running API server/controller-manager/scheduler processes have the <em>old</em> certs loaded in memory until restarted. Static Pods are owned by the local kubelet (not the API server), so <code>kubectl delete pod</code> can't touch them — you force the reload by moving each manifest out of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and back (or removing the running container with <code>crictl rm</code>), either of which makes kubelet recreate the Pod from disk. Plain <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> is not a reliable substitute — the kubelet usually finds the containers already running on restart and leaves them alone. <code>admin.conf</code> does NOT auto-rotate the way kubelet's own client cert does, so any copied kubeconfig goes stale unless refreshed manually.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>
        </div>

