            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ startupProbe Gates the Other Two — And securityContext Has Two Layers</h5>
                <p>Until <code>startupProbe</code> succeeds, Kubernetes does <strong>not</strong> run <code>livenessProbe</code> or <code>readinessProbe</code> at all — this exists specifically for slow-booting apps (a JVM warming up, a container running migrations on first start) that would otherwise get liveness-killed and restart-looped before they ever finish starting. Once <code>startupProbe</code> reports success a single time, it stops running permanently for that container's lifetime, and the other two probes take over as normal. Size <code>failureThreshold × periodSeconds</code> to comfortably exceed your worst-case cold-start time (30 × 10s = 300s above is generous). Separately, <code>securityContext</code> exists at <strong>both</strong> <code>spec.securityContext</code> (pod-level, applies to every container as a default) and <code>spec.containers[].securityContext</code> (container-level) — on any field set in both places, the <strong>container-level value always wins</strong>. This lets you set a strict pod-wide baseline (<code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>) while still tuning one specific sidecar container differently if it genuinely needs to.</p>
                <p>One more pairing worth memorizing cold: <code>resources.requests</code> drives <strong>scheduling</strong> — the scheduler only places a pod on a node that has that much capacity free — while <code>resources.limits</code> drives <strong>runtime enforcement</strong>: a container hitting its CPU limit gets throttled (slowed down, not killed), but a container hitting its <strong>memory</strong> limit gets OOM-killed outright (memory can't be throttled the way CPU can). Under-requesting to "sneak" a pod onto a full node is a common anti-pattern that backfires as soon as real usage climbs toward the (higher) limit.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Multi-Container Pod (Sidecar Pattern) — Exam Favourite</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">The CKA frequently asks you to add a sidecar container to an existing pod</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-with-logger
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  containers:
  - name: web                           <span class="comment"># Main application container</span>
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000
    volumeMounts:
    - name: shared-logs
      mountPath: /var/log/app
  - name: log-sidecar                   # ← Sidecar: ships logs
    image: busybox:1.36
    command: ["sh", "-c", "tail -f /var/log/app/access.log"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: shared-logs                 # ← Same volume = shared filesystem
      mountPath: /var/log/app
  initContainers:                       # ← Runs BEFORE main containers
  - name: init-db-check
    image: busybox:1.36
    command: ["sh", "-c", "until nslookup easypj-db-svc; do echo waiting; sleep 2; done"]
  volumes:
  - name: shared-logs
    emptyDir: {}                        # ← Temporary shared storage</code></pre>
