            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Pod Scheduling Controls — nodeSelector, nodeAffinity, tolerations</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Not in cka.txt's base B.1 template, added here because Workloads &amp; Scheduling is a core CKA domain and these fields are among the most commonly tested in the whole exam</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-scheduled
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  nodeSelector:                         # ← Simplest form: exact label match, AND'ed together
    disktype: ssd                       <span class="comment"># Only schedule onto nodes labeled disktype=ssd</span>
  affinity:
    nodeAffinity:                       # ← More expressive than nodeSelector
      requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:   # ← HARD rule — Pod stays Pending if unmet
        nodeSelectorTerms:
        - matchExpressions:
          - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
            operator: In                <span class="comment"># In | NotIn | Exists | DoesNotExist | Gt | Lt</span>
            values: ["antarctica-east1", "antarctica-west1"]
      preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:  # ← SOFT rule — best-effort, never blocks
      - weight: 50                      <span class="comment"># 1-100; scheduler sums weights of satisfied preferences</span>
        preference:
          matchExpressions:
          - key: workload-tier
            operator: In
            values: ["frontend"]
  tolerations:                          # ← Lets this pod land on nodes that repel others
  - key: "dedicated"
    operator: "Equal"                   <span class="comment"># Equal (needs value) | Exists (matches any value)</span>
    value: "easypj"
    effect: "NoSchedule"                <span class="comment"># NoSchedule | PreferNoSchedule | NoExecute</span>
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Taint <code>effect</code></th><th>New Pods (no matching toleration)</th><th>Pods Already Running There</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>NoSchedule</strong></td><td>Blocked from scheduling</td><td>Untouched — kept running</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>PreferNoSchedule</strong></td><td>Avoided if a better node exists (soft)</td><td>Untouched — kept running</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>NoExecute</strong></td><td>Blocked from scheduling</td><td>Evicted immediately (or after <code>tolerationSeconds</code>)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ nodeSelector + nodeAffinity Are ANDed, and Tolerations Only REMOVE a Block</h5>
                <p>If a pod sets <strong>both</strong> <code>nodeSelector</code> and <code>affinity.nodeAffinity</code>, the node must satisfy <strong>both</strong> — they don't override each other, they combine. Within <code>nodeAffinity</code> itself: multiple entries in <code>nodeSelectorTerms</code> are OR'd (any one matching term is enough), but multiple <code>matchExpressions</code> inside one term are AND'd (all must match). Separately — and this is the trap that costs the most exam minutes — a <strong>toleration does not attract a pod to a tainted node</strong>, it only removes the taint's repulsion so scheduling there becomes <em>possible</em>. If you want a pod to actually land on specific nodes (not just be allowed to), pair the toleration with a <code>nodeSelector</code>/<code>nodeAffinity</code> targeting those same nodes' labels — tolerations and affinity solve two different problems that look similar. Also remember: manually setting <code>spec.nodeName</code> bypasses the scheduler (and therefore taint checking) entirely for initial placement, but a <code>NoExecute</code> taint will still evict that pod afterward.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.2: Deployment Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.2 Deployment Template (with strategy)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Deployments manage ReplicaSets which manage Pods. The <code>strategy</code> field controls how updates are rolled out.</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Deployment — Rolling Update Strategy</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3                           # ← Desired pod count
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate                 # ← RollingUpdate | Recreate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1                       <span class="comment"># Extra pods during update (can be % or int)</span>
      maxUnavailable: 0                 # ← Zero-downtime: never go below replicas
  selector:
    matchLabels:                        # ← MUST match template.metadata.labels
      app: easypj
  template:                             # ← Pod template (same fields as B.1)
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj                     # ← MUST match selector.matchLabels
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: "128Mi"
            cpu: "250m"
          limits:
            memory: "256Mi"
            cpu: "500m"
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /health
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 5</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Behavior</th><th>When to Use</th><th>Downtime?</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>RollingUpdate</strong></td>
                            <td>Gradually replaces pods one by one</td>
                            <td>Production — zero-downtime deploys</td>
                            <td>❌ No</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Recreate</strong></td>
                            <td>Kills ALL old pods, then creates new ones</td>
                            <td>Dev/test, or when old+new can't coexist — e.g. an incompatible DB schema migration where the old and new app versions can't safely run against the same database at the same time</td>
                            <td>✅ Yes</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Label Mismatch = Deployment Stuck</h5>
                <p>If <code>spec.selector.matchLabels</code> doesn't match <code>spec.template.metadata.labels</code>, the Deployment can't find its pods. Kubernetes will reject the manifest outright. This is the #1 YAML mistake — always ensure these labels are identical.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.3: Service Templates ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.3 Service Templates (ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, Headless, ExternalName)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Services expose pods to the network. Five types exist, each for a different access pattern — the first three form a superset ladder (a NodePort also gets a ClusterIP; a LoadBalancer also gets a NodePort), while Headless and ExternalName are special-purpose variants that skip the normal virtual-IP proxying entirely.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Service Types — Access Levels</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                     │
  │  ☁️ INTERNET                                                        │
  │     │                                                               │
  │     ▼                                                               │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                                │
  │  │  LoadBalancer    │ ← Cloud provider provisions external LB       │
  │  │  (external IP)  │   Port: 80 → targetPort: 8000                 │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                                │
  │           │                                                         │
  │     ┌─────┴─────┐                                                   │
  │     ▼           ▼                                                   │
  │  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐                                             │
  │  │NodePort│  │NodePort│ ← Every node opens port 30000-32767        │
  │  │ :30080 │  │ :30080 │   Access: &lt;nodeIP&gt;:30080                 │
  │  └───┬────┘  └───┬────┘                                             │
  │      │           │                                                  │
  │      └─────┬─────┘                                                  │
  │            ▼                                                        │
  │     ┌─────────────┐                                                 │
  │     │  ClusterIP   │ ← Internal only (default)                     │
  │     │  10.96.0.100 │   Access: easypj-web-svc.easypj.svc:80       │
  │     └──────┬──────┘                                                 │
  │            │                                                        │
  │     ┌──────┴──────┐                                                 │
  │     ▼             ▼                                                 │
  │  ┌──────┐     ┌──────┐                                              │
  │  │ Pod  │     │ Pod  │ ← selector: app=easypj matches these        │
  │  │:8000 │     │:8000 │                                              │
  │  └──────┘     └──────┘                                              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Headless vs ExternalName — the Two That Skip the VIP</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  NORMAL Service (ClusterIP/NodePort/LB)      HEADLESS (clusterIP: None)
  ───────────────────────────────────         ──────────────────────────
  Client asks DNS for easypj-web              Client asks DNS for easypj-db
        │                                            │
        ▼                                            ▼
  ONE virtual IP returned                    MULTIPLE A records returned —
  (10.96.0.100 — load-balanced                 one PER READY POD:
   by kube-proxy to a random pod)               easypj-db-0.easypj-db... → 10.244.1.5
        │                                        easypj-db-1.easypj-db... → 10.244.2.7
        ▼                                        easypj-db-2.easypj-db... → 10.244.3.9
  kube-proxy forwards to any                 Client picks/connects to a SPECIFIC pod —
  matching pod — client never                 no load-balancing, no VIP in between
  sees which pod answered                     (needed for StatefulSet, see B.15)

  EXTERNAL NAME (type: ExternalName)
  ───────────────────────────────────
  Client asks DNS for easypj-legacy-db
        │
        ▼
  CoreDNS returns a CNAME: db.prod.example.com   ← no IP, no proxying, no selector
        │
        ▼
  Client's own resolver follows the CNAME and connects DIRECTLY —
  kube-proxy and the Service's "IP" never enter the picture at all
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ClusterIP Service (internal only — default)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-svc
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  type: ClusterIP                       <span class="comment"># Default — can omit</span>
  selector:
    app: easypj                         # ← MUST match pod labels
  ports:
  - port: 80                            <span class="comment"># Service port (what clients connect to)</span>
    targetPort: 8000                    <span class="comment"># Container port (where app listens)</span>
    protocol: TCP</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>NodePort Service (external via node IP:port)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-nodeport
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
  - port: 80                            <span class="comment"># Service port</span>
    targetPort: 8000                    <span class="comment"># Container port</span>
    nodePort: 30080                     # ← 30000-32767 range (omit for auto)</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>LoadBalancer Service (cloud provider LB)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-lb
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
  - port: 80                            <span class="comment"># External port</span>
    targetPort: 8000                    <span class="comment"># Container port</span>
  <span class="comment"># Cloud provider assigns EXTERNAL-IP automatically</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Headless Service (no cluster VIP — DNS returns pod IPs directly)</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Required for StatefulSets (see B.15) — gives each pod its own stable DNS name instead of one shared virtual IP</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-db
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  clusterIP: None                       # ← THIS single field makes it headless
  selector:
    app: easypj-db
  ports:
  - port: 5432                          <span class="comment"># PostgreSQL port</span>
    targetPort: 5432</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ExternalName Service (CNAME alias to an off-cluster host)</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">No selector, no ports, no proxying — pure DNS-level redirection to something outside the cluster</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-legacy-db
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  type: ExternalName
  externalName: db.prod.example.com     # ← DNS returns this CNAME, nothing else</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Access</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Imperative</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>ClusterIP</strong></td>
                            <td>Internal only</td>
                            <td>DB, internal APIs</td>
                            <td><code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>NodePort</strong></td>
                            <td>External via nodeIP:port</td>
                            <td>Dev/test, on-prem</td>
                            <td><code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --type=NodePort --port=80</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td>
                            <td>External via cloud LB</td>
                            <td>Production, cloud</td>
                            <td><code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --type=LoadBalancer --port=80</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Headless</strong></td>
                            <td>No VIP — DNS returns pod IPs directly</td>
                            <td>StatefulSets needing stable per-pod DNS (easypj-db)</td>
                            <td>No imperative shortcut — set <code>clusterIP: None</code> in YAML</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>ExternalName</strong></td>
                            <td>CNAME alias, no proxying</td>
                            <td>In-cluster name for an external/legacy DB or API</td>
                            <td><code>kubectl create service externalname easypj-legacy-db --external-name=db.prod.example.com</code></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Headless ≠ No Networking, and ExternalName ≠ A Real Service</h5>
                <p>A Headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>) still does real work — CoreDNS resolves it to <strong>one A record per ready pod</strong> instead of one shared VIP, which is exactly what gives StatefulSet pods stable individual addresses like <code>easypj-db-0.easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local</code> (see B.15). It is not "no DNS," it's "different DNS." <code>ExternalName</code> is the opposite extreme: it has no <code>selector</code>, no <code>ports</code>, no endpoints, and kube-proxy never touches it — it is purely a CNAME record returned by CoreDNS. This means an ExternalName Service does <strong>not</strong> get a ClusterIP, doesn't show up as a proxied target in <code>kubectl get endpoints</code>, and any TLS/mTLS the client expects must be handled by the client itself, since there's no Kubernetes networking layer in between to enforce it. A common exam trap: expecting <code>kubectl describe svc</code> on an ExternalName Service to show an IP or endpoints — it never will, by design.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.4: PV + PVC Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.4 PersistentVolume + PVC Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>PVs are cluster-level storage resources; PVCs are namespace-scoped claims that bind to PVs. Admin creates PV, developer creates PVC.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>PV ↔ PVC Binding Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ADMIN creates:                  DEVELOPER creates:          Pod uses:
  ┌──────────────┐               ┌────────────────┐        ┌──────────┐
  │ PersistentVol│               │ PersistentVol  │        │   Pod    │
  │              │    BINDING    │ Claim          │ MOUNT  │          │
  │ capacity:10Gi│◄────────────▶│ request: 10Gi  │───────▶│ volumes: │
  │ accessMode:  │  (automatic) │ accessMode:    │        │  - pvc:  │
  │  RWO         │               │  RWO           │        │  easypj- │
  │ storageClass:│               │ storageClass:  │        │  pvc     │
  │  manual      │               │  manual        │        │          │
  └──────────────┘               └────────────────┘        └──────────┘
     ↑                              ↑
     Must match:                    Must match:
     - accessModes                  - storageClassName
     - storageClassName             - capacity ≤ PV capacity
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>PersistentVolume (admin creates)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: easypj-pv                       <span class="comment"># PVs are cluster-scoped (no namespace)</span>
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 10Gi                       # ← Storage amount
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                       # ← RWO | ROX | RWX
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain # ← Retain | Delete | Recycle
  storageClassName: manual              # ← MUST match PVC
  hostPath:                             # ← Dev only! Use cloud storage in prod
    path: /data/easypj-db</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>PersistentVolumeClaim (developer creates)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: easypj-pvc
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                       # ← Must match PV
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi                     # ← Must be ≤ PV capacity
  storageClassName: manual              # ← Must match PV</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Access Mode</th><th>Abbreviation</th><th>Description</th><th>Use Case</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>ReadWriteOnce</strong></td><td>RWO</td><td>Read-write by a single node</td><td>Database (PostgreSQL)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ReadOnlyMany</strong></td><td>ROX</td><td>Read-only by many nodes</td><td>Shared config/assets</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ReadWriteMany</strong></td><td>RWX</td><td>Read-write by many nodes</td><td>Shared uploads (NFS)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Retain Doesn't Mean "Reusable" — It Means "Manual Cleanup"</h5>
                <p>When a PVC bound to a <code>Retain</code> PV is deleted, the PV is <strong>not</strong> deleted and its data is <strong>not</strong> wiped — but the PV also doesn't automatically become available for a new claim. It moves to phase <code>Released</code> and stays there, unusable, until an admin manually clears <code>spec.claimRef</code> (or deletes and recreates the PV) to make it <code>Available</code> again. This is intentional — it stops a stray new PVC from silently claiming a PV that still has another team's production data on it — but it surprises people who expect "Retain" to mean "instantly reusable." <code>Delete</code> reclaim policy has the opposite trade-off: convenient auto-cleanup, but a mistaken PVC deletion destroys the backing disk with no recovery path.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Common Exam Scenario vs. Wrong Answer</h5>
                <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> "Provision 10Gi of storage for easypj-db using a PV/PVC pair with storageClassName manual." <strong>Correct:</strong> both objects use the exact same <code>storageClassName</code>, the PVC's <code>accessModes</code> is a subset the PV offers, and the PVC's requested <code>storage</code> is ≤ the PV's <code>capacity.storage</code>. <strong>Wrong answer that looks right:</strong> leaving <code>storageClassName</code> off the PVC entirely — if the cluster has a <em>default</em> StorageClass configured, an empty <code>storageClassName</code> doesn't mean "match any PV," it means "dynamically provision from the default class," so your PVC binds to a brand-new dynamically-provisioned volume instead of the hand-crafted PV you were supposed to use, and the PV you created sits forever <code>Available</code> and unused.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.5: StorageClass Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.5 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-5">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.5 StorageClass Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>StorageClasses enable <strong>dynamic provisioning</strong> — no pre-created PVs needed. When a PVC references a StorageClass, Kubernetes automatically creates the PV.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Static vs Dynamic Provisioning</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  STATIC Provisioning (B.4)              DYNAMIC Provisioning (B.5)
  ──────────────────────────             ──────────────────────────

  1. Admin creates PV manually           1. Admin creates StorageClass
     apiVersion: v1                          apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
     kind: PersistentVolume                  kind: StorageClass
     capacity: 10Gi                          provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com
           │                                       │
  2. Dev creates PVC                     2. Dev creates PVC (same)
     storageClassName: manual                storageClassName: fast-storage
           │                                       │
  3. K8s binds PVC → PV                  3. K8s AUTO-CREATES PV + binds!
     (must find matching PV)                 (provisioner handles it)
           │                                       │
           ▼                                       ▼
  ⚠️ If no PV matches → Pending          ✅ PV created on-demand = no Pending

  💡 Exam: Dynamic is preferred in cloud. Static for hostPath/local dev.
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>StorageClass — Dynamic Provisioning</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-storage
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner  # ← Or: ebs.csi.aws.com, pd.csi.storage.gke.io
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer    # ← Delay until pod schedules (topology-aware)
reclaimPolicy: Delete                      # ← Delete PV when PVC is deleted
allowVolumeExpansion: true                 # ← Allow PVC resize</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Binding Mode</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Best For</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Immediate</strong></td><td>PV created as soon as PVC is created</td><td>Single-zone clusters</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>WaitForFirstConsumer</strong></td><td>PV created when first pod using PVC is scheduled</td><td>Multi-zone clusters (prevents zone mismatch)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ allowVolumeExpansion Only Grows — It Never Shrinks, and Doesn't Grow the Filesystem</h5>
                <p><code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code> lets you edit a PVC's <code>spec.resources.requests.storage</code> to a larger value and have the underlying disk resize — but only if the CSI driver behind the StorageClass actually supports expansion (most cloud CSI drivers do; some in-tree/legacy ones don't). Two traps: you can never request a <em>smaller</em> size — Kubernetes rejects any shrink attempt outright, so an over-provisioned PVC is a one-way decision. And resizing the block device is not the same as resizing the filesystem on it — for a pod that's already running, the filesystem is typically grown automatically on the next mount/restart, but if you see the PVC report the new size while <code>df -h</code> inside the container still shows the old size, that's this gap, not a failure.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.6: Role + RoleBinding Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.6 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-6">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.6 Role + RoleBinding Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Namespace-scoped RBAC — grants permissions within a specific namespace.</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Role (namespace-scoped permissions)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  namespace: easypj                     # ← Scoped to this namespace
  name: easypj-role
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]                       # ← "" = core API group (pods, services, etc.)
  resources: ["pods", "services"]       # ← What resources
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]       # ← What actions allowed
- apiGroups: ["apps"]                   # ← apps group (deployments, replicasets)
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "update"]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>RoleBinding (assigns role to subject)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  namespace: easypj                     # ← Same namespace as the Role
  name: easypj-binding
subjects:                               # ← WHO gets the permissions
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
roleRef:                                # ← WHICH role to assign
  kind: Role                            <span class="comment"># Role or ClusterRole</span>
  name: easypj-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Common API Groups</h5>
                <p><code>""</code> (core): pods, services, secrets, configmaps, PVCs, namespaces, nodes, events. <code>"apps"</code>: deployments, replicasets, statefulsets, daemonsets. <code>"rbac.authorization.k8s.io"</code>: roles, rolebindings. <code>"networking.k8s.io"</code>: networkpolicies, ingresses.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ roleRef Is Immutable — You Can't Edit a Binding onto a Different Role</h5>
                <p>Once a RoleBinding is created, its <code>roleRef</code> block (kind/name/apiGroup) is immutable — <code>kubectl edit</code> or <code>apply</code> against a changed <code>roleRef</code> is rejected by the API server. To reassign <code>easypj-binding</code> from <code>easypj-role</code> to a different role, you must delete and recreate the binding (<code>subjects</code>, by contrast, IS mutable — you can freely add/remove who the binding applies to). This is a frequent exam gotcha when a scenario says "grant this SA the permissions of a different role" and the intuitive first move — edit the existing binding in place — silently fails or errors depending on how you apply it.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.7: ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.7 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-7">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.7 ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Cluster-wide RBAC — grants permissions across <strong>all namespaces</strong> or on cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PVs).</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ClusterRole (cluster-wide permissions)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: easypj-cluster-reader           # ← No namespace (cluster-scoped)
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["nodes"]                  # ← Cluster-scoped resource
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["persistentvolumes"]      # ← Also cluster-scoped
  verbs: ["get", "list"]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ClusterRoleBinding — Grants the ClusterRole Cluster-Wide</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-cluster-binding          # ← No namespace
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj                     # ← SA's home namespace is still required here
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: easypj-cluster-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Role + RoleBinding</th><th>ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>Single namespace</td><td>All namespaces / cluster resources</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Use for</strong></td><td>App-level access (easypj pods)</td><td>Admin access (nodes, PVs)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Has namespace?</strong></td><td>Yes (in metadata)</td><td>No</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Example</strong></td><td>easypj-sa can read pods in the easypj namespace</td><td>easypj-sa can read nodes cluster-wide</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Subjects Aren't Only ServiceAccounts</h5>
                <p>A binding's <code>subjects</code> list accepts three kinds: <code>ServiceAccount</code> (an identity for a pod, always namespaced — used throughout this appendix), <code>User</code> (a human identity authenticated via client certificate, OIDC token, etc. — Kubernetes has no built-in User object, the name is whatever the authentication layer asserts), and <code>Group</code> (a set of Users, also asserted by the authentication layer, e.g. <code>system:masters</code>). A ClusterRoleBinding granting <code>easypj-cluster-reader</code> to a human admin instead of a ServiceAccount would swap the subject to <code>{kind: User, name: jane, apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io}</code> — same <code>roleRef</code>, same effect, just a different kind of identity on the receiving end.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Don't Reach for cluster-admin as a Shortcut</h5>
                <p>The built-in <code>cluster-admin</code> ClusterRole grants <code>*</code> verbs on <code>*</code> resources in <code>*</code> API groups — total control of the cluster. It's tempting to bind a ServiceAccount to it "just to unblock a task" during the exam, but this is exactly the over-privileging the CKA (and especially CKS) wants you to avoid: a compromised pod running as that SA now owns the cluster. Build a scoped ClusterRole (or Role) listing only the <code>resources</code>/<code>verbs</code> actually needed — the exam grader checks for least-privilege, not just "does it work."</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.8: NetworkPolicy Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.8 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-8">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.8 NetworkPolicy Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>NetworkPolicies control pod-to-pod traffic. By default, all pods can talk to all pods. A NetworkPolicy restricts that — <strong>deny by default, allow explicitly</strong>.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>NetworkPolicy — easypj Traffic Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  namespace: easypj                                              │
  │                                                                 │
  │  ┌──────────────┐     ✅ port 5432    ┌──────────────────┐     │
  │  │  easypj-web  │ ──────────────────▶ │  easypj-db       │     │
  │  │  app: easypj │                     │  app: easypj-db  │     │
  │  └──────────────┘                     └──────────────────┘     │
  │                                               ▲                 │
  │  ┌──────────────┐     ❌ BLOCKED              │                 │
  │  │  other-pod   │ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ┘                 │
  │  │  app: hacker │    NetworkPolicy denies                       │
  │  └──────────────┘    non-matching pods                          │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Default-Deny-All (Both Ingress + Egress) — Apply This FIRST</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Zero-trust starting point: one empty podSelector blocks every direction of traffic for every pod in the namespace, then you layer specific allow policies on top</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}                       # ← Empty = every pod in the namespace
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  - Egress                              <span class="comment"># ← With no rules below, BOTH directions are denied</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>NetworkPolicy — Allow Only easypj-web → easypj-db</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: db-restrict
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector:                          # ← Apply to these pods
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-db
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress                             # ← Control incoming traffic
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:                      # ← Only allow FROM these pods
        matchLabels:
          app: easypj
    ports:
    - port: 5432                        # ← Only on PostgreSQL port
      protocol: TCP</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ NetworkPolicy Requires a CNI That Supports It</h5>
                <p>NetworkPolicies only work if your CNI plugin enforces them. <strong>Calico</strong>, <strong>Cilium</strong>, <strong>Weave Net</strong> support them. <strong>Flannel does NOT</strong>. On the CKA exam, the cluster uses a supporting CNI. If you apply a NetworkPolicy and traffic still flows unrestricted, check your CNI plugin.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Ingress-Only Deny-All — Narrower Alternative (Common Exam Question!)</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Use this INSTEAD of the both-directions default-deny-all above when the scenario only asks you to lock down incoming traffic and outbound/egress must stay unrestricted (e.g. the pod still needs to call an external API freely) — applying both this and default-deny-all together is redundant on the Ingress side, since default-deny-all already covers it</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: deny-all-ingress
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}                       # ← Empty = applies to ALL pods in namespace
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress                             # ← Block all incoming traffic ONLY — egress is untouched
  <span class="comment"># No ingress rules = nothing allowed</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Allow-DNS-Egress (Pair This With ANY Egress Deny-All)</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Every pod needs to resolve names via CoreDNS — this is the rule that keeps DNS working once egress is locked down</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-dns-egress
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}                       # ← All pods need DNS
  policyTypes:
  - Egress
  egress:
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector: {}             # ← Matches kube-system, where CoreDNS lives
    ports:
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53                          <span class="comment"># Most DNS queries are UDP</span>
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53                          <span class="comment"># TCP fallback for large/truncated responses</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ A Bare Default-Deny Breaks DNS — the #1 NetworkPolicy Exam Trap</h5>
                <p>The instant a NetworkPolicy with <code>policyTypes: [Egress]</code> selects a pod — even the empty-<code>podSelector</code> <code>default-deny-all</code> above — that pod becomes default-deny for egress with <strong>zero exceptions</strong>, which also blocks its outbound queries to CoreDNS on UDP/TCP port 53. Every in-cluster name lookup (<code>easypj-db-svc</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc</code>, external DNS) starts failing immediately, even though the pod itself never changed. Symptoms look like a completely unrelated bug — <code>curl</code> timeouts, "could not resolve host," a Deployment stuck because its init container can't reach a Service by name — and it's easy to spend the exam's limited time debugging application config instead of realizing the NetworkPolicy is the actual cause. The fix is always the same: pair <strong>any</strong> egress-denying policy with an <code>allow-dns-egress</code> rule like the one above. This is explicitly called out in the CKA curriculum's NetworkPolicy objectives — expect it to be tested directly.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 NetworkPolicy Logic</h5>
                <p><strong>No policy</strong> = all traffic allowed. <strong>Policy with empty podSelector</strong> = applies to all pods. <strong>Policy with no ingress rules</strong> = deny all ingress (same idea for egress). Policies are <strong>additive</strong>, not overriding — a pod matched by two policies gets the union of both policies' allow rules; you cannot "un-deny" one policy's restriction from another policy. Combine deny-all + specific allow policies (web→db, allow-DNS-egress) for a "zero trust" network model — the CKA exam loves this exact pattern.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.9: ServiceAccount Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.9 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-9">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.9 ServiceAccount Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>ServiceAccounts provide identity for pods. Each pod runs as a ServiceAccount — always create a dedicated one instead of using <code>default</code>.</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ServiceAccount</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
automountServiceAccountToken: false     # ← Security: don't auto-mount token
                                        <span class="comment">#   Only set true if pod needs API access</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Security Best Practice</h5>
                <p>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> unless your pod actually needs to talk to the Kubernetes API. The default SA token mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> gives the pod API access. If compromised, attackers could query the API. The CKA exam may ask you to configure this.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — ServiceAccount lifecycle</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create SA imperatively</span>
kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Assign to a pod (in pod spec)</span>
<span class="comment"># spec.serviceAccountName: easypj-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify which SA a pod uses</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># easypj-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Check if token is mounted</span>
kubectl exec easypj-web -n easypj -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  namespace  token</span>

<span class="comment"># Full RBAC chain: create SA → create Role → bind them</span>
kubectl create sa easypj-sa -n easypj
kubectl create role easypj-role --verb=get,list --resource=pods -n easypj
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-binding --role=easypj-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.10: PodDisruptionBudget Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.10 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-10">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.10 PodDisruptionBudget Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>PDBs protect your app during voluntary disruptions (node drain, cluster upgrade). They guarantee minimum availability.</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>PodDisruptionBudget</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-pdb
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  minAvailable: 2                       # ← At least 2 pods must stay running
  <span class="comment"># OR: maxUnavailable: 1              # ← At most 1 pod can be down</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                       # ← Protects pods with this label</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example (3 replicas)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>minAvailable: 2</code></td><td>Keep at least 2 running</td><td>Can evict max 1 at a time</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>maxUnavailable: 1</code></td><td>At most 1 can be down</td><td>Same effect for 3 replicas</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>minAvailable: "50%"</code></td><td>Keep at least 50% running</td><td>Can evict 1 of 3</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ PDB Blocks kubectl drain!</h5>
                <p>If a PDB prevents eviction, <code>kubectl drain</code> will hang indefinitely. Common CKA trap: you try to drain a node but it just waits. Check PDBs: <code>kubectl get pdb -A</code>. If <code>minAvailable</code> equals the current replica count and there's only one node, the drain can never succeed. You must either scale up first or temporarily lower the PDB's <code>minAvailable</code>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 A PDB Only Constrains VOLUNTARY Disruptions</h5>
                <p>A PodDisruptionBudget governs disruptions the cluster <em>chooses</em> to perform — <code>kubectl drain</code>, the Cluster Autoscaler scaling a node down, a manual eviction API call. It has zero effect on <strong>involuntary</strong> disruptions: a node crashing, a kernel panic, a hardware failure, or the kubelet getting OOM-killed. In those cases pods simply go away regardless of what the PDB says — there's no "budget" to enforce because nothing asked permission. Design for both: a PDB for graceful maintenance windows, and enough <code>replicas</code> spread across enough nodes (via topology spread or anti-affinity) that an involuntary node loss alone doesn't take the whole app down.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.11: Ingress Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.11 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-11">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.11 Ingress Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Ingress provides HTTP/HTTPS routing from external traffic to internal Services. Requires an Ingress Controller (e.g., nginx) to be installed in the cluster.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Ingress Routing — easypj Example</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  Internet
     │
     ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              Ingress Controller (nginx)                   │
  │                                                          │
  │  Rule 1: easypj.example.com/                             │
  │           └──▶ Service: easypj-web-svc:80                │
  │                                                          │
  │  Rule 2: easypj.example.com/api/                         │
  │           └──▶ Service: easypj-api-svc:80                │
  │                                                          │
  │  Rule 3: admin.example.com/                              │
  │           └──▶ Service: admin-svc:80                     │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
     │                    │                    │
     ▼                    ▼                    ▼
  ┌────────┐         ┌────────┐         ┌────────┐
  │easypj  │         │easypj  │         │ admin  │
  │web pods│         │api pods│         │ pods   │
  └────────┘         └────────┘         └────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Ingress — Host &amp; Path-Based Routing</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-ingress
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx               # ← Which Ingress Controller to use
  rules:
  - host: easypj.example.com            # ← Domain name routing
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /                         # ← URL path
        pathType: Prefix                # ← Prefix | Exact | ImplementationSpecific
        backend:
          service:
            name: easypj-web-svc        # ← Target Service name
            port:
              number: 80                # ← Target Service port
      - path: /api
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: easypj-api-svc
            port:
              number: 80</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>pathType</th><th>Matches</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Prefix</strong></td><td>URL prefix match</td><td><code>/jobs</code> matches <code>/jobs</code>, <code>/jobs/123</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Exact</strong></td><td>Exact URL match only</td><td><code>/jobs</code> matches only <code>/jobs</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Ingress with TLS (HTTPS)</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">The CKA may ask you to configure TLS termination — create the Secret first</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create TLS secret</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key -n easypj</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Reference in Ingress</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-ingress-tls
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  tls:                                  # ← TLS termination
  - hosts:
    - easypj.example.com
    secretName: easypj-tls              # ← Secret with tls.crt + tls.key
  rules:
  - host: easypj.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: easypj-web-svc
            port:
              number: 80</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Ingress Is Inert Without a Controller</h5>
                <p>Creating an Ingress object does nothing by itself — it's a routing <em>intent</em>, not a running proxy. A controller (nginx, Traefik, HAProxy, a cloud LB controller…) must be deployed and configured to <code>watch</code> Ingress objects whose <code>ingressClassName</code> it claims. On the CKA exam, if <code>curl</code> to your Ingress host times out, first check <code>kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx</code> (or wherever the controller lives) before debugging your YAML — a perfectly correct Ingress with no controller watching it produces identical symptoms to a broken one.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Common Exam Scenario vs. Wrong Answer</h5>
                <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> "Expose the easypj-web Service at <code>easypj.example.com/</code> using Ingress." <strong>Correct:</strong> create the Ingress with the cluster's actual <code>ingressClassName</code> (check <code>kubectl get ingressclass</code> first — don't assume <code>nginx</code>), a <code>host</code> rule, and a <code>backend.service.port.number</code> that matches the Service's <code>port</code> (not its <code>targetPort</code>). <strong>Wrong answer that looks right:</strong> pointing <code>backend.service.port.number</code> at the container's port (e.g. 8000) instead of the Service's exposed port (80) — the Ingress applies cleanly with no error, but every request 503s because the controller is asking the Service for a port it doesn't have.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.11b: Gateway + HTTPRoute Template (Gateway API) ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.11b ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-11b">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.11b Gateway + HTTPRoute Template (Gateway API)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>The <strong>Gateway API</strong> is the official Kubernetes SIG-Network project positioned as the successor to Ingress (the upstream docs describe it exactly that way — a successor, not a deprecation notice; Ingress remains fully supported). <code>GatewayClass</code>, <code>Gateway</code>, and <code>HTTPRoute</code> all graduated to stable <code>gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1</code> in the Gateway API's 1.0 release, alongside <code>GRPCRoute</code>. Instead of one flat object crammed with vendor-specific annotations, it splits routing into three role-oriented kinds — <strong>GatewayClass</strong> (which controller implements this?), <strong>Gateway</strong> (the actual listener: IP/port/protocol/TLS, owned by the platform/infra team), and <strong>HTTPRoute</strong> (which path goes to which Service, owned by the application team, e.g. easypj). This separation lets a platform team hand out safe, self-service routing to app teams without giving them access to the shared listener.</p>
            <p><strong>When you'd reach for this over Ingress:</strong> your cluster already runs a Gateway API implementation (modern nginx-gateway-fabric, Envoy Gateway, Cilium, Istio, or a cloud provider's managed gateway), you need native traffic splitting/weighting between backends for a canary rollout, or multiple teams need to attach routes to a shared entry point without touching each other's config. Gateway API is not yet the dominant CKA exam topic — Ingress (B.11) still is — but the CKA curriculum lists it explicitly, and exam scenarios increasingly ask you to recognize or migrate one to the other.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Gateway API — Role-Oriented Object Chain</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  GatewayClass (cluster-scoped — owned by the INFRA VENDOR)
   "which controller implements this class?"
   gatewayClassName: nginx
        │
        ▼
  Gateway (namespaced — owned by the PLATFORM team)
   "the actual listener: IP / port / protocol / TLS"
   metadata.name: easypj-gateway
   spec.listeners: [{name: http, port: 80, protocol: HTTP}]
   spec.listeners[].allowedRoutes.namespaces.from: Same | All | Selector
        │  (HTTPRoute attaches UP via parentRefs)
        ▼
  HTTPRoute (namespaced — owned by the APP team, e.g. easypj)
   "which path/host goes to which backend Service"
   spec.parentRefs: [{name: easypj-gateway}]
   spec.hostnames: [easypj.example.com]
   spec.rules: [{matches:[path:/], backendRefs:[easypj-web]}]
        │
        ▼
  Service: easypj-web (ClusterIP, port 80)
        │
        ▼
  Pods (selector: app=easypj)
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Gateway — Listener Definition</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Gateway
metadata:
  name: easypj-gateway
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  gatewayClassName: nginx               # ← Infra: which controller implements it
  listeners:
  - name: http
    protocol: HTTP                      <span class="comment"># HTTP | HTTPS | TLS | TCP | UDP</span>
    port: 80
    allowedRoutes:
      namespaces:
        from: Same                      # ← Only routes in THIS namespace may attach
                                        <span class="comment">#   (Same | All | Selector)</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>HTTPRoute — Path-Based Routing to a Service</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: easypj-route
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  parentRefs:                           # ← Attaches UP to the Gateway
  - name: easypj-gateway
  hostnames:
  - easypj.example.com
  rules:
  - matches:
    - path:
        type: PathPrefix                # ← PathPrefix | Exact | RegularExpression
        value: /
    backendRefs:                        # ← Target Service (supports weighted splitting)
    - name: easypj-web
      port: 80
  - matches:                            # ← A second rule for /api
    - path:
        type: PathPrefix
        value: /api
    backendRefs:
    - name: easypj-api
      port: 80</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Ingress (B.11)</th><th>Gateway API (B.11b)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Object model</strong></td><td>1 flat object, vendor annotations for extras</td><td>3 role-oriented objects (GatewayClass/Gateway/HTTPRoute)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Traffic splitting</strong></td><td>Controller-specific annotations only</td><td>Native <code>backendRefs[].weight</code> — no annotations</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Cross-namespace routing</strong></td><td>Vendor annotation hacks</td><td><code>allowedRoutes</code> + <code>ReferenceGrant</code>, standardized</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Status reporting</strong></td><td>Implementation-specific, often just events</td><td>Standardized <code>.status.parents[].conditions</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Protocol coverage</strong></td><td>Mostly HTTP/HTTPS</td><td>HTTP/gRPC/TLS/TCP/UDP via separate *Route kinds</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Team ownership split</strong></td><td>Single object, hard to delegate safely</td><td>Infra owns Gateway, app team owns HTTPRoute</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Gateway API CRDs Aren't Built Into the API Server</h5>
                <p>Unlike Ingress (a core-ish API always present), Gateway API's <code>Gateway</code>/<code>HTTPRoute</code>/<code>GatewayClass</code> kinds are Custom Resource Definitions that must be installed separately (e.g. the standard channel CRD bundle) before a controller can even be deployed. <code>kubectl get httproute</code> failing with <code>error: the server doesn't have a resource type "httproute"</code> almost always means the Gateway API CRDs are missing — not that your YAML is wrong. Also: an HTTPRoute whose <code>parentRefs</code> name/namespace doesn't match any real Gateway sits silently inert with no traffic and no loud error — check <code>kubectl get httproute easypj-route -o yaml</code> and read <code>.status.parents[].conditions</code> for the real reason (commonly <code>Accepted: False, reason: NoMatchingParent</code>).</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Exam Migration Pattern</h5>
                <p>A realistic scenario: "Convert the existing <code>easypj-ingress</code> to the Gateway API." Map it field by field — Ingress's <code>ingressClassName</code> becomes the Gateway's <code>gatewayClassName</code>; each Ingress <code>rules[].host</code> becomes an HTTPRoute <code>hostnames</code> entry; each <code>rules[].http.paths[]</code> becomes an HTTPRoute <code>rules[].matches[].path</code> + <code>backendRefs</code>; TLS moves from the Ingress <code>tls[]</code> block onto the Gateway's listener (a Gateway listener can terminate TLS directly). The one thing that has no Ingress equivalent: the HTTPRoute must <code>parentRefs</code> an already-existing Gateway — create the Gateway first, or the HTTPRoute has nothing to attach to.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.12: ConfigMap + Secret Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.12 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-12">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.12 ConfigMap + Secret Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>ConfigMaps store non-sensitive config; Secrets store sensitive data (base64-encoded, not encrypted by default).</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ConfigMap</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj
data:                                   # ← Key-value pairs (plain text)
  DEBUG: "False"
  DB_HOST: "easypj-db-svc"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*"
  DB_NAME: "easypj_db"</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Secret (base64-encoded values)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secret
  namespace: easypj
type: Opaque                            # ← Most common type
data:                                   # ← Values MUST be base64-encoded
  SECRET_KEY: c2VjcmV0MTIz              <span class="comment"># echo -n 'secret123' | base64</span>
  DB_PASSWORD: cGFzc3dvcmQ=             <span class="comment"># echo -n 'password' | base64</span>
  DB_USER: ZWFzeXBq                     <span class="comment"># echo -n 'easypj' | base64</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Secret — stringData Alternative (skip the base64 step)</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">stringData is write-only: the API server base64-encodes each value and merges it into .data the moment you apply — it never appears back in a `kubectl get` output</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secret-plain
  namespace: easypj
type: Opaque
stringData:                             # ← API server base64-encodes for you on apply
  SECRET_KEY: "secret123"               <span class="comment"># No manual `base64` piping needed</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 data vs. stringData — Same Secret, Two Input Formats</h5>
                <p><code>data</code> holds values you must base64-encode yourself before writing the YAML (<code>echo -n 'secret123' | base64</code>) — this is what B.12's first Secret template above does. <code>stringData</code> holds plain, human-readable text and lets the API server do the base64-encoding for you at apply time, so you never hand-run <code>base64</code> during the exam. Both fields describe the <strong>same underlying object</strong> and both end up stored as base64 in etcd — <code>stringData</code> is purely a write-time convenience, not a different storage format. One asymmetry to remember: run <code>kubectl get secret easypj-secret-plain -o yaml</code> and you will only ever see <code>data</code> (already-encoded) in the response — <code>stringData</code> is consumed on write and never echoed back, so don't expect to find it there when auditing a live Secret.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — imperative ConfigMap/Secret creation</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ConfigMap imperatively</span>
kubectl create configmap easypj-config \
  --from-literal=DEBUG=False \
  --from-literal=DB_HOST=easypj-db-svc \
  --from-literal=ALLOWED_HOSTS='*' \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Create Secret imperatively (auto-encodes to base64!)</span>
kubectl create secret generic easypj-secret \
  --from-literal=SECRET_KEY=secret123 \
  --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=password \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Decode a secret value</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-secret -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.data.DB_PASSWORD}' | base64 -d
<span class="comment"># password</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>ConfigMap/Secret → Pod — Three Injection Methods</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  METHOD 1: Environment Variables (most common)                  │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────                 │
  │  env:                                                           │
  │  - name: DB_HOST                                                │
  │    valueFrom:                                                   │
  │      configMapKeyRef:                                           │
  │        name: easypj-config                                      │
  │        key: DB_HOST                                             │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  METHOD 2: envFrom (all keys at once)                           │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────                          │
  │  envFrom:                                                       │
  │  - configMapRef:                                                │
  │      name: easypj-config                                        │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  METHOD 3: Volume Mount (as files)                              │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────                              │
  │  volumes:                                                       │
  │  - name: config-vol                                             │
  │    configMap:                                                    │
  │      name: easypj-config                                        │
  │  volumeMounts:                                                  │
  │  - name: config-vol                                             │
  │    mountPath: /etc/config     ← Each key becomes a file         │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Secrets Aren't Encrypted by Default!</h5>
                <p>Kubernetes Secrets are only base64-encoded, not encrypted. Anyone with API access can decode them. For real security: enable <strong>encryption at rest</strong> (<code>EncryptionConfiguration</code>), use <strong>RBAC</strong> to restrict Secret access, and consider external tools like <strong>HashiCorp Vault</strong> or <strong>Sealed Secrets</strong>.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.13: HorizontalPodAutoscaler Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.13 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-13">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.13 HorizontalPodAutoscaler Template (HPA)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>The HPA is a controller that watches a metric (usually CPU or memory utilization) and adjusts a Deployment's or StatefulSet's <code>replicas</code> count to keep that metric near a target. It scales <strong>horizontally</strong> — adding/removing pod copies — as opposed to a VerticalPodAutoscaler, which resizes a pod's own <code>resources.requests/limits</code> in place (VPA isn't part of the core CKA curriculum, but knowing the distinction avoids confusing "scale out" with "scale up" on exam wording).</p>
            <p><strong>When you'd use it:</strong> any stateless workload with variable load — the easypj-web tier during a hiring-season traffic spike is the textbook case. It does <em>not</em> fit StatefulSet workloads where each replica has a unique identity and data (easypj-db); scaling those safely is a manual, deliberate operation, not something you want an automatic controller doing.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>HPA Control Loop — How Scaling Decisions Happen</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
                     every --horizontal-pod-autoscaler-sync-period (default 15s)
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │                                                                      │
   ▼                                                                      │
  metrics-server ──▶ HPA controller ──▶ compare avg CPU% vs target (70%)  │
  (reads pod cpu          │                                               │
   usage via kubelet)     ├─ usage &gt; target → scale UP  (≤ maxReplicas)  │
                          ├─ usage &lt; target → scale DOWN (after           │
                          │                    stabilizationWindowSeconds)│
                          ▼                                               │
              PATCHES Deployment.spec.replicas ───────────────────────────┘
                          │
                          ▼
              ReplicaSet creates/terminates Pods to match new replica count
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>HorizontalPodAutoscaler — CPU Utilization Target</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-hpa
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:                       # ← What to scale
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj-web
  minReplicas: 2                        # ← Floor — never scale below this
  maxReplicas: 10                       # ← Ceiling — never scale above this
  metrics:
  - type: Resource                      <span class="comment"># Resource | Pods | Object | External</span>
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization               # ← % of the pod's cpu REQUEST
        averageUtilization: 70          # ← Scale to keep avg CPU ~70% of requests
  behavior:                             # ← Optional: tame flapping
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300   # ← Wait 5 min of low usage before scaling down
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 0     # ← Scale up immediately, no delay</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — imperative HPA creation &amp; inspection</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create the same HPA imperatively (quick exam shortcut)</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment easypj-web -n easypj \
  --cpu-percent=70 --min=2 --max=10

<span class="comment"># Watch live status — CURRENT column blank/&lt;unknown&gt; = metrics-server problem</span>
kubectl get hpa easypj-web-hpa -n easypj -w
<span class="comment"># NAME              REFERENCE                TARGETS   MINPODS   MAXPODS   REPLICAS</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-hpa    Deployment/easypj-web    45%/70%   2         10        3</span>

<span class="comment"># Diagnose a stuck HPA</span>
kubectl describe hpa easypj-web-hpa -n easypj
kubectl top pods -n easypj                 <span class="comment"># requires metrics-server</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Metric <code>type</code></th><th>Source</th><th>Example Use</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Resource</strong></td><td>Built-in CPU/memory from metrics-server</td><td>Scale easypj-web on CPU utilization (most common on exam)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Pods</strong></td><td>Custom metric averaged across pods</td><td>Scale on requests-per-second per pod</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Object</strong></td><td>Metric on one specific object (e.g. an Ingress)</td><td>Scale on total Ingress requests/sec</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>External</strong></td><td>Metric from outside the cluster</td><td>Scale on a cloud queue's message backlog</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ No Resource Requests = HPA Frozen at minReplicas</h5>
                <p>Utilization is a <em>percentage of the request</em>, not an absolute number. If <code>easypj-web</code>'s containers omit <code>resources.requests.cpu</code>, the HPA has nothing to compute a percentage against and simply cannot scale — it silently sits at <code>minReplicas</code> forever with <code>TARGETS</code> showing <code>&lt;unknown&gt;/70%</code>. This is a favorite CKA trap: the HPA YAML is perfectly correct, the bug is missing <code>requests</code> on the target Deployment (B.2). Also remember: manually running <code>kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5</code> while an HPA is active is a losing battle — the HPA's next reconcile pass will patch replicas back to whatever it computes.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Common Exam Scenario vs. Wrong Answer</h5>
                <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> "Autoscale easypj-web between 2 and 10 replicas, targeting 70% CPU." <strong>Correct:</strong> HPA with <code>scaleTargetRef</code> pointing at the exact Deployment name/namespace, sane min/max, AND confirm <code>metrics-server</code> is actually running (<code>kubectl get deploy metrics-server -n kube-system</code>) before declaring done. <strong>Wrong answer that looks right:</strong> a syntactically valid HPA applied against a Deployment whose pods have no CPU requests — it creates cleanly, shows up in <code>kubectl get hpa</code>, and never scales, which examiners specifically check for.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.14: Job + CronJob Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.14 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-14">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.14 Job + CronJob Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>A <strong>Job</strong> runs a pod (or several) to completion — unlike a Deployment, it doesn't keep pods running forever, it runs them until they succeed a set number of times. A <strong>CronJob</strong> wraps a Job on a schedule, exactly like Unix cron. Together they're how you run one-off or recurring batch work — a Django migration, a nightly database backup — without permanently occupying cluster capacity.</p>
            <p><strong>When you'd use each:</strong> a bare Job for a manual, one-time task you trigger yourself (easypj-migrate before a release); a CronJob for anything recurring on a schedule (easypj-backup every night at 02:00). Never use a Deployment for either — a Deployment expects its pods to run forever and will endlessly restart a container that exits 0 after finishing its work.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>CronJob → Job → Pod — Three Nesting Levels</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  CronJob: easypj-backup
   "WHEN to run"  spec.schedule: "0 2 * * *"  (cron syntax, cluster-controller TZ)
   spec.concurrencyPolicy: Forbid            (don't overlap runs)
        │
        │  at 02:00 daily, the CronJob controller creates a...
        ▼
  Job: easypj-backup-28421900  (auto-named, timestamp suffix)
   "WHAT counts as done"  spec.completions / spec.parallelism / spec.backoffLimit
        │
        │  the Job controller creates...
        ▼
  Pod: easypj-backup-28421900-x7k2p
   "the actual work"  spec.template.spec.restartPolicy: OnFailure (NOT Always!)
                       spec.template.spec.containers[0].command: [pg_dump ...]
        │
        ▼
  Container exits 0 → Pod Succeeded → Job marks itself Complete
  Container exits ≠0 → retried up to backoffLimit → then Job marks Failed
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Job — Run-to-Completion (e.g. a one-off DB migration)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-migrate
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  completions: 1                        # ← How many successful pods needed
  parallelism: 1                        # ← How many run at once
  backoffLimit: 4                       # ← Retries before marking the Job Failed
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 300            # ← Optional: kill the whole Job after 5 min
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: OnFailure          # ← MUST be OnFailure or Never (not Always)
      containers:
      - name: migrate
        image: easypj:v1
        command: ["python", "manage.py", "migrate"]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>CronJob — Scheduled Job (e.g. nightly DB backup)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: easypj-backup
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"                 # ← 02:00 daily (min hour dom month dow)
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid             # ← Allow | Forbid | Replace
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3         # ← Keep last 3 successful Jobs (debugging)
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1             # ← Keep last 1 failed Job
  startingDeadlineSeconds: 100          # ← Skip a run if missed by more than this
  jobTemplate:                          # ← Everything below is a Job spec (B.14 above)
    spec:
      backoffLimit: 2
      template:
        spec:
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          containers:
          - name: backup
            image: easypj:v1
            command: ["sh", "-c", "pg_dump easypj > /backup/db-$(date +%F).sql"]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th><code>concurrencyPolicy</code></th><th>Behavior</th><th>Use Case</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Allow</strong> (default)</td><td>Runs overlap freely — a new Job starts even if the previous one is still running</td><td>Idempotent, independent jobs</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Forbid</strong></td><td>Skips the new run entirely if the previous one hasn't finished</td><td>easypj-backup — never run two pg_dumps concurrently</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Replace</strong></td><td>Cancels the still-running previous Job and starts the new one</td><td>Only the latest run's result matters</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — Job/CronJob lifecycle &amp; debugging</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Imperative Job (great exam shortcut, then edit the yaml if needed)</span>
kubectl create job easypj-migrate --image=easypj:v1 \
  -n easypj -- python manage.py migrate

<span class="comment"># Trigger a CronJob's Job manually right now (doesn't wait for the schedule)</span>
kubectl create job easypj-backup-manual --from=cronjob/easypj-backup -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Watch a Job to completion</span>
kubectl get job easypj-migrate -n easypj -w

<span class="comment"># Inspect logs of a completed Job's pod</span>
kubectl logs -n easypj job/easypj-migrate

<span class="comment"># List Jobs a CronJob has spawned</span>
kubectl get jobs -n easypj -l job-name --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ restartPolicy: Always Is Rejected — And Deleting a Job Orphans Its Pods</h5>
                <p>A Job's pod template <strong>must</strong> set <code>restartPolicy: OnFailure</code> or <code>Never</code> — <code>Always</code> (the Pod default) is rejected outright by the API server, because a Job's whole point is to let containers finish and exit; a restart-forever policy would make "completion" impossible to detect. Separately: <code>kubectl delete job easypj-migrate</code> also deletes its pods by default (cascading delete) — but if you ever pass <code>--cascade=orphan</code>, the pods are left running disconnected from any controller, invisible to <code>kubectl get jobs</code> yet still consuming resources.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Reading Cron Syntax Fast</h5>
                <p><code>schedule</code> is five fields: <code>minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week</code>. <code>"0 2 * * *"</code> = 02:00 every day. <code>"*/15 * * * *"</code> = every 15 minutes. <code>"0 0 * * 0"</code> = midnight every Sunday. The CronJob controller's clock is the <strong>kube-controller-manager's</strong> local time (UTC by default) unless you set the newer <code>spec.timeZone</code> field — don't assume it matches your own laptop's timezone when debugging "why didn't it run."</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.15: StatefulSet Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.15 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-15">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.15 StatefulSet Template (volumeClaimTemplates)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>A StatefulSet is Deployment's cousin for workloads that need <strong>stable identity</strong> — a predictable name, a predictable network address, and its own dedicated disk that follows it across reschedules. This is the correct workload kind for easypj-db (PostgreSQL): each replica is not interchangeable the way easypj-web's pods are.</p>
            <p><strong>When you'd use it instead of a Deployment:</strong> any clustered stateful system where replicas are NOT identical copies — databases (Postgres, MySQL, MongoDB, Cassandra), message brokers (Kafka, RabbitMQ clusters), or anything that needs to know "which replica am I" (e.g. replica 0 is primary, 1 and 2 are read replicas). If your pods are truly stateless and interchangeable, a StatefulSet is unnecessary overhead — use a Deployment (B.2).</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>StatefulSet Identity — Ordinal Names, Stable DNS, Per-Pod Storage</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  Headless Service: easypj-db  (clusterIP: None — see B.3)
   gives ONE DNS A record PER ready pod, not a shared VIP
        │
        ├── easypj-db-0.easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local ──▶ Pod easypj-db-0
        │                                                        └─ PVC: data-easypj-db-0 (10Gi)
        ├── easypj-db-1.easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local ──▶ Pod easypj-db-1
        │                                                        └─ PVC: data-easypj-db-1 (10Gi)
        └── easypj-db-2.easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local ──▶ Pod easypj-db-2
                                                                 └─ PVC: data-easypj-db-2 (10Gi)

  Creation order:  -0 fully Running+Ready, THEN -1 starts, THEN -2 starts
  Deletion order:  reverse (-2 first, then -1, then -0)
  Reschedule:      easypj-db-1 always comes back as "-1", same PVC re-attached
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>StatefulSet — PostgreSQL with Per-Pod Storage</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: easypj-db
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceName: easypj-db                # ← MUST be a headless Service (clusterIP: None, see B.3)
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:                        # ← MUST match template.metadata.labels (immutable)
      app: easypj-db
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-db
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: postgres
        image: postgres:16
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5432
        volumeMounts:
        - name: data                    # ← Matches a volumeClaimTemplates entry below
          mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data
  volumeClaimTemplates:                 # ← ONE PVC PER POD — never shared
  - metadata:
      name: data
    spec:
      accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
      storageClassName: fast-storage    # ← Dynamic provisioning (see B.5)
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 10Gi</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Deployment (B.2)</th><th>StatefulSet (B.15)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Pod naming</strong></td><td>Random suffix (easypj-web-7d9f8c-x2j4k)</td><td>Stable ordinal (easypj-db-0, -1, -2)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Storage</strong></td><td>Shared volume, or none</td><td>Dedicated PVC per pod via <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Network identity</strong></td><td>Any pod behind the Service VIP</td><td>Stable per-pod DNS via headless Service</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Startup/shutdown order</strong></td><td>All pods created in parallel</td><td>Strictly ordered (0, then 1, then 2…)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Scaling down</strong></td><td>Pod + any shared storage reference gone</td><td>Pod deleted, PVC <em>kept</em> — needs manual cleanup</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Scaling Down Does NOT Delete the PVCs</h5>
                <p>Running <code>kubectl scale statefulset easypj-db --replicas=1</code> terminates <code>easypj-db-2</code> and <code>easypj-db-1</code>, but their PVCs (<code>data-easypj-db-1</code>, <code>data-easypj-db-2</code>) are deliberately left behind — this is a safety feature, not a bug, so you never lose a database's data from an accidental scale-down. If you scale back <em>up</em>, the returning pods reattach their old PVCs and their old data. If you actually want the data gone, you must delete those PVCs yourself. Also remember: <code>serviceName</code> must point at an existing <strong>headless</strong> Service (clusterIP: None) — if you forget to create it, or create a normal ClusterIP Service instead, the StatefulSet's pods still come up, but stable per-pod DNS never works and any client relying on <code>easypj-db-0.easypj-db...</code> resolution fails.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Common Exam Scenario vs. Wrong Answer</h5>
                <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> "Deploy a 3-replica PostgreSQL StatefulSet named easypj-db with 10Gi of dedicated storage per replica." <strong>Correct:</strong> create the headless Service <em>first</em> (or at least before checking pod-0's readiness), then the StatefulSet with a matching <code>serviceName</code> and a <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> block. <strong>Wrong answer that looks right:</strong> reusing a single shared PVC across all 3 replicas via a plain <code>volumes:</code> + <code>volumeMounts:</code> block (copied from a Deployment mental model) — this applies cleanly but every replica writes to the SAME disk, which is exactly wrong for a clustered database and will corrupt PostgreSQL's data directory the moment two replicas start.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.16: LimitRange + ResourceQuota Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.16 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-16">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.16 LimitRange + ResourceQuota Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>These two objects are how a cluster admin governs resource usage per namespace. <strong>LimitRange</strong> operates <em>per object</em> — it sets defaults for pods/containers that omit <code>resources</code>, and enforces min/max bounds on any that specify them. <strong>ResourceQuota</strong> operates on the <em>whole namespace</em> — a hard aggregate ceiling across every object of that type combined.</p>
            <p><strong>When you'd use them:</strong> in a shared multi-tenant cluster, give every team's namespace (e.g. <code>easypj</code>) both — a LimitRange so a developer who forgets <code>resources:</code> still gets sane defaults instead of "unbounded," and a ResourceQuota so that team's <em>total</em> footprint can't starve other teams sharing the cluster. This is exam-relevant governance content, distinct from B.13's HPA (which reacts to load) — these are static admission-time caps.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>LimitRange (per-object) vs ResourceQuota (namespace aggregate)</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  namespace: easypj
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ResourceQuota: easypj-quota                                       │
  │   hard.requests.cpu: "4"     ← SUM of every pod's requested cpu    │
  │   hard.pods: "20"            ← at most 20 pods total, ever         │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │  LimitRange: easypj-limits                                    │ │
  │  │   applies its default/min/max to EACH container individually  │ │
  │  │                                                                │ │
  │  │   Pod A (no resources set)     Pod B (resources: {cpu: "2"})  │ │
  │  │    ↓ LimitRange fills in       ↓ LimitRange checks vs max     │ │
  │  │   cpu: 250m (defaultRequest)   "2" &gt; max "1" → REJECTED       │ │
  │  │   cpu: 500m (default limit)    at admission time              │ │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │       Every pod that IS admitted still counts toward the           │
  │       ResourceQuota's namespace-wide running total ▲                │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>LimitRange — Per-Container Defaults &amp; Bounds</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-limits
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container                     # ← Container | Pod | PersistentVolumeClaim
    default:                            # ← Applied if a container OMITS limits
      cpu: "500m"
      memory: "256Mi"
    defaultRequest:                     # ← Applied if a container OMITS requests
      cpu: "250m"
      memory: "128Mi"
    max:                                # ← Reject any container asking for more
      cpu: "1"
      memory: "512Mi"
    min:                                # ← Reject any container asking for less
      cpu: "100m"
      memory: "64Mi"</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ResourceQuota — Namespace-Wide Aggregate Cap</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "4"                   # ← SUM across every pod in the namespace
    requests.memory: 8Gi
    limits.cpu: "8"
    limits.memory: 16Gi
    pods: "20"                          # ← Object-count caps work too
    persistentvolumeclaims: "5"
    services.loadbalancers: "2"         # ← Cap expensive cloud resources</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th><code>type</code> (LimitRange)</th><th>Governs</th><th>Typical Field</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Container</strong></td><td>Each container inside a pod individually</td><td>cpu/memory default, defaultRequest, min, max</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Pod</strong></td><td>The SUM across all containers in one pod</td><td>min, max (no default — pods don't have direct resources)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>PersistentVolumeClaim</strong></td><td>Requested storage size per PVC</td><td>min, max on <code>storage</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ A ResourceQuota on requests/limits Makes Bare Pods Un-Admissible</h5>
                <p>The instant a namespace has a ResourceQuota that constrains <code>requests.cpu</code>/<code>requests.memory</code> (or the limits equivalents), the API server refuses to admit <em>any</em> new pod whose containers don't explicitly set those fields — even ones that would otherwise be fine. This is precisely why a LimitRange with sane <code>default</code>/<code>defaultRequest</code> values is paired with a ResourceQuota in practice: the LimitRange fills in the missing fields at admission time, BEFORE the ResourceQuota check runs, so bare pods stay deployable. Deploy a ResourceQuota alone, without a LimitRange, and you'll see a wave of confusing <code>forbidden: failed quota</code> errors from developers who never set resources before.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Inspecting Usage vs. the Cap</h5>
                <p><code>kubectl describe resourcequota easypj-quota -n easypj</code> shows a live <code>Used</code> vs <code>Hard</code> table for every governed resource — the fastest way to see why a deploy is being rejected. <code>kubectl describe limitrange easypj-limits -n easypj</code> shows the same for the per-object defaults/bounds. Both are namespace-scoped objects with NO cluster-wide equivalent — there is no "ClusterResourceQuota" in vanilla Kubernetes (some distributions add one as an extension).</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.17: CRD + sample Custom Resource Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.17 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-17">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.17 CustomResourceDefinition + sample Custom Resource Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>A <strong>CustomResourceDefinition (CRD)</strong> teaches the API server a brand-new <code>kind</code> — after applying one, that kind behaves exactly like a built-in resource: it's stored in etcd, has RBAC rules, supports <code>kubectl get/describe/apply</code>, and can be watched. The CRD alone only adds the <em>type</em>; a <strong>controller/operator</strong> is the separate piece of software that watches instances of that type and actually does something in response — the CRD is the noun, the controller is the verb.</p>
            <p><strong>When you'd use this:</strong> when you're extending Kubernetes to manage something it doesn't natively understand — a <code>Backup</code> resource that an operator turns into scheduled pg_dump jobs, a <code>Certificate</code> resource that cert-manager turns into a real TLS secret, or an internal platform team's own abstraction (e.g. an <code>EasyPjApp</code> CRD that bundles Deployment+Service+Ingress behind one simpler object for other teams to use). On the CKA exam, CRD questions are usually about applying a given CRD + CR correctly and verifying with <code>kubectl explain</code> — you are not expected to write a controller.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>CRD Teaches the Type; a Controller Makes It DO Something</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  Step 1 — Admin applies the CRD (once, cluster-wide)
   CustomResourceDefinition: backups.easypj.example.com
    "there is now a kind called Backup, group easypj.example.com"
        │
        ▼
  Step 2 — API server now accepts Backup objects
   kubectl get backups          ← works, just like any built-in kind
   kubectl explain backup.spec  ← shows YOUR schema's fields
        │
  Step 3 — Someone applies a Custom Resource (an INSTANCE of Backup)
   kind: Backup, metadata.name: nightly, spec.schedule: "0 2 * * *"
        │  stored in etcd — but nothing happens yet without a watcher!
        ▼
  Step 4 — A controller/operator Deployment watches Backup objects
   "I see a new/changed Backup → reconcile → create a CronJob (B.14)
    that runs pg_dump on the schedule specified in spec.schedule"
        │
        ▼
  Reconciliation loop keeps real cluster state matching desired CR spec
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>CustomResourceDefinition — Teach the API Server a New Kind</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
kind: CustomResourceDefinition
metadata:
  name: backups.easypj.example.com      # ← MUST be &lt;plural&gt;.&lt;group&gt;
spec:
  group: easypj.example.com             # ← Becomes part of the new apiVersion
  scope: Namespaced                     # ← Namespaced | Cluster
  names:
    plural: backups                     # ← Used in kubectl get backups
    singular: backup
    kind: Backup                        # ← Used in YAML's kind: field
    shortNames: ["bk"]                  # ← kubectl get bk (alias)
  versions:
  - name: v1
    served: true                        # ← Is this version reachable via the API?
    storage: true                       # ← Exactly ONE version is the storage one
    schema:
      openAPIV3Schema:                  # ← Gives validation + kubectl explain support
        type: object
        properties:
          spec:
            type: object
            properties:
              schedule: { type: string }
              retentionDays: { type: integer }</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Sample Custom Resource — An Instance of the New Kind</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: easypj.example.com/v1       <span class="comment"># ← group/version from the CRD above</span>
kind: Backup                            # ← kind from the CRD's names.kind
metadata:
  name: nightly
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"                 # ← Must match the CRD's openAPIV3Schema
  retentionDays: 7</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Built-in Resource (e.g. Deployment)</th><th>Custom Resource (e.g. Backup)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Defined by</strong></td><td>Compiled into the API server</td><td>A CustomResourceDefinition you apply</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Acts on its own?</strong></td><td>Yes — built-in controllers ship with every cluster</td><td>No — needs a separately-deployed controller/operator</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Validation</strong></td><td>Hard-coded Go structs</td><td><code>openAPIV3Schema</code> you define yourself</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Upgrade path</strong></td><td>Cluster version upgrade</td><td>Add a new CRD <code>versions[]</code> entry (+ conversion webhook if the shape changed)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ A CRD With No Controller Is Just Fancy Storage</h5>
                <p>It's completely valid — and a common exam trap — to apply a CRD and a matching CR and have <code>kubectl get backups</code> show <code>nightly</code> looking perfectly healthy, while absolutely nothing happens. The CRD only registers the schema and lets etcd store instances; without a controller Deployment actively watching that <code>kind</code>, no CronJob gets created, no backup ever runs. If a scenario says "verify the CRD works," check for a controller pod (usually in its own namespace, e.g. <code>easypj-operator-system</code>) — not just that <code>kubectl apply</code> succeeded.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 kubectl explain Works on Your Custom Kind Too</h5>
                <p>Once the CRD's <code>openAPIV3Schema</code> is in place, <code>kubectl explain backup.spec</code> prints your own field descriptions exactly like it does for built-in resources — a fast way to verify a CRD's schema landed correctly. Also useful: <code>kubectl api-resources | grep backup</code> confirms the CRD is registered and shows its <code>APIVERSION</code>/<code>NAMESPACED</code> columns at a glance.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.18: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL) Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.18 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-18">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.18 ValidatingAdmissionPolicy Template (CEL — built-in policy, no webhook)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>A <strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> lets you enforce custom admission rules using <strong>CEL (Common Expression Language)</strong> expressions evaluated directly inside the API server — no external webhook server to write, deploy, secure, or scale. It's the modern, lower-operational-overhead alternative to a <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code> for a large class of policies (anything expressible as a boolean check over the incoming object).</p>
            <p><strong>When you'd use this over a webhook:</strong> whenever the rule can be written as a CEL expression against the object — "every Deployment's containers must set resource limits," "no Pod may run as root," "Services can't be type LoadBalancer outside the platform namespace." Reach for a full webhook (e.g. Kyverno, OPA Gatekeeper, or a hand-rolled one) only when you need something CEL can't express — calling out to an external system, complex multi-object correlation, or mutating (not just validating) the object.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy vs. a Traditional Webhook</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  TRADITIONAL ValidatingWebhookConfiguration     ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (B.18)
  ────────────────────────────────────────      ────────────────────────────────
  1. kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml                1. kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml
          │                                              │
  2. API server → HTTPS call OUT to a            2. API server evaluates the CEL
     separate webhook Pod/Service                    expression IN-PROCESS
          │  (extra network hop,                        │  (no network hop,
          │   webhook must be up,                        │   nothing else to keep
          │   TLS cert must be valid)                     │   running or patch)
          ▼                                              ▼
  3. Webhook Pod runs its own logic,             3. Built-in evaluator returns
     returns allowed:true/false                      true/false from the expression
          │                                              │
          ▼                                              ▼
  4. API server allows/denies based on            4. API server allows/denies based
     the webhook's HTTP response                      on the CEL result directly

  ⚠️ If the webhook Pod is down and              ✅ Nothing external to be down —
     failurePolicy: Fail → EVERY matching            the policy is as available as
     request cluster-wide gets rejected               the API server itself
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy — Require resources.limits on Every Container</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-resource-limits
spec:
  failurePolicy: Fail                   # ← Fail | Ignore — what to do on evaluation error
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
    - apiGroups: ["apps"]
      apiVersions: ["v1"]
      operations: ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]  # ← Which verbs trigger this policy
      resources: ["deployments"]
  validations:
  - expression: >-                      # ← CEL expression — MUST evaluate to true to allow
      object.spec.template.spec.containers.all(c,
        has(c.resources) && has(c.resources.limits))
    message: "every container must set resources.limits"   # ← Shown to the user on denial</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding — Scope the Policy &amp; Pick the Action</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: require-resource-limits-binding
spec:
  policyName: require-resource-limits   # ← Which ValidatingAdmissionPolicy to bind
  validationActions: ["Deny"]           # ← Deny | Warn | Audit (can combine Warn+Audit)
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector: {}               # ← Empty = all namespaces
                                        <span class="comment"># Or scope it, e.g. to only the easypj namespace:</span>
                                        <span class="comment"># matchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: easypj}</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th><code>validationActions</code></th><th>Effect on a Failing Request</th><th>Use For</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Deny</strong></td><td>Request is rejected outright, like a normal admission failure</td><td>Enforced policy, e.g. blocking privileged pods</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Warn</strong></td><td>Request succeeds, but a warning is returned to the client (visible in <code>kubectl apply</code> output)</td><td>Soft rollout — tell developers before enforcing</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Audit</strong></td><td>Request succeeds, a failure is recorded in the audit log only, no client-visible signal</td><td>Silent compliance monitoring</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ The Policy and the Binding Are Two Separate Objects — Both Are Required</h5>
                <p>A <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> with no <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding</code> pointing at it enforces <strong>nothing</strong> — the policy defines the rule, but the binding is what actually scopes it to namespaces/resources and switches on an enforcement action. This split exists on purpose (one policy, many bindings with different scopes/actions), but it's a common exam trap: applying just the ValidatingAdmissionPolicy and expecting it to start blocking things immediately. Also watch <code>failurePolicy</code>: <code>Fail</code> means a CEL expression that errors at evaluation time (e.g. referencing a field that doesn't exist on some object) blocks the request; <code>Ignore</code> lets it through — choose <code>Fail</code> for security-critical policies, <code>Ignore</code> only when the policy is best-effort.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 CEL Cheat Notes for the Exam</h5>
                <p>Inside a <code>validations[].expression</code>, <code>object</code> is the incoming resource (new state); on UPDATE operations, <code>oldObject</code> is also available for before/after comparisons (e.g. blocking a field from being changed after creation). <code>has(x)</code> checks a field is set without erroring if it's absent. <code>.all(var, condition)</code> / <code>.exists(var, condition)</code> iterate lists — exactly like the example above checking every container. CEL has no side effects and cannot mutate the object — for that you'd still need a mutating webhook.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

