        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-c">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">🚨 Appendix C · Common Errors and Solutions</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">Quick reference table — Error → Cause → Fix for the most common Kubernetes issues you'll encounter on the CKA exam and in production.</p>

        <div class="info-box note">
            <h5>📌 How to use this appendix</h5>
            <p>This appendix is your fast-triage command post. ~90% of real Kubernetes incidents — and nearly every CKA troubleshooting task — are diagnosed by the same two-step reflex: <code>describe</code> (READ THE EVENTS section at the bottom) + <code>logs --previous</code> (the last crashed container's output). From there you isolate the failure by <strong>layer</strong>, working outward from the smallest blast radius to the largest: <strong>pod</strong> → <strong>node/kubelet</strong> → <strong>control plane</strong> (via <code>crictl</code> when <code>kubectl</code> itself is unreachable) → <strong>network/DNS</strong>. This appendix is organized in four tiers, each answering a different question at a different pace:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>§C.1 — Quick Reference Card:</strong> the compact lookup table. Optimized for <em>memory recall under exam pressure</em> — glance, recognize, act.</li>
                <li><strong>§C.2 — Expanded Triage Matrix:</strong> a richer table that adds the exact <strong>FIRST COMMAND</strong> to type for each symptom, plus symptoms the compact card doesn't cover (DNS failures, empty Service endpoints, a missing ConfigMap/Secret reference, an Ingress that silently 404s). Use this when you need to actually <em>act</em>, not just recall.</li>
                <li><strong>§C.3 — Copy-Paste Triage Templates:</strong> thirteen complete, runnable command sequences you can paste directly into a terminal — no adaptation required beyond swapping in your pod/namespace names.</li>
                <li><strong>§C.4 — Exit-Code &amp; Condition Glossary:</strong> what every status string and numeric exit code actually <em>means</em> at the kernel/kubelet level, so you're not just pattern-matching strings you don't understand.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>Troubleshooting Decision Tree — Where to Start</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
  Something is wrong!
        │
        ▼
  ┌───────────────────┐     YES     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Pod running?       ├───────────▶│ Check: logs, exec, describe       │
  │ kubectl get pods   │            │ → CrashLoopBackOff (app crash)    │
  └────────┬──────────┘            │ → OOMKilled (memory limit)        │
           │ NO                     └──────────────────────────────────┘
           ▼
  ┌───────────────────┐     YES     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Pod Pending?       ├───────────▶│ Check: describe pod events        │
  │                    │            │ → Insufficient resources          │
  └────────┬──────────┘            │ → No matching PV                  │
           │ NO                     │ → Taints/tolerations mismatch     │
           ▼                        └──────────────────────────────────┘
  ┌───────────────────┐     YES     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
  │ ImagePullBackOff?  ├───────────▶│ Check: image name, tag, registry  │
  │                    │            │ → Wrong image name/tag            │
  └────────┬──────────┘            │ → Missing imagePullSecrets        │
           │ NO                     └──────────────────────────────────┘
           ▼
  ┌───────────────────┐     YES     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Node NotReady?     ├───────────▶│ SSH to node, check kubelet        │
  │ kubectl get nodes  │            │ → systemctl status kubelet        │
  └────────┬──────────┘            │ → journalctl -u kubelet           │
           │ NO                     └──────────────────────────────────┘
           ▼
  ┌───────────────────┐
  │ API/Network issue? │─────▶ Check kubeconfig, API server, certs
  └───────────────────┘
</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>🔬 Why this exact branch order — the mechanism behind the tree</h5>
                <p>This decision tree isn't arbitrary — it's ordered by <strong>blast radius</strong>, smallest first, because that's also the order the Kubernetes control plane itself evaluates a Pod's lifecycle. A Pod object always passes through the same sequence of gates before it can run: (1) the <strong>scheduler</strong> must bind it to a node — if this gate fails you get <code>Pending</code>; (2) the <strong>kubelet</strong> on that node must pull the image and construct the container — if this gate fails you get <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>/<code>CreateContainerConfigError</code>; (3) the container's own entrypoint process must stay alive — if this gate fails you get <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>/<code>OOMKilled</code>. Each gate is checked by a <em>different</em> component (scheduler vs. kubelet vs. container runtime vs. kernel cgroup controller), which is exactly why the fix for each layer lives in a different place. Working top-down through the tree mirrors this real pipeline: you're not guessing, you're asking "which gate did this Pod fail at?" and the answer tells you which component's logs to read next.</p>
                <p>The last two branches — <strong>Node NotReady</strong> and <strong>API/Network issue</strong> — sit below all the Pod-level branches on purpose: they're <em>cluster-wide</em> failures that will make every Pod-level symptom look confusing and inconsistent at the same time (a Node that stops heartbeating will show unrelated pods flipping to <code>Unknown</code>/evicting simultaneously; a dead API server makes <code>kubectl</code> itself lie to you or hang). If you're seeing multiple, unrelated pods fail in the same few seconds, stop drilling into individual pods and jump straight to these two checks — that pattern is almost always a node or control-plane problem wearing a pod-level disguise.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <p><strong>Exam Debugging Shortcuts:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> — Always start here; the <strong>Events</strong> section at the bottom shows why things failed</li>
                <li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> — See logs from the LAST crashed container</li>
                <li><code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code> — Cluster-wide timeline of issues</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- ─────────── §C.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sc-1">
            <h3 class="section-h">C.1 Quick Reference Card: Error → Cause → Fix</h3>
            <p>This is the Edition-1-style compact lookup card — the twelve errors and status strings you will see the most often, in the CKA exam and in the wild. It's deliberately terse: one line per error, no more than a category, a one-clause cause, and a one-clause fix. The goal is <strong>recall speed</strong>, not completeness — when you're 40 minutes into a timed exam and a pod won't come up, you want your eyes to land on the right row in under two seconds. For the exact command to run next, jump to the richer §C.2 table below.</p>
