                            <td>Fix/remove the finalizer, or fix the webhook; force-delete only as a last resort</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p>💡 <strong>~90% start with:</strong> <code>describe</code> (READ THE EVENTS) + <code>logs --previous</code>. Then isolate by layer: pod → node/kubelet → control plane (<code>crictl</code>) → network/DNS.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Real-world nuance the exam doesn't test</h5>
                <p>In production, several of these symptoms compound. A Node under disk pressure will start evicting pods, which can look like a wave of <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> across unrelated workloads — the real root cause is the node's <code>DiskPressure</code> condition, not the app. Always check <code>kubectl describe node &lt;node&gt;</code> → <strong>Conditions</strong> before you chase individual pods when multiple, unrelated pods on the <em>same node</em> start failing at the same time. Similarly, a CoreDNS outage manifests as connection failures in a dozen different services simultaneously — resist the urge to debug each Service individually; check <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> first.</p>
            </div>

            <h4>🔬 Mechanism Deep Dives — the "why" behind four rows the table can't fully explain</h4>
            <p>A lookup table tells you <em>what</em> to type. It can't tell you <em>why</em> the symptom exists in the first place — and without that, you're pattern-matching strings instead of actually understanding the failure. The four boxes below go one layer deeper on the rows that most often get memorized without being understood.</p>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📌 FailedScheduling — what the scheduler is actually doing when it "can't find a fit"</h5>
                <p>The scheduler evaluates every Pending Pod against every Node in two sequential stages. <strong>Filtering</strong> runs a chain of predicate checks per node — enough allocatable CPU/memory left? does a <code>nodeSelector</code>/<code>nodeAffinity</code> rule match this node's labels? does the pod carry a <code>toleration</code> for every one of this node's <code>NoSchedule</code>/<code>NoExecute</code> taints? is a requested <code>hostPort</code> already taken? Any node that fails even one predicate is dropped from consideration entirely — it never reaches the second stage. <strong>Scoring</strong> then ranks the <em>surviving</em> nodes (spread pods evenly, prefer nodes with the requested image already cached, honor pod (anti-)affinity preferences) and binds the pod to the highest scorer. <code>FailedScheduling</code> means the filtering stage eliminated <strong>every single node</strong> — the Events message literally lists how many nodes failed for which reason (e.g. <code>0/3 nodes are available: 1 Insufficient memory, 2 node(s) had untolerated taint</code>), which is why reading that line carefully is faster than guessing.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📌 CreateContainerConfigError — a kubelet-time failure, not an API-server-time one</h5>
                <p>This one surprises people because it happens <em>after</em> <code>kubectl apply</code> already succeeded — the API server accepts the Pod spec even if it references a ConfigMap/Secret key that doesn't exist, because the API server never resolves that reference; it just stores the pod spec as-is. The resolution happens much later, on the <em>node</em>, when the kubelet's pod-sync loop actually tries to materialize the container: it calls out to the API server to fetch the referenced ConfigMap/Secret object, extracts the named key, and injects it as an env var or volume file. If that object or key is missing at <em>that</em> moment, the kubelet can't construct a valid container spec and reports <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code> instead of ever starting the container process — so there's no exit code, no logs, nothing to see with <code>kubectl logs</code>. The fix is always to make the referenced object/key actually exist (or fix the typo in the pod spec's <code>key:</code> field); once it does, the kubelet's next sync pass — no pod restart needed — resolves it automatically.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📌 DNS NXDOMAIN — why the same lookup can fail as a short name but work as an FQDN</h5>
                <p>CoreDNS resolution inside a pod is governed by <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>, which the kubelet auto-generates with a <code>search</code> list (<code>&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>, <code>svc.cluster.local</code>, <code>cluster.local</code>, then your node's own upstream domains) and an <code>ndots:5</code> option. <code>ndots:5</code> means: if the name you're looking up has <strong>fewer than 5 dots</strong>, the resolver does <em>not</em> try it as-is first — it appends each search-list suffix in turn and tries those first, only falling back to the bare name (via the upstream, non-cluster resolver) last. A short name like <code>easypj-web</code> (0 dots) gets <code>easypj-web.easypj.svc.cluster.local</code> tried first, which succeeds inside the right namespace — but if you're calling it from a <em>different</em> namespace than the Service lives in, every suffix in your search list is wrong and you get <code>NXDOMAIN</code> even though the Service is perfectly healthy. A fully-qualified name (5 dots, ending in a trailing structure the resolver recognizes as complete) skips the whole search-list expansion and resolves directly — which is exactly why "use the FQDN" is the reliable fix, not a workaround.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📌 etcd unavailable — quorum, not just "is the process running"</h5>
                <p>etcd is a Raft-consensus store: every write must be acknowledged by a <strong>majority</strong> of etcd members before it's considered committed, and reads served by a follower are only safe once that follower has caught up to the leader. A 3-node etcd cluster tolerates exactly <strong>one</strong> member being down (2 of 3 is still a majority); a 5-node cluster tolerates two. This is why "etcd unavailable" is scarier than a single dead pod — if you lose quorum (e.g. 2 of 3 members down), the <em>entire cluster</em> stops accepting writes, including the write that would normally record a Pod's status change, even though the API server process itself may still be technically running. <code>etcdctl endpoint health</code> and <code>etcdctl member list</code> tell you not just "is etcd running" but "does the cluster currently have quorum" — always check quorum status before assuming a single-member restart will fix things, and never restore a single member's data directory from an old snapshot without also correctly regenerating that member's identity in the cluster's member list, or you'll corrupt the Raft log for the whole cluster.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Copy-Paste Triage Templates ─── -->
        <!-- ─────────── §C.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sc-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">C.3 Copy-Paste Triage Templates: Detailed Fix Walkthroughs</h3>
            <p>These are complete, runnable command sequences — not just a single command, but the whole drill-down you'd actually type in order, from first symptom to confirmed fix. They cover the thirteen scenarios that account for the overwhelming majority of both real production incidents and CKA troubleshooting-domain tasks. Every block is safe to copy-paste as-is; just swap in your own pod/namespace/node names. The first six below (universal first move, CrashLoopBackOff, ImagePullBackOff, Pod Pending/FailedScheduling, PVC Pending, and Service-has-no-endpoints) are the ones you'll reach for constantly. The remaining seven (DNS resolution, Ingress not routing, control-plane-down, RBAC Forbidden, Node NotReady, certificate expiry, and a Pod stuck Terminating) are rarer but far more painful when you don't already know the sequence cold — they're exactly the kind of scenario where fumbling for the right command costs you exam minutes or production downtime.</p>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 CKA Debug Pattern (memorize this!)</h5>
                <p><code>describe</code> → <code>logs</code> → <code>exec</code> → <code>events</code>. Start with <code>describe</code> for events, check <code>logs</code> for app errors, <code>exec</code> into the pod to test connectivity, and <code>get events</code> for cluster-wide issues.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — the universal first move (works for almost any failing pod)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Whatever is broken, start here — this three-command combo solves the</span>
<span class="comment"># majority of pod-level incidents before you even reach for a specialized drill:</span>

kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; | sed -n '/Events/,$p'
<span class="comment"># Prints just the Events tail — the human-readable "why" Kubernetes already</span>
<span class="comment"># figured out for you (scheduling failures, pull errors, probe failures...)</span>

kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt; --previous       <span class="comment"># last crashed container's stdout/stderr</span>

kubectl get events -n &lt;ns&gt; --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp | tail
<span class="comment"># Namespace-wide timeline — catches issues that don't belong to any single</span>
<span class="comment"># pod (e.g. a FailedCreate on the ReplicaSet, or a quota rejection)</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📌 Why this works so well</h5>
                <p>Kubernetes controllers write their reasoning into the Events stream as they act — the scheduler, the kubelet, the ReplicaSet controller, and the image-pull machinery all emit an Event the instant something doesn't go as planned. You are almost never the first to "discover" a failure; the control plane already diagnosed it and left a note. The single biggest speed-up you can make to your troubleshooting habit is training yourself to read that note before forming your own hypothesis.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — CrashLoopBackOff deep debug</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check pod status and restart count</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME         READY   STATUS             RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5          2m</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. See why it crashed (logs from PREVIOUS container)</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web -n easypj --previous
<span class="comment"># ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'django'</span>
<span class="comment"># → Fix: wrong image or missing dependency</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Check events for scheduling/pull issues</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web -n easypj | tail -20
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  BackOff  kubelet  Back-off restarting failed container</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. If env var issue — check ConfigMap exists</span>
kubectl get configmap easypj-config -n easypj
kubectl get secret easypj-secret -n easypj</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — ImagePullBackOff / ErrImagePull deep debug (easypj app image)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Confirm the status and read the exact pull failure reason</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web -n easypj | tail -15
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  Failed  kubelet  Failed to pull image "registry.internal/easypj:v2.4":</span>
<span class="comment">#   rpc error: code = Unknown desc = failed to authorize: 401 Unauthorized</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Two common root causes — tell them apart from the Events text:</span>
<span class="comment">#    a) "manifest unknown" / "not found"  → wrong image name or tag (typo, bad build)</span>
<span class="comment">#    b) "401 Unauthorized" / "no basic auth credentials" → private registry, no pull secret</span>

<span class="comment"># 3a. Fix a bad name/tag — correct the Deployment spec and re-apply</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web easypj-web=registry.internal/easypj:v2.5 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 3b. Fix missing registry auth — create an imagePullSecret and attach it</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.internal \
  --docker-username=easypj-ci --docker-password='&lt;token&gt;' \
  -n easypj
kubectl patch serviceaccount default -n easypj \
  -p '{"imagePullSecrets": [{"name": "regcred"}]}'
<span class="comment"># — or reference it directly under the pod spec's imagePullSecrets: field</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Confirm recovery</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -n easypj -w</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ ImagePullBackOff vs ErrImagePull — they're not the same moment</h5>
                <p><code>ErrImagePull</code> is the <em>first</em> failed attempt; <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> is what the kubelet reports while it's <strong>waiting out an exponential backoff</strong> before retrying. If you fix the credential or the tag but still see <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>, that's expected — the kubelet hasn't hit its next retry window yet. You don't need to delete the pod to force a retry in most cases; wait a few seconds and re-check. If you're on the exam clock and can't wait, delete the pod (its owning Deployment/ReplicaSet immediately recreates it, forcing an instant new pull attempt).</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — Pending Pod / FailedScheduling debug</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check WHY it's pending</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web -n easypj | grep -A5 Events
<span class="comment"># 0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient memory</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check node resources</span>
kubectl top nodes
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 "Allocated resources"

<span class="comment"># 3. If taint issue — check node taints</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep Taints
<span class="comment"># Taints: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Fix: add toleration to pod OR remove taint</span>
kubectl taint node worker-1 node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule-</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — PVC Pending debug (storage-specific — different root causes than Pod Pending)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check the PVC's own status first — it's Pending independently of any pod</span>
kubectl get pvc easypj-data -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME         STATUS    VOLUME   CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-data  Pending                                       fast-ssd       3m</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. See exactly why binding failed</span>
kubectl describe pvc easypj-data -n easypj | tail -10
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  ProvisioningFailed  persistentvolume-controller</span>
<span class="comment">#   storageclass.storage.k8s.io "fast-ssd" not found</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Check what StorageClasses actually exist</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># NAME            PROVISIONER             RECLAIMPOLICY   VOLUMEBINDINGMODE</span>
<span class="comment"># standard (default) rancher.io/local-path Delete          WaitForFirstConsumer</span>

<span class="comment"># 4a. Fix: correct the storageClassName in the PVC (delete + recreate — PVC spec</span>
<span class="comment">#     is immutable after creation), or</span>
<span class="comment"># 4b. If using static provisioning, create a matching PV by hand:</span>
kubectl get pv                                <span class="comment"># any Available PV with matching accessModes/capacity?</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Special case: WaitForFirstConsumer binding mode — the PVC stays Pending</span>
<span class="comment">#    ON PURPOSE until a pod that uses it is scheduled. This is NORMAL, not a bug:</span>
kubectl get sc fast-ssd -o jsonpath='{.volumeBindingMode}'
<span class="comment"># WaitForFirstConsumer  ← don't "fix" this; create the consuming pod instead</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📌 Pod Pending vs PVC Pending — read the right object</h5>
                <p>These look similar (both say "Pending") but are diagnosed by <code>describe</code>-ing <em>different objects</em>. A Pod stuck Pending because it references an unbound PVC will show <code>FailedScheduling</code> in its own Events — but the <em>real</em> error message lives on the PVC, not the pod. If a pod's Events just say something vague like "pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims," stop looking at the pod and go straight to <code>kubectl describe pvc</code>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — Service has no endpoints (easypj unreachable internally)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. An empty ENDPOINTS list is the single biggest tell in Service debugging</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME         ENDPOINTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web   &lt;none&gt;      10m        ← no backing pods registered!</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Compare the Service's selector to the actual pod labels</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-web -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'
<span class="comment"># {"app":"easypj-web","tier":"frontend"}</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj --show-labels
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-7c4f  app=easypj-web  ← missing "tier=frontend"! selector mismatch</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Even with matching labels, a pod that's NOT Ready is excluded from endpoints —</span>
<span class="comment">#    check readiness explicitly:</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj -o wide
<span class="comment"># READY column shows 0/1 → readiness probe is failing, not just "not up yet"</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Fix: correct the label (or the selector) to match, and/or fix the readiness probe</span>
kubectl label pod easypj-web-7c4f tier=frontend -n easypj --overwrite

<span class="comment"># 5. Prove it from inside the cluster's network — never trust it until you've</span>
<span class="comment">#    actually curled it from another pod:</span>
kubectl run tmp --image=busybox:1.36 -it --rm --restart=Never -n easypj \
  -- wget -qO- http://easypj-web.easypj.svc.cluster.local:80</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — DNS resolution check</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Reproduce the failure from inside the cluster's network, not from your laptop</span>
kubectl run tmp --image=busybox:1.36 -it --rm --restart=Never -- \
  nslookup easypj-web.easypj.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Server:    10.96.0.10</span>
<span class="comment"># ** server can't find easypj-web.easypj.svc.cluster.local: NXDOMAIN</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Is CoreDNS even up?</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># coredns-5d78c9869d-abcde   0/1   CrashLoopBackOff   ← there's your answer</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. If CoreDNS pods are healthy, check its own logs for Corefile errors</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># 4. If the name IS correct but resolution still fails intermittently, suspect</span>
<span class="comment">#    a short-name / ndots mismatch — always test the FULLY-QUALIFIED name first:</span>
kubectl run tmp --image=busybox:1.36 -it --rm --restart=Never -- \
  nslookup easypj-web.easypj.svc.cluster.local     <span class="comment"># FQDN — should always work</span>
kubectl run tmp --image=busybox:1.36 -it --rm --restart=Never -- \
  nslookup easypj-web                               <span class="comment"># short name — depends on search domains/ndots</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Fix: restart/redeploy CoreDNS, correct its Corefile ConfigMap, or use the</span>
<span class="comment">#    FQDN in application config instead of relying on short-name resolution</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — Ingress not routing (easypj returns 404/timeout from outside)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. First isolate the layer: does the Service work from INSIDE the cluster?</span>
<span class="comment">#    (reuse the "Service has no endpoints" drill above). If yes, the problem</span>
<span class="comment">#    is specifically in the Ingress layer, not the Pods or the Service.</span>
kubectl get ingress -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME         CLASS    HOSTS              ADDRESS   PORTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-ing   &lt;none&gt;   easypj.example.com           80      5m   ← no ADDRESS!</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. An empty ADDRESS column almost always means no controller has claimed</span>
<span class="comment">#    this Ingress — check that an Ingress Controller is actually running:</span>
kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx
<span class="comment"># No resources found in ingress-nginx namespace.   ← there's your answer</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. If a controller IS running, the next most common cause is a class</span>
<span class="comment">#    mismatch — the Ingress must name the controller that should serve it:</span>
kubectl get ingress easypj-ing -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.ingressClassName}'
kubectl get ingressclass
<span class="comment"># NAME    CONTROLLER             PARAMETERS</span>
<span class="comment"># nginx   k8s.io/ingress-nginx   &lt;none&gt;              ← must match spec above</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Confirm the Ingress's own path/backend rules actually point at the</span>
<span class="comment">#    right Service and port — a typo here silently 404s even with a healthy</span>
<span class="comment">#    controller and a healthy Service:</span>
kubectl describe ingress easypj-ing -n easypj | grep -A5 Rules

<span class="comment"># 5. Fix: install the controller (Helm chart or manifest), or set</span>
<span class="comment">#    ingressClassName to match an installed IngressClass, or correct the</span>
<span class="comment">#    backend service/port in the Ingress rule.</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📌 Ingress is a two-object contract — read the right half</h5>
                <p>An <code>Ingress</code> object by itself does nothing — it's just a routing <em>request</em> that sits inert in etcd until some <em>Ingress Controller</em> (nginx, Traefik, cloud LB controller, etc.) watches for Ingress objects matching its class and programs an actual reverse proxy accordingly. This two-object design is exactly why "Ingress not routing" has two independent failure modes that look identical from the outside (a browser timeout or 404) but are diagnosed completely differently: no controller running at all (nothing is listening on the LoadBalancer/NodePort the Ingress expects), versus a controller that's healthy but ignoring this specific Ingress because <code>ingressClassName</code> doesn't match any <code>IngressClass</code> it's watching. Always check <strong>both</strong> — a running controller pod doesn't guarantee it's the one serving your Ingress.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — control plane down (kubectl itself fails) — go node-local</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Symptom: EVERY kubectl command hangs or refuses to connect —</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl itself is not the problem; the API server behind it is.</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># The connection to the server 10.0.0.1:6443 was refused</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. SSH to a control-plane node — kubectl is useless from here on</span>
ssh cp-1

<span class="comment"># 2. Are the static pods (apiserver, etcd, controller-manager, scheduler)</span>
<span class="comment">#    even running as containers? kubectl can't tell you — crictl can:</span>
sudo crictl ps -a
<span class="comment"># CONTAINER   IMAGE                    STATE      NAME</span>
<span class="comment"># a1b2c3d4    kube-apiserver:v1.31.0   Exited     kube-apiserver   ← DEAD</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Read that container's logs directly through the container runtime</span>
sudo crictl logs a1b2c3d4
<span class="comment"># Error: failed to create listener: listen tcp 0.0.0.0:6443: address already in use</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Confirm the kubelet itself is alive — it's what restarts static pods</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -f

<span class="comment"># 5. The fix lives in the static-pod manifest directory — kubelet watches this</span>
<span class="comment">#    directory and auto-restarts anything you fix here, no "kubectl apply" needed:</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># fix the bad flag/port/cert path, save — kubelet picks it up within ~20s</span></code></pre>
