            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Why "Terminating" isn't a state kubectl delete -f can fix</h5>
                <p>Per the official docs, a Pod stuck in <code>Terminating</code> means "a deletion has been issued for the Pod, but the control plane is unable to delete the Pod object" — the object is being kept alive <em>on purpose</em> by its own metadata, specifically a non-empty <code>metadata.finalizers</code> list. A finalizer is a promise: "don't actually erase this object until whatever controller registered this key has finished its cleanup and removed its own entry from the list." The API server enforces that promise for every object with a <code>deletionTimestamp</code> set — it will not fully delete the object while any finalizer string remains, no matter how many times you re-run <code>kubectl delete</code>. This is exactly why running <code>kubectl delete pod &lt;pod&gt; --force --grace-period=0</code> repeatedly does nothing: force-delete only skips the graceful-termination wait for the container process, it does <strong>not</strong> bypass finalizers. The only two real fixes are (1) get the controller that owns the finalizer healthy again so it finishes its job and removes its own finalizer entry, or (2) manually strip the <code>finalizers</code> array yourself — which is safe only when you're certain the cleanup that finalizer was protecting either already happened or doesn't matter, since you're bypassing a safety mechanism, not completing it.</p>
            </div>

