        <!-- ─────────── §C.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sc-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">C.4 Exit-Code &amp; Condition Glossary</h3>
            <p>Status strings like <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> and numeric exit codes like <code>137</code> are not arbitrary — each one maps to a specific, well-defined event at the OS/kernel, container-runtime, or kubelet level. Knowing what each one <em>actually means</em> (not just "that's the crash one") lets you skip straight to the right fix instead of guessing. This is especially important for exit codes: the number itself tells you whether the process crashed on its own (an app bug) or was killed by something external (the kernel, the kubelet, or an operator), which are two completely different classes of problem.</p>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 The exit-code math is worth memorizing</h5>
                <p>Any exit code in the 128+N range means the process was terminated by <strong>signal N</strong>, not by its own <code>return</code>/<code>exit()</code> call. <code>137 = 128 + 9</code> → signal 9 is <code>SIGKILL</code> (immediate, un-catchable — this is what OOMKilled uses). <code>139 = 128 + 11</code> → signal 11 is <code>SIGSEGV</code>, a real memory-safety bug in native code. <code>143 = 128 + 15</code> → signal 15 is <code>SIGTERM</code> (a polite "please shut down," which a well-behaved app can catch and use to drain connections before exiting — this is what a normal <code>kubectl delete pod</code> or a failed liveness probe sends first). If your app routinely exits 143 during rolling updates, that's usually fine; if it routinely exits 137, you have a memory problem, not an app-logic problem. Codes <em>below</em> 128 are a different family entirely and mean the container's own entrypoint process decided to exit — <code>0</code> is success, <code>1</code>-<code>2</code> are the app's own generic/misuse errors, and <code>126</code>/<code>127</code> specifically mean the shell that launched your entrypoint couldn't even <em>execute</em> it (permission bit missing, or the binary/interpreter isn't in the image at all) — those two are almost always an image-build problem, not a runtime problem, so no amount of `describe`-ing the Pod will show you the fix; you have to look at the Dockerfile.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Signal / Code</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Exit 0</strong></td>
                            <td>Container exited cleanly. Note: for a long-running Deployment pod this still triggers a restart (kubelet expects it to run forever); it's only "success" for a Job/CronJob container.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Exit 1</strong></td>
                            <td>Generic application error — an uncaught exception, a failed assertion, a bad config value the app itself rejected. Always start with <code>logs --previous</code>.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Exit 2</strong></td>
                            <td>Shell-level misuse — a builtin/script was called with a bad argument, or (in a bare <code>sh -c</code> entrypoint) a syntax error in the command line itself. Rare for compiled apps, common in hand-written entrypoint shell scripts. Check the exact <code>command</code>/<code>args</code> the container was launched with.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Exit 126</strong></td>
                            <td>The command specified in <code>command</code>/<code>ENTRYPOINT</code> was found on disk but is <strong>not executable</strong> — usually a missing <code>chmod +x</code> in the image build, or a Windows-authored script with the wrong line endings/no shebang. This is a container-<em>image</em> bug, not a Kubernetes config bug — fix the Dockerfile and rebuild.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Exit 127</strong></td>
                            <td>The command specified in <code>command</code>/<code>ENTRYPOINT</code> was <strong>not found</strong> at all — wrong binary path, a base image that doesn't include the interpreter you assumed (e.g. <code>bash</code> in a <code>-slim</code> or <code>distroless</code> image), or a typo. Fixable from the Pod spec only if the fix is the <code>command:</code> field itself; otherwise it's an image rebuild.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Exit 137 (128+9)</strong></td>
                            <td><code>SIGKILL</code> — almost always <strong>OOMKilled</strong> (container exceeded its memory limit) or an external <code>kill -9</code>. Un-catchable: the app gets zero warning and zero chance to clean up.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Exit 139 (128+11)</strong></td>
                            <td><code>SIGSEGV</code> — a genuine segmentation fault in native/compiled code (a Go/C/C++/Rust binary, or a native extension under Python/Node). Not something <code>resources.limits</code> or Kubernetes config can fix — this is an application bug that needs a core dump or debugger, not a triage command.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Exit 143 (128+15)</strong></td>
                            <td><code>SIGTERM</code> — a graceful-shutdown request, typically from a failed liveness probe or a normal pod termination/rolling update. The app has until <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> (default 30s) to exit cleanly before a follow-up SIGKILL.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong></td>
                            <td>Not an exit code — a kubelet <em>state</em>. The container keeps exiting (any code) and the kubelet is backing off restart attempts exponentially (10s, 20s, 40s... capped at 300s/5 minutes) to avoid hammering a broken app. The backoff timer resets to 10s once a container has stayed up and running for 10 minutes straight — so a pod that crashes once a day is never actually "backed off," only one that crashes repeatedly in a short window.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong></td>
                            <td>Kubelet failed to pull the image and is now in the same exponential backoff pattern before retrying the pull.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>ErrImagePull</strong></td>
                            <td>The specific, immediate failure of a single pull attempt (auth rejected / tag not found / network unreachable) — this is what precedes and explains an <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>CreateContainerConfigError</strong></td>
                            <td>The container spec references a ConfigMap, Secret, or a specific key inside one, and that reference cannot be resolved — the object or key genuinely doesn't exist in that namespace.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>OOMKilled</strong></td>
                            <td>The kernel's cgroup memory controller killed the container because it exceeded <code>resources.limits.memory</code>. Shows up as Exit 137 with <code>Reason: OOMKilled</code> in the Last State.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Evicted</strong></td>
                            <td>The <em>node</em> (not the container's own limit) was under resource pressure — disk, memory, or PIDs — and the kubelet proactively removed lower-priority pods to protect node stability. Different from OOMKilled: this is a node-level decision, not a per-container limit breach.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Pending</strong></td>
                            <td>The pod exists in etcd but has not yet been bound to a node — the scheduler couldn't find a fit (insufficient resources, taint/toleration mismatch, affinity rule, or an unbound PVC).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>NodeNotReady</strong></td>
                            <td>The kubelet on that node has stopped sending healthy heartbeats to the API server within the node-monitor-grace-period (default 50s per the current kube-controller-manager reference) — could mean the kubelet crashed, the node lost network, or it's out of resources.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Forbidden (403)</strong></td>
                            <td>The request reached the API server and was <em>authenticated</em> successfully, but RBAC authorization denied it — the subject (user/SA) has no Role/ClusterRole binding granting that verb on that resource.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Unauthorized (401)</strong></td>
                            <td>The request never even got to the authorization stage — authentication itself failed: an invalid, expired, or missing token/client certificate. Distinct from 403: this is "who are you?" failing, not "you can't do that."</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Terminating (stuck)</strong></td>
                            <td>Not a Pod phase — a display state kubectl shows for any object with a non-null <code>metadata.deletionTimestamp</code>. The object stays in etcd, undeleted, until every string in its <code>metadata.finalizers</code> list has been removed by whatever controller registered it.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📌 401 vs 403 — the distinction the exam loves to test</h5>
                <p>These are commonly confused because both "deny" the request, but they fail at different pipeline stages. <strong>401 Unauthorized</strong> means Kubernetes couldn't verify <em>who</em> you are — bad kubeconfig, expired client certificate, wrong token. <strong>403 Forbidden</strong> means Kubernetes knows exactly who you are and has decided you're not allowed to do <em>that specific thing</em> — this is always an RBAC (Role/ClusterRole + Binding) gap, never a credentials problem. If you get 403, do not touch your kubeconfig or certificates — go straight to <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> and the Role/RoleBinding objects.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Evicted vs OOMKilled — don't conflate node pressure with container limits</h5>
                <p>These are frequently mixed up because both are "killed for using too much memory," but the layer is different. <strong>OOMKilled</strong> is enforced by the Linux kernel's cgroup controller against a single container's own <code>resources.limits.memory</code> — it happens even if the node overall has plenty of free RAM. <strong>Evicted</strong> is a kubelet-level decision made when the <em>node as a whole</em> crosses an eviction threshold (e.g. available memory/disk drops below a configured percentage) — the kubelet then picks pods to remove based on QoS class (BestEffort pods go first, Guaranteed pods go last) regardless of whether any individual pod exceeded its own limit. A pod with no memory limit at all can still be Evicted.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Appendix C Summary ─── -->
        <span class="ch-jump" id="appendix-c-cheatsheet"></span>
        <div class="summary-box">
            <h4>🚨 Appendix C — Quick Debug Cheat Sheet</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>✅ <strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> → <code>logs --previous</code> → fix config/code</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> → check image name, add imagePullSecrets</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Pending Pod</strong> → <code>describe pod</code> → resources, taints, affinity</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Pending PVC</strong> → match PV accessMode + storageClassName + capacity</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>NodeNotReady</strong> → SSH, <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Forbidden</strong> → <code>auth can-i</code> → create Role + RoleBinding</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>connection refused</strong> → verify pod running + Service selector + NetworkPolicy</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>cert expired</strong> → <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>etcd unavailable</strong> → check etcd pod/certs, restore from backup</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>unable to connect</strong> → fix kubeconfig, check API server</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>CreateContainerConfigError</strong> → the ConfigMap/Secret/key it references doesn't exist — create it</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Service: no endpoints</strong> → <code>get endpoints</code> is empty → fix the selector or the readiness probe</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>DNS NXDOMAIN</strong> → check CoreDNS pods/logs first, then use the FQDN not the short name</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>control-plane down (kubectl dead)</strong> → SSH to the node, use <code>crictl</code> + <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>, fix the static-pod manifest</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Ingress not routing</strong> → confirm the controller is installed and <code>ingressClassName</code> matches</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Pod stuck Terminating</strong> → check <code>metadata.finalizers</code>; fix the owning controller/webhook, or strip the finalizer as a last resort</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Exit 137 = SIGKILL</strong> (OOMKilled) · <strong>Exit 143 = SIGTERM</strong> (graceful) · <strong>401</strong> = who-are-you fails · <strong>403</strong> = RBAC denies</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </article>

    
