        <!-- ─── A ─── -->
        <div id="gd-a" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">A</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Admission Controller</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Plugin that intercepts API requests <em>after</em> authentication/authorization but <em>before</em> persistence. Examples: <code>LimitRanger</code>, <code>PodSecurity</code>, <code>MutatingWebhook</code>. Can mutate or validate objects.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Affinity</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Rules controlling pod placement. <code>nodeAffinity</code> targets specific nodes; <code>podAffinity</code>/<code>podAntiAffinity</code> attracts/repels pods relative to other pods. Softer than nodeSelector.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Aggregation Layer</strong></td><td></td><td>Extends the core API server without modifying Kubernetes itself. Additional APIs register as <code>APIService</code> objects and appear to <code>kubectl</code>/clients under the <strong>same front door</strong> (same host/port, same auth) as built-in resources. This is how <code>metrics.k8s.io</code> (Metrics Server) and many operator-provided custom APIs are exposed — the apiserver proxies matching requests to the backing extension service instead of etcd.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Annotation</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Key-value metadata attached to objects. Unlike labels, annotations are <strong>NOT</strong> used for selection. Used for: build info, config hints (<code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target</code>), tool metadata.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>API Group</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Logical grouping of API resources. Core group: <code>""</code> (pods, services). Named groups: <code>apps</code> (deployments), <code>batch</code> (jobs), <code>networking.k8s.io</code> (networkpolicies), <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>API Server</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Central management hub of Kubernetes (<code>kube-apiserver</code>). All <code>kubectl</code> commands and internal components communicate through it. Runs on port <strong>6443</strong>. Handles AuthN → AuthZ → Admission → etcd persistence.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>AppArmor</strong></td><td></td><td>A Linux Security Module (LSM) that confines what an individual container process can do at the kernel level — restricting file paths it may read/write and network access it may use, via a named profile loaded on the node. Applied through <code>securityContext.appArmorProfile</code> (or the legacy <code>container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/&lt;ctr&gt;</code> annotation on older clusters). Complements <code>seccomp</code> (which filters syscalls) rather than replacing it — AppArmor is path/resource-based, seccomp is syscall-based.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── B ─── -->
        <div id="gd-b" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">B</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Base64</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Encoding (NOT encryption) used by Secrets. <code>echo -n "password" | base64</code> to encode; <code>echo "cGFzc3dvcmQ=" | base64 -d</code> to decode. Anyone with RBAC access to the Secret can decode it.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>BGP</strong></td><td></td><td>Border Gateway Protocol — the internet's inter-domain routing protocol, repurposed by some CNI plugins (notably <strong>Calico</strong> in its default "BGP mode") to advertise pod-subnet routes between nodes instead of using an overlay/tunnel. Each node peers with its neighbors (or a route reflector) and announces "I own this pod CIDR block," letting packets route natively without VXLAN encapsulation overhead. Contrast with Flannel's VXLAN overlay approach.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Binding</strong></td><td></td><td>API object that ties a pod to a node. The Scheduler creates a Binding when it assigns a pod. Rarely used directly — understanding helps when debugging <code>Pending</code> pods.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Blue-Green Deployment</strong></td><td></td><td>Deployment strategy: run two identical environments (blue=current, green=new), switch traffic instantly via Service selector change. Not built-in — implemented using label swaps on Services.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>BoltDB</strong></td><td></td><td>The embedded, single-file, on-disk key-value store that <strong>etcd</strong> uses under the hood to persist its committed data (etcd itself adds the Raft consensus, MVCC, and gRPC API layers on top). Paired with etcd's write-ahead log (WAL) — the WAL captures writes durably first, then they're applied to the BoltDB-backed store. Knowing this explains why etcd data directories contain a <code>member/snap</code> and <code>member/wal</code> structure.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── C ─── -->
        <div id="gd-c" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">C</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>cAdvisor</strong></td><td></td><td>Container Advisor — the per-node component, embedded directly inside the <strong>kubelet</strong>, that collects resource usage (CPU, memory, filesystem, network) and performance stats for every running container. It's the raw data source the kubelet's <code>/metrics/cadvisor</code> endpoint exposes and that <strong>Metrics Server</strong> scrapes to power <code>kubectl top</code> and HPA. You never run it separately — it ships inside kubelet.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Calico</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>CNI plugin providing pod networking <strong>and</strong> NetworkPolicy enforcement. Most popular choice for CKA-style clusters. Supports BGP routing and IPIP tunneling.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Capability (Linux)</strong></td><td></td><td>A granular slice of root's privilege (e.g. <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code>, <code>CHOWN</code>, <code>SYS_TIME</code>) that Linux lets you grant or drop independently of running as full root. Kubernetes exposes this via <code>securityContext.capabilities.add/drop</code>. Hardening best practice: <code>drop: ["ALL"]</code> then add back only what the container truly needs — CKS/CKA harden-the-pod tasks lean heavily on this.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CertificateSigningRequest</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>API object to request a certificate signed by the cluster CA. Used for user authentication: create CSR → approve → extract cert. <code>kubectl certificate approve &lt;name&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>cgroup</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Linux kernel feature that limits, accounts for, and isolates resource usage (CPU, memory, I/O) of process groups. Kubernetes uses cgroups to enforce <code>resources.limits</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CIDR</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Classless Inter-Domain Routing — notation for IP address ranges. Example: <code>10.244.0.0/16</code> = pod network, <code>10.96.0.0/12</code> = service network. Used in <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> and NetworkPolicy.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Cilium</strong></td><td></td><td>An eBPF-based CNI plugin that programs the Linux kernel directly instead of relying on iptables/IPVS chains — delivering pod networking, NetworkPolicy enforcement (including L7-aware policies), and can even fully replace kube-proxy. Ships with <strong>Hubble</strong>, a built-in observability layer for visualizing service-to-service traffic flows. Increasingly the default CNI in modern managed Kubernetes offerings because of eBPF's performance advantage over iptables at scale.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Cloud Controller Manager (CCM)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>An <strong>optional</strong> control plane component, separate from <code>kube-controller-manager</code>, that links the cluster to a specific cloud provider's API — running provider-specific controllers for node lifecycle (detecting/removing nodes the cloud deleted), routes, and the <strong>Service type LoadBalancer</strong> controller (the piece that actually calls AWS/GCP/Azure to provision an external load balancer when you create a <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service). Splitting this out of core Kubernetes lets cloud vendors ship and version their own integration independently of the main release cycle. On bare-metal/on-prem clusters (typical CKA lab environments) there is usually no CCM at all, which is exactly why a <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service sits in <code>&lt;pending&gt;</code> unless something like MetalLB fills that role.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Cluster Autoscaler</strong></td><td></td><td>An add-on (not built into core Kubernetes, and distinct from HPA/VPA) that changes the <strong>number of nodes</strong> in a cluster — adding nodes when pods fail to schedule due to insufficient capacity, and removing underutilized nodes when their pods could be safely rescheduled elsewhere. Cloud-provider aware: it talks to the underlying node group/instance-group API (AWS Auto Scaling Group, GCP Managed Instance Group, etc.) to actually provision or terminate VMs. Complements HPA (which scales pod <em>replica count</em> against existing node capacity) and VPA (which right-sizes a pod's requests) — the three are often deployed together, each solving a different axis of the same capacity problem.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ClusterIP</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Default Service type — accessible only <strong>within</strong> the cluster. Gets a virtual IP from the service CIDR. Access via <code>&lt;svc-name&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ClusterRole</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Cluster-wide permission set (no namespace). Used for cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PVs) or cross-namespace access. Bound via <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CNI</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Container Network Interface — plugin standard for pod networking. Provides IP allocation and inter-pod connectivity. Examples: Calico, Flannel, Cilium, Weave Net.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ConfigMap</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Stores non-sensitive configuration as key-value pairs. Injected into pods via: environment variables (<code>envFrom</code>), individual env vars (<code>configMapKeyRef</code>), or volume mounts (files).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Container Runtime</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Software that actually runs containers. Kubernetes supports any CRI-compatible runtime. Standard: <code>containerd</code>. Alternative: <code>CRI-O</code>. Docker was removed in v1.24.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>containerd</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Industry-standard container runtime. Manages the complete container lifecycle: pull images, create containers, manage storage, networking. Default runtime for most K8s distributions.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Context (kubeconfig)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A named tuple of <strong>cluster + user + namespace</strong> stored in <code>~/.kube/config</code> that <code>kubectl</code> resolves before every command. Switch active context with <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code>; inspect with <code>kubectl config get-contexts</code> / <code>current-context</code>. On the CKA exam, always verify your context (and thus which cluster and namespace you're operating against) before running destructive commands — a wrong context is a top time-waster.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Control Plane</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The "brain" of the cluster running on master/control-plane nodes. Components: API Server, etcd, Scheduler, Controller Manager. Manages cluster state and makes scheduling decisions.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Controller</strong></td><td></td><td>The generic reconciliation-loop pattern underlying almost every Kubernetes object: a controller continuously watches the API server, compares <strong>actual state</strong> to the object's <strong>desired (spec) state</strong>, and takes action to converge them. Deployment, ReplicaSet, DaemonSet, StatefulSet, Job, and even Node lifecycle management are all controllers running inside <code>kube-controller-manager</code> — "Kubernetes is just controllers watching etcd" is a useful mental model for the whole system.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Cordon</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Mark a node as <strong>unschedulable</strong> — no new pods will be placed, but existing pods remain. <code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code>. First step before maintenance. Reverse: <code>uncordon</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CoreDNS</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>DNS server built into Kubernetes (runs as a Deployment in <code>kube-system</code>). Resolves service names: <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>. Debug: <code>kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox -- nslookup &lt;svc&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CRD (CustomResourceDefinition)</strong></td><td></td><td>Extend the Kubernetes API with your own resource types. Combined with a custom controller creates an <strong>Operator</strong>. Example: <code>kind: Certificate</code> (cert-manager).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CRI</strong></td><td></td><td>Container Runtime Interface — protocol between kubelet and container runtime. Replaced Docker-specific Dockershim in K8s v1.24.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CronJob</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Runs Jobs on a schedule (cron syntax). Example: <code>schedule: "0 2 * * *"</code> = daily at 2 AM. Creates Job objects which create Pods. Good for backups, report generation.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CSI</strong></td><td></td><td>Container Storage Interface — standard for exposing storage systems to Kubernetes. Drivers: <code>ebs.csi.aws.com</code>, <code>pd.csi.storage.gke.io</code>, <code>disk.csi.azure.com</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── D ─── -->
        <div id="gd-d" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">D</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>DaemonSet</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Ensures exactly <strong>one pod per node</strong> (or per matching node). Used for: log collectors (Fluentd), monitoring agents (node-exporter), CNI plugins, kube-proxy. Pods survive <code>kubectl drain --ignore-daemonsets</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Defragmentation</strong></td><td></td><td>The process of reclaiming wasted disk space inside etcd's data file after normal compaction. etcd compaction removes old key revisions but leaves gaps in the underlying BoltDB file — <code>etcdctl defrag</code> physically rewrites the file to shrink it back down. Run it during maintenance windows (it briefly blocks the member) and on one member at a time in a multi-node etcd cluster to avoid a quorum-wide stall.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Deployment</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Manages ReplicaSets for <strong>declarative updates</strong>. Supports rolling updates, rollbacks, and scaling. The most common workload controller: <code>kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Descheduler</strong></td><td></td><td>An optional add-on controller (not built into core Kubernetes) that periodically evicts pods to re-balance the cluster against current policy — for example moving pods off an over-utilized node, or off a node whose taints/affinity rules changed after the pod was already scheduled. The default scheduler only places <em>new</em> pods; the Descheduler is what corrects placement drift for pods already running.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Drain</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Evict <strong>all pods</strong> from a node for maintenance. Cordons the node first, then evicts pods. Flags: <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> (required), <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code>. Managed pods are rescheduled elsewhere.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Dynamic Provisioning</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The mechanism by which a <strong>StorageClass</strong> automatically creates a matching PersistentVolume the moment a PersistentVolumeClaim references it — no admin has to hand-craft a PV in advance. Contrast with <strong>static provisioning</strong>, where an admin pre-creates PVs for PVCs to bind to. Most cloud/CSI-backed clusters default to dynamic provisioning via a default StorageClass.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── E ─── -->
        <div id="gd-e" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">E</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>eBPF</strong></td><td></td><td>Extended Berkeley Packet Filter — a Linux kernel technology that lets sandboxed programs run safely inside the kernel itself (attached to network, syscall, or tracing hook points) without writing a kernel module. It's the foundation of modern high-performance CNIs and observability tooling — <strong>Cilium</strong> uses it for networking/NetworkPolicy, and tools like Hubble/Falco use it for deep visibility with far less overhead than iptables or userspace agents.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>emptyDir</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Temporary volume created when a pod starts, <strong>deleted when pod is removed</strong>. Shared between containers in the same pod (sidecar pattern). <code>volumes: [{name: shared, emptyDir: {}}]</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Endpoint / EndpointSlice</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The actual pod IP:port combinations backing a Service. When a Service selector matches pods, Kubernetes auto-creates these. Debug: <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> (or <code>kubectl get endpointslices</code>) — if empty, selector/labels mismatch. <strong>EndpointSlices</strong> are the modern, scalable replacement for the original monolithic <code>Endpoints</code> object: instead of one giant object listing every backing pod (which gets huge and slow to update for Services with thousands of pods), EndpointSlices shard the same information across multiple smaller objects (default max 100 addresses each), dramatically reducing update/broadcast cost at scale. kube-proxy and most controllers now read EndpointSlices by default.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Ephemeral Container</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A special, temporary container type injected into an <strong>already-running</strong> pod for interactive troubleshooting — added via <code>kubectl debug easypj-web-abc123 -it --image=busybox --target=easypj-web</code> rather than by editing <code>pod.spec</code> directly (there's no way to add one with <code>kubectl edit</code>; it goes through a dedicated <code>ephemeralcontainers</code> API subresource). The go-to fix when <code>kubectl exec</code> can't help — a crashed container has no running process to exec into, or a hardened <strong>distroless</strong> image has no shell/debugging tools at all. Ephemeral containers get no resource guarantees, are never automatically restarted, cannot define <code>ports</code>/probes, and — like any other container in a pod — can never be removed once added; the whole pod must be deleted/recreated to get rid of one. Pair with <code>shareProcessNamespace: true</code> to see and signal processes in the pod's other containers from inside the ephemeral one.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Distributed key-value store holding <strong>all cluster state</strong>. Runs on control plane (port 2379). Backup: <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code>. Restore: <code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code>. Always requires TLS flags.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Eviction</strong></td><td></td><td>The removal of a pod from a node, triggered either <strong>voluntarily</strong> (a <code>kubectl drain</code> or PodDisruptionBudget-respecting eviction API call) or <strong>involuntarily</strong> by the kubelet under node resource pressure (memory/disk) — the latter is called a <strong>node-pressure eviction</strong> and follows a QoS-based order: BestEffort pods are evicted first, then Burstable, then Guaranteed last. Evicted pods show status <code>Evicted</code> in <code>kubectl get pods</code> and must be manually deleted/recreated.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── F ─── -->
        <div id="gd-f" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">F</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Finalizer</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Metadata key that prevents object deletion until cleanup is done. When you delete an object with finalizers, it enters <code>Terminating</code> state. The controller removes the finalizer after cleanup, then K8s completes deletion.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Flannel</strong></td><td></td><td>Simple CNI plugin using overlay networking (VXLAN). Easy to set up but does <strong>NOT</strong> support NetworkPolicies. Use Calico or Cilium instead for production.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>FQDN</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Fully Qualified Domain Name for Services: <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>. Same-namespace pods can use just <code>&lt;svc&gt;</code>. Cross-namespace requires at least <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>fsGroup</strong></td><td></td><td>A supplemental group ID (GID) set in a pod's <code>securityContext.fsGroup</code> that Kubernetes applies to all mounted volumes' ownership — the kubelet <code>chown</code>s/<code>chgrp</code>s the volume's files to this group (and sets the setgid bit on directories) before the container starts, so a non-root container process can still read/write files on a volume owned by a different UID. Essential for running hardened <code>runAsNonRoot</code> containers against PersistentVolumeClaims that would otherwise be root-owned.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── G ─── -->
        <div id="gd-g" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">G</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Garbage Collection</strong></td><td></td><td>Automatic cleanup of orphaned objects. When a Deployment is deleted, its ReplicaSets and Pods are garbage collected via <strong>ownerReferences</strong>. Cascade modes: <code>Foreground</code>, <code>Background</code>, <code>Orphan</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Gateway API</strong></td><td></td><td>Next-gen routing API (successor to Ingress). Resources: <code>GatewayClass</code>, <code>Gateway</code>, <code>HTTPRoute</code>. More expressive than Ingress — supports header-based routing, traffic splitting, and cross-namespace references.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>GitOps</strong></td><td></td><td>An operating model where a Git repository is the <strong>single source of truth</strong> for desired cluster state, and a controller (ArgoCD, Flux) continuously reconciles the live cluster to match what's committed — instead of engineers running <code>kubectl apply</code> by hand. Changes flow through pull requests (reviewed, versioned, revertible), and the controller detects/corrects any manual drift automatically. A common CKA-adjacent concept even though GitOps tooling itself isn't on the exam.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>GracePeriod</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Time Kubernetes waits for a pod to shut down gracefully (default: <strong>30 seconds</strong>). Override: <code>kubectl delete pod &lt;name&gt; --grace-period=0 --force</code>. During this time, SIGTERM is sent, then SIGKILL after timeout.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Guaranteed (QoS)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The highest of the three <strong>QoS Classes</strong> — assigned automatically when every container in the pod sets <code>requests == limits</code> for both CPU and memory. Guaranteed pods are the <strong>last</strong> to be evicted under node resource pressure, making this the right choice for critical stateful workloads. See also: <strong>QoS Class</strong>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── H ─── -->
        <div id="gd-h" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">H</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>HA</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>High Availability — a control-plane topology with <strong>three or more</strong> control-plane nodes (to survive a member failure while keeping etcd quorum), a load balancer (or stacked VIP) in front of the API servers, and an etcd cluster spanning all control-plane nodes (stacked) or a dedicated external etcd cluster. CKA exam scenarios sometimes reference multi-control-plane clusters — knowing that etcd quorum math (N/2+1) drives the "why 3, not 2" design choice matters.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Headless Service</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A Service created with <code>clusterIP: None</code> — it gets <strong>no</strong> virtual IP or load-balancing. Instead, DNS returns the individual pod IPs directly (one A record per ready pod), giving each pod its own resolvable address. This is what makes <strong>StatefulSets</strong> work: each replica (e.g. <code>db-0.db-svc.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>) gets a stable, individually addressable DNS name instead of being hidden behind a shared VIP.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Helm</strong></td><td></td><td>The de-facto Kubernetes package manager. A <strong>Chart</strong> bundles templated manifests plus a <code>values.yaml</code> of overridable defaults (image tag, replica count, resource limits, …) into one versioned, shareable unit — install one with <code>helm install easypj-web ./easypj-chart --set image.tag=v2</code>. Each installed Chart becomes a tracked <strong>Release</strong>, which is what makes <code>helm upgrade</code> (apply a new Chart version or values) and <code>helm rollback easypj-web 1</code> (revert to a prior release revision) possible — Helm keeps release history the way <code>kubectl rollout history</code> keeps Deployment revisions, but at the whole-application-bundle level instead of per-object. Not invoked directly on the CKA exam, but real clusters (including exam-adjacent troubleshooting scenarios) are frequently already running Helm-installed workloads, so recognizing <code>app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm</code> labels and Helm-owned Secrets (which store release state) matters in practice.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>hostPath</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Volume that mounts a file/directory from the <strong>host node's filesystem</strong> into a pod. Used for: dev/test, static pods reading node configs. <strong>Not safe for production</strong> — data only on one node.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Automatically scales pod replicas based on CPU/memory metrics (requires metrics-server). <code>kubectl autoscale deploy easypj-web --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── I ─── -->
        <div id="gd-i" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">I</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>ImagePullPolicy</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Container spec field (<code>spec.containers[].imagePullPolicy</code>) controlling when the kubelet re-pulls an image: <code>Always</code> (re-check the registry every time the pod starts, even if a matching image already exists locally — the default whenever the tag is <code>:latest</code> or omitted), <code>IfNotPresent</code> (use the local copy if present, only pull if missing — the default for any other explicit tag), and <code>Never</code> (only use a local image, fail if absent — common for locally-built/pre-loaded images in air-gapped or kind/minikube setups). A frequent CKA gotcha: tagging an image <code>easypj:latest</code> silently forces <code>Always</code> pulls even if you didn't set the field, which can surprise you when a node has no registry access.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Informer</strong></td><td></td><td>A client-side caching/watch primitive from <code>client-go</code> that underlies almost every controller: it lists the current state of a resource type once, then keeps a local in-memory cache up to date via a long-lived <strong>watch</strong> stream, and fires event callbacks (Add/Update/Delete) to the controller's reconciliation logic. This is why controllers react to changes near-instantly without polling the API server — understanding Informers explains the "controllers watch, they don't poll" behavior referenced throughout the exam's troubleshooting scenarios.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Ingress</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>API object defining HTTP/HTTPS routing rules. Routes external traffic to Services based on host/path. Requires an <strong>Ingress Controller</strong> (nginx, traefik). Supports TLS termination.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Ingress Controller</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Component that <em>implements</em> Ingress rules. It's a pod running a reverse proxy (nginx, HAProxy). Without it, Ingress resources have no effect. Install: <code>kubectl apply -f nginx-ingress.yaml</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Init Container</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Runs <strong>before</strong> main containers. Must complete successfully before app containers start. Used for: waiting for dependencies, database migrations, config generation. Defined in <code>spec.initContainers[]</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>IPVS</strong></td><td></td><td>IP Virtual Server — a high-performance <code>kube-proxy</code> mode that programs Service routing using in-kernel hash tables instead of the sequential rule chains iptables mode relies on. Lookup time stays roughly constant no matter how many Services exist, whereas iptables mode's linear rule-chain scan slows down as Service count grows — making IPVS the better choice for very large clusters. Enable with <code>--proxy-mode=ipvs</code> on kube-proxy (requires the <code>ip_vs</code> kernel modules loaded).</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── J ─── -->
        <div id="gd-j" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">J</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Job</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Run-to-completion workload. Pod runs once (or N times), then stops. <code>restartPolicy: Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code>. Used for: backups, data processing, one-time migrations.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>JSONPath</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Query language for extracting data from JSON output. <code>kubectl get pods -o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.name}'</code>. Powerful for scripting and CKA exam output formatting.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── K ─── -->
        <div id="gd-k" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">K</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>kubeadm</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Official tool to bootstrap Kubernetes clusters. <code>kubeadm init</code> (control plane), <code>kubeadm join</code> (workers), <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (version upgrades), <code>kubeadm certs</code> (certificate management). Core CKA tool.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kubeconfig</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The YAML file (default <code>~/.kube/config</code>) that stores <strong>clusters</strong> (API server address + CA cert), <strong>users</strong> (client cert/token/exec-plugin credentials), and <strong>contexts</strong> (cluster+user+namespace tuples) that <code>kubectl</code> reads on every invocation. Merge multiple files with <code>KUBECONFIG=file1:file2 kubectl config view --flatten</code>; point at a specific one with <code>--kubeconfig</code> or the <code>KUBECONFIG</code> env var — a frequent CKA exam requirement when switching between clusters.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kubectl</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>CLI for communicating with the Kubernetes API server. Every CKA task uses it. Config: <code>~/.kube/config</code>. Structure: <code>kubectl [verb] [resource] [name] [flags]</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Agent running on <strong>every node</strong>. Receives pod specs from API server, ensures containers are running and healthy. Manages probes, reports node status. Not a pod — it's a systemd service: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The control plane binary that runs almost all of Kubernetes's built-in <strong>controllers</strong> as a single process — Node controller, Deployment/ReplicaSet/DaemonSet/StatefulSet/Job controllers, ServiceAccount controller, and more — each one watching the API server and reconciling actual state toward desired state. Distinct from the optional <strong>Cloud Controller Manager</strong>, which runs the cloud-provider-specific controllers separately so this binary can stay cloud-agnostic. Runs as a static pod (<code>kube-controller-manager-&lt;node&gt;</code>) on every control-plane node in a kubeadm cluster; check its health with <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-controller-manager</code> or <code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kube-proxy</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Network proxy on each node maintaining network rules (iptables/IPVS). Implements Service abstraction — routes traffic to pod endpoints. Runs as a DaemonSet in <code>kube-system</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Control plane component that assigns pods to nodes. Considers: resource requests, nodeSelector, affinity, taints/tolerations, PV topology. If it can't find a node → pod stays <code>Pending</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Kustomize</strong></td><td></td><td>Template-free, Kubernetes-native config management built directly into <code>kubectl</code> (<code>kubectl apply -k ./overlays/prod</code>) — no separate binary or templating language required. The core pattern is <strong>base + overlays</strong>: a <code>base/</code> directory holds the plain, environment-agnostic manifests (e.g. the easypj Deployment and Service) referenced by a <code>kustomization.yaml</code>; each environment gets its own overlay directory (<code>overlays/dev</code>, <code>overlays/prod</code>) with a small <code>kustomization.yaml</code> that <strong>patches</strong> just what differs — bumping <code>replicas</code> to 5 and the image tag to <code>easypj:v2</code> for prod, for instance — without ever editing or duplicating the base YAML. This composability (patch, not copy) is Kustomize's main advantage over maintaining separate full manifest sets per environment, and unlike Helm it needs no templating syntax (<code>{{ }}</code>) mixed into your YAML — every file stays valid, plain Kubernetes YAML on its own.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── L ─── -->
        <div id="gd-l" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">L</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Label</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Key-value pair attached to objects for <strong>identification and selection</strong>. Used by selectors, services, deployments: <code>app: easypj</code>. Filter: <code>kubectl get pods -l app=easypj</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Lease</strong></td><td></td><td>Lightweight object for leader election and node heartbeats. kubelet creates a Lease in <code>kube-node-lease</code> namespace — if it stops updating, node is marked <code>NotReady</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>LimitRange</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Sets default/min/max resource constraints <strong>per pod/container</strong> in a namespace. If a pod doesn't specify resources, LimitRange can inject defaults. Prevents runaway resource consumption.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Liveness Probe</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Health check: "is the container alive?" If it fails, kubelet <strong>restarts</strong> the container. Types: <code>httpGet</code>, <code>tcpSocket</code>, <code>exec</code>. Configure <code>initialDelaySeconds</code> to avoid false kills on startup.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Service type that provisions an <strong>external</strong> cloud load balancer (ELB/NLB on AWS, Azure LB, GCP LB) pointing at the Service's nodes/pods. Requires a cloud-controller-manager integration to actually provision the LB — on bare-metal/on-prem clusters (common in CKA exam environments) a <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service stays stuck in <code>&lt;pending&gt;</code> for its EXTERNAL-IP unless something like MetalLB is installed to fulfill the request. Automatically creates a NodePort and ClusterIP underneath it too.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── M ─── -->
        <div id="gd-m" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">M</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Manifest</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>YAML or JSON file declaring the desired state of Kubernetes objects. Applied with <code>kubectl apply -f manifest.yaml</code>. The declarative approach — Kubernetes reconciles actual state to match.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Metrics Server</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Cluster add-on that collects CPU/memory usage from kubelets. Required for <code>kubectl top nodes</code>, <code>kubectl top pods</code>, and HPA autoscaling. Install: <code>kubectl apply -f components.yaml</code> from GitHub.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Mirror Pod</strong></td><td></td><td>The read-only, API-visible representation of a <strong>Static Pod</strong>. When kubelet starts a static pod from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>, it also creates a corresponding "mirror" object in the API server so you can <code>kubectl get pods</code> and see it — but you <strong>cannot edit or delete it through the API</strong> (changes must be made to the manifest file on disk, which kubelet then re-mirrors). This is why control-plane component pods (<code>kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt;</code>, etc.) appear in <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> yet resist normal <code>kubectl delete</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Multi-container Pod</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Pod with multiple containers sharing network and volumes. Patterns: <strong>Sidecar</strong> (helper alongside main), <strong>Ambassador</strong> (proxy), <strong>Adapter</strong> (transform output). Init containers run before app containers.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Mutating Webhook</strong></td><td></td><td>An admission webhook (<code>MutatingWebhookConfiguration</code>) invoked <strong>before</strong> validating webhooks, during the mutating phase of admission control, that can <strong>modify</strong> an incoming object before it's persisted — e.g. injecting a sidecar container, setting default resource limits, or adding a label. Runs as an external HTTP service the apiserver calls out to. Contrast with a Validating Webhook, which can only accept/reject, never change the object.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>MVCC</strong></td><td></td><td>Multi-Version Concurrency Control — the technique etcd uses internally to store every key as a series of historical <strong>revisions</strong> rather than overwriting in place. Every write bumps etcd's global revision counter, which is what enables features like <code>etcdctl watch</code> (stream changes since a revision) and consistent snapshot reads. It's also why etcd needs periodic <strong>compaction</strong> (to prune old revisions) and <strong>defragmentation</strong> (to reclaim the resulting disk gaps).</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── N ─── -->
        <div id="gd-n" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">N</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Namespace</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Virtual cluster within a cluster. Provides resource isolation, RBAC boundaries, and resource quotas. Default namespaces: <code>default</code>, <code>kube-system</code>, <code>kube-public</code>, <code>kube-node-lease</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>NetworkPolicy</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Firewall rules for pod traffic. Controls ingress (incoming) and egress (outgoing) at L3/L4. <strong>Requires supporting CNI</strong> (Calico, Cilium). No policy = all traffic allowed. Empty podSelector = all pods.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Node</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A worker machine (VM or physical) in the cluster. Runs kubelet, kube-proxy, and container runtime. Control plane nodes also run API server, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>NodePort</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Service type that opens a port (30000-32767) on <strong>every node's IP</strong>. External access via <code>&lt;nodeIP&gt;:&lt;nodePort&gt;</code>. Automatically creates a ClusterIP too. Good for dev/on-prem.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>nodeSelector / Node Affinity</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The two mechanisms for constraining <em>which nodes</em> a pod may be scheduled onto. <code>nodeSelector</code> is the simple, original form — a flat map of labels that must <strong>all</strong> match a node exactly (hard requirement only). <strong>Node Affinity</strong> (<code>spec.affinity.nodeAffinity</code>) is the more expressive successor: supports <code>In</code>/<code>NotIn</code>/<code>Exists</code> operators, and both <code>requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution</code> (hard, like nodeSelector) and <code>preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution</code> (soft, weighted preference) forms.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── O ─── -->
        <div id="gd-o" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">O</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>OCI</strong></td><td></td><td>Open Container Initiative — the vendor-neutral standards body (under the Linux Foundation) whose specs are why an image built with <code>docker build</code> or <code>buildah</code> runs unmodified under <code>containerd</code> or <code>CRI-O</code> on any Kubernetes cluster. Three specs matter here: the <strong>Image Spec</strong> (the on-disk/registry layer format every K8s container image follows — this is what <code>docker push</code>/<code>crictl pull</code> actually push and pull), the <strong>Runtime Spec</strong> (how a compliant runtime like <code>runc</code> must unpack and execute a container's filesystem bundle), and the <strong>Distribution Spec</strong> (the registry HTTP API — the reason any OCI-compliant registry, Docker Hub, ECR, GCR, Harbor, all interoperate). "OCI-compliant" is what lets Kubernetes be runtime-agnostic via the CRI plug-point instead of being hard-wired to one container engine.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>OOMKilled</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The container status shown when the kernel's OOM (Out-Of-Memory) killer terminates a container for exceeding its <code>resources.limits.memory</code> — visible via <code>kubectl describe pod</code> as <code>Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled</code>, exit code 137. Unlike a Liveness Probe failure (which is a graceful kubelet-initiated restart), OOMKill is an abrupt kernel-level SIGKILL — the process gets no chance to clean up. Fix by raising the memory limit or reducing actual usage; CPU limits, by contrast, only throttle and never OOMKill.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>OPA Gatekeeper</strong></td><td></td><td>A policy engine implemented as a Kubernetes admission webhook, built on <strong>Open Policy Agent (OPA)</strong> and its Rego policy language. Lets cluster admins define custom admission rules (e.g. "all pods must have resource limits," "images must come from an approved registry") as <code>ConstraintTemplate</code>/<code>Constraint</code> CRDs instead of writing a bespoke webhook server. A common alternative/predecessor to <strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong>, which does similar in-cluster policy enforcement using CEL instead of Rego and without needing an external webhook.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Operator</strong></td><td></td><td>Pattern: custom controller + CRD that automates complex application lifecycle management. Examples: Prometheus Operator, cert-manager, PostgreSQL Operator. Extends Kubernetes capabilities.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ownerReferences</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A metadata field on an object (<code>metadata.ownerReferences</code>) that links it to its "parent" — e.g. a Pod's ownerReference points to the ReplicaSet that created it, which in turn points to its owning Deployment. This is what powers <strong>cascading deletion</strong>: <code>kubectl delete deployment</code> follows the ownership chain down and garbage-collects the ReplicaSets and Pods automatically. Deletion propagation can be controlled with <code>--cascade=orphan</code> (keep children, remove only the parent) or <code>--cascade=foreground</code> (block until all children are gone).</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── P ─── -->
        <div id="gd-p" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">P</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>PersistentVolume (PV)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Cluster-level storage resource provisioned by admin (or dynamically by StorageClass). <strong>Cluster-scoped</strong> — no namespace. Bound to PVCs. Reclaim policies: <code>Retain</code>, <code>Delete</code>, <code>Recycle</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>User's <strong>request</strong> for storage. Namespace-scoped. Binds to a PV matching accessMode, capacity, and storageClass. Pod references PVC in <code>volumes[].persistentVolumeClaim.claimName</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Pod</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Smallest deployable unit — one or more containers sharing network namespace (same IP) and volumes. Usually one container per pod. Created by Deployments, not directly. <code>kubectl run nginx --image=nginx</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PodDisruptionBudget (PDB)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Guarantees minimum availability during <strong>voluntary disruptions</strong> (node drain, upgrades). <code>minAvailable: 2</code> or <code>maxUnavailable: 1</code>. Can block <code>kubectl drain</code> if violated.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PodSecurityAdmission</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Built-in admission controller enforcing Pod Security Standards at namespace level. Modes: <code>enforce</code>, <code>audit</code>, <code>warn</code>. Label: <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PodSecurityPolicy (PSP)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td><strong>Removed</strong> cluster-scoped resource that used to govern pod-level security (privilege escalation, host namespaces, allowed volume types, etc.) via an admission-controller-enforced policy object — <strong>deprecated in v1.21</strong> and <strong>fully removed in v1.25</strong>. Superseded by the simpler <strong>Pod Security Admission</strong> (built-in, namespace-label-driven, three fixed Privileged/Baseline/Restricted profiles) or third-party policy engines like OPA Gatekeeper/Kyverno for anything PSP-style flexibility that PSA doesn't cover. Knowing PSP is gone matters on the exam: any question or old reference material mentioning it is either testing legacy knowledge or describing a cluster running an EOL Kubernetes version — modern clusters use Pod Security Admission instead.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PodSecurityStandard</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Three profiles: <strong>Privileged</strong> (unrestricted), <strong>Baseline</strong> (sensible defaults), <strong>Restricted</strong> (hardened). Applied via PodSecurityAdmission labels on namespaces.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PriorityClass</strong></td><td></td><td>A <strong>cluster-scoped</strong> object defining a named integer priority value that pods reference via <code>spec.priorityClassName</code> to influence two things: <strong>scheduling order</strong> (higher-priority pending pods are scheduled first) and <strong>preemption</strong> (the scheduler may evict lower-priority pods to make room for a higher-priority one that can't otherwise fit). Kubernetes ships built-in classes like <code>system-cluster-critical</code> for its own components; user workloads get custom classes to protect critical services from being starved out under resource pressure.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Probe</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Health check mechanism. Three types: <strong>Liveness</strong> (restart if fail), <strong>Readiness</strong> (remove from service if fail), <strong>Startup</strong> (disable other probes until success). Methods: httpGet, tcpSocket, exec.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── Q ─── -->
        <div id="gd-q" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">Q</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>QoS Class</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Every pod is automatically assigned one of three Quality-of-Service classes based on how its containers set <code>resources.requests</code>/<code>limits</code>, and this class directly determines <strong>eviction order</strong> under node memory pressure: <strong>Guaranteed</strong> (requests == limits for every resource on every container — evicted last), <strong>Burstable</strong> (at least one request set, but not equal to limits — evicted second), and <strong>BestEffort</strong> (no requests/limits set at all — evicted first). Check a running pod's class with <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}'</code>. This is a frequent CKA troubleshooting angle: understanding why a specific pod was OOMKilled/evicted before its neighbors often comes down to its QoS class.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Quorum</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The minimum number of etcd cluster members — <strong>N/2 + 1</strong> — that must be reachable and agree before a write can be committed via Raft consensus. For a 3-member etcd cluster, quorum is 2 (it tolerates 1 member down); for 5 members, quorum is 3 (tolerates 2 down). This is the math behind why HA control planes are sized to <strong>odd</strong> numbers (3, 5, 7) — an even count adds a member without improving fault tolerance, since quorum still rounds up the same way. If quorum is lost, the etcd cluster (and therefore the whole API server) stops accepting writes until enough members return.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── R ─── -->
        <div id="gd-r" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">R</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Raft</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The distributed consensus protocol etcd uses to keep its cluster members in agreement. One member is elected <strong>leader</strong> (via a randomized-timeout election); the rest are <strong>followers</strong>. All writes go through the leader, which replicates them to followers and only commits once a <strong>quorum</strong> (N/2+1) has acknowledged. If the leader fails, a new election happens automatically. Understanding Raft explains etcd behaviors like brief write unavailability during leader elections and why odd cluster sizes matter.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>RBAC</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Role-Based Access Control — who can do what. Four objects: <code>Role</code> (ns-scoped permissions), <code>ClusterRole</code> (cluster-wide), <code>RoleBinding</code> (links subject→Role), <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Readiness Probe</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Health check: "is the pod ready for traffic?" If it fails, pod is <strong>removed from Service endpoints</strong> (no restart). Critical for zero-downtime deployments during rolling updates.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Reclaim Policy</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The field on a PersistentVolume (<code>spec.persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy</code>) that governs what happens to the underlying storage once its bound PVC is deleted. <strong>Retain</strong> — keep the PV and its data around (status becomes <code>Released</code>, requires manual admin cleanup before reuse); <strong>Delete</strong> — automatically delete both the PV object and its backing storage (the default for most dynamically-provisioned StorageClasses); <strong>Recycle</strong> — deprecated, performed a basic <code>rm -rf</code> scrub before making the PV available again (removed in favor of dynamic provisioning).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ReplicaSet</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Ensures a specified number of pod replicas are running. Managed by Deployments — rarely created directly. <code>kubectl get rs</code> shows ReplicaSets behind a Deployment.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ResourceQuota</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Limits <strong>total</strong> resource consumption per namespace (cpu, memory, pods count, PVCs). Prevents one team from consuming all cluster resources. Applied at namespace level.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Role</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A <strong>namespace-scoped</strong> permission set — a list of allowed API-group/resource/verb rules that only apply within the namespace the Role was created in. Bound to a subject (user, group, or ServiceAccount) via a <strong>RoleBinding</strong> in that same namespace. Contrast with <strong>ClusterRole</strong>, which is cluster-scoped and can grant access across namespaces or to cluster-scoped resources like Nodes and PVs.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>RoleBinding</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Binds a Role (or ClusterRole) to subjects (users, groups, ServiceAccounts) within a <strong>single namespace</strong>. <code>kubectl create rolebinding name --role=role --serviceaccount=ns:sa -n ns</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Rolling Update</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Default Deployment strategy: gradually replace old pods with new ones. Controlled by <code>maxSurge</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code>. Zero-downtime when combined with readiness probes.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>RuntimeClass</strong></td><td></td><td>A <code>node.k8s.io</code> cluster-scoped resource that lets a single cluster offer <strong>multiple container runtime configurations</strong> side by side, selected per-pod via <code>spec.runtimeClassName</code>. Each <code>RuntimeClass</code> maps to a named CRI <code>handler</code> pre-configured on the node (in containerd's <code>config.toml</code> or CRI-O's config) — e.g. a <code>gvisor</code> RuntimeClass for application-level sandboxing of untrusted/multi-tenant workloads, or a <code>kata</code> RuntimeClass that runs the pod inside a lightweight VM for hardware-level isolation, alongside the plain <code>runc</code> default for trusted, performance-sensitive pods. Can also declare <code>overhead</code> (extra CPU/memory the runtime itself consumes, which the scheduler then accounts for) and <code>scheduling</code> constraints (node selector/tolerations) so pods only land on nodes that actually have the handler installed. Pods with no <code>runtimeClassName</code> set fall back to the node's default runtime.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── S ─── -->
        <div id="gd-s" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">S</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>seccomp</strong></td><td></td><td>Secure Computing Mode — a Linux kernel facility that filters which <strong>syscalls</strong> a container process is allowed to make, blocking or killing on anything not on an allow-list. Configured via <code>securityContext.seccompProfile</code>; the recommended CKA/CKS-hardening setting is <code>type: RuntimeDefault</code>, which applies the container runtime's own curated safe-syscall profile. Complements <strong>AppArmor</strong> (path/resource-based) and Linux <strong>Capabilities</strong> (privilege-based) as the third leg of container kernel hardening.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Secret</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Stores sensitive data (base64-encoded, <strong>not encrypted by default</strong>). Types: <code>Opaque</code> (generic), <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> (certs), <code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code> (registry auth). Protect with RBAC + encryption at rest.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>SecurityContext</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Security settings at pod or container level. Controls: <code>runAsUser</code>, <code>runAsNonRoot</code>, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>, <code>capabilities</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code>. CKA tests pod-level and container-level contexts.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Selector</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Label-based mechanism to identify a set of objects. Used by Services (<code>spec.selector</code>), Deployments (<code>spec.selector.matchLabels</code>), and NetworkPolicies (<code>spec.podSelector</code>).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Service</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Stable network endpoint for a set of pods. Types: <code>ClusterIP</code> (internal), <code>NodePort</code> (external:30000+), <code>LoadBalancer</code> (cloud LB), <code>ExternalName</code> (DNS alias). Selector matches pod labels.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ServiceAccount</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Identity for pods to authenticate to the API server. Every namespace has a <code>default</code> SA. Best practice: create dedicated SA with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> unless needed.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Sidecar</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Additional container in a pod running alongside the main container. Pattern: log shippers, proxies (Envoy/Istio), config reloaders. Shares volumes and network with main container.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Startup Probe</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A probe that protects <strong>slow-starting</strong> containers from being killed prematurely by the Liveness Probe. While a Startup Probe is configured and hasn't yet succeeded, all other probes (Liveness, Readiness) are <strong>disabled</strong> — giving a container with a long boot sequence (e.g. loading a large dataset, running migrations) as much time as it needs without tripping a Liveness restart loop. Once it succeeds once, it stops running and normal probing takes over.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>StatefulSet</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Manages stateful applications with <strong>stable identity</strong>: ordered pod names (db-0, db-1), persistent storage per pod, ordered deployment/scaling. Used for: databases, ZooKeeper, Kafka.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Static Pod</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Pod managed directly by <strong>kubelet</strong> (not API server). Defined in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. Used for control plane components: API server, scheduler, controller-manager, etcd. Mirror pod appears in API.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>StorageClass</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Defines types of storage for <strong>dynamic provisioning</strong>. When a PVC references a StorageClass, K8s auto-creates the PV. Key fields: <code>provisioner</code>, <code>volumeBindingMode</code>, <code>reclaimPolicy</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── T ─── -->
        <div id="gd-t" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">T</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Taint</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Mark on a node that <strong>repels pods</strong> unless they have a matching toleration. Effects: <code>NoSchedule</code>, <code>PreferNoSchedule</code>, <code>NoExecute</code>. Control plane has default taint. <code>kubectl taint nodes node key=val:NoSchedule</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>TLS</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Transport Layer Security — mutual TLS (mTLS) secures every control-plane connection: apiserver↔etcd, apiserver↔kubelet, kubelet↔apiserver, and client↔apiserver. Every component authenticates with a client certificate signed by the cluster CA, and certificates are what <code>kubeadm certs</code> manages, renews, and inspects. CKA exam tasks around expired certs, CSR approval, and etcd TLS flags all trace back to this.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>TokenRequest</strong></td><td></td><td>The API (and underlying mechanism) that issues <strong>short-lived, audience-bound, projected ServiceAccount tokens</strong> — the modern replacement for the old long-lived, non-expiring ServiceAccount Secret tokens (deprecated as an insecure default since v1.24). Requested automatically by the kubelet when a pod mounts a <code>projected</code> volume with a <code>serviceAccountToken</code> source, or manually via <code>kubectl create token &lt;sa-name&gt;</code>. Tokens auto-rotate and expire, dramatically shrinking the blast radius of a leaked token.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Toleration</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Pod spec field allowing scheduling on <strong>tainted nodes</strong>. Must match taint key, value, and effect. <code>spec.tolerations: [{key: "key", operator: "Equal", value: "val", effect: "NoSchedule"}]</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Topology Spread Constraint</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A scheduling rule (<code>spec.topologySpreadConstraints</code>) that keeps a set of pods <strong>evenly distributed</strong> across a chosen topology domain — commonly node zone, region, or hostname — using <code>maxSkew</code> to cap how uneven the spread is allowed to get. More flexible than podAntiAffinity for this purpose: designed specifically for balanced spread across failure domains rather than just "not on the same node," improving resilience if an entire zone goes down.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── U ─── -->
        <div id="gd-u" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">U</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Uncordon</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Make a cordoned node <strong>schedulable again</strong>. <code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code>. Final step after maintenance. Existing pods won't move back automatically — new pods may be scheduled there.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Union FS (OverlayFS)</strong></td><td></td><td>The layered filesystem technology (most commonly Linux's <strong>OverlayFS</strong>) that container images and runtimes rely on to stack read-only image layers under a thin, writable "container layer" — giving each container an apparently full, independent filesystem while sharing the underlying layers on disk (huge storage/startup savings when many containers share a base image). This layering is also why an image is described as a set of layers, and why deleting a container discards only its writable top layer, leaving the shared base layers untouched.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Upgrade (kubeadm)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>CKA-critical procedure: <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> → <code>kubeadm upgrade apply vX.Y.Z</code> on control plane, then upgrade kubelet/kubectl on each node. Always upgrade <strong>one minor version at a time</strong> (e.g., 1.29→1.30).</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── V ─── -->
        <div id="gd-v" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">V</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>An <strong>in-cluster</strong> admission policy mechanism (GA since v1.30) that evaluates <strong>CEL (Common Expression Language)</strong> expressions directly inside the API server — no external webhook server to deploy, secure, or keep available. Lets admins express rules like "all Deployments must set resource limits" as a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> + <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding</code> pair. Faster and more reliable than an equivalent Validating Webhook since there's no network hop, and it can't fail-open/closed due to webhook unavailability.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Validating Webhook</strong></td><td></td><td>An admission webhook (<code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code>) invoked during the validating phase of admission control — after all mutating webhooks have run — that can only <strong>accept or reject</strong> a request, never modify it. Used to enforce policy (e.g. OPA Gatekeeper, Kyverno in validate mode) against the final, fully-mutated version of an object before it's persisted to etcd.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Velero</strong></td><td></td><td>A backup/restore tool for Kubernetes clusters — snapshots namespace resources (as versioned archives, typically to S3-compatible object storage) <strong>and</strong> triggers coordinated PersistentVolume snapshots via each cloud/CSI provider's native snapshot API. Used for disaster recovery, cluster migration, and namespace-level restore testing. Not on the CKA exam directly, but a common real-world answer to "how do you back up a cluster beyond just etcd."</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>veth pair</strong></td><td></td><td>A virtual Ethernet device pair — two connected virtual network interfaces that act like a cable with a plug on each end. One end lives inside a pod's network namespace (appearing as <code>eth0</code>); the other end is placed on the node's root network namespace and attached to a bridge (or the CNI's equivalent). This is the fundamental plumbing that connects a pod's isolated network namespace to the node's network — every pod effectively gets one veth pair per network interface.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Volume</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Storage attached to a pod. Types: <code>emptyDir</code> (temp), <code>hostPath</code> (node fs), <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code> (PVC), <code>configMap</code>, <code>secret</code>, <code>projected</code>. Defined in <code>spec.volumes[]</code>, mounted via <code>volumeMounts[]</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>volumeClaimTemplates</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A StatefulSet spec field that auto-generates a <strong>dedicated, uniquely-named PVC per pod replica</strong> (e.g. <code>data-db-0</code>, <code>data-db-1</code>) instead of every replica sharing one PVC. This is what gives each StatefulSet pod its own stable, independent storage that follows it across rescheduling — the storage half of the "stable identity" guarantee (Headless Service DNS is the networking half).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>volumeMode</strong></td><td></td><td>A PV/PVC field controlling whether the volume is presented as a mounted <strong>Filesystem</strong> (the default — a formatted filesystem you read/write files on) or a raw <strong>Block</strong> device (no filesystem, the container gets direct block-device access). Block mode is used by specialized workloads like databases that manage their own on-disk format for performance.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>VolumeMount</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>How a volume is mounted inside a container. Specifies: <code>name</code> (matches volume name), <code>mountPath</code> (path in container), optional <code>readOnly</code> and <code>subPath</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>VPA</strong></td><td></td><td>Vertical Pod Autoscaler — an add-on (not built into core Kubernetes) that automatically right-sizes a pod's <code>resources.requests</code> (and optionally limits) based on observed historical usage, instead of scaling replica <em>count</em> like HPA does. Runs in <code>Off</code> (recommend only), <code>Initial</code> (set on pod creation), or <code>Auto</code> (evicts and recreates pods to apply new sizing) mode. Generally should <strong>not</strong> be combined with HPA on the same resource metric — they can fight each other.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── W ─── -->
        <div id="gd-w" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">W</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>WaitForFirstConsumer</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A StorageClass <code>volumeBindingMode</code> setting that <strong>delays</strong> PV binding (and, for dynamic provisioning, actual volume creation) until a pod that will use the PVC is actually scheduled — instead of binding immediately when the PVC is created. This matters in topology-aware clusters (e.g. multi-zone) because it lets the provisioner create the volume in the <strong>same zone</strong> the scheduler ultimately picks for the pod, avoiding a pod stuck <code>Pending</code> because its bound volume lives in a zone none of its eligible nodes are in.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>WAL</strong></td><td></td><td>Write-Ahead Log — etcd durably appends every incoming write to this sequential log <strong>before</strong> acknowledging it or applying it to the underlying BoltDB store, guaranteeing no committed write is lost even if etcd crashes immediately after. On restart, etcd replays the WAL to recover any state not yet flushed to the main store. This is the standard "log first, apply second" durability pattern used by most databases.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Webhook</strong></td><td></td><td>HTTP callback for admission control. <code>MutatingAdmissionWebhook</code> can modify objects; <code>ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</code> can accept/reject. Used by tools like cert-manager, OPA/Gatekeeper.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Worker Node</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Node that runs application workloads (pods). Contains kubelet, kube-proxy, and container runtime. Joined to cluster via <code>kubeadm join</code>. Managed from control plane.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── Commonly Confused Terms ─── -->
        <div class="cka-gotcha">
            <h5>⚠️ Frequently Confused Term Pairs</h5>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Label vs Annotation</strong> — Labels are for <em>selection</em> (selectors use them); Annotations are <em>metadata only</em></li>
                <li><strong>Cordon vs Drain</strong> — Cordon stops <em>new</em> pods; Drain evicts <em>existing</em> pods too</li>
                <li><strong>Role vs ClusterRole</strong> — Role is namespace-scoped; ClusterRole is cluster-wide</li>
                <li><strong>PV vs PVC</strong> — PV is the <em>actual storage</em> (admin); PVC is the <em>request</em> (developer)</li>
                <li><strong>Liveness vs Readiness</strong> — Liveness <em>restarts</em> container; Readiness <em>removes from service</em></li>
                <li><strong>DaemonSet vs Deployment</strong> — DaemonSet: one pod per node; Deployment: specified replica count</li>
                <li><strong>ConfigMap vs Secret</strong> — ConfigMap: plain text config; Secret: base64 sensitive data</li>
                <li><strong>Taint vs Toleration</strong> — Taint is on <em>nodes</em> (repels); Toleration is on <em>pods</em> (allows)</li>
                <li><strong>NodePort vs LoadBalancer</strong> — NodePort: nodeIP:port; LoadBalancer: cloud-provisioned external IP</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

