        <div class="info-box note">
            <h5>🏢 Modern Alternative: Gateway API</h5>
            <p>The diagram above shows the classic <strong>Ingress</strong> path (E.7) — still the most common way to expose HTTP(S) traffic and still the object the CKA exam tests. Section <strong>E.7b</strong> below shows the newer <strong>Gateway API</strong> (<code>Gateway</code> + <code>HTTPRoute</code>) doing the exact same job for the exact same <code>easypj-web</code> Service, with a role split between platform and app teams and far more expressive routing. Either can sit in the "Ingress" box in this diagram — you don't run both for the same traffic.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="diagram-container">
            <div class="diagram-title">🔑 Apply Order — Not Just a Checklist, a Dependency Chain</div>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Object</th><th>Why THIS order (not another)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td>Namespace (E.1)</td><td>Every other object's <code>metadata.namespace: easypj</code> references a namespace that must already exist — applying anything else first fails with <code>namespaces "easypj" not found</code>.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2</td><td>ConfigMap + Secret (E.2, E.3)</td><td>Pods read these via <code>envFrom</code>/<code>secretKeyRef</code> at container-start time — if the pod starts before they exist it goes <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code>, not a graceful retry.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3</td><td>StorageClass + PVC (E.4)</td><td>The database can't boot without somewhere to write; a StatefulSet whose <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> can't bind sits stuck at <code>Pending</code>.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>4</td><td>StatefulSet + headless Service (E.5)</td><td>The web tier's DB connection will refuse/timeout until PostgreSQL is <code>Ready</code> — applying web here just means its first few restarts are wasted retries.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>5</td><td>Job: db-migrate, then Job: collectstatic (E.12)</td><td>The schema must exist before any web pod serves a request against it; static assets should be gathered before traffic arrives so the first page load isn't unstyled.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>6</td><td>Deployment + Service (E.6)</td><td>The app itself — everything before this step exists <em>to support</em> this step.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>7</td><td>Ingress / Gateway+HTTPRoute (E.7, E.7b)</td><td>External routing goes last on purpose — you don't want internet traffic reaching a Service with zero ready endpoints behind it.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>8</td><td>NetworkPolicy (E.8)</td><td>Lock down traffic only <em>after</em> you've confirmed the allowed paths actually work — applying a default-deny policy before wiring is verified turns every future symptom into "is it the policy, or is it broken?"</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>9</td><td>RBAC, PDB, HPA, CronJob (E.9–E.11)</td><td>Hardening and scaling controls — they refine an already-working deployment, they don't gate whether it works at all.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <p><strong>One-liner:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f manifests/ --recursive</code> generally tolerates being applied in any order for objects that only reference each other by name (a Deployment referencing a not-yet-created Secret just retries internally) — but <strong>Namespace and Secrets first</strong> is still the safe habit, and a Job like <code>db-migrate</code> genuinely must complete (not just be applied) before you can trust the web tier is serving against the right schema. Kustomize (E.15) and Helm (E.14) both resolve dependency order for you automatically.</p>
        </div>

