        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.7 Ingress ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.7 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-7">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.7 Ingress</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">07-ingress.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-ingress
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: networking
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "false"
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  rules:
    - host: easypj.local
      http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: easypj-web
                port:
                  number: 80
          - path: /api
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: easypj-web
                port:
                  number: 80</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Ingress Traffic Flow</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  Client Request                 Ingress Controller          Backend Pods
  ────────────                   ──────────────────          ────────────
  GET easypj.local/      ──▶   nginx pod (DaemonSet)  ──▶  easypj-web-xxx
  GET easypj.local/api   ──▶   matches path rules     ──▶  easypj-web-yyy
  GET other.local/       ──▶   404 (no matching rule)
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Ingress Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>Ingress resource alone does nothing — you need an <strong>Ingress Controller</strong> installed (nginx, traefik)</li>
                    <li><code>ingressClassName</code> replaced the deprecated <code>kubernetes.io/ingress.class</code> annotation in v1.19+</li>
                    <li><code>pathType: Prefix</code> matches <code>/api</code> and <code>/api/jobs</code>; <code>Exact</code> matches only <code>/api</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Real-world trap:</strong> the <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /</code> annotation above only does something meaningful when the matched <code>path</code> contains a capture group (e.g. <code>/api(/|$)(.*)</code>) that <code>rewrite-target</code> then substitutes into — with a plain <code>path: /api</code> and no capture group, as here, it's effectively a no-op for this specific route. It's included to show the annotation's existence, not as a template to copy-paste unmodified onto a path that needs an actual rewrite.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.7b Gateway API ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.7b ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-7b">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.7b Gateway API — Gateway &amp; HTTPRoute (modern alternative to Ingress)</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>🏢 Why a Second Way to Route Traffic?</h5>
                <p>The <strong>Gateway API</strong> is the officially sanctioned successor to Ingress within SIG-Network. It solves two things Ingress never solved cleanly: <strong>role separation</strong> (the platform/infra team owns the <code>Gateway</code> — listeners, TLS, which namespaces may attach; the app team owns the <code>HTTPRoute</code> — hostnames, path matches, backends) and <strong>expressiveness</strong> (native header/weight-based routing, traffic splitting, and cross-namespace backend references without vendor-specific annotations). It does not replace Ingress overnight — both are valid, stable APIs and CKA still exams Ingress — but expect Gateway API to keep growing in real-world clusters.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">07b-gateway.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Gateway
metadata:
  name: easypj-gateway
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: networking
spec:
  gatewayClassName: nginx             <span class="comment"># or istio, envoy-gateway, cilium — needs its controller installed</span>
  listeners:
    - name: https
      protocol: HTTPS
      port: 443
      hostname: easypj.example.com
      tls:
        mode: Terminate
        certificateRefs:
          - name: easypj-tls
      allowedRoutes:
        namespaces:
          from: Same                  <span class="comment"># only HTTPRoutes in THIS namespace may attach to this Gateway</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">07c-httproute.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: easypj-route
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: networking
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - name: easypj-gateway            <span class="comment"># attaches this HTTPRoute to the Gateway above</span>
  hostnames:
    - "easypj.example.com"
  rules:
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
            value: /
      backendRefs:
        - name: easypj-web
          port: 80
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
            value: /api
      backendRefs:
        - name: easypj-web
          port: 80</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Role Split: Platform Team vs. App Team</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
   PLATFORM / INFRA TEAM OWNS                    APP TEAM OWNS
   ┌───────────────────────────┐   parentRefs   ┌───────────────────────────┐
   │ Gateway: easypj-gateway    │◀───────────────│ HTTPRoute: easypj-route    │
   │  gatewayClassName: nginx   │                 │  hostnames: easypj.        │
   │  listener :443 HTTPS       │                 │            example.com     │
   │  TLS termination           │                 │  path matches → /  , /api  │
   │  allowedRoutes: Same ns    │                 │  backendRefs → easypj-web  │
   └───────────────────────────┘                 └──────────────┬────────────┘
                                                                   │
                                                                   ▼
                                                        Service: easypj-web:80
                                                        (same Service E.6 creates —
                                                         Ingress and Gateway both
                                                         route to it, never both live)
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Ingress vs. Gateway API — What Actually Changes</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>The Gateway API CRDs (<code>Gateway</code>, <code>HTTPRoute</code>, <code>GatewayClass</code>, <code>ReferenceGrant</code>, …) are <strong>not built into core Kubernetes</strong> — they must be installed separately (<code>kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/gateway-api/releases/…/standard-install.yaml</code>) before you can create any of these objects.</li>
                    <li>A <code>Gateway</code> alone routes zero traffic without a <strong>GatewayClass</strong> backed by a running controller (nginx gateway fabric, Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium) — the exact same "the object does nothing on its own" trap as Ingress + IngressClass.</li>
                    <li>Cross-namespace backends need an explicit <code>ReferenceGrant</code> in the target namespace — Gateway API defaults to deny, unlike Ingress which has no concept of namespace boundaries for backends at all.</li>
                    <li><code>backendRefs</code> supports a <code>weight</code> field for canary/blue-green traffic splitting across two Services — doing the same with Ingress requires nginx-specific <code>canary-weight</code> annotations that don't portable across controllers.</li>
                    <li>CKA's official curriculum still centers on Ingress — treat E.7b as real-world/CKAD-adjacent knowledge, and always check the current exam objectives before assuming Gateway objects are in scope.</li>
                    <li><strong>Notice E.7 and E.7b intentionally use different hostnames/TLS postures</strong> — E.7's Ingress targets <code>easypj.local</code> with no TLS (<code>ssl-redirect: "false"</code>), the bare-bones shape most CKA labs use since it needs no real DNS or certificate; E.7b's Gateway targets the registrable <code>easypj.example.com</code> with TLS termination via a <code>easypj-tls</code> Secret (the kind of Secret a <code>cert-manager</code> <code>ClusterIssuer</code> would populate in a real cluster). Both still route to the same <code>easypj-web</code> Service — pick whichever hostname/TLS combination matches the environment you're actually deploying to, don't mix the two conventions for the same object.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>Migrating without downtime:</strong> Ingress and Gateway API can run side-by-side — nothing forces an all-or-nothing cutover. Stand up the Gateway + HTTPRoute pointing at the same <code>easypj-web</code> Service, verify with <code>kubectl get httproute -n easypj</code> and <code>kubectl describe httproute easypj-route -n easypj</code> (check the <code>Accepted</code> and <code>ResolvedRefs</code> conditions), confirm traffic flows, then delete the old Ingress object once you're confident.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.8 NetworkPolicy ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.8 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-8">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.8 NetworkPolicy</h3>
            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Prerequisite</h5>
                <p>NetworkPolicies require a CNI that supports them (Calico, Cilium). Flannel does <strong>NOT</strong> enforce network policies.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">08-networkpolicy.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Default deny all ingress in namespace</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-ingress
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}                 <span class="comment"># All pods in namespace</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
---
<span class="comment"># Allow Django to receive traffic from Ingress</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-web-ingress
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8000
---
<span class="comment"># Allow Django → PostgreSQL traffic only</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-postgres-from-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-postgres
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj-web
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432
---
<span class="comment"># Default deny all EGRESS in namespace — pairs with the ingress deny above.</span>
<span class="comment"># Without this, "default-deny" only ever locks the front door; every pod can</span>
<span class="comment"># still call out anywhere it wants.</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-egress
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}                 <span class="comment"># All pods in namespace</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
---
<span class="comment"># Django's own permitted egress: DB + DNS only. Add this BEFORE relying on</span>
<span class="comment"># default-deny-egress above, or the web tier goes dark on apply.</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-web-egress
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
  egress:
    - to:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj-postgres
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432
    - to:                          <span class="comment"># DNS — CoreDNS/kube-dns lives in kube-system</span>
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53
---
<span class="comment"># PostgreSQL needs DNS too (client tooling, pg_dump in the E.12 CronJob) but</span>
<span class="comment"># no other outbound access — it should never need to call OUT anywhere else.</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-postgres-egress-dns
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-postgres
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
  egress:
    - to:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Network Policy Effect — What's Allowed &amp; What's Blocked</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌── ingress-nginx namespace ───┐     ┌────── easypj namespace ────────────┐
  │                               │     │                                    │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐         │     │  ┌──────────────┐                  │
  │  │ Ingress         │─── ✅ ──┼────▶│  │ easypj-web   │                  │
  │  │ Controller      │  :8000  │     │  │ (Django)     │                  │
  │  └─────────────────┘         │     │  └──────┬───────┘                  │
  │                               │     │         │ ✅ :5432                 │
  └───────────────────────────────┘     │         ▼                          │
                                        │  ┌──────────────┐                  │
  ┌── any other namespace ────┐         │  │ easypj-      │                  │
  │                            │        │  │ postgres     │                  │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐      │        │  └──────────────┘                  │
  │  │ Random Pod      │── ❌ ─┼───X   │                                    │
  │  └─────────────────┘      │        │  ❌ All other ingress denied       │
  └────────────────────────────┘        └────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ The DNS Trap — Egress Lockdown's #1 Self-Inflicted Outage</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>A <code>default-deny-egress</code> policy (above) blocks DNS by default</strong> — DNS queries are just UDP/TCP traffic on port 53 like anything else, and Kubernetes NetworkPolicy has no built-in exception for CoreDNS/kube-dns. The instant this policy is applied without an accompanying DNS-allow rule, every pod in <code>easypj</code> that resolves a hostname (including <code>easypj-postgres</code> itself, or any external API call) starts failing with <code>could not resolve host</code> / <code>i/o timeout</code> — with nothing in <code>kubectl get networkpolicy</code> hinting why.</li>
                    <li><code>allow-web-egress</code> and <code>allow-postgres-egress-dns</code> (above) are exactly what closes that gap — both explicitly permit UDP/TCP 53 to the <code>kube-system</code> namespace (where CoreDNS runs on most clusters) <em>before</em> <code>default-deny-egress</code> locks everything else down.</li>
                    <li><strong>Apply order matters here specifically:</strong> if you apply <code>default-deny-egress</code> before the allow-rules exist, there's a real (if brief) window where the web tier can't reach the DB or resolve anything. On a live cluster, apply the allow-rules first, or apply all four NetworkPolicy objects from one file in the same <code>kubectl apply -f</code> call so they land atomically.</li>
                    <li><strong>Quick diagnostic:</strong> <code>kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox -n easypj --restart=Never -- nslookup easypj-postgres</code> — if this hangs or times out right after tightening egress policy, DNS egress is the first thing to check, not the application code.</li>
                    <li>This mirrors the same lesson as E.7/E.7b's "the object routes nothing without a controller" gotcha and E.9's "the Role grants nothing the token can't present" gotcha — a NetworkPolicy, RBAC Role, or Ingress/Gateway can each be textbook-correct YAML and still produce a completely broken cluster if one adjacent piece (a controller, a mounted token, a DNS allow-rule) isn't also in place.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.9 RBAC ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.9 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-9">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.9 RBAC (Role &amp; RoleBinding)</h3>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>Why easypj-web Needs Its Own ServiceAccount at All</h5>
                <p>Every pod authenticates to the API server as <em>some</em> identity. If you never set <code>serviceAccountName</code>, a pod silently runs as its namespace's <code>default</code> ServiceAccount — which, on many clusters, other workloads also use, meaning an RBAC grant meant for one app quietly leaks to every pod that forgot to set an explicit SA. Giving <code>easypj-web</code> its <strong>own</strong> narrowly-scoped SA (<code>easypj-web-sa</code>) means the Role below applies to easypj — and only easypj.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">RBAC Chain — Who Can Do What, and Where the Wiring Actually Connects</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
   Pod (E.6 easypj-web)              RoleBinding                Role                  Resources
   ┌───────────────────┐   uses    ┌──────────────────┐  grants ┌────────────────┐  reachable
   │ serviceAccountName:│─────────▶│ easypj-web-      │────────▶│ easypj-role     │─────────────▶
   │  easypj-web-sa      │  subject │ binding           │ roleRef│  get/list/watch │  configmaps,
   └───────────────────┘           │  (this namespace) │        │  configmaps,    │  secrets (ns:
                                    └──────────────────┘        │  secrets;       │  easypj only —
                                                                  │  get/list pods  │  Role ≠ ClusterRole,
                                                                  └────────────────┘  no cross-ns reach)

   kubectl auth can-i get secrets -n easypj --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa
   └─▶ this is the exact question the chain above answers: yes, because the arrows connect end to end.
       Break ANY one arrow (wrong SA name, RoleBinding in the wrong namespace, roleRef pointing at a
       Role that doesn't exist) and the answer flips to "no" with no error at apply time — only at
       the moment something tries to use the missing permission.
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">09a-serviceaccount.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-sa
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: rbac
automountServiceAccountToken: false   <span class="comment"># Best practice: opt-in only</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">09b-role.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-role
  namespace: easypj
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["configmaps", "secrets"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["get", "list"]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">09c-rolebinding.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: easypj-web-sa
    namespace: easypj
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <strong>⚡ Imperative equivalents:</strong>
                <code>kubectl create sa easypj-web-sa -n easypj</code>
                <code>kubectl create role easypj-role --verb=get,list,watch --resource=configmaps,secrets -n easypj</code>
                <code>kubectl create rolebinding easypj-web-binding --role=easypj-role --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-web-sa -n easypj</code>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ RBAC Gotchas — Including One Hiding in This Very Manifest Set</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Look closely at <code>09a-serviceaccount.yaml</code>:</strong> it sets <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>. That means <code>easypj-web-sa</code>'s token is <em>not</em> mounted into the pod by default — so even though the Role/RoleBinding above correctly grant <code>get/list/watch</code> on configmaps/secrets/pods, the <code>easypj-web</code> Deployment (E.6) never sets <code>automountServiceAccountToken: true</code> at the Pod level to override it. Net effect: this Role is currently <strong>dead weight</strong> — granted but unusable — because Django never actually calls the Kubernetes API. If a future feature needs the app to introspect its own pods, you'd flip <code>automountServiceAccountToken: true</code> in E.6's pod spec; until then, leaving the token unmounted is correctly following least-privilege (an app that never calls the API shouldn't carry API credentials it can leak).</li>
                    <li><code>Role</code> + <code>RoleBinding</code> are namespace-scoped — they only ever grant access to objects <em>inside <code>easypj</code></em>. Needing cluster-wide or cross-namespace access (e.g. reading Nodes, or Secrets in another team's namespace) requires <code>ClusterRole</code> + <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code> instead — a completely different pair of kinds, not a flag on these ones.</li>
                    <li><code>rules</code> is additive and default-deny: nothing not explicitly listed is granted, and there's no "deny" verb — you can't subtract access with a second Role, only grant more via a second RoleBinding.</li>
                    <li>An empty <code>apiGroups: [""]</code> means the <strong>core API group</strong> (pods, configmaps, secrets, services, …) — a common exam trap is expecting to write <code>apiGroups: ["core"]</code>, which doesn't exist as a string; core is always the empty string.</li>
                    <li>Always verify with <code>kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;resource&gt; -n easypj --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa</code> rather than trusting the YAML by eye — RBAC failures are silent at <code>apply</code> time and only surface as a 403 from the API server when something actually tries to use the missing permission.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.10 PDB ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.10 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-10">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.10 PodDisruptionBudget</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Purpose</h5>
                <p>Guarantees at least 2 Django pods are always running during voluntary disruptions (node drain, cluster upgrades).</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">10-pdb.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-pdb
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: availability
spec:
  minAvailable: 2                 <span class="comment"># Always keep 2+ pods running</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>CKA Tip:</strong> Use <code>minAvailable</code> when you know the minimum OR <code>maxUnavailable</code> when you want flexibility. With 3 replicas and <code>minAvailable: 2</code>, only 1 pod can be disrupted at a time.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.11 HPA ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.11 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-11">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.11 Horizontal Pod Autoscaler</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">11-hpa.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-hpa
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: autoscaling
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj-web
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: memory
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 80
  behavior:
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300   <span class="comment"># Wait 5 min before scaling down</span>
      policies:
        - type: Pods
          value: 1
          periodSeconds: 60             <span class="comment"># Remove max 1 pod per minute</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">HPA Scaling Behavior</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  CPU Load     Replicas     Action
  ────────     ────────     ──────
    20%          2          Minimum (idle)
    50%          2          Still within threshold
    70%          3          Scale up triggered (target: 70%)
    90%          5          Aggressive scale up
   100%         10          Maximum reached
    60%         10          Stabilization window (5 min wait)
    40%          9          Scale down: -1 pod/min
    20%          2          Back to minimum
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <strong>⚡ Imperative equivalent:</strong>
                <code>kubectl autoscale deployment easypj-web --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70 -n easypj</code>
