        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.12 Jobs (Migration + CronJob Backup) ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.12 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-12">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.12 Jobs &amp; CronJobs</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Three Batch Workloads, Three Different Shapes</h5>
                <p>Not every workload is a long-running Pod. This section covers all three batch patterns easypj needs: a one-time <strong>Job</strong> for database migration (<code>db-migrate</code>), a second one-time <strong>Job</strong> for gathering static assets (<code>collectstatic</code>), and a recurring <strong>CronJob</strong> for nightly database backups (<code>db-backup</code>). All three run <code>restartPolicy: Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code> — never <code>Always</code>, which is only valid for Pods/Deployments — because a batch task is expected to finish, not run forever.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">12a-db-migrate-job.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-db-migrate
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: migration
spec:
  backoffLimit: 3                 <span class="comment"># Retry up to 3 times</span>
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 300      <span class="comment"># Timeout after 5 minutes</span>
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
        - name: migrate
          image: registry.example.com/easypj:5.2.5   <span class="comment"># same image E.6 runs — built in E.13</span>
          command: ["python", "manage.py", "migrate", "--noinput"]
          envFrom:
            - configMapRef:
                name: easypj-config
            - secretRef:
                name: easypj-secrets
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 100m
              memory: 128Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 250m
              memory: 256Mi</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Why collectstatic Is Its Own Job</h5>
                <p>Django doesn't serve CSS/JS/images from the app process in production — <code>manage.py collectstatic</code> gathers every static file (from every app + admin) into one <code>STATIC_ROOT</code> directory, which is then served by whitenoise, a CDN, or a dedicated static-file path on the Ingress. Skipping this step is the single most common "the site works but looks broken (no CSS)" bug in a freshly deployed Django app — migrations succeed, the app boots, but every page renders unstyled.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">12b-collectstatic-job.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-collectstatic
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: static-assets
spec:
  backoffLimit: 2                 <span class="comment"># Fewer retries than migrate — this is idempotent but non-critical</span>
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 180
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
        - name: collectstatic
          image: registry.example.com/easypj:5.2.5   <span class="comment"># same image E.6 runs — built in E.13</span>
          command: ["python", "manage.py", "collectstatic", "--noinput"]
          envFrom:
            - configMapRef:
                name: easypj-config
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 100m
              memory: 128Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 250m
              memory: 256Mi</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>Run order:</strong> <code>db-migrate</code> → <code>collectstatic</code> → roll out <code>easypj-web</code> (E.6). Migrations must land before the app serves traffic against the new schema; static assets should be gathered before (or during) rollout so the very first request against a new pod already finds its CSS/JS. Neither Job needs to block the other — they touch different resources (the DB vs. a static-file volume/CDN) and can run in parallel if your pipeline supports it.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">12c-db-backup-cronjob.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: easypj-db-backup
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: backup
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"          <span class="comment"># Daily at 2:00 AM</span>
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid       <span class="comment"># Don't overlap backup runs</span>
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      backoffLimit: 2
      template:
        spec:
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          containers:
            - name: backup
              image: postgres:15-alpine
              command:
                - /bin/sh
                - -c
                - |
                  pg_dump -h $DB_HOST -U postgres -d $DB_NAME \
                    -Fc -f /backup/easypj-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).dump
              envFrom:
                - configMapRef:
                    name: easypj-config
              env:
                - name: PGPASSWORD
                  valueFrom:
                    secretKeyRef:
                      name: easypj-secrets
                      key: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
              resources:
                requests:
                  cpu: 100m
                  memory: 128Mi
                limits:
                  cpu: 250m
                  memory: 256Mi
              volumeMounts:
                - name: backup
                  mountPath: /backup      <span class="comment"># WITHOUT this, the dump is written to the Job pod's</span>
          volumes:                        <span class="comment"># writable layer and vanishes forever when the pod is GC'd</span>
            - name: backup
              persistentVolumeClaim:
                claimName: easypj-backup-pvc</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">12d-backup-pvc.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: easypj-backup-pvc
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: backup
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce              <span class="comment"># One CronJob run at a time (concurrencyPolicy: Forbid above)</span>
  storageClassName: easypj-storage
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 20Gi              <span class="comment"># Sized for retention window × dump size, not 1 dump</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ The Backup That Wasn't — a Real Production Trap</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>A CronJob whose container writes to a plain path with <strong>no <code>volumeMounts</code> / <code>volumes</code></strong> looks like it succeeds — <code>kubectl get jobs</code> shows <code>Complete</code>, the pod's exit code is 0 — but the dump file lived only in that Job pod's ephemeral container filesystem and was deleted the moment the pod was garbage-collected. You have zero backups and zero errors telling you so, until the day you actually need to restore.</li>
                    <li>Mounting <code>easypj-backup-pvc</code> (above) fixes it — the dump now survives pod deletion. <code>successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3</code> only controls how many old <em>Job objects</em> Kubernetes keeps around for <code>kubectl logs</code>; it has nothing to do with how many dump <em>files</em> accumulate on the PVC — that's on you to prune (a second cheap CronJob, or a lifecycle policy if you point this at object storage instead).</li>
                    <li>For anything beyond a lab, point backups at <strong>object storage</strong> (S3/GCS/MinIO via <code>aws s3 cp</code> in the same container) rather than an in-cluster PVC — a PV bound to a single failed node doesn't help you when that node is the disaster you're recovering from.</li>
                    <li>An untested backup is a hypothesis, not a backup — periodically run <code>pg_restore</code> against a scratch database to confirm the dump is actually restorable.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.13 Dockerfile + .dockerignore ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.13 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-13">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.13 Dockerfile (Multi-Stage) &amp; .dockerignore</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Why Multi-Stage?</h5>
                <p>Every manifest in E.1–E.12 assumes an image called <code>registry.example.com/easypj:5.2.5</code> already exists. This is the Dockerfile that builds it. A <strong>multi-stage build</strong> uses one throwaway stage to compile things (a C compiler, PostgreSQL headers) and a second, much smaller stage to actually ship — the compiler never makes it into the image that runs in your cluster.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">Dockerfile</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-dockerfile"><span class="comment"># ---- Stage 1: build wheels (compiler + headers live ONLY here) ----</span>
FROM python:3.13-slim AS builder
WORKDIR /app
RUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    build-essential libpq-dev &amp;&amp; rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip wheel --no-cache-dir --wheel-dir /wheels -r requirements.txt

<span class="comment"># ---- Stage 2: slim runtime image (this is what ships) ----</span>
FROM python:3.13-slim
WORKDIR /app
RUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    libpq5 &amp;&amp; rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
    &amp;&amp; groupadd -g 1000 app &amp;&amp; useradd -u 1000 -g app app
COPY --from=builder /wheels /wheels
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --no-index --find-links=/wheels -r requirements.txt
COPY . .
USER 1000                          <span class="comment"># must match runAsUser: 1000 in E.6's securityContext</span>
EXPOSE 8000                        <span class="comment"># must match containerPort: 8000 in E.6</span>
CMD ["gunicorn", "easypj.wsgi:application", \
     "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8000", "--workers", "3"]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">.dockerignore</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-text">.git
.gitignore
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.sqlite3
db.sqlite3
.env
.venv/
venv/
node_modules/
*.md
k8s/
manifests/</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Build-Stage Flow — What Ships vs. What Doesn't</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  STAGE 1: builder (discarded)         STAGE 2: runtime (this ships)
  ┌───────────────────────────┐        ┌───────────────────────────┐
  │ python:3.13-slim            │        │ python:3.13-slim            │
  │ + build-essential (gcc..)   │  COPY  │ + libpq5 ONLY (no compiler) │
  │ + libpq-dev (headers)       │ /wheels│ pip install --no-index      │
  │ pip wheel → /wheels/*.whl   │───────▶│   --find-links=/wheels      │
  └───────────────────────────┘        │ USER 1000 (non-root)        │
                                          │ EXPOSE 8000                  │
                                          │ CMD gunicorn ...             │
                                          └───────────────────────────┘
  Result: the final image never contains a compiler, build headers, or
  apt package lists — smaller image, smaller attack surface, faster pulls.
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Dockerfile Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>Multi-stage keeps <code>build-essential</code> and <code>libpq-dev</code> OUT of the final image — only the compiled <code>.whl</code> files plus the runtime library (<code>libpq5</code>) ship. Skipping this step roughly doubles image size and ships a C compiler into production for no reason.</li>
                    <li><code>USER 1000</code> here must match (or be compatible with) the Pod's <code>securityContext.runAsUser: 1000</code> / <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> in E.6 — a mismatch doesn't fail the <code>docker build</code>, it fails at <strong>runtime</strong> with a permissions error the first time the process tries to write anywhere.</li>
                    <li><code>.dockerignore</code> excluding <code>manifests/</code> and <code>k8s/</code> stops your own Kubernetes YAML (and any Secret values you keep alongside it, even briefly) from ever ending up baked into an image layer where <code>docker history</code> or a registry scan could expose them.</li>
                    <li><code>COPY requirements.txt .</code> happens BEFORE <code>COPY . .</code> in both stages — deliberate layer-caching. Docker only re-runs the (slow) <code>pip install</code> layer when <code>requirements.txt</code> changes, not on every source-code edit.</li>
                    <li><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> in E.6 means this container can't write anywhere except its declared <code>emptyDir</code> mounts — if the app needs to write logs, cache files, or uploaded media at runtime, that path must be an explicit volume, not something this Dockerfile can fix.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>Build &amp; push:</strong> <code>docker build -t registry.example.com/easypj:5.2.5 . &amp;&amp; docker push registry.example.com/easypj:5.2.5</code> — then bump the tag in E.6's Deployment (or E.14's <code>values.yaml</code> / E.15's <code>images:</code> overlay field) and roll out with <code>kubectl rollout restart deploy/easypj-web -n easypj</code>.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.14 Helm Chart Skeleton ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.14 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-14">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.14 Helm Chart Skeleton</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Packaging E.1–E.12 for Reuse</h5>
                <p>A Helm chart takes every manifest in this appendix (ConfigMap, Secret, Deployment, StatefulSet, Ingress, HPA, PDB…) and turns them into <strong>templates</strong> driven by one <code>values.yaml</code> file. One versioned, installable artifact instead of a folder of loose YAML — install it, upgrade it, and roll it back with a single command each.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Chart Directory Layout</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
easypj/
├── Chart.yaml                 <span class="comment"># chart metadata: name, chart version, appVersion</span>
├── values.yaml                <span class="comment"># default configuration — overridable per install</span>
├── templates/
│   ├── _helpers.tpl           <span class="comment"># reusable named templates (labels, full names)</span>
│   ├── configmap.yaml         <span class="comment"># renders E.2</span>
│   ├── secret.yaml            <span class="comment"># renders E.3</span>
│   ├── deployment.yaml        <span class="comment"># renders E.6 (Deployment half)</span>
│   ├── service.yaml           <span class="comment"># renders E.6 (Service half)</span>
│   ├── statefulset.yaml       <span class="comment"># renders E.5</span>
│   ├── ingress.yaml           <span class="comment"># renders E.7</span>
│   ├── hpa.yaml                <span class="comment"># renders E.11</span>
│   ├── pdb.yaml                 <span class="comment"># renders E.10</span>
│   └── NOTES.txt               <span class="comment"># printed to stdout after `helm install`</span>
└── charts/                     <span class="comment"># sub-chart dependencies (empty for easypj)</span>
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">Chart.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v2
name: easypj
description: easypj Django job board on Kubernetes
type: application
version: 0.1.0            <span class="comment"># CHART version — bump on any template/values change</span>
appVersion: "5.2.5"       <span class="comment"># APP version — matches the Docker image tag from E.13</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">values.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">replicaCount: 3
image:
  repository: registry.example.com/easypj
  tag: "5.2.5"
  pullPolicy: IfNotPresent
config:
  debug: "False"
  allowedHosts: "easypj.local"        <span class="comment"># matches E.7's Ingress host (ingress.yaml renders E.7, not E.7b's Gateway)</span>
resources:
  requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi }  <span class="comment"># must mirror E.6's Deployment exactly — this chart templates E.6</span>
  limits:   { cpu: 500m, memory: 512Mi }
autoscaling:
  enabled: true
  minReplicas: 2                      <span class="comment"># matches E.11's HPA minReplicas — NOT replicaCount above</span>
  maxReplicas: 10
  targetCPUUtilizationPercentage: 70
ingress:
  enabled: true
  host: easypj.local                  <span class="comment"># same host as E.7 — this chart's ingress.yaml renders the classic Ingress</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">templates/deployment.yaml (excerpt)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}
  <span class="comment"># ...</span>
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: easypj
          image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}"
          resources: {{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 16 }}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Helm Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>values.yaml</code> is only the DEFAULT — every field is overridable at install/upgrade time with <code>-f values-prod.yaml</code> or <code>--set image.tag=5.2.6</code>, without touching a single template file.</li>
                    <li><code>{{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 16 }}</code> is the standard idiom for dropping an arbitrary nested map into a template at the right indentation — get the <code>nindent</code> number wrong and you get invalid YAML only at <strong>render</strong> time, not at edit time.</li>
                    <li><code>helm template ./easypj</code> renders the chart to plain YAML locally without touching the cluster at all — always dry-run this (or <code>helm install --dry-run --debug</code>) before an <code>install</code>/<code>upgrade</code> on anything you haven't tested.</li>
                    <li><code>Chart.yaml</code>'s <code>version</code> (chart packaging version) and <code>appVersion</code> (application version) are independent fields — bump <code>version</code> every time templates or <code>values.yaml</code> change, even when <code>appVersion</code> (the Django release) hasn't moved.</li>
                    <li>A failed <code>helm upgrade</code> can leave a release stuck in <code>pending-upgrade</code> — <code>helm rollback</code> or <code>helm history</code> + a fresh <code>upgrade</code> is usually the fix; avoid manually editing Helm's Secret-backed release storage.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">helm-lifecycle.sh</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># First install — creates the release AND the namespace</span>
helm install easypj ./easypj -n easypj --create-namespace

<span class="comment"># Upgrade an existing release with prod-specific overrides</span>
helm upgrade easypj ./easypj -n easypj -f values-prod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Instant revert to a previous revision</span>
helm rollback easypj 1 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># See what's actually installed vs. what values were used</span>
helm get values easypj -n easypj
helm history easypj -n easypj</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.15 Kustomize Base + Overlays ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.15 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-15">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.15 Kustomize Base &amp; Dev/Prod Overlays</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Patch, Don't Template</h5>
                <p>Kustomize takes the opposite philosophy from Helm: <strong>no templating language at all</strong>. You write one <code>base/</code> of plain, valid YAML (the exact objects from E.1–E.12), then layer environment-specific <code>overlays/</code> that patch just the fields that differ — replica counts, image tags, a <code>DEBUG</code> flag. Kustomize ships inside <code>kubectl</code> itself (<code>kubectl apply -k</code>) — nothing extra to install.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Kustomize Directory Layout</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
kustomize/
├── base/
│   ├── kustomization.yaml      <span class="comment"># lists every raw manifest this base owns</span>
│   ├── namespace.yaml          <span class="comment"># = E.1</span>
│   ├── configmap.yaml          <span class="comment"># = E.2</span>
│   ├── deployment.yaml         <span class="comment"># = E.6 (Deployment half)</span>
│   ├── service.yaml            <span class="comment"># = E.6 (Service half)</span>
│   └── ingress.yaml            <span class="comment"># = E.7</span>
└── overlays/
    ├── dev/
    │   ├── kustomization.yaml
    │   └── replicas-patch.yaml <span class="comment"># patches replicas: 1, DEBUG=True</span>
    └── prod/
        ├── kustomization.yaml
        └── replicas-patch.yaml <span class="comment"># patches replicas: 3, image tag 5.2.5</span>
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">base/kustomization.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
namespace: easypj                    <span class="comment"># every resource below gets this namespace injected</span>
commonLabels:
  app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj     <span class="comment"># stamped onto every resource AND its selectors</span>
resources:
  - namespace.yaml
  - configmap.yaml
  - deployment.yaml
  - service.yaml
  - ingress.yaml</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">overlays/dev/kustomization.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
resources:
  - ../../base
patches:
  - path: replicas-patch.yaml
images:
  - name: registry.example.com/easypj
    newTag: dev
configMapGenerator:
  - name: easypj-config
    behavior: merge                  <span class="comment"># merge INTO the base ConfigMap, don't replace it</span>
    literals:
      - DEBUG=True</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">overlays/dev/replicas-patch.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  replicas: 1</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
resources:
  - ../../base
patches:
  - path: replicas-patch.yaml        <span class="comment"># sets replicas: 3</span>
images:
  - name: registry.example.com/easypj
    newTag: "5.2.5"</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Decision Guide — Helm vs. Kustomize</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐
  │ Tool        │ Best For                             │ Mechanism                     │
  ├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Helm        │ Packaging/distribution, versioned    │ Go templates + values.yaml;  │
  │             │ releases, rollback, sharing a         │ helm install/upgrade/rollback │
  │             │ reusable chart across teams           │                                │
  ├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Kustomize   │ Environment overlays of YAML you      │ Template-free patches; built  │
  │             │ already own (dev/staging/prod)        │ into kubectl -k, no install    │
  └────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘
  Many teams use BOTH: Helm to install third-party charts (postgresql,
  ingress-nginx, cert-manager) and Kustomize to overlay their OWN easypj
  manifests on top of that foundation.
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Kustomize Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>Kustomize has <strong>zero templating language</strong> — every resource is either a literal file or a strategic-merge / JSON6902 patch. Reaching for an <code>{{ if }}</code> is the sign you actually want Helm.</li>
                    <li><code>configMapGenerator</code> / <code>secretGenerator</code> append a content hash to the generated object's name (e.g. <code>easypj-config-8f7d6c2gh6</code>) and Kustomize automatically rewrites every reference to it — this is what makes a ConfigMap/Secret content change trigger a rolling restart for free, with zero extra wiring.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl apply -k</code> points at a <strong>directory</strong> containing a <code>kustomization.yaml</code>, never at a single YAML file directly — pointing it at a manifest file instead of the directory is a common typo.</li>
                    <li>Patches under <code>patches:</code> apply in the order listed — when two patches touch the same field, order determines the final value.</li>
                    <li><code>commonLabels</code> in the base rewrites label selectors too, not just metadata labels — changing it after objects already exist can silently break Service→Pod selector matching (immutable selector fields will reject the change on already-applied Deployments).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">kustomize-commands.sh</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply a whole environment in one shot</span>
kubectl apply -k kustomize/overlays/dev
kubectl apply -k kustomize/overlays/prod

<span class="comment"># Or pipe the standalone kustomize CLI into kubectl</span>
kustomize build kustomize/overlays/prod | kubectl apply -f -

<span class="comment"># Preview the fully-rendered YAML — no apply, safe to run any time</span>
kubectl kustomize kustomize/overlays/prod

<span class="comment"># See exactly what would change before applying</span>
kubectl diff -k kustomize/overlays/prod</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.16 Production Hardening Add-ons ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.16 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-16">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.16 Production Hardening Add-ons — Beyond the Core Set</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Not in the Original Blueprint — Added Because Real Clusters Need Them</h5>
                <p>E.1–E.15 is a complete, exam-aligned manifest set on its own. This section layers on five more patterns that a production Kubernetes deployment of <code>easypj</code> would realistically need but that don't map to any single CKA exam objective: pulling a <strong>private</strong> registry image, spreading replicas for real (not just PDB-protected) high availability, shutting down gracefully instead of dropping in-flight requests, capping what the whole namespace can consume, and tolerating a slow application boot. Each is a small, targeted patch to manifests you already have — none of them replace anything in E.1–E.15.</p>
            </div>

            <h4>E.16a Pulling from a Private Registry</h4>
            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ A Gap Hiding in Plain Sight</h5>
                <p>Every manifest in this appendix references <code>registry.example.com/easypj:5.2.5</code> (E.6, E.12, E.13, E.14, E.15) — but a registry named like that is almost certainly <strong>not</strong> anonymous/public. Nothing in E.1–E.15 tells the kubelet how to authenticate to it. Without an <code>imagePullSecrets</code> reference, every pod that needs this image sits in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> with <code>unauthorized: authentication required</code> the moment it's scheduled onto a node that hasn't already cached the image.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">16a-registry-pull-secret.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-registry-cred
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: registry-auth
type: kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson    <span class="comment"># the ONLY type the kubelet knows how to use for pulls</span>
data:
  .dockerconfigjson: <span class="comment"># base64 of a docker config.json — generate it, don't hand-write it (see below)</span></code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">06a-django-deployment.yaml (excerpt — pod spec addition)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  <span class="comment"># ... existing template.spec fields from E.6 unchanged ...</span>
  imagePullSecrets:
    - name: easypj-registry-cred     <span class="comment"># references the Secret above — add to E.6, E.12a, E.12b too</span></code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <strong>⚡ Imperative equivalent (the recommended way to create this Secret — never hand-encode a dockerconfigjson):</strong>
                <code>kubectl create secret docker-registry easypj-registry-cred --docker-server=registry.example.com --docker-username=&lt;user&gt; --docker-password=&lt;pass&gt; -n easypj</code>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ imagePullSecrets Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>imagePullSecrets</code> is a list on the <strong>Pod spec</strong>, not the container — it lives beside <code>containers:</code>, not inside a container entry.</li>
                    <li>It only authenticates <strong>pulls</strong>; it grants no other permission and is unrelated to the RBAC ServiceAccount/Role/RoleBinding chain in E.9.</li>
                    <li>To avoid adding this to every Deployment/Job individually, you can instead patch the namespace's <code>default</code> ServiceAccount's own <code>imagePullSecrets</code> field — every pod that doesn't set its own <code>serviceAccountName</code> then inherits it automatically. <code>easypj-web</code> sets <code>serviceAccountName: easypj-web-sa</code> explicitly (E.9), so that SA needs the patch instead, or every Deployment/Job needs its own explicit field as shown above.</li>
                    <li>Helm (E.14) and Kustomize (E.15) can both template/patch this in centrally — one <code>values.yaml</code> field or one strategic-merge patch, instead of hand-editing every manifest.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h4>E.16b Spreading Replicas for Real HA</h4>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>PDB Protects Against Drains — It Doesn't Prevent Co-Location</h5>
                <p>E.10's PDB (<code>minAvailable: 2</code>) guarantees the cluster won't voluntarily evict too many <code>easypj-web</code> pods at once — but nothing in E.1–E.15 stops the scheduler from placing all 3 replicas on the <em>same node</em> in the first place. If that one node fails, the PDB has no say — all 3 go down together, PDB or not. <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> closes that gap by actively spreading pods across failure domains at scheduling time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">06a-django-deployment.yaml (excerpt — pod spec addition)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  <span class="comment"># ... existing template.spec fields from E.6 unchanged ...</span>
  topologySpreadConstraints:
    - maxSkew: 1                        <span class="comment"># at most 1 pod of imbalance between any two zones</span>
      topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
      whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule  <span class="comment"># hard requirement — use ScheduleAnyway to make it a soft preference</span>
      labelSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: easypj-web
    - maxSkew: 1
      topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname   <span class="comment"># also never double up on the SAME node</span>
      whenUnsatisfiable: ScheduleAnyway     <span class="comment"># soft — don't leave pods Pending if only 2 nodes exist</span>
      labelSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: easypj-web</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ topologySpreadConstraints Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule</code> is a hard constraint — on a small cluster (fewer zones/nodes than replicas), this can leave pods stuck <code>Pending</code> forever. Know your cluster's topology before choosing <code>DoNotSchedule</code> over <code>ScheduleAnyway</code>.</li>
                    <li>The <code>topologyKey</code> must be a label that actually exists on your Nodes — <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code> is populated automatically by most cloud providers' cloud-controller-manager, but a bare-metal/kind/minikube cluster typically has only one zone (or none), making the zone constraint a no-op there. <code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code> always works since every Node has a unique hostname.</li>
                    <li>This is a scheduling-time guarantee, not a runtime one — it doesn't rebalance already-running pods if the topology changes later (a node joins/leaves). A new rollout re-evaluates it; existing pods don't move on their own.</li>
                    <li>Don't confuse this with <code>podAntiAffinity</code> — both spread pods, but <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> is the newer, simpler, and generally preferred API for this exact use case.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h4>E.16c Graceful Shutdown</h4>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>What Actually Happens When a Pod Is Deleted</h5>
                <p>Every rolling update (E.6), every drain the PDB (E.10) protects against, every scale-down — all of them delete pods. Without a graceful shutdown path, <code>gunicorn</code> can be killed mid-request the instant it receives <code>SIGTERM</code>, dropping whatever request it was serving. The default <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> is <strong>30</strong>, and a <code>preStop</code> hook lets the container do something deliberate with that window before the signal even lands.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">06a-django-deployment.yaml (excerpt — pod &amp; container spec addition)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45     <span class="comment"># give gunicorn's --workers 3 time to finish in-flight requests</span>
  containers:
    - name: django
      <span class="comment"># ... existing container fields from E.6 unchanged ...</span>
      lifecycle:
        preStop:
          exec:
            command: ["/bin/sh", "-c", "sleep 5"]  <span class="comment"># let the Service's endpoint removal propagate BEFORE gunicorn stops accepting</span></code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Pod Termination Timeline — Where the preStop Sleep Actually Helps</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  t=0s   kubectl delete pod / rollout replaces pod / node drain evicts pod
         │
         ├─▶ Pod marked Terminating; REMOVED from Service endpoints
         │   (this propagates to kube-proxy / ingress across the cluster —
         │    NOT instantly; a few hundred ms to a couple seconds is normal)
         │
         ├─▶ preStop hook runs: `sleep 5`                       ◄── E.16c's fix
         │   (buys time for the endpoint removal above to actually reach
         │    every node BEFORE SIGTERM — without this, some requests can
         │    still be routed here for a moment after the process exits)
         │
         ├─▶ SIGTERM sent to gunicorn — workers stop accepting NEW connections,
         │   finish requests already in flight
         │
         ├─▶ terminationGracePeriodSeconds counts down (45s here, from t=0)
         │
         └─▶ SIGKILL if the process hasn't exited by the grace period deadline
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Graceful Shutdown Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>The <code>preStop</code> sleep and <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> are independent, additive: the <em>total</em> time before <code>SIGKILL</code> is the full grace period, counted from t=0 — the sleep just delays when <code>SIGTERM</code> is sent within that same window. Set the grace period comfortably longer than <code>preStop</code>'s sleep plus however long the slowest in-flight request should be allowed to finish, or the app gets <code>SIGKILL</code>ed mid-cleanup.</li>
                    <li>This is <strong>orthogonal</strong> to <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>/<code>runAsNonRoot</code> (E.6's <code>securityContext</code>) and to the <code>readinessProbe</code> — readiness controls when a pod <em>starts</em> receiving traffic; graceful shutdown controls how it stops.</li>
                    <li>CronJob/Job pods (E.12) don't need this pattern — they're expected to run to completion and exit on their own, not be interrupted mid-request the way a long-lived Service-backed Deployment pod is.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h4>E.16d Namespace-Wide Guardrails: ResourceQuota &amp; LimitRange</h4>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>E.6's Resources Cap ONE Container — These Cap the Whole Namespace</h5>
                <p>E.6, E.5, E.12 each set <code>resources.requests</code>/<code>limits</code> on their own container — but nothing stops a future teammate from adding a fourth workload to <code>easypj</code> with no limits at all, or accidentally scaling <code>easypj-web</code> to 500 replicas. A <code>ResourceQuota</code> caps the namespace's total consumption; a <code>LimitRange</code> supplies a default for any container that forgets to set its own <code>resources</code> block, and rejects requests/limits outside a sane range.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">16d-resourcequota.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "4"
    requests.memory: 4Gi
    limits.cpu: "8"
    limits.memory: 8Gi
    pods: "20"                    <span class="comment"># hard cap on total pod count in the namespace</span>
    persistentvolumeclaims: "5"   <span class="comment"># db-data + backup + any future PVC — prevents storage sprawl</span>
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-limits
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  limits:
    - type: Container
      default:                    <span class="comment"># applied to any container with NO limits set</span>
        cpu: 500m
        memory: 512Mi
      defaultRequest:             <span class="comment"># applied to any container with NO requests set</span>
        cpu: 100m
        memory: 128Mi
      max:                        <span class="comment"># hard ceiling — a container CANNOT request/limit above this</span>
        cpu: "2"
        memory: 2Gi
      min:                        <span class="comment"># hard floor — rejects absurdly small requests too</span>
        cpu: 50m
        memory: 64Mi</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ ResourceQuota &amp; LimitRange Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Once ANY ResourceQuota exists in a namespace that constrains cpu or memory, every new Pod in that namespace MUST specify <code>resources.requests</code>/<code>limits</code> explicitly, or the Pod is rejected outright at admission</strong> — this is exactly why <code>LimitRange</code>'s <code>default</code>/<code>defaultRequest</code> matters: it auto-fills the gap so pods that forgot to set resources don't suddenly start failing to even create the moment E.16d is applied.</li>
                    <li><code>ResourceQuota</code> counts against the SUM across the namespace — with <code>easypj-web</code> at 3 replicas × (100m request / 500m limit) from E.6, plus the DB and Jobs, headroom against <code>requests.cpu: "4"</code> gets consumed faster than it looks from any single manifest.</li>
                    <li>Apply <code>ResourceQuota</code>/<code>LimitRange</code> LATE in the deployment order (after E.1–E.12 are already running) — applying it FIRST, before any workload exists, is safe too, but retrofitting it onto an already-running namespace whose existing pods exceed the new quota does NOT evict them; it only blocks NEW pod creation and scale-ups until the quota has headroom.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe resourcequota easypj-quota -n easypj</code> shows used vs. hard limits at a glance — the fastest way to see why a new Deployment is stuck refusing to schedule additional replicas.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h4>E.16e startupProbe — Tolerating a Slow Boot</h4>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Why Not Just Loosen livenessProbe's <code>initialDelaySeconds</code>?</h5>
                <p>E.6's <code>livenessProbe</code> has <code>initialDelaySeconds: 15</code> — fine for a normally-booting gunicorn worker, but if a future deploy adds a slow one-time warm-up (loading an ML model, priming a cache) that occasionally takes 60+ seconds, that fixed delay either has to grow for EVERY future rollout (masking real hangs for 60s every time) or the pod gets killed mid-boot on the slow days. A <code>startupProbe</code> solves this cleanly: while it hasn't yet succeeded, <code>livenessProbe</code> and <code>readinessProbe</code> are disabled entirely.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">06a-django-deployment.yaml (excerpt — container spec addition)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">containers:
  - name: django
    <span class="comment"># ... existing container fields from E.6 (including livenessProbe/readinessProbe) unchanged ...</span>
    startupProbe:
      httpGet:
        path: /health/
        port: 8000
      failureThreshold: 30        <span class="comment"># 30 × 5s = up to 150s to boot before Kubernetes gives up</span>
      periodSeconds: 5</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ startupProbe Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>While <code>startupProbe</code> is running (and hasn't yet succeeded), <code>livenessProbe</code> is completely suppressed — a container that's genuinely hung during boot won't get killed until <code>startupProbe</code> itself exhausts its own <code>failureThreshold</code>, so don't set that number so high it defeats the point of having a liveness probe at all.</li>
                    <li>Once <code>startupProbe</code> succeeds a single time, Kubernetes stops calling it for the rest of that container's life — <code>livenessProbe</code>/<code>readinessProbe</code> take over from that point on exactly as configured in E.6.</li>
                    <li>Total tolerated startup time is <code>failureThreshold × periodSeconds</code> — 150s in the example above. This is a much cleaner knob than inflating <code>livenessProbe.initialDelaySeconds</code>, which would apply the same slow-start assumption to every single restart forever, not just the genuinely slow ones.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>None of E.16 is required to pass the CKA exam</strong> — <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code>, <code>ResourceQuota</code>/<code>LimitRange</code>, and <code>startupProbe</code> do all appear on the current CKA curriculum as standalone topics (they're examined independently of this appendix), but <code>imagePullSecrets</code> and graceful-shutdown patterns lean more real-world/production. Treat E.1–E.15 as "what you need to pass," and E.16 as "what you'd actually want running on Friday afternoon before a long weekend."</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
        <span class="ch-jump" id="se-extras-takeaways"></span>
        <div class="key-takeaways">
            <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Apply order tracks the dependency chain, not just a numbering scheme</strong> — Namespace → Config/Secret → Storage → DB → migrate/collectstatic Jobs → web → Ingress/Gateway → policies/hardening. Each step exists to support the one after it.</li>
                <li><strong>The DB is a StatefulSet</strong> with a headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>) and <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code>, giving it stable network identity and per-pod persistent storage — a Deployment cannot give you either.</li>
                <li><strong>Web is a Deployment</strong> with 3 replicas, resource requests+limits, liveness/readiness probes, a hardened <code>securityContext</code>, an HPA (2→10), and a PDB (<code>minAvailable: 2</code>) — every one of those is a separate manifest (E.6, E.10, E.11) working together, not one big config block.</li>
                <li><strong>Schema changes and static-asset collection run as one-shot Jobs</strong> (<code>db-migrate</code>, <code>collectstatic</code> — E.12), never inline in the web container's entrypoint at scale — an entrypoint migration racing 3 replicas booting simultaneously is a classic self-inflicted outage.</li>
                <li><strong>Default-deny NetworkPolicy + targeted allows</strong> is the baseline (E.8); RBAC (E.9) grants the app's ServiceAccount only <code>get/list/watch</code> on configmaps/secrets/pods — and even that grant is currently unused because the token isn't mounted (see E.9's gotcha box).</li>
                <li><strong>Ingress (E.7) and Gateway API (E.7b) are interchangeable fronts</strong> for the exact same <code>easypj-web</code> Service — pick one per environment, never run both live for the same traffic, and know Ingress is still what the CKA exam tests today.</li>
                <li><strong>Package it with Helm (E.14) or Kustomize (E.15)</strong> — or both: Helm for versioned, distributable releases with rollback; Kustomize for template-free environment overlays of YAML you already own. Many real teams use Helm for third-party charts and Kustomize for their own app.</li>
                <li><strong>The Dockerfile (E.13) is the missing link</strong> — every manifest here assumes <code>registry.example.com/easypj:5.2.5</code> already exists; E.13 is what actually builds and ships that image.</li>
                <li><strong>E.16 goes beyond the exam blueprint on purpose</strong> — private-registry auth, real cross-node spreading, graceful shutdown, namespace-wide quotas, and slow-boot tolerance aren't required to pass the CKA, but a cluster running E.1–E.15 alone and calling it "production" is missing all five.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ROUNDUP ─────────── -->
        <span class="ch-jump" id="se-extras-gotchas"></span>
        <div class="cka-gotcha">
            <h5>⚠️ Gotchas Roundup — The Cross-Section Traps</h5>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Secrets are base64, not encrypted at rest</strong> unless you enable <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> on the API server (E.3) — keep real values out of git regardless.</li>
                <li><strong>HPA silently reports <code>&lt;unknown&gt;/70%</code></strong> when metrics-server isn't installed OR the Deployment has no <code>resources.requests.cpu</code> — HPA computes utilization as usage÷request, and with no request in the denominator it simply cannot compute (E.11).</li>
                <li><strong><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> (E.6) breaks anything that writes to disk</strong> — mount an explicit <code>emptyDir</code> (or PVC) at every path the app needs to write, including <code>/tmp</code>. This is exactly the constraint E.13's Dockerfile has to be built around.</li>
                <li><strong>PDB (E.10) only guards <em>voluntary</em> disruptions</strong> — node drains, cluster upgrades, manual evictions. It does nothing for a node that hard-crashes; that's what replica count + anti-affinity are for.</li>
                <li><strong>Ingress needs an Ingress Controller; Gateway needs a GatewayClass</strong> (E.7, E.7b) — neither the <code>Ingress</code> nor the <code>Gateway</code> object routes one byte of traffic on its own without a running controller behind it. Same trap, two different API surfaces.</li>
                <li><strong>A CronJob without a mounted volume "succeeds" while backing up nothing</strong> (E.12) — the exit code is 0, the dump just never left the ephemeral Job pod. Silent, expensive, and only discovered at restore time.</li>
                <li><strong>An RBAC Role can be perfectly correct and still grant nothing usable</strong> (E.9) — if the ServiceAccount's token isn't mounted (<code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>, and no Pod-level override), the permissions exist on paper only.</li>
                <li><strong>Default-deny egress silently breaks DNS</strong> (E.8) — a NetworkPolicy denying all egress with no explicit UDP/TCP 53 allow-rule to <code>kube-system</code> means every pod loses hostname resolution, including the web tier resolving <code>easypj-postgres</code> itself. No error at apply time — only <code>could not resolve host</code> at runtime.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
        <span class="ch-jump" id="se-extras-speed"></span>
        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
            <ul>
                <li><code>kubectl apply -k &lt;overlay&gt;</code> applies a whole environment (namespace, config, workloads, networking) in one shot — no manual step ordering needed once the Kustomize base/overlays exist.</li>
                <li><code>kubectl rollout status deploy/easypj-web -n easypj</code> — watch a rolling update converge in real time instead of polling <code>get pods</code>.</li>
                <li><code>kubectl get all,ing,pvc,netpol -n easypj</code> — one-glance inventory of almost everything in this appendix, across every resource type, in a single command.</li>
                <li><code>helm rollback easypj &lt;REVISION&gt;</code> — instant revert of a bad release without reconstructing what the previous state was by hand.</li>
                <li><code>kubectl create job --from=cronjob/db-backup backup-now -n easypj</code> — clone a CronJob's <code>jobTemplate</code> into an immediate one-off Job, for an ad-hoc backup without waiting for 2 AM.</li>
                <li><code>kubectl diff -k kustomize/overlays/prod</code> — see exactly what would change before you commit to an apply, on any overlay.</li>
                <li><code>kubectl scale deploy/easypj-web --replicas=5 -n easypj</code> — manual scale in place; the HPA (E.11) will fight this back toward its own computed target on the next sync, so treat this as a temporary override, not a lasting change.</li>
                <li><code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web -n easypj</code> — instant rollback to the previous ReplicaSet if a new image breaks something; add <code>--to-revision=N</code> to target a specific prior revision instead of just the last one.</li>
                <li><code>kubectl logs job/easypj-db-migrate -n easypj</code> — read a completed (or still-running) Job's output directly by Job name; no need to look up the underlying Pod name first.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

