        <span class="ch-jump" id="se-extras-mistakes"></span>
        <div class="cka-gotcha">
            <h5>⚠️ Common Manifest Mistakes on CKA</h5>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Wrong namespace</strong> — Forgetting <code>namespace: easypj</code> in metadata, so object lands in <code>default</code></li>
                <li><strong>Label mismatch</strong> — Service <code>selector</code> doesn't match Deployment's <code>template.metadata.labels</code> → 0 endpoints</li>
                <li><strong>Port mismatch</strong> — Service <code>targetPort</code> doesn't match container <code>containerPort</code> → connection refused</li>
                <li><strong>Secret key typo</strong> — <code>secretKeyRef.key</code> doesn't match actual Secret data key → pod fails to start</li>
                <li><strong>Missing resources</strong> — No <code>resources.requests</code> means scheduler can't account for pod needs → OOM kills</li>
                <li><strong>PVC accessMode</strong> — Using <code>ReadWriteMany</code> with a StorageClass that only supports <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> → PVC stays Pending</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
        <span class="ch-jump" id="se-extras-lab"></span>
        <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
            <h4>🧠 Appendix E — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (8)</h4>
            <p class="section-lead">Scenario-style recall across the whole manifest set — 8 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                <div class="eq-question">The web pods are <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> with "could not connect to server: Connection refused" in their logs. The PostgreSQL StatefulSet shows <code>Running</code>. What's the likely cause, and how do you fix it?</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Two usual suspects: (1) the web Deployment came up before PostgreSQL finished its own startup — <code>Running</code> just means the container process started, not that <code>pg_isready</code> passed yet — or (2) a NetworkPolicy (E.8) is blocking port 5432. Check that <code>allow-postgres-from-web</code> permits ingress <em>from</em> <code>app: easypj-web</code> <em>to</em> <code>app: easypj-postgres</code> on TCP 5432, and confirm <code>DB_HOST</code> in the ConfigMap (E.2) matches the headless Service's exact name.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>This is why E.5's readiness probe (<code>pg_isready</code>) exists — the Service only routes to pods that pass it. The web tier itself has no built-in wait — that's a job-level concern, not something the Deployment manifest solves for you, which is exactly why <code>db-migrate</code> (E.12) runs as a separate Job rather than inline in the web container's startup.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                <div class="eq-question">You applied the HPA (E.11) but <code>kubectl get hpa -n easypj</code> shows <code>TARGETS</code> as <code>&lt;unknown&gt;/70%</code>. Why, and what are the two things to check?</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>Either <strong>metrics-server</strong> isn't installed in the cluster, or the <code>easypj-web</code> Deployment has no <code>resources.requests.cpu</code> set. HPA computes utilization as <code>usage ÷ request</code> — with no request in the denominator, the math is undefined and it reports <code>unknown</code> instead of a percentage.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl top pods -n easypj</code> is the fastest sanity check — if that command itself errors, metrics-server is the problem. If it returns numbers but the HPA still shows <code>unknown</code>, go straight to E.6's <code>resources.requests</code> block.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                <div class="eq-question">During a node drain, all three <code>easypj-web</code> pods got evicted at once and the site went down. How does this appendix's manifest set prevent that, and why didn't it work this time?</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The PodDisruptionBudget (E.10, <code>minAvailable: 2</code>) is designed to force the drain to evict at most 1 web pod at a time. If all 3 went down together, either the PDB was never applied, its <code>selector</code> doesn't actually match <code>app: easypj-web</code> (a silent label typo), or the disruption was <em>involuntary</em> (a node hard-failure, not a graceful drain) — which a PDB cannot prevent by design.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pdb -n easypj</code> should show <code>ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS: 1</code> with 3 healthy replicas. If it shows 0, something else already consumed the budget (e.g. one pod already unhealthy for an unrelated reason) — the PDB is protecting a budget of 1, not guaranteeing 3 are always safe from everything.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                <div class="eq-question">A teammate committed the Secret manifest (E.3) with real values to git. Is base64 encoding enough protection? What's the full remediation?</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>No — base64 is <strong>encoding</strong>, not encryption; anyone can run <code>echo &lt;value&gt; | base64 -d</code>. Rotate <code>DJANGO_SECRET_KEY</code> and <code>DB_PASSWORD</code> / <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code> immediately, purge the values from git history (not just a new commit — history), and move to Sealed Secrets, SOPS, or an external secrets store so real values never touch git again. Enable <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> on the API server for at-rest protection in etcd too.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>Rotating the value alone isn't enough if the old value stays live in git history forever — anyone with repo access (current or former) can still read it. This is the exact gap E.3's gotcha box warns about.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                <div class="eq-question">You need the same manifests for dev (1 replica, <code>DEBUG=True</code>) and prod (3 replicas, <code>DEBUG=False</code>) without maintaining two full copies of every YAML file. Which tool from this appendix, and what's the structure?</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><strong>Kustomize (E.15)</strong>: one <code>base/</code> of plain YAML, plus <code>overlays/dev</code> and <code>overlays/prod</code>, each with a <code>replicas-patch.yaml</code> and a <code>configMapGenerator</code> merge to flip <code>DEBUG</code>. Apply with <code>kubectl apply -k kustomize/overlays/&lt;env&gt;</code>.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>Helm (E.14) could also solve this via <code>-f values-dev.yaml</code> vs <code>-f values-prod.yaml</code> — but the question is specifically about patching YAML you already own without introducing a templating language, which is Kustomize's whole design philosophy (see E.15's Helm-vs-Kustomize decision table).</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                <div class="eq-question">After a fresh deployment, migrations ran fine and the app is reachable, but every page is missing its CSS/JS — it "works" but looks completely broken. What step did the deployment miss?</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>The <strong>collectstatic Job</strong> (E.12, <code>12b-collectstatic-job.yaml</code>) — Django needs <code>manage.py collectstatic</code> to gather every app's static assets into <code>STATIC_ROOT</code> before they can be served. Run it as a Job (or as a build-time step baked into the image) and serve the result via whitenoise, a CDN, or a dedicated Ingress path.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>This is the single most common "it deployed but looks broken" symptom for a freshly containerized Django app — nothing crashes, nothing errors in the logs, the HTML renders, it's just unstyled. See E.12's info-box for why this is a separate Job from <code>db-migrate</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                <div class="eq-question">You need to trigger an ad-hoc database backup right now instead of waiting for the 02:00 <code>db-backup</code> CronJob (E.12). What's the command, and what would you check afterward to confirm it actually worked?</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p><code>kubectl create job --from=cronjob/db-backup backup-now -n easypj</code> — this clones the CronJob's <code>jobTemplate</code> into an immediate one-off Job. Afterward, confirm the dump file actually landed on the <code>easypj-backup-pvc</code> volume (E.12's <code>12d-backup-pvc.yaml</code>) — not just that the Job shows <code>Complete</code>, since a Job can exit 0 while writing to a path with no mounted volume, silently losing the dump (see E.12's backup gotcha box).</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>"The Job succeeded" and "the backup exists" are two different claims — a Job's exit code only confirms the container's process returned 0, never that the file it wrote actually persisted anywhere durable. Always verify the artifact itself, not just the exit code — this is exactly the trap E.12's backup gotcha box warns about with the un-mounted-volume scenario.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                <div class="eq-question">Your platform team wants to switch <code>easypj</code> from Ingress to the Gateway API. Which two objects replace the Ingress, who typically owns each, and what has to be installed first?</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <p>A <strong><code>Gateway</code></strong> (owned by the platform/infra team — listeners, TLS termination, which namespaces may attach) and an <strong><code>HTTPRoute</code></strong> (owned by the app team — hostnames, path matches, <code>backendRefs</code> to <code>easypj-web</code>). Before either can be created, the Gateway API CRDs must be installed separately (they are NOT built into core Kubernetes), and a controller backing some <code>GatewayClass</code> (nginx gateway fabric, Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium) must be running — otherwise the <code>Gateway</code> routes zero traffic, same as an <code>Ingress</code> with no controller. See E.7b.</p>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>The role split (platform vs. app team) is the main real-world reason teams migrate — it lets infra own TLS/listener policy centrally while app teams self-serve routing changes without filing a ticket, something Ingress's flat, single-object model never cleanly supported.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>
        </div>

    </article>

    
