            <!-- ─────────── §F.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sf-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">F.4 Practice Lab Recommendations</h3>

            <p>Reading about <code>kubectl</code> commands and typing them under a two-hour countdown are two completely different skills — the exam doesn't just test whether you know the right command, it tests whether you can produce it fast enough, with your hands already knowing the muscle memory, while a timer erodes your margin for error. That's what practice labs are for: not "learning Kubernetes" (you've done that in Chapters 1–45), but compressing your execution time down to exam pace. The table below ranks platforms by how closely they mimic real exam conditions, because a lab that teaches concepts slowly is not the same tool as one that trains speed.</p>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th style="width:20%;">Platform</th><th style="width:12%;">Cost</th><th style="width:12%;">CKA Sim?</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>killer.sh</strong></td><td>Free (2 sessions with exam purchase)</td><td>⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</td><td><strong>Most realistic.</strong> Included with CKA exam purchase. Harder than real exam. Use both sessions in final week.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>KodeKloud CKA</strong></td><td>~$15-25/mo</td><td>⭐⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Structured course with hands-on labs. Great for beginners. Mock exams included. Lightning labs for speed practice.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Killercoda</strong></td><td>Free</td><td>⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Free browser-based K8s scenarios. Good for daily 15-min practice. By the makers of killer.sh.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Kubernetes the Hard Way</strong></td><td>Free</td><td>⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Kelsey Hightower's manual cluster setup. Deepens understanding but NOT exam format. Do once for learning.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Play with Kubernetes</strong></td><td>Free</td><td>⭐⭐</td><td>4-hour free browser clusters. Good for quick experiments when you don't have local setup.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Local (kubeadm/kind/minikube)</strong></td><td>Free</td><td>⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Practice kubeadm installs, upgrades, etcd backup locally. Closest to exam for cluster-admin tasks.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>Recommended Study Path:</strong> KodeKloud (learn concepts + labs) → Killercoda (daily practice) → killer.sh (final 2 sessions before exam). Practice until you can complete killer.sh with 20+ minutes to spare.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🔁 Repetition Targets — Drill These Until They're Boring</h5>
                <p>Some tasks are worth far more practice reps than others because they are high-weight (Cluster Architecture, 25%) AND easy to get subtly wrong under time pressure the first few times. Set explicit repetition targets rather than "practicing until it feels okay":</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>etcd backup/restore — at least 5 times.</strong> The <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code>/<code>restore</code> flags (<code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code>, <code>--endpoints</code>) are easy to fat-finger once, and a restore that silently points at the wrong data-dir looks like it succeeded until the API server comes back with stale data. Five clean reps builds the reflex to double-check the data-dir and restart order every time.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cluster upgrades — at least 3 times.</strong> The <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> → drain → <code>kubeadm upgrade apply/node</code> → package upgrade → uncordon sequence has several ordering traps (upgrading kubelet before kubeadm, forgetting to drain first). Three full run-throughs on a throwaway multi-node cluster turns a nervous checklist-read into an automatic sequence.</li>
                    <li><strong>Multi-node kubeadm init/join/CNI — until it's muscle memory.</strong> Build kubeadm-the-hard-way style clusters repeatedly (not just once for understanding) so that control-plane init, worker join, and CNI installation stop requiring you to think about the order of operations.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 LAB — Break-and-Fix easypj on a Real kubeadm Cluster</h5>
                <p>The single best practice exercise combines cluster building with troubleshooting, because that's exactly the shape of a real CKA exam: a working system you must diagnose and repair against the clock.</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Stand up a two-node <code>kubeadm</code> cluster (one control-plane, one worker) — this alone drills F.4's init/join/CNI repetition target.</li>
                    <li>Deploy the full easypj / jobpost stack onto it: the PostgreSQL StatefulSet, the Django Deployment, its Service, and an Ingress (or a Gateway/HTTPRoute pair — see F.6) in front of it. Confirm the whole path works end-to-end before breaking anything.</li>
                    <li>Deliberately sabotage one component at a time and time yourself fixing each: delete a Secret the Django Deployment depends on, corrupt a liveness/readiness probe's path or port, taint the worker node so the StatefulSet's Pod can't (re)schedule.</li>
                    <li>For each sabotage, run the F.7 six-step troubleshooting loop from a cold start — don't peek at what you broke. This is the closest rehearsal you can build for the exam's real "diagnose and repair" troubleshooting style, and it exercises Storage, Workloads, Networking, and Troubleshooting domains in a single lab session.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </section>

