        <!-- ═══════════════════════ F.6 What Changed in 2025 ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §F.6 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sf-6">
            <h3 class="section-h">F.6 What Changed in 2025</h3>

            <p>The CKA syllabus is not frozen — the Linux Foundation and CNCF review it roughly once a year and quietly swap in whatever tooling has become mainstream cluster-admin practice. The 2025 revision is the biggest tooling refresh in several cycles: it modernizes the exam's expectations to match how real teams run Kubernetes today, where hand-rolled YAML has largely been replaced by package managers (Helm), overlay-based config management (Kustomize), extensible APIs (CRDs), a next-generation ingress model (Gateway API), and storage that provisions itself on demand. If your study materials predate 2025, this section is the delta you cannot skip.</p>

            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>🗓️ Why This Matters for Your Study Plan</h5>
                <p>These additions are not obscure trivia — they are now examinable, meaning a real performance-based task can ask you to install a Helm chart, apply a Kustomize overlay, read a CRD, or expose a Service through the Gateway API. Skipping this section because "it's new" is exactly how candidates lose easy points on tasks that are actually simpler than the classic YAML equivalents once you know the one or two commands involved.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th style="width:16%;">Area</th><th>What's Now In Scope</th><th>Why It Was Added</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>⎈ Helm</strong></td>
                            <td>Install / upgrade / rollback releases (<code>helm install</code>, <code>helm upgrade</code>, <code>helm rollback</code>); inspect a release's values (<code>helm get values</code>, <code>helm show values</code>); <code>helm.sh/docs</code> is an allowed in-exam documentation site.</td>
                            <td>Helm is the de facto package manager for Kubernetes — the vast majority of production clusters install at least some components (ingress controllers, monitoring stacks, cert-manager) via Helm charts rather than raw manifests. An admin who can't operate a chart lifecycle can't operate a modern cluster.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>📁 Kustomize</strong></td>
                            <td>Bases + overlays directory layout; deploying with <code>kubectl apply -k</code> (no separate binary needed — Kustomize is built into kubectl); patches (strategic-merge and JSON 6902) and common labels/annotations via <code>commonLabels</code> / <code>labels</code>.</td>
                            <td>Kustomize is Kubernetes' native answer to "the same app, different environments" — one base manifest set, then dev/staging/prod overlays that patch just the differences. It ships inside <code>kubectl</code> itself, so there is zero excuse not to know it.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>🧩 CRDs</strong></td>
                            <td>Reading and applying CustomResourceDefinitions and the custom resources they define; inspecting them with <code>kubectl get crd</code> and discovering their schema with <code>kubectl explain &lt;custom-kind&gt;</code> exactly like a built-in resource.</td>
                            <td>The entire Kubernetes ecosystem (cert-manager, Prometheus Operator, Argo CD, service meshes) is built on CRDs. You will never author a CRD's schema on the exam, but you must be comfortable reading one, applying an instance of it, and troubleshooting it with the same <code>describe</code>/<code>get events</code> reflexes you use for Pods.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>🚪 Gateway API</strong></td>
                            <td><code>GatewayClass</code> / <code>Gateway</code> / <code>HTTPRoute</code> as the designated successor to Ingress; <code>gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io</code> is an allowed in-exam documentation site alongside kubernetes.io.</td>
                            <td>Ingress has known limitations (one annotation dialect per controller, weak support for multi-protocol/multi-team routing). Gateway API is the community's role-oriented, portable replacement, and 2025 marks the point where the exam expects you to be fluent in both the old and new models simultaneously.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>💾 Dynamic provisioning</strong></td>
                            <td>StorageClasses and PVCs that auto-create their backing PVs (no admin pre-provisioning step); reclaim policies (<code>Retain</code> vs <code>Delete</code>) and volume binding modes (<code>Immediate</code> vs <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code>).</td>
                            <td>Static PV pre-provisioning is still testable, but real clusters overwhelmingly rely on a default StorageClass to provision storage on demand. The exam now expects you to reason about which reclaim policy and binding mode a given scenario needs, not just wire up a static PV/PVC pair.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Old Core vs. 2025 Additions — What Stayed, What's New</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────── STILL CORE (unchanged) ──────────────────────────┐
  │  Pods · Deployments · Services · ConfigMaps/Secrets · RBAC · NetworkPolicy│
  │  Static PV/PVC · kubeadm init/join/upgrade · etcd backup/restore         │
  │  Ingress (still fully examinable — NOT replaced, only joined)            │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                       +
  ┌─────────────────────── 2025 ADDITIONS (new surface) ─────────────────────┐
  │  Helm install/upgrade/rollback   │  Kustomize bases + overlays (-k)      │
  │  CRDs: get/explain/apply         │  Gateway API: GatewayClass/Gateway/   │
  │                                    │  HTTPRoute (alongside Ingress)      │
  │  Dynamic provisioning: StorageClass → auto-created PV, reclaim policy,   │
  │  volume binding mode                                                     │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>🔑 MUST-REMEMBER: Ingress Is Not Retired</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>Ingress is <strong>still fully on the exam</strong> — Gateway API was <em>added</em>, it did not <em>replace</em> anything in the syllabus. Expect either model to show up, sometimes in the same exam sitting.</li>
                    <li>Know how the <strong>same Service</strong> can be exposed both ways: an <code>Ingress</code> resource with <code>rules[].http.paths[]</code>, or a <code>Gateway</code> + <code>HTTPRoute</code> pair with <code>parentRefs</code>/<code>backendRefs</code>. Being able to translate one model to the other under time pressure is exactly the kind of task the exam rewards.</li>
                    <li>Since both <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code> and <code>gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io</code> are allowed in-exam, the winning move is never to memorize either resource's full field list — it's to know <em>which page to open</em> and copy the working example straight from the docs, then edit the host/path/service name for your task.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>Study Priority:</strong> Don't treat F.6 as a separate study block bolted onto the end — practice Helm, Kustomize, and Gateway API tasks interleaved with your normal Networking and Cluster Architecture drills. In the real exam these additions are woven into ordinary tasks (e.g. "install this chart, then expose it"), not isolated into their own dedicated questions.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ F.7 Exam-Day Doc Bookmarks ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §F.7 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sf-7">
            <h3 class="section-h">F.7 Exam-Day Doc Bookmarks</h3>

            <p>The CKA is explicitly <strong>open-book</strong> — the exam UI ships a browser tab that can load exactly four domains: <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code>, <code>kubernetes.io/blog</code>, <code>helm.sh/docs</code>, and <code>gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io</code> (Gateway API is CKA-only — see F.6). Nothing else loads — no Stack Overflow, no ChatGPT, no GitHub, no personal notes app, no second monitor with your own bookmarks. Per the Linux Foundation's official resources-allowed policy you may have only one of these four tabs open at a time, though you're free to switch which allowed site is loaded in it whenever you like. That makes the allowed sites your <em>only</em> external memory for the full two hours, so treating them as a last resort instead of a first-class tool is the single biggest unforced error candidates make.</p>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>⚡ The Core Exam-Day Technique</h5>
                <p>Never scroll a docs page hunting for the right section — <strong>search it</strong>. Every allowed site has a search box or you can use <code>Ctrl+F</code>/browser find once the page is loaded. Once you find the example manifest for the resource you need, <strong>copy the whole YAML block straight into your terminal file</strong> (vim's <code>:set paste</code> first, so auto-indent doesn't mangle it) and edit only the fields the task actually asks for — names, namespaces, selectors, ports. This is dramatically faster and more accurate than typing a NetworkPolicy or Gateway manifest from memory, and it's exactly why the exam is open-book in the first place: Kubernetes rewards administrators who know <em>where</em> to look, not ones who've memorized every field.</p>
            </div>

            <p>The table below is your pre-bookmark list. Open every one of these pages in the allowed tab <strong>before</strong> the clock starts (during the setup/check-in phase, or as your first 60 seconds if the browser only becomes available once the exam begins), so that under pressure you are just switching tabs, not typing search queries from scratch.</p>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th style="width:20%;">Page</th><th style="width:38%;">Path (relative to kubernetes.io unless noted)</th><th>Used For / When You Reach For It</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubectl Cheat Sheet</strong></td><td><code>/reference/kubectl/cheatsheet/</code></td><td>The single highest-value bookmark — every common imperative command, output format, and resource shorthand in one page. Your default first stop when you blank on exact flag syntax.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubeadm upgrade</strong></td><td><code>/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/kubeadm-upgrade/</code></td><td>The exact <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> / <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> sequence plus the kubelet/kubectl package-upgrade steps per node — copy the drain → upgrade → uncordon order verbatim.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd backup &amp; restore</strong></td><td><code>/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/</code></td><td>The full <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> / <code>snapshot restore</code> commands with the correct <code>--cacert</code>/<code>--cert</code>/<code>--key</code> flags — these paths differ per cluster and are painful to recall from memory under pressure.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>RBAC</strong></td><td><code>/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/</code></td><td>Role vs ClusterRole, RoleBinding vs ClusterRoleBinding YAML shape, and the verb/resource/apiGroup field layout — copy the example Role and edit verbs/resources for the task's exact permissions.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Network Policies</strong></td><td><code>/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/</code></td><td>The <code>podSelector</code>/<code>ingress</code>/<code>egress</code> block structure is easy to get subtly wrong from memory (namespaceSelector vs podSelector, missing the <code>- {}</code> empty rule for "allow all"). Copy a worked example and adapt the selectors.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Ingress</strong></td><td><code>/concepts/services-networking/ingress/</code></td><td>Full Ingress resource shape with <code>rules[].http.paths[]</code>, <code>pathType</code>, and <code>backend.service</code> — the classic L7 routing task.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Gateway API</strong></td><td><code>gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io</code> (separate allowed domain)</td><td><code>GatewayClass</code>/<code>Gateway</code>/<code>HTTPRoute</code> examples for the 2025-syllabus Ingress successor — see F.6. This is a different site than kubernetes.io, so bookmark it separately.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Persistent Volumes</strong></td><td><code>/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/</code></td><td>PV/PVC field reference — <code>accessModes</code>, <code>capacity</code>, <code>persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy</code>, and how a PVC's <code>storageClassName</code> and selectors bind it to a matching PV.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Storage Classes</strong></td><td><code>/concepts/storage/storage-classes/</code></td><td>Dynamic-provisioning parameters — <code>provisioner</code>, <code>reclaimPolicy</code>, <code>volumeBindingMode</code> — the 2025-syllabus companion to Persistent Volumes (see F.6).</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Configure Pod/Container</strong></td><td><code>/tasks/configure-pod-container/</code></td><td>An umbrella section covering liveness/readiness/startup probes, resource requests/limits, security contexts, and env-from-ConfigMap/Secret patterns — bookmark the index page and drill into the specific sub-task from there.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Pod Security Standards</strong></td><td><code>/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/</code></td><td>The Privileged/Baseline/Restricted level definitions and exactly which fields each level forbids — needed when a task asks you to label a namespace for enforcement or fix a Pod that violates a given level.</td></tr>
