            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🩺 Memorize This Loop — It Solves Most of the 30% Troubleshooting Domain</h5>
                <p>Troubleshooting is the single highest-weighted domain (see F.2), and unlike Helm/Kustomize/Gateway API syntax, you cannot simply copy the fix from a docs page — you have to diagnose first. The workflow below is the same six-step loop experienced admins run reflexively in production, and it is exactly what the exam's troubleshooting tasks are built to reward. Run it in order, every time, without skipping steps even when you think you already know the answer — jumping straight to a guessed fix is how candidates "fix" the wrong thing and waste their remaining time budget.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">The Six-Step Troubleshooting Loop</div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  1. kubectl get nodes / pods -A
     → Scan for NotReady nodes or Pods not in Running/Completed. This is
       your triage step — it tells you WHERE to look before you dig in.

  2. kubectl describe &lt;object&gt;
     → Read the Events block at the BOTTOM first, not the spec at the top.
       Events tell you what the control plane actually tried and failed
       to do (ImagePullBackOff, FailedScheduling, FailedMount, etc.).

  3. kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; [-p]
     → Application-level errors live here. Use -p (--previous) when the
       container has already crashed and restarted — the CURRENT log may
       just be the fresh, empty start of the new attempt.

  4. kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp
     → Cluster-wide chronological view. Useful when the failure isn't on
       the object you're inspecting but upstream (e.g. a node cordoned,
       a webhook rejecting admission, a quota exceeded elsewhere).

  5. On the node itself: systemctl status kubelet; journalctl -u kubelet
     → For control-plane/node-level failures (kubelet won't start, static
       pod manifest malformed, CNI plugin missing) that never surface in
       kubectl output because the API server itself is what's unhealthy.

  6. Fix → re-apply → kubectl get to CONFIRM the fix landed
     → Never assume a fix worked. Re-run the same get/describe you used
       to diagnose it and see the object actually reach the healthy
       state before moving to the next question.
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p>Notice the shape of the loop: steps 1-4 are almost pure <code>kubectl</code> (works from your local terminal against any context), while step 5 requires <code>sudo</code> access on the node itself — which the exam grants you specifically for this class of problem. A common failure mode is candidates who only know steps 1-4 and get stuck on any fault that lives below the API server (a misconfigured kubelet, a missing CNI binary, a corrupted static pod manifest) because they never practiced dropping onto the node. Practice step 5 deliberately — it covers a disproportionate share of the hardest Cluster Architecture and Troubleshooting tasks.</p>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>Bookmark discipline:</strong> 17 tabs open at once is chaos, not speed. Instead, bookmark the pages above in your browser's bookmarks bar (not just as open tabs) during setup, grouped left-to-right roughly in the order you're likely to need them: cheat sheet and RBAC/NetworkPolicy first (used constantly), then storage/Ingress/Gateway API, then the specialized ones (TLS, DNS debugging, JSONPath) you'll only open once or twice. A one-click bookmarks bar beats re-searching from a blank tab every time.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

