            <div class="exam-question-item">
                <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                <div class="eq-question">You restored an etcd snapshot but the API server still shows the old, stale data. What's the first thing to check?</div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                    <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">confirm the data-dir, then force a static-pod reload</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Confirm the static pod manifest points at the RESTORED data-dir</span>
grep -A2 data-dir /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. Force kube-apiserver/etcd to pick up the new data (no dynamic reload):</span>
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml /tmp/ &amp;&amp; sleep 20 &amp;&amp; \
  sudo mv /tmp/etcd.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

<span class="comment"># 3. Verify the control plane is back and serving the restored data:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="eq-explanation">
                        <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                        <p>A restore that silently points at the wrong data-dir looks like it succeeded — the command exits cleanly — but the running API server keeps serving from its old in-memory/on-disk state until it's actually restarted against the new snapshot. This is exactly why F.4 recommends practicing etcd backup/restore at least 5 times: the failure mode is invisible until you specifically check for it.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
