        <section class="section-block" id="sh-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">H.3 etcd Restore [8 min]</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📋 Task</h5>
                <p>A previous etcd snapshot exists at <code>/opt/etcd-backup/snapshot.db</code>. Restore etcd from this snapshot to a new data directory <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>. Then update the etcd static pod to use the restored data directory.</p>
            </div>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>💡 Hints</strong></summary>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>Approach</h5>
                    <p>Two steps: (1) <code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code> to a new directory, (2) edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> to point <code>--data-dir</code> and the hostPath volume to the new path. The kubelet will auto-restart etcd.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>✅ Solution</strong></summary>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">solution-h3.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Restore snapshot to new directory</span>
export ETCDCTL_API=3
etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup/snapshot.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Edit the etcd static pod manifest</span>
<span class="comment"># Change --data-dir flag AND the hostPath volume</span>
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># In the container spec, change:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># To:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># In the volumes section, change:</span>
<span class="comment">#   hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#     path: /var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># To:</span>
<span class="comment">#   hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#     path: /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Wait for etcd to restart (kubelet detects manifest change)</span>
<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd
kubectl get nodes  <span class="comment"># Should show Ready</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Critical: TWO Changes Required</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>You must change BOTH the <code>--data-dir</code> flag AND the <code>hostPath.path</code> in the volume mount</li>
                        <li>If you only change one, etcd will fail to start and the entire cluster goes down</li>
                        <li>Don't panic if <code>kubectl</code> stops working temporarily — the API server depends on etcd</li>
                        <li>Wait 1-2 minutes for the kubelet to detect the manifest change and restart the pod</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </details>
        </section>

        <!-- H.4 -->
            <!-- ─────────── §H.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sh-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">H.4 Cluster Upgrade — Control Plane [10 min]</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📋 Task</h5>
                <p>Upgrade the control plane node <code>controlplane</code> from Kubernetes v1.30.0 to v1.31.0. Upgrade kubeadm first, then the control plane components, then kubelet and kubectl. Ensure the node is schedulable after the upgrade.</p>
            </div>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>💡 Hints</strong></summary>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>Approach</h5>
                    <p>Order matters: (1) upgrade kubeadm, (2) <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code>, (3) <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>, (4) drain node, (5) upgrade kubelet+kubectl, (6) restart kubelet, (7) uncordon. On Debian/Ubuntu, use <code>apt-mark unhold</code> → install → <code>apt-mark hold</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>✅ Solution</strong></summary>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">solution-h4.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Upgrade kubeadm</span>
sudo apt-mark unhold kubeadm
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.0-*
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify upgrade plan</span>
sudo kubeadm upgrade plan

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Apply the upgrade</span>
sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Drain the control plane node</span>
kubectl drain controlplane --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Upgrade kubelet and kubectl</span>
sudo apt-mark unhold kubelet kubectl
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.0-* kubectl=1.31.0-*
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubectl

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Restart kubelet</span>
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Uncordon the node</span>
kubectl uncordon controlplane

<span class="comment"># Step 8: Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># controlplane should show v1.31.0 and Ready</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Upgrade Order is Critical</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Always upgrade kubeadm <strong>before</strong> running <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code></li>
                        <li>Always upgrade kubelet <strong>after</strong> the control plane components</li>
                        <li>Never skip minor versions — upgrade one version at a time (1.30→1.31, not 1.29→1.31)</li>
                        <li>Don't forget <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> before restarting kubelet</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </details>
        </section>

        <!-- H.5 -->
            <!-- ─────────── §H.5 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sh-5">
            <h3 class="section-h">H.5 Cluster Upgrade — Worker Node [7 min]</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📋 Task</h5>
                <p>Upgrade worker node <code>worker-1</code> to Kubernetes v1.31.0. The control plane has already been upgraded. Ensure workloads are safely evicted during the upgrade and the node is schedulable afterwards.</p>
            </div>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>💡 Hints</strong></summary>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>Approach</h5>
                    <p>From the control plane: drain the worker. SSH to the worker: upgrade kubeadm, run <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> (NOT <code>apply</code>), upgrade kubelet+kubectl, restart. Back on control plane: uncordon.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>✅ Solution</strong></summary>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">solution-h5.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># FROM CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 1: Drain the worker node</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># SSH TO WORKER NODE:</span>
ssh worker-1

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Upgrade kubeadm</span>
sudo apt-mark unhold kubeadm
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.0-*
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Upgrade node config (NOT "apply" — that's for control plane only)</span>
sudo kubeadm upgrade node

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Upgrade kubelet and kubectl</span>
sudo apt-mark unhold kubelet kubectl
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.0-* kubectl=1.31.0-*
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubectl

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Restart kubelet</span>
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Exit back to control plane</span>
exit

<span class="comment"># FROM CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 7: Uncordon the worker</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker-1 should show v1.31.0 and Ready</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Worker vs Control Plane Difference</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Workers use <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> — NOT <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code></li>
                        <li>You must SSH to the worker to run upgrade commands — kubectl runs on control plane</li>
                        <li>Drain happens from control plane, upgrade happens on the worker node itself</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </details>
        </section>

    </article>

