            <!-- ─────────── §I.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sI-1">
            <h3 class="section-h">I.1 Well-Known Node Labels</h3>
            <p>These labels are automatically populated by the kubelet or cloud provider. They are used by the scheduler for node affinity, topology spread constraints, and zone-aware scheduling.</p>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Example Value</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Set By</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code></td><td><code>node-1</code></td><td>Node hostname — used for per-node affinity/anti-affinity</td><td>kubelet</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/os</code></td><td><code>linux</code></td><td>Operating system (linux/windows)</td><td>kubelet</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/arch</code></td><td><code>amd64</code></td><td>CPU architecture (amd64/arm64)</td><td>kubelet</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code></td><td><code>us-east-1a</code></td><td>Cloud availability zone — zone-aware scheduling</td><td>cloud provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>topology.kubernetes.io/region</code></td><td><code>us-east-1</code></td><td>Cloud region — region-aware scheduling</td><td>cloud provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/instance-type</code></td><td><code>m5.xlarge</code></td><td>Cloud instance type — schedule by machine size</td><td>cloud provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name</code></td><td><code>easypj</code></td><td>Auto-set to namespace/node name — used in selectors</td><td>API server</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — using well-known labels</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View all labels on a node</span>
kubectl get node node-1 --show-labels

<span class="comment"># Schedule easypj pods only on Linux amd64 nodes</span>
kubectl get nodes -l kubernetes.io/os=linux,kubernetes.io/arch=amd64

<span class="comment"># Find nodes in a specific availability zone</span>
kubectl get nodes -l topology.kubernetes.io/zone=us-east-1a

<span class="comment"># Use in node affinity for easypj deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># spec.affinity.nodeAffinity.requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:</span>
<span class="comment">#   nodeSelectorTerms:</span>
<span class="comment">#     - matchExpressions:</span>
<span class="comment">#         - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone</span>
<span class="comment">#           operator: In</span>
<span class="comment">#           values: ["us-east-1a", "us-east-1b"]</span></code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §I.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sI-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">I.2 Well-Known Annotations</h3>
            <p>Annotations hold non-identifying metadata. These well-known annotations are recognized by Kubernetes components and common tools.</p>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Annotation</th><th>Used On</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code></td><td>Deployments</td><td>Records why a rollout happened — shown in <code>kubectl rollout history</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/description</code></td><td>Any resource</td><td>Human-readable description of the resource</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration</code></td><td>Any resource</td><td>Stores the last <code>kubectl apply</code> manifest — used for 3-way merge</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>scheduler.alpha.kubernetes.io/defaultTolerations</code></td><td>Namespaces</td><td>Default tolerations applied to all pods in namespace</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class</code></td><td>StorageClass</td><td>Marks the default StorageClass (<code>"true"</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/ingress.class</code></td><td>Ingress</td><td>Legacy ingress controller selector (replaced by <code>ingressClassName</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/exclude-from-external-load-balancers</code></td><td>Nodes</td><td>Exclude node from cloud load balancer backends</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — working with annotations</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Record change cause for rollout history</span>
kubectl annotate deployment easypj-web -n easypj \
  kubernetes.io/change-cause="Updated to v2.1 with new job filters"

<span class="comment"># View rollout history (shows change-cause)</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         Initial deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         Updated to v2.1 with new job filters</span>

<span class="comment"># Or use --record flag (deprecated but still works)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web django=easypj/jobpost:v2.2 -n easypj --record

<span class="comment"># Check the default StorageClass</span>
kubectl get storageclass -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.metadata.annotations["storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class"]=="true") | .metadata.name'</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §I.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sI-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">I.3 Well-Known Taints</h3>
            <p>Taints are automatically applied by the node lifecycle controller or kubelet. Understanding them is critical for troubleshooting scheduling issues.</p>

                <!-- Flow diagram: taint effect decision -->
                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #2c3e50; color: white;">Pod Submitted</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #e67e22; color: white;">Node Has Taint?</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #27ae60; color: white;">Pod Tolerates It?</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #3498db; color: white;">✅ Scheduled</div>
                </div>
                <div class="flow-diagram" style="margin-top: 8px; justify-content: center;">
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #e74c3c; color: white;">NoSchedule → Blocked</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">|</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #c0392b; color: white;">NoExecute → Evicted</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">|</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #f39c12; color: #333;">PreferNoSchedule → Soft Avoid</div>
                </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Taint Key</th><th>Effect</th><th>When Applied</th><th>Impact</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>By kubeadm on control plane nodes</td>
                            <td>Prevents workloads on control plane (add toleration to override)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/not-ready</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code> + <code>NoExecute</code></td>
                            <td>Node condition NotReady</td>
                            <td>Stops scheduling; evicts pods after <code>tolerationSeconds</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code> + <code>NoExecute</code></td>
                            <td>Node condition Unknown (network issue)</td>
                            <td>Same as not-ready but for network partition</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>Disk usage exceeds eviction threshold</td>
                            <td>Stops new pods from scheduling; does NOT evict existing</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/memory-pressure</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>Memory usage exceeds eviction threshold</td>
                            <td>Stops new pods from scheduling</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/pid-pressure</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>PID usage exceeds eviction threshold</td>
                            <td>Stops new pods from scheduling</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/network-unavailable</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>Node's network is not configured (pre-CNI)</td>
                            <td>Removed once CNI plugin sets up networking</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td><code>kubectl cordon</code> or <code>kubectl drain</code></td>
                            <td>Prevents new pods; existing pods continue running</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — working with taints</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View taints on a node</span>
kubectl describe node node-1 | grep -A5 Taints

<span class="comment"># Check all taints across all nodes</span>
kubectl get nodes -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | .metadata.name + ": " + 
  ([.spec.taints[]? | .key + "=" + (.value // "") + ":" + .effect] | join(", "))'

<span class="comment"># Tolerate control-plane taint (e.g., for single-node clusters)</span>
<span class="comment"># In pod spec:</span>
<span class="comment"># tolerations:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - key: "node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane"</span>
<span class="comment">#     operator: "Exists"</span>
<span class="comment">#     effect: "NoSchedule"</span>

<span class="comment"># Schedule easypj pods with custom tolerations</span>
kubectl run easypj-debug --image=easypj/jobpost:v2.1 -n easypj \
  --overrides='{"spec":{"tolerations":[{"key":"dedicated","operator":"Equal","value":"easypj","effect":"NoSchedule"}]}}'

<span class="comment"># Remove a taint</span>
kubectl taint nodes node-1 dedicated=easypj:NoSchedule-

<span class="comment"># Common troubleshooting: pod stuck Pending due to taints</span>
kubectl describe pod &lt;pending-pod&gt; | grep -A3 Events
<span class="comment"># 0/3 nodes are available: 3 node(s) had untolerated taint {key: value}</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>Taint Troubleshooting on the CKA</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Pod stuck Pending</strong> — check <code>kubectl describe pod</code> Events for "untolerated taint" messages</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Pods evicted after drain</strong> — <code>NoExecute</code> taints evict pods that don't tolerate them. DaemonSet pods tolerate everything by default</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Toleration matching</strong> — <code>operator: Exists</code> matches any value; <code>operator: Equal</code> requires exact value match</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Empty key + Exists</strong> — tolerates ALL taints (used by DaemonSets): <code>{"operator": "Exists"}</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §I.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sI-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">I.4 Recommended Labels for Applications</h3>
            <p>These labels follow the Kubernetes recommended labeling convention (<code>app.kubernetes.io/*</code>) and are used by tools like Helm, ArgoCD, and kubectl.</p>

            <!-- Anatomy card: how labels fit together -->
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>🧬 Anatomy of easypj Labels</h5>
                <p>Think of these labels as a hierarchy: <strong>part-of</strong> (project) → <strong>name</strong> (app) → <strong>component</strong> (role) → <strong>instance</strong> (deployment) → <strong>version</strong> (release)</p>
            </div>
            <div class="lifecycle-chain">
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #8e44ad; color: white;">part-of: easypj</div>
                <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #2980b9; color: white;">name: jobpost</div>
                <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #27ae60; color: white;">component: web</div>
                <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #e67e22; color: white;">instance: easypj-web</div>
                <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #c0392b; color: white;">version: v2.1</div>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Example Value</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/name</code></td><td><code>jobpost</code></td><td>The name of the application</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/instance</code></td><td><code>easypj-web</code></td><td>A unique instance name (for multiple deployments)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/version</code></td><td><code>v2.1</code></td><td>Current application version</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/component</code></td><td><code>frontend</code></td><td>Component within the architecture (frontend, backend, db)</td></tr>
